window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-08-04T10:59:02.767542Z","count":288,"pages":[{"title":"Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-ufo-cases-in-ain-still-resist/","description":"Ain has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation? Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation? Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation? Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation? Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation? Ain has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history. Ain has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a celebrated “landing”, a pilot confrontation or a long running mass flap. ain Introduction The most important case is now the Montluel sighting of 14 May 2022. A witness reported a large, silent, dark rectangular form crossing the night sky, and the French government’s investigation unit classified it as unexplained in March 2026. Yet this does not mean that an extraordinary craft was proved to exist. Ain’s records repeatedly demonstrate the difference between an unidentified observation, a weak report and a genuinely strong mystery: the outcome depends less on how strange a story sounds than on whether investigators can reconstruct its timing, direction, apparent size, weather conditions and possible conventional causes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... Montluel: Ain’s strongest unresolved case At about 3.10 am on 14 May 2022, a single witness standing on a balcony in Montluel noticed what he described as a grey, anthracite coloured rectangular form moving from west to east. It appeared from behind a hill to the south west, travelled horizontally for roughly eight seconds and disappeared behind another hill to the south east. According to the account, it carried no visible lights, left no trail and made no sound. The witness also reported a faint white halo and an apparent distortion of clouds around the form. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... These details would ordinarily be difficult to assess because there was no photograph, video, radar return or second independent observer. What elevated the case above many single witness reports was the amount of follow up information available. The witness completed GEIPAN’s technical questionnaire three days after the event and later cooperated with a site reconstruction. Investigators used the local terrain and reported line of sight to estimate that anything passing behind the final hill would have needed to be at least about 15 metres long and travelling at no less than approximately 130 kilometres per hour. GEIPAN judged the resulting file to have relatively strong informational “consistency”, meaning that a useful amount of apparently reliable and testable detail had been collected. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... The investigation considered aircraft, a dirigible, a low cloud, insects and other possibilities. GEIPAN noted that some calculated distances, sizes and speeds could be compatible with an aircraft, but found no proposed explanation that satisfactorily accounted for the complete description. The case was therefore placed in category D, reserved for observations that remain unidentified after investigation, with a moderately high strangeness score and a comparatively strong consistency score. The classification was published in its revised form in March 2026. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... That conclusion must be read carefully. It establishes that GEIPAN did not identify the reported phenomenon, not that the witness’s estimates were exact or that an unknown machine was independently detected. Apparent size is particularly uncertain when an object has no known distance, and unusual impressions such as cloud distortion may be perceptual rather than physical. GEIPAN itself stresses that it works from testimony, often from only one person, and that estimates of distance, speed and trajectory can be altered by normal limits of perception and memory. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Even with those reservations, Montluel is the most substantial unresolved entry currently visible in Ain’s official record. It combines prompt reporting, a cooperative witness, a terrain reconstruction and an unsuccessful search for a conventional match. What it lacks is equally important: independent corroboration or instrumental evidence that could confirm the object’s presence, dimensions or movement. The 2007 “diamond” that followed the wind On 2 October 2007, a resident near Neuville les Dames saw a large, multicoloured diamond like form moving slowly and silently at apparently low altitude. The witness followed it by car for five or six kilometres towards Vonnas, took photographs and encountered other people who were also watching it. A notice in the regional newspaper Le Progrès subsequently brought forward further reports. At first glance, the episode had many of the features that make a UFO case sound persuasive: several witnesses, daylight observation, sustained duration, movement across the landscape and photographs. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHATILLON SUR CHALARONNE (01) 02.10.2007 GEIPAN... The same abundance of information, however, made the case easier to explain. Investigators reconstructed a movement from south south west towards north north east. Its estimated average speed was about 20 kilometres per hour, close to the approximately 22 kilometre per hour southerly wind measured at Lyon. The object’s shape and colours also resembled a cluster of small metallic party balloons filled with helium. GEIPAN consequently classified the sighting as category B: probably identified, in this instance as festive balloons carried by the wind. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHATILLON SUR CHALARONNE (01) 02.10.2007 GEIPAN... This is one of Ain’s most useful cases because it reverses a common assumption. More witnesses do not automatically make an extraordinary interpretation more likely. They can strengthen confidence that something real was present while simultaneously giving investigators enough viewpoints, timings and trajectory information to discover an ordinary cause. In the Vonnas case, the testimony was considered highly consistent, but the phenomenon itself became less mysterious once its speed and direction were compared with the weather. The photographs did not settle the matter by revealing a perfectly recognisable object. Instead, they formed one part of a broader reconstruction that included witness locations, reported movement and meteorological data. That is typical of stronger UFO investigations: no single dramatic clue carries the conclusion. A green fireball over Bourg en Bresse During the night of 16–17 April 2010, a motorist and her daughter saw a fast moving luminous phenomenon near Bourg en Bresse. They described a descending green ball that divided into two before disappearing. Without comparative information, the colour change and splitting could easily have been interpreted as the manoeuvre or breakup of an unusual craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... GEIPAN instead matched the report to a bright meteor, or fireball. The observation time, direction, apparent high altitude path and green light were consistent with a meteor recorded and photographed by an amateur monitoring station at Chaligny, south of Nancy. The green colour was associated with atmospheric ionisation during the object’s passage, while fragmentation accounted for the apparent division into two parts. The case was classified B as a probable atmospheric entry. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... This case carries more evidential weight than a generic suggestion that the witness “probably saw a meteor”. A separate station recorded the same event, allowing investigators to compare the Ain testimony with an observation made from far away. It illustrates why reports spread across several departments can be especially valuable. A brilliant meteor may look close to each observer even when it is more than 100 kilometres away, and its apparent horizontal or descending motion can vary with viewing geometry. The Bourg en Bresse event also cautions against treating colour, fragmentation or rapid acceleration as inherently exotic. Meteors can produce intense green, blue or orange light, break into separate luminous pieces and vanish suddenly. In an unprepared eyewitness account, those natural features may resemble powered movement or deliberate action. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Weak records are not unresolved mysteries Ain’s archive also contains reports that sound unusual but cannot support a firm conclusion. On 22 April 2017, a witness in Ambérieu en Bugey reported three stationary lights arranged horizontally, followed several minutes later by two additional lights forming an L shaped pattern. The lights were said to remain"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-5f73e4/","description":"Balloons, meteors and incomplete testimony show why dramatic sightings can weaken once weather, trajectory and independent records are checked.","breadcrumb":"Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases How Ain's Strangest Sightings Became Less Mysterious Ain UFO Files Explained Cases Explained Cases How Ain's Strangest Sightings Became Less Mysterious Balloons, meteors and incomplete testimony show why dramatic sightings can weaken once weather, trajectory and independent records are checked. Balloons, meteors and incomplete testimony show why dramatic sightings can weaken once weather, trajectory and independent records are checked. dataset evidence The diamond shaped balloon cluster The green fireball over Bourg en Bresse Why weak evidence is not a mystery ain explained cases Introduction Several of Ain’s most dramatic UFO reports became less mysterious when investigators stopped concentrating on appearance and checked movement, weather and independent records. A large, silent “diamond” followed across the countryside in 2007 moved at almost the same speed and in the same direction as the wind, making a cluster of helium balloons the most likely explanation. A green fireball seen near Bourg en Bresse in 2010 matched a meteor independently recorded by an observing station more than 100 kilometres away. Other reports could not be tested because essential details were missing. These cases matter because they show that an ordinary explanation does not require witnesses to be dishonest or foolish. Real objects can look extraordinary when their distance is unknown, reflective surfaces change shape in sunlight, or a brief event is seen from a moving car. They also show why “unidentified” and “unexplained” are not interchangeable: sometimes the evidence supports a conventional cause, while sometimes there is simply too little evidence to reach any conclusion. The diamond shaped balloon cluster At about 1 pm on 2 October 2007, a man at Neuville les Dames noticed what appeared to be a large, multicoloured diamond shaped object moving slowly and silently at low altitude. He followed it by car for roughly five or six kilometres towards Vonnas, took photographs and encountered other people watching the same phenomenon. Later enquiries and a newspaper appeal brought the number of reported witnesses still higher. Some described an object as large as a house; others saw a rounded diamond, triangular surfaces, metallic reflections or something hanging beneath it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHATILLON SUR CHALARONNE (01) 02.10.2007 GEIPAN... On first reading, the case had several features normally associated with a strong UFO report: daylight visibility, a long observation, photographs and multiple witnesses at different locations. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for investigating unusual aerial phenomena, therefore sent a team to Ain. Investigators interviewed witnesses, retraced the vehicle routes, consulted the gendarmerie and examined possible balloon activity in the region. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete67 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The decisive evidence came not from the object’s apparent shape but from its reconstructed track. Timing information embedded in the photographs suggested that it covered approximately 7.5 kilometres in 22 minutes, giving an average speed of about 20 kilometres per hour. Its probable south south west to north north east movement closely followed the southerly wind recorded at Lyon–Saint Exupéry, where speeds began at about 22 kilometres per hour. Periods when the object seemed stationary were also compatible with gusty airflow rather than controlled manoeuvring. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHATILLON SUR CHALARONNE (01) 02.10.2007 GEIPAN... Investigators considered several kinds of lighter than air object. A conventional hot air balloon fitted the speed but not the witnesses’ descriptions, and experienced observers said they had neither heard a burner nor seen its flame. Decorative paper balloons associated with a recent aerial festival resembled the reported diamond form, but their normal burning time was too short to fit the full observation. GEIPAN ultimately preferred a cluster of reflective Mylar party balloons filled with helium. Two witness details were particularly relevant: one person saw what looked like a cord hanging below, while another thought the “object” was made from several balloons forming a rounded diamond. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete67 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The precise launch was never identified, so the explanation is not proved in the strictest sense. GEIPAN classified the case as B, meaning a probable identification rather than certainty. Nevertheless, the balloon hypothesis explains the slow silent movement, shifting appearance, metallic reflections, irregular drift and close agreement with the wind without requiring an unknown aircraft. GEIPAN’s conclusion was that the file was highly informative but not highly strange: the many witnesses and photographs strengthened the reconstruction, yet that reconstruction weakened the extraordinary interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHATILLON SUR CHALARONNE (01) 02.10.2007 GEIPAN... The case also demonstrates why several witnesses do not automatically provide several independent measurements. People standing near one another can accurately confirm that something was present while still sharing the same uncertainty about its distance. A small nearby balloon cluster and a very large distant craft can subtend a similar angle in the sky. Once an object’s distance is guessed incorrectly, estimates of its height, dimensions and speed can all become exaggerated. The green fireball over Bourg en Bresse A different form of comparison resolved the report from the night of 16–17 April 2010. At about 1.10 am, a woman driving near Bourg en Bresse and her daughter saw a bright object move extremely rapidly across the sky. During its descending passage, the green luminous ball appeared to split into two before disappearing. Seen without context, the colour, speed and fragmentation could easily suggest a damaged or manoeuvring craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... In this case, however, investigators had an independent physical record. An amateur meteor station at Chaligny, south of Nancy, photographed a bright meteor at the relevant time. GEIPAN compared that recording with the witnesses’ questionnaire, drawings and reconstructed viewing angle from inside the car. The timing, direction, high altitude trajectory and rapid movement were consistent with the recorded event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... The green colour was not treated as evidence of exotic propulsion. Bright meteors can produce vivid colours as the incoming body and surrounding atmosphere are heated and ionised. Fragmentation is also a familiar feature of fireballs: a body breaking apart during atmospheric entry can appear to divide into two or more lights before fading. GEIPAN therefore classified the Bourg en Bresse report as a probable meteor, again in category B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... This is a stronger kind of explanation than resemblance alone. Investigators did not merely say that the witnesses’ description “sounded like” a meteor. They matched it to an event observed independently, at a known time, from another location. That external record reduced the freedom to invent explanations after the fact. It also showed how deceptive distance can be: although the fireball seemed connected to the local landscape, GEIPAN concluded that it was more than 100 kilometres away. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURG EN BRESSE (01) 17.04.2010 GEIPAN... The witnesses were therefore broadly right about what they saw—a fast, bright, green object that divided—but the interpretation changed once the observation was placed within a wider geographical record. The case did not collapse because testimony was worthless. It was resolved because testimony could be checked against evidence that existed independently of it. Comment le Geipan enquête sur les objets volants non identifiés Channel: BFMTV Open on YouTube Why weak evidence is not a mystery Not every Ain report can be matched to wind data or a meteor photograph. The department’s official file also includes category C cases from places such as Hauteville Lompnes, Ambérieu en Bugey and Loyettes. In GEIPAN’s system, category C does not mean that investigators found a highly strange phenomenon. It means that the available information was too incomplete or unreliable to support either a conventional identification or a genuinely unexplained classification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAMBERIEU EN BUGEY (01) 22.04.2017 GEIPANApril 22, 2017 — AMBERIEU EN BUGEY (01) 22.04.2017... Le cas est classé C, témoignage... Published: April 22, 2017 This distinction is central to understanding UFO records. GEIPAN evaluates both strangeness , meaning how difficult the reported features"},{"title":"Montluel","url":"/montluel/","description":"The 2022 Montluel sighting became Ain's strongest unresolved case after a detailed reconstruction failed to find a satisfactory conventional match.","breadcrumb":"Which UFO Cases in Ain Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Montluel Why Did the Montluel Sighting Remain Unexplained? Ain UFO Files Montluel Montluel Why Did the Montluel Sighting Remain Unexplained? The 2022 Montluel sighting became Ain's strongest unresolved case after a detailed reconstruction failed to find a satisfactory conventional match. The 2022 Montluel sighting became Ain's strongest unresolved case after a detailed reconstruction failed to find a satisfactory conventional match. event time window What the witness reported How investigators reconstructed the flight What remains uncertain ain montluel Introduction The Montluel sighting remains unexplained because investigators could reconstruct much of the witness’s viewing geometry, yet none of the conventional explanations tested matched the whole account. At about 3.10 am on 14 May 2022, a man on his balcony reported a large, dark rectangle moving silently across the southern sky. He said it followed a level path between two hills, briefly passed behind foreground landmarks and appeared to distort distant cloud around its edges. There was no photograph, second witness or confirmed radar return. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... That combination makes Montluel an important case in Ain’s UFO history, but not proof of an extraordinary aircraft. Its value lies in the unusually detailed investigation of a short, single witness observation. A site visit, terrain measurements, timing tests and checks of aviation, weather and perceptual explanations narrowed the possibilities without establishing what was actually seen. In March 2026, the French space agency’s investigation service, GEIPAN, classified the report as category D: unidentified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... SPÉCIALE OVNI ET EXTRATERRESTRE: UN PILOTE ET UN EXPERT DU CENTRE NATIONAL RACONTENT LEUR EXPÉRIENCE Channel: LEGEND Open on YouTube What the witness reported The witness had been working at home during the night and went onto his balcony for a cigarette. From Montluel’s higher ground, the balcony overlooked the Rhône valley through a gap between hills. The view included the town’s church tower, nearby trees and, farther south, the general directions of Lyon–Saint Exupéry Airport, the La Valbonne military camp and the Bugey nuclear site. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. According to the initial technical questionnaire, completed three days later, the object appeared on his right and travelled from west to east. He described it as rectangular, anthracite grey and much darker than the moonlit night sky. It displayed no obvious navigation lights, emitted no visible trail and produced no sound. He initially estimated that it remained visible for about eight seconds and subtended roughly 20 by 40 millimetres when compared with an object held at arm’s length. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... During the later reconstruction, the account became more spatially specific. The witness said the rectangle first emerged near a tall fir tree, moved just above another tree and passed partly behind the upper section of the church spire. He then changed position along the balcony, briefly losing sight of it while putting down his cigarette. It continued behind further vegetation and finally disappeared behind the eastern hill without re emerging. Investigators measured the apparent path between approximate azimuths of 153 and 101 degrees. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Two reported details made the observation stranger than a simple dark silhouette. The witness described a faint border around the object which he struggled to characterise as light, and said distant clouds appeared deformed as it passed in front of them. His comparison with the visual distortion around a black hole expressed what the effect looked like to him; it was not evidence that any comparable physical process occurred. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTLUEL (01) 14.05.2022 GEIPAN... The weather did not immediately supply an explanation. Data from Lyon–Saint Exupéry, around 15 kilometres away, indicated almost no wind, visibility of about 20 kilometres and generally clear conditions. The Moon was nearly full and low in the south west, helping to brighten the sky behind a dark object. These conditions were compatible with seeing a silhouette, although they did not establish its distance or nature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. How investigators reconstructed the flight GEIPAN received the completed questionnaire on 17 May 2022. Radar information was requested in July that year, and an investigator visited the site on 1 July 2023. The final investigation report was dated February 2026, almost four years after the sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The reconstruction materially changed one important figure: duration. Although the witness’s first estimate was eight seconds, repeating his movements and timing the reconstructed passage produced a possible viewing time of roughly 20 to 28 seconds. This discrepancy matters because speed calculations are highly sensitive to duration. GEIPAN retained both ends of the range rather than treating either recollection as exact. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Terrain offered a partial distance constraint that is absent from many night sky reports. If the object really disappeared behind the final hill while maintaining the described straight course, it had to be beyond that ridge rather than close to the balcony. The investigation model placed its nearest distance at roughly one kilometre and its altitude at about 250 metres. These are conditional results: they follow from accepting the reported occlusion, trajectory and viewing angles, rather than from an independent measurement of the object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Investigators also tested the witness’s ability to judge apparent size. A comparison involving the full Moon suggested that he might overestimate angular size by approximately a factor of three. GEIPAN therefore calculated several possibilities rather than simply converting his original arm’s length estimate into a fixed dimension. Under the minimum distance model, the object’s estimated length ranged from about 15 metres after applying the correction to nearly 60 metres using the uncorrected initial estimate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Using a modelled travel distance of about one kilometre, the eight second estimate implied roughly 450 kilometres per hour, whereas 28 seconds gave about 130 kilometres per hour. GEIPAN consequently described 15 metres and 130 kilometres per hour as minimum values within its chosen geometric scenario—not direct measurements of a confirmed craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This distinction is central to understanding the case. Human observers cannot normally determine the distance, size and true speed of an unfamiliar object against the sky without a reliable reference. GEIPAN’s own methodology warns that witnesses may unknowingly infer distance first and then derive an apparently convincing speed from that assumption. Montluel is stronger than a wholly unconstrained light report because hills and foreground objects supplied references, but its numerical reconstruction still depends on the accuracy of one person’s memory and interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Erreurs de perception issues de mécanismes instantanés (autocinétique) ou de constructions mentales (le témoin évalue une distance et e... PHÉNOMÈNES INEXPLIQUÉS: Pourquoi le sujet OVNI revient en force ? Le Journal de l'Espace 341 Channel: Le Journal de l'Espace Open on YouTube Why ordinary aircraft did not settle the case The reconstructed dimensions and speed were not aerodynamically impossible. GEIPAN acknowledged that an object 15–60 metres long travelling at 130–450 kilometres per hour falls within the broad physical range of conventional aircraft. The problem was matching that possibility to the reported appearance: a dark, sharply rectangular form flying very low, silently and without visible lights. Investigators considered a large drone less plausible because the calculated minimum size was already substantial. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. No relevant aircraft appeared on the radar restitution supplied by the French air defence organisation now known as CAPCODA. That absence weakened a straightforward aircraft identification, but it did not prove that nothing physical crossed the area. Radar coverage and display products do not guarantee detection of every low, small or poorly reflecting target, while the investigation report itself noted that the supplied maps contained false tracks and limited useful information. The safe conclusion is therefore that no supporting radar match was found, not that radar"},{"title":"Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny?","url":"/which-aisne-ufo-stories-withstand/","description":"Aisne has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, well-documented “classic” encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny? Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny? Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny? Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny? Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny? Aisne has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, well documented “classic” encounter. Aisne has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, well documented “classic” encounter. Its record is better understood as a mixture of a striking but poorly preserved 1954 story, later reports of unusual lights, and official cases that were mostly explained or left unclassified because the evidence was inadequate. aisne Introduction The most dramatic local account concerns a supposed landed object and small figure near Soissons during France’s great UFO wave of October 1954. Yet the surviving account is brief, second hand and difficult to verify against accessible contemporary records. By contrast, recent cases in the national GEIPAN archive often contain videos, questionnaires, radar checks or astronomical comparisons. Those investigations have tended to weaken extraordinary interpretations rather than strengthen them. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, October 8, Soissons, Aisne Patrick Grossufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Soissons, Aisne... Soissons and the 1954 wave The best known Aisne story is dated 8 October 1954 and attributed to the Soissons area. According to later UFO catalogues, a man and his son reportedly saw a metallic, saucer shaped object on the ground in a field. A small figure was said to have climbed a ladder into it before the object rose rapidly and disappeared. The account is usually traced to a report in the regional newspaper L’Union dated 13 October, but the readily accessible catalogue entries do not reproduce a complete contemporary article or identify the witnesses. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, October 8, Soissons, Aisne Patrick Grossufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Soissons, Aisne... That evidential weakness matters. The story contains all the elements that make a case memorable—two witnesses, a landed craft, a humanoid figure and a rapid departure—but little of the information needed to test it. There is no accessible witness interview, precise location, observation duration, description of lighting conditions, physical trace report or surviving official investigation in the sources currently available online. One specialist catalogue openly notes that its underlying link is broken, leaving the episode dependent on later summaries of an alleged newspaper item. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org URECA T URECAT UFO Related Entities Catalog Patrick Grossufo UFOS at close sight: URECAT 000285 October 8, 1954, Soissons, Aisne, France... Published: October 8, 1954 The timing nevertheless explains why the story entered UFO literature. France experienced an exceptional wave of flying saucer reports in the autumn of 1954. The nearby Quarouble case in the Nord department, publicised in September, involved railway worker Marius Dewilde’s claim that he saw a landed elongated object and two small figures. Reports of discs, luminous objects and “flying cigars” then multiplied across France, receiving intense newspaper coverage. [Complete France] completefrance.com flying saucers in france a history of ufo sightings M. Nicolaï was not the first in France to report a close encounter with alien beings. In 1954, railway worker...Read more... This setting permits two competing readings. UFO writers treat the geographical spread and recurring descriptions as evidence that witnesses were encountering a real, unusual phenomenon. Sceptical interpretations emphasise social transmission: once newspapers supplied a vocabulary of saucers, cigars, small beings and landed machines, ambiguous sights and local stories could be interpreted through the same framework. The Soissons report cannot decisively support either position because its original evidential foundation is too thin. It should therefore be labelled historically notable but weakly documented , rather than unresolved in the stronger investigative sense. An unresolved case normally has enough reliable information to resist plausible explanations. The Soissons story survives mainly as a striking claim whose basic details can no longer be tested. What the official files actually show France’s official approach is unusually valuable for assessing the later Aisne record. GEIPAN, a technical unit of the French space agency CNES, collects and analyses reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Reports may arrive directly from witnesses or through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities. Investigators compare testimony with meteorology, astronomy, aircraft activity, satellites and other known causes before assigning a classification. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... The classification letters are often misunderstood. A case marked C is not a particularly mysterious sighting. It means that the available information is too weak or incomplete for a dependable conclusion. B cases are probably identified, while the modern D categories are reserved for sufficiently consistent reports that remain unexplained after investigation. GEIPAN also evaluates a report’s “consistency”—the quality and quantity of usable information—separately from its “strangeness”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The accessible Aisne entries are dominated by B and C classifications rather than strong unexplained cases. Examples include: Trélou sur Marne, 13 January 1979: classified C because reliable information was lacking. Saint Quentin, 26 April 1981: classified B, indicating a probably identified phenomenon, although the public index does not specify the proposed identification. Cuffies, 19 May 2009: classified C for insufficient reliable information. Saint Quentin, 14 January 2012: classified C after a more detailed examination. Condé sur Aisne, 6 August 2015: classified C because investigators lacked precise information and independent corroboration. Laon, 20 May 2021: classified B and attributed to a bird. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This pattern does not prove that nothing unusual has ever appeared over Aisne. It shows that the public official record currently offers little support for claims of a robust local mystery involving well corroborated objects, radar confirmation or physical effects. Saint Quentin: three lights, limited evidence The Saint Quentin observation of 14 January 2012 is one of the more instructive local files because GEIPAN tested an aviation explanation rather than merely listing the report. A single witness described three luminous points moving together in a regular, straight path. Investigators examined primary radar data and found an airliner at high altitude near Saint Quentin approximately seven minutes before the estimated observation time. Its recorded direction did not match the witness’s account, so the radar track could not simply be declared the answer. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Aisne... La trace radar (primaire) des avions ayant survolé la zone à ce moment ne donne qu'un avion de ligne à haute altitude à 21h48 (... That mismatch might sound intriguing, but it did not turn the report into a strong unexplained case. The estimated time and direction depended on one person’s recollection, with no independent witness, photograph or precise angular measurements. GEIPAN judged the case to have low consistency and low strangeness because three lights moving steadily in a straight line are compatible with ordinary aerial activity. It was classified C because there was not enough information to choose confidently among the possibilities. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Aisne... La trace radar (primaire) des avions ayant survolé la zone à ce moment ne donne qu'un avion de ligne à haute altitude à 21h48 (... The case illustrates an important distinction. “The identified aircraft did not fit perfectly” does not mean “an extraordinary craft was detected”. It means that one proposed explanation could not be matched securely to an imprecisely recorded event. Without better timing, a second viewing position or stronger sensor data, the residual uncertainty is mainly evidential. Laon: how video can mislead The Laon case from the night of 19–20 May 2021 began with something that older UFO reports usually lack: a video supplied to GEIPAN. Yet the presence of footage did not make the observation more mysterious. Investigators concluded that the moving object was a bird, and the case was classified B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This result reflects a common"},{"title":"GEIPAN Files","url":"/geipan-files/","description":"Aisne's official files are dominated by probable explanations and weak evidence rather than well-supported unexplained encounters.","breadcrumb":"Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny?","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Files What Do Aisne's Official UFO Files Show? Aisne UFO Files GEIPAN Files GEIPAN Files What Do Aisne's Official UFO Files Show? Aisne's official files are dominated by probable explanations and weak evidence rather than well supported unexplained encounters. Aisne's official files are dominated by probable explanations and weak evidence rather than well supported unexplained encounters. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies weak and unexplained reports Saint Quentin, Laon and other investigated cases Why uncertainty is not the same as extraordinary evidence aisne geipan files Introduction Aisne ’s official UFO record is less dramatic than its folklore. The cases published by GEIPAN—the French space agency unit that investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena—are dominated by probable conventional explanations and files that cannot be assessed properly because essential information is missing. The accessible record includes reports from Saint Quentin, Laon, the Soissons area and smaller communes, but it does not reveal a strong local collection of well corroborated unexplained encounters. That distinction matters. GEIPAN’s archive is not simply a list of things that remained “unidentified” when first seen. Investigators compare witness accounts with astronomy, aircraft activity, satellites, meteorology, photographs, video and other available evidence. A case may end as explained, probably explained, insufficiently documented or genuinely unresolved. In Aisne, the recurring pattern is not extraordinary objects surviving rigorous checks; it is ordinary looking causes, ambiguous visual impressions and reports too weak to support a firm conclusion. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... How GEIPAN separates weak cases from unexplained ones GEIPAN is a technical department of CNES, France’s national space agency. Its remit is to collect, analyse, investigate, publish and archive reports of unusual aerial or aerospace phenomena. It works with organisations including the gendarmerie, police, the Air and Space Force, Météo France and scientific specialists, but it is not an extraterrestrial research body or a real time national air defence service. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Its classification system is crucial for reading the Aisne files correctly: Category A means the phenomenon has been identified without meaningful ambiguity. Category B means GEIPAN considers a particular explanation very probable, although the evidence may not permit absolute proof. Category C means the report cannot be analysed adequately because reliable information is lacking. Category D is reserved for a phenomenon that remains unexplained despite sufficient evidence and investigation. Older or revised records may use D, while more recent methodology distinguishes between cases of moderate and stronger evidential consistency. GEIPAN assesses both consistency —the amount and reliability of the information—and residual strangeness , meaning how far the report still lies from known phenomena after investigation. A puzzling story with vague timing, no precise direction and no usable recording may therefore become Category C rather than D. Conversely, a report can sound unusual yet receive a B classification when one conventional cause fits most of the facts. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... This prevents one of the most common misunderstandings in UFO reporting: “unidentified” does not always mean that investigators discovered something genuinely resistant to explanation. It may mean only that the witness supplied too little information to test the possibilities. Nationally, CNES reports that 24.6 per cent of archived phenomena are clearly identified, 39.7 per cent probably identified, 32.4 per cent unidentified for lack of data and only 3.3 per cent unidentified after investigation. Aisne’s better known official files broadly resemble that evidence heavy national pattern. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Saint Quentin shows the limits of an older file The Saint Quentin case of 26 April 1981 involved three listed witnesses and a red, apparently spherical light seen at about 10pm. One witness described it as roughly half the apparent size of the Moon, moving slowly and displaying a trail or tail. The report entered the official archive through a gendarmerie statement and is classified B, meaning that GEIPAN regarded a conventional identification as probable rather than certain. The database labels the precise identified phenomenon only as unspecified. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT QUENTIN (02) 26.04.1981 T3SAINT QUENTIN (02) 1981. Date d'observation. 26/04/1981. Région. Picardie. Département. Aisne. Clas... At first glance, several witnesses might appear to make the incident especially strong. Yet witness numbers alone do not settle what was seen. People observing together are not fully independent sensors: they share the same viewing conditions, may discuss the object during the sighting and can reinforce a common interpretation. The useful questions are whether the accounts were recorded separately, whether directions and angular elevations agree, whether the time is precise and whether aircraft, astronomical or atmospheric checks can reproduce the observation. The Saint Quentin file also illustrates the unevenness of historical documentation. Older cases frequently depend heavily on police or gendarmerie records, because investigators did not have today’s satellite databases, digital imagery, flight tracking resources or standardised online questionnaires. GEIPAN itself notes that gendarmerie statements were often the central document in earlier investigations and that old classifications can be reconsidered when better methods or new information become available. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... The responsible conclusion is therefore narrower than either “solved” or “extraordinary”. The case contains more substance than a lone undocumented anecdote, but its B status indicates that the official assessment favours an ordinary phenomenon. It does not provide the kind of residual anomaly that would justify treating Saint Quentin as a landmark unexplained Aisne encounter. Laon: a strange looking video becomes a probable bird The Laon report of 20 May 2021 is one of the clearest demonstrations of what modern investigation can add. Shortly after midnight, a witness recorded a small pale or amber object apparently making rapid, irregular movements. The witness described abrupt changes of direction, a brief pause and a fast departure. On a phone screen, such motion can look highly unusual, particularly when the object is distant, poorly focused and filmed against a largely featureless night sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr LAON (02) 20.05.2021LAON (02) 20.05.2021; Date d'observation. 20/05/2021; Région. Picardie; Département. Aisne; Classification... Unlike many older reports, the file contains a technical questionnaire, the original video, still photographs, a reconstruction and an investigation report. GEIPAN classified it B and identified the probable phenomenon as a bird. The archive gives the case a relatively good consistency score of 0.70 but low residual strangeness of 0.28. In practical terms, investigators had enough material to examine the claim, and the better the case became documented, the less extraordinary it appeared. [Geipan] geipan.fr LAON (02) 20.05.2021LAON (02) 20.05.2021; Date d'observation. 20/05/2021; Région. Picardie; Département. Aisne; Classification... This is an important result rather than a dismissal. A nearby bird illuminated by street lighting, moonlight or the camera’s processing can appear as an indistinct bright body. Its natural turns may look like extreme acceleration when distance and scale are unknown. Autofocus, image stabilisation, hand movement and digital zoom can further separate apparent movement in the recording from the object’s real path. The Laon case therefore shows why video is not automatically decisive evidence. A recording can improve a file because it preserves motion and timing, but it can also expose how easily an ordinary nearby object becomes ambiguous when the image contains no reliable reference points. Here, later analysis weakened the unusual interpretation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 ·"},{"title":"Soissons 1954","url":"/soissons-1954/","description":"The famous Soissons landing story is memorable, but its missing witnesses, location and original records make firm conclusions impossible.","breadcrumb":"Which Aisne UFO Stories Withstand Scrutiny?","level":2,"text":"Soissons 1954 Did a Saucer Really Land Near Soissons? Aisne UFO Files Soissons 1954 Soissons 1954 Did a Saucer Really Land Near Soissons? The famous Soissons landing story is memorable, but its missing witnesses, location and original records make firm conclusions impossible. The famous Soissons landing story is memorable, but its missing witnesses, location and original records make firm conclusions impossible. event time window The reported craft, figure and witnesses How the 1954 French UFO wave shaped the story Why the surviving evidence remains too weak aisne soissons 1954 Introduction The Soissons landing claim is Aisne ’s most memorable UFO story from France’s extraordinary autumn 1954 sighting wave. In its familiar form, a father and son saw a saucer resting in a field near Soissons, watched a small figure climb a ladder into it, and then saw the object depart rapidly. Yet almost every detail needed to investigate that account is missing: the witnesses remain unnamed, the field is unidentified, the time is unknown, and no accessible interview, police report, photograph or trace examination has been found. The case is therefore not a strong “unexplained landing”. It is a striking story preserved through later summaries of a regional newspaper report. Even its commonly quoted date, 8 October 1954, appears less secure than catalogue listings suggest. The best supported conclusion is that an unusual claim was reported near Soissons in early October 1954, but that the surviving evidence is too fragmentary to establish what happened. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Soissons, Aisne, occupant, humanoid, multiple, anonymous, soucoupe, avenue, father, son, automobilists, morning, landing, ladder, occupan... The reported craft, figure and witnesses The core account is short. A man and his son were reportedly travelling near Soissons when they saw a saucer shaped object standing in a field. A small humanoid figure was said to have climbed a ladder into the craft; the ladder then retracted and the object rose or shot away at high speed. Some later catalogue wording describes the object as metallic, although the surviving source trail does not make it clear whether that adjective appeared in the original report or entered through later retelling. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org 1954 10 08 france soissons Summary rewritten, was \"The French ufology magazine \"Lumières Dans la Nuit\" indicated that in France, in Soissons, in October 1954, a fat... Published: October 1954 This combination of a landed machine, an occupant and a mechanical boarding detail makes the story unusually vivid. It also places the report among the close encounter narratives that distinguished the French wave of 1954 from ordinary distant light sightings. Specialist catalogues consequently list Soissons as a humanoid or occupant case rather than simply an unidentified object in the sky. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org URECAT UFO related entities reports for year 195423 Jan 2019 — Catalog of UFO related entities reports for year 1954:; October 8, 1954... Published: October 8, 1954 The apparent specificity is deceptive, however. The account does not preserve answers to basic questions: Were the witnesses in a moving or stationary vehicle? How far were they from the object? Was the observation made in daylight, twilight or darkness? How large did the object appear? How long was it visible? Was the “small man” clearly seen as a living figure, or inferred from a distant shape? Did the object leave marks, damaged vegetation or disturbed soil? Did anyone revisit the field? Did the witnesses speak to police, gendarmes or another investigator? Without those details, even the most memorable elements cannot be tested. The word “ladder”, for example, sounds precise, but there is no surviving witness description explaining its dimensions, appearance or movement. It may represent something clearly observed, an interpretation made at the time, or language introduced while the story was condensed. A date that became firmer in the catalogues The incident is widely labelled “8 October 1954”, but the source chain published by specialist researcher Patrick Gross points to a less exact chronology. Gross reports that author Jean Sider, writing in 1997, cited page 2 of the 13 October 1954 edition of the regional newspaper L’Union . Sider’s summary reportedly placed the event merely at the beginning of October, at an unspecified hour. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Soissons, Aisne, occupant, humanoid, multiple, anonymous, soucoupe, avenue, father, son, automobilists, morning, landing, ladder, occupan... This creates an important distinction between the date attached to the catalogue entry and the date demonstrably supplied by the cited newspaper summary . A catalogue needs a date under which to organise a case, but that administrative convenience should not be mistaken for contemporary precision. Unless the original article contains a clearer date, 8 October is best treated as conventional rather than securely established. The uncertainty affects more than chronology. Investigators normally compare a report with weather, astronomical objects, aircraft movements and other sightings at the same time. None of those checks can be performed reliably when both the hour and potentially the day are unknown. How the original story reached later UFO literature The most useful way to assess Soissons is to follow its documentary trail. The alleged contemporary foundation is an article in L’Union of Reims dated 13 October 1954. L’Union was the principal regional daily serving areas including the Marne and Aisne, so it is a plausible venue for a report from the Soissons district. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia L'Union (journal français L'Union (journal français However, the accessible online case summaries do not reproduce a complete scan or transcription of the Soissons article. Instead, the story is presented through later citations and compressed catalogue entries. Gross’s reconstruction identifies Sider’s 1997 book as the source for the newspaper reference. Another catalogue entry says an online database once carried a similar version, but its underlying link was broken. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org 1954 10 08 france soissons Summary rewritten, was \"The French ufology magazine \"Lumières Dans la Nuit\" indicated that in France, in Soissons, in October 1954, a fat... Published: October 1954 The surviving chain can therefore be represented cautiously as: reported newspaper item in 1954 → Sider’s summary in 1997 → later digital catalogues and databases Each stage may preserve the essence of the story, but each also creates opportunities for detail to be shortened, standardised or altered. “Near Soissons” can become simply “Soissons”. “At the beginning of October” can acquire a single calendar date. A “saucer” can become a “metallic saucer shaped object”. A brief report that may have contained uncertainty can turn into a confident one sentence case synopsis. This does not prove that the story was invented. It means the present version is several steps removed from the witnesses. Until the original article is located and read in full, it is impossible to know whether later summaries accurately retain its wording, qualifications and context. How the 1954 French wave shaped the story The Soissons claim appeared during an exceptional period of French press interest in flying saucers. During September and October 1954, newspapers carried repeated accounts of luminous discs, elongated “cigars”, landed objects and strange occupants. Contemporary coverage sometimes described the reports collectively as a tide sweeping the country, while other articles referred openly to a saucer “psychosis”. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org HERE IS THE FLYING SAUCER FILE... We intend to tell you how these mysterious objects appeared in our world, how, from the astonishme... That climate matters because eyewitness reports do not emerge in a cultural vacuum. By early October, readers had already encountered stories using a recognisable vocabulary: circular craft, small beings, roadside encounters, landings and sudden departures. A person seeing something ambiguous could interpret it through that imagery. Newspapers also had an incentive to print unusual local stories because saucers had become a national talking point. The best known comparison is the Quarouble case in the Nord department, reported in September 1954. Railway worker Marius Dewilde claimed to have encountered a landed object and two small beings near his home. Whatever the ultimate explanation of Quarouble, its prominent treatment gave French readers a ready made model of a craft on the ground with diminutive occupants. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia UFO sightings in France UFO sightings in France Soissons echoed several features already circulating in"},{"title":"Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With...","url":"/why-alliers-strangest-sightings-changed/","description":"Allier has a real but modest UFO history. Its public record is not dominated by a single landmark encounter, radar interception or military mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With... Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With Investigation Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With... Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With... Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With Investigation Allier has a real but modest UFO history. Its public record is not dominated by a single landmark encounter, radar interception or military mystery. Allier has a real but modest UFO history. Its public record is not dominated by a single landmark encounter, radar interception or military mystery. Instead, it consists mainly of brief reports of unusual lights, several incomplete investigations and a growing number of cases later explained as ordinary astronomical, atmospheric or human made phenomena. allier Introduction The strongest publicly documented cases include the unexplained but poorly evidenced Yzeure sighting of 1979, the partly unresolved Cosne d’Allier episode of 1985, the clearly explained Gannat lights of 1988 and a red “bar” near Saint Pourçain in 1998 that was eventually identified as the Moon seen through cloud. More recent reports have been attributed to Jupiter, satellite reflections, the International Space Station and a balloon. [lasemainedelallier.fr] lasemainedelallier.fr Open source on lasemainedelallier.fr. What the official record actually contains France’s official body for these reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES. It uses the term unidentified aerial phenomenon rather than UFO because witnesses may be seeing a light, atmospheric effect or perceptual illusion rather than a physical craft. GEIPAN collects testimony, works with police and gendarmerie records, consults scientific specialists and publishes its conclusions while protecting witness identities. It does not investigate extraterrestrial visitation as such. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Its classifications are important when reading Allier’s files: A: the phenomenon has been identified with a high level of confidence. B: an explanation is considered probable. C: the available information is too weak or incomplete for a reliable analysis. D: the phenomenon remains unexplained despite sufficiently useful evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... That distinction prevents a common misunderstanding. A category C case is not necessarily more mysterious than an explained case. It means the record is inadequate: the observation may have been ordinary, but too little information survives to demonstrate what it was. The accessible GEIPAN listings for Allier include reports from Yzeure, Cosne d’Allier, Gannat, Châtel de Neuvre, Cérilly, Seuillet, Moulins, Nizerolles, Arronnes and Lurcy Lévis. Within that group, several are classified A, one is B and several are C. The pattern is dominated by misidentifications and incomplete files rather than robust category D incidents. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The early cases: intriguing descriptions, limited evidence The oldest prominent entry concerns Yzeure, near Moulins, on 24 September 1979 . A witness reportedly noticed what first resembled an aircraft trail, followed by an orange disc like object that appeared stationary for roughly ten to fifteen seconds. It then seemed to accelerate, become intensely white and disappear. No corroborating witness was found and the surviving information was insufficient for a dependable reconstruction, so GEIPAN classifies it as category C. [La Semaine de l'Allier] lasemainedelallier.fr Open source on lasemainedelallier.fr. This is a good example of the difference between an unexplained story and strong evidence. The reported colour change and sudden departure sound striking, but there is no known photograph, radar return, physical trace or independent account. Distance and speed could not be measured. A bright aircraft, meteor, reflection or other ordinary source cannot now be demonstrated, yet neither can an exotic explanation. The Cosne d’Allier case of 21 January 1985 is better documented but contains two different episodes that are sometimes blurred together. Flashes seen around a ruined house were quickly traced to a short circuit involving an electric fence. Earlier that evening, however, a motorist and his wife reported a yellow triangular light that appeared to follow their car before vanishing suddenly. Local brigades saw nothing, and investigators obtained no additional information capable of identifying it. GEIPAN therefore retains category C rather than treating the triangular light as a confirmed unexplained craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOSNE D'ALLIER (03) 21.01.1985 GEIPAN... Apparent pursuit is not, by itself, evidence that a light is following a vehicle. A distant source such as a planet, aircraft or fixed light can remain in roughly the same position relative to a moving observer, creating a powerful impression of shared movement. In this case, however, the original observational detail is too thin to test that possibility properly. A similarly fragile file arose in October 1994 near Cérilly or Lurcy Lévis , where an off duty gendarme reportedly discovered a circle of stony fragments while gathering mushrooms. Leaves touching the material were said to have acquired a greenish yellow colour. Darkness was approaching, and when the witness returned the following day he could not relocate the site. With no recoverable location, sample or photograph, GEIPAN classifies the incident as category C. [La Semaine de l'Allier] lasemainedelallier.fr Open source on lasemainedelallier.fr. The witness’s occupation may make the report worth recording, but professional reliability cannot replace missing physical evidence. Because the supposed trace was never recovered, investigators could not determine whether it was industrial debris, burnt material, dumped waste, a natural deposit or something else. Aviation : Visite privée de la base aérienne du Mont Angel Channel: MonacoInfo Open on YouTube Gannat and Saint Pourçain: two mysteries that did not survive investigation On 6 October 1988 , several people around Gannat saw three bluish lights moving slowly across the night sky. Gendarmes attended and observed the phenomenon themselves. An initial check for helicopter activity produced no answer, which could easily have allowed the event to enter local folklore as an official multi witness UFO case. The eventual explanation was much less dramatic: a hypermarket was testing powerful laser projectors for an advertising campaign. GEIPAN lists the case as category A. The incident matters because it shows why police participation or multiple witnesses do not automatically prove that an object was extraordinary. They establish that something was genuinely visible, not what caused it. [La Semaine de l'Allier] lasemainedelallier.fr Open source on lasemainedelallier.fr. The 5 December 1998 observation near Monétay sur Allier , originally associated with Saint Pourçain sur Sioule, is even more instructive. Three members of a family saw a horizontal formation of vivid red lights near the eastern horizon. The lights seemed to advance and retreat before disappearing. The case was initially left unexplained and was once classified D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... A later review reconstructed the sightline using the witnesses’ driveway and hedge. Astronomical software placed the Moon almost exactly at the reported bearing and elevation. Broken cloud could expose narrow portions of the low, reddened Moon, turning it into a row or bar of glowing patches. Changing gaps in the cloud could also create the impression that the lights were approaching or receding. GEIPAN contacted a witness, confirmed the viewing geometry and reclassified the case as A: a moonrise misidentification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... This revision substantially weakens the original UFO claim. It also demonstrates the value of preserving exact times, landmarks, sketches and witness contact details. A description alone sounded unusual; the geometry made the explanation testable. The nationwide display of 5 November 1990 Allier also participated in one of France’s most famous mass sighting nights. At around 7 pm on 5 November 1990 , witnesses across the country reported a large luminous formation, often perceived as a low flying structured object. Gendarmerie records preserve descriptions of reddish or orange trails, bright leading lights and movement from west to east. Some local files contained numerous witness statements, while another recorded two farmers describing a glowing trail lasting tens of seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. CNES later concluded that the principal display was the"},{"title":"Solved Mysteries","url":"/solved-mysteries/","description":"Lasers, the Moon and rocket debris explain some of Allier's most dramatic reports once timing, direction and viewing conditions are rebuilt.","breadcrumb":"Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With...","level":2,"text":"Solved Mysteries How Allier's Best UFO Mysteries Were Explained Allier UFOs Solved Mysteries Solved Mysteries How Allier's Best UFO Mysteries Were Explained Lasers, the Moon and rocket debris explain some of Allier's most dramatic reports once timing, direction and viewing conditions are rebuilt. Lasers, the Moon and rocket debris explain some of Allier's most dramatic reports once timing, direction and viewing conditions are rebuilt. mechanism The Gannat laser display The red Moon near Saint Pourcain The nationwide rocket re entry of 1990 allier solved mysteries Introduction Some of Allier ’s most impressive UFO reports became less mysterious only after investigators rebuilt the scene: the exact time, the direction witnesses were facing, the height of the phenomenon above the horizon and what human made or astronomical sources occupied that part of the sky. Three cases show the value of this approach particularly well. Blue lights watched for hours near Gannat in 1988 came from commercial laser projectors. A silent red “bar” near Saint Pourçain in 1998 matched the rising Moon behind broken cloud. Reports from Allier during France’s extraordinary UFO wave of 5 November 1990 formed part of a nationwide event caused by the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. [Geipan] geipan.fr GANNAT (03) 1988 GEIPAN... These explanations matter because none depends on dismissing the witnesses as foolish or dishonest. In each case, the original sight was genuinely unusual from the observer’s position. The solution emerged by comparing testimony with independent evidence that could be checked: commercial activity, landscape geometry, astronomical calculations and spaceflight records. The Gannat lights came from a laser display On 6 October 1988, at about 9.30 pm, several people in the Gannat area saw three bluish sources of light moving slowly across the sky. The observation lasted long enough to attract official attention. Gendarmes attended and saw the phenomenon themselves, which finally disappeared at around 12.30 am. The duration, multiple witnesses and police confirmation made it a stronger report than a fleeting light seen by one person. [Geipan] geipan.fr GANNAT (03) 1988 GEIPAN... Investigators initially considered an aircraft explanation, including the possibility of a helicopter. That was reasonable: slowly moving lights apparently hanging over one area can suggest an aircraft operating at night. The inquiry did not, however, find a flight that accounted for what had been seen. The decisive lead came from the ground rather than the air. A hypermarket was testing laser projectors for a forthcoming promotional event. GEIPAN now classifies the case as category A, meaning that it considers the phenomenon identified without significant ambiguity. [Geipan] geipan.fr GANNAT (03) 1988 GEIPAN... The reconstruction explains several features that might otherwise sound difficult to reconcile with an advertising display. A projector does not need to create a solid beam visible from every direction. Its light can become conspicuous where it strikes haze, cloud or airborne particles, producing luminous patches that appear detached from the ground. Sweeping the projectors changes the position of those patches, so witnesses can perceive separate lights travelling slowly through the sky. That mechanism also explains why the phenomenon persisted for roughly three hours without behaving like a normal aircraft. A helicopter would have consumed fuel, produced sound and followed a recognisable flight path. Repeated projector tests could continue in the same general area, stop and restart, or move in smooth patterns while remaining silent at the witnesses’ locations. The Gannat case therefore carries an important caution for Allier’s UFO history. Multiple witnesses and gendarmerie observation establish that something unusual looking was present; they do not establish that it was an unknown craft. Corroboration strengthens the reality of the observation, but identifying its source still requires checking activity on the ground. The red bar near Saint Pourçain was the Moon A more striking reversal concerns the sighting recorded near Saint Pourçain sur Sioule, now catalogued by GEIPAN under Monétay sur Allier. At 7.45 pm on 5 December 1998, three members of a family looked towards the eastern horizon through an overcast sky. They described a long horizontal line containing intensely red globes. It appeared to move towards them and then away before disappearing after about 30 seconds. It was silent, and two witness statements were taken. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... For years, the case could easily be presented as unresolved. A 2012 local newspaper summary still described the red rectangular formation and said that insufficient information had been collected to explain it. GEIPAN subsequently revisited the file using improved astronomical software and more precise reconstruction of the viewing position. The official classification changed from category D, unexplained with usable evidence, to category A: the rising Moon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... The solution depended on unusually useful features of the property. Investigators established that the witnesses were looking along a narrow driveway approximately 15 metres long and 2.5 metres wide. Its alignment provided a reference for the sighting’s bearing, calculated at roughly 65 degrees east. The witnesses’ drawing, including the apparent position above a hedge, indicated an elevation of about four degrees. Astronomical reconstruction placed the Moon at approximately four degrees elevation and 66 degrees east at 7.45 pm—essentially the same point in the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... That positional match is much stronger than simply noting that the Moon was visible somewhere that evening. It connects the reported phenomenon to a specific celestial body at the reported time, bearing and height. The unusual appearance can be explained by cloud. A low Moon is commonly yellow, orange or red because its light passes through a greater depth of atmosphere than when it is high in the sky. Broken or uneven cloud can hide most of the lunar disc while allowing light through narrow openings. Instead of seeing a familiar circle, observers may see a row, bar or cluster of reddish patches with no obvious connection to the Moon. GEIPAN concluded that changing gaps or opacity in the cloud produced the reported line of red globes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... The impression that the object advanced and retreated also has a plausible perceptual explanation. As a cloud opening widens, brightens or becomes more transparent, the light can seem to approach; as the opening narrows or darkens, it can seem to recede. With no visible structure, known distance or fixed scale, the eye interprets changes in brightness and apparent size as movement through space. The abrupt disappearance follows naturally if thicker cloud covered the Moon again. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... GEIPAN also contacted a witness during the re examination. According to its case note, the witness confirmed the observation point, the alignment of the driveway and the historical height of the hedges, and accepted that the described mechanism could account for the experience. This does not make witness agreement a scientific requirement, but it strengthens the reconstruction by showing that it was tested against the remembered layout rather than inferred solely from an old summary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONETAY SUR ALLIER (03) 05.12.1998 GEIPAN... The case is especially valuable because it demonstrates how an official conclusion can change. “Unexplained” did not mean that investigators had proved the absence of an ordinary cause. It meant that the cause had not yet been demonstrated. Once the viewing geometry and lunar position were reconstructed, the earlier mystery weakened substantially. The 1990 UFO wave was falling rocket debris The most dramatic example connected with Allier was not a local event in the narrow sense. On the evening of 5 November 1990, witnesses across France reported an enormous luminous formation crossing the sky. GEIPAN received reports through gendarmerie brigades nationwide; its archive includes testimony from Louroux de Beaune in Allier. More than a thousand witnesses were eventually associated with the event, making it one of the largest French UFO reporting waves on record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Descriptions varied sharply. People reported rows or triangles of lights, a vast dark structure, separate luminous objects and something apparently flying slowly at low altitude."},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases/","description":"The Yzeure, Cosne-d'Allier and Cerilly reports remain open mainly because the surviving evidence is too weak to test.","breadcrumb":"Why Allier's Strangest Sightings Changed With...","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Why Allier's Unresolved Cases Remain So Uncertain Allier UFOs Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Why Allier's Unresolved Cases Remain So Uncertain The Yzeure, Cosne d'Allier and Cerilly reports remain open mainly because the surviving evidence is too weak to test. The Yzeure, Cosne d'Allier and Cerilly reports remain open mainly because the surviving evidence is too weak to test. dataset evidence Yzeure's orange disc report The two episodes at Cosne d'Allier Missing traces, measurements and corroboration allier unresolved cases Introduction Allier ’s best known unresolved sightings remain uncertain for a less dramatic reason than the word “unresolved” suggests: the evidence is too incomplete to support either a firm conventional explanation or an extraordinary one. The Yzeure report of an orange disc, the unexplained part of the Cosne d’Allier incident and the unusual ground marks reported near Cérilly are all classified C by GEIPAN, the French space agency’s official investigation unit. In GEIPAN’s system, category C means that a phenomenon could not be identified because reliable data were missing; it is distinct from category D, in which useful evidence survives but the event still resists explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr information. Classification D... That distinction is central to Allier’s UFO history. These cases are open files, but not strong evidence for exotic craft. Their descriptions can sound remarkable, yet none produced the measurements, recoverable material, independent observations or detection records needed to test what witnesses believed they saw. Yzeure’s orange disc report At 7.42 am on 24 September 1979, a witness at Yzeure, beside Moulins, first noticed what appeared to be an aircraft’s vapour trail. According to the official summary, the witness then saw an orange, disc shaped object apparently remain stationary for ten to fifteen seconds. It reportedly accelerated suddenly, became brilliant white and disappeared. No second witness was found, and GEIPAN classified the case C because the available information was insufficient. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANYZEURE (03) 24.09.1979YZEURE (03) 24.09.1979. Date d'observation. 24/09/1979. Région. Auvergne. Département. Allier. Classification... The account contains several features that make it memorable: a definite shape, a colour change, a period of apparent hovering and a rapid departure. None of those features, however, was independently measured. A single observer looking at an object high in the sky usually has no secure reference for its distance or size. Without distance, apparent speed cannot be converted into physical speed; something nearby moving moderately can resemble something distant moving extraordinarily fast. “Stationary” is similarly difficult to establish. An aircraft moving towards or away from an observer may show little sideways motion, while a bright point seen against an almost featureless sky can appear fixed. The transition from orange to white might reflect a changing viewing angle, atmospheric extinction near the horizon, sunlight striking a surface or simply a shift in perceived brightness. These are possible mechanisms rather than retrospective solutions: the record does not preserve enough directional, meteorological or aviation information to choose among them. The report’s opening detail—a trail initially interpreted as coming from a jet—is potentially important, but it cannot now be evaluated properly. The available summary does not establish whether the trail and disc were physically connected, whether the “disc” was a compact bright section of the same phenomenon, or whether two unrelated objects were seen in succession. No photograph, radar report, aircraft identification or angular position sequence is published with the summary. GEIPAN therefore records an unexplained observation through lack of information, not an object shown to have performed impossible manoeuvres. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANYZEURE (03) 24.09.1979YZEURE (03) 24.09.1979. Date d'observation. 24/09/1979. Région. Auvergne. Département. Allier. Classification... Later reporting has not materially strengthened the case. A local retrospective repeats the official description, but does not introduce a second witness, contemporary photograph or newly recovered technical record. The account has remained stable as a story while remaining weak as a testable event. [La Semaine de l'Allier] lasemainedelallier.fr 21 janvier 1985.... Description: Dans la soirée du 21 janvier 1985 entre 23h 30 et 24h des témoins vont apercevoir des flashes de lumiè... The two episodes at Cosne d’Allier The Cosne d’Allier file is particularly easy to misread because it combines two separate incidents from the evening of 21 January 1985. One was solved during the gendarmerie investigation. The other remained unidentified because the enquiry found no corroborating information. Treating them as a single extended manifestation exaggerates the mystery and obscures the most useful part of the investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOSNE D'ALLIER (03) 21.01.1985 GEIPAN... Late that night, witnesses saw repeated white flashes close to the ground around the ruins of a house. Gendarmes returned to the location and reproduced the effect. Wind and damp conditions were causing a poor contact between an electric fence wire and its support, producing short circuits and flashes. Witness statements described several horizontal bursts, their low height and the otherwise silent surroundings; the physical inspection supplied a direct cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The earlier event, observed by a married couple while travelling by car between about 8.45 and 9.15 pm, was different. Their statements describe a yellow, triangular light that seemed to stretch into a rectangle, sometimes appeared blue and brighter, and later diminished until it resembled a star. At one point the couple felt that the light was following or overtaking them. GEIPAN’s summary says it then disappeared abruptly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOSNE D'ALLIER (03) 21.01.1985 GEIPAN... This is a genuine two witness report, which gives it more weight than Yzeure’s solitary observation. Yet the witnesses were together in the same moving vehicle and were viewing the same scene from almost the same position. Their accounts are mutually supportive, but they are not independent in the stronger evidential sense of observers at separated locations establishing a common trajectory. The apparent pursuit is also difficult to interpret without bearings. A distant light can remain at roughly the same angle to a moving car and seem to accompany it, an effect familiar with the Moon, planets, towers and distant aircraft. Changes in trees, bends, gradients and the vehicle’s own movement can make a fixed or slowly moving light appear to approach, withdraw or cross the road. This does not prove that the Cosne light was astronomical or terrestrial. It shows why the witnesses’ impression of being followed cannot establish the object’s actual motion on its own. The gendarmes made useful checks. Their report records that officers on duty in the area at around 9.30 pm had noticed nothing abnormal, that neighbouring brigades received no reports from other members of the public along the route, and that no further information capable of explaining the couple’s observation was discovered. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Those negative findings cut both ways. They reduce the likelihood of a large, conspicuous phenomenon visible across a wide area, but they do not eliminate a local or direction dependent light. They also leave no observational baseline from which to calculate altitude, distance, angular speed or route. The report contains estimates of colour and apparent form, but no photograph, timed sequence of bearings, physical effect on the car, radar confirmation or separately positioned witness. The solved electric fence flashes are therefore not evidence that every observation that evening had the same cause. Equally, the unresolved road sighting should not inherit mystery from the visually striking but completely explained flashes. The careful conclusion is narrower: one episode was reproduced and identified; a second could not be reconstructed well enough to identify. GEIPAN consequently retains the combined file as category C, explicitly describing the problem as a lack of reliable information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOSNE D'ALLIER (03) 21.01.1985 GEIPAN... Cérilly’s lost ground marks The Cérilly case of 18 October 1994 differs from Yzeure and Cosne d’Allier because it did not begin with a light in the sky. At about 2 pm, an off duty gendarme gathering mushrooms reportedly found a ground circle composed of stone fragments resembling clinker. Leaves touching the fragments had taken on what was described as a sulphurous green colour. As darkness approached, he left"},{"title":"Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History","url":"/alpes-de-haute-provence/","description":"Alpes-de-Haute-Provence occupies an unusually prominent place in French UFO history because of one event: the alleged Valensole encounter of 1 July 1965.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History Alpes de Haute Provence occupies an unusually prominent place in French UFO history because of one event: the alleged Valensole encounter of 1 July 1965. Alpes de Haute Provence occupies an unusually prominent place in French UFO history because of one event: the alleged Valensole encounter of 1 July 1965. Farmer Maurice Masse reported seeing a compact landed craft, two small occupants and a beam like device that temporarily immobilised him. alpes de haute provence Introduction Alpes de Haute Provence occupies an unusually prominent place in French UFO history because of one event: the alleged Valensole encounter of 1 July 1965. Farmer Maurice Masse reported seeing a compact landed craft, two small occupants and a beam like device that temporarily immobilised him. Gendarmes recorded marks in the soil, and the case was later placed in the unexplained category by France’s official UFO investigation service. Yet the surviving evidence rests chiefly on one witness, belated specialist investigation and disputed physical traces. Military helicopter activity, misperception and fabrication have all been proposed without conclusively resolving the story. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page The department’s wider record is less dramatic. Public GEIPAN data contain dozens of reports from places including Meyronnes, Volx, Les Mées, Sisteron, Barcelonnette and Digne les Bains, but most have been explained, judged probably explained or left undecidable because information is missing. Only Valensole and the much later Volx sighting stand out as officially unexplained cases in the current departmental dataset. That distinction matters: “unexplained” means that investigators could not identify a phenomenon from the available evidence, not that they confirmed an extraordinary vehicle. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr alpes de haute provence OVNI dans le Alpes de Haute Provence (04) — CarteOvni.frToutes les observations d'OVNI dans le département Alpes de Haute Provence (04)... Valensole: the department’s defining case Maurice Masse, a 41 year old lavender farmer, said that he was working near Valensole early on 1 July 1965 when he heard a whistling sound. Approaching what he initially thought might be a helicopter, he reportedly saw an oval or rugby ball shaped object resting above the ground on several supports. Near it were two small figures. Masse said that one pointed a tube like instrument towards him, after which he became unable to move until the figures entered the object and it departed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page Accounts published over the years vary in wording and detail. The object has been described as roughly the size of a Renault Dauphine, with a transparent upper section, a sliding opening and six leg like supports around a central pivot. Masse consistently rejected the press label of “flying saucer” and later insisted that he knew what helicopters looked and sounded like. Nevertheless, the more elaborate versions familiar from books and documentaries should not automatically be treated as exact reproductions of his earliest statement. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole The story spread rapidly through Valensole and then the national press. This speed is important for two opposing reasons. It brought gendarmes to the alleged site while traces could still be examined, but it also exposed the witness to journalists, investigators, believers and curious visitors before a controlled scientific inquiry could be organised. The case became a media event almost immediately rather than an incident preserved under laboratory conditions. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole What the gendarmes found Gendarmes recorded a depression or disturbed area at the location identified by Masse. Subsequent accounts referred to a central mark, radiating grooves and damaged vegetation. Claims were later made that lavender failed to grow normally at the spot and that soil samples showed an unusual calcium level, although no radioactivity was detected. Supporters regarded these features as possible landing traces or environmental effects from an unknown machine. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page GEIPANle GEPANNo information is available for this page These traces are the principal reason Valensole is stronger than an ordinary distant light report. They provide something beyond the witness’s recollection. They are not, however, proof that the reported craft produced them. The site was agricultural land, alternative causes were not comprehensively eliminated at the outset, and some of the more dramatic vegetation claims were documented or interpreted long after the event. No recovered component, photograph, instrument reading or independently measured flight path links the marks to an airborne object. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole The timing of the formal UFO investigation is another limitation. France did not create GEPAN, the original CNES study group, until 1977, twelve years after the encounter. Its later assessment therefore depended heavily on gendarmerie papers, press material, subsequent interviews and retrospective examination rather than immediate scientific measurements. GEIPAN still lists Valensole as an unexplained case, but that classification records the failure to establish a satisfactory conventional account; it does not certify every detail in the accumulated narrative. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. Helicopter, hoax or genuinely unresolved? The most persistent conventional explanation is a military helicopter. Exercise “Provence 65” was taking place in the region, and French Army light aviation used Alouette helicopters during the manoeuvres. Contemporary press reports said military sources considered an Alouette a likely explanation. An unfamiliar landing, turbine noise, crew members crouching near the aircraft and rotor downwash could conceivably have been transformed by distance, surprise and later retelling into Masse’s extraordinary description. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole This hypothesis fits the regional aviation context better than an extraterrestrial interpretation, but it has weaknesses. Masse said the machine had no rotor or blades and denied mistaking a helicopter. No publicly established flight record identifies a particular aircraft and crew at the field, while the alleged immobilisation and unusual occupants require additional psychological or narrative assumptions. A variation proposes an unauthorised American helicopter, but this has even less direct documentary support. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole Other sceptical proposals focus on the ground rather than the sky. Marks may have resulted from agricultural equipment, including a liquid fertiliser tank, and the damaged soil or vegetation may have had an ordinary farming cause. A deliberate story has also been suggested, possibly influenced by an earlier illustrated tale containing a landed saucer, small beings and a paralysing ray. Critics of that theory note that the similarities are incomplete and that there is no evidence Masse read the publication. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole Rencontre de Valensole A more recent psychological interpretation argues that Masse may have encountered an ordinary military stimulus but experienced an acute dissociative or trauma related reaction that altered his perception and movement. This offers a possible natural route to the sensation of paralysis without accusing him of conscious deceit. It remains a retrospective clinical hypothesis, however, formed without examining Masse at the time and dependent on contested reconstructions of his wartime experience. [Academia] academia.edu Open source on academia.edu. Valensole is therefore best described as unresolved but evidentially limited. The early gendarmerie involvement, named witness and physical marks distinguish it from a casual anecdote. Against that stand the absence of independent eyewitnesses to the central encounter, uncertainty over the traces, nearby military activity and decades of narrative embellishment. Later reporting has expanded the story’s cultural importance far more than it has strengthened its physical evidence. The 1976 Meyronnes military reports The department’s clearest military witness episode occurred around Meyronnes, in the upper Ubaye"},{"title":"Other Cases","url":"/other-cases/","description":"Military witnesses at Meyronnes and a brief sighting at Volx show why some reports remain unresolved even when the evidence is thin.","breadcrumb":"Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History","level":2,"text":"Other Cases Which Other Local UFO Reports Remain Unresolved? UFOs of Alpes de Haute Provence Other Cases Other Cases Which Other Local UFO Reports Remain Unresolved? Military witnesses at Meyronnes and a brief sighting at Volx show why some reports remain unresolved even when the evidence is thin. Military witnesses at Meyronnes and a brief sighting at Volx show why some reports remain unresolved even when the evidence is thin. dataset evidence The 1976 Meyronnes military sightings The unexplained 2012 Volx object What GEIPAN classifications do and do not mean alpes de haute provence other cases Introduction Beyond the famous Valensole case, Alpes de Haute Provence has a quieter collection of reports that remain unresolved for very different reasons. At Meyronnes in December 1976, several military witnesses reported slow, silent objects over a mountain valley, but the surviving file lacks enough reliable measurements to test ordinary explanations. At Volx in August 2012, a lone observer gave a detailed account of a dark, fast moving object, prompting a much fuller investigation; GEIPAN still lists the phenomenon as unidentified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVOLX (04) 08.08.2012 GEIPAN... The contrast matters. Meyronnes is officially classified C , meaning that insufficient dependable information prevents a sound conclusion. Volx is classified D , meaning that investigators retained significant residual strangeness after examining the available evidence. Neither classification confirms an extraordinary craft. Together, the cases show why “unresolved” can mean either an intriguing observation that resisted proposed explanations or simply a file too incomplete to decide. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan D1/D2), D1 et D2 étant des subdivisions de D. La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN. La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D1/D2) s'ap... The 1976 Meyronnes military sightings The Meyronnes file concerns events on two consecutive nights, 8 and 9 December 1976, in the Ubayette valley near the former commune of Meyronnes. At about 11 pm, military personnel reported seeing several round objects moving slowly and silently from the south east towards the north west. GEIPAN’s summary says that the observations lasted only a few seconds, although the original gendarmerie dossier contains several witness statements and supporting diagrams. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEYRONNES (04) 08.12.1976 GEIPAN... The surviving police file is more substantial than a simple anecdote. It was assembled as a formal gendarmerie procedure, included a synthesis, witness interviews, photographs and sketches, and was circulated to civil and military authorities. One diagram attempted to record angular separations, an estimated elevation of about 20 degrees and a possible distance of several kilometres. Such estimates can help reconstruct an event, but in this case they were approximate rather than instrument derived. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witnesses’ military status gives the report some historical interest. Service personnel working in a mountain environment could reasonably be expected to notice aircraft, flares or unusual activity. Yet occupation alone does not guarantee accurate estimates of distance, size or speed, particularly at night, when an isolated light may offer no reliable scale. The file does not contain radar confirmation, photographs of the objects, recovered material or measured flight data. An unusual secondary detail entered the case from a dog handling unit roughly ten kilometres away. According to the official summary, its ten dogs howled abnormally on the same two nights. Another witness described an earlier incident, about six weeks before, in which a red light rose near the foot of the ridges and disappeared behind a summit. These details suggest that investigators looked for a wider pattern, but neither establishes that the animals, the earlier light and the military observations had the same cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEYRONNES (04) 08.12.1976 GEIPAN... The authorities did not simply file the statements and forget them. Surveillance was reportedly maintained from 12 to 30 December, but no further phenomenon was seen. That negative result weakens any suggestion of persistent or routinely repeated activity in the valley, although it cannot explain what the witnesses had already reported. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEYRONNES (04) 08.12.1976 GEIPAN... Why Meyronnes is not an “unexplained craft” case GEIPAN classifies Meyronnes as C , with the description “lack of reliable information”. That distinction is crucial. The file records an unidentified observation, but it does not supply enough dependable information for investigators to show that familiar causes were systematically eliminated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEYRONNES (04) 08.12.1976 GEIPAN... Several ordinary possibilities therefore remain open: distant aircraft or helicopters seen without an obvious sound; military illumination or training activity for which no complete record survives; stars or planets appearing to move because the witnesses or their vehicle were moving; atmospheric distortion or lights partly obscured by mountain ridges; inaccurate recollection of duration, number or trajectory when statements were formalised. None of these explanations is demonstrated in the published file. Equally, the file does not demonstrate controlled manoeuvres, extraordinary acceleration or a physical object with known dimensions. The most responsible conclusion is the official one: the incident is undecidable because its evidence is too weak, not because an exotic interpretation has prevailed. Ovnis et nucléaire : ce que le GEIPAN a découvert Michael Vaillant Channel: The Paranormal Show Open on YouTube The unexplained 2012 Volx object The Volx report is different in both form and investigative depth. At about 11 pm on 8 August 2012, a witness was lying on a terrace watching the stars when a dark object crossed the sky from north east to south west. He described it as resembling a weaver’s shuttle: elongated, brown green, with diffuse edges and a dim, dark red circular area in its centre. It appeared to obscure or distort the star field behind it, travelled in a straight line and produced no audible noise. The entire observation lasted approximately five seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVOLX (04) 08.08.2012 GEIPAN... The witness contacted GEIPAN later that month and subsequently made a statement at the Manosque gendarmerie. Because the account was unusual but contained uncertainties, a local investigator visited the site with him in January 2015. The scene was reconstructed twice to refine the duration, viewing angles and trajectory. Investigators estimated that the phenomenon entered view at about 30 degrees elevation in the north east, passed close to the zenith and disappeared behind vegetation at about 27 degrees elevation in the south west. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete374 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This fieldwork is the strongest feature of the case. It moved the inquiry beyond the witness’s initial written description and tested whether his account remained reasonably consistent at the location. GEIPAN found one understandable discrepancy in his original drawing of the direction of travel, but did not regard it as undermining the whole testimony. The witness had also made a sketch and noted the time shortly after the sighting, reducing—but not eliminating—the risk of later memory expansion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete374 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Investigators checked several conventional categories. No relevant bright planet was above the horizon, and no particular aircraft, satellite or other recorded aerial traffic was identified for the stated time. Meteorological data indicated a clear sky, good visibility and a westerly wind. A Météo France specialist reportedly recognised no known weather phenomenon matching the account. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete374 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The object’s apparent movement also seemed inconsistent with something passively carried by the wind. GEIPAN calculated a range of hypothetical sizes and speeds based on different assumed altitudes. At very low altitude, an animal might fit parts of the speed and size relationship, but not the reported shape and colour. At typical helicopter or aircraft altitudes, the absence of engine noise became difficult to reconcile with the object passing almost overhead in a quiet setting. At greater assumed altitudes, the calculated dimensions or velocity became implausibly large. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete374 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... These calculations illustrate the case’s interest, but they"},{"title":"Valensole","url":"/valensole/","description":"The 1965 Valensole case remains compelling because of its named witness and ground marks, but neither proves the reported craft existed.","breadcrumb":"Why Valensole Still Defines a Department's UFO History","level":2,"text":"Valensole Did the Valensole Evidence Survive Serious Scrutiny? UFOs of Alpes de Haute Provence Valensole Valensole Did the Valensole Evidence Survive Serious Scrutiny? The 1965 Valensole case remains compelling because of its named witness and ground marks, but neither proves the reported craft existed. The 1965 Valensole case remains compelling because of its named witness and ground marks, but neither proves the reported craft existed. event time window Maurice Masse's earliest account What the ground traces can establish Helicopter, hoax and psychological explanations alpes de haute provence valensole Introduction The Valensole encounter remains the defining UFO case of Alpes de Haute Provence because it combines three features rarely found together: a named local witness, a reported landed object with occupants, and marks examined by gendarmes soon afterwards. Yet serious scrutiny leaves a more cautious conclusion than the legend suggests. Maurice Masse plainly reported an extraordinary experience, and an unusual depression existed where he said the object had stood. Neither fact establishes that a craft or non human visitors were present. The strongest surviving material is the gendarmerie documentation, but even that records a single witness claim at a site already disturbed by curious visitors. Later stories about paralysing rays, lasting crop damage and chemically altered soil are harder to assess because some details emerged weeks or years later, and no controlled scientific investigation began in 1965. GEIPAN still lists Valensole as unexplained, but its own summary says the classification was assigned “by default” because of the case’s strangeness, not because an official inquiry verified an exotic vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALENSOLE (04) 01.07.1965 GEIPAN... Maurice Masse’s earliest account At about 5.45 am on 1 July 1965, lavender farmer Maurice Masse said he heard a whistling noise while working near Valensole. According to the later official summary, he approached a dark, matt object resting on the ground, watched two figures beside it for four or five minutes, and saw them re enter before the object rose obliquely and disappeared towards Manosque. He returned to the spot roughly ten minutes later and found a star like disturbance with a cylindrical hole in wet earth. That evening, he said, the soil had become “hard as cement”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALENSOLE (04) 01.07.1965 GEIPAN... The chronology matters because the familiar full narrative did not appear in one pristine statement. Masse first spoke publicly on 2 July. Local gendarmes interviewed him that evening, accompanied him to the field at about 10 pm, and took a supplementary statement later that night. A separate site report followed after measurements and photographs. GEIPAN records that a much more detailed addition was supplied on 18 August, nearly seven weeks later. That later account included his approach to within about seven metres, two occupants crouching near lavender, and temporary immobilisation after one pointed an object towards him. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALENSOLE (04) 01.07.1965 GEIPAN... This does not prove that the later details were invented. Witnesses often disclose experiences gradually, particularly when embarrassed, shocked or repeatedly questioned. However, the distinction between the first core report and later elaboration is essential. The “beam” or tube that paralysed Masse is now one of the most memorable elements of Valensole, but it was not preserved as part of a single immediate, independently recorded narrative. Early newspaper coverage also shows that accounts were already inconsistent. A report in Le Méridional on 4 July described one small being in an orange suit and stated that the witness spoke only of the incident the next day after discussing it with family and friends. The same article portrayed Masse as a colourful storyteller given to imaginary fishing exploits. By contrast, Le Provençal presented him as neither eccentric nor a prankster and reported that investigators found him detailed and consistent under questioning. These sharply different portrayals demonstrate how rapidly local reputation became part of the argument for or against the case. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, the Maurice Masse sighting in the Press, France, 1965... Masse’s sincerity is therefore relevant but not decisive. A credible person may accurately report an unusual event while misidentifying its cause; equally, a reputation for storytelling does not establish fraud. No second witness saw the object or occupants, and the gendarmerie found no further physical clue in the surrounding area. GEIPAN states explicitly that no additional testimony was collected. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALENSOLE (04) 01.07.1965 GEIPAN... What the ground traces can establish The marks are the feature that raises Valensole above an unsupported anecdote. Gendarmes documented a central cylindrical hole and shallow radiating grooves. A later summary describes the hole as approximately 18 centimetres across and 40 centimetres deep, with four shallow furrows forming a cross. Contemporary press gave different measurements, including a depth of 80 centimetres, illustrating that even apparently simple physical details were not transmitted consistently. [wikipedia.org] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole — Wikipédia Rencontre de Valensole — Wikipédia The defensible conclusion is limited: an unusual looking disturbance existed at the place Masse identified. It cannot be shown that the marks were made at the time of his sighting, that they were caused by one object, or that their geometry matched the reported six supports and central pivot. The official case page says many people had already visited and trampled the site before the gendarmerie learnt of the event through public rumour at about 7.30 pm on 2 July. A newspaper account similarly reported that the furrows had been flattened by visitors. The location was therefore compromised before systematic examination began. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALENSOLE (04) 01.07.1965 GEIPAN... That contamination affects more than footprints. Investigators lacked a secured perimeter, an untouched control area and a documented chain of custody for every sample. There was no photograph of the alleged object in place, no recovered fragment, no instrument reading and no independently observed landing or departure. The marks remain corroboration that something disturbed the soil, not corroboration of Masse’s explanation for it. Later claims are often more impressive than the immediate record. Accounts have asserted that lavender deteriorated along the departure path, that plants failed to grow at the central site for years, that the soil contained unusually high calcium, and that no radioactivity was detected. Researcher Pierre Guérin proposed that electromagnetic effects might explain the calcium level and infertility. Yet this was an interpretation, not a demonstrated mechanism, and the absence of an early controlled agricultural survey makes it difficult to separate extraordinary effects from soil treatment, local chemistry, mechanical disturbance or subsequent human activity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Valensole — Wikipédia Rencontre de Valensole — Wikipédia The “hard as cement” description is similarly suggestive without being diagnostic. Wet, compacted or chemically treated soil can harden for ordinary reasons. A hardened patch does not identify what compressed or altered it. The physical evidence is therefore genuine in the modest sense that marks were recorded, but weak in the stronger sense required to prove a landed machine. They Landed in His Field – The Untold Truth Behind the Valensole UFO Encounter (1965) Channel: The Deep Dark Secrets Open on YouTube Does the helicopter explanation fit? A helicopter was proposed almost immediately, not decades later. Provence was hosting military exercises, and contemporary military sources said participating helicopters had probably flown over Valensole and might have landed nearby. Le Monde published the explanation within days, linking Masse’s report to the manoeuvres. The local press also noted that the “Provence 65” exercise had involved aircraft operating from Saint Auban. [Le Monde.fr] lemonde.fr Le Monde.fr L A SOUCOUPE VOLANTE ÉTAIT SANS DOUTE UN HÉLICOPTÈRE Le Monde.fr L A SOUCOUPE VOLANTE ÉTAIT SANS DOUTE UN HÉLICOPTÈRE The theory has several attractions. Masse initially investigated because the sound made him think a helicopter had landed. A turbine could account for a whistle, a compact helicopter seen from an unfamiliar angle might look unlike an ordinary aircraft, and crew members could be perceived as small if distance or scale were misjudged. Landing gear and a central underside fitting also provide a conventional framework for some of the shapes he described."},{"title":"Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade","url":"/alpes-maritimes/","description":"Alpes-Maritimes has one of France’s richer departmental archives of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its history is more instructive than...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade Alpes Maritimes has one of France’s richer departmental archives of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its history is more instructive than... Alpes Maritimes has one of France’s richer departmental archives of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its history is more instructive than sensational. Reports cluster around the densely populated Riviera, the open sea, mountain viewpoints and the celebrated Col de Vence. alpes maritimes Introduction The most important conclusion is therefore a cautious one. Alpes Maritimes has produced memorable testimony, recurring local folklore and several initially puzzling incidents, but no publicly documented case from the department establishes an extraterrestrial or technologically extraordinary origin. The strongest cases are those in which the original strangeness can be reconstructed; the weakest are isolated recollections, distant lights and photographs lacking independent measurements. Why the department produces so many reports The geography of Alpes Maritimes is unusually good at creating ambiguous views. A witness can look from elevated ground across the Mediterranean, over coastal cities, towards heavily used air routes and into rapidly changing weather. Distance is difficult to judge over dark sea or mountain terrain, while lights from aircraft, ships and settlements may appear detached from any visible structure. This matters because most UFO reports begin with uncertainty about range. A small nearby balloon and a large distant aircraft can occupy the same apparent size in the sky. At night, an observer may also lose the horizon and interpret a ship’s lights as hovering above the water. GEIPAN’s 2016 Vence investigation demonstrated precisely this problem: two elongated luminous forms that appeared stationary and then vanished were judged probably to be cruise ships offshore. Daylight comparison photographs helped locate the true horizon, while calculated positions and dimensions matched maritime traffic. Their abrupt disappearance was consistent with the vessels switching off prominent lights as they moved away. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVENCE (06) 09.10.2016 GEIPAN... The department also contains several reliable sources of unusual looking aerial activity: Nice Côte d’Azur Airport and regional flight paths , producing aircraft seen head on, turning or apparently hovering. Helicopter operations , including emergency, police and civil protection missions over difficult terrain. Marine traffic , whose lights may be mislocated when sea and sky merge after dark. Tourist and festive objects , especially helium balloons, illuminated balloons and lanterns. Satellites, the International Space Station and rocket debris , which can appear silent, bright and unfamiliar. Mountain meteorology , including cloud illumination, halos and deceptive visibility. These are not speculative catch all explanations. They recur in GEIPAN’s own classifications for Alpes Maritimes, which include weather balloons, children’s balloons, Mylar balloons, lanterns, the International Space Station, aircraft, a meteoroid, rocket components, Venus, cloud effects and ships. Recent published entries continue the same pattern: a 2023 Cannes report was identified as Venus, a 2024 Villeneuve Loubet case as a Mylar balloon, and a May 2025 Nice case as a lantern. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Nice mass sighting that lost its mystery One of the department’s most striking historical reports occurred over Nice on 19 December 1981. Several witnesses watched between roughly 50 and 100 bright objects moving slowly and silently in a loose formation. Most were described as round and white; three trailing objects appeared more tube like and alternated between white and orange. Two witnesses made statements to the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... For years, the case carried the prestige of an official unexplained classification. Its scale seemed impressive: many objects, a long observation and more than one witness. But later review weakened rather than strengthened the extraordinary interpretation. GEIPAN noted that the movement, silence and grouped appearance strongly resembled a mass balloon release. The date was also significant: a Saturday morning immediately before the Christmas school holiday, when a release of balloons carrying children’s messages was plausible. The file was consequently reinterpreted as a probable festive balloon event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... The Nice case is a useful warning against treating an old “unexplained” label as permanent. Classification may reflect the information and investigative tools available at the time, not the inherent quality of the phenomenon. GEIPAN explicitly re examines older files using improved mapping, astronomical software, meteorological information and accumulated experience. Nationally, the organisation says that many former unexplained cases have been reassessed and identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Cannes in 2015: a long sighting, but ordinary behaviour On the morning of 16 June 2015, a couple on a beach at Cannes watched a changing group of bright forms for approximately 45 minutes. The main witness described first one object and later six, with apparent shapes shifting between a triangle, circle, moving cluster and line. The lights seemed to blink or change brightness and moved irregularly before adopting a steadier path. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete466 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Duration sometimes makes a case sound stronger: surely, the argument goes, witnesses observing for three quarters of an hour would recognise something mundane. In practice, long duration can favour slow windborne objects. GEIPAN concluded that the behaviour was consistent with a cluster of reflective Mylar balloons being moved by light and variable coastal winds. Their surfaces could produce changing flashes as they turned in sunlight, while a group of tethered balloons could repeatedly alter its apparent geometry. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete466 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This case also illustrates why shape reports should be treated carefully. Witnesses often describe not the outline of a solid body, but the temporary arrangement of lights. A group that changes from “triangle” to “line” may be several independent objects viewed at different angles, rather than one craft changing form. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Menton and Sospel: unresolved does not mean extraordinary The eastern side of the department has generated a small local cluster around Menton and Sospel. Nice Matin reported that, by July 2021, GEIPAN’s database contained seven observations from Menton and one from Sospel. Two had been identified conclusively, while six were classed as category C: not identified because the available information was insufficient. [Nice Matin] nicematin.com Nice Matin Des OVNIS traversent ils le ciel de Menton? Nice Matin Des OVNIS traversent ils le ciel de Menton? Category C is easily misunderstood. It does not mean that investigators found strong evidence for an anomalous craft. It usually means that a plausible explanation cannot be demonstrated because the account lacks precise timing, corroborating witnesses, original imagery, positional data or other checks. GEIPAN separates such cases from genuinely persistent anomalies because evidential weakness and unexplained strangeness are not the same thing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN A Menton report from 20 May 2013 makes the distinction clear. A man on the beach saw a white object within a translucent, concentric looking halo moving above the sea for 10 to 15 seconds. Investigators considered an aircraft surrounded by an optical halo, but the witness’s speed estimate appeared inconsistent with that idea. With only one witness and too little information to test the discrepancy, the case remained category C. That label preserves uncertainty; it does not confirm that the witness saw an exotic vehicle. [Nice Matin] nicematin.com Nice Matin Des OVNIS traversent ils le ciel de Menton? Nice Matin Des OVNIS traversent ils le ciel de Menton? Other Menton cases were resolved more firmly. A dark object filmed in November 2011 was judged to be a helium character balloon carried by the wind. Investigators compared its movement with the local wind direction and identified a"},{"title":"Landmark Cases","url":"/landmark-cases/","description":"The best-known local cases became less mysterious when investigators reconstructed balloons, wind, lighting and witness perspective.","breadcrumb":"Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade","level":2,"text":"Landmark Cases How Riviera UFO Classics Lost Their Mystery Alpes Maritimes Landmark Cases Landmark Cases How Riviera UFO Classics Lost Their Mystery The best known local cases became less mysterious when investigators reconstructed balloons, wind, lighting and witness perspective. The best known local cases became less mysterious when investigators reconstructed balloons, wind, lighting and witness perspective. event time window The 1981 Nice mass sighting The 2015 Cannes balloon cluster Why official classifications can change alpes maritimes landmark cases Introduction Two of the Riviera’s most memorable UFO reports became markedly less mysterious when investigators revisited the witnesses’ descriptions alongside wind records, lighting conditions and viewing geometry. In Nice on 19 December 1981, several people watched dozens of bright objects cross the morning sky. Originally left unexplained, the case was later reclassified as a probable mass release of festive balloons. In Cannes on 16 June 2015, six apparently luminous bodies changed from a triangle into a circle and then a straight line; GEIPAN concluded that tethered reflective balloons moving in weak, shifting coastal winds could reproduce the whole sequence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... Neither explanation is a recovered object identification. No launch organiser was traced and no balloon was collected. Both are category B cases: the French official investigation considers the proposed explanation highly probable rather than proved beyond doubt. Their importance within Alpes Maritimes UFO history lies precisely there. They show how an impressive report can lose most of its residual strangeness once motion, sunlight and witness perspective are reconstructed. The 1981 Nice mass sighting Late on the morning of Saturday 19 December 1981, a postal worker in central Nice noticed roughly 50 coloured, round objects approaching from the north east. He alerted colleagues at a nearby post office, where five other people reportedly joined the observation. The objects passed close to their apparent overhead position and continued towards the south west, remaining visible until about 10.50 am. Only two witnesses later gave formal statements to the gendarmerie, but their accounts preserved enough detail for a much later review. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. One witness estimated between 80 and 100 brilliant white objects, spread through a loose line and reflecting light like polished aluminium. The other recalled about 50 round objects in yellow, green, red and blue. Both described slow, silent movement without smoke or a trail. Three objects near the rear appeared tubular to one observer and seemed to alternate between white and orange; the second witness instead said that some members of the group appeared to switch on and off. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At first glance, the number of objects and the apparent changes in colour make the event sound more difficult to explain than an isolated light. Yet the collective behaviour is the strongest clue to an ordinary cause. The objects all travelled slowly in broadly the same direction, did not make sudden independent departures and eventually disappeared behind cloud, roofs or surrounding terrain. Their loose spacing and mixed shapes are compatible with balloons released together and gradually spreading as they drifted. Why balloons fitted better on review The original case had been placed in category D, meaning that it remained unexplained with the information then assessed. GEIPAN later revisited it using mapping tools, archived weather observations and greater experience of recurring misidentifications. The review found several mutually supporting features: The timing was suggestive. The sighting occurred on the last Saturday morning before the 1981 Christmas school holidays. Investigators proposed that a school or seasonal event might have released a large number of balloons, possibly carrying children’s messages. No particular release was identified, so this remains contextual support rather than direct proof. The speed matched light winds. Nice Airport recorded wind of roughly 7 kilometres per hour during the relevant period. A long, slow transit lasting many minutes is much more characteristic of passive airborne objects than of aircraft performing coordinated manoeuvres. The reported brightness depended on viewpoint. Coloured or metallic balloons can appear white when they reflect the Sun directly. As irregular or cylindrical balloons rotate, their reflective faces alternately brighten, darken or reveal a different colour, creating an impression of flashing. The unusual “tubes” need not have been separate craft. A mixed release can include elongated balloons as well as conventional round ones. Rotation would also change their apparent width and colour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... The largest apparent difficulty was direction. Witnesses consistently placed the movement from north east to south west, while the surface wind at the airport was closer to north to south. GEIPAN did not ignore that mismatch. Its review noted that the observing site was about six kilometres from the measuring station, that the objects were above ground level, and that Nice’s urban terrain and nearby mountains encourage local variations. The wind later turned towards an east to west flow, making an intermediate direction aloft plausible, although not demonstrated by a direct upper air measurement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... That qualification matters. The investigators did not discover a record proving where the balloons were launched. Their conclusion was instead cumulative: number, formation, silence, speed, seasonal setting, reflections and wind were all mutually compatible with a balloon release, while the report contained no acceleration, physical effect, radar confirmation or independently measured feature that required an exotic vehicle. GEIPAN therefore moved the case from unexplained category D to category B, recording “probable festive balloons”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNICE (06) 19.12.1981 GEIPAN... The 2015 Cannes balloon cluster The Cannes incident began at about 9.25 am on 16 June 2015, when a couple resting on a beach at Cannes la Bocca noticed six components high in a clear daytime sky. The principal witness initially perceived a triangular formation. It then appeared round, divided into pairs and finally became a straight line, sometimes resembling a tilted vertical cigar. Alternate elements looked brilliant or flashing, while the others appeared white or pale grey. The group moved only slightly at first, changed direction and finally rose from view at approximately 10.10 am. No sound was heard. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCANNES (06) 16.06.2015 GEIPAN... The duration made a fleeting meteor or fast aircraft implausible, but it also created a problem for the witness’s own assessment. Without a known size or distance, there was no reliable basis for judging the objects to be higher or larger than passing aeroplanes. Six small balloons relatively nearby can subtend the same visual angle as six large objects much farther away. Against an open blue sky, with no fixed reference at the objects’ altitude, human vision cannot resolve that ambiguity. GEIPAN’s report identified a coherent explanation: a cluster of helium filled foil balloons, commonly called Mylar balloons, still connected by their strings. The explanation addressed not only their general appearance but the apparently intelligent rearrangements that made the episode memorable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCANNES (06) 16.06.2015 GEIPAN... Reflective foil behaves dramatically in sunlight. A face angled towards the observer may flare like a star; a face turned away looks dull white or grey. Each balloon can rotate independently while remaining tied to the same cluster, so bright points seem to blink in alternating positions. Changes between a triangle, circle, pairs and a line can result from the three dimensional bundle turning, stretching and partially folding. The objects need not physically assemble into precise geometric formations; the geometry is the two dimensional pattern projected towards the witnesses at each moment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete466 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The wind explains the sequence Weather evidence supplied the most case specific part of the reconstruction. At the beginning of the observation, the recorded wind was very weak and variable, approximately 5.6 kilometres per hour. It strengthened to about 9.3 kilometres per hour by the end and settled into a more consistent easterly direction. The report associated this change with the morning transition from the overnight land breeze to the developing daytime coastal circulation. [GEIPAN]"},{"title":"Coastal Illusions","url":"/coastal-illusions/","description":"Sea horizons, mountain viewpoints, aircraft routes and marine traffic repeatedly make ordinary lights appear stationary, airborne or strangely shaped.","breadcrumb":"Why So Many Riviera UFO Reports Fade","level":2,"text":"Coastal Illusions Why Riviera Lights Look Like UFOs Alpes Maritimes UFOs Coastal Illusions Coastal Illusions Why Riviera Lights Look Like UFOs Sea horizons, mountain viewpoints, aircraft routes and marine traffic repeatedly make ordinary lights appear stationary, airborne or strangely shaped. Sea horizons, mountain viewpoints, aircraft routes and marine traffic repeatedly make ordinary lights appear stationary, airborne or strangely shaped. mechanism How distance and darkness distort position Ships, aircraft and satellites mistaken for craft What unresolved category C cases actually mean alpes maritimes coastal illusions Introduction Coastal lights become UFO reports in Alpes Maritimes because the Riviera repeatedly removes the visual clues people normally use to judge distance, height and movement. After dark, the Mediterranean can merge with the sky, leaving no obvious horizon. A ship may therefore appear to hover in the air; an aircraft approaching almost head on may seem stationary; and a single distant light can seem to drift even when it is not moving at all. This is not merely a generic explanation applied after the event. A 2016 sighting from Vence was reconstructed by France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, using daylight photographs, horizon measurements and maritime positions. Two apparently stationary luminous forms were judged probably to have been cruise ships offshore. The case shows why Riviera reports can feel genuinely extraordinary to witnesses while still having an ordinary source. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVENCE (06) 09.10.2016 GEIPAN... The important dividing line is therefore not between sincere and unreliable witnesses. It is between observations that preserve enough geographical and timing information to test, and those that remain ambiguous because the sea, terrain and darkness have erased the necessary reference points. How darkness moves the horizon Human vision estimates an object’s position partly by comparing it with surrounding features. On land, buildings, trees, roads and ridges provide scale. Looking south from the hills above Nice, Cagnes sur Mer or Vence, however, a witness may see little more than a dark expanse interrupted by a few bright points. Without a visible waterline, there may be no reliable way to tell whether a light is on the sea, just above it or much higher in the sky. The Vence case of 9 October 2016 provides an unusually clear demonstration. Shortly after midnight, two witnesses saw two similar luminous forms several kilometres away, one roughly towards Corsica and the other towards Nice. The forms looked stationary and disappeared abruptly. GEIPAN later compared the night photographs with an image taken in daylight and established that one light lay on the true horizon; the second could be placed by correlation. Calculations of distance, dimensions and speed were consistent with cruise ships travelling on an offshore route. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVENCE (06) 09.10.2016 GEIPAN... This reconstruction matters because the witnesses had not imagined the lights. Their error concerned where the lights were located. GEIPAN specifically noted that the similar colour and brightness of sea and sky made the horizon difficult to distinguish. Once that boundary disappeared, illuminated parts of the ships could be interpreted as separate objects suspended above the Mediterranean. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVENCE (06) 09.10.2016 GEIPAN... The same loss of reference can distort movement. A faraway vessel moving towards or away from the observer changes its angular position very slowly. Over several minutes it may appear to hover. A turn can then create what looks like sideways acceleration, while the extinguishing or screening of deck lights can produce an apparently instantaneous disappearance. Aviation safety guidance describes closely related night time illusions. The US Federal Aviation Administration warns that obscured horizons, scattered lights and dark, featureless terrain can cause serious errors in perceived position and distance. It also identifies autokinesis : when someone fixes their gaze on an isolated stationary light against darkness, the light may appear to move. These effects are well documented because pilots must be trained not to trust unsupported visual impressions at night. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots On the Riviera, the ingredients often occur together: a weak or invisible sea horizon; one or two exceptionally bright lights; few nearby objects against which to judge scale; an observer positioned high above the coast; atmospheric haze or glare around the light; prolonged staring after the object has attracted attention. The resulting report may contain accurate details about colour and brightness but unreliable estimates of altitude, distance, size and speed. Those estimates are usually not lies or embellishments. They are attempts by the brain to build a three dimensional scene from insufficient information. Ships that appear to hover Cruise ships, ferries, cargo vessels and large yachts can all produce unfamiliar configurations of light. From a distance, the hull may be invisible while illuminated decks, bridge windows, mast lights and decorative lighting remain conspicuous. The witness does not see “a ship”; they see a line, rectangle, cluster or pair of lights with darkness beneath it. This is particularly relevant in Alpes Maritimes because large passenger vessels commonly anchor or travel off Cannes, Nice and Villefranche sur Mer. The volume of cruise activity has been substantial enough to prompt new regional restrictions: reporting on the policy recorded 175 ships anchored in Cannes Bay and 103 in Villefranche sur Mer Bay during 2024. These figures do not explain any individual UFO report, but they show that brightly illuminated offshore vessels are a routine part of the local night environment rather than a remote possibility. [Seatrade Cruise News] seatrade cruise.com Seatrade Cruise NewsFrench authorities unveil unified cruise rules for Riviera ports14 Oct 2025 — In 2024, 175 ships anchored in Cannes b... Several features make them deceptive. Only the upper lights survive at distance. Atmospheric haze, sea mist and the curvature of the Earth can reduce the visibility of a dark hull before they obscure its brightest lights. What remains may look like a structured luminous object floating above the water. The apparent shape is made from lights, not solid edges. A row of cabin windows can suggest a long cigar. Two illuminated deck sections may resemble separate objects. A bright central superstructure with weaker lights extending sideways can be interpreted as a disc or triangle. Motion towards the observer is difficult to see. A vessel following a route nearly aligned with the witness’s line of sight can remain in the same part of the visual field even while travelling at normal speed. Lights can vanish without the vessel vanishing. A ship may turn, place part of its structure between the observer and the light, reduce decorative illumination or switch off a conspicuous display. In the Vence investigation, GEIPAN considered it plausible that the ships’ prominent lights were extinguished as they moved away after a maritime salute near San Remo. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVENCE (06) 09.10.2016 GEIPAN... An abrupt disappearance therefore carries less evidential weight than it may seem. For a self luminous point seen against darkness, turning the light away is visually equivalent to the whole object ceasing to exist. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Aircraft over the sea look unlike aircraft Aircraft are another persistent source of coastal misidentification, especially when only their strongest lights are visible. A conventional airliner is easy to recognise when seen broadside in daylight. At night, viewed nearly head on, it may be reduced to one brilliant white light with no visible wings, fuselage or obvious sideways motion. Nice Côte d’Azur Airport makes this mechanism especially relevant. Its runway procedures interact closely with the Mediterranean and the steep terrain surrounding the city. Aviation guidance for Nice describes visual segments and turns at low altitude over water, early turns towards the sea, and substantial helicopter activity around the airport’s control zone. [Ministères Transition Écologique] ecologie.gouv.fr Ministères Transition Écologique From a balcony, beach or mountain viewpoint, these movements can produce several classic UFO impressions. An approaching aircraft may appear fixed in place because it is moving mainly along the observer’s line of sight. Its landing lights become steadily brighter, encouraging the impression that a"},{"title":"Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification","url":"/ardeche-ufos-between-mystery-and/","description":"Ardèche has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record contains several unexplained reports, yet no single incident has achieved the national...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification Ardèche has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record contains several unexplained reports, yet no single incident has achieved the national... Ardèche has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record contains several unexplained reports, yet no single incident has achieved the national importance of France’s best known cases. ardeche Introduction That mixture matters. Ardèche is not well described either as a UFO “hotspot” or as a place where every sighting has been debunked. It is better understood as a department with a scattered archive: one notably puzzling historical case, a small number of other unresolved or unusable reports, and a larger group illustrating how terrain, darkness, atmospheric conditions and unfamiliar objects can make ordinary lights appear remarkable. Pranles in 1979: Ardèche’s strongest official case On the night of 21 July 1979, a man and his wife near Pranles watched a luminous phenomenon above a ridge in the direction of Serre de Pieroulet. According to the later GEIPAN review, the principal witness described a stationary yellow orange source resembling a powerful beacon. From it came a blue beam that appeared to rotate through 360 degrees and sweep the ground. The observation lasted from approximately 10.45 pm until 11.28 pm, with no unusual sound reported. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... The witness went to fetch a camera, but the light disappeared before he could photograph it. The following day, he learnt that a neighbouring couple had seen a light in the same direction at roughly the same time. One of those neighbours later supplied a second formal account, confirming the fixed yellow orange glow and blue illumination over the ground, although the description was less detailed. The local gendarmerie was informed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... GEIPAN—the unit of the French space agency CNES that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena—eventually retained the case in category D, meaning that it remained unidentified after investigation. Its modern assessment gave the report relatively high scores for both strangeness and consistency. The agency considered a hovering helicopter with a searchlight, but found difficulties with the reported blue colour, the continuously rotating beam, the length of the observation and the absence of sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... The case nevertheless has substantial limitations. There is no photograph, radar return, physical trace or instrument reading. GEIPAN could not conduct a modern reconstruction because the principal witness had died and the other observers could no longer be found. The agency explicitly described the file as close to the boundary between an unexplained case and one weakened by insufficient evidence. Its D classification records the failure to identify the phenomenon; it does not establish that an unconventional aircraft was present. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... Pranles therefore deserves attention without exaggeration. It is stronger than a solitary, fleeting light because two nearby households reported corresponding features. Yet all surviving evidence remains testimonial, and the most useful follow up work became impossible through the passage of time. The January 2013 sightings: dramatic reports, probable space debris A much more public episode occurred late on 7 January 2013. Witnesses in several parts of Ardèche described a bright object with a long trail moving across the night sky. Local newspaper coverage linked reports from Guilherand Granges, Privas and Le Pouzin, while noting that a commercial pilot over Normandy had apparently observed the same broad phenomenon at the same time. The newspaper’s language naturally foregrounded the UFO question, but the wide geographical spread was itself a clue that the object was high in the atmosphere rather than manoeuvring locally over Ardèche. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréARDECHE Un Ovni observé dans le ciel ardéchois?January 21, 2013 — 21 Jan 2013 — Ce lundi 7 janvier, à 23h45 très exactement, un Ovni a t il surv... Published: January 21, 2013 GEIPAN’s associated Ardèche file was based on an observation from the Col de l’Escrinet near Saint Étienne de Boulogne. The witness reported a fire coloured light with a broad yellow trail travelling from west to east. Investigators connected it with a simultaneous aeronautical report and concluded that it was probably atmospheric re entry debris, possibly associated with the Iridium 33 satellite. The case was classified B: probably identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT ETIENNE DE BOULOGNE (07) 07.01.2013 GEIPAN... This episode demonstrates why multiple witnesses do not automatically make an event more mysterious. Independent observations over a large area can strengthen the conclusion that something physical occurred, while simultaneously favouring a conventional explanation such as a meteor, rocket component or satellite fragment. Reports of a glowing object crossing the sky “horizontally” can also be misleading because observers have no direct way to judge its altitude, distance or true path. The 2013 reporting did strengthen the basic claim that an unusual luminous event was visible. It weakened, rather than strengthened, the idea of a structured craft flying over individual Ardèche towns. GEIPAN’s reconstruction converted a cluster of apparently local sightings into one probable high altitude re entry event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT ETIENNE DE BOULOGNE (07) 07.01.2013 GEIPAN... What the wider official record contains GEIPAN’s public database lists Ardèche cases across several decades, but the classifications show why raw sighting totals must be treated cautiously. Category A means identified with high confidence, B means probably identified, C indicates that the evidence is too poor for a reliable conclusion, and D is reserved for cases that remain unidentified after investigation. These categories measure the outcome and quality of an investigation, not the likelihood of an extraterrestrial origin. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Among the more notable entries are: Saint Sauveur de Montagut, 26 November 1993: classified D in GEIPAN’s case index, indicating a strange report considered to have moderate or strong consistency. Châteaubourg, 19 August 1993: classified C because reliable information was lacking. Thueyts, 8 November 2013, and Chomérac, 2002 and 2011: also placed in category C, leaving them unresolved chiefly because the evidence was inadequate. Saint Remèze, 25 March 2009: associated with Venus, although displayed in the database as category C. Toulaud, 8 May 1980: classified A and identified as Venus. Vernoux en Vivarais, 25 February 1975: classified A as an astronomical phenomenon. Annonay, 2004: classified B, meaning a conventional identification was considered probable. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN These entries do not form a clear chronological “flap” comparable with France’s nationwide wave of 1954. They are dispersed across years and locations, and many are single witness or information poor files. Apparent clusters can also reflect publicity: once a newspaper publishes one striking account, other observers may recognise that they saw the same event and come forward, as happened in January 2013. The database likewise offers no strong Ardèche pattern involving radar interceptions, military pursuit or repeated pilot encounters. The 2013 aeronautical report is useful corroboration, but it supported an atmospheric re entry explanation. In the publicly accessible official record, the department’s UFO history is primarily one of civilian visual observations rather than documented defence incidents. L’ex directeur du GEPAN brise le silence (J.J Velasco) Channel: Biomécanique Open on YouTube When photographs make a case less mysterious The 2020 Borée case, observed by walkers on Mont Mézenc, is a useful counterweight to the assumption that photographs inevitably strengthen an extraordinary interpretation. Four people saw a silent, reflective object that seemed to rotate and change direction. One witness took photographs and a short video, describing the object as a large silvery white moon or bubble. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOREE (07) 05.08.2020 GEIPAN... GEIPAN found that the imagery revealed features consistent with a reflective foil balloon, possibly shaped like a letter or number. Sunlight flashing from its metallic surface accounted for the changing brightness."},{"title":"Likely Explanations","url":"/likely-explanations-c25d6b/","description":"Space debris, balloons, Venus and bright stars explain many dramatic Ardeche sightings once timing, photographs and weather are examined.","breadcrumb":"Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification","level":2,"text":"Likely Explanations What Really Caused Ardeche's Explained Sightings? Ardeche UFOs Likely Explanations Likely Explanations What Really Caused Ardeche's Explained Sightings? Space debris, balloons, Venus and bright stars explain many dramatic Ardeche sightings once timing, photographs and weather are examined. Space debris, balloons, Venus and bright stars explain many dramatic Ardeche sightings once timing, photographs and weather are examined. mechanism The 2013 fireball and probable re entry debris How photographs identified the Boree balloon Why planets and stars are mistaken for craft ardeche likely explanations Introduction Many of Ardèche’s most dramatic UFO reports became less mysterious when investigators stopped treating the witness’s first interpretation as the starting point. A fiery “craft” crossing the department in 2013 was probably re entering space debris. A silent, reflective object photographed above Mont Mézenc in 2020 was identified as a metallic party balloon. Other apparently hovering or manoeuvring lights have matched Venus, Capella and similar bright celestial objects. These explanations do not imply that witnesses were careless or dishonest. They show how distance, darkness, mountain terrain, atmospheric shimmer and unfamiliar viewing angles can transform ordinary objects into convincing anomalies. Ardèche is especially instructive because its official files include both weakly explained and genuinely unresolved reports. The solved cases demonstrate what investigators can establish when they have accurate timing, direction, photographs, weather data and astronomical reconstruction—and what remains uncertain when those details are missing. 4:58 GOFAST UFO Analysis (yeah no, probably just a balloon) Channel: EdwardCurrent · Views: 6.5K · Uploaded: June 2021 · Length: 4 minutes 58 seconds Open on YouTube The 2013 fireball was probably re entry debris Late on 7 January 2013, people at several locations in Ardèche reported an exceptionally bright phenomenon moving across the sky. Observations were associated with Guilherand Granges, Privas, Le Pouzin and the Col de l’Escrinet near Saint Étienne de Boulogne. A witness at the col described a fire coloured light with a broad yellow trail crossing from west to east. The event seemed sufficiently large, bright and structured to invite talk of an unidentified craft. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr SAINT ETIENNE DE BOULOGNE (07) 07.01.2013 GEIPAN... The geographical spread was the first important clue. This was not merely a light hovering over one Ardèche valley. At approximately the same time, the pilot of an Airbus A340 near Deauville in Normandy reported a brilliant object with a long tail and white fragments. The pilot, who said he had seen space re entries before, immediately suspected falling orbital debris. Reports separated by hundreds of kilometres strongly suggested a high altitude event visible across a wide area. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete395 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... GEIPAN, the investigation unit of the French space agency CNES, compared the reports rather than assessing each one in isolation. The accounts agreed on several features associated with atmospheric entry: a long luminous path, fragmentation, great brightness and a duration substantially longer than that of many ordinary meteors. Witness estimates ranged from about 15 seconds to nearly a minute. GEIPAN judged that this slow, fragmenting display fitted artificial space debris better than a conventional meteor. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete395 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A catalogued fragment from the Iridium 33 satellite initially appeared to be a possible match. Its predicted decay time was within roughly half an hour of the sightings, but orbital decay forecasts carried a margin of error of about an hour. There was also a significant problem: the calculated location of that particular fragment’s re entry did not fit the French observations well. GEIPAN therefore did not claim that the named fragment had been conclusively identified. Its more cautious conclusion was that another, possibly uncatalogued fragment from the same debris cloud could have produced the event. The file was classified B—probably explained, rather than identified beyond doubt. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete395 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Weather also helps explain why reports clustered in the Rhône valley and Ardèche. GEIPAN found that much of France was under cloud, whereas the lower Rhône valley had clearer skies. The apparent local concentration may therefore reflect where the phenomenon could be seen, not where it occurred. Had skies been clear across more of France, the same event might have generated a much larger national wave of reports. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete395 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The case is a useful warning about scale. A luminous event tens of kilometres above Earth can look as though it is passing just beyond a nearby ridge. With no reliable distance reference, observers naturally interpret its brightness and angular size in local terms. In reality, the “Ardèche UFO” was probably a remote event whose visibility happened to be favoured by the department’s clear patch of sky. Photographs turned the Mont Mézenc mystery into a balloon The 5 August 2020 observation near Borée initially sounded more difficult to dismiss. Four members of a family hiking on Mont Mézenc watched a silent reflective object for roughly ten minutes. It appeared to rotate, change shape, descend into a valley and later rise again. One observer initially perceived something like a large silvery disc at close range; the others described a transparent or iridescent bubble. The principal witness took 11 photographs and a short video. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOREE (07) 05.08.2020 GEIPAN... Those impressions contained many familiar ingredients of an extraordinary UFO report: silence, apparent hovering, changing geometry, flashes of light and movement that seemed purposeful. The location also carried cultural baggage. The witness later connected the sighting with stories about unusual lights around the uplands sometimes called the “Burle triangle”. Yet the recorded imagery allowed investigators to test the object’s appearance and motion independently of that narrative. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete620 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... GEIPAN concluded that the object was a novelty balloon made from metallised Mylar, perhaps shaped as the letter O or Q or as the number zero. Several details converged on that explanation: In at least one photograph, the outline resembled a hollow letter or number rather than a solid disc. The aluminised surface produced intense, intermittent reflections as the balloon turned in sunlight. Its apparently changing form was consistent with a flexible balloon rotating and being seen from different angles. Its slow, irregular early movement and steadier later drift matched an object passing through turbulent air near the mountain before entering a more regular southerly airflow. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOREE (07) 05.08.2020 GEIPAN... Image analysis was crucial. Using IPACO, software designed for examining anomalous photographs, investigators estimated that a balloon 0.8 to 1 metre across could fit the apparent size. During the first phase it could have been approximately 215 to 320 metres away, not 30 or 40 metres as one witness thought. In the later photographs it could have been roughly 1.9 to 2.3 kilometres distant. A reconstruction of the terrain and image sequence also produced a generally southward path consistent with the prevailing wind. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOREE (07) 05.08.2020 GEIPAN... This distance error explains much of the reported strangeness. Without knowing an object’s true size, the eye cannot determine its range reliably against an open sky. A one metre balloon several hundred metres away and a much larger craft nearby can occupy the same apparent angle. Once the witness interpreted the object as close, ordinary drifting seemed like controlled manoeuvring and reflections could be understood as lights or windows. The case also shows why photographs do not automatically strengthen an extraordinary claim. Images can preserve the ambiguity that excited the witness, but they may also reveal details too subtle to recognise in real time. Here, the photographic sequence strengthened the report’s evidential consistency while weakening its exotic interpretation. GEIPAN assigned it category A: identified as a Mylar balloon. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOREE (07) 05.08.2020 GEIPAN... 3:17 FLYING Explained: Should We Be Worried About Space Debris? Channel: Flying Magazine · Views: 795 ·"},{"title":"Pranles Case","url":"/pranles-case/","description":"Two neighbouring couples reported a fixed orange light and rotating blue beam, leaving Ardeche's strongest official UFO case unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Ardeche UFOs Between Mystery and Misidentification","level":2,"text":"Pranles Case Why the Pranles UFO Case Still Puzzles Ardeche UFOs Pranles Case Pranles Case Why the Pranles UFO Case Still Puzzles Two neighbouring couples reported a fixed orange light and rotating blue beam, leaving Ardeche's strongest official UFO case unresolved. Two neighbouring couples reported a fixed orange light and rotating blue beam, leaving Ardeche's strongest official UFO case unresolved. event time window What the witnesses reported that night Why GEIPAN could not identify the phenomenon How strong is the surviving evidence? ardeche pranles case Introduction The Pranles sighting of 21 July 1979 remains the most detailed unexplained incident in Ardèche’s public GEIPAN archive. Two neighbouring couples reported a stationary orange yellow light in the same direction, accompanied by blue illumination sweeping across the mountainside. The principal witness watched for about 43 minutes and produced an unusually technical written account; a neighbour later confirmed the central features to the gendarmerie. GEIPAN’s modern review found no satisfactory astronomical, meteorological or conventional lighting explanation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... Yet “unexplained” is not the same as “extraordinary craft confirmed”. No photograph of the phenomenon survives, only two of the four observers gave formal statements, and no radar record, physical trace or independent instrument measurement is known. By the time GEIPAN revisited the file, the principal witness had died and the others could not be located. Pranles is therefore important because it combines unusually rich testimony with evidence gaps that can no longer be repaired. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... What the witnesses reported that night The observation began at approximately 10.45 pm at a rural home in the commune of Pranles, north west of Privas. The principal witness and his wife were looking towards the Serre de Pieroulet ridge when they noticed a powerful, fixed light. The witness described a slightly oval yellow orange halo containing two intensely bright central points, with a smaller, fainter light displaced to one side. The main source remained in the same part of the sky until 11.28 pm. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... The most unusual reported feature was not the orange light itself but the illumination associated with it. According to the principal account, a broad beam appeared to rotate clockwise through 360 degrees, completing one revolution roughly every 25 seconds. As it passed, distant terrain and trees seemed to acquire a strong bluish cast. The witness believed the sweep was continuous and regular rather than an intermittent flash or an accidental reflection. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... Conditions were described as favourable for observation: a clear, moonless, normally starry night, with an open view of sharply defined ridges and complete silence around the house. GEIPAN later checked records from the Montélimar weather station, about 28 kilometres away, and found a clear sky, light northerly wind and reported visibility of approximately 15 kilometres. These conditions do not guarantee accurate estimates of distance or brightness, but they reduce the likelihood that dense cloud, fog or poor visibility created the entire display. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... The principal witness did not merely call the phenomenon “a light”. His report included sketches, apparent colour temperatures, the direction and period of rotation, comparisons with familiar sources, and observations of which slopes were or were not illuminated. At the end of the sighting, as he prepared a telephoto camera, he reported that the central glow intensified, extinguished abruptly and left one point apparently retreating and fading within two or three seconds. No photograph was taken. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... A neighbouring couple provided partial corroboration. At about 11 pm, while closing their shutters, they saw an orange light in the direction of the same ridge. The husband told the gendarmerie that the glow sometimes became blue or varied in intensity, while bluish light illuminated part of the mountain towards Creysseilles. He considered it fixed and watched for around ten minutes before the couple went to bed. They did not learn of their neighbours’ observation until the following morning. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... That second account is valuable but less independent than the phrase “four witnesses” may suggest. Four people reportedly saw something, divided between two homes, but only the two men supplied statements preserved in the official file. The wives were not formally interviewed. Moreover, the neighbour confirmed an orange source and blue illumination but did not clearly describe the precisely defined rotating beam, twin central points or dramatic final departure reported by the principal witness. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... Why GEIPAN could not identify the phenomenon The local gendarmerie was contacted shortly after the event. Its file contained the principal witness’s detailed report, the neighbour’s interview and a photograph of the landscape taken from the first home. The surviving paperwork shows that the incident entered an official reporting channel rather than emerging decades later as an unsupported anecdote. However, the photograph recorded the location, not the phenomenon itself. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... When GEIPAN re examined the old file, investigators used the witness’s landmarks and historical aerial material to reconstruct the viewing direction. They calculated an azimuth of about 288 degrees, towards the north west, and placed the relevant ridge roughly two kilometres from the witnesses. Because the reported source appeared beyond the foreground crest and because no suitable higher ground lay directly behind it, GEIPAN concluded that a simple fixed lamp on the visible mountainside did not fit the account well. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... Ordinary astronomical objects were also poor matches for the description. A planet or bright star might appear stationary and orange near the horizon, but it would not physically illuminate successive sections of terrain with an apparently rotating blue beam. Satellites, meteors and re entering debris would move across the sky and normally remain visible for a much shorter period. The reported combination of prolonged immobility, repeated ground illumination and abrupt extinction therefore produced a relatively high “strangeness” score in GEIPAN’s assessment. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... GEIPAN considered a hovering helicopter carrying a searchlight the nearest conventional comparison. A helicopter could remain in one general area and direct a powerful beam across the landscape. The hypothesis nevertheless left several difficulties: no engine noise was reported, the source was said to be perfectly stationary for nearly three quarters of an hour, the blue beam appeared different in colour from the orange source, and its repetitive 360 degree sweep had no evident operational purpose. GEIPAN did not identify a particular aircraft, exercise or search operation that could be checked against the sighting. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PRANLES (07) 21.07.1979 GEIPAN... None of those objections rules out an aircraft conclusively. Sound can be weakened by distance, wind and intervening terrain; apparent immobility is hard to judge at night; and coloured illumination can be altered by atmospheric scattering, vegetation, visual contrast or the properties of the lamp. The principal witness’s estimate that the source was only 100 to 150 metres above the terrain was an impression rather than a measured altitude. Without a confirmed distance, even careful estimates of size, power and movement remain uncertain. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word AC JPA MPD Rapport enqueteur PRANLES (8) 21.07.1979 reprise anonymisation.docx... Other terrestrial possibilities face similar problems. A rotating beacon, searchlight, temporary lighting rig or light source partly concealed behind a ridge might explain the regular sweep more"},{"title":"Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny","url":"/why-ardennes-ufo-stories-changed-under/","description":"The UFO history of the Ardennes is less a story of one decisive “classic case” than of repeated reports that became more ordinary, or less evidentially...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny The UFO history of the Ardennes is less a story of one decisive “classic case” than of repeated reports that became more ordinary, or less evidentially... The UFO history of the Ardennes is less a story of one decisive “classic case” than of repeated reports that became more ordinary, or less evidentially secure, when investigators revisited them. ardennes Introduction That pattern matters. It shows the difference between a witness having a genuinely puzzling experience and investigators possessing evidence of an unknown craft. In the Ardennes, the strongest surviving stories are often the most vivid, but not necessarily the best supported. The official archive contains no well corroborated local case combining independent witnesses, photographs, physical traces and confirmed radar or aviation data. Instead, its value lies in showing how geography, perception, incomplete police records and later reconstruction can change a case’s status. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN What the official record actually contains France has maintained an unusual degree of continuity in the public investigation of unidentified aerospace phenomena. The programme began within the French space agency, CNES, in 1977 and now operates as GEIPAN, which collects witness statements, examines possible explanations and publishes case files while protecting witnesses’ identities. It works with the gendarmerie, police, aviation and defence bodies, weather services, scientific specialists and trained local investigators. GEIPAN deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” because a witness may have seen a light or atmospheric effect rather than a solid object. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Its classifications are crucial to reading the Ardennes archive correctly: A means the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B indicates a probable identification. C means that the available information is too weak or incomplete for a reliable conclusion. D is reserved for cases that remain unidentified after investigation. A category C report is therefore not strong evidence of an extraordinary object. It means that investigators cannot fairly settle the matter because essential details are missing. Across the national database, CNES currently reports that only a small minority remain unidentified after investigation, while a much larger share lack enough data for a firm result. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... The Ardennes files fit that national pattern. Database entries include older reports from 1957 and Donchery in 1979, but both are category C cases marked by a lack of reliable information. Bogny sur Meuse in 1994 and Sedan in 2008 received the same broad judgement. These entries establish that reports occurred; they do not establish a department wide flap, a recurring craft or a verified military connection. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANObservation type [Select]... (DPT) ARDENNES (08) 30.10.1957 30/10/1957 C Ardennes Manque d'informations... Gernelle: an orange “object” on the ground One of the clearest examples of later reassessment occurred at Gernelle, south east of Charleville Mézières, during the early hours of 17 January 1982. Two people looking from their home described an intense orange ball apparently resting in a field and illuminating its surroundings. They watched for roughly 15 minutes and believed that it rose from the ground, leaving what looked like a coloured gaseous layer. When they examined the supposed site the next day, they found no marks in the frozen, snow covered ground. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 GEIPAN... The original report was once listed as unexplained. Decades later, GEIPAN reconstructed the sky and found the Moon almost exactly in the reported direction: close to due east, only about 2.3 degrees above the horizon. A low Moon can appear unusually orange because its light passes through a greater thickness of atmosphere. Its apparent contact with a distant ridge or field can also make it seem like a nearby object rather than a remote astronomical body. The report’s other features supported that interpretation. The light remained nearly stationary, was described as a large orange sphere, and the witnesses did not separately mention seeing the Moon. No physical trace was found where the object was thought to have stood. GEIPAN therefore judged a lunar misidentification highly probable. The case illustrates a recurring difficulty in UFO testimony: witnesses can describe the appearance of a light sincerely and accurately while misjudging its distance, size and relationship to the landscape. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 GEIPAN... Fagnon: the missing video and the return of Venus At Fagnon, near Charleville Mézières, a witness reported a brilliant object shortly before dawn on 14 August 1998. It appeared larger and brighter than a star, seemed almost stationary and was visible in the direction of Charleville. The witness also said that a similar phenomenon had been seen three days earlier. A video cassette was reportedly associated with the declaration, but it was no longer available when the case received a detailed modern review. The second reported witness had not been formally interviewed, and the original gendarmerie questioning was brief. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word RB MPD Fagnon(08090) 14 08 1998 CR enquête V8.2.docx... Despite those weaknesses, this was not an untestable case. The date, approximate time and viewing direction allowed investigators to reconstruct the astronomical conditions. Venus was present at an azimuth of about 70 degrees, matching the direction towards Charleville Mézières, and stood roughly ten degrees above the horizon. It was strongly illuminated and bright enough to appear much larger than its true point like image, especially through atmospheric haze or a window. Clear weather meant that it should have been plainly visible. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word RB MPD Fagnon(08090) 14 08 1998 CR enquête V8.2.docx... GEIPAN concluded that the description corresponded closely to Venus and reclassified the report as category A. The absent video limits independent checking, but it does not strengthen the extraordinary interpretation. The surviving positional and timing evidence supports the planetary explanation, while no preserved image contradicts it. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word RB MPD Fagnon(08090) 14 08 1998 CR enquête V8.2.docx... This case is important because it shows why lost photographs or recordings should not automatically be treated as suppressed proof. An unavailable recording is simply unavailable evidence. The assessment must rest on what can still be examined. Why NOBODY Talks About The Real UFO Geography Channel: Map Pack Open on YouTube Bogny sur Meuse: vivid detail without enough measurements The 9 January 1994 Bogny sur Meuse report is harder to explain, but also weaker than its colourful description initially suggests. A passenger in a moving vehicle reported a luminous phenomenon seen at several points along the journey. Its apparent form changed from rectangular to diamond shaped and then oval, with lights of different colours. She became frightened when it seemed to approach. The driver saw something during only one part of the episode and did not give or sign a separate statement. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOGNY SUR MEUSE (08) 09.01.1994 GEIPAN... Investigators lacked the basic measurements needed to reconstruct the event: reliable directions, elevation above the horizon, observation duration and angular size. It was also unclear whether the driver’s brief sighting matched what the passenger described. GEIPAN therefore changed the case from its former unexplained status to category C, meaning that the information was not reliable or complete enough to support a conclusion. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr BOGNY SUR MEUSE (08) 09.01.1994 GEIPAN... That is not equivalent to identifying the phenomenon. Possibilities such as aircraft lights, astronomical objects, reflections or changing viewing angles remain unproven. But an unexplained label would be equally misleading because the apparent transformations may have resulted from the car’s movement, shifting sightlines or several separate lights being interpreted as one object. Without precise contemporaneous data, later investigators cannot distinguish those possibilities. Vireux Wallerand: the department’s most dramatic weak case The most striking Ardennes narrative in the official archive concerns Vireux Wallerand, near Givet, on"},{"title":"Case Reviews","url":"/case-reviews/","description":"Moon, Venus and missing measurements turned several famous Ardennes reports from mysteries into probable explanations or inconclusive files.","breadcrumb":"Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny","level":2,"text":"Case Reviews How Ardennes UFO Cases Lost Their Mystery Ardennes UFO Files Case Reviews Case Reviews How Ardennes UFO Cases Lost Their Mystery Moon, Venus and missing measurements turned several famous Ardennes reports from mysteries into probable explanations or inconclusive files. Moon, Venus and missing measurements turned several famous Ardennes reports from mysteries into probable explanations or inconclusive files. dataset evidence What GEIPAN Categories Actually Mean Gernelle, Fagnon and the Power of Reconstruction Why Incomplete Records Remain Inconclusive ardennes case reviews Introduction Several of the best known UFO reports from the Ardennes became less mysterious when France’s official investigators revisited the original files. In some cases, modern astronomical reconstruction placed the Moon or Venus exactly where witnesses had reported a bright, apparently hovering object. In others, the absence of precise times, directions, independent testimony or surviving photographs made any confident explanation impossible. That distinction is central to understanding the department’s official record. A sighting may have been genuinely baffling to the witness without remaining unexplained after investigation. Equally, a file may remain unsettled not because the phenomenon was exceptionally strange, but because too little reliable information survives. The reclassification of cases at Gernelle and Fagnon shows the power of reconstruction; older reports from across the Ardennes show the limits imposed by incomplete records. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN What GEIPAN Categories Actually Mean GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, does not classify cases solely by how unusual a witness’s description sounds. Its method weighs two related factors: the remaining strangeness after ordinary explanations have been examined, and the “consistency” of the evidence. Consistency depends on the quantity and quality of the information available, including the number of witnesses, the precision of their statements, photographs or recordings, and whether the accounts are independent and internally coherent. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN The familiar letter categories therefore describe the state of an investigation rather than the emotional force of the original experience: Category A indicates that investigators consider an ordinary identification virtually established. Category B means that an ordinary explanation is probable but not demonstrated as completely as in an A case. Category C means that the material is too weak or incomplete to support a sound conclusion. Category D , sometimes divided into D1 and D2, is reserved for sufficiently well documented cases that remain unexplained after investigation. The most common misunderstanding concerns category C. It is not a lesser form of “unexplained UFO”. It means that the case cannot be used reliably either to prove an ordinary explanation or to establish a genuinely unidentified phenomenon. GEIPAN’s methodology explicitly describes such observations as unusable for a firm conclusion. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN This makes later reclassification possible. An older report may initially appear unexplained because the investigating gendarmes recorded only a short statement. Decades later, software can reconstruct the positions of planets and the Moon, while maps and archived weather observations can test the reported direction and visibility. Conversely, no amount of modern computing can recover a missing observation time, an unrecorded line of sight or a videotape that has disappeared. Gernelle: When the “Object” Became the Moon The Gernelle sighting of 17 January 1982 is one of the clearest examples of a dramatic Ardennes report being reduced to a probable astronomical misidentification. Two witnesses described a strong orange light that appeared to be close to or resting upon the ground. The object seemed to illuminate its surroundings, rise gradually and then depart in the direction of Sedan. Such details gave the episode the appearance of a structured event rather than a simple point of light in the sky. The later review tested that impression against the astronomical conditions. Investigators found that the Moon was low in the relevant part of the sky at approximately the reported time. Its reddish or orange appearance was consistent with viewing it close to the horizon and potentially through broken cloud or atmospheric haze. GEIPAN therefore reclassified the case as category B, with a red or orange Moon considered the probable explanation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANGERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 17/01/1982 B Ardennes Lune rousse 28/11/2017 1982 01 17... GIVET (08) 20.0... The apparent movement did not rule out the Moon. GEIPAN noted that witnesses often describe a bright celestial object as retreating when cloud increasingly obscures it. As the visible patch becomes dimmer and smaller, the brain can interpret the change as an object moving away rather than as a stationary object being covered. At Gernelle, the Moon’s real gradual rise during the observation could also have contributed to the impression that the light had lifted from the ground. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 GEIPAN... The reported direction towards Sedan was close enough to the reconstructed lunar bearing to support the hypothesis, particularly because witnesses commonly use a familiar town as an approximate directional marker. The review calculated that the Moon and the general direction of Sedan differed by roughly 20 degrees, an error that is not exceptional in an unaided verbal estimate made during an unusual nocturnal observation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 GEIPAN... Yet the classification remained B rather than A. Investigators did not possess the witnesses’ exact address, so they could not reconstruct every nearby tree, building or obstruction along the line of sight. Detailed local weather information was also unavailable, preventing certainty about the pattern of cloud in front of the Moon. The case lost most of its mystery, but the surviving evidence did not justify claiming absolute proof. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GERNELLE (08) 17.01.1982 GEIPAN... Gernelle illustrates why a witness’s description of an object “on the ground” cannot automatically be treated as a measured distance. In darkness, with few reference points, the angular position of a low celestial body can be mentally attached to a field, ridge or distant settlement. The observation remains a real experience; what changes is the interpretation of where the light was situated. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Fagnon: Venus Recovered from a Thin File The Fagnon case of 14 August 1998 shows an even stronger reclassification. A witness reported a bright, silent light visible before sunrise in the direction of Charleville Mézières. It looked larger and brighter than a star, appeared broadly stationary or very slow moving, and remained visible as daylight increased. A similar phenomenon had reportedly been seen three days earlier. At the time, the gendarmerie conducted no substantial on site investigation. The statement was brief, the second witness was not formally interviewed and a videotape said to show the phenomenon was later unavailable. The case was not examined in depth until 2018, when investigators used modern mapping, astronomical software and archived meteorological information to reconstruct the observation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word RB MPD Fagnon(08090) 14 08 1998 CR enquête V8.2.docx... The reported characteristics pointed towards a celestial object: a bright point, low in the eastern or north eastern sky, silent, nearly stationary and visible around dawn. Investigators considered the planets capable of matching the description and found Venus in the relevant direction. Its position corresponded with the witness’s line of sight towards Charleville Mézières, while its exceptional brightness explained why it appeared much more prominent than an ordinary star. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word RB MPD Fagnon(08090) 14 08 1998 CR enquête V8.2.docx... The repeated sighting also strengthened rather than weakened the Venus explanation. A planet returns to almost the same part of the pre dawn sky on successive mornings. A nearby aircraft, meteor or transient atmospheric light would not normally reproduce the same broad position and"},{"title":"Vireux Case","url":"/vireux-case/","description":"The Ardennes' most dramatic sighting featured a silent cigar-shaped craft, alleged beings and a nearby nuclear site, but weak corroboration.","breadcrumb":"Why Ardennes UFO Stories Changed Under Scrutiny","level":2,"text":"Vireux Case Did a Cigar Shaped Craft Hover Near Givet? Ardennes UFO Files Vireux Case Vireux Case Did a Cigar Shaped Craft Hover Near Givet? The Ardennes' most dramatic sighting featured a silent cigar shaped craft, alleged beings and a nearby nuclear site, but weak corroboration. The Ardennes' most dramatic sighting featured a silent cigar shaped craft, alleged beings and a nearby nuclear site, but weak corroboration. event time window The Garden Witness's Ten Minute Account Claims About Chooz and the Apparent Occupants What Corroboration and Records Are Missing ardennes vireux case Introduction The Vireux Wallerand case of 20 July 2009 is the most dramatic official UFO report in the Ardennes , but not the department’s strongest evidence. A gardener said that, at about midday, he watched a silent, cigar shaped object hovering among nearby houses for roughly ten minutes. He described reflective metal, a bluish grey haze and possible articulated arms or living occupants. He also believed that the object was pointing towards the Chooz nuclear power station. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, initially treated the report as unexplained. A later review reached a different conclusion. The claimed object was so large, close and conspicuous that its failure to attract any independent witness became a central problem. No photograph, trace, confirmed flight incident or supporting neighbourhood testimony emerged. In 2021, the case was publicly listed as category C: too poorly supported to interpret reliably, rather than a verified unexplained craft. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr VIREUX WALLERAND (08) 20.07.2009 GEIPAN... The Garden Witness’s Ten Minute Account The observation was reported as beginning at around noon while the witness, a 60 year old man, was working in his vegetable garden in an urban part of Vireux Wallerand, near Givet. In his police statement, he placed the phenomenon about 30 metres from him and approximately ten metres above houses on Avenue de Gaulle. He estimated it at about 20 metres long and three to four metres in diameter, comparing it to a small submarine. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... He described the object as stationary and completely silent. Its exterior supposedly resembled highly polished steel or chrome, with sharply defined edges reflecting the light. Much of the body, however, appeared obscured by what he called a bluish grey mist, soot or blurring layer. Sections of the surface seemed to become briefly visible before disappearing again behind this apparent distortion. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... The strangest details concerned movement on top of the object. The witness reported seeing “agitation” around an area he interpreted as a hatch or cockpit. He could not decide whether he was looking at articulated mechanical arms, living beings or both. Moving rounded shapes were interpreted as helmets, while a larger implement was compared to a racket being passed along the hull, perhaps to collect samples from the atmosphere. GEIPAN later stressed that this portion of the account blended observation with inference: indistinct movements became occupants, helmets and sampling activity through the witness’s interpretation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... After about ten minutes, the witness went indoors while the phenomenon was supposedly still present. His accounts gave fear as the reason: he worried about being noticed and initially imagined that Russian or Chinese personnel might be conducting espionage. He later said that he returned with a camera, but the object had gone. The report was not made immediately. He told his wife about it seven days later, approached a local investigator and made a statement to the gendarmerie on 28 July. A GEIPAN questionnaire followed in September. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... These delays do not prove that the report was false. People can hesitate before describing an experience they expect others to ridicule. They do, however, reduce the opportunity to secure time sensitive evidence, such as photographs, same day witness interviews, precise weather observations or records of unusual activity in the immediate area. Claims About Chooz and the Apparent Occupants The nearby Chooz nuclear power station gives the story much of its lasting intrigue. The witness said the front of the cigar shaped object was directed north east, towards the plant, and later reasoned that foreign powers or extraterrestrial visitors might have been observing it. In his questionnaire, he estimated the plant to be roughly seven kilometres from his position and associated the alleged activity on the craft with atmospheric sampling or investigation of the nuclear site. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... That association should be treated cautiously. Pointing in the general direction of Chooz is not evidence that the power station was the object’s target. A stationary elongated shape must face some direction, and the witness’s belief that it was spying on the plant was an interpretation added to the visual description. The official documents contain no reported security alert at Chooz, no evidence of interference with the installation and no independent observation from personnel at the site. The same distinction applies to the alleged occupants. The witness did not report clearly resolved human like figures with identifiable bodies or faces. He described poorly defined movement through an obscuring effect and then proposed several possible meanings: mechanical limbs, living beings, helmets and a sampling device. This makes the claim more vivid, but not more secure. Each additional conclusion depends on the accuracy of the preceding one. The witness’s certainty also increased after the event. His questionnaire records that he had previously been doubtful about UFOs but became convinced that he had seen an extraterrestrial aircraft. He also said that a regional newspaper mentioned UFOs the following day, prompting him to connect his own experience with the broader subject. GEIPAN’s file does not identify that article or establish that it concerned the same time and place, so it cannot be treated as corroboration. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... This progression illustrates an important evidential problem. A witness may sincerely remember seeing a bright, elongated and confusing phenomenon while later becoming more confident about what it represented. Confidence in an interpretation is not the same as independent confirmation of the original visual details. A Close Object That Nobody Else Reported The central weakness is not simply that there was one witness. It is the conflict between the claimed circumstances and the absence of other witnesses. According to the final account, a 20 metre craft hovered at very short range in a built up area at midday. It was not high overhead or briefly crossing the sky. It was supposedly visible horizontally, among or above neighbouring houses, for about ten minutes. GEIPAN concluded that an object of that apparent size and position should have been difficult for the surrounding neighbourhood to miss. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr VIREUX WALLERAND (08) 20.07.2009 GEIPAN... Both the gendarmerie and a GEIPAN investigator made neighbourhood enquiries. No one supplied an independent matching account, and the town hall reported no abnormal event. The witness later asserted that neighbours had seen the phenomenon but refused to admit it to investigators. No supporting statement from those neighbours appears in the published file. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... This absence carries more weight than it would in a fleeting night time light case. A distant object may easily be missed because only one person happens to look up. Here, however, the witness described something comparable in length to a large vehicle, almost motionless at close range, in daylight and within the normal field of view. The more literally those estimates are accepted, the harder the lack of corroboration becomes to explain. The witness also told the second investigator that he had seen the same type of phenomenon about 15 days earlier while accompanied by his wife, yet said that she had not seen it. GEIPAN included this point in its assessment of the account’s poor consistency. It does not disprove the July report, but it adds another claimed sighting without an independent observer. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR JPA V1 MPD.docx... Louis Bertignac Festi'Meuse 2009 Channel: Rebe1le Open on YouTube What the Official Checks Found The official enquiry did more than record the testimony. Investigators photographed the viewing location, reconstructed the line of sight,"},{"title":"How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence","url":"/how-arieges-ufo-mysteries-changed-with/","description":"Ariège has one famous “UFO case”, but it is important for the opposite reason from the one usually implied.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence Ariège has one famous “UFO case”, but it is important for the opposite reason from the one usually implied. Ariège has one famous “UFO case”, but it is important for the opposite reason from the one usually implied. The strange lights seen over Bélesta on 16 October 1954 became a classic story of France’s great flying saucer wave, supported by numerous witnesses, press coverage and the presence of gendarmes. ariege Introduction The wider official record is less dramatic but more instructive. Ariège sightings published by GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, include mistakes involving the Moon, Venus, reflections, atmospheric re entry, lanterns and satellite formations. One older report remains unassessable because the surviving information is inadequate. Taken together, these cases show how unfamiliar lights, mountain horizons, incomplete testimony and media expectations can turn ordinary events into enduring mysteries. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Bélesta: the department’s defining case On the evening of 16 October 1954, inhabitants of Bélesta, in the Pays d’Olmes, watched luminous objects apparently manoeuvring above the Rocher de Millet. Contemporary accounts described lights rising, descending, disappearing and returning, with the display continuing for roughly half an hour. The phenomenon attracted a crowd, and gendarmes attended after residents reported what they were seeing. A subsequent article in La Dépêche du Midi , written by the president of the nearby Lavelanet flying club, helped give the event an authoritative air. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!22 May 2009 — A Bélesta des dizaines de personnes ont pu témoigner du ballet des boules lumi... Published: May 2009 The timing greatly magnified its significance. France was in the middle of the autumn 1954 flying saucer wave, when newspapers were publishing reports from towns and villages across the country. Bélesta therefore fitted an already familiar public narrative: multiple witnesses, moving lights, official attention and an apparently inexplicable performance in the night sky. It was repeatedly cited in later UFO literature as a strong collective observation. RetroNews Le site de presse de la BnF [retronews.fr] retronews.fr Le site de presse de la BnF« L'ère de la vaisselle volante »: l'apparition médiatique...5 Jan 2021 — En 1947, un fait diver... That reputation collapsed in May 2009. Five local men associated with the episode explained that the display had been created as a prank. According to their account, the device incorporated a bicycle fork, handlebar and wheel, with powerful electric lamps attached to the structure. By manipulating the lights from the hillside, they could make them appear to move independently against the darkness. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!22 May 2009 — A Bélesta des dizaines de personnes ont pu témoigner du ballet des boules lumi... Published: May 2009 The confession cannot reconstruct every perception reported in 1954, but it directly addresses the core spectacle at the named location and was given by the people claiming responsibility. No comparably strong evidence has emerged for an additional unexplained object above Bélesta that night. Later reporting therefore weakened the original UFO claim decisively rather than merely offering one speculative alternative. Why so many witnesses were still mistaken Bélesta is useful because it demonstrates that a large witness count does not guarantee an extraordinary cause. The observers were not necessarily inventing what they saw. They really were watching unusual lights. The error lay in judging their distance, size, motion and nature without visible reference points. At night, a bright light against a dark mountain or sky can be difficult to place. A stationary or mechanically moved lamp may appear to hover, accelerate or change direction because the observer cannot see the supporting apparatus or surrounding terrain. Separate lights can also be mentally grouped into a single structured object. Once people begin exchanging interpretations, later witnesses may approach the scene already expecting a flying saucer. The presence of gendarmes likewise proves that a report was taken seriously, not that an exotic craft was verified. French gendarmes record witness statements and pass relevant material into the official reporting system; their role is to document observations, locations and circumstances rather than certify extraterrestrial explanations. [Gendarmerie Nationale] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel Bélesta consequently stands as a warning against several common assumptions: Multiple sincere witnesses can share the same misinterpretation. Official attendance is not the same as official confirmation. A technically simple deception can produce complex testimony. Early press authority can preserve a weak case for decades. A celebrated unresolved case may change category when new testimony appears. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the official Ariège record contains GEIPAN was established within CNES in 1977 to collect, investigate and publish reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its task is not to prove or disprove extraterrestrial visitation. It compares witness accounts with astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human made causes, then assigns a classification reflecting both the available information and the remaining strangeness. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A category A case is identified; category B means that the proposed explanation is considered highly probable. Category C indicates that the report cannot be analysed adequately because essential information is missing. The rare D categories are reserved for sufficiently documented observations that remain unexplained after investigation. A category C report is therefore not strong evidence of an anomaly: it is an evidence gap. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The publicly indexed Ariège cases show a clear pattern. Cos, 2 April 1977 — category C. Witnesses reported an elongated glow apparently separating slowly from a cloud and following a straight course. The account sounds unusual, but GEIPAN’s published classification is C because reliable information is insufficient. It is unresolved only in the limited sense that the surviving record cannot support a secure analysis. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Foix, 4 July 1979 — category A. A witness observed a luminous phenomenon for about two minutes through a window. GEIPAN’s later review judged the event to have low strangeness and attributed it to the Moon. This is a familiar observational problem: cloud, haze, window frames and apparent motion created by the observer can make a bright celestial body seem like a nearby object. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Bénagues, 26 October 1989 — category B. The phenomenon was assessed as an atmospheric re entry. Burning spacecraft or rocket debris can produce bright fragments travelling together over a wide area, sometimes appearing slow because they are high and distant. The resulting display may look like a formation of controlled objects even though it follows a ballistic path. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Montégut en Couserans, 12 April 2008 — category B. GEIPAN linked the sighting to Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. Near the horizon, atmospheric turbulence can make Sirius flicker, change colour and seem to move, particularly when viewed between trees or above uneven ground. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Lavelanet, 11 March 2011 — category A. The reported object was identified as Venus. The planet is often mistaken for an airborne light because it can be exceptionally bright and remain visible in twilight, while movement by the observer or passing clouds creates an illusion of motion. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Gourbit, 28 June 2011 — category A. GEIPAN attributed the appearance to reflections. This case is especially relevant to photographic UFO claims: reflections inside windows or camera optics can create bright shapes that appear suspended over the landscape but are actually produced between the light source,"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-b221a8/","description":"GEIPAN's Ariege cases reveal recurring mistakes involving planets, stars, re-entry debris and incomplete witness information.","breadcrumb":"How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence","level":2,"text":"Official Files What Ariege's Official UFO Record Really Shows Official Files Official Files What Ariege's Official UFO Record Really Shows GEIPAN's Ariege cases reveal recurring mistakes involving planets, stars, re entry debris and incomplete witness information. GEIPAN's Ariege cases reveal recurring mistakes involving planets, stars, re entry debris and incomplete witness information. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies identified and unresolved reports The Moon, Venus, Sirius and atmospheric re entry cases Why missing information is not evidence of the extraordinary ariege official files Introduction Ariège’s official UFO record is striking not because it contains a hard core of extraordinary incidents, but because it shows how often an initially puzzling sight can be reduced to a familiar cause. In the published GEIPAN cases reviewed for the department, investigators identify or provisionally explain reports through the Moon, Venus, Sirius, atmospheric re entry, satellites, a sky lantern and camera reflections. Three reports are classified as lacking enough reliable information, rather than as unexplained craft. That distinction matters. GEIPAN, the unit of France’s national space agency CNES that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, does not treat every report without a firm solution as equally mysterious. Its files separate strong identifications, probable explanations and cases that cannot be assessed properly. Ariège’s record therefore says more about mistaken interpretation and missing evidence than about objects defying conventional explanation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; BENAGUES (09) 26.10.1989, 26/10/1989, B; MONTEGUT EN COUSERANS (09) 12.04.2008, 12/04/... What the classifications actually mean GEIPAN’s letter grades describe the state of the evidence after investigation. They are not a scale running from “ordinary” to “alien”. A Category A case has been identified with a high degree of confidence. A Category B case has a likely conventional explanation, although some uncertainty remains. Category C indicates that the available information is insufficient or unreliable. Category D is reserved for reports that remain unexplained after investigation and have enough evidential substance to support further analysis. GEIPAN says its assessments consider both the consistency of the evidence and the residual strangeness left after possible explanations have been tested. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr evolution classification des cas evolution classification des cas This framework prevents two common errors. First, an unidentified report is not automatically an extraordinary one. Second, a probable identification is not necessarily a proven reconstruction of every detail remembered by a witness. The distinction is visible across Ariège’s files. Some observations match an astronomical object so closely that GEIPAN assigns Category A. Others fit satellites, lanterns or atmospheric events but retain a small doubt because the direction, timing or weather information is incomplete. Category C reports generally tell investigators too little to choose confidently between several ordinary possibilities. In practical terms, Ariège’s official record breaks down into three broad groups: Firmly identified: the Moon, Venus, camera reflections and a Starlink satellite train. Probably identified: Sirius, atmospheric re entry, a sky lantern and ordinary satellites affected by visual illusion. Unassessable: reports from Cos, Saint Girons and Taurignan Vieux that lack sufficiently detailed testimony. None of those classifications establishes a technically strong unexplained case in the department’s published GEIPAN set. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Moon became two very different “objects” The Moon provides the clearest demonstration of how landscape, cloud and expectation can change the apparent character of a familiar object. At Foix on 4 July 1979, a witness returning home at about 2am reported a large red orange mass shaped rather like an artillery shell. It appeared silent, remained visible for about two minutes and then disappeared into the landscape. The report was originally among cases placed in Category C, but GEIPAN later revisited it. Investigators compared the reported direction, duration, colour and disappearance with the Moon’s position near the horizon and reclassified the incident as Category A. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The important point is not that the witness failed to see anything. GEIPAN’s interpretation is that the witness saw the Moon but did not recognise it. Near the horizon, the Moon can look unusually orange or red, appear larger than expected and vanish behind terrain. With no visible detail connecting the light to a familiar lunar disc, fear and fatigue can encourage a more threatening interpretation. A second lunar case occurred at Sentenac d’Oust on 30 July 2016. At around 4.15am, a witness saw a bright orange light roughly 20 to 25 degrees above the horizon through broken cloud. The light seemed to stretch into an inverted L before fading. The witness considered a forest fire and an object entering the atmosphere. GEIPAN reconstructed the sightline and concluded that the Moon was being seen through an irregular opening in the clouds; the illuminated gap, rather than the full lunar disc, created the changing shape. The case was classified A. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Together, the two reports explain why “it did not look like the Moon” is not decisive evidence against a lunar identification. At Foix, the horizon altered its colour and apparent form. At Sentenac d’Oust, clouds acted as a moving mask. The object remained ordinary while the viewing conditions made its appearance unfamiliar. Venus and Sirius looked active without moving Two other Ariège files concern bright astronomical objects whose apparent behaviour was more dramatic than their real movement. UAP Evidence Found? What The Pentagon Actually Admitted Channel: Brain Twist Open on YouTube Venus over Lavelanet A witness near Lavelanet filmed a white light on several mornings in February and March 2011. It remained almost motionless for as long as 45 minutes, appeared to scintillate and faded as daylight increased. Repetition might seem to strengthen a mysterious claim, but in this case it gave investigators a testable pattern. GEIPAN checked the viewing direction towards the south south east and reconstructed the sky at about 7am on the relevant dates. Venus occupied the reported part of the sky and was bright enough to be conspicuous to the naked eye. Its repeated appearance at approximately the same time and bearing, followed by its loss in daylight, supported an astronomical explanation rather than a manoeuvring object. The case was classified A. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The reported flickering did not require Venus itself to change. Light passing through turbulent layers of air is refracted unevenly, especially when an object is relatively low in the sky. This can produce apparent flashes, colour changes and small positional shifts. Sirius over Montégut en Couserans On 12 April 2008, two witnesses watched a stationary, strongly scintillating light for about ten minutes. It appeared round, brighter than an ordinary star and flashed several colours, with blue particularly noticeable. They had enough time to fetch binoculars before it disappeared behind a hill. GEIPAN’s sky chart placed Sirius—the brightest star in the night sky—low in the south west at the time. Bright stars close to the horizon often flash through several colours because their light travels through more atmosphere than it does when they are overhead. The disappearance behind local terrain also fitted the account. GEIPAN classified the report B rather than A because the witnesses had not recorded the direction precisely enough to eliminate every residual doubt. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The contrast between Lavelanet and Montégut shows why similar explanations can receive different grades. Venus at Lavelanet was supported by repeated observations, recorded timing, filmed material and a firm viewing direction. Sirius was highly plausible at Montégut, but the less exact directional evidence justified a more cautious conclusion. Fireballs, lanterns and satellites Not all of Ariège’s explained reports were stationary lights. Several involved apparent flight, rapid passage or groups of moving points. At Bénagues on 26 October 1989, a witness saw a “ball of fire” cross the sky in only three or four seconds. GEIPAN judged atmospheric re entry to be the likely explanation and classified the case B. The short duration and rapid, luminous passage are fundamentally different from the prolonged hovering often implied in popular UFO imagery."},{"title":"Belesta Hoax","url":"/belesta-hoax/","description":"The celebrated Belesta sighting survived for decades before local men revealed the simple lamp device behind the spectacle.","breadcrumb":"How Ariege's UFO Mysteries Changed With Evidence","level":2,"text":"Belesta Hoax How the Belesta UFO Case Fell Apart Ariege UFOs Belesta Hoax Belesta Hoax How the Belesta UFO Case Fell Apart The celebrated Belesta sighting survived for decades before local men revealed the simple lamp device behind the spectacle. The celebrated Belesta sighting survived for decades before local men revealed the simple lamp device behind the spectacle. event time window What witnesses saw above the Rocher de Millet How the lamp mechanism created convincing motion Why the case remained credible until 2009 ariege belesta hoax Introduction The Bélesta lights of 16 October 1954 became Ariège’s most celebrated UFO story because they seemed to offer unusually strong evidence: dozens of witnesses, repeated movements above a recognisable ridge, colour changes, gendarmes at the scene and a detailed report in the regional press. Yet the spectacle was not an unexplained aircraft. In 2009, local men disclosed that they had created it with powerful electric lamps mounted on a makeshift mechanism assembled from bicycle parts. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 That admission did more than solve an old village mystery. It exposed how a deliberately produced light display could acquire authority through collective testimony, official attention and decades of repetition. Bélesta therefore remains central to Ariège’s UFO history, but as a warning against equating numerous sincere witnesses with an extraordinary cause. Later reporting weakened the original claim decisively, while also revealing that suspicions of a trick had circulated long before the public confession. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr L'observation «officielle» dans «La Dépêche» de 1954... À 22 h 05 au sud ouest de Belesta ils peuvent observer la dernière phase du p... What witnesses saw above the Rocher de Millet The display began at about 9.30 pm on a Saturday evening. Residents saw a bright glow near the rocky horizon south west of Bélesta, around the height commonly identified as the Rocher de Millet. The light appeared to rise above the ridge, descend behind it and return several times. As people gathered outside, the single light was reportedly joined by a second and then a third, with witnesses describing differences in brightness and colour. The performance lasted until shortly after 10 pm. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr L'observation «officielle» dans «La Dépêche» de 1954... À 22 h 05 au sud ouest de Belesta ils peuvent observer la dernière phase du p... The darkness made the display look more elaborate than the apparatus producing it. Observers could see luminous points but not the people, terrain or supporting mechanism behind them. With no visible structure connecting the lights to the hillside, their repeated emergence above the ridge could be interpreted as climbing, hovering or manoeuvring in open sky. Later summaries described the event as a half hour “round” or ballet of luminous balls, language that helped turn an ambiguous visual performance into a memorable aerial encounter. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 A crowd formed in the village, and the gendarmes were called. Their presence became one of the case’s strongest reputational assets. In later retellings, the involvement of uniformed witnesses could sound like official verification of an unknown craft. In reality, it established that the lights were visible and sufficiently puzzling to prompt a response. It did not determine their distance, construction or origin. Modern French investigations likewise treat police or gendarmerie statements as valuable testimony rather than automatic proof that a phenomenon is genuinely unexplained. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 The event also received unusually favourable press treatment. A lengthy article appeared in La Dépêche du Midi a few days later, written by Doctor Millet, then president of the Lavelanet flying club. His aviation connection gave the account an appearance of informed judgement, even though no aircraft, instrument reading, photograph or physical trace was produced. The lasting case was therefore built principally from eyewitness descriptions and the authority attached to those who reported them. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 How the lamp mechanism created convincing motion In May 2009, René Lagarde and other local men said that the display had been a prank organised by a group of young friends who were bored that evening. Lagarde named a circle that included Jean and André Sibra, Gérard Pibouleau and Gérard Coléra. Their device reportedly used a bicycle fork, handlebar and wheel, with powerful electric lamps fixed to the assembly. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 The surviving descriptions do not amount to a modern engineering reconstruction, and the exact movements of every lamp cannot now be demonstrated frame by frame. The basic principle, however, fits the central observation closely. A wheel or rotating frame allowed separate lights to change position relative to one another. Raising, lowering or turning the assembly near the ridge could make lamps appear above the horizon, disappear behind the rock and re emerge elsewhere. Switching individual lights on and off would further suggest that one object had divided into several, or that several objects were independently manoeuvring. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr L'observation «officielle» dans «La Dépêche» de 1954... À 22 h 05 au sud ouest de Belesta ils peuvent observer la dernière phase du p... The Rocher de Millet was essential to the illusion. The ridge functioned like the edge of a darkened stage: it concealed the operators and most of the apparatus while exposing only the brightest components. Witnesses looking from the village had no dependable way to tell whether the lamps were close to the rocks, far beyond them or high in the sky. A light with no visible surroundings provides few reliable clues to its dimensions or range, so modest physical movement can be perceived as a much larger aerial displacement. This is a recurring problem in investigations of unusual lights. France’s official GEIPAN service, part of the national space agency CNES, stresses the need to collect precise information about direction, duration, viewing geometry and environmental reference points. Its published material notes that apparently moving or extraordinary lights can receive ordinary explanations once their actual circumstances are reconstructed. Even a flickering lantern may appear to travel at remarkable speed when observers cannot judge its distance. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr n the sky, found rational explanations.Read more... Bélesta therefore does not show that the crowd imagined lights that were never present. The witnesses saw a real, intentionally created spectacle. What failed was their interpretation of its scale and location. Reports of rising, descending and changing formation were reasonable descriptions of appearance, but they were not reliable measurements of an object’s flight. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why the case looked stronger in 1954 The prank occurred during the peak period of France’s autumn 1954 flying saucer wave. Newspapers were carrying reports from numerous towns, while radio and public discussion had made luminous discs and mysterious craft a familiar explanation for anything unusual in the sky. Lagarde later said that the national excitement surrounding such reports helped inspire the group’s joke. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 610511 ovni a belesta en 1954 c etait une blague May 22, 2009 — Ovni à Bélesta en 1954: c'était une blague!... On était en pleine période d'apparitions d'OVNI dans le ciel de France... Published: May 22, 2009 That timing affected both the pranksters and their audience. The young men knew that moving lights would be interpreted through the flying saucer stories already circulating. Witnesses, meanwhile, did not"},{"title":"What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals","url":"/what-aubes-ufo-record-actually-reveals/","description":"Aube has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals Aube has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark mystery. Aube has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark mystery. aube Introduction The clearest evidence comes from GEIPAN, the unit of France’s national space agency that examines reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its published Aube cases repeatedly show the same lesson: “unidentified” often means that the information was insufficient, not that investigators found evidence of an unknown craft. Several reports were resolved as Venus, Sirius or an airliner; others were placed in category C because reliable data were missing. No publicly listed Aube case in the available official record stands out as a strong, well corroborated category D mystery. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANROMILLY SUR SEINE (10) 15.07.2015 15/07/2015 C Aube Manque d'informations fiables 12/09/2016 2015 07 15... Published: July 15, 2015 A department of modest cases, not a famous UFO hotspot Aube’s official record is geographically scattered. Reports appear around Troyes and its suburbs, Romilly sur Seine, Brienne le Château, Chavanges, Villiers sous Praslin and on major roads crossing the department. This distribution does not reveal an obvious hotspot, military corridor or persistent concentration around one installation. It looks more like the reporting pattern expected from a largely rural department with several towns, open horizons and busy road routes. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANROMILLY SUR SEINE (10) 15.07.2015 15/07/2015 C Aube Manque d'informations fiables 12/09/2016 2015 07 15... Published: July 15, 2015 The published cases also vary considerably in quality. Some involved a single witness looking through a window or from a moving vehicle. Others were reported long enough after the event that exact direction, elevation, duration or weather conditions could not be established. These limitations matter because a bright star low on the horizon, an approaching aircraft or a distant light seen through haze can appear stationary, change colour, or seem to move when the observer moves. GEIPAN’s classification system is therefore essential to reading the record correctly: Category A means the phenomenon was identified with high confidence. Category B means an ordinary explanation is considered very probable. Category C means the available information is too weak or incomplete for a dependable conclusion. Categories D1 and D2 are reserved for cases that remain unexplained after investigation, with D2 indicating the stronger degree of strangeness and evidential consistency. GEIPAN stresses that category C is fundamentally a data problem. It should not be treated as evidence for an exotic object. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr missions methodes et resultats Mission & Geipan GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... The older stories: memorable but poorly anchored Romilly sur Seine in 1954 One of Aube’s earliest frequently repeated stories dates from 3 November 1954, during the celebrated French “flying saucer” wave. A café owner in Romilly sur Seine was said to have seen an oval object, apparently about the apparent size of a fist, oscillating from side to side. A neighbouring garage owner reportedly believed that two objects were present. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes!7 Jun 1971 — Le 3 novembre 1954, à Romilly sur Seine, un cafetier observe un objet ovale, gros comme un poing... The date gives the report historical interest. France was then experiencing intense press coverage of supposed discs, cigars and luminous objects, and reports were often published rapidly with little technical checking. Yet the Romilly story survives mainly through later local history and specialist retellings. The available version lacks an original newspaper clipping, precise time, viewing direction, duration, weather record or formal investigation. One specialist catalogue explicitly notes that its source was not given and that the description is too vague to support a reasoned explanation. [ufology.patrickgross.org] ufology.patrickgross.org Son voisin, un ovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 novembre, Romilly...Le 3 novembre 1954, à Romilly sur Seine, un cafetier observe un objet o... That makes the case historically relevant but evidentially weak. It may record a sincere observation, but there is no sound basis for deciding between an astronomical object, aircraft, balloon, atmospheric effect or something less ordinary. The Clérey “cigar” of 1965 A more vivid local account concerns a group at a campsite near Clérey in August 1965. According to later retellings, the witnesses first noticed a red ball whose apparent altitude changed. As it seemed to approach, they described it as elongated or cigar shaped. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes!7 Jun 1971 — Le 3 novembre 1954, à Romilly sur Seine, un cafetier observe un objet ovale, gros comme un poing... The group setting is potentially useful because multiple witnesses can reduce the risk of a purely individual mistake. However, agreement within a group is not the same as independent corroboration: witnesses who discuss an ambiguous light while watching it can gradually adopt the same description. The surviving public material also does not provide the full observational geometry, contemporary photographs, radar records or an official investigative file. The Clérey account is consequently one of Aube’s more colourful stories, but later repetition has not materially strengthened it. It remains an anecdotal case whose apparent movement and changing form could have been affected by distance, haze, fading daylight or shifting perception. What official investigations actually found The strongest part of Aube’s record begins in the period covered by GEIPAN’s public case files. These cases are valuable not because they prove extraordinary activity, but because they show how investigators test ordinary explanations against the witness account. Venus over Brienne On 5 February 2009, a report from Brienne le Château was ultimately classified as category A and attributed to Venus. A separate observation from nearby Brienne la Vieille four days later was classified B, also with Venus considered the probable explanation. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANROMILLY SUR SEINE (10) 15.07.2015 15/07/2015 C Aube Manque d'informations fiables 12/09/2016 2015 07 15... Published: July 15, 2015 Venus is a recurrent source of UFO reports because it is exceptionally bright and can appear surprisingly low in the evening or morning sky. When viewed through thin cloud or from a moving car, it may seem to flicker, change colour or keep pace with the observer. The paired Brienne reports are therefore more useful as an example of repeated astronomical misidentification than as evidence of a local flap. A similar pattern occurred on the A26 between Mailly le Camp and Troyes on 1 November 2010. GEIPAN classified that case B and identified Sirius as the highly probable source. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Three lights and a humming sound at Sainte Savine The Sainte Savine observation of 1 July 2014 is more intriguing on first reading. A witness described three white lights at the extremities of a form, with a red light near the centre, accompanied by a humming noise. GEIPAN considered a drone possible but concluded that the report lacked enough reliable information and classified it C. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This distinction is important. The combination of multiple lights and sound suggests a physical aircraft like source more readily than an astronomical object. However, without accurate angular size, altitude, trajectory, duration, photographs or independent witnesses, investigators could not test the drone hypothesis properly. The case is therefore unresolved in the limited sense that no final identification was reached. It is not an official finding that the object displayed extraordinary performance. Nothing in the published summary establishes rapid acceleration, impossible manoeuvres, radar confirmation or effects on the surroundings. Romilly sur Seine in 2015 On 15 July 2015, a witness in central Romilly sur Seine was watching the night sky from an upstairs roof window shortly before midnight. The report involved lights or objects whose apparent movement was described in relation"},{"title":"Classic Sightings","url":"/classic-sightings/","description":"Romilly-sur-Seine and Clerey produced Aube's most memorable early stories, but missing records leave both open to ordinary explanations.","breadcrumb":"What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"Classic Sightings Why Aube's Famous Sightings Remain Unproven Aube UFOs Classic Sightings Classic Sightings Why Aube's Famous Sightings Remain Unproven Romilly sur Seine and Clerey produced Aube's most memorable early stories, but missing records leave both open to ordinary explanations. Romilly sur Seine and Clerey produced Aube's most memorable early stories, but missing records leave both open to ordinary explanations. event time window The 1954 Romilly sur Seine report The 1965 Clerey campsite sighting How missing records weaken both cases aube classic sightings Introduction Aube’s two best remembered early UFO stories are intriguing because they are vivid, local and easy to retell. On 3 November 1954, a café owner at Romilly sur Seine reportedly watched an oval object oscillating in the sky, while a neighbouring garage owner thought there were two objects. In August 1965, campers at Clérey reportedly saw a red ball change altitude, approach and take on a cigar like appearance. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... Neither case, however, is supported by the material needed to establish an extraordinary event. No accessible contemporary investigation file provides precise times, bearings, angular elevations, weather conditions, photographs, radar returns or independent statements from each witness. Later summaries preserve the stories but not the evidence required to test them. The fairest conclusion is therefore not that the sightings were disproved, but that both remain historically memorable and evidentially weak. Exeter History Minute Exeter UFO Channel: Exeter Historical Society Open on YouTube The 1954 Romilly sur Seine report The Romilly account belongs to the great French flying saucer wave of 1954, a period in which reports appeared rapidly across the country and received intense press attention. UFO researcher Jacques Vallée later recalled collecting newspaper stories from the French and Italian wave, illustrating how strongly the phenomenon circulated through the media and popular culture of the time. [WIRED] wired.com Jacques Vallée Still Doesn't Know What UFOs Are Despite his extensive research, Vallée maintains that he does not know what UFOs truly are but believes the answer lies beyond simplistic... A modern local history compilation gives the essential Romilly story in only a few lines. A café owner was said to have observed an oval object on 3 November, apparently as large as a fist at arm’s length, moving or oscillating from side to side. A garage owner nearby reportedly believed that two objects were visible. The summary supplies no names, exact observation time, duration, viewing direction or account of how far apart the witnesses were. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... Those omissions prevent even basic reconstruction. “The size of a fist” describes apparent size, not physical size: a small nearby object and a much larger distant one can occupy the same angle in the observer’s view. Likewise, sideways oscillation can describe the genuine movement of an object, atmospheric distortion, involuntary eye movement or the autokinetic effect, in which an isolated light against a dark or featureless background appears to wander. The supposed second witness helps less than it first appears. The surviving summary says that the garage owner counted two objects, while the café owner apparently described one. That could represent useful independent corroboration, but it could equally reflect different viewing positions, reflections, stars, aircraft lights or later compression of two imperfect recollections. Without separate original statements, it is impossible to establish whether the men observed simultaneously, discussed the sighting before being interviewed, or even agreed on the objects’ position and behaviour. Romilly’s aviation setting is relevant but inconclusive. The town had a longstanding military aerodrome and aviation depot, and its air base was active in 1954. Aircraft, training activity and meteorological equipment therefore belong among the ordinary possibilities that should have been checked. [Forgotten Airfields] forgottenairfields.com airfield romilly closed 1140 Forgotten AirfieldsRomilly (CLOSED)9 Dec 2013 — The airfield was built in near the end of World War I. Romilly Airfield was used by the A... A separate Romilly report from 16 October 1954 was reportedly attributed by the base weather station to a luminous weather balloon, showing that such objects were present in the local sky during the same flap period. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... That does not prove that the 3 November object was a balloon. It does show why the absence of a weather station record, balloon release time, flight check or reliable direction is so damaging. The most obvious local explanations cannot now be tested against the claim. The report’s position at the tail end of the 1954 wave also matters. By November, French readers had already encountered months of stories involving discs, globes, cigars and landed machines. Aube’s local retrospective lists numerous reports from July to October, including lights, spheres and alleged close encounters. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... That atmosphere does not mean witnesses invented what they saw. It does mean that ambiguous aerial impressions were already being interpreted through a widely available flying saucer vocabulary. The 1965 Clérey campsite sighting The Clérey story is more visually dramatic but even less securely dated. A later local account places it during an evening in August 1965. Gérard Gateau, camping with friends, reportedly noticed a red ball in the sky. It changed altitude, approached and was then described by the witnesses as a cigar. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... The presence of several people could have made this a valuable case. Multiple witnesses may help confirm that an external stimulus was present, especially when they observe independently from different positions. Yet the available version does not state how many friends were present, whether each supplied a statement, whether their descriptions agreed, or whether the cigar shape emerged only after they had discussed the sighting together. The description itself is compatible with several ordinary effects. A distant aircraft seen nearly head on can begin as a concentrated red or white light and gradually reveal an elongated arrangement of lights as its angle changes. Haze, thin cloud and poor night time depth perception can make an approaching light appear to rise, fall or alter shape. A bright source seen with unfocused vision may also spread into a bar or cigar like form. No surviving details allow those possibilities to be ranked. The account provides no exact date, clock time, compass direction, duration, elevation or sound. There is no known photograph, physical trace, radar report or record from an aviation authority. Even the apparent approach cannot be converted into a speed or distance because there is no fixed reference point or triangulation. There is also a small but revealing problem in the later record. The same local compilation follows the Clérey story with a December 1966 observation at Saint Julien les Villas, but refers to the witness there as “Gilbert Gateau” rather than Gérard. It is unclear whether this is a typographical error, a different person or a sign that information was merged during later retelling. [Site de histoiredetroyes!] histoiredetroyes.jimdofree.com Site de histoiredetroyes!O.V.N.I Site de histoiredetroyes... The discrepancy does not discredit the Clérey witnesses, but it illustrates why retrospective summaries cannot substitute for dated statements and original files. Later UFO literature sometimes treats repeated observations by one person as reinforcing credibility. In practice, repetition can cut both ways. It may indicate that an attentive observer encountered more than one unusual stimulus, but it may also show a continuing tendency to interpret distant lights in extraordinary terms. Without documentation of the later sighting, it cannot be used to strengthen the earlier one. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why missing records weaken both cases The central weakness is not that an ordinary explanation has been conclusively identified. It is that the evidence needed to distinguish among explanations was either never collected or has not remained publicly accessible. France’s official UFO investigation"},{"title":"Official Cases","url":"/official-cases-1c7219/","description":"Official Aube files show how Venus, Sirius, aircraft and missing data can turn ordinary lights into apparently unexplained events.","breadcrumb":"What Aube's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"Official Cases How GEIPAN Explained Aube's Strange Lights Aube UFOs Official Cases Official Cases How GEIPAN Explained Aube's Strange Lights Official Aube files show how Venus, Sirius, aircraft and missing data can turn ordinary lights into apparently unexplained events. Official Aube files show how Venus, Sirius, aircraft and missing data can turn ordinary lights into apparently unexplained events. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies reports Venus and Sirius in the Aube files Why category C does not mean alien craft aube official cases Introduction Aube’s official UFO record is less a catalogue of mysterious craft than a practical lesson in how ordinary lights become extraordinary reports. In the files published by GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, bright planets, stars, aircraft, lanterns, military activity and electrical faults account for many of the department’s apparently strange sightings. Other reports remain unclassified in practical terms because witnesses supplied too little reliable information. The distinction matters. GEIPAN’s category C does not mean that investigators detected something beyond conventional explanation. It means that the report could not be properly assessed. By contrast, category A indicates a firmly identified phenomenon, while category B denotes a probable conventional explanation. Read this way, the Aube archive contains no compelling pattern of well documented unknown craft. Its strongest lesson is that identification depends on precise times, viewing directions, weather, photographs, flight information and independent witnesses—not simply on how strange an experience felt. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN How GEIPAN classifies a strange light GEIPAN begins with a witness account, not with an assumption that a physical vehicle was present. Its preferred French term describes an unidentified aerospace phenomenon , allowing for the possibility that the witness saw a star, reflection, atmospheric effect or perceptual illusion rather than an object. The agency compares the account with known astronomical, meteorological, aviation and human perception explanations. Reports and conclusions are then published anonymously. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Two ideas govern the classification: strangeness and consistency . Strangeness measures how poorly the best ordinary hypothesis fits the reported experience. Consistency concerns the quantity and reliability of the available evidence—such as the number of witnesses, the precision of their answers, photographs, videos, radar traces and the internal coherence of the testimony. This produces four practically important outcomes: Category A: the explanation is effectively demonstrated. Category B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, though not proved beyond every doubt. Category C: the evidence is too incomplete or unreliable for a sound assessment. Categories D1 and D2: the case remains unexplained despite sufficient investigation, with D2 reserved for stronger and stranger evidence. GEIPAN explicitly states that a report becomes category C when its evidential consistency is inadequate for either confirming an explanation or validating an unexplained classification. Category C therefore sits between “explained” and “genuinely unexplained”; it is an unusable or underdetermined case, not a residual category for alien craft. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN This is particularly important in Aube, where several reports involved one witness, a brief sighting through a window or windscreen, uncertain directions, no usable imagery and no independent confirmation. The official archive records such cases at Villiers sous Praslin, Troyes, Romilly sur Seine and other locations as lacking reliable information. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Venus and Sirius in the Aube files Bright astronomical bodies provide some of the clearest Aube examples because they can be checked retrospectively. Investigators can reconstruct the sky for the reported time and place, compare the object’s position with the witness’s viewing direction and test whether its apparent behaviour matches a planet or star. Venus over Brienne On 5 February 2009, a motorist near Brienne le Château noticed an intensely bright light between about 8.45 pm and 9 pm. He stopped his vehicle and photographed it, watching for roughly ten to twelve minutes while it appeared stationary. The observation attracted local newspaper attention, but GEIPAN’s reconstruction showed that Venus occupied the relevant part of the sky. The report was classified A: a misidentification of the planet. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A similar report followed at Brienne la Vieille on 9 February. Several people were reportedly frightened by a large, intensely luminous, multicoloured light that seemed stationary for as long as two hours. Only one detailed testimony was collected, however, and the submitted photographs were of poor quality. GEIPAN reconstructed the evening sky and found Venus in the west south west at about 20 degrees above the horizon, with a brightness consistent with the reported dominant light. It classified the sighting B rather than A because the viewing direction was not recorded precisely enough for an entirely conclusive identification. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete234 Compte rendu enquete234 That difference between A and B is instructive. The proposed cause was the same in both Brienne cases, but the strength of the documentation differed. A probable explanation does not become weaker merely because the witness was sincere or frightened; equally, GEIPAN did not claim complete certainty when a crucial directional measurement was absent. Venus is unusually effective at producing such reports because it is the brightest night sky object after the Moon. Seen alone in twilight or through gaps in cloud, it can dominate the surrounding sky and appear larger than a normal point of light. Its slow apparent movement is difficult to notice without a fixed reference, encouraging descriptions such as “hovering” or “stationary”. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science Venus: Facts Science Venus: Facts The second Brienne account may also have been influenced by publicity surrounding the first. GEIPAN’s case analysis noted that some descriptions could have been affected by the earlier report in the local press. This does not imply dishonesty. It reflects a recognised problem in witness evidence: once people have heard a vivid description, later memories and interpretations can absorb some of its language or imagery. GEIPAN treats emotion, cultural expectations and delayed recollection as factors that may increase the perceived strangeness of an otherwise ordinary sight. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete234 Compte rendu enquete234 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Sirius seen from the road On 1 November 2010, a motorist travelling along the A26 from Mailly le Camp towards Troyes reported an intense white halo that seemed to move or “flutter” in the sky. The witness was driving at motorway speed, and the report did not establish an exact observation point or duration. Nevertheless, the vehicle was travelling generally north to south and the light was seen ahead through the windscreen. Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, was in the corresponding direction. GEIPAN classified the case B: a probable observation of Sirius. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A later case at Chavanges offers an even clearer version of the same mechanism. During nights in February 2019, a witness repeatedly watched a bright, apparently low and nearly stationary light for periods approaching an hour. Two photographs helped investigators identify it as Sirius, and the report was classified A. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr n simultanée durera environ 6 minutes... Sirius can look much less star like than readers may expect. It is the brightest star visible from Earth, and when it is low in the sky its light passes through a comparatively thick and turbulent layer of atmosphere. Refraction and scintillation can make it flicker, fluctuate in brightness and flash different colours. Against a dark or featureless background, a stationary light may also appear to drift because of the autokinetic effect, a visual illusion recognised in aviation guidance. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science Skywatching FAQ Science Skywatching FAQ Movement by the observer adds another layer. A light viewed through a moving windscreen may appear to change position against nearby trees, road furniture, reflections or parts of the vehicle. Without an accurately recorded bearing, the witness’s"},{"title":"Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","url":"/why-audes-best-known-ufo-cases-remain/","description":"Aude has a genuine but comparatively modest place in France’s recorded UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Aude has a genuine but comparatively modest place in France’s recorded UFO history. Aude has a genuine but comparatively modest place in France’s recorded UFO history. Its public record is not dominated by a famous nationwide “landing” case, a military interception or a well documented radar encounter. aude Introduction The two most notable cases occurred at Port la Nouvelle in January 1988 and Fontiès d’Aude in May 1995. Both remain in category D in the archive of GEIPAN, the unit of the French space agency CNES that collects and investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. “Unresolved”, however, does not mean “confirmed extraordinary craft”. It means that the available information did not support a satisfactory identification. In Aude, that distinction is especially important because the strongest sounding reports are also limited by single witness testimony, incomplete corroboration or uncertain links between the sighting and alleged physical effects. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988. Date d'observation. 26/01/1988. Région. Languedoc Roussillon. Départeme... What the official record contains GEIPAN’s archive provides the clearest evidence based starting point for examining Aude. The organisation is part of CNES and describes its role as collecting testimony, analysing data, conducting multidisciplinary investigations and publishing anonymised case files. It is not a military alert service and does not claim to investigate extraterrestrial life. Its classifications concern how well a reported phenomenon can be identified from the evidence available. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPANGEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives information on u... In broad terms: Category A means the phenomenon was identified with high confidence. Category B means an explanation is considered probable. Category C means there is too little reliable information to reach a conclusion. Category D is reserved for reports that remain unidentified after assessment and possess a meaningful degree of strangeness and evidential consistency. The surviving Aude files span different kinds of reports rather than one clear “flap”. Older entries include unresolved or inadequately documented observations at Moussan, Port la Nouvelle, Fontiès d’Aude, Lézignan Corbières, Peyriac de Mer, Port Leucate and Fleury. Other reports were attributed to atmospheric re entry, space debris, a meteor, an aircraft, the International Space Station, an ordinary satellite, a satellite train, birds, a laser or a public light display. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN This distribution matters. It suggests that Aude’s archive is not strong evidence for a single recurring unknown technology or a concentrated local hotspot. It is better understood as a cross section of the difficulties involved in observing the sky: distance is hard to judge, lights lack scale, familiar objects can look unfamiliar at night, and modern satellites add patterns that earlier generations of witnesses rarely encountered. Port la Nouvelle, 1988: the coastal case that stayed unresolved On 26 January 1988, at about 9.15 pm, a witness at Port la Nouvelle reported repeatedly seeing a large object at apparently low altitude. Its outline was compared to a helmet with two wing like extensions. The object was described as silent, with a white internal light that later appeared orange, before it departed rapidly. GEIPAN retained the report as category D. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988. Date d'observation. 26/01/1988. Région. Languedoc Roussillon. Départeme... The case is memorable because it combines several classic features of close range UFO testimony: an apparently structured shape, silence, changing light and rapid departure. Port la Nouvelle’s position on the Mediterranean coast also invites questions about aircraft, maritime activity and unusual viewing conditions over dark water. Yet the documentary limits are substantial. The public summary indicates that no further useful information could be gathered. There is no cited radar return, photograph, physical trace, independent instrument record or confirmed second witness. Nor does the surviving summary establish the object’s actual distance or height. An apparently “imposing” object can be much smaller and closer than assumed, or larger and farther away; without a known scale, estimated size and speed remain highly uncertain. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr port la nouvelle 1988 0101123 Observation OVNI à Port La Nouvelle — janvier 1988Le 26 janvier 1988 à 21h15 un témoin observe plusieurs fois un objet imposant, volant à... The category D label therefore reflects an unresolved testimony, not a verified encounter with an exotic vehicle. The report may describe something genuinely unusual, but the surviving evidence is too sparse to discriminate confidently between an unknown object, an unusual view of conventional activity or a perceptual misjudgement. Later publication has preserved the claim but has not materially strengthened it. Fontiès d’Aude, 1995: a dramatic report with an unproven aftermath The Fontiès d’Aude case is the most elaborate officially unresolved report associated with the department. Between 25 and 28 May 1995, at roughly 3.30 am, a couple reportedly saw a large dark object hovering above their house. The principal witness thought its appearance could resemble a helicopter, but said it produced no noise, smoke or trail. During an observation lasting several minutes, it allegedly directed five or six beams of light towards the ground before leaving at moderate speed. The household’s dogs were said to have hidden indoors without barking. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr FONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... The account became more serious in the days that followed. According to the witness, the dogs developed vomiting and diarrhoea, his wife experienced health problems, he himself felt unusually tired, and vegetation in the illuminated area appeared to deteriorate. He also mentioned the sudden hospital admission of a neighbour. Alarmed, he contacted the civil security authorities to ask whether the events might be connected. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr FONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... GEIPAN could not identify the object, which is why the case remains in category D. Crucially, however, its investigation also failed to establish any causal relationship between the observation and the reported effects on people, animals or plants. The archive’s wording separates those two conclusions: the aerial phenomenon was not explained, but the alleged medical and environmental consequences were not demonstrated. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr FONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... That separation is the key to interpreting the case fairly. Symptoms such as tiredness, stomach illness or plant damage can have many unrelated causes. To connect them scientifically with the reported object would require timely medical records, veterinary evidence, plant or soil sampling, a defined exposure mechanism and comparison with unaffected areas. The public summary does not document such a chain of evidence. The behaviour of the dogs is similarly suggestive but not conclusive. Animals may react to sounds, odours, human anxiety or disturbances imperceptible to their owners. Their reported reaction adds human interest and may support the sincerity of the witness, but it cannot identify what was overhead. Fontiès d’Aude is therefore stronger as a detailed witness narrative than as a physical evidence case. It remains legitimately unresolved within the official archive, yet later investigation did not validate its most dramatic implication: that the object caused illness or environmental damage. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why most Aude reports acquired ordinary explanations The resolved cases are as important as the unexplained ones because they show how strange an identifiable object can appear under difficult conditions. Aude’s archive contains examples involving astronomical or orbital objects, atmospheric events, aviation and wildlife. A report at Pennautier on 20 October 1990 was attributed to atmospheric re entry. A Saint Hilaire sighting from August 2012 was classified as a meteor or fireball. An observation near Gruissan in April 1998 concerned falling space debris, while flashes reported at Port la Nouvelle in August 2019 were"},{"title":"Fonties Claims","url":"/fonties-claims/","description":"The 1995 report combined a hovering object with later claims of illness and plant damage, but investigators found no proven causal link.","breadcrumb":"Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","level":2,"text":"Fonties Claims Did the Fonties d'Aude Sighting Cause Physical Effects? Aude UFO Files Fonties Claims Fonties Claims Did the Fonties d'Aude Sighting Cause Physical Effects? The 1995 report combined a hovering object with later claims of illness and plant damage, but investigators found no proven causal link. The 1995 report combined a hovering object with later claims of illness and plant damage, but investigators found no proven causal link. event time window The reported object, beams and animal behaviour The later health and vegetation claims Why investigators could not establish causation aude fonties claims Introduction The Fontiès d’Aude case began with a striking but uncorroborated night time sighting and became more controversial only afterwards. Between 25 and 28 May 1995, at about 3.30 am, a couple reported a large, dark and silent object hovering above their home. They said it directed several beams of light towards the ground before departing. During the following week, they associated illnesses affecting themselves and their dogs, damage to vegetation and a neighbour’s hospital admission with the encounter. Investigators could not identify the object, so the sighting remains officially unresolved. They did not, however, establish that it caused any of the reported physical effects. Alternative explanations were available for the human symptoms, the neighbour’s condition and the damaged plants. This distinction makes Fontiès d’Aude important within Aude’s UFO history: it is an unresolved observation, but not a verified case of injury, radiation or environmental contamination. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... The reported object, beams and animal behaviour The principal witness was a 60 year old man, although the official summary describes the observation as having been made by a couple. The precise date was uncertain even in the submitted account: GEIPAN records it only as occurring between 25 and 28 May. The observation lasted several minutes sometime between 3 am and 4 am. Weather conditions were entered as unknown, and the publicly available case metadata gives no measured distance between the witnesses and the phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... According to the account, the object was above the witnesses’ house and appeared comparable in apparent size or general bulk to a Sikorsky style helicopter. It was dark or black, stationary at first and completely silent. The witnesses noticed no smoke or visible exhaust. Before leaving at what they considered a moderate speed, it reportedly illuminated the ground five or six times with beams resembling powerful searchlights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... Those details explain why the report was not readily dismissed as a simple distant point of light. A hovering, helicopter sized form with directed beams would be more structured than the stars, satellites and meteors responsible for many ordinary UFO reports. Silence was also central to the witnesses’ sense that the object was unusual. Yet the description has serious limitations. No photograph, video, radar return or instrument reading is published with the case. The public record gives no dependable angular measurements, altitude, compass bearing or distance. “Helicopter sized” therefore describes an impression rather than a measured physical size: a smaller nearby object and a larger distant one can appear much the same to an observer, particularly at night. The behaviour of the couple’s dogs added a memorable element. During the sighting, the animals reportedly entered the house, hid and did not bark. This could indicate that they reacted to something in their environment, but it does not identify what that stimulus was. Dogs may respond to unfamiliar movement, light, vibrations, their owners’ behaviour or sounds that humans do not notice. Without an independent record of their behaviour, the reaction remains part of the testimony rather than separate proof of an extraordinary object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... The later health and vegetation claims The case expanded considerably during the week after the sighting. The witnesses reported that their dogs developed vomiting and diarrhoea. The wife experienced unspecified health problems, while her husband felt unusually tired. They also noticed deterioration among plants in an area they believed had been illuminated by the object’s beams. A nearby elderly resident was admitted to hospital around the same period. Alarmed by this combination, the principal witness contacted the civil protection authorities to ask whether the events might be connected. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... The chronology is important. The dogs’ symptoms and most of the human or botanical concerns were not described as immediate effects occurring while the object was overhead. They were noticed or interpreted over the following days. That delay does not make causation impossible, but it greatly widens the range of potential explanations. Gastrointestinal illness in dogs, fatigue, medication related symptoms, age related hospitalisation and plant stress are all common events that can occur without sharing a cause. The reported pattern also risks being made stronger in retrospect. Once the witnesses feared that the light beams might have been harmful, otherwise unrelated developments could be grouped around the sighting: one person felt tired, another was unwell, the dogs became sick, plants looked damaged and a neighbour entered hospital. Each claim may have been sincerely reported, but a collection of events occurring after the same night does not by itself show that they arose from the same exposure. The vegetation offered the most apparently testable claim because the witnesses associated damage with a particular illuminated area. Investigators nevertheless observed similar deterioration among plants outside the supposed beam zone. That weakened the idea of a localised effect produced by the object. If plants beyond the claimed exposure area showed the same symptoms, ordinary heat, dryness, disease, soil conditions or seasonal stress became more plausible than a sharply bounded external influence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... No published laboratory analysis is attached to the current public case page showing unusual radiation, chemical residues, altered soil or a distinctive biological injury. Nor does the accessible record provide veterinary test results tying the dogs’ illness to a particular toxin or form of irradiation. The physical effect claims therefore remain observations and suspicions rather than medically or experimentally demonstrated consequences. Théma 1996 HD OVNI Chercheurs d'OVNI + Débat + E T Roswell Friedman Channel: totophman Open on YouTube Why investigators could not establish causation The investigation reached two separate findings that are sometimes blurred together in retellings. First, it did not produce a satisfactory identification of the aerial phenomenon. Second, it did not establish a causal link between that phenomenon and the subsequent illnesses or plant damage. The first finding kept the observation in category D; it did not reverse the second finding or turn the alleged effects into confirmed evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... Several ordinary explanations were considered sufficient to break the proposed chain of causation: The wife’s symptoms could be related to medical treatment she was already receiving. The husband’s fatigue could be explained by the high temperatures cited in the investigation summary. The elderly neighbour’s hospital admission was not regarded as surprising given his age. Comparable vegetation damage was present away from the area supposedly struck by the beams. The dogs’ later sickness was reported, but no specific mechanism connecting it to the sighting was demonstrated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFONTIES D'AUDE (11) 2 .05.1995 GEIPAN... These explanations do not prove that every symptom had been diagnosed correctly. Their significance is more limited and more practical: they show that the UFO hypothesis was not necessary to account for the aftermath. To demonstrate that the object caused physical effects, investigators would have needed evidence distinguishing the alleged exposure zone and affected subjects from normal local conditions. Useful evidence might have included prompt medical examinations, veterinary analyses, controlled plant and soil samples, radiation measurements, clearly mapped damage or comparison with unaffected specimens. Nothing of that strength appears in the public file. The uncertain timing also complicates the case. The witnesses could not provide a definite date within a four day period, and the official metadata"},{"title":"Port la Nouvelle","url":"/port-la-nouvelle/","description":"The 1988 coastal sighting remains unexplained, but the surviving record lacks the independent evidence needed to establish distance, size or speed.","breadcrumb":"Why Aude's Best Known UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","level":2,"text":"Port la Nouvelle What Was Seen Over Port la Nouvelle in 1988? Aude UFO Files Port la Nouvelle Port la Nouvelle What Was Seen Over Port la Nouvelle in 1988? The 1988 coastal sighting remains unexplained, but the surviving record lacks the independent evidence needed to establish distance, size or speed. The 1988 coastal sighting remains unexplained, but the surviving record lacks the independent evidence needed to establish distance, size or speed. event time window The witness account and reported object Why the coastal setting complicates identification What the surviving evidence can and cannot prove aude port la nouvelle Introduction On 26 January 1988, at about 9.15 pm, a 50 year old man at Port la Nouvelle reported seeing a large, silent, illuminated object moving at apparently low altitude. He compared its outline to a helmet with two wings and said a white light within it turned orange before the object departed rapidly. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, still lists the case as category D: unidentified after investigation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... That classification makes Port la Nouvelle one of the more notable reports in Aude, but the surviving evidence is much weaker than the vivid description may suggest. There was only one recorded witness, no known photograph, radar return, physical trace or independently documented second viewpoint. Most importantly, the distance was not established. Without distance, the reported size, height and speed cannot be converted from impressions into reliable measurements. The case is therefore genuinely unresolved, but it is not strong evidence for an extraordinary craft. Vie extraterrestre : bientôt des preuves ? Channel: Usbek & Rica Open on YouTube The witness account and reported object GEIPAN’s published record fixes the observation at 9.15 pm on Tuesday, 26 January 1988. The witness was a man aged 50, described as curious and actively attentive rather than merely catching a fleeting light in peripheral vision. The conditions are recorded as cloudy or with some clear spells, and the surrounding land use category is “water surfaces”, reflecting Port la Nouvelle’s lagoon and harbour environment. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... According to the official summary, the witness saw the phenomenon several times during the incident. He described a single imposing object, apparently flying low and changing direction. Its form reminded him of a helmet with two wings. A white internal light became orange, no sound was heard, and the object finally seemed to move away quickly. GEIPAN’s short conclusion says that no further information could be gathered and that the phenomenon remained unexplained. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... The database adds several details not included in the summary. It records the viewing direction as 270 degrees, or due west, and gives an apparent size of about ten metres long by five metres high. It also labels the motion as variable, involving apparent acceleration or deceleration, and records “total silence”. Yet the distance field is explicitly marked as not provided. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... That missing distance is central. A witness cannot normally determine that an unfamiliar light is ten metres long simply from vision alone. The same angular outline could belong to a small nearby object, a larger object much farther away or several lights perceived as the edges of one dark body. The figures in metres should therefore be read as the witness’s inferred dimensions, not as independently measured physical dimensions. The same caution applies to “low altitude”. Unless the object passed in front of or behind a landmark of known height, or was seen from a second location, its elevation above the ground could not be calculated. GEIPAN itself warns that witnesses often report distance and speed for unrecognised objects even though those quantities cannot be securely assessed without an established scale. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Why the coastal setting complicates identification Port la Nouvelle is not simply an isolated seaside village beneath an empty sky. It is a Mediterranean harbour and industrial transport site, with shipping activity, roads, rail infrastructure and the lights associated with a working port. The commune also lies beside lagoons and a long coastline, creating open sightlines with few nearby objects against which an aerial light’s distance can be judged. [bouygues construction.com] bouygues construction.com Bouygues ConstructionPort of Port La NouvellePort La Nouvelle is France's third largest Mediterranean port. 225 meters long and 36 meters... Open water can make a light look unusually prominent while removing familiar depth cues such as buildings, trees and street lines. Cloud layers may also hide parts of an aircraft, alter the apparent colour of its lamps or reflect light in ways that make separate sources seem joined. These are general identification difficulties rather than a demonstrated solution to the 1988 report. The surviving file does not contain enough information about cloud height, visibility, wind, the witness’s exact position or the object’s angular elevation to test them properly. The recorded direction introduces another important qualification. A bearing of 270 degrees points west. From Port la Nouvelle, the Mediterranean is principally to the east, so the database entry does not straightforwardly describe an object travelling out over the open sea. It may instead place the sightline inland or across the lagoon and port approaches, depending on the witness’s location. The public record does not provide a map, address or sufficiently detailed trajectory with which to reconstruct the geometry. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... This matters because later retellings can easily let the word “coastal” do more evidential work than it deserves. The case does not document an object emerging from the sea, entering the water or being observed by sailors. Nor does it establish a connection with harbour radar, marine traffic control or an aircraft operating offshore. Its setting broadens the range of possible light sources, but it supplies no independent sensor evidence. Silence is similarly less decisive than it first appears. If the phenomenon was truly close and as large as estimated, the absence of engine noise would be striking. If it was much farther away, however, sound might have been faint, delayed or masked by local noise and wind. Because neither distance nor ambient sound conditions were documented, silence cannot by itself determine what kind of object was present. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the surviving evidence can and cannot prove The strongest evidence is the existence of a specific, apparently sincere report preserved in an official CNES archive. The account contains several connected features rather than a bare claim of a distant point of light: an unusual outline, changing direction, a white to orange colour change, apparent low level movement and an absence of sound. GEIPAN describes the case as strange to very strange and of medium to strong consistency, and the category D classification remained on the file when it was updated in June 2021. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... Nevertheless, almost every feature depends on the perception and recollection of one observer. The archive identifies only one witness and lists no environmental effect. It does not publish a photograph, video, contemporaneous sketch, radar plot, aircraft check, shipping record or statement from an independent observer. It also gives no usable observation duration, exact viewing position or measured angular size. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr PORT LA NOUVELLE (11) 26.01.1988 GEIPAN... Those omissions prevent several basic tests: Size cannot be established. The ten by five metre estimate has no confirmed range behind it. Speed cannot be calculated. “Rapid” departure and variable motion describe appearance, not measured velocity. Altitude cannot be established. “Low” may reflect how large or bright the phenomenon looked. Direction changes cannot be reconstructed. The file supplies a westward viewing bearing but no plotted sequence of azimuth and elevation. Silence cannot be evaluated against range. The object may have been close, distant or not a single solid body. Alternative explanations cannot be checked retrospectively. The public case page contains no documented comparison with local air traffic, shipping activity, astronomical"},{"title":"Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and...","url":"/aveyrons-ufo-cases-between-mystery-and/","description":"Aveyron has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and... Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and Misidentification Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and... Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and... Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and Misidentification Aveyron has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Aveyron has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a large military encounter, radar incident or mass sighting. Instead, it contains a small collection of reports ranging from meteors and aircraft misidentifications to incomplete cases and one notably unresolved observation near Millau in 1999. aveyron Introduction The clearest overall finding is that most reported phenomena in Aveyron have either received ordinary explanations or could not be investigated properly because essential information was missing. The department is therefore most useful as a lesson in how UFO reputations are built: a few striking stories attract lasting attention, while the larger official record is mainly composed of brief lights, distant objects and cases constrained by limited testimony. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; MILLAU (12) 16.03.1999, 16/03/1999, D · Aveyron, Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de cons... What the official record actually contains France’s official body for investigating unusual aerial reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects testimony, checks possible explanations and publishes anonymised case files. Its partners include the gendarmerie, police, air force, civil aviation specialists, meteorologists and scientific experts. Reports made to gendarmes can be recorded in formal statements and passed to GEIPAN for assessment. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN collecte les témoignages relatifs aux phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés (PAN), généralement en témoign... Published counts have placed Aveyron at roughly 22 GEIPAN cases, only a small fraction of the French total. A local press review in 2023 also cited 22 reports for the department. A third party index of the GEIPAN database divided these into three category A cases, nine category B cases, eight category C cases and two category D cases, though such totals can change when old files are re examined or newly published. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr la frenesie des soucoupes volantes 11364718 La frénésie des soucoupes volantes27 Jul 2023 — Le groupe d'étude GEIPAN, qui étudie ces phénomènes, aurait comptabilisé 300 cas d'Ovnis... These categories do not measure how likely a report is to involve an extraordinary craft: A means the phenomenon was identified after investigation. B means it was probably identified. C means it remained unidentified because the information was insufficient or unusable. D means it remained unidentified after investigation. GEIPAN evaluates both the consistency of the available information and the amount of unexplained strangeness left after conventional possibilities have been tested. A category C report is therefore not strong evidence of an exotic event; it usually means that investigators did not have enough reliable material to decide. [Geipan] geipan.fr FAQLa classification repose sur une évaluation quantitative et qualitative de deux paramètres: la consistance (C) de l'observation... That distinction is particularly important in Aveyron. The department has several unresolved looking entries, but many are unresolved because they were brief, involved a single witness or lacked photographs, independent observers and precise positional data. Millau 1999: Aveyron’s strongest official mystery The most important official case occurred at Millau on 16 March 1999. GEIPAN classifies it as category D, describing it as a strange or very strange phenomenon supported by evidence of medium to strong consistency. It remains the clearest Aveyron example of a report that investigators did not simply identify or dismiss for lack of information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. That classification matters, but it should not be overstated. “Unidentified after investigation” does not mean that GEIPAN concluded that an extraterrestrial craft was present. It means that the available account retained significant unusual features after investigators considered known explanations. GEIPAN itself stresses that classifications can change when new information, better astronomical calculations or renewed analysis becomes available. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... The Millau case also illustrates a frustrating feature of departmental UFO research: the public case summary establishes its official status but offers less readily accessible narrative detail than many readers expect. There is no widely documented radar confirmation, physical trace, instrument recording or publicly established military interception associated with it. Its importance rests on the quality and strangeness assigned to the witness material, not on a body of independent technical evidence. For that reason, Millau is best described as unresolved but not evidentially decisive . It is stronger than a poorly documented category C report, yet far below the standard that would be needed to establish an unknown aircraft, advanced technology or non human origin. Roumagnac: the story that shaped Aveyron’s UFO reputation The most famous Aveyron narrative began in June 1966 at an isolated farm near Roumagnac, in the Sévérac area. Later accounts describe a series of encounters involving luminous spheres, cigar shaped objects, unusual sounds, bodily effects and, in the most elaborate versions, small figures or occupants. The reports are said to have continued intermittently for several years and to have involved members of the farming household. [L'Antre de la curiosité] antredelacuriosite.wordpress.com L'Antre de la curiositéLes Ovnis de L'Aveyron L'Antre de la curiositéLes Ovnis de L'Aveyron This is the episode commonly known as the “balls of Aveyron”. Its appeal is obvious. Unlike an ordinary report of a distant point of light, the story developed into a prolonged close encounter narrative tied to a specific farm, family and landscape. Later UFO writers and local enthusiasts treated it as one of France’s most unusual rural cases, and investigators continued revisiting the location decades afterwards. A UFO research group reported meeting the central surviving witness and conducting a renewed visit in September 2020; the case was also the subject of a specialist conference in 2022. [AIREPAN] airepan.com Rencontres ufologiques d'OVNI Languedoc Rencontres ufologiques d'OVNI Languedoc The problem is that the richness of the story exceeds the strength of the surviving evidence. Much of the accessible material comes from UFO organisations, paranormal blogs, podcasts and retrospective retellings rather than a complete contemporary official file. Details differ between summaries, and some extraordinary elements appear more prominently in later versions than in the brief descriptions of the earliest sightings. There is no clearly established chain of physical evidence, authenticated contemporary imagery or publicly documented radar record. Nor is Roumagnac presented in the searchable GEIPAN material as a landmark official category D investigation comparable with the best documented French cases. The absence of such a file does not prove that nothing unusual happened, particularly because the first reports pre dated the creation of France’s official UFO study body in 1977. It does, however, prevent the story from carrying the evidential weight sometimes assigned to it. The most defensible assessment is that Roumagnac is historically important but weakly verifiable . It matters because it influenced Aveyron’s UFO folklore and demonstrates how a repeated family account can grow into a major case. It does not presently provide robust public evidence for the elaborate interpretation built around it. Ordinary explanations behind many Aveyron reports The official files show how frequently unfamiliar viewing conditions turn ordinary phenomena into memorable UFO reports. At Salles la Source on 17 February 2016, a witness saw a luminous point moving rapidly along a descending path for only three to five seconds. GEIPAN matched it to a bright meteor reported by more than 120 witnesses across southern France and classified the case A. The strength of that explanation came not from dismissing the witness but from comparing the report with many observations of the same astronomical event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A report made while travelling on the N88 between Rodez and Albi on 20 February 2009 was likewise classified B as a probable meteor. The distinction from the Salles la Source case reflects the degree of certainty: the meteor explanation was"},{"title":"Millau 1999","url":"/millau-1999/","description":"Millau remains Aveyron's strongest official mystery, but its category D status is not proof of an extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and...","level":2,"text":"Millau 1999 Why Millau 1999 Still Remains Unresolved Aveyron UFOs Millau 1999 Millau 1999 Why Millau 1999 Still Remains Unresolved Millau remains Aveyron's strongest official mystery, but its category D status is not proof of an extraordinary craft. Millau remains Aveyron's strongest official mystery, but its category D status is not proof of an extraordinary craft. event time window What the witness material established What GEIPAN's category D rating means Why the case still falls short of decisive evidence aveyron millau 1999 Introduction The Millau sighting of 16 March 1999 is Aveyron ’s clearest officially unresolved aerial case. At about 8 pm, a witness reported two luminous phenomena crossing the sky very rapidly: a small orange ball and a second light resembling a ball of sparks or flames. The observation lasted only a few seconds and was reportedly silent. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unusual aerospace reports, ultimately placed the case in category D: unidentified after investigation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Le 16 mars 1999 vers 20 heures, un témoin observe le passage très rapide de deux phénomènes aériens constitués d'une petite boule orange... That conclusion makes Millau important, but not because it proves that an extraordinary craft flew over Aveyron. It matters because it survived the ordinary identification process better than most local reports. At the same time, the case rests principally on one brief visual account, without publicly documented photographs, instrument readings or independent witnesses. Millau therefore shows both the value and the limits of an unresolved classification: investigators could not establish a satisfactory conventional explanation, but the surviving evidence is not strong enough to establish what the phenomena actually were. What the witness material established The core report is strikingly simple. The witness saw two luminous phenomena travelling rapidly across the night sky near Millau at around 8 pm. GEIPAN’s summary distinguishes between a small orange ball and a second, less compact phenomenon described as a ball of sparks. Both passed within a matter of seconds, and no accompanying noise was reported. [Geipan] geipan.fr MILLAU (12) 16.03.1999Le 16 mars 1999 vers 20 heures, un témoin observe le passage très rapide de deux phénomènes aériens constitué... Those details give the case more substance than a vague report of a stationary light. There was an apparent movement, a short sequence involving two visually different phenomena and a reported absence of sound. GEIPAN’s database describes the residual phenomenon as strange to very strange and assigns the available information medium to strong consistency. That is why the case appears as category D rather than category C, which is used when information is too weak or incomplete for a meaningful conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr MILLAU (12) 16.03.1999 16/03/1999 D Aveyron Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de consistance moyenne à forte 22/03/2007 1999... Nevertheless, some of the most dramatic sounding features are less secure than they first appear. A witness can reliably describe a light as crossing the visible sky quickly, but cannot normally calculate its true speed without knowing its distance. A small nearby object, a distant aircraft and a much more remote atmospheric phenomenon can all produce rapid angular movement from a particular viewpoint. GEIPAN itself warns that witnesses commonly assign distance, size and velocity to unidentified phenomena even when those quantities cannot be measured from the observation alone. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The silence is similarly relevant but not decisive. A distant phenomenon may be inaudible, aircraft noise may arrive after the light has passed, and a very short observation gives the witness little time to detect or associate a sound with what was seen. Silence weakens some nearby aircraft interpretations, but it does not independently prove that the phenomena lacked an ordinary source. Most importantly, the published case summary identifies a single witness. There is no public indication of a photograph, video, recovered material or simultaneous report from a separate location. GEIPAN regards testimony as central evidence, but its own methodology gives greater weight to cases supported by multiple independent witnesses, precise answers, images or instrument records. Millau’s account was sufficiently coherent to investigate, yet remained dependent on the perception and recollection of one person during an event lasting only seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Why the obvious explanations did not settle it A meteor or atmospheric re entry is an immediate possibility when a witness describes a silent, fast moving ball of orange light accompanied by sparks. Bright meteors can cross the sky within seconds, fragment visibly and show orange, white or other colours. Modern well recorded fireballs demonstrate that such events can leave luminous trails, break into pieces and produce very different descriptions depending on the observer’s position. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor SocietyFireballsThe International Meteor Organization's Fireball Data Center (FIDAC) collects fireball reports from aroun... GEIPAN did not accept that explanation as sufficient for the whole Millau report. The official summary, repeated in contemporary regional coverage, states that the trajectory attributed to one of the two phenomena appeared to rule out an atmospheric re entry. The wording is cautious: it does not declare a natural explanation impossible, but indicates that the reported path did not fit the expected model closely enough for the case to be classified as identified or probably identified. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Montez en soucoupe Rappel des phénomènes qui se sont produits en Aveyron et répertoriés et consignés par le GEIPAN (Groupe d'études et d'informations sur le... That point is central to the unresolved rating, but it also exposes a limitation. Reconstructing a trajectory from a short observation requires dependable estimates of direction, elevation and movement across known reference points. With one observer and no recorded image, even a sincere account may not preserve the geometry accurately enough to distinguish a straight atmospheric path from an apparently changing course. GEIPAN recognises that remembered trajectories can be distorted by viewing conditions, the observer’s own movement and the brain’s reconstruction of a rapidly unfolding event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The proximity of military facilities around the Larzac plateau also prompted checks. Regional reporting based on the GEIPAN file says that enquiries concerning nearby military activity did not identify the phenomenon. The Larzac military camp extends into territory associated with Millau and has long been used for army training, so checking military sources was reasonable. A failure to match the sighting to known activity, however, means only that investigators obtained no confirming identification; it is not evidence that the lights were a secret military vehicle. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Montez en soucoupe Rappel des phénomènes qui se sont produits en Aveyron et répertoriés et consignés par le GEIPAN (Groupe d'études et d'informations sur le... Nor does the public summary establish that every conceivable aircraft, flare, munition, satellite event or atmospheric effect was individually eliminated. Official investigations work with the records, expertise and witness detail available at the time. GEIPAN notes that older cases may have been investigated without the digital astronomical tools, online flight data and improved analytical methods now routinely available. Some cases remain unresolved because no hypothesis explains every reported feature with sufficient confidence, not because every conventional cause has been conclusively disproved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What category D actually means GEIPAN classifies reports using two broad measures: the consistency of the evidence and the amount of residual strangeness left after known explanations have been considered. Consistency depends on the quantity and reliability of the information, including the number and independence of witnesses, the precision of their accounts and the presence of photographs or other records. Residual strangeness reflects how poorly the best conventional hypothesis fits the observation as reported. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Under"},{"title":"Roumagnac","url":"/roumagnac/","description":"The Roumagnac story shaped local UFO folklore, but later retellings are far richer than the surviving contemporary evidence.","breadcrumb":"Aveyron's UFO Cases Between Mystery and...","level":2,"text":"Roumagnac How Roumagnac Became Aveyron's UFO Legend Aveyron UFOs Roumagnac Roumagnac How Roumagnac Became Aveyron's UFO Legend The Roumagnac story shaped local UFO folklore, but later retellings are far richer than the surviving contemporary evidence. The Roumagnac story shaped local UFO folklore, but later retellings are far richer than the surviving contemporary evidence. entity The encounters reported from 1966 onwards How later retellings expanded the story What can and cannot be verified today aveyron roumagnac Introduction Roumagnac became Aveyron ’s defining UFO story not because it produced the department’s strongest surviving evidence, but because a modest series of reported lights grew over decades into an elaborate narrative of pursuit, humanoid occupants, missing time and extraterrestrial contact. The core account concerns the Lortal family at an isolated farm in the hamlet of Roumagnac, near La Fouillade, beginning in June 1966. Family members reportedly watched luminous balls moving close to the ground and apparently returning to a larger, upright object. Further encounters were later attributed chiefly to the family’s 20 year old son, Robert. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr la frenesie des soucoupes volantes 11364718 La frénésie des soucoupes volantes27 Jul 2023 — La plus étrange des histoires fut celle d'un habitant du hameau de Roumagnac, commune de... The difficulty is that the richest versions were recorded or published years later. No publicly accessible photograph, instrument reading, recovered material, medical file or contemporary official investigation establishes what caused the reported phenomena. Roumagnac is therefore best understood as both a UFO case and a case study in legend making: an initially unusual but poorly documented family experience was progressively expanded by specialist magazines, books, illustrated retellings, conferences and the witness’s much later claims. The encounters reported from 1966 onwards The earliest published narrative places the first major episode on the evening of 15 June 1966. According to later summaries of the family testimony, Robert’s grandmother or mother in law alerted the household after seeing what looked like fires on the surrounding hills. Several luminous spheres were then said to move across fields and hedges towards the farm, travelling slowly and at low altitude. Six lights were reportedly watched by members of the household for roughly half an hour, at times within tens of metres of the buildings. One allegedly vanished abruptly rather than fading or moving away. [airepan.com] airepan.com Rencontres ufologiques d'OVNI Languedoc Rencontres ufologiques d'OVNI Languedoc AIREPANNovember 19, 2022 — 19 Nov 2022 — La ferme aveyronnaise de Roumagnac était vouée... Published: November 19, 2022 Accounts subsequently described repeated appearances over the following months. The lights were said to travel close to the ground, follow paths around the property and behave in ways the witnesses interpreted as deliberate. In the version later printed by investigator Georges Metz, the luminous balls sometimes appeared to enter or emerge from a larger vertical object compared with a shell or projectile standing in a field. The object was then said to depart rapidly and silently. [pdfcoffee.com] pdfcoffee.com ns, dirigée maintenant par Joël Mesnard...Read more... This description contains the elements that first distinguished Roumagnac from an ordinary distant light report. The witnesses were not merely describing a point in the sky. They claimed repeated, nearby activity around a familiar farm, apparently observed by several members of one family. The setting also helped the story’s appeal: an isolated old farmhouse, dark fields and witnesses accustomed to the local landscape offered an ideal stage for a narrative in which strange lights seemed to intrude repeatedly into everyday rural life. Yet almost all accessible detail comes through UFO publications rather than a contemporary police record or newspaper investigation. The first substantial treatment identified by later researchers appeared in the specialist magazine Lumières dans la nuit in issues 107, 108 and 109, published in 1970, approximately four years after the initial event. The magazine returned to the affair in 1974 and again in a series published in 2006 and 2007. That publication history is important: it shows that the case entered the record through private UFO investigators, after memories had already had time to develop, rather than through a publicly available file created immediately after the sightings. [AIREPAN] ovni languedoc.com sur les traces des boules de laveyron par Vincent Sep 26... Lumières dans la nuit » elle y a consacré les numéros 107, 108, 109...Read more... Published: September 26, 2020 January 1967 and the pursuit narrative The best known individual episode was attributed to Robert on 11 January 1967. In later retellings, he drove after a white luminous ball in his father’s Peugeot 203 along a country road near the farm. His car reportedly stalled at the top of a rise, after which he saw a larger craft over a field. Some versions describe it as a disc with two transparent domes containing helmeted figures in green clothing. Robert was also said to have felt intense heat or hot air and to have experienced temporary immobility or unusual sleep patterns afterwards. [AIREPAN] ovni languedoc.com sur les traces des boules de laveyron par Vincent Sep 26... Lumières dans la nuit » elle y a consacré les numéros 107, 108, 109...Read more... Published: September 26, 2020 These details are much more dramatic than the original family observation of moving lights, but their documentary foundation is difficult to assess. The surviving online sources largely repeat later reconstructions rather than reproduce a dated statement made by Robert immediately after the event. Claims about paralysis, medical consequences and clearly visible occupants therefore need to be treated as components of the developed narrative, not as independently verified findings. The same caution applies to the exact chronology. Modern accounts often list incidents on several nights in January 1967 and sometimes extend activity to 1969 or even 1972. Other summaries describe the principal phase simply as June 1966 to January 1967. The differences may reflect separate reported episodes, but they also reveal how difficult it is to reconstruct a precise sequence when later publications have become the principal sources. [ovni languedoc.com] ovni languedoc.com AIREPANAveyron ArchivesOn ne présente plus Robert Lortal qui fut de 1966 à 1969 au cœur d'une des grandes énigmes de l'ufologie française... How later retellings expanded the story Roumagnac’s transformation into a legend occurred in stages. The first stage turned a family’s repeated light sightings into the “balls of Aveyron”, a named case with a recognisable visual pattern. Fernand Lagarde, associated with Lumières dans la nuit , was credited with directing the early private investigation. A summary was subsequently included in his 1973 book Mystérieuses soucoupes volantes , while Jacques Vallée discussed the case in The Invisible College . These publications carried Roumagnac beyond its immediate locality and placed it within wider arguments about UFOs that appeared to behave purposefully. [pdfcoffee.com] pdfcoffee.com ns, dirigée maintenant par Joël Mesnard...Read more... A second step came through visual popularisation. The affair was represented in Jacques Lob and Robert Gigi’s 1975 illustrated work OVNI: Dimensions autres . A surviving archive catalogue describes the volume as including the “balls of Aveyron”. Illustration inevitably gave the story a more definite form: vague lights and uncertain shapes could be presented as coherent machines and encounters, even though an artist’s reconstruction is not photographic evidence. [HelloAsso] helloasso.com Hello Asso Librairie du SCEAU/Archives OVNIBD Hello Asso Librairie du SCEAU/Archives OVNIBD The largest expansion came four decades after the original events. Georges Metz’s book Ovnis en France made Robert the central figure in an extended “contactee” story. Metz stated that he had revisited an already known case and was disclosing material supposedly withheld for 40 years. The book presented Robert not simply as a witness to lights and a craft, but as someone selected by non human visitors and involved in continuing contact. [pdfcoffee.com] pdfcoffee.com ns, dirigée maintenant par Joël Mesnard...Read more... In this later account, Robert reportedly said that he had voluntarily spent about a year in a secret underground base in the Himalayas. Stories presented at UFO meetings added medical examinations, reproductive sampling, journeys in advanced craft and instruction about humanity, health and extraterrestrial civilisation. These claims were promoted in talks and specialist gatherings during the 2010s and 2020s, long after"},{"title":"Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion","url":"/why-bas-rhins-ufo-record-still-divides/","description":"Bas-Rhin has a long but uneven UFO history. Reports stretch from the French “flying-saucer” wave of 1954 to smartphone videos submitted in the 2020s, yet the...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion Bas Rhin has a long but uneven UFO history. Reports stretch from the French “flying saucer” wave of 1954 to smartphone videos submitted in the 2020s, yet the... Bas Rhin has a long but uneven UFO history. Reports stretch from the French “flying saucer” wave of 1954 to smartphone videos submitted in the 2020s, yet the department has produced only one widely documented official case that remains genuinely unexplained: the Sélestat incident of 30 October 1994. bas rhin Introduction That pattern matters. Bas Rhin contains Strasbourg Airport and, historically, the military radar complex at Drachenbronn, so unusual lights are often remembered in an aviation or defence setting. The surviving evidence, however, does not establish a repeated radar confirmed phenomenon or a recognised departmental “hotspot”. Instead, it shows how ordinary objects can appear extraordinary at night, how contemporary photographs and videos can assist identification, and how an unresolved classification may reflect missing evidence rather than proof of an exotic craft. The Sélestat triangle remains the key case The most important Bas Rhin UFO report occurred shortly before 2am on 30 October 1994. According to the case summary published by GEIPAN, the French government unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, a couple travelling by car first saw a rotating circle of light with regularly spaced lights around its edge. After leaving Sélestat, they reported encountering the phenomenon again, now apparently joined by a triangular form. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le 30 octobre 1994 vers 01h45, un couple en voiture observe un cercle lumineux tournant sur lui même et sur la périphérie duquel se trouv... The witnesses said the luminous formation remained close to their vehicle over part of their journey. Later summaries describe a triangle apparently following the car for several kilometres before departing rapidly. The case entered the official archive with a classification of D, meaning that it remained unidentified after investigation rather than merely lacking enough information for analysis. GEIPAN described it as a strange or very strange phenomenon supported by evidence of medium to strong consistency. [pokaa.fr] pokaa.fr Open source on pokaa.fr. Several features make Sélestat stronger than the average local report: there were two witnesses travelling together; the observation reportedly developed through several stages rather than lasting only a few seconds; the witnesses described a structured arrangement of lights; the event was reported to the gendarmerie, creating a formal documentary record; GEIPAN did not close the file with a conventional identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le 30 octobre 1994 vers 01h45, un couple en voiture observe un cercle lumineux tournant sur lui même et sur la périphérie duquel se trouv... None of this demonstrates that a physical triangular craft flew alongside the vehicle. The central difficulty is that the publicly accessible case material contains no radar return, independently timed observation, photograph, video or recovered physical trace. Both witnesses were in the same moving car, so their accounts are not fully independent. Darkness, motion, uncertain distance and the absence of fixed scale can also make a distant aircraft or group of lights appear low, large and pace matched with a vehicle. The Sélestat report therefore deserves to be called unresolved, but not confirmed. Its importance rests on the detail and apparent sincerity of the testimony, together with the failure of the original investigation to produce a satisfactory explanation. Its limitations are equally clear: the case depends overwhelmingly on human recollection, and no later evidence has materially strengthened the claim. Drachenbronn: a military connection without radar confirmation Military radar enters Bas Rhin UFO history most clearly through an incident on the night of 11–12 April 1980. Several witnesses travelling around Surbourg, Merkwiller Pechelbronn and Preuschdorf watched a bright coloured light for more than half an hour. Some associated its apparent position with the radar installations of Drachenbronn Air Base, prompting the natural question of whether a mysterious object had approached a sensitive air defence site. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... Drachenbronn was not an incidental landmark. Developed from the 1950s within the former Hochwald fortifications, its detection and control centre monitored air movements across eastern France. The radar site’s prominence above the Alsace plain made it a powerful reference point for witnesses looking towards the north western horizon. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Drachenbronn Air Base Drachenbronn Air Base The incident was initially treated as unexplained, but GEIPAN later revisited it using astronomical calculations, mapped sightlines and accumulated experience from similar cases. The agency found that Venus was exceptionally bright and low in the exact general direction reported. The object’s long duration, apparent stillness whenever the car stopped and apparent movement whenever the car moved all matched a familiar “following light” illusion: a remote object seems to travel with an observer because its direction changes very little against nearer scenery. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN also compared the witnesses’ geographical references. The more precise sightlines towards the Liebfrauenberg area corresponded closely with Venus’s calculated bearing. The planet would have gradually disappeared behind local terrain at roughly the reported time. One witness described a final rapid departure, but this element conflicted with parts of the other testimony and was judged insufficient to overturn the astronomical match. The case is now classified A, meaning identified, with Venus given as the explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... This reassessment weakens claims of a military encounter. No official radar detection is presented in the file, and the witnesses’ references to the base appear to have been attempts to describe direction rather than evidence obtained from military personnel. The case remains valuable because it demonstrates why proximity to radar does not itself constitute radar corroboration. 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube From the 1954 wave to later local reports Bas Rhin participated in the nationwide surge of “flying saucer” stories during autumn 1954. A Strasbourg newspaper report from 6 October recorded telephone calls claiming that a saucer had been seen, or had even landed, near the Orangerie. Another October report described a bright, melon shaped object with a white orange trail over the department. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Ufologie ALSACAT Ufologie ALSACAT These accounts are historically interesting, but evidentially weak. They belong to a period when French newspapers were carrying a remarkable concentration of saucer stories, particularly during October 1954. The Strasbourg material available online is largely retrospective or reproduced by specialist catalogues rather than supported by complete witness files, precise timings, astronomical reconstruction or physical evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 The language used in the contemporary coverage also reflects the media climate. Reports compared supposed objects with melons, cigars, barrels and brushes, showing how witnesses and journalists tried to fit unfamiliar lights into the visual vocabulary of the national flap. Such descriptions should not be treated as technical observations of shape. A bright meteor, aircraft seen at an unusual angle, planet, balloon or even a repeated rumour could acquire a much more structured form during retelling. Local press remains essential for reconstructing this period. Digitised Bas Rhin newspapers are available through national and regional archive portals, including Gallica and Alsatian press collections. These archives can confirm what was printed and when, but newspaper publication alone cannot establish that the reported object behaved as described. [bnf.fr] gallica.bnf.fr Gallica Presse du Bas Rhin Gallica Presse du Bas Rhin There were further Alsatian UFO references in February 1973, later recalled by Strasbourg’s regional press, but the accessible summaries do not provide enough primary documentation to elevate them to the level of the Sélestat or 1980 files. They are best treated"},{"title":"Radar Claims","url":"/radar-claims/","description":"Bas-Rhin's military associations sound dramatic, yet the best-known Drachenbronn report was later matched closely to Venus rather than radar activity.","breadcrumb":"Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion","level":2,"text":"Radar Claims Did Bas Rhin's Radar Sites Ever Confirm a UFO? Bas Rhin UFOs Radar Claims Radar Claims Did Bas Rhin's Radar Sites Ever Confirm a UFO? Bas Rhin's military associations sound dramatic, yet the best known Drachenbronn report was later matched closely to Venus rather than radar activity. Bas Rhin's military associations sound dramatic, yet the best known Drachenbronn report was later matched closely to Venus rather than radar activity. entity How Drachenbronn entered the UFO story Why Venus matched the 1980 sighting The difference between nearby radar and radar evidence bas rhin radar claims Introduction Bas Rhin ’s strongest military themed UFO story is also one of its clearest cautionary examples. On the night of 11–12 April 1980, four witnesses travelling through the area around Surbourg, Merkwiller Pechelbronn and Preuschdorf watched a bright, coloured light for more than half an hour. Several described it as being near, or apparently above, the radar installations of Drachenbronn Air Base. Yet the surviving official file contains no radar detection. When GEIPAN later reconstructed the sightlines, timing and sky conditions, it found a close match with Venus low in the north western sky and reclassified the formerly unexplained case as identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... The episode matters because it separates three ideas that are often blurred together in UFO retellings: seeing something near a military site, believing that a radar should have detected it, and possessing an actual radar record. Only the first applies securely to the 1980 Bas Rhin case. How Drachenbronn entered the story Drachenbronn was not an ordinary airfield. Its detection and control centre occupied underground sections of the Hochwald fortifications, while radar equipment on the ridge monitored air movements across eastern France. The site had no runway or resident aircraft fleet; its purpose was surveillance and air traffic control rather than flying operations. This unusual combination of hilltop antennas, underground command rooms and military secrecy gave the base a powerful place in the local imagination. [DNA] dna.fr Drachenbronn Ex base aérienne 901. « Riesling radar », c’est (presque) fini [Diaporama]... That background influenced how the April 1980 observation was described. Between approximately 11.50pm and 12.30am, witnesses saw a luminous phenomenon while travelling by car and after stopping at different locations. It appeared stationary when they stopped, but seemed to move with them when they drove. Statements recorded by the gendarmerie included references to the Drachenbronn radars, the Liebfrauenberg estate and nearby villages. One witness said the light remained close to the base’s radar installations; another said it appeared to be stationed above Drachenbronn. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... Those phrases make the case sound more precise than it was. GEIPAN’s later examination found that the witnesses did not consistently identify the same landmark. Two placed the light above the Liebfrauenberg, while references to the radar site were looser and sometimes geographically confused. Depending on which landmark was intended, the corresponding directions differed by roughly 20 to 30 degrees. One witness who was not from the area may also have adopted the military reference from the others. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... The important point is not that the witnesses were careless or dishonest. At night, a distant light has no obvious scale or distance, and unfamiliar terrain makes alignment with landmarks difficult. Once someone associates a light with a conspicuous military installation, later descriptions can become organised around that interpretation even when the original line of sight was uncertain. 15:34 Why Science Embraces UAP: The End of Stigma and the Rise of Data Driven Research Channel: UFO to UAP: The Evolution of the Unexplained · Views: 2 · Uploaded: June 2026 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Why Venus matched the 1980 sighting GEIPAN originally inherited the case under the name “Soultz sous Forêts 1980” with a category D classification, meaning it had not been explained at the time. A modern re examination changed the assessment. The official case page, updated in June 2021, now classifies the phenomenon as category A: an identified observation of Venus. Its recorded residual strangeness is extremely low. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... Several features aligned closely with the planet. At 11.50pm, Venus was exceptionally bright, with an apparent magnitude of about –4.4. It lay at an azimuth of approximately 301 degrees and only 6.2 degrees above the horizon. From the more clearly described observation points near Merkwiller and Hattenweg, a sightline towards the Liebfrauenberg also pointed close to Venus’s position. The intervening terrain did not initially conceal the planet. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... The low elevation explains much of the reported appearance. Light from an astronomical object close to the horizon passes through a thicker and less stable section of the atmosphere than light seen overhead. Turbulence and refraction can make a bright point seem to flicker, change colour, jump slightly or momentarily distort. GEIPAN has found the same combination—great brightness, coloured scintillation and apparent movement—in numerous other planetary misidentifications. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le 1er avril 2017 à 22h30 un témoin et deux autres personnes sont intrigués par la présence d'un point très lumineux à faible altitude da... The duration is also significant. An object remained visible for more than 30 minutes in roughly the same sector of a clear, starry sky. That behaviour is more consistent with a slowly setting planet than with an aircraft performing unusual manoeuvres close to a radar station. GEIPAN calculated that local terrain would begin to obscure Venus at about 12.20am. The witnesses placed the disappearance at approximately 12.25am or 12.30am. A difference of five or ten minutes is not surprising given uncertain recollections of time, the witnesses’ changing positions and the uneven horizon. The close agreement strengthened the astronomical explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... There is also a useful negative clue: none of the witnesses separately noticed Venus, despite its exceptional brightness and its presence in the reported direction. If the mysterious light had been another object, Venus should have provided an obvious reference point. Its absence from the accounts suggests that Venus was the light they were describing. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube The light that seemed to follow the car One of the most memorable parts of the story was the impression that the phenomenon moved alongside the witnesses. It reportedly remained fixed when the car stopped, resumed moving when the car set off and appeared to pass landmarks or follow bends in the road. GEIPAN identifies this as the “following light” illusion. A planet is so distant that its direction changes almost imperceptibly during an ordinary car journey. Nearby trees, houses and hills, by contrast, sweep rapidly across the observer’s field of view. The stationary distant light can therefore seem to keep pace with the vehicle while the foreground landscape moves around it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... Once the observers assumed that the light was nearby, the illusion became more convincing. A fixed point beyond a church could appear to “go around” the building as the road changed direction. At a junction, stopping the car would also stop the apparent motion. The result could resemble an intelligently controlled object matching the vehicle’s speed, even though no movement by the light itself was required. This mechanism does not explain every reported detail equally well. One witness described two discs joined by a line and said the object crossed the road before accelerating towards Lobsann. Another reported a rapid departure with a yellow trail. GEIPAN treated these claims cautiously because they were not shared consistently by the other witnesses and conflicted with parts of the same witness’s account. The reported final “escape” may also have developed from fading, atmospheric distortion or the light’s disappearance behind terrain. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... Discarding an isolated detail simply because it does not fit would be poor practice. In this case, however, the disputed movements came from one or two accounts while"},{"title":"Sélestat Case","url":"/sélestat-case/","description":"The 1994 Sélestat encounter remains Bas-Rhin's strongest unexplained case, but its reputation rests mainly on two witnesses and no physical proof.","breadcrumb":"Why Bas Rhin's UFO Record Still Divides Opinion","level":2,"text":"Sélestat Case Why the Sélestat Triangle Remains Unresolved Bas Rhin UFOs Sélestat Case Sélestat Case Why the Sélestat Triangle Remains Unresolved The 1994 Sélestat encounter remains Bas Rhin's strongest unexplained case, but its reputation rests mainly on two witnesses and no physical proof. The 1994 Sélestat encounter remains Bas Rhin's strongest unexplained case, but its reputation rests mainly on two witnesses and no physical proof. event time window What the witnesses reported that night Why GEIPAN left the case unidentified What the missing evidence means bas rhin Introduction The Sélestat incident of 30 October 1994 remains the most substantial unresolved UFO case in Bas Rhin because it combines a long, close range report, two named in the file witnesses, prompt gendarmerie statements and an official GEIPAN classification of D: unidentified after investigation. Yet the same records that make the case memorable also expose its limits. There is no photograph, physical trace or confirmed radar return, and nearly every extraordinary detail depends on observations made from one moving car in darkness and poor weather. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSELESTAT (67) 30.10.1994 GEIPAN... The central question is therefore not whether the witnesses described something strange. They plainly did. It is whether the available evidence can establish what caused the experience. More than three decades later, the answer remains no. The incident deserves its unresolved status, but it does not provide proof of an unknown craft. What the witnesses reported that night At about 1.45am, a couple were driving away from friends when they noticed a luminous formation above the built up area. Their statements describe an initial circular ring composed of regularly spaced, bright rectangular lights. One witness compared them to white green halogen lamps and estimated the formation to be only several metres above the ground. The other placed it somewhat higher, at roughly 15 to 20 metres. Both described rotating lights and an apparently geometrical arrangement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The first sighting was brief. According to the woman’s statement, the ring was visible for only four or five seconds before disappearing behind rooftops as the car moved on. After the couple left the settlement, however, they said the phenomenon reappeared over open ground to the right of their vehicle. What had initially looked like a circle now appeared incorporated into, or surrounded by, an equilateral triangle edged with similar lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The driver estimated the triangle at about ten metres across and said it remained roughly 100 metres to the right of the car, at an altitude of 10 to 15 metres. He reported changing speed to test whether it was following: when he accelerated, it appeared to accelerate; when he slowed, it remained alongside. The woman likewise said it repeatedly returned to the same relative position and seemed not to overtake them. The car was travelling at about 80 kilometres per hour during part of the encounter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The couple described further changes as they continued through several localities. At one stage the formation reportedly moved over nearby houses while keeping the same brightness. Later, near a slip road, it appeared in front of the car, perhaps 50 metres away and tens of metres above the ground. The driver flashed his headlights. He then described the object rocking or “flapping”, rotating several times, changing from a triangle into something resembling a boomerang and departing rapidly over the fields. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. No sound was heard. The windows remained closed, but both witnesses insisted that there was no audible engine noise or external disturbance. They reported no failure of the car, radio or lights, and the gendarmerie recorded no physical effect on the vehicle or surrounding environment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Why the testimony carries weight The strongest part of the Sélestat case is not any single estimated measurement. It is the broad agreement between the two statements about the sequence: a luminous ring over the settlement, a triangular formation beside the car, lights around its perimeter, apparent matching of the vehicle’s speed, silence and a final rapid departure. The witnesses were questioned separately in formal gendarmerie proceedings on the afternoon of the event. Their accounts were therefore recorded within hours rather than reconstructed years later. The file also includes a drawing showing a rounded triangular form with a circular illuminated centre and arrows indicating rotation. That documentation gives the case considerably more evidential value than an anonymous anecdote or a late retelling in UFO literature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Neither statement reads like a simple claim of seeing “a triangle”. Both contain changes in position, brightness and apparent form. The woman distinguished the fleeting first observation from the longer roadside encounter and acknowledged turning around repeatedly to maintain sight of it. The driver described attempts to test the phenomenon by changing speed and flashing his headlights. Such details make the testimony internally rich, even though detail alone cannot guarantee accuracy. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The statements also include potentially credibility enhancing qualifications. The witnesses did not report occupants, messages, bodily effects, missing time or damage. The woman said she was curious rather than frightened and explained that her interest in drawing might account for her close attention to shape. Both denied that the display resembled lasers they had previously seen. These points do not prove the observation was objectively accurate, but they make it difficult to dismiss as a highly embellished paranormal narrative. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The problem with the “second couple” The current GEIPAN summary contains an important claim: it says that another couple, travelling in a different car during the same period and on the same road, described a remarkably similar phenomenon. That wording has been repeated in later local reporting and greatly strengthens the popular version of the case, because four witnesses in two vehicles would provide partial independent corroboration. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSELESTAT (67) 30.10.1994 GEIPAN... The attached primary documents do not clearly support that reading. The two published witness files correspond to the man and woman in the same car. Their statements refer to one another as partners travelling together, and the gendarmerie’s closing report states that, as of 20 December 1994, these two were the only people who had reported the phenomenon to the unit. Investigators had contacted residents and local brigades without obtaining additional witnesses. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The couple did mention passing or meeting other vehicles, but none of their drivers is documented as seeing the formation. The woman recalled that they crossed a car during a gap in the observation. The man said they encountered about ten vehicles during the journey, but none stopped or later came forward in the published investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This discrepancy matters. It does not invalidate the original couple’s testimony, but it means the publicly available evidence should not be described confidently as two independent couples unless further documentation emerges. On the records currently attached to the case, the safest assessment is that there were two witnesses sharing one vehicle, not four witnesses in two vehicles. 26:16 Ep. 3 Black Triangle UFOs Phoenix Lights, Hudson Valley, Belgium The Basement Office Channel: New York Post · Views: 845.6K · Uploaded: June 2019 · Length: 26 minutes Open on YouTube Why GEIPAN left the case unidentified GEIPAN classifies a case as D when no satisfactory explanation remains after investigation and the available information is considered sufficiently consistent for analysis. Its method separates “strangeness” — how difficult the reported features are to match with known phenomena — from “consistency”, meaning the quantity and assessed reliability of the data. The Sélestat page describes the event as strange to very strange, with medium to strong consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSELESTAT (67) 30.10.1994 GEIPAN... The gendarmerie did attempt several conventional checks. Investigators contacted a military airbase operations room. The response recorded in the file was that no corresponding object had been detected by its radar. They also questioned the manager of a nearby nightclub, who said its show lasers did not"},{"title":"Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports","url":"/why-bouches-du-rhone-produces-so-many/","description":"Bouches-du-Rhône has one of France’s richest public records of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but that does not make it a proven paranormal hotspot.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports Bouches du Rhône has one of France’s richest public records of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but that does not make it a proven paranormal hotspot. Bouches du Rhône has one of France’s richest public records of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but that does not make it a proven paranormal hotspot. The department combines a large population, clear Mediterranean skies, dense air traffic, military aviation, ports, industrial sites and extensive horizons over sea and wetlands. bouches du rhone Introduction The official record is mixed. Cases from Marseille, Martigues , Istres, Aix en Provence and smaller communes include misidentified planets, the Moon, aircraft, meteors, lanterns, birds and reflections. Others remain unresolved because the available testimony is reasonably strong or, more commonly, because too little reliable information survives. Two older cases — Martigues in 1998 and La Fare les Oliviers in 2001 — retain GEIPAN’s unexplained classification, but neither amounts to evidence of an extraterrestrial craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Why the department produces so many reports Bouches du Rhône contains several very different observing environments within a relatively small area. Marseille offers millions of possible viewing opportunities from flats, hills and coastal roads. The Étang de Berre area lies among Marseille Provence Airport, industrial complexes and heavily used aviation corridors. Farther west, the flat Crau landscape and Camargue margins provide unusually open views, while the hills around Aix, Aubagne and Marseille can obscure the lower part of the sky and make rising celestial objects appear to emerge from the ground. Military aviation adds another layer. Air Base 701 at Salon de Provence has operated since 1937 and hosts major flying and training activities. Istres also has a long military aviation association. These connections make aircraft explanations locally important, but the mere presence of airbases does not prove a military link to any particular sighting. GEIPAN can request radar information through French air defence arrangements when an investigation warrants it; its general method also draws on police, gendarmerie, civil aviation, meteorological and scientific expertise. [Ministère des Armées] defense.gouv.fr base aerienne 701 salon provence Ministère des ArméesBase aérienne 701 Salon de ProvenceImplantée sur le territoire salonais depuis 1937, la base aérienne 701 « Général P... A historical claim that Bouches du Rhône was France’s leading department for UFO reports circulated widely in 2011. At that time, a press summary stated that GEIPAN had recorded 40 cases in the department since 1977, including 11 then lacking a plausible explanation. Those figures are useful as a snapshot, not as a permanent ranking: the database changes when new reports are added and old cases are re examined. [Linternaute.com] linternaute.com 1011037 bouches du rhone 1er. Les Bouches du Rhône, paradis des Ovnis?3 Nov 2011 — Les Bouches du Rhône sont le département où l'on observe le plus d'Objets vola... National statistical work also cautions against treating raw sighting totals as evidence of unusual activity. An analysis of French reports found that population density and several environmental or land use variables affected the geographical distribution of cases. In practical terms, more observers, more infrastructure and more visible aerial activity can create more reports without requiring more genuinely anomalous objects. [arXiv] arxiv.org Spatial Point Pattern Analysis of the Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in FranceSeptember 2, 2015... Published: September 2, 2015 Marseille’s red “half sphere” became a lesson in reconstruction One of the department’s most instructive cases occurred in Marseille shortly after 1 a.m. on 31 July 1994. Two witnesses reported a large red half sphere above hills east of the city. They said it turned yellow, appeared to shrink and finally disappeared as a thin line of light. The observation sounded striking enough to remain initially unclassified through lack of information. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 31.07.1994 GEIPAN... Eighteen years later, investigators returned to the location at the corresponding time and reconstructed the sight line. The result supported a much less exotic explanation: the rising Moon, reddened and distorted near the horizon. GEIPAN reclassified the case as category A, meaning the phenomenon was identified with high confidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 31.07.1994 GEIPAN... The case matters because it shows how an honest and detailed report can still be misleading. Near the horizon, atmospheric absorption can turn the Moon deep red or orange. Hills may conceal its lower portion, creating a dome or half sphere. Thin cloud and changing visibility can produce apparent contraction, deformation or disappearance. Without a recognisable lunar outline, witnesses may judge its size and distance incorrectly. It also shows the value of delayed reinvestigation. Later reporting weakened rather than strengthened the original mystery, not because the witnesses were dismissed, but because the geometry could finally be tested. The episode is a better example of successful case reconstruction than of an unexplained encounter. The 2009 Marseille cluster shows how ordinary causes combine Several Marseille area reports from 2009 illustrate why groups of sightings can resemble a local “flap” even when they do not share one cause. GEIPAN’s database for that year includes cases attributed to reflections, the Moon, a meteor, a probable lantern and a photographic bird artefact, alongside reports left unclassified because of insufficient information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... A particularly useful file began with observations from a ninth floor kitchen on 4 and 8 February. A witness described red orange luminous forms moving or changing position. For the main observation, investigators found that the object appeared in precisely the direction of the setting Moon, partly hidden by cloud. GEIPAN classified it as a definite lunar misidentification. Another light moving with the wind was considered compatible with a festive sky lantern, although the information was not strong enough for the same level of certainty. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 04.02.2009 GEIPAN... This mixed explanation is important. Investigators do not have to find a single cause for every detail in a witness’s account. A person watching the sky over several nights may see the Moon, an aircraft and a lantern, then mentally group the experiences because they share a colour or emotional effect. Other department records from the same period reinforce the pattern: An Istres sighting on 25 January 2009 was identified as Venus. A Marseille event on 30 March was attributed to a meteor. A Marseille photograph from September was classified as a bird artefact. An Aix en Provence report in July 2010 was also identified as Venus. An Istres observation in August 2012 was identified as the International Space Station. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The cumulative lesson is not that every witness “just saw a light”. It is that bright astronomical objects and familiar aerial traffic can look profoundly unfamiliar when seen through cloud, near the horizon, without scale or from a moving viewpoint. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Martigues and the Étang de Berre remain the strongest local hotspot Martigues appears repeatedly in the historical database and has gained a reputation as a French UFO hotspot. The surrounding geography helps explain why. The commune sits between the Mediterranean and the Étang de Berre, close to industrial installations, ports, airports and civil and military flight paths. Lights can be viewed across water at great distance, with little information about their size, speed or altitude. Many Martigues area files are not unresolved. Entries from 1980 and other years were classified as identified or as lacking reliable information. The database therefore does not support the idea that every local report represents the same continuing phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The most notable exception is the Martigues case of 27 September 1998. GEIPAN retains it as category D, describing it as strange to very strange and of medium to strong consistency. That makes it one of the department’s better official unresolved cases. The public index, however, supplies much"},{"title":"Marseille Cases","url":"/marseille-cases/","description":"Marseille's best-known reports show how the Moon, meteors, lanterns, birds and reflections can create convincing but ordinary UFO experiences.","breadcrumb":"Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports","level":2,"text":"Marseille Cases How Marseille's Strangest UFO Reports Were Explained Bouches du Rhone Marseille Cases Marseille Cases How Marseille's Strangest UFO Reports Were Explained Marseille's best known reports show how the Moon, meteors, lanterns, birds and reflections can create convincing but ordinary UFO experiences. Marseille's best known reports show how the Moon, meteors, lanterns, birds and reflections can create convincing but ordinary UFO experiences. event time window The red half sphere case of 1994 The mixed cluster of sightings in 2009 Why reconstruction can overturn a mystery bouches du rhone marseille cases Introduction Marseille’s most instructive UFO reports are not the ones that resisted explanation, but those that initially sounded extraordinary and were later matched to ordinary objects. A red “half sphere” above the eastern hills became a rising Moon; a glowing form seen through broken cloud was the setting Moon; a luminous disc in a photograph was a kitchen light reflection; and other reports from 2009 were attributed, with varying confidence, to a meteor, lanterns and a bird caught by a camera. These cases matter within the UFO history of Bouches du Rhône because they show what identification actually requires. Investigators did not simply attach familiar labels to unfamiliar stories. They compared the reported time, direction, angular movement, weather, horizon and photographic conditions with objects that should have been present. The results also demonstrate an important distinction: some Marseille cases are firmly explained, while others merely have a plausible conventional hypothesis because the original evidence is too limited. France Just Changed the Game on UAP Investigation Channel: Disclosure Status Open on YouTube The red half sphere of 1994 Shortly after 1 a.m. on 31 July 1994, two witnesses in Marseille saw something striking above the hills east of the city. They described a red half sphere, apparently about twice the size of the Moon, which became yellow, seemed to shrink and finally disappeared as a thin horizontal line of light. The witnesses contacted the gendarmerie, made statements that day and left investigators with a vivid report but no photograph, radar record or independent group of observers. The case was therefore originally placed in the category used for reports lacking enough information. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 31.07.1994 GEIPAN... The eventual explanation came eighteen years later. In 2012, an internet correspondent noticed that the Moon’s position could be recreated after approximately one saros, an astronomical cycle of about 18 years after which the geometry of the Sun, Earth and Moon becomes broadly similar. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation group, arranged for an investigator to return to the observation site at the corresponding time. The original witness no longer lived there, but identified the former flat and viewing direction with enough precision for the scene to be reconstructed. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete55 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... At about 1 a.m. on 10 August 2012, the observers saw the Moon rise in the direction given by the witness, initially hidden by a house and the distant terrain. It appeared yellow orange, moved towards the right and seemed to decrease slightly in size as it climbed. Astronomical calculations placed the 1994 Moon at an altitude of roughly 5.2 degrees and an azimuth of 73.3 degrees at 1.15 a.m.—closely matching the reported time and easterly line of sight. The reconstruction’s map also showed that the 1994 and 2012 lunar directions differed only slightly from the witness’s position. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete55 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The surrounding hills supplied the final part of the illusion. From one nearby position, only the upper portion of the Moon was initially visible above a ridge. As it passed behind a lower gap in the terrain, more of its lower edge appeared, creating the confusing impression that the light was descending or changing shape rather than rising normally. A roofline in front of the original flat could have produced a similar masking effect. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete55 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... GEIPAN acknowledged three imperfect matches. The witnesses remembered a deeper red than the yellow orange Moon seen in 2012; the final thin line was not reproduced exactly; and the witness later recalled a full Moon although the original gendarmerie account referred to a half sphere. None was considered strong enough to overturn the identification. A low Moon can appear orange or red because its light passes through a longer stretch of atmosphere, which scatters more blue light; dust and pollution can deepen the colour. Thin cloud near the horizon could also reduce a partly obscured disc to a narrow strip, even when the sky overhead appears clear. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete55 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The apparent size was not a decisive objection either. The Moon does not suddenly double in angular diameter at the horizon, but observers commonly perceive it as much larger when it is framed by buildings, hills or other foreground features. The mechanism of this “Moon illusion” is still debated, but the perceptual effect itself is well established. In 2012, GEIPAN reclassified the Marseille report as category A: an identified case caused by the Moon at rising. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Dust or pollution can also deepen the reddish color.) Why do we see NASA ScienceThe Moon Illusion: Why Does the Moon Look So Big...This happens because the Moon's light travels a longer distance through t... Marseille’s mixed cluster of 2009 The Marseille records from 2009 are sometimes more useful when read together than separately. They do not describe one coherent wave or a repeated craft. Instead, they form a compact portfolio of unrelated reports generated by celestial objects, airborne lights and photographic effects. The official database lists Marseille cases that year as a reflection, the Moon, a probable meteor, possible lanterns and a probable bird. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... 11 January: a luminous disc that existed only in the photograph. A resident photographed snow covered Marseille between 4 and 5 p.m. and later noticed a bright circular object in the image. She had seen no such object while taking the picture. GEIPAN identified it as the reflection of a fluorescent kitchen light in the window and classified the case A. The absence of a direct visual observation was crucial: the anomaly belonged to the photographic setup, not to the sky outside. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARSEILLE (13) 11.01.2009 GEIPAN... 4 and 8 February: separate observations with different likely causes. A witness looking from a ninth floor kitchen reported reddish orange luminous forms on two nights. The first moved across the field of view in the direction of the wind and was considered possibly consistent with a flying lantern, although the information was insufficient for a firm conclusion. During the principal observation on 8 February, the witness and her son saw a red orange sphere appear to rise and descend. GEIPAN calculated that it lay exactly in the direction of the setting Moon, partly hidden by clouds, and classified that part of the report A. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 04.02.2009 GEIPAN... That case illustrates why a witness can describe real movement even when the source is astronomical. Passing cloud can reveal and conceal different portions of a lunar disc, while gaps with sloping edges can make it appear to climb, drop, deform or change brightness. The Moon’s colour near the horizon can also shift towards orange and red. The reported behaviour therefore need not have been invented or carelessly described; it can have been a sincere account of a misleading view. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 04.02.2009 GEIPAN... 30 March: a brief luminous object judged probably meteoric. GEIPAN’s case index classifies this Marseille observation as category B, meaning probably identified, with a meteoroid as the proposed cause. A meteor can produce an intense coloured streak lasting only seconds, and a brighter object may illuminate the landscape or appear much nearer than its true altitude. The lower confidence rating indicates that the fit was considered persuasive rather than demonstrated beyond reasonable doubt. [Geipan] geipan.fr MARSEILLE (13) 30.03.2009 GEIPANMarch 30, 2009 — MARSEILLE (13) 30.03.2009... MARSEILLE (13) 30.03.2009... Published: March 30, 2009 12 June: the silent black triangle that remained under documented. At 11.10 p.m., one witness reported star like lights outlining a large dark triangular mass moving slowly north to south towards the sea. No sound was heard and no"},{"title":"Martigues","url":"/martigues/","description":"Martigues has one of the department's strongest UFO reputations, but aviation, industry and long views across water complicate every claim.","breadcrumb":"Why Bouches du Rhone Produces So Many UFO Reports","level":2,"text":"Martigues Is Martigues Really a French UFO Hotspot? Bouches du Rhone Martigues Martigues Is Martigues Really a French UFO Hotspot? Martigues has one of the department's strongest UFO reputations, but aviation, industry and long views across water complicate every claim. Martigues has one of the department's strongest UFO reputations, but aviation, industry and long views across water complicate every claim. population context The sightings that built Martigues' reputation How water, industry and flight paths distort perception What remains unresolved and what does not bouches du rhone martigues Introduction Martigues deserves its reputation as one of the most frequently reported UFO locations in Bouches du Rhône, but not as a place where the evidence points clearly to extraordinary craft. The public GEIPAN record contains six Martigues cases between 1980 and 2021. Only the September 1998 sighting remains officially unexplained; the others were attributed, or considered plausibly attributable, to stars, a weather balloon, lanterns or an inadequately documented natural phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARTIGUES (13) 27.09.1998 GEIPAN... The location nevertheless matters. Martigues occupies a visually complicated meeting point between the Mediterranean, the Étang de Berre, the Canal de Caronte and a heavily industrialised landscape. Observers can look across long, dark stretches of water towards aircraft, refinery lights, port activity, hills and distant settlements. Marseille Provence Airport lies on the opposite side of the lagoon, while the large military air base at Istres operates to the north west. The result is an unusually fertile setting for sincere but difficult to interpret reports. [Ministère des Armées] defense.gouv.fr nouvelle convention entre base aerienne 125 mairie distres Ministère des ArméesNouvelle convention entre la base aérienne 125 et...14 Aug 2024 — Succédant à l'école d'aviation créée en 1917, la B... The sightings that built Martigues’ reputation Martigues’ “hotspot” status comes principally from repetition. Reports entered the official archive in 1980, 1982, 1994, 1998, 2018 and 2021. That is a notable concentration for one commune, but the pattern is less mysterious when the individual cases are examined. Four have identified or probable explanations, one lacks enough reliable information, and one remains unexplained. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr Carte Ovni.fr Observations OVNI à Martigues — Carte Ovni.fr Observations OVNI à Martigues — CarteOvni.fr... The cases also do not form a consistent physical pattern. They include points of light, flashes, a luminous circular form and two red objects. They occurred in different seasons and over more than four decades. There is no recurring description of a particular craft, direction of travel, landing site or physical effect. What repeats is the observing environment: darkness, distant lights, restricted visual references and uncertainty about range. The 1980 light was probably a bright star On 15 September 1980, several people watched a bright point from their home between about 7.40 p.m. and 9.30 p.m. It appeared larger than an ordinary star, varied in colour and intensity, and was eventually lost towards the north west. Investigators concluded that the witnesses had probably been looking at Arcturus, one of the brightest stars in the night sky and well placed for observation at that time of year. The case is classified as probably identified rather than conclusively solved. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 1980 0900803 Observation OVNI à Martigues — septembre 1980 — CarteOvni.fr... This is an important early example because changing colour and brightness often sound more mechanical than astronomical in witness accounts. Near the horizon, however, turbulence and refraction can make a bright star flash through different colours. Without nearby objects providing scale, the observer may also perceive a stationary light as hovering nearby rather than lying at an effectively infinite distance. The 1982 flashes were weakened by poor viewing conditions The January 1982 incident originally carried more weight. Two witnesses, observing through partly opened bedroom shutters between approximately 5.45 and 6.15 a.m., reported repeated appearances of an intense white light with a green halo. A third sighting, known only through another witness’s account, described a bright bluish half sphere at about 7 a.m. The phenomenon was said to be silent and close to the motorway bridge. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 1982 0100911 Observation OVNI à Martigues — janvier 1982 — CarteOvni.fr... A later GEIPAN review substantially weakened the case. The direct witnesses saw only brief flashes through a narrow opening while still in bed. When one looked from the balcony, the light was no longer present. Capella, a bright star low in the north west, was considered possible because atmospheric turbulence can produce colour changes, although the witnesses’ restricted sight line made that fit uncertain. Ball lightning was also considered because of the reported rain, short duration, intense luminosity and coloured halo. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 1982 0100911 Observation OVNI à Martigues — janvier 1982 — CarteOvni.fr... Neither explanation could be established. GEIPAN therefore moved the report from the unexplained category to classification C, meaning that reliable information was insufficient. That change is crucial: the case was not “debunked” in the sense that one answer was proved, but it ceased to count as strong unexplained evidence because the original observations could not support a firm reconstruction. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 1982 0100911 Observation OVNI à Martigues — janvier 1982 — CarteOvni.fr... The 1994 object was probably a weather balloon At about 3 a.m. on 29 July 1994, an insomniac witness saw a luminous round shape with flashes around its edge from a bedroom window. After a short investigation, the official assessment favoured a weather balloon. The case remains classified as probably identified because the surviving evidence did not permit absolute confirmation. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 1994 0701362 Observation OVNI à Martigues — juillet 1994 — CarteOvni.fr... A balloon can appear much stranger than expected at night. Its envelope may catch sunlight while the observer below is in darkness, and attached equipment can produce irregular glints. With no clear information about distance, size or wind, a slowly drifting balloon may seem stationary or controlled. 6:17 UFO hoax fools several French media • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 9.0K · Uploaded: November 2021 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube The unresolved 1998 sighting remains the strongest case The central Martigues case occurred at about 3 a.m. on 27 September 1998. One witness described a round, yellowish luminous object against a hillside. It moved very slowly for roughly five minutes, then appeared to leave at great speed and disappear. GEIPAN classifies it as D: strange or highly strange, with evidence considered of medium to strong consistency and no explanation established. The file was most recently updated in June 2021. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARTIGUES (13) 27.09.1998 GEIPAN... This report is more difficult to dismiss than the clearly astronomical cases because the witness perceived a distinct change from slow movement to rapid departure. A hillside could also have provided more visual reference than an empty sky. Nevertheless, the evidential base is narrow: there was one recorded witness, no photograph, no video, no reported radar confirmation and no independent observation preserved in the public summary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARTIGUES (13) 27.09.1998 GEIPAN... The apparent acceleration is therefore unresolved rather than verified. A distant light becoming obscured by terrain or cloud can appear to vanish suddenly. Movement by the observer, shifting atmospheric conditions or an incorrect assumption about the light’s distance can also exaggerate perceived speed. None of those possibilities has been demonstrated in this case, but the available material is not sufficient to establish an extraordinary manoeuvre either. Later reports were more readily explained In December 2018, a motorist saw two vivid red lights in a cloudy southern sky. After reaching a destination, the witness photographed and filmed them as they appeared to rise on the same path and disappear. GEIPAN considered Thai style sky lanterns the probable explanation: their colour, paired movement, duration and progressive disappearance were compatible with objects drifting together in the wind. Conflicting weather readings from nearby stations prevented complete confirmation. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr martigues 2018 1250667 martigues 2018 1250667 The August 2021 case shows an even clearer perceptual mechanism. Two people watched a faint point"},{"title":"Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation","url":"/calvados-ufos-between-legend-and/","description":"Calvados has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single well-documented mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation Calvados has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single well documented mystery. Calvados has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single well documented mystery. calvados Introduction The most famous local story is a reported 1973 encounter involving an army helicopter approaching Caen Carpiquet, allegedly supported by radar. It sounds impressive, yet the publicly available record rests mainly on a pilot’s later recollection rather than contemporary radar plots, control room statements or an accessible military report. By contrast, recent cases investigated by the French space agency’s GEIPAN have produced detailed files and mostly ordinary explanations. The central lesson from Calvados is therefore not that nothing unusual has been reported, but that the strongest sounding story is poorly documented while the best documented stories are largely explained. The 1973 Caen Carpiquet encounter A former French Army Light Aviation pilot, Daniel Michau, has described an incident said to have occurred in 1973 while he was flying an Alouette II helicopter from Rennes Saint Jacques towards Caen. According to his later account, he and another man saw a huge, elongated object pass extremely quickly near the aircraft at about 11 in the morning. Michau said that, after he called Caen Carpiquet, the control tower told him that radar had detected an exceptionally fast target moving across the area. [forum sceptique.com] forum sceptique.com L'OVNI Français du pilote militaire Daniel MICHAU Forum Sceptique... The aviation setting makes this Calvados’s most compelling UFO narrative. Michau was not describing an indistinct light seen from a bedroom window, but an apparent close encounter during a military flight, followed by what he remembered as radar confirmation. Versions circulating online have added estimates of roughly 8,000 to 12,000 kilometres per hour and claims that two radar operators witnessed the track. [Webastro] webastro.net Donc à Mach 8 cet OVNI Webastro[OVNIs/PANs] Paroles de pilotes et d'officiels Page 412 Sept 2010 — 4 témoins: les deux opérateurs radar de la base militaire... Those additions are also where the evidential problems begin. No publicly accessible contemporary radar trace, air traffic log, military incident report or signed statement from either radar operator has emerged in the sources reviewed. The precise speed range appears in later UFO discussions rather than in an authenticated operational document. Sceptical commentary has also noted the physical tension in the account: an object passing nearby at several times the speed of sound should normally produce conspicuous aerodynamic effects, yet the story describes neither a shock wave nor disturbance to the helicopter. [forum sceptique.com] forum sceptique.com L'OVNI Français du pilote militaire Daniel MICHAU Forum Sceptique... This does not prove that Michau invented the experience. A trained pilot may sincerely recall seeing something startling, and radar operators can detect tracks that are difficult to interpret in real time. It does mean that “pilot plus radar” should not be treated as established fact in the same way as a preserved multi sensor case file. After several decades, memory can also preserve the core of an event while altering dimensions, timing and dialogue. The fairest classification is therefore historically important but weakly documented . It is the leading aviation case associated with Calvados, but its reputation is stronger than its surviving evidence. The 1954 wave reached rural Calvados Calvados also appears in catalogues of France’s great autumn 1954 flying saucer wave. That national episode generated an extraordinary volume of newspaper reports, especially during October and November, at a time when “saucers”, cigar shaped craft and alleged occupants were major public and press themes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Local entries include reports associated with Creully, Longues sur Mer, Clarbec and Laize la Ville. A specialist catalogue lists a November sighting at Clarbec involving a nocturnal light or aerial object, while explicitly leaving open an ordinary helicopter explanation. The same catalogue describes the Laize la Ville item as supported by wholly insufficient information. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap sightings catalogue... The Creully story is more colourful. In a retelling derived from period reporting, a resident going to fetch coal in the early morning supposedly encountered an extraordinary scene later framed as a possible landing or visitation. The surviving account is vivid, but it lacks the kind of documentation now expected in a serious investigation: a clearly identified primary witness file, measured traces, reliable photographs, independent interviews and prompt technical checks. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, Longues sur Mer, Calvados... These cases matter because they show that Calvados participated in the cultural atmosphere of 1954. They do not establish a concentrated local landing wave. Many entries survive through newspaper summaries or later catalogues, sometimes with inconsistent locations, dates and interpretations. Their historical value is stronger than their scientific value. Researchers wishing to reconstruct this period face a practical archival problem. The Calvados departmental archives hold extensive newspaper and periodical collections, while the National Library of France’s old local press portal identifies hundreds of historical newspaper holdings connected with the department. Much relevant material is therefore potentially recoverable, but it requires title by title and date by date checking rather than reliance on modern UFO lists. [calvados.fr] archives.calvados.fr Presse et revuesLes collections de presse et de revues collectées et conservées par les Archives du Calvados comptent plus de 3 800 titre... What the official Calvados cases reveal France has an unusually transparent official system for civilian reports. GEIPAN, a unit of the National Centre for Space Studies, collects witness testimony, seeks supporting records and publishes many files. Reports may also begin with statements made to the gendarmerie, which has a formal procedure for recording unidentified aerial phenomena. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. The categories are often misunderstood. A report may be classified as identified, probably identified, unusable because information is insufficient, or unexplained after investigation. “Unidentified” does not mean extraterrestrial, and a weak file is not automatically a strong mystery. GEIPAN itself emphasises that witnesses can experience something genuinely extraordinary while the eventual cause remains ordinary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The published Calvados material contains many examples of this difference. Saint Gatien des Bois, 27 November 2011: several family members reported a stationary luminous phenomenon near the village church. The case was later classified B, meaning probably identified, as an illuminated decorative “sky rose” or projected light effect. The multiple witnesses made the observation real as an experience, but did not make the initial interpretation correct. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Saint Gatien des Bois, 11 December 2013: a school pupil travelling inside a moving bus saw two small dark balls joined by a bar. GEIPAN found the apparent object extremely small and the circumstances poor for measurement or corroboration. It classified the report C because there was too little reliable information to investigate meaningfully. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Honfleur towards Blonville sur Mer, 20 August 2000: a reported aerial light was ultimately classified A and attributed to a laser. This coastal setting is significant because entertainment and advertising lights can be projected over long distances and appear detached from their source, particularly in humid or cloudy air. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Trouville sur Mer, 11 January 2014: another case received an A classification, meaning identified. GEIPAN’s case index records it among the department’s resolved reports. A March 2013 Trouville observation was likewise attributed to an aircraft condensation trail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Caen, 19 October 2021: an apparently unusual object was classified A as debris. The case illustrates how an observation can enter the UFO"},{"title":"1973 Encounter","url":"/1973-encounter/","description":"A pilot's dramatic 1973 account remains Calvados's best-known UFO story, but the missing contemporary records sharply limit what can be verified.","breadcrumb":"Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation","level":2,"text":"1973 Encounter Did Radar Confirm the Caen Carpiquet UFO? Calvados UFOs 1973 Encounter 1973 Encounter Did Radar Confirm the Caen Carpiquet UFO? A pilot's dramatic 1973 account remains Calvados's best known UFO story, but the missing contemporary records sharply limit what can be verified. A pilot's dramatic 1973 account remains Calvados's best known UFO story, but the missing contemporary records sharply limit what can be verified. event time window The pilot's account and the alleged encounter What survives of the radar confirmation claim Speed, shock waves and the missing records calvados 1973 encounter Introduction The Caen Carpiquet helicopter encounter is Calvados ’s best known aviation related UFO story, but the often repeated claim that radar confirmed an object travelling at extraordinary speed is much less secure than it sounds. Former French Army Light Aviation pilot Daniel Michau said that, during a flight to Caen in 1973, he and a companion were overtaken by an enormous, silent, grey object. He later recalled that a Caen controller reported seeing a fast moving radar echo at the same time. [danielmichau.blogspot.com] danielmichau.blogspot.com Daniel MICHAU: OVNI / expérience vécue Ce récit remonte à 1973, j'étais un jeune pilote de l'ALAT du 3° GHL.... J'allais à Caen Carpiquet où se tenait à l'époque, l'école des... The account is striking because it combines an apparently credible aviation witness, a close daylight observation and alleged instrument corroboration. Yet no contemporary radar plot, control room log, military report or independent statement from a radar operator has entered the public record. Even the incident’s exact date remains unclear. The case is therefore important to Calvados’s UFO history not as a proven “pilot and radar” event, but as an example of how a powerful later testimony can acquire evidential details that the surviving documents cannot verify. 6:22 1973: UFO SPOTTING in WARMINSTER, WILTSHIRE Nationwide Weird and Wonderful BBC Archive Channel: BBC Archive · Views: 22.8K · Uploaded: August 2023 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube The pilot’s account and the alleged encounter Michau first published a detailed version of the story on his personal website in April 2008, roughly 35 years after the reported flight. He wrote that he had been a young pilot with the French Army Light Aviation’s 3rd Helicopter Group and was flying an Alouette II from Rennes Saint Jacques towards Caen Carpiquet. The destination was a military school then based at Carpiquet, and the time was approximately 11 in the morning. [danielmichau.blogspot.com] danielmichau.blogspot.com Daniel MICHAU: OVNI / expérience vécue Ce récit remonte à 1973, j'étais un jeune pilote de l'ALAT du 3° GHL.... J'allais à Caen Carpiquet où se tenait à l'époque, l'école des... The destination is historically plausible. French defence archives confirm that an inter service school for female military personnel opened at Carpiquet on 1 April 1973. The former airbase had been transferred to civil aviation administration in 1969, while military facilities continued to be used for training establishments. That background supports Michau’s description of the purpose and setting of the flight, although it does not authenticate the encounter itself. [Images Defense] imagesdefense.gouv.fr Images Defense Images Défense Ouverte le 1er avril 1973 et implantée dans le quartier Koenig situé à Carpiquet près de Caen, l'EIPMF a pour mission de donner une forma... According to Michau, the other man in the helicopter suddenly called his attention to something passing on their right. Michau described it as an immense, dull grey object resembling a streamlined airship or cigar, perhaps 200 to 300 metres long, with no windows, lights, visible exhaust or obvious appendages. It appeared pointed towards the front and possibly at the rear. The sighting lasted only about one second, or perhaps one and a half seconds in later retellings. [danielmichau.blogspot.com] danielmichau.blogspot.com Daniel MICHAU: OVNI / expérience vécue Ce récit remonte à 1973, j'étais un jeune pilote de l'ALAT du 3° GHL.... J'allais à Caen Carpiquet où se tenait à l'époque, l'école des... Michau emphasised that the object made no sound and caused no noticeable movement of the helicopter. The engine and radio reportedly continued to operate normally, with no vibration, electrical interference or other physical effect. He did not claim to know that it was extraterrestrial; his stated position was that he had seen something he could not identify. [danielmichau.blogspot.com] danielmichau.blogspot.com Daniel MICHAU: OVNI / expérience vécue Ce récit remonte à 1973, j'étais un jeune pilote de l'ALAT du 3° GHL.... J'allais à Caen Carpiquet où se tenait à l'époque, l'école des... Later television and online versions added more colour. Michau compared the object’s apparent size to an ocean liner and said that he quickly saw its circular rear profile diminishing into the distance. He also acknowledged that a televised reconstruction contained a shadow he had never reported, an important reminder that visual reconstructions should not be treated as records of the original observation. [Reddit] reddit.com Today, we have Daniel Michau with us for an exceptional testimony A French helicopter pilot, witness of a UAP in 1973Daniel Michau, an Army Light Aviation pilot in 1973, was flying in a helicopter... The brevity of the encounter is central to its interpretation. One second is long enough for a startled observer to register shape, colour and motion, but it is a poor basis for reliable measurements of distance and physical size. Without a known range, an object that appeared enormous and distant could have been smaller and nearer, while an error in judging relative motion could have made an ordinary object seem exceptionally fast. Pilot experience improves familiarity with aircraft and aerial conditions, but it cannot by itself solve the distance size ambiguity in a sudden, unexpected sighting. What survives of the radar confirmation claim Michau said that he contacted Caen by radio immediately after the object passed. In his 2008 account, he recalled reporting that the helicopter had been overtaken by a huge, silent airship like object. The response, as he remembered it, was that controllers had seen an echo cross the radar display from north to south at very high speed. [danielmichau.blogspot.com] danielmichau.blogspot.com Daniel MICHAU: OVNI / expérience vécue Ce récit remonte à 1973, j'étais un jeune pilote de l'ALAT du 3° GHL.... J'allais à Caen Carpiquet où se tenait à l'époque, l'école des... That statement is the foundation of the case’s reputation as a radar confirmed encounter. However, it is still Michau’s recollection of what an unnamed controller told him. The available sources do not provide: a photograph, printout or recording of the radar display; the radar’s type, location, range scale or operating mode; the time and coordinates of the contact; the number of sweeps on which it appeared; the names or signed statements of the controllers; a radio transcript or air traffic log; a military incident report; confirmation that the visual object and radar return followed the same path. These omissions prevent a technical assessment of the radar claim. A recorded track extending over several sweeps, correlated in time and position with the helicopter crew’s observation, would be materially stronger than a brief or isolated return. Without the original data, it is impossible to determine whether the alleged echo represented a solid target, interference, an anomalous propagation effect, a processing artefact or an unrelated aircraft. Online retellings frequently describe four witnesses: Michau, his companion and two radar operators. They also claim that the operators estimated a speed between 8,000 and 12,000 kilometres per hour by timing the return’s movement across the radar screen. The discoverable trail for these details leads largely to forum discussions and reposted summaries from around 2010, not to an authenticated radar report or direct testimony from either operator. [Webastro] webastro.net OVNIs/PANs] Paroles de pilotes et d'officiels vlcsna10.jpg. Ce récit... 8000 et 12000 km/h. 4 témoins: les deux opérateurs radar de...Read more... This distinction matters. “A pilot later said that a controller reported a radar echo” is support for the pilot’s sincerity and memory of the conversation. It is not equivalent to “radar independently confirmed the same object at a measured speed”. The latter wording assumes several links in the evidential chain that have not been demonstrated. The identity of the second person in the helicopter is also uncertain in the public documentation. Later versions name him as Jean Luc Bideau, but no independently accessible statement from that individual has been found in the source trail. The name also creates obvious search confusion with a well known Swiss actor"},{"title":"Official Cases","url":"/official-cases-27d36c/","description":"GEIPAN files show how lasers, projected lights and weak observation conditions turned several striking Calvados reports into ordinary or unusable cases.","breadcrumb":"Calvados UFOs Between Legend and Explanation","level":2,"text":"Official Cases What Official Calvados UFO Files Actually Show Calvados UFOs Official Cases Official Cases What Official Calvados UFO Files Actually Show GEIPAN files show how lasers, projected lights and weak observation conditions turned several striking Calvados reports into ordinary or unusable cases. GEIPAN files show how lasers, projected lights and weak observation conditions turned several striking Calvados reports into ordinary or unusable cases. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies reported phenomena Lasers, sky roses and misleading lights Why some reports remain unusable rather than unexplained calvados official cases Introduction France’s official files do not reveal a hidden concentration of extraordinary craft over Calvados . They show something more useful: how impressive looking reports can become ordinary once investigators reconstruct the viewing conditions, compare directions with weather and local activity, and distinguish a genuinely unexplained event from one that is merely under documented. The clearest Calvados examples involve lasers or powerful event projectors, lightweight debris, lanterns and probable drones. Other reports were placed in the insufficient information category because the witness account could not support a reliable reconstruction. This distinction matters. A case classified as unusable is not evidence of an unknown phenomenon; it means that too little dependable information survives to decide what was seen. The official record therefore weakens several dramatic interpretations while illustrating how easily distance, darkness, cloud layers and unfamiliar lighting can mislead even sincere witnesses. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... How GEIPAN classifies reported phenomena GEIPAN is the unidentified aerospace phenomena investigation and information group operated by France’s national space agency, CNES. It receives reports directly from witnesses and through bodies including the gendarmerie, police, civil aviation and the armed forces. Investigators may compare testimony with meteorological observations, astronomical data, aircraft movements, photographs, maps and local enquiries before publishing a case file. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Its classification system is best read as a judgement about the evidence, not as a scale running from mundane to alien: Category A means that the reported phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. Category B means that an ordinary explanation is judged probable, although some direct confirmation may be missing. Category C means that the available information is too weak or incomplete for a dependable conclusion. Category D is reserved for cases that remain unexplained after investigation despite having enough usable evidence to examine properly. CNES’s published national figures underline the importance of Category C. It reports that 24.6 per cent of phenomena are clearly identified and 39.7 per cent probably identified, while 32.4 per cent remain unidentified because of insufficient data. Only 3.3 per cent are listed as unexplained after investigation. In other words, the largest “unidentified” group consists not of strong mysteries but of reports that cannot be tested adequately. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... GEIPAN also records two separate measures in many newer files. Strangeness describes how unusual the reported phenomenon appears, while consistency concerns the amount of reliable, objectified information available. A strange account with one witness, no image and uncertain timing may therefore have little investigative value. Conversely, an initially puzzling report supported by photographs, precise directions and weather data may be resolved quite firmly. The Calvados files repeatedly demonstrate this difference. [Geipan] geipan.fr BAYEUX (14) 22.10.2023 GEIPAN... Lasers, sky roses and lights projected onto cloud Some of the most visually striking official cases in Calvados were eventually linked to ground based lighting. These reports are especially instructive because a projected patch of light can appear to be a self contained object high in the sky. The beam itself may be invisible in clear air, leaving only a luminous disc, oval or group of moving patches on a cloud layer. The Honfleur–Blonville laser case On 20 August 2000, a report made while travelling from Honfleur towards Blonville sur Mer was later classified Category A and identified as a laser. The public catalogue gives little narrative detail, but its importance lies in the final status: this was not simply a vague light dismissed without inquiry. GEIPAN recorded it as a positively identified lighting phenomenon and revisited the file before its 2017 update. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANde HONFLEUR (14) vers BLONVILLE SUR MER (14) 20.08.2000 GEIPANAugust 20, 2000 — Geipan Groupe d’études et d’informations sur les... Published: August 20, 2000 The location makes such an explanation plausible. The Honfleur–Deauville–Trouville coast contains hotels, nightlife venues, seasonal entertainment and outdoor events capable of using strong promotional projectors. From a moving vehicle, apparent motion produced by the observer’s own travel can combine with the scanning movement of a beam. With no reliable distance reference against a dark sky, a patch projected on cloud may seem much larger, faster or higher than it really is. The case should not be stretched beyond what the official file supports: the publicly indexed material does not preserve a detailed reconstruction comparable with more recent investigations. Its value is narrower. It establishes that at least one seemingly anomalous coastal report in Calvados was officially resolved as laser illumination rather than an aircraft or unknown vehicle. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Saint Gatien des Bois changed from unexplained to probably explained The 27 November 2011 Saint Gatien des Bois case is more revealing because GEIPAN later reversed its original assessment. At about 6.50 pm, a motorist and her children saw an intense, stationary white phenomenon above the village church. Another witness subsequently provided a separate account. The absence of sound and the apparently fixed luminous forms initially made the event difficult to identify. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT GATIEN DES BOIS (14) 27.11.2011 GEIPAN... The first investigation rejected the possibility of powerful event projectors because the weather information then consulted appeared to show no cloud in the relevant area. Without cloud, there would be no surface on which the light could form the observed patches. The case was consequently classified D, meaning unexplained despite investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT GATIEN DES BOIS (14) 27.11.2011 GEIPAN... A later review reached a different conclusion. More detailed meteorological work indicated that banks of low stratocumulus cloud were probably present. That single change transformed the interpretation. Circular white patches, variable in number, could have been the impact points of a rapidly moving sky projector on the cloud base. Very clear air would explain why witnesses saw the illuminated patches but not the beams connecting them to the ground. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT GATIEN DES BOIS (14) 27.11.2011 GEIPAN... Investigators also identified a nearby practical source. The golf course north of Saint Gatien was known to hold outdoor events, and a contractor working there at the time offered a projector model whose technical characteristics matched the reported appearance. GEIPAN could not prove that the machine was operating at the exact moment of the sighting. However, its presence, the probable cloud layer, the shapes and brightness, the absence of noise and the compatibility of the viewing directions produced a coherent explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT GATIEN DES BOIS (14) 27.11.2011 GEIPAN... The revised classification was Category B: probable projected light on cloud. This is one of the strongest lessons in the Calvados archive. “Unexplained” was not treated as a permanent verdict. When improved weather analysis supplied a missing piece of evidence, GEIPAN reassessed the case and published the change rather than preserving its more mysterious earlier status. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT GATIEN DES BOIS (14) 27.11.2011 GEIPAN... There is still a legitimate limitation. No invoice, operator’s statement or equipment log proves that the projector was switched on that evening. The official conclusion is therefore probable rather than certain. Yet the explanation accounts for several otherwise odd features at once, especially the bright shapes without visible connecting beams. It is more economical than assuming a silent object hovering above the village. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on"},{"title":"Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery","url":"/cantals-ufo-story-beyond-the-mystery/","description":"Cantal’s UFO history is dominated by one event: the alleged close encounter at Cussac on 29 August 1967.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery Cantal’s UFO history is dominated by one event: the alleged close encounter at Cussac on 29 August 1967. Cantal’s UFO history is dominated by one event: the alleged close encounter at Cussac on 29 August 1967. Two children reported seeing four small dark figures beside a bright, rounded object in a field near the village. cantal Introduction Cantal’s UFO history is dominated by one event: the alleged close encounter at Cussac on 29 August 1967. Two children reported seeing four small dark figures beside a bright, rounded object in a field near the village. Their distress, the prompt involvement of the gendarmerie and later investigation by France’s official UFO study group gave the story unusual staying power. Yet there are no photographs, radar returns or securely documented physical traces, and later researchers have shown that important details changed or appeared only after repeated interviews. Beyond Cussac, the official record is modest rather than dramatic. GEIPAN, the French space agency service that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, lists several Cantal reports, but recent examples have usually proved to be stars, balloons or projected lights. Others remain unclassified only because the information is inadequate. Cantal therefore offers a useful lesson in UFO evidence: its most famous case remains disputed, while the clearer modern cases mostly demonstrate how ordinary objects can look extraordinary under unfamiliar conditions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Cometa 0 GEIPANcometaL'incident survenu à Cussac, par une matinée d'août 1967, a fait l'objet d'une contre enquête du GEPAN qui en a conforté la d... Cussac: the encounter that defined the department On the morning of 29 August 1967, a brother and sister were tending cattle near Cussac, south west of Saint Flour. The boy was about thirteen and his sister about nine. According to the account that became established, they noticed four short, dark beings near a rounded, intensely bright object. The figures appeared to rise from the ground and enter or merge with the object, which then climbed away. The children reportedly described a whistling sound; later accounts also referred to heat, disturbed animals and an unpleasant sulphurous smell. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Cussac Rencontre de Cussac The case mattered immediately because it was not merely a distant light in the sky. It was presented as a daylight observation at relatively close range, involving an apparent craft and occupants. The children returned visibly frightened, and their father—then the mayor of Cussac—contacted the authorities. A gendarmerie response and early press coverage gave the narrative an official and public foundation that most rural sighting stories never acquired. The regional newspaper La Montagne published the story at the beginning of September, after which private UFO investigators began collecting fuller accounts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Cussac Rencontre de Cussac Several features made the witnesses seem credible. They were young rural children engaged in an ordinary task, not known UFO enthusiasts. Their emotional reaction was reportedly seen by adults, including a farmer who encountered them soon afterwards. They broadly agreed on the central sequence: unfamiliar figures, a luminous machine and a rapid departure. Later investigators also regarded deliberate invention as unlikely, even when they disagreed sharply about what the children had actually seen. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Cussac Rencontre de Cussac That is the strongest part of the case. The evidence supports the narrower proposition that the children experienced something alarming and interpreted it as highly unusual. It does not establish that the figures were non human or that the object was an extraterrestrial vehicle. How investigation changed the story Cussac was examined repeatedly, but not under ideal conditions. Private groups investigated during the months following the event. France’s official Group for the Study of Unidentified Aerospace Phenomena, GEPAN, conducted a field investigation in 1978—eleven years after the sighting. Its team interviewed the witnesses and reconstructed viewing positions and distances. The later COMETA report, an influential but non governmental study written by former military and technical figures, stated that the GEPAN inquiry had reinforced the basic description given by the children. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Cometa 0 GEIPANcometaL'incident survenu à Cussac, par une matinée d'août 1967, a fait l'objet d'une contre enquête du GEPAN qui en a conforté la d... The long delay is crucial. By 1978, the witnesses had heard their experience retold by journalists and several generations of investigators. Memory is not a recording: repeated questioning can stabilise a central narrative while altering dimensions, wording and peripheral details. In Cussac, early and later versions differ over the exact shape and size of the object, whether supporting legs were visible and what the children initially thought the figures were. One colourful sentence attributed to the girl in press retellings was later denied. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rencontre de Cussac Rencontre de Cussac Claims of physical evidence are particularly weak. Later descriptions sometimes say that grass was scorched or dried where the object had rested. The critical re examination published by the French sceptical organisation Cercle Zététique argues that this circular mark was not reported in the initial newspaper coverage or by the first private investigators. It also says the boy himself appeared unaware of such a trace years later. An odour described as sulphurous was reportedly noticed well after the event and at some distance from the alleged landing point, making its connection to the sighting uncertain. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique This does not prove that every later detail was false. It does mean that the most dramatic physical claims cannot be treated as contemporary, independently verified evidence. Cussac remains fundamentally a witness case. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The helicopter dispute The principal conventional explanation is that the children saw a helicopter, its passengers or crew distorted by distance, glare and surprise. A light helicopter could account for a rounded cabin, landing gear, vertical ascent, animal disturbance and people standing nearby. Strong sunlight reflected by a curved canopy might obscure the tail structure and make the cabin appear like a brilliant sphere. Cantal was not isolated from helicopter activity: the sceptical investigation identified possible civil, utility, tourist, training and gendarmerie operations that deserved checking. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique The GEPAN investigators considered a helicopter because it resembled the reported phenomenon in several respects, but rejected it. In the archived wording reproduced by the sceptical study, they cited the object’s apparent shape, brilliance and trajectory, the reported silence while it was on the ground, the unusual appearance of the occupants, the odour and the speed or manner of disappearance. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique Critics reply that many of these objections depend on uncertain perception. The observation distance was reconstructed at roughly 70–80 metres, with part of the scene obscured by a hedge. A nearby tractor, cattle and a barking dog could have masked rotor noise. The object was observed in bright conditions, and the children’s attention shifted between the figures, animals and departing light. The sceptical team also demonstrated during a later site visit that terrain could reduce or briefly conceal the sound of a low flying helicopter. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique The helicopter theory nevertheless has a major weakness: no particular aircraft, crew or flight has been positively identified. Suggestions involving an Alouette II, an electricity network inspection, a training flight or a gendarmerie aircraft remain possibilities rather than documented solutions. Nor does the theory reproduce every reported detail comfortably, especially the appearance and apparent movement of the four figures. The fairest conclusion is therefore not “helicopter proven”, but “helicopter insufficiently excluded”. Cussac has not been demonstrated to involve an exotic craft, yet the surviving documentation cannot securely reconstruct an ordinary flight either. What Cantal’s other reports reveal The wider GEIPAN record does not show Cantal as a persistent national"},{"title":"Other Sightings","url":"/other-sightings/","description":"Cantal's wider record shows how stars, balloons and projected lights can appear extraordinary when observations are brief or poorly documented.","breadcrumb":"Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery","level":2,"text":"Other Sightings Why Most Cantal UFO Reports Fade Away Other Sightings Other Sightings Why Most Cantal UFO Reports Fade Away Cantal's wider record shows how stars, balloons and projected lights can appear extraordinary when observations are brief or poorly documented. Cantal's wider record shows how stars, balloons and projected lights can appear extraordinary when observations are brief or poorly documented. dataset evidence What the official records contain Recurring stars, balloons and light effects Unexplained versus insufficiently documented cantal other sightings Introduction Most officially recorded UFO reports from Cantal do not lead to an enduring mystery. Outside the disputed 1967 Cussac encounter, the department’s small GEIPAN record is largely made up of sightings that were either traced to ordinary objects or could not be assessed because the original information was too poor. Recent examples have involved a bright star, a weather balloon and powerful event lighting projected on to cloud. Older files often contain little more than a brief statement or gendarmerie record. This matters because a witness can give an honest and detailed account while still misidentifying what was seen. Distance removes scale, darkness removes landmarks, atmospheric turbulence makes stars appear mobile and multicoloured, and lights on cloud can look like self contained objects. Cantal’s record therefore illustrates a less dramatic but more useful side of UFO investigation: the mystery often fades when position, weather, timing and local activity are reconstructed. What the official records contain GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena service of France’s national space agency CNES, does not treat every unexplained report as evidence of an extraordinary craft. It compares the account with astronomical, meteorological, aviation and human made explanations, then assesses both the remaining strangeness and the “consistency” of the evidence — meaning the quantity and reliability of the information available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... Its classifications make an important distinction: A means the phenomenon has been identified without significant ambiguity. B means an ordinary explanation is considered highly probable. C means no reliable conclusion can be reached because information is missing or unusable. D is reserved for a phenomenon that remains unexplained despite sufficiently substantial evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr baisse cas d GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — (niveau d'étrangeté, validation des hypothèses envisageables et con... This prevents a common misunderstanding. A category C report is not necessarily a stronger mystery than an identified category A case. It may simply be a weak file: an uncertain date, one brief account, no usable direction, no angular size, no photographs and no way to contact the witness. GEIPAN explicitly describes category C observations as non analysable rather than positively unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... Cantal’s published entries fit that pattern. The clearly resolved files include a weather balloon at Mauriac in 1990, projected event lighting near Pierrefort in 2022 and the star Arcturus near Saint Urcize in 2024. Other entries — including reports from Pierrefort in 1960 and 1978, Champs sur Tarentaine Marchal in 1977 and Montsalvy in 2012 — are classified C because investigators lacked enough dependable information to decide what caused them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The result is not a substantial cluster of well documented unexplained events. It is a small mixed archive in which the strongest modern evidence has generally made the reports less mysterious. Stars, balloons and projected lights Arcturus: a moving, colour changing “object” The Saint Urcize case of 20 September 2024 shows how an ordinary star can produce a surprisingly elaborate report. At about 10.23 pm, a traveller staying in a hired camper van noticed a bright point towards the north west. It appeared to shimmer, change between yellow white and red and move abruptly. He filmed it twice and took several photographs, giving GEIPAN more material than is available in many UFO cases. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The additional evidence did not confirm an object manoeuvring over the landscape. A reconstruction based on the witness’s position, a daylight reference photograph and astronomy software placed the bright star Arcturus close to the reported direction. Arcturus was low in the sky, at an elevation of roughly nine degrees, where its light would have travelled through a long and turbulent path in the atmosphere. GEIPAN concluded that this turbulence accounted for the strong scintillation and apparent colour changes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The apparent movement had two likely sources. First, a highly zoomed handheld telephone magnifies tiny movements of the camera. With no hill, tree or building visible in the recording, the viewer cannot easily separate camera shake from movement of the light. Second, a stationary point seen against a dark, featureless background can seem to wander through small involuntary eye movements, an effect often called autokinesis. GEIPAN classified the case A: an observation of Arcturus. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case is valuable precisely because the witnesses supplied photographs and video. Recording the light strengthened the investigation but weakened the exotic interpretation. It showed that images alone are not decisive; investigators still need orientation, camera behaviour and fixed reference points. Mauriac: a weather balloon mistaken for something stranger A Mauriac observation dated 4 January 1990 is listed by GEIPAN as a category A weather balloon case. The surviving online summary is sparse, so it does not support a detailed reconstruction of what the witness originally described. It nevertheless provides a second recurring Cantal explanation: a high altitude balloon appearing bright, slow moving or nearly stationary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Weather balloons are particularly effective UFO lookalikes because their apparent behaviour depends on distance and illumination. A balloon can reflect sunlight strongly even when the observer’s surroundings are becoming dark. At great range, its envelope may appear only as a brilliant point, while its instrument package and suspension line remain invisible. Its movement can also be difficult to judge without a fixed foreground reference. GEIPAN notes that balloons may seem star like, oscillate, drift slowly or appear temporarily motionless. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Cantal’s upland landscape can make those uncertainties more noticeable. A light observed beyond a ridge offers few clues to distance, height or actual size. Something small and nearby, large and distant, or extremely high can produce a similar angular appearance. The Mauriac classification does not imply carelessness by the witness; it shows how little visual information the unaided eye receives from a remote illuminated object. 8:14 Are they aliens? Channel: ReasonTV · Views: 20.7K · Uploaded: October 2022 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Pierrefort: lights on cloud, not objects among the clouds The most instructive modern case occurred near Pierrefort on 21 August 2022. Four people watching from the terrace of a country house saw several white oval forms moving in regular pairs across a cloudy night sky. The lights followed repeated, slightly rising paths and remained visible for about five minutes. One witness supplied testimony and a video. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Their appearance suggested separate luminous objects. The investigation instead linked them to powerful moving event lights, sometimes called sky tracers, directed on to the underside of cloud. A celebration was taking place at Paulhac, approximately 11 kilometres from the observation point, and the entertainment company confirmed that it had equipment capable of projecting strong moving beams into the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Cloud was central to the illusion. The projected beam became conspicuous where it struck the cloud layer, creating a bright oval patch, while the section of beam travelling through relatively clear air remained difficult or impossible to see. To a distant observer, the bright patch could therefore appear detached from any source on the"},{"title":"Cussac","url":"/cussac/","description":"The 1967 Cussac encounter rests on frightened child witnesses, but later interviews and disputed physical traces complicate the case.","breadcrumb":"Cantal's UFO Story Beyond the Mystery","level":2,"text":"Cussac What Happened at Cussac in 1967? Cantal UFOs Cussac Cussac What Happened at Cussac in 1967? The 1967 Cussac encounter rests on frightened child witnesses, but later interviews and disputed physical traces complicate the case. The 1967 Cussac encounter rests on frightened child witnesses, but later interviews and disputed physical traces complicate the case. event time window The children's original account How later investigations changed the story Which evidence still stands up cantal cussac Introduction The Cussac encounter began on the morning of 29 August 1967, when two children tending cattle outside the Cantal village reported four small dark figures beside a brilliant rounded object. They said the figures rose or leapt into it before it departed with a whistle, intense light and a brief strange smell. The children returned home badly frightened, and gendarmes visited the site later that day. That core story is well established. The harder question is what it proves. Cussac has no photograph, radar record, recoverable material or securely preserved contemporary police file. Its strongest evidence is the apparent sincerity and immediate distress of two young witnesses. Its weaknesses lie in viewing distance, a very brief observation, uncertain physical traces and details that were expanded, altered or challenged during investigations conducted years later. The result is neither a confirmed extraordinary event nor an easy hoax: it is Cantal’s defining unresolved witness case, but one whose evidential value has often been overstated. The children’s original account François, aged thirteen, and his nine year old sister Anne Marie were watching the family’s cattle near a road outside Cussac at about 10.30 in the morning. François’s attention was reportedly drawn towards a pasture across the road when the family dog reacted to the herd. The children then noticed several dark figures near a bright object partly framed by a hedge. In the account that circulated soon afterwards, the figures were roughly child sized but oddly proportioned and completely dark. François initially seems to have wondered whether they were unfamiliar children. He and his sister then described movements that appeared impossible or at least highly unusual: the figures rose from the ground and entered, or seemed to be absorbed into, a shining sphere. One figure allegedly returned briefly before following the others. The object then climbed away, becoming too bright to watch. The whole principal observation may have lasted only about thirty seconds. [gregorygutierez.com] gregorygutierez.com l obversation de cussac en 1968 historique des debats L'observation de Cussac en 1967: historique des débats... The reported distance matters. Later reconstructions commonly placed the object and figures around 70 to 80 metres from the children, rather than at truly close conversational range. A hedge stood near or between the witnesses and the reported scene, reducing confidence in fine details close to the ground. This makes descriptions such as webbed feet, supporting legs beneath the sphere or small handheld objects much less secure than the broader impressions of dark figures and a bright rounded form. [gregorygutierez.com] gregorygutierez.com l obversation de cussac en 1968 historique des debats L'observation de Cussac en 1967: historique des débats... The children’s reaction is one of the case’s better supported features. A farmer whom they encountered soon afterwards recalled that they appeared panicked and asked whether he had seen or heard anything. Their father also saw them return home crying. Later accounts described continuing shock, disturbed sleep and eye irritation in François. These observations support the conclusion that the children were genuinely frightened; they do not identify the cause of that fear. [gregorygutierez.com] gregorygutierez.com l obversation de cussac en 1968 historique des debats L'observation de Cussac en 1967: historique des débats... What was reported immediately The central narrative entered public circulation quickly. Radio Luxembourg reportedly covered the story the following day, and the regional newspaper La Montagne interviewed the family for an article published on 1 September. Other newspapers and specialist UFO publications followed. That short interval limits the possibility that the entire incident was invented years afterwards, but it does not mean every familiar detail appeared in the earliest version. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique The stable elements appear to have been: two frightened child witnesses; [researchgate.net] researchgate.net Source details in endnotes. four dark, small looking figures; a conspicuous rounded or spherical object; a rapid departure involving bright light and a whistling or rushing sound; disturbance among the dog and cattle. More elaborate features are harder to date securely. Webbed feet, an object like a mirror in one figure’s hand, landing supports, a spiral ascent and pronounced heat effects appear most clearly in later published or reconstructed accounts. Some may reflect genuine recollection. Others may have been sharpened through questioning, illustration and repeated retelling. This distinction is central to Cussac. A report can remain consistent in its broad outline while becoming progressively more detailed. Greater detail does not automatically mean stronger evidence, especially when it emerges after witnesses have seen their experience represented in newspapers, drawings and UFO literature. The gendarmerie visit and the missing record The children’s father, who was also the village mayor, contacted the authorities. Gendarmes from the Saint Flour and Neuvéglise area reportedly arrived at approximately 4pm, several hours after the sighting. They examined the location and spoke to those involved. Later summaries say they noticed an unusual smell and a patch of yellowed or dried grass. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique This official response is often presented as if it supplied independent physical confirmation. In reality, its value is limited by a serious archival problem: no contemporary written gendarmerie statement or site report has been securely produced as the foundational document of the case. Later GEPAN material reportedly stated that the officers had not considered it necessary to prepare a formal record. What survives is therefore mainly retrospective information about what the gendarmes were said to have observed. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique That difference is important. A dated police report could establish precisely: who inspected the site; what was present when they arrived; the dimensions and position of any grass discoloration; whether the smell was detected independently; what the children said before extensive media attention; what ordinary aircraft or local activity were checked. Without such a document, later claims about “officially confirmed traces” should be treated cautiously. The grass evidence is particularly unstable. Early UFO writing described a circular landing trace. Yet François reportedly told investigators in 1983 that he had never personally seen a trace in the field. Other accounts refer only to a four or five metre area of yellowed grass, without soil sampling, photographs, measurements or proof that the discoloration appeared after the sighting. [gregorygutierez.com] gregorygutierez.com l obversation de cussac en 1968 historique des debats L'observation de Cussac en 1967: historique des débats... A smell described as sulphur, ozone or something similarly sharp was also reported. François later characterised it as fleeting, while the village field guard said he noticed an odour only when visiting the pasture that evening. Smell is real evidence in the everyday sense, but it is difficult to preserve or identify retrospectively. It could be compatible with exhaust, disturbed vegetation, agricultural material or another local source as readily as with an unknown machine. [gregorygutierez.com] gregorygutierez.com l obversation de cussac en 1968 historique des debats L'observation de Cussac en 1967: historique des débats... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube How later investigations changed the story Cussac was repeatedly investigated, but the most influential inquiry occurred long after the event. France’s National Centre for Space Studies created GEPAN, its first official unit for studying unidentified aerospace phenomena, in 1977. In 1978 Claude Poher and three colleagues travelled to Cussac, interviewed the witnesses, measured the terrain and attempted a reconstruction—roughly eleven years after the original sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr depuis 1977, date de la création du GEPAN (Groupe d’Étude des Phénomènes"},{"title":"From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris","url":"/charente-maritime/","description":"Charente-Maritime has no single, securely documented UFO incident that demonstrates extraordinary technology.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris Charente Maritime has no single, securely documented UFO incident that demonstrates extraordinary technology. Charente Maritime has no single, securely documented UFO incident that demonstrates extraordinary technology. charente maritime Introduction The department’s most instructive case occurred near Royan in 1985, when a hot metal cylinder fell into a field and initially resisted military and scientific identification. Later technical work identified it as part of a German wartime smoke producing weapon, overturning an earlier space debris theory. Other reports around La Rochelle, Fouras, Saujon and Breuil Magné illustrate the main recurring explanations: aircraft seen head on, illuminated balloons and sky lanterns. Meanwhile, the celebrated Taupignac “landed saucer” story of 1954 remains memorable but rests on anonymous witnesses and retrospective retellings rather than a strong contemporary investigation. The 1954 wave left stories, not firm evidence Charente Maritime participated in the extraordinary national surge of “flying saucer” reports during the autumn of 1954. The best known local story is associated with Taupignac, a hamlet near Breuillet and Royan. Accounts usually date the event to 4 or 11 October—an uncertainty that already signals problems in the surviving record. According to later UFO catalogues, three motorists from Bordeaux saw a red glow beside the road at about 7.30 pm. They reportedly stopped and observed a circular object, perhaps six metres wide, carrying a luminous orange red dome and hovering silently above a field. Some versions add four small beings who hurried into the craft before it departed, while others describe only the object’s movement behind trees. The witnesses were unnamed, and the narrative expanded as it passed through books and catalogues. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente Maritimeovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente M... Taupignac became part of wider UFO literature through authors such as Jacques Vallée, but repetition is not the same as corroboration. The date varies between sources, no original witness statements are readily available in the official GEIPAN archive, and the more sensational occupants appear chiefly in later summaries. Even a detailed pro UFO case compilation has to assemble the story from secondary authors rather than a preserved police file or contemporary technical inquiry. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente Maritimeovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente M... A second local report came from Saint Savinien on 7 October 1954. UFO author Aimé Michel described an orange luminous disc flying at low altitude, stopping and then departing westwards. A regional newspaper reportedly mentioned the incident several days later, but later investigators found little more to work with; the witness had died by the time sceptical researchers attempted follow up inquiries. The case therefore remains unsubstantiated rather than positively unexplained. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 7 octobre, Saint Savinien, Charente Maritimeovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 7 octobre, Saint Savinien, Charente M... These reports matter mainly because they show how a national media wave took local form. During October 1954, French newspapers carried dozens of accounts involving discs, glowing globes, cigar shapes and alleged occupants. Similar descriptions could encourage witnesses to interpret unfamiliar lights through a shared “flying saucer” vocabulary. That does not prove the Charente Maritime witnesses invented their experiences, but it makes independent confirmation especially important—and that confirmation is missing. The falling cylinder near Royan The department’s most substantial official investigation began on 25 February 1985 at Saint Sulpice de Royan. Two agricultural workers heard a whistling sound before a metal cylinder fell into a field, landing within roughly 50 metres of one witness and bouncing several times. The object was about 50 centimetres long and 15 centimetres in diameter. It was so hot that it could not be handled for around an hour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN... This was much stronger evidence than a distant light. There were witnesses, a recovered object, a known impact site, gendarmerie involvement and access to specialist laboratories. The Rochefort air base initially found that the cylinder did not match familiar French or foreign missile components, and no radioactivity was detected. Investigators also checked the Landes missile testing area, civil air traffic authorities and the military air bases at Cognac and Cazaux without finding a launch or exercise matching the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN... Attention then turned to orbital debris. NASA and North American Aerospace Defense Command information suggested that part of the launch vehicle associated with the Soviet Cosmos 1629 satellite might have re entered around the relevant date. GEPAN—the predecessor of today’s GEIPAN—provisionally treated this as a plausible explanation and retained the cylinder for further examination. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN... That provisional conclusion did not survive later analysis. The current GEIPAN record classifies the case as identified and describes the cylinder as part of a German Second World War ground weapon designed to generate smoke, which had apparently ignited spontaneously. In other words, the object genuinely fell hot from the air, but it was neither an alien craft nor Soviet space debris. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN... The case is important because it demonstrates how official conclusions can change. The early investigators were not irrational to consider a rocket component: they had an unfamiliar manufactured cylinder, evidence of intense heating and approximate information about a possible re entry. Yet the physical artefact allowed later experts to test that theory rather than preserve it indefinitely as a mystery. The recovered object ultimately weakened, rather than strengthened, the extraordinary interpretation. It also shows why some older summaries can mislead. A historical SEPRA publication described the Royan object while discussing difficult investigations and noted the absence of a matching military operation. Read without the later case update, that material can make the incident appear permanently unresolved. The present GEIPAN classification is the more complete assessment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 1:15:04 The New Era in UFO Research with Jacques Vallée Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove · Views: 43.6K · Uploaded: January 2023 · Length: 1 hour 15 minutes Open on YouTube Aviation explains some lights—but not by assumption alone La Rochelle’s airport and the former military aviation presence at Rochefort form an obvious part of the department’s sighting environment. An aircraft approaching almost directly towards an observer may look like a brilliant, stationary light. When it turns, its landing lights can dim or vanish abruptly, producing an apparent acceleration or disappearance. One GEIPAN investigation considered exactly this mechanism after a witness north east of La Rochelle reported a reddish orange light that changed to white, diminished and disappeared within seconds. Investigators checked the weather, astronomical objects, La Rochelle airport procedures and possible military traffic. They found that the appearance was compatible with aircraft landing lights, potentially involving an approach towards Rochefort or a more distant military departure, but the sighting geometry and air traffic information were too poor for a confident identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete357 Compte rendu enquete357 That distinction is essential. “Probably an aircraft” is not equivalent to “proved to be an aircraft”. GEIPAN’s own report judged the aviation hypotheses weak because there was no confirming traffic record and the witness had supplied insufficient positional data for a reliable reconstruction. The case illustrates the correct role of nearby airfields: they provide hypotheses to test, not an automatic answer. A separate La Rochelle report from 5 December 2012 remained still less conclusive. A sixth floor resident described an intensely bright, silent phenomenon apparently concealing a dark object and remaining stationary for about"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-0e4366/","description":"Taupignac and Saint-Savinien became famous during France's 1954 UFO wave, but missing testimony and later retellings limit what can be trusted.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave How Strong Were Charente Maritime's 1954 Saucer Stories? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave How Strong Were Charente Maritime's 1954 Saucer Stories? Taupignac and Saint Savinien became famous during France's 1954 UFO wave, but missing testimony and later retellings limit what can be trusted. Taupignac and Saint Savinien became famous during France's 1954 UFO wave, but missing testimony and later retellings limit what can be trusted. event time window Taupignac's changing dates and expanding story Saint Savinien and the limits of late follow up How the national media wave shaped local reports charente maritime 1954 wave Introduction Charente Maritime ’s celebrated flying saucer stories from autumn 1954 are historically important but evidentially weak. Taupignac, near Breuillet and Royan, became known for an alleged hovering disc and, in later versions, four small occupants. Saint Savinien contributed a simpler report of an orange luminous disc that supposedly stopped in flight. Neither case is supported by a surviving contemporary witness statement, a detailed police file, physical evidence or a modern reconstruction based on named witnesses. The central problem is not that the reports have been conclusively disproved. It is that their published histories grew stronger while their documentary foundations remained thin. Dates shifted, details accumulated, witnesses stayed unidentified or became unavailable, and later catalogues repeatedly copied earlier summaries. The result is a revealing part of Charente Maritime’s UFO history: two stories whose fame greatly exceeds the quality of the records from which they were built. Taupignac’s date problem and expanding story The Taupignac incident is usually placed in early or mid October 1954, but even its date cannot be established securely. One compilation traces the story to the regional newspaper Sud Ouest of 10 October, which reportedly dated the observation to 4 October. Aimé Michel and several later catalogues instead placed it on 11 October. Some retellings then described the event as occurring “the next day” after another incident, creating further opportunities for chronological confusion. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente Maritimeoctobre 1954: \"N 150 entre Saintes et Royan 10.54\" \"St Savinien 10.54\" Les auteu... That uncertainty matters because the date is not a minor decorative detail. It determines which newspaper reports were genuinely contemporary, whether apparently similar sightings occurred before or after Taupignac, and whether later writers were independently confirming the case or merely repeating the same source with an altered date. A securely investigated incident should normally have at least a stable day, location, witness list and first account. Taupignac lacks several of these basic anchors. The least elaborate version concerns three motorists from Bordeaux who noticed a red light near the coast or countryside around Royan. They approached and reportedly saw a circular object with a red orange dome hovering low over or near a field. The object was said to depart rapidly after passing through several colours. A local history summary for Breuillet similarly records that three motorists reported a circular craft preceded by a red glow above Taupignac on 11 October, and that the affair was taken up by the press. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Breuillet (Charente Maritime Breuillet (Charente Maritime Later versions became substantially more dramatic. The object was described as roughly six metres across, hovering about ten metres above the ground or settling among trees. Four beings, commonly said to be about one metre tall, were added beneath or beside it. In one version, two motorists entered the woods and watched the figures hurry aboard. In another, the beings directed a beam of light towards the witnesses before the craft took off. These are not harmless differences in wording: they transform a distant light report into an alleged close encounter involving occupants and hostile or defensive action. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente Maritimeoctobre 1954: \"N 150 entre Saintes et Royan 10.54\" \"St Savinien 10.54\" Les auteu... The surviving source trail does not show that each added detail came from an original witness. Instead, it runs largely through later UFO books, magazines and catalogues, including works associated with Aimé Michel, Jacques Vallée, Charles Garreau and Raymond Lavier. One detailed case compilation openly presents multiple, inconsistent summaries assembled from those publications rather than from a preserved interview or official investigation. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 4 octobre 1954, Taupignac, Charente Maritimeoctobre 1954: \"N 150 entre Saintes et Royan 10.54\" \"St Savinien 10.54\" Les auteu... This leaves several unanswered questions: Who were the three motorists, and were all three interviewed separately? What did the earliest newspaper actually say about occupants, distance and duration? Did the original report describe an object on the ground, hovering, or merely disappearing behind trees? Were the four beings present in the first account, or introduced through later retelling? Was the location precisely identified and inspected? Until original documentation answers those questions, the most cautious description is that Taupignac was a press reported observation of a red light or disc like object whose narrative later expanded. Calling it a documented “landing” or “encounter with beings” gives greater certainty to the later version than the surviving evidence permits. Saint Savinien and the limits of late follow up The Saint Savinien report, dated 7 October 1954, is less elaborate but scarcely better documented. Aimé Michel’s 1958 account states that at about 7.45 pm an orange luminous disc was seen flying at low altitude, stopping and then leaving towards the west. Another later source gave the time as 7.15 pm. No witness name, observation duration, viewing direction at the start, angular size or weather information survives in the widely circulated summary. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 7 octobre, Saint...L'ufologue Aimé Michel avait rapporté dans son livre de 1958 que le 7 octobre 195... Those omissions make even basic interpretation difficult. An apparently stationary light can result from an aircraft travelling nearly towards the observer, while changes in direction may be exaggerated when distance and scale are unknown. A bright astronomical object, meteor, balloon or aircraft can sometimes appear orange near the horizon. None can be identified confidently here, because the report is too abbreviated to test them. Sceptical investigators Gérard Barthel and Jacques Brucker later attempted to revisit Saint Savinien but reported that the witness had died. Whatever one thinks of their wider approach to UFO cases, that outcome illustrates the irreversible cost of delayed investigation. They could not obtain a fresh narrative, compare it with the published summary, inspect the viewing point with the witness or ask whether aircraft, clouds and landmarks had affected the impression of stopping. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 7 octobre, Saint...L'ufologue Aimé Michel avait rapporté dans son livre de 1958 que le 7 octobre 195... Saint Savinien therefore should not be described as a case that resisted a thorough investigation. There was too little surviving material for a thorough investigation to occur. Its status is better expressed as unsubstantiated or insufficiently documented , not as positively unexplained. An unexplained case has usually retained enough reliable information to exclude common possibilities without reaching a final identification. Saint Savinien does not meet that standard. The report did acquire a second life through Michel’s geographical pattern building. He placed Saint Savinien among observations on 7 October that appeared to form straight line arrangements across France. This idea, known as orthoteny, treated mapped alignments as evidence that reports might follow organised routes. Saint Savinien was linked with Saint Plantaire, Montlevicq and Jettingen in one proposed line. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Cassis, Corbigny, Puymoyen, Marcillac; 7 octobre 1954, Cassis, Bouches du Rhône Patrick Gross5 May 2026 — sa source était le journal Le Provençal, Saint Savinien; Saint Plant... Published: May 2026 The alignment added theoretical importance to an otherwise slight report, but it did not improve the underlying testimony. A point on a map is only as reliable as its date, location and original description. Jacques Vallée, who initially took Michel’s idea seriously, later used computer modelling and concluded that such"},{"title":"Royan Cylinder","url":"/royan-cylinder/","description":"The hot cylinder recovered near Royan moved from mystery to space-debris theory before experts identified a wartime smoke weapon.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Identified Debris","level":2,"text":"Royan Cylinder Why the Royan Cylinder Was Not Space Debris Charente Maritime Royan Cylinder Royan Cylinder Why the Royan Cylinder Was Not Space Debris The hot cylinder recovered near Royan moved from mystery to space debris theory before experts identified a wartime smoke weapon. The hot cylinder recovered near Royan moved from mystery to space debris theory before experts identified a wartime smoke weapon. entity The fall, witnesses and recovered object Why investigators suspected a rocket component How later analysis changed the official verdict charente maritime royan cylinder Introduction The metal cylinder that fell near Royan in February 1985 was not space debris and did not provide evidence of an exotic craft. It was eventually identified as the rear section of a German wartime ground launched smoke or rocket weapon whose remaining propellant had ignited decades after the Second World War. What makes the case important is not the object’s mystery but the way the explanation changed as stronger evidence became available. Unlike most sightings in Charente Maritime , the Saint Sulpice de Royan incident produced witnesses, impact marks, a recoverable object and several technical examinations. Investigators first ruled out known contemporary missiles, then provisionally linked the cylinder to a recently decayed Soviet rocket stage. Only later did markings, construction details and trajectory work point back to the German occupation of the Royan area. GEIPAN now classifies the case as category A: an identified phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Royan, la tragédie de 1945, par Eric Renoux. Unipop 20/02/2023. Channel: Unipop Pessac Open on YouTube The fall, witnesses and recovered object On 25 February 1985, two agricultural workers were in separate fields at Saint Sulpice de Royan when they heard a whistling sound. One witness saw a rotating object approaching before it struck the ground. It landed within about 50 metres of him, cut a furrow roughly 1.8 metres long and up to 30 centimetres deep, hit a bank and bounced several times before stopping. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN The cylinder was approximately 52 centimetres long and 15 centimetres across. One end carried a ring of 26 conical openings, while the opposite end appeared to have been torn away from a more substantial assembly. Its exterior was heavily oxidised, and dark deposits around the openings suggested that hot gases had passed through them. These features made it look less like random scrap metal than part of a manufactured propulsion system. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The most striking detail was its temperature. The witnesses could feel intense heat close to the surface and were unable to handle the object. Even the first gendarmes reportedly had to wait until the air gendarmerie team arrived, about an hour after the initial call, before it could be removed safely. That heat naturally encouraged the idea that the cylinder had just travelled through the atmosphere or had been expelled by an operating rocket. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN The incident was therefore much better documented than a typical report of a distant light. Investigators had: two nearby witnesses who heard and saw the descent; a physical object that could be retained for examination; measurable impact and rebound marks; prompt involvement by the local gendarmerie and the Rochefort air base; evidence that the object had recently undergone intense combustion. There is a small archival complication over the date. GEIPAN’s current case page and database consistently give 25 February, whereas the opening lines of one scanned investigation report refer to the alert and fall as occurring on 26 February. The surviving testimony elsewhere in the file, and GEIPAN’s modern classification, support 25 February as the official case date. The discrepancy appears to be a clerical inconsistency rather than evidence of two separate events. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Why investigators suspected a rocket component The Rochefort air base examined the cylinder first. Missile specialists could not match it to a French weapon or to any foreign missile system known to them, and a Geiger counter test detected no radioactivity. Checks were also made with the Landes missile testing centre, regional air traffic authorities and the military air bases at Cognac and Cazaux. None reported a launch, exercise or accident that could account for the object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Those negative findings did not establish an extraterrestrial origin. They simply removed the most obvious contemporary explanations. The combination of apparent exhaust ports, scorching, great heat and a descent from above led GEPAN—the French national study group that preceded today’s GEIPAN—to investigate whether a piece of artificial space hardware had re entered the atmosphere. The timing produced a plausible lead. The Soviet satellite Cosmos 1629 had been launched on 21 February 1985 by a Proton rocket. Its payload carried catalogue number 15574, while an associated rocket body was catalogue number 15575 and was recorded as decaying from orbit around 24 February. Information supplied through NASA and the North American Aerospace Defense Command therefore appeared to place a suitable piece of orbital debris in the atmosphere at almost exactly the right time. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN GEPAN provisionally concluded that the Royan cylinder might be part of the fourth stage propulsion system used in the Cosmos 1629 launch. This was not a reckless guess. It joined three real observations: an object shaped like a propulsion component, evidence of intense heating and a known rocket body returning to Earth during the same period. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Yet the match was circumstantial. A decaying rocket stage can re enter anywhere along a broad orbital path, and temporal proximity alone cannot show that one particular fragment landed in a particular field. More importantly, the cylinder itself was available for direct testing. Once the material evidence began to contradict the orbital theory, the coincidental timing of the Soviet re entry ceased to be persuasive. 13:40 1,000 Pounds of Space Debris DESTROYS Kenyan Village Channel: ViewCation · Views: 920 · Uploaded: January 2025 · Length: 13 minutes Open on YouTube How later analysis changed the verdict The cylinder was retained by GEPAN rather than discarded after the provisional space debris finding. That decision was crucial. Further examinations were undertaken by the Centre for Research and Studies at Arcueil and other technical specialists. They discovered inscriptions and markings establishing that the component was German and dated from the Third Reich period. The proposed link to Cosmos 1629 was consequently abandoned. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Additional analysis identified the object more specifically as the rear or propulsive section of a German ground to ground weapon described in the GEIPAN file as a “smoke launcher”. The term requires care. German Nebelwerfer weapons were literally named “smoke launchers”, reflecting their origins and association with chemical or smoke troops, but the family also fired high explosive and other battlefield rockets. The label therefore does not mean that every such projectile merely released harmless smoke. [Cairn.info] shs.cairn.info revue historique des armees 2018 4 page 115 les traces des bombes fusées des fronts de l'Atlantique...1 Sept 2022 — Bien que le terme Nebelwerfer signifie littéralement lanceur de... The cylinder’s ring of openings, heat damage and apparent separation from a larger body were consistent with a rocket motor section rather than a spacecraft fuel tank. Investigators concluded that old propellant had ignited spontaneously, perhaps after warming or being disturbed by a minor shock. The resulting thrust launched the surviving motor section across the local countryside before it dropped into the field. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT SULPICE DE ROYAN (17) 25.02.1985 GEIPAN Trajectory calculations placed its probable starting point about 3.3 to 3.6 kilometres north west of the impact site. According to GEIPAN’s reconstruction, that direction led towards marshland where German forces had reportedly disposed of munitions while trapped in the Royan pocket at the end of the war. The area had indeed been a heavily militarised German stronghold guarding the Gironde estuary until its capture in April 1945, making the survival of abandoned ordnance locally credible. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT"},{"title":"Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","url":"/why-charentes-ufo-cases-remain/","description":"Charente has no single UFO incident comparable in fame or evidential depth to France’s best-known cases.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Charente has no single UFO incident comparable in fame or evidential depth to France’s best known cases. Charente has no single UFO incident comparable in fame or evidential depth to France’s best known cases. Its documented history is instead a small, uneven collection of radar returns, solitary visual observations and luminous phenomena reported from the 1950s to the present. charente Introduction Two Charente reports remain officially unidentified after investigation: a silent brown oval seen over Angoulême in 2009 and a repeatedly appearing light observed at Ruffec in 2022. Neither produced photographs, physical traces, radar confirmation or supporting witnesses. Other intriguing reports, including high speed radar echoes at Cognac air base in 1957, are classified as cases with insufficient reliable information rather than genuine unresolved anomalies. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRUFFEC (16) 13.09.2022 GEIPAN... The department’s record therefore matters less as evidence of extraordinary craft than as a clear example of how uncertainty survives. A case may remain unexplained because it is genuinely unusual, but also because evidence was collected late, instruments were unavailable or a witness’s description could not be checked independently. What the official record contains GEIPAN was created by the French space agency CNES in 1977. It collects reports from witnesses and public authorities, checks possible astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human explanations, preserves case documents and publishes classifications. It does not regard an unidentified report as proof of an extraterrestrial vehicle. Its stated role is to investigate an observation, not to validate the interpretation attached to it by a witness or the media. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... The terminology is important. In GEIPAN’s system: A means the phenomenon has been identified. B means an ordinary explanation is considered probable. C means there is not enough reliable information to reach a sound conclusion. D means the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. A D classification records residual uncertainty. It does not establish that the reported object had extraordinary technology, or even that the witness correctly judged its distance, size and speed. Category C is particularly relevant in Charente, where several reports are too poorly documented for meaningful reconstruction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANqualitative expert evaluationJuly 16, 2014 — by JP ROSPARS · 2014 · Cited by 1 — Each expert has classified the reports he/she exam... Published: July 16, 2014 The accessible GEIPAN listing for Charente includes reports from Cognac, Villebois Lavalette, Angoulême, Jurignac, La Couronne, Ruffec and Saint Séverin. Among those displayed, only the Angoulême case of 15 August 2009 and the Ruffec case of 13 September 2022 carry a D classification. Entries such as the 1957 Cognac radar incident, the 1977 Villebois Lavalette light and several later observations are classified C because reliable information is missing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The 1957 Cognac radar echoes Charente’s most striking aviation related report occurred at Cognac air base on 17 December 1957. Four military personnel reportedly saw an unidentified echo appear on a radar display at 15:01 Universal Time. According to the later GEIPAN summary, the return travelled from a range of about 40 kilometres to 16 kilometres before disappearing among fixed echoes. Its roughly 30 second track implied a calculated speed of about 2,880 kilometres per hour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... A second return appeared at 15:45, beginning at about 37 kilometres and disappearing at 25 kilometres after approximately 15 seconds. It produced the same estimated speed. Two witnesses prepared an illustrated report, giving the incident more documentary substance than a story reconstructed solely from memory decades later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... Those figures make the incident sound dramatic, but the evidential weaknesses are decisive. There was no corresponding visual sighting, no surviving multi radar confirmation mentioned in the public summary and too little technical information to exclude equipment effects, propagation anomalies, interference or an incorrectly interpreted conventional target. GEIPAN therefore classifies it C, explicitly citing insufficient information rather than treating the returns as an unexplained aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... The location nevertheless gives the story enduring interest. Cognac Châteaubernard is a major military aviation site associated with flight training and, in its modern role, remotely piloted aircraft operations. That makes careful checking of aircraft movements and radar conditions especially relevant to reports from the surrounding area. It does not, however, turn every unexplained light or return near Cognac into a military encounter. [Ministère des Armées] defense.gouv.fr Ministère des ArméesL'école des pilotes des Forces aériennes suisses s'...L'école des pilotes des Forces aériennes suisses s'entraîne su... The 1957 case illustrates an important distinction. Instrumental evidence may appear more objective than eyewitness testimony, but a radar return is not self explanatory. Without the equipment settings, weather conditions, calibration history, operator records and independent sensor data, a speed calculated from a plotted echo can be precise without being conclusive. Angoulême’s silent oval At about 13:15 on 15 August 2009, a witness standing in a garden in Angoulême reported seeing a matt brown, oval shaped object travel in a straight line from east to west, towards Cognac. The object appeared to pass above nearby rooftops and made no audible sound. No second witness was identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009 GEIPAN... GEIPAN considered several ordinary possibilities. The witness rejected an aircraft or helicopter, while investigators also examined nearby objects or activities such as a rugby ball, clay pigeon shooting, a flying disc and model aircraft. A meteor related explanation was considered because the observation occurred close to the annual Perseid meteor shower. None could be matched confidently to the account. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009 GEIPAN... The case’s unresolved status rests partly on a procedural failure. The gendarmerie report reached GEIPAN too late for investigators to verify local air traffic with air traffic control. That missing check is central: the official record does not show that aircraft were positively excluded, only that the witness did not believe the object was one and that investigators could no longer test the possibility. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009 GEIPAN... A meteor is not a particularly comfortable fit either. Meteors are normally brief luminous events, whereas the witness described a brown, apparently solid oval in daylight. Yet judgments about colour, altitude, shape and silence are vulnerable when an isolated object is viewed against the sky without a known scale. An object thought to be large and distant may instead be smaller and closer; apparent silence may simply reflect distance, wind or short exposure. GEIPAN assigned the report a relatively high strangeness score and retained it as category D. The result is defensible in the narrow sense that no tested explanation was consolidated. Nevertheless, the case remains a single witness observation without imagery, physical effects or independent sensor support. Its classification preserves a question; it does not transform the description into verified performance data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009 GEIPAN... ✈️ Dans le cockpit des pilotes de drones de la base militaire de Cognac Channel: France 3 Nouvelle Aquitaine Open on YouTube Ruffec’s repeating light The Ruffec observation is Charente’s most recent officially unresolved case. At around 21:00 on 13 September 2022, a witness in a garden saw a white point of light towards the west. It appeared to travel through an arc, disappear and then repeat a similar movement in the same region of sky. Each cycle lasted roughly six seconds. After shifting slightly downwards and towards the west south west, the light disappeared permanently after about five minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRUFFEC (16) 13.09.2022 GEIPAN... Unlike many brief light"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-5c3bad/","description":"Charente's two category D cases remain unexplained, but both depend on unsupported eyewitness testimony and incomplete checks.","breadcrumb":"Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases What Keeps Charente's Two Best Cases Open? Charente UFOs Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases What Keeps Charente's Two Best Cases Open? Charente's two category D cases remain unexplained, but both depend on unsupported eyewitness testimony and incomplete checks. Charente's two category D cases remain unexplained, but both depend on unsupported eyewitness testimony and incomplete checks. dataset evidence The silent brown oval seen over Angouleme The repeating white light observed at Ruffec Why unresolved does not mean extraordinary charente unresolved cases Introduction Charente ’s two publicly accessible cases classified D — unidentified after investigation are not its most dramatic reports, but they are its most carefully preserved unresolved ones. In Angoulême in 2009, a witness described a silent, matt brown oval crossing the daytime sky. In Ruffec in 2022, another witness watched a white light repeat the same brief movement for about five minutes. Neither case has photographs, instrument readings, physical traces or independent witnesses. They remain open because investigators could not make the reported behaviour fit a known explanation with enough confidence, not because the evidence demonstrates an extraordinary craft. GEIPAN’s current Charente listing identifies these as the department’s two category D reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009ANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009. Résumé. Observation du passage silencieux E O d'un objet ovoïde marron. Traject... That distinction matters. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation unit, evaluates both residual strangeness — how poorly the observation fits familiar phenomena after checking — and consistency , meaning the amount and reliability of information available. A report can therefore remain unidentified while still resting mainly on one person’s unsupported recollection. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... The silent brown oval over Angoulême At about 13:15 on 15 August 2009, a person watching from the garden of their home in Angoulême reported seeing a matt brown, egg shaped or oval object travel in a straight line from east to west, towards Cognac. The witness heard no sound. GEIPAN’s summary does not record lights, exhaust, a trail, changes of direction or any interaction with the surroundings. No second witness came forward. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009ANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009. Résumé. Observation du passage silencieux E O d'un objet ovoïde marron. Traject... The case is memorable because it was a daylight sighting of an apparently solid shape rather than a distant night time light. Even so, almost every important physical property remained uncertain. With no known size or distance, an object that appeared to pass over roofs could not be reliably converted into a true altitude, speed or dimension. Silence is similarly ambiguous: it may indicate an unusual object, but it can also result when an ordinary aircraft is distant, when wind carries sound away or when the observation is too brief for noise to be noticed. Investigators considered aircraft and helicopters, but the decisive weakness was timing. The gendarmerie statement reached GEIPAN too late for air traffic checks to establish what had been operating over the area. The witness rejected an aircraft explanation, yet that personal judgement could not substitute for contemporary flight records. The delay did not prove that an aircraft was responsible; it removed one of the best ways of testing the possibility. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009ANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009. Résumé. Observation du passage silencieux E O d'un objet ovoïde marron. Traject... Several nearby object explanations were also examined, including a rugby ball, a clay target, a flying disc and a model aircraft. GEIPAN judged that these did not fit the account, principally because the witness perceived the phenomenon as travelling clearly above the rooftops. That reasoning depends heavily on the accuracy of the witness’s depth and height judgement, however. A small, nearby object against an empty sky can be difficult to place because there are few visual cues from which to infer distance. An atmospheric entry was another possibility. The date fell close to the annual Perseid meteor maximum, but the reported matt brown oval and straight, apparently controlled looking passage were not an obvious meteor description. More importantly, no corroborating reports were found to establish a wider fireball event. GEIPAN therefore had no consolidated explanation and classified the observation D, assigning a recorded strangeness value of 0.62 and describing its consistency as medium to strong. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009ANGOULEME (16) 15.08.2009. Résumé. Observation du passage silencieux E O d'un objet ovoïde marron. Traject... The official label should nevertheless be read alongside GEIPAN’s own 2011 description of the case among its then published D1 reports. The agency explained that D1 cases were strange but comparatively limited in consistency, typically involving a single witness without photographic or video evidence. Angoulême’s unresolved status is therefore best understood as a credible looking account that resisted the available checks, not as a heavily corroborated event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr nouveaux cas D1 GEIPANLe GEIPAN publie de nouveaux cas classés D114 Dec 2011 — Le GEIPAN publie de nouveaux cas classés D1. Publication date. 14... ANGO... The repeating white light at Ruffec The Ruffec report began at around 21:00 on 13 September 2022. A 40 year old resident was sitting on steps outside the kitchen while speaking on the telephone and watching a sky containing stars, aircraft and the remnants of storm activity. The witness first took a white point of light for an aircraft. It moved across a short section of sky, disappeared, then reappeared at approximately its starting position and repeated the movement. The cycle lasted roughly six seconds and continued, with some variation, for about five minutes before the apparent track shifted slightly downwards and towards the west south west. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The repetition is what kept the case unusual. A single aircraft could explain a white moving point, but not an object apparently resetting to the same starting position every few seconds. The witness compared the effect to a light mounted on a rotating sphere: each turn would make the light visible briefly, then conceal it before the next pass. This was an analogy rather than evidence of a rotating object, but it captures the pattern the investigation had to explain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN reconstructed the viewing direction more carefully than the original report allowed. The witness initially described looking south west, but comparison with photographs and later correspondence placed the phenomenon more nearly west, drifting towards the west south west. The investigation also established that the observation took place during nautical twilight, with no Moon present. Jupiter and Saturn were in other parts of the sky, while the bright star Arcturus was visible in the west. None matched the reported repetitive arc and disappearance pattern. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Weather conditions complicated the interpretation. A storm cell and substantial cloud cover existed in the region, although the witness said the section of sky containing the light appeared clear. Investigators examined whether intermittent lightning might have illuminated ice crystals or cloud structures. They found that the repetition was too regular for normal lightning, while strong winds at altitude made a stable reflective formation difficult to sustain in one place for five minutes. The hypothesis was not mathematically impossible, but it lacked a workable physical match to the observation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Aircraft were a stronger possibility. Flight tracking reconstruction showed nearly a dozen airliners in the wider sky between about 20:55 and 21:10, mostly travelling from the south west towards the north east — broadly compatible with the direction first described. The light was also comparable in brightness and appearance to an aircraft lamp, and the witness had initially interpreted it that way. Against this, the number and rhythm of the repeated appearances appeared much greater than the number of aircraft actually crossing the relevant track. GEIPAN scored the aircraft hypothesis at 0.475: plausible in several respects, but not strong enough to account for the complete sequence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A moving"},{"title":"Cognac Radar","url":"/cognac-radar/","description":"The 1957 Cognac radar returns looked extraordinarily fast, but missing technical records prevent a reliable conclusion.","breadcrumb":"Why Charente's UFO Cases Remain Unresolved","level":2,"text":"Cognac Radar Did Cognac Radar Track Something Extraordinary? Charente UFOs Cognac Radar Cognac Radar Did Cognac Radar Track Something Extraordinary? The 1957 Cognac radar returns looked extraordinarily fast, but missing technical records prevent a reliable conclusion. The 1957 Cognac radar returns looked extraordinarily fast, but missing technical records prevent a reliable conclusion. event time window The two radar tracks recorded in December 1957 Why the calculated speed is not conclusive How Cognac's military aviation role shapes the debate charente cognac radar Introduction The Cognac radar case is Charentes most dramatic instrument based UFO report, but it is not the departments strongest evidence for an extraordinary aircraft. On 17 December 1957, military personnel at Cognac air base reportedly watched two unidentified echoes cross a radar display. Each track was later calculated at about 2,880 kilometres per hour, an exceptional speed for the period. Yet no object was seen in the sky, no aircraft was sent to intercept it, and the surviving public file does not provide enough technical data to establish that the echoes represented solid objects at all. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, therefore classifies the incident as category C: too poorly documented for a reliable conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... That distinction is crucial. The Cognac returns may have been unusual radar targets, atmospheric effects, equipment artefacts or aircraft whose movements were incompletely recorded. The evidence does not identify the cause, but neither does it justify treating the calculated speed as a confirmed performance measurement. The two radar tracks recorded in December 1957 According to GEIPANs present case summary, four military personnel were present at Cognac air base when the first echo appeared at 15:01 Universal Time on 17 December 1957. It was reported at a range of roughly 40 kilometres, moved towards the radar and disappeared at about 16 kilometres after approximately 30 seconds. Dividing the 24 kilometre change in displayed range by the elapsed time gives 0.8 kilometres per second, or 2,880 kilometres per hour. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... A second echo appeared at 15:45. This return was said to move from approximately 37 kilometres to 25 kilometres in 15 seconds. The same simple calculation again produces 2,880 kilometres per hour. GEIPAN says that two witnesses prepared an illustrated report, although four military personnel reportedly observed the first sequence at the radar position. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... The surviving online record adds several details, but they are not always easy to reconcile. The witness entry for 17 December gives a local time of 16:01, equivalent to 15:01 Universal Time, and notes patches of fog. It also records an electronic or instrumental observation rather than a conventional visual sighting. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 T1 GEIPAN... The attached archive additionally includes a testimony entry dated 27 December 1957, ten days after the two tracks described in GEIPANs main narrative. Its metadata gives a local time of 14:30 and identifies a single phenomenon, while the indexed text of the military report refers to an unusual track detected by the base radar on that date. GEIPAN nevertheless presents the overall case under 17 December and summarises only the two returns at 15:01 and 15:45. This may reflect more than one episode preserved in the same file, a dating or cataloguing problem, or incomplete digitisation of the original paperwork. The public material does not explain the discrepancy. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 27.12.1957 T2 GEIPAN... That archival uncertainty does not prove that the observations were mistaken. It does, however, make a precise reconstruction harder. A strong radar case normally depends on an internally consistent timeline, the radars operating characteristics, original plots or photographs, weather data, maintenance information and records of known air traffic. Cognacs public file provides only part of that chain. Why the calculated speed is not conclusive The figure of 2,880 kilometres per hour is often the most memorable part of the story. In late 1957, such a speed would have seemed striking, especially on a French military radar screen. But the figure is a calculation based on reported range changes and estimated durations. It is not an independent measurement preserved in a complete radar recording. Several uncertainties limit what can safely be inferred. The radar equipment is insufficiently described. The published summary does not specify the radar model, wavelength, pulse settings, antenna rotation rate, range accuracy, display scale or the filtering used to suppress unwanted echoes. Without those details, it is impossible to estimate the likely measurement error or determine how confidently an operator could time a brief return. The original track data are not publicly available in a form suitable for modern analysis. GEIPAN refers to drawings made by witnesses, but the accessible case summary does not contain a continuous electronic record showing successive positions, signal strength, altitude, transponder response or Doppler velocity. The speed is therefore reconstructed from a small number of reported range points rather than verified from raw data. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... A change in displayed range is not automatically a complete flight path. The calculation assumes that the echo represented one physical target moving continuously between the stated ranges during the stated period. If the displayed return shifted, faded, reappeared, merged with clutter or was mistakenly associated with another echo, the apparent movement would not describe the motion of a single object. The disappearance occurred among fixed echoes. GEIPAN says the first return vanished at about 16 kilometres in the fixed echoes. Fixed or ground clutter consists of radar reflections from terrain, buildings and other stationary features. Such clutter can conceal a genuine aircraft return, but it can also make an uncertain target harder to follow or distinguish. The National Weather Service defines clutter as unwanted radar echoes that interfere with the observation of desired targets and notes that ground clutter may obscure other returns close to the radar. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO CTL] COGNAC (16) 17.12.1957 GEIPAN... The neat repetition of exactly the same calculated speed for both tracks is intriguing, but it does not necessarily strengthen the case. It may reflect similar motion by two targets. It may also arise from rounded range readings and rounded observation times: 24 kilometres in 30 seconds and 12 kilometres in 15 seconds are mathematically identical ratios. With no raw timing record, it is unclear how precisely those intervals were measured. The safe conclusion is therefore narrower than either a sensational or dismissive reading. Operators apparently saw short lived moving echoes and considered them sufficiently unusual to document. The surviving information does not demonstrate that the radar accurately tracked solid craft travelling at 2,880 kilometres per hour. Radar echoes do not always represent aircraft Radar is powerful precisely because it can detect objects that are difficult or impossible to see. It is not, however, an infallible camera. A display shows returned radio energy after it has interacted with the atmosphere, terrain, objects and the radar system itself. Operators must decide which returns correspond to aircraft and which are interference, clutter or propagation effects. One established problem is anomalous propagation, sometimes called ducting. Under unusual temperature and humidity conditions, a radar beam can bend towards the ground instead of following its expected path. It may then reflect from distant terrain or structures and create unexpected blips. The US Federal Aviation Administration warns that anomalous propagation can produce numerous extraneous returns on an operators display. [FAA] faa.gov ENR 1.1: General RulesThe bending of radar pulses, often called anomalous propagation or ducting, may cause many extraneous blips to... Other possible sources of confusing radar returns include weather, birds, insects, interference from another transmitter, reflections outside the main radar beam and equipment faults. Modern systems use techniques such as Doppler processing, moving target indication, transponder correlation and automated filtering to reduce these problems, although none removes every ambiguity. The Met Office notes that hills, buildings, birds and insects can all interfere with radar observations, while its technical material also"},{"title":"Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher","url":"/strange-lights-and-changing-verdicts/","description":"Cher has a genuine UFO history, but not a single landmark case that withstands scrutiny as compelling evidence of an extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher Cher has a genuine UFO history, but not a single landmark case that withstands scrutiny as compelling evidence of an extraordinary craft. Cher has a genuine UFO history, but not a single landmark case that withstands scrutiny as compelling evidence of an extraordinary craft. cher Introduction The department’s most instructive episodes are the reported sightings around Bourges during France’s 1954 “flying saucer” wave; a disputed multi witness observation near Nançay and Vouzeron in 1987; the spectacular Soyuz debris fall of December 1988; and more recent Bourges and Vierzon reports resolved as ordinary aerial objects. The overall picture is not one of a sustained unexplained “flap”, but of scattered cases whose quality ranges from historically interesting to evidentially weak, with later investigation generally weakening rather than strengthening extraordinary interpretations. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org BOURGES, September 27. Mr. Robert Patient, 35, assistant inspector of the P.T.T., living in Bourges, who... The 1954 wave reached Bourges, but the record is fragile Cher appears in accounts of the great French sighting wave of autumn 1954, when newspapers across the country carried reports of discs, glowing objects and apparent close encounters. The department’s best known local story concerns Robert Patient, a postal services inspector from Bourges, who said that while travelling with his family near the hamlet of Jou, north of the city, he saw an intensely bright object apparently descend towards a field and then seem to follow the car. Versions of the report appeared in several newspapers, helping it circulate well beyond the department. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org BOURGES, September 27. Mr. Robert Patient, 35, assistant inspector of the P.T.T., living in Bourges, who... The episode is memorable because it contains several features that made 1954 reports persuasive to contemporary readers: a named adult witness, family members said to be present, movement apparently linked to a car, and a rural setting in which an unfamiliar light could seem close. Yet the surviving material is mainly a chain of press retellings rather than a modern investigative file. There is no known radar confirmation, photograph, physical trace or surviving contemporary technical reconstruction establishing the object’s distance, altitude or true motion. Those gaps are crucial. A bright astronomical object, aircraft landing lights or another distant light can appear to follow a moving car because the observer’s position changes while the object remains at a great distance. Without precise bearings and timings, apparent pursuit cannot be distinguished from this well known perspective effect. The repetition of the same account in several newspapers increases its historical visibility, but does not create independent corroboration when the reports derive from one original statement. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org BOURGES, September 27. Mr. Robert Patient, 35, assistant inspector of the P.T.T., living in Bourges, who... Other Cher entries from 1954 are even thinner. A Bourges report dated 13 October was described in the press as a bright, billiard ball like object, while catalogue references also place observations at Bourges on 23 September and near La Chapelle Hugon on 3 October. These cases survive principally through later catalogues, newspaper extracts or specialist compilations, often without sufficient information for a reliable explanation. They are useful evidence that Cher participated in the national media and reporting wave, but poor evidence for reconstructing what was physically present in the sky. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Enfin, Bourges a, lui aussi, sa soucoupe volante, laquelle ovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 13 octobre, Bourges, CherPAS UNE SOUCOUPE VOLANTE mais une \"boule de billard\" a été vue sur Bo... The safest conclusion is that 1954 was Cher’s clearest historical “flap” period in cultural terms, not its strongest evidential period. More people were primed to notice and report unusual lights, newspapers actively sought such stories, and similar language travelled rapidly between reports. That does not prove that every observation was mistaken, but it makes retrospective certainty especially difficult. Bourges in 1980: a vivid report with too little information A gendarmerie file records an observation made on 11 November 1980 by occupants of a car in the Bourges area. The principal witness described a luminous green object, roughly spherical or hemispherical, moving across the sky for about a minute. Other occupants also reported seeing a bright green form before it disappeared behind higher ground. The statements were formally taken by the gendarmerie, giving the case a firmer documentary basis than most 1954 newspaper stories. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Official paperwork, however, should not be confused with physical verification. The file establishes that people made the report and records what they remembered; it does not independently establish the object’s size, range or nature. Estimates such as “football sized” or comparisons with the Moon describe apparent size, and can apply equally to a small nearby object or a much larger distant one. GEIPAN lists the Bourges case as category C, meaning that the available information is insufficient for a dependable conclusion. That status is more accurately described as unresolved through lack of data than as evidence of an exotic object. No instrument record, recoverable flight information or physical trace appears to have converted the witness account into a testable event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Possible conventional explanations include a meteor, aircraft seen under unusual lighting, or another bright transient phenomenon. None can be demonstrated confidently from the surviving record. The case therefore deserves a place in Cher’s UFO history because of its multiple witnesses and gendarmerie documentation, but it remains weak as evidence: striking testimony, limited measurement and no decisive follow up. The 1987 Nançay–Vouzeron case changed under re examination On 18 August 1987, three residents in the Nançay and Vouzeron area reported an unusual luminous phenomenon at about 10.15 pm. One witness described two lights moving together without noise. Two others reported a bright white, elongated or cigar shaped light which appeared to turn and depart rapidly towards Vierzon; one of them also recalled smaller white lights and a flashing red light. The gendarmerie took statements within days and forwarded the completed file to the then GEPAN, the predecessor of GEIPAN. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 At first sight, this is among Cher’s stronger cases. It involved observations from two locations, more than one witness and relatively prompt official reporting. The clear weather also reduced the likelihood that cloud alone created the effect. Yet GEIPAN’s later re examination exposed several weaknesses. The witness descriptions were not fully consistent. The Nançay witness may have seen something different from the two Vouzeron witnesses, despite the broadly similar direction of appearance. More importantly, the witnesses had discussed the event before making their formal statements, creating a possible route for memories and interpretations to influence one another. GEIPAN also noted that the evidence was not precise enough to reconstruct a single object’s trajectory with confidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 The later investigation found that the reported features closely resembled aircraft lighting: strong white lights, an apparent elongated shape, a turn, a flashing red light and silence at a poorly known distance. Modern flight path reconstruction showed that aircraft leaving Paris for West Africa commonly pass over this region and turn south south west near Nançay, broadly matching the described movement. Landing or forward facing lights can dominate an aircraft’s appearance and obscure its weaker navigation lights, while distance and crosswind can make engine noise inaudible. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 GEIPAN could not identify a particular 1987 flight because too much time had passed and the necessary traffic data were unavailable. It therefore did not classify the case as a confirmed aircraft. Instead, the former category D assessment was changed to category C: the aircraft explanation was considered plausible, but the evidence was too incomplete for a definitive identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This revision is"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-bd7e46/","description":"Cher's 1954 reports became famous through newspapers, but thin sourcing makes the original sightings difficult to reconstruct.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave Why Cher's 1954 UFO Wave Remains Elusive Cher UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave Why Cher's 1954 UFO Wave Remains Elusive Cher's 1954 reports became famous through newspapers, but thin sourcing makes the original sightings difficult to reconstruct. Cher's 1954 reports became famous through newspapers, but thin sourcing makes the original sightings difficult to reconstruct. event time window The Robert Patient sighting near Jou Bourges and La Chapelle Hugon reports How press coverage shaped the local wave cher 1954 wave Introduction Cher’s 1954 UFO wave is remembered less because it produced a well documented landmark case than because local stories were rapidly turned into national “flying saucer” news. The best known report came from Robert Patient, a postal services inspector from Bourges who said that a brilliant object descended near the hamlet of Jou and then appeared to follow his car. Other reports from Bourges, Vierzon, Rians, the La Guerche sur l’Aubois area and the road from La Chapelle Hugon added to the impression that unusual objects were appearing across the department. What survives, however, is mainly newspaper copy rather than investigation files. There are named witnesses and some useful details, but few precise directions, measurements or independently recorded statements. Several newspapers repeated almost identical wording, creating an appearance of extensive corroboration when they were often reproducing the same agency report. Cher therefore matters as a case study in how the autumn 1954 wave was reported, amplified and remembered—not as strong evidence that extraordinary craft were operating over the department. The Robert Patient sighting near Jou On the evening of Thursday 23 September 1954, Robert Patient, aged 35, was travelling north from Bourges towards Vasselay with his wife and two children. Near the hamlet of Jou, the family reportedly saw an extremely bright object that seemed to descend towards a field. The account described a reddish glow near the supposed landing point and an incandescent filament extending from a hemispherical form. After a dark interval of roughly 20 to 30 seconds, another light appeared above the horizon. Patient stopped the car, then continued driving; the object allegedly followed silently for several hundred metres before disappearing. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 23, Vierzon, Cher... It was an unusually vivid story by the standards of Cher’s 1954 reports. Patient was identified by name, age, occupation and address. His wife and children were said to be present, while newspapers also mentioned broadly simultaneous observations from Bourges, Saint Doulchard and Vierzon. One local treatment portrayed Patient as calm, technically knowledgeable and uninterested in publicity. Such biographical details made the account sound more substantial than an anonymous report of a distant light. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 23, Vierzon, Cher... Yet the surviving record does not establish that the family observed a nearby structured object. The reported diameter of about 15 metres and filament length of roughly 50 metres were estimates made without a known distance. A light low on the horizon can appear to descend into a field even when it is many kilometres away. The impression that it follows a moving car can also arise through parallax: a distant object changes position very little against the landscape as the observer travels, making it seem to keep pace with the vehicle. The claimed darkness between appearances is equally ambiguous. It might indicate that a light was briefly obscured by terrain, trees, cloud or the car’s changing orientation. It could also reflect a genuinely intermittent source. Without a route reconstruction, bearings, weather information or separate interviews with each family member, the possibilities cannot be tested confidently. Most importantly, the supposedly supporting observations are poorly documented. The newspaper articles say that other people in Bourges, Saint Doulchard and Vierzon saw a luminous object at “corresponding” times, but they do not provide enough information to demonstrate that all were watching the same phenomenon. A later account attributed to Aimé Michel links Patient’s observation with a bright “fiery pot” reported by Edmond Trochu at Vierzon during the same night, yet similarity in date and description does not by itself establish a common object. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 23, Vierzon, Cher... The Jou incident is therefore unresolved in the limited historical sense that no secure explanation can now be proved. That is not the same as evidentially strong. Its apparent precision largely comes from a colourful newspaper narrative whose measurements and sequence cannot be independently checked. Bourges and La Chapelle Hugon reports The impression of a department wide wave grew during the following weeks as Le Berry Républicain collected reports from several parts of Cher. Its coverage mixed adult witnesses, children, named local figures and openly cautious commentary, showing that the paper was not simply endorsing every observation in the same way. One report concerned two women near La Guerche sur l’Aubois. At about 7.30 pm, Mrs Rousselet, who lived at the Moulin de l’Oie farm, reportedly saw a large reddish luminous ball that sometimes appeared cigar shaped and contained thread like features. Mrs Villepreux, a lock keeper, was said to have witnessed it as well. During the early hours of the following Sunday, Marcel Debrade reported a greenish, cup shaped object near the Claurie hillside while walking from the train towards his parents’ home. The road and fields were said to have been illuminated before darkness returned. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, Le Berry Républicain, Bourges, France, 5 octobre 1954... The same newspaper article supplied the report most closely associated with La Chapelle Hugon. At around 9 pm on Sunday 3 October, cyclist Robert Verdenal was returning from La Chapelle Hugon towards Grossouvre when a circular form lit by fiery red glows appeared ahead of him. It seemed to remain stationary briefly and then disappeared eastwards. The paper added that Verdenal had a companion and insisted that he had not suffered a hallucination. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, Le Berry Républicain, Bourges, France, 5 octobre 1954... This is sometimes catalogued as the “La Chapelle Hugon” sighting, although the report places Verdenal on a journey from the village rather than fixing the object directly above it. Later UFO literature gave the location additional importance because Aimé Michel included La Chapelle Hugon in one of his proposed straight line arrangements of sightings. He argued that observations from La Chapelle Hugon, Château Chinon, Montbéliard and Grandvillars fell on the same alignment. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 octobre, Montbéliard, DoubsJanuary 17, 2006 — Aimé Michel estimait que les cas de La Chapell... Published: January 17, 2006 That claim is much less persuasive when viewed against the density of reporting on 3 October. Specialist catalogues contain dozens of French observations for that evening, and the exact time, position and direction of many are uncertain. With a large number of points available, some approximate alignments will occur by chance, especially when cases are selected after being plotted. The La Chapelle Hugon story also lacks the accurate coordinates and timing needed to test a narrow geographical line. It remains an interesting part of the history of Michel’s “orthoteny” theory, but not convincing evidence of coordinated aerial activity. The Bourges report of 13 October is better defined geographically but still difficult to interpret. At about 11.30 pm, eight people near the corner of rue Gambon reportedly watched an orange luminous ball in the direction of the Pont Merlan passage. The object, described as about the apparent size of a billiard ball, seemed to move horizontally and vertically while varying in brightness. It remained visible for roughly 15 minutes, and a more distant point was reportedly still visible around midnight. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 13 octobre, Bourges, Cher... The newspaper treated the number and social standing of the witnesses as reasons to trust the report. It argued that an established grocer and his family were unlikely to invent the story and that eight observers ruled out collective hallucination. That reasoning supports sincerity, but not necessarily accurate"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-4a42cc/","description":"Gendarmerie and GEIPAN records show how vivid Cher sightings could remain unresolved or become more ordinary after re-examination.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights and Changing Verdicts in Cher","level":2,"text":"Official Files How Official Reviews Changed Cher's Strongest Cases Cher UFOs Official Files Official Files How Official Reviews Changed Cher's Strongest Cases Gendarmerie and GEIPAN records show how vivid Cher sightings could remain unresolved or become more ordinary after re examination. Gendarmerie and GEIPAN records show how vivid Cher sightings could remain unresolved or become more ordinary after re examination. dataset evidence The unresolved green object over Bourges The Nancay Vouzeron multi witness report Why aircraft became the leading explanation cher official files Introduction Official records do not turn Cher’s strongest UFO reports into proof of extraordinary craft. They do something more useful: they preserve what witnesses actually said, show what investigators checked and reveal how a case can move from “unexplained” to “probably ordinary” without being solved beyond doubt. In Cher, the clearest examples are a puzzling daylight incident near Bourges in 1980 and a three witness night time report around Nançay and Vouzeron in 1987. The Bourges case remained unclassified in practical terms because investigators lacked enough reliable information. The Nançay–Vouzeron case was later downgraded from a genuinely unexplained file to one too weak for that label, with aircraft becoming the leading explanation. Together, the files show the crucial difference between an object that has defeated investigation and a report that cannot be resolved because the necessary measurements, flight records or independent testimony were never obtained. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGES (18) 26.10.1980 GEIPAN... What the official files can actually establish France’s official UFO archive is maintained by GEIPAN, a technical unit of the French space agency CNES. It receives reports directly and through bodies including the gendarmerie, police, civil aviation and the armed forces, then publishes anonymised testimony and investigation findings. GEIPAN deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerial phenomenon” because a witness may have seen a light, reflection or atmospheric effect rather than a solid object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Its classifications are evidential judgements, not rankings of how impressive a story sounds. Category A means a phenomenon has been identified; B means it has probably been identified; C means the available information is insufficient; and D is reserved for a phenomenon that remains unidentified after investigation. GEIPAN assesses both residual strangeness and the “consistency” of the file: the quantity, precision, independence and reliability of the information available. A vivid account can therefore end in Category C if it lacks timings, angular measurements, photographs or records against which the testimony can be tested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN This distinction is particularly important in Cher. The surviving files contain credible seeming witnesses, prompt reports to the authorities and apparently unusual movements, but they rarely contain instrumented evidence. A gendarmerie statement proves that a report was formally made and records the witness’s words close to the event. It does not by itself prove the witness’s estimate of distance, altitude, speed or physical shape. The unresolved object over Bourges On 26 October 1980, at about 10.35 in the morning, an amateur pilot watching from his property near Bourges saw several flashes that reminded him of aircraft beacons. He then reported a much brighter flame like light, followed by black smoke or a dark trail. He heard no explosion or engine noise and feared that an aircraft had crashed, prompting contact with the nearest control tower and a gendarmerie search. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGES (18) 26.10.1980 GEIPAN... The official record is more restrained than some later summaries of the incident. In particular, the surviving GEIPAN page and gendarmerie document describe bright flashes, a flame and a dark smoke trail; they do not establish that the main object was green. Describing it as “the green Bourges object” therefore adds a colour detail that the accessible primary file does not securely support. That discrepancy is a small but instructive example of how memorable UFO descriptions can become sharper in retelling than they were in the original record. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGES (18) 26.10.1980 GEIPAN... The authorities treated the report seriously because an aviation accident was a plausible immediate concern. Patrols and enquiries found no crashed aircraft, no second witness, no reported aircraft in difficulty and no known local rocket or flare firing. The witness himself was regarded as knowledgeable about aviation, which explains why the apparent beacon like flashes and subsequent flame were alarming. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Yet those negative checks did not establish an extraordinary object. They only ruled out several specific possibilities that could be verified locally. No photograph, physical fragment, radar return, exact bearing sequence or measured duration appears in the published file. There was also no independent witness with whom the path could be triangulated. GEIPAN consequently lists the case as Category C, “lack of reliable information”, rather than Category D, unexplained after adequate investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGES (18) 26.10.1980 GEIPAN... Several ordinary possibilities remain open, although none can now be demonstrated. A distant aircraft could produce bright intermittent reflections before becoming obscured by a dark exhaust or cloud feature; a short lived meteor or atmospheric event might create a luminous flare and trail; and the witness’s impression of a flame descending near Bourges may have been affected by uncertain distance. The file does not contain enough geometry or timing to discriminate between those possibilities. Its unresolved status is therefore mainly an evidence gap, not evidence that conventional explanations failed. That is the case’s real importance in Cher’s UFO history. It shows that even a competent witness and a rapid official response cannot compensate for missing measurements. The gendarmerie established that no obvious crash had occurred, but the investigation was designed primarily to check public safety concerns, not to reconstruct the phenomenon scientifically. The Nançay–Vouzeron multi witness report The 18 August 1987 case initially looked stronger. At about 22.15, three residents in two Cher communities reported an unusual luminous phenomenon. One witness observed from Nançay, while two others watched from Vouzeron. A witness contacted the gendarmerie the next day; statements were taken on 21 and 24 August, the local enquiry closed on 11 September, and the file was passed to GEPAN, GEIPAN’s predecessor. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 The reports shared a broadly similar direction of arrival but differed in important details. The Nançay witness described two lights moving together, without blinking or sound. One Vouzeron witness recalled a bright white, cigar shaped glow that turned and accelerated towards Vierzon during an observation lasting about five minutes. The second Vouzeron witness described a large white light, then two smaller white lights and a flashing red light beneath it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 Those differences weakened the assumption that all three had necessarily seen one object. GEIPAN noted that the Nançay description could refer to a different aircraft or light source from that seen at Vouzeron. It also found a potential problem with witness independence: the Nançay witness knew the other two and discussed the event with them on the morning after the sighting, before the formal statements were completed. Multiple witnesses increase evidential value only when their observations can be shown to be independent and geometrically compatible. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 Weather records supported the witnesses’ claim that conditions were generally clear. That made a distant aerial object readily visible, but did not determine its identity. More seriously, the 1987 investigation had preserved no usable contemporary air traffic record, angular positions or angular size measurements. Investigators had not resolved the discrepancies between the accounts or established whether the witnesses’ descriptions had influenced one another. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete27 Notes d'enquete27 When GEIPAN re examined the old file decades later, it concluded that aircraft could reproduce most of the reported features. Modern flight path reconstruction showed that services leaving Paris for West Africa regularly cross the Nançay area and turn towards the south or south west there. The reported change of direction towards Vierzon was therefore consistent with an"},{"title":"Why Correze Matters in French UFO History","url":"/why-correze-matters-in-french-ufo/","description":"Corrèze has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landing” or a famous military encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Correze Matters in French UFO History Why Correze Matters in French UFO History Why Correze Matters in French UFO History Why Correze Matters in French UFO History Why Correze Matters in French UFO History Corrèze has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landing” or a famous military encounter. Corrèze has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landing” or a famous military encounter. correze Introduction The strongest overall conclusion is therefore restrained. Corrèze has produced unusual lights, apparent objects and small clusters of witnesses, but the surviving evidence is usually incomplete. Some reports have been identified as aircraft, reflections or probable light projections; others remain unclassified only because reliable information is missing. The department’s UFO history is most useful as a study in how sightings are reported, investigated and sometimes weakened by later checking. [Geipan] geipan.fr Temoin 6photos.pdf Questionnaire terre R.pdf... En conséquence le GEIPAN conclut uniquement sur ce qui est décrit et fait l’étrangeté vé... The 1954 sightings that put Corrèze on the UFO map The autumn of 1954 produced an exceptional surge of flying saucer reports across France. Within Corrèze, observations associated with Tulle and Ussel became especially influential because the writer Aimé Michel incorporated them into an alleged geographical alignment running broadly from Bayonne to Vichy. This supposed line, commonly known from the initials of those two cities, became one of the best known arguments in early French UFO literature. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The original claim appeared impressive. Reports attributed to Bayonne, Lencouacq, Tulle, Ussel, Gelles and Vichy were said to have occurred on 24 September 1954 and to form an almost straight line across France. For readers at the time, the apparent combination of timing and geometry suggested that the sightings might represent the route of a controlled object rather than a random collection of mistakes. Corrèze occupied the middle of this proposed pattern, giving two relatively obscure local reports an importance far beyond their individual evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Later archival checking badly damaged the argument. Researchers comparing the published accounts with contemporary newspaper material found that the dates had been compressed or copied incorrectly. The Ussel report appears to belong to 20 September and the Tulle report to 22 September, rather than both occurring on the 24th. Other points in the alignment were also assigned different or uncertain dates. Once the reports were restored to their likely chronology, the supposed same day pattern largely disappeared. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org 24 septembre 1954, la fameuse \"ligne BAVIC\" pour \"BAyonne VIChy\"; mais en... Tulle [En fait le 22 septembre], Type IV; Ussel [En fait le... This does not establish what the individual witnesses saw. It does, however, weaken the claim that the Corrèze observations were parts of a coordinated flight path. The episode remains important because it demonstrates a recurring problem in historical UFO research: an error in a newspaper date can be repeated in books, maps and later summaries until it appears to be a solid fact. In this instance, the most celebrated interpretation became less persuasive when researchers returned to the underlying chronology. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. What the official Corrèze files contain France has maintained an unusually formal system for handling reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES, receives and analyses testimony, sometimes with information supplied by the gendarmerie, civil aviation, the armed forces and weather services. Cases are classified from A, meaning identified, through B, probably identified, and C, unidentified because information is inadequate, to D, unexplained after investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The distinction between C and D is crucial when reading Corrèze’s records. A class C case is not an official declaration that something extraordinary occurred. It normally means that investigators could not reach a dependable conclusion because the observation was too brief, the direction was uncertain, supporting witnesses were unavailable or useful photographs and measurements did not exist. GEIPAN’s published national figures also show that fully unexplained class D cases form only a small minority of its archive. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Several Corrèze files illustrate the range of outcomes. Laroche près Feyt, 11 May 1977: A witness reported two enormous orange luminous balls apparently stationary about 50 metres above the ground and strongly illuminating the area. Roughly a minute later, the witness heard what sounded like helicopter blades and saw an intense yellow light. No supporting testimony was collected, and the case was classified C because reliable information was insufficient. The mention of rotor noise makes an aircraft related explanation plausible, but the surviving record cannot establish one. [Geipan] geipan.fr LAROCHE PRES FEYT (19) 11.05.1977 GEIPAN... Brive la Gaillarde, 11 January 1979: Several testimony records describe an apparently slow, oval object seen in an urban setting at about 6.45 am. Although the witness description included red and green colouring and an estimated size of several metres, GEIPAN ultimately classified the incident A, meaning identified. The public summary does not name the specific identified phenomenon, which limits what readers can independently assess, but the official classification does not support treating it as an unresolved Corrèze encounter. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Near Saint Privat, 16 November 2002: A witness saw a fast moving light resembling a rocket for approximately two seconds. With no sustained observation, corroborating evidence or detailed trajectory, GEIPAN classified the report C. Its unresolved status reflects the extreme brevity of the event rather than high evidential strength. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[D980] vers SAINT PRIVAT (19) 16.11.2002 GEIPAN... These cases show why catalogue labels should not be mistaken for measures of strangeness. The Laroche près Feyt report sounds much more dramatic than the Saint Privat flash, yet both entered the same category because investigators lacked enough dependable material to decide what happened. UFO hoax fools several French media • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English Open on YouTube Favars: many observers, but only limited evidence One of Corrèze’s more interesting recent reports occurred at Favars on 29 November 2019. Shortly before 10 pm, a witness noticed what initially appeared to be a very bright star. Nine other people were reportedly called onto the terrace and watched the phenomenon’s changing appearance, including apparent disappearances, divisions and movements. Attempts were made to record it with a mobile telephone, and additional video material was later supplied. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. At first glance, the number of people involved makes the case sound unusually strong. The evidential difficulty is that GEIPAN received only one detailed testimony, while the movements described by the principal witness were not confirmed by the one additional witness whom investigators were able to contact. Nor were the movements apparent in the submitted videos. This illustrates the difference between having several people present and having several independent, well documented accounts. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Investigators considered an astronomical misidentification likely. The reported duration of about 80 minutes and the intense scintillation were consistent with a bright star seen low through turbulent air, while an out of focus mobile phone image could make a point of light appear to change shape. Yet the obvious stellar candidates did not fit all the reported directions and times. Sirius had not risen at the beginning of the observation, while Procyon did not match the witness’s stated position. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN therefore classified Favars as C rather than declaring it explained or genuinely anomalous. The most economical reading is that an astronomical object probably contributed to the experience, while uncertain directions, incomplete witness collection and ambiguous video prevented a secure identification. The case was not strengthened by later investigation; instead, the enquiry exposed discrepancies between the most striking parts of the account and the available corroboration. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Recent sightings and ordinary explanations"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave/","description":"Tulle and Ussel became famous through a supposed same-day UFO route, but corrected newspaper dates largely dismantled the pattern.","breadcrumb":"Why Correze Matters in French UFO History","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave How a Date Error Made Corrèze Famous Correze UFO Files 1954 Wave 1954 Wave How a Date Error Made Corrèze Famous Tulle and Ussel became famous through a supposed same day UFO route, but corrected newspaper dates largely dismantled the pattern. Tulle and Ussel became famous through a supposed same day UFO route, but corrected newspaper dates largely dismantled the pattern. event time window What witnesses in Tulle and Ussel reportedly saw How the Bayonne Vichy alignment was constructed Why later archive checks weakened the claim correze 1954 wave Introduction Corrèze became famous in the history of France’s 1954 UFO wave largely because two reports near Tulle and Ussel were placed on an apparently remarkable straight line running from Bayonne to Vichy. The six point “BAVIC” alignment was presented as evidence that sightings reported on 24 September 1954 followed a geographical pattern too precise to be accidental. Later archive checks removed the foundation of that claim. The Tulle observation appears to have occurred on 22 September, while the incident near Ussel was reported as taking place on 20 September. Several other points on the line were also wrongly dated or poorly located. Once the newspaper chronology was corrected, there was no convincing same day route through Corrèze. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 20 septembre, Ussel, Corrèze... This does not prove that the witnesses saw nothing unusual. It does mean that the most celebrated interpretation of their reports was built from unreliable catalogue data. The episode matters because it shows how a modest local sighting can acquire an impressive theoretical meaning when publication dates, observation dates and approximate place names are treated as interchangeable. Flying Carrots & Little Divers The French UFO Wave of 1954 The Dark Histories Podcast Channel: Vintage Radio Waves Open on YouTube What witnesses in Tulle and Ussel reportedly saw The two Corrèze cases were quite different. They were not matching descriptions of one object moving across the department, and the reported times did not form a clear journey from one town to the other. At Tulle, the surviving account concerns a brief observation attributed to a man named Besse, usually described as a draughtsman or engineer employed by the French electricity authority. He reportedly saw a saucer like object change colour, passing through shades described as pink, green and yellow. Some later versions say that he watched it through powerful binoculars, although that detail was itself disputed in subsequent summaries. The report was short, with little information about direction, angular size, duration, weather or surrounding reference points. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Mr. Besse, an engineer with the French Electricity Board, claimed to have seen a saucer turn pink, then green, then yellow. VICHY... This lack of detail makes the Tulle case difficult to assess independently. Rapidly changing colours can be reported when a bright astronomical object is viewed through unstable air, while aircraft lights and other distant sources can also appear to change colour. Yet the surviving evidence is too sparse to select a particular explanation with confidence. Its importance came chiefly from its assigned position and date, not from the strength of its witness documentation. The Ussel report was more elaborate. It actually occurred at La Chassagne, near Alleyrat, several kilometres outside Ussel. According to an account published in Le Parisien Libéré , farm worker Elie or Etienne Cisterne was returning a tractor after working a neighbouring field when he saw a luminous object apparently coming towards him. He reportedly jumped down and lay on the ground in fear. The light remained nearby for several minutes, then seemed to move again, prompting him to flee. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 20 septembre, Ussel, Corrèze... Two other people supplied limited supporting observations. The farm owner, Mr Brindel, saw a large moving reddish glow in the direction of the property while returning from Ussel. A servant named Maria also reportedly noticed the luminous phenomenon but did not initially consider it important. An upper part of a tree was later said to show signs resembling heat damage. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 20 septembre, Ussel, Corrèze... These details made Ussel the most memorable Corrèze point on the proposed line. However, the evidence was not as strong as later catalogue labels such as “multiple witnesses” or “physical traces” might suggest. Cisterne supplied the principal close account; the other witnesses saw a distant glow rather than necessarily confirming its shape, height or behaviour. No surviving technical examination establishes when the tree damage occurred or what caused it. The report therefore remains an intriguing but weakly documented historical incident, not a verified encounter with a structured craft. The location is also important. La Chassagne was more than seven kilometres from central Ussel and, according to later geographical checking, several kilometres away from the exact BAVIC line. Calling it simply “Ussel” made the plotted point appear cleaner than the underlying report justified. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 20 septembre, Ussel, Corrèze... How the Bayonne–Vichy alignment was constructed French writer Aimé Michel developed the alignment argument while studying the dense national wave of reports from autumn 1954. In his 1958 book Mystérieux Objets Célestes , he proposed that sightings occurring within a short interval sometimes lay along straight lines—or, more precisely, along great circles on the Earth’s surface. He called the idea orthoteny. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Aimé Michel Aimé Michel Its best known example joined Bayonne in south western France to Vichy in the centre. Six reports attributed to 24 September were plotted at: Bayonne; Lencouacq; Tulle; [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Source details in endnotes. Ussel; [tourisme hautecorreze.fr] tourisme hautecorreze.fr Source details in endnotes. Gelles; Vichy. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Mr. Besse, an engineer with the French Electricity Board, claimed to have seen a saucer turn pink, then green, then yellow. VICHY... On a map, the points appeared to form a line approximately 485 kilometres long. Corrèze occupied its central portion, so Tulle and Ussel became crucial links rather than peripheral reports. A coincidence involving six of nine reported locations seemed far harder to dismiss than an alignment of only two or three. The theory was attractive because it appeared to turn chaotic newspaper stories into measurable evidence. A straight line suggested organisation: perhaps a route, corridor or relationship between events. Yet the individual reports did not resemble successive sightings of one object. Their descriptions varied, and their reported times did not progress along the line in the order expected from a simple flight path. Contemporary criticism therefore questioned what physical process the geometry was supposed to represent, even before the dates themselves were re examined. [rr0.org] rr0.org Orthoteny, the \"Straight Line Mystery Orthoteny, the \"Straight Line Mystery There was also a methodological problem. In any sufficiently large collection of places, especially during a nationwide reporting wave, researchers can draw many possible lines after seeing where the points fall. The probability of finding a striking arrangement depends on how many dates, locations, tolerances and combinations were available for inspection—not merely on the apparent precision of the selected line. Astronomer Donald Menzel challenged the statistical reasoning in the 1960s. Jacques and Janine Vallée subsequently used computer simulations to examine whether alignments could arise in artificial sighting distributions. Their work indicated that many apparently notable lines could be produced by chance, although debate continued over particular examples. The BAVIC line nevertheless retained special status because six supposedly simultaneous reports seemed unusually persuasive. [cantab.net] cantab.net Alumni Menzel < Furness forumla In Part 1 of his article, which appeared in the May/June, 1965, number of this REVIEW, he asked Aimé Michel several questions concerning... Aliens & UFOs: The 1954 UFO Invasion of France, a wave of UFO sightings that occurred in France Channel: williamdefalco Open on YouTube Why the dates collapsed under archive checking The decisive weakness emerged when Michel Jeantheau returned to contemporary newspapers during the 1970s. Instead of relying on later catalogues and national press summaries, he compared the claimed dates with articles"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files/","description":"GEIPAN records range from identified incidents to poorly documented mysteries, revealing why unresolved does not always mean extraordinary.","breadcrumb":"Why Correze Matters in French UFO History","level":2,"text":"Official Files What Do Corrèze's Official UFO Files Prove? Correze UFO Files Official Files Official Files What Do Corrèze's Official UFO Files Prove? GEIPAN records range from identified incidents to poorly documented mysteries, revealing why unresolved does not always mean extraordinary. GEIPAN records range from identified incidents to poorly documented mysteries, revealing why unresolved does not always mean extraordinary. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies reports from A to D Key cases from Laroche près Feyt to Favars Why witnesses, video and duration can still leave doubt correze official files Introduction Corrèze’s official UFO files do not prove that an extraordinary craft visited the department. They show something more useful: how reports that initially sound remarkable can end up in very different evidential categories. In the public GEIPAN archive, Corrèze cases include identified aircraft, the International Space Station, a satellite flare and other probable explanations, alongside reports left unresolved because the available information was too weak. The archive page reviewed for this article lists no Corrèze case in the strongest unexplained category, D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN That distinction matters because “unidentified” can mean two almost opposite things. A well documented phenomenon may remain unexplained despite investigation, but a case may also remain unidentified because investigators lack precise directions, independent statements, original photographs or reliable timing. The Corrèze record is dominated by the second problem. From the sparse 1977 report at Laroche près Feyt to the videos recorded near Favars in 2019, the recurring lesson is that an intriguing account is not necessarily a strong case. How GEIPAN’s A to D system changes the meaning of “unexplained” GEIPAN is the French national unit that collects, analyses and publishes reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. It operates within the French space agency, CNES, and can receive information from private witnesses, the gendarmerie, police, civil aviation and other public bodies. Its investigation may involve checking aircraft movements, astronomical positions, satellites, weather conditions and the geometry of the observation. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Its familiar classifications are best read as assessments of both explanation and evidence quality: A: the reported phenomenon has been identified. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, although not demonstrated with complete certainty. C: the information is insufficiently reliable or complete to reach a sound conclusion. D: the phenomenon remains unexplained after investigation despite evidence of sufficient quality and quantity. This means C is not a halfway version of D. A C case is generally an evidential failure: investigators cannot establish what happened because the record is too incomplete. A D case is potentially more interesting because the unresolved status survives a meaningful investigation. CNES’s current national figures illustrate the difference. It reports that 24.6 per cent of archived phenomena are clearly identified, 39.7 per cent probably identified and 32.4 per cent unresolved for lack of data. Only 3.3 per cent remain unidentified after investigation. In other words, most officially unresolved reports belong to the low information category, not the small group of comparatively robust mysteries. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... The categories are nevertheless expert judgements rather than mechanical verdicts. A study presented at a GEIPAN workshop examined the consistency of historical GEPAN assessments and found that experts agreed on the same four part classification in about 65 per cent of multiply assessed reports. Agreement improved when A and B were combined, but the results still showed that judgement calls about information quality, witness reliability and strangeness can affect classification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANqualitative expert evaluationJuly 16, 2014 — by JP ROSPARS · 2014 · Cited by 1 — A classification will be considered as reliable if... Published: July 16, 2014 For Corrèze, the practical consequence is clear: the letter attached to a file must be read alongside the investigation notes. A classification tells readers where the case finished, but the reasoning explains why. What the Corrèze case list contains The GEIPAN search results reviewed for Corrèze span observations from 1977 to 2022. They form a small and uneven dataset rather than a continuous record of activity. Some entries preserve old gendarmerie material; others began as modern online witness reports. The list includes: Laroche près Feyt, 1977 — C , insufficient reliable information. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr laroche pres feyt 1977 0500406 laroche pres feyt 1977 0500406 Brive, 1979 — A , identified. Brive la Gaillarde, 1983 — B , probably identified. Saint Privas, 1993 — C , insufficient reliable information. Ussac, 2010 — B , attributed to the International Space Station. Meyrignac l’Église, 2011 — B , attributed to an Iridium satellite flare. Favars, 2019 — C , insufficient reliable information, with an astronomical misidentification considered plausible. Malemort sur Corrèze, 2020 — B , attributed to a satellite. Seilhac, 2021 — A , identified as an aircraft. The A20 between Brive and Limoges, 2022 — B , attributed to a pink sky glow. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Several conclusions follow immediately. First, the archive is not a catalogue containing only mysterious events. Identified and probably identified reports are retained because they show what witnesses originally found puzzling and how that puzzle was resolved. Secondly, Corrèze’s public case list contains more ordinary explanations than enduring mysteries. Thirdly, its C cases should not be counted as evidence equivalent to a D case. The list is also shaped by reporting behaviour. It records cases submitted or preserved through official channels, not every unusual light ever seen in the department. A rise or gap in entries can reflect public awareness, changes in reporting systems, archive survival and GEIPAN publication practices as much as any change in the sky. 14:41 GEIPAN : dans les coulisses de l’organisme qui étudie les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés Channel: france tv · Views: 4.5K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 14 minutes Open on YouTube Laroche près Feyt: an official file without enough evidence The Laroche près Feyt report dates from 11 May 1977 and is classified C. GEIPAN’s searchable index describes it simply as a case lacking reliable information. That wording is the central fact: the official archive confirms that a report existed, but it does not convert the report into a substantiated extraordinary event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN This older file illustrates the limitations of historical official material. A gendarmerie statement can preserve a witness’s account more securely than a later retelling, but official provenance does not automatically supply missing measurements. Investigators still need dependable information about direction, elevation, duration, movement, weather, celestial objects, aircraft activity and the witness’s position. When these details are absent or too uncertain, competing explanations cannot be tested properly. The surviving classification therefore answers one question but leaves another open. It establishes that GEIPAN could not identify the reported phenomenon from the available record. It does not establish that investigators encountered a well documented object displaying capabilities inconsistent with aircraft, astronomy or atmospheric effects. This is why Laroche près Feyt should be described as unresolved through insufficiency, not as a confirmed anomaly. Its value within Corrèze’s UFO history lies largely in showing how far an official file can fall short of evidential completeness. Favars: many observers and video, but still a weak case The Favars report of 29 November 2019 is the most revealing Corrèze example because it appears, at first glance, to contain several features that ought to strengthen a case. A witness noticed an unusually bright, scintillating light shortly before 10 pm. Nine other people reportedly came outside to see it. The observation continued for roughly 80 minutes, and video recordings were eventually supplied to investigators. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAVARS (19) 29.11.2019 GEIPAN... The descriptions included apparent appearances and disappearances, separation into parts and movement away from the main light. Those details give the account its strangeness. Yet GEIPAN classified it C rather than D, assigning moderate consistency to the evidence and stating that the case could not be identified because reliable"},{"title":"One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive","url":"/corse-du-sud/","description":"Corse-du-Sud has one standout official UFO case rather than a long catalogue of celebrated mysteries.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive Corse du Sud has one standout official UFO case rather than a long catalogue of celebrated mysteries. Corse du Sud has one standout official UFO case rather than a long catalogue of celebrated mysteries. Near Sainte Lucie de Porto Vecchio on 28 August 2014, a lone witness reported a large, silent, dark circular mass moving slowly across the early morning sky, accompanied by ordered groups of smaller dark shapes. corse du sud Introduction That judgement deserves attention, but not exaggeration. There was no photograph, no identified second witness, no confirmed radar target and no measurable distance or size. Elsewhere in the department’s official record , reported UFOs have been identified as a balloon or a bright meteor, while another case was too poorly documented to assess. The most defensible picture is therefore of a small and uneven archive: one genuinely unresolved report, several ordinary misidentifications and a number of stories whose evidential value is limited. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The 2014 Zonza sighting At about 5 am on 28 August 2014, a man staying at a campsite near Sainte Lucie de Porto Vecchio walked across an unlit courtyard after using a computer. Looking upwards between trees, he said he saw a large black circular form with several lights arranged near its centre. Ahead of and behind it were rows of smaller dark groups, all moving slowly and silently on a straight course from the direction of Conca towards Sardinia. He estimated that the observation lasted roughly ten minutes. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The witness did not describe dramatic acceleration, sharp turns, a landing or any physical effects. The formation simply continued until distance and the surrounding landscape removed it from view. He could not estimate its altitude, range or true dimensions, and he was uncertain whether some of the smaller shapes were triangular. These limitations matter because an object’s apparent size and speed cannot be calculated without reliable distance information. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... His immediate interpretation was not extraterrestrial. Because of the nearby military presence and contemporary events in the Middle East, he wondered whether he had seen some form of military activity. The gendarmerie statement records that he hesitated to report the incident for fear of ridicule, but attended the station later that day. This prompt formal record is stronger than a recollection first published years afterwards, although it does not establish that the perceived objects were physical craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The witness also said that he briefly spoke to another person at the campsite who appeared to confirm seeing the phenomenon. That individual was never identified or interviewed. GEIPAN therefore treated the file as a single witness case, not a corroborated multiple observer event. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Why GEIPAN left it unexplained GEIPAN is a technical service of France’s National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES. It collects reports, checks possible astronomical, meteorological, aviation and space related explanations, and publishes anonymised case material. Its classifications assess both the consistency of the available testimony and the degree of strangeness remaining after investigation. Category A denotes an identified phenomenon, B a probable explanation, C insufficient usable information and D an observation that remains unexplained after examination; older records may subdivide D into D1 and D2. [Geipan] geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPANFebruary 26, 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évalua... Published: February 26, 2021 In the Zonza investigation, the weather was described as clear with little cloud. The Moon was not visible, Venus and Jupiter were low in the east, and no notable satellite passage matched the account. Investigators checked a recorded re entry of the Fengyun satellite, but its timing and direction were inconsistent with the reported movement. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A nearby entertainment projector was also considered. Investigators contacted a Porto Vecchio nightclub equipped with such a device and were told that it had not been operating at 5 am. Cloud projection was additionally judged unpromising because the sky contained little cloud and the alleged phenomenon included dark masses rather than only moving light patches. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Aircraft were an obvious possibility because southern Corsica contains Figari airport and lies near the Solenzara military airfield. The report stated that Figari was closed to traffic during the relevant hours and that military radar had recorded no air activity in the area at that time. The investigator was unable to obtain a full meeting with officials at Solenzara, however, so the aviation check was not as complete as a reader might wish. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The inquiry briefly considered a visual or neurological disturbance resembling an ocular migraine. That idea could reproduce a dark circular form and points of light, but the witness reported no headache or known medical problem and claimed that another person had also noticed the event. GEIPAN rated this and the other proposed explanations as very weak or nil, concluding that no plausible identification had been found. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The public case page was updated in June 2026 and now displays the broader classification D, while its narrative still states that the original conclusion was D1. This is best read as a change in presentation or classification terminology, not as new evidence strengthening the sighting. The essential judgement remains the same: the phenomenon was not identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (2A) 28.08.2014 GEIPAN... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the case does not prove The official status “unexplained” means that the investigation did not establish a satisfactory conventional cause. It does not mean that an extraordinary vehicle was demonstrated. GEIPAN’s own method separates residual strangeness from proof of origin, and its case files can be revisited when better information becomes available. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Several weaknesses prevent the Zonza report from becoming strong physical evidence: No image or instrument record. The witness considered fetching a camera but did not do so. No traceable second witness. The unidentified camper could not be questioned independently. No confirmed radar return. The investigation reported no military radar activity corresponding to the sighting. No distance or scale. The apparent “large mass” could not be converted into a physical measurement. Uncertain viewing conditions. The report contains some tension between the witness’s memory that moonlight made the campsite easy to see and the astronomical check indicating that the Moon was not visible. [Geipan] geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 Modéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... There is also a possible resemblance to nocturnal bird formations. Large birds can appear as dark groups, move in coordinated lines and remain surprisingly quiet at a distance, particularly against a brightening pre dawn sky. Yet the reported central circular mass with internal lights does not fit that idea neatly, and the published GEIPAN report did not develop a bird hypothesis. It therefore remains a possible retrospective interpretation, not a demonstrated solution. The case is consequently stronger than an unsupported internet anecdote but weaker than a multi sensor aviation incident. Its principal value lies in"},{"title":"Official Record","url":"/official-record/","description":"The department's official archive contains one unresolved report alongside balloons, meteors and cases too weakly documented to judge.","breadcrumb":"One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive","level":2,"text":"Official Record How Unusual Is Corse du Sud's UFO Record? Corse du Sud UFOs Official Record Official Record How Unusual Is Corse du Sud's UFO Record? The department's official archive contains one unresolved report alongside balloons, meteors and cases too weakly documented to judge. The department's official archive contains one unresolved report alongside balloons, meteors and cases too weakly documented to judge. dataset evidence The small and uneven GEIPAN case archive Identified reports, weak files and the lone unresolved case What the pattern says about evidence and misidentification corse du sud official record Introduction Corse du Sud ’s official UFO record is unusual mainly because it is so small and so heavily weighted towards ordinary explanations. GEIPAN, the French space agency service that investigates unidentified aerial reports, has published files from the department involving a scientific balloon, a bright meteor, aircraft, a laser, rocket debris and a star. Two reports could not be assessed properly because dependable information was lacking. Only one geographically valid Corse du Sud case is presently classified as genuinely unexplained: the 2014 sighting near Zonza. [Geipan] geipan.fr AJACCIO (2A) 14.12.1976 14/12/1976 A Corse du Sud Ballon scientifique ou technologique 13/09/2011 1976 12 14... That does not make the Zonza report trivial, but it does put it in perspective. The official archive does not show a sustained local “flap”, a concentration of radar incidents or repeated encounters around an airport or military installation. It shows something more prosaic and more useful: how dramatically different kinds of evidence can sit under the same popular label of “UFO”, from conclusively identified objects to reports that remain mysterious only because there is too little information to test them. The small and uneven GEIPAN archive GEIPAN is part of France’s National Centre for Space Studies, CNES. It generally uses the term unidentified aerospace phenomenon rather than “UFO”, because witnesses may have perceived a light, atmospheric effect or distant object rather than a physical craft. Its classifications are based on two linked questions: how strange the observation remains after conventional explanations have been tested, and how consistent or dependable the available evidence is. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The categories are easy to misunderstand: A means the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B means a conventional explanation is probable, although not demonstrated as completely as in an A case. C means GEIPAN could not reach a judgement because the information was inadequate or unreliable. D1 or D2 means the phenomenon remained unexplained after investigation, with D2 reserved for cases combining greater strangeness and stronger evidential consistency. A C case is therefore not “almost unexplained”. It is an unusable file. GEIPAN itself stresses that C reports belong apart from A, B and D cases because investigators have not been able to make a meaningful assessment at all. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. For Corse du Sud, the published results contain a core of at least eight geographically valid departmental entries: Date and placeGEIPAN classificationPublished explanation or statusAjaccio, 14 December 1976AScientific or technological balloonDepartment wide report, 8 March 1987ABright meteor or fireballOta, 27 September 1993CInsufficient reliable informationTavaco, 8 August 1996BLaserFigari, 18 July 2007BAircraftZonza, 28 August 2014D1UnexplainedCuttoli Corticchiato, 7 April 2021ARocket related materialSan Gavino di Tenda, 10 December 2025ASirius [Geipan] geipan.fr AJACCIO (2A) 14.12.1976 14/12/1976 A Corse du Sud Ballon scientifique ou technologique 13/09/2011 1976 12 14... Even this small count requires caution. GEIPAN search results have also labelled reports from Bastia and San Martino di Lota as belonging to Corse du Sud, although both places are officially in Haute Corse. The Bastia entry was identified as an airliner, while the San Martino di Lota report was classified C for lack of reliable information. Their appearance under Corse du Sud seems to be a database or historical coding problem rather than evidence from the department itself. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This matters because a simple filtered count can overstate the department’s record. The archive is a working historical database assembled from reports created under different administrative codes and investigative systems. It should not be treated as a perfectly cleaned statistical dataset. Identified reports dominate the record The clearest pattern is not mystery but misidentification. Objects that appeared remarkable to witnesses were usually recognised once investigators compared their appearance, direction, timing and behaviour with known phenomena. The 1976 Ajaccio case, for example, is classified A as a scientific or technological balloon. Balloons can appear stationary or move slowly, change brightness as they rotate, and seem much larger than they are when viewed without a reliable distance reference. Those characteristics make them recurrent sources of unusual aerial reports, particularly when seen at altitude in clear light. The official conclusion does not imply that the witness invented anything; it means the described phenomenon was matched confidently to a conventional object. [Geipan] geipan.fr AJACCIO (2A) 14.12.1976 14/12/1976 A Corse du Sud Ballon scientifique ou technologique 13/09/2011 1976 12 14... The department wide report of 8 March 1987 was identified as a fireball: an exceptionally bright meteor. Such events can produce intense light, apparent colour changes, fragmentation and a path visible across a wide area. Their speed and brilliance can make them seem closer, lower or more structured than they really are. In classification terms, this is again an A case—an initially unidentified spectacle that ceased to be mysterious once it was compared with a known astronomical event. [Geipan] geipan.fr DEP) CORSE DU SUD (2A) 08.03.1987 08/03/1987 A Corse du Sud Bolide 15/03/2010 1987 03 08... ERQUY (22) 20.04.2016 20/04/2... The Tavaco case of August 1996 was placed in category B with a laser as the probable explanation. Unlike an A judgement, category B leaves some uncertainty, but GEIPAN considered the identified cause more likely than an unknown phenomenon. The Figari report of July 2007 was similarly judged probably attributable to an aircraft. The proximity of Figari airport makes aircraft identification especially relevant, although the B classification signals that the supporting evidence was not complete enough for absolute certainty. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANTAVACO (2A) 08.08.1996 08/08/1996 B Corse du Sud Laser 22/03/2007 1996 08 08... Two newer entries reinforce the same overall pattern. At Cuttoli Corticchiato on 7 April 2021, the reported phenomenon was identified as rocket related material. At San Gavino di Tenda on 10 December 2025, GEIPAN concluded that the light was Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. Both received category A classifications. These cases show why the archive cannot be read as a historical list frozen around the classic flying saucer era: modern space activity and familiar astronomical objects continue to generate reports. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The Sirius identification is particularly instructive. Bright stars close to the horizon can appear to flash, change colour or move slightly because atmospheric turbulence distorts their light and because involuntary eye movements are more noticeable when the surrounding sky offers few reference points. A report can therefore be sincere, vivid and still arise from a stationary astronomical source. GEIPAN’s system is designed to separate the witness’s experience from the later physical explanation rather than dismissing the experience itself. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANSAN GAVINO DI TENDA (20) 10.12.2025 10/12/2025 A Corse du Sud Sirius (étoile) 27/02/2026 2025... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Weak files are not unexplained phenomena The 1993 Ota report was classified C because GEIPAN lacked reliable information. The publicly searchable record does not support treating it as a strong mystery: the classification means the investigators could not determine what happened, not that conventional possibilities had been eliminated. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. That distinction is essential in a department with so few files. Placing Ota alongside Zonza under a loose heading such as “unsolved cases” would conceal the most important difference between them. Zonza was investigated and still resisted a satisfactory explanation. Ota remained undecidable because the"},{"title":"Zonza Sighting","url":"/zonza-sighting/","description":"The 2014 Zonza report remains unexplained, but its missing images, scale and confirmed second witness sharply limit what it can prove.","breadcrumb":"One Unexplained Case in a Sparse UFO Archive","level":2,"text":"Zonza Sighting Why the Zonza Sighting Remains Unexplained Corse du Sud Zonza Sighting Zonza Sighting Why the Zonza Sighting Remains Unexplained The 2014 Zonza report remains unexplained, but its missing images, scale and confirmed second witness sharply limit what it can prove. The 2014 Zonza report remains unexplained, but its missing images, scale and confirmed second witness sharply limit what it can prove. event time window What the witness reported before dawn Which conventional explanations investigators tested Why unexplained does not mean extraterrestrial corse du sud zonza sighting Introduction The Zonza sighting remains unexplained because investigators could not match the witness’s account to aircraft, satellites, atmospheric debris, clouds or local lighting. At about 5 am on 28 August 2014, near Sainte Lucie de Porto Vecchio, a man reported a large, silent, dark circular form moving slowly towards Sardinia, accompanied by ordered groups of smaller dark shapes. He reported it to the gendarmerie that evening, and a GEIPAN investigator later visited the site. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... That official attention makes Zonza the most substantial unresolved case in Corse du Sud ’s published record. It does not, however, turn the sighting into proof of an extraordinary craft. There is no photograph, no identified second witness, no confirmed radar return and no usable estimate of distance or size. GEIPAN’s D1 classification means that a moderately strange case survived investigation with reasonable testimonial consistency—not that an extraterrestrial explanation was established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANZONZA (2A) 28.08.2014 GEIPAN... What the witness reported before dawn The observation took place at a campsite on the eastern side of southern Corsica. According to the investigation report, the witness had awakened because of the heat, used a computer in a communal bungalow and checked the time before walking back across the unlit grounds at about 5 am. Looking upwards between trees, he noticed a formation travelling on a straight course from the direction of Conca towards Sardinia. He estimated that it remained visible for approximately ten minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The central feature was described as an enormous black circle against a clear sky. Its rim appeared lighter, while between four and eight points of light formed another circle near its centre. The witness could not decide whether the dark form was a flat disc or something more spherical. In front of it were two rows of several smaller dark groups, apparently followed by further groups behind. He thought some components might have been triangular but repeatedly expressed uncertainty about that detail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Everything reportedly moved at the same slow, constant speed. There was no engine noise, sudden acceleration, turn, hovering phase, landing or physical effect. The objects simply continued away until terrain and distance prevented further observation. This matters because the account contains no obvious sign of controlled manoeuvring beyond an orderly looking formation and straight movement. GEIPAN itself noted that the reported motion showed no clearly intentional behaviour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The witness’s first interpretation was military rather than extraterrestrial. Solenzara Air Base lies north of the area, and he associated the sighting with military activity linked in his mind to events in Syria and Iraq. He returned to bed afterwards and reported no fear, illness or environmental disturbance. Later that day, after speaking to colleagues and friends, he attended the local gendarmerie because he felt the observation deserved to be recorded despite fearing ridicule. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The unconfirmed second observer The most tantalising part of the account is also one of its weakest. The witness said that he encountered another man near the campsite’s staff entrance and asked whether he was hallucinating. According to the witness, the man replied that he had seen it too, then continued on his way. The witness did not know his name, and neither the gendarmerie nor the GEIPAN investigator located him. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This cannot be treated as independent corroboration. Every detail about the exchange comes through the principal witness, and the unknown man provided no description, drawing or statement of his own. GEIPAN consequently recorded the case as having no confirmed additional witness. The alleged encounter may support the witness’s sincerity, but it cannot demonstrate that two people independently perceived the same shapes in the same way. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Which conventional explanations investigators tested GEIPAN did more than record the narrative. An investigator was assigned on 5 September, roughly a week after the event, and later interviewed the witness, inspected the location and produced a questionnaire and reconstruction. Weather, astronomical conditions, satellites, atmospheric re entry, civil aviation, military activity and local lighting were considered. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Aircraft and military traffic. Figari Airport was reportedly closed to normal traffic between midnight and 6.30 am. Investigators were unable to obtain a direct explanation from officials at Solenzara Air Base, although they were told that fighter traffic in the wider region was heavy at the time. The report nevertheless states that military radar showed no aerial activity in the relevant zone and time period. GEIPAN regarded ordinary aircraft as a poor fit because of the silence, unusual appearance and absent radar trace. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... That finding is useful but not absolute. The published report does not reproduce primary radar logs, specify the radar coverage involved or explain whether small, low or non transponding objects would necessarily have been detected. “No radar activity reported” therefore weakens a conventional aircraft explanation without proving that the sky was physically empty. Satellites and atmospheric debris. Investigators checked for notable satellite passages and considered a re entering spacecraft because a slow, extended formation can sometimes be produced by fragmenting debris. A Feng Yun satellite re entry occurred that day, but GEIPAN found that its time and direction did not match the observation. Meteor activity and satellite re entry were rated as very weak explanations because the witness described persistent dark masses, not a brief succession of luminous fragments. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Clouds. The weather check found generally clear conditions, light winds and only limited cloud cover. A lenticular or otherwise unusually shaped cloud was considered because a dark mass can stand out against a brightening pre dawn sky. Investigators rejected it chiefly because the reported movement did not agree with the measured wind and because the central lights and organised surrounding groups remained unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Projected lighting. A sky projector at a Porto Vecchio nightclub was checked. The establishment reportedly confirmed that it was not operating at 5 am. With very little cloud on which a beam could appear, GEIPAN judged this explanation very weak. In any case, projected light would have difficulty accounting for the witness’s repeated description of black, apparently structured forms. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ZONZA (20) 28.08.2014 [E Cgei CR T,C,P,D S] compte rendu enquete v2 0"},{"title":"Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark","url":"/why-cote-dor-became-a-french-ufo/","description":"Côte-d’Or has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, decisive “spaceship” case.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark Côte d’Or has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, decisive “spaceship” case. Côte d’Or has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, decisive “spaceship” case. cote d or Introduction The strongest local material is preserved by GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES. Its files do not support claims of confirmed extraterrestrial craft. They instead divide Côte d’Or’s history into explained cases, reports too poorly documented to assess, and a very small unresolved remainder. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr t of the CNES (French Space Agency). Its mission...Read more... The 1954 cluster at Poncey sur l’Ignon The most substantial early Côte d’Or case occurred at Poncey sur l’Ignon on 2 and 4 October 1954, during the peak of France’s famous autumn “flying saucer” wave. Several residents reported separate luminous phenomena over two evenings, and gendarmes later recorded testimony, inspected disturbed ground and photographed the site. That combination of multiple witnesses, contemporary police documentation and a possible physical trace makes Poncey more significant than a simple newspaper anecdote. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... On 2 October, a farm worker finishing her evening duties saw an orange luminous form with greenish reflections. She initially considered the Moon, then interpreted the light as a vertical cylindrical object moving near the buildings. Relatives and a neighbour saw a light departing towards the south, although their descriptions were less detailed. GEIPAN found that the Moon was low in the south west and could not exclude a lunar misidentification partly altered by clouds. Because the viewing position and direction were not recorded precisely enough, the episode remains classified C: not analysable because reliable information is lacking. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... The events of 4 October were more elaborate. At about 7.30 pm, a young woman looking from a bedroom window reported a bright, internally illuminated, mushroom like or globular form above a tree. She judged it to be roughly 50 metres away and became frightened enough to take her child to a neighbour’s home. About an hour later, another resident arriving from work briefly saw a fast, luminous cigar shaped form crossing the sky from north to south. These observations were made separately, and no witness saw a craft land. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... Between the two reports, residents discovered a shallow rectangular disturbance in a nearby field, surrounded by overturned clods of earth. Gendarmes examined the marks the following evening and photographed them a day later. They investigated whether two family members had staged a joke—particularly relevant because the men had reportedly discussed making a saucer related prank—but rejected them as the likely authors after checking their movements. Wild boar activity, another prankster or ordinary ground disturbance remained possible. The photographs were taken after witnesses had walked around the area, reducing their evidential value. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... The central weakness is that no evidence proves the light caused the marks. The visual report, the later sky sighting and the disturbed soil were separate events connected mainly by proximity and timing. GEIPAN also found that a vehicle headlamp from a nearby road might have produced the first light, although the surviving information was too imprecise to test that idea properly. A private investigator revisited the location in 2015, but the original residents had moved or died and attempts to contact a surviving witness were unsuccessful. The case therefore stayed in category C rather than becoming an officially unexplained category D case. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... Poncey matters because it demonstrates both the value and the limits of early records. The gendarmerie preserved far than normally survives from a rural sighting, yet the file still lacks exact sight lines, uncontaminated trace evidence and a demonstrated link between the reported light and the ground marks. GEIPAN also notes that witnesses repeatedly referred to the flood of “flying saucer” stories then appearing in the press, showing that the national wave influenced how unfamiliar lights were interpreted and described. [Geipan] geipan.fr PONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 04.10.1954 GEIPAN... Reports from Brazey en Morvan in 1968 A less secure but locally memorable episode emerged around Brazey en Morvan in the summer of 1968. A contemporary newspaper report reproduced in a specialist press archive described two farmers who saw a brilliant white object resting or appearing to rest on a hill opposite their field. They said a dazzling, sparkling light then seemed to approach them. The report added that another local farmer had described an object several weeks earlier. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org De plus, le 21 juillet dernier, à quelques kilomètres de là, un autre objet avait été vu par un agriculteur de Brazey en Morvan.Les genda... The gendarmes of Liernais reportedly found no physical trace, while residents openly discussed the possibility of a hoax. The newspaper treated the witnesses as apparently sincere but left the basic question unresolved. This distinction is important: honest testimony does not guarantee an accurate estimate of distance, size or motion, particularly when a bright light is seen against distant terrain. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org De plus, le 21 juillet dernier, à quelques kilomètres de là, un autre objet avait été vu par un agriculteur de Brazey en Morvan.Les genda... Unlike Poncey, the Brazey material is not supported in the available public record by a detailed modern GEIPAN reconstruction. It should therefore be treated as an archival press case rather than a robust unresolved incident. It is useful evidence of local UFO interest and witness reporting in 1968, but not strong evidence of an extraordinary physical object. Une Histoire d'archives: des OVNIS en Bourgogne Channel: France 3 Bourgogne Franche Comté Open on YouTube The late 1970s concentration Côte d’Or’s official files show a noticeable concentration of reports between 1977 and 1979. These include cases at Vitteaux, Chenôve, Bierre lès Semur, Dijon, Argilly and Arc sur Tille. The cluster is historically interesting, although the cases did not all describe the same phenomenon and should not be treated as a single coordinated “flap”. GEIPAN now classifies some as explained, some as too incomplete to analyse and only one as unexplained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... A “saucer” that was probably the Moon At Bierre lès Semur in July 1978, a camper reported an orange, stationary light shaped like an upturned plate. He took three photographs, but nothing useful appeared after development. The object was low in the south west, and its later apparent movement was poorly documented. Originally regarded as unexplained, the case was subsequently reassessed by GEIPAN. [Geipan] geipan.fr BIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.1978 GEIPAN... Astronomical reconstruction placed the setting Moon at an azimuth matching the reported direction and only about two degrees above the horizon. Its reddish orange appearance, low elevation and partial obstruction by forming clouds could explain both the colour and the distorted “saucer” shape. Movement of cloud gaps may also have created an impression that the light itself shifted. GEIPAN consequently reclassified the case B, meaning that a lunar explanation is considered highly probable. [Geipan] geipan.fr BIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.1978 GEIPAN... This reassessment is one of the department’s clearest examples of later investigation weakening an apparently exotic claim. It does not imply that the witness invented anything. It shows how a familiar astronomical body can look unfamiliar when seen near the horizon, through cloud and under conditions of fatigue or surprise. Archive evidence Geipan logo GEIPAN / Wizly 08 · Wikimedia Commons · 2005 · 117278775 GEIPAN is explicitly cited in the article as the principal archive for Côte‑d’Or UFO cases; its official logo is a clear, recognisable, directly relevant institutional identifier, whereas the other candidates are generic town hall photos or unrelated FBI documents. Public domain · View original record The frightening Argilly pursuit At Argilly on 3 June 1979, a motorist reported an orange luminous ball in her rear view mirror that seemed to follow"},{"title":"1970 s Cases","url":"/1970-s-cases/","description":"Cote-d'Or's 1977-79 reports range from probable Moon and planet sightings to incomplete files and one officially unresolved case.","breadcrumb":"Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark","level":2,"text":"1970 s Cases Why Cote d'Or's 1970 s UFO Wave Unravelled Cote d'Or UFOs 1970 s Cases 1970 s Cases Why Cote d'Or's 1970 s UFO Wave Unravelled Cote d'Or's 1977 79 reports range from probable Moon and planet sightings to incomplete files and one officially unresolved case. Cote d'Or's 1977 79 reports range from probable Moon and planet sightings to incomplete files and one officially unresolved case. event time window The spread of reports from Vitteaux to Arc sur Tille Moon, planets and the illusion of pursuit Explained, incomplete and unresolved verdicts cote d or 1970 s cases Introduction Between 1977 and 1979, Côte d’Or produced a small but revealing run of UFO reports. They did not describe one coherent “wave” or a single type of object. Instead, the official files range from a light conclusively identified as the Moon, through probable astronomical misidentifications and cases too incomplete to judge, to the unusual Arc sur Tille observation that remains officially unresolved. That mixture matters more than the raw number of reports. It shows how apparently dramatic experiences can separate under investigation: a “saucer” may become a low Moon seen through cloud, a light that seems to pursue a car may match a bright planet, and a striking account may remain unclassified simply because essential directions, timings or corroboration are missing. Arc sur Tille stands apart, but unresolved does not mean extraterrestrial. It means that the surviving evidence has not supported a sufficiently strong ordinary explanation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanVITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977 T2VITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977; Observation date. 30/05/1977; region. Bourgogne; Department. Côte d'Or; Cl... The spread from Vitteaux to Arc sur Tille The late 1970s reports were scattered across Côte d’Or rather than concentrated around one obvious hotspot. The official record includes Vitteaux in May 1977, Chenôve in November 1977, Bierre lès Semur in July 1978, Dijon in September 1978, Argilly in June 1979 and Arc sur Tille in December 1979. Their final classifications span the full range from explained to unresolved. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanVITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977 T2VITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977; Observation date. 30/05/1977; region. Bourgogne; Department. Côte d'Or; Cl... This is an important corrective to the idea of a unified local “UFO wave”. The reports occurred over two and a half years, in different settings and under different viewing conditions. Some involved motorists, others witnesses at home or outdoors. Some described stationary lights; others included apparent pursuit, rapid departure or a structured form near the ground. The similarities are mostly broad features common to many night sky reports: brightness, low apparent altitude, unusual colour and difficulty judging distance. GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, does not classify cases merely by how strange a witness found them. Its system weighs both residual strangeness and “consistency” or evidential strength, including the quantity, precision and reliability of the available information. Category A means that the phenomenon has been identified without ambiguity; B indicates a highly probable explanation; C means that the report cannot be analysed reliably; and D is reserved for an observation still unexplained after investigation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GeipanLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... Applied to Côte d’Or, that framework produces a less dramatic but more informative pattern: Vitteaux, 30 May 1977: category A, identified as the Moon. Chenôve, 10 November 1977: category B, probably identified, although the public listing does not name the retained phenomenon. Bierre lès Semur, 18 July 1978: category B, probably a reddish Moon near the horizon. Dijon, 17 September 1978: category C, insufficient reliable information. Argilly, 3 June 1979: category C, with a bright astronomical object considered probable but unconfirmed. Arc sur Tille, 8 December 1979: category D, still officially unexplained. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The result is not a progression towards stronger evidence. It is a sorting process. Several reports weaken when viewing geometry and the night sky are reconstructed; two remain unusable because the documentation is inadequate; only one retains both sufficient substance and no accepted explanation. Moon, planets and the illusion of pursuit Vitteaux: an identified Moon The Vitteaux report of 30 May 1977 is the clearest example of an ordinary object producing an extraordinary impression. GEIPAN ultimately classified it A and identified the phenomenon as the Moon. The importance of the case lies less in its surviving narrative than in its verdict: the most firmly resolved late 1970s report in Côte d’Or was not an unusual craft but a familiar astronomical body viewed under misleading conditions. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanVITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977 T2VITTEAUX (21) 30.05.1977; Observation date. 30/05/1977; region. Bourgogne; Department. Côte d'Or; Cl... The Moon is a recurrent source of UFO reports because observers frequently underestimate the distance of a bright object near the horizon. Atmospheric haze can turn it orange or red, while cloud can conceal parts of its disc, distort its outline or make it appear to brighten, fade and reappear. Without visible stars, landmarks or a clear horizon, a distant celestial body can be mentally placed just above fields, trees or buildings. The key distinction is between seeing and interpreting. A witness may accurately report a conspicuous light while misjudging its size, distance and motion. GEIPAN’s own investigations stress that an explanation can combine a known physical object, such as the Moon or a planet, with ordinary effects of perception. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GeipanLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... Bierre lès Semur: the “saucer” at four in the morning The Bierre lès Semur case is the best documented Côte d’Or example of how later reconstruction can overturn an initially mysterious verdict. At about 4 am on 18 July 1978, a student camping near the hamlet of Lucenay saw a stationary, fire coloured light low in the sky. He described it as resembling a saucer or an inverted plate, fetched a camera and took three photographs. He then reported that it moved slowly and afterwards departed rapidly towards the south. No noise was heard. The photographs yielded no usable image, and gendarmes found no additional witness. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanBIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.197828 Jul 2017 — Témoignages. Témoignages. BIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.1978. Résumé. Courte observatio... The case was originally associated with Précy sous Thil and classified D. Decades later, GEIPAN re examined it using improved mapping and astronomical software. The Moon was found at an azimuth of about 240 degrees—the direction of Dompierre en Morvan from the witness’s position—and only about two degrees above the horizon. Its position, low elevation and reddish orange appearance closely matched the first and best documented part of the report. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanBIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.197828 Jul 2017 — Témoignages. Témoignages. BIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.1978. Résumé. Courte observatio... Cloud was also forming. A partly obscured lunar disc could therefore have appeared flattened or plate like rather than circular. GEIPAN considered the reported final movement less secure because it was only briefly described. A moving gap in the clouds could have made the visible portion of the Moon appear to shift, accelerate or disappear in a new direction. The agency consequently reclassified the case B: a probable sighting of a reddish setting Moon. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanBIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.197828 Jul 2017 — Témoignages. Témoignages. BIERRE LES SEMUR (21) 18.07.1978. Résumé. Courte observatio... This reclassification is especially instructive because the original account contained features often treated as evidence against an astronomical explanation: an apparently structured shape, a change of speed and a rapid departure. Yet those details were not equally well supported. The stationary orange light had a precise celestial match; the dramatic departure was brief, lacked measurements and was not corroborated. Investigation did not require dismissing the witness. It required separating the firmly recorded observation from the witness’s interpretation of its final seconds. Argilly: why a planet can seem to follow a car At about 11.30 pm on 3 June 1979, a motorist near Argilly saw an orange luminous ball in her rear view mirror. She believed it was following her, became frightened and sounded"},{"title":"Poncey 1954","url":"/poncey-1954/","description":"The 1954 Poncey case combines several witnesses, police photographs and soil marks, but no proven link between the light and the trace.","breadcrumb":"Why Cote d'Or Became a French UFO Landmark","level":2,"text":"Poncey 1954 Did Anything Land at Poncey in 1954? Cote d'Or UFOs Poncey 1954 Poncey 1954 Did Anything Land at Poncey in 1954? The 1954 Poncey case combines several witnesses, police photographs and soil marks, but no proven link between the light and the trace. The 1954 Poncey case combines several witnesses, police photographs and soil marks, but no proven link between the light and the trace. event time window The sightings of 2 and 4 October What the gendarmes found in the field Why the case remains unanalysable cote d or poncey 1954 Introduction Poncey sur l’Ignon produced Côte d’Or’s best documented early UFO episode, but not a proven landing. On 2 and 4 October 1954, several residents reported unusual orange or yellow lights. After the principal sighting on 4 October, disturbed turf was found in a nearby field; gendarmes inspected it, questioned witnesses and photographed the marks. This unusually rich paper trail helped turn a small rural incident into one of the department’s landmark cases. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanPONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 02.10.1954 T1 GEIPAN... The evidence nevertheless falls short at its most important point. Nobody saw an object touch the ground, and nothing demonstrated that the light observed from a house had caused the excavation found later. Possible explanations for the separate elements include the Moon, vehicle headlights, animal disturbance and a prank, but the surviving information is too incomplete to confirm any of them. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s unit for unidentified aerospace phenomena, therefore classifies Poncey as category C: a case that cannot be analysed reliably because essential information is missing. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... The Best Kept Secret in UFOlogy with Jacques Vallee Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube The sightings of 2 and 4 October The episode began at about 7.40 pm on Saturday 2 October. A farm worker who had just finished milking cows noticed what seemed at first to be moonlight illuminating the courtyard. Looking towards its source, she said she saw a vertically oriented luminous form, estimated at roughly 1.5 metres high and 80 centimetres wide. She described an orange light with a greenish reflection and believed the object was moving at low altitude near the buildings. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... She called relatives and a neighbour. These additional witnesses did not obtain the same detailed view; they mainly saw a receding light travelling generally from north to south. The neighbour compared it with a football shaped orange light and reported no sound. The distinction matters because the often repeated description of a sharply defined cylinder comes largely from one witness, while the others confirmed only a distant luminous phenomenon. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... GEIPAN considers a lunar misidentification possible for this first evening. The Moon was only about six degrees above the south western horizon, and cloud movement could have made it seem displaced or in motion. Against that explanation, the witnesses described the light as travelling towards the south and the principal witness insisted that it did not look like the Moon. The original statements, however, do not establish her exact position, viewing direction or whether she could see the Moon and the reported object simultaneously. Without those details, the hypothesis cannot be tested decisively. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... Two evenings later, at about 7.30 pm on 4 October, a young woman was closing the shutters of an upstairs bedroom at a small group of dwellings known locally as the asbestos factory, around one kilometre south of Poncey. Looking north towards a plum tree, she saw a strong yellowish light. Her description suggested a mushroom like or globular shape, oval above and rounded below, resembling a transparent globe illuminated from within. She estimated that it was about 50 metres away, approximately 15 metres above the ground and comparable in apparent size with a cooker. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... The observation was brief. She heard no unusual sound and could not say confidently whether the light was stationary or moving. Frightened, she took her child and went to a neighbour’s home. The two women and a girl remained indoors; none of them independently saw the luminous phenomenon. When two male relatives returned at about 8.30 pm, they found the women visibly alarmed and went to inspect the field in the direction of the sighting. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... A further report followed at approximately 8.45 pm. An 18 year old returning from work said he briefly saw a wingless, cigar shaped luminous form moving rapidly from north to south, with green and orange reflections. His position was about one to one and a half kilometres from the earlier witness, and the exact observation point was not preserved. The hour long separation, uncertain geometry and differing descriptions mean that the two sightings cannot confidently be treated as views of the same phenomenon. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... Les extraterrestres apprécieraient ils la Bourgogne ? Archive INA Channel: INA Société Open on YouTube What the gendarmes found in the field The ground marks made Poncey more than another report of a light in the sky. In the field adjoining the courtyard, residents found a roughly rectangular shallow excavation measuring about 1.5 metres long and between 50 and 70 centimetres wide. The surface was depressed by approximately five centimetres. Broken earth appeared at both ends, while clods of turf and soil were distributed around the feature over an area with a radius of roughly four metres. Many pieces had landed grass side down. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... Witnesses also mentioned live white grubs among the disturbed soil. That detail later complicated the suggestion that wild boar had dug the ground while searching for food: the shape was described as comparatively regular, and the grubs apparently remained uneaten. Boar activity was not impossible—animals might have been interrupted—but it did not neatly account for the straightish edges, the positioning of the clods or the reported rectangular outline. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... The gendarmerie was notified on the evening of 5 October. Officers inspected the site and assembled a photographic record on 6 October. The official GEIPAN page retains the case file, a reconstruction map, a period map and the gendarmerie report, giving Poncey much firmer documentary foundations than sightings known solely from later books or press summaries. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanPONCEY SUR L'IGNON (21) 02.10.1954 T1 GEIPAN... Yet the delay reduced the value of the physical evidence. The photographs were taken about two days after the alleged event, and witnesses had already walked around or across the site. No controlled sampling procedure, uncontaminated comparison soil or laboratory result survives that can identify heat, pressure, chemicals, radiation or another unusual physical process. The photographs document that the ground was disturbed; they do not establish how, when or by whom. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... The gendarmes did investigate a possible joke. Two family members admitted that, before returning home, they had discussed frightening the women with a story about a flying saucer. Their plans made them obvious suspects once the marks were discovered. After checking their movements, however, the officers did not consider them responsible for creating the trace. That finding weakens one specific prank theory but does not exclude an unknown prankster, an earlier ordinary disturbance or an account altered by the excitement surrounding the sightings. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 octobre, Poncey Sur l'Ignon, Côte d'Or... Later UFO literature often strengthened the story beyond the contemporary evidence. Some retellings described an object landing, the soil being “sucked upwards”, extensive military analysis or a direct match between the craft and the hole. The surviving gendarmerie based"},{"title":"Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","url":"/which-cotes-darmor-ufo-cases-still-defy/","description":"Côtes-d’Armor has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landmark” encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Côtes d’Armor has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landmark” encounter. Côtes d’Armor has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landmark” encounter. cotes d armor Introduction The most important lesson is therefore not that the department is an extraordinary UFO hotspot. It is that Côtes d’Armor provides a clear local example of how sightings change when investigators obtain better timings, directions, astronomical data and aviation information. Some reports remain unresolved, including cases at Binic in 1990 and Dinan in 1999. Yet “unresolved” does not mean extraterrestrial: it usually means that the surviving evidence is insufficient to identify the cause confidently. [Geipan] geipan.fr DINAN (22) 07.09.1999 07/09/1999 D Côtes d'Armor Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de consistance moyenne à forte 22/03/2007 ... The official record is mixed, not mysterious throughout France has maintained an unusual degree of institutional continuity in UFO investigation. The National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES, created its specialist unit in 1977. Now known as GEIPAN, it collects witness accounts, investigates them, preserves the records and publishes conclusions. It can consult the gendarmerie, civil aviation, the air force, the navy, weather services, astronomers and specialists in satellite trajectories or image analysis. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Son histoire GEIPANQu'est ce que le GEIPAN? Les enquêteurs répartis sur tout le territoire français participent, eux aussi bénévolement, à l’évaluation des cas d’observation.... L’... GEIPAN’s classifications are crucial to reading the Côtes d’Armor files correctly: A means the phenomenon has been identified. B means an explanation is considered very probable. C means the information is too weak or incomplete for a reliable conclusion. D1 or D2 denotes a case that remains unexplained after investigation, with the distinction reflecting the strength and strangeness of the evidence. The more detailed A/B/C/D1/D2 system has been used since 2008, while older files may retain broader legacy classifications. A category D case is therefore not official confirmation of an exotic craft. It means that investigators could not match the reported event to a satisfactory conventional explanation on the evidence available. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... Across Côtes d’Armor, the database contains examples from every evidential level. Reports from Meslin, Ploubalay, Le Cambout, Étables sur Mer and several other communes are classified C because dependable information is missing. Others have been solved or probably solved as meteors, aircraft, satellites, lanterns, drones or atmospheric re entry. Only a small number appear in the more intriguing D category. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The 1954 wave left colourful but fragile local stories Any history of UFOs in Côtes d’Armor must acknowledge the French sighting wave of 1954, when reports of “flying saucers”, luminous objects and alleged occupants appeared across the country. At the time, the department was still officially called Côtes du Nord; the name changed to Côtes d’Armor in 1990. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. One frequently repeated local account concerns Mégrit. A farmer, usually named in later compilations as Henri Lehérissé, allegedly said that a small disc landed in his farmyard and that two child sized beings could be seen through an opening. Another story from Saint Maudan involved a young witness and an alleged strange encounter in November 1954. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org 1954 11 14 france stmaudan 1954 11 14 france stmaudan These accounts are memorable, but their evidential value is limited. The surviving narratives come mainly through later UFO catalogues and repeated summaries rather than a readily accessible official investigation containing original interviews, physical evidence, photographs or independently verified measurements. Details can also shift as stories are copied between publications. That does not prove that the witnesses invented their experiences. It means that the claims cannot now be tested to the standard applied to a modern GEIPAN case. The 1954 material is best treated as part of the department’s folklore and press era UFO history, not as strong evidence of a landed craft or occupants. Binic and Dinan are the strongest official unknowns Two entries stand out because GEIPAN’s public search records list them as category D: Binic on 22 March 1990 and Dinan on 7 September 1999. The database describes both as strange or very strange phenomena supported by evidence of medium to strong consistency. [Geipan] geipan.fr DINAN (22) 07.09.1999 07/09/1999 D Côtes d'Armor Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de consistance moyenne à forte 22/03/2007 ... That wording makes them more significant than the many category C reports, but it should not be overstated. An official D classification indicates that investigators lacked a convincing explanation; it does not establish what physically caused the sighting. The public summaries available through the searchable index provide less detail than would be needed for a fresh independent reconstruction, such as precise lines of sight, complete meteorological conditions, aircraft movements and the full sequence of witness interviews. Binic is particularly interesting because it lies on the Bay of Saint Brieuc, where observers can see maritime lights, coastal traffic, aircraft and astronomical objects over a broad horizon. Those conditions can produce compelling visual impressions, especially when distance is impossible to judge. Yet merely naming possible sources is not a solution. Without matching one to the reported time, bearing and behaviour, the case properly remains unresolved. The Dinan report deserves similar restraint. Its D classification makes it a legitimate part of the department’s core UFO record, but later searchable material has not produced a decisive identification. The intellectually honest position lies between two common errors: dismissing the witness simply because no object was recovered, or treating the absence of an explanation as evidence of non human technology. 6:43 Explained: \"Go Fast\" UFO Video Not Low and Not Fast Like a Balloon! Channel: Mick West · Views: 154.3K · Uploaded: June 2019 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Plérin shows how several sightings can become one misleading case The Plérin file from late December 1994 is one of the most instructive cases in Côtes d’Armor. Four witnesses reported observations made between 28 and 31 December, and the accounts were recorded together in one gendarmerie statement. At first glance, that grouping could suggest a sustained local wave involving repeated manifestations of the same unknown object. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN’s later re examination separated the material into several distinct phenomena. Two witnesses saw one event on the evening of 29 December. Another witness reported observations on the mornings of 29, 30 and 31 December, with a fourth person present on one occasion. Further observations were assigned to 28 and 31 December. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This separation matters. When reports made by different people at different hours are bundled into a single file, the combined narrative may appear more coherent and extraordinary than any one observation actually was. Once investigators distinguish evening events from early morning ones, the possibility of different ordinary causes becomes much stronger. The case was ultimately classified C because the surviving information was not reliable or complete enough for firm identification. Plérin is therefore not a solved case, but neither is it a robust unexplained incident. Its main value is methodological: chronology, witness independence and accurate grouping can radically alter how impressive a cluster appears. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Recent cases reveal the recurring explanations The modern Côtes d’Armor record contains several useful examples of initially puzzling observations being identified after investigation. At Lannion on 23 July 2017, a luminous object was eventually matched to the International Space Station. The case received an A classification, meaning the explanation was considered established. The station can appear unusually bright and move steadily without the flashing navigation lights associated with an ordinary aircraft, making it"},{"title":"Official Unknowns","url":"/official-unknowns/","description":"Binic and Dinan remain the department's strongest official unknowns, but their D classifications do not identify what witnesses saw.","breadcrumb":"Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Official Unknowns Why Binic and Dinan Remain Officially Unresolved Cotes d'Armor UFOs Official Unknowns Official Unknowns Why Binic and Dinan Remain Officially Unresolved Binic and Dinan remain the department's strongest official unknowns, but their D classifications do not identify what witnesses saw. Binic and Dinan remain the department's strongest official unknowns, but their D classifications do not identify what witnesses saw. event time window What witnesses reported in Binic and Dinan What a GEIPAN category D classification means Possible explanations and the missing evidence cotes d armor official unknowns Introduction Binic in 1990 and Dinan in 1999 are the two most significant unresolved cases in Côtes d’Armor’s published official UFO record. Both remain classified D by GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena. That status means investigators did not find a satisfactory conventional explanation after examining the available evidence. It does not mean that either sighting has been identified as an exotic craft, still less as extraterrestrial technology. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanBINIC (22) 22.03.1990 GEIPAN... The cases are intriguing for different reasons. Binic involves several people reporting apparently related events, including a low flying orange disc and unusual lights behind nearby hills. Dinan rests on a single experienced sky watcher, but the official account says that checks with aerodromes and civil and military authorities failed to connect the observation with known aircraft or radar traffic. At the same time, neither file contains the kind of surviving physical or instrument data that would allow a firm identification today. France's Official UFO Investigation Agency (GEIPAN) Channel: Kryptoscipher Open on YouTube What witnesses reported in Binic and Dinan Binic: repeated lights and a reported low altitude disc The Binic events occurred on 22 March 1990. GEIPAN’s summary says that three witnesses separately saw a strange luminous phenomenon, on more than one occasion. One witness reported being overflown at low altitude by an orange disc that produced an unusual rushing or rustling air sound. The encounter frightened him sufficiently that he fell from his moped. Another witness reportedly saw a similar craft twice and noticed strange glows behind the surrounding hills. GEIPAN states that its investigation found no explanation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanBINIC (22) 22.03.1990 GEIPAN... This is stronger than a simple report of a distant point of light. The claimed disc shape, colour, sound and low apparent altitude gave investigators several descriptive features to consider. The involvement of three witnesses also potentially raises the case’s evidential value, although the official summary makes clear that the observations were separate rather than one simultaneous, mutually confirmed view from the same position. That distinction matters: separate reports may support one another, but only if their timings, directions and descriptions can be shown to match closely. The surviving public summary does not provide photographs, radar records, measured angles, a confirmed flight path or physical traces from the reported fall. Nor does it establish the object’s actual altitude or distance. A witness who sees an unfamiliar light against a dark sky normally has no reliable scale against which to judge size, range or speed. The description of being “overflown” is therefore an interpretation of the visual and acoustic experience, not an independently measured trajectory. Binic nevertheless remains noteworthy within Côtes d’Armor because the reported combination is unusual: repeated observations, more than one witness, apparent structure, sound and a close pass impression. An independent database derived from GEIPAN’s public material currently lists Binic as one of only two category D cases among 34 departmental records, the other being Dinan. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr binic 1990 0301196 22 mars 1990... L'enquête n'a pas permis de trouver une explication à ce phénomène.... La carte des observations d'OVNI... Dinan: a dark barrel like form changing direction The Dinan report concerns a night time observation in 1999 by a 60 year old amateur astronomer. While observing the Andromeda Galaxy, he noticed with the unaided eye a silent, non luminous, brown object moving at a steady speed. According to GEIPAN, it initially followed a north to south path, then changed direction sharply towards the north north west before turning north. The witness’s sketch represented the form as barrel like, and the observation lasted about one minute. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanDINAN (22) 07.09.1999DINAN (22) 07.09.1999; Date d'observation. 07/09/1999; Région. Bretagne; Département. Côtes d'Armor; Class... The observer’s astronomical interest is relevant but should not be overstated. Someone accustomed to watching the night sky may be less likely than a casual observer to mistake a bright planet for a moving object. Dinan was not, however, a luminous astronomical object report. It concerned a dark shape seen against the sky without optical magnification. Even an experienced observer can have difficulty estimating the form and path of a dim, unfamiliar object when its distance is unknown. What makes Dinan particularly important is the reported official follow up. GEIPAN says that investigators made detailed enquiries with neighbouring aerodromes and civil and military authorities. Neither air traffic examination nor available radar traces allowed the sighting to be associated with an aircraft. The case was therefore left unexplained. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanDINAN (22) 07.09.1999 GEIPAN... That negative result is meaningful, but narrower than it may first appear. It indicates that investigators did not find a matching recorded aircraft or radar contact. It does not demonstrate that the reported form was a solid craft, nor that every small, low, distant or poorly reflective object would necessarily have appeared on the radar systems consulted. Birds, balloons, lightweight airborne material and some aircraft operating outside an expected record can be difficult to exclude completely after the event. There is also an unresolved archival problem in the public file. The case title and database date identify the observation as 7 September 1999, whereas the narrative describes the night of 7–8 August. The same mismatch appears in both GEIPAN’s current page and an independent reproduction of its data. It may be a transcription error, but without the underlying dated documents the correct night cannot safely be chosen. This matters because checks of weather, Moon position, aircraft movements and possible balloon activity depend on the exact date. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr dinan 1999 0901538 Observation OVNI à Dinan — septembre 1999 — CarteOvni.frObservation OVNI à Dinan — septembre 1999. 7 septembre 1999 Côtes d'Armor (22) 👁️... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What a GEIPAN category D classification means GEIPAN is a technical unit within CNES, France’s national space agency. Its purpose is to collect, investigate, archive and publish reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. It works with witnesses and may draw on the gendarmerie, police, aviation organisations, defence bodies, weather specialists and scientific experts. GEIPAN explicitly says that it is not an extraterrestrial research organisation and does not treat the word “UFO” as evidence that a physical vehicle was present. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. Under the current classification framework: A means a phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B means an explanation is considered probable. C means the information is inadequate or unreliable for a sound assessment. D means the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. GEIPAN’s more detailed method, introduced in 2008, assesses both the residual strangeness of a report and its “consistency”: the quantity, precision and reliability of the information supporting it. Modern unexplained cases may be divided into D1 and D2, while older records such as Binic and Dinan can retain the broader legacy D label. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr Geipan Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Geipan Mission & Geipan GEIPAN This makes D different from “insufficient information”, but it should not be read as “confirmed extraordinary object”. A D classification records the outcome of an investigation: the available conventional hypotheses did not satisfactorily account for the reported experience. It does not specify what actually caused that experience. The distinction is especially important with historical cases. GEIPAN acknowledges that older investigations lacked many tools now available to investigators, including convenient digital access to"},{"title":"Solved Sightings","url":"/solved-sightings/","description":"GEIPAN records show how separated timelines and better data can turn an apparent UFO wave into several ordinary events.","breadcrumb":"Which Cotes d'Armor UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Solved Sightings How Cotes d'Armor UFO Mysteries Were Reassessed Cotes d'Armor UFOs Solved Sightings Solved Sightings How Cotes d'Armor UFO Mysteries Were Reassessed GEIPAN records show how separated timelines and better data can turn an apparent UFO wave into several ordinary events. GEIPAN records show how separated timelines and better data can turn an apparent UFO wave into several ordinary events. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies and investigates reports Why the Plérin sightings were split apart Aircraft, meteors, satellites, drones and lanterns cotes d armor solved sightings Introduction Official investigations have changed the meaning of many Côtes d’Armor UFO reports. Apparent waves have often broken apart once investigators separated observations by date, time, direction and witness. Objects that seemed to belong to one prolonged mystery were reassessed as unrelated events involving lightning, Venus, aircraft, satellites, meteors, atmospheric re entry, drones or lanterns. Other files were downgraded not because investigators proved an ordinary cause, but because the evidence was too incomplete to support any firm conclusion. The Plérin reports of late December 1994 are the clearest local example. Initially, several sightings made over four nights could be read as a cluster. GEIPAN’s later review instead treated each observation on its own evidence. One was probably lightning over the sea, another was consistent with Venus, while others remained unusable because their timings and descriptions were too weak. The result was not a single solved UFO case, but the dismantling of an apparent wave into several different evidential problems. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 31.12.199411 Dec 2017 — Or, la présence d'un orage en mer, visible depuis la côte, est avérée par l'enquête de gendarme... How GEIPAN tests a sighting GEIPAN, the French national body within the space agency CNES that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, does not classify a case solely by asking whether the witness appeared sincere. Its process begins with the testimony, then compares the reported time, position, movement, appearance and duration against known astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human made phenomena. Depending on the case, investigators may consult the gendarmerie, civil aviation, defence bodies, weather services, astronomers, satellite data and image analysis specialists. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPANA classification methodology consolidated over decades Sighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while maintaining the anonymity of wit... Two concepts shape the final judgement. Residual strangeness measures how poorly the best conventional hypothesis fits after investigation. Consistency measures whether the available information is detailed, coherent and reliable enough to support a conclusion. A striking story may therefore receive a weak classification if its timing is approximate, its direction is unknown or its documentation was created long after the event. Conversely, a relatively ordinary looking light can be identified confidently when its path and timing match an aircraft or satellite precisely. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... The categories are easy to misunderstand: A means the phenomenon has been identified with a high degree of confidence. B means a particular explanation is considered probable. C means the evidence is insufficient or unreliable, so the case cannot be assessed properly. D1 and D2 are unexplained cases, with D2 requiring stronger strangeness and stronger supporting evidence. A category C report is therefore not halfway between “explained” and “alien”. It sits outside that scale because investigators lack the information needed to decide. GEIPAN itself stresses that C cases should be treated separately from A, B, D1 and D2 cases. [Geipan] geipan.fr Around 200 lead to investigations with diffusion on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) of sighting reports and investigation conclusions... This distinction matters greatly in Côtes d’Armor. Some of its most atmospheric reports survive as mysteries mainly because essential data were never recorded. The database describes files from Plérin, Ploubalay, Le Cambout and Plévenon as lacking reliable information. Those entries preserve the fact that something was reported, but they do not provide positive evidence for an extraordinary object. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 28.12.1994Department Côtes d'Armor Classification C Date of update 11/12/2017 Type of phenomenon. Age 30 Genre H Condition... Why the Plérin “wave” was split apart The Plérin dossier demonstrates how grouping reports can create a stronger impression than the evidence warrants. The surviving records refer to observations on 28, 29, 30 and 31 December 1994, involving different witnesses and different times of day. Some occurred in the early evening; others were reported between roughly 6am and 6.45am. Treating all of them as appearances by one object would require assuming a connection that the witness evidence did not establish. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 31.12.199411 Dec 2017 — Or, la présence d'un orage en mer, visible depuis la côte, est avérée par l'enquête de gendarme... GEIPAN therefore divided the material into separate phenomena rather than preserving it as one continuous incident. That analytical decision was as important as any proposed explanation. Once the reports were detached from one another, the apparent pattern weakened: a succession of unusual lights over several days became a set of observations with different likely causes and different evidential quality. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 31.12.199411 Dec 2017 — Or, la présence d'un orage en mer, visible depuis la côte, est avérée par l'enquête de gendarme... The evening observation of 29 December was reassessed against weather evidence. A storm at sea, visible from the coast, was confirmed through the gendarmerie enquiry. GEIPAN concluded that flashes associated with that storm offered a probable explanation and classified the report B. This did not mean that the witnesses had fabricated anything. It meant that a real, distant meteorological event could account for lights that appeared unusual from their viewpoint. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 31.12.199411 Dec 2017 — Or, la présence d'un orage en mer, visible depuis la côte, est avérée par l'enquête de gendarme... Part of the 31 December material was separately attributed, with probability rather than absolute proof, to Venus. Bright planets can appear much larger or more mobile than expected when they are viewed through haze, thin cloud, intervening branches or from a moving vehicle. Without a measured angle or fixed landmark, a witness may also underestimate how long a bright astronomical object has remained in the same area of sky. GEIPAN consequently recorded one Plérin file as a category B Venus identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; VENUS — PLERIN (22) 31.12.1994, 31/12/1994, B · Côtes d'Armor, Vénus; PLERIN (22) 31.12.1... Other Plérin reports did not receive equally neat solutions. The observation dated 28 December was placed in category C, with very low consistency, because GEIPAN judged that dependable information was missing. Another 31 December file was also classified C. These reports were not disproved; they were found too poorly documented to sustain either an identification or a strong unexplained classification. [Geipan] geipan.fr PLERIN (22) 28.12.1994Department Côtes d'Armor Classification C Date of update 11/12/2017 Type of phenomenon. Age 30 Genre H Condition... This mixed outcome is the real lesson of the case. An official review does not always replace a dramatic account with one universal explanation. It may instead reveal that the original “case” was an artificial bundle: one weather event, one probable planet and several observations that cannot now be reconstructed. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Timing and direction can overturn first impressions Witness descriptions commonly emphasise colour, shape or apparent movement. Investigators often find that the more decisive facts are less dramatic: the exact minute, the compass direction, the elevation above the horizon and whether the observer was stationary. Those details allow a report to be checked against objects that were actually present in that part of the sky. The October 2013 case at Plélo shows the value of this approach. The witness reported three lights moving together. A check of satellite passage data found that the Chinese Yaogan 17 satellite triplet crossed at the relevant time on a trajectory corresponding to the observation. GEIPAN classified"},{"title":"From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies","url":"/from-humanoids-to-airliners-in-creuse/","description":"Creuse has produced intriguing UFO reports, but its documented history is defined less by a single landmark mystery than by a series of cases showing how...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies Creuse has produced intriguing UFO reports, but its documented history is defined less by a single landmark mystery than by a series of cases showing how... Creuse has produced intriguing UFO reports, but its documented history is defined less by a single landmark mystery than by a series of cases showing how ordinary lights, aircraft and astronomical effects can appear extraordinary. creuse Introduction The department’s most dramatic older story, reported near Alleyrat during France’s 1954 flying saucer wave, involved a supposed humanoid with powerful lights. Yet a later investigator reported that the witness may have invented the encounter to explain injuries received in a fight. Taken together, the Creuse record is valuable not as evidence of a persistent unknown presence, but as a compact demonstration of how press excitement, human perception, delayed reporting and later technical checks can transform the meaning of a sighting. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The 1954 Alleyrat encounter The best known early Creuse story appeared in the regional newspaper La Montagne on 28 October 1954, near the height of France’s autumn flying saucer wave. A 47 year old man named Bousard said that, while cycling near La Badière in the commune of Alleyrat at about 10.45 pm, he encountered a roughly human sized figure beside the road. He described the being as dressed like a diver, with intense blue lights directed towards him and weaker green lights near its head. He claimed that an undefined pressure or blast threw him across the road and left him temporarily unable to call out. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The article reported that Bousard complained of pain in his right hand and leg, and that gendarmes found his hand swollen and his writing impaired. His bicycle apparently showed no sign of a crash. These details gave the account the appearance of a physical close encounter rather than a distant light in the sky. The same article also mentioned an earlier claim by Alice Paquet of nearby Saint Pardoux le Neuf, who reportedly saw a silent, unidentified craft overhead. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The difficulty is that the surviving evidence is largely journalistic. There is no publicly presented medical examination, physical trace, photograph or independent witness confirming Bousard’s alleged encounter. More damagingly, a later ufological investigation reportedly contacted a gendarme involved in the original deposition. According to that second hand account, Bousard was known locally for boasting, had been beaten by young men and used the flying saucer excitement of 1954 to explain his injuries more favourably. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... That later explanation is not itself supported by a published original police document or a recorded interview available for independent checking. It should therefore be treated as a plausible debunking claim rather than a conclusively demonstrated solution. Even so, it substantially weakens the case. The Alleyrat episode rests on one sensational testimony, reported during a nationwide wave in which newspapers were actively seeking dramatic saucer stories, while the later account offers a conventional motive for the injuries that originally made the story impressive. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... Stars mistaken for a coordinated phenomenon Creuse’s most instructive official case occurred in January 1989. At about 8.30 pm, the gendarmerie group in Guéret asked its local brigades to look eastwards at a reported phenomenon. Personnel in several places, including Évaux les Bains, Chambon sur Voueize, Chénérailles, Jarnages, Gouzon and Bellegarde en Marche, reported highly luminous, multicoloured, scintillating points. The observations continued on several evenings. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... The involvement of several gendarmerie brigades might initially suggest an unusually strong case. Multiple trained observers were looking in the same general direction, and the observations were recorded through official channels rather than reconstructed decades later. GEIPAN nevertheless classified the episode as category A, meaning that it considered the phenomenon unambiguously identified. Its conclusion was that atmospheric conditions were causing stars to scintillate strongly, producing apparent flashes and changes of colour. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... This case shows why witness number alone does not guarantee that a phenomenon is genuinely anomalous. When observers are directed towards the same part of the sky, they may independently inspect the same bright star or group of stars and interpret unusually strong twinkling in similar ways. The reports corroborate that something striking was visible, but they do not establish that it was moving through the atmosphere or behaving like a vehicle. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... It also illustrates the practical importance of precise direction, duration and weather information. The gendarmerie asks witnesses to record the date, time, apparent position, colours, movement and presence or absence of sound because these details allow investigators to compare a report with astronomical objects, aircraft and atmospheric conditions. Without that framework, the authority of the witnesses could easily overshadow a relatively simple explanation. [Gendarmerie Nationale] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie NationaleComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés dans le ciel? Gendinfo, Gendarmerie nationale... OVNI : Analyse des images avec un pilote de chasse Channel: ATE CHUET Open on YouTube The unresolved Saint Sulpice light On 13 October 1988, two witnesses at Saint Sulpice le Dunois watched a yellow luminous ball for roughly half an hour from about 8.30 pm. The principal witness compared its apparent size with a car headlamp and said that it made rapid back and forth movements without producing any detectable sound. The witness’s partner also saw the phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT SULPICE LE DUNOIS (23) 13.10.1988 GEIPAN... GEIPAN classified the report as category C, meaning that it could not be analysed properly because reliable information was missing. The file contains the broad description and a gendarmerie record, but the agency states that no further information could be collected. It is therefore not an officially unexplained case in the stronger category D sense; it is an under documented observation for which competing hypotheses cannot be tested. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT SULPICE LE DUNOIS (23) 13.10.1988 GEIPAN... Apparent rapid movement by a point source can arise from several effects, including involuntary eye movements, shifting reference points, thin cloud, atmospheric distortion or the observer’s own movement. A distant aircraft, astronomical object or other light might also appear silent when distance and wind make its sound inaudible. None of these possibilities can be selected confidently here because the surviving record lacks the necessary positional, meteorological and trajectory data. GEIPAN’s category C is therefore a statement about evidential limits, not an endorsement of an extraordinary interpretation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT SULPICE LE DUNOIS (23) 13.10.1988 GEIPAN... Photographs that clarified the answer Sermur’s “rectangular structure” A witness observing the stars from a hill above Sermur on 6 August 2018 noticed five closely spaced lights and took a six second photograph. When enlarged, the image appeared to show a flat rectangular structure made up of repeated white and red elements. The description sounds more unusual than the original naked eye observation because the camera recorded the light source repeatedly as it moved during the long exposure. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanSERMUR (23) 06.08.2018 GEIPAN... GEIPAN found that the pattern matched an airliner. Red and white anti collision flashes repeated at roughly one second intervals, while a continuous green line was consistent with the aircraft’s starboard navigation light. The object’s north to south track east of the witness would have exposed the starboard side, and its apparent angular movement was compatible with a commercial aircraft at cruising altitude. Routes from Paris towards Africa also"},{"title":"Alleyrat 1954","url":"/alleyrat-1954/","description":"The 1954 Alleyrat story became Creuse's most dramatic UFO claim, but later testimony suggested the injuries may have had an ordinary cause.","breadcrumb":"From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies","level":2,"text":"Alleyrat 1954 Did the Alleyrat Humanoid Encounter Really Happen? Creuse UFOs Alleyrat 1954 Alleyrat 1954 Did the Alleyrat Humanoid Encounter Really Happen? The 1954 Alleyrat story became Creuse's most dramatic UFO claim, but later testimony suggested the injuries may have had an ordinary cause. The 1954 Alleyrat story became Creuse's most dramatic UFO claim, but later testimony suggested the injuries may have had an ordinary cause. event time window Bousard's roadside account The evidence reported in 1954 The later fight explanation creuse alleyrat 1954 Introduction The Alleyrat encounter of 26 October 1954 is Creuse ’s most dramatic historical UFO story, but it is not one of its strongest cases. Aimé Bousard, a 47 year old agricultural worker or farmer from La Vaureille, said that a human sized figure beside a country road shone intense blue lights at him, threw him across the road with an invisible force and left him temporarily unable to call for help. Gendarmes reportedly saw his swollen hand and disturbed ground at the site. Yet the case depended on Bousard alone, no flying object was seen, and no medical report or surviving police file has been published. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... More seriously, a later investigation claimed that Bousard had actually been injured in a fight with local young men and invented the extraordinary encounter to explain his bruises. That explanation is plausible but also imperfectly documented: it survives through later UFO literature rather than an available original interview or official record. The fairest judgement is therefore that Alleyrat is a disputed, probably unreliable humanoid report—not a conclusively solved hoax, but far too weak to support claims of an unknown visitor. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... Bousard’s roadside account Bousard reportedly visited the mairie, or town hall, at Alleyrat to collect a document on the evening of Tuesday 26 October. He left the village by bicycle at about 10.30 pm and reached a nearby roadside location around 10.45 pm. The original regional report named the place as La Badière, although syndicated versions variously printed La Madière or similar spellings. This inconsistency appears to reflect copying and typesetting rather than evidence of several different incidents. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... According to the account published by La Montagne on 28 October, Bousard noticed someone or something on the verge. He stopped but remained astride his bicycle. At first he thought the figure was an ordinary man. When he leaned forward to look more closely, it rose suddenly and directed two powerful, pale blue lights towards him. Bousard estimated its height at roughly 1.6 metres and described clothing resembling a diver’s suit, with weaker green lights positioned on either side of the head. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The most extraordinary part of the story followed immediately. Bousard said he felt a pressure like an extremely violent gust that hurled him to the opposite side of the road. For about ten minutes, he claimed, he could neither shout nor summon help while the blue lights remained trained on him. The lights then went out, the figure crossed the road and disappeared so suddenly that he could not determine where it had gone. He reported pain in his right hand and one or both legs. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... Bousard explicitly denied being drunk, hallucinating or encountering a resident of the district. That defensive wording matters. It shows that obvious conventional objections were already circulating when the story was first reported, rather than being invented only by later sceptics. La Montagne also said most Alleyrat residents remained doubtful, even though the same article described Bousard as having a reputation for being sensible. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, Alleyrat, France, 1954... No spacecraft appeared anywhere in his narrative. Later catalogues often classified Alleyrat as a humanoid or close encounter case because of the alleged figure, suit, lights and bodily effects, but calling it a “flying saucer landing” adds something the witness did not claim to see. Even Jacques Vallée’s condensed catalogue version noted that no craft was observed. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The evidence reported in 1954 The case gained credibility because the newspaper did not present it as testimony alone. Gendarmes who received Bousard’s statement reportedly considered him to be in genuine discomfort. His right hand was swollen, writing was difficult, and his bicycle showed no obvious sign of a fall or collision. At the indicated roadside location, the soil was said to have been freshly disturbed by a roughly circular rubbing about 70 centimetres across. No footprints were visible nearby. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, Alleyrat, France, 1954... Those details sound impressive, but their evidential value is limited. The injuries were observed, not explained. A swollen hand and painful leg establish that Bousard had been hurt. They do not show when, where or how the injuries occurred. No publicly available doctor’s examination linked them to heat, radiation, electrical exposure or any other unusual mechanism. The bicycle proves little. The lack of visible crash damage may count against a hard cycling accident, but Bousard said he had been thrown away from the bicycle. It does not exclude a stumble, an assault or injuries sustained somewhere else. The ground disturbance was ambiguous. The reported patch was small, contained no footprint and was not described as burned, chemically altered or carrying an identifiable residue. There is no indication that samples were collected, photographed or preserved. Disturbed soil beside a rural road could have many mundane causes. There was no independent witness. Nobody confirmed the figure, the coloured lights, Bousard’s immobility or his claimed disappearance. The physical observations were made only after Bousard identified the location. The documentation is also weaker than repeated references to “the gendarmes” may suggest. The surviving public record is principally a newspaper account and later reproductions of it. No original deposition, site sketch, officer’s report, medical certificate or named gendarme testimony has been made available for independent examination. Consequently, readers cannot tell how closely the published narrative followed Bousard’s actual words or whether the police regarded the soil mark as significant. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... \"The Heaviest Cavalry in the West\": The French Gendarmes Channel: SandRhoman History Open on YouTube How the press enlarged the story The timing could hardly have been more favourable for a spectacular claim. France was in the middle of its celebrated autumn 1954 flying saucer wave, when newspapers were publishing frequent stories about discs, cigar shaped craft, landings and alleged occupants. Contemporary foreign coverage described the country as gripped by saucer excitement, while Time remarked that parts of the French press were enthusiastically multiplying stories of “Martians”. [Time] content.time.com Science: Martians over France 25, 1954... With Narcy's \"hairy Martian\" as a starting point, the French press ran wild, and a deluge of Martians has been raining down... La Montagne introduced the Alleyrat report with playful language implying that Creuse had at last received a flying saucer visitor with magical powers. Yet Bousard had described neither a saucer nor any other vehicle. The framing converted an unexplained roadside figure into a contribution to the national saucer wave before the evidence had been independently tested. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière, Creuse... The story then travelled rapidly through regional and national newspapers. Syndicated versions retained the central sequence—suited figure, blue beams, violent pressure and bodily pain—but introduced small variations. Bousard appeared as Aimé or André; the locality became La Badière, La Madière or La Madrière; and one version gave the figure’s height as 1.8 metres rather than 1.6 metres. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, France 1954, 26 octobre, La Badière,"},{"title":"Official Cases","url":"/official-cases-c518f6/","description":"Creuse's official files show how stars, aircraft, balloons and re-entering space debris can produce convincing UFO reports.","breadcrumb":"From Humanoids to Airliners in Creuse Skies","level":2,"text":"Official Cases Why So Many Creuse UFOs Had Ordinary Causes Official Cases Official Cases Why So Many Creuse UFOs Had Ordinary Causes Creuse's official files show how stars, aircraft, balloons and re entering space debris can produce convincing UFO reports. Creuse's official files show how stars, aircraft, balloons and re entering space debris can produce convincing UFO reports. dataset evidence Stars and the 1989 gendarmerie alert Aircraft and long exposure photography Balloons, re entry and unresolved records creuse official cases Introduction Official records from Creuse show that several convincing UFO reports had ordinary causes once investigators compared witness accounts with astronomy, aviation data, photography and known aerial activity. Bright stars produced a multi brigade gendarmerie alert in 1989; a seemingly structured object photographed near Sermur in 2018 proved consistent with an airliner recorded during a six second exposure; a roadside sighting in 2024 was identified as a crewed gas balloon; and reports from the nationwide event of 5 November 1990 fitted the re entry of Soviet rocket debris. One 1988 file remained unclassified, not because officials found strong evidence of something extraordinary, but because the surviving information was too poor to analyse. [GEIPAN] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... This distinction matters. In the GEIPAN system, a category A case is explained without ambiguity, category B has a highly probable explanation, category C cannot be analysed because information is missing, and category D remains unexplained despite usable evidence. Creuse’s official portfolio is therefore mainly a record of identification and evidential limits, rather than a collection of strong unresolved cases. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Stars and the 1989 gendarmerie alert The January 1989 case is the clearest example of official involvement making a sighting look stronger before ultimately helping to explain it. At about 8.30 pm, the gendarmerie group at Guéret asked brigades across Creuse to look eastwards at an unusual phenomenon. Officers at Évaux les Bains, Chambon sur Voueize, Chénérailles, Jarnages, Gouzon and Bellegarde en Marche reported very bright, scintillating points displaying varied colours. Similar observations continued on several evenings. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... A report involving several police stations can sound like independent confirmation of an extraordinary object. In reality, the brigades had been directed to inspect the same part of the sky, so the reports were not six unrelated discoveries. They were coordinated observations of a persistent visual stimulus. That made comparison possible: the lights stayed in positions consistent with stars, returned on subsequent nights and displayed the rapid colour changes associated with atmospheric scintillation. GEIPAN classified the case A and recorded the phenomenon simply as astronomical. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) CREUSE (23) 27.01.1989 GEIPAN... Scintillation is the familiar twinkling caused when starlight passes through moving layers of air with different temperatures and densities. Near the horizon, the light travels through more atmosphere and may appear to flash red, green, blue or white. A bright star can therefore resemble a rotating beacon or a distant craft with coloured lights, especially when haze, temperature gradients or unstable air strengthen the effect. The case also shows why official witnesses should not be dismissed, but should not be treated as immune to visual error. The gendarmes accurately reported what they saw: bright, coloured, flickering points. The mistake lay in interpreting an unfamiliar appearance as a potentially unusual aerial phenomenon. Their geographical spread actually helped resolve the incident, because observations from several brigades supported a fixed astronomical source rather than a nearby object travelling through Creuse. The Sermur photograph looked stranger than the object Near Sermur on 6 August 2018, a witness photographed what appeared to be five aligned white elements accompanied by red points and interpreted the image as a flat rectangular structure moving through the sky. Unlike many distant light reports, this case contained a photograph, camera information, stars that could be mapped and a stated direction of travel. Those details allowed a much more exact reconstruction. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word SERMUR (23) 06.08.2018 [E Cgei CR T,C S] compte rendu enquete.docx... The photograph was taken with a six second exposure. During that interval, the moving light left a track while its flashing lamps appeared repeatedly along it. Investigators found five repetitions of white streaks and red points, spaced at roughly one second intervals. That rhythm matched the flashing anti collision lights of an aircraft. A continuous greenish line also matched the starboard navigation light, which was the side expected to face the witness if the aircraft was travelling north to south while remaining east of the observation point. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word SERMUR (23) 06.08.2018 [E Cgei CR T,C S] compte rendu enquete.docx... The apparent geometry was therefore produced by time rather than by a row of separate lights fixed to a rectangular craft. A camera with a long exposure records successive moments in one image. Steady lamps become lines; flashing lamps become dotted or segmented trails. The resulting pattern can look more structured than anything visible to the naked eye. Other checks reinforced the aircraft explanation. The photographed light moved about five degrees during the six second exposure, or roughly one degree per second, which GEIPAN considered compatible with an airliner at cruising altitude. Routes from Paris towards Africa also crossed the relevant area on a suitable track. Investigators could not identify the exact flight because the report was processed too late, but they assessed the aircraft hypothesis at 92 per cent and classified the case A. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word SERMUR (23) 06.08.2018 [E Cgei CR T,C S] compte rendu enquete.docx... The investigation also exposed a memory problem. The witness later described a diagonal, descending path, yet comparison with the background stars showed that the track was approximately parallel to the horizon. GEIPAN noted that the observation occurred in August but the questionnaire was completed in December. By then, the witness may have been describing an interpretation of the photograph rather than an exact memory of the original movement. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word SERMUR (23) 06.08.2018 [E Cgei CR T,C S] compte rendu enquete.docx... This is one of Creuse’s most useful cases because the photograph did not merely fail to prove an extraordinary object; it preserved the mechanism of the mistake. The repeating flashes, colours, angular movement and orientation all supplied independent tests of the same explanation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons and the value of checking the wider sky A 15 September 2024 report made while travelling along the N145 between Saint Priest la Plaine and La Souterraine was later classified A as a crewed gas balloon. GEIPAN’s database also records a related sighting on the same date from Rilhac Rancon in neighbouring Haute Vienne, identified in the same way. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casMission & Geipan... 2024 11 25, voir. BURNHAUPT LE BAS (68) 24.11.2024... [N145] DE SAINT PRIEST LA PLAINE (23) V... Published: November 25, 2024 That cross border match is important. A balloon seen at a distance may appear motionless, unusually large or difficult to place against an empty sky. Its apparent shape can change with viewing angle, while sunlight on the envelope may make it far brighter than expected. A single witness may have little basis for estimating its height or distance. Reports from separated locations, however, can reveal that observers are looking at the same high altitude object rather than at something close to either witness. The 2024 case illustrates a modern advantage over older files: investigators can compare photographs, timings, known balloon activity and geographically separated reports while the information is still recent. The explanation does not depend on telling the witness that the object “must have been” a balloon. It depends on showing that a real balloon was in the appropriate sky and produced compatible observations along its route. Le CNES ouvre ses archives sur les OVNI Archive INA Channel: INA Société Open on YouTube The 1990 re entry turned many lights into one apparent craft On 5"},{"title":"How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record?","url":"/how-strong-is-the-deux-sevres-ufo-record/","description":"Deux-Sèvres has a modest but revealing UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record? How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record? How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record? How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record? How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record? Deux Sèvres has a modest but revealing UFO history. Deux Sèvres has a modest but revealing UFO history. It is not known for a large radar incident, a military interception or a famous landing claim supported by physical evidence. deux sevres Introduction The department’s most important official case occurred near Sainte Soline on 16 August 1980, when a witness described a silent, intensely red object shaped like a mushroom cap moving above fields and the village. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, still classifies it as unexplained. Yet the surviving public file contains only one witness account, no photograph, no radar confirmation and no trace on the ground. That contrast defines much of the Deux Sèvres story: some reports remain genuinely unidentified, but none provides strong evidence of an extraordinary craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... The Sainte Soline case stands apart At about 3.30 am on 16 August 1980, a witness returning from a village dance reported seeing an object over a field near Sainte Soline. It was described as resembling the cap of a mushroom, approximately 15 metres across and emitting a powerful red light. The witness approached to within an estimated 400 metres, became frightened and left. During the journey away, the object was reportedly seen above the village, still glowing red and making no sound. Investigators found no damage or marks on the soil or vegetation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN places the case in category D, meaning that the phenomenon remained unidentified after investigation rather than merely lacking enough information for analysis. The agency describes it as an observation of medium to strong consistency and moderate to high strangeness. This makes Sainte Soline the clearest official unresolved case currently associated with Deux Sèvres. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... That classification deserves careful interpretation. A category D finding does not mean that GEIPAN confirmed a physical vehicle, still less an extraterrestrial one. It means that investigators could not match the available account to a sufficiently convincing conventional explanation. GEIPAN states that its classifications weigh both the residual strangeness of a report and its “consistency”: the amount and reliability of the information gathered. A single account without photographs, independent witnesses or material evidence is inherently less secure than a case supported by several separate forms of evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... The main weakness is therefore not an obvious contradiction in the witness’s story but the narrow evidence base. The reported size and distance were estimates made at night, and the apparent movement of a light against a dark landscape can be difficult to judge accurately. There is no publicly documented radar return, aviation report, second viewing position or physical residue against which the account can be tested. The case remains unresolved, but unresolved is not the same as evidentially strong. The 1980 reports suggest a cluster, not a proven flap Sainte Soline was not the only unusual report recorded in Deux Sèvres in 1980. Less than a month later, during the night of 9–10 September, a witness at Courlay described a low, elongated luminous phenomenon moving in jerks near a road before rising and disappearing. No engine noise or unusual smell was reported. GEIPAN classifies the Courlay case as category C, meaning that the available information is insufficient for a reliable conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Liste des nouveaux cas... COURLAY (79) 10.09.1980 Classification: C Département: Deux Sèvres Date de... The two cases create the appearance of a small local cluster: both involved a solitary witness, nocturnal conditions, a low or apparently low light source, silence and unusual movement. However, those similarities are too general to establish that the same phenomenon caused both reports. Many unrelated objects can appear silent at a distance, and witnesses commonly underestimate the distance to isolated lights when there are few visual reference points. Other records from the period reinforce the impression that 1978–80 was the department’s most active era in the surviving official archive. GEIPAN lists a February 1978 report at Beauvoir sur Niort as probably identified, although its public catalogue does not specify the retained explanation. A March 1978 observation at Saint Maixent l’École and the 1980 Courlay report are category C cases, indicating inadequate data rather than unexplained phenomena of high evidential quality. [Geipan] geipan.fr BEAUVOIR SUR NIORT (79) 1978 26/02/1978 B Deux Sèvres Phénomène identifié non indiqué 03/10/2008 1978 02 26... This is better described as a group of reports than a major “flap”. A true flap normally involves numerous observations over a short period, preferably from independent locations and with some corroborating information. The Deux Sèvres cases are sparse and unevenly documented. Sainte Soline remains notable because it survived investigation without an explanation, not because the surrounding reports prove a sustained aerial event. The disputed Bressuire story of 1954 Deux Sèvres also appears in accounts of France’s celebrated 1954 flying saucer wave. Contemporary newspaper reports described a slaughterhouse employee travelling to work near Bressuire on 3 October. He allegedly saw a circular craft beside a small figure wearing something resembling a diving suit; according to the published story, the figure moved towards him before the witness fled and the object departed rapidly. Versions of the report appeared in several French newspapers, although they disagreed about such basic details as the witness’s age and the spelling of his name. [Academia] academia.edu The Worldwide UFO Wave of 1954 Academia(PDF) The Worldwide UFO Wave of 1954The second occurred at dawn when Angelo Girardo, a 55 year old stockyard worker, was going to... The Bressuire account is colourful, but its historical value is weakened by signs that it may have been a press driven or invented story. A specialist chronology of the 1954 wave points to reporting in the Deux Sèvres edition of La Nouvelle République du Centre Ouest stating that the affair was a hoax. The absence of a modern GEIPAN case file, reliable investigative documents or stable biographical details makes the dramatic newspaper versions difficult to treat as dependable testimony. [IntCat] intcat.blogspot.com intcat 1954 intcat 1954 This case illustrates a wider problem with 1950s UFO history. National newspapers frequently repeated short agency reports without conducting their own local investigation. Once copied, a claim could acquire the appearance of multiple source confirmation even when every version ultimately came from the same original story. The differences between the Bressuire reports are therefore more important than their number. For Deux Sèvres, Bressuire is best regarded as part of the cultural history of the 1954 wave rather than as a robust unresolved encounter. It shows how quickly the language of humanoids, circular craft and extraordinary speed entered local reporting, but later information appears to weaken rather than strengthen the original claim. Most modern cases have ordinary explanations The later GEIPAN record is dominated less by spectacular close encounters than by familiar sources of confusion: event lighting, illuminated clouds, lanterns and incomplete witness information. In August 1997, a report from Niort involving a pink glow in the sky was classified B, with a “sky rose” or ground based lighting display considered the probable explanation. In September 2015, lights seen while travelling between Airvault and Viennay were also classified B and attributed to a Thai lantern. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A particularly useful recent example occurred at Coulon during the night of 2–3 July 2025. A witness saw seven or eight green lights arranged in a rotating circle. They appeared to move abruptly, combine into a teardrop shape and pass above the house before returning to a circular formation. The observation lasted about five minutes and produced no photograph or video. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN concluded that the lights were probably produced by powerful ground based event projectors reflecting from a low cloud layer. Weather data indicated extensive cloud cover at suitable altitudes, while Niort lay roughly nine kilometres from the witness—well within the possible operating range of such projectors. Investigators could not identify the"},{"title":"Claims Explained","url":"/claims-explained/","description":"Later evidence weakened the dramatic Bressuire story, while many newer Deux-Sevres sightings received ordinary explanations.","breadcrumb":"How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record?","level":2,"text":"Claims Explained How Deux Sevres UFO Stories Changed Over Time Deux Sevres UFOs Claims Explained Claims Explained How Deux Sevres UFO Stories Changed Over Time Later evidence weakened the dramatic Bressuire story, while many newer Deux Sevres sightings received ordinary explanations. Later evidence weakened the dramatic Bressuire story, while many newer Deux Sevres sightings received ordinary explanations. event time window The disputed Bressuire humanoid story The small cluster of reports around 1980 Lanterns, event lighting and modern misidentifications deux sevres claims explained Introduction Deux Sèvres UFO stories have changed less because the sky became less mysterious than because investigations became more systematic. The department’s most dramatic old claim — a landed disc and a small humanoid near Bressuire in October 1954 — was repeated for decades in books and catalogues, but later testimony attributed the story to a joke invented by the witness. More recent reports have usually involved lights rather than occupants, and several have been matched to ordinary sources such as event projectors and floating lanterns. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 octobre 1954, Bressuire, Deux Sèvres... That does not mean every Deux Sèvres sighting has been solved. The red, silent object reported at Sainte Soline in 1980 remains officially unexplained. The broader historical pattern is nevertheless clear: later evidence weakened the department’s most spectacular encounter story, while improved checking of weather, lighting, movement and local events has explained many newer observations. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... The disputed Bressuire humanoid story At dawn on 3 October 1954, Angelo Girardo, described in most accounts as a 55 year old worker travelling to his job, supposedly encountered an object beside the road near Bressuire. The familiar version says that the craft was circular, about three metres wide and resting near the grain silos of an agricultural cooperative. A small figure wearing something like a diving suit stood beside it, moved towards Girardo and then returned to the object, which rose at extraordinary speed. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 octobre 1954, Bressuire, Deux Sèvres... The story appeared during France’s intense autumn 1954 “flying saucer” wave, when newspapers were publishing reports of discs, landings and strange occupants from across the country. National papers including Le Figaro , La Croix , France Soir and Libération were subsequently cited as early sources for the Bressuire account. Later writers such as Aimé Michel and Jacques Vallée reduced the story to a compact catalogue entry, helping it circulate far beyond Deux Sèvres. [openedition.org] journals.openedition.org Open Edition JournalsEnquêtes sur les soucoupes volantesby P Lagrange · 1990 · Cited by 32 — Il publie en 1954 Les Soucoupes volantes vie... The apparent abundance of references can be misleading. Most later entries did not represent new witnesses or independent investigations; they repeated the same original claim. One database reportedly contained 18 versions of the Bressuire incident, with inconsistent dates, times and descriptions. Girardo was variously said to have travelled by bicycle or motor scooter, while the figure appeared in some versions with a helmet and in others bare headed. Even the date drifted between 27 September, 2 October, 3 October and 13 October. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 octobre 1954, Bressuire, Deux Sèvres... This is a useful warning about historical UFO catalogues. Repetition can make a story look multiply confirmed when several entries actually descend from one newspaper report. Copying also introduces small alterations that later readers may mistake for additional detail rather than transmission errors. Why the hoax claim carries weight The decisive challenge appeared years later. According to accounts citing investigator René Pacaut, Girardo’s widow said that her late husband had invented the encounter to fool or impress his friends and workmates. Later critical catalogues therefore labelled Bressuire a hoax rather than an unexplained landing. One reconstruction also says that the Deux Sèvres edition of La Nouvelle République du Centre Ouest discussed the joking nature of the story as early as 4 October 1954, although the archival article itself is not readily available in the public material examined here. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 3 octobre 1954, Bressuire, Deux Sèvres... The hoax conclusion is persuasive but should still be described accurately. There is no surviving public confession from Girardo, and the key statement came through his widow or acquaintances after his death. It is therefore a reported admission, not a recorded first person retraction. Even so, it is stronger negative evidence than the original case possesses positive evidence: the sighting had one witness, no photograph, no known physical trace, no independent observer and no documented official investigation. Later reporting consequently weakened the claim rather than strengthening it. Nothing emerged to corroborate the alleged object or occupant, while the hoax testimony provided a straightforward reason why such a dramatic story had left no supporting evidence. Bressuire is best treated not as an unresolved close encounter, but as a probable fabrication preserved by repeated copying. GEIPAN : Tout savoir sur les OVNIS et Phénomènes Aérospatiaux (PAN) Channel: Science & Vie Open on YouTube A small cluster around 1980 By the late 1970s and early 1980s, French UFO reporting had entered a different phase. The state backed investigation service established by the French space agency in 1977 collected witness statements and gendarmerie material, attempted conventional identifications and assigned classifications according to the quality and strangeness of each case. The surviving Deux Sèvres files from this period are therefore more structured than the Bressuire narrative, although their evidence remains uneven. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — S'il y a de fortes chances pour que ce phénomène puisse recevoir une explication rationnelle (satellites, lanterne... The official database lists reports at Beauvoir sur Niort and Saint Maixent l’École in 1978. The Beauvoir case received a category B classification, indicating that investigators considered a conventional explanation probable, while the Saint Maixent report was placed in category C because reliable information was insufficient. The entries illustrate an important distinction: an unidentified report may be weak because too little was recorded, rather than because the observation resisted every plausible explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANGeipan Groupe d’études et d’informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés.... NIORT (79)... The most important local case came at Sainte Soline on 16 August 1980. At approximately 3.30 am, a witness returning from a village dance reported a bright red object above a field. It was described as shaped like the cap of a mushroom, perhaps 15 metres across, silent and visible from an estimated distance of about 400 metres. The witness later thought it was above the village. Investigators recorded no marks on the ground or damage to vegetation, and GEIPAN continues to classify the case as category D — unexplained after investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... Sainte Soline differs sharply from Bressuire. It does not depend on a later chain of sensational retellings, and there is no reported confession that the witness invented it. Yet it remains evidentially limited. There was one known witness, no photograph, no radar return and no material trace. Estimates of size, distance and altitude were made at night, when a bright isolated light can appear nearer, larger or more mobile than it really is. A second nocturnal report at Courlay followed in September 1980, contributing to the impression of a brief local concentration of sightings. The reports are sometimes treated as a Deux Sèvres “flap”, but the term should not imply a coordinated or independently confirmed event. The surviving cases involved separate witnesses and different descriptions, with no established common source. The 1980 period therefore occupies a middle position in the department’s history. It contains the strongest officially unresolved Deux Sèvres report, but not a body of mutually supporting evidence for unusual craft. It is more accurately described as a small cluster of mixed quality observations. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lanterns and projected light Later Deux"},{"title":"Sainte Soline","url":"/sainte-soline/","description":"The 1980 Sainte-Soline case remains officially unexplained, but its single-witness evidence leaves major questions unresolved.","breadcrumb":"How Strong Is the Deux Sevres UFO Record?","level":2,"text":"Sainte Soline Why Sainte Soline Remains Unexplained but Unproven Deux Sevres UFOs Sainte Soline Sainte Soline Why Sainte Soline Remains Unexplained but Unproven The 1980 Sainte Soline case remains officially unexplained, but its single witness evidence leaves major questions unresolved. The 1980 Sainte Soline case remains officially unexplained, but its single witness evidence leaves major questions unresolved. entity What the witness reported that night How GEIPAN assessed the case Why unresolved does not mean extraordinary deux sevres sainte soline Introduction The Sainte Soline sighting of 16 August 1980 is the best known officially unexplained UFO case in Deux Sèvres, but it is not the department’s strongest proof of an extraordinary object. At about 3.30 am, a lone motorist returning from a village dance reported a large, intensely red, mushroom cap shaped object above farmland. He approached before becoming frightened and driving away, later seeing the light apparently above the village. It made no sound, and investigators found no marks on the ground or vegetation. France’s official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, still places the case in category D: unidentified after investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... That status makes Sainte Soline important, but also easy to overstate. The case rests on one person’s night time observations, with no photograph, independent witness, instrument reading or physical trace presented in the public record. It is therefore a useful example of the difference between an account that has not been explained and evidence that positively supports an unusual craft. What the witness reported that night GEIPAN dates the observation to 3.30 am on Saturday, 16 August 1980. The witness was a 30 year old man travelling through the rural landscape around Sainte Soline after attending a public dance. He reported seeing a single object above a field, described as resembling the cap of a mushroom and emitting a powerful red or orange red light. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... The estimated diameter was about 15 metres. According to GEIPAN’s summary, the witness moved closer until he believed himself to be roughly 400 metres away. Fear then replaced curiosity, and he left the area. During his journey, he reportedly saw the object again, or continued to follow its apparent position, as it seemed to pass above the village. It remained brightly illuminated and completely silent. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... Several details explain why the account attracted official interest: The light was described as far more substantial than a distant star. The witness perceived a defined form rather than merely an isolated point of light. The phenomenon seemed to change position in relation to the field and village. No engine, rotor or other mechanical noise was heard. The witness believed the observation occurred at comparatively close range. These features give the report what GEIPAN calls residual “strangeness”: even after considering familiar objects and phenomena, the description does not fit neatly into an obvious category. The agency’s public entry characterises Sainte Soline as a strange to very strange phenomenon with medium to strong consistency. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... Yet nearly all the apparently impressive measurements came from the witness’s visual judgement. The reported 15 metre diameter depended on the assumed distance, while the assumed distance depended on interpreting an unfamiliar light against a dark rural landscape. Neither figure was independently measured. The phrase “mushroom cap” should also be read as a descriptive comparison, not as evidence that the witness resolved the structure of a solid machine. A bright light can create glare, a halo or an illuminated patch of cloud that appears much larger than its source. Conversely, a genuinely large object at 400 metres ought to have offered considerable visual detail if visibility was good. The public summary records colour, shape and silence, but not recognisable surfaces, windows, markings or structural components. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... How GEIPAN assessed the case GEIPAN is the present day successor to the French official unit that collected and investigated reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. It operates within the French space agency, CNES, and classifies cases according to two main factors: the reliability and quantity of the available information, called consistency, and the amount of strangeness remaining after ordinary explanations have been examined. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... Its four principal outcomes are: Category A: conclusively identified. Category B: probably identified. Category C: unidentified because essential information is missing. Category D: unidentified after investigation. Sainte Soline is category D rather than category C. That distinction means the investigators considered the case sufficiently developed to assess, yet did not find a conventional explanation that satisfactorily matched the report. GEIPAN notes that category D is generally used following a field investigation involving the witness, although the level of surviving public documentation varies between older cases. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... The classification is not a judgement that the witness saw an extraterrestrial vehicle. GEIPAN explicitly states that it has found no proof of an unknown spacecraft or extraterrestrial presence in its investigations. A D classification records an investigative outcome: the reported phenomenon has not been identified. It does not identify the phenomenon as something extraordinary. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... That caution is particularly important for an older case. GEIPAN’s current classification system was revised in 2008, long after the 1980 observation, and older files can be revisited when new information or improved explanatory knowledge becomes available. Sainte Soline’s public entry was updated in June 2021, but the classification remained D. The update therefore maintained the unresolved status; it did not add new corroborating evidence or transform the report into a confirmed physical event. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... The official assessment does support two limited conclusions. First, the report was not simply discarded as an obvious fabrication or trivial misidentification. Secondly, no proposed explanation was considered persuasive enough to close the case. Those are meaningful findings, especially within the relatively modest UFO record of Deux Sèvres. They are still much narrower than the claim that a structured, unknown craft was objectively present over Sainte Soline. The Mapping of Venus. Sky at Night audio. August 1980. Channel: Martin Mobberley Open on YouTube The single witness problem The central weakness of Sainte Soline is not that the witness’s account is demonstrably false. It is that almost every important feature depends on the perceptions and estimates of one person. A single witness can report an event honestly and accurately. Nevertheless, independent witnesses provide one of the most valuable checks in an unusual sighting. They can establish whether the phenomenon was visible from different positions, whether its apparent movement was real and whether descriptions of its colour, size and shape agree. Sainte Soline offers no such comparison in the published case data: GEIPAN records one witness and one phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE SOLINE (79) 16.08.1980 GEIPAN... The absence of another report is especially notable because the object was said to have passed over or near the village while emitting a violent red light. That does not prove that nothing was present; the observation occurred at 3.30 am, when most residents would have been asleep. Even so, a second account from the village would have substantially strengthened the claim and helped reconstruct the object’s route. The witness’s movement also complicates interpretation. Because he approached, withdrew and continued travelling, the reported change in the object’s position cannot be separated easily from changes in his own viewpoint. A stationary or distant light may seem to accompany a moving observer, while intervening trees, buildings and changes in road direction can make it appear to shift relative to the landscape. Without a second viewing position or a precisely reconstructed route, the apparent passage from field to village remains difficult to test. Night time distance estimation presents a related problem. People normally judge distance from familiar scale, surface detail, atmospheric perspective, binocular vision and the relationship between objects. Most of those cues weaken when the target is"},{"title":"Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded","url":"/why-dordognes-ufo-mysteries-often-faded/","description":"Dordogne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded Dordogne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Dordogne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a single celebrated “unknown” comparable with France’s best known cases. dordogne Introduction Several Dordogne reports initially sounded dramatic: two green discs near Eymet, a blue violet ball that apparently shocked a witness, a fluorescent green oval passing at low altitude, and repeated “machines” filmed over Carlux. Later examination generally weakened exotic interpretations. Some cases were convincingly identified; others remained incomplete because the evidence was too sparse. In the searchable public GEIPAN material, Dordogne appears to have produced more explained or weakly documented reports than robust, persistently unexplained ones. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le 24 avril 1976 vers 16h un couple dans la campagne remarque le bruit inhabituel d'un fort déplacement d'air suivi de l'apparition à 300... What the official record actually means France investigates reports of unusual things in the sky through GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects witness accounts directly and through organisations such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation, then compares them with weather, astronomical, aviation and other technical information. A report enters the system because the witness could not identify the phenomenon at the time, not because officials believe it involved an extraordinary vehicle. [gouv.fr] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés...2 Jul 2022 — Une fois les témoignages établis par procè... GEIPAN calls these reports unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its present classification considers both strangeness , meaning what unusual features remain after investigation, and consistency , meaning the amount and reliability of the available information. Broadly: A indicates a phenomenon that has been identified with high confidence. B indicates a probable identification. C means there is not enough reliable information to reach a firm conclusion. D1 or D2 is reserved for cases retaining significant unexplained features after investigation, with D2 representing the more strongly supported category. The distinction between C and D is especially important. A case is not scientifically impressive merely because nobody has explained it. Missing times, uncertain directions, absent photographs or delayed testimony may leave a report unresolved only because it cannot be tested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That distinction shapes Dordogne’s history. The department’s official material contains interesting witness experiences, but much of it falls into three groups: identifiable objects, probable conventional explanations and reports too incomplete to carry much evidential weight. Eymet in 1976: the department’s most striking early case On 24 April 1976, at about 4pm, a couple in the countryside near Eymet reportedly heard an unusual rush of air and saw two luminous green discs roughly 300 metres away. The description has the qualities that make a sighting memorable: daylight, two witnesses, apparent proximity, colour, movement and sound. It is one of the clearest candidates for a “landmark” Dordogne incident in the official archive. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le 24 avril 1976 vers 16h un couple dans la campagne remarque le bruit inhabituel d'un fort déplacement d'air suivi de l'apparition à 300... Yet GEIPAN does not classify Eymet as an unexplained D case. It places it in category B and associates the observation with electricity or plasma. The surviving public summary is brief, so readers should resist turning the classification into greater certainty than the record supports. A B classification means the proposed explanation is considered probable, not proved beyond doubt. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case matters because it illustrates a recurring problem in historical UFO research. A vivid narrative can survive for decades while the detailed investigative chain remains difficult for the public to reconstruct. Important questions remain hard to assess from a short summary alone: the exact weather, whether there were power lines or electrical disturbances nearby, how independently the two witnesses described the discs, and how soon their statements were recorded. A second Dordogne report from the same year shows the opposite evidential problem. An observation at La Bachellerie on 24 December 1976 is listed as category C because reliable information is lacking. That does not strengthen the case for an unknown craft; it means the record is too thin to support either an ordinary or an extraordinary conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The 1988 luminous ball that appeared to affect a witness The Saint Michel de Villadeix incident of 1 December 1988 is Dordogne’s most instructive example of a case becoming less mysterious after re examination. At about 8.20am, a man walking with his dog saw an intense blue violet sphere near the road. He described it as brighter than an arc welder, moving rapidly and silently. During the three or four second event he felt pressure and an electric discharge, while his dog ran towards home and stopped responding to commands. The witness made a statement to the gendarmerie on the day and another ten days later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988Dordogne... classification explicité dans une note d'enquête.... Le GEIPAN classe le cas... The incident was originally treated as unexplained. Later GEIPAN investigators reconsidered it as a probable electrical or plasma phenomenon. Their reasoning was unusually concrete: the light appeared close to intersecting power lines and a transformer; its movement followed the direction of one line; nearby residents reportedly experienced a short voltage drop at the relevant time; the witness’s sensations were compatible with an electrical disturbance. GEIPAN also gave greater weight to the witness’s immediate statement than to his later account. The second version introduced several balls and placed the phenomenon farther away, after the witness had been approached by neighbours and the media. Investigators regarded the earliest testimony as less vulnerable to memory change or outside influence. The case is now category B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988Dordogne... classification explicité dans une note d'enquête.... Le GEIPAN classe le cas... This reassessment does not prove that a particular form of ball lightning travelled along the line. Electrical plasma events are themselves difficult to reconstruct retrospectively. But the revised interpretation fits the location, power disturbance and physical sensations better than the unsupported idea of a structured craft. The case also reveals why witness sincerity is not the same as factual accuracy. Nothing in GEIPAN’s account suggests fabrication. A person can honestly report an extraordinary experience while misjudging distance, number, size or context. The changed second statement demonstrates how quickly a short event may develop into a more elaborate memory without deliberate dishonesty. Conférence Saint Quentin Astronomie PAN/OVNIs : le GEIPAN mène l'enquête depuis 48 ans Channel: Saint Quentin Astronomie Open on YouTube Military aviation created one of Dordogne’s strangest “objects” Shortly after midnight on 25 June 2019, witnesses at La Roche Chalais saw what they described as a low flying fluorescent green oval with red lights at the rear. It produced an unfamiliar humming or rushing air sound and remained visible for about a minute. The appearance did not resemble an ordinary helicopter to them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANROCHE CHALAIS (LA) (24) 25.06.2019Dordogne. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 12/03/2021. Type de phénomène. Hélicoptère. Etr... The eventual explanation was highly specific. An international military exercise was taking place about 40 kilometres away and involved American CV 22 Osprey tiltrotor aircraft. Lights near the blade tips could produce two virtual green discs as the rotors turned. Local people had also reported helicopters and green light in the exercise area. The witnesses’ home lay on a plausible route between the training zone and the military base at Mont de Marsan. GEIPAN classified the case A: a military aircraft observed under unfamiliar conditions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANROCHE CHALAIS (LA) (24) 25.06.2019Dordogne. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 12/03/2021. Type de phénomène. Hélicoptère."},{"title":"Electrical Lights","url":"/electrical-lights/","description":"The Eymet and Saint-Michel-de-Villadeix sightings show how vivid reports can weaken when electrical clues and early testimony are examined.","breadcrumb":"Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded","level":2,"text":"Electrical Lights Were Dordogne's Brightest UFOs Electrical Events? Dordogne UFOs Electrical Lights Electrical Lights Were Dordogne's Brightest UFOs Electrical Events? The Eymet and Saint Michel de Villadeix sightings show how vivid reports can weaken when electrical clues and early testimony are examined. The Eymet and Saint Michel de Villadeix sightings show how vivid reports can weaken when electrical clues and early testimony are examined. mechanism The green discs reported near Eymet The blue violet sphere at Saint Michel de Villadeix Why early testimony and power system clues mattered dordogne electrical lights Introduction Two of Dordogne ’s most dramatic UFO reports became less mysterious when investigators looked closely at electricity, weather and the order in which witnesses told their stories. Near Eymet in April 1976, a couple described green luminous discs plunging towards the ground during violent weather while nearby television sets reportedly lost sound and picture. At Saint Michel de Villadeix in December 1988, a man saw an intensely bright blue violet sphere near power lines, felt pressure and an electrical discharge, and watched his dog flee. Neither case is now treated by France’s official investigators as strong evidence of an unknown craft. GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, classifies both as category B: probably explained, in this instance as electrical or plasma phenomena. The second case is especially instructive because the witness’s account changed after contact with neighbours and the media. Together, the incidents show why vivid testimony must be compared with power system clues, weather conditions and the earliest available statement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEYMET (24) 24.04.1976 GEIPAN... The green discs reported near Eymet On 24 April 1976, at about 4pm, a couple were in their garden in the countryside near Eymet. The weather was stormy, with large dark clouds obscuring the Sun. According to the gendarmerie report, an abnormal noise resembling a powerful rush of air drew their attention. A few hundred metres away they saw two luminous, jade green discs. The lights appeared to plunge almost vertically towards the ground at very high speed and then disappear. The brief display was said to occur again twice. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEYMET (24) 24.04.1976 GEIPAN... The observation sounded striking because several features appeared to reinforce one another: two witnesses, daylight, apparent proximity, unusual colour, rapid movement and an associated noise. Early newspaper coverage pushed the story towards the language of flying saucers, even suggesting that the objects had caused a snowstorm. The surviving police file, however, records a more cautious sequence. A violent gust followed the lights, snow then fell, and residents in the area reportedly experienced temporary loss of television sound and picture. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Those details changed the direction of the inquiry. A local woman familiar with storms reportedly heard an unusually loud thunderclap and went indoors. Investigators considered the television interference, severe local weather and luminous effects together rather than treating the discs as isolated flying objects. Their report proposed an electrical disturbance associated with a rare form of storm activity, described at the time as a possible “white storm”. GEIPAN’s current summary similarly calls the incident a probable electromagnetic phenomenon caused by a local weather event and places it in the electricity and plasma category. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEYMET (24) 24.04.1976 GEIPAN... This does not mean that investigators reproduced the event or proved the exact physics. “Plasma” is sometimes used too loosely in UFO discussion, as though naming ionised gas automatically solves a case. In Eymet, the responsible conclusion is narrower: the timing of the lights, gust, snowfall and television disturbance gives an electrical weather explanation more support than the idea of two engineered objects. The case also illustrates how reporting can amplify a sighting. The gendarmerie file criticised inconsistencies and sensational wording in the press coverage. One later article presented the witnesses as denying that they had seen UFOs at all and describing the occurrence instead as rare atmospheric magnetism. Whether that wording precisely reflected their scientific understanding is uncertain, but it shows that the public “UFO” narrative was not a stable, uncontested version of the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The blue violet sphere at Saint Michel de Villadeix The Saint Michel de Villadeix case occurred on 1 December 1988 at about 8.20am. A man walking near his home with his dog reported an intense light roughly 60 metres away. He compared its brightness with an electric arc and described a round, blue violet form travelling silently in a straight south to north direction at very high speed. The observation lasted only three or four seconds. During it, he felt pressure and an electrical shock; his dog ran towards the house and ignored his commands. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988 GEIPAN... As a narrative, it contains features often regarded as signs of a high quality close encounter: a nearby luminous object, a physical effect on the witness and unusual animal behaviour. The immediate surroundings, however, supplied unusually specific conventional clues. The light was reported close to a crossing of two electricity lines and a transformer. Its course was aligned with one of the lines. Residents experienced a sudden, brief voltage drop at approximately the same time, while the witness’s physical sensations were themselves compatible with a strong electrical disturbance. GEIPAN argues that ionisation of air or airborne material in an intense electric field could produce a luminous form, comparing the basic principle with gas glowing inside a neon tube. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988 GEIPAN... The hypothesis is plausible without being a complete laboratory reconstruction. Scientific reviews describe ball lightning and related luminous electrical events as rare, incompletely understood phenomena reported most often in association with lightning or strong electrical conditions. Many proposed mechanisms exist, but no single model accounts securely for every report. That uncertainty matters: Saint Michel de Villadeix should not simply be labelled “ball lightning” as though the term identified a proven mechanism. The better supported claim is that a short lived electrical light near power infrastructure fits the observed setting more closely than an independent airborne vehicle does. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The Riddle of Ball Lightning: A Review One of the most intriguing and enduring scientific challenges is to find an explanation for ball lightning, the shining fireballs that so... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The account that changed after ten days The most important evidence in the Saint Michel de Villadeix file is not the colour of the sphere or the frightened dog. It is the existence of two gendarmerie statements. The witness made his first deposition on the day of the event. Ten days later, he gave another account. According to GEIPAN’s re examination, the later version introduced several luminous balls and placed the phenomenon farther away, in a field rather than near the road, transformer and crossing power lines. The 1988 assessment had relied heavily on that expanded version and left the case unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988 GEIPAN... When GEIPAN revisited the case, it gave greater weight to the first statement. That was not because early testimony is automatically accurate, but because it was recorded before prolonged discussion and publicity. The later account had followed numerous contacts or approaches from neighbours and media representatives. GEIPAN concluded that these interactions could have influenced the witness, even without any deliberate embellishment. Once the initial location and single light description were restored to the centre of the analysis, the connection with the electricity network became much stronger. The case was reclassified from D, unexplained, to B, probably explained as an electrical and plasma phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT MICHEL DE VILLADEIX (24) 01.12.1988 GEIPAN... Research on eyewitness memory supports the general caution behind that decision. Memory is reconstructive rather than a fixed recording. Information encountered after an event can alter later recall, and repetition can increase both"},{"title":"Military Aircraft","url":"/military-aircraft/","description":"The La Roche-Chalais case shows how unfamiliar military aircraft, rotor lights and exercise routes can create a convincing UFO impression.","breadcrumb":"Why Dordogne's UFO Mysteries Often Faded","level":2,"text":"Military Aircraft The Military Aircraft Mistaken for a Dordogne UFO Dordogne UFOs Military Aircraft Military Aircraft The Military Aircraft Mistaken for a Dordogne UFO The La Roche Chalais case shows how unfamiliar military aircraft, rotor lights and exercise routes can create a convincing UFO impression. The La Roche Chalais case shows how unfamiliar military aircraft, rotor lights and exercise routes can create a convincing UFO impression. entity What witnesses saw at La Roche Chalais How the CV 22 Osprey created green discs Why exercise routes and unfamiliar sounds solved the case dordogne military aircraft Introduction The fluorescent green “UFO” reported at La Roche Chalais in northern Dordogne on 25 June 2019 was almost certainly a military aircraft. A witness awakened by an unfamiliar droning sound saw a low, slow moving oval of green light, with smaller red lights behind it, crossing the night sky towards the south west. The appearance was extraordinary, but the timing, route, sound and lighting all matched a rare American CV 22 Osprey taking part in the international military exercise Athéna 2019. France’s official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, ultimately classified the case as identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 The case matters within Dordogne’s UFO history because it demonstrates how a sincere and apparently detailed observation can be transformed by unfamiliar aviation technology. The witnesses did not simply mistake an ordinary aeroplane light for something exotic. They encountered an unusual tiltrotor aircraft whose illuminated spinning blades can resemble glowing discs, operating after midnight on a route that local residents would have had little reason to expect. Un Ovni a t il vraiment été filmé pendant le jubilé de la reine ? Chasseurs de Fake Lumni Channel: Lumni Open on YouTube What witnesses saw at La Roche Chalais At approximately 12.30 am, a resident in a hamlet south east of La Roche Chalais was awakened by a buzzing or droning noise outside her open bedroom window. She initially considered familiar possibilities, including the local medical helicopter, but the object she then saw did not look or sound like the helicopters she knew. She described a fluorescent green oval, apparently flying at low altitude and modest speed, with several weaker red signals towards the rear. The passage lasted about a minute before the object disappeared towards the south west. Her partner also came to the window, although only one full witness statement entered the official file. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 Several details made the experience compelling. It involved both light and sound, continued long enough for the primary witness to wake another person, and seemed to show a structured object rather than a distant point. The estimated size was about six metres, although size and altitude are notoriously difficult to judge at night without a known reference. No photograph was taken, and the investigation recorded no direct radar trace linking a particular aircraft to the house. Those limitations matter: the identification rests on a strong convergence of circumstances rather than on an individually confirmed flight track. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 The witness’s interpretation was also understandable. A green oval accompanied by an unfamiliar mechanical hum does not resemble the everyday appearance of a passenger aircraft. Even the suggestion of a conventional helicopter seemed inadequate to her because the sound, shape and colour were unlike anything she had previously encountered. GEIPAN did not treat that reaction as foolish or dishonest. Its investigation instead asked what real aircraft present in the region could produce precisely those unusual features. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 How the CV 22 created green discs The CV 22 Osprey is neither a conventional helicopter nor an ordinary fixed wing aeroplane. It is a tiltrotor: its two large engine and rotor assemblies can point upwards for vertical take off and hovering, then rotate forwards for faster aeroplane like flight. The United States Air Force uses the CV 22 for long range special forces transport, including low level operations by day or night. Its hybrid configuration gives it a silhouette, motion and acoustic character unfamiliar to most members of the public. [Air Force Special Operations Command] afsoc.af.mil Air Force Special Operations Command CV 22 Osprey Air Force Special Operations CommandCV 22 Osprey AFSOC Air ForceThe CV 22 Osprey is a tiltrotor aircraft that combines the vertical t... The decisive feature at La Roche Chalais was the lighting fitted near the rotor blade tips. CV 22 blades have controllable, night vision compatible tip lights intended to help crews and ground personnel see the rotor area during darkness. As each illuminated point spins rapidly, persistence of vision makes its circular path appear continuous. Instead of seeing individual green lamps, an observer can therefore perceive a bright ring or “virtual disc”. Two rotors produce two such discs, but their apparent separation and shape vary with viewing angle, distance, darkness and the visibility of the aircraft body. From some directions they can overlap visually and resemble a single oval. [The Aviationist] theaviationist.com The AviationistThis is not a flying saucer but a CV 22 tilt rotor aircraft at nightAugust 16, 2013 — 16 Aug 2013 — Once the rotor is work... Published: August 16, 2013 This mechanism accounts for the most distinctive element of the report without forcing the testimony into the shape of an ordinary helicopter. The witness saw fluorescent green light because the rotor tip lights were green. She perceived an oval because rotating points of light generated luminous rotor circles viewed obliquely. The weaker red lights behind the green area were consistent with aircraft anti collision or navigation lighting visible near the tail. GEIPAN noted that a red anti collision light on the aircraft’s rear section would have been on the side presented to the witnesses during the reported right to left passage. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 The sound fits as well. An Osprey’s two large prop rotors create a powerful combination of blade noise, low frequency vibration and displaced air. To a person familiar only with common civilian helicopters, it may register as a strange mixture of buzzing, breath like airflow and propulsion noise rather than as an immediately recognisable aircraft. GEIPAN considered that unfamiliarity important: both the visual “discs” and the sound arose from genuine features of a rare machine, not from obvious observational incompetence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête February 2, 2021 — 9 Oct 2019 — domicile de La Roche Chalais. CV 22 Osprey qui a la particularité d'avoir des lumières vertes au bout des... Published: February 2, 2021 ASTRONOGEEK🪐Déclassification des OVNIs : à quoi s'attendre ? REACTION Channel: The World with D Open on YouTube Why the military exercise solved the case An unusual aircraft is only a useful explanation if there is evidence that one was operating in the right region at the right time. In this case, the observation coincided with Athéna 2019, an international special forces aviation exercise centred on Mont de Marsan air base. An official French aeronautical notice created temporary restricted and danger zones for the exercise between 17 and 27 June. It warned of numerous French and foreign military aircraft operating at different heights and under varied flight conditions across a wide area, including sectors around Angoulême and Dignac north of Dordogne. [SIA] sia.aviation civile.gouv.fr SIASIA Aviation Civile SIASIA Aviation Civile Published: May 27, 2019 The relevant zones were active late on 24 June and into the early hours of 25 June. GEIPAN established that operations were taking place around Angoulême and Dignac during the period surrounding the La Roche Chalais sighting. Aircraft involved in Athéna reportedly included three CV 22"},{"title":"Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious","url":"/why-doubs-ufo-reports-became-less/","description":"Doubs has a documented history of unusual-sky reports, but the official record is less mysterious than its popular reputation might suggest.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious Doubs has a documented history of unusual sky reports, but the official record is less mysterious than its popular reputation might suggest. Doubs has a documented history of unusual sky reports, but the official record is less mysterious than its popular reputation might suggest. doubs Introduction The department nevertheless offers a useful cross section of French UFO history. It includes older reports recorded by the gendarmerie, modern sightings tested against astronomical software and air traffic data, recurring misidentifications around Pontarlier and Besançon, and a small residue of cases that cannot be reconstructed reliably. The central lesson is not that witnesses invented their experiences. It is that sincere, sometimes vivid observations can become far less mysterious once direction, timing, radar tracks and viewing conditions are checked. What the official record contains GEIPAN was created within the National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES, in 1977. It collects first hand reports, investigates them using recognised scientific knowledge and publishes anonymised files. Its role is not to prove or disprove extraterrestrial visitation. In fact, it prefers the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon”, because a witness may have seen a light or visual effect rather than a solid flying object. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Cases are graded according to both their remaining strangeness and the strength of the available information: A: confidently identified; B: probably identified; C: not identifiable because important information is missing; D: still unidentified after investigation. That distinction is essential when reading the Doubs files. A category C report is not strong evidence of an unknown craft. It usually means that investigators lacked an exact time, dependable direction, independent witnesses, photographs or other material needed to test explanations. Category D has a narrower meaning: the evidence was considered sufficient for study, but no satisfactory explanation was found. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr Open source on estrepublicain.fr. The department search results currently published by GEIPAN include reports from Pontarlier, Besançon, Vieilley, Étupes, Rochejean, Fourg, Baume les Dames, Seloncourt, Béthoncourt, Montferrand le Château and several smaller communes. In the visible group, the identified or probable causes include the International Space Station, satellites, aircraft, balloons, sky lanterns, Venus and even a bird. Several category C entries are explicitly described as lacking reliable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... This makes Doubs important less for one celebrated “landing case” than for the variety of mundane phenomena that can produce convincing UFO reports. Its official history is primarily a history of investigation and correction. The Vieilley fireball was a military jet One of the department’s strongest modern investigations began at Vieilley on the evening of 19 May 2022. A witness heard an exceptionally powerful, thunder like roar and then saw what appeared to be a huge red orange ball travelling rapidly towards Besançon. The object seemed low, round and fiery. It remained visible for roughly seven to ten seconds, while the sound continued after it had disappeared. There was no photograph or video, but the description was dramatic enough to suggest a meteor, an aircraft emergency or something more unusual. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.202219 May 2022 — VIEILLEY (25) est à son domicile. Le PAN se dirige en direction de Besançon. Le témoin l'a ob... Published: May 2022 The decisive evidence came from aviation records. GEIPAN compared the account with aircraft tracking information and consulted the National Air Operations Centre. The military confirmed that two Mirage 2000 fighters were nearby. One passed about two kilometres south east of the observation point on a course consistent with the witness’s description. Investigators concluded that the apparent “fireball” was a Mirage using afterburner at low altitude. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.202219 May 2022 — VIEILLEY (25) est à son domicile. Le PAN se dirige en direction de Besançon. Le témoin l'a ob... Published: May 2022 That explanation accounts for the complete combination of reported features rather than merely matching one detail. An afterburner can appear as an intense orange red light when an aircraft is viewed from behind or at a narrow angle. The fighter’s speed explains the brief observation, while the delayed and lingering noise follows naturally from the slower propagation of sound. GEIPAN therefore assigned the case category A. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.202219 May 2022 — VIEILLEY (25) est à son domicile. Le PAN se dirige en direction de Besançon. Le témoin l'a ob... Published: May 2022 Vieilley matters because it shows what a high quality explanation looks like. The witness’s impressions of size, distance and form could not be independently measured, but the direction, timing and sound could be tested against external records. The case became more reliable after investigation, yet less mysterious. It also provides a genuine military connection without requiring speculation about secret vehicles. Doubs lies within airspace where military aircraft may be encountered, and unusual lighting or viewing angles can conceal a familiar aircraft’s wings and body. GEIPAN has documented the same broader problem elsewhere in France: aircraft can appear stationary, triangular or saucer like when perspective, motion and lighting disguise their normal shape. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Venus and the problem of the brilliant fixed light A much quieter case occurred at Étupes before dawn on 22 August 2017. A witness watched a bright, nearly stationary light in the east for about half an hour and recorded it with a tablet and camcorder. Magnification appeared to show a changing shape with colours and a pointed lower edge. Taken alone, the footage might look more unusual than the original point of light. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Département Doubs Classification A Date de. Etrangeté 0.05 Consistance 0.75 … moin observe depu... Investigators used landscape details visible after sunrise to estimate the viewing direction. At approximately 6.08 am, the light was around azimuth 84 degrees. Astronomical reconstruction placed Venus in that same direction, shining at an apparent magnitude of about minus four. Its brightness, position and lack of meaningful movement matched the observation. The case was classified A. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Département Doubs Classification A Date de. Etrangeté 0.05 Consistance 0.75 … moin observe depu... The strange forms in the recordings did not strengthen the UFO interpretation. Consumer cameras cannot resolve a planet as a detailed disc. Heavy zoom, imperfect focus, camera movement, lens aperture effects and electronic processing can turn a bright point into a pulsating or oddly shaped image. Colour fringes may likewise be produced by optical aberration rather than by the object itself. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Département Doubs Classification A Date de. Etrangeté 0.05 Consistance 0.75 … moin observe depu... An older report from Cussey sur l’Ognon illustrates the same pattern. On 17 February 1980, a witness observed a large yellowish point that remained fixed for more than ten minutes. Local press coverage suggested that other people in the area had seen something similar, but the gendarmerie did not locate additional witnesses who could contribute usable statements. A sky chart showed Venus shining brightly in the reported direction. Because the estimated elevation did not match perfectly, investigators treated the identification as probable rather than certain and classified the case B. [Geipan] geipan.fr CUSSEY SUR L'OGNON (25) 17.02.1980Département. Doubs. Classification. B. Date de mise a jour. 23/10/2009. Type de phénomène. Phénom... The contrast between Étupes and Cussey sur l’Ognon is instructive. Both probably involved Venus, but the 2017 file contained better timing, imagery and landscape references. The later investigation could reconstruct the geometry with much greater precision. The difference between A and B therefore reflects evidential confidence, not a difference in how sincere or impressed the witnesses were. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K"},{"title":"Solved Sightings","url":"/solved-sightings-c89df3/","description":"From a Mirage afterburner to Venus and satellites, familiar objects have produced some of Doubs' most convincing UFO reports.","breadcrumb":"Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Solved Sightings The Ordinary Objects Behind Doubs' Strangest Sightings Solved Sightings Solved Sightings The Ordinary Objects Behind Doubs' Strangest Sightings From a Mirage afterburner to Venus and satellites, familiar objects have produced some of Doubs' most convincing UFO reports. From a Mirage afterburner to Venus and satellites, familiar objects have produced some of Doubs' most convincing UFO reports. mechanism The Vieilley fireball and the Mirage 2000 Venus, zoom artefacts and fixed bright lights Satellites, lanterns, balloons and other recurring causes doubs solved sightings Introduction Some of Doubs ’ strangest reported objects were not distant mysteries but familiar lights seen in unfamiliar ways. A low flying Mirage 2000 in afterburner appeared as an enormous red orange fireball; Venus became a changing, pointed object when filmed out of focus; and satellites, the International Space Station, balloons and sky lanterns produced silent lights whose apparent motion was difficult to judge. These cases matter because they show how an honest and detailed account can still be misleading. Witnesses generally describe what they genuinely perceived, but darkness removes reliable clues to distance, size and speed. Cameras can add shapes and colours that were never present in the sky. Investigators therefore have to compare the account with aircraft tracks, astronomical positions, satellite passages, wind conditions and the behaviour of photographic equipment. In Doubs, those comparisons have resolved a substantial part of the modern official record. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.2022 GEIPANMay 19, 2022 — Observation du déplacement rapide d'une grosse boule de feu et son assourdissant... Published: May 19, 2022 The Vieilley fireball was a Mirage 2000 The most vivid example occurred at Vieilley on the evening of 19 May 2022. A resident first heard an exceptionally powerful roar, likened to thunder but much louder. Turning towards the sound, she saw a huge, round, red orange light moving rapidly towards Besançon. It seemed close to the ground and appeared to be on fire, although it left none of the luminous trail normally associated with a meteor. The visual observation lasted about seven to ten seconds; the noise remained audible for roughly another 30 seconds. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.2022 GEIPANMay 19, 2022 — Observation du déplacement rapide d'une grosse boule de feu et son assourdissant... Published: May 19, 2022 Taken alone, the description was dramatic. A “gigantic” fiery sphere, apparently travelling at high speed and low altitude, could easily be remembered as an unknown craft or a near impacting object. The combination of sound and light also made the report stronger than a distant point of light: the witness had experienced a sudden, physically impressive event rather than merely noticing something unusual among the stars. The investigation changed the interpretation because it obtained information independent of the witness. France’s National Air Operations Centre confirmed that two Mirage 2000 fighters were nearby at the relevant time. One passed about two kilometres south east of the observation point on a course consistent with the reported movement. GEIPAN concluded that the witness had seen the rear of a Mirage flying at low altitude with its afterburner operating, and classified the case as category A, meaning securely identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.2022 GEIPANMay 19, 2022 — Observation du déplacement rapide d'une grosse boule de feu et son assourdissant... Published: May 19, 2022 An afterburner injects additional fuel into a jet’s exhaust, producing a brilliant flame and a major increase in noise. Viewed from behind at night, the aircraft’s body may be difficult or impossible to distinguish. The dominant visual impression is then the bright exhaust, which can resemble a compact orange or red ball. Perspective compounds the effect: an aircraft moving partly away from an observer may show little obvious sideways displacement, while the absence of visible terrain between witness and light makes its altitude and physical size almost impossible to estimate. The sound did not contradict the aircraft explanation. It helped confirm it. Noise arriving from a fast moving aircraft can seem detached from the light because sound travels far more slowly than light. The witness may continue hearing the engines after the aircraft itself has passed out of sight, especially when terrain, buildings and the aircraft’s changing direction affect how the sound reaches the ground. In the Vieilley case, the roar, the brief observation, the steady high speed and the fiery colour all fitted the same source. This is an unusually strong solved case because the explanation does more than resemble the report. It matches the location, time, direction, noise and visual appearance, while the military authority independently confirmed the relevant flight. It also demonstrates why “military connection” does not automatically imply a secret encounter. Here, military data removed the mystery rather than adding to it. Venus, zoom artefacts and fixed bright lights A different mechanism operated at Étupes before dawn on 22 August 2017. At about 4.30 am, a witness noticed a bright light fixed in the eastern sky and recorded it using a tablet and camcorder during an observation lasting around half an hour. In the enlarged footage, the light appeared to have a changing form, including a rounded shape with a point beneath it and shifting colours. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Doubs. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 15/03/2018. Type de... Vénus à l'azimut 84° et... The crucial evidence came from a later outdoor recording that included recognisable parts of the landscape. Investigators used those reference points to estimate that the light was at an azimuth of about 84 degrees at 6.08 am. Astronomical reconstruction placed Venus at the same bearing, shining at an apparent magnitude of approximately –4. GEIPAN therefore classified the case as a certain observation of Venus. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Doubs. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 15/03/2018. Type de... Vénus à l'azimut 84° et... The photographs and video did not disprove that identification. They explained why the planet had looked unusual. A planet is effectively a tiny point of light to ordinary consumer cameras. Strong digital zoom cannot recover surface detail that the lens and sensor never resolved. Instead, it enlarges blur, electronic processing and the shape of the camera’s aperture. When focus is inaccurate, a bright point can spread into a disc, polygon, ring or pointed form. Small movements of the camera make the enlarged blur quiver or deform. Colour fringes can arise from chromatic aberration, atmospheric disturbance, sensor noise and automatic processing. A shape that appears to pulse, rotate or change colour in a zoomed recording may therefore describe the imaging system rather than the object. GEIPAN specifically concluded that the forms and colours in the Étupes footage were optical and electronic artefacts caused by defocus and magnification. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Doubs. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 15/03/2018. Type de... Vénus à l'azimut 84° et... The case is valuable because it reverses a common assumption about photographic evidence. Recording a light does not necessarily make an identification more dependable. A wide view containing the horizon, landscape and stars can provide useful direction and scale. A tightly zoomed, unfocused blob often removes those references and magnifies defects instead. Venus is particularly effective at generating such reports. It is brighter than every natural object in the night sky except the Moon and can remain apparently stationary for long periods. Near the horizon, atmospheric turbulence may make it flicker or change colour. Without a visible frame of reference, a witness can interpret slight bodily or camera movement as motion by the light itself. In the Étupes file, the witness’s basic unaided eye description — a large, star like light with little movement — supported the planetary explanation more strongly than the distorted close ups did. [Geipan] geipan.fr ETUPES (25) 22.08.201722 Aug 2017 — Doubs. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 15/03/2018. Type de... Vénus à l'azimut 84° et... Satellites can look controlled or impossibly smooth Several Doubs reports have been attributed to satellites or the International Space Station. The official database identifies ISS observations at Rochejean on 19 April 2011, Fourg on 14 June 2011, Pontarlier on 17 August 2013 and Frambouhans on 17 July 2022. A report from Les Auxons on 19 June 2017 was assessed as probably caused by a satellite. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casDoubs, Vénus, 15/03/2018, 2017 08 22, voir. FRANOIS (25) 06.02.2019,"},{"title":"GEIPAN Files","url":"/geipan-files-aadf4b/","description":"Doubs case files show how timing, direction, photographs and missing evidence separate solved reports from genuinely unresolved ones.","breadcrumb":"Why Doubs UFO Reports Became Less Mysterious","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Files What the Official Doubs UFO Files Actually Show Doubs UFOs GEIPAN Files GEIPAN Files What the Official Doubs UFO Files Actually Show Doubs case files show how timing, direction, photographs and missing evidence separate solved reports from genuinely unresolved ones. Doubs case files show how timing, direction, photographs and missing evidence separate solved reports from genuinely unresolved ones. dataset evidence How GEIPAN grades unusual sky reports Solved, probable and information poor cases What category C and D really mean doubs geipan files Introduction GEIPAN’s Doubs files show that an official “UFO case” is not automatically an unexplained one. Most published reports from the department were classified as identified or probably identified after investigators compared the witness account with astronomical positions, satellite passes, aircraft activity, weather, photographs and the reported direction of travel. Other cases were placed in category C because the evidence was too incomplete to support either an identification or a meaningful mystery. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des cas; PONTARLIER (25) 22.06.2021, 22/06/2021, B · Doubs, Ballon d enfant; VIEILLEY (25) 19.05.2... The distinction matters. A bright light may receive category A when its position matches Venus exactly, category B when photographs strongly suggest a balloon but do not prove it, or category C when the time, bearing or witness information is too uncertain to test. Category D is reserved for a report that remains unexplained despite having enough substance to investigate. In the Doubs material, the recurring lesson is therefore evidential rather than sensational: precise timing and direction often solve cases, while missing information usually weakens them rather than making them more extraordinary. How GEIPAN grades unusual sky reports GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES, collects, analyses and publishes reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. It was created in 1977 and uses a national network that can draw on gendarmes, volunteer investigators and specialists in fields such as astronomy, meteorology, aviation and image analysis. Its purpose is to explain observations where possible and preserve the remaining cases, not to begin from the assumption that witnesses saw extraterrestrial craft. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Its familiar A to D scale is based on two linked questions: how strong is the information, and how strange does the phenomenon remain after ordinary explanations have been tested? GEIPAN describes these qualities as the case’s consistency and residual strangeness . Consistency rises when the file contains accurate times, reliable directions, several compatible witnesses, original photographs, identifiable landmarks or independent records. Residual strangeness falls when those details fit a known object or event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... The categories mean: A — identified: the explanation is considered secure. B — probably identified: one ordinary explanation fits very well, although the evidence does not establish it beyond reasonable doubt. C — insufficiently documented: the phenomenon cannot be identified because essential information is missing, unreliable or unusable. D — unexplained after investigation: the evidence was adequate for investigation, but no satisfactory explanation was found. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr vestigation... This framework prevents two common mistakes. First, category B does not mean that investigators merely guessed. It normally means that an explanation accounts for the object’s appearance, motion, timing and circumstances but lacks one final confirmation, such as the recovery of the balloon or identification of the person who released a lantern. Secondly, category C is not a stronger version of category D. It is an information failure. GEIPAN may not know what the witness saw, but it also lacks enough evidence to treat the observation as a robust anomaly. GEIPAN can revisit C and D cases when new material appears. A later witness, a better date, an original photograph or an archived aviation record may change the classification. The letter attached to a file is therefore the outcome of the evidence available at a particular stage, not an unalterable judgement on the witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr vestigation... Exact times and directions turn lights into known objects The most decisive Doubs classifications are those in which the reported geometry can be reconstructed. A witness may describe an object as stationary, enormous or strangely coloured, but GEIPAN first asks where it appeared, at what time and how it moved against the landscape. The Étupes report of 22 August 2017 is a clear example. At about 4.30 am, a witness watched and recorded a bright, nearly stationary light in the eastern sky for roughly half an hour. Zoomed images appeared to show changing shapes and colours. Those features might look unusual when viewed alone, but a daylight video contained enough landscape detail for investigators to estimate the line of sight. At 6.08 am, the object’s estimated bearing was about 84 degrees. Astronomical software placed Venus at the same bearing, shining at approximately magnitude −4. GEIPAN classified the case A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The images did not overturn that identification. GEIPAN explained that heavy digital or optical zoom on a point like light can produce defocused shapes, chromatic fringes and apparent changes caused by the camera’s aperture, electronics and movement. In other words, the odd looking object in the recording was partly an image of Venus and partly an image of how the camera handled Venus. The case illustrates why a photograph is not automatically stronger evidence than naked eye testimony: its value depends on whether investigators can establish scale, focus, direction and the camera’s limitations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several other Doubs files were solved through time and track matching. Reports from Rochejean on 19 April 2011, Fourg on 14 June 2011 and Pontarlier on 17 August 2013 were classified A as sightings of the International Space Station. A later report from Frambouhans on 17 July 2022 received the same identification. These cases show why a silent light can seem more unusual than an aircraft: the ISS can be exceptionally bright, cross a large part of the sky steadily and then disappear when it enters the Earth’s shadow. Matching the reported minute and trajectory with an orbital pass can turn a seemingly untraceable object into a highly specific identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The same principle applies to less exotic astronomical objects. A 1980 report from Grandfontaine was classified B as the Moon. The important point is not that witnesses cannot recognise familiar objects under normal conditions. It is that haze, cloud gaps, unfamiliar viewing angles, partial obstruction and the lack of a visible horizon can make even the Moon or a planet appear detached from its ordinary context. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Aircraft records resolved the Vieilley “fireball” The Vieilley sighting of 19 May 2022 demonstrates the value of checking an observation against records unavailable to the witness. A single observer heard an exceptionally loud, thunder like noise and saw a large red orange luminous form moving rapidly towards Besançon. It was visible for only several seconds, and no photograph or video was taken. On description alone, the report could have suggested a meteor, a burning object or an unusually low craft. GEIPAN examined the relationship between the sound, motion and reported direction. The National Air Operations Centre of the French Air and Space Force confirmed that two Mirage 2000 aircraft were operating near the area at the relevant time. Investigators concluded that the phenomenon was an aircraft seen under conditions that made its lights and exhaust appear like a fiery ball. The case was classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This was not simply an argument that “there are aircraft in the sky”. The explanation gained weight because a specific military patrol was independently placed near the observation at the correct time. The powerful continuing noise also fitted fast military jets better than a silent astronomical object. GEIPAN noted that the file had only medium consistency"},{"title":"What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals","url":"/what-dromes-ufo-record-actually-reveals/","description":"Drôme has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals Drôme has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Drôme has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. The official record contains several striking reports—most notably at Chabeuil in 1978, Donzère in 1980 and Barret de Lioure in 2008—but it does not reveal a sustained pattern of verified extraordinary craft, radar encounters or military confrontations. drome Introduction A November 2025 export of the public GEIPAN database contained 32 cases assigned to Drôme. Ten were classified as explained with high confidence, eight as probably explained, eleven as too poorly documented for a firm conclusion, and only three as unresolved. Those totals should be treated as a dated snapshot rather than an immutable count, because GEIPAN can reassess older files; the Barret de Lioure case, for example, was updated again in June 2026. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The three cases that carry most weight Chabeuil, 28 February 1978 At about 7.30 pm, a woman driving with her son reported a bright orange object moving rapidly from north to south. According to GEIPAN’s case summary, the observation lasted roughly three minutes. The light appeared to stop, then resumed a slower southward movement before vanishing abruptly. No unusual sound was heard, no physical trace was found and no independent witness came forward. GEIPAN nevertheless classified the report as category D, meaning that it remained unexplained and was considered sufficiently strange and reasonably well documented. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The incident matters because it is one of the department’s clearest officially unresolved visual cases. Its duration is longer than a typical meteor, while the reported stop and start movement is not an obvious fit for a simple aircraft seen under normal conditions. Yet the evidential limits are equally important. The case rests on two related witnesses travelling together, with no photograph, radar record, instrument reading or confirmed observation from the nearby population. Chabeuil’s association with aviation can make the story sound more significant than the documents justify. The department’s main airfield lies between Chabeuil and Valence, but the published GEIPAN record does not report an air traffic control alert, a pilot encounter, a radar return or a military investigation tied to the sighting. The airport’s presence broadens the range of conventional possibilities; it does not establish an aviation connection to the unidentified object. Musée de la Résistance en Ligne [museedelaresistanceenligne.org] museedelaresistanceenligne.org Musée de la Résistance en Ligne Aérodrome de Valence Musée de la Résistance en Ligne Aérodrome de Valence Donzère, 3 December 1980 The Donzère report is the most dramatic close range account in Drôme’s official file. Shortly before 11 pm, a motorist said that he encountered a large circular object about 150 metres ahead of him, hovering approximately ten metres above the road. He described it as fluorescent green and silent. After he stopped, it allegedly approached his vehicle; frightened, he drove on, passed beneath it and believed that it followed him for roughly a kilometre before accelerating away in the opposite direction. The encounter was said to have lasted three or four minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Investigators identified another person who recalled seeing a blue glow that evening, but the second statement was too brief to confirm the motorist’s detailed account. An appeal for witnesses and enquiries in the area produced no decisive corroboration. GEIPAN retained the case as category D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Donzère is memorable because it contains the features often associated with a strong UFO narrative: apparent proximity, a structured object, movement responsive to the witness and an emotional reaction. Those same features can encourage overinterpretation. There is no known photograph, physical effect, radar trace or fully independent description of the object. Distance and height estimates made at night are also vulnerable to error, especially when the size of the light source is unknown. The case is unresolved in the precise sense that no satisfactory explanation was established—not in the stronger sense that an exotic vehicle was demonstrated. Barret de Lioure, 8 July 2008 The Barret de Lioure observation is arguably the department’s best modern unresolved case because the witnesses were experienced sky observers using astronomical equipment. From the summit area of the mountain of Bergiès, they noticed a bright point that appeared and disappeared several times. Through the 9×50 finder attached to a telescope, each described an oval, steel grey object moving slowly southwards; one also reported a pendulum like motion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A GEIPAN investigator visited the site about a month later, found nothing physically abnormal and regarded the principal witness as sincere and competent. The original assessment described the case as unexplained, of moderately high strangeness and good evidential consistency. The current public page, updated on 16 June 2026, displays category D, while still reproducing text referring to the older D1 subcategory. This is a useful reminder that classifications and labels can change without the underlying sighting becoming either solved or more extraordinary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Experience strengthens the witnesses’ ability to describe where they were looking and how they used the equipment, but it does not remove every ambiguity. A small finder scope provides limited detail, atmospheric turbulence can distort point like sources, and no image or instrumental recording was obtained. The case therefore remains noteworthy rather than conclusive. The late 1970s cluster was not a single coherent flap Several Drôme reports fall between 1977 and 1981, which can create the impression of a concentrated UFO wave. The dates include reports from Chabeuil, Châteauneuf de Galaure, Lus la Croix Haute, Fay le Clos, Donzère and Crest. On closer examination, however, the cases differ sharply in quality and probable cause. At Châteauneuf de Galaure in January 1979, a witness reported a triangular object with coloured lights, downward facing beams and a whistling sound passing close to a car. The same person later described another prolonged display involving several lights. Despite the dramatic details and claimed duration, no confirming witnesses were obtained, and GEIPAN classified the file as category C because reliable information was insufficient. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At Lus la Croix Haute, a large ring in a field was initially treated as potentially unusual. Later information indicated that mushrooms grew both inside and outside the circle, undermining the idea that the mark represented a mysterious landing trace. The case was ultimately classed as explained. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréDRÔME/ARDÈCHE. Ovni: la vérité n'est pas forcément Le Dauphiné LibéréDRÔME/ARDÈCHE. Ovni: la vérité n'est pas forcément Other reports from the same period were attributed to Venus. This matters because bright planets near the horizon can appear unusually large, coloured, low and even mobile when viewed from a moving vehicle or through broken cloud. GEIPAN’s Drôme records include such explanations at Châteaudouble, Fay le Clos and, much later, Beauvoisin. The local cluster is therefore better understood as a mixture of unrelated events reported during a culturally active period for UFO interest, rather than evidence of one repeated phenomenon moving through the department. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 9:46 Tracking Down a Tic Tac UFO Channel: Mick West · Views: 87.6K · Uploaded: November 2021 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why ordinary objects repeatedly look extraordinary here Drôme combines open valleys, mountainous horizons, busy transport corridors, small airfields and large areas with dark skies. These conditions are good for observing the heavens, but they also produce difficult sight lines and misleading impressions of distance. Several recurring explanations stand out in the official record: Bright stars and planets: Venus, Sirius and Capella have accounted for long, apparently stationary or colour changing lights. Aircraft: Navigation lights, landing lights and military flying can appear to hover when an aircraft is approaching almost directly towards an observer. Atmospheric and astronomical events: A parhelion, or “sun dog”, explained two bright objects seen beside the setting Sun at"},{"title":"Common Explanations","url":"/common-explanations/","description":"Planets, aircraft, lanterns, atmospheric effects and satellites explain much of Drome's apparent UFO activity and weaken claims of a single local flap.","breadcrumb":"What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"Common Explanations Why Drome's Skies So Often Look Unusual Drome UFOs Common Explanations Common Explanations Why Drome's Skies So Often Look Unusual Planets, aircraft, lanterns, atmospheric effects and satellites explain much of Drome's apparent UFO activity and weaken claims of a single local flap. Planets, aircraft, lanterns, atmospheric effects and satellites explain much of Drome's apparent UFO activity and weaken claims of a single local flap. mechanism Planets, stars and misleading motion Aircraft, lanterns and transport corridors Re entries, satellites and false sighting clusters drome common explanations Introduction Many of Drôme’s apparent UFO reports began with something genuinely seen but wrongly interpreted: a bright planet near a ridge, a star distorted by turbulent air, an approaching aircraft, a drifting lantern, a satellite train or debris burning through the atmosphere. The department’s official record therefore looks less like one continuous “flap” than a succession of unrelated viewing mistakes produced by similar conditions. Those conditions matter. Drôme combines dark rural skies, long valleys, broken mountain horizons and busy transport routes. At night, distance and scale become difficult to judge. A light with no visible body may seem close, structured or responsive; movement by the observer can make it appear to accelerate or change direction. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, has identified Drôme reports as Venus, Sirius, Capella, aircraft, lanterns, condensation trails, spacecraft and satellite trains. These explanations do not settle every local case, but they substantially weaken the idea that most reports point to a single extraordinary cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Planets and stars can appear to manoeuvre Some of Drôme’s clearest identifications involved objects that never moved through the local sky at all. GEIPAN classified a 1977 report from Châteaudouble as Venus, a 2016 observation at Châteauneuf de Galaure as Sirius, a 2017 report from Beauvoisin as Venus and another from Albon as Capella. These were not vague suggestions applied to anonymous lights: investigators could compare the reported direction, elevation and time with the calculated position of the astronomical object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Beauvoisin case illustrates how terrain can help both create and solve a mystery. The witness photographed the light in relation to the surrounding relief. GEIPAN used that landscape reference to refine its position and found that it corresponded with Venus. A mountain skyline that initially made the light seem like an object hovering over a particular place ultimately supplied the fixed reference needed to identify it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr L'analyse de la photographie du témoin donnant la position du PAN par rapport au relief permet de préciser cette position et par là la co... Bright stars can be more deceptive than their familiar names suggest. Sirius and Capella are point like sources, so atmospheric turbulence can produce rapid changes in apparent brightness, colour and position. The effect is strongest near the horizon because their light passes through a longer and more disturbed path through the atmosphere. A low star may therefore flash red, blue or white and seem to quiver, descend behind a ridge or move sideways. [StarChild] starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov Star Child Why do stars twinkle? Why do stars twinkle? StarChild NASAThis is because there is a lot more atmosphere between you and a star near the horizon t... Dark surroundings add another perceptual problem. When a person watches an isolated light without trees, buildings or a clear horizon beside it, tiny eye movements can be interpreted as movement by the light itself. This is known as the autokinetic effect. It can make a stationary star or planet appear to drift, dart or reverse direction, especially when the observer stares at it for an extended period. The illusion is well known in night flying, where stars and planets can be mistaken for approaching aircraft. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Sensory illusions in aviation Sensory illusions in aviation This helps explain why sincere testimony can contain apparent manoeuvres that no astronomical chart predicts. A witness may correctly report a bright light and honestly describe motion, yet the motion may have arisen from a moving vehicle, an obscured horizon, head movements or the eye’s difficulty in fixing an isolated point. Such reports are not fabrications. They show why eyewitness certainty alone cannot establish the behaviour, distance or size of a nocturnal light. Aircraft and road movement distort distance Drôme is not an empty viewing platform. The Rhône corridor carries extensive road and rail traffic, while local and overflying aircraft provide moving lights at varying heights and angles. Valence Chabeuil airport supports business and private flying, aerial work, leisure flights and military activity, with published local information describing tens of thousands of annual aircraft movements. That does not explain every report near Valence, but it makes aviation a routine possibility rather than an exceptional coincidence. [La Drôme Tourisme] drome tourism.com La Drôme Tourisme Aéroport Valence Chabeuil La Drôme Tourisme Aéroport Valence Chabeuil Published: December 16, 2025 GEIPAN classified a 2011 report from Loriol sur Drôme as probably an aircraft. Aircraft are particularly easy to misread when seen almost head on. A landing light can remain in roughly the same part of the sky while steadily growing brighter, creating the impression of a hovering object that suddenly approaches. When the aircraft turns, its main light may disappear and coloured navigation or anti collision lights may become visible, making one object appear to change form or divide into several lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Observation from a moving car introduces further errors. Nearby trees, poles and ridges sweep across the visual field, while a distant light changes position only slightly. The brain may attribute this relative motion to the light rather than the vehicle. A driver following bends along a valley can consequently report that a light paced the car, moved from one side of the road to the other or stopped whenever the vehicle stopped. There is also a selection effect. An ordinary aircraft is most likely to be recognised when its fuselage, engine noise or familiar blinking pattern is obvious. It becomes a UFO report when only an unusual viewing angle is available: through cloud, above a dark ridge, during a turn, or at enough distance for separate lights to merge. The reports retained in an unusual phenomena archive are therefore not a representative sample of everything flying over Drôme; they are the occasions on which normal recognition failed. Condensation trails can produce a similar transformation. GEIPAN identified a 2016 Bourg lès Valence report as an aircraft condensation trail. Near sunrise or sunset, a high altitude trail may remain strongly illuminated after the ground below has entered shadow. A short bright section can resemble a glowing cigar, flame or descending object, while wind spreads and distorts the trail until its aircraft origin is no longer obvious. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Lanterns become silent formations Floating lanterns account for several reports in the department. GEIPAN classified observations at Bourg lès Valence in April 2014 and Romans sur Isère on New Year’s Day 2015 as probable lanterns. Their timing and behaviour fit a common pattern: warm coloured lights, no engine noise, slow collective movement and gradual disappearance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Lanterns are unusually effective UFO generators because they combine several apparently strange qualities: They are bright enough to be noticed but usually too distant for their paper bodies to be seen. They travel with the wind, so several lights may follow similar paths while changing their spacing. Flames fluctuate, giving the impression of pulsing or changing intensity. Individual lanterns extinguish at different moments, which can look like abrupt disappearance. A group released together may resemble a formation moving under coordinated control. The absence of sound can strengthen rather than weaken the extraordinary interpretation. Witnesses often expect an aircraft to be audible, but small wind borne lights may be completely silent at normal viewing distances. Without a visible structure, their distance is also unconstrained. A lantern a few hundred metres away can be imagined as a much larger object several kilometres distant. Celebrations create short"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-eca6a4/","description":"Chabeuil, Donzere and Barret-de-Lioure remain unexplained, but each is limited by missing images, instruments or independent witnesses.","breadcrumb":"What Drome's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Which Drome UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Which Drome UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Chabeuil, Donzere and Barret de Lioure remain unexplained, but each is limited by missing images, instruments or independent witnesses. Chabeuil, Donzere and Barret de Lioure remain unexplained, but each is limited by missing images, instruments or independent witnesses. dataset evidence Chabeuil and the stop start orange light Donzere and the close range road encounter Barret de Lioure and the telescope witnesses drome unresolved cases Introduction Only three cases in a November 2025 snapshot of the official GEIPAN database remained unresolved in Drôme: Chabeuil in 1978, Donzère in 1980 and Barret de Lioure in 2008. That makes them the central evidence base for the department’s enduring UFO claims—but not proof that extraordinary craft visited the area. GEIPAN’s category D means that a phenomenon remained unidentified after investigation, rather than that investigators established an exotic origin. Nationally, such cases form only a small minority of its published files. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFichiers CSV CasGEIPAN continue à publier l'ensemble de ses archives sur son site public www.geipan.fr. é de différentes observatio... Each of the three has a feature that resists an easy explanation. Chabeuil involved an orange light apparently stopping and restarting; Donzère was reported as a silent, close range object that followed a car; and Barret de Lioure was seen through an astronomical finder by experienced observers. Yet none produced a photograph, radar return, physical trace or instrument record capable of settling the matter. Their strength lies mainly in the detail and apparent sincerity of the testimony—not in independently measurable evidence. PHÉNOMÈNES INEXPLIQUÉS: Pourquoi le sujet OVNI revient en force ? Le Journal de l'Espace 341 Channel: Le Journal de l'Espace Open on YouTube Chabeuil and the stop start orange light At around 7.30 pm on 28 February 1978, a 30 year old woman driving with her ten year old son reported a very bright orange object moving rapidly across the sky from north to south. The observation reportedly lasted about three minutes. During that period, the phenomenon appeared to stabilise, then resumed a slower southward movement before disappearing instantaneously. Neither witness heard any sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHABEUIL (26) 28.02.1978 GEIPAN... The basic agreement between the two accounts gives Chabeuil more weight than a wholly solitary sighting. Both witnesses described a single orange or fire coloured phenomenon, variable movement and complete silence. Their descriptions of its apparent size were also broadly similar: the mother compared it with a car, while her son said it looked as large as their Citroën Dyane. GEIPAN retained the case in category D, describing it as a strange or very strange phenomenon with medium to strong consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHABEUIL (26) 28.02.1978 T1 GEIPAN... The reported pause is the most difficult detail to fit to a straightforward meteor. Meteors normally cross the visible sky continuously and briefly rather than stopping for an appreciable interval and then departing slowly. The three minute duration also works against a conventional shooting star. The abrupt disappearance is less diagnostic, however: an aircraft turning away, a distant light becoming obscured or a bright object entering cloud can all seem to vanish suddenly. There are important weaknesses. The witnesses were mother and child travelling together, not two independent observers whose accounts developed separately. The son was ten years old, and the surviving database entries provide little information about viewing angle, angular size, weather, cloud cover or identifiable stars. The adult witness’s estimated distance of 150 metres is especially uncertain because a lone light in a dark sky offers no reliable scale. Apparent distance and physical size cannot be calculated without knowing what the object was or having a fixed background reference. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHABEUIL (26) 28.02.1978 T1 GEIPAN... No trace was found and no additional witness was identified, despite the observation occurring near an inhabited and road served area. There is also no published photograph, flight record, radar detection or instrument reading associated with it. GEIPAN’s own summary therefore supports two conclusions at once: something unusual was sincerely reported, but the evidence was insufficient to determine what it was. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHABEUIL (26) 28.02.1978 GEIPAN... A perceptual explanation cannot be excluded. GEIPAN notes that a bright point against a dark, poorly referenced sky may appear to move, stop or change direction through effects such as autokinesis, in which involuntary eye movements make a fixed light seem mobile. That possibility does not neatly explain every reported feature, particularly the initial rapid transit, but the limited positional data prevent a reliable reconstruction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANComprendre un PhénomèneCette FAQ est dédiée aux personnes souhaitant signaler une observation de Phénomènes Aérospatiaux Non Identi... Chabeuil therefore remains a respectable unresolved light report, not a strongly evidenced close encounter. Its survival in category D reflects the lack of a satisfactory identification combined with a reasonably coherent account. It does not overcome the absence of objective corroboration. Donzère and the close range road encounter The Donzère case of 3 December 1980 is the most dramatic of Drôme’s three unresolved files. Shortly before 11 pm, a motorist travelling near Roussas reported seeing a large circular object roughly 150 metres ahead of him. He described it as fluorescent green, silent and stationary about ten metres above the road. When he stopped, the object allegedly moved slowly towards his vehicle. He drove on, passed beneath it and believed it followed him for about a kilometre before accelerating away in the opposite direction. The episode reportedly lasted three or four minutes. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr De couleur vert fluorescent il est… Observation OVNI à Donzere — décembre 1980 CarteOvni.frLe 03 décembre 1980 vers 23h un automobiliste aperçoit à 150 m devant lui, un ob... This account carries greater apparent strangeness than Chabeuil because the witness described not merely a distant light but a structured, nearby object whose movements seemed related to his own. A large circular form hovering over a road, approaching a stopped vehicle and then following it is not an obvious match for a star, planet or ordinary meteor. GEIPAN consequently categorised the file as a strange to very strange phenomenon of medium to strong consistency and left it unresolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. There was also a possible second observation. Another man at Les Granges Gontardes reported seeing a distant phenomenon or glow that evening. His database entry places the observation at about 9.30 pm, however, rather than close to the motorist’s reported 11 pm encounter, and describes the phenomenon as very far away. This makes it weak corroboration at best. It may indicate unusual lights were visible in the wider area, but it does not independently confirm a large object hovering over the road or pursuing a car. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Donzère’s narrative detail is both its main strength and its main vulnerability. Close range descriptions can appear evidentially powerful, but distance, altitude and dimensions judged at night are highly dependent on assumptions. A witness who believes a light is 150 metres away will interpret changes in brightness or bearing as local manoeuvres; the same visual information could look quite different if the source were much farther away. Without photographs, fixed angular measurements or a reconstructed line of sight, the claimed ten metre altitude and large diameter cannot be independently checked. The impression that a light is following a vehicle also has a familiar geometric explanation. A distant source can maintain roughly the same bearing as an observer moves, creating the sense that it is travelling alongside or behind the car. This “following” effect is often associated with the Moon, planets or distant lights. It does not automatically solve Donzère, because the witness also described passing beneath a circular object, but it shows why apparent pursuit is not by itself proof of responsive movement. No known physical effect strengthens the account. The car was not reported as damaged or disabled, and the published summaries contain no radar trace, photograph, landing mark, vegetation change or measurable electromagnetic disturbance. Searches for further witnesses did not produce confirmation of the motorist’s detailed sequence. The official"},{"title":"Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions","url":"/why-essonnes-ufo-cases-often-defy-first/","description":"Essonne has produced a steady record of unusual-light reports, but its documented UFO history is less a catalogue of inexplicable craft than a lesson in how...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions Essonne has produced a steady record of unusual light reports, but its documented UFO history is less a catalogue of inexplicable craft than a lesson in how... Essonne has produced a steady record of unusual light reports, but its documented UFO history is less a catalogue of inexplicable craft than a lesson in how ordinary objects become extraordinary under difficult viewing conditions. Published cases include glowing spheres, hovering lights, apparent formations and lights moving close to the ground. essonne Introduction The most interesting Essonne case may therefore be Saint Hilaire in 1981, not because it proves anything exotic, but because it resisted a simple explanation and was later downgraded from “unexplained” to “insufficient or unsuitable evidence”. That change captures the department’s wider pattern: sincere witnesses, sometimes striking descriptions, but rarely the independent records needed to establish what physically happened. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPANClassification. Comprendre un phénomène... Essonne. Classification. C. Date de mise a jour... What Essonne’s case record actually shows GEIPAN is a technical unit within the French National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES. It collects witness statements, conducts investigations, publishes anonymised files and classifies reports according to how strange the event remains after analysis and how reliable the available information is. Category A means an identification is regarded as secure; B means a probable identification; C means the evidence is inadequate or unusable; and D is reserved for phenomena that remain unidentified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN That distinction matters in Essonne because an unresolved report is not automatically a strong report. A case can remain open simply because there was one witness, no photograph, no precise bearing, a delayed statement or no opportunity to inspect the location promptly. Conversely, a dramatic looking account can receive an A or B classification when its direction, timing and movement correspond closely to an astronomical body or known local activity. The department’s published cases repeatedly fall into several broad groups: Astronomical misidentifications , especially the Moon, Venus or effects associated with moonlight. Celebration objects , including sky lanterns and illuminated balloons. Fixed or nearby lighting , mistaken for objects suspended in the sky. Scientific or technical light sources , particularly around the research centres of the Saclay plateau. Poorly documented ground level events , where no aerial origin can be demonstrated. This does not mean witnesses were dishonest. GEIPAN stresses that most reports begin with something genuinely seen and that deliberate hoaxes are rare. The investigative problem is separating the visible stimulus from the distance, size, speed and identity unconsciously assigned to it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Saint Hilaire, 1981: the department’s most ambiguous encounter During the night of 3–4 October 1981, a couple driving near the “cabane à Pierrot” crossroads at Saint Hilaire noticed flashes in a field, followed by three red lights apparently moving towards them. They heard no sound and left quickly because they were frightened. The gendarmerie was informed several days later and found no physical traces when officers visited the site. Only one combined witness account was formally recorded. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPANClassification. Comprendre un phénomène... Essonne. Classification. C. Date de mise a jour... The case was originally classified as D, suggesting that it remained unexplained. A later review changed it to C. The reason was not that investigators had discovered a definitive conventional source, but that the phenomenon appeared to be on or near the ground. Nothing established that it had arrived by air, departed through the air or was an airborne object at all. GEIPAN concluded that identifying unexplained activity on private ground, without evidence of an aerospace component, fell outside its proper remit. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPANClassification. Comprendre un phénomène... Essonne. Classification. C. Date de mise a jour... Investigators nevertheless considered vehicle based explanations. Changes between white flashes and red lights could have resulted from a vehicle turning, exposing different lamps to the witnesses. Farm machinery, motorcycles or an unauthorised vehicle entering the field were all possible, although activity at about 2.30 a.m. on a Sunday appeared unusual. No one interviewed the owner or occupier of the land, leaving an obvious investigative gap. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPANClassification. Comprendre un phénomène... Essonne. Classification. C. Date de mise a jour... The description that the lights were coming closer is also less decisive than it first sounds. A light that becomes brighter as it turns towards an observer can create the impression of forward movement even if its actual position changes little. Without angular measurements, a second independent statement, ground traces or a clear account of how the lights disappeared, the direction and distance cannot be reconstructed reliably. Saint Hilaire is therefore neither convincingly explained nor a strong unexplained UFO case. Its present C classification is a judgement about evidential limits. The event may have been genuinely puzzling to the witnesses, but the surviving record cannot establish whether it involved a vehicle, people in the field, an unusual lighting arrangement or something else. When the Moon and planets appear to move Several Essonne reports demonstrate how astronomical objects can seem low, mobile or structurally complex when seen through cloud, vegetation or a moving vehicle. UFO hoax fools several French media • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English Open on YouTube Épinay sous Sénart and the “structured” Venus On 23 and 25 August 1980, a woman in a fourth floor flat at Épinay sous Sénart observed an exceptionally bright, apparently stationary object in the eastern sky during the early morning. Through binoculars she described a dark oval base, illuminated diagonal features and structures resembling antennae. The object gradually faded and appeared to rise before disappearing around dawn. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete50 Ce courrier est réceptionné en date... Le cas est classé PAN C, bien que l'hypothèse d'une observation de la planète Vénus soit jugée pr... The original investigators already suspected Venus. A later review reconstructed the sky and found the planet in the reported direction at the relevant time. Its brightness, low elevation, repeated appearance in almost the same position and gradual change as dawn approached were all consistent with that identification. The case, previously treated cautiously, was reclassified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete50 Ce courrier est réceptionné en date... Le cas est classé PAN C, bien que l'hypothèse d'une observation de la planète Vénus soit jugée pr... The apparent structure does not require a structured object. Binocular movement, atmospheric distortion and the eye’s attempt to interpret an intense unresolved point can produce spikes, extensions or dark areas. Fatigue may have amplified the effect: both observations began at about 3.30 a.m. Briis sous Forges to Limours, 2026 A particularly clear modern example occurred on 1 January 2026. A motorist travelling between Briis sous Forges and Limours saw a yellow orange sphere low ahead, apparently descending rapidly and seeming to accompany the moving car before disappearing near the horizon. The report contained useful directional estimates and drawings, giving the investigation relatively good consistency despite the lack of a recording. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[R] DE BRIIS SOUS FORGES (91) VERS LIMOURS (91 GeipanDate d'observation. 01/01/2026. Région. Ile de France. Département. Essonne. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 27/04/2026. Ty... GEIPAN compared the reported bearing with astronomical data and found a close match with the setting Moon, then very low at an azimuth of roughly 309 degrees. Clouds could explain its rapid disappearance, while the atmosphere near the horizon accounted for its orange colour and softened outline. The impression that it followed the vehicle resulted from parallax: nearby trees and road features move rapidly across the driver’s view, while the"},{"title":"Sky Illusions","url":"/sky-illusions/","description":"Several striking Essonne sightings became understandable once investigators matched their direction and movement to the Moon, Venus or atmospheric optics.","breadcrumb":"Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions","level":2,"text":"Sky Illusions When the Moon and Venus Look Like UFOs Essonne UFOs Sky Illusions Sky Illusions When the Moon and Venus Look Like UFOs Several striking Essonne sightings became understandable once investigators matched their direction and movement to the Moon, Venus or atmospheric optics. Several striking Essonne sightings became understandable once investigators matched their direction and movement to the Moon, Venus or atmospheric optics. mechanism The structured Venus seen from Epinay sous Senart Why the Moon seemed to follow a moving car Moon dogs, memory and delayed witness reports essonne sky illusions Introduction Several striking Essonne UFO reports became much less mysterious once investigators compared the witnesses’ viewing direction, time and apparent movement with the positions of the Moon or Venus. The clearest examples are a “structured” light seen through binoculars at Épinay sous Sénart in 1980, a yellow orange sphere apparently accompanying a car between Briis sous Forges and Limours in 2026, and a second Moon like object reported at Gif sur Yvette in 2003. These cases matter because they show that an ordinary astronomical source need not look ordinary. Near the horizon, the Moon can appear enlarged, orange, distorted and surprisingly close. A remote light can seem to move when the observer or surrounding landscape moves. Venus, dazzling against a dark sky, can acquire false edges or “structures” when viewed through imperfect optics or window glass. In Essonne, these mechanisms have produced sincere reports whose unusual appearance was real to the observer, even though the source itself was familiar. The structured Venus at Épinay sous Sénart At about 3.30 am on 23 August 1980, a woman living on the fourth floor of a block of flats at Épinay sous Sénart noticed an exceptionally bright, stationary light in the east. It was visible through her curtains and resembled a very large, brilliant star. She looked again at roughly the same time two mornings later and found the object in almost the same place. On the second occasion she watched it for around two hours and examined it with binoculars. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The binocular view transformed a point of light into something apparently constructed. The witness drew a dark oval base, two luminous sloping lines, a dark upper portion and what appeared to be antenna like projections. The object gradually faded and disappeared at about 5.30 am. After reading a newspaper report of unusual lights elsewhere in the Paris region, she contacted the gendarmerie. Officers interviewed her and inspected the location, but found no additional witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The original GEPAN investigators already considered Venus a likely explanation, although the file was initially placed in category C because the available evidence was limited. A later GEIPAN re examination reconstructed the sky for Épinay sous Sénart. Venus was low in the east at the stated time, at an azimuth of about 63 degrees and an elevation of roughly three degrees. Its position on 25 August was nearly identical to its position two days earlier, matching the witness’s observation that the light reappeared in the same area. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The disappearance also fitted an astronomical source. The light faded as dawn approached, when the brightening sky would steadily reduce the contrast of Venus. GEIPAN found that an earlier astronomical document had been misread because its times were given in Universal Time rather than local time; correcting that error strengthened rather than weakened the Venus identification. The case is now classified A, meaning that GEIPAN regards the identification as established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Why Venus acquired “antennae” The witness’s detailed sketch does not by itself show that the source possessed a detailed physical structure. Venus is far too small to be resolved as a shaped object with ordinary binoculars. What the viewer receives is an intense point of light, altered by the optical system, atmospheric turbulence and any glass between the observer and the sky. GEIPAN noted that the witness apparently used binoculars from behind the apartment window and did not report opening it. Reflections between glass surfaces, dirt or moisture on the window, poor focusing, misaligned binoculars and optical flare can spread a brilliant point into lines, lobes or dark edged forms. Once those irregularities resemble parts of a familiar object, the brain may organise them into a stable structure. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The strongest evidence for Venus is therefore not a vague resemblance. It is the combination of several independent matches: the correct compass direction, the correct low elevation, exceptional brightness, immobility, repetition at the same time on two mornings and gradual loss of visibility during dawn. The complex binocular appearance is the least reliable part of the report because it depended most heavily on viewing conditions and interpretation. The case also illustrates why witness sincerity and mistaken identification are not opposites. GEIPAN explicitly stated that the woman’s honesty and credibility were not in doubt. Its conclusion was that she accurately reported what she perceived but assigned an artificial structure to a distorted view of Venus under nocturnal, tiring conditions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEPINAY SOUS SENART (91) 23.08.1980 GEIPAN... Why the Moon seemed to follow a moving car A particularly clear Essonne example occurred between Briis sous Forges and Limours shortly before dawn on 1 January 2026. Between about 6.35 and 6.40 am, a motorist saw a diffuse yellow orange sphere ahead of the vehicle. Its outline appeared slightly irregular, it seemed to be moving at low altitude, and it disappeared rapidly near the horizon. The observation lasted less than a minute. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[R] DE BRIIS SOUS FORGES (91) VERS LIMOURS (91) 01.01.2026 GEIPAN... The most unsettling feature was the apparent relationship between the light and the car. As the witness drove, the object seemed to move with the vehicle or maintain its place ahead. This behaviour can suggest deliberate pursuit because nearby objects normally change position when an observer travels past them. GEIPAN reconstructed the journey from the witness’s questionnaire, drawings and estimated directions. The reported position closely matched the Moon, which was setting very low in the north west at an azimuth of approximately 309 degrees. Clouds in that direction could account for its quick disappearance, while the low elevation and intervening atmosphere explained its orange colour and softened outline. The case was classified A as an observation of the setting Moon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[R] DE BRIIS SOUS FORGES (91) VERS LIMOURS (91) 01.01.2026 GEIPAN... The illusion of accompaniment follows from simple perspective. A tree, house or road sign is close enough for the observer’s changing position to produce a large shift against the background. The Moon is so distant that travelling a few hundred metres makes almost no visible difference to its direction. The landscape therefore sweeps past while the Moon remains nearly fixed, creating the impression that the distant light is keeping pace with the car. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[R] DE BRIIS SOUS FORGES (91) VERS LIMOURS (91) 01.01.2026 GEIPAN... This effect becomes especially persuasive on a winding or undulating road. Trees may repeatedly hide and reveal the light; bends place it alternately ahead or to one side; slopes make it seem to rise or descend. The observer experiences a sequence of changing views and may naturally attribute those changes to the object rather than to the car, road and foreground. The Moon’s appearance near the horizon adds to the misidentification. NASA notes that a low Moon commonly looks more yellow or orange than one high in the sky and may also appear unusually large, although the apparent enlargement is principally a perceptual illusion rather than a physical magnification by the atmosphere. Atmospheric refraction can also flatten or distort its outline close to the horizon. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science The Moon Illusion: Why Does the Moon Look So Big Sometimes? NASA ScienceJanuary 24, 2023 — The Moon DOES look more yellow near the horizon... There's one notable way in which the Moon's appearan... Published: January 24, 2023 The Briis–Limours report is unusually useful because it preserves more than a label such as “probably the Moon”. GEIPAN had a reconstructed route, estimated bearings, drawings and astronomical data. Although there was only one witness and no usable recording, the directional agreement was strong enough for investigators to reject a separate moving object. UFO lookalike? Close up of"},{"title":"Saint Hilaire","url":"/saint-hilaire/","description":"Saint-Hilaire remains puzzling, but missing witnesses, measurements and site enquiries prevent the 1981 lights from becoming a strong UFO case.","breadcrumb":"Why Essonne's UFO Cases Often Defy First Impressions","level":2,"text":"Saint Hilaire Why Saint Hilaire Remains Puzzling but Unproven Essonne UFOs Saint Hilaire Saint Hilaire Why Saint Hilaire Remains Puzzling but Unproven Saint Hilaire remains puzzling, but missing witnesses, measurements and site enquiries prevent the 1981 lights from becoming a strong UFO case. Saint Hilaire remains puzzling, but missing witnesses, measurements and site enquiries prevent the 1981 lights from becoming a strong UFO case. event time window What the couple reported in October 1981 Why GEIPAN changed the case from D to C Vehicle theories, missing enquiries and evidential gaps essonne saint hilaire Introduction Saint Hilaire remains one of Essonne ’s most intriguing UFO reports, but not one of its strongest. At about 2.30 am on 4 October 1981, a couple driving home near the Cabane à Pierrot crossroads saw intense white flashes apparently rising from a field. After turning towards Saint Hilaire and stopping roughly 200 metres farther on, they reported a red light near ground level, followed by two more lights forming a triangle. The lights then seemed to move towards them, prompting the couple to leave. No sound was heard and no physical trace was subsequently found. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... The incident matters because it shows the difference between an event that has not been explained and one supported by strong evidence. France’s official investigators initially placed Saint Hilaire in category D, meaning that it remained unidentified. A later review changed it to category C, not because a conventional cause had been proved, but because essential checks and measurements were missing and the phenomenon may never have been airborne. The case is therefore puzzling, yet unable to carry much evidential weight. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPAN... What the couple reported in October 1981 The witnesses were travelling from Étampes along the N191, now the D191, towards Saint Hilaire. As they approached the fork between the roads towards Ablis and Dourdan, the driver saw a violent white flash coming from the field inside the junction. He believed that the source was something positioned on the ground. He then described two further flashes, each separated by about 15 seconds, although GEIPAN’s later narrative refers elsewhere to a total of four flashes, leaving a small but relevant inconsistency in the record. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... The couple continued along the local road towards Saint Hilaire. The passenger kept watching the field and then alerted the driver to a red light. After stopping, he described a sharply defined, slightly flickering red circle, comparable in brightness and colour to a car’s brake light but apparently much larger. He estimated its diameter at about one metre and placed it at ground level. Four or five seconds later, two matching lights appeared below it, creating a triangular arrangement. He estimated that the lights were about five metres apart. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... According to the statement, the three lights immediately began coming towards the couple across the field. Both became frightened, returned to their car and drove away without watching how the event ended. They heard no engine, impact, voices or other noise. The driver could say only that the weather was cool, reasonably fine and dry; he could not remember whether the sky was clouded. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... The changing viewpoint is important. The flashes were first noticed from a moving car approaching the junction. The later red lights were seen after the witnesses had turned onto another road and stopped. GEIPAN reconstructed the initial viewing direction as roughly north west and the later direction as north east. The observation was also discontinuous because the driver was concentrating on the road and changing direction while his wife continued looking towards the field. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... The total duration cannot be established confidently. GEIPAN estimated that the described sequence may have lasted about one minute, while the original local investigator recorded approximately four minutes. That difference matters because perceived speed, spacing and movement are difficult to reconstruct without a reliable timeline. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... Why the first investigation left so much unresolved The gendarmerie did not learn of the incident directly from the couple. A GEPAN correspondent—the local representative of the organisation that preceded GEIPAN—heard about it through a member of the witnesses’ family and contacted the authorities on 9 October, five days after the sighting. Officers inspected the location that day but found no ground traces. The formal interview did not take place until 3 November, almost a month after the event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... Although there were two people in the car, the surviving official record does not provide two independently developed accounts. The gendarmerie considered the couple’s descriptions identical and retained only the husband’s statement. The document simply records his assertion that his wife saw exactly the same phenomenon. This means the case cannot be treated as two fully independent testimonies that separately confirm the light sequence, dimensions and apparent approach. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... GEIPAN’s 2018 review raised the possibility that the witnesses had discussed the event extensively before being interviewed. Because the authorities became involved through family talk rather than a spontaneous report, details may already have been repeated, interpreted and consolidated. This does not imply dishonesty. It means that agreement between the couple cannot reliably distinguish shared observation from normal mutual influence after a frightening experience. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... No unrelated witness was found. There was no photograph, film, sound recording or instrument reading. Investigators obtained no angular measurements showing how large the lights appeared against the witnesses’ field of view, no reliable distance to the source and no account of its disappearance. The estimates of a one metre light and five metre spacing were therefore perceived physical dimensions, not values derived from surveying or triangulation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR TA JPA MPD.doc... The absence of angular measurements is especially damaging. A light cannot be assigned a physical diameter unless its distance is known. A small lamp nearby and a powerful lamp much farther away can occupy the same apparent size. Darkness also removes many of the visual references people use to judge range, making estimates of dimensions and movement particularly uncertain. The inspection of the field supplied little additional help. Officers found no mark that could be connected to the event, but the lack of tracks did not prove that no vehicle had entered. GEIPAN noted that a vehicle might leave little visible evidence on sufficiently dry ground. Conversely, the absence of traces also deprived the case of the physical corroboration that could have distinguished an unusual object from ordinary activity in the field. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPAN... One potentially decisive enquiry was never made: the owner or operator of the land was identified but not questioned. Investigators therefore did not establish whether machinery, workers, contractors, trespassers or unusual activity had been present that night. This single omission left the most straightforward family of explanations largely untested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HILAIRE (91) 04.10.1981 GEIPAN... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why GEIPAN changed the case from D to C GEIPAN classifies reports by considering both their residual strangeness and their consistency. Consistency includes the quantity, precision and reliability of the information: whether accounts are independent, whether measurements exist, whether documentation supports the testimony and whether the circumstances can be reconstructed. A report can consequently sound extraordinary yet remain too poorly documented to qualify as a robust unexplained case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. Saint Hilaire was originally classified as D after the 1981 gendarmerie enquiry, indicating that no explanation had been established. During a later programme of reassessing older files, GEIPAN reconstructed the route, consulted historical"},{"title":"Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened","url":"/why-eure-et-loirs-ufo-mysteries-often/","description":"Eure-et-Loir has a real but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened Eure et Loir has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Eure et Loir has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Its strongest material is not a single “landmark” encounter with physical traces, radar confirmation or lasting official mystery. eure et loir Introduction The most important episode occurred around Authon du Perche in March 1980, when several gendarmes reported luminous objects and briefly gave the case unusual authority. Decades later, however, the French official investigation service GEIPAN concluded that the first phase was most probably Venus, while the remaining phases lacked enough reliable information for a firm judgement. That mixture—sincere witnesses, striking descriptions and weak measurement—is typical of Eure et Loir’s record. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... The first saucer stories reached Chartres in 1952 Eure et Loir’s modern UFO story began in the early 1950s, when reports of “flying saucers” were spreading through French and international newspapers. A local historical account based on coverage in L’Écho Républicain records several observations on 4 June 1952 around Chartres and Ymonville. Workers near Chartres Cathedral reportedly saw a white disc climb rapidly while rotating. Elsewhere, witnesses described a silvery, S shaped trail, while a woman at Ymonville reported a pale yellow disc moving silently and leaving small flakes behind it. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... These stories matter less as proof of extraordinary craft than as evidence of how quickly a new visual language took hold. “Disc”, “saucer” and “mysterious machine” became ready made descriptions for lights, vapour trails and distant aerial objects. Even the surviving local account preserves disagreement at the time: one observer rejected an aircraft explanation, while another compared the trail with those left by wartime fighters. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... The reporting also needs to be treated cautiously. The cases survive chiefly through retrospective summaries of newspaper coverage rather than complete witness interviews, weather records or original technical investigations. Eure et Loir’s digitised and catalogued local press holdings are extensive, covering Chartres, Dreux, Châteaudun, Nogent le Rotrou and surrounding districts, but locating an article is not the same as confirming the event it describes. Newspapers preserve what witnesses said; they do not necessarily establish what was in the sky. [Presse Ancienne] presselocaleancienne.bnf.fr Presse Ancienne Villes, villages d'Eure et Loir Presse locale ancienne Presse Ancienne Villes, villages d'Eure et Loir Presse locale ancienne The 1965 Dreux sighting gained authority from its witness The department’s best known early case involved Alexandre Ananoff, a respected writer and advocate of astronautics. On 19 August 1965, Ananoff and other witnesses near Dreux reportedly watched an intense light in a clear sky. Seen through binoculars, it appeared brighter than Venus, translucent and very fast. A second passage was reported later that evening, drawing national press and broadcast interest. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... The case became memorable because Ananoff was regarded as technically informed and anything but fanciful. Yet expertise in astronautics did not provide objective distance, altitude or speed. The air base at Évreux, which had reportedly registered two passages, suggested a weather balloon, rocket or satellite. Ananoff disputed those possibilities because the reported motion seemed too fast or irregular. The surviving account therefore leaves a genuine disagreement, not a confirmed exotic object. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... Later retellings did not add radar plots, photographs or independently preserved tracking data. The case remains historically interesting because a prominent witness gave the story credibility, but its evidential strength is limited by the same problem found in many visual sightings: apparent speed cannot be measured reliably when the object’s distance is unknown. Authon du Perche in 1980: the department’s most instructive case On 10 March 1980, an automobilist reportedly alerted gendarmes to a strange light between Les Étilleux and Nogent le Rotrou. At about 10.10 pm, a gendarme at Authon du Perche then saw a bright ball or disc with his wife. Other officers joined him, describing colour changes from orange to red and apparent movements punctuated by stops. Four gendarmes later drove towards the reported area and observed further red orange lights, followed by what they described from a stationary position as a silent, low, oval luminous object with several distinct regions of light. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... At first glance, this is the kind of account that seems difficult to dismiss. There were trained officers, multiple observers and a formal gendarmerie record. A private research group also interviewed witnesses and produced drawings. For years, the case was treated as unexplained. GEIPAN’s later reassessment substantially weakened that interpretation. Investigators divided the evening into three separate phases rather than assuming that every witness had followed one continuous object. The first phase, seen from the gendarmerie, was judged very probably to have been Venus low on the horizon. Atmospheric turbulence, the visual illusion in which a stationary light appears to move, binocular defects and one observer’s astigmatism were all considered capable of producing the reported changes in movement, colour and shape. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... The other two phases were not positively explained, but neither were they retained as strong unexplained cases. GEIPAN found that the gendarmerie report combined the witnesses’ statements rather than preserving separate accounts, making it impossible to measure how closely their descriptions really agreed. Basic information such as exact directions, angular heights and apparent dimensions was missing. The private report also contained confusing or contradictory details gathered under conditions GEIPAN could not verify. The second and third phases were therefore placed in category C: not analysable because reliable data were insufficient. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... This distinction is central to understanding Eure et Loir’s UFO history. “Not explained” is not the same as “evidence of an extraordinary craft”. In the Authon case , one phase received a conventional explanation and the rest became evidentially indeterminate. The later investigation weakened the original unified story while leaving open the narrow possibility that the travelling officers saw something not recoverable from the surviving record. Weak cases remain weak even when the description is dramatic The Saussay report of 17 March 1986 is a good example. At about 4 am, one witness described a slowly moving luminous sphere that appeared to illuminate roughly 1,000 square metres of ground. The sighting lasted about 20 seconds. No corroborating testimony or further useful information was obtained, and GEIPAN classified it as category C because reliable data were lacking. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAUSSAY (28) 17.03.1986 GEIPAN... The description sounds impressive because it includes apparent ground illumination, a feature often treated as evidence that a light was nearby and physically powerful. Yet without angular measurements, a defined viewing direction, meteorological information, other witnesses or a trace at the site, even that detail cannot establish distance or size. Bright lights can appear to illuminate terrain, while memory may enlarge the estimated area after a brief nocturnal observation. A later case at Rohaire shows how delay further reduces evidential value. A witness reported in 2014 that, two years earlier, he had watched an intense red and white light apparently climb, descend, change direction and divide. GEIPAN noted that the questionnaire was incomplete, the duration and speed were not supplied, and photographs mentioned by the witness were never submitted despite requests. The case was classified C, meaning that no responsible conclusion could be reached. [Geipan] geipan.fr ROHAIRE"},{"title":"Authon Case","url":"/authon-case/","description":"The 1980 Authon case gained authority from multiple gendarmes, but later review split the event and explained its first phase as Venus.","breadcrumb":"Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened","level":2,"text":"Authon Case Did Gendarmes Mistake Venus for a UFO? Eure et Loir UFOs Authon Case Authon Case Did Gendarmes Mistake Venus for a UFO? The 1980 Authon case gained authority from multiple gendarmes, but later review split the event and explained its first phase as Venus. The 1980 Authon case gained authority from multiple gendarmes, but later review split the event and explained its first phase as Venus. event time window What the gendarmes reported that night Why GEIPAN divided the sighting into three phases What remains unresolved and why the evidence is weak eure et loir authon case Introduction The Authon du Perche sighting of 10 March 1980 is the most detailed official UFO case in Eure et Loir , but it is not a single unexplained encounter. Several gendarmes reported changing lights from their barracks and later from a patrol car. The episode gained weight because trained officers participated and produced an official report. Yet a later review by GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation group, divided the night into three separate phases rather than treating every observation as one object. It concluded that the first and best documented phase was very probably Venus. The two later phases were left without reliable explanations, not because they presented strong evidence of an extraordinary craft, but because the surviving information was too confused and incomplete to analyse properly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... That distinction makes Authon especially instructive. Multiple sincere witnesses can still misidentify a familiar astronomical object, while an “unresolved” remainder may merely reflect poor records rather than compelling evidence. What the gendarmes reported that night The sequence began at about 21:30, when officers heard a radio message concerning an unidentified aerial phenomenon reported by a motorist on the D13 between Les Étilleux and Nogent le Rotrou. Gendarmes looked in the indicated direction but initially saw nothing. The motorist was never properly identified or interviewed in the surviving file, so GEIPAN later excluded this preliminary report from its analysis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... At approximately 22:10, a gendarme at the Authon du Perche barracks noticed a luminous ball while closing the shutters of his accommodation. His wife also saw it. He alerted his superior and other officers, who watched from the barracks towards Les Étilleux. Their collective description presented the light as a ball or disc that changed from bright orange to red, appeared to move in successive jumps and then stopped abruptly. The original report also described it as roughly ten times larger than the visible stars. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... At about 22:40, four of the gendarmes left in a vehicle, travelling along the D9 towards Les Étilleux and then Coudray au Perche. During the journey they noticed a dark red luminous mass, at one point described as three reddish orange lights. They later stopped near Moulin Neuf, around five kilometres from the barracks, where they reported a further stationary, silent phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... The official report’s description of this final observation was much more elaborate. It referred to an oval object apparently at low altitude, with dazzling lights in its lower portion, predominantly red orange lighting above, red points arranged along a horizontal line and a surrounding glow or “halo”. The officers estimated a length of about ten metres, a height of three metres and an altitude of roughly 150 metres. Those figures, however, were visual estimates made at night with no established distance or physical reference against which the object’s size could be measured. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. No supporting witnesses were located in the settlements through which the patrol passed. There were also no photographs, instrument readings, radar records, ground traces or independently measured bearings. The surviving evidence consists mainly of a three page preliminary gendarmerie report and a much longer private investigation produced later in 1980 by the Groupe de Recherches Cosmographiques in Le Mans. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... Why GEIPAN divided the sighting into three phases The officers understood the night as a connected pursuit of one unusual object. GEIPAN’s later reviewers considered that assumption unsafe. Changes in location, appearance and observing conditions made it more likely that the witnesses had encountered several distinct lights. The review therefore separated the case into: 1. the luminous object watched from the Authon barracks; 2. the reddish lights noticed while the patrol was moving; 3. the oval or structured phenomenon reported after the car stopped near Moulin Neuf. This separation is central to the modern assessment. A report involving “five gendarmes and a UFO” sounds like one well corroborated event. In fact, not every officer observed every phase, and the descriptions were not preserved as five clean, independent accounts. The official report combined the witnesses’ impressions into a collective narrative. That prevents investigators from determining which details were independently noticed, which were agreed after discussion and which came from only one observer. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN also treated the private investigation cautiously. Its transcripts and drawings preserve useful detail, but the agency did not control the interviews or know enough about how questions were asked. Some exchanges were confusing or suggestive, and individual accounts diverged on important points. In one surviving transcript, for example, one witness said the lights appeared bright orange to him while acknowledging that a colleague had described them as red. He was also less certain about the object’s outline, saying it appeared round at the barracks but only vaguely rectangular near Moulin Neuf. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These differences do not imply dishonesty. They show why a group report is not automatically equivalent to five mutually confirming observations. Once witnesses talk together, pursue a light together and later answer questions about a shared episode, their accounts may overlap without providing fully independent corroboration. Comment le Geipan enquête sur les objets volants non identifiés Channel: BFMTV Open on YouTube Why Venus fits the first phase GEIPAN found that Venus was low in the relevant part of the sky and setting during the first observation. The night was reportedly clear enough for stars to be visible, yet the witnesses did not separately mention Venus, despite its prominence. For the reviewers, the simplest explanation was that the bright “object” was Venus itself. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... Several reported characteristics support that interpretation. A brilliant ball much larger than the stars. Venus is normally the brightest planet seen from Earth and can appear exceptionally conspicuous against a dark sky. A point source has no obvious physical boundary to the naked eye, so witnesses cannot reliably translate its apparent glare into a true angular size. The gendarmes’ statement that the light looked ten times larger than surrounding stars recorded an impression, not a measurement. Orange and red colour changes. A bright celestial object near the horizon is viewed through a much greater depth of atmosphere than one high in the sky. Turbulence and atmospheric dispersion can cause rapid changes in brightness and colour, sometimes spreading the light into reddish, greenish or rainbow like flashes. Venus is susceptible to such effects when it is setting low over a land horizon. [Sky at Night Magazine] skyatnightmagazine.com Image: The Venus green flash effect can be observed over a land horizon.... It’s also not limited to a sea horizon and can be seen to oc... Sudden jumps, pauses and sideways motion. GEIPAN invoked the autokinetic illusion: when a small, isolated light is watched against a dark background with few stable visual references, it may seem to wander even though it is stationary. The effect is recognised in aviation because pilots can perceive a fixed light as moving during dark night conditions. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAUTHON DU PERCHE (28) 10.03.1980 GEIPAN... Strange shapes through binoculars. Binoculars do not necessarily resolve a bright point into a reliable image. Poor focus, internal reflections, optical aberrations and movement of the instrument can transform an intense light into discs, spikes, coloured points or apparent structures. GEIPAN noted both problems with the binocular use and the astigmatism of one"},{"title":"Reports Over Time","url":"/reports-over-time/","description":"Eure-et-Loir's reports evolved from vivid newspaper stories to formal records that often exposed missing measurements and ordinary causes.","breadcrumb":"Why Eure et Loir's UFO Mysteries Often Weakened","level":2,"text":"Reports Over Time How Eure et Loir's UFO Story Changed Over Time Eure et Loir UFOs Reports Over Time Reports Over Time How Eure et Loir's UFO Story Changed Over Time Eure et Loir's reports evolved from vivid newspaper stories to formal records that often exposed missing measurements and ordinary causes. Eure et Loir's reports evolved from vivid newspaper stories to formal records that often exposed missing measurements and ordinary causes. event time window The 1952 press driven saucer reports The 1965 Dreux sighting and witness credibility How later GEIPAN files separated mystery from missing data eure et loir reports over time Introduction Eure et Loir ’s UFO story did not simply become more sceptical with time; it became more measurable. In 1952, local newspapers could turn a white disc, a shining trail or a silent yellow light into a vivid “flying saucer” story without recording the viewing angle, duration, weather or precise direction. By the 1980s, gendarmerie statements and official case files preserved, but they also revealed how often the crucial measurements were still missing. That change matters because an official looking document is not automatically strong evidence. Eure et Loir’s later files contain sincere witnesses, including police officers, yet several cases were eventually attributed to Venus, sunrise or other ordinary phenomena. Others remained unclassified or unexplained chiefly because investigators lacked reliable information. The department’s historical pattern is therefore not a march from ridicule to proof. It is a shift from memorable headlines towards records that make the weaknesses of eyewitness evidence easier to see. The 1952 reports were stories before they were investigations The first prominent local saucer reports appeared on 4 June 1952, during a period when French newspapers were giving increasing attention to mysterious aerial objects. Around Chartres Cathedral, workers reportedly saw a sharply outlined white disc rise rapidly while rotating. Another observer described a short, silvery trail shaped like an “S”, while a woman at Ymonville said that a pale yellow disc crossed the starry sky silently and left small flakes behind it. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... These accounts were visually rich but technically poor. The surviving summaries do not provide dependable angular sizes, compass bearings, calibrated timings or meteorological checks. Even the descriptions reveal disagreement. A postal official reportedly dismissed the possibility of a jet trail, whereas another local witness compared the luminous streak with trails left by wartime fighters. The press preserved both claims, but apparently did not resolve them through observation records or consultation with several independent specialists. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... That is the central limitation of the 1952 material. Descriptions such as “disc”, “plate sized” or “moving rapidly” sound precise in ordinary speech, yet they do not establish physical size or speed. A distant object and a nearby object can occupy the same apparent width in the sky. Unless distance is known, a witness cannot reliably convert apparent motion into extraordinary velocity. The newspaper setting also influenced how unusual sights were described. “Flying saucer” had become a familiar cultural label, so witnesses and reporters could fit very different observations—discs, trails, lights and falling material—into the same developing story. Contemporary French coverage elsewhere shows the same mixture of eyewitness excitement, aeronautical speculation and pressure for scientific investigation. Eure et Loir was participating in a national media moment rather than producing a coherent local cluster of mutually confirming observations. [Le Monde.fr] lemonde.fr Publié le 19 juin 1952 à 00h00, modifié le 19 juin 1952 à 00h00... Ce phénomène a été également signalé par un avion courrier d'Air Fran... None of this proves that every 1952 report had an ordinary cause. It means that the surviving evidence is insufficient to test the more dramatic interpretations. The reports remain valuable as press history: they show how the department learnt the language of saucers. As case evidence, however, they are weak because the newspaper narratives contain more imagery than measurement. The 1965 Dreux case gained weight from a respected witness The sighting near Dreux on 19 August 1965 was more persuasive to contemporary readers because its principal witness, Alexandre Ananoff, was a recognised promoter of astronautics. He had spent decades explaining rockets and space travel to the French public and had advised Hergé during the development of the lunar Tintin stories. His reputation made it harder to dismiss the observation as the invention of an uninformed or attention seeking witness. [Société Astronomique de France] saf astronomie.fr Open source on saf astronomie.fr. According to the local historical account, Ananoff’s son first noticed a strong light at about 8.05 pm. Ananoff then examined it with binoculars and described it as two or three times brighter than Venus, translucent in appearance, silent and apparently travelling at remarkable speed. A second passage was reported after 11 pm. Other local people, including a neighbour and the mayor of Montreuil, were also said to have observed the phenomenon, and national journalists and broadcasters took an interest. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... The nearby air base at Évreux reportedly suggested a weather balloon, rocket or satellite. Ananoff rejected those possibilities because the object seemed too fast for a balloon and too irregular for an orbiting body. That exchange gave the story the appearance of an informed technical dispute. Yet the surviving account does not provide the underlying tracking material that would allow a modern reader to test either position. There is no published radar plot, triangulation, photograph or independently measured trajectory in the accessible record. [Alain Denizet] alaindenizet.fr Alain Denizet Soucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain DenizetSoucoupes volantes en Eure et Loir Alain Denizet... Ananoff’s credibility therefore improves confidence that he genuinely saw something striking. It does not establish what he saw. Expertise in rockets does not remove the standard limitations of observing an isolated light: without a known distance, apparent size and speed remain uncertain, while binocular focus, atmospheric distortion and expectations can alter the perceived shape. The Dreux case illustrates an important distinction that recurs throughout Eure et Loir’s history. Witness credibility and evidential completeness are separate questions. A reliable person may report honestly and carefully, but the event can still resist identification because the observation did not generate enough objective data. Later retellings strengthened Ananoff’s status as an impressive witness; they do not appear to have strengthened the physical case. The Flying Saucer Mystery (1952) Vintage UFO Documentary Donald E. Keyhoe Channel: Gemini International Movies & TV Open on YouTube Official files made missing data visible France’s official UFO service, now known as GEIPAN—the Study and Information Group on Unidentified Aerospace Phenomena—uses classifications that distinguish identified cases from those lacking sufficient information and those that remain genuinely unusual after investigation. Since 2008, its work has placed greater emphasis on the separate ideas of “strangeness” and “consistency”: how unusual a reported phenomenon appears, and how strong and usable the underlying information is. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... This approach changes the meaning of “unidentified”. A case may be unidentified because it is highly strange despite good documentation. It may also remain unresolved because the file is too incomplete to support any conclusion. In Eure et Loir, several official records fall into the second category. At Saussay on 17 March 1986, a witness reported a luminous sphere moving slowly at about four in the morning. It was said to illuminate roughly 1,000 square metres of ground and to remain visible for around 20 seconds. The account sounds dramatic, but GEIPAN classified it C, meaning that reliable information was lacking. Only one witness was recorded, no corroborating testimony was obtained, and the brief file offered no measurements with which to reconstruct the geometry or test possible sources of light. [Geipan]"},{"title":"Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","url":"/eures-ufo-stories-between-legend-and/","description":"Eure has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Eure has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Eure has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Its best known story is the extraordinary 1954 Vernon sighting, in which a local businessman reportedly watched a huge luminous “cigar” release smaller discs above the Seine. eure Introduction The wider official record is more sobering. Most reports from Eure are either too incomplete to resolve or have eventually been attributed to ordinary causes such as aircraft, lanterns, kites and camera reflections. The department therefore offers a useful cross section of French UFO history: one famous but weakly archived legend, one unusually interesting military case, and numerous examples showing why witness testimony alone rarely settles what happened. Vernon in 1954: Eure’s landmark sighting The night of 22–23 August 1954 produced the incident most closely associated with UFOs in Eure. At about 1 am, Vernon businessman Bernard Miserey reportedly returned home, put his car away and noticed an unusual pale illumination over the town. Looking towards the Seine, he said he saw an enormous luminous object standing vertically in the sky. Smaller, disc like lights allegedly emerged from its lower part, moved rapidly and returned or disappeared. The episode was said to last for much of an hour. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, August 22 23, Vernon, EureVallée indicates that the witness is a Vernon businessman, Bernard Miserey, who h... The story appeared in the Paris newspaper Libération on 25 August 1954 and was soon amplified by illustrated popular press coverage. Later accounts claimed that Miserey’s statement was entered at Vernon police station and that an engineer and two police officers had reported related observations. Those additional witnesses are important because they would make the event more than a lone nocturnal impression, but the accessible record consists largely of later summaries rather than independently preserved statements from each person. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Bernard Miserey showing, at the edge of the Seine, in Vernon, the direction taken by the saucers. Our draughtsman reproduced on...Read more... Vernon became memorable because its imagery was unusually elaborate. A vast vertical “cigar” releasing smaller discs fitted a pattern promoted by early French UFO writers, who interpreted such objects as carriers or “mother ships”. Aimé Michel and Jacques Vallée later repeated the case, helping it enter international UFO literature. Yet repetition is not the same as corroboration: many modern descriptions ultimately trace back to the same short newspaper report and early authors who were already convinced that some sightings represented unknown machines. [wasabisys.com] s3.us west 1.wasabisys.com Wasabi TechnologiesAnatomy of a phenomenon... Vernon businessman, Bernard Miserey, had just put his car away when, coming out of the gara... What can reasonably be accepted? There is good evidence that a report was made and publicised in August 1954. Miserey appears to have been a named, local witness rather than an anonymous storyteller invented decades later. The town, approximate time and broad description remained relatively stable in subsequent retellings. The case also predates France’s official UFO investigation service, so the absence of a modern GEIPAN file is unsurprising. What is missing is more decisive. There is no surviving photograph, film, instrument reading or physical trace. The original statements of the supposed supporting witnesses are not readily available for comparison. Estimates such as the object’s altitude, distance and size were made without triangulation. Even the duration and number of smaller objects vary across accounts. The surviving record therefore establishes a striking report, not a verified aerial event of the dimensions later claimed. Astronomical or meteorological explanations have occasionally been suggested in general discussions of the case, but no reconstruction has conclusively matched every reported feature. Conversely, the available evidence is too incomplete to justify rejecting ordinary explanations. A bright astronomical body, illuminated cloud, balloon, aircraft lights or several unrelated stimuli could have been interpreted as one structured scene. The proper assessment is historically important but evidentially unresolved . The 1978 Évreux case: a visual report with radar support Eure’s most substantial official case occurred on 23 April 1978 at Base aérienne 105, the military airfield at Évreux Fauville. Between 20:35 and 20:51 Universal Time, an operations duty officer reportedly saw a red luminous point between 10 and 15 degrees above the horizon, roughly north west of the base. Personnel in the control tower also observed it as its brightness diminished. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At 20:44, the base radar registered a weak return about 18 nautical miles away. The echo appeared to move towards the north west at an estimated 30 to 40 knots and remained detectable for approximately seven minutes. The officer prepared a written report the following day. GEIPAN’s published reassessment rejects a straightforward helicopter or balloon identification and classifies the case as D1: unexplained after investigation, with moderate strangeness and reasonably good consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case matters because it did not begin with a casual observer who later contacted a UFO group. It arose inside an operational military environment, involved more than one visual observer and included an apparent instrument contact. Those features make it considerably stronger than a typical report of a distant light. They do not make it conclusive. The radar recording itself has not been preserved, leaving only the written description. The return was rated at very low intensity, and a second radar station at Cinq Mars la Pile did not confirm it. GEIPAN therefore acknowledges that a false or secondary radar echo cannot be excluded. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Venus was visible in roughly the same general sector that evening. GEIPAN nevertheless judged a direct confusion unlikely because the reported light was higher in the sky and apparently moved from an azimuth of about 300 to 330 degrees. That comparison weakens the simplest astronomical explanation, but it does not prove that the visual light and radar return were the same physical object. Their overlap in time and direction is suggestive rather than demonstrated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case is therefore best described as Eure’s strongest officially unresolved sighting , but not as a confirmed radar tracked craft. Its value lies precisely in the tension between credible circumstances and inadequate surviving instrument data. 3:25 UFO Sightings: Navy Pilots Share Their Experiences NOVA PBS Channel: NOVA PBS Official · Views: 111.0K · Uploaded: January 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 25 seconds Open on YouTube What the wider GEIPAN record shows France’s official investigation body, GEIPAN, was created within the national space agency CNES in 1977. It collects reports, investigates possible explanations and publishes case files. Its classifications distinguish identified cases, probable identifications, reports that cannot be resolved because information is insufficient, and the small minority that remain unexplained after investigation. Across its national archive, CNES reports that only about 3.3 per cent fall into the last category. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES Eure’s published cases follow that broader pattern. The archive contains interesting experiences, but very few withstand scrutiny as strong unknowns. Le Vaudreuil, 5 April 1978. A factory guard reported a white ball making rapid movements high above a forest on five occasions before fading away. No supporting witness or further evidence was obtained, and the case was classified C because reliable information was lacking. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Bacqueville, 4 January 1994. A frightened witness described a rotating cone with coloured bands, later flashes, bodily sensations and unusual behaviour by his dog. The report was once treated as unexplained, but GEIPAN’s later review downgraded it to C. Investigators considered a promotional searchlight, aircraft or helicopter, while noting that missing duration, angular measurements and disappearance details prevented a firm conclusion. The witness’s intense fear was also judged likely to have affected later perceptions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr."},{"title":"Evreux 1978","url":"/evreux-1978/","description":"The Evreux air-base case combined military witnesses with a weak radar return, making it Eure's strongest but still inconclusive official case.","breadcrumb":"Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","level":2,"text":"Evreux 1978 Did Radar Make the Evreux Sighting Stronger? Eure UFOs Evreux 1978 Evreux 1978 Did Radar Make the Evreux Sighting Stronger? The Evreux air base case combined military witnesses with a weak radar return, making it Eure's strongest but still inconclusive official case. The Evreux air base case combined military witnesses with a weak radar return, making it Eure's strongest but still inconclusive official case. dataset evidence What air base personnel saw and recorded Why the radar return remains important False echoes, Venus and the limits of the evidence eure evreux 1978 Introduction The Évreux case of 23 April 1978 is arguably the strongest officially documented UFO incident in Eure, but “strongest” does not mean conclusive. Personnel at Base aérienne 105 saw a red point of light north west of the airfield, and a military radar then displayed a faint return in roughly the same sector for seven minutes. That combination is more interesting than an unsupported eyewitness story because it appears to join human observation with an instrument reading. Yet the radar trace was weak, was not preserved, and was not confirmed by another surveillance station. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... The case therefore occupies an awkward middle ground. France’s official investigators rejected some straightforward explanations and still classify the phenomenon as unidentified, but they also warn that a false radar echo cannot be excluded. Rather than proving that an unusual craft crossed Eure’s skies, Évreux shows how apparently corroborating evidence can strengthen a report without making it secure. What air base personnel saw and recorded The incident began on Sunday evening, 23 April 1978, at approximately 20:35 Universal Time, or 22:35 local time. According to the surviving official summary, a telephone call prompted the operational duty officer at Évreux air base to look towards an estimated bearing of 300 degrees, broadly north west. He saw a single red light between about 10 and 15 degrees above the horizon. Personnel in the control tower also observed it, and its brightness reportedly diminished during the sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... The published witness data describe the phenomenon only as a point like red object, with an apparent size compared to a planet. There was no reported shape, structure, beam, exhaust or close approach. No reliable distance was established, so estimates of its actual size and altitude are impossible. The visual component was therefore unusual mainly because of its apparent movement and duration, not because witnesses saw a detailed machine. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... The duty officer asked for radar assistance. At 20:44, the radar operator reported an echo at a bearing of about 300 degrees and a range of 18 nautical miles, roughly 33 kilometres. It was said to move towards 330 degrees at an estimated 30 to 40 knots, or approximately 56 to 74 kilometres per hour. Both the visual observation and radar watch ended at about 20:51, giving the radar episode a duration of seven minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... This sequence is the core reason the case matters. The radar was not consulted days later in an attempt to find supporting data; it was brought into the observation while the light was still visible. The reported bearing also placed the return in the same general part of the sky. That is genuine evidential value, even though it falls short of demonstrating that the radar and the red light represented the same physical object. The officer wrote his report on 24 April. A separate radar observation report apparently gave the observation date as the 24th rather than the 23rd, but the official review treats this as a probable typing error. The matching times and sequence make contemporaneous radar involvement plausible, although the dating inconsistency is another reminder that the surviving case file is not a flawless instrumental record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... Why the radar return remains important Radar can provide information that eyesight cannot, particularly an apparent range, direction and pattern of movement. In the Évreux case, the reported return was not merely a momentary flash on the screen. It persisted for seven minutes and appeared to change bearing. That duration makes it harder to dismiss as a single random pulse or an operator glancing at an isolated speck. The return also appeared during the visual observation. In a well documented radar visual case, agreement between two independent sensing methods can reduce the chance that a witness simply mistook a star or planet. Modern scientific proposals for studying unidentified aerial phenomena similarly emphasise simultaneous measurements from different sensors because corroboration helps distinguish real targets from visual misperceptions and instrument artefacts. [arXiv] arxiv.org The Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based ObservatoriesMay 29, 2023... Published: May 29, 2023 Évreux nevertheless lacked the information needed to make that principle decisive. The original radar recording is not available in the public file. Readers cannot inspect the raw signal, successive plots, antenna scans or any automatic track history. The published account relies on the operator’s written report and the later interpretation of investigators. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... The return’s intensity was rated only one out of five. Such a weak signal may come from a small or poorly reflecting target, but it is also more vulnerable to noise, clutter, propagation effects and indirect reflections. Without the recording, it is impossible to test how consistently the echo appeared on successive rotations or whether its reported movement reflected a true track rather than shifting patches of interference. There was also no confirmation from the military surveillance centre at Cinq Mars la Pile. That absence does not automatically disprove the Évreux detection: different radar systems can have different coverage, sensitivity, filtering and viewing geometry. However, a second station registering a corresponding target would have made the case substantially stronger. GEIPAN specifically cites the lack of confirmation as a reason for caution. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... The fairest description is therefore limited instrumental corroboration . The radar report raises the case above an ordinary light in the sky sighting, but it does not supply a preserved, independently verified trajectory. The Moon and Venus: online observation – 23 Apr. 2023 Channel: The Virtual Telescope Project Open on YouTube False echoes, Venus and the limits of the evidence Could Venus explain the red light? Venus was conspicuous in the western sky that evening and lay near the initial reported bearing. GEIPAN calculated its approximate azimuth as 296 to 299 degrees, close to the officer’s first estimate of 300 degrees. The planet was low, however, descending from roughly four degrees to about 1.5 degrees above the horizon. The unexplained light was estimated much higher, at 10 to 15 degrees, and was later associated with movement towards a bearing of 330 degrees. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAUVILLE (27) 23.04.1978 GEIPAN... GEIPAN therefore regards a simple identification with Venus as extremely unlikely. That judgement is reasonable if the recorded angular positions are dependable. A difference of six degrees or more in elevation is large enough to matter, and a planet cannot physically travel 30 degrees northwards in a few minutes. Even so, the Venus issue cannot be dismissed entirely. It is surprising that trained personnel did not mention such a bright nearby reference point, particularly when judging the position of another point like light. Human estimates of elevation and bearing can be inaccurate when an object is distant, the horizon is poorly defined or the observer has no measuring instrument. Venus might have contributed to confusion without explaining every reported feature. The description of decreasing brightness also has ordinary possibilities. Thin or broken cloud can alter a celestial light’s intensity, while atmospheric extinction becomes stronger near the horizon. Yet those effects would not naturally explain the stated change of bearing or the reported radar track. Venus is therefore a relevant complication rather than a complete solution. Could the radar have shown a false target? Radar does not display only aircraft. Depending on the equipment and conditions, returns can be produced by terrain, weather, birds, atmospheric refraction, equipment effects or signals reaching the receiver by an indirect path. Aviation guidance recognises that abnormal"},{"title":"Vernon 1954","url":"/vernon-1954/","description":"The Vernon sighting became Eure's most famous UFO story, but its dramatic details rest on a thin and heavily recycled record.","breadcrumb":"Eure's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","level":2,"text":"Vernon 1954 How Vernon Became Eure's Great UFO Legend Eure UFOs Vernon 1954 Vernon 1954 How Vernon Became Eure's Great UFO Legend The Vernon sighting became Eure's most famous UFO story, but its dramatic details rest on a thin and heavily recycled record. The Vernon sighting became Eure's most famous UFO story, but its dramatic details rest on a thin and heavily recycled record. event time window What Bernard Miserey reportedly saw How newspapers and UFO writers amplified the story Which claims survive close scrutiny eure vernon 1954 Introduction Vernon’s celebrated UFO story began in the early hours of 23 August 1954, when local businessman Bernard Miserey said he saw a vast luminous object above the Seine release several smaller discs. The imagery was unforgettable: a vertical “cigar” hanging over the town like a carrier craft, with glowing saucers dropping from it and racing away. Newspapers quickly turned the report into a visual spectacle, and influential UFO writers later treated Vernon as an early landmark of France’s great 1954 sighting wave. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org The sighting in Vernon, France, 1954 Patrick GrossBernard Miserey saw it at approximately one o'clock. Bernard Miserey became f... Yet Vernon became a legend on a much thinner foundation than its fame suggests. There is no known photograph of the phenomenon, no preserved instrument record, no physical trace and no readily accessible set of original witness statements. Most detailed modern versions descend from a small group of newspaper articles and books that repeatedly borrowed from one another. The result is a historically important UFO report, but not a securely reconstructed aerial event. What Bernard Miserey reportedly saw The central account places Miserey outside his home at about 1 am, after he had put his car in a garage near the Seine. He reportedly noticed that the dark town was being illuminated by an unusual pale light. Looking across the river and towards the hills, he saw what he described as an enormous luminous cylinder or cigar standing motionless in a vertical position. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, August 22 23, Vernon, EureBernard Miserey, resident of Vernon, in the Eure, was the witness of an astonishi... Smaller luminous objects then appeared beneath it. In the best known version, five disc like forms emerged one after another, initially seeming to fall before changing direction and speeding away. They were described as silent and surrounded by reddish light. One allegedly descended lower than the others, towards the bridge or river, causing Miserey to fear that it might be approaching him. The entire scene was said to have lasted roughly three quarters of an hour. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org The sighting in Vernon, France, 1954 Patrick GrossBernard Miserey saw it at approximately one o'clock. Bernard Miserey became f... Those details make Vernon sound unusually specific, but the precision is deceptive. Accounts differ over whether the main object was vertical or horizontal, whether the discs emerged from its bottom or front, and exactly what the smaller lights did. The illustrated report in France Dimanche , for example, showed a horizontal cigar even though later summaries commonly described it as vertical. A caption explained that an artist had added the objects and flight paths to a photograph according to the witness’s instructions; the resulting image was therefore a reconstruction, not photographic evidence of the sighting. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Bernard Miserey montrant, au bord de la Seine, à Vernon, la direction prise par les soucoupes. Notre dessinateur a...Read more... The number of secondary objects is generally given as five, but later catalogues and retellings sometimes introduce different totals or more elaborate movements. Such variation is common when a short initial report is expanded through repeated publication. It does not necessarily show that Miserey changed his story, but it prevents every familiar detail from being treated as equally secure. How the story entered the newspapers The earliest traceable national coverage appeared only two days later. The Paris newspaper Libération published an article on 25 August under a headline stating that a flying saucer had detached itself from a cigar in the sky above Vernon. Provincial newspapers reproduced or summarised the report during the following days, while France Dimanche supplied the memorable staged photograph and drawn in objects in its 5 September issue. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: articles de journaux, 1950 à 1959Libération France, 25 août 1954 \"Une soucoupe volante se détache d'un cigare... This rapid circulation mattered. France’s much larger autumn UFO wave had not yet reached its peak. Vernon arrived early enough to provide journalists and later investigators with a ready made narrative pattern: a large carrier shaped object producing smaller saucers. Jacques Vallée would describe it as the first important landmark of the 1954 wave, while Aimé Michel used it as a leading example of the “cloud cigar” or mother ship category. [Internet Archive] archive.org Internet Archive Anatomy of a Phenomenon Internet Archive Anatomy of a Phenomenon The press treatment also gave the report qualities that made it unusually durable: A named witness: Miserey was identified as a local businessman rather than an anonymous passer by. A recognisable setting: the Seine, the Vernon bridge and the hills offered a strong visual stage. A long duration: a reported observation lasting around 45 minutes sounded less easily dismissed than a fleeting light. Mechanical looking behaviour: smaller discs apparently emerging from a larger body suggested purposeful machinery. Illustration: the manipulated press photograph allowed readers to “see” a scene for which no actual image existed. The image was especially powerful because later readers could easily mistake an illustrated reconstruction for something closer to documentary evidence. Even where captions made its nature clear, the combination of a real photograph of Miserey, a real Vernon landscape and drawn aerial objects blurred the boundary between testimony and visual confirmation. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Bernard Miserey montrant, au bord de la Seine, à Vernon, la direction prise par les soucoupes. Notre dessinateur a...Read more... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The supporting witnesses are harder to verify Later versions often say that Miserey reported the incident at Vernon police station and that his account was supported by an engineer and two police officers. One commonly repeated formulation describes an army laboratory engineer travelling on a nearby road, with two policemen also seeing related phenomena. If original, independent statements from those witnesses survived, they would substantially strengthen the case. [Scribd] fr.scribd.com Open source on scribd.com. The difficulty is that the accessible record rarely presents those statements themselves. Instead, their existence is asserted in later UFO books, catalogues and summaries. Basic questions therefore remain unanswered. Did all witnesses observe the same object at the same time? Did they independently report discs emerging from a cylinder? Were their directions of view consistent? Did police record formal depositions, informal remarks or merely a summary of Miserey’s complaint? Vallée, writing in the 1960s, treated the alleged police testimony as evidence whose reliability would ordinarily have been respected in a criminal matter. That was an argument about the social treatment of UFO witnesses, however, not a publication of the underlying police document. His book also relied heavily on Aimé Michel’s earlier interpretation of the French wave. [Internet Archive] archive.org Internet Archive Anatomy of a Phenomenon Internet Archive Anatomy of a Phenomenon This distinction is central to assessing Vernon. There may well have been additional witnesses, but “several people reportedly confirmed it” is not equivalent to comparing several preserved, contemporaneous accounts. Without the originals, later writers cannot determine whether the testimony was genuinely independent or whether the secondary witnesses merely reported unusual lights that were subsequently folded into Miserey’s more dramatic narrative. Why Vernon suited the UFO theories of the 1950s By the late 1950s, Aimé Michel had developed a classification in which giant cigars or cloud like forms acted as sources for smaller UFOs. Vernon became one of the defining examples. Vallée then carried the case into English language literature in Anatomy of a Phenomenon , first published in 1965. He grouped Vernon with other reports in which huge cylindrical forms supposedly generated"},{"title":"Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled","url":"/why-finisteres-strangest-ufo-cases/","description":"Finistère has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single famous “landing” or a proven military encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled Finistère has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single famous “landing” or a proven military encounter. Finistère has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single famous “landing” or a proven military encounter. Its strongest record is a patchwork of roadside observations, unusual lights near airports and naval facilities, meteor like events, and a small number of cases that remained unexplained after investigation. finistere Introduction This mixed picture matters. Finistère contains major civilian and military aviation sites, including Brest airport and naval air bases around Landivisiau and the Crozon peninsula. That creates both opportunities for informed checking and many ordinary sources of misidentification. The official record therefore supports neither blanket dismissal nor claims of confirmed extraordinary craft. It shows how witness testimony, geography, weather, radar checks and later reconstruction can strengthen, weaken or occasionally leave a report unresolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN What the official record actually contains France’s principal public body for investigating unusual aerial reports is GEIPAN, part of the national space agency CNES. It uses the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” rather than assuming that every report involves a physical flying object. Its classifications distinguish clearly explained cases, probable explanations, reports too weak to analyse and incidents that remain unexplained despite the available information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLe glossaire du Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANLe glossaire du Geipan GEIPAN In simplified form: Category A means the phenomenon has been identified without meaningful ambiguity. Category B means GEIPAN considers one explanation highly probable. Category C means there is not enough dependable information for analysis. Category D means the event remains unexplained after investigation. D1 is used for an unexplained but moderately supported case, often involving one witness and no recording. D2 is reserved for exceptionally strange, strongly documented reports involving independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLe glossaire du Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANLe glossaire du Geipan GEIPAN Finistère’s published files include examples across most of these levels. The department has straightforward identifications such as a boat, a light aircraft, a meteoroid and an apparent bird image; weak cases lacking reliable detail; and a smaller number of unresolved reports. The database should not be read as a list of alien craft. It is an archive of observations reported as unexplained at the time, many of which were subsequently interpreted through conventional causes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The archive is also not static. Older files may be reconsidered when better astronomical software, mapping tools, flight information or investigative experience becomes available. The 1981 Guipavas case, for example, was originally treated as unexplained but was later reclassified as a probable meteoroid. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUIPAVAS, BREST (29) 22.11.1981 GEIPAN... Landivisiau 1979: the department’s landmark unresolved case The incident most likely to be regarded as Finistère’s classic UFO case occurred near Landivisiau on 18 March 1979. At about 9.15 pm, a motorist and her passenger reported seeing a dark, greyish fluorescent object, described as rocket shaped, only a few metres above the main road. It appeared silent and moved slowly northwards as their car passed beneath or very close to it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979Le 18 mars 1979 vers 21h 15 une automobiliste et sa passagère aperçoivent un objet sombre de couleur gris fluo... Roughly half an hour later, on returning through the area, the driver reported another unusual form near the same stretch of road, this time resembling a flat wing. GEIPAN retains the case in Category D: unexplained despite information considered sufficiently substantial for analysis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979Le 18 mars 1979 vers 21h 15 une automobiliste et sa passagère aperçoivent un objet sombre de couleur gris fluo... The report is memorable because it was not simply a distant point of light. The claimed proximity, apparent structure, silence and second observation made it more difficult to reduce immediately to a star or ordinary aircraft seen far away. The Landivisiau area also has an obvious aviation connection, which encourages questions about military aircraft, training activity or unusual viewing angles. Those features do not make the account conclusive. The observations were brief, made at night in continuous rain and from a moving vehicle. Such conditions make distance, altitude, shape and speed particularly unreliable. A large conventional aircraft farther away can appear like a small silent object nearby, while reflections or illuminated cloud can produce sharp looking forms without revealing their true scale. The case also lacks the evidence that would move it beyond witness testimony: no photograph, radar confirmation, recovered material or independent instrument record has emerged publicly. Its lasting status is therefore unresolved , not confirmed. It remains important because no conventional explanation has been established, but its evidential ceiling is low enough that extraordinary conclusions would go beyond the record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979Le 18 mars 1979 vers 21h 15 une automobiliste et sa passagère aperçoivent un objet sombre de couleur gris fluo... Two modern cases that resisted detailed investigation La Forêt Fouesnant, 2018 Shortly after 7.20 am on 10 December 2018, a driver leaving home near La Forêt Fouesnant noticed a low altitude luminous phenomenon over a field. The witness described a sharply defined yellow bar with a bright green central light. It allegedly moved slowly, turned towards the witness, remained stationary and then departed eastwards into mist. The discontinuous observation lasted about a minute and a half. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFORET FOUESNANT (LA) (29) 10.12.2018 GEIPAN... GEIPAN considered several possibilities, including a drone, projected light, agricultural machinery and a wind borne object such as a lantern. Investigators found no agricultural vehicle in the relevant fields. The reported changes of direction and stationary phase counted against a freely drifting object, while the appearance and apparent scale did not fit an ordinary drone particularly well. The drone explanation was judged the strongest of the conventional options, but still below GEIPAN’s threshold for adopting it as probable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFORET FOUESNANT (LA) (29) 10.12.2018 GEIPAN... The investigation included a site visit, photographs of the setting, estimates of dimensions and trajectory, and a cognitive interview intended to retrieve the witness’s memory without overly suggestive questioning. GEIPAN regarded the testimony as reasonably consistent and classified the case D1. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFORET FOUESNANT (LA) (29) 10.12.2018 GEIPAN... The limitations are substantial. There was only one witness, no image of the phenomenon itself and no independent sensor record. The reported radio problem in the witness’s car occurred after the observation, but the file does not establish a causal connection. The most responsible conclusion is therefore that the case was not satisfactorily explained, while remaining too weakly corroborated to demonstrate an extraordinary object. Crozon, 2020 An even more extensively studied case occurred on the Crozon peninsula on 13 January 2020. A driver reported three red rectangular lights that maintained their relative positions before moving southwards, apparently descending in a bouncing or irregular fashion and then accelerating out of sight. The observation took place during poor weather, from inside a running vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCROZON (29) 13.01.2020 GEIPAN... GEIPAN examined a wide range of explanations, including drones, helicopters, aircraft, lighthouse or semaphore lights, wind turbine lighting, projected beams, internal reflections and vehicle lights. Investigators contacted the nearby naval air station at Lanvéoc Poulmic, which reported that none of its aircraft or drones was operating in the relevant manoeuvre. A French Navy drone instructor also stated that the reported appearance did not resemble known military drone lighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCROZON (29) 13.01.2020 GEIPAN... Noise"},{"title":"Landivisiau","url":"/landivisiau/","description":"Finistere's best-known unresolved sighting remains compelling because of its claimed proximity, yet rain, darkness and missing records limit what it can prove.","breadcrumb":"Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled","level":2,"text":"Landivisiau What Keeps the Landivisiau Sighting Unresolved? Finistere UFOs Landivisiau Landivisiau What Keeps the Landivisiau Sighting Unresolved? Finistere's best known unresolved sighting remains compelling because of its claimed proximity, yet rain, darkness and missing records limit what it can prove. Finistere's best known unresolved sighting remains compelling because of its claimed proximity, yet rain, darkness and missing records limit what it can prove. event time window What the two witnesses reported Why the setting complicates the account What evidence is still missing finistere landivisiau Introduction The Landivisiau sighting of 18 March 1979 remains Finistère’s best known officially unresolved UFO report because the witnesses described something apparently close, structured and silent rather than a distant point of light. At about 9.15 pm, a motorist and her passenger reported passing beneath a grey, rocket shaped object over an expressway. Roughly half an hour later, the driver said she encountered a flatter, wing like form near the same place. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that now holds the file, continues to classify the case as unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... Yet “unexplained” is not the same as “confirmed extraordinary craft”. The observations lasted only seconds, in continuous rain and darkness, from a moving car. No photograph, physical trace, radar record or independently documented third party sighting appears in the published file. The case survives because no conventional explanation was established—not because the surviving evidence proves what the witnesses thought they saw. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... What the two witnesses reported The first observation occurred at approximately 9.15 pm on a wet Sunday evening. According to GEIPAN’s summary, the driver and her passenger saw a dark object described as fluorescent grey and shaped rather like a rocket. They placed it about three metres above the expressway and said it was moving slowly northwards without making any sound. The car continued beneath it, so the encounter was necessarily brief. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... The archived entry for one of the witnesses identifies her as a 20 year old woman and records continuous rain, a single object, slow apparent movement and complete silence. The reported dimensions were approximately one metre across and three metres long. Significantly, however, the database gives no measured distance from witness to phenomenon. The apparently precise dimensions therefore seem to be visual estimates rather than values derived from triangulation or a known reference object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUIPAVAS (29) 18.03.1979 T2 GEIPAN... At about 9.45 pm, while travelling back along the route, the driver reported a second episode near the same location. This time the passenger is not described as sharing the observation. The driver said she saw a flat “wing”, estimated at around three square metres, composed of a thin grey metallic lattice. She also reported an undulating motion and said the object stopped three or four metres ahead of the vehicle. Alarmed, she accelerated and passed beneath it. Again, she noticed no engine noise, lights or heat. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... These details give the story its lasting force. A close object with an apparent structure is harder to dismiss casually than an anonymous light high in the sky. The two stage encounter also seems, at first sight, to offer repetition: the driver reported an unusual object in approximately the same place twice within half an hour. That repetition does not necessarily amount to independent corroboration. The second description came from the same driver after she had already experienced and discussed an alarming first sighting. The reported forms also differ markedly: first a three dimensional, rocket like body, then a thin, lattice like wing. That could mean two objects, changing perspective, an inaccurately perceived conventional object, or an account shaped by the difficulty of interpreting a brief visual stimulus. The surviving record cannot distinguish between those possibilities. Why the setting complicates the account The central difficulty is not whether the witnesses were sincere. Nothing in the public file establishes deception, and the report was serious enough to enter the official archive. The difficulty is whether sincere observation under these conditions could supply dependable measurements of shape, distance and movement. It was night, rain was falling continuously, and the witnesses were travelling by car. Windscreen reflections, water droplets, glare, road lighting and the restricted field of view inside a moving vehicle can all make position and outline difficult to judge. GEIPAN itself emphasises the poor weather and the brevity of both observations when explaining why the phenomenon could not be identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... Nearness was perceived, not measured The claim that the object was only a few metres away is the case’s most striking feature, but also one of its most fragile. Human vision does not directly measure distance. It infers it from cues such as known size, binocular disparity, surface detail, motion and the relationship between an object and its surroundings. At night, many of those cues are reduced or absent. This creates a circular problem. The witnesses could estimate the object’s size only by assuming a distance, while estimating its distance partly from its apparent size. Without a photograph, a second viewing position, an identifiable background feature or a surveyed line of sight, the reported metres cannot be checked. GEIPAN’s witness record accordingly leaves the distance field unspecified, even though the narrative contains close range estimates. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUIPAVAS (29) 18.03.1979 T2 GEIPAN... The apparent stopping of the second object in front of the car is similarly difficult to reconstruct. Relative motion can make a distant or laterally moving object appear stationary when observer and object briefly align. Conversely, something fixed beside or above the road may appear to move because the car is moving. This does not establish an explanation, but it prevents the reported movement from being treated as an independently measured flight path. Témoignage : un OVNI dans le jardin Archive INA Channel: INA Société Open on YouTube Silence proves less than it appears to The witnesses’ insistence on complete silence is often presented as evidence against an aircraft or helicopter. At very close range, a powered aircraft would normally be expected to be audible, which is one reason the account remains puzzling. But the lack of sound also conflicts with the claimed proximity if the phenomenon was a large conventional aircraft. There are several uncertainties. The windows may have been closed because of the rain; the car’s own engine, tyres and wipers would have produced background noise; and the encounters lasted only seconds. Sound can also arrive after the visual impression when an aircraft is farther away than assumed. None of this identifies the object, but it means “silent” cannot by itself establish either nearness or exotic propulsion. A broad sceptical review of French official UFO studies has warned against accepting witness estimates of dimensions, distance and motion simply because the witnesses appear honest or competent. Its authors argue that visual estimates made in unusual conditions require independent checks and that official “unexplained” classifications have sometimes persisted when initial investigations left ordinary hypotheses insufficiently tested. That critique does not solve Landivisiau, but it directly addresses the kind of evidential weakness present here. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical Inquirer The aviation connection is suggestive but undocumented GEIPAN’s case summary notes proximity to Brest Guipavas airport. The wider area also has substantial aviation activity, making aircraft related explanations an obvious line of enquiry. But the published page does not provide air traffic logs, military flight information, radar data, aircraft identification checks or meteorological measurements beyond continuous rain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLANDIVISIAU (29) 1979 GEIPAN... This absence cuts both ways. There is no public record showing that an aircraft was responsible. Equally, there is no radar or operational evidence confirming an unknown vehicle hovering over the road. A nearby aviation environment raises plausible conventional possibilities; it does not automatically explain the sighting or turn it into a military encounter. What the investigation established The gendarmerie inspected the matter but found no physical trace. GEIPAN’s published"},{"title":"Modern Cases","url":"/modern-cases/","description":"The La Foret-Fouesnant and Crozon reports show how site visits, military checks and reconstruction can narrow possibilities without resolving a case.","breadcrumb":"Why Finistere's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unsettled","level":2,"text":"Modern Cases How Far Did Investigators Get in Crozon and Fouesnant? Finistere UFOs Modern Cases Modern Cases How Far Did Investigators Get in Crozon and Fouesnant? The La Foret Fouesnant and Crozon reports show how site visits, military checks and reconstruction can narrow possibilities without resolving a case. The La Foret Fouesnant and Crozon reports show how site visits, military checks and reconstruction can narrow possibilities without resolving a case. dataset evidence The 2018 and 2020 witness accounts Conventional explanations that were tested Why both cases remained unresolved finistere modern cases Introduction Two modern Finistère sightings show both the value and the limit of a serious UFO investigation. At La Forêt Fouesnant in December 2018 and near Crozon in January 2020, GEIPAN investigators visited the locations, interviewed the witnesses in depth, reconstructed sight lines and tested explanations involving drones, aircraft, projected lights, windborne objects and local military activity. In neither case did they find an ordinary explanation that fitted all the reported details. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFORET FOUESNANT (LA) (29) 10.12.2018 GEIPAN... That does not make either sighting evidence of an extraordinary craft. Both depended on a single witness, neither produced a photograph or video of the phenomenon, and important measurements such as distance and size were reconstructed after the event. Their real significance within Finistère’s UFO history is narrower but useful: they demonstrate how much investigators can eliminate while still being unable to establish what actually happened. The 2018 and 2020 witness accounts A luminous bar over La Forêt Fouesnant Before sunrise on 10 December 2018, a woman driving along the roughly 300 metre entrance to her rural home north of La Forêt Fouesnant noticed a luminous form over a neighbouring field. She described a pale yellow horizontal bar with an intense fluorescent green circular light at its centre. According to her account, it moved slowly, turned towards her, remained apparently stationary for about 20 seconds and then changed direction before disappearing into mist. She opened the car windows but heard no sound from it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The complete sequence was not observed from one fixed position. The witness stopped her car three times while progressing along the drive, so her own movement complicated any estimate of the phenomenon’s course. During a later reconstruction, investigators timed the three phases at about 30 seconds each, giving a total discontinuous observation of roughly a minute and a half. They also measured the viewing directions and compared the apparent positions with fields, trees and the local horizon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witness reported a very large apparent width when asked to reproduce it at arm’s length. Depending on an assumed distance, that estimate would imply an object ranging from several metres to hundreds of metres across. GEIPAN did not treat those calculated dimensions as dependable physical measurements. The witness acknowledged difficulty judging size, and investigators explicitly considered that she had overestimated the apparent width. This is an important restraint: the reconstruction made the report more precise, but it did not turn an impression of size into proof of a huge nearby object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The investigators considered her account coherent and information rich. They found no evident prior fixation on UFOs and no reason to suspect deliberate fabrication. Even so, credibility is not the same as observational accuracy. A sincere witness can misjudge range, motion or scale in darkness, especially when the object has no known background against which to measure it. GEIPAN’s assessment therefore supported the honesty and internal consistency of the report, not the literal accuracy of every estimated dimension. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Three red rectangles near Crozon The Crozon report occurred at about 7.48 am on 13 January 2020. A lone driver was travelling on an unlit rural road south of Crozon in heavy rain and strong gusting wind when he saw two bright red horizontal rectangles beyond or above a stand of pine trees. A third similar rectangle then appeared. He said the lights moved alongside his route, stopped near a junction and later departed southwards, first slowly and then with a rapid apparent acceleration towards the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr CROZON (29) 13.01.2020 [E Cgei CR T,C,D S] compte rendu enquete GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... At their closest apparent position, the witness estimated that the three rectangles occupied about two thirds of the width of his windscreen. He compared them to large, sharply outlined vehicle rear lights containing small moving points. No enclosing structure was visible. The engine was running, the windows were closed, rain was falling and wind was buffeting the vehicle, all of which reduced the evidential force of the reported silence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr CROZON (29) 13.01.2020 [E Cgei CR T,C,D S] compte rendu enquete GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... GEIPAN began with a remote inquiry, followed by a cognitive interview, a site inspection and a visual reconstruction. A cognitive interview is designed to recover details by returning a witness mentally to the circumstances of an event without relying solely on leading questions. In this case, however, the original technical questionnaire was completed about five months after the sighting, and the field reconstruction took place roughly a year later. GEIPAN warned that such delays can alter or consolidate memories, even when the witness remains confident and coherent. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr CROZON (29) 13.01.2020 [E Cgei CR T,C,D S] compte rendu enquete GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The Crozon account was nevertheless unusually detailed for a single witness report. Investigators mapped the route, checked the terrain, modelled what could be visible from each position and examined the surrounding aviation, lighthouse and military environment. The result was a strong narrative record but no independent record of the lights themselves. Missing Cargo Escape From Tarkov Under a Minute Quest Guide Lighthouse Skier eft Channel: TheBeardedMato Open on YouTube Conventional explanations that were tested La Forêt Fouesnant: the drone came closest A drone was the leading conventional possibility at La Forêt Fouesnant. A remotely piloted craft could, in principle, move slowly, hover and turn towards an observer. Its motor might also have been masked by the running car engine, so GEIPAN did not regard the absence of sound as decisive. Passing mist apparently obscuring the light also favoured some kind of physical object rather than a beam projected directly onto cloud. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several details weakened that hypothesis. The reported yellow bar and green centre appearance did not resemble ordinary drone navigation lighting. The very large apparent width was difficult to reconcile with a small recreational model, although that objection depends heavily on the unreliable size estimate. The flight would also have occurred before dawn on a Monday in a sparsely populated area, outside the ordinary rules then governing recreational night flying. Investigators noted a model aircraft club about 11 kilometres away but found no evidence connecting it to the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN ultimately judged a drone the strongest hypothesis, but still well below the threshold at which it could be adopted as the probable explanation. That wording matters. It means “least unsatisfactory among those considered”, not “almost certainly a drone”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFORET FOUESNANT (LA) (29) 10.12.2018 GEIPAN... Investigators also examined agricultural and entertainment lighting. They contacted the people farming the relevant fields, who checked their records and said neither they nor their machinery had been working there that morning. A powerful beam reflected from low cloud was geographically possible because cloud bases were reported at roughly 720 to 1,020 metres, but the nearest known nightclub was about eight kilometres away. More importantly, the witness described clean, stable edges and said wisps of mist passed in front of the phenomenon. A projected patch on cloud would normally be expected to look more diffuse and to remain associated with the cloud layer rather than appear locally hidden by nearer mist. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A lantern or balloon carried by the wind was also considered. The initial direction was not wholly incompatible with the"},{"title":"Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious","url":"/why-gards-strangest-lights-rarely-stayed/","description":"Gard has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious Gard has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history. Gard has a genuine but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record contains reports from Nîmes, Alès, Saint Gilles, Uzès, Pont Saint Esprit and smaller rural communes, yet it does not reveal a single, well documented “landmark encounter” comparable with France’s best known cases. gard Introduction A few Gard reports remain unclassified because the available information is inadequate, but that is not the same as evidence of an exotic craft. The public files of GEIPAN, the French government body that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, show a mixture of solved cases , probable explanations and reports too incomplete for a firm conclusion. They also demonstrate why Gard’s aviation activity around Nîmes Garons must be considered whenever an unusual light or moving object is reported. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — Tout d'abord, le GEIPAN collecte les témoignages relatifs aux phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés (PAN), généra... Gard’s official UFO record France uses the term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” for reports commonly described as UFO sightings. GEIPAN, part of the French space agency CNES, receives witness accounts directly or through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities. Investigators compare testimony with aircraft movements, weather, astronomical objects and satellite data before assigning a classification. [Gendarmerie National] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés...2 Jul 2022 — Le GEIPAN est également un lieu d'archives... The classifications matter: Category A means that the phenomenon has been identified. Category B indicates a probable explanation. Category C means that reliable information is insufficient. Category D is reserved for cases that remain unidentified after investigation despite reasonably strong information. Gard’s searchable record is dominated by the first three categories. Published examples include Venus, the Moon, satellites, the International Space Station, aircraft, illuminated balloons and atmospheric re entry. Some Nîmes and Saint Gilles reports are classed C because investigators lacked enough dependable detail, not because the files demonstrate extraordinary performance or non human technology. [Geipan] geipan.fr NIMES (30) 13.10.2015 13/10/2015 C Gard Manque d'informations fiables 02/11/2020 2015 10 13... Published: October 13, 2015 This distinction is central to understanding the department’s history. “Unidentified” often describes the limits of the evidence rather than the nature of the object. The Nîmes cases show how sightings are resolved Nîmes accounts for several published reports and offers a useful cross section of the explanations found throughout Gard. On 15 August 2015, a man watching the sky with his wife and daughter saw a very bright point towards the south east. It remained visible for roughly ten seconds and then went out. The witness himself suspected an Iridium satellite flare, a once familiar effect produced when a satellite’s reflective surface briefly directed sunlight towards an observer. GEIPAN classified the case A, treating the satellite explanation as established. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A separate Nîmes report from 6 September 2016 was identified as Venus. Another, on 17 December 2016, was judged probably to involve a sky lantern. Two reports from October 2015 remained in category C because the information supplied was insufficiently reliable, while an observation on 14 October was attributed to an aircraft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Together, these cases illustrate several recurring difficulties: Brightness can be misleading. Venus, satellites and aircraft landing lights can appear unusually intense, especially in clear southern skies. Apparent stillness does not prove hovering. An aircraft approaching almost directly towards an observer may show little sideways movement. Lights can disappear abruptly. A satellite may enter Earth’s shadow, an aircraft may turn, or a reflective surface may cease directing sunlight towards the witness. A vague report may remain formally unidentified. Without an exact time, direction, duration and angular movement, investigators may be unable to test ordinary explanations. The unresolved Nîmes entries therefore should not be grouped with cases supported by radar data, multiple independent observers or detailed physical evidence. Their official status principally records an evidence gap. The 1984 road observation was weakened by reconstruction One of the more instructive historical files concerns observations made while travelling on the A9 motorway from Nîmes towards Montpellier on 5 January 1984. The archive separates the testimony into reports from different witnesses. When GEIPAN later revisited the material, it identified one of the reported phenomena as Aldebaran, a bright star whose position could appear striking from a moving vehicle under the geographical conditions described. That portion of the case was reclassified as astronomical. Another part remained category C because the surviving information was not sufficient for a secure reconstruction. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This is a common problem in vehicle sightings. The observer’s changing position makes a distant fixed light appear to move relative to trees, hills, road bends and the windscreen. Reflections inside the car may add further confusion. Unless investigators know the exact route, direction of travel, viewing angles and timing, even sincere testimony can be difficult to interpret decades later. The 1984 file is important within Gard not because it proves an extraordinary event, but because later analysis weakened at least part of the original mystery. It demonstrates the value of revisiting old reports with astronomical software and improved geographical reconstruction. Atmospheric re entries produced some of the most dramatic displays Several Gard observations that might have looked especially unusual were connected with objects returning through the atmosphere. On 18 October 2009, people in and around Alès were among numerous witnesses across France who saw a conspicuous aerial display. GEIPAN linked the event to the atmospheric re entry of the Centaur stage of an Atlas rocket and the release of residual fuel. Because the phenomenon was visible over a broad area, reports from different locations could be compared with the calculated trajectory. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. At Saint Bonnet du Gard on 17 January 2014, another report was eventually classified A as an atmospheric re entry. The case page, updated in January 2026, records a very low strangeness rating, reflecting the close fit between the observation and the identified cause. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Re entry events can look unlike ordinary meteors. They may last longer, fragment into several lights and cross a large portion of the sky. Witnesses separated by many kilometres may consequently believe that an object passed close to their own town, even though it was at great altitude. These cases also explain why clusters of reports do not automatically imply a local “flap”. One distant event can generate testimony from Gard and several neighbouring departments at almost the same time. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons, lanterns and satellites dominate recent explanations Recent Gard records contain several examples of small human made objects being mistaken for something more unusual. In Uzès on 8 August 2020, a photographed object was classified B and judged probably to be a reflective foil balloon. GEIPAN’s case file includes an investigation report and the witness photograph, giving investigators more material than is available in many light only sightings. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. At Pont Saint Esprit on 12 December 2017, the likely explanation was an illuminated balloon. At Saint Ambroix in January 2017, investigators favoured an aircraft. A report from Vergèze in August 2017 was identified as a satellite, while the International Space Station explained an observation at Moulézan in December 2018. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. These explanations are not arbitrary labels. Each object has recognisable behaviour: Foil balloons may flash, rotate and seem metallic as they catch sunlight. Illuminated balloons and sky"},{"title":"Re entries","url":"/re-entries/","description":"Long-lasting, fragmented lights over Gard were sometimes rocket stages burning up high above several departments at once.","breadcrumb":"Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Re entries When Rocket Debris Lit Up Gard's Sky Gard UFO Files Re entries Re entries When Rocket Debris Lit Up Gard's Sky Long lasting, fragmented lights over Gard were sometimes rocket stages burning up high above several departments at once. Long lasting, fragmented lights over Gard were sometimes rocket stages burning up high above several departments at once. event time window The 2009 Atlas Centaur display near Ales The 2014 Saint Bonnet du Gard identification Why one distant re entry creates many local reports gard re entries Introduction Some of Gard’s most dramatic “UFO” reports were not local objects at all. They were high altitude events visible across large parts of France: rocket stages, released propellant or natural meteoroids producing bright, unfamiliar displays far above the department. A witness near Alès might therefore report the same event as observers hundreds of kilometres away, although each person describes it against a different horizon and with no obvious sense of its true distance. The clearest Gard example occurred on 18 October 2009, when two luminous points and a broad whitish halo crossed the sky. GEIPAN, the French government’s unidentified aerospace phenomena investigation service, linked reports from Alès and many other places to the Centaur upper stage used to launch the American DMSP F18 military weather satellite. The stage’s post launch operations and release of residual propellant produced a display that looked unusual but was neither unexplained nor confined to Gard. [Geipan] geipan.fr DEBRIS SPATIAUX (RETOMBEE) — NATIONAL (FR) 18.10.2009 GEIPAN... A second Gard report, from Saint Bonnet du Gard in January 2014, is often grouped with atmospheric re entry cases. It was indeed an object entering the atmosphere, but the evidence points to a natural rocky meteoroid rather than rocket debris. That distinction matters: similar looking lights can arise from different mechanisms, and the investigation depends on timing, direction, duration and reports from outside the department. The 2009 Atlas Centaur display near Alès At about 21:15 local time on Sunday 18 October 2009, observers across France reported one or two brilliant points travelling generally from the south towards the north west. Many described a pale halo or comet like trail surrounding or following the lights. The passage was silent, a detail that made it harder for witnesses to associate the phenomenon with familiar aircraft. [Geipan] geipan.fr DEBRIS SPATIAUX (RETOMBEE) — NATIONAL (FR) 18.10.2009 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s national case file includes a testimony from Alès alongside reports from the Drôme, Hautes Pyrénées, Ariège, Pyrénées Orientales, Hérault, Morbihan, Aube, Yonne, Rhône and several other departments. The geographical spread is crucial. It shows that the light was not hovering over one Gard town or following a route through the department at ordinary aircraft height. It was a distant event seen from widely separated viewpoints. [Geipan] geipan.fr DEBRIS SPATIAUX (RETOMBEE) — NATIONAL (FR) 18.10.2009 GEIPAN... The source was associated with the launch of DMSP F18, a United States military meteorological satellite carried by an Atlas V rocket from Vandenberg Air Force Base in California earlier that day. After releasing the satellite, the Centaur upper stage conducted additional operations involving its remaining cryogenic propellant. Satellite observers had anticipated that France would be well placed to see the burn and fuel release. [satobs.org] satobs.org RE: DMSP F18 search elements; Centaur fuel dump alertCentaur fuel dump alert From: Daniel Deak. France is very well placed for the burn a... GEIPAN concluded that the French reports corresponded to the Centaur stage’s residual fuel release rather than to an unknown craft. The two luminous points, diffuse halo and lack of sound were consistent with material illuminated at extreme altitude. The case was classified A, meaning the phenomenon was identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr DEBRIS SPATIAUX (RETOMBEE) — NATIONAL (FR) 18.10.2009 GEIPAN... Strictly speaking, the visible 2009 spectacle was more complicated than the popular phrase “rocket burning up” suggests. The satellite had already been deployed, and much of what witnesses saw was connected with the upper stage’s manoeuvres and vented propellant. Expanding gas or fuel can scatter sunlight and form a large glowing cloud, while the stage itself remains visible as a compact point. From the ground, the combination may resemble paired objects travelling inside a luminous envelope. That mechanism explains why accounts could sound more elaborate than a simple “moving star”. Different observers concentrated on different features: two points, a single leading light, a tail, a misty glow or an apparent change in shape. These variations do not require multiple objects over separate towns. They are normal consequences of viewing one large, distant display from different angles and under different local sky conditions. The 2014 Saint Bonnet identification On 17 January 2014 at 19:06, a powerful white light with a trail crossed the sky over eastern France. A witness at Saint Bonnet du Gard, north east of Nîmes, reported seeing the phenomenon very low above the northern horizon. GEIPAN treated the Gard testimony as part of the same event recorded by observers much farther north. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr meteore france 2014 GEIPANUn météore remarquable dans l'Est de la France le 17 janvier 2014 GEIPAN... Automatic sky cameras helped reconstruct what had happened. The luminous body travelled at altitudes of roughly 88 to 51 kilometres near Nancy and entered at about 23 kilometres per second. Investigators judged it most probably to be a small rocky body originating in the asteroid belt. The Gard witness was apparently among the most southerly people to report it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr meteore france 2014 GEIPANUn météore remarquable dans l'Est de la France le 17 janvier 2014 GEIPAN... This was therefore an atmospheric entry, but not a rocket stage re entry. GEIPAN’s database lists the Saint Bonnet case as identified, while its accompanying account describes a meteoroid. The episode is useful precisely because it prevents an overly simple interpretation of Gard’s long distance sky displays. “Atmospheric re entry” can refer broadly to both artificial hardware returning from orbit and natural material entering from space; establishing which one was responsible requires more than the appearance of a trail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr meteore france 2014 GEIPANUn météore remarquable dans l'Est de la France le 17 janvier 2014 GEIPAN... The 2014 event also demonstrates how badly witnesses can underestimate distance and altitude. Some observers elsewhere placed the light only a few hundred metres above the ground, even though camera measurements located the luminous path tens of kilometres high. From Saint Bonnet du Gard, a fireball above north eastern France could appear close to the horizon without being anywhere near Gard itself. GEIPAN specifically highlighted this recurring error in reports of exceptionally bright phenomena. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr meteore france 2014 GEIPANUn météore remarquable dans l'Est de la France le 17 janvier 2014 GEIPAN... Duration provides another important distinction. Witnesses described the January 2014 meteoroid crossing part of the sky in only one to three seconds. Artificial re entry break ups generally appear slower, often persisting long enough for several glowing pieces to travel together in a steady direction. A technical guide from the Aerospace Corporation notes that a slow, aircraft like movement with a long luminous trail, or a compact cluster of fragments moving at similar speeds, strongly favours space debris; a very rapid flash is more characteristic of a meteor. [Aerospace Corporation] aerospace.org Aerospace CorporationWhat Does a Reentry Look LikeIf an object moves slowly and steadily across the sky at a speed similar to how a fast... 1:05 Rocket re entering atmosphere results in impressive light show Channel: GBC News · Views: 5.9K · Uploaded: June 2022 · Length: 1 minute 5 seconds Open on YouTube Why one distant event creates many local reports A rocket stage or meteoroid does not need to pass directly over Gard to generate convincing reports from the department. At heights of tens or hundreds of kilometres, a luminous object can be visible across an enormous area. Each observer sees it projected against a different part of the landscape, creating the understandable impression that it is above a nearby road, hill or village. Several effects make these events especially easy to misread: There is little reliable sense of scale. Against a dark sky, witnesses cannot tell whether a light is a small object nearby or a large, bright event hundreds of kilometres away. Fragmentation looks structured. Pieces separating from a rocket body may remain on similar paths, producing an apparent formation of"},{"title":"Solved Cases","url":"/solved-cases/","description":"Gard's official files show how witness detail, astronomy and flight checks turned many strange lights into identifiable objects.","breadcrumb":"Why Gard's Strangest Lights Rarely Stayed Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Solved Cases How Investigators Explained Gard's UFO Reports Gard UFO Files Solved Cases Solved Cases How Investigators Explained Gard's UFO Reports Gard's official files show how witness detail, astronomy and flight checks turned many strange lights into identifiable objects. Gard's official files show how witness detail, astronomy and flight checks turned many strange lights into identifiable objects. dataset evidence What GEIPAN's classifications actually mean Nimes cases involving Venus, satellites and aircraft Why weak testimony can remain officially unidentified gard solved cases Introduction Most UFO reports in Gard were not solved by discovering a single dramatic clue. They were solved by reconstructing ordinary details: the witness’s position, viewing direction, exact time, apparent motion, weather, astronomical objects and known traffic in the sky. In the official GEIPAN archive, Gard sightings have been attributed to Venus, stars, satellites, aircraft, sky lanterns and balloons. Other reports remain unclassified chiefly because investigators received too little reliable information to test any explanation properly. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT AMBROIX (30) 18.01.2017 18/01/2017 B Gard Avion 30/08/2017 2017 01 18... [A9] DE NIMES (30) VERS MONTPELLIER (34) 05.0... Published: January 18, 2017 That distinction is the key to understanding solved UFO cases in the department. An identified report does not mean that the witness was dishonest or careless: familiar objects can look genuinely strange when seen briefly, without distance cues or under unusual lighting. Equally, an officially unidentified report is not automatically stronger evidence for an extraordinary craft. In GEIPAN’s system, some cases remain unidentified simply because the evidence is too incomplete to support either identification or meaningful exclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr vestigation... What GEIPAN’s classifications actually mean GEIPAN is the unit of the French space agency CNES responsible for collecting, investigating and publishing reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its work begins with testimony rather than with an assumption that a physical object was present. Witnesses are asked to complete a technical questionnaire covering matters such as location, time, duration, direction, angular size, movement and observing conditions. Photographs, videos, sketches or other records may supplement the account, but many Gard files depend largely on what one person remembered seeing. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... The resulting letter classification describes the outcome of the investigation: Category A: the phenomenon has been identified. Category B: an explanation is considered probable, although not demonstrated with the same confidence as an A case. Category C: the phenomenon could not be identified because the information is inadequate. Category D: the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation despite sufficiently substantial evidence. GEIPAN also assesses the consistency of the evidence and the residual strangeness left after possible explanations have been examined. A case with a vivid description may still have low consistency if it comes from a lone observer, lacks photographs or has an uncertain direction and time. Conversely, a visually unimpressive light can be easy to identify when the timing and geometry match a known planet, aircraft or satellite. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This is why the word “unidentified” must be read carefully in Gard’s records. Category C often means “not enough dependable data”, not “ordinary explanations were ruled out”. GEIPAN can revisit C and D cases if new information becomes available, so classifications are investigative judgements rather than permanent declarations about the nature of an object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr vestigation... Nîmes cases solved by astronomy and satellites Nîmes provides some of the clearest examples of how a striking light can become identifiable once its time and direction are checked against the sky. On 6 September 2016, a Nîmes observation was classified A and identified as Venus. The planet is a recurrent source of UFO reports because it can be exceptionally bright, is often seen low in twilight or darkness and may appear to shimmer, change colour or move when viewed through disturbed air or against drifting cloud. With no obvious foreground reference, an observer may also find it difficult to judge whether the light is nearby or many millions of kilometres away. The decisive point is not merely that Venus was visible, but that its position matched the reported direction and time. [Geipan] geipan.fr NIMES (30) 06.09.2016 06/09/2016 A Gard Vénus 18/05/2020 2016 09 06... Published: September 6, 2016 An older case from 5 January 1984 shows how an astronomical identification can emerge even from a more complicated account made during a journey from Nîmes towards Montpellier. One component of that report was eventually classified A as a confusion with Aldebaran, a bright star. GEIPAN’s published summary says that the star was observed in a temporarily favourable geographical configuration, meaning that the changing road direction and sightlines made a fixed celestial object appear more puzzling than it would have from a stationary viewpoint. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The same journey also produced a separate component that remained category C. That contrast is instructive. Investigators could match one observation to a star because enough geometry survived in the record, while the other lacked sufficiently reliable information. A single episode can therefore contain both an identified sighting and an officially inconclusive one without contradiction. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A further Nîmes case, dated 15 August 2015, concerned an intensely bright point seen towards the south east for about ten seconds before it disappeared. The witness himself suggested an Iridium satellite flare after consulting GEIPAN material. These flares occurred when sunlight reflected from the highly polished antenna surfaces of older Iridium communications satellites, producing a brief, brilliant point that could brighten and fade without crossing much of the visible sky. GEIPAN classified the sighting A. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The case matters because it demonstrates that investigation is not always a contest between an insistent witness and a sceptical authority. Witnesses may offer useful hypotheses, especially when they report the time precisely and understand what information is needed. The investigator’s role is then to test whether the proposed satellite, planet or star was actually in the correct place and could reproduce the reported appearance. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Aircraft checks around Nîmes and rural Gard Aircraft are another recurring explanation, particularly in a department containing busy civil and military aviation routes and the Nîmes Garons airport area. At night, an approaching aircraft may seem almost stationary because it is travelling mainly towards the observer. Its landing lights can dominate while the navigation lights remain difficult to see, creating the appearance of one brilliant object hovering or moving unusually slowly. A Nîmes report from 14 October 2015 was classified A as an aircraft. The archive also records a category B aircraft explanation at Saint Ambroix on 18 January 2017, a category A airliner identification at Saint Laurent le Minier on 8 July 2014, and a probable aircraft explanation at Montmirat on 19 August 1993. Taken together, these cases show that aviation checks were useful well beyond the immediate surroundings of Nîmes. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT AMBROIX (30) 18.01.2017 18/01/2017 B Gard Avion 30/08/2017 2017 01 18... [A9] DE NIMES (30) VERS MONTPELLIER (34) 05.0... Published: January 18, 2017 Solving an aircraft case normally requires more than noticing that aeroplanes exist nearby. Investigators must compare the reported direction and movement with plausible flight paths, consider whether the object’s apparent speed could have been distorted by distance, and examine the sequence of visible lights. A white light that later reveals red or green points, changes direction slowly or fades as it turns away is much more compatible with an aircraft than with a fixed astronomical object. The difference between categories A and B is important here. A precise correspondence with a known flight or an unmistakable lighting pattern may justify category A. Where the behaviour resembles an aircraft but no definitive flight record or exact geometry is available, category B is more cautious. It records the most"},{"title":"Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence","url":"/why-gers-ufo-reports-often-change-with/","description":"Gers has a documented history of unusual lights and objects in the sky, but its official record is less dramatic than popular UFO folklore might suggest.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence Gers has a documented history of unusual lights and objects in the sky, but its official record is less dramatic than popular UFO folklore might suggest. Gers has a documented history of unusual lights and objects in the sky, but its official record is less dramatic than popular UFO folklore might suggest. The department’s strongest cases are not confirmed encounters with extraordinary craft. gers Introduction Several cases are especially instructive. A multi witness observation at Saint Lary was eventually matched to the star Capella. A helicopter crew near Gaujan reported lights that did not appear on air traffic systems, yet detailed reconstruction identified flashes from Starlink satellites. By contrast, the 2010 Mirande lights remain unclassified beyond “insufficient information”. Together, these incidents show the difference between a genuinely unexplained report and one that is merely awaiting the right comparison data. What the official record says The main public source for UFO history in Gers is GEIPAN, the unit of the French space agency CNES that collects, investigates, archives and publishes reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its database includes direct testimony, technical questionnaires, photographs, videos, gendarmerie records and investigation reports where these are available. GEIPAN investigates reported observations rather than claims about extraterrestrial visitors, and its remit does not extend to paranormal experiences unconnected with something seen in the sky. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Aerospace Phenomena) sightings... Its classifications are important: A means the phenomenon has been identified. B means it has probably been identified. C means there is not enough dependable information to reach a conclusion. D means the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. These categories do not measure how impressive a story sounds. A vivid case may be placed in C because essential timings, directions or supporting evidence are missing, while an apparently puzzling multi witness event may become A after astronomical or satellite reconstruction. Across France, GEIPAN’s published statistics dated 25 June 2026 covered 3,368 classified cases. About 28 per cent were A, 38.8 per cent B, 30.1 per cent C and 3.1 per cent D. The national pattern therefore provides a useful caution for reading the Gers files: most investigated reports are identified or probably identified, and many of the remainder are unresolved through lack of data rather than because investigators found evidence of an extraordinary machine. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr StatistiquesGEIPAN calcule des statistiques \"dynamiques\", uniquement avec les données liées aux cas classés ・ 3368 Cas Statistiques du... The Gers entries found in the official database follow that broader pattern. They include identified stars, aircraft, meteors, launcher effects and satellite flashes, probable identifications such as balloons, and a number of older or incomplete reports in category C. The available official record does not establish Gers as a major concentration of category D cases. Saint Lary: credible witnesses, ordinary star The Saint Lary observation of 6 August 1991 is one of the clearest lessons in the department’s UFO history because it involved several credible observers, including a gendarme. Shortly after midnight, a couple reported an unusual coloured light. A duty gendarme also saw it and observed it carefully through binoculars from about 00:35 until 01:29. The testimony was reasonably detailed, and GEIPAN explicitly stated that the sincerity and credibility of the three witnesses were not in doubt. Originally treated as an unresolved or insufficiently explained report, the case was later re examined using improved astronomical software and investigative experience. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991 GEIPAN... The reconstruction showed that the light’s position, duration, apparent size and changing colours were consistent with Capella , one of the brightest stars in the night sky. Atmospheric turbulence can make a bright star low in the sky appear to flash, change colour or move slightly, especially when watched for a long period through binoculars. GEIPAN accordingly reclassified the case as A. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991 GEIPAN... This does not mean the witnesses invented what they saw. It shows that witness reliability and interpretation are separate questions. A sincere, careful observer can accurately report an unusual looking light while being mistaken about its distance, movement or physical nature. The Saint Lary file is therefore more valuable than a weaker unresolved account: it demonstrates how later analysis can materially change a case without discrediting those involved. The same explanation appears elsewhere in the Gers record. A report from Solomiac on 16 August 2022 was also classified A and attributed to Capella, underlining how repeatedly the same bright astronomical object can generate different local UFO reports. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de casMARCIAC (32) 09.02.2022, 09/02/2022, B, Gers, Bolide, 12/07/2024, 2022 02 09, voir. SOLOMIAC (32) 16.08.2022, 16/08/2022... Published: February 9, 2022 Gaujan: a helicopter crew and invisible satellites The Gaujan case of 26 November 2024 initially looked stronger than a typical ground based light report. It involved professional aviation witnesses, an airborne viewpoint and checks against ordinary air traffic. During an early morning helicopter flight from Lourdes towards Toulouse, the pilot and flight assistant noticed two or three lights appearing, disappearing and seemingly moving erratically. The assistant checked Flightradar and ADS B data, while air traffic control at Toulouse Blagnac reportedly confirmed that no corresponding aircraft appeared on radar. A doctor and nurse aboard the helicopter also saw the phenomenon after it was pointed out to them. Photographs and video were supplied, giving the case relatively good informational consistency. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. The absence of an aircraft return did not, however, mean the lights were untraceable. GEIPAN reconstructed the sky with astronomical software and examined satellite passages. Investigators concluded that the crew had seen brief reflections from groups of Starlink satellites , sometimes called cluster flares. The geometry was particularly favourable: the Sun was about 38 degrees below the horizon, the lights appeared roughly above its hidden position, and numerous Starlink satellites occupied the relevant part of the sky. The case was classified A. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. Gaujan matters because it removes two common assumptions. First, pilots are not immune to unfamiliar visual effects; their expertise improves the quality of aviation observations but does not give automatic knowledge of every new satellite phenomenon. Second, a light’s absence from air traffic radar does not imply exotic technology. Satellites are outside normal aircraft tracking systems, and brief sunlight reflections may appear highly dynamic even though the satellites themselves follow predictable orbits. The case also reflects a change in modern UFO reporting. Older Gers files frequently concern isolated stars or distant aircraft. Recent cases increasingly involve large satellite constellations, whose reflections can produce strings, clusters or intermittent flashes unlike the familiar passage of a single steady satellite. 1:43 Line of lights in the sky: Starlink satellite train seen over south central Pa. Channel: FOX43 News · Views: 55.2K · Uploaded: June 2023 · Length: 1 minute 43 seconds Open on YouTube Mirande: unresolved because the trail went cold The Mirande report of 26 November 2010 is a useful example of category C: a strange account that could not be investigated well enough to justify either an identification or a genuinely unexplained classification. Between about 23:30 and midnight, two witnesses saw five or six yellow orange lights making rapid, disorderly curves around another apparently fixed light. The moving points reportedly vanished abruptly, while the central light faded more gradually. The witnesses heard no sound. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr MIRANDE (32) 26.11.201026 Nov 2010 — Date d'observation. 26/11/2010; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Gers; Classification. C; Dat... GEIPAN considered several possibilities. Floating lanterns were examined but appeared difficult to reconcile with the reported speed and circling motion. A pyrotechnic or electrical display was also considered, although investigators found no evidence of a major local celebration"},{"title":"Solved Cases","url":"/solved-cases-9bedff/","description":"The Saint-Lary and Gaujan cases show how stars and satellite reflections can look extraordinary until timing and sky position are reconstructed.","breadcrumb":"Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence","level":2,"text":"Solved Cases How Gers's Most Convincing UFO Sightings Were Solved Gers UFOs Solved Cases Solved Cases How Gers's Most Convincing UFO Sightings Were Solved The Saint Lary and Gaujan cases show how stars and satellite reflections can look extraordinary until timing and sky position are reconstructed. The Saint Lary and Gaujan cases show how stars and satellite reflections can look extraordinary until timing and sky position are reconstructed. mechanism Saint Lary and the misidentified star Capella Gaujan and the rise of Starlink flare reports Why credible witnesses can still misread the sky gers solved cases Introduction Some of Gers’s most convincing UFO reports were solved not by questioning the honesty of the witnesses, but by reconstructing exactly where they were, when they looked and what should have been visible in that part of the sky. At Saint Lary in 1991, three witnesses — including a gendarme who watched through binoculars for nearly an hour — mistook the bright star Capella for an unusual coloured light. Near Gaujan in 2024, a helicopter crew and medical passengers saw points of light that appeared and vanished without corresponding aircraft or radar contacts. Orbital calculations instead placed numerous Starlink satellites in the precise region where sunlight could make them flare. These cases matter because they show what “explained” should mean in the Gers record. An identification is strongest when it accounts for the reported direction, timing, appearance and behaviour together. Credible testimony remains valuable, but it describes an experience; astronomical and satellite reconstruction can reveal what produced it. Saint Lary and the star that looked alive Shortly after midnight on 6 August 1991, a couple in Saint Lary contacted the gendarmerie at Jegun about a strange light in the sky. The duty gendarme could also see it from about 00:35 and observed it carefully with binoculars until 01:29. The resulting file was unusually substantial for a simple light report: three witnesses, a prolonged observation and a relatively precise description. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, states that the witnesses’ sincerity and credibility were never in doubt. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991; Date d'observation. 06/08/1991; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Gers... The object appeared as a luminous point whose colour changed. Watching it for so long, and confirming it through binoculars, made an ordinary mistake seem less likely. Yet those details ultimately supported the conventional explanation. The light remained in broadly the same area for more than an hour, behaved like a distant point source and displayed colours consistent with a bright star seen through disturbed air. When the old file was reassessed using improved astronomical software, Capella occupied the relevant part of the sky. GEIPAN changed the case from its earlier inconclusive status to category A, meaning identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991; Date d'observation. 06/08/1991; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Gers... Why Capella can appear extraordinary Capella is one of the brightest stars visible from the Northern Hemisphere. When a bright star is comparatively low in the sky, its light passes through a longer and more turbulent path in the atmosphere. Small changes in air density continually bend the incoming light, altering its apparent brightness, colour and position. This effect, called scintillation, is stronger near the horizon and can create rapid red, green, blue or white flashes. [International Cloud Atlas] cloudatlas.wmo.int Other factors being equal, scintillation is more pronounced for longer paths traversed by the light through the atmosphere... Binoculars do not necessarily remove the illusion. They make the coloured fluctuations easier to see, while offering few distance clues because a star remains an unresolved point. A witness may therefore perceive a pulsing or rotating object rather than a fixed astronomical source. Slight involuntary eye movements and the absence of nearby reference points can also make a stationary light seem to wander. The Saint Lary solution did not depend merely on saying that the object “looked like a star”. Several parts of the account converged: Sky position: Capella was in the reported viewing area. Duration: a star could remain visible throughout the prolonged observation. Appearance: scintillation accounted for the changing colours and brightness. Angular form: the phenomenon remained essentially a luminous point rather than showing a reliably observed structure. Behaviour over time: it did not depart, approach or cross the sky as an aircraft would. GEIPAN’s conclusion explicitly separated perception from interpretation. The witnesses really did see a striking light; the mistake lay in assigning unfamiliar behaviour to it. Night time surprise, fatigue and sustained attention made the ordinary source appear exceptional. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991SAINT LARY (32) 06.08.1991; Date d'observation. 06/08/1991; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Gers... The identification is also supported by a later Gers case. A report from Solomiac on 16 August 2022 was likewise classified as Capella, illustrating that the same bright star can generate apparently unusual observations decades apart when its position and atmospheric conditions are favourable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casListe des cas revisités; BOULAUR (32) 24.03.1984, 24/03/1984, C · Gers, Manque d'informations fiables; SAINT LARY... Gaujan and the Starlink flashes beyond radar The Gaujan case of 26 November 2024 initially appeared more difficult to dismiss. During an early morning helicopter flight from Lourdes towards Toulouse, the pilot and flight assistant saw two or three luminous points appearing and disappearing in an apparently irregular fashion. The observation continued for roughly five minutes. A doctor and nurse travelling aboard the medical flight later saw the lights after the crew pointed them out. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO] GAUJAN (32) 26.11.2024Observation depuis un hélicoptère d'apparition et disparition de plusieurs points lumineux dans le cie... The crew treated the sighting as a possible aviation matter. The assistant checked Flightradar and Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast data, while air traffic control at Toulouse Blagnac confirmed that no corresponding radar target was present. Photographs and video were also supplied. Those checks strengthened the report as evidence that something visible was present, but they did not establish that the lights were flying within controlled airspace. Satellites would not appear as nearby aircraft on the systems being consulted. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO] GAUJAN (32) 26.11.2024Observation depuis un hélicoptère d'apparition et disparition de plusieurs points lumineux dans le cie... GEIPAN reconstructed the scene with Stellarium astronomy software and satellite orbital data. The lights were seen at about ten degrees above the horizon, vertically aligned with the Sun’s position beneath it. At the time, the Sun was approximately 38 degrees below the horizon. Numerous Starlink satellites were passing through the same restricted area of sky. That combination matched a recognised phenomenon known as Starlink cluster flares. GEIPAN classified the sighting as category A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO] GAUJAN (32) 26.11.2024Observation depuis un hélicoptère d'apparition et disparition de plusieurs points lumineux dans le cie... Why the satellites seemed to appear from nowhere A Starlink flare is not the same thing as the familiar “train” of newly launched satellites moving in a line. Satellites already distributed through their operational orbits can reflect sunlight towards an observer for only a few moments. As different spacecraft pass through the favourable reflection geometry, one point brightens, fades and is replaced by another nearby. To an observer, the lights may seem to jump, circle, reverse direction or repeatedly materialise in one small patch of otherwise dark sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The geometry is counter intuitive because the observer may be in full darkness while satellites hundreds of kilometres above Earth remain illuminated by the Sun. Research into unusually bright Starlink flares has shown that reflections from the spacecraft structure can become bright enough to attract airline crews’ attention and be reported as unidentified phenomena. The effect is particularly associated with observations near the horizon and in the direction of the Sun below it. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Extreme Flaring of Starlink Satellites arXiv Extreme Flaring of Starlink Satellites GEIPAN had begun receiving numerous reports of such lights from long and medium haul pilots from"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-00ec40/","description":"Mirande shows why a case can remain unclassified without becoming evidence of an extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"Why Gers UFO Reports Often Change With Better Evidence","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases When Does an Unsolved Gers UFO Case Mean Anything? Gers UFOs Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases When Does an Unsolved Gers UFO Case Mean Anything? Mirande shows why a case can remain unclassified without becoming evidence of an extraordinary craft. Mirande shows why a case can remain unclassified without becoming evidence of an extraordinary craft. dataset evidence What witnesses reported over Mirande Why GEIPAN classified the case as insufficient information The difference between unexplained and poorly documented gers unresolved cases Introduction An unsolved UFO report in Gers does not automatically point to an extraordinary craft. In many cases, it means that the evidence was never strong enough to test ordinary explanations properly. The Mirande sighting of 26 November 2010 is the clearest local example: two witnesses described several yellow orange lights moving rapidly around a stationary light, yet GEIPAN ultimately placed the case in category C — unidentified because reliable information was insufficient. [Geipan] geipan.fr MIRANDE (32) 26.11.2010 GEIPAN... That distinction matters. A category C file records a limit of knowledge, not a finding that aircraft, balloons, lanterns, astronomical objects or human activity have been eliminated. Mirande remains open because the report reached investigators too late for effective local enquiries, not because an exhaustive investigation uncovered evidence of exotic technology. Read carefully, the case is less a mystery solved halfway than an investigation that could never properly begin. What witnesses reported over Mirande Between about 11.30 pm and midnight on 26 November 2010, two witnesses in Mirande reported seeing five or six points of yellow orange light. They described the lights as similar in apparent size, silent and moving in fast, irregular curves around another light that appeared fixed. The moving lights reportedly went out abruptly, while the central light faded more gradually. [Geipan] geipan.fr MIRANDE (32) 26.11.2010 GEIPAN... The account is striking because it describes an organised looking arrangement rather than a lone point crossing the sky. Apparent movement around a fixed centre can suggest coordination, especially when the observer has no visible horizon, known distance or reference object against which to judge the scene. Yet the published record contains no instrumental measurement of the lights’ altitude, distance, speed or separation. “Fast” therefore describes how the movement looked to the witnesses, not a calculated velocity. GEIPAN considered several ordinary possibilities. Sky lanterns were discussed, but one witness believed the reported movements were too fast and too tightly centred on the fixed light. A pyrotechnic or electrical display associated with a celebration was also considered. Investigators found no indication of a major festival in Mirande that night, but that did not establish that no private event, small display or other local activity had occurred. The official summary also rejected a simple natural explanation on the basis of the complexity of the reported movements. [Geipan] geipan.fr MIRANDE (32) 26.11.2010 GEIPAN... These points narrowed the obvious suggestions without resolving the event. The lantern hypothesis was challenged mainly by remembered motion, while the entertainment display hypothesis lacked timely local confirmation. Neither was decisively disproved by photographs, wind reconstruction, launch records, multiple independent sight lines or physical traces. Why GEIPAN could not classify it as genuinely unexplained GEIPAN’s categories are intended to separate cases that have been identified, probably identified, left unusable through weak evidence, and still unexplained after a sufficiently strong investigation. Category C applies when the available information is too limited or unreliable to support a conclusion. Category D is reserved for reports that retain significant unexplained features despite having enough dependable evidence for serious analysis. [Geipan] geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN... Under GEIPAN’s current approach, investigators assess two linked qualities: Residual strangeness: how poorly the best known explanation fits after investigation. Consistency: how much dependable, objective information supports the reported details. Consistency depends on matters such as the number and independence of witnesses, the precision of their answers, internal coherence, photographs or recordings, and whether the phenomenon can be located accurately enough to test competing hypotheses. GEIPAN’s methodology expressly states that a case becomes unusable when its apparent strangeness is not matched by sufficiently strong information. [Geipan] geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN... Mirande failed chiefly on consistency. GEIPAN said the events were already too old by the time they could be examined properly. Investigators could no longer conduct a meaningful search for other witnesses or mount an effective local enquiry. The case was therefore classified C for lack of information. [Geipan] geipan.fr MIRANDE (32) 26.11.2010 GEIPAN... That conclusion is narrower than “no explanation exists”. It means that the remaining hypotheses could not be verified or rejected with confidence. GEIPAN’s formal procedure recognises exactly this problem: when an interpretation remains plausible but the evidence cannot test it, the case is insufficiently consistent. Stronger cases require confirmed location, corroboration and enough information to challenge all reasonable explanations. [Geipan] geipan.fr Procedure classif PAN Procedure classif PAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the investigation was missing The witnesses supplied a narrative, but a narrative alone cannot establish the physical behaviour of distant lights. Several missing pieces prevented the Mirande account from becoming a robust case. Precise geometry was absent. Investigators needed measured directions, elevations and changes in position from identifiable observation points. Without them, it is impossible to determine whether the lights were close together, widely separated or merely aligned from one viewpoint. Distance was unknown. A nearby light moving slowly can appear faster than a distant light covering a larger path. With no known distance, descriptions of rapid acceleration or curved motion cannot be converted into meaningful performance figures. Independent corroboration was not secured in time. Two witnesses are more useful than one only when their accounts can be compared and their viewing positions established. Additional observers elsewhere in or around Mirande might have provided intersecting lines of sight, revealed a wider local display or contradicted the apparent central arrangement. Contemporary local checks were no longer practical. A prompt enquiry could have asked residents, event organisers, emergency services and nearby businesses about lanterns, fireworks, illuminated balloons, advertising devices or private celebrations. Once sufficient time had passed, memories faded and short lived records may no longer have existed. No instrumental record fixed the appearance. A photograph or video would not necessarily have solved the case, but it could have preserved angular movement, brightness changes and timing. Even then, useful analysis would have required metadata, a known camera position and reference points. These are not unusually demanding standards invented to dismiss the report. They reflect the basic data problem recognised in modern official investigations elsewhere: analysis is repeatedly hindered when observations lack multiple measurements, sensor information, metadata and baseline data. NASA’s independent study on unidentified anomalous phenomena reached the same broad conclusion, stressing that poor calibration and incomplete supporting information make firm assessment difficult. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science Independent Study Team Report NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportSeptember 13, 2023 — At present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, th... Published: September 13, 2023 Unexplained is not the same as poorly documented The word “unexplained” is often used too loosely. It can describe at least three very different situations. A report may be temporarily unidentified because nobody has yet compared it with flight paths, astronomical positions, satellite activity or weather data. It may be poorly documented , meaning that several explanations remain possible but the evidence cannot discriminate between them. Or it may be genuinely resistant to explanation , supported by dependable information"},{"title":"Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter","url":"/why-girondes-strangest-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Gironde has a substantial but uneven UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter Gironde has a substantial but uneven UFO history. Gironde has a substantial but uneven UFO history. Its best known case is the June 1978 Gujan Mestras incident, in which several witnesses reported an orange, oval light accompanied by a low noise and apparent failures in street lighting. gironde Introduction That outcome is typical of Gironde’s wider record. The department has produced reports involving lights, aircraft, astronomical objects, balloons, meteors and photographic artefacts, but many have eventually been explained or judged too poorly documented to assess. The result is not a catalogue of proven exotic craft. It is a useful local study in how unusual sightings arise, how official investigators reconstruct them, and why a memorable experience can remain emotionally convincing even after its evidential value has weakened. The Gujan Mestras case of June 1978 The most important Gironde incident occurred around midnight on 18–19 June 1978 in and around Gujan Mestras, near Arcachon Bay. Several people reported hearing a strong, dull noise before seeing an orange luminous phenomenon described as oval. According to GEIPAN’s summary, witnesses watched it above the built up area as faults affected the local street lighting network. The light then appeared to rise and gradually disappear. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978Le 19 juin 1978 entre 23h et 0h45 plusieurs personnes entendent unbruit sourd et fort puis observent u... The case matters because it contains several features that normally make a UFO report more interesting than a fleeting light in the sky: there were multiple witnesses; the observation lasted long enough for people to describe movement and appearance; sound was reported as well as light; electrical disturbances were said to coincide with the phenomenon; statements were collected through an official police or gendarmerie process; witnesses were recorded not only in Gujan Mestras but also in La Réole, elsewhere in Gironde. [Geipan] geipan.fr GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978; Date d'observation. 18/06/1978; Région. Aquitaine; Département. Giro... These elements create the impression of a physically present object interacting with its surroundings. They also explain why the case continues to appear in French UFO chronologies and secondary accounts. But each feature requires caution. A shared time window does not by itself prove that every witness saw the same object, and a reported lighting failure does not establish that the light caused it. Without detailed electricity network records, precise timing and a demonstrated mechanism, the electrical effect remains a correlation reported by witnesses rather than verified physical evidence. There is also a small dating complication. GEIPAN’s case title refers to 19 June, while its database gives the observation date as 18 June, reflecting an event that crossed midnight. That is not a serious contradiction, but it illustrates why the original documents and times matter more than a simplified case label. [Geipan] geipan.fr GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978; Date d'observation. 18/06/1978; Région. Aquitaine; Département. Giro... Most importantly, GEIPAN now classifies the incident as B rather than D. In the agency’s system, category B indicates that the phenomenon is probably identified; category D is reserved for cases that remain unexplained after investigation. The public summary calls the phenomenon identified but does not state the exact proposed cause. That makes Gujan Mestras neither a clean debunking nor a strong unresolved case. It is better described as a striking multi witness report whose present official status is probably explained, but whose published explanation is frustratingly incomplete. [Geipan] geipan.fr GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978GUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978; Date d'observation. 18/06/1978; Région. Aquitaine; Département. Giro... What the official Gironde record actually contains France is unusual in maintaining a public state archive of unidentified aerospace reports. GEIPAN, created within CNES in 1977, gathers witness statements submitted online or passed through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities. Investigators compare testimony with astronomical data, weather, aircraft movements, satellite re entries and other possible causes before publishing a classification. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... This system is important for understanding Gironde because a database entry is not an endorsement of an extraordinary object. It records a reported experience and the result of an assessment. GEIPAN says that, nationally, most sufficiently documented cases are explained through misidentification or perception error, while a much smaller proportion remain genuinely unexplained. A large additional group cannot be assessed because the available information is inadequate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Aerospace Phenomena) sightings... Gironde’s entries illustrate all three outcomes. Probable or definite identifications. A report made from the A63 south of Bordeaux on 7 October 2013 was classified B and attributed to an airliner. An aviation case over Bordeaux on 14 April 2010 was also classified B, with Venus identified as the cause. A photograph taken in Bordeaux on 12 May 2009 was judged to contain a biological photographic artefact rather than an anomalous aircraft. [Geipan] geipan.fr A63] de BORDEAUX (33) VERS BAYONNE (64) 07.10.2013 07/10/2013 B Gironde Avion de ligne 18/11/2013 2013 10 07... Published: October 7, 2013 More recent examples follow the same pattern. A January 2022 report from Sainte Hélène was classified A as a bright meteor. A January 2025 observation at Artigues près Bordeaux was classified B as a Mylar balloon. A report from Porchères in August 2024 had also received a B classification by January 2026. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Cases weakened by missing information. Bordeaux has also generated reports that GEIPAN could not reconstruct reliably. An observation dated 5 April 2010 was placed in category C because dependable information was lacking. The same classification appears for cases from Carbon Blanc in 2015, Langon in 2011, Les Peintures in 2009 and Latresne in 1995. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Category C is easily misunderstood. It does not mean that investigators found an inexplicable craft. It means they could not reach a sound conclusion, often because the report arrived late, contained no independent witness, lacked an original photograph or gave insufficient direction, duration and angular information. Such cases remain unidentified in an everyday sense, but they are weak evidence because almost no hypothesis can be tested properly. Reports solved through careful reconstruction. The Carcans case of 27 September 2011 shows the value of detailed checking. An amateur astronomer saw an unexpectedly bright, apparently stationary light low in the east and used a nearby tree as a reference point. GEIPAN’s investigation drew on the witness questionnaire, astronomical reconstruction and local geography rather than treating the witness’s experience as self explanatory. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete329 Compte rendu enquete329 This type of report is particularly instructive. Familiarity with the night sky can improve a witness’s description, but it does not make misidentification impossible. Bright planets near the horizon can appear unusually large, unstable or mobile because of atmospheric distortion and involuntary eye movements. The same principle applies to pilots, police officers and military personnel: professional experience can strengthen parts of a statement without turning visual perception into an infallible measuring instrument. Bordeaux : la soucoupe volante a été transférée au Bassins à flot Channel: Sud Ouest Open on YouTube Bordeaux aviation does not produce a clear radar mystery Gironde contains one of south west France’s major aviation centres. Bordeaux Mérignac Airport, busy commercial routes, military activity and aircraft approaching or leaving the region all create opportunities for unusual lights to be seen under unfamiliar angles. That setting may sound promising to readers looking for radar confirmed UFOs, but the accessible official record does not reveal a landmark Gironde case supported by both strong radar data and a corresponding unknown visual target. Instead, aviation often supplies the explanation. GEIPAN’s A63 case was attributed to an airliner, while the April 2010 Bordeaux aviation report was linked to"},{"title":"Official Record","url":"/official-record-634f2c/","description":"Gironde's archive is dominated by aircraft, planets, meteors, balloons and weak reports rather than a clear body of unexplained craft.","breadcrumb":"Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter","level":2,"text":"Official Record What Gironde's UFO Archive Really Reveals Gironde UFOs Official Record Official Record What Gironde's UFO Archive Really Reveals Gironde's archive is dominated by aircraft, planets, meteors, balloons and weak reports rather than a clear body of unexplained craft. Gironde's archive is dominated by aircraft, planets, meteors, balloons and weak reports rather than a clear body of unexplained craft. dataset evidence Explained cases involving aircraft, planets and meteors Why category C reports remain weak evidence How detailed reconstruction changes a sighting gironde official record Introduction Gironde ’s official UFO archive does not reveal a coherent sequence of mysterious craft operating over Bordeaux, Arcachon Bay or the Médoc. It reveals something more ordinary, but more useful: repeated encounters with aircraft, planets, meteors, satellites, balloons, lanterns, drones and visual effects that initially looked unfamiliar to witnesses. A smaller group of reports remains uncertain chiefly because investigators lacked dependable information, not because the evidence pointed strongly towards an extraordinary vehicle. That distinction is central to reading the public archive maintained by GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES. Its records preserve what witnesses reported, but they also show what happened when investigators checked directions, times, weather, astronomical positions, air traffic and the behaviour of familiar objects. In Gironde, those reconstructions usually reduce rather than strengthen the mystery. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... What the classifications really mean GEIPAN’s archive is an investigation database, not a list of confirmed anomalies. Category A is used when a phenomenon is identified with high confidence. Category B means that an ordinary explanation is considered probable, although not established as completely as in an A case. Category C denotes a report that cannot be assessed adequately because reliable information is missing. Only category D is intended for cases that remain unexplained despite sufficiently substantial investigation. This matters because “unidentified” can describe two very different situations. A category D report has resisted explanation after the available evidence has been examined. A category C report is unresolved because the evidence is too weak, incomplete or inconsistent to support a sound conclusion. Treating both as equivalent would make a poorly remembered light as significant as a well documented event that survived detailed checking. The wider national figures reinforce this point. CNES reports that roughly a quarter of archived phenomena are clearly identified and about two fifths probably identified. Nearly a third remain unidentified for lack of data, while only a small minority remain unexplained after investigation. Gironde’s records broadly fit that pattern: identified or probably identified reports form the visible centre of the archive, while weak category C files should not be mistaken for a hidden body of strong unexplained evidence. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Aircraft, planets and meteors explain many reports The most revealing Gironde cases are often those in which an observation sounded strange until investigators reconstructed the viewing conditions. In October 2013, a motorist travelling from Bordeaux towards Bayonne saw a white point apparently moving south, disappearing and then seeming to return north. The witness noticed neither navigation lights nor an obvious aircraft shape. GEIPAN checked the position of the low evening Sun and data from flight tracking records. It concluded that the witness had probably seen two aircraft crossing at different moments, their bodies brightly illuminated while their wings and normal identifying features were difficult to distinguish. What felt like one object reversing direction was more plausibly two separate aeroplanes. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[A63] de BORDEAUX (33) VERS BAYONNE (64) 07.10.20137 Oct 2013 — Type de phénomène. Avion de ligne. Etrangeté. 0.38. Documents. Docu... A May 2018 report from Mios shows how an astronomical object can appear active rather than fixed. The witness filmed a bright light that seemed to move from side to side, rise and fall, approach and retreat, and change colour. GEIPAN matched its position closely to Jupiter, then explained the reported motion through autokinesis—the tendency of a stationary point of light to appear to move when viewed against a dark, featureless background. Atmospheric turbulence accounted for the colour and brightness changes, while cloud ended the observation. The case was classified A. [Geipan] geipan.fr MIOS (33) 03.05.20183 May 2018 — Département Gironde Classification A. Type de phénomène Jupiter Etrangeté 0.20 Consistance 0.45... Published: May 2018 Other Gironde entries identify Venus in reports from Pauillac, Gujan Mestras and Saint Christoly Médoc. These cases are not trivial simply because the answer is a planet. Venus is exceptionally bright, is often seen near the horizon and can appear to pulse, drift or change colour through haze and atmospheric disturbance. Without fixed reference points, witnesses can also misjudge whether it is stationary, approaching or moving alongside them. The archive’s repeated planetary identifications therefore demonstrate a recurring mechanism rather than a series of unrelated mistakes. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Fast, dramatic events also enter the system. A January 2022 report from Sainte Hélène was classified as a fireball—a particularly bright meteor—while a 1991 set of observations from Gironde and the Hautes Pyrénées was judged consistent with atmospheric re entry. Such phenomena can produce multiple lights, fragmentation, changing colours and apparently level motion across the sky. Their brief duration leaves witnesses little opportunity to estimate distance or speed, making them easy to interpret as structured objects travelling at low altitude. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) GIRONDE, HAUTES PYRENEES (33, 65) 10.12.1991Département. Gironde. Classification. B. Date de mise a jour. 17/01/2012. Type de... The same pattern extends to more recent technology and aerial clutter. The public Gironde listings include cases attributed to a satellite, rocket components, a helicopter, a condensation trail, Mylar balloons and a probable drone near Bazas in January 2025. The changing mixture reflects what is present in the sky at a particular period. Older reports may be dominated by aircraft and astronomical bodies; newer ones increasingly include consumer drones, satellite trains and lightweight illuminated objects. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lanterns and balloons create convincing “craft” Small airborne objects account for a conspicuous part of the modern Gironde archive. Reports involving Chinese lanterns appear at Andernos les Bains, Le Porge, Port Sainte Foy et Ponchapt, Saint Sulpice et Cameyrac, Mios and other locations. Their behaviour readily produces UFO like descriptions: orange or red lights, silence, slow movement, loose formations and disappearance without an obvious descent. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The June 2018 Andernos les Bains case is a useful example. Witnesses saw groups of bright red lights moving slowly and silently before vanishing behind trees and houses. Investigators compared the apparent route with the wind and found the behaviour compatible with sky lanterns. The conclusion was category B rather than A because no lantern was recovered and no launch was directly documented, but the objects’ colour, speed, grouping and wind borne direction supported a probable conventional explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This illustrates why category B should not be translated as “still genuinely mysterious”. It often means that investigators found an explanation that fits the observation well, but cannot prove it through physical recovery, an identified operator or an independently recorded launch. In practical terms, the remaining uncertainty may concern which lantern, aircraft or drone was involved—not whether the report demonstrates an unknown form of technology. Reflective balloons produce a different illusion. A Mylar balloon can rotate, flash sunlight, appear metallic and alternate between bright and dark surfaces. Its irregular motion may suggest control, especially when distance is underestimated. Gironde’s archive includes Mylar balloon identifications at Mérignac and Saint Médard d’Eyrans, showing how an object with no propulsion can look manoeuvrable when seen briefly and"},{"title":"Gujan Mestras","url":"/gujan-mestras/","description":"Gironde's most famous sighting combined multiple witnesses, noise and reported power failures, yet GEIPAN now considers it probably explained.","breadcrumb":"Why Gironde's Strangest UFO Cases Still Matter","level":2,"text":"Gujan Mestras Was Gironde's Landmark UFO Case Ever Solved? Gujan Mestras Gujan Mestras Was Gironde's Landmark UFO Case Ever Solved? Gironde's most famous sighting combined multiple witnesses, noise and reported power failures, yet GEIPAN now considers it probably explained. Gironde's most famous sighting combined multiple witnesses, noise and reported power failures, yet GEIPAN now considers it probably explained. event time window What witnesses reported on 18 19 June 1978 How strong were the sound and lighting claims? Why GEIPAN changed the case to category B gironde gujan mestras Introduction The Gujan Mestras incident of 18–19 June 1978 is Gironde ’s landmark UFO case because it appeared to offer more than a distant light: several witnesses described an orange luminous form, a heavy rumbling sound and a temporary failure of public lighting. Early retellings treated those elements as evidence that a physical object had passed over the town and affected its surroundings. The official position is now much less dramatic. GEIPAN, the investigation unit of the French space agency CNES, classifies the case as B , meaning probably identified, and summarises it as a probable meteorological phenomenon associated with threatening storm conditions. Yet the explanation remains disputed because the published record does not identify one precise mechanism that accounts convincingly for every reported detail. Gujan Mestras is therefore not a strong officially unexplained case, but neither is it a textbook identification supported by decisive measurements. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978 GEIPAN... What witnesses reported on 18–19 June 1978 The central observation occurred around midnight in the built up area of Gujan Mestras, near Arcachon Bay. GEIPAN places the overall reporting window between approximately 23:00 and 00:45, although individual statements refer to different moments within that period. The date is consequently shown as 18 June in the database while the case title uses 19 June: the events crossed midnight rather than occurring on two separate nights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978 GEIPAN... Two young men were near the town hall when, according to their gendarmerie statements, they heard a loud, dull noise apparently coming from the direction of the municipal stadium. They then saw an oval phenomenon emitting orange, red and green colours, with white lights around it. Both estimated that it was roughly 300 metres high, although that figure was an unaided judgement made at night and cannot be treated as a measured altitude. They said it appeared to move towards them, turned and then disappeared. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Their accounts are strikingly similar, but they were not fully independent: the witnesses were together, reacted together and were interviewed on the same morning. This makes their agreement meaningful as evidence of a shared experience, but less powerful than two observers reporting separately from widely separated positions. Their estimates of an object four or five metres across also depended entirely on an assumed distance. A light of unknown size at an unknown range cannot yield a reliable physical diameter from naked eye observation alone. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUJAN MESTRAS (33) 19.06.1978 T1 GEIPAN... A separate group travelling by car reported an orange luminous ball in the direction of La Réole, several dozen kilometres east of Gujan Mestras. One driver described seeing it repeatedly during the journey: it seemed nearly stationary at first, later moved slowly, briefly dimmed and then became bright again. He reported a covered, stormy sky, no visible stars, no trail and no accompanying sound. GEIPAN concluded that there was insufficient information to establish that the La Réole light and the Gujan Mestras phenomenon were the same event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That distinction is important. Combining all five witnesses creates the impression of a large phenomenon observed across Gironde. Separating the reports produces a more cautious picture: one local episode involving sound, coloured light and a lighting failure, plus a distant observation of an orange light under cloudy conditions. Similar colour and approximate timing do not establish a common source when directions, durations and exact times are uncertain. How strong were the sound and lighting claims? The reported interruption of street lighting is the feature that made Gujan Mestras famous. The two principal witnesses said that lighting around the town hall and nearby streets went out for roughly three minutes and returned after the luminous phenomenon had disappeared. In popular retellings, this sequence became an electrical effect caused by a passing craft. The original statements, however, establish only that the events appeared close together in time. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. No publicly available electricity network record demonstrates that an external object disrupted the supply. Nor does the file present instrument readings, damaged equipment, voltage data or an engineering examination proving electromagnetic interference. The failure may have involved the power network, the local lighting controls or only part of the town. Without such records, “the object switched off the lights” is an interpretation rather than an observed fact. A sceptical reconstruction published in Les OVNI du CNES notes that the street lamps were reportedly controlled by photoelectric sensors. A sufficiently bright flash could therefore have caused the system to behave as though daylight had arrived, switching lamps off until the sensors readjusted. The authors criticised later energy calculations because they assumed that the light and the switching event were causally connected, then used uncertain witness estimates of distance and brightness to calculate the supposed output of the phenomenon. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique Microsoft Word Observatoire zététiqueMicrosoft Word OvniDuCnes chapitre6.doc... This sensor hypothesis is plausible but not proven. The available file does not show which cells controlled which lamps, their switching threshold, whether they responded simultaneously or whether a lightning flash could reproduce the reported three minute interruption. A mundane electrical fault during stormy weather remains possible as well. The central evidential weakness is therefore not the absence of a conceivable explanation, but the absence of technical records capable of choosing between them. The sound evidence has similar limitations. Witnesses described a heavy or muffled rumble and associated it with the luminous form. Thunder is an obvious candidate because storm conditions were reported, but the file does not provide a timed sequence precise enough to match a lightning flash with a delayed thunderclap. Other possible sources—including aircraft, vehicles or activity near the stadium—were not conclusively eliminated in the surviving public material. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Sound and light can readily be fused into a single event when they occur close together, particularly during a surprising night time observation. GEIPAN itself now stresses that human reports combine what was physically perceived with interpretation, emotion, memory and familiar cultural images. That does not imply dishonesty: witnesses may describe an experience sincerely while assigning one cause to several unrelated stimuli. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMISSIONS, METHODES ET RESULTATS GEIPAN GEIPANMISSIONS, METHODES ET RESULTATS GEIPAN Rendez vous d’Athéna 6 Phénomènes Aérospatiaux Non identifiés Channel: AED SNC IHEDN Open on YouTube Could it have been an aircraft, meteor or storm effect? Several ordinary explanations have been proposed, but none fits every detail without difficulty. An aircraft or helicopter could produce coloured navigation lights, a strong sound and the impression of approach followed by a turn. Gujan Mestras also lies in an aviation rich area, with Bordeaux Mérignac airport to the north east and Cazaux air base and military ranges to the south. A bright landing light seen through broken cloud can appear larger, change intensity abruptly and obscure the structure carrying it. The public case file, however, contains no confirmed flight identification, radar correlation or military activity record tied to the reported time. [Observatoire zététique] zetetique.fr Observatoire zététique Microsoft Word Observatoire zététiqueMicrosoft Word OvniDuCnes chapitre6.doc... A bright meteor or fireball could explain an orange or reddish luminous form, apparent flames and possibly a delayed rumbling noise. It might also momentarily affect light sensitive controls if exceptionally bright. The sceptical analysis rejected a straightforward fireball explanation,"},{"title":"Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter","url":"/why-haut-rhins-strangest-sightings-still/","description":"Haut-Rhin has a long record of unusual lights and objects reported over Mulhouse, Colmar, the Rhine plain and the Vosges foothills, but only one publicly...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter Haut Rhin has a long record of unusual lights and objects reported over Mulhouse, Colmar, the Rhine plain and the Vosges foothills, but only one publicly... Haut Rhin has a long record of unusual lights and objects reported over Mulhouse, Colmar, the Rhine plain and the Vosges foothills, but only one publicly documented case stands out as a genuinely difficult official file: the six minute Huningue sighting of 26 May 1980. haut rhin Introduction This makes Haut Rhin more instructive than sensational. Its history shows how an apparently extraordinary sighting can emerge from an ordinary but visually complex environment: busy cross border airspace, an international airport, mountain horizons, clear rural viewpoints and changing aerial technology. It also shows the difference between an unresolved case and evidence of an extraordinary craft. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, has left Huningue unexplained, but it has not treated that classification as proof of an alien or otherwise exotic origin. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... Huningue in 1980: the department’s strongest case At about 7 p.m. on 26 May 1980, two people walking near Huningue reported seeing an apparently metallic object in a clear sky. One witness, described in the official file as an aviation engineer and navigator, said it had a round base and a silver coloured dome. Its darker underside appeared to show an intermittent reddish glow. The object seemed to hover roughly 200 to 300 metres above the ground, oscillate around its axis and then climb through successive levels before disappearing into cloud. The observation reportedly lasted about six minutes, with no sound or condensation trail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... The witness’s aviation background gives the account more interest than a brief anonymous light report, but it does not remove the central uncertainties. No photograph, instrument recording tied conclusively to the object or independent group of witnesses is published with the case. Estimates of size, height and distance made against an open sky are also vulnerable to error because observers usually lack fixed reference points. The most frequently repeated supporting detail is a radar return recorded at Basel–Mulhouse Airport. GEIPAN’s summary is careful: an echo was present, but it was buried among private and general aviation traffic and was therefore of little investigative value. No corresponding report was obtained from France, Germany or the other nearby border areas. GEIPAN ultimately classified the case as category D, meaning that it remained unexplained after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... That radar detail matters, but less than it may first appear. It does not establish that controllers tracked the witnesses’ object, nor that the echo performed the movements described from the ground. In crowded airspace, an unattributed return is not automatically anomalous. The file is therefore best understood as a reasonably substantial unresolved sighting rather than a confirmed radar visual encounter. The 1954 wave reached Haut Rhin Haut Rhin participated in the great French “flying saucer” wave of autumn 1954, when newspapers across the country carried a rapid succession of reports involving discs, luminous cigars and supposed landings. The surviving departmental accounts are culturally important, but most are preserved through press cuttings or later catalogues rather than detailed contemporary investigations. A report from Rixheim dated 28 September described an orange, elongated object travelling at very high speed towards Mulhouse from the direction of Basel. The unnamed witness compared it both to a comet and to a neon tube. There were no photographs, measurements or additional named witnesses. The regional ALSACAT catalogue judges that a meteor is possible, while stressing that the information is too limited for certainty. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOs at close sight: ALSACAT, UFOs in Alsace, France Case of Rixheim, on September 28, 1954... Published: September 28, 1954 Reports published around 20 October were more dramatic. Near Issenheim, Lucien Fiesch was said to have encountered a roughly spherical object apparently hovering close to the ground. At Guebwiller, Pierre Ackermann and two schoolchildren reportedly saw a bright disc moving silently at an estimated altitude of about 400 metres. A further account from Soultzmatt concerned an elongated object travelling at high speed. These stories appeared alongside numerous French reports using similar imagery, including discs, glowing lower sections and silent rapid departures. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 20 octobre, Guebwiller, Haut Rhin... Their concentration in the same period makes them significant to the department’s folklore, but not necessarily to their physical reliability. Newspaper competition, repeated visual language and widespread public discussion of “saucers” can shape how ambiguous lights are noticed and described. The Rixheim account’s resemblance to a fast meteor is particularly important: a natural object can appear elongated, orange and extraordinarily swift while remaining visible for only a short time. The 1954 material therefore documents a real social and reporting wave in Haut Rhin. It does not establish that one type of unknown machine was repeatedly operating over the department. Some accounts may reflect astronomical events or aircraft; others are simply too abbreviated to assess. Why the local sky produces persuasive misidentifications Haut Rhin contains several ingredients that make honest mistakes both likely and convincing. The southern department lies beneath busy international air routes and includes Basel–Mulhouse Airport. Aircraft viewed head on can appear stationary for several minutes, while landing lights may seem unusually bright or disappear abruptly when a plane turns. The official Burnhaupt le Bas case of 24 November 2024 was resolved as an airliner after investigators compared the observation with flight tracking information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBURNHAUPT LE BAS (68) 24.11.202426 Mar 2026 — Département Haut Rhin Classification A. Type de phénomène Avion de ligne Etrangeté 0... Helicopters can create a similar effect. A November 2015 sighting made while travelling from Munster towards Colmar was classified by GEIPAN as a helicopter after reconstruction and examination of the available evidence. Such cases are useful because they show how movement relative to a car, a valley or a distant horizon can make a conventional aircraft appear to hover or change course unexpectedly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D417] de MUNSTER (68) vers COLMAR (68) 13.11.2015[D417] de MUNSTER (68) vers COLMAR (68) 13.11.2015. Date d'observation. 13/11/201... Bright astronomical and atmospheric events also recur. A report from Walbach on 2 October 2013 was classified as a fireball, while later Haut Rhin files have identified lasers and pyrotechnic flares. These phenomena can look more structured than they are: cloud layers may produce glowing patches, laser beams can terminate in apparent luminous points, and a fragmenting meteor may resemble several lights flying in formation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANWALBACH (68) 02.10.2013 02/10/2013 A Haut Rhin Bolide 11/02/2014 2013 10 02... Published: October 2, 2013 The department’s mixture of open plains and mountain viewpoints adds another difficulty. Observers around Colmar, the Rhine plain and the Munster valley can see lights at great distances without being able to judge their scale. A nearby small object and a distant large one may occupy the same apparent angle in the sky. Without triangulation, even a sincere duration and direction estimate may not yield a reliable altitude, speed or size. Modern cases: drones, lanterns and satellites Recent Haut Rhin reports are generally better documented than the 1954 stories, yet they are also more often explained. This is not a contradiction. Modern investigations can use digital photographs, precise times, satellite predictions, aviation records and knowledge of newer technologies unavailable to earlier researchers. On 15 August 2024, witnesses in Mulhouse photographed two steady red lights flanking a flashing green light. The object first appeared stationary above a neighbouring roof, then accelerated and vanished behind it. GEIPAN regarded the colour pattern, hovering and rapid movement as strongly characteristic of a drone. The observation occurred shortly before a fairground firework display, providing a plausible reason"},{"title":"Sightings Over Time","url":"/sightings-over-time/","description":"Haut-Rhin's reports shifted from press-driven saucer stories to cases increasingly tested against aircraft, drones, meteors and digital records.","breadcrumb":"Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter","level":2,"text":"Sightings Over Time How Haut Rhin's UFO Reports Changed Over Time Haut Rhin UFOs Sightings Over Time Sightings Over Time How Haut Rhin's UFO Reports Changed Over Time Haut Rhin's reports shifted from press driven saucer stories to cases increasingly tested against aircraft, drones, meteors and digital records. Haut Rhin's reports shifted from press driven saucer stories to cases increasingly tested against aircraft, drones, meteors and digital records. event time window The 1954 Reporting Wave Why the Local Sky Creates Convincing Mistakes How Modern Tools Resolve More Cases haut rhin sightings over time Introduction Haut Rhin ’s UFO history has changed less because strange things stopped appearing in the sky than because investigators gained better ways to identify them. In 1954, local newspapers described discs, glowing spheres and “saucers” around Guebwiller, Issenheim, Rixheim and the Vosges foothills, often from a single short account with no photograph, precise timing or formal investigation. By the 2010s and 2020s, comparable reports could be checked against flight paths, satellite passes, meteor data, photographs and the known behaviour of drones or sky lanterns. The result is a marked shift from press driven mystery to evidence based sorting. Haut Rhin still produces convincing reports, particularly around Mulhouse, Colmar and the Rhine plain, but recent official files are dominated by aircraft, drones, meteors, lanterns and other identifiable objects. That does not prove every old sighting was ordinary. It does show how strongly the apparent mystery of a report depends on the information available at the time. The 1954 Reporting Wave The autumn of 1954 produced France’s best known wave of “flying saucer” reports. Newspapers repeatedly used the language of discs, cigars and mysterious machines, giving witnesses a ready made vocabulary for unfamiliar lights. Haut Rhin formed a small but revealing part of that national episode: several local reports appeared within weeks, yet most survive as press stories rather than case files containing interviews, weather checks, maps or supporting records. One of the better documented local newspaper accounts concerned Guebwiller on 20 October 1954 . Pierre Ackermann, an employee at a textile factory, reportedly saw an intensely bright round disc pass across the sky at about 7.40 in the morning. The observation lasted roughly ten seconds and was silent. Two schoolboys were said to have confirmed seeing a bright disc at around the same time. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, France 1954, October 20, Guebwiller, Haut Rhin... The presence of several witnesses makes the Guebwiller story more interesting than an anonymous newspaper snippet, but its apparent precision is deceptive. Ackermann’s estimate that the object was 300 to 400 metres high could not have been securely calculated from an unaided observation against the sky. Its reported size was expressed as an apparent measurement at that supposed distance, not as a physical dimension established by triangulation. The description could fit an unusual aircraft view, a bright meteor or another short lived luminous phenomenon, but the surviving details are insufficient to choose confidently among them. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, France 1954, October 20, Guebwiller, Haut Rhin... The same cluster included a more dramatic claim from Issenheim , where Lucien Fiesch was reported to have seen a spherical object about six metres wide hovering approximately one metre above the ground. According to the published account, he became frightened and returned to his workplace. This is potentially a close range encounter, yet the source trail leads back to newspaper reporting rather than a preserved contemporary investigation. There is no known photograph, trace evidence, detailed site plan or independent corroboration strong enough to test the reported dimensions and height. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org L'un des témoins, Lucien Fiesch, a aperçu près de Issenheim, un engin sphérique d'environ six mètres de...Read more... Other entries are even thinner. At Willer , later catalogues placed a disc sighting in early October, but the contemporary newspaper apparently supplied almost no detail. Compilers subsequently disagreed over the exact date, illustrating how easily a brief press item could acquire false precision as it passed through books and databases. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 30, Willer, Haut Rhin... The weakness is not necessarily in witness honesty. People may accurately report that they saw something striking while being mistaken about its distance, size or nature. The larger problem is that the 1954 material often preserves the conclusion—“disc”, “saucer” or “machine”—better than the raw observation. Important diagnostic details such as direction, angular height, cloud cover, duration measured by a watch and the movements of nearby aircraft were rarely recorded consistently. Media culture also shaped the reports. Once newspapers began printing saucer stories, an unfamiliar orange streak or luminous oval was more likely to be described using the fashionable imagery of the wave. Regional coverage could then encourage further witnesses to interpret later observations in the same terms. This does not mean that reports were invented collectively. It means that expectation influenced what details were noticed, remembered and communicated. From Saucer Shapes to Identifiable Lights After the creation of France’s official investigation service within the national space agency CNES in 1977, reports increasingly entered a structured system rather than remaining only in newspaper columns. Today’s GEIPAN collects witness questionnaires and may supplement them with sketches, photographs, videos and detection records. It publishes reports and conclusions while protecting witness identities. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr aintaining the anonymity of witnesses. GEIPAN is...Read more... The change can be seen clearly in Haut Rhin’s official case list. Reports from the late twentieth century onwards are not merely labelled “UFO”. They are classified by the explanation found—or by the reason that no reliable conclusion can be reached. A 1990 report at Moosch , for example, was attributed to a laser. A 2008 observation at Carspach was classified as a meteoroid. Reports at Rixheim, Riedisheim and Soppe le Bas between 2012 and 2013 were judged likely to involve sky lanterns, while a Munster report was attributed to a child’s balloon. A 2013 case at Steinbach was associated with the Moon, and one at Walbach with a bright fireball. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN These explanations show how different ordinary objects can produce similar testimony. A sky lantern can appear to hover, change direction in the wind and vanish when its flame fails. A meteor can seem much lower and closer than it really is, particularly when it crosses behind hills or clouds. The Moon can appear to move when seen intermittently through branches, mist or a moving vehicle. Lasers projected onto cloud can create patches of light with no visible beam or obvious source. Aircraft have become especially important in the local record. In November 2021, a report made while travelling between Sierentz and Habsheim was identified as an aeroplane. A November 2024 observation at Burnhaupt le Bas was likewise classified as an airliner. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN This pattern is unsurprising in southern Haut Rhin. EuroAirport Basel–Mulhouse–Freiburg is a major international airport serving roughly 100 destinations; it recorded 9.6 million passengers in 2025. Approaching or departing aircraft can therefore be seen from many parts of the department, sometimes almost head on. In that geometry, the landing lights may look stationary while the aircraft’s red, green or white navigation lights create an apparently structured object. [EuroAirport] euroairport.com Open source on euroairport.com. Aircraft can also seem silent when they are distant, travelling across the wind or observed through closed windows. A turn towards the observer can make separate lights appear to merge, while a banked turn can reveal or conceal lights suddenly. These effects help explain why witnesses may sincerely describe a hovering triangle, a rapidly changing configuration or a light that abruptly accelerates. Ils ont vu des OVNI Archive INA Channel: INA Société Open on YouTube Why the Local Sky Creates Convincing Mistakes Haut Rhin combines several environments that make distance and motion difficult to judge. The Rhine plain provides long views with few foreground reference points. The Vosges foothills create broken horizons where lights can appear, disappear"},{"title":"Huningue Case","url":"/huningue-case/","description":"Huningue remains Haut-Rhin's strongest unresolved case, but its witness and radar evidence still falls short of proving an exotic craft.","breadcrumb":"Why Haut Rhin's Strangest Sightings Still Matter","level":2,"text":"Huningue Case What Makes the Huningue Sighting So Difficult? Haut Rhin Huningue Case Huningue Case What Makes the Huningue Sighting So Difficult? Huningue remains Haut Rhin's strongest unresolved case, but its witness and radar evidence still falls short of proving an exotic craft. Huningue remains Haut Rhin's strongest unresolved case, but its witness and radar evidence still falls short of proving an exotic craft. event time window The Six Minute Sighting What the Radar Return Can Prove Why GEIPAN Left the Case Unexplained haut rhin huningue case Introduction The Huningue sighting of 26 May 1980 is the strongest publicly documented unresolved UFO case in Haut Rhin , but “unresolved” is not the same as “proved extraordinary”. Two walkers reported watching a silent, metallic looking, dome shaped object for about six minutes near the French–Swiss–German border. One witness had professional aviation experience, and Basel–Mulhouse Airport recorded an unidentified radar echo. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, nevertheless described that echo as too entangled with normal air traffic to be useful. No photograph, securely matched radar track or independent third group of witnesses survives in the published record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... That combination explains the case’s lasting importance. The observation was longer, more detailed and better investigated than most Haut Rhin reports, yet the evidence stops just where firm identification would require objective measurements. Huningue is therefore best treated as a credible but incomplete report: difficult to explain confidently, and equally difficult to use as proof of an exotic craft. The Six Minute Sighting At about 7 p.m. on Monday 26 May 1980, two people walking in an urbanised area near Huningue noticed an unusual object in a reportedly clear sky. GEIPAN’s public summary concentrates on the account of a 50 year old male witness described as an airborne mechanical officer or engineer and navigator. He portrayed the phenomenon as a single solid looking object rather than an indistinct point of light. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... The reported form was unusually specific. The witness described a round base topped by a silver coloured dome, giving the whole object a metallic appearance. Its underside looked darker and showed an intermittent reddish glow. It appeared to remain stationary at first, while seeming to oscillate around its axis. The witnesses heard no engine, rotor or other sound and saw no condensation trail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... The ending was more complex than a simple disappearance. According to the official summary, the object climbed towards cloud in successive stages or “levels”, rather than accelerating away in one continuous movement. The observation lasted approximately six minutes. GEIPAN’s database records an elevation of about 60 degrees, a change of direction or flight level, and apparent motion ranging from stationary to slow and variable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... Several features make this account harder to dismiss than a momentary flash: Duration: six minutes gave the witnesses time to observe shape, colour and movement. Structured appearance: the description involved a dome, base and darker underside, not merely a luminous dot. Changing behaviour: hovering, oscillation and stepped ascent formed a sequence rather than a single ambiguous movement. Witness experience: one observer’s aviation background may have made ordinary aircraft characteristics more familiar to him. None of those points is decisive. Professional experience can improve vocabulary and observational discipline, but it does not provide accurate range, size or altitude without instruments. Nor does a detailed description guarantee that the perceived structure belonged to a nearby physical object. Human vision can impose form on distant, bright or partly obscured phenomena, especially when their distance is unknown. The Missing Measurements The most important weakness is hidden inside the most dramatic part of the story: the claim that the object hovered only 200 to 300 metres above the ground. GEIPAN’s structured record does not give a measured witness to object distance, and it lists the apparent size as unspecified. The altitude quoted in the narrative was therefore an estimate, not an instrument reading. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... That distinction matters because an object seen against open sky provides few reliable depth cues. GEIPAN’s own public guidance states that beyond roughly ten metres, people cannot reliably estimate the distance of an unfamiliar phenomenon. Apparent size, actual size and distance are interdependent: a small nearby object and a much larger distant one can occupy the same angle in the observer’s field of view. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANComprendre un PhénomèneEstimation de taille et de distance d'un phénomène inconnu Au delà de 10 m, les yeux et le cerveau sont inca... Without a measured angular size or a known landmark that the object passed in front of or behind, the published account cannot establish: its actual dimensions; its true altitude; whether it was genuinely hovering; its speed during the climb; or the amount of energy and acceleration that its movement would have required. The impression of hovering is especially vulnerable to distance uncertainty. A distant aircraft moving towards or away from an observer can appear almost stationary for a time. Atmospheric haze, viewing angle and a lack of visible wings can further weaken familiar aircraft cues. This does not explain Huningue by itself, but it shows why the witnesses’ spatial estimates cannot be treated as measurements. The date also introduces an unresolved documentary problem. GEIPAN’s public record lists the weather conditions as unknown, even though its narrative says the sky was clear and later describes the object disappearing into cloud. The two statements are not necessarily contradictory: the immediate viewing direction may have been clear while clouds were present farther along the apparent path. Even so, the absence of a published meteorological reconstruction leaves investigators unable to test cloud height, visibility, haze, wind direction or unusual optical conditions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... Top 5 Strangest UFO Sightings of the 1980s 👽 The Silent Files Channel: The Silent Files Open on YouTube What the Radar Return Can Prove The radar element is the main reason Huningue is sometimes presented as more than a witness only case. GEIPAN states that Basel–Mulhouse Airport recorded an echo around the relevant period. In principle, a correlated radar detection could establish that a physical target occupied the same area of sky and performed the same movements reported by the witnesses. The published Huningue material does not demonstrate that correlation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s wording is notably cautious. The echo was “buried” among private and general aviation traffic and was therefore of little investigative value. The summary does not publish a plotted track, altitude sequence, speed calculation, transponder status or exact time comparison linking the return to the visual object. It also does not say that controllers observed an anomalous craft executing the reported stationary phase and stepped climb. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... The radar return can consequently support only a limited statement: an unidentified or unattributed echo existed in busy airspace. It cannot establish from the available public information that: 1. the echo represented the object seen from Huningue; 2. it was a single coherent target rather than ordinary traffic or radar clutter; 3. it hovered at the estimated altitude; 4. it lacked a conventional flight plan or identification; 5. or it displayed performance beyond known aircraft. This is the central difference between a radar associated report and a confirmed radar visual case. A strong correlation requires agreement in time, bearing, elevation or altitude, direction and movement. Ideally, investigators would also have the original radar recording, controller testimony and a clear separation from known traffic. Huningue’s published file supplies none of those supporting details. Busy cross border airspace makes caution particularly important. Huningue lies near Basel, Saint Louis and the international airport, where civil, private and general aviation movements can produce multiple possible targets. GEIPAN did not claim that the radar return behaved impossibly; it said that surrounding traffic prevented useful interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHUNINGUE (68) 26.05.1980 GEIPAN... Calling the incident a “radar"},{"title":"What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal","url":"/what-haute-corses-ufo-files-actually/","description":"Haute-Corse has a documented UFO history, but not a strong body of evidence for extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal Haute Corse has a documented UFO history, but not a strong body of evidence for extraordinary craft. Haute Corse has a documented UFO history, but not a strong body of evidence for extraordinary craft. The most useful records are held by GEIPAN, the investigation unit of France’s national space agency, CNES. They show a modest collection of reports around Bastia, the eastern coastal plain and the central mountains. haute corse Introduction Two investigations are especially revealing. In 2011, five divers reported a bright, apparently stationary object offshore near the Golo river, but GEIPAN judged that successive airliners were the probable explanation. In 2013, five lights moving in formation over Bastia were identified as an approaching Airbus. These cases matter because they demonstrate both why northern Corsica can produce convincing UFO impressions and how flight movements, viewing geometry and delayed reporting can weaken an apparently dramatic story. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 What the official record actually contains GEIPAN collects witness accounts, checks possible astronomical, meteorological and aviation explanations, and publishes anonymised files. Its classifications distinguish identified cases from genuinely unexplained ones: category A means identified, B means probably identified, C means that the evidence is insufficient, and D means the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. A category C case is therefore not strong evidence of something exotic; it normally means investigators lacked enough reliable information to reach any conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification into A, B, C, D Page Des Mentions Légales GEIPAN No, GEIPAN is a technical department of CNES (Centre National d’Études Spatiales), a public, i... The public Haute Corse listings include several cases concentrated between 1980 and 1983: Favalello, 3 March 1980: classified B, with Venus considered the probable source. Vezzani, 27 April 1980: classified C because the available information was unreliable or incomplete. Borgo, 3 August 1981: classified C for the same reason. Vescovato, 8 May 1983: classified C because investigators lacked dependable evidence. Santo Pietro di Venaco, 11 January 2003: classified B and attributed to a meteoroid. San Martino di Lota, 30 August 2009: classified C because the date or location could not be established precisely. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This is not a classic “flap” in the sense of many mutually supporting observations of the same phenomenon over a short period. The 1980–83 concentration is noticeable, but the records describe separate events with different weaknesses and proposed explanations. Three were left in category C, not because investigators found compelling unknown objects, but because the reports could not support a confident reconstruction. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The database also contains administrative inconsistencies that require care. Some later Bastia area entries are labelled with the old Corsican department number “20” or are displayed under Corse du Sud even though Bastia is in Haute Corse. The location and investigation text, rather than the department field alone, therefore provide the safer guide to whether a case belongs in this history. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 The 2011 divers’ sighting off the Golo On the morning of 20 August 2011, five amateur divers travelled by inflatable boat from Taverna to a diving site known as Punta d’Arco, offshore from the mouth of the Golo on the eastern side of Haute Corse. After a dive of roughly 45 minutes, they noticed a bright phenomenon that appeared motionless in a clear sky. According to the principal account, it remained visible for about five minutes, disappeared, then reappeared in a somewhat different form before vanishing again. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 At first glance, the case had several attractive features: five people were reportedly present, the weather was excellent, the object was seen in daylight, and a video made before the dive contained a small white point in the sky. Yet nearly every apparent strength came with an important limitation. Only one of the five divers was formally interviewed. The relevant observation itself was not filmed. The white point appeared in footage taken before the dive, so investigators could not establish that it was the same thing later reported by the group. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The delay was particularly damaging. GEIPAN received the report more than a month after the event and could no longer request useful radar information. A local investigator was not assigned until April 2012, about eight months after the sighting. By then, estimates of apparent size, exact direction and sequence depended heavily on memory. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 Investigators nevertheless checked several independent lines of enquiry. Bastia Poretta air traffic staff found that the period was exceptionally busy: between 10 and 11 a.m. there were five departures and six arrivals linking Corsica with the mainland, as well as numerous aircraft crossing the island’s wider airspace. Nothing abnormal had been recorded by the control tower, aviation gendarmerie, local gendarmerie brigades or Solenzara air base. The tower also had a view towards the coastal sector concerned, yet no separate report was logged. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 GEIPAN considered helicopters and a maritime surveillance aircraft but found difficulties with each idea, particularly the reported silence and apparent dimensions. Its preferred explanation was a succession of airliners viewed almost head on. Sunlight reflecting from aircraft surfaces, combined with approach lights and heat distortion, could make distant aircraft appear bright, nearly stationary and capable of changing shape. An aircraft approaching an observer has little apparent sideways motion, while much of its engine noise is directed behind it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The case was classified B: aircraft were judged probable rather than conclusively proved. That distinction is appropriate. GEIPAN did not match every reported appearance to a specific flight, and the other four witnesses were never interviewed. On the other hand, the reported large apparent size sat uneasily with the complete absence of reports from beaches, nearby holiday sites, aviation personnel and local media. Investigators considered it likely that the witness had overestimated the object’s angular size months after the event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The 2011 incident is therefore one of Haute Corse’s better documented cases, but its documentation weakened rather than strengthened the extraordinary interpretation. It shows how a real observation by sincere witnesses can remain impressive while still having a plausible conventional cause. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Five lights over Bastia in 2013 A second substantial investigation began with an observation from a roof terrace in central Bastia on 5 August 2013. At approximately 11.50 p.m., four people saw five lights moving from north to south. One witness described an initial V formation followed by apparent geometrical changes. The lights were star like, made no audible sound and remained visible for roughly 30 seconds before seeming to descend towards the horizon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 Only one of the four observers gave a detailed account, but the investigator regarded that person as calm, sincere and not especially interested in UFOs. Credibility, however, did not settle the identity of the lights. The crucial evidence came from Bastia Poretta airport. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 The final scheduled flight from Paris had been seriously delayed and landed at 12.02 a.m. Air traffic staff explained that aircraft arriving from Paris commonly pass over or near Cap Corse, continue southwards offshore from Bastia and then turn towards the airport. The delayed Airbus A320 would therefore have crossed the relevant part of the sky at approximately the reported time. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 GEIPAN concluded that the five lights were the aircraft’s position and approach lights. Their apparent geometry could change as"},{"title":"Aircraft Cases","url":"/aircraft-cases/","description":"The 2011 divers' sighting and 2013 Bastia lights show how approaching aircraft can appear silent, stationary or oddly formed.","breadcrumb":"What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Aircraft Cases How Aircraft Became Haute Corse's Most Convincing UFOs Haute Corse UFOs Aircraft Cases Aircraft Cases How Aircraft Became Haute Corse's Most Convincing UFOs The 2011 divers' sighting and 2013 Bastia lights show how approaching aircraft can appear silent, stationary or oddly formed. The 2011 divers' sighting and 2013 Bastia lights show how approaching aircraft can appear silent, stationary or oddly formed. mechanism The divers' sighting off the Golo Five lights over Bastia Viewing geometry, glare and delayed reporting haute corse aircraft cases Introduction Some of Haute Corse ’s most convincing UFO reports appear to have been ordinary aircraft seen under unusually deceptive conditions. The clearest examples are the five divers who watched a bright, apparently stationary object offshore near the Golo river in August 2011, and the witnesses who saw five silent lights changing formation over Bastia in August 2013. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s unit for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, concluded that the first case was probably a succession of airliners and identified the second as a delayed Air Corsica Airbus approaching Bastia Poretta Airport. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 These conclusions matter because neither sighting initially sounded much like a conventional aeroplane. The reported objects seemed silent, oddly shaped and either stationary or moving as a coordinated formation. Yet the geography of Haute Corse’s eastern coast places observers beside busy approach routes over the sea, where aircraft may be seen nearly head on, with powerful lights masking their bodies and little sideways movement to reveal their speed. The divers’ sighting off the Golo On the morning of 20 August 2011, five amateur divers surfaced from a dive near Punta d’Arco, roughly 5.4 kilometres off the eastern Corsican coast and close to the mouth of the Golo. At about 10am, while removing their equipment aboard an inflatable boat, they noticed a luminous phenomenon that appeared motionless in the sky. It remained visible for several minutes, disappeared, then seemed to return in a different form with a brilliant light beside it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The principal witness described an elongated, metallic grey object with indistinct edges, rather like a rugby ball, although another diver reportedly perceived something more triangular. A very bright white light appeared on or near it. The observers heard no engine noise and believed the object was stationary. Those features made the report memorable: several witnesses were present, the sky was clear, and the sighting lasted far longer than a fleeting meteor or momentary reflection. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 There were nevertheless serious evidential limitations. Only one of the five divers was interviewed directly by the investigator. A second witness agreed to complete a questionnaire but never returned it, while the other three wished to remain anonymous. The observation itself was not filmed. A white point visible in video recorded before the dive might have been related, but GEIPAN could not establish that it was the same object later seen after the divers resurfaced. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The timing of the report also weakened the investigation. GEIPAN received the questionnaire more than a month after the event, too late to request the relevant radar record. Field enquiries did not begin until the following April, almost eight months after the sighting. By then investigators had to reconstruct the case through witness memory, airport activity records and visits to nearby locations rather than through complete contemporaneous data. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 A crowded hour over the eastern coast The aviation evidence strongly favoured an aircraft explanation. Bastia Poretta’s traffic records showed five departures and six arrivals between 10am and 11am that Saturday. The day was classified as exceptionally busy because it fell during the August holiday season, and air traffic staff reported that many additional flights crossed above Corsica on international routes. The airport lay less than ten kilometres from the Golo area and almost directly along the relevant line of sight. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 Nothing unusual was reported by Bastia Poretta air traffic control, the air transport gendarmerie, the local gendarmeries at Borgo and Cervione, or Solenzara air base. People working near the coast also recalled no unusual event, despite beaches and holiday accommodation being busy at the time. There was no corresponding local press report. The absence of independent confirmation does not prove that the divers saw an aeroplane, but it sits uneasily with the very large apparent object later described by the principal witness. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 GEIPAN considered helicopters and maritime patrol aircraft as alternatives. Helicopters regularly crossed between Corsica and Italy or operated locally for rescue, tourism and photography. A military maritime aircraft might also fly circuits offshore. Both possibilities were judged less satisfactory because a nearby helicopter or low flying patrol aircraft ought to have produced more obvious noise. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 The preferred explanation was therefore not one mysterious aircraft performing a sequence of abrupt changes, but several unrelated airliners appearing successively in roughly the same part of the sky. One may have produced the white point seen before the dive; another, approaching Bastia nearly head on with lights illuminated, may have created the main post dive observation. GEIPAN classified the case as category B: probably identified as aircraft rather than proven beyond all doubt. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete326 Compte rendu enquete326 That distinction is important. The investigators did not match every phase to a named flight, because the delayed report had cost them the radar evidence needed for a precise reconstruction. Their conclusion rested instead on the dense traffic, the airport alignment, the lack of anomalous aviation reports and the known visual effects of approaching aircraft. The case was weakened substantially, but not solved with the documentary precision achieved in the later Bastia incident. 4:00 STRANGE LIGHTS IN THE SKY Pilots UFO Report at High Altitude Channel: VASAviation · Views: 44.4K · Uploaded: February 2024 · Length: 4 minutes Open on YouTube Five lights over Bastia At about 11.50pm on 5 August 2013, four people were on a roof terrace in central Bastia overlooking the sea. One noticed five white points travelling from north to south. They initially formed a V, point first, and appeared to move together. As the others joined him, the lights seemed to change position and trace geometric figures, described as resembling a figure of eight around an imaginary axis. The entire observation lasted approximately 30 seconds. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 Again, the report contained the elements that often make aircraft misidentifications sound extraordinary. The lights were described as star like and equal in brightness. No fuselage was visible. They appeared to belong to one large object, made no audible noise and seemed to descend before disappearing. The main witness remained convinced that he had not seen an aeroplane. GEIPAN nevertheless considered him sincere, calm and genuinely interested in finding an explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 Investigators found a highly specific match. Air Corsica flight AT514 from Paris, operated by an Airbus A320, should have landed at Bastia at 11pm but was severely delayed. Airport documentation recorded its landing at exactly 12.02am, only minutes after the sighting. The aircraft approached in clear conditions, and the observed north to south movement offshore was consistent with a normal route towards Bastia Poretta. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 Aircraft arriving from Paris commonly crossed the Cap Corse area and passed offshore beside Bastia before turning towards the airport. GEIPAN’s reconstruction showed two possible approach patterns, one running relatively close to the city and another making a wider loop over the sea. Both would have placed the aircraft within the witnesses’ broad viewing sector before landing south of Bastia. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete407 Compte rendu enquete407 The five visible points were interpreted as a combination of position lights and powerful forward facing or wing mounted lights. As the A320 turned and descended, the apparent spacing of those"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-d8abc5/","description":"The official archive separates probable explanations from genuinely unresolved reports and cases too weak to classify.","breadcrumb":"What Haute Corse's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Official Files Which Haute Corse UFO Cases Remain Unexplained? Official Files Official Files Which Haute Corse UFO Cases Remain Unexplained? The official archive separates probable explanations from genuinely unresolved reports and cases too weak to classify. The official archive separates probable explanations from genuinely unresolved reports and cases too weak to classify. dataset evidence What GEIPAN's classifications mean The 1980 83 cluster and later reports Why missing details can leave a case unresolved haute corse official files Introduction Haute Corse has officially unresolved UFO reports, but the crucial point is that none of the department’s published GEIPAN cases is currently classified as a strong, investigated unknown. The records that remain open are category C cases: reports that could not be identified because the surviving information was too incomplete, imprecise or unreliable to support a firm conclusion. That is very different from a category D case, in which a sufficiently detailed investigation leaves a genuinely unexplained phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casCase list; SANTO PIETRO DI VENACO (2B) 11.01.2003, 11/01/2003, B; FAVALELLO (2B) 03.03.1980, 03/03/1980, B; VEZZ... The distinction changes how Haute Corse’s UFO history should be read. Borgo’s low flying luminous object in 1981 sounds dramatic, while Vezzani’s close orange sphere in 1980 appears even stranger at first glance. Yet neither file contains enough independent or technical evidence to establish what was actually present. The official archive therefore preserves real witness experiences and unresolved questions, but not a verified Haute Corse mystery supported by radar, photographs, physical traces or several independent observation points. [Geipan] geipan.fr VEZZANI (2B) 27.04.1980 T2VEZZANI (2B) 1980. Date d'observation. 27/04/1980. Région. Corse. Département. Haute Corse. Classificatio... What GEIPAN’s classifications mean GEIPAN is the unit within France’s national space agency, CNES, that collects, investigates, anonymises and publishes reports of unidentified aerial or aerospace phenomena. Its classification system assesses two related factors: how strange an observation remains after ordinary explanations have been tested, and how consistent the available evidence is. Consistency depends on the quantity and reliability of information, including witness detail, corroboration, photographs, recordings and the coherence of separate accounts. [Geipan] geipan.fr ClassificationA more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN. It is based on 2 main criteria: the weirdne... The categories are easy to misread: A: the phenomenon has been identified with little or no ambiguity. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, though not completely proved. C: the case cannot be assessed properly because reliable information is missing. D: the phenomenon remains unexplained despite evidence substantial enough to investigate. GEIPAN further distinguishes D1 cases, which retain moderate consistency, from D2 cases supported by stronger evidence such as independent witnesses, recordings or physical effects. Category C is therefore not a weaker form of D. It is a different evidential condition: investigators do not have enough dependable material to decide whether the event was ordinary or genuinely anomalous. [Geipan] geipan.fr ClassificationA more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN. It is based on 2 main criteria: the weirdne... This matters particularly in Haute Corse because its publicly listed historical cases divide between probable explanations and insufficient data files. Favalello in March 1980 was classified B, with Venus judged the likely source. Santo Pietro di Venaco in January 2003 was also B, attributed to a meteoroid. By contrast, Vezzani, Borgo, Vescovato and San Martino di Lota were placed in category C. The archive does not list any of these four as a robust unexplained D case. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casCase list; SANTO PIETRO DI VENACO (2B) 11.01.2003, 11/01/2003, B; FAVALELLO (2B) 03.03.1980, 03/03/1980, B; VEZZ... The 1980–83 cluster The concentration of reports between 1980 and 1983 is the most noticeable pattern in Haute Corse’s GEIPAN record. It includes Favalello and Vezzani in 1980, Borgo in 1981 and Vescovato in 1983. However, the cases were separated by months or years, described different phenomena and ended with different assessments. They do not form a clearly connected wave of sightings of one recurring object. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casCase list; SANTO PIETRO DI VENACO (2B) 11.01.2003, 11/01/2003, B; FAVALELLO (2B) 03.03.1980, 03/03/1980, B; VEZZ... Favalello provides an important control case within the cluster. Its classification as probably Venus shows that a report could remain striking to the witness while still being matched to a familiar astronomical source. Bright planets can appear unusually large, coloured or mobile when viewed close to the horizon, particularly when haze, cloud or intervening terrain removes ordinary distance cues. GEIPAN’s conclusion does not prove every detail of the recollection was wrong; it means Venus was considered the explanation most capable of accounting for the observation as a whole. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANFAVALELLO (2B) 03.03.1980, 03/03/1980, B, Haute Corse, Vénus, 21/04/2008, 1980 03 03, voir. FONTEVRAUD L'A... The other three early cases did not produce equally persuasive identifications. Their category C status is sometimes described loosely as “unexplained”, but the database gives the more cautious reason: a lack of reliable information. In practical terms, investigators could not reconstruct the viewing direction, angular movement, duration, weather, astronomical background or possible aircraft traffic with enough confidence to test the main hypotheses. [Geipan] geipan.fr VEZZANI (2B) 27.04.1980 T2VEZZANI (2B) 1980. Date d'observation. 27/04/1980. Région. Corse. Département. Haute Corse. Classificatio... Vezzani’s close orange light At Vezzani on 27 April 1980, the published testimony describes an orange, fiery looking spherical object seen at about 00:30. The witness estimated it to be roughly the apparent size of a football, stationary, silent and only five or six metres away. Those details make the account one of the most immediately striking in the department’s archive. [Geipan] geipan.fr VEZZANI (2B) 27.04.1980 T2VEZZANI (2B) 1980. Date d'observation. 27/04/1980. Région. Corse. Département. Haute Corse. Classificatio... They also expose its central weakness. Distance and physical size are exceptionally difficult to estimate for an isolated light at night. A witness who sees no visible structure, shadow, surface detail or interaction with nearby objects may interpret an unknown angular size as a nearby object when it is actually a distant light. The published entry supplies a police document and two testimonies, but GEIPAN still classified the event C rather than D, indicating that the available material was not consistent enough to establish the geometry or exclude ordinary sources. [Geipan] geipan.fr VEZZANI (2B) 27.04.1980 T2VEZZANI (2B) 1980. Date d'observation. 27/04/1980. Région. Corse. Département. Haute Corse. Classificatio... The responsible conclusion is not that the witness saw nothing. It is that the reported distance of a few metres cannot be treated as independently established. Without a precise line of sight, duration, environmental reference point or corroborating physical effect, the most extraordinary part of the account is also the least testable. Borgo’s low flying luminous object The Borgo report of 3 August 1981 is the richest of Haute Corse’s category C cases. At about 22:55, a 70 year old witness reported watching an object for roughly 40 seconds at an estimated distance of less than 400 metres. It appeared to move slowly from east to west, close to the ground, with a rounded front, a bright halo and a large multicoloured flame. The glow reportedly illuminated the surrounding landscape. The witness feared it might strike the mountain but heard no impact. [Geipan] geipan.fr BORGO (2B) 03.08.19813 Aug 1981 — BORGO (2B) 03.08.1981. Date d'observation. 03/08/1981. Région. Corse. Département. Haute Corse. C... Several features invite conventional possibilities: a distant aircraft seen at an unusual angle, a helicopter, a meteor like event misjudged as slow, or an object whose apparent descent was created by the mountain skyline. None can be demonstrated from the public summary. GEIPAN records that no additional information was obtained during the inquiry that would permit an explanation. That is why the case stayed C. The obstacle was not necessarily a shortage of possible explanations, but the absence of data capable of choosing between them. [Geipan] geipan.fr BORGO (2B) 03.08.1981BORGO (2B) 03.08.1981. Observation date. 03/08/1981. region."},{"title":"Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain","url":"/why-haute-garonnes-strangest-sightings/","description":"Haute-Garonne has an unusual place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, universally accepted “classic” encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain Haute Garonne has an unusual place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, universally accepted “classic” encounter. Haute Garonne has an unusual place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, universally accepted “classic” encounter. Its importance comes from Toulouse: the department is home to the French space agency, CNES, and to GEIPAN, France’s official unit for collecting and examining reports of unidentified aerial phenomena. haute garonne Introduction The department’s published record is mixed. Most reported lights and objects have been attributed to meteors, aircraft, satellites, lanterns, drones, atmospheric effects or insufficient information. A small number have remained unexplained for a time, only to be downgraded when later reviews exposed gaps in the testimony. The most substantial currently unresolved local case is a 2018 sighting at Sainte Foy de Peyrolières, but even that rests on one witness, no photograph or video, and incomplete supporting data. Haute Garonne’s real lesson is therefore less about confirmed extraordinary craft than about how difficult observations are tested, revised and sometimes left open. Why Toulouse sits at the centre of French UFO investigation GEIPAN was created within CNES in 1977 and is based in Toulouse. It collects reports, seeks ordinary physical or human explanations and publishes anonymised case files. The organisation deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerial phenomenon” because witnesses may have seen a light, optical effect or sequence of events rather than a solid flying object. It does not describe itself as an extraterrestrial research body. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Although its permanent staff is small, GEIPAN can draw on volunteer investigators and specialists in astronomy, meteorology, photography, psychology and other fields. It also works with the police, gendarmerie, civil aviation, defence authorities and weather services. Gendarmerie statements and site investigations can enter the archive, while aviation and military bodies may be asked for flight, radar or operational information. [Gendarmerie National] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés dans le ciel? Gendinfo, Gendarmerie nationale... This institutional presence matters when interpreting Haute Garonne’s statistics. A comparatively active reporting record need not mean that the department’s skies are physically more anomalous. Awareness of GEIPAN, Toulouse’s large population and the volume of air and space activity around the city may all increase the chance that an unusual sight is noticed, reported and preserved. A national statistical study of French reports found that population density and several environmental or industrial variables affected the geographical pattern, warning against treating raw sighting totals as direct measures of unexplained activity. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Toulouse and its surroundings also present a crowded observational environment. Blagnac airport, general aviation, helicopters, military traffic, drones, satellites and launches can produce lights or apparent movements that are difficult to judge at night. That makes the department valuable as an investigation laboratory, but it also makes simple claims of a local “hotspot” unreliable. The Sainte Foy de Peyrolières case: the strongest unresolved file On 8 July 2018, at about 11.30 pm, a resident of Sainte Foy de Peyrolières, west of Toulouse, reported a brief and complex display seen from his garden. He first noticed what he compared with a shooting star, followed by groups of lights and a dark form that appeared to change from a line or rectangle into a chevron like shape. The episode lasted roughly 20 seconds and was described as silent. A second person was present but did not manage to see the phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete679 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The case received substantially more attention than a routine questionnaire. The witness contacted GEIPAN within minutes, later supplied a detailed written account, maps and ten reconstruction drawings, completed a technical questionnaire and took part in an on site reconstruction and cognitive interview. That prompt initial contact and sustained cooperation improved the file’s evidential value: investigators were not relying solely on a story reconstructed many years later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete679 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... GEIPAN tested several conventional possibilities. A Starlink train was excluded because the first operational group was not launched until 2019. CNES space surveillance staff found no matching atmospheric re entry, and the reported stable, warm white lights did not resemble the changing colours and trails typical of many re entries. A professional firework display was considered but did not fit the long apparent horizontal movement, and no matching display was identified nearby. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete679 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... Aircraft remained a more serious possibility. Perspective can make an approaching formation appear nearly stationary or cause a line of lights to change apparent shape when it turns. The witness also reported several recognisable aircraft about 15 minutes later. Civil aviation radar material was obtained for a limited area and time window, but the recorded traffic did not reproduce the reported pattern. That result was not decisive, however, because the radar interval did not fully cover uncertainties in the witness’s timing, and complete military radar information was unavailable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete679 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... GEIPAN ultimately retained the case as category D1: an unexplained phenomenon with meaningful strangeness, but not the strongest level of evidential consistency. The limitations are fundamental: there was only one direct witness; no photograph, video or physical trace was obtained; apparent speed, distance and size could not be independently measured; the supporting radar record was incomplete rather than positively anomalous; some of the most extraordinary features lasted only fractions of a second. The case is therefore genuinely unresolved in the narrow sense that no tested explanation reproduced the full account. It is not positive evidence of an exotic vehicle. Its importance lies in the unusually detailed investigation and in the clear boundary between “not explained” and “proved extraordinary”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Two former mysteries weakened by later review Haute Garonne’s archive demonstrates why an initial unexplained classification should never be treated as permanent. Two prominent local cases were reassessed after GEIPAN applied stricter standards. The 2011 Toulouse triangle At about 3.32 am on 24 August 2011, a witness looking through a window reported a dark triangular shape moving silently southwards over Toulouse. Orange rectangular lights were said to form rows around its underside. The sighting lasted approximately five seconds before buildings blocked the view. There was no photograph, no second witness and no matching radar trace. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULOUSE (31) 24.08.2011 GEIPAN... The shape and direction were broadly compatible with an aircraft, perhaps a business or military type, but the lighting pattern was not readily matched. Silence was also difficult to assess because the witness was indoors, although GEIPAN reasoned that a very low aircraft should probably still have been audible. The report was originally placed in category D1. During a later review, however, the witness declined further contact, leaving investigators unable to refine the viewing geometry, confirm details or test alternatives. GEIPAN concluded that the case no longer had enough reliable information to sustain an unexplained classification and moved it to category C: unusable through lack of dependable data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULOUSE (31) 24.08.2011 GEIPAN... This does not establish that the witness invented the event. It means that a strange five second impression, unsupported by independent evidence, cannot carry the same weight as a documented multi witness or instrumented observation. National Centre of Space Studies of France Wikipedia audio article Channel: wikipedia tts"},{"title":"Case Reviews","url":"/case-reviews-8f4a8a/","description":"GEIPAN's later reviews show how weak geometry, fading memories and missing corroboration can turn a mystery into an unusable case.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain","level":2,"text":"Case Reviews Why Official UFO Verdicts Can Change Haute Garonne Case Reviews Case Reviews Why Official UFO Verdicts Can Change GEIPAN's later reviews show how weak geometry, fading memories and missing corroboration can turn a mystery into an unusable case. GEIPAN's later reviews show how weak geometry, fading memories and missing corroboration can turn a mystery into an unusable case. policy intervention How Toulouse became France's investigation centre The downgraded Toulouse triangle and Saint Gaudens lights What separates unresolved, weak and explained reports haute garonne case reviews Introduction Official UFO verdicts can change because “unexplained” is not the same as “proved extraordinary”. In Toulouse, the French space agency’s investigation unit, GEIPAN, revisits older files when improved methods, fresh evidence or a closer reading of the original record alters the balance between a report’s strangeness and its reliability. Several Haute Garonne cases once treated as unexplained have consequently been downgraded—not because investigators found a definitive conventional answer, but because the available testimony could no longer support a firm conclusion. The distinction matters. A case may remain puzzling in everyday language while becoming unusable as evidence. The Castanet Tolosan triangle lost an apparent radar confirmation; a separate Toulouse triangle weakened when its sole witness declined further contact; and the Saint Gaudens lights were reassessed as possibly occurring on distant terrain rather than in the sky. Together, these files show how poor geometry, missing corroboration and the passage of time can turn an apparent mystery into a classification of insufficient information. How Toulouse became France’s investigation centre GEIPAN is based within the Centre national d’études spatiales, or CNES, in Toulouse. Created in 1977 under an earlier name, the unit collects reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, investigates possible explanations, preserves case material and publishes anonymised files. Its remit is evidential rather than speculative: it examines what witnesses observed and whether known astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical, technological or perceptual causes can account for it. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... A report normally passes through receipt, initial assessment, investigation, classification, anonymisation and publication. Witness accounts may be checked against flight information, weather records, astronomical reconstructions, photographs, video, radar material and observations from other people. Difficult cases can involve field investigators or specialists from several disciplines. GEIPAN’s archive is therefore not a fixed catalogue of verdicts but a working record whose entries may be amended. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ation. Classification B... The present system separates four broad outcomes: A: the phenomenon has been identified. B: an ordinary explanation is considered very probable. C: the phenomenon cannot be identified because the evidence is inadequate. D: it remains unidentified after investigation despite sufficiently useful evidence. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr ly identified after investigation... Classification depends on two linked judgements. “Strangeness” measures how poorly the observation fits known phenomena after plausible explanations have been tested. “Consistency” measures the amount and reliability of the available information. A highly unusual description therefore needs especially strong support: independent witnesses, accurate directions and timings, photographs, radar returns or other objective material. An unusual story backed only by a short, imprecise account may belong in category C rather than D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLe glossaire du GeipanCLASSIFICATION DES CAS. La classification d'un cas nécessite l'évaluation, d'une part, de son degré de consis... This approach also explains why later review can make a verdict less dramatic. Investigators may discover that supposed corroboration was spurious, that witness drawings do not establish the geometry once attributed to them, or that crucial questions were never asked while memories were fresh. GEIPAN explicitly allows C and D cases to be revisited when new information becomes available, while its archive reviews have also applied newer software and accumulated investigative experience to older files. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ation. Classification B... The Toulouse area triangle that lost its radar support The Castanet Tolosan case of 1 September 2000 illustrates how one apparently objective detail can transform a sighting—and how the case can collapse when that detail is re examined. At about 5.30 am, a man briefly saw illuminated points forming what he interpreted as a silent triangular shape. It moved out of view behind a building, and no second witness was found. The initial investigation nevertheless appeared to contain an unusually important piece of corroboration: Toulouse airport radar had registered three unknown tracks in the south east Toulouse area at about 5.40 am. For a time, the proximity in time and place made the radar returns look like evidence that a physical object had been present. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTANET TOLOSAN (31) 01.09.2000 GEIPAN... Specialists from the French civil aviation authority subsequently determined that the tracks were not meaningful targets. They were parasitic responses associated with an aircraft beyond the radar’s effective range. The supposed instrument confirmation therefore had no demonstrated connection with the witness’s triangle. What remained was a single fleeting visual report with no photograph and poorly defined duration, size, spacing or appearance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTANET TOLOSAN (31) 01.09.2000 GEIPAN... When GEIPAN revisited the file 18 years later, it did not claim to have identified what the witness saw. Instead, it judged that too many basic questions were unanswerable. It was unclear whether the orange description referred to individual lights or to a perceived solid form joining them. “Barely enough time to see it” might have meant a fraction of a second or several seconds, a difference that substantially changes the range of possible explanations. The file also lacked the angular separation of the lights and useful detail about halos or movement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTANET TOLOSAN (31) 01.09.2000 GEIPAN... GEIPAN noted that witnesses can perceive separate lights as the corners of a single dark object, even when no connecting structure has been established. Depending on the missing timing and visual details, possibilities might have included meteoric activity, illuminated birds or another ordinary grouping of lights. None could be properly tested after so many years. The case was therefore changed from D to C: not explained, but no longer evidentially strong enough to count as an investigated unknown. Its published consistency score is only 0.10, compared with a strangeness score of 0.30. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTANET TOLOSAN (31) 01.09.2000 GEIPAN... The lesson is sharper than a simple debunking. Radar evidence is valuable only when the return itself is valid and can be related to the observation through compatible time, position and motion. A coincidental radar artefact may make a weak visual report look independently confirmed, even though the two events have no physical connection. Les archives du CNES sur les OVNI Archive INA Channel: Ina Sciences Open on YouTube A second Toulouse triangle weakened when the inquiry stopped Another triangular report, observed in Toulouse on 24 August 2011, reached category D1 before later being downgraded to C. At about 3.32 am, a witness looking at the stars through a window reported a dark triangular form moving silently towards southern Toulouse. Orange rectangular lights were said to form rows around its underside. The observation lasted about five seconds before buildings obscured the view. There was no photograph, radar confirmation or independent witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULOUSE (31) 24.08.2011 GEIPAN... The description was genuinely difficult to match with a familiar object. Investigators considered whether an aircraft might account for the shape and trajectory, but the reported lighting did not resemble a known configuration. A very low aircraft might also have been expected to produce detectable noise. GEIPAN therefore did not replace the original unexplained verdict with a conventional identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULOUSE (31) 24.08.2011 GEIPAN... The reassessment instead turned on the strength of the file. Most of its informational value came from the witness’s drawing and short account. The witness had not originally made a gendarmerie statement and later declined an"},{"title":"Sainte Foy","url":"/sainte-foy/","description":"The 2018 Sainte-Foy case remains unexplained, but its single witness and incomplete radar coverage sharply limit what can be claimed.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Garonne's Strangest Sightings Remain Uncertain","level":2,"text":"Sainte Foy What Makes the Sainte Foy Sighting Unresolved? Haute Garonne Sainte Foy Sainte Foy What Makes the Sainte Foy Sighting Unresolved? The 2018 Sainte Foy case remains unexplained, but its single witness and incomplete radar coverage sharply limit what can be claimed. The 2018 Sainte Foy case remains unexplained, but its single witness and incomplete radar coverage sharply limit what can be claimed. event time window The twenty second sighting and witness account Explanations tested by investigators Why category D1 does not prove an exotic craft haute garonne sainte foy Introduction The Sainte Foy de Peyrolières sighting is unresolved because investigators could not make any ordinary explanation fit the witness’s full account with sufficient confidence. On 8 July 2018, a resident west of Toulouse reported a silent sequence of lights and dark geometric forms crossing much of the night sky in roughly 20 seconds. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerial phenomena, tested satellites, atmospheric re entry, fireworks, aircraft, visual after images and drones before placing the case in its unexplained D1 category. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 That classification makes Sainte Foy one of Haute Garonne ’s most substantial modern unresolved files, but it does not establish that an extraordinary craft was present. The event was seen by only one person, produced no photograph, video or physical trace, and lacked the timely military radar record that might have confirmed or contradicted the reported flight path. Its real importance lies in the tension between an unusually detailed account and unusually limited independent evidence. The twenty second sighting and witness account The observation began at about 11.30 pm in the witness’s garden at Sainte Foy de Peyrolières, around 25 kilometres west of central Toulouse. The sky was reportedly clear and well starred. Meteorological information consulted by GEIPAN indicated weak north westerly wind, good horizontal visibility and no detected cloud layer below 7,500 metres at Toulouse Blagnac. The witness was seated with his mother, although she did not see the main phenomenon because it unfolded too quickly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 Both had first noticed what they regarded as a shooting star. About a minute later, according to the developed account, the witness saw a separate sequence that he divided into five phases: 1. Five or six bright points appeared with a descending, cascading effect before fading. 2. Four lights formed a horizontal line and seemed to approach, revealing what looked like a dark rectangular shape. 3. The alignment changed direction and appeared to become triangular or chevron shaped. 4. The form seemed to accelerate extremely rapidly across the sky without noise or a visible trail. 5. It appeared rectangular again, slowed and faded into the distance. The whole sequence was later estimated at approximately 20 seconds, although the most striking acceleration phase occupied only one or two seconds. GEIPAN’s reconstruction placed the apparent route broadly from north towards south south east. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 The unusually prompt first report is one of the case’s strengths. At 11.41 pm, about ten minutes after the event, the witness left a 149 second telephone message for GEIPAN. This reduced the risk that the central story had been built entirely through years of retelling. He subsequently supplied a typed narrative, ten reconstruction drawings, annotated maps and a sky chart, completed a technical questionnaire and took part in an on site reconstruction and cognitive interview in December 2018. Further measurements and exchanges continued over several years, and the case went before GEIPAN’s expert committee in 2022 and 2023. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 Prompt reporting, however, did not eliminate uncertainty. The first voicemail described a much briefer event of two or three seconds involving four aligned lights, a reduction to two lights and a triangular appearance. The later account expanded the duration to about 20 seconds and divided it into five detailed phases. That does not prove invention: an initial message made in a shocked state may be compressed, while later questioning can recover omitted details. Nevertheless, the difference matters because estimates of speed, direction and shape depend heavily on timing that was reconstructed rather than instrumentally recorded. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 There was also a small but relevant interruption in viewing. The witness later described jumping and having to clear or look past a large tree, losing sight of the phenomenon for about a second. Elsewhere, he indicated that he had not lost it from view. GEIPAN treated this as the account’s principal internal contradiction. In an event lasting only seconds, a one second obstruction creates the possibility that lights seen before and after the tree were not the same object or formation, even though investigators considered that possibility unlikely because their appearance seemed continuous to the witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 En quête d'ovnis : qui sont les chasseurs d'aliens ? Channel: Investigation Open on YouTube Explanations tested by investigators GEIPAN did not identify one obvious answer and stop. Its final report examined seven broad possibilities: a train of satellites, fragments from an atmospheric re entry, fireworks, a single aircraft, several aircraft in formation, retinal persistence caused by bright lights, and a coordinated drone display. The opening shooting star was treated separately from the later phenomenon and may simply have directed the witness’s attention towards the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 Satellites and atmospheric debris were poor matches for the reported manoeuvres and changing geometry. CNES’s space surveillance specialists found several orbiting objects moving generally north–south or south–north within the relevant period, but not at matching times or in a configuration corresponding to the groups of lights. They also found no atmospheric re entry on that date. GRAVES, the French space surveillance radar, did not provide evidence of a relevant low orbit object. These checks substantially weakened a space object explanation, but they did not constitute radar tracking of the reported phenomenon at low altitude. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 Fireworks could superficially account for cascading lights and changing arrangements, particularly on a summer Sunday near the Toulouse area. Investigators found no identified display at the relevant time, however, and judged the later long horizontal movement incompatible with normal fireworks. The reported initial elevation of about 65 degrees would also have required a substantial professional launch if the display were distant. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête August 7, 2024 — 8 Jul 2024 — Réponse du COSE: « Objet: SAINTE FOY DE PEYROLIERES (31) 08.07.2018: demande visibilité. Il y a une diza... Published: August 7, 2024 A visual after image was considered because the witness had been looking towards the illuminated façade of the house before turning to the dark sky. Strong lights can leave temporary retinal images. GEIPAN rejected this as a complete explanation because the reported lights changed their relative positions and appeared to remain in an external part of the sky while the witness followed them. The exact brightness and type"},{"title":"Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers","url":"/haute-loire/","description":"Haute-Loire has a genuine but modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers Haute Loire has a genuine but modest UFO history. Haute Loire has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its record is not dominated by a single nationally celebrated encounter, nor by strong radar, military or aviation evidence. haute loire Introduction Several modern Haute Loire reports have been identified as the Moon, a balloon or space launch activity. Another remains unclassified because the available information is too poor, not because investigators established that something extraordinary occurred. The older cases are more intriguing as social and local history, but generally lack the documents, measurements and independent corroboration needed for a firm conclusion. Haute Loire therefore matters less as an unexplained hotspot than as a compact example of how unusual lights become UFO stories—and how later inquiry can strengthen, weaken or leave them unresolved. The 1954 wave reached Haute Loire France experienced an extraordinary surge of “flying saucer” reporting in the autumn of 1954. Haute Loire contributed several stories during this period, particularly in October. Most survive through press clippings or later catalogues rather than through detailed contemporary investigations, so they need to be treated as reported observations rather than verified events. At Aurec sur Loire, a couple camping in the area reportedly saw two bright objects cross the sky in succession. Each resembled a large headlight, travelled roughly north to south and appeared to emit an orange red beam. The available account gives an estimated altitude and rapid movement, but no instrument readings, photographs or precise angular measurements. It is therefore impossible to test whether the witnesses saw aircraft, astronomical objects, meteors or something less familiar. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, October 3, Ronsenac, CharenteTeyssier, who were camping at Aurec sur Loire in Haute Loire, saw in the sky a... A more widely reported incident occurred around Le Puy and Chadrac in mid October. Accounts attributed to the Chadrac meteorological station described a bright, apparently stationary object observed through an optical instrument for an extended period. It was compared with an upright olive shaped body bearing a dark feature. Reports from elsewhere in Haute Loire, including Saint Maurice de Lignon, described what appears to have been the same phenomenon more simply: a brilliant, motionless point resembling a star. That contrast is important. One description sounds like a structured craft; another sounds much more like a bright astronomical or high altitude object distorted by distance and optics. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 16 octobre, Brioude...L'auteur indique que le 16 octobre 1954, la station météorologique de Chadrac... At Saint Cirgues, two residents were said to have watched two luminous balls apparently joined by a rod. The object reportedly remained still for about 15 minutes before departing rapidly. The story appeared in more than one newspaper, but repetition across newspapers does not necessarily mean multiple independent investigations: press agencies routinely circulated the same short report to several titles. No surviving technical evidence establishes the object’s distance, size or speed. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 18 octobre, Saint...Deux habitants de Saint Cirgues (Haute Loire) ont aperçu dans le ciel, à très ha... Yssingeaux produced another memorable account on 31 October. A local businessman reportedly saw a large ball with a sparkling tail descend so rapidly that he initially thought an aircraft was falling in flames. It then seemed to stop, move away and disappear after roughly ten to fifteen minutes. A meteor would fit the fiery ball and tail but not the reported duration or halt; equally, estimates of duration and motion can become distorted when a witness lacks fixed distance cues. The surviving report is vivid but too compressed for a reliable reconstruction. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org YSSINGEAUX, 1er novembre (A.F.P.) Un négociant d'Yssingeaux a aperçu hier soir, vers 21 heures, dans le ciel... Other alleged 1954 sightings are even weaker. A catalogue entry for Fay sur Lignon supplies little more than a place and an approximate date, with uncertainty over whether it refers to Fay sur Lignon or Saint Germain Laprade. The later compiler who examined the entry judged the information insufficient for analysis. Such fragments should not be counted as robust cases merely because they appear in a UFO catalogue. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Début octobre 1954, Fay sur Lignon, Haute Loire Patrick Grossovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, Début octobre, Fay sur... Taken together, the Haute Loire reports show a local cluster during a national media wave, but not a coordinated event demonstrated by matching trajectories, timings or physical evidence. Their concentration in October 1954 may reflect a mixture of genuinely unusual observations, ordinary objects seen under difficult conditions and heightened public attention to “flying saucers”. The 1975 Saint Jean de Nay affair Haute Loire’s most distinctive local UFO story may be the incident remembered around Saint Jean de Nay in the autumn of 1975. According to a retrospective report based on local archives, four witnesses claimed that a flying saucer had moved around the hamlet of Montagnazet, provoking a brief search or “hunt” across the surrounding plateau. [Le Progrès] leprogres.fr Le Progrès Haute Loire Il y a 50 ans, la traque d'un ovni sur le plateau...November 23, 2025 — 13 Oct 2025 — quatre témoins affirment avoir vu une soucoupe vol... Published: November 23, 2025 The episode matters because it appears to have involved several people and an immediate community response, rather than a single witness quietly reporting a light years later. Multiple witnesses can improve a case when their accounts are recorded separately, agree on measurable details and can be matched against weather, astronomy and aircraft activity. Yet the publicly accessible summary does not establish that such a disciplined investigation occurred. Nor does it point to radar confirmation, photographs, landing traces or a surviving official conclusion. The case therefore occupies an awkward middle ground. It is stronger as local history than an anonymous rumour, but weaker as physical evidence than its dramatic “saucer hunt” framing suggests. Retrospective anniversary coverage can preserve valuable testimony, although it can also compress disagreements and later embellishments into a cleaner story. Until the original witness statements, sketches, precise times and investigative records are compared directly, the Saint Jean de Nay incident is best described as an interesting, incompletely documented claim. What official investigations found France has had an official system for collecting and analysing unusual aerial observations since 1977. GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES, publishes anonymised case material and seeks conventional scientific explanations before leaving a phenomenon unidentified. Its classifications distinguish identified cases, probable identifications, cases that cannot be judged because evidence is inadequate, and sufficiently well documented cases that remain unexplained. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. The surviving Haute Loire files are especially useful because they show three very different outcomes. Auvers, 17 August 1980: Witnesses reported a luminous crescent that appeared to move and follow them. The inquiry identified it as the Moon. This is a classic perceptual effect: when observers travel along a road, a distant astronomical object changes little relative to the horizon and can appear to accompany the vehicle. GEIPAN classified the case as identified. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Saint Cirgues, 9 October 1994: A later Haute Loire case was assessed as probably caused by a metallic foil balloon. It received a B classification, meaning that investigators considered the proposed explanation convincing but lacked the absolute proof required for a fully identified A case. This should not be confused with the separate Saint Cirgues press report from October 1954. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Salettes, 19 June 2008: A motorist saw a strong yellow light moving silently through the countryside and felt that it was following the vehicle. GEIPAN classified the report C because reliable information was missing. That category does not mean “confirmed anomaly”. It means that the evidence is too limited to support a sound judgement."},{"title":"Official Findings","url":"/official-findings-62aacf/","description":"GEIPAN records show how reports of pursuing lights and silent objects became the Moon, a balloon or an unresolved case with too little evidence.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers","level":2,"text":"Official Findings What GEIPAN Found in Haute Loire's UFO Reports Haute Loire UFOs Official Findings Official Findings What GEIPAN Found in Haute Loire's UFO Reports GEIPAN records show how reports of pursuing lights and silent objects became the Moon, a balloon or an unresolved case with too little evidence. GEIPAN records show how reports of pursuing lights and silent objects became the Moon, a balloon or an unresolved case with too little evidence. dataset evidence The Moon that appeared to follow a car The balloon explanation and the limits of proof Why an unclassified case is not confirmed evidence haute loire official findings Introduction France’s official UFO service, GEIPAN, reached three notably different conclusions when it revisited later reports from Haute Loire . A bright crescent that seemed to pursue a car was identified as the setting Moon. A silent metallic object watched for half an hour was judged probably to have been a drifting Mylar balloon. A powerful yellow light reported near Salettes remained unclassified because the evidence was too incomplete to test properly. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980. Date d'observation. 17/08/1980. Région. Auvergne... Témoignages. Témoignages. AUVERS... Together, the cases show why an official file marked “unidentified” or “insufficient information” is not confirmation of an extraordinary craft. GEIPAN separates the strangeness of a story from the strength of the underlying evidence. A report may sound highly unusual yet remain scientifically weak if investigators lack precise directions, timings, independent witnesses or contemporary records. Conversely, an initially puzzling incident may become ordinary when astronomical calculations, terrain reconstruction or experience with balloons supplies the missing comparison. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The Moon that appeared to follow a car The Auvers case of 17 August 1980 is the clearest Haute Loire example of a dramatic encounter losing its mystery on re examination. At about 10 p.m., two people at a forest house noticed an intensely bright phenomenon towards the west. Through binoculars, it appeared as a large crescent emitting strong rays. They entered a vehicle to approach it, but the light then seemed to move rapidly and follow them. Frightened, they turned back; the phenomenon appeared to stop and subsequently disappeared. Searches found no ground or vegetation traces, and no additional witnesses provided corroboration. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980. Date d'observation. 17/08/1980. Région. Auvergne... Témoignages. Témoignages. AUVERS... The case was originally classified as unexplained, but GEIPAN later changed it to category A, meaning that the phenomenon was considered identified after investigation. Its conclusion was a misidentification of the Moon near the horizon. The Moon occupied the relevant part of the sky, matched the reported crescent shape, brightness, colour and duration, and was not otherwise mentioned by the witnesses. GEIPAN stressed that it did not doubt their sincerity. The error lay in judging what the light was, how far away it was and how it was moving—not in inventing the sighting. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The sensation of pursuit is a recognised problem when a very distant light is observed from a moving vehicle. Because the Moon is effectively fixed relative to the traveller over a short journey, it may remain in the same part of the windscreen while nearby trees, bends and hills sweep past. Once the observer assumes that the light is a nearby object, its stable direction can be interpreted as deliberate movement matching the car. Turns may make it appear to cross behind the vehicle, change sides or stop when the vehicle stops. GEIPAN describes this effect in other investigations as the “following ball” illusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete12 Notes d'enquete12 Auvers is important because the later explanation did not depend on dismissing frightened witnesses as unreliable. GEIPAN regarded the two person account as reasonably consistent and the location description as useful. The decisive change came from reconstructing the sky and comparing the reported behaviour with a known perceptual effect. Emotion may then have reinforced the impression that the light was acting intentionally: after the witnesses became afraid, each apparent movement was interpreted within the idea of pursuit. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980AUVERS (43) 17.08.1980. Date d'observation. 17/08/1980. Région. Auvergne... Témoignages. Témoignages. AUVERS... That distinction matters throughout Haute Loire’s UFO record. A sincere account can preserve the experience accurately—bright light, apparent motion, fear—while still assigning the wrong cause, distance and scale to what was seen. The balloon explanation and the limits of proof A second official finding concerned Saint Cirgues on the morning of 9 October 1994. One witness, positioned during a hunt, watched a silent, bright cylindrical object for roughly 25 to 30 minutes. It appeared white with metallic silver reflections and included blue and red features. A second witness nearby independently described a partly cylindrical, partly rounded object reflecting sunlight and moving through the landscape. Both observations lasted unusually long, and the gendarmerie prepared a plan of the area. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T1 · SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2. Témoin. Date de l'observation... Those details gave the case more substance than a brief report of a distant point of light. There were two separately recorded witnesses, lengthy observations and useful descriptions of the object’s appearance and movement. Yet GEIPAN eventually judged that the same details favoured a mundane explanation: a metallised Mylar balloon. It reclassified the formerly unexplained case as category B, indicating a probable rather than conclusively demonstrated identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T1 · SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2. Témoin. Date de l'observation... The proposed explanation drew on a combination of appearance, motion and local conditions: The silver or metallic reflections were compatible with a foil balloon catching the morning sunlight. The slow, mainly horizontal and silent movement fitted an object drifting in weak local air currents. The low movement within a mountain hollow could be explained by cool, relatively still air near the ground. The reported rise towards the end of the observation was consistent with warming air expanding inside the balloon and increasing its buoyancy. One of the witnesses independently considered a balloon possible. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T1 · SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2. Témoin. Date de l'observation... GEIPAN also proposed a plausible sequence beginning the previous evening. A balloon released on the Saturday could have cooled after sunset, lost buoyancy and descended into a hollow. As sunlight warmed it the following morning, it could first have drifted close to the ground and later risen. This was a reconstruction rather than proof of a particular launch: investigators did not identify the owner, release point or surviving balloon. That missing chain of custody is why “probable balloon” is more accurate than “balloon proven beyond doubt”. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T1 · SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2. Témoin. Date de l'observation... The Saint Cirgues reassessment also shows how institutional experience changes conclusions. GEIPAN stated that investigators had relatively little experience of balloon cases when the sighting was first processed. By the later review, comparisons with a larger body of reports made the combination of metallic reflections, slow drifting and silent movement more recognisable. The evidence itself had not become stronger; the range of tested explanations had improved. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T1 · SAINT CIRGUES (43) 09.10.1994 T2. Témoin. Date de l'observation... An earlier aviation case near Brioude illustrates both the strength and the limits of balloon reasoning. On 20 September 1956, two military pilots attempted to approach an aluminium coloured sphere but could not close the apparent distance. The Puy de Dôme observatory reported a large translucent spherical balloon drifting at about 20 kilometres altitude that day. GEIPAN classified the case B because the observed object was probably that stratospheric balloon, although its origin was never established. Calculations indicated that it remained many kilometres from the aircraft even after the pilots climbed, helping explain why it seemed unreachable. [Geipan] geipan.fr"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-ab6a6e/","description":"October 1954 brought a cluster of vivid reports, but weak records make it difficult to separate unusual events from misidentification and media momentum.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights, Local Legends and Official Answers","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave Why Did Haute Loire See So Many UFOs in 1954? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave Why Did Haute Loire See So Many UFOs in 1954? October 1954 brought a cluster of vivid reports, but weak records make it difficult to separate unusual events from misidentification and media momentum. October 1954 brought a cluster of vivid reports, but weak records make it difficult to separate unusual events from misidentification and media momentum. event time window The main sightings across the department How newspapers shaped the local wave What explanations still fit the reports haute loire 1954 wave Introduction Haute Loire did experience a noticeable cluster of “flying saucer” reports in October 1954, but the surviving evidence does not show that the department became an exceptional centre of unexplained activity. What remains is a small group of vivid newspaper stories from Aurec sur Loire, Le Puy and Chadrac, Saint Cirgues and Yssingeaux, together with a few poorly documented catalogue entries. They appeared during the peak of a nationwide French reporting wave in which newspapers published large numbers of accounts of luminous balls, discs, cigars and supposed machines. The local cases matter because they show how a sighting wave develops. Ordinary lights were described in increasingly mechanical language, similar reports were reproduced across several newspapers, and estimates of altitude, speed and shape were printed without the measurements needed to verify them. Some observations remain genuinely difficult to reconstruct. Others fit balloons, bright planets, meteors or errors of perception at least as well as an unknown craft. Later research has therefore weakened the idea of a coherent Haute Loire “flap”, even while preserving several intriguing individual stories. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr poursuite publication 1954 Il faut savoir que durant l’année 1954 et particulièrement en octobre, la presse nationale et régionale a publié un grand nombre d’articl... The main sightings across the department The apparent wave unfolded within a few weeks. Its best known reports were not all alike: some involved fast moving lights, one concerned a nearly stationary object examined through an optical instrument, another resembled two linked luminous spheres, and the final major report described a fiery object with a sparkling tail. That diversity is important. It argues against treating every sighting as one repeated type of phenomenon. Aurec sur Loire: two lights in succession One of the earliest reports concerned a couple from Saint Étienne who were camping at Aurec sur Loire. Newspaper accounts said that they saw a brilliant object resembling a large headlight moving rapidly on a north to south course at an estimated altitude of about 2,000 metres. It was said to project an orange or red orange beam. After it disappeared, a second similar object supposedly followed the same route. Some versions added that other people saw several comparable objects above the church tower at Aurec, briefly appearing to stop before moving towards the south west. This extra detail does not occur consistently across the surviving reports, which illustrates a recurring difficulty: the basic story was copied and sometimes expanded as it passed between newspapers and later catalogues. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org et Mme Teyssier, de Saint Etienne, qui campaient à Aurec sur Loire (Haute Loire), ont aperçu dans le ciel un objet lumineux donnant l'imp... The account sounds precise because it gives a direction, altitude and colour. In practice, however, the reported altitude was almost certainly an unaided estimate. Without knowing an object’s actual size or distance, a witness cannot reliably calculate its height or speed. A distant aircraft, two separate aircraft, meteors or bright lights viewed through haze could all create parts of the description. The apparent beam is more difficult to assess, but it might have been glare, a cloud effect or an inference based on the “headlight” appearance. The value of the Aurec report is therefore not that it proves two structured craft crossed the department. It shows how a short visual observation could quickly acquire the language of altitude, formation and directed illumination despite the absence of photographs, timings, weather data or independent witness statements. Chadrac: the strongest looking report is also the most ambiguous The most substantial Haute Loire account came from the meteorological station at Chadrac, near Le Puy. Contemporary newspaper versions reported that a brilliant object was watched through an optical instrument for roughly 45 minutes, from about 5.50 pm to 6.35 pm. It was described as an upright olive shaped body, sometimes with a dark “antenna” at the top and dark markings across its surface. Bright reflections were compared with aluminium. The involvement of a weather station gives the case an immediate appearance of authority. Yet the same account records that the station initially suspected Jupiter. A similar phenomenon was reportedly seen from a meteorological centre in neighbouring Cantal, while other reports from southern and central France described bright, slow or stationary objects during the same period. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This is where the Chadrac story becomes more interesting as an identification problem than as evidence of a craft. A bright point observed through ordinary binoculars or a small telescope can appear enlarged, elongated, coloured or divided by optical defects, atmospheric turbulence and imperfect focus. The “antenna” and dark bands may therefore have been details produced by the viewing conditions rather than physical structures. Jupiter alone does not neatly settle the report. A planet would not truly vanish suddenly unless cloud, terrain, equipment movement or a change in attention removed it from view. But the newspaper’s statement that sudden disappearance disproved Jupiter is itself too confident: no precise account survives of how the disappearance occurred. It is unclear whether the object faded, entered cloud, was lost while the instrument was adjusted or simply could no longer be relocated. A high altitude balloon is another serious possibility. Stratospheric balloons can appear almost motionless, shine brightly in low sunlight, change apparent form and disappear when illumination or cloud conditions alter. Reports from 16 October elsewhere in France were later connected with high altitude research balloons launched from Italy, and an object was photographed from the Haute Provence Observatory during that broader episode. The exact date attached to the Chadrac observation varies in later retellings, so the balloon evidence cannot be treated as a conclusive identification; it does show, however, that unusual high altitude objects were present in French skies during the relevant days. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The most defensible conclusion is that Chadrac remains unresolved at the level of the surviving newspaper account, but not strongly anomalous. The long duration and apparent stillness favour a planet, balloon or distant atmospheric object more than a fast manoeuvring machine. The mechanical looking details are weakened by the likelihood that they emerged only after optical magnification. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Saint Cirgues: the “flying balance” On 18 October, two residents near Saint Cirgues were reported to have seen two luminous balls apparently connected by a glowing rod. Later versions said that the centre remained fixed while the two ends oscillated like the pans of a balance. The phenomenon was visible for about 15 minutes and was then said to depart rapidly. The image was memorable enough to enter later UFO literature as a “flying balance”. Yet nearly all the published detail appears to descend from a short press item and subsequent retellings rather than a surviving interview, police report or field investigation. The witnesses were described generally as local residents or farmers; their names, exact viewing position, elevation angle and observation conditions are not available. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. A suggested explanation is a pair of linked or closely travelling balloons. Two illuminated balloon envelopes, or a balloon system carrying equipment below or between visible components, could produce a double bodied appearance. Slow swinging around a central point would also be compatible with objects suspended in air currents. The claimed high speed departure is the main obstacle to that explanation, but sudden acceleration is particularly difficult to judge when a distant light fades, turns, enters cloud or loses sunlight. A stationary object that becomes invisible can easily"},{"title":"Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope","url":"/haute-marnes-ufo-record-under-the/","description":"Haute-Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope Haute Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Haute Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Its best documented episode occurred at Saint Dizier air base on 20 August 1978, when five service personnel watched lights apparently stop, zigzag and change formation above or near one of France’s principal military aviation sites. haute marne Introduction That pattern defines the department’s record. Haute Marne has produced intriguing testimony, including reports from military witnesses and police files, but no published case combines testimony with radar data, instrument readings, physical traces or imagery strong enough to demonstrate an extraordinary aircraft. The official GEIPAN archive instead contains a mixture of unresolved but poorly constrained observations and cases later attributed to ordinary causes such as the International Space Station, the Moon, Jupiter and activity on the ground. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPANOctober 22, 2019 — SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978... SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 T1... Le... Published: October 22, 2019 The Saint Dizier lights of 1978 The department’s most important case began at about 10.50 pm on Sunday 20 August 1978 at Air Base 113, Saint Dizier. A serviceman on duty noticed three lights moving from east to west, apparently above the town. He described them as roughly the apparent size of ping pong balls and said their colour changed towards orange. Another serviceman was called, and the pair went outside to the aircraft parking area, where three more military witnesses joined them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPANOctober 22, 2019 — SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978... SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 T1... Le... Published: October 22, 2019 The witnesses reported that the lights merged or reduced to a single visible point, which then appeared to perform irregular movements. These included pauses, reversals, changes of speed and zigzags. No sound was heard. The light eventually travelled northwards and faded from view. The following day, the commander of Bombing Squadron 02/094 contacted the gendarmerie. Statements were taken from the five witnesses between 21 August and 11 September, after which the file was forwarded to GEPAN, the predecessor of today’s GEIPAN. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPANOctober 22, 2019 — SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978... SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 T1... Le... Published: October 22, 2019 Several details make the incident more interesting than a routine light report: there were five identified witnesses rather than one; they were military personnel observing from an operational air base; the report reached the gendarmerie immediately rather than emerging years later; the witnesses described changes in relative position and apparently irregular motion; the original paperwork survived and could be reassessed. These strengths concern the reliability of the reporting process, however, not necessarily the nature of the object. Military personnel can be careful observers, but they face the same fundamental difficulty as anyone watching an isolated light at night: without a known distance, its size, speed and manoeuvres cannot be calculated. Why the original classification changed GEPAN originally classified the Saint Dizier incident as category D, meaning that it remained unexplained after investigation. Decades later, GEIPAN reopened the file using its newer classification method and analytical experience. In 2019 it changed the result to category C: the case could not be reliably interpreted because essential information was missing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPANOctober 22, 2019 — SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978... SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 T1... Le... Published: October 22, 2019 Investigators considered whether a powerful entertainment light, sometimes called a sky tracer, could have projected moving spots onto low cloud. That possibility fits several reported features: multiple coloured lights apparently becoming one, abrupt zigzags, stationary phases, silent movement and a visibly expanded luminous point rather than a sharply defined craft. Probable cloud cover would also have provided a screen on which such a beam could appear. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPANOctober 22, 2019 — SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978... SAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 T1... Le... Published: October 22, 2019 The proposed explanation is not conclusive. Such equipment was still uncommon in France during the late 1970s, and investigators could not identify a particular venue or event using it within the surrounding area. Yet failure to locate an entertainment light four decades later does not prove that none existed. Records from a temporary fair, private celebration or short lived establishment may never have survived. Most importantly, the published case contains no confirmed radar return, interception, photograph, measurable angular track or independent observation from outside the base. The setting therefore raises the case’s historical interest without supplying the technical corroboration readers might expect from the phrase “military UFO encounter”. Air Base 113 had radar and air traffic facilities, and later became deeply associated with French strategic and combat aviation, but no publicly released radar evidence is attached to this observation. [gouv.fr] imagesdefense.gouv.fr Images DefenseActivité de la base aérienne 113 à Saint Dizier.Activité de la base aérienne 113 à Saint Dizier. Photographe(s): André Bra... The fairest judgement is that the witnesses probably saw a real luminous phenomenon, but the record does not show that it was a structured vehicle or that it performed extraordinary physical manoeuvres. The later category C assessment weakens the case as evidence for an unknown aircraft, while preserving it as Haute Marne’s best documented unresolved observation. Wassy, Peigney and the limits of witness reports The other historical cases in GEIPAN’s public Haute Marne record are less dramatic, but they illustrate why “unidentified” can mean very different things. At Wassy on 21 October 1977, a witness reported a flashing yellow ball moving from west to east at about 6.30 pm. It was visible for only a few seconds, produced no reported sound or trail, and generated no additional witnesses found by the gendarmerie. The weather was highly stormy, with numerous flashes of lightning across the region. GEIPAN classifies the case as C because the available information is insufficient, not because investigation established an exotic object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANWASSY (52) 21.10.1977WASSY (52) 21.10.1977. Date d'observation. 21/10/1977. Région. Champagne Ardenne. Département. Haute Marne. Cl... The storm offers several conventional possibilities, including an unusual perception of lightning, a distant aircraft seen through broken cloud or another short lived luminous event. None can be demonstrated from the sparse description. This is precisely the problem with category C cases: they may sound strange, but they lack enough dependable data to distinguish between competing explanations. A more recent report came from Peigney, near Langres, before dawn on 17 August 2016. A witness saw lights apparently at ground level on a hill about two kilometres away. GEIPAN concluded that the observation was only mildly unusual and probably involved an agricultural machine or ordinary vehicle, possibly with people moving nearby carrying torches. Distance, darkness and the movement of separate lights could have created the impression of a rotating or unusual illuminated object. The case was classified B, indicating a probable conventional explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Peigney case is useful because it demonstrates how a report that initially appears aerial may turn out not to involve the sky at all. A bright source observed across fields in darkness can be difficult to place accurately. Without clear reference points, witnesses may misjudge whether a light is on the ground, just above a ridge or much farther away. La base aérienne de Saint Dizier accueille deux mythes de la BD Channel: Armée de l'Air et de l'Espace Open on YouTube The thin record of the 1954 French flap France experienced an exceptional surge of flying saucer reporting in the autumn of 1954. Newspapers carried hundreds of accounts involving luminous balls, discs, cigar shaped objects and alleged landings. Haute Marne appears in specialist compilations of this wave, although its reported incidents are generally much weaker than the famous national cases. One surviving"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-46688d/","description":"Reports from Wassy, Peigney and later cases show how weather, ground vehicles and bright celestial objects can create convincing UFO impressions.","breadcrumb":"Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases Why So Many Haute Marne UFOs Became Identified Explained Cases Explained Cases Why So Many Haute Marne UFOs Became Identified Reports from Wassy, Peigney and later cases show how weather, ground vehicles and bright celestial objects can create convincing UFO impressions. Reports from Wassy, Peigney and later cases show how weather, ground vehicles and bright celestial objects can create convincing UFO impressions. dataset evidence From storm lights to mistaken aerial objects How distance and darkness distort perception What GEIPAN classifications actually mean haute marne explained cases Introduction Many Haute Marne UFO reports became less mysterious once investigators stopped treating every light as a distant object in the sky. The most persuasive ordinary explanations involved thunderstorms, vehicles or people on the ground, bright astronomical objects and an orbiting spacecraft seen under unfamiliar conditions. In other cases, including Wassy in 1977, an ordinary cause was plausible but the surviving information was too weak to prove it. This distinction matters. An “unidentified” report does not necessarily describe an extraordinary craft; it may simply lack the timing, direction, duration, photographs or independent witnesses needed for a firm identification. Haute Marne’s official record shows the full range: a poorly documented storm time observation, probable ground activity near Peigney, and recent cases securely matched to the Moon, Jupiter and the International Space Station. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANWASSY (52) 21.10.1977 GEIPAN... From storm lights to mistaken aerial objects Wassy: plausible weather, insufficient proof At about 6.30 pm on 21 October 1977, a witness at Wassy reported a yellow, flashing ball travelling from west to east. The observation lasted only a few seconds. No sound or trail was noticed, and a gendarmerie inquiry found no other witnesses. The official file also records particularly stormy weather and numerous lightning flashes across the region that day. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANWASSY (52) 21.10.1977 GEIPAN... Those circumstances make an atmospheric explanation plausible. A brief luminous event seen during an active thunderstorm could have been lightning viewed at an unusual angle, illumination within or behind cloud, or another weather related effect whose apparent shape was difficult to judge. Yet the evidence does not support choosing confidently between those possibilities. There is no photograph, measured bearing, precise angular path or corroborating account with which to reconstruct the event. GEIPAN therefore lists Wassy as category C: not identified because reliable information is lacking. That is different from category D, in which a phenomenon remains unidentified despite an investigation supported by sufficiently substantial evidence. The Wassy file does not establish an exotic event, but neither does it demonstrate a particular meteorological mechanism. It is best understood as a weakly constrained report made under conditions already capable of producing unusual light effects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANWASSY (52) 21.10.1977 GEIPAN... Peigney: the “objects” were probably on the hillside The Peigney reports show more clearly how distance and darkness can lift ordinary ground activity into an apparently aerial mystery. At 5.45 am on 17 August 2016, a witness looking west from a terrace saw a large, stationary orange glow accompanied by three smaller lights. Through binoculars, the smaller lights seemed to rotate around the larger one. The witness estimated that the display was about two kilometres away, on or near a country hillside. It disappeared while the witness went to fetch a camera. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPEIGNEY (52) 17.08.2016 GEIPAN... GEIPAN found little reason to place the lights in the air. A track ran through or near the relevant area, making a vehicle or agricultural machine a straightforward possibility. People moving around it with torches could have created the impression that smaller lights were circling a central glow. Investigators also considered a fire with people carrying lamps or torches. The file was classified B, meaning that the phenomenon was probably identified rather than conclusively demonstrated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPEIGNEY (52) 17.08.2016 GEIPAN... A similar observation by the same witness strengthened that interpretation. In the 2016 police statement, the witness recalled seeing moving lights around a central flame like glow on 4 June 2012, at about 3 am, on almost the same hillside. The witness also noted that a church in the area could accommodate scout groups. GEIPAN considered a campfire or organised outdoor activity another plausible source for both the central light and the moving points around it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPEIGNEY (52) 17.08.2016 GEIPAN... The repetition did not necessarily indicate a recurring aerial phenomenon. Instead, it supplied useful geographical context: the same distant location could host vehicles, fires or people moving after dark. Once the presumed altitude was removed from the account, the apparent “formation” could be reconstructed as several independent lights at ground level. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Vicq: photographs turned strange shapes into the Moon and Jupiter A 2025 report from Vicq demonstrates how modern records can be resolved more firmly. At about 1 am on 6 March, a witness saw two white luminous forms low in the sky above nearby houses. The witness took two smartphone photographs, one with digital zoom, and later reported that the lights had disappeared when viewed again roughly fifteen minutes afterwards. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVICQ (52) 06.03.2025 GEIPAN... GEIPAN analysed the images with IPACO, image examination software used in its investigations. The recorded positions agreed with the Moon and Jupiter as they were setting. Trees partly obscured the Moon, preventing its familiar disc from being recognised, while slight camera movement probably produced an irregular, flattened V shape in one image. The shape changed between photographs, supporting an imaging effect rather than a stable object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVICQ (52) 06.03.2025 GEIPAN... The apparent shift of Jupiter relative to the Moon was attributed to the witness changing position between photographs, producing parallax against nearby roofs and vegetation. Astronomical reconstruction placed both bodies in the reported direction at 1.01 and 1.02 am. Their disappearance fifteen minutes later was consistent with the Moon and Jupiter dropping behind trees and buildings. A possible third point was probably Capella, a bright star. GEIPAN classified the case A: identified as the Moon and Jupiter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVICQ (52) 06.03.2025 GEIPAN... This case is instructive because photographs did not automatically make the report extraordinary. Instead, they preserved enough information to test the witness’s impression against the sky at a precise time and place. The images strengthened the investigation while weakening the interpretation of structured aerial objects. Vals des Tilles: the curved light was the ISS At 6.40 am on 2 March 2025, a witness at Vals des Tilles watched a bright point move steadily from the west towards the south for about 20 to 30 seconds. The witness considered the International Space Station but rejected it because the light seemed dimmer than expected and its route appeared to curve. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALS DES TILLES (52) 02.03.2025 GEIPAN... CNES orbital specialists confirmed that the ISS was passing at the reported time and position. Its steady speed and point like white appearance matched the description. The comparatively low brightness was consistent with the approaching dawn, while the apparently bending path resulted from perspective as the station followed its orbital track across a curved Earth and the observer’s view of the sky. GEIPAN also noted that, had the reported object been something else, the witness should have been able to see the independently confirmed ISS as a second moving light. The case was classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVALS DES TILLES (52) 02.03.2025 GEIPAN... The important point is not simply that satellites are often reported as UFOs. The witness already knew what the ISS could look like. The misidentification arose because the real pass did not match an overly rigid expectation that it should always be exceptionally bright and should cross the sky along an apparently straight line. How distance and darkness distort perception The Peigney, Vicq and Vals des Tilles reports involve different physical sources, but they share one observational problem: a bright light against a dark or poorly detailed background provides few reliable clues about distance."},{"title":"Saint Dizier","url":"/saint-dizier/","description":"Five servicemen reported strange lights over Saint-Dizier, but the surviving file cannot establish distance, speed or a physical craft.","breadcrumb":"Haute Marne's UFO Record Under the Microscope","level":2,"text":"Saint Dizier Did the Saint Dizier Lights Defy Explanation? Haute Marne UFOs Saint Dizier Saint Dizier Did the Saint Dizier Lights Defy Explanation? Five servicemen reported strange lights over Saint Dizier, but the surviving file cannot establish distance, speed or a physical craft. Five servicemen reported strange lights over Saint Dizier, but the surviving file cannot establish distance, speed or a physical craft. event time window What the five military witnesses reported Why the official classification changed Radar, sky tracers and the evidence gap haute marne saint dizier Introduction The Saint Dizier lights remain Haute Marne ’s strongest historical UFO case, but not its strongest proof of an extraordinary craft. On 20 August 1978, five servicemen at Air Base 113 reported a silent light that appeared to stop, zigzag, reverse and change formation. The incident was reported to the air gendarmerie the next morning, producing named witness statements and an official file rather than a story reconstructed decades later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Yet the surviving evidence cannot establish the lights’ distance, altitude, true speed or physical size. Only the first witness saw the initial three light formation, no instrument was used, and the published dossier contains no corroborating radar plot, photograph, film or physical trace. GEIPAN therefore changed the case from “unexplained” to category C: not identified because the information is too weak to support a firm conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPAN... What the five military witnesses reported The observation began at about 10.50 pm in the buildings of Bombing Squadron 02/094. The principal witness, a sergeant on duty, looked from a first floor window and saw three lights approaching from the east, apparently over Saint Dizier. He described them as arranged first in a triangle and then in changing relative positions, including a line and side by side formations. Their colour shifted towards orange, and he believed they were moving westwards towards the base. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. After losing sight of them behind part of the building, he called a second serviceman. By that stage, the three lights appeared to have combined into one luminous point above the GERMAS maintenance hangar. The two men moved outside to the aircraft parking area and were joined by three further military witnesses. They described the single light as brighter than a star, orange or bright yellow, silent and intermittently stationary. When it moved away, they said it advanced irregularly, with short pauses, zigzags and apparent backward motions before fading towards the north or north east. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The entire episode was estimated at roughly fifteen minutes, although the witnesses did not all observe the same phases. The sergeant alone reported the first ten minutes involving three separate lights. The other four mainly saw the later single light phase, and two explicitly said they would not have noticed anything had the sergeant not drawn their attention to it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That distinction is crucial. “Five military witnesses” can suggest five independent confirmations of the whole event. In reality, the strongest and strangest feature — three lights changing formation and apparently merging — rests on one person’s testimony. The group evidence is better for the existence of a conspicuous moving light than for the claim that several objects performed coordinated manoeuvres. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DIZIER (52) 20.08.1978 GEIPAN... Why the report initially looked persuasive The case has several genuine strengths. It was reported promptly: the commander of Bombing Squadron 02/094 telephoned the gendarmerie on 21 August, the day after the sighting. The principal witness was questioned that day, two others on 24 August, a fourth on 5 September and the fifth on 11 September. The investigation was then closed and forwarded to GEPAN, the original CNES unit responsible for unidentified aerospace phenomena. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The official setting also matters. Air Base 113 was not an incidental viewing location but an operational military airfield, and the observation occurred around the buildings and parking area of a strategic bombing squadron. The servicemen were familiar with aircraft activity and repeatedly said that the light did not resemble an aeroplane or an ordinary star. Their accounts agreed on several broad features: a bright coloured light, silence, a stationary interval, irregular movement and gradual loss of visibility with distance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These points strengthen confidence that the witnesses experienced something they found genuinely unusual. They do not, however, provide a measurement of what was in the sky. Familiarity with aircraft may help a witness recognise a conventional aircraft seen under normal conditions, but it cannot by itself yield the distance to an isolated light at night. Without distance, even a careful observer cannot turn an apparent displacement into a reliable speed or acceleration. The gendarmerie itself now stresses that accurate direction, duration, landscape position, dimensions and movement are essential because witness perception can misjudge distance and trajectory. [Gendarmerie National] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés dans le ciel? Gendinfo, Gendarmerie nationale... The missing measurements The Saint Dizier file repeatedly uses language that sounds quantitative but is not. The lights were said to be “the size of a ping pong ball”, at an altitude used by transport aircraft, and moving faster than contemporary military aircraft. None of those comparisons can be converted into physical dimensions or velocity from the surviving record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A ball comparison is useful only when the distance between the ball and the observer is specified. A ping pong ball held at arm’s length covers a very different angle from one imagined several metres away. The investigators had no measured angular size and no elevation angle at the beginning or end of the observation. They also lacked a timed sequence showing the width and duration of each reported zigzag. GEIPAN concluded that these omissions made the apparently extreme motion impossible to calculate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The same problem affects silence. A nearby powered craft making no noise would be unusual. A distant light, reflection or projected spot could be silent from the witnesses’ position. Because the range was unknown, the absence of sound does not establish proximity, advanced propulsion or even the presence of a solid airborne object. GEIPAN deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” because a report may concern a light or visual effect rather than a physical vehicle. [Gendarmerie National] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés dans le ciel? Gendinfo, Gendarmerie nationale... The published file also contains no photograph, film, instrument reading, environmental effect or radar return linked to the observation. The 2019 review states that no optical instrument was used and that the long delay prevented any retrospective check of local aircraft activity. It reconstructed the astronomical and weather conditions, but those later calculations could not replace measurements that were never taken in 1978. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This is the central “missing proof”. The file records sincere descriptions of motion, but it does not preserve the geometry needed to prove extraordinary motion. A light that seems to reverse or dart across the sky may represent a nearby object, a distant object, a beam on cloud or a perceptual effect. Those possibilities predict radically different physical speeds, and the Saint Dizier evidence cannot distinguish among them. 22:50 7 CAS D’OVNIS OFFICIELLES complètement fous ( CRNS, GEIPAN… je t’explique tout ) Channel: INDIANA SPACE · Views: 96.6K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 22 minutes Open on YouTube Radar and the airbase assumption Because the observation occurred at a military airbase, later retellings can easily acquire an implied radar component. The available official record does not support that embellishment. The gendarmerie file documents witness interviews and a site description, while GEIPAN’s review discusses weather, astronomy, possible satellites and the inability to verify historical aviation traffic. Neither published document reports a simultaneous radar contact corresponding to the light. [GEIPAN] cnes"},{"title":"From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle","url":"/haute-saone/","description":"Haute-Saône has a genuine but comparatively modest place in French UFO history. Its record is not dominated by a single nationally famous encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle Haute Saône has a genuine but comparatively modest place in French UFO history. Its record is not dominated by a single nationally famous encounter. Haute Saône has a genuine but comparatively modest place in French UFO history. Its record is not dominated by a single nationally famous encounter. haute saone Introduction The department’s most important feature is the presence of Luxeuil Saint Sauveur Air Base. Military and civilian aircraft provide obvious explanations for many unusual lights, yet they also complicate investigations when witnesses report something close to the base that does not seem to behave, sound or look like familiar traffic. The evidence therefore supports neither a hidden local “hotspot” nor the dismissal of every report. It shows a small, uneven archive in which most claims are weak or explainable, while a limited number retain genuine uncertainty. The 1954 wave reached Haute Saône France experienced an extraordinary surge of flying saucer reports during the autumn of 1954. Haute Saône contributed several stories to that wider wave, particularly around Jussey, Aillevillers and Varigney. These accounts are important as social and press history, but most survive through later catalogues and newspaper retellings rather than complete contemporary case files. That distinction matters. A story can be vivid without being evidentially strong. Many 1954 reports were collected years later by UFO writers, sometimes without named witnesses, original statements, weather checks or precise observing conditions. Jussey’s alleged landing and occupants The most dramatic Haute Saône story was said to have occurred near Jussey at about 7pm on 1 October 1954. Two young people reportedly saw a luminous white disc descend into a meadow. According to later published versions, two tall figures dressed in white emerged and gestured towards the witnesses, who fled. The episode became part of catalogues compiled by writers including Jacques Vallée, Charles Garreau and Raymond Lavier. However, the surviving account has serious weaknesses: the witnesses are unnamed, the location is imprecise, no contemporary physical traces are documented, and the story appears mainly through publications issued long after the event. Even a detailed specialist compilation notes the dependence on these secondary sources rather than presenting an original police statement or newspaper interview. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, October 1, Jussey, Haute Saône Patrick GrossIn 1969, ufologist Jacques Vallée indicated in his catalog of... Jussey is therefore best treated as local folklore generated within the 1954 wave , not as a verified close encounter. Its importance lies in showing how quickly the national imagery of discs and humanoid occupants entered provincial reporting. Varigney’s red light A better attested but still poorly documented report was placed at Varigney, near Dampierre lès Conflans, on the evening of 17 October 1954. Motorists reportedly stopped after seeing a bright red object or glow in fields beside the road. One man and his daughter approached before the light appeared to move rapidly towards them and rise. Later summaries differ in wording and detail, and no surviving technical investigation establishes the object’s distance, size or actual movement. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Des automobilistes se sont arrêtés sur la route D 10 quand ils ont remarqué un objet lumineux rouge lumineux... The reported experience could have involved a nearby light source, an astronomical object distorted by poor depth perception, an aircraft viewed almost head on or an embellished press account. Without independent records, none of these possibilities can be tested decisively. The industrial setting is sometimes overlooked. Varigney was a working foundry settlement in 1954, shortly before its major works closed in February 1955. [Bourgogne Franche Comté Heritage Portal] patrimoine.bourgognefranchecomte.fr forges de Varigney (Haute Saône), Bourgogne Franche Comté Heritage PortalUsine métallurgique dite forges de Varigney, actuellement...Acheté vers 1790 par Claude Antoine V... Local lighting, vehicles and industrial activity should therefore be considered before assuming that the reported glow was an isolated object in an otherwise dark landscape. Aillevillers and the meteor explanation A report from Aillevillers on the morning of 16 October described an elongated orange glow moving extremely fast. This sounds impressive when reproduced as a UFO sighting, but the wider evidence strongly favours a meteor or fireball. Contemporary UFO researcher Aimé Michel himself connected reports at the same time with a large meteor visible across France. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Reports: [Ref.Read more the 1954 French flap, October 16, Aillevillers, Haute Saône26 Oct 2019 — But as early as 1958, ufologist Aimé Michel had publishe... This is an instructive case because it demonstrates the value of geographical comparison. A brilliant object reported from many separated locations at the same time is more likely to have been a high altitude atmospheric or astronomical event than a low flying craft over one commune. Vesoul and the problem of date confusion A curious report from Vesoul was published in January 1954, months before the famous autumn wave. Newspaper material described a strange luminous object seen around dawn, while related observations elsewhere in Franche Comté resembled a red fireball with sparks and a bluish trail. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 9 janvier, Vesoul, Haute Saône.... soucoupe volante tout comme cigare comète comtois... La soucoupe... The language of “flying saucers” encouraged an exotic reading, but the short duration, fireball appearance and trailing light are typical of meteor observations. The case also warns against treating every report dated 1954 as part of the October flap. The French press was already using saucer terminology early in the year, and conventional celestial events could be absorbed into that vocabulary. Why the 1954 stories remain uncertain The historical cases are memorable, but they rarely meet modern investigative standards. Their main shortcomings recur across the department: Late or indirect sourcing: several stories are known chiefly through books or catalogues published years later. Incomplete witness information: names, exact positions, viewing directions and eyesight conditions are often absent. No instrumented evidence: there are no reliable photographs, radar records or recovered materials. Uncertain chronology: retellings sometimes alter times, distances or sequences of events. Strong cultural pressure: newspapers in autumn 1954 were actively seeking and publishing saucer stories from across France. None of this proves that witnesses invented what they saw. It means that an unusual experience cannot now be reconstructed with enough precision to distinguish between a distant astronomical object, aircraft, balloon, local light, misunderstanding or genuinely unidentified phenomenon. The old cases consequently have more value as evidence of a reporting wave than as proof of unusual vehicles operating over Haute Saône. Their concentration in late September and October reflects the timing of national publicity at least as clearly as it reflects any physical pattern. Visite base de la BA116 Luxeuil St Sauveur 11 07 2023 ba116 Channel: Spotters 70 Aviation Open on YouTube Luxeuil Air Base changes the local picture Haute Saône’s UFO history cannot be understood without Luxeuil Saint Sauveur Air Base, commonly known as BA 116. The military site developed from an early aviation ground used before the First World War and became a modern NATO standard base in the 1950s. It has operated successive generations of combat aircraft and remains a major feature of the department’s airspace. [Luxeuil Vosges Sud] luxeuil vosges sud.fr la base aerienne 116 dans le ciel luxovien depuis 1911 la base aerienne 116 dans le ciel luxovien depuis 1911 This creates a basic interpretive problem. Residents may be familiar with fighters, yet familiarity does not guarantee correct identification under cloud, at night or from an unexpected angle. Landing lights can appear stationary when an aircraft approaches directly. Turns can make lights vanish suddenly. Several aircraft in formation can resemble one large dark shape, while wind and atmospheric conditions can make jet noise seem weaker, delayed or displaced. Military activity also leaves genuine information gaps. Public flight tracking services do not show every operation, and investigators may have only limited access to detailed exercises. That"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-d6a020/","description":"Jussey, Varigney, Aillevillers and Vesoul reveal how vivid saucer stories mixed with meteors, local lights and weak sourcing.","breadcrumb":"From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave How Strong Were Haute Saone's 1954 UFO Reports? Haute Saone UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave How Strong Were Haute Saone's 1954 UFO Reports? Jussey, Varigney, Aillevillers and Vesoul reveal how vivid saucer stories mixed with meteors, local lights and weak sourcing. Jussey, Varigney, Aillevillers and Vesoul reveal how vivid saucer stories mixed with meteors, local lights and weak sourcing. event time window The Jussey and Varigney encounter stories Meteor explanations at Aillevillers and Vesoul Press culture, missing records and changing retellings haute saone 1954 wave Introduction Haute Saône’s 1954 UFO record looks impressive only until its cases are separated by source quality. The department produced two memorable close encounter stories — Jussey and Varigney — but neither is supported by a surviving public investigation file, named testimony collected at the time or documented physical evidence. By contrast, the lights reported at Vesoul, Aillevillers and Hurecourt fit large, multi department meteor events remarkably well. The result is not a single unexplained “wave” of equivalent incidents. It is a mixed historical cluster: ordinary astronomical phenomena reported in saucer language, dramatic stories repeatedly copied through UFO catalogues, and disputed claims whose later retellings became more definite than their foundations justified. This distinction matters because Haute Saône shows, in miniature, how France’s celebrated 1954 wave was assembled: genuine observations, mistaken interpretations, newspaper enthusiasm and poorly preserved testimony all entered the same record. The Jussey story rests on a missing foundation The most striking Haute Saône claim was placed near Jussey at about 7pm on 1 October 1954. In the familiar version, two young people saw a luminous white disc move through the sky, descend into a field and apparently land. Two very tall figures dressed in white emerged and gestured towards them. Frightened, the witnesses fled; later retellings add that the object departed soon afterwards. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 1, Jussey, Haute Saône... That is an unusually vivid story, but almost every element needed to assess it is absent. The witnesses remain anonymous. The precise field is unidentified. There is no publicly available gendarmerie statement, signed testimony, sketch, description of viewing conditions or record of traces at the alleged landing site. Even the duration is uncertain. One later database claimed an observation lasting more than 12 minutes, but that detail does not appear in the short early summaries and illustrates how catalogue entries can acquire unsupported precision as they are copied. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 1, Jussey, Haute Saône... The source trail is also revealing. Jacques Vallée published a brief catalogue version in the 1960s, while Charles Garreau and Raymond Lavier supplied a more conversational account in the 1970s. Only much later did researcher Jean Sider identify an alleged contemporary source: the Besançon newspaper Le Comtois of 5 October 1954. The surviving specialist compilation lists that newspaper item but does not reproduce it, so readers cannot presently compare the published clipping with the later wording. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 1, Jussey, Haute Saône... There is an additional complication. UFO historian Loren Gross wrote that Vallée had learnt that the case was a hoax. A later check of the cited 1964 magazine issue failed to locate Vallée making that statement, however. The alleged hoax finding therefore cannot safely be treated as established. Nor does the failure to verify it make the encounter authentic. It leaves Jussey in an awkward middle category: too poorly sourced to support a strong conclusion, but not cleanly debunked by the evidence currently available. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 1, Jussey, Haute Saône... A helicopter has sometimes been suggested because a lighted aircraft descending near two white clad crew members could, at a distance, supply the basic ingredients of the account. Yet no particular flight has been identified, and the reported sequence is too vague for a meaningful aircraft comparison. The most defensible judgement is therefore weak and unresolved , rather than confirmed, disproved or evidentially strong. Jussey matters less as proof of an extraordinary landing than as an example of narrative survival. A few dramatic sentences — disc, landing, tall occupants and gestures — were sufficiently memorable to circulate through books and international databases for decades, while the identities, original questions and local investigative context disappeared. Varigney is richer in detail but more deeply disputed The Varigney incident of 17 October 1954 initially appears much stronger. At about 8.30pm, motorists on the D10 near Dompierre lès Conflans reportedly noticed a bright red light standing in a pasture. They alerted people at a nearby café, including its proprietor, Mr Beuclair, his family and a level crossing keeper named Barrat. Beuclair and his daughter Jeanne supposedly approached the light, which moved towards them and stopped roughly 20 metres away. Later accounts describe a circular or hemispherical upper body, silence and parallel red and white rays emerging from underneath before the object departed rapidly. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 17 octobre, Varigney, Haute Saône... Compared with Jussey, this story offers a named family, several claimed witnesses, a road, a pasture and a sequence of actions. It also contains the kind of sensory detail that makes an account feel observationally strong. Yet its apparent precision mostly comes from books written after the event, each drawing on earlier UFO literature. The widely repeated summary in Vallée’s catalogue ultimately points back to Aimé Michel’s treatment, while later versions elaborate the scene without supplying an accessible contemporary interview or official report. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 17 octobre, Varigney, Haute Saône... The description of Beuclair is a warning sign. Some catalogues call him a policeman. Other versions describe him as the café proprietor, and a sceptical inquiry by Gérard Barthel and Jacques Brucker stated that he was actually a factory worker. Their account further claimed that another witness had confirmed the whole affair as a hoax. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 17 octobre, Varigney, Haute Saône... That sceptical conclusion is important, but it is not fully transparent. The published summary does not provide the confirming witness’s name, a transcript or enough investigative documentation for an independent reader to reproduce the finding. Barthel and Brucker were also controversial figures whose broad attack on French close encounter cases has itself been criticised for inadequately documented fieldwork. Their verdict cannot simply be ignored, but neither should “hoax” be presented as securely demonstrated from the surviving public record. Other ordinary explanations remain possible. A stationary red light in a field might have been attached to agricultural or industrial equipment, a vehicle, railway activity or a deliberately constructed prank. Varigney was a foundry settlement, and local roads, homes, railway infrastructure and industrial lighting make a completely isolated luminous object less certain than later summaries imply. Depth perception at night is notoriously unreliable when a light has no visible background, so estimates such as 20 metres, apparent approach and low altitude cannot be treated as measured values. There is also a meteorological balloon context worth remembering. Four days after Varigney, a regional newspaper reported that a radio sonde marked “U.S. Army” had been found hanging from a small balloon and parachute at Diénay in neighbouring Côte d’Or. This does not explain Varigney: the reported colour, movement and close approach do not match a straightforward identification. It does show, however, that unfamiliar airborne equipment was circulating in the wider region and could readily be interpreted through the language of “saucers”. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, Le Courrier de Saône et Loire, France, page 6, 21 octobre 1954... Varigney is consequently the strongest narrative in the departmental cluster, but not the strongest evidence. Its multiple claimed witnesses raise its interest above Jussey, while the contradictory occupations, missing original statements and disputed hoax finding sharply reduce confidence. It is best classified as unresolved in the surviving record, with a substantial"},{"title":"Villers Triangle","url":"/villers-triangle/","description":"The 2019 triangle report stands out for two witnesses and official review, yet aircraft activity near BA 116 complicates every interpretation.","breadcrumb":"From 1954 Saucer Stories to an Unresolved Triangle","level":2,"text":"Villers Triangle What Flew Over Villers lès Luxeuil in 2019? Haute Saone UFOs Villers Triangle Villers Triangle What Flew Over Villers lès Luxeuil in 2019? The 2019 triangle report stands out for two witnesses and official review, yet aircraft activity near BA 116 complicates every interpretation. The 2019 triangle report stands out for two witnesses and official review, yet aircraft activity near BA 116 complicates every interpretation. entity The witnesses' route and reported observations What the official investigation could establish Aircraft, formation lights and the unresolved remainder haute saone villers triangle Introduction The Villers lès Luxeuil sighting of 26 November 2019 is Haute Saône’s strongest modern unidentified aerial phenomenon case, but not because it proves that an extraordinary craft crossed the department. It stands out because two people travelling in separate cars described a large, dark, triangular form near their home; investigators reconstructed the route, obtained military and civilian traffic information, examined radar data and tested several conventional explanations. None fitted every reported detail. The central difficulty is the location. Villers lès Luxeuil lies close to Luxeuil Saint Sauveur Air Base, or BA 116, where Mirage 2000 5F fighters were operating that evening. A civilian Robin DR400 was also approaching the base. Aircraft activity therefore offers a serious explanation, not a speculative afterthought. Yet discrepancies in timing, apparent size, elevation, shape and especially sound prevented France’s official GEIPAN investigation from identifying either aircraft with confidence. The case is unresolved in the limited sense that the available evidence does not support a secure identification—not that an exotic origin has been established. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.2019 GEIPAN... The witnesses’ route and reported observations The witnesses were a married couple returning from Vesoul in separate vehicles after collecting one of their cars from a garage. The wife, designated T2 in the official file, drove ahead; her husband, T1, followed. Their journey produced two reported episodes, although only the second was seen by both people. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... Shortly after 5pm, near Saulx de Vesoul, T1 noticed a white light that he believed was beneath a low, completely overcast sky. He initially considered an aircraft coming towards him. According to his account, the light then appeared to rise vertically into the cloud, disappear and reappear several seconds later over another part of the scene. He flashed his headlights and telephoned his wife, but she did not see it. The road subsequently descended, ending his view. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... This first episode is weaker than the later triangle report. It had one witness, no photograph and no independent confirmation. Distance and movement were judged from a moving car without a reliable scale. T1 later suggested that the light and the object at Villers might have been the same phenomenon following a route across Haute Saône, but GEIPAN explicitly treated that as his personal interpretation rather than an established trajectory. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... The more important observation occurred as the couple reached Villers lès Luxeuil, about 13 to 15 minutes farther along their route. T2 slowed near a bend after noticing something above or ahead of her. T1 saw what had attracted her attention, pulled over, lowered his windows and switched off the radio to listen. Both described a dark triangular structure accompanied by a large white light and a smaller fixed red light. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... Their perspectives were not identical. T2 viewed the phenomenon through her windscreen for roughly eight to ten seconds while continuing to drive. She thought it was nearly stationary and that her car passed beneath or beyond it. She described visible edges, an aluminium coloured thickness on two sides and an interior that seemed strangely empty or transparent. She also said it appeared to tilt or roll before she lost sight of it. T1, who had stopped, watched for nearer 20 seconds and perceived a very slow movement from right to left until a building obstructed his view. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... Both accounts emphasised the same striking features: an apparently large triangle, a dominant white light, a smaller red light and no obvious engine noise. T1 estimated an altitude below 100 metres and a speed below about 50 kilometres per hour. Those figures, however, were visual impressions rather than measured values. Without knowing the object’s true size, an observer cannot reliably derive its distance, altitude or speed from angular movement alone. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... The witnesses’ drawings and later reconstruction gave investigators more material than is available in most local sighting reports. They also exposed inconsistencies. T2’s later description of which way the triangle pointed did not fully match her original drawing, while tests indicated that both witnesses tended to overestimate apparent angular size. GEIPAN considered such overestimation common, particularly when an unexpected sight is emotionally impressive. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... These differences do not show that the couple invented the event. Brief observations from moving vehicles commonly produce divergent geometry. They do, however, limit how literally details such as “directly overhead”, “less than 100 metres” or “too large to cover with an outstretched hand” can be used in testing candidate aircraft. What the official investigation could establish The initial enquiries began quickly. Two investigators associated with a UFO research group met the witnesses on 10 December 2019, revisited the locations, took photographs and measurements, and recorded their accounts. The couple submitted GEIPAN technical questionnaires on 17 December. GEIPAN—the unit of France’s National Centre for Space Studies that investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena—then assumed responsibility for the case, with one of the original investigators continuing the work in an official capacity. Further interviews in 2022 attempted to clarify the chronology. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... The final investigation report, dated January 2025, runs to 41 pages. It incorporates site reconstructions, weather and astronomical checks, aircraft tracks, radar information and enquiries with the air gendarmerie at BA 116. That depth makes Villers lès Luxeuil unusual within Haute Saône’s UFO record, where many older stories survive only as short press reports or later catalogue entries. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete725 VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.201920 Jan 2025 — Compte tenu de l'étrangeté persistante après enquête et de la bonne consistance du... Several basic points were reasonably well supported: The sky was fully or almost fully overcast, with a low cloud layer, during evening twilight. Two people reported seeing the second phenomenon from separate cars, though they discussed it immediately afterwards and therefore cannot be treated as wholly independent witnesses. A civilian light aircraft crossed the relevant field of view at low altitude while approaching BA 116. Four Mirage 2000 5F fighters were involved in an exercise beginning around the same period. No additional witness, photograph, video or physical effect was found. Neither the crew of the civilian aircraft nor another known observer reported an unusual object. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLERS LES LUXEUIL (70) 26.11.2019 GEIPAN... Chronology became the investigation’s most troublesome variable. The questionnaires placed the Villers observation at approximately 17:20, but later reconstruction offered"},{"title":"Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained","url":"/why-haute-savoies-ufo-stories-rarely/","description":"Haute-Savoie has produced a steady stream of unusual-sky reports, but its documented UFO history is more revealing for the number of cases later explained...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained Haute Savoie has produced a steady stream of unusual sky reports, but its documented UFO history is more revealing for the number of cases later explained... Haute Savoie has produced a steady stream of unusual sky reports, but its documented UFO history is more revealing for the number of cases later explained than for any single landmark mystery. The official record includes observations from Annecy, Annemasse, Morzine, Rumilly, Sallanches, Sciez, Thônes and the Mont Blanc area. haute savoie Introduction The best known local episode came in May 2013, when separate witnesses reported strange objects around Mont Blanc and Chamonix. It briefly looked like a regional cluster, yet follow up reporting weakened the more extraordinary interpretation: one photographed object was identified as an aircraft, while another report rested on a distant, short observation by a single witness. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc?18 May 2013 — Charles Inderschit s'est rendu à la gendarmerie de Sallanches pour témoigner de ce... Published: May 2013 Taken as a whole, Haute Savoie’s record is a useful case study in how mountainous terrain, busy airspace, bright astronomical objects and modern airborne devices can turn an initially puzzling sight into an apparently structured craft. What the official record actually contains France’s principal public body for investigating unusual aerial reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects witness accounts, sometimes alongside police or gendarmerie statements, and compares them with astronomical, meteorological, aviation and satellite information. Its database is therefore not a register of alien spacecraft. It is a collection of reports that began as unidentified observations, including many later resolved through ordinary explanations. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN’s classification system is important when reading Haute Savoie cases: A means the phenomenon has been identified without ambiguity. B means an explanation is considered highly probable. C means the case cannot be properly analysed because the information is insufficient or unreliable. D is reserved for reports that remain unexplained despite evidence judged adequate for investigation. Modern D cases may be divided into D1 and D2 according to their strangeness and evidential consistency. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d baisse cas d This distinction prevents a common misunderstanding. A category C report is not strong evidence of an extraordinary event. It means that the case file is too poor to decide what happened. Likewise, a B classification does not claim absolute proof, but indicates that the proposed conventional explanation fits the available evidence well. The Haute Savoie entries visible in GEIPAN’s searchable archive are dominated by A, B and C classifications. Examples include an identified weather or scientific balloon over Annemasse in 2007, a probable meteor over Cranves Sales in 2012, a probable fireball over Bons en Chablais later that year, a laser at Le Grand Bornand in 2014, a planet seen from Saint Jorioz in 2015 and an under documented observation at Annecy in 2018. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The official record therefore does not support the idea that Haute Savoie contains a recognised concentration of well evidenced unexplained cases. Its stronger pattern is investigative attrition: once enough information becomes available, many reports move towards familiar aerial or astronomical causes. The Mont Blanc reports of May 2013 The department’s most memorable press driven episode began in mid May 2013, when a witness reported lights or objects over the Mont Blanc area. According to local coverage, Charles Inderschit took his account to the gendarmerie at Sallanches. The brigade told the newspaper that it had received only one report of that kind, which it considered insufficient on its own to trigger an alert to GEIPAN. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc?18 May 2013 — Charles Inderschit s'est rendu à la gendarmerie de Sallanches pour témoigner de ce... Published: May 2013 That detail matters because the early wording suggested that several UFOs might have crossed one of Europe’s most closely watched mountain landscapes. Yet the available evidence did not establish a large, independently corroborated event. There was no reported radar confirmation, no sequence of observations from separated locations and no published aviation record showing an unidentified object entering controlled airspace. The press quickly raised sky lanterns as a possible explanation. Small paper lanterns carrying a flame had already caused mass UFO reports elsewhere in the wider Alpine region, including a prominent cluster in neighbouring Savoie during the summer of 2012. Their warm lights, slow drift and tendency to appear in groups can create the impression of silent craft flying in formation. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. That suggestion was plausible but not proven for the Sallanches report. A single distant observation rarely contains enough detail to distinguish lanterns from aircraft lights, balloons or other illuminated objects. What weakened the Mont Blanc story was not a decisive debunking of every reported light, but the absence of the supporting evidence expected from a major aerial incident. A second report followed near Chamonix on 27 May. A witness described a white object crossing the sky above the Mont Blanc massif between the Aiguilles de Chamonix and the Dôme du Goûter. The sighting lasted roughly eight to ten seconds. The observer estimated that the object was beneath high cloud at an altitude of about 6,000 to 7,000 metres, but the accompanying photograph showed little more than a small white point against a blue sky. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréOVNI / HAUTE SAVOIE Un étrange “machin” dans le ciel de Chamonix lundi OVNI / HAUTE SAVOIE Un étrange “machin” dans le ciel de Chamonix lundi... À cette di... Within a day, follow up reporting concluded that the object was an aircraft. The episode illustrates a recurrent problem in UFO photography: a distant aeroplane viewed almost head on, without a visible fuselage or wings, may appear as a bright compact shape. A still image can remove the motion and changing perspective that would make the identification obvious to an observer at closer range. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréOVNI/HAUTE SAVOIE Le “machin” n’était qu’un avion OVNI/HAUTE SAVOIE Le “machin” n’était qu’un avion... La photo a été prise depuis Chamonix, en direction... Later reporting therefore weakened, rather than strengthened, the idea of a Mont Blanc UFO wave. The May incidents remain part of local folklore because of their dramatic setting, but the public record does not show a coherent, multi witness event surviving investigation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why Haute Savoie produces convincing false alarms Haute Savoie has several characteristics that can make ordinary objects appear highly unusual. These do not explain every report automatically, but they help account for recurring descriptions of silent lights, sudden movement and objects seeming to hover over mountain ridges. Mountain horizons distort distance. A light seen above a dark ridge may be nearby, many kilometres away or vastly farther in space. Without visible landmarks at the same distance, witnesses can easily misjudge an object’s altitude, size and speed. An aircraft moving towards the observer may seem stationary; one turning away can appear to vanish abruptly. Air traffic is abundant and varied. The department lies near Geneva’s international airport and contains active local aviation around Annecy, Annemasse and the Alpine valleys. Passenger jets, private aircraft, helicopters, gliders, paragliders and mountain rescue operations may all be visible against uncluttered sky. Their lights can appear separated or geometrically arranged when the body of the aircraft is hidden by darkness, haze or viewing angle. The terrain blocks sound. Reports often emphasise that an object was silent. In mountainous country, however, wind direction, distance and intervening slopes can prevent aircraft noise from reaching the observer. Silence is therefore useful descriptive information but not, by itself, evidence against an aeroplane or helicopter. Bright objects are isolated"},{"title":"Official Records","url":"/official-records/","description":"GEIPAN files show that balloons, meteors, planets, satellites, lasers and aircraft account for many reported UFOs in Haute-Savoie.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained","level":2,"text":"Official Records What Haute Savoie's UFO Files Actually Reveal Haute Savoie UFOs Official Records Official Records What Haute Savoie's UFO Files Actually Reveal GEIPAN files show that balloons, meteors, planets, satellites, lasers and aircraft account for many reported UFOs in Haute Savoie. GEIPAN files show that balloons, meteors, planets, satellites, lasers and aircraft account for many reported UFOs in Haute Savoie. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies identified, probable and weak cases Recurring explanations across towns and Alpine valleys Why category C does not mean genuinely unexplained haute savoie official records Introduction Haute Savoie ’s official UFO files reveal a pattern that is more instructive than sensational. Reports that began as strange lights or apparently structured objects were often reclassified after investigators checked astronomical positions, aircraft activity, meteor reports, balloons, satellites, weather conditions and local light sources. The resulting explanations include Jupiter and Venus, the International Space Station, aircraft formations, fireballs, balloons and a nightclub laser projected onto cloud. Other files remain undecided not because they contain compelling evidence of an unknown craft, but because the witness information is too incomplete or unreliable to test. This distinction matters. France’s official GEIPAN archive is not a catalogue of confirmed exotic objects; it records observations that witnesses could not identify at the time. In Haute Savoie, the strongest recurring lesson is that “unidentified by the witness” and “unexplained after investigation” are very different conclusions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr na reported by one or more direct witnesses of the...Read more... How GEIPAN separates explained, probable and weak cases GEIPAN, the investigation and information group operated by the French space agency CNES, collects direct witness reports and may supplement them with photographs, videos, sketches, police or gendarmerie statements and information from aviation, astronomy and meteorology. Its formal assessment considers two broad factors: the remaining strangeness after ordinary explanations have been tested, and the “consistency” of the file — meaning the quantity and reliability of the available information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr mitted and collected during the investigation. GEIPAN methodology... The letters attached to cases describe the result of that process: Category A: the phenomenon has been identified clearly. Category B: an ordinary explanation is considered highly probable, although not demonstrated beyond every possible doubt. Category C: the phenomenon cannot be identified because the evidence is inadequate. Category D: the phenomenon remains unidentified despite an investigation based on sufficiently useful evidence. The difference between C and D is essential when reading Haute Savoie’s files. Both may lack a final identification, but for opposite reasons. A D case has resisted explanation after meaningful analysis. A C case has not supplied enough dependable material for meaningful analysis in the first place. GEIPAN can revisit either category when new evidence appears. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr mitted and collected during the investigation. GEIPAN methodology... CNES’s national figures also put the local archive in perspective. Its current project page says that roughly a quarter of recorded phenomena are clearly identified, about two fifths are probably identified, nearly a third cannot be identified for lack of data, and only a small minority remain unidentified after investigation. Those are national rather than Haute Savoie percentages, but they explain why an archive search produces many A, B and C files without implying a large body of strong mysteries. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Ordinary objects repeatedly became extraordinary reports Haute Savoie’s geography helps familiar objects look unfamiliar. Mountain ridges obstruct normal reference points, valleys restrict the apparent field of movement, and clouds can conceal the source of a beam or the body of an aircraft. Distant lights may appear motionless, while a bright planet seen through thin cloud can seem to pulse, change colour or follow a road. The official files show several different versions of this basic problem. Planets and satellites mistaken for nearby lights A report from Saint Jorioz on 10 September 2010 was classified A and identified as Jupiter. Another Saint Jorioz observation, made on 4 July 2015, was also classified A as a planet. At Annemasse, a sighting on 8 April 2012 was attributed to Venus. These records demonstrate why a brilliant point of light can produce a sincere UFO report even when it is effectively stationary in astronomical terms. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANSAINT JORIOZ (74) 10.09.2010 10/09/2010 A Haute Savoie Jupiter 17/03/2011 2010 09 10... Published: September 10, 2010 The mountainous view can strengthen the illusion. Without a visible horizon, witnesses may underestimate the object’s distance and treat small movements of their own vehicle or viewpoint as motion by the light. Atmospheric turbulence near ridges can also make a planet shimmer or change colour. GEIPAN’s identification does not mean that witnesses fabricated these effects; it means that the reported direction, time and appearance matched a known astronomical object. The archive records a similarly clear identification at Annecy le Vieux on 13 June 2011, where the light was classified A as the International Space Station. A satellite or orbital station can be especially puzzling because it moves silently, remains visible for only a few minutes and may fade abruptly when it enters Earth’s shadow. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons that seemed controlled or structured Balloons recur in the department’s official record. The Annemasse case of 30 June 2007 was classified A as a balloon, as was another Annemasse report from 22 January 2022. An Annecy le Vieux observation from 20 October 2002 received a B classification, with a child’s balloon considered the probable explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This category is broader than the familiar round party balloon. Reflective foil balloons can flash brightly, change apparent shape as they rotate and travel in ways that seem deliberate when different layers of air carry them in different directions. Scientific or weather balloons may be seen at great altitude, where their lack of obvious sound and slow movement make judging their size almost impossible. The important evidential point is that investigators do not need to prove that an object looked ordinary. They need to show that its position, direction of travel, appearance and duration are compatible with a documented or physically plausible balloon. A classification of B records that fit while preserving some uncertainty. Meteors reported as craft or fiery objects Two 2012 Haute Savoie reports were classified B as bright meteoric events. The observation at Bons en Chablais on 16 September was attributed to a fireball, while the Cranves Sales report of 17 December was also classified as a fireball. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Bright meteors frequently generate dramatic descriptions because they develop quickly and offer witnesses little time to judge what they are seeing. Fragmentation can resemble several lights moving together. A luminous trail may be interpreted as exhaust, while perspective can make a steep atmospheric path appear almost horizontal. Colour is also unreliable: the object, its ionised trail, cloud cover and the observer’s visual response can all influence whether it appears white, green, orange or red. For investigators, the decisive evidence is often not one witness’s estimate of size or distance but agreement between reports made over a wide area. A phenomenon described independently from separated locations at a similar time is more likely to be a high altitude meteor than a low object moving over one Haute Savoie town. The Grand Bornand lights show how local checks change a case The 7 January 2014 Grand Bornand report is one of the clearest demonstrations of official investigative reasoning. A witness saw around fifteen blue or white lights moving rapidly and silently in the sky towards the south. On description alone, the display could sound like a formation of manoeuvring objects. GEIPAN instead identified a nearby source at La Clusaz, about four kilometres from the observation point. A nightclub there used a laser that projected a rapidly circling white spot. The reported lights disappeared when the sky cleared,"},{"title":"Mont Blanc","url":"/mont-blanc/","description":"The May 2013 reports looked like a regional UFO cluster, but later evidence reduced them to one weak sighting and one aircraft.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Savoie's UFO Stories Rarely Stay Unexplained","level":2,"text":"Mont Blanc Did UFOs Really Cross Mont Blanc in 2013? Haute Savoie UFOs Mont Blanc Mont Blanc Did UFOs Really Cross Mont Blanc in 2013? The May 2013 reports looked like a regional UFO cluster, but later evidence reduced them to one weak sighting and one aircraft. The May 2013 reports looked like a regional UFO cluster, but later evidence reduced them to one weak sighting and one aircraft. event time window The Sallanches report and the first press claims The Chamonix photograph identified as an aircraft Why the alleged UFO wave weakened under scrutiny haute savoie mont blanc Introduction The supposed Mont Blanc UFO wave of May 2013 did not survive close examination. Two episodes reported within little more than a fortnight created the impression that unusual craft might be crossing the massif: red lights near Domancy, outside Sallanches, followed by a bright white object photographed from Chamonix. The first was traced to sky lanterns released at a wedding. The second was identified in follow up reporting as sunlight reflecting from an aircraft. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? The episode matters within Haute Savoie ’s UFO history because it shows how a “wave” can form through timing and publicity rather than through connected events. Both reports were initially puzzling, both were framed through the language of UFOs, and both concerned the visually dramatic Mont Blanc area. Yet they involved different objects, different conditions and separate conventional explanations. Later information weakened, rather than strengthened, the idea of an extraordinary regional phenomenon. 14:41 GEIPAN : dans les coulisses de l’organisme qui étudie les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés Channel: france tv · Views: 4.5K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 14 minutes Open on YouTube The Sallanches report and the first press claims The story began with an observation made at about 11.45 pm on Saturday 11 May 2013. Charles Inderschit, a resident of Domancy, said that he and his 12 year old son saw numerous red luminous balls near a chapel on the road linking Sallanches and Le Fayet. He described the lights as silent and apparently suspended in the air before they moved vertically and disappeared. The observation lasted roughly one minute. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? The reported number was striking. Inderschit spoke of around 30 lights, while photographs supplied to the newspaper showed luminous marks against the night sky. He also claimed that his camera stopped working afterwards, which added an apparently mysterious detail to the account. However, there was no independent evidence that the malfunction had any connection with the lights. A broken camera after an observation may be memorable, but without technical examination it does not establish electromagnetic interference or any other unusual effect. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Inderschit reported the incident to the Sallanches gendarmerie. The officers told Le Dauphiné Libéré that it was the only report of its kind they had received and that the information was insufficient to alert GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that examines reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. CNES likewise confirmed that it had not been notified. The mayor of Domancy had not heard of other observations when contacted by the newspaper. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Those details substantially limit the strength of the original case. Two related witnesses saw the event together, but there was no cluster of independent observers reporting from different positions, no radar or aviation evidence, and no official investigation capable of reconstructing the lights’ direction, altitude or distance. The witnesses’ impression that the objects were stationary or moving vertically was necessarily based on viewing small lights against a dark sky with few reliable distance markers. The wedding lantern explanation The mystery was resolved on 18 May, only hours after the initial newspaper coverage appeared. A newly married man contacted the Sallanches newsroom and said that his wedding had taken place at the Tour Carrée in Domancy on 11 May, in the same area and at the relevant time. He and his guests had released approximately 15 sky lanterns. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. Sky lanterns are small paper balloons lifted by heated air from a flame. Seen at night, they can resemble orange or red balls travelling silently in formation. Their motion depends on local air currents: individual lanterns may drift apart, appear almost stationary from one viewing angle, rise suddenly or vanish as their flames go out. That behaviour closely matches the central features of the Domancy account without requiring an exotic object. There is a discrepancy between the estimated numbers: the witnesses counted about 30 lights, whereas the wedding party reportedly launched around 15 lanterns. This does not overturn the identification. Estimates made during a brief night time observation can be unreliable, especially when lights move, disappear behind terrain or cloud, and reappear. Camera movement or long exposure can also make individual lights seem elongated or multiply represented. More importantly, the wedding account supplied a specific source at the correct place, date and time. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTE SAVOIE. Des Ovni ont ils survolé le Mont Blanc? The explanation was not merely a generic suggestion that the lights “might have been lanterns”. It came from someone claiming direct responsibility for releasing them. That makes the Domancy case considerably stronger as an identification than a retrospective guess based only on visual resemblance. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The Chamonix photograph identified as an aircraft A second report emerged on Monday 27 May, roughly two weeks after the Domancy sighting. At precisely 12.29 pm, according to the timestamp on his mobile phone photograph, a Chamonix meteorologist noticed a white object crossing the sky above the Mont Blanc massif. He observed it between the Aiguilles de Chamonix and the Dôme du Goûter. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The witness said that the object moved slightly faster than an airliner and made no distinctive sound. He estimated its altitude at between 6,000 and 7,000 metres, below the cirrus clouds he had been monitoring. The entire observation lasted only eight to ten seconds. One photograph showed a small bright point, but the object occupied too few pixels to reveal a reliable shape or structure. No second witness was identified in the press report. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The witness’s profession made the report interesting, but expertise does not remove the limitations created by distance and brevity. Estimating the speed and altitude of an unidentified point requires knowing its size and range. Without that information, a relatively large distant aircraft can appear similar to a smaller, nearer object. Sound is equally unreliable in mountainous surroundings: distance, wind and the delay between light and sound can make an aircraft seem silent during a short observation. Later on 29 May, Le Dauphiné Libéré published an identification offered by Jacques Fabry, who studied aircraft condensation effects. He argued that the bright point was sunlight reflected from an aircraft’s fuselage, with the glare enhanced by moisture or condensation particles associated with the aircraft. The newspaper consequently reported that the “strange object” was an aeroplane. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréOVNI/HAUTE SAVOIE. Le “machin” n’était qu’un avion Le Dauphiné LibéréOVNI/HAUTE SAVOIE. Le “machin” n’était qu’un avion The published follow up did not provide a flight number, radar plot or independently reproduced optical analysis. The identification should therefore be described as persuasive rather than demonstrated beyond all doubt. Nevertheless, an aircraft is a much better fit than an unknown craft: the object followed a rapid crossing path at an estimated cruising altitude, appeared in daylight, was represented"},{"title":"Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues","url":"/why-haute-viennes-ufo-record-still/","description":"Haute-Vienne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues Haute Vienne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Haute Vienne has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by repeated landings, radar chases or a single celebrated “close encounter”. haute vienne Introduction The central lesson is therefore not that Haute Vienne forms an unexplained UFO hotspot. It is that the department provides a useful miniature of French UFO investigation: an unusual observation may initially sound extraordinary, but its evidential value depends on the number of witnesses, the quality of contemporary records, photographs or video, weather and astronomical data, and whether aircraft movements can be reconstructed. Most Haute Vienne cases weaken under that scrutiny. The 11 April 1953 Limoges sighting remains the notable exception, although “unresolved” does not mean extraterrestrial. The Limoges case that still resists explanation The most important case in Haute Vienne’s official UFO record occurred at 9.10 pm on 11 April 1953. Three people at a French Air Force storage facility in Limoges reportedly watched a large red orange light move silently across the sky. According to the account preserved by GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, an Air Force captain described the light travelling east to west, reversing direction and following an irregular, angular path for about six minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.195316 Jan 2017 — LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953. Date d'observation. 11/04/1953. Région. Limousin. Département. Haute... That movement is what makes the report unusual. A conventional aircraft could change course, but the witnesses apparently saw no navigation lights, heard no engine and described frequent abrupt alterations in direction. A meteor would not reverse course, while a stationary star or planet should not trace an extended broken path unless the perceived movement resulted from witness or reference point effects. GEIPAN currently classifies the incident as category D, meaning that it remains unidentified after assessment. The organisation describes the phenomenon as strange to very strange and regards the case as having moderate to strong consistency. The surviving documentation includes a formal report rather than merely a recollection posted decades later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.195316 Jan 2017 — LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953. Date d'observation. 11/04/1953. Région. Limousin. Département. Haute... Several limitations nevertheless prevent the case from becoming conclusive evidence of an extraordinary craft: The observation produced no photograph, film, radar return or physical trace. The description available to the public is brief and does not reconstruct the witnesses’ exact positions and sightlines in modern detail. Angular movements reported in a dark sky are difficult to translate into real speed or distance when an object’s size and altitude are unknown. The original investigators could not consult the modern collections of satellite, civil aircraft and meteorological data now used in comparable cases. The witnesses’ military setting makes the report more interesting, not infallible. Professional observers can provide careful accounts, but they remain subject to visual misperception, especially when judging an isolated light without a stable frame of reference. The proper conclusion is that the Limoges event is unresolved on the surviving evidence—not that it demonstrated unknown technology or an extraterrestrial visit. The late 1970s cluster: more reports, weaker foundations Haute Vienne’s official catalogue becomes noticeably busier between 1978 and 1979. Cases were recorded at Pierre Buffière, Javerdat, Saint Junien, Châteauponsac and Le Vigen. At first glance this resembles a local “flap”, the UFO term for a short period containing several reports. Closer examination shows that the incidents were not descriptions of one repeated phenomenon and did not produce a coherent flight path, common object or linked group of witnesses. At Pierre Buffière on 22 July 1978, an observation was ultimately classified as category A—identified with high confidence—as Venus. At Javerdat on 14 November, the reported phenomenon was judged probably to have been an atmospheric re entry. The Le Vigen sighting of 20 December 1979, involving an apparently stationary orange object followed by a rapid departure, was also reclassified as an astronomical misidentification. These outcomes illustrate how a bright celestial object may appear to move when watched through cloud, haze or an unstable foreground. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953, 11/04/1953, D; PIERRE BUFFIERE (87) 22.07.1978, 22/07/1... The Saint Junien report of 25 November 1978 is less satisfactory. It concerned a luminous object said to make rapid movements, but GEIPAN classified it as category C because the available information was insufficient for a reliable conclusion. That distinction matters: category C does not mean that investigators found an anomalous object. It means that the evidence was too incomplete to identify—or meaningfully test—the observation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv The Châteauponsac animal report The most dramatic story from this period came from Châteauponsac in November 1979. A witness reported seeing, at an estimated distance of about 100 metres, an object carrying several lights arranged in a diamond like pattern. It was said to depart northwards while leaving whitish smoke. Sheep in a field roughly 600 metres away reportedly became highly agitated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Animal reactions often give a UFO story an appearance of independent confirmation. In practice, they are difficult to interpret unless the behaviour was recorded contemporaneously and alternative disturbances can be excluded. Sheep may react to vehicles, dogs, unfamiliar lights, human movement, weather or other noises unnoticed by the principal witness. There is no published indication that veterinary findings, measurable residues or instrument records established a connection between the animals and the reported object. The official catalogue marks the case as not classified rather than category D. It should therefore not be promoted as one of France’s officially unexplained cases. Its vivid features make it an important part of local UFO history, but its evidential base is thin. Taken together, the 1978–79 reports do not establish a concentrated incursion over Haute Vienne. They are better understood as a mixed group: several identifiable astronomical or atmospheric events, one under documented light report and one dramatic but unverified close range claim. How later investigations found ordinary causes The modern Haute Vienne files are particularly valuable because they show how an investigation can move beyond witness description. GEIPAN compares reported direction, duration and appearance with meteorological records, astronomical objects, photographs, video, flight tracking information and, in some cases, French military radar data. GEIPAN itself stresses that its role is to collect, analyse and archive reports—not to prove extraterrestrial visitation or search for futuristic technology. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 7:47 UFOs: Retired Navy Commander Describes His Sighting In 2004 The Overview NBC News Channel: NBC News · Views: 3.1M · Uploaded: April 2021 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Saint Junien’s “four lights” in the clouds On 19 February 2022, a motorist near Saint Junien saw four brilliant white lights in a low, heavily clouded sky. He also perceived what looked like a grey metallic section emerging from the clouds. The event sounded structured and mechanical, and it had an evidential advantage absent from most historical cases: the vehicle’s dashboard camera recorded it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The recording did not strengthen the craft interpretation. GEIPAN concluded that the lights were very probably bright openings and sunlight reflections within a changing cloud layer. The apparent pulsing and metallic looking segment could be reproduced by alterations in cloud brightness and movement. The case received a category B classification, indicating a probable identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case is a useful warning about photographs and video. A recording is not automatically proof of an object; sometimes it reveals that the witness’s interpretation was understandable but mistaken. A lantern"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-b786aa/","description":"Later reports show how planets, clouds, lanterns and atmospheric events can create convincing but misleading UFO impressions.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases How Haute Vienne's UFO Mysteries Were Explained Haute Vienne UFOs Explained Cases Explained Cases How Haute Vienne's UFO Mysteries Were Explained Later reports show how planets, clouds, lanterns and atmospheric events can create convincing but misleading UFO impressions. Later reports show how planets, clouds, lanterns and atmospheric events can create convincing but misleading UFO impressions. dataset evidence The mixed sighting cluster of 1978 and 1979 What photographs and video changed The recurring causes behind unusual lights haute vienne explained cases Introduction Most reported UFOs in Haute Vienne did not remain mysterious once investigators compared witness descriptions with astronomy, weather, flight paths and the behaviour of familiar objects. The department’s records include Venus mistaken for a hovering light, a probable atmospheric re entry, the rising Sun distorted by broken cloud, balloons carried by the wind, sunlight shining through cloud gaps, an airliner seen from an unfamiliar angle and a sky lantern whose flame went out. These explanations matter because several observations initially sounded far stranger than their eventual causes. The pattern is not that witnesses invented what they saw. In many cases, they accurately reported a striking light or shape but misjudged its distance, movement or physical form. Haute Vienne therefore offers a useful lesson in how an unidentified sighting becomes identified: not through ridicule, but by matching direction, time, weather, apparent motion and recorded images against testable alternatives. OVNI en Picardie Épisode 3 Les agents du GEIPAN Channel: France 3 Hauts de France Open on YouTube The mixed sighting cluster of 1978 and 1979 Haute Vienne’s late 1970s reports are sometimes treated as a small local wave. Five official cases were recorded at Pierre Buffière, Javerdat, Saint Junien, Châteauponsac and Le Vigen between July 1978 and December 1979. Yet the cases do not describe a single recurring craft or a linked route across the department. They range from astronomical misidentification to incomplete testimony and one genuinely unresolved incident. At Pierre Buffière on 22 July 1978, the reported object was ultimately identified as Venus. Bright planets are unusually effective UFO lookalikes because observers often assume that any intense light must be relatively close. Without visible stars, buildings or a clear horizon to provide scale, a planet can appear to hover, drift or react to the observer’s own movement. GEIPAN places the Pierre Buffière case in category A, its classification for an observation explained with high confidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr PIERRE BUFFIERE (87) 22.07.1978 Classification: A Department: Haute Vienne Date of update: 28/04/2010... The Javerdat report of 14 November 1978 was assessed as a probable atmospheric re entry. Re entering space debris can fragment into several bright points, change colour, leave glowing trails and remain visible for much longer than an ordinary meteor. From the ground, the fragments may look like a formation of controlled objects even though they are following the same ballistic path. The official Haute Vienne catalogue describes the Javerdat observation specifically as a probable re entry rather than an unexplained vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv The Saint Junien case of 25 November 1978 reached a different outcome. A rapidly moving luminous object was reported, but the surviving information was insufficient for a dependable reconstruction. GEIPAN classifies it as category C, meaning that the evidence is too incomplete or unreliable to reach a sound conclusion. This is an important distinction: category C does not indicate an extraordinary object. It means that the case cannot be properly tested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Châteauponsac, dated to November 1979, remains the strongest unresolved case in this small cluster. The account involved a diamond shaped object and alleged abnormal behaviour among sheep. GEIPAN lists it as category D, rather than offering an ordinary identification. Its presence prevents the period from being described as wholly explained, but it does not turn the other reports into corroborating sightings. The dates, appearances and circumstances are too different to establish a shared cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Le Vigen observation shows most clearly how later review could transform an apparently extraordinary report. On the morning of 20 December 1979, three witnesses saw a bright red orange mass in a cloudy south eastern sky. Its reported form varied between rectangular, oval and circular. It appeared stationary, then seemed to rotate into a cigar shape and depart rapidly with a bright trail. The case was originally treated as unexplained, but GEIPAN later re examined it using improved software and accumulated investigative experience. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The revised explanation was the rising Sun seen through an uneven opening in low cloud. The Sun was in the correct direction and at the low elevation needed to produce the reported red colour. Moving cloud altered the visible portion of the solar disc, creating changing shapes, while the closing gap made the light appear to accelerate into the cloud. Witness statements themselves contained clues: the object was compared with the Sun, was intermittently hidden by dark cloud and disappeared into the cloud layer. GEIPAN reclassified the incident as category A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case is especially instructive because it had three witnesses and formal police statements. Good documentation preserved the observation, but it also preserved enough detail to solve it. Multiple sincere witnesses can share the same mistaken interpretation when all are looking at the same unfamiliar optical effect. What photographs and video changed Cameras did not automatically settle Haute Vienne sightings in favour of something exotic. Instead, later photographic and video cases often gave investigators more opportunities to identify ordinary causes. A particularly clear example occurred at Saint Junien on 19 February 2022. A driver saw four intensely bright, apparently static lights and a grey silver segment emerging from a low, heavily clouded sky. The witness described the feature as a “singularity”, and the scene was recorded by a dashboard camera. At first glance, the combination of pulsing lights and a solid looking grey form could suggest a partly concealed object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Frame by frame examination weakened that interpretation. The bright areas were visible at moments before the witness reached the point where the sighting became striking, and they did not behave differently from other breaks in the cloud. GEIPAN concluded that the lights were probably openings and sunlight reflections within a moving cloud layer. Changes in brightness produced the apparent pulsing, while the cloud structure supplied the impression of a metallic surface. The case was classified B, meaning that an ordinary explanation was considered very probable, though not demonstrated with absolute certainty. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The footage mattered because it recorded more than the witness’s most memorable few seconds. Human attention tends to isolate the moment when an appearance becomes unusual; continuous video can reveal that the phenomenon developed gradually or resembled surrounding features. In this instance, the camera did not show an object emerging from cloud. It showed how shifting light and cloud could be perceived as one. Photographs also assisted the investigation of a light seen at Beynac on 15 August 2023. The witness watched a slowly moving yellow light with an orange plume for about 40 seconds. It then appeared to shrink to a smaller point and vanish abruptly. The description, enlarged photograph, slow movement and local wind direction were all consistent with a sky lantern. Its sudden disappearance could be explained by the extinction of the flame. GEIPAN classified the case B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Here, magnification did not reveal a detailed machine. It showed a configuration compatible with a small flame illuminating a lightweight lantern. The weather evidence also mattered: the observed direction of travel agreed with weak winds recorded at a station about eight kilometres away. That agreement provided a physical explanation for the movement without requiring powered flight. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Images are therefore most useful when they can be combined with time, direction and environmental data. A blurred bright patch alone may add little. A photograph linked to wind records, cloud"},{"title":"Limoges 1953","url":"/limoges-1953/","description":"Three military-site witnesses reported a silent red-orange light whose abrupt turns still resist a confident explanation.","breadcrumb":"Why Haute Vienne's UFO Record Still Intrigues","level":2,"text":"Limoges 1953 Why the 1953 Limoges Sighting Remains Unresolved Haute Vienne Limoges 1953 Limoges 1953 Why the 1953 Limoges Sighting Remains Unresolved Three military site witnesses reported a silent red orange light whose abrupt turns still resist a confident explanation. Three military site witnesses reported a silent red orange light whose abrupt turns still resist a confident explanation. event time window What the three witnesses reported Why the movement appeared so unusual What the surviving evidence cannot prove haute vienne limoges 1953 Introduction The Limoges sighting of 11 April 1953 remains the strongest officially unresolved case in Haute Vienne ’s UFO record. At about 9.10 pm, a French Air Force captain at the Limoges Romanet storage depot reported a large red orange light moving silently across a clear night sky. He described repeated angular course changes, a reversal of direction and a final descent or dive. Two other servicemen reportedly watched part of the event. GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of France’s space agency CNES, still classifies the case as category D: strange, reasonably consistent and not satisfactorily explained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953 GEIPAN... Its importance should not be overstated. There was no photograph, radar detection, recovered object or physical trace. Only the captain supplied a surviving first hand account, and crucial measurements such as distance and altitude were estimates rather than instrument readings. The case remains unresolved because the reported movement fits no obvious conventional explanation, but the evidence is too limited to establish what actually produced the light. What the three witnesses reported The sighting began while the captain was serving as officer of the week at Air Force Depot 603 in Limoges Romanet. His report, dated 14 April—three days after the observation—states that he noticed a large red orange point moving from east to west at what appeared to be a very high altitude. The sky was described as very clear. He could distinguish no solid shape, engine noise or luminous trail. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan limoges 1953 pv n5096 1953309698 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1953 04 00005, LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... The captain estimated that the phenomenon crossed and recrossed a sector covering roughly 45 degrees of sky. Rather than following a smooth line, it appeared to travel along an irregular broken path, with deviations of about 15 degrees from its general direction. He claimed that these changes occurred every second. On reaching the western end of its apparent route, the light reversed through 180 degrees while seeming to gain altitude, then headed back east through further successive changes of course. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953 GEIPAN... During the observation, the captain alerted an adjutant chief who happened to be passing and a sentry stationed near the water tower. These two men reportedly followed the light for a further two or three minutes. From their perspective, it resembled a star moving away, distinguishable from the visible stars chiefly by its red orange colour. The account says that it continued changing course, entered a prolonged dive and was eventually lost from view. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan limoges 1953 pv n5096 1953309698 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1953 04 00005, LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... The duration is not entirely consistent across the surviving material. The captain’s original report says “about eight minutes” and even includes a handwritten calculation based on 480 seconds. GEIPAN’s later case summary gives six minutes for the 45 degree return journey. This may be a simple summarising error or a different interpretation of when the timed portion began, but there is no additional testimony with which to resolve it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953 GEIPAN... Why the movement appeared so unusual Colour and silence alone do not make the observation exceptional. Aircraft can appear silent when distant, and atmospheric conditions can alter the colour of stars, planets, balloons and aircraft lights. The difficult feature is the reported combination of a long observation, repeated sharp deviations, a complete reversal and a final dive. A meteor is a poor fit for that full sequence. Meteors generally follow a continuous apparent path for seconds rather than reversing direction and remaining visible for several minutes. A normal aircraft could turn and reverse, but the captain described changes every second along a broken line rather than a broad, aerodynamically smooth manoeuvre. No wings, navigation light pattern, engine sound or exhaust trail was identified. GEIPAN consequently states that the report does not evoke any of the familiar misidentifications considered in its assessment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953 GEIPAN... The timing also makes a simple drifting balloon less persuasive. A balloon might alter direction when entering different air currents, but an apparent 180 degree reversal over a limited part of the sky, accompanied by near continuous angular deviations, would require either unusually complex winds or a misleading visual impression. The surviving file contains no weather balloon launch record, wind profile or meteorological reconstruction that would allow the idea to be tested properly. The report nevertheless describes only angular motion against the sky. Without a measured distance, altitude or physical size, those angles cannot be converted into a reliable speed or flight path. A nearby small light and a distant large one can produce the same apparent size. Similarly, what looks like a sharp change in direction may reflect the observer’s changing viewpoint, movement of the eyes or head, or uncertainty in judging a light against a dark background. One relevant perceptual mechanism is the autokinetic effect, in which an isolated point of light appears to wander because the observer lacks stable visual references. The effect is well established in vision research and is recognised as a particular problem in night aviation. It can make a stationary or slowly moving light appear to drift or change direction. [skybrary.aero] skybrary.aero Autokinetic EffectThe autokinetic effect (also referred to as autokinesis) is a phenomenon of visual perception in which a statio... Autokinesis is therefore a plausible source of at least some of the reported zigzagging, especially because the later witnesses said the phenomenon looked star like. It is not, however, a complete demonstrated solution. The captain described an extended east west east displacement over approximately 45 degrees, not merely small local wandering. Three people reportedly followed the phenomenon, although the record does not establish whether they independently agreed on every manoeuvre or adopted the captain’s interpretation after he drew their attention to it. How the report entered official channels The Limoges case is stronger than a distant recollection because the central account was written almost immediately and passed through a documented military chain. The captain addressed his report to Lieutenant Colonel Caperan, director of Depot 603 and commander of the Limoges base, on 14 April 1953. The depot forwarded it on 17 April, and it was received by the Air Ministry’s administrative system on 21 April. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan limoges 1953 pv n5096 1953309698 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1953 04 00005, LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... On 27 April, the Air Force equipment service sent the account to the office of the Secretary of State for Air. On 7 May it was transmitted to the scientific bureau of the Air Armed Forces General Staff under file reference 1000/CAB/CT.4. The surviving pages retain official stamps, routing slips and the captain’s sketch of the broken east west east trajectory. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan limoges 1953 pv n5096 1953309698 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1953 04 00005, LIMOGES (87) 11.04.1953, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... This paper trail confirms that a report was made at the time and treated as worthy of circulation. It does not show that the Air Ministry conducted a full technical investigation. The surviving documents contain no recorded interviews with the other two witnesses, no astronomical comparison, no check of military or civilian flights, no balloon inquiry and no calculation based on triangulation or radar. The phrase “three witnesses” can therefore be misleading. Three servicemen reportedly saw the light, but GEIPAN notes that only one testimony was collected. The two supporting observers strengthen the claim that a visible phenomenon was present, yet their"},{"title":"Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained","url":"/why-hautes-alpes-ufo-reports-rarely/","description":"Hautes-Alpes has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark encounter or a strong body of unexplained evidence.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained Hautes Alpes has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark encounter or a strong body of unexplained evidence. Hautes Alpes has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single landmark encounter or a strong body of unexplained evidence. hautes alpes Introduction The French space agency’s GEIPAN database nevertheless preserves several Hautes Alpes reports that could not be resolved because the information was too limited. These category C cases should not be mistaken for confirmed anomalies: they are records in which investigators lacked sufficiently reliable evidence to reach a conclusion. Taken as a whole, the department’s case history is therefore valuable less as proof of extraordinary craft than as a study of how landscape, distance, atmospheric conditions, expectation and incomplete documentation can turn ordinary lights into enduring mysteries. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN What the official record contains GEIPAN, the unit of France’s National Centre for Space Studies, has collected and investigated reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena since 1977. It works with the gendarmerie, police, civil aviation, defence and scientific specialists, but it is not an extraterrestrial research organisation or an emergency air surveillance service. Its stated purpose is to compare witness accounts with recognised astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human perception explanations. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... The Hautes Alpes entries visible in the public catalogue include reports from Saint Bonnet en Champsaur in 1977, Aiguilles in 1978, Tallard in 2004, Ceillac in 2008, Chanousse in 2011, La Saulce and Ribiers in 2012, Briançon and the Tallard–Gap area in 2013, La Roche des Arnauds in 2015 and La Saulce again in 2019. Most received an identified or probably identified classification. The explanations recorded by GEIPAN include a star, Venus, an insect, a Thai style sky lantern and persistent aircraft condensation trails. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPAN’s classifications are important: Category A means the phenomenon was identified with a high degree of certainty. Category B means the proposed conventional explanation is considered probable. Category C means the available information is too weak or incomplete for a dependable assessment. Category D is reserved for sufficiently documented cases that remain unexplained after investigation. The distinction between C and D is crucial. A case may remain technically unidentified because no useful photograph, independent witness account, precise direction or timing was obtained. That evidential gap does not itself indicate extraordinary behaviour. GEIPAN assesses both the reported strangeness and the consistency of the supporting information, and it notes that apparently dramatic descriptions can emerge from ordinary phenomena combined with perceptual error. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN No category D case appears among the Hautes Alpes entries in the relevant public catalogue listing. The unresolved looking entries are category C reports, including Saint Bonnet en Champsaur in 1977 and Laragne Montéglin in 2013, for which GEIPAN records a lack of reliable information rather than evidence of an anomalous vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Tallard sightings of November 2013 The best known modern Hautes Alpes episode began around Tallard aerodrome in early November 2013. Jean Boyer, a retired civil aviation technician, reported seeing unusual lights on several evenings. Descriptions published in the regional press included a vertical luminous line surrounded by blue, red and yellow lights, followed on another evening by a brilliant white, oval looking point in the direction of Sisteron. Gendarmes were informed, and journalists from Le Dauphiné Libéré also reported seeing the later light before it disappeared behind cloud. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The witness’s aviation background gave the story additional local credibility. It suggested that the observer was accustomed to aircraft and airport activity, while the apparent repetition over several evenings encouraged newspapers to describe the events as a cluster. Yet professional experience does not automatically resolve the central problem in nocturnal light sightings: without dependable angular measurements, distance, size and speed cannot be inferred merely from appearance. Even pilots can misjudge the altitude or range of an isolated light. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 The interpretation shifted quickly. Marc Bretton of the Baronnies Provençales observatory examined the available photographs and video and concluded that the images showed bright celestial points affected by camera instability, poor focus and nearby artificial lighting. He judged Venus to be the likely source for observations made in the south west during the early evening and Jupiter for later observations towards the east. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. GEIPAN’s public catalogue subsequently classified the Tallard–Gap report dated 6 November 2013 as category A and identified the phenomenon as a star. That official conclusion does not necessarily reconstruct every light described during the week; contemporary reporting itself indicates that the witness believed the first observation differed from the later one. It does, however, seriously weaken claims that the reported sequence represented a coherent display by an unusual aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Tallard episode matters because it shows how a local “flap” can develop. A credible witness reports a puzzling light, publicity encourages heightened sky watching, and later observations are interpreted through the developing story. Separate events—possibly involving different stars, planets, camera artefacts or atmospheric conditions—can then be grouped into one apparent phenomenon. In this case, later investigation reduced rather than strengthened the extraordinary interpretation. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. La Saulce and the strange cloud of 2012 Another widely reported incident occurred in January 2012, when a resident of La Saulce photographed an elongated white and dark formation near the Blaux mountain, across the departmental boundary in Alpes de Haute Provence. The witness described something resembling a white body with a black plume moving towards the ground before disappearing behind the mountain. The Hautes Alpes gendarmerie was notified and the photographs were referred for examination. [lepoint.fr] lepoint.fr Open source on lepoint.fr. Early press accounts preserved the uncertainty and sometimes labelled the formation a UFO, although the description was at least as suggestive of an atmospheric or aviation effect as of a solid object. Perspective was especially important: a distant trail aligned with a mountain slope can appear to descend towards the surface even when it remains level or is drifting high in the atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN eventually classified the case as category B, concluding that the witness had very probably seen a persistent aircraft condensation trail distorted by wind shear. The contrast between its white and grey portions was attributed to uneven illumination around sunrise, with part of the trail lit and another part still in shadow. This explanation accounts for both the plume like form and the impression of a changing, partly dark object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case is a useful example of why photographs are not automatically decisive. A photograph may record a genuine object or atmospheric structure while offering little information about its distance, movement or scale. Without a sequence of accurately timed images, known landmarks, weather data and the observer’s exact viewing direction, a familiar high altitude trail can appear close, vertical and physically substantial. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A later La Saulce report, dated December 2019, was likewise classified by GEIPAN as category A and attributed to an aircraft condensation trail. The"},{"title":"GEIPAN Record","url":"/geipan-record/","description":"The official catalogue shows a department dominated by stars, planets, lanterns, insects and aircraft trails rather than well-documented anomalies.","breadcrumb":"Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Record What the Official UFO Record Actually Shows Hautes Alpes UFOs GEIPAN Record GEIPAN Record What the Official UFO Record Actually Shows The official catalogue shows a department dominated by stars, planets, lanterns, insects and aircraft trails rather than well documented anomalies. The official catalogue shows a department dominated by stars, planets, lanterns, insects and aircraft trails rather than well documented anomalies. dataset evidence The Hautes Alpes cases in the public catalogue Why category C does not mean unexplained craft The recurring explanations behind unusual lights hautes alpes geipan record Introduction France’s official GEIPAN catalogue gives a fairly clear answer to the central question about UFO reports in Hautes Alpes : the public record is dominated by ordinary objects and optical effects, not by well documented unexplained craft. Among the 13 catalogue cases clearly labelled with the department number 05, five are classified as identified, six as probably identified and two remain unclassified because the evidence is insufficient. None is a category D case—GEIPAN’s designation for a sufficiently investigated report that remains unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ly identified after investigation... The explanations are revealing. Bright stars and planets repeatedly looked like hovering, moving or colour changing objects. Aircraft condensation trails appeared to fall towards the mountains. A close insect became a mysterious streak in a photograph. Other reports were attributed to a sky lantern, or to two separate events—military aircraft noise and unrelated lights—being perceived as one incident. The catalogue therefore matters less as a collection of extraordinary encounters than as an evidence map showing how unfamiliar appearances are created, reported and investigated in a mountainous department. The Hautes Alpes cases in the public catalogue The clearly local entries span more than four decades, from Saint Bonnet en Champsaur in August 1977 to La Saulce in December 2019. They include reports from Aiguilles, Tallard, Ceillac, Chanousse, Ribiers, Briançon, Laragne Montéglin, La Roche des Arnauds, Gap and Embrun. Some began with calls to the gendarmerie; others came from photographs or later witness questionnaires. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casGeipan. Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés. Le Geipan... Hautes Alpe... Taken together, the 13 cases divide into three groups: Five category A cases , in which GEIPAN considered the phenomenon identified: Arcturus at Chanousse, bright stars in the Tallard–Gap observations, Venus at La Roche des Arnauds, sky lanterns at Embrun and aircraft condensation trails at La Saulce. Six category B cases , in which the conventional explanation was judged probable rather than certain: an aircraft near Aiguilles, a star at Tallard, an insect in a Ceillac photograph, a persistent condensation trail at La Saulce, Venus and Sirius at Ribiers, and a sky lantern at Briançon. Two category C cases , at Saint Bonnet en Champsaur and Laragne Montéglin, where the information was not adequate to settle the matter. No category D case among the clearly labelled Hautes Alpes entries. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ly identified after investigation... This distribution does not prove that every unusual light ever seen in the department had a mundane cause. GEIPAN’s archive contains reports submitted to or inherited by the organisation, not a complete census of everything residents have observed. It does show, however, that the cases reaching the official catalogue have generally become less mysterious when investigators obtained useful directions, times, photographs, weather information or astronomical comparisons. The catalogue itself also requires careful reading. One exported row associates Hautes Alpes with a 1982 case titled Lauzet Ubaye and numbered for the neighbouring department of Alpes de Haute Provence. That kind of administrative inconsistency is a reminder that database totals should not be treated as perfectly clean statistics without checking the individual case titles and locations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPANFichiers CSV Cas... Hautes Alpes Provence Alpes Cote d'azur SAINT BONNET EN CHAMPSAUR (05) 07.08.1977. Observation d'un phénomène... Why category C does not mean unexplained craft GEIPAN’s classification system makes a distinction that is frequently lost in UFO discussions. Category C does not mean that investigators discovered an extraordinary object they could not explain. It means that the report could not be reliably analysed because important information was absent, imprecise or contradictory. Category D is the classification reserved for cases that remain unidentified after investigation despite having enough evidence to support meaningful analysis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ly identified after investigation... The Saint Bonnet en Champsaur case of 7 August 1977 shows the difference. Several people reportedly saw an orange light near the top of a hill, followed by what appeared to be a large triangular or cone shaped form advancing towards them. Frightened, the witnesses drove away. When they returned with other people, the phenomenon was gone. The gendarmerie found no ground traces and obtained no supporting testimony from other local residents. GEIPAN consequently records the case as category C for lack of reliable information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The account is striking, but its dramatic quality cannot replace missing evidence. There was no photograph, measured bearing, dependable distance estimate, physical trace or independent observation from a separate location. The apparent size and shape could not be reconstructed with confidence. Nor could investigators determine whether the orange form was a nearby illuminated object, a distant light partly obscured by terrain or vegetation, an atmospheric effect, or something else. Laragne Montéglin on 12 November 2013 was also placed in category C. A witness described a multicoloured vertical light that was initially stationary before travelling in a straight line and then curving. GEIPAN considered possible links with a microlight or helicopter because the movement and location were compatible with small aircraft activity. It could not, however, match the reported lighting pattern to a known flight or confirm an aircraft operating at the relevant time. With no photograph, video or second witness, neither the aircraft hypothesis nor an alternative could be validated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases remain open in an administrative sense, but they are not strong evidence for an exotic vehicle. Their principal unresolved feature is the shortage of testable information. A future reclassification would require new evidence—such as another independent account, an original image, precise timings or confirmed aviation data—not simply a more imaginative interpretation of the existing testimony. Neil deGrasse Tyson thinks UFOs are FAKE Channel: Through Conversations Podcast Open on YouTube Stars and planets behind apparently moving lights Astronomical misidentification is the strongest recurring pattern in the Hautes Alpes catalogue. Bright objects low over a mountain horizon can scintillate, change colour, appear enlarged by haze and seem to move when the observer changes position. Without a clear horizon or reliable angular reference, a fixed celestial object may look much closer than it is. The Ribiers report of 24 January 2012 is a particularly useful example. A motorist saw an intense white light that appeared stationary at low altitude. After he resumed driving, it seemed to follow him and stop whenever he stopped. Later, from a different viewing direction, he saw reddish orange lights. GEIPAN compared the reported bearings with the night sky and found bright Venus low in the south west during the first part of the observation and Sirius in the south east during the second. The apparent pursuit was consistent with the familiar effect in which a distant object maintains almost the same bearing as a moving observer. Because the witness’s directions were not exact, the case was classified B rather than A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At Chanousse in September 2011, witnesses reported an oval object with three differently coloured lights, apparently stationary before moving slowly north. The setting time, direction and orange, strongly scintillating appearance matched Arcturus as it descended towards the western horizon. GEIPAN classified the observation as A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. La Roche des Arnauds in January 2015 provides an even cleaner reconstruction. A witness saw an orange ball that appeared brighter and larger than what they believed to be Venus, then disappeared about ten minutes"},{"title":"Tallard Flap","url":"/tallard-flap/","description":"A week of puzzling lights near Tallard became a local UFO flap before astronomy and official review weakened the extraordinary interpretation.","breadcrumb":"Why Hautes Alpes UFO Reports Rarely Stayed Unexplained","level":2,"text":"Tallard Flap How Tallard's UFO Flap Unravelled Hautes Alpes UFOs Tallard Flap Tallard Flap How Tallard's UFO Flap Unravelled A week of puzzling lights near Tallard became a local UFO flap before astronomy and official review weakened the extraordinary interpretation. A week of puzzling lights near Tallard became a local UFO flap before astronomy and official review weakened the extraordinary interpretation. event time window The sightings that started the story Why witnesses and newspapers saw a cluster How astronomy and GEIPAN changed the verdict hautes alpes tallard flap Introduction Tallard’s November 2013 “UFO flap” began with a genuinely striking cluster of reports: several people, including gendarmes and a retired civil aviation technician, watched bright, coloured lights for long periods from Tallard and Gap. More sightings followed over the next few evenings, and local newspapers briefly treated the episode as a growing mystery. Yet the case weakened rather than strengthened with investigation. Astronomical positions matched the reported directions, the lights behaved like fixed celestial objects, and poor quality zoomed footage exaggerated their shapes. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, eventually classified the central incident as an identified case involving bright stars and Jupiter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete38 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The episode remains important within the UFO history of Hautes Alpes because it shows how a flap can form without fraud or foolish witnesses. Several real lights were seen, but separate astronomical objects, changing viewing directions, atmospheric scintillation and press attention were gradually assembled into the appearance of one continuing phenomenon. 24:18 UFO & UAP. Your Astronomy Sucks 3 Channel: science out there · Views: 2.6K · Uploaded: July 2021 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube The sightings that started the story The core incident occurred during the night of 6–7 November 2013. According to GEIPAN’s later reconstruction, seven people observed lights from two locations: the Gap–Tallard aerodrome and the gendarmerie operational centre in Gap. The Tallard group included the principal witness, a child and three gendarmes; two further witnesses watched from Gap. Only one of the Gap observers completed a full written questionnaire, while the principal Tallard witness later supplied additional details by telephone. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete38 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... From the aerodrome, witnesses reported several apparently stationary lights in different parts of the sky. One, towards the east, was described as an enormous blue green white luminous ball with what looked like two panels beneath it. Another appeared towards the mountain of Céüse in the north west, while a third emerged towards the south east. The colours varied, and some of the lights seemed to possess fins, rays or appendages. The principal witness filmed them, but GEIPAN later found the recording blurred and heavily zoomed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete38 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... At the Gap gendarmerie centre, an officer who had received the original telephone report looked outside at about 1 a.m. He saw a bright, scintillating ball towards the south east. It remained fixed for roughly an hour but appeared to shift with the turning sky. That detail would later become especially significant: an object that stays in the same celestial position while moving gradually with the stars is behaving like an astronomical body, not a hovering aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete38 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The reports sounded more impressive because the witnesses were not all casual observers. Jean Boyer was described in contemporary coverage as a retired technician from the French civil aviation administration. Gendarmes saw or discussed some of the lights, and Boyer said that radar contacts had revealed nothing unusual. Professional experience and official involvement gave the story an authority that a lone anonymous sighting would not have possessed. They did not, however, provide independent measurements of distance, speed or physical structure. [ledauphine.com] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTES ALPES D'étranges phénomènes dans le ciel de...November 9, 2013 — 9 Nov 2013 — Seul, Jean Boyer a observé en direction de Céüze « une ligne lum... Published: November 9, 2013 Why Tallard looked like a developing flap The first newspaper reports appeared on 8 and 9 November. Boyer described a vertical luminous line surrounded by blue, red and yellow lights in the direction of Céüse. He had contacted the gendarmerie because he did not want to keep the observation to himself. The language of the reports remained questioning rather than wholly sensational, but references to UFOs and radar quickly turned an uncertain light observation into a public mystery. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréHAUTES ALPES D'étranges phénomènes dans le ciel de...November 9, 2013 — 9 Nov 2013 — Seul, Jean Boyer a observé en direction de Céüze « une ligne lum... Published: November 9, 2013 Further observations then gave the impression of repetition. On 10 November, Boyer and a companion watched a brilliant white, apparently oval light towards Sisteron at about 6 p.m. Journalists who went to the scene also saw the point before clouds obscured it. Boyer estimated its apparent size as no more than a pinhead, yet its brightness and changing appearance encouraged speculation about an asteroid or some other unusual object. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. By then the story contained the main ingredients of a local flap: Repeated reports within a few nights. A light seen once can be dismissed as an isolated uncertainty; several reports create an expectation of recurrence. Multiple observers. Gendarmes, journalists and a former aviation technician appeared to confirm that something objective was present. Changing descriptions. Lines, coloured lights, an oval form and several directions could be interpreted as different manifestations of one phenomenon. Aviation surroundings. Tallard’s aerodrome made radar, aircraft and technical expertise seem directly relevant. Rapid media feedback. Once the first reports were public, residents and officials were actively looking at bright points in the sky and comparing them with the Tallard story. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. This does not mean witnesses merely copied one another. It means that the category through which they interpreted an unfamiliar light had changed. An unusually bright star seen before the publicity might have been ignored. Seen during a reported UFO wave, the same light was more likely to be watched, photographed, reported and connected to the earlier incident. The apparent unity of the flap is also questionable. The first night involved several lights around midnight, whereas the later white object was observed near 6 p.m. and in a different direction. Contemporary reports themselves note that the positions and colours changed from one evening to another. Those differences are easier to understand if separate celestial objects were being observed at different hours than if one unknown craft repeatedly returned while altering its position and appearance. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Astronomy changed the shape of the case The first public challenge arrived quickly. On 11 November, Marc Bretton of the Baronnies Provençales Observatory examined the available photographs and video. He argued that the supposed structure in the images resulted from camera instability and poor focus applied to a bright point source. Depending on the time and direction, he considered Venus or Jupiter the likely objects. Boyer accepted that one southern light could have been a star but maintained that his original sighting had looked different. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. GEIPAN’s later reconstruction was more detailed. Using the reported directions and times, investigators compared the observations with a computer model of the sky. At 11.50 p.m., Jupiter was conspicuous in the east; Vega lay low in the north west; Sirius was rising in the south east; and Rigel was also visible in that sector. No Moon was present, the weather was clear, and there were no relevant passages of bright satellites or Iridium flares. An airline flight passed nearby at about 12.47 a.m., but its crew reported nothing unusual, and the reported lights had"},{"title":"Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious","url":"/why-hautes-pyrenees-ufo-cases-rarely/","description":"The UFO history of Hautes-Pyrénées is less a story of one celebrated, department-defining encounter than of scattered lights, ambiguous shapes and reports...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious The UFO history of Hautes Pyrénées is less a story of one celebrated, department defining encounter than of scattered lights, ambiguous shapes and reports... The UFO history of Hautes Pyrénées is less a story of one celebrated, department defining encounter than of scattered lights, ambiguous shapes and reports that usually became less mysterious once investigators obtained enough information. hautes pyrenees Introduction That distinction matters. In the official French system, an unexplained case is not automatically a strong case, still less evidence of an extraterrestrial vehicle. Hautes Pyrénées illustrates the difference particularly well: its archive contains credible witnesses and genuinely puzzling experiences, but little publicly documented radar, photographic or physical evidence capable of establishing an extraordinary event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN A scattered record rather than a major UFO hotspot Public reporting has sometimes portrayed Hautes Pyrénées as relatively quiet compared with more populous French departments. A local newspaper examining a December 2018 sighting stated that GEIPAN had recorded 16 departmental cases since the organisation’s creation in 1977, against far larger totals in departments such as Haute Garonne and Bouches du Rhône. That historical count should not be treated as a permanent total, because the database continues to receive, publish and revisit cases. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Un ovni dans le ciel des Hautes Pyrénées? Un témoin raconte Un ovni dans le ciel des Hautes Pyrénées? Un témoin raconte The broader pattern is nevertheless clear. The department has no publicly established counterpart to France’s best known close encounters, such as Trans en Provence or Valensole. Most identifiable Hautes Pyrénées reports concern distant lights or short observations without physical traces, independent instrument readings or several unrelated witnesses. The landscape can make such experiences especially striking. Mountain ridges obscure the true horizon; valleys restrict lines of sight; clouds and moisture alter the appearance of lights; and bright planets can seem suspended over dark terrain. Pic du Midi and the department’s astronomy culture may encourage careful attention to the sky, but an observatory’s presence does not turn nearby reports into astronomical confirmation. Scientific instruments normally examine narrowly defined targets and do not provide continuous surveillance of everything visible over the Pyrenees. Local history websites preserve older newspaper snippets about cigar shaped objects and unexplained lights in Bigorre. These compilations are useful leads, but they are not equivalent to complete investigation files. Many lack the original article, precise viewing geometry, weather records or follow up testimony needed for a reliable modern assessment. The departmental archives and the National Library of France have digitised substantial collections of regional newspapers, offering a route for checking such claims against contemporary reporting rather than later retellings. [loucrup65.fr] bonus.loucrup65.fr Des Ovnis en BigorrePlusieurs personnes se trouvant à la piscine de Tarbes, ont aperçu pendant plusieurs secondes, volant dans le ci... The cases that best explain the local pattern The Tarbes triangle of 2018 One of the department’s more visually dramatic modern reports concerned a dark triangular form over Tarbes on 6 November 2018. The witness described five faint lights forming a V and a slow, silent passage lasting roughly 10 to 15 seconds. At first glance, the account resembles the classic “black triangle” reports familiar from UFO literature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTARBES (65) 06.11.2018 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s investigation identified several weaknesses. The report was formally submitted in September 2019, long after the stated observation, and there was uncertainty about the exact date. The witness remembered a clear, starry sky, whereas meteorological records indicated substantial cloud cover. No second witness, photograph or radar detection was found. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTARBES (65) 06.11.2018 GEIPAN... Investigators concluded that a formation of birds, probably illuminated from below by urban lighting, was the most likely explanation. Birds flying in a V can appear to form the outline of a single solid object, particularly when their bodies intermittently reflect street lighting. Early November also falls within a migration period, and the reported slow, silent movement was compatible with that interpretation. GEIPAN classified the case B: probably identified, rather than conclusively proved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTARBES (65) 06.11.2018 GEIPAN... The case matters because it demonstrates how an apparently structured craft can arise from several separate lights. It also shows why delayed reporting is a serious obstacle. Even a sincere and detailed witness may unintentionally merge the original perception with later reflection, online comparisons or assumptions about shape. Venus over Tarbes in December 2018 A different Tarbes area report briefly attracted regional attention just before Christmas 2018. Witnesses described a bright point seen at around 7.30 in the morning. It appeared stationary for a long period and was photographed before apparently departing rapidly towards the east. The airport control tower reported no corresponding aviation incident, while the Lourdes astronomy club cautioned that most such observations eventually receive conventional explanations. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Un ovni dans le ciel des Hautes Pyrénées? Un témoin raconte Un ovni dans le ciel des Hautes Pyrénées? Un témoin raconte Two days later, the regional press published the identification: the light was Venus, then conspicuous in the morning sky. Its apparent sudden movement was not supported by independent tracking and was most plausibly a perceptual impression associated with changing viewing conditions, cloud, the observer’s movement or the loss of a visual reference. [nrpyrenees.fr] nrpyrenees.fr L'Ovni observé dimanche dans les Hautes Pyrénées était la planète Vénus! L'Ovni observé dimanche dans les Hautes Pyrénées était la planète Vénus! This episode is valuable because the initial report contained several features often treated as signs of an extraordinary object: long duration, apparent immobility, photographs and a seemingly rapid departure. None proved decisive. A photograph of a bright dot does not establish its distance or movement, while a lack of airport reports merely indicates that air traffic staff had not detected or received notice of an aviation event. Venus and a meteor at Lourdes in 2013 A particularly clear official case began with reports of bright, silent balls of light seen around Lourdes between 22 and 28 September 2013. One object remained near the horizon for long periods on several evenings and seemed to follow the observer. GEIPAN compared the stated direction with astronomical positions and found that it closely matched Venus. A second, less prominent point was consistent with Saturn. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLOURDES (65) 22.09.2013 GEIPAN... The same witness also reported a separate orange object moving rapidly for a few seconds. Investigators identified that event as a bright meteor entering the atmosphere. GEIPAN therefore classified the overall file A, meaning that the phenomena were considered identified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLOURDES (65) 22.09.2013 GEIPAN... The Lourdes case shows why reports containing several unusual lights must be separated into individual events. A witness can encounter a planet and a meteor within the same period, then understandably interpret them as related. Investigation may instead reveal two ordinary phenomena with entirely different causes. Bagnères de Bigorre in 1978 On 28 May 1978, a witness near Bagnères de Bigorre reported coloured halos and luminous rays in the direction of the rising Sun. The display lasted between six and ten minutes. Later summaries describe a lenticular cloud as the probable explanation, and GEIPAN lists the case as category B. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr bagneres de bigorre 1978 0500521 bagneres de bigorre 1978 0500521 Lenticular clouds form in stable air flowing across mountains. Their smooth, layered shapes can resemble discs, while sunlight passing through cloud droplets or ice crystals may create coloured edges, halos and rays. The Pyrenean setting therefore supplies a strong meteorological context without requiring the witness to have imagined the display. The report also warns against treating duration"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-789b48/","description":"Venus, meteors and bird formations explain several of the department's most dramatic modern UFO reports.","breadcrumb":"Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases How Ordinary Skies Created Extraordinary UFO Reports Hautes Pyrenees UFOs Explained Cases Explained Cases How Ordinary Skies Created Extraordinary UFO Reports Venus, meteors and bird formations explain several of the department's most dramatic modern UFO reports. Venus, meteors and bird formations explain several of the department's most dramatic modern UFO reports. event time window The Tarbes triangle and the bird formation explanation Venus and meteors over Tarbes and Lourdes Why distance, darkness and delayed memory mislead witnesses hautes pyrenees explained cases Introduction Several of the most dramatic modern UFO reports around Tarbes and Lourdes appear to have been produced by ordinary objects seen under unusually misleading conditions. A silent triangle over Tarbes was probably a flock of migrating birds reflecting city light. Bright “moving” objects near Lourdes matched Venus, while a rapidly crossing orange ball was consistent with a meteor. These explanations do not imply that witnesses invented what they saw. They show how darkness, uncertain distance and brief viewing times can turn familiar phenomena into convincing structured craft. The cases matter within the UFO history of Hautes Pyrénées because investigators could test them against astronomy, weather, migration patterns, photographs and viewing directions. The result is a useful contrast with reports that remain unidentified only because evidence is missing. Here, later investigation generally weakened the extraordinary interpretations while explaining why the original experiences felt so persuasive. The Tarbes triangle was probably a formation of birds On 6 November 2018, a woman walking her dog in Tarbes reported seeing what appeared to be a small, dark triangular craft. Five faint white lights formed a V. The phenomenon moved slowly and silently, apparently passed above her, then seemed to travel between buildings in the Martinet district. The observation lasted only about 10 to 15 seconds, but the perceived solidity of the triangle made it memorable enough for the witness to submit a report to GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête May 4, 2021 — Le PAN est décrit comme un triangle noir avec 5 lumières blanches disposées en forme de V.... Situation astronomique: une... Published: May 4, 2021 The description initially sounds more difficult to explain than a simple distant light. The witness did not merely report luminous points; she believed she could see a black surface connecting them. She also rejected an aircraft or drone because there was no engine noise, no flashing navigation lights and no obvious mechanical movement. Yet those same details led GEIPAN towards an ornithological explanation rather than an exotic vehicle. A flock of large birds flying in a V can create the impression of a single triangular body. Against a dark sky, the observer may see only pale undersides or patches of plumage catching illumination from streets below. The eye then joins those separate points into one coherent outline. The five dim, slightly blurred lights described at Tarbes were consistent with intermittent reflection from birds rather than fixed lamps mounted on a craft. Silence and relatively slow apparent movement also fitted a flock better than a low flying aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête May 4, 2021 — Le PAN est décrit comme un triangle noir avec 5 lumières blanches disposées en forme de V.... Situation astronomique: une... Published: May 4, 2021 The date strengthened that interpretation. Early November falls within the autumn migration period for several large species, including cranes and geese. Hautes Pyrénées lies close to major routes towards the Iberian Peninsula, and cranes regularly gather at the Puydarrieux reservoir before crossing or moving along the Pyrenean corridor. Local bird organisations describe crane passage in the department during October and November, with the strongest regional movement often occurring in the first two thirds of November. [lpo.fr] lpo.fr Organbidexka: un bilan en demi teinte! LPO (Ligue pour la Protection des Oiseaux) Agir pour la biodiversitéDecember 7, 2023 — Du 15 juillet au 15 novembre, notre équipe a étu... Published: December 7, 2023 GEIPAN therefore classified the Tarbes case as category B: a probable misidentification, rather than a completely demonstrated one. That qualification is important. No flock was independently recorded at the precise place and minute of the sighting, and no photograph captured the formation. The explanation rests on the close match between the reported shape, lighting, speed, silence, season and local migration environment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr L’hypothèse d’une méprise avec des oiseaux migrateurs est renforcée par le fait que l’observation ait eu lieu début novembre, c’est à dir... Why the triangle appeared low and solid The witness estimated that the phenomenon was only about five storeys high and believed it passed between two buildings. GEIPAN did not treat that distance estimate as a reliable measurement. At night, an observer viewing an unfamiliar silhouette has no dependable way to separate a small nearby object from a much larger distant one unless there are clear reference points at the same distance. The buildings provided an apparent frame, but not necessarily a true intersection with the flight path. A more distant formation could have disappeared behind roofs or entered a restricted line of sight, creating the impression that it had travelled through the gap. Once the lights were mentally grouped as parts of one object, the dark space between them could be perceived as a physical surface. There were also uncertainties in the reconstruction. The account reached GEIPAN in September 2019, nearly a year after the stated event. The witness said she had made notes, which supports the recorded date, but some remembered conditions did not match available records. She recalled a clear, starry sky; meteorological data from Tarbes–Lourdes–Pyrénées indicated considerable cloud, fog around 23:00 and visibility of only about 800 metres before conditions improved later. GEIPAN noted that either the date, the recalled weather or both might have become imprecise. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête May 4, 2021 — Le PAN est décrit comme un triangle noir avec 5 lumières blanches disposées en forme de V.... Situation astronomique: une... Published: May 4, 2021 This discrepancy does not discredit the witness. It demonstrates why delayed testimony is difficult to use for exact geometry. People can retain the central image of an event while becoming less accurate about time, cloud cover, direction or duration. In this case, the remembered “black triangle” remained vivid, but the details needed to calculate its true height and route were much less secure. 3:20 How scientists use math to help explain UFO videos Channel: PBS NewsHour · Views: 11.7K · Uploaded: April 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 20 seconds Open on YouTube Venus created moving lights near Lourdes A report made from the D940 while travelling from Lamarque Pontacq towards Lourdes on 13 February 2011 involved a luminous ball close to the horizon. The motorist perceived movement and changes of colour, features that can make a planet seem unlike an astronomical object. GEIPAN nevertheless classified the case as a confirmed sighting of Venus. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Venus is especially effective at producing UFO reports because it can be far brighter than any star. Near the horizon, its light passes through a thicker and more turbulent layer of atmosphere. Refraction and variations in air density can make it shimmer, change colour or appear to jump slightly. Trees, bends in the road and the motion of a car can add apparent lateral movement. A stationary planet may therefore seem to pace the vehicle, descend towards the landscape or change position whenever foreground objects interrupt the view. The Lourdes road case is instructive because the witness was not simply looking at a fixed light from a stationary position. Movement of the observer changed the relationship between Venus and nearby landscape features. This parallax is negligible for Venus itself, which is effectively at an infinite distance for everyday viewing, but substantial for trees, ridges, buildings and roadside objects. The background light consequently appears to shift relative to the foreground. A second Lourdes report, covering observations on 22 and 23 September 2013, combined two different astronomical phenomena. On the first evening, a bright orange or luminous object remained visible in a direction matching Venus. GEIPAN calculated that the witness’s estimated bearing was approximately 241 degrees, while Venus lay at about 235 degrees. Photographs were also consistent with a"},{"title":"Unresolved Files","url":"/unresolved-files-8dc4b8/","description":"Some Hautes-Pyrenees cases remain unidentified because investigators lacked enough reliable detail to test ordinary explanations.","breadcrumb":"Why Hautes Pyrenees UFO Cases Rarely Stay Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Files When Does an Unexplained Sighting Mean Anything? Hautes Pyrenees UFOs Unresolved Files Unresolved Files When Does an Unexplained Sighting Mean Anything? Some Hautes Pyrenees cases remain unidentified because investigators lacked enough reliable detail to test ordinary explanations. Some Hautes Pyrenees cases remain unidentified because investigators lacked enough reliable detail to test ordinary explanations. dataset evidence What GEIPAN categories A, B and C actually mean The incomplete Bagnères de Bigorre report of 2022 Missing data, delayed testimony and weak documentary trails hautes pyrenees unresolved files Introduction An unexplained sighting does not necessarily amount to strong evidence of an unknown craft. In Hautes Pyrénées, the clearest example is the Bagnères de Bigorre report of September 2022. A witness described a pale yellow luminous ball moving past a fifth floor window, but the official investigation could not establish its direction, apparent size, brightness, distance or exact path. GEIPAN therefore classified the case as category C: not explained, but not sufficiently documented to analyse. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... This distinction is central to reading the department’s official UFO record. Some cases remain unidentified because good information survives after ordinary explanations have been tested. Others remain unidentified because investigators never obtained enough information to perform those tests. The second kind should not be treated as evidence that something extraordinary occurred. It is evidence that the available record has reached its limit. What categories A, B and C actually mean GEIPAN, the unit of France’s national space agency CNES that examines reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, does not use “unexplained” as a single catch all verdict. Its classifications reflect both the strangeness of a reported event and the consistency of the evidence supporting it. Consistency depends on the amount of information available and the assessed reliability of its sources, including the number and independence of witnesses, the precision of their answers and the existence of photographs or other records. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN The categories most relevant to the Hautes Pyrénées archive are: Category A: the observation has been explained without significant ambiguity. Category B: investigators regard a conventional explanation as highly probable, although it may not be proved beyond every possible doubt. Category C: the report cannot be properly analysed because essential information is absent or insufficiently reliable. Category D: no satisfactory explanation has been found despite evidence considered sufficiently substantial to support the reported strangeness. GEIPAN further distinguishes D1 cases of medium consistency from D2 cases supported by particularly strong or multiple evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN Category C is therefore not halfway between “explained” and “alien”. It is a failure to reach an evidential decision. A case may sound striking in narrative form while remaining impossible to test. Without viewing angles, timings, environmental details or independent confirmation, investigators may be unable to compare it properly with aircraft, celestial objects, reflections, balloons, birds, meteors or lights on the ground. Category B is also commonly misunderstood. It does not mean that investigators identified a particular aircraft by registration number or recovered the object responsible. It means that the known explanation fits the report well enough to be considered very probable. Conversely, category D is not a declaration that an extraordinary vehicle existed. It records that the reported phenomenon remained unexplained after an investigation judged sufficiently well supported to justify that conclusion. GEIPAN itself cautions that classifications other than fully established A cases can change when fresh information or better analysis becomes available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN This framework prevents two opposite errors. The first is to dismiss every unidentified report as worthless. The second is to treat every absence of an explanation as positive evidence for an exotic one. Hautes Pyrénées contains reports across this spectrum, but the unresolved Bagnères file belongs firmly at the information poor end. The incomplete Bagnères de Bigorre report of 2022 During the early hours of 23 September 2022, a visitor staying at a thermal residence in Bagnères de Bigorre noticed something bright through the curtains while watching television. The witness initially thought it was the Moon. About five minutes later, after approaching the window, the witness reported seeing a pale yellow luminous ball with a thin brown and yellow outline. It appeared to move horizontally to the left and vanished after roughly five seconds. No photograph was taken, no physical trace was reported and no second witness was identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... The report reached GEIPAN through the local gendarmerie two days later. That quick approach to the authorities is useful because it produced a contemporary written statement rather than a story first recorded years afterwards. It also shows that the witness was genuinely concerned by the experience. Neither point, however, establishes what was seen. Sincerity and accurate identification are different questions. GEIPAN asked the witness to complete its technical questionnaire in November 2022 and sent a further request in March 2023. By the time its investigation report was prepared in April 2024, no reply had been received. The gendarmerie interview therefore remained the sole source available for analysis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... That record preserved the basic narrative but omitted measurements needed to reconstruct the event. Investigators did not know the object’s azimuth, meaning its compass direction; its angular elevation above or below the horizon; its apparent size; the precise extent of its movement; its brightness; its possible sound; or a reliable estimate of distance. The witness supplied no sketch or photograph showing the view from the window. Descriptions such as “to my left”, “above the street” and “at eye level” were too broad to convert into a dependable trajectory. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... Weather records offered some context. At the Tarbes–Lourdes–Pyrénées airport station, about 18 kilometres away, visibility was reported as very good to excellent, winds were light and no cloud was detected by the station’s ceilometer. Those conditions do not identify the phenomenon. Nor do they rule out local effects that might not have been present at the airport station. They merely remove some obvious possibilities, such as a distant light heavily distorted by extensive recorded cloud cover, while leaving numerous other explanations untested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... GEIPAN ultimately considered no specific hypothesis because the basic geometry was missing. Its report assessed the consistency of the case as weak: one witness, no image or video, and numerous absent technical details. It classified the observation as C because there were insufficient data, not because all conventional explanations had been investigated and rejected. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word BAGNERES DE BIGORRE (65) 23.09.2022 CR final EN COURS MPD.docx... That is the most important fact about the case. “No hypothesis considered” can sound more mysterious than “probable balloon” or “probable reflection”, but here it signals a more elementary problem. Investigators could not reliably determine which hypotheses deserved testing. 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube What the missing measurements could have changed The absent details were not administrative trivia. Each would have narrowed the possible origins of the light. A measured direction and elevation could have allowed comparison with the Moon, bright planets, stars, known flight paths and fixed lights on surrounding buildings or hills. The witness’s first"},{"title":"Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists...","url":"/why-one-hauts-de-seine-ufo-case-still/","description":"Hauts-de-Seine has a documented UFO history, but not a large collection of strong, unresolved incidents.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists... Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists Explanation Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists... Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists... Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists Explanation Hauts de Seine has a documented UFO history, but not a large collection of strong, unresolved incidents. Hauts de Seine has a documented UFO history, but not a large collection of strong, unresolved incidents. The official record is dominated by brief urban sightings later attributed to aircraft, balloons, drones, satellites, atmospheric effects or illuminated displays. hauts de seine Introduction That contrast is the key to understanding the department. Hauts de Seine offers a useful example of how an unidentified light becomes a report, how investigators compare it with ordinary objects, and why “unexplained” is not the same as “extraterrestrial”. It also shows how modern photographs and videos can weaken rather than strengthen an extraordinary interpretation when wind, flight paths and image details point to something mundane. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... The Vaucresson case remains the central mystery At about 9.40 pm on 1 January 1981, two police officers in Vaucresson reported seeing a yellow orange light between an estimated 100 and 150 metres above the ground. As they watched, they believed they could distinguish a circular, plate like object rotating on itself. It moved slowly and steadily, apparently descending, displayed several flashing lights and left a short trail. No sound was reported. The observation lasted roughly one minute before the phenomenon disappeared behind the landscape. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... The case matters because it is the clearest official outlier in the department. GEIPAN, the unit of the French national space agency CNES that collects and investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, classifies Vaucresson as category D: unexplained despite evidence regarded as sufficiently substantial for assessment. Its published summary describes the phenomenon as strange or very strange, with medium to high consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... Several features give the report more weight than an ordinary light in the sky account. There were two witnesses rather than one, both were police officers, the duration was long enough for continued observation, and the description included movement, shape, lighting and silence. The account is therefore not merely a single glimpse of a distant point. Even so, important weaknesses remain. The file contains no photograph, video, radar confirmation, physical trace or clearly documented independent witness. The reported altitude cannot be treated as a measurement: observers generally cannot calculate the distance of an unfamiliar light without knowing its true size. A small nearby object and a large distant object can produce similar visual impressions. GEIPAN itself warns that apparent altitude, speed and proximity are frequently misjudged in night time observations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The case is consequently unresolved in a limited sense. Investigators did not identify the phenomenon from the information available; they did not establish that it was an unconventional craft. Possibilities such as an aircraft viewed at an unusual angle, a balloon carrying lights, an atmospheric effect or another man made object cannot now be tested rigorously because the surviving record lacks the necessary positional, meteorological and traffic data. Vaucresson remains the strongest Hauts de Seine case because no ordinary explanation has been demonstrated. It is not, however, strong evidence of extraterrestrial activity. Its lasting interest comes from the credibility of the witnesses combined with the incompleteness of the evidence. Meudon shows why a dramatic account may still be weak Shortly before 10.30 pm on 4 July 1980, a witness at Meudon reported a very bright orange circular or disc shaped light moving slowly and silently. A smoke like trail appeared beside it. After less than a minute, the disc disappeared, leaving a yellow light visible in its place. The description is memorable, particularly because it combines apparent shape, colour, movement and a trail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEUDON (92) 04.07.1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN nevertheless classifies the case as category C, meaning that it cannot be analysed because reliable information is missing. The distinction is crucial. A category C case has not survived investigation as an unexplained mystery; investigators lack enough dependable material to decide what happened. The Meudon file contains an official police record, but no meaningful reconstruction, corroborating testimony or technical evidence capable of testing possible explanations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMEUDON (92) 04.07.1980 GEIPAN... The reported trail could suggest an aircraft, rocket, firework, meteor or illuminated airborne debris. The change from an orange disc to a yellow light may have been a genuine change in the phenomenon, but it could also reflect shifting perspective, cloud, fading combustion or the limitations of human vision at night. None of those possibilities can be confirmed from the published evidence. Meudon therefore illustrates a recurring mistake in UFO discussions: unusual wording is often confused with evidential strength. A vivid description can be sincere and still be too incomplete to support a conclusion. In the official system, Vaucresson is unresolved after consideration; Meudon is unresolved because adequate consideration is no longer possible. Most published cases have ordinary explanations The broader Hauts de Seine record is much less mysterious than its two early 1980s cases. GEIPAN’s published listings include reports from Nanterre, Boulogne Billancourt, Clamart, Sceaux, Chaville, Garches, Châtillon, Courbevoie, Asnières sur Seine, Colombes, Montrouge, La Garenne Colombes, Issy les Moulineaux and Châtenay Malabry. Most have been classified as certainly or probably identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The recurring explanations reveal what people most often mistake for extraordinary craft in a densely populated urban department: Balloons and flying lanterns: reports have been attributed to children’s balloons, reflective Mylar balloons, weather balloons, thermal balloons and Thai lanterns. Aircraft and drones: cases around Courbevoie, Châtenay Malabry, Boulogne Billancourt and the A86 have been linked to airliners, light aircraft, other aircraft or drones. Astronomical and atmospheric effects: an observation at Sceaux was identified as a sundog, while a Châtillon report was attributed to the International Space Station. Urban lighting: an observation at La Garenne Colombes was traced to a light display, while reports at Clamart and Chaville were linked to illuminated “sky rose” effects. Insufficient evidence: a smaller group, including the 1997 Boulogne Billancourt case and the 2014 Colombes report, remains too poorly documented for a firm assessment even where a likely explanation has been suggested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN This pattern does not show that witnesses were dishonest or careless. Many of the objects involved are genuinely difficult to identify. A reflective balloon can brighten dramatically as it rotates. A lantern can appear to stop, rise or change direction as wind varies. An approaching aircraft may look stationary because it is moving almost directly towards the observer. A satellite or the International Space Station can seem unusually bright and then vanish when it enters the Earth’s shadow. Urban viewing conditions intensify the problem. Buildings restrict sight lines, leaving witnesses with only a short segment of a longer flight path. Street lighting reduces sensitivity to faint visual cues. Noise may be masked by traffic or windows, encouraging the impression that a distant aircraft is silent. Without a recognisable outline, distance and size are almost impossible to judge confidently. Cas d’observations de présence d’ovnis recensés en Belgique en 1992 Channel: Radio Canada Archives Open on YouTube The Garches video demonstrates how investigations resolve cases The Garches sighting of 14 October 2021 is one of the department’s most instructive modern cases because the witness recorded two videos. Shortly before 4 pm, the witness noticed a bright object moving northwards and initially thought it might be a helicopter light. A cable or rod seemed to hang beneath it. On reviewing the footage, the witness perceived an intensely white light beside a darker circular form that appeared to"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-111858/","description":"Most local reports were traced to aircraft, balloons, lanterns, satellites or lighting effects that can appear extraordinary in urban night skies.","breadcrumb":"Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists...","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases What Hauts de Seine UFO Witnesses Were Actually Seeing Hauts de Seine UFOs Explained Cases Explained Cases What Hauts de Seine UFO Witnesses Were Actually Seeing Most local reports were traced to aircraft, balloons, lanterns, satellites or lighting effects that can appear extraordinary in urban night skies. Most local reports were traced to aircraft, balloons, lanterns, satellites or lighting effects that can appear extraordinary in urban night skies. mechanism The objects most often mistaken for UFOs How urban conditions distort distance and movement What photographs, videos and flight data can reveal hauts de seine explained cases Introduction Most reported UFOs in Hauts de Seine have turned out to be familiar objects or lighting effects seen under unfamiliar conditions. The department’s official files repeatedly identify aircraft, balloons, lanterns, satellites, drones, astronomical objects and beams reflected from low cloud. These explanations matter because the original observations were often genuinely puzzling: ordinary objects can appear silent, stationary, enormous or strangely shaped when viewed at night without reliable clues to their distance. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, distinguishes between fully identified cases, probable identifications, reports that lack enough information and sightings that remain unexplained after investigation. Across its national archive, CNES currently reports 24.6 per cent as firmly identified and 39.7 per cent as probably identified, compared with 3.3 per cent left unexplained after investigation. The Hauts de Seine record broadly reflects that pattern, with its strongest modern evidence usually supporting mundane explanations rather than exotic craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The objects most often mistaken for UFOs Aircraft approaching Paris Aircraft are among the easiest objects to misread in the western Paris suburbs. An airliner approaching almost directly towards an observer may show powerful landing lights while its red, green and flashing navigation lights remain difficult to see. Because there is little sideways movement, it can appear to hover. When it turns, the previously “stationary” light may suddenly move away or vanish behind buildings. A clear example occurred at Courbevoie on 12 March 2015. A witness filmed two lights that initially seemed fixed before moving in succession and disappearing behind the urban skyline. GEIPAN matched them with airliners descending towards an airport and classified the report as fully explained. Its investigation noted that forward facing landing lights can remain visible from more than 20 kilometres away and can overpower the aircraft’s smaller navigation lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr On rappelle qu'un avion vu se préparant à atterrir, donc vu de face, peut être vu à plus de 20 km grâce à ses feux d'atterrissage, lesque... This geometry can produce several apparently extraordinary features at once: Hovering: an aircraft flying nearly along the observer’s line of sight changes position very slowly against the background. Sudden acceleration: once the aircraft turns across the observer’s view, its angular movement becomes much more obvious. Changing shape: landing lights, wing lights and cabin illumination become visible at different angles. Silence: the aircraft may be distant, travelling into the wind or masked by road noise and buildings. The absence of audible engine noise is therefore weak evidence by itself. In a densely built department crossed by major Paris region flight paths, a bright silent light is more likely to be distant than truly noiseless. Balloons, drifting debris and lanterns Small windborne objects account for many reports that seem especially difficult to judge. A balloon has no fixed scale in the observer’s field of view, so its distance, altitude and speed cannot be calculated by eye. A small object a few hundred metres away can easily be interpreted as something large and distant. At Sceaux on 4 June 2023, a witness saw and filmed a dark, rounded object apparently edged by flame like features. GEIPAN judged it to be a metallic foil balloon. The shifting appearance was consistent with a reflective, irregular object rotating as it drifted through changing sunlight. The case was classified as fully identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Other local files show how broad this category can be. Three dark circular objects over Issy les Moulineaux in March 2008 were judged probably to be balloons of unknown origin, while a large dark form over Courbevoie in July 2012 was attributed to airborne debris carried at a constant speed by the wind. Reports of small silver objects at Clamart and yellow orange forms at Châtillon were also assessed as probable balloons. The important clue in such cases is not merely that the object drifts, but that its direction and speed agree with measured winds. Illuminated paper lanterns create a different but equally persuasive illusion. Their warm flame produces a steady orange or reddish glow; their bodies and supporting frames are usually invisible at night. They therefore appear as self luminous spheres moving silently in formation. A lantern may brighten as it turns, fade in thin cloud or seem to disappear instantly when its flame goes out. Four red lights seen from Montrouge shortly after midnight on 21 July 2009 displayed this familiar combination: low apparent altitude, silent movement, reddish colour and sudden extinction. GEIPAN classified the incident as a probable lantern release. Similar reports were made at nearby Bagneux the following night, at Clamart in December 2011 and at Asnières sur Seine in June 2014, where approximately 20 orange lights were seen on a Friday night in circumstances suggestive of a celebratory launch. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The number of lights does not make such an observation less conventional. A cluster often strengthens the lantern explanation because releases commonly occur at weddings, parties and public celebrations. OVNI en Picardie Épisode 3 Les agents du GEIPAN Channel: France 3 Hauts de France Open on YouTube Satellites and the International Space Station Satellites are increasingly prominent in UFO reports because they are bright, silent and apparently unconnected to any visible structure. They cross the sky in smooth straight lines without the flashing lights expected from aircraft. They may also disappear abruptly when they enter the Earth’s shadow, creating the impression that a luminous object has switched off or accelerated away. A particularly instructive Hauts de Seine case occurred at Châtillon during the night of 16–17 July 2022. Two witnesses saw a bright “sphere” travelling rapidly above La Défense and recorded it on a telephone. GEIPAN compared the observation time, direction and elevation with orbital data and found a close match with a pass of the International Space Station. The case was classified as fully identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The video initially appeared to support the idea of a round object because magnified frames showed two concentric circles. Investigators found that these were produced when the telephone failed to focus on a distant point of light. In better focused sections, the supposed sphere returned to a simple luminous point. This is an important warning: enlarging a blurred image can turn a camera artefact into an apparent physical structure. How urban conditions distort distance and movement The Hauts de Seine skyline gives observers plenty of foreground objects but surprisingly few useful references for judging something in the open sky. Buildings may establish direction, yet they do not reveal whether a light is 200 metres away or 20 kilometres away. Once distance is unknown, estimates of size, altitude and speed become largely speculative. GEIPAN identifies several recurring perception effects. A lone light can appear to wander because of the autokinetic effect , a visual illusion in which a stationary point seems to move when there is no stable background. An aircraft can appear motionless when it approaches the observer directly. Conversely, an object moving with the witness may seem to pivot around a fixed point in the landscape. Observers may then construct an apparent flight path from several impressions that were never measured. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Urban surroundings amplify these problems in several ways. Interrupted viewing changes the apparent trajectory. A light passing behind a tower, roofline or tree is not continuously tracked. When it reappears, the mind may connect the two visible positions with a turn or burst of speed that did not occur. Street lighting reduces night vision. Bright windows, vehicle headlights and illuminated signs make"},{"title":"Vaucresson","url":"/vaucresson/","description":"Two police officers reported a silent rotating object in 1981, but the case remains unexplained because the surviving evidence cannot test ordinary","breadcrumb":"Why One Hauts de Seine UFO Case Still Resists...","level":2,"text":"Vaucresson Why the Vaucresson UFO Case Remains Unresolved Hauts de Seine UFOs Vaucresson Vaucresson Why the Vaucresson UFO Case Remains Unresolved Two police officers reported a silent rotating object in 1981, but the case remains unexplained because the surviving evidence cannot test ordinary Two police officers reported a silent rotating object in 1981, but the case remains unexplained because the surviving evidence cannot test ordinary event time window What the two police officers reported Why GEIPAN classified the case as unexplained Which ordinary explanations can no longer be tested hauts de seine vaucresson Introduction The Vaucresson UFO case remains the strongest officially unexplained sighting recorded in Hauts de Seine , but its importance rests on a narrow foundation. At about 9.40 pm on 1 January 1981, two police officers reported a slowly moving, apparently rotating phenomenon with yellow orange and flashing lights. They watched it for roughly a minute before the landscape blocked their view. France’s official UFO investigation service still places the case in category D, meaning that no satisfactory explanation was found and that the testimony was considered sufficiently substantial to assess. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... That classification does not establish that the officers saw an exotic craft. The surviving file contains no photograph, radar return, measured position, physical trace, weather information or documented independent witness. Even some seemingly firm details in the published summary become less certain when compared with the individual witness records. Vaucresson is therefore best understood as a credible but incomplete observation: intriguing enough to resist identification, yet too poorly documented to test ordinary explanations properly. What the two police officers reported GEIPAN’s case summary states that the two officers first noticed a scintillating yellow orange light between an estimated 100 and 150 metres above the ground. As it progressed, they believed they could distinguish a circular or plate like object rotating on itself. It moved slowly and regularly, apparently began to descend, displayed several flashing lights and left a short trail. The report says that it made no sound and remained visible for approximately one minute before disappearing behind the landscape. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... This is more detailed than the department’s many brief reports of isolated lights. The witnesses did not merely describe a point that appeared and vanished. Their account included apparent structure, changing illumination, rotation, movement and a period of sustained observation. Two people were present, and both were serving police officers, making deliberate fabrication less immediately plausible than in an anonymous or unsupported story. Professional status, however, should not be confused with instrumental accuracy. Police officers may be trained to observe people, vehicles and events on the ground, but that does not give them a reliable way to measure the altitude, size or distance of an unfamiliar light at night. GEIPAN itself stresses that witnesses commonly misjudge distance, speed and trajectory when they do not know an object’s true size. Its investigators treat testimony as essential but potentially affected by perception, emotion, interpretation and memory. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The two officers also appear to have observed the event together rather than from separate locations. Their reports therefore provide corroboration that something unusual was seen, but they are not equivalent to two independent sightings from different angles. Shared viewing conditions can produce shared uncertainty, especially if the witnesses discuss what they think they are seeing while the observation is still in progress. Why GEIPAN classified the case as unexplained The current GEIPAN page gives Vaucresson a category D classification and describes it as a strange to very strange phenomenon with medium to strong consistency. The entry was most recently updated on 23 June 2021, indicating that the unexplained status survived a later review rather than remaining solely as an untouched label from 1981. The official record nevertheless ends with a blunt limitation: no other information was collected, so the phenomenon remains unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... GEIPAN classifications weigh two broad questions. The first is strangeness: how difficult is the reported phenomenon to reconcile with known objects or effects? The second is consistency: how much reliable information supports the account? Under the modern system, category D is reserved for sufficiently supported cases in which no proposed explanation adequately accounts for the reported features. Cases lacking enough usable information are normally placed in category C instead. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Vaucresson sits awkwardly near the boundary between those ideas. It has two identified occupational witnesses, a specified date and time, a duration, a general description and a reported path. Those features give the case more substance than a fragmentary report. Yet the absence of measurements and supporting records severely limits any reconstruction. Its category D status means that GEIPAN did not find an adequate identification; it does not mean that all conventional possibilities were positively eliminated. This distinction is central to the case. “Unexplained” describes the outcome of an investigation using the available information. It does not certify that the reported object possessed extraordinary technology, nor does it identify its origin. GEIPAN explicitly states that its work concerns unexplained aerospace phenomena rather than proving extraterrestrial visitation, and that it has found no evidence establishing the alien hypothesis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The individual records reveal important uncertainties The polished case summary gives the impression of a single, coherent description. The individual witness entries show a less complete picture. One officer’s record describes a single, slowly moving phenomenon on a descending trajectory, but gives no apparent size, no measured distance and no known weather conditions. Its shape is entered only as an uncategorised form, while the field for noise is marked unknown rather than silent. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 01.01.1981 T1 GEIPAN... The second officer’s entry describes a single slow phenomenon as spherical or ball shaped and multicoloured. It records total silence, but again provides no apparent size, no distance and no weather information. Even the witness’s reference frame is marked unknown. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 01.01.1981 T2 GEIPAN... These differences are not evidence that the sighting was invented. Two truthful observers often choose different descriptions for an unfamiliar visual event. A luminous sphere may also appear plate like when its lights change or when the observer believes that a solid body has become visible around them. Nevertheless, the discrepancies weaken attempts to treat every detail in the combined summary as independently confirmed by both witnesses. The estimated altitude of 100 to 150 metres is particularly fragile. Neither individual entry supplies a distance measurement, and both omit apparent size. Without knowing the phenomenon’s real dimensions, the officers could not derive its altitude from sight alone. A small object relatively nearby and a much larger object farther away could occupy the same angle in the sky. The published height should therefore be read as an impression, not a surveyed position. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANVAUCRESSON (92) 1981 GEIPAN... There is also no precise public account of the officers’ viewing location, the object’s compass bearing, its angular elevation, the direction in which it moved or the part of the landscape that eventually concealed it. Those missing coordinates prevent a modern investigator from plotting the path against nearby roads, flight routes, terrain or celestial objects. 6:01 Officer Spots a U.F.O. During a High Speed Chase! NASA's Unexplained Files Channel: Science Channel · Views: 90.7K · Uploaded: July 2022 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Which ordinary explanations can no longer be tested Several conventional explanations can be proposed in principle, but the surviving evidence is too sparse to confirm or eliminate them responsibly. An aircraft seen under unusual conditions remains possible because the officers reported regular slow movement and several flashing lights. An aircraft approaching nearly head on can appear unusually slow or temporarily stationary, while its illuminated fuselage may be perceived as a circular body and its navigation lights as"},{"title":"Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is","url":"/why-heraults-ufo-record-looks-stranger/","description":"Hérault has a substantial record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is less sensational than the raw number of sightings...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is Hérault has a substantial record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is less sensational than the raw number of sightings... Hérault has a substantial record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is less sensational than the raw number of sightings might suggest. herault Introduction This distinction matters. In the classification used by GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, a case marked “C” is not a robust mystery. It means that the evidence is too weak to establish what happened. Hérault’s most instructive contribution to French UFO history is therefore not one extraordinary incident, but a varied departmental sample showing how strange experiences arise, how investigators test ordinary explanations and why some reports remain open without becoming persuasive evidence of an unknown craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN What the official record says GEIPAN collects witness accounts directly and through bodies including the gendarmerie, police and civil aviation authorities. It may compare testimony with weather, astronomy, aircraft movements, photographs, radar information and reconstructions of the witness’s viewing position. Its published categories separate identified cases, probable identifications, cases that cannot be assessed reliably and genuinely unexplained reports surviving investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The current system can be read as follows: A: the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B: a conventional explanation is considered probable. C: the available information is insufficient or unreliable. D: the phenomenon remains unidentified after meaningful investigation. That framework prevents a common misunderstanding about Hérault’s files. A long list of reports is not the same as a long list of unexplained events. Nationally, CNES says that only about 3.3 per cent of published phenomena remain unidentified after investigation, while much larger shares are identified, probably identified or unusable because of missing data. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... The searchable Hérault record contains cases from Montpellier, Béziers, Sète, Agde, Valras Plage, Poussan, Saint Thibéry, Sérignan, Loupian and numerous smaller communities. The geographical spread reflects the department’s mixture of a large urban centre, busy roads, coastal resorts, nightlife, aviation routes and extensive open skies. It does not, by itself, establish a single hotspot or recurring unknown phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The older reports are intriguing but poorly preserved One of the earliest official Hérault entries concerns Montpellier on 19 January 1975. The surviving GEIPAN page refers to a gendarmerie record but classifies the incident as C because there is not enough reliable information to identify the phenomenon. That makes it historically interesting as an early departmental report, but evidentially weak. The official file does not justify treating it as a confirmed sighting of a structured craft or an unexplained aerial technology. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTPELLIER (34) 19.01.1975Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés.... Classification. C... A case from Saint Geniès des Mourgues on 23 August 1976 reached a different conclusion. GEIPAN classified it B and associated the observation with a helicopter. This is an important contrast: two accounts from the same mid 1970s period can retain a similar place in popular UFO storytelling while having very different official outcomes—one too incomplete to judge, the other probably explained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Later historical files show the same problem. Reports from Béziers on 21 June 1997, the road from Montpellier towards Saint Mathieu de Tréviers on 28 June 1998, and Saint Geniès de Fontedit on 11 November 1999 were all placed in category C. Their presence in the archive demonstrates that something was reported, not that an anomalous object was established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Nom du cas Date d'observation Classification Département Phénomene Date de mise a jour Date d'observation Lien... BEZIERS... Older testimony is particularly vulnerable to missing times, uncertain directions, imprecise angular measurements and delayed reconstruction. GEIPAN stresses that estimates of distance, size and speed are often interpretations rather than direct observations. Emotion, memory and cultural expectations may also influence how an unfamiliar light becomes a story about a large, fast or structured object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Agde–Béziers light shows how strangeness can be created A useful Hérault case occurred on the D912 road from Agde towards Béziers during the early hours of 13 June 2011. A motorist and passengers watched a blue white oval patch apparently moving rapidly and unpredictably across the cloud layer. The observation lasted roughly half an hour and was described in terms suggesting an intense torch shining above or upon the clouds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN concluded that the witnesses had probably seen entertainment lighting from one of the nightclubs in the Agde–Béziers area. A powerful beam can become visible mainly where it strikes low cloud, while the connecting shaft remains difficult or impossible to see. The result can resemble a self contained luminous object racing across the sky. Investigators considered the timing—around 2 a.m. during a festive holiday period—the cloud conditions, colour, movement and duration collectively more consistent with nightclub lighting than with a flying craft. The case was classified B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This explanation was probabilistic rather than absolute: GEIPAN did not identify one particular projector. Nevertheless, it accounted for the reported behaviour without requiring an object to be present at the apparent location. The case demonstrates why locating the light source is often more important than analysing the supposed movement of the “object”. A beam sweeping across clouds can produce rapid angular motion that no physical aircraft at the assumed distance could easily match. The same mechanism matters elsewhere in coastal Hérault. Resort nightlife, seasonal events and humid or cloudy marine air create good conditions for projected light to appear detached from its source. Reports from this environment should therefore be checked against entertainment venues and event lighting before extraordinary interpretations are considered. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Meteors, satellites and planets explain many brief sightings Several Hérault observations were resolved through astronomy rather than aviation. At Montpellier on 11 December 2009, GEIPAN classified the reported phenomenon as a meteoroid. Poussan’s event of 14 March 2014—a rapidly moving white flash with a short luminous trail—was identified as a bright meteor or fireball and placed in category A. A striking flash can look close, controlled or horizontal because a witness has no reliable distance reference, but its short duration and trail are characteristic diagnostic clues. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Recherche de casCase name Observation date Classification Department Phenomenon Date of update Observation date Link... MO... Two reports from March 2012, at Saint Mathieu de Tréviers and Valras Plage, were classed B and associated with atmospheric re entry. Such events can fragment into several lights moving together, creating the impression of a formation or a large object with illuminated sections. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... At Saint Thibéry on 12 July 2015, a witness beside the River Hérault saw a point of light flashing regularly, moving in a straight line and then disappearing. GEIPAN considered a satellite the probable explanation. A satellite can brighten and fade as its reflective surfaces change angle, while entry into the Earth’s shadow can make it vanish abruptly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT THIBERY (34) 12.07.2015Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés.... Classification... Venus accounted for a Montpellier report on 17 July 2012. Bright planets are recurrent"},{"title":"False Alarms","url":"/false-alarms/","description":"Nightclub beams, meteors, aircraft and bright celestial objects can appear fast, structured or close when distance cues are missing.","breadcrumb":"Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is","level":2,"text":"False Alarms How Ordinary Lights Become Herault UFO Reports Herault UFO Files False Alarms False Alarms How Ordinary Lights Become Herault UFO Reports Nightclub beams, meteors, aircraft and bright celestial objects can appear fast, structured or close when distance cues are missing. Nightclub beams, meteors, aircraft and bright celestial objects can appear fast, structured or close when distance cues are missing. mechanism The Agde Beziers Nightclub Light Case Meteors, Satellites and Bright Planets Why Clouds and Distance Distort Perception herault false alarms Introduction Many Hérault UFO reports begin with a real light but acquire their strangeness through uncertain distance, darkness, cloud and movement. A nightclub beam can appear as a solid disc racing over the countryside. Venus, Jupiter and a bright star can form an apparently engineered triangle. An approaching airliner may seem stationary before suddenly changing shape or brightness. A meteor hundreds of kilometres away can look as though it has fallen behind the next hill. These are not invented experiences. They are sincere observations made under conditions in which the human eye has too few reference points to judge size, speed and range reliably. Hérault is particularly suited to such false alarms: its coastal nightlife produces powerful skyward lighting, its open horizons expose bright celestial objects, and aircraft serving Montpellier and Béziers cross dark areas where navigation lights can become visually detached from the aeroplane carrying them. The department’s official records show how ordinary sources repeatedly become extraordinary looking reports. The Agde–Béziers Nightclub Light Case One of the clearest Hérault examples occurred on the night of 13 June 2011, while a witness was travelling on the D912 from Agde towards Béziers. The observation was sufficiently unusual to be reported to GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES. Yet the final classification was B: a conventional explanation was judged highly probable. Investigators concluded that the moving luminous patches were most likely entertainment lighting from a nightclub or similar event projected onto low cloud. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D912] de AGDE (34) vers BEZIERS (34) 13.06.2011... faisceaux peuvent ne pas être visibles. La localisation et la recherche précise... The explanation fits both the place and the appearance. The Agde–Béziers coastal corridor contains resorts, clubs, campsites and seasonal entertainment venues. A rotating spotlight or laser aimed upwards may strike the underside of cloud and create a bright patch that appears to sweep rapidly across the sky. Because the illuminated patch can be kilometres from the projector, a modest rotation at ground level produces apparently dramatic motion overhead. The most misleading feature is often the absence of a visible connecting beam. People naturally expect a searchlight to look like a continuous luminous column. In reality, the beam becomes visible from the side only when enough droplets, dust or aerosols scatter light towards the observer. The cloud impact can therefore be bright while the intervening beam remains faint or invisible. The witness sees a compact moving form but not the ordinary apparatus producing it. GEIPAN specifically notes that mobile patches on a cloud ceiling are a frequent source of misidentification and that changing atmospheric conditions can conceal the beams. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D912] de AGDE (34) vers BEZIERS (34) 13.06.2011... faisceaux peuvent ne pas être visibles. La localisation et la recherche précise... This can produce several apparently “craft like” qualities at once: A defined shape: the beam’s footprint may look circular, oval or disc like on the cloud. Extreme speed: the patch can cross a wide angle of sky in seconds without any physical object travelling that route. Abrupt turns: programmed lighting rigs can reverse, pause or jump between positions. Silent movement: the light source may be distant, while the illuminated cloud itself makes no sound. Disappearance without departure: the patch vanishes when the beam moves away, the cloud thins or the light is switched off. The Agde–Béziers case matters because it demonstrates how a report may contain accurate descriptive details—rapid motion, sharp changes of direction and a visible luminous “object”—while the interpretation of a flying body is mistaken. GEIPAN’s general methodology explicitly lists nightclub lasers among familiar phenomena that can surprise witnesses, especially when perception adds apparent solidity or motion to an ordinary source. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr celui à l’origine de l’observation car il y a toujours quelque chose dans le ciel, les canulars sont très rares:... avion sans aile... The classification is still appropriately cautious. Investigators did not identify and inspect a particular lighting rig operating at a named venue. They judged that the characteristics were sufficiently typical of entertainment lighting to make further venue by venue searching disproportionate. The case is therefore not proof tied to one projector, but a strong probable identification based on mechanism, environment and low residual strangeness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D912] de AGDE (34) vers BEZIERS (34) 13.06.2011... faisceaux peuvent ne pas être visibles. La localisation et la recherche précise... Meteors, Satellites and Bright Planets Natural and orbital lights create a different class of Hérault false alarm. Unlike nightclub beams, these objects really are moving through the sky—or appear to move because Earth is rotating—but their distance and scale are difficult to infer without specialist information. Three lights become a triangle over Montpellier At 4.03 am on 17 July 2012, a Montpellier witness saw two exceptionally bright points towards the eastern sky. Reviewing a video later revealed a third, fainter point, and the group appeared to form a triangle. The same arrangement was seen again two days later. A triangular pattern can easily suggest a single structured object, particularly when the dark space between the lights is mentally filled in as a solid body. GEIPAN reconstructed the sky and found a precise astronomical match. The two brightest sources were Venus and Jupiter; the third was the star Aldebaran. Their recorded positions matched the witness’s video, and the case was classified A, fully identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTPELLIER (34) 17.07.201217 Jul 2012 — MONTPELLIER (34) 17.07.2012. Date d'observation. 17/07/2012. Région. Languedoc Roussillon... This case exposes a basic but powerful perceptual shortcut. When several lights form a simple geometric pattern, observers may group them as parts of one object rather than treat them as unrelated points at vastly different distances. No visible surface is required. The imagined triangle is produced by the arrangement of the lights and the brain’s preference for coherent forms. A similar mechanism can operate with aircraft formations, separate stars or navigation lights. Geometry alone is weak evidence of a structured craft unless witnesses also see an independently defined edge, surface or obstruction of background stars. UAP Investigation with Public Data (ScienceWriters2023) Channel: Mick West Open on YouTube Sirius appears active over Saussines In Saussines on 3 March 2022, witnesses observed what they described as a curious bright “star” in the western sky and recorded four videos. The quantity of material made this more than a fleeting, undocumented impression: GEIPAN regarded the testimony as detailed and the case as having good evidential consistency. Nevertheless, analysis identified the source as Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. The case was classified A. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le cas présente une bonne consistance en raison de la présence d’un témoignage de qualité, bien détaillé et de quatre vidéos qui ont été... Bright stars near the horizon can look far less stable than they do high overhead. Their light travels through a thicker, more turbulent layer of atmosphere, causing rapid changes in brightness, apparent position and colour. To an observer without a firm horizon or foreground reference, the result can resemble pulsing, hovering or small erratic movements. A further illusion, known as autokinesis, can make a stationary point appear to wander when watched against a dark, featureless background. Aviation guidance treats this as a genuine safety issue: a small fixed light may seem to move because the eye lacks a stable reference against which to measure it. GEIPAN likewise cites an apparently moving star as an example of strangeness added by perception rather than by the original phenomenon. [skybrary.aero] skybrary.aero To prevent this illusion, focus the eyes on objects at varying distances and avoid fixating on one target... The Saussines case is useful precisely because the"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-1fae2c/","description":"Herault has many recorded sightings, but most are explained, probably explained or too poorly documented to judge.","breadcrumb":"Why Herault's UFO Record Looks Stranger Than It Is","level":2,"text":"Official Files Do Herault's UFO Files Contain Any Strong Mysteries? Herault UFO Files Official Files Official Files Do Herault's UFO Files Contain Any Strong Mysteries? Herault has many recorded sightings, but most are explained, probably explained or too poorly documented to judge. Herault has many recorded sightings, but most are explained, probably explained or too poorly documented to judge. dataset evidence How GEIPAN Classifies Herault Cases Explained, Unresolved and Unusable Reports Why Case Numbers Can Mislead herault official files Introduction Hérault’s official UFO archive contains plenty of unusual experiences, but it does not reveal a hidden collection of strong, instrument backed mysteries. A review of GEIPAN’s downloadable case data, dated 27 November 2025, finds 65 Hérault entries: 14 classified as identified, 31 as probably identified and 20 as too poorly documented for a reliable conclusion. None carried the classification reserved for a genuinely unexplained case that remained strange after a substantial investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv That result needs careful wording. It does not mean every witness imagined something, or that every detail has been explained. It means that the department’s unresolved reports generally fail because information is missing, while the better documented cases repeatedly lead back to aircraft, astronomical objects, meteors, satellites, lanterns, drones, reflections and projected lighting. Hérault’s files are valuable less as evidence of unknown craft than as a record of how ordinary phenomena can become sincerely puzzling observations. How GEIPAN Classifies Hérault Cases GEIPAN is the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of France’s space agency, CNES. It receives accounts directly from witnesses and through organisations such as the gendarmerie, police and civil aviation authorities. Investigators may compare testimony with weather records, astronomical calculations, aircraft activity, radar information, photographs and reconstructions made from the witness’s location. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. Its four broad classifications are essential to understanding the Hérault archive: A: the reported phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B: a conventional explanation is considered probable, although some uncertainty remains. C: the event cannot be identified because the evidence is insufficient, unreliable or contradictory. D: a sufficiently well documented phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. The crucial distinction is between C and D. A C file is unresolved in the everyday sense that no final object has been named, but it is not necessarily mysterious in an evidential sense. It often lacks a precise time, direction, duration, independent witness, original photograph or other information needed to test possible explanations. A D case has survived that testing with both meaningful documentation and significant residual strangeness. GEIPAN says it assesses both the consistency of the available evidence and the strangeness remaining after ordinary possibilities have been considered. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This explains why a dramatic account can receive a weaker classification than a visually mundane report. A detailed observation of Venus may be firmly classed A because its position can be calculated. A reported dark craft passing silently overhead may be classed C because the account arrived late, came from one witness and supplied no measurements or corroborating evidence. The balance seen in Hérault also resembles GEIPAN’s national picture. CNES currently reports that 24.6 per cent of published phenomena are clearly identified, 39.7 per cent probably identified, 32.4 per cent unidentified for lack of data and only 3.3 per cent unidentified after investigation. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. Explained, Unresolved and Unusable Reports Identified cases usually turn on a checkable detail Hérault’s A files show what happens when investigators obtain enough information to match a report with a known source. The explanations are varied rather than dominated by one single cause. In Montpellier in March 1985, a witness repeatedly saw an exceptionally bright point moving slowly across the sky. The investigation identified Venus, whose brightness and apparent motion fitted the observation. In February 1998, another Montpellier witness reported a black airborne object; investigators traced it to a kite used that day by a professional photographer and demonstrated the match to the witness. Other Hérault A cases involve meteors, satellites, astronomical bodies and entertainment lighting. At Lézignan la Cèbe on 14 September 2013, witnesses saw successive white circles moving across the clouds. Their appearance matched a powerful event or nightclub projector, and a nearby light source was established. At Poussan on 14 March 2014, a bright object crossing part of the sky in about one second with a short trail was identified as a meteor. These cases are not dismissals based merely on familiarity. The conclusion comes from comparing timing, direction, movement, duration and appearance with a specific conventional phenomenon recorded or reproducible at the scene. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv The Poussan case of 7 June 2013 is particularly instructive because GEIPAN initially had no obvious source. A witness repeatedly saw a fixed white light with an apparently structured glow beneath it. An investigator searched the area at night but did not immediately locate the cause. Further examination nevertheless showed that the effect was a reflection from exterior lighting rather than an aerial object. The case was ultimately classed A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That sequence matters: failure to find an explanation on the first visit did not make the phenomenon extraordinary, while later identification did not mean the witness had fabricated the sighting. The experience was real; the interpretation of its distance and nature was mistaken. L’ex directeur du GEPAN brise le silence (J.J Velasco) Channel: Biomécanique Open on YouTube Probable explanations form the largest group The B category is the largest in the November 2025 Hérault dataset. These are not fully proven identifications, but the reported characteristics fit an ordinary source better than an unknown one. The recurring explanations reveal the department’s observational environment. Along the coast and around Montpellier, Béziers and the Étang de Thau, witnesses frequently view aircraft, satellites, astronomical objects and lights near a low horizon. Summer celebrations and tourist activity add lanterns, illuminated balloons and projected beams. Fast meteors can seem to fall into the sea or nearby countryside because observers have no reliable distance cue. GEIPAN’s search results identify examples including: Thai lanterns in Montpellier in June 2010 and Saint Gély du Fesc in March 2013; an illuminated balloon at Mèze in August 2012; an astronomical object at Notre Dame de Londres in September 2012; projected entertainment lighting near Mons in September 2013; aircraft or helicopters in several older and more recent reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Saint Geniès des Mourgues observation of 23 August 1976 shows why classifications can change. The case had once been treated as unexplained, but a later reassessment concluded that a helicopter was the probable source and reclassified it B. GEIPAN explicitly revisits older files when improved software, accumulated experience or newly available information permits a more precise conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This weakens the idea that an old unexplained label permanently establishes a mystery. A classification records the assessment possible at a particular stage of the investigation; it can be revised when a conventional explanation becomes better supported. A much newer example comes from Frontignan on 2 September 2022. A witness saw a powerful flashing green light apparently stationary beneath low storm clouds. GEIPAN consulted a drone operator in the Sète area, who confirmed that the colour and flashing pattern were strongly characteristic of a drone. The case was classed A in the archive, illustrating how local technical knowledge can turn a striking light into a practical identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. C cases preserve uncertainty, not strong proof The 20 C entries in the November 2025 dataset are the easiest to misread. Some contain dramatic descriptions: triangles, cigar shaped masses, lights apparently passing overhead or dark forms seemingly close to the ground. Yet their official status reflects evidential weakness rather than successful"},{"title":"Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist...","url":"/which-ille-et-vilaine-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Ille-et-Vilaine has a genuine but modest UFO history: a small number of officially unresolved sightings sit among many reports later attributed to the Moon,...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Ille et Vilaine has a genuine but modest UFO history: a small number of officially unresolved sightings sit among many reports later attributed to the Moon,... Ille et Vilaine has a genuine but modest UFO history: a small number of officially unresolved sightings sit among many reports later attributed to the Moon, satellites, meteors, aircraft, balloons or artificial lighting. ille et vilaine Introduction The wider record is equally instructive. Ille et Vilaine participated in the great French sighting wave of 5 November 1990, now firmly linked by the French space agency to the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. Other once mysterious local incidents have been reclassified after investigators reconstructed the sky, weather and viewing geometry. The department therefore offers a useful lesson in UFO evidence: credible people can accurately report something startling while being mistaken about its distance, size or nature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTFORT SUR MEU (35) 27.11.1987 GEIPAN... What the official record actually contains France has an unusually accessible public UFO archive. GEIPAN, a technical unit of the French space agency CNES, collects, investigates, anonymises and publishes reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its investigators begin with direct witness testimony and a detailed questionnaire, then may consult photographs, video, astronomical information, meteorological records, aviation data and other relevant evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUnderstanding a Phenomenon GEIPAN GEIPANUnderstanding a Phenomenon GEIPAN Its classifications are important because “unidentified” does not mean “alien”. Category A is used when a phenomenon is identified with high confidence; B when the proposed identification is considered very probable; C when the available information is insufficient; and D when no explanation has been established after investigation. The D category may be divided between D1, for moderately well supported unexplained events, and D2, for cases combining high strangeness with particularly strong evidence such as independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr · Classification B: Phenomenon probably identified after investigation. · Classification C:...Read more... The published Ille et Vilaine catalogue contains reports spread across several decades and locations, including Montfort sur Meu, Pleine Fougères, Noyal sur Vilaine, La Fresnais, Miniac Morvan, Vitré, Dinard, Rennes, Cesson Sévigné, Châteaugiron, Saint Coulomb and Étrelles. The outcomes are mixed: identified or probably identified events dominate, several older files lack enough information, and only a small number remain officially unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN That pattern matters more than the raw number of reports. The departmental record is not a continuous history of mysterious craft. It is a collection shaped by reporting habits, news coverage, weather, astronomical conditions, new technology in the sky and the variable quality of witness information. Noyal sur Vilaine: the enduring 1990 triangle The clearest unresolved historical case in Ille et Vilaine occurred on 26 October 1990, shortly before the much larger national wave of 5 November. A motorist returning from work near Noyal sur Vilaine reported an object described as triangular and approximately the apparent size of an aircraft. According to the official summary, it changed direction, moved without audible noise and could not be identified. GEIPAN classified the report as D, describing it as strange to very strange with medium to strong consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990 GEIPAN... The case is memorable because it contains several features commonly regarded as significant in UFO reports: a defined geometric shape, apparent manoeuvring, silence and an aircraft like scale. Yet the publicly available summary is extremely brief. It does not present independent witnesses, radar confirmation, photographs, a measured distance or a documented exclusion of every aircraft and astronomical possibility. That creates a sharp distinction between unexplained and strongly evidenced . The report is unexplained because no satisfactory identification was reached from the information in the file. It is not proof that an unusual physical vehicle was present. Apparent size cannot be converted into true size without knowing distance, while perceived speed and direction can be distorted when a witness is moving in a car and lacks fixed reference points in a dark sky. The timing also invites an understandable but potentially misleading connection with the events of 5 November. There is no official evidence that the Noyal sur Vilaine observation formed part of that later national episode. They occurred ten days apart and had different reported characteristics. Treating them as one “flap” may be tempting, but it goes beyond what the records demonstrate. Étrelles 2014: the strongest modern case The Étrelles observation of 1 June 2014 is the department’s most substantial recent unresolved case. Two people travelling in a work vehicle briefly saw a dark mass carrying two small, faint lights. They reported that it suddenly accelerated at apparently extreme speed and disappeared. GEIPAN conducted a field investigation, considered several possible explanations and concluded that none matched the account well. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANETRELLES (35) 01.06.2014 GEIPAN... The witnesses were judged highly reliable, and the observing conditions were described as favourable. A meteor or fireball was specifically considered but did not adequately reproduce the reported appearance and behaviour. The case was ultimately classified D1: unexplained, with good consistency and moderate strangeness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANETRELLES (35) 01.06.2014 GEIPAN... That classification is carefully limited. The observation was very brief, the two witnesses immediately discussed what they had seen and therefore were not wholly independent, and no photograph or video was obtained. GEIPAN also noted uncertainty over the phenomenon’s trajectory and stated that the existence of a material object was possible rather than established. These weaknesses prevented the stronger D2 classification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANETRELLES (35) 01.06.2014 GEIPAN... Étrelles therefore illustrates both the value and the limits of witness credibility. Two dependable observers can make a case more consistent, particularly when their descriptions broadly agree. Reliability does not solve the basic geometry, however. With no measured range, a small nearby object and a larger distant object may create similar impressions. A sudden disappearance may represent acceleration, but it can also result from loss of illumination, changing viewing angle, obstruction or a brief observation ending before the witnesses can orient themselves. Later review has not supplied a conventional identification, so the case remains genuinely open within the official archive. What it supports is the modest conclusion that two credible people reported a short lived aerial event that investigators could not confidently explain. The 5 November 1990 wave over Ille et Vilaine On the evening of 5 November 1990, residents across France reported luminous formations, coloured lights, trails and apparently enormous silent objects moving generally from west to east. Ille et Vilaine witnesses were among them. A surviving gendarmerie file records two farmers who described a reddish or orange luminous trail, a large round light and a smaller point above it, visible for roughly 20 to 40 seconds. Both contacted the authorities after hearing national news reports about the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Contemporary press material preserved in the file shows why the incident became famous. Witnesses throughout France described triangles, dark masses, multiple lights and objects apparently flying at low altitude. Calls reached gendarmeries, fire services, weather offices and newspapers, while early official comments were cautious and sometimes contradictory. The lack of an immediate, confident explanation allowed the story to develop as a national UFO mystery. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The later CNES reconstruction is much stronger. GEIPAN identifies the principal phenomenon as the atmospheric re entry of the third stage of a Soviet Proton rocket. Fragmenting debris crossed the sky at an altitude decreasing from roughly 90 to 50 kilometres, producing an extended procession of luminous pieces and trails. A"},{"title":"1990 Wave","url":"/1990-wave-9fbd5a/","description":"A dramatic mass sighting over Ille-et-Vilaine became a national UFO mystery before CNES linked it to a fragmenting Soviet rocket stage.","breadcrumb":"Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"1990 Wave How a Rocket Re entry Became a UFO Wave Ille et Vilaine UFOs 1990 Wave 1990 Wave How a Rocket Re entry Became a UFO Wave A dramatic mass sighting over Ille et Vilaine became a national UFO mystery before CNES linked it to a fragmenting Soviet rocket stage. A dramatic mass sighting over Ille et Vilaine became a national UFO mystery before CNES linked it to a fragmenting Soviet rocket stage. event time window What witnesses across Ille et Vilaine saw How the Soviet rocket stage was identified Why separate lights looked like solid craft ille et vilaine 1990 wave Introduction On the evening of 5 November 1990, people across Ille et Vilaine saw groups of brilliant lights moving across a clear sky. Some described a luminous procession; others perceived an enormous dark triangle or elongated craft carrying several lights. Similar reports arrived almost simultaneously from much of France, creating one of the country’s largest and most memorable UFO waves. The central event is now well explained. France’s space agency, CNES, identified the phenomenon as the atmospheric re entry and fragmentation of a Soviet Proton rocket stage associated with the launch of the Gorizont 21 communications satellite. Orbital calculations matched the timing, direction and national spread of the observations. GEIPAN, the CNES unit responsible for investigating unusual aerospace reports, classifies the event as category A: identified without significant ambiguity. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Its importance to Ille et Vilaine’s UFO history lies not in an unresolved mystery, but in what it reveals about eyewitness evidence. A real and extraordinary event occurred, yet its height, distance and structure were widely misperceived. Separate burning fragments moving together could convincingly resemble the lights of one vast, low flying craft. What witnesses across Ille et Vilaine saw The main display began at about 7 pm. CNES later reported receiving information from more than a thousand witnesses nationwide, many through the National Gendarmerie. The phenomenon crossed the sky over several minutes rather than flashing past like a conventional meteor, giving observers time to watch it, interpret it and sometimes alert other people. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s national case file contains statements from numerous towns and departments. The Ille et Vilaine material includes reports associated with Sougéal, Saint Broladre, Saint Senoux, Châteaugiron and Nouvoitou. These locations are spread from the department’s northern edge near Mont Saint Michel Bay to the country south east of Rennes, showing that the local sightings were not confined to one village or one viewing position. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The descriptions varied, but several recurring elements also appeared elsewhere in France: numerous white, yellow, orange or reddish lights; a broad formation travelling generally from west south west towards east north east; glowing trails or smaller lights apparently following larger ones; slow, smooth and often silent movement; an impression that the phenomenon was at aircraft height; a dark triangular, rectangular or cigar shaped body seemingly connecting the lights. Those reports should not be dismissed as inventions. Witnesses were responding to an unusually large, bright and complex spectacle. Even CNES astronaut Jean Pierre Haigneré observed the event and regarded it as striking, while technicians at the CNES balloon facility at Aire sur l’Adour delayed a launch to watch it. The experience was genuinely dramatic even though its cause was conventional. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The geographical pattern is crucial. An individual report of a slow triangular object over Ille et Vilaine might initially invite comparison with aircraft, helicopters or the unresolved Noyal sur Vilaine sighting of 26 October 1990. On 5 November, however, comparable observations appeared along a broad corridor across France at the same time. That large scale coherence points away from a local craft and towards a distant phenomenon visible over hundreds of kilometres. How the Soviet rocket stage was identified The object responsible for the wave was catalogued as 20925, with the international designation 1990 094C. It was connected with the Soviet Gorizont 21 mission, launched aboard a Proton K rocket from Baikonur on 3 November 1990, two days before the sightings. Gorizont 21 itself was a communications satellite intended for geostationary orbit; the object that re entered was discarded launch hardware rather than the working satellite. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... CNES did not announce the answer instantly. Its security services were overwhelmed by calls on the evening of the event, and SEPRA—the predecessor of GEIPAN—began checking with civil and military aviation authorities, meteorological services and organisations tracking objects in orbit. NASA supplied confirmation on 8 November that object 1990 094C had re entered on 5 November. CNES orbital specialists then confirmed a track crossing France from the Bay of Biscay towards Alsace, and the finding was released to news agencies on 9 November. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... A member of an experienced observing network had already alerted SEPRA on 6 November that the phenomenon was probably the re entry of 1990 094C. According to the GEIPAN account, this observer calculated that the object passed roughly 103 kilometres above the Bay of Biscay and left eastern France at about 83 kilometres altitude. The early identification matters because it was based on orbital information, not an explanation invented later merely to fit the publicity. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The case was also reported in the contemporary national press. By 10 November, Le Monde described the UFO as identified with the disintegration of parts of a Soviet rocket during atmospheric re entry. A follow up account noted that the debris had spent a short period in orbit before entering the denser atmosphere on the evening of 5 November. [Le Monde.fr] lemonde.fr l ovni du 5 novembre est identifie 3978101 1819218 Le Monde.frL'OVNI du 5 novembre est... identifié10 Nov 1990 — Il s'agit de la désintégration, lors de la rentrée dans l'atmosphère, de mo... The reconstructed path GEIPAN later modelled the event using CNES software designed to simulate the atmospheric braking of debris with different shapes and densities. The simplified model represented the rocket stage with a cylindrical body and two spherical tank like components. In reality, fragmentation probably produced tens or hundreds of pieces with different masses, forms and rates of deceleration. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The reconstruction placed the beginning of major fragmentation at approximately 94 kilometres altitude over the Bay of Biscay. GEIPAN acknowledged uncertainty about exactly how far the debris cloud stretched along its direction of travel, because the number and physical properties of the pieces were unknown. The cross track position—the corridor followed across France—was considerably less uncertain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... During the visible phase, the fragments descended through roughly 90 to 50 kilometres altitude. Their speed fell from around 7 kilometres per second to about 2 kilometres per second, equivalent to approximately 25,000 to 7,200 kilometres per hour. Those figures are incompatible with the slow aircraft height object perceived by many witnesses, but they are consistent with orbital debris losing speed as it broke apart in the upper atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... 24:46 Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick West Channel: Cool Worlds · Views: 365.2K · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Why separate lights looked like solid craft The hardest part of the rocket explanation is not establishing that a re entry occurred. The orbital identification is strong. The harder question is why sincere observers—including experienced aircrew—described an object that seemed structured, low and sometimes enormous. The answer begins with the night sky’s lack of reliable distance cues. When an unfamiliar light is seen against darkness, the observer generally has no known size, surface detail or background reference from which to calculate its distance. Brightness can create an unconscious impression of proximity. Once a witness assumes that the lights are only a few hundred metres"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-2fe8a9/","description":"Two credible but brief encounters remain officially unexplained, despite field investigation and careful limits on what the evidence can prove.","breadcrumb":"Which Ille et Vilaine UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Why Do These Two Ille et Vilaine Cases Remain Open? Ille et Vilaine UFOs Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Why Do These Two Ille et Vilaine Cases Remain Open? Two credible but brief encounters remain officially unexplained, despite field investigation and careful limits on what the evidence can prove. Two credible but brief encounters remain officially unexplained, despite field investigation and careful limits on what the evidence can prove. dataset evidence What the witnesses reported What GEIPAN could and could not establish How strong is the surviving evidence? ille et vilaine unresolved cases Introduction Noyal sur Vilaine in 1990 and Étrelles in 2014 remain the two clearest officially unresolved aerial reports in Ille et Vilaine , but they are not equally well documented. At Noyal sur Vilaine, a lone motorist reported a large, silent triangular object that changed direction; the surviving public record is striking but extremely sparse. At Étrelles, two witnesses in the same vehicle described a dark mass with two dim lights, followed by an apparently abrupt, high speed departure. That later case prompted interviews, a site reconstruction and checks against weather, aviation and meteor data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990 GEIPAN... Both cases remain open because investigators could not match the reports convincingly to a known phenomenon. That does not mean an extraordinary craft was established. “Unresolved” means that no proposed explanation adequately accounted for the information available, while crucial measurements or independent records needed for a firm conclusion were absent. What the witnesses reported Noyal sur Vilaine: a silent triangle in 1990 At about 19:40 on 26 October 1990, a 40 year old man was driving home from work near Noyal sur Vilaine when he reported seeing a single object in the sky. GEIPAN’s public case data describe an estimated distance of roughly 500 metres to one kilometre, a completely silent observation and an apparent volume comparable to a large passenger aircraft. The witness characterised the object as triangular or conical, with variable motion rather than a simple steady passage. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990NOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990. Témoin. Date de l'observation. 26/10/1990. Age. 40. Genre. H. R... The official summary reduces the episode to its most important features: an aircraft sized triangular object, a change of direction and movement without audible noise. No identification was found, and the case remains in category D—GEIPAN’s designation for a phenomenon that is still unidentified after examination rather than merely unanalysable for lack of information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990 GEIPAN... Those details make the case memorable, particularly because the observation occurred only ten days before the famous nationwide reports of 5 November 1990. That later wave is now attributed by CNES to an atmospheric re entry, but the timing alone does not connect the two events. Noyal sur Vilaine involved a separate date, an individual report and an apparently manoeuvring geometric form. It should therefore not be absorbed automatically into the explanation of the November wave. The official catalogue continues to list the Noyal case separately as unresolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casNOYAL SUR VILAINE (35) 26.10.1990, 26/10/1990, D, Ille et Vilaine, Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de consistance... The difficulty is that almost everything depends on one person’s visual judgement. The public file contains no photograph, radar return, second witness, precise angular measurements or detailed investigative report. Estimates such as “the size of a Boeing 747” or a distance below one kilometre cannot be treated as physical measurements when the object’s true distance was unknown. A small, nearer light arrangement and a large, distant object can produce similar apparent sizes, especially during a short night time encounter. Noyal sur Vilaine is therefore unresolved in a narrow but legitimate sense: the description is unusual and no convincing identification was recorded, yet the surviving evidence is too limited to reconstruct the event securely. It is stronger than a vague light report, but much weaker than a case supported by independent observations or instruments. Étrelles: two lights beneath a dark mass The Étrelles encounter occurred shortly after 23:00 on 1 June 2014 in the Piquet industrial zone north of the town. Two people were travelling together in a service vehicle at only about 15–20 kilometres per hour. They said that a phenomenon appeared ahead and almost above the car, paused very briefly, then vanished towards the east at what seemed to be extraordinary speed. The entire event lasted only two or three seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Both witnesses described two small, round lights, white with a slight green tint. The lights remained at a constant separation and appeared to be attached to, or positioned beneath, a compact dark mass. One witness believed that this mass blocked visible stars and estimated its apparent size as roughly twice that of a large people carrier. Neither witness heard an engine, rotor noise or any other sound associated with the phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Their accounts were similar but not identical. One witness concentrated mainly on the two lights and said he could not describe a definite surrounding shape. The other reported a vague rectangular or solid dark form and described a bent trajectory: first approaching from the right, then turning sharply before heading east. The investigators treated that claimed turn as important, because a genuine change of course would make a simple meteor explanation much less satisfactory. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witnesses reported the incident promptly. A gendarmerie statement was taken the following morning, and one witness contacted GEIPAN. The police record is valuable because it captures the basic description before a long period of retelling: two aligned lights, sudden acceleration or a sharp directional change, silence and a duration of only a few seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. What GEIPAN could establish The Étrelles investigation was considerably more substantial than the surviving work on Noyal sur Vilaine. Two GEIPAN investigators visited the site on 30 June 2014. They interviewed the witnesses separately using a structured cognitive interview approach, returned with them to the observation point and recorded estimated directions, elevations, landmarks and environmental conditions. Follow up telephone interviews examined the timing and the reported change of course. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The reconstruction supported several relatively secure points. The witnesses had an open view from a slow moving vehicle; the night was clear, calm and starry; nearby buildings and vegetation did not significantly obstruct the relevant part of the sky. Both witnesses identified broadly the same disappearance point on the eastern horizon. Their descriptions also agreed on two lights, silence and a startlingly rapid departure. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Investigators checked military air control information. The French national air operations centre reported ordinary airliner traffic but no unusual radar trace at the relevant place and time. This finding did not confirm the witnesses’ object, because radar coverage and detectability are never absolute. It did, however, leave investigators without an identified aircraft that matched the report. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A surveillance camera at a nearby aerospace electronics site was also considered. Its recordings showed nothing abnormal, but the investigation established that the camera was directed towards the entrance gate rather than the area of sky in question. Its failure to record the phenomenon was therefore neutral rather than disconfirming evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete113 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Identifier les OVNIS / Super Pouvoirs Anti Mythos Info ou Mytho Channel: Info ou Mytho ? Open on YouTube Why the meteor explanation fell short The most concrete conventional candidate was a bright meteor recorded that night. Astronomical information placed a fragmented meteor at 23:12:30 local time, potentially visible from Étrelles. Its division"},{"title":"From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights","url":"/indre-et-loire/","description":"Indre-et-Loire has a long and colourful UFO history, but its surviving evidence is less mysterious than local folklore sometimes suggests.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights Indre et Loire has a long and colourful UFO history, but its surviving evidence is less mysterious than local folklore sometimes suggests. Indre et Loire has a long and colourful UFO history, but its surviving evidence is less mysterious than local folklore sometimes suggests. The department’s best known story is the alleged 1954 encounter near Marcilly sur Vienne, where quarry workers reportedly saw a disc and a helmeted figure. indre et loire Introduction The official record is more prosaic. GEIPAN, the unit of France’s space agency that investigates unusual aerial phenomena, has repeatedly identified local reports as stars, satellites, lanterns, lasers, meteors, aircraft or lights on the ground. Its published Indre et Loire cases contain no robustly documented example currently classified as genuinely unexplained. That does not mean witnesses imagined everything. It shows how real observations can acquire an extraordinary appearance through distance, darkness, weather, unfamiliar technology and incomplete information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The 1954 encounter that defined Touraine’s UFO story The department’s landmark case reportedly occurred at about 4.30 pm on 30 September 1954, during France’s celebrated autumn “flying saucer” wave. The location is variously described as Marcilly sur Vienne or Nouâtre, reflecting the quarry’s position near the two communes. Contemporary and later summaries identify the principal witness as Georges Gatay, a site foreman working with a team of labourers. In the familiar version, Gatay walked a short distance from the others and saw a small human like figure standing near a grey, domed disc hovering roughly a metre above the ground. The figure supposedly wore a helmet or opaque visor. Gatay said he felt unable to move. The being then vanished abruptly, while the craft rose with a whistle and disappeared into a bluish haze. Other workers were said to have seen at least part of the scene, and later retellings added headaches, insomnia or temporary paralysis. Sources disagree over whether there were seven, eight or eleven witnesses and over exactly what each person observed. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The case mattered because it appeared to offer nearly everything that post war ufologists wanted from a close encounter: daylight, several witnesses, a structured craft, a reported occupant and bodily effects. It was quoted by influential writers including Jacques Vallée and became one of the better known French “humanoid” cases outside Valensole and Quarouble. Its timing also amplified its impact. Newspapers were then reporting apparent saucers across France, so a strange local story could be rapidly absorbed into a national narrative. Why the case is now deeply doubtful The evidence is weaker than the dramatic story implies. No photograph, instrument record or physical trace is known. Most accessible accounts are compilations written years after the event, often repeating one another. Even the number of witnesses and the craft’s precise appearance vary between versions. More damagingly, later sceptical accounts state that Gatay acknowledged fabricating the incident. A detailed specialist case file reproduces both the original pro UFO literature and later references describing the affair as a hoax or “a joke that went too far”. The same compilation notes that investigator René Pacaut revisited the area in 1971 and encountered information casting doubt on the story. Although the original admission is difficult to verify independently from readily accessible primary documentation, the allegation is serious enough that the case cannot responsibly be treated as an unresolved landing. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The safest assessment is therefore historically important but evidentially discredited . The incident remains central to Indre et Loire’s UFO culture because of the publicity it received, not because it supplies dependable evidence of an unknown craft. Saint Roch: intriguing traces, missing documentation A second local story concerns Saint Roch on 28 May 1977. According to local investigator Rémy Borne, a witness saw a black rectangular object apparently descending beside an electricity pole. The gendarmerie reportedly visited the site and noted disturbed or spiralled grass. The account appears in local reporting about Borne’s catalogue of Touraine sightings, which covers reports collected from press archives and personal testimony between 1935 and 2019. [info tours.fr] info tours.fr Info Tours OVNI en Touraine: un livre pour raconter 85 ans d’observations Info Tours OVNI en Touraine: un livre pour raconter 85 ans d’observations This is exactly the sort of case that sounds stronger when summarised than when examined. Police attendance and ground marks can give a report an official aura, but neither establishes what produced the disturbance. A useful investigation would need the original gendarmerie report, witness statements taken close to the event, measurements, photographs, weather records and evidence excluding vehicles, animals, wind effects or ordinary work at the site. Those materials are not presented in the accessible local account. The case should consequently be described as unresolved in local tradition but weakly documented , rather than as a confirmed landing. Its value is mainly archival: it shows how alleged physical traces and gendarmerie involvement became recurring markers of credibility in regional UFO narratives. What the official files actually contain GEIPAN was created within the French National Centre for Space Studies in 1977. It collects reports, works with police and gendarmerie officers, consults investigators and scientific specialists, and publishes anonymised case files. Its task is to explain unusual experiences where possible—not to search for extraterrestrial visitors. It deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” because witnesses may be seeing a light, atmospheric effect or optical impression rather than a solid object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN A review of GEIPAN’s downloadable case data, current through late November 2025, produces 35 entries assigned to Indre et Loire. Sixteen are classified A, meaning an explanation is established or regarded as effectively certain; twelve are B, where a conventional explanation is considered probable; and seven are C, meaning there is not enough reliable information to decide. None is classified D1 or D2—the categories reserved for adequately documented cases that retain significant strangeness after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPANFichiers CSV Cas... NEUVY LE ROI (37) 07.12.1979 07/12/1979 37 Indre et Loire Centre NEUVY LE ROI (37) 07.12.1979. Observations pa... That distinction matters. A C case is not a particularly strong mystery. It is usually a report too incomplete to analyse properly. GEIPAN itself notes that C cases sit apart from the sequence running from identified to genuinely unexplained because investigators cannot reach a sound conclusion from the available material. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Les OVNI, phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés Eclairages Channel: ANews Sécurité Open on YouTube Three investigations that changed the local story Neuvy le Roi: several witnesses, but still the Moon On 7 December 1979, several motorists around Neuvy le Roi reported a luminous phenomenon low in the eastern sky. Descriptions differed: witnesses compared it with a ball, a parachute or a capital letter L. Multiple independent observers and apparently sincere testimony initially made the incident seem substantial. A later GEIPAN review concluded that the object’s direction, colour, apparent size, duration and position matched the rising Moon. The witnesses had not necessarily seen it inaccurately; rather, they interpreted an unfamiliar view of it as something nearby or moving. Night driving, fatigue, intervening landscape features and changing viewpoints can make a stationary astronomical object appear to follow a vehicle or alter position. GEIPAN now classifies the case A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This is one of the department’s most instructive cases because it demonstrates that several credible witnesses do not automatically rule out misidentification. Independent people sharing the same road, viewing conditions and unusual Moon appearance can make the same mistake. The 1993 “flap”: nightclub lasers over the countryside During 1993,"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-49af96/","description":"GEIPAN's local files show how the Moon, nightclub lasers and incomplete reports can turn sincere observations into apparent mysteries.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights","level":2,"text":"Official Files Why Indre et Loire's Official UFO Cases Were Explained Touraine UFOs Official Files Official Files Why Indre et Loire's Official UFO Cases Were Explained GEIPAN's local files show how the Moon, nightclub lasers and incomplete reports can turn sincere observations into apparent mysteries. GEIPAN's local files show how the Moon, nightclub lasers and incomplete reports can turn sincere observations into apparent mysteries. dataset evidence What the GEIPAN classifications mean The Moon at Neuvy le Roi and lasers over Touraine Why incomplete cases are not the same as unexplained cases indre et loire official files Introduction Indre et Loire ’s official UFO archive is notable less for an extraordinary unsolved encounter than for showing how ordinary phenomena can look extraordinary at night. In the files published by GEIPAN, the investigation unit of France’s national space agency, striking local reports have been attributed to the rising Moon, nightclub lasers, bright stars and sky lanterns. Other reports remain unclassified because the information is too incomplete to support any conclusion. That distinction matters. A case labelled “unidentified” in everyday conversation may ultimately be explained, while a file that cannot be solved because essential details are missing is not evidence of an unknown craft. In the current published department record reviewed for this page, no Indre et Loire case meets GEIPAN’s standard for a well documented unexplained event. The archive instead offers a useful lesson in how witness sincerity, visual illusion, atmospheric conditions and retrospective investigation can interact. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... What the GEIPAN classifications mean GEIPAN, the Study and Information Group on Unidentified Aerospace Phenomena, has operated within the French space agency CNES since 1977. It receives testimony, creates a case file, conducts an initial assessment, investigates where possible, anonymises the material and publishes a classification. Its role is to analyse reported aerial phenomena using established scientific knowledge, not to prove extraterrestrial visitation or investigate paranormal claims. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... The four main classifications are easy to misunderstand: A means the phenomenon has been identified after investigation. B means an explanation is considered probable, although not established with the same confidence as an A case. C means the case cannot be identified because too little reliable information is available. D means the phenomenon remains unidentified despite an investigation supported by sufficient information. GEIPAN reaches these decisions by considering both the report’s consistency —the quantity and reliability of the available evidence—and its residual strangeness after ordinary explanations have been tested. A vivid story can therefore have low evidential value, while a multiple witness report can still receive an A classification if its direction, timing and appearance match a known object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN This is particularly important when reading older local files. Several Indre et Loire reports were first placed in C or D categories and reconsidered years later as better mapping, astronomical software and accumulated experience made specific explanations possible. A change of classification does not necessarily mean that the original investigators were careless. It may mean that they lacked the tools, reference cases or environmental information available to later analysts. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete10 Notes d'enquete10 The Moon at Neuvy le Roi One of the strongest Indre et Loire examples began as a genuinely impressive multiple witness report. At about 9.15 pm on 7 December 1979, people travelling on separate roads near Neuvy le Roi saw a large red orange light low in the eastern sky. One driver described changing shapes, including something resembling a capital L and later a reddish parachute with a dark mass beneath it. A father and daughter in another car described an enormous orange red ball that remained visible for much of their journey towards the town. Gendarmes took statements from three witnesses within days of the event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete10 Notes d'enquete10 The witnesses’ descriptions made the phenomenon seem nearby and mobile. It appeared to travel beside one car, disappear behind farm buildings and re emerge, while its apparent size was estimated as much greater than the Moon. Yet the same details eventually supplied the solution. When GEIPAN re examined the file, it reconstructed the witnesses’ routes and lines of sight. Both groups had been looking east or east north east at almost the same time. The phenomenon stayed at a very low angular elevation, was intermittently hidden by buildings or terrain, and seemed to keep pace with moving vehicles. That apparent pursuit is a familiar perspective effect: an extremely distant object changes direction so little relative to the observer that it can seem to accompany a car. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete10 Notes d'enquete10 Astronomical reconstruction showed the Moon rising at an azimuth of about 67 degrees, in the reported direction. It was in a gibbous phase, four days before the last quarter, which could produce the curved or parachute like outline described when partly obscured by cloud. Its reddish colour was consistent with a low Moon seen through a long stretch of atmosphere. The familiar “Moon illusion” also helps explain why it appeared exceptionally large near the horizon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete10 Notes d'enquete10 The case is valuable because the explanation does not require the witnesses to have been dishonest or foolish. GEIPAN explicitly found the testimony detailed and consistent and did not question the witnesses’ sincerity. It concluded that the observed position, duration, colour, shape and behaviour all corresponded to the rising Moon and reclassified the case from C to A. Strong testimony had recorded a real visual experience; it was the perceived distance and nature of the light that were mistaken. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete10 Notes d'enquete10 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lasers over Touraine A very different source of apparent craft emerged in the department during the early 1990s: entertainment lasers projected into the sky. These could create blue or pale shapes on cloud layers, sweep from side to side and appear to hover above roads. To drivers unfamiliar with the technology, the projected light could look like a thin, structured object moving deliberately. The best local reference case occurred near L’Île Bouchard on 11 December 1993. GEIPAN’s archive identifies the phenomenon as a laser and classifies the case A. The investigation traced the displays to a nightclub device marketed as a “Sky Rose”, providing direct local evidence that such equipment was operating in the area and producing unusual aerial effects at the time. The case later became a comparison point for reassessing similar reports elsewhere in Indre et Loire and beyond. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANGeipan Groupe d’études et d’informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés.... ILE BOUCHARD... That comparison was central to the re examination of a report now listed as Saint Branchs, dated 16 May 1993 and also known in earlier records as Saint Germain sur Vienne or Cormery. Between midnight and 1 am, three people travelling in a car repeatedly noticed an elongated bluish form near the apparent height of the headlights. It moved continuously from side to side and remained difficult to place at any definite distance. The observation continued intermittently over roughly four kilometres, although only one formal witness statement was collected. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT BRANCHS (37) 16.05.1993 GEIPAN... GEIPAN considered the appearance characteristic of a nightclub laser: bluish, thin, apparently insubstantial, illuminated from below and moving in regular lateral sweeps. Cloud towards the northern horizon could have acted as a projection surface even though the sky looked clearer along parts of the witnesses’ route. Investigators could not identify the exact projector a quarter of a century later, but the close match with documented laser cases led them to regard the explanation as very probable and to change the case from its former D status to A. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT BRANCHS (37) 16.05.1993 GEIPAN... The apparent"},{"title":"Marcilly Case","url":"/marcilly-case/","description":"The department's most famous close encounter became a ufology classic, but conflicting testimony and a reported confession badly weaken it.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Misidentified Lights","level":2,"text":"Marcilly Case Did Touraine's Famous Close Encounter Ever Happen? Touraine UFOs Marcilly Case Marcilly Case Did Touraine's Famous Close Encounter Ever Happen? The department's most famous close encounter became a ufology classic, but conflicting testimony and a reported confession badly weaken it. The department's most famous close encounter became a ufology classic, but conflicting testimony and a reported confession badly weaken it. event time window What Georges Gatay and the quarry workers reportedly saw How the story became an international UFO classic Conflicting accounts, missing evidence and the alleged admission indre et loire marcilly case Introduction The alleged encounter near Marcilly sur Vienne on 30 September 1954 is the best known UFO story from Indre et Loire , but it is not a secure unexplained case. Quarry foreman Georges Gatay reportedly saw a small, helmeted figure beside a hovering disc in broad daylight, while several workmates claimed to witness at least part of the episode. Newspapers quickly transformed the account into a dramatic story of a landed craft, paralysis and mysterious physical effects. Its fame rests largely on those striking details and its place in France’s extraordinary 1954 wave of “flying saucer” reports. Its credibility rests on much less. There is no known photograph, physical trace, official technical record or surviving set of independent witness statements. Published versions conflict over the number of witnesses and what they actually saw, while a later investigation reportedly produced an admission that the affair had been invented. The responsible verdict is therefore not “confirmed encounter” or even a strong unresolved case, but a culturally important story whose evidential foundations have substantially collapsed. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... Crashed UFO Prank Channel: Just For Laughs Gags Open on YouTube What Gatay and the quarry workers reportedly saw The incident was said to have occurred at about 4.30 pm in a sand and gravel quarry beside the road near Marcilly sur Vienne, close to Nouâtre. The two place names are often used interchangeably in later literature because the site lay in the area between the communes. Gatay, the works foreman, was reportedly standing apart from the other labourers when he noticed an object hovering near the edge of the excavation. Contemporary regional reporting described the weather as clear and the quarry machinery as noisy enough to explain why nobody had heard the object arrive. [noyers nouatre.fr] noyers nouatre.fr Open source on noyers nouatre.fr. In the newspaper version, the craft was circular, metallic grey and topped by a dome. What looked like rapidly turning helicopter blades were said to be attached to its upper part. It hovered roughly one metre above the ground rather than making a conventional landing. Beside it stood a figure about 1.50 to 1.55 metres tall, wearing a neutral coloured suit, ankle boots and a helmet whose surface resembled clouded or opaque glass. The figure reportedly held a thick pistol like object or tube, while a bright disc on its chest projected an intense light. [noyers nouatre.fr] noyers nouatre.fr Open source on noyers nouatre.fr. Gatay’s later, more elaborate account added a dreamlike feeling before the sighting and an inability to move during it. He said the figure vanished instantaneously, as though an image had been erased, rather than walking or climbing visibly into the craft. The object then supposedly rose in jerks, produced a whistling sound and disappeared into a bluish haze. Some retellings attributed insomnia, headaches, loss of appetite or other physiological effects to Gatay and, less consistently, to the whole group. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... These details are memorable, but they do not form one stable testimony. The earliest press material speaks of Gatay and six companions, yet later catalogues commonly describe Gatay plus seven workers or a team of eight. One passage in the regional account even refers to Gatay and five labourers before naming additional men and a lorry driver. The number is therefore not a trivial clerical issue: it shows how difficult it is to establish who was present, who saw the figure, who saw only the object and who merely agreed with a drawing afterwards. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... The surviving narrative also blurs direct observation and group confirmation. Gatay was explicitly identified as the first and principal witness. The other workers were said to have looked in his direction and recognised the same general scene, but readily accessible accounts do not preserve six or seven full, separately recorded descriptions. Claims of “concordant testimony” came mainly through journalists and later UFO writers, not through a published bundle of signed statements that can now be compared line by line. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... How the story became an international UFO classic Marcilly appeared at precisely the right moment to become famous. France was experiencing an intense wave of saucer reporting in September and October 1954, with newspapers carrying stories of discs, cigar shaped objects, strange occupants and landed machines from numerous regions. A daylight encounter involving labourers, a structured craft and an apparently non human pilot fitted the dominant news pattern almost perfectly. National and foreign coverage followed the first regional reports within days. Surviving bibliographies identify treatment in La Nouvelle République du Centre Ouest , France Soir , the London Sunday Dispatch , France Dimanche , Ici Paris , Paris Match , Radar and Le Courrier de l’Ouest . [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... The press presentation encouraged belief by emphasising the men’s apparent respectability. The regional reporter described them as sincere and worthy of trust and said that unnamed “professional investigators” had also been impressed by their manner. Another account stressed that the workers were well known locally and appeared unlikely to organise an enormous practical joke. Such character judgements mattered in 1954, when there was no photograph or instrument reading to examine: the reader was effectively asked to decide whether ordinary working men looked honest. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... Yet sincerity as perceived by a reporter is not the same as factual accuracy. A group can misinterpret an event, conform to a dominant account, repeat a workplace joke or appear convincing while withholding information. The press articles give little detail about the alleged “professional” inquiries: the investigators are not consistently named, their methods are unclear, and no technical report has emerged showing measurements, site tests or individually isolated interviews. The contemporary material establishes that a story was told and widely publicised; it does not independently establish that the described object existed. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 30 septembre, Marcilly sur Vienne, Indre et Loire... Ufologist Jacques Vallée gave Marcilly lasting international visibility. In Passport to Magonia , he presented Gatay as the head of a team of eight construction workers and treated the sudden disappearance of the figure and object as an example of the strange, apparition like qualities found in close encounter reports. His catalogue reduced the event to a potent formula: a disc at ground level, a nearby humanoid, an unusual disappearance and physiological effects. That compact entry was repeatedly copied into later catalogues and humanoid databases. [archive.org] ia601409.us.archive.org Passport to Magonia—UFOs, Folklore, and Parallel Worlds, Jacques Vallée (1993 Archive.org... This process made the case sound better documented than it was. Each repetition gave the impression of another source, although many entries ultimately led back to the same newspaper reports or to a small number of early UFO books. Later writers also regularised uncertain details. The craft became a “disc at ground level” even though the regional account said it remained airborne; effects on Gatay became effects on all witnesses; and the total number of observers shifted without explanation. Marcilly’s international status therefore reflects successful transmission through"},{"title":"Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious","url":"/why-indres-ufo-record-is-more-revealing/","description":"Indre has a modest but revealing official UFO record.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious Indre has a modest but revealing official UFO record. Indre has a modest but revealing official UFO record. The publicly available GEIPAN archive contains a small group of reports from places including Issoudun, Le Blanc, Écueillé, Palluau sur Indre, Vatan, Châtillon sur Indre, Lacs, Luant and Mâron. indre Introduction That does not make the department’s UFO history meaningless. Indre combines dark rural skies, scattered settlements and a deep aviation heritage centred on Châteauroux Déols. Its reports show how unfamiliar lights can appear structured, silent or close, and how later investigation often changes the interpretation without implying that witnesses were dishonest. What the official record actually shows France’s official body for such reports is GEIPAN, part of the national space agency CNES. It investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena rather than searching for extraterrestrial visitors. Its classifications describe the outcome of an investigation: category A means an identified phenomenon; B means a probably identified phenomenon; C means that reliable information is insufficient; and D1 or D2 denotes a residual anomaly whose strangeness remains after examination, with differing evidential strength. Cases may be reconsidered when new records or improved analytical tools become available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Indre entries illustrate why those distinctions matter. An unexplained report is not necessarily a mysterious phenomenon: it may simply be impossible to test because the date is uncertain, the direction was not recorded, there was only one witness or the account reached investigators too late. The department’s published cases broadly fall into three groups: Probably or firmly explained: the Vatan aircraft sighting, the Écueillé fireball, the Luant observation of Sirius, probable lanterns at Châtillon sur Indre and Lacs, and probable balloons at Mâron. Too poorly documented to decide: the older reports from Issoudun and Le Blanc. A physical anomaly with a likely ordinary cause: the flattened crop circle discovered near Palluau sur Indre, where investigators favoured human action even though they could not demonstrate it conclusively. This is not the profile of a sustained unexplained “flap”. The reports are separated by years or decades, occurred under different circumstances and do not describe a consistent object, route or behaviour. Issoudun and Le Blanc: striking stories, weak evidence The 23 January 1977 Issoudun report is the most visually elaborate case in the departmental archive. At about 1.45 am, a witness reportedly noticed an orange light approaching from a distance and alerted other people. They then described a group of closely spaced luminous balls and one apparently rectangular form with illuminated openings, all moving without audible sound. GEIPAN left the case in category C because the information was insufficient for a dependable conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPANFichiers CSV Cas... LE BLANC (36) 1980 01/06/1980 36 Indre Centre LE BLANC (36) 1980. Observation d'une forme lumineuse immobile. ... The multiple witnesses give the account more interest than a solitary glimpse. Even so, the surviving summary does not establish reliable angular measurements, distance, altitude or a documented comparison with aircraft movements and astronomical objects. Descriptions such as “football sized” also express apparent size rather than physical dimensions: a small nearby object and a large distant object can subtend the same angle in the sky. Without dependable positional data, the impression of a rectangular craft cannot be tested against a formation of lights, aircraft seen head on, atmospheric distortion or another ordinary source. The Le Blanc case, dated in the archive to 1980, is stranger in a different way. A witness said that an orange oval light remained roughly 50 metres from the home for about 40 minutes. The reported dimensions were approximately 0.8 metres high by 0.4 metres wide. The light had disappeared when the witness returned after going indoors, and no trace was found. A second person reportedly recalled a luminous ball in the area about a month earlier. GEIPAN again assigned category C because the evidence was inadequate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The reported duration argues against a brief meteor, but it does not by itself establish a nearby object. In darkness, observers are generally poor at judging the distance of an isolated light when there are no visible reference points. A stationary astronomical object, a distant terrestrial light, a reflection or an intermittent source can seem much closer than it is. The lack of physical traces neither disproves the observation nor strengthens the extraordinary interpretation. These two cases remain unresolved in the limited administrative sense that no cause was established. They are not equivalent to GEIPAN’s higher quality D cases, where an unusual phenomenon remains after investigators have obtained reasonably consistent and testable information. Vatan: the case that makes the aviation connection concrete On 17 August 1993, two witnesses near Vatan observed two white lights accompanied by flashing red lights. The formation appeared to move slowly and no engine noise was heard. Such a description can easily sound like a large silent object, especially at night. The subsequent inquiry found that a military DC 8 arriving from England had been looking for the landing runway and making wide turns over the area. Its landing lights and navigation beacons matched the observation, and GEIPAN classified the case as A: identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPANFichiers CSV Cas... LE BLANC (36) 1980 01/06/1980 36 Indre Centre LE BLANC (36) 1980. Observation d'une forme lumineuse immobile. ... This is one of the most useful Indre cases because the explanation addresses several details at once. Landing lights viewed nearly head on can dominate the appearance of an aircraft and conceal its outline. Red anti collision or navigation lights can appear separated from the main glare. Sound may arrive late or be inaudible because of distance, wind direction and background noise. Wide turns can also make an aircraft appear to hover, reverse direction or move implausibly slowly. The Vatan investigation demonstrates why checking aviation records matters more than simply matching a witness description to a familiar shape. The reported lights were real, but their interpretation changed when investigators identified the aircraft’s movements. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why Châteauroux’s air history matters — and why it proves little by itself Indre’s association with aviation is unusually strong. An aircraft factory was established at Déols in 1936, and from 1951 Châteauroux became a major American and NATO logistical centre. The Châteauroux Déols complex supported United States Air Forces in Europe and other allied air forces until the American departure following France’s withdrawal from NATO’s integrated military command structure. [gouv.fr] servicehistorique.sga.defense.gouv.fr Service Historique de la Défense Base aérienne 103 Châteauroux Service Historique de la Défense Base aérienne 103 Châteauroux That history makes aircraft activity an essential line of inquiry in local UFO reports. Military transports, maintenance flights, unusual approach patterns and unfamiliar lighting can all produce observations that differ from routine civilian traffic. It would nevertheless be misleading to turn the former base into evidence of a hidden UFO connection. The published Indre record does not show a documented chain of radar incidents, military interceptions or official concern about unexplained craft around Châteauroux. Nor does the chronology support a simple relationship: the American military presence ended before most of the department’s published GEIPAN cases occurred. The strongest demonstrated aviation link is ordinary rather than exotic—the identified DC 8 at Vatan. Fireballs, lanterns and stars Several Indre reports show how common sky phenomena can acquire unusual features when seen briefly or without a clear scale. At Écueillé, three witnesses described a brilliant white ball with a trail, moving rapidly before turning red and disappearing within seconds. GEIPAN judged it a probable atmospheric entry, meaning a bright meteor or fireball. The changing colour, luminous trail, high speed and"},{"title":"Likely Explanations","url":"/likely-explanations-33bba4/","description":"The Vatan aircraft case and other explained sightings show how ordinary lights can appear silent, structured or close when distance and scale are unclear.","breadcrumb":"Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Likely Explanations How Ordinary Lights Became Indre UFO Reports Indre UFOs Likely Explanations Likely Explanations How Ordinary Lights Became Indre UFO Reports The Vatan aircraft case and other explained sightings show how ordinary lights can appear silent, structured or close when distance and scale are unclear. The Vatan aircraft case and other explained sightings show how ordinary lights can appear silent, structured or close when distance and scale are unclear. mechanism The Vatan DC 8 identification and its aviation record Fireballs, stars, lanterns and balloons in the official archive How motion, sound delay and poor scale create false impressions indre likely explanations Introduction Most investigated UFO reports in Indre did not require an exotic explanation. They were traced, with varying degrees of confidence, to aircraft, meteors, bright stars, airborne lanterns or balloons. The most instructive example is the 1993 Vatan case: two witnesses reported slow, silent lights, yet investigators established that a DC 8 was making wide turns while trying to locate a runway. Other cases were matched to a brief atmospheric fireball, Sirius, drifting lanterns and wind borne balloons. [Geipan] geipan.fr VATAN (36) 1993 GEIPAN... These explanations matter because the original observations were not necessarily careless or fabricated. At night, a light often provides no reliable clue to its distance, physical size or speed. Navigation lamps can suggest a structured object; a distant aircraft may be inaudible; lanterns can appear to fly in formation; and a brilliant star can seem to flicker or move. Indres official record therefore shows how ordinary sources become convincing UFO reports when visual impressions are stronger than the available measurements. The Vatan lights were a DC 8 looking for a runway At about 11 pm on 17 August 1993, two witnesses in the Vatan area saw two white lights accompanied by flashing red lights. The group appeared to move slowly, and no engine noise was heard. Those features could easily suggest a large, organised object: paired lights may look like the front of a craft, while red flashes resemble deliberate signalling rather than a random natural phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr VATAN (36) 1993 GEIPAN... The investigation produced a specific aviation match. GEIPANs case summary states that a military DC 8 arriving from England was searching for its landing runway and making broad loops over the area. Its landing lights accounted for the bright white sources, while its normal aircraft lighting accounted for the red flashes. The case is classified A, meaning that the phenomenon was identified rather than merely considered a likely possibility. [Geipan] geipan.fr VATAN (36) 1993 GEIPAN... The aircrafts manoeuvre helps explain why the witnesses initial interpretation was so different from the later finding. An aeroplane following a straight, familiar route usually passes predictably across the sky. One making wide turns may repeatedly change apparent direction, seem to slow down or present its lights almost head on. When viewed head on, most of the aircrafts body can disappear into darkness while its strongest forward facing lights remain visible. What the observer perceives is then not an aeroplane with lights, but several lights whose relationship has to be inferred. The absence of audible noise was not decisive evidence against an aircraft. Sound weakens with distance and can be masked by wind, buildings, vegetation or ordinary background noise. Light reaches the observer effectively at once, whereas aircraft sound arrives later and may appear to come from a position the aircraft has already left. When the range is unknown, witnesses can also underestimate how far away a bright source is and consequently expect it to sound much louder. Vatan is important within Indres UFO history because the explanation rests on contemporaneous aviation information, not simply on a resemblance noticed years later. It demonstrates the value of checking traffic, flight paths and landing activity before treating silence, apparent formation or unusual motion as evidence of an unknown vehicle. It also cautions against assuming that a locally familiar sky will always contain locally familiar aircraft behaviour. A few seconds over cueill matched a fireball The cueill case presents almost the opposite visual problem. Instead of lights lingering and turning, three witnesses described a brilliant object crossing the sky for only a few seconds. It was initially white, left a trail, changed towards red and then disappeared. GEIPAN judged that this combination displayed the characteristic appearance of an atmospheric entry and classified the case B, or probably identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECUEILLE (36) 1982 GEIPAN... A bright meteor, often called a fireball or bolide when exceptionally luminous, can look far closer than it is. Its rapid appearance gives observers little time to establish direction or compare it with familiar landmarks. Fragmentation, a glowing trail and colour changes can be interpreted as an object emitting exhaust, changing power or undergoing controlled manoeuvres. In reality, the changing colour may arise as heated material and surrounding gases radiate while the object loses energy in the atmosphere. The cueill report also illustrates why duration is such an important diagnostic clue. A phenomenon lasting several seconds and moving rapidly along a continuous path is a better match for an atmospheric entry than for a star or lantern. Conversely, a light that remains in approximately one area for many minutes is unlikely to be a meteor. GEIPANs conclusion did not depend on the witnesses using astronomical terminology; it depended on matching the reported speed, trail, brightness, colour change and abrupt disappearance to a known class of event. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECUEILLE (36) 1982 GEIPAN... There is a small archival complication: the case title gives 1982, while the descriptive text refers to 21 October 1981. The published page lists the formal observation date as 21 October 1982. That inconsistency does not undermine the proposed physical mechanism, but it is a reminder that older records can contain transcription or dating problems and should not be presented with more precision than the archive itself supports. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECUEILLE (36) 1982 GEIPAN... 24:46 Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick West Channel: Cool Worlds · Views: 365.2K · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Sirius at Luant showed how a fixed light can appear active GEIPANs record for Luant on 7 November 2015 is classified B and identifies the likely source as Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. The public search record is brief, but the classification is useful because stars are often overlooked in UFO discussions: witnesses naturally expect a star to look stable, whereas a bright star low in the sky can flash, change colour and appear to shift slightly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr CHANCEAUX PRES LOCHES (37) 19.06.1980 19/06/1980 C Indre et Loire Manque d'informations fiables 23/10/2017 1980 06 19... Atmospheric turbulence bends and mixes the incoming light, producing rapid scintillation. Sirius is especially conspicuous because its brightness makes those fluctuations easy to notice. Near the horizon, its light passes through more atmosphere than when it is high in the sky, strengthening apparent red, blue or white flashes. Thin cloud, haze, branches and small movements of the observers head may further create the impression that the source is pulsing or moving. A stationary light can also appear to drift when there are few visual reference points. This effect becomes stronger when an observer watches intensely for several minutes. A nearby roofline or tree can reveal that the light remains in the same position, but in an open rural sky the eye has little against which to judge motion. The Luant classification therefore represents more than the simple statement that it was a star: it shows how an astronomical object can produce a dynamic visual experience without itself moving perceptibly during the observation. The practical distinction is whether the light maintains the same relationship to the horizon and stars over time. A genuine nearby object should usually show measurable displacement, changing angular size or interaction with foreground features. A star may seem agitated but remains astronomically fixed on the timescale of a short sighting. Lanterns produced silent formations at Chtillon and Lacs On 6 March 2011, two witnesses at Chtillon sur Indre watched several red orange lights pass in a straight line at intervals from about 10.15 pm. They heard no sound and recorded photographs and video. GEIPAN compared the footage with confirmed examples of airborne lanterns and concluded that the witnesses had probably observed a sequence of such lanterns. The witnesses themselves had not excluded"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-30e082/","description":"Indre's best-known unresolved reports remain intriguing because the stories are vivid, but the surviving evidence is too weak to test them properly.","breadcrumb":"Why Indre's UFO Record Is More Revealing Than Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Why Indre's Strangest Sightings Remain Unproven Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Why Indre's Strangest Sightings Remain Unproven Indre's best known unresolved reports remain intriguing because the stories are vivid, but the surviving evidence is too weak to test them properly. Indre's best known unresolved reports remain intriguing because the stories are vivid, but the surviving evidence is too weak to test them properly. event time window What witnesses reported at Issoudun and Le Blanc Why apparent size, distance and shape are unreliable at night What category C means and why these cases are not strong anomalies indre unresolved cases Introduction Issoudun and Le Blanc contain the two best known unresolved sightings in Indre ’s official record, but “unresolved” needs careful interpretation. The Issoudun witnesses described six orange lights, including an apparently rectangular object with illuminated openings, moving silently during the early hours of 23 January 1977. Near Le Blanc in July 1980, a witness reported an oval orange glow apparently hovering about 50 metres from a house for roughly 40 minutes; the same file also records a second, less detailed account from the surrounding area. Neither case was identified, yet neither is classed as a strong unexplained anomaly. GEIPAN, the investigation unit of France’s national space agency CNES, placed both in category C: reports that cannot be assessed properly because reliable information is missing. Their importance within Indre’s UFO history therefore lies less in what they prove than in what they reveal about the limits of old witness testimony. Vivid descriptions can survive for decades even when the measurements needed to test them do not. What witnesses reported at Issoudun and Le Blanc The two cases differ considerably. Issoudun was a multiple witness observation involving moving lights and an apparent structured form. Le Blanc centred on a largely stationary light reported at close range by one principal witness. What connects them is not a shared object or pattern of behaviour, but the absence of enough dependable data to distinguish an unusual event from a mistaken interpretation. Six orange lights over Issoudun At about 1.45 am on 23 January 1977, a witness in Issoudun noticed an orange light in the distance that seemed to be approaching. Other people were awakened and joined the observation. According to GEIPAN’s summary, the witnesses then saw several luminous balls packed closely together. One formation or object appeared different from the others: it was described as rectangular, narrower towards the rear and fitted with illuminated openings. The lights reportedly moved slowly and produced no audible sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANISSOUDUN (36) 23.01.1977 GEIPAN... The published witness records identify two women, aged 60 and 40, although the narrative indicates that additional people were present. Both surviving witness entries describe six orange or fire coloured phenomena, slow movement, an urban setting and complete silence. One record compares the apparent size with a football; the other includes both a football and a car as visual comparisons. Weather information is incomplete: one entry says conditions were unknown, while the other records cloud with some clear spells. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANISSOUDUN (36) 23.01.1977 T2 GEIPAN... GEIPAN considered a transport aircraft because of aviation activity around Châteauroux, but the hypothesis could not be confirmed from the available information. That is an important distinction. The official file does not say that an aircraft explanation was disproved. It says that investigators lacked enough material to establish whether an aircraft was responsible. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANISSOUDUN (36) 23.01.1977 GEIPAN... The multiple witnesses make Issoudun more interesting than an unsupported second hand story. They show that several people perceived something sufficiently unusual to discuss and remember. Multiple witnesses do not, however, automatically supply independent evidence. People observing together can share the same viewing position, visual limitations and assumptions; later witnesses may also be influenced by what the first person has already said. GEIPAN’s methodology therefore considers not only witness numbers but also the precision, independence and reliability of the information they provide. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN A long lasting orange glow near Le Blanc The case listed as “Le Blanc 1980” combines two reports from communities around Le Blanc rather than describing a single object seen by several independent observers. GEIPAN’s current page gives the principal event as occurring at about 2.45 am on 25 July 1980. A witness said that an orange, oval luminous form was visible approximately 50 metres from the home. Its estimated dimensions were about 0.8 metres high and 0.4 metres wide, and it reportedly remained stationary for around 40 minutes. The light was gone after the witness went into the kitchen. No ground trace was found. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLE BLANC (36) 1980LE BLANC (36) 1980; Date d'observation. /06/1980; Région. Centre; Département. Indre; Classification. C; D... The same official file mentions another person who reported a luminous ball roughly a month earlier. That light was said to have been about 50 metres above the ground and to have followed the witness’s vehicle. GEIPAN states that no further information was collected about either phenomenon. The archive’s date field remains only partly resolved, displaying June 1980 even though the principal narrative specifies 25 July. That discrepancy is itself a warning against treating every database field as an exact reconstruction of the original event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLE BLANC (36) 1980LE BLANC (36) 1980; Date d'observation. /06/1980; Région. Centre; Département. Indre; Classification. C; D... The reported 40 minute duration initially appears to strengthen the principal Le Blanc account: a prolonged observation should permit careful inspection. Yet duration is valuable only when accompanied by useful reference points. The surviving summary does not provide a measured bearing, elevation, movement against landmarks, photographs, instrument readings or a documented sequence of changes. Nor does it explain how the claimed 50 metre distance and sub metre dimensions were calculated. The absence of physical traces is not evidence that nothing unusual was present; a light in the sky need not affect the ground. It nevertheless removes one possible route to independent testing. Once no photograph, trace or contemporaneous measurement survives, investigators are left mainly with a narrative whose most striking details depend on uncertain visual estimates. Why size, distance and shape are unreliable at night Descriptions such as “football sized”, “car sized” or “0.8 metres high” sound precise, but they often combine two separate judgements: how large something looked and how far away the witness believed it was. Without a known distance, the eye chiefly registers angular size—the portion of the visual field occupied by an object. A small nearby light and a much larger distant source can appear equally large. NASA uses the Sun and Moon as a familiar example: they have vastly different physical diameters but appear almost the same size because their distances differ so greatly. [NASA Science] eclipse2017.nasa.gov exploring angular diameter We have total solar eclipses visible on the surface of Earth because even though the diameter of the sun is 1,392,600 km and the diameter... This problem is central to both Indre cases. The Issoudun comparisons do not establish that the lights had the physical diameter of footballs. They indicate an impression produced against the sky. Similarly, the Le Blanc dimensions are meaningful only if the claimed 50 metre distance was established independently. If the light was actually hundreds of metres or several kilometres away, its physical size would have been entirely different. A dark or sparsely detailed background also weakens the visual cues normally used to estimate range. Aviation guidance treats night observation as a recognised human factors problem because isolated lights, uncertain horizons and limited terrain detail can generate incorrect impressions of distance, position and movement. Bright lights may be visible over unexpectedly long ranges at night, while differences in apparent brightness can be mistaken for changes in proximity. [FAA] faa.gov Fitness for Flight Autokinesis Although exposure to total darkness for at least 30 minutes is required for complete dark adaptation, a pilot can achieve a moderate degr... Shape is vulnerable for similar reasons. A witness may perceive several points of light as parts of one larger structure, especially when the dark body connecting them cannot be seen."},{"title":"Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange","url":"/why-iseres-strangest-sightings-rarely/","description":"Isère has a long but fragmented UFO record rather than one universally recognised landmark encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange Isère has a long but fragmented UFO record rather than one universally recognised landmark encounter. Isère has a long but fragmented UFO record rather than one universally recognised landmark encounter. The department’s official files include reports from Alpine villages, the Grenoble conurbation, roads across the plateau and the Rhône valley. Yet the clearest pattern is not a concentration of extraordinary machines. isere Introduction The most important date is 5 November 1990, when witnesses around Grenoble joined a nationwide wave of reports. That episode initially looked like evidence of a vast object crossing France, but the main phenomenon was traced to the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. Isère’s more recent cases tell a similar story: careful investigation often reduces an apparently strange light or shape to a probable conventional cause, while older reports commonly remain unclassified because too little reliable material survives. [lemonde.fr] lemonde.fr Le Monde.fr L'OVNI du 5 novembre est identifiéNovember 10, 1990 — Publié le 10 novembre 1990 à 00h00, modifié le 10 novembre 1990 à 00h00... L'objet lumineux observé dans la... Published: November 10, 1990 What the official record actually contains France’s principal public body for examining UFO reports is GEIPAN, a specialist unit of the French space agency CNES. It collects witness accounts submitted directly or through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities, then compares them with astronomical, meteorological and aviation information. Its purpose is to identify reported phenomena where possible, not to prove or disprove extraterrestrial visitation. [Gendarmerie Nationale] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie Nationale Comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés Gendarmerie NationaleComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés...July 2, 2022 — 2 Jul 2022 — Une fois les témoignages é... Published: July 2, 2022 GEIPAN assesses both the amount of dependable information in a case and the degree of strangeness remaining after investigation. Its public files range from clearly explained reports to cases that cannot be evaluated properly because essential details are missing. The letter attached to a file therefore needs careful reading: an unresolved or poorly documented report is not automatically evidence of an extraordinary craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The searchable Isère archive includes reports from Valbonnais in 1976, Chirens in 1976, Bourgoin Jallieu in 1981, Villard de Lans in 1982, Saint Pierre de Chartreuse in 1983, Échirolles in 2024 and numerous other locations. Several older entries are classified as cases lacking reliable information, including Valbonnais, Villard de Lans and Saint Pierre de Chartreuse. That makes them part of the department’s UFO history, but not strong evidential cases: their enduring mystery comes largely from missing data rather than from well documented anomalous behaviour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr SAINT PIERRE DE CHARTREUSE (38) .07.1983 /07/1983 C Isère Manque d'informations fiables 13/06/2018 1983 07 01... The archive also contains straightforward identifications. A 1983 sighting near Le Passage and Romagnieu was attributed to an aircraft. A 2010 report on the road between Pierre Châtel and La Motte d’Aveillans was identified as a sky lantern. An observation at Les Avenières in 2014 was attributed to the International Space Station, while a Saint Égrève report from the same year was judged to be a very probable bright meteor. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases matter because they reveal the real composition of the local record. “UFO” usually describes the witness’s uncertainty at the moment of observation. It does not describe the final finding. The night of 5 November 1990 The most memorable episode connected with Isère occurred shortly after 7 pm on 5 November 1990. More than a thousand people across France reported a large luminous phenomenon travelling through the clear night sky. Witness descriptions varied considerably: some saw a formation of lights, some an immense structured object, and others separate glowing bodies. Reports reached the press, gendarmerie units and civilian UFO investigators. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le 5 novembre 1990 de très nombreux témoins se sont manifestés auprès de la gendarmerie, auprès de la presse, auprès de groupes ufologiques... At least one later UFO reporting compilation records an orange ball moving silently to the north east of Grenoble that evening. As a secondary catalogue rather than a surviving official case file, it provides evidence that the national event was observed locally, but it does not establish the precise form, distance or size of what the Grenoble witness saw. [Ovni France] ovni france.fr temoignages par ordre chronologique temoignages par ordre chronologique CNES identified the main event as the atmospheric re entry of material from a Soviet Proton rocket. NASA orbital data placed the re entry at the correct time and along a trajectory consistent with the phenomenon seen over France. Contemporary national reporting announced the identification within days. GEIPAN later revisited the event with a computer simulation and concluded that multiple fragments, glowing plasma and the geometry of a shallow re entry could account for the dispersed lights and the impression of a huge dark structure. [lemonde.fr] lemonde.fr Le Monde.fr L'OVNI du 5 novembre est identifiéNovember 10, 1990 — Publié le 10 novembre 1990 à 00h00, modifié le 10 novembre 1990 à 00h00... L'objet lumineux observé dans la... Published: November 10, 1990 The explanation is stronger than a loose resemblance. It links a known object, a calculated orbital decay, the reported time, the broad direction of movement and a mass of geographically distributed observations. The apparent silence is not surprising for an event occurring at extreme altitude, while the perceived closeness or enormous size cannot be trusted without familiar reference points. Separate fragments moving together can also be mentally grouped into the outline of one solid object. Some UFO writers have argued that individual reports from the night contain details not fully captured by the rocket explanation, such as apparent low altitude, changes of direction or unusually close encounters. Those claims should be considered individually rather than used to reject the identified re entry. Witness recollections collected after a highly publicised event can vary, and the exact paths and appearances of individual fragments are not perfectly reconstructable. The best supported conclusion is therefore that the nationwide wave, including the main sighting around Grenoble, was produced by the re entry, while any purportedly separate local event would require independent evidence of its own. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Le 5 novembre 1990 de très nombreux témoins se sont manifestés auprès de la gendarmerie, auprès de la presse, auprès de groupes ufologiques... For Isère’s UFO history, 5 November 1990 is important less as an unsolved encounter than as an unusually clear lesson in how a genuine and spectacular aerospace event can generate sincere but sharply different descriptions. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Recent cases show how identification works Échirolles, 29 July 2024 Shortly after sunrise, two people in Échirolles watched a white luminous object move slowly and silently from south to north for about five minutes. One witness recorded 44 seconds of video. The case initially allowed several possibilities because the object followed a steady path and its elongated appearance could resemble an aircraft fuselage. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECHIROLLES (38) 29.07.2024ECHIROLLES (38) 29.07.2024. Date d'observation. 29/07/2024. Région. Rhône Alpes. Département. Isère. Clas... GEIPAN checked the aircraft hypothesis against radar information and found no suitable aeroplane. Investigators then examined the recorded shape, apparent angular size, sunlight conditions and likely wind behaviour. They concluded that a cluster of white balloons, perhaps between roughly 80 centimetres and one metre in overall length and tens of metres from the witnesses, was the more plausible explanation. Variable winds and low level wind shear prevented a completely demonstrable reconstruction, so the case was classified as probably explained rather than certain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECHIROLLES (38) 29.07.2024ECHIROLLES (38) 29.07.2024. Date d'observation."},{"title":"1990 Wave","url":"/1990-wave/","description":"The spectacular lights seen around Grenoble on 5 November 1990 were closely matched to a Soviet rocket stage re-entering the atmosphere.","breadcrumb":"Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange","level":2,"text":"1990 Wave Did a Giant UFO Cross Grenoble in 1990? Isere UFOs 1990 Wave 1990 Wave Did a Giant UFO Cross Grenoble in 1990? The spectacular lights seen around Grenoble on 5 November 1990 were closely matched to a Soviet rocket stage re entering the atmosphere. The spectacular lights seen around Grenoble on 5 November 1990 were closely matched to a Soviet rocket stage re entering the atmosphere. event time window What witnesses across Isere reported How the rocket re entry identification was made Why some descriptions still appeared extraordinary isere 1990 wave Introduction The spectacular lights seen around Grenoble on 5 November 1990 were almost certainly not a giant craft crossing Isère. They were part of a nationwide French sighting wave caused chiefly by the atmospheric re entry and fragmentation of the third stage of a Soviet Proton rocket. Shortly after 7 pm, more than a thousand witnesses across France watched a bright, extended formation of lights travel through a clear evening sky. Many sincerely described triangles, illuminated structures, low flying objects or several craft moving together. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Grenoble’s reports matter because they show how an accurately observed aerospace event can still produce radically misleading impressions of size, distance and shape. The rocket identification is supported by orbital information, the timing and direction of the sightings, the broad geographical pattern and later computer modelling by France’s space agency. Some individual descriptions remain difficult to reconstruct, but they do not provide strong evidence that a separate unknown object crossed Grenoble alongside the debris. What witnesses across Isère reported At about 7 pm on Monday 5 November, people in and around Grenoble looked up to see a collection of brilliant lights moving across the sky. The display was unusually large and prolonged compared with a familiar shooting star. Instead of one point flashing past in a second or two, witnesses saw numerous luminous fragments, sometimes with glowing trails, retaining a broadly coherent arrangement as they travelled. From the ground, such a formation could resemble a single enormous object with lights fixed to its underside. Other observers interpreted the separated fragments as several objects flying in formation. Reports elsewhere in France included triangles, cigar like shapes, platforms, rows of lights and vast dark bodies outlined by luminous points. The national archive is important when assessing Grenoble: descriptions differed greatly, but their times and broad movement formed one connected event rather than a series of independent local encounters. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The Alpine setting may have reinforced the sense of proximity. Around Grenoble, ridges and steep valley sides provide familiar visual reference points for aircraft and ordinary lights. Yet a brilliant object tens of kilometres above the ground does not behave like a nearby aircraft against that landscape. Without a known physical size, the eye cannot reliably calculate its distance. A distant, intensely bright debris cloud may therefore appear to be just above the mountains, moving over a road or passing behind a ridge when its true path is much higher and farther away. Witnesses were not foolish or dishonest. They were trying to interpret an unfamiliar spectacle using familiar categories. A line of lights suggests windows; three bright points suggest a triangle; a dim region between fragments can be perceived as the body of a dark craft. These impressions can feel especially convincing when several people watch together and agree that the lights form a structured object. The event also lasted long enough for observers to study it rather than glimpse it. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, says the slowing and spreading debris took several minutes to cross France. That duration made the phenomenon seem deliberate and controlled, unlike the popular image of debris simply dropping vertically from the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... How the rocket re entry identification was made The central piece of evidence was not a resemblance suggested years later. A known artificial object was returning through the atmosphere at the relevant time. CNES and later GEIPAN identified it as the third stage of a Soviet Proton launcher, catalogued as object 20925, or 1990 094C. The rocket had launched a satellite two days earlier. Orbital information received after the sightings placed its re entry on 5 November, matching the French observations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... On 10 November, Le Monde reported that the object seen on 5 November had been identified as fragments of a Soviet rocket disintegrating during atmospheric re entry. The conclusion was supported by information from aerospace and meteorological authorities rather than by witness testimony alone. [Le Monde.fr] lemonde.fr l ovni du 5 novembre est identifie 3978101 1819218 Le Monde.frL'OVNI du 5 novembre est... identifié10 Nov 1990 — Il s'agit de la désintégration, lors de la rentrée dans l'atmosphère, de mo... GEIPAN’s later reconstruction gives the event a more precise physical shape. According to its analysis, the debris descended from roughly 90 kilometres to about 50 kilometres in altitude while slowing from approximately 7 kilometres per second to 2 kilometres per second. Fragmentation may have begun at about 94 kilometres over the Bay of Biscay. The resulting cloud contained pieces of different sizes, shapes and densities, spreading over tens of kilometres along the flight path. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... That model explains several features reported from Isère and elsewhere: Many lights rather than one: tanks, structural sections and smaller pieces separated as the stage broke apart. A formation that seemed solid: fragments shared the same general trajectory and retained relative positions for part of the flight. Slow apparent movement: a distant object crossing the observer’s line of sight can appear much slower than its true speed. Changes in brightness: individual fragments heated, rotated, faded or broke into smaller pieces. Different shapes from different places: observers viewed the elongated debris cloud from different angles. A low altitude impression: exceptional brightness made the phenomenon appear closer than it was. In 2015, GEIPAN published a new simulation created with CNES’s DEBRISK software. Because the exact construction and fragmentation sequence could not be reproduced, the model simplified the rocket stage into bodies of different densities. GEIPAN acknowledged uncertainty about how far the cloud stretched along its direction of travel. It nevertheless found much less uncertainty about the main path across France. This is an important distinction: the reconstruction is not a frame by frame recording, but the overall trajectory and nature of the event are well supported. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The national spread of the sightings is itself powerful evidence. A truly local object above Grenoble would not naturally produce a near simultaneous chain of reports across much of France following a compatible route. A high altitude re entry, however, can be visible across a very wide area. Isère was one viewing region within that larger corridor. 50:27 The Terrifying Voronezh UFO Incident: Strange Otherworldly Beings Invaded The USSR Lights Out 123 Channel: Lights Out · Views: 138.2K · Uploaded: October 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube Why the display looked like a giant craft The most memorable reports were not simply of burning debris. Witnesses sometimes described an immense structured object, apparently moving at aircraft height. This gap between physical cause and reported appearance is the main reason the case remains discussed. Human vision cannot measure the distance of an isolated light accurately when there is no recognised object of known size beside it. Beyond relatively short distances, people infer range from context. A passenger aircraft seems distant because its size, sound and expected speed are familiar. An unidentified group of lights has no such scale. The same angular pattern might therefore be interpreted as a small formation nearby"},{"title":"Case Files","url":"/case-files/","description":"Isere's official files show how balloons, drones, meteors, aircraft and satellites can produce sincere but misleading sightings.","breadcrumb":"Why Isere's Strangest Sightings Rarely Stay Strange","level":2,"text":"Case Files What Isere's UFO Case Files Actually Reveal Isere UFOs Case Files Case Files What Isere's UFO Case Files Actually Reveal Isere's official files show how balloons, drones, meteors, aircraft and satellites can produce sincere but misleading sightings. Isere's official files show how balloons, drones, meteors, aircraft and satellites can produce sincere but misleading sightings. dataset evidence How GEIPAN assesses evidence and strangeness Modern cases with probable conventional causes Why unresolved does not mean extraordinary isere case files Introduction Isère’s official UFO files reveal a process of identification rather than a catalogue of unexplained craft. A witness reports something genuinely puzzling: silent orange lights, a bright point moving across the sky, a dark shape passing overhead or an object apparently behaving unlike an aircraft. Investigators then test the account against flight data, satellite predictions, weather, astronomy, video measurements, local events and the limits of human perception. The result is often less dramatic but more informative. Some Isère reports have been identified as the International Space Station, sky lanterns or meteors. Others have been judged probably caused by drones or balloons because the available evidence fits those explanations better than any alternative. Older files frequently remain open not because they contain strong evidence of something extraordinary, but because too little dependable information survives to test the possibilities. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN How GEIPAN turns a sighting into a case GEIPAN, the specialist unit of the French space agency CNES, does not begin by asking whether a witness has seen an alien vehicle. It begins with a narrower question: what known phenomenon best matches the reported time, direction, movement, appearance and circumstances? Its classification system separates the strength of the evidence from the strangeness of the original experience. “Consistency” measures the quantity, reliability and objectivity of the available information. “Residual strangeness” measures how unusual the event still appears after investigators have compared it with conventional possibilities. A case can therefore sound extraordinary at first but become ordinary once better evidence is introduced. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN The four main outcomes are: A: the phenomenon has been identified after investigation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. B: a conventional explanation is considered probable, although not proved beyond all doubt. C: the phenomenon cannot be identified because essential information is missing. D: the phenomenon remains unidentified despite investigation. This distinction is crucial when reading Isère’s archive. Category C does not mean that investigators found a well documented anomaly. It means they lacked enough material to reach a reliable conclusion. Category D is the genuinely unresolved category, although even that label records the state of the evidence rather than confirming an extraordinary origin. GEIPAN can revisit C and D files when new information becomes available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN GEIPANComment le GEIPAN classe t il les cas d’observations? GEIPAN The national figures reinforce that point. As of 31 March 2026, GEIPAN reported that 66.5 per cent of its published cases were classified A or B, 30.3 per cent were category C because of insufficient information, and 3.2 per cent remained category D. The broad pattern in Isère fits this larger picture: most useful conclusions come either from identifying a conventional cause or recognising that the surviving evidence is too weak to support one. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN The strongest explanations come from matching several clues An explanation becomes persuasive when it accounts for several features at once. A mere resemblance is not enough. Investigators look for agreement between the proposed cause and the observation’s timing, path, speed, colour, sound, apparent size and environmental setting. That process is visible in Isère’s better documented modern files. They show different levels of certainty, from cases with a directly confirmed source to cases where one hypothesis simply fits the evidence much better than its rivals. Sky lanterns near Pierre Châtel On 6 March 2010, motorists travelling between Pierre Châtel and La Motte d’Aveillans saw about fifteen red orange lights moving silently through the night sky. The observation lasted more than five minutes, and one witness recorded it on a mobile telephone. A group of evenly spaced, glowing objects moving without engine noise can easily appear organised or technological, particularly when distance and altitude are impossible to judge. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D115] de PIERRE CHATEL (38) vers MOTTE D'AVEILLANS (LA) (38) 06.03.2010 GEIPAN... The case became unusually strong because the investigation did not rely only on visual similarity. After local press coverage, a person came forward to say that thirteen sky lanterns had been released during a private celebration. A gendarmerie enquiry independently linked the lights to that event, about 2.5 kilometres from the witnesses. GEIPAN therefore classified the case A. The decisive evidence was not simply that the lights looked like lanterns; it was the match between their appearance, number, behaviour, location and an identified launch. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D115] de PIERRE CHATEL (38) vers MOTTE D'AVEILLANS (LA) (38) 06.03.2010 GEIPAN... This case also shows how publicity can help rather than distort an investigation. Newspaper coverage sometimes encourages exaggeration, but here it reached the people who knew the source of the lights. The International Space Station over Les Avenières On 3 August 2014, a witness at Les Avenières watched a bright white point move silently from the south west towards the north east at roughly the apparent speed of an aircraft. The object produced no flashing navigation lights and no engine sound, making it seem unfamiliar. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAVENIERES (LES) (38) 03.08.2014 GEIPAN... The timing and path closely resembled an International Space Station pass. Orbital information confirmed that the ISS was visible from the area between approximately 22:36 and 22:45, travelling in the same direction described by the witness. GEIPAN classified the report A because the predicted passage matched the observation and there was no separate sighting that would require a second object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAVENIERES (LES) (38) 03.08.2014 GEIPAN... The lesson is broader than this single file. Satellites are most conspicuous when the observer is in darkness but the spacecraft remains illuminated by the Sun. They can appear extremely bright, travel smoothly and vanish when they enter Earth’s shadow. Without checking orbital data, those features may look stranger than they are. GEIPAN : Tout savoir sur les OVNIS et Phénomènes Aérospatiaux (PAN) Channel: Science & Vie Open on YouTube A meteor over Saint Égrève GEIPAN’s Isère records also include a Saint Égrève observation from 13 March 2014 classified A as a bright meteor. Such events often generate reports of fast lights, sudden flashes, changing colour or apparent fragmentation. Because a meteor can cross a large portion of the sky in seconds, witnesses may describe extreme speed or an object seeming much lower and closer than it really was. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Meteor explanations become stronger when reports from different locations describe the same short lived path at the same time. They are also supported by characteristic duration: an event lasting only a few seconds is more consistent with atmospheric entry than with a hovering aircraft or satellite. A vivid experience can therefore be entirely sincere while its estimated distance, size and trajectory are mistaken. Probable causes are not the same as proven identities Category B files are especially useful because they show where official investigation stops short of certainty. Investigators may have a strong conventional hypothesis but no direct confirmation of the exact object involved. The conclusion is therefore “probably explained”, not “definitively solved”. A likely drone at Anthon At Anthon on 22 September 2021, two residents heard a high pitched whistling noise and then saw a dark mass cross their field of view in about one second. No photograph was taken, and only one formal witness account was available. These limitations reduced"},{"title":"Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","url":"/juras-ufo-stories-between-legend-and/","description":"Jura has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a large body of well-documented, unexplained encounters.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence Jura has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a large body of well documented, unexplained encounters. Jura has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a large body of well documented, unexplained encounters. jura Introduction The official record therefore tells a more restrained story than local legend sometimes suggests. Jura has produced dramatic witness accounts, multi witness light observations and reports passed to the gendarmerie, yet the published files of GEIPAN—the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena—do not reveal a strong department wide pattern of radar contacts, military interceptions or technically supported unknown craft. Instead, they show how extraordinary impressions can arise from the Moon, aircraft, meteors, balloons and lanterns, while a smaller number of cases remain unclassified for lack of usable evidence rather than because investigators proved them extraordinary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr vestigation. Classification C... Prémanon in 1954: Jura’s landmark case The best known Jura incident occurred near Prémanon in the Haut Jura on 27 September 1954, during the celebrated French “flying saucer wave”. According to the account that eventually entered UFO literature, three children encountered a metallic looking, roughly rectangular object near their home during stormy weather. The object was described as about two metres high, standing on bent supports. One child reportedly threw a stone and then a toy projectile at it before being knocked down as it moved forward. [Geipan] geipan.fr Jura... Son rapport a été présenté au Congrès de Lyon en 1992 (\"Actes des sixièmes Rencontres européennes de Lyon, 1 2 3 mai 1992\", SOS... Those striking details made Prémanon unusually memorable. Unlike the distant lights that dominate many reports, this was presented as a close encounter with a structured object that seemed to interact physically with a witness. Regional and national press coverage helped transform the event into the “Prémanon saucer”, with some reports adding descriptions of supposed occupants. A later compilation identifies coverage in Le Progrès on 3 October 1954 and in the Paris press during the same period. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org En raison de certaines informations de presse plus ou moins exactes, le Commandant de Section a cru devoir procéder à une nouvelle enquêt... Yet the surviving evidence is much weaker than the vivid narrative implies. GEIPAN classifies Prémanon as category C, meaning that the phenomenon could not be identified because the available information is insufficient or inadequately confirmed. That is not the same as category D, which is reserved for a phenomenon remaining unidentified after a sufficiently developed investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Jura... Son rapport a été présenté au Congrès de Lyon en 1992 (\"Actes des sixièmes Rencontres européennes de Lyon, 1 2 3 mai 1992\", SOS... A case shaped by retelling Prémanon is particularly valuable as a study of how a UFO story develops over time. Researcher Thierry Pinvidic revisited the episode decades later, interviewing people and examining the relationship between witness memory, press reporting and local folklore. His wider academic treatment explored how apparently factual testimony can gradually become part of a modern legend without requiring deliberate deception by the original witnesses. [Persee] persee.fr comm 0588 8018 1990 num comm 0588 8018 1990 num Later summaries have repeated the story with varying times, spellings and details. Some databases even duplicated the incident or presented conflicting versions. This does not prove that nothing unusual happened, but it makes precise reconstruction difficult. The later investigation is itself retrospective, while the public GEIPAN entry notes that important assertions still require verification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Jura... Son rapport a été présenté au Congrès de Lyon en 1992 (\"Actes des sixièmes Rencontres européennes de Lyon, 1 2 3 mai 1992\", SOS... The most defensible conclusion is therefore limited. Several children probably experienced something that frightened and puzzled them, and the incident received prompt local attention. What cannot now be established securely is the exact appearance of the object, whether it moved as described, whether physical contact occurred, or how much of the familiar story came from later interpretation. Rather than Jura’s strongest unexplained case, Prémanon may be its clearest example of the distance that can grow between an original event and its cultural afterlife. The 1970s brought Jura’s clearest cluster The official catalogue shows a noticeable concentration of Jura reports during the second half of the 1970s. These include Saint Amour in December 1976; several observations around Saint Claude, Lavans lès Saint Claude and neighbouring communities in 1977; Chaussin in August 1977; a journey from Chemilla towards Saint Julien sur Suran in December 1977; and Tavaux in June 1978. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This resembles a local “flap”, meaning a period when reports become unusually frequent. However, the files do not demonstrate that all these witnesses observed one recurring phenomenon. Different incidents received different classifications, and several lack enough detail for confident comparison. The Saint Claude area is the most interesting part of the cluster because some events involved multiple witnesses or observations from more than one locality. GEIPAN’s material for 13 June 1977 includes testimony associated with Saint Claude and Lavans lès Saint Claude. The case is classified B, indicating that investigators considered a conventional explanation probable, although the public catalogue does not name the identified phenomenon in its summary. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A separate December 1977 file describes several people seeing a luminous phenomenon moving across the sky. Witnesses reported an elongated white light that appeared to become a fading red point, without any noticeable sound. The file contains a gendarmerie report but remains category C because the surviving information is not sufficiently reliable to determine the cause. The observation date is recorded as 4 December even though the case title refers to 7 December, a discrepancy that itself warns against treating catalogue titles as exact narratives. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Only days later, on 19 December, another observer travelling between Chemilla and Saint Julien sur Suran reported something unusual. GEIPAN ultimately classified that sighting A and identified the Moon as the source. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Taken together, the 1977 reports do not provide compelling evidence for a single object repeatedly operating over southern Jura. They are better understood as several independent observations occurring during a period of heightened public attention, with outcomes ranging from probable identification to inadequate evidence and a confirmed astronomical misidentification. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What later Jura cases were actually seeing From the late 1970s onwards, the Jura catalogue becomes a useful cross section of ordinary phenomena that can look highly unusual under poor viewing conditions. The official classifications include aircraft, atmospheric entries, balloons, the Moon and a sky lantern. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casSAINT CLAUDE (39) 07.12.1977, 04/12/1977, C, Jura, Manque d'informations fiables, 05/11/2010, 1977 12 04, voir. [D1... Among the clearer examples are: Morez, 11 August 1988: classified B, with an aircraft considered the probable explanation. Orgelet, 1 November 2007: classified B as an atmospheric re entry or similar luminous atmospheric event. Dole, 19 September 2010: classified B as a balloon. Châtelay, 7 March 2014: classified A as the Moon. Augea, 6 December 2018: classified A as a bright meteor. Dole, 5 August 2020: classified B as a sky lantern. Dole, 29 July 2021: classified A as a bright meteor. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casSAINT CLAUDE (39) 07.12.1977, 04/12/1977, C, Jura, Manque d'informations fiables, 05/11/2010, 1977 12 04, voir. [D1... These explanations are not trivial dismissals. A bright meteor can illuminate clouds, fragment, change colour and appear far lower than it is. An aircraft seen head on may seem stationary before suddenly moving sideways as its flight path becomes apparent. The Moon near the horizon can appear enlarged, distorted or"},{"title":"1970 s Flap","url":"/1970-s-flap/","description":"A burst of reports around southern Jura mixed multi-witness lights, incomplete files and sightings later linked to ordinary phenomena.","breadcrumb":"Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","level":2,"text":"1970 s Flap Was Jura's 1970 s UFO Flap One Phenomenon? Jura UFOs 1970 s Flap 1970 s Flap Was Jura's 1970 s UFO Flap One Phenomenon? A burst of reports around southern Jura mixed multi witness lights, incomplete files and sightings later linked to ordinary phenomena. A burst of reports around southern Jura mixed multi witness lights, incomplete files and sightings later linked to ordinary phenomena. event time window Where and when the main reports occurred What witnesses and gendarmerie files recorded Why the cases point to several different causes jura 1970 s flap Introduction Jura’s late 1970s “UFO flap” was not a single prolonged encounter or a wave of matching craft. It was a compact run of reports—most noticeably from December 1976 to 1979—in which witnesses described bright balls, elongated lights, silent movement and apparent changes of direction. Saint Claude produced two of the most memorable reports, but the official record also covers Saint Amour, Chaussin, Tavaux, Sellières and roads through the southern Jura. Taken together, the files point towards several different causes rather than one mysterious phenomenon. One dramatic Saint Claude event was judged probably to have been an atmospheric re entry or bright meteor. Two long lasting roadside sightings were later identified as the Moon. Most of the remaining reports were left in category C, meaning that they could not be identified because the surviving information was inadequate—not that investigators had established the presence of an extraordinary object. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr saint claude 1977 0600412 Observation OVNI à Saint Claude — juin 1977 CarteOvni.frLe 13 juin 1977 vers 0h 05 des automobilistes constatent que la route s'éclaire... Where and when the main reports occurred The concentration began on 26 December 1976 near Saint Amour, in the north of the department. Two witnesses reported red and pale yellow lights approaching from the west and tracing curved or looping paths above Mont Février, close to a radio relay station. They also described a red ball apparently rising from the area of a high voltage line and meeting the other lights. No supporting witness was found, however, and an employee at the nearby relay station had noticed nothing. GEIPAN therefore classified the case C for lack of information. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The strongest cluster came in 1977: 13 June, Saint Claude: motorists saw the road become almost daylight bright for four or five seconds as a luminous ball with a trail crossed from west to east. Other people in the area reported a flash or an explosive sound. 15 August, Chaussin: two witnesses described a slowly moving luminous sphere that appeared to stop briefly before accelerating towards the north east. 7 December, Saint Claude: several people watched an elongated white light cross the sky, change into a fading red point and disappear. The file also includes a separate orange ball report from 4 December. 19 December, between Chemilla and Saint Julien sur Suran: a motorist followed a pale, apparently flat topped luminous object for about half an hour without getting closer to it. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr saint claude 1977 0600412 Observation OVNI à Saint Claude — juin 1977 CarteOvni.frLe 13 juin 1977 vers 0h 05 des automobilistes constatent que la route s'éclaire... Reports continued in 1978 and 1979 but were more widely distributed. At Tavaux on 7 June 1978, a motorist and passenger first noticed a distant point of light, then described an orange disc rising vertically and disappearing into cloud. A second person came forward after press coverage and reported a yellow orange circular light later that evening. In May 1979, a driver near Sellières saw a red orange ball with what she called corkscrew like “antennae”. A September 1979 report from Foucherans, submitted only in 2007, described a bright metallic looking sphere that seemed to stop overhead and then reverse direction. GEIPAN’s published catalogue lists Tavaux as category C, while its later analysis identified the Sellières object as the setting Moon. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Calling this sequence a “flap” is reasonable in the limited sense that several reports appeared within a relatively short period. It should not imply that all Jura residents were observing one repeated visitor. The dates, locations, durations and apparent behaviour vary too greatly, and the official assessments range from identified or probably identified phenomena to cases that are simply too incomplete to settle. What witnesses and gendarmerie files recorded The Jura cases are useful because they preserve more than a list of lights. Several reports reached the gendarmerie, allowing investigators to compare witness statements, look for physical traces and seek independent observations. France’s official UAP system has long relied on the gendarmerie to take statements and pass relevant files to the CNES investigation service. Gendarmes are not dispatched to hunt supposed spacecraft; their role is to record observable facts, establish timings and directions, identify witnesses and note possible conventional causes. [Gendarmerie Nationale] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie Nationale Comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés Gendarmerie Nationale Comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés The 13 June 1977 Saint Claude case had the best combination of brevity, multiple observers and physically plausible effects. The luminous object lasted only seconds, carried a visible trail and was accompanied by reports of a flash or detonation. The gendarmerie searched for evidence of an impact but found no fall site. GEIPAN nevertheless considered an atmospheric re entry probable. A bright meteoric fireball can light the landscape, fragment into a trail and generate delayed explosive sounds without leaving recoverable material near the witnesses. The lack of debris therefore does not count strongly against that explanation. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr saint claude 1977 0600412 Observation OVNI à Saint Claude — juin 1977 CarteOvni.frLe 13 juin 1977 vers 0h 05 des automobilistes constatent que la route s'éclaire... The December Saint Claude report was less conclusive. Several observers described a white elongated form travelling west to east before becoming a red point. That sequence resembles a distant object burning or breaking up during atmospheric entry, and GEIPAN explicitly noted the similarity. Yet the observations were not precise enough to confirm a trajectory, duration or astronomical match, so the case remained C rather than B. No ground trace was found, but here too that result does not establish that the object was either solid or nearby. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Other reports had weaker supporting evidence. The Saint Amour case involved two witnesses but no confirmation from the nearby communications facility. Chaussin entered the record after local press coverage, raising the possibility that witnesses were prompted to report something they had not initially regarded as extraordinary. Tavaux gained a second witness in the same way, but the reported times were not identical and the descriptions were too general to demonstrate that both people saw the same object. Those weaknesses do not show dishonesty. They simply limit how confidently investigators can reconstruct distance, size and motion from lights seen against an otherwise featureless sky. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Foucherans account is weaker still because it was reported approximately 28 years after the alleged event. A late statement can preserve a sincere memory, but investigators cannot reliably recover the weather, aircraft movements, viewing geometry or exact date decades afterwards. GEIPAN’s own summary therefore treats an aircraft turning towards and then away from the witness, or a balloon moving between different wind layers, as possibilities that can no longer be properly tested. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why the cases point to several different causes The most important finding is not that every Jura sighting has been solved. It is that the reports separate into distinct explanatory groups. Fast, brilliant events fit atmospheric entries. The June 1977 Saint Claude object crossed the scene in seconds, illuminated the road, left a trail and was associated with explosive sounds. Those features are characteristic of a bright fireball or re entering material. The December report from the same town may belong to the same broad family, although its documentation is not sufficient for a firm identification. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr saint"},{"title":"Prémanon","url":"/prémanon/","description":"Jura's famous 1954 encounter became more vivid through press coverage, later retellings and uncertain witness reconstruction.","breadcrumb":"Jura's UFO Stories Between Legend and Evidence","level":2,"text":"Prémanon How Did the Prémanon UFO Story Change? Jura Prémanon Prémanon How Did the Prémanon UFO Story Change? Jura's famous 1954 encounter became more vivid through press coverage, later retellings and uncertain witness reconstruction. Jura's famous 1954 encounter became more vivid through press coverage, later retellings and uncertain witness reconstruction. event time window What the children were reported to have seen What the surviving records can actually confirm How press coverage and folklore reshaped the case jura Introduction The Prémanon encounter of 27 September 1954 is Jura’s best known UFO story, but the version usually repeated today is not a stable eyewitness account. It is a layered narrative assembled from children’s statements, two gendarmerie reports, rapidly changing newspaper stories and retrospective interviews conducted decades later. The earliest record describes a strange metallic looking form, a red light and marks in a field. Within days, the press had turned these uncertain elements into a landed spacecraft, a rectangular “Martian”, physical paralysis and even an interplanetary skirmish. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... Later investigation has weakened the case substantially. The principal child witness eventually said that the affair began with an invented school composition inspired by a frightening local shepherd, after which suggestive adult questioning helped convert a ghost story into a UFO encounter. More recent research has also offered ordinary explanations for the red light and supposed landing traces. Yet France’s official UFO unit, GEIPAN, still classifies Prémanon as category C—insufficient reliable information—rather than formally declaring the case solved. [agence martienne.fr] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... What were the children reported to have seen? The incident took place at about 8.30 pm near an isolated farm in the combe of Mont Fier, roughly three kilometres from Prémanon and close to the Swiss border. It was dark, with heavy rain and stormy conditions. The official GEIPAN summary, derived from contemporary records, names three children aged 12, nine and eight, although newspaper coverage sometimes counted four participants or concentrated on only two. This variation in the number of witnesses is an early warning that there was never one uncontested version of events. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... In the familiar account, 12 year old Raymond went outside after hearing the dog. He reportedly encountered an aluminium coloured rectangular form about two metres high and one metre wide. It appeared partly split down the middle and seemed to have bent supports near its base. Raymond was said to have thrown a stone and then fired a toy dart at it, hearing a metallic sound on impact. As the form moved towards him, he fell or was knocked down before retreating indoors. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... His sister Janine was reported to have seen a similar rectangular form near the barn or doorway. Two girls also described a red light moving or swaying close to the ground at some distance from the house. No witness claimed to have watched the supposed object depart, which matters because later accounts freely connected the red light in the field with the rectangular figure near the farm, treating one as a spacecraft and the other as its occupant. That connection was an interpretation, not a directly witnessed sequence. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... The children’s original language appears to have been less extraterrestrial than later headlines implied. One early report said that they told their school that they had seen “ghosts”. The terms “flying saucer”, “Martian”, “robot” and “interplanetary vehicle” were supplied or encouraged by adults operating amid France’s intense 1954 saucer wave. The distinction is crucial: the children may have described something frightening or invented a frightening story, but the fully developed UFO interpretation was socially constructed around them. [agence martienne.fr] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... What can the surviving records confirm? Two gendarmerie reports were prepared within days of the alleged event. Their existence gives Prémanon more documentary substance than many newspaper only cases from the 1954 wave. The first report, dated 29 September, recorded the children’s account cautiously. A second report, dated 1 October, followed after press coverage had already begun and presented the testimony and ground marks more confidently. [O.P Éditions] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... The gendarmes did not simply dismiss the children. The second report argued that they remained consistent and lived in an isolated setting where they supposedly had little exposure to newspapers or cinema. It also described a broad ring of flattened grass, roughly 3.5 metres across externally and 2.5 metres internally, together with four holes arranged within it. Some local observers thought the marks were not ordinary human or animal trampling. These details later became central to claims that a craft had landed in the field. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... The apparent precision is misleading, however. Heavy rain had fallen, curious visitors had already crossed the site, and the first press account described the marks as vague. The holes were also said to be merging with cattle tracks. The records establish that disturbed vegetation and depressions were noticed, but they do not establish when the marks were made, what caused them or whether they had any connection to the children’s story. [O.P Éditions] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... Nor do the records independently prove the most dramatic details. There is no photograph of the alleged figure itself, no recovered object, no medical evidence of injury or paralysis, no instrument reading and no confirmed observation by an adult. The metallic noise, the forward movement and the physical contact all depend on children’s statements transmitted through adult interviewers. Even the parents were doubtful: contemporary accounts say they refused to accept the story, while Raymond’s mother was later quoted as insisting that he had not gone outside that evening. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s present classification reflects these weaknesses. Category C means that there is not enough dependable information to identify or assess the reported phenomenon. It does not mean that investigators found a robust unexplained object. GEIPAN acknowledges the later claim that the affair was an invented story that escaped the children’s control, but says that this interpretation still awaits sufficient official verification. The result is an archival limbo: too compromised to count as strong unexplained evidence, but not formally closed as an identified event. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr PREMANON (39) 27.09.1954 GEIPAN... How did newspapers turn uncertainty into a Martian encounter? Prémanon became famous because the story altered rapidly during its first fortnight in print. Researchers who compared the two gendarmerie reports with roughly 30 early articles found that the accounts differed so substantially that even a basic summary has to combine incompatible sources. Some reports featured two children, others three or four. The distance of the red light changed. Details absent from one account appeared as established facts in another. [O.P Éditions] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... The earliest widely copied article appeared on 30 September. Several regional and national newspapers then reused its wording, sometimes with minimal alteration. Other correspondents produced competing versions, and information passing informally from local gendarmes entered press reports without a clear chain of attribution. Because articles were rarely signed or sourced, repetition created the appearance of corroboration even when several papers were copying the same underlying story. [O.P Éditions] editions.agence martienne.fr O.P Éditions... Le Progrès supplied the image that came to define the case: the “passengers” of the Prémanon saucer supposedly resembled giant sugar cubes. Its report separated the rectangular form from the red light and then joined them into a speculative spacecraft narrative—the rectangular being as a creature, the glowing object as its vehicle. The language was playful and openly conjectural, but later UFO books often retained the colourful details while losing the journalistic uncertainty surrounding them. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, rencontre rapprochée du 3e type à Prémanon, France, 1954... The story grew still more elaborate in illustrated and popular weekly coverage. One version said that the form embraced or “kissed” Raymond with something cold, leaving him"},{"title":"What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal","url":"/what-landes-ufo-records-actually-reveal/","description":"Landes has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a celebrated landing, recovered material or a tightly documented mass...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal Landes has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a celebrated landing, recovered material or a tightly documented mass... Landes has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by a celebrated landing, recovered material or a tightly documented mass sighting. landes Introduction The most notable official case occurred at Cazères sur l’Adour in 2002, where a witness reported a fire coloured ball with flashing lights making repeated movements; the French space agency’s investigation service left it unexplained. Yet “unexplained” does not mean extraterrestrial. Much of the Landes record demonstrates the opposite lesson: unusual appearances are frequently produced by familiar objects, while some dramatic reports remain open simply because the testimony is too late, vague or incomplete to test properly. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... What the official record contains France’s principal public archive is maintained by GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena study and information group within CNES, the national space agency. It collects reports from witnesses and authorities, examines possible astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human perception explanations, and publishes anonymised conclusions. GEIPAN explicitly says that it does not investigate alien visitation; it studies reported phenomena using established knowledge. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Its classifications are essential to reading the Landes cases correctly: A means the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B means an explanation is considered probable. C means the available information is insufficient for a dependable conclusion. D is reserved for a phenomenon that remains unexplained after investigation despite reasonably useful evidence. This distinction prevents two common mistakes. A category C report is not necessarily more mysterious than an explained case; it may simply be less usable. A category D report is stronger in the limited sense that investigators found the evidence sufficiently substantial to examine but could not identify the phenomenon. Even nationally, only a small minority of GEIPAN’s published cases remain unexplained after investigation, while roughly a third cannot be resolved because the data are inadequate. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN The Landes catalogue stretches from retrospective reports concerning the 1970s to cases assessed in the 2020s. Published entries include observations at Benquet, Tilh, Pissos, Vielle Soubiran, Bourriot Bergonce, Cazères sur l’Adour, Misson, Escource, Garein, Soustons, Dax, Biscarrosse and coastal holiday towns. The spread is geographically broad rather than concentrated around a single recognised hotspot. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN Cazères sur l’Adour: the strongest unresolved case At about 10 pm on 1 November 2002, a witness at Cazères sur l’Adour reported a fire coloured luminous ball carrying several flashing lights. According to GEIPAN’s summary, it made several back and forth movements and disappeared after roughly 30 seconds. The witness also believed that pilots had observed the phenomenon. GEIPAN classified the case D, describing it as strange to very strange and of medium to strong evidential consistency. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... This is the clearest official candidate for a landmark Landes case because it is the department’s conspicuous unresolved entry in the accessible catalogue. Its reported movement appears harder to reconcile with a simple stationary planet or drifting lantern, and the reference to possible pilot witnesses raises the possibility of independent corroboration. The limitations are equally important. The published webpage provides only a short summary. It does not display a radar plot, photograph, instrument reading or detailed independent pilot statement. Nor does it establish that the pilots saw precisely the same phenomenon from a known position at the same time. The case therefore deserves the description unexplained , but not claims of confirmed extraordinary technology. Later publication has not visibly strengthened it. The online record still rests on the original reported appearance and movement rather than on newly disclosed physical evidence. Its significance is consequently narrow but real: it is the best official example in Landes of an observation that survived the identification process, while remaining too sparsely documented in public to support a more ambitious conclusion. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... When “unidentified” really means “insufficient evidence” Several Landes reports sound dramatic at first reading but were classified C because investigators could not obtain enough dependable information. These cases are central to understanding why a UFO archive cannot be read as a catalogue of unexplained craft. L'histoire du premier cas d'Ovni dans le Pas de Calais à Quarouble en 1954 Channel: BFM Grand Lille Open on YouTube Bourriot Bergonce, 1994 A witness reported in 2010 an experience dating from childhood on 10 November 1994. While travelling in the back of a car on a country road, the witness recalled an intense green light and a nearby point of light seen through treetops. The point allegedly accelerated, changed direction sharply and vanished northwards without sound. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr BOURRIOT BERGONCE (40) 10.11.1994 GEIPAN... GEIPAN noted that the colour and speed initially suggested a bright meteor, re entering debris or similar fireball. The apparent turn was harder to accommodate, although investigators considered whether two separate meteors had been mentally joined into one event. Because the report was made about 16 years later, involved one witness and lacked contemporary records, GEIPAN concluded that no firm judgement was possible and assigned category C. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr BOURRIOT BERGONCE (40) 10.11.1994 GEIPAN... The case illustrates how memory can preserve a striking core experience while losing the precise timing, angles, sequence and environmental details needed to reconstruct it. The unexplained turn may represent a genuinely unusual movement, two overlapping events, a change in the observer’s viewpoint or a later reconstruction of a brief childhood perception. The surviving evidence cannot discriminate among them. Dax, 2018 The Dax report is a particularly useful example because GEIPAN published its doubts in detail. A single witness said that, at around 1.30 am in December 2018, three silent phenomena passed rapidly in succession: two white oval forms and one black triangular form. The sighting lasted between 30 seconds and a minute before nearby buildings blocked the view. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Investigators considered flying lanterns and reflections in a window but found difficulties with both. The reported forms and direction of travel did not fit lanterns well, while the witness said the window was opened during the observation, weakening a reflection hypothesis. Nevertheless, the date was uncertain, some statements were contradictory, photographs showed only the surroundings rather than the objects, and the witness did not answer several follow up questions. GEIPAN also noted that a television programme about UFOs had aired around the possible date, which could have influenced the language or interpretation of the report without implying dishonesty. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The final assessment was category C: fairly high reported strangeness but low evidential consistency. GEIPAN described the witness as apparently sincere, yet sincerity could not replace missing measurements and unresolved contradictions. That is an important distinction throughout the Landes material. A person may faithfully report what they believe they saw while investigators remain unable to establish what occurred. The cases that ordinary objects explained The more recent Landes catalogue is dominated not by unexplained machines but by familiar lights appearing under deceptive conditions. GEIPAN has identified or provisionally identified reports as aircraft, satellites, balloons, planets, condensation trails, illuminated cloud effects and flying lanterns. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN Concrete examples include: A 2010 observation at Garein was attributed to Venus. Another at Escource in 2010 was identified as Sirius. A 2012 report at Saint Martin de Seignanx and a 2014 sighting at Vieux Boucau les Bains were judged"},{"title":"Cazeres Case","url":"/cazeres-case/","description":"The 2002 Cazeres-sur-l'Adour sighting remains Landes' clearest official mystery, but its public evidence is still limited.","breadcrumb":"What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Cazeres Case Why the Cazeres UFO Case Remains Unresolved Landes Cazeres Case Cazeres Case Why the Cazeres UFO Case Remains Unresolved The 2002 Cazeres sur l'Adour sighting remains Landes' clearest official mystery, but its public evidence is still limited. The 2002 Cazeres sur l'Adour sighting remains Landes' clearest official mystery, but its public evidence is still limited. event time window What the witness reported Why GEIPAN classified the case D Evidence gaps and later reassessment landes cazeres case Introduction The Cazères sur l’Adour sighting of 1 November 2002 is the strongest unresolved case in Landes ’ accessible official UFO record, but it is not a richly documented mystery. At about 10 pm, a witness reported an orange, fire coloured ball with several flashing lights. It appeared to move back and forth before disappearing after roughly 30 seconds. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, classified the phenomenon as category D: unidentified after investigation. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... That classification makes Cazères more significant than a report left open merely because nothing useful was recorded. Yet the publicly available evidence remains narrow: one direct witness, no published photograph or video, no publicly documented radar return and only an unconfirmed suggestion that pilots also saw the phenomenon. The case therefore deserves attention as Landes’ clearest official mystery, but not as proof of an extraordinary craft. What the witness reported GEIPAN dates the observation to 10 pm on Friday, 1 November 2002, in Cazères sur l’Adour, an agricultural commune in eastern Landes. Its case summary describes a single luminous ball, orange or “fire” coloured, carrying several flashing lights. The witness said it performed repeated back and forth movements and vanished after about 30 seconds. No sound was reported. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... The database adds details not included in the short narrative. It records very cloudy or overcast conditions, places the observation against the sky and lists the reported phenomenon as spherical. The file gives an estimated diameter of three metres and an estimated distance of 350 metres, although its apparent speed is marked unknown. It also records no specified environmental effects. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... Those figures can create a misleading impression of precision. A witness who sees an unfamiliar light at night normally has no dependable way to determine both its distance and physical size without a known object at the same range. A light judged to be three metres wide at 350 metres could instead be smaller and nearer, or much larger and farther away. GEIPAN itself warns that witnesses commonly infer distance, speed and trajectory from objects they cannot identify, even though those quantities may be impossible to establish visually. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The public metadata identifies the direct witness as a ten year old boy. Age does not invalidate an observation, and curiosity rather than panic is recorded as his reaction. It does, however, make the absence of a full publicly accessible interview, sketch or independent adult account especially important. The surviving case page tells readers what he reported, but provides little opportunity to assess how the description was elicited, whether an adult helped reconstruct it or how firmly the distance and size estimates were established. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... Why GEIPAN classified the case D GEIPAN is a technical unit of CNES, the French space agency. It collects testimony, compares reported phenomena with astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and perceptual explanations, and publishes anonymised findings. It is not an organisation dedicated to proving extraterrestrial visitation; its stated method uses established scientific knowledge and human factors analysis. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... In GEIPAN’s system, a category D case is one that remains unidentified after investigation. This differs from category C, where the information is too weak or incomplete to support a reliable conclusion. Modern assessments weigh two broad factors: the residual strangeness of the observation after ordinary explanations have been tested, and its “consistency”, meaning the quantity and reliability of the information collected. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Cazères page calls the phenomenon “strange to very strange” and describes its consistency as medium to strong. That language explains why it was not simply filed as unusable testimony. The combination of a defined time and place, short duration, reported movement, changing lights, colour and absence of sound apparently gave investigators enough structure to treat it as a genuine identification problem. The published conclusion remained D when the online record was updated on 23 June 2021. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... Nevertheless, the bare classification does not reveal which hypotheses were examined or why each failed. The public page contains no visible analytical report setting out checks against aircraft movements, helicopters, astronomical objects, meteors, fireworks, balloons or atmospheric effects. It therefore establishes that GEIPAN did not identify the report, but not that every conceivable conventional explanation was positively ruled out. There is also a historical complication. The observation occurred before GEIPAN adopted its more detailed D1 and D2 categories in 2008. Today, D1 denotes a strange unresolved case and D2 a very strange one, with stronger evidence demanded as strangeness rises. Cazères remains labelled simply D on the public page. Readers should not silently upgrade it to the strongest modern D2 standard without a published reassessment showing that it meets that threshold. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The uncertain claim about pilots The most potentially important line in the official summary says that the phenomenon “seems” also to have been observed by pilots. If independently confirmed, aviation witnesses could turn a single observer report into a multi witness event and perhaps provide information about altitude, direction or air traffic. The wording is notably cautious. GEIPAN does not identify the pilots, state how many there were, specify whether they were airborne or on the ground, or reproduce separate statements from them. There is no published flight report, radio transcript, radar record or matching aviation chronology on the case page. The apparent pilot observation is therefore best treated as an investigative lead or second hand claim, not as confirmed corroboration. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr CAZERES SUR L'ADOUR (40) 01.11.2002 GEIPAN... This distinction matters because pilot testimony is often granted automatic authority in UFO discussions. GEIPAN’s own guidance is more restrained: experienced observers can still misidentify celestial bodies, aircraft or re entering debris, while unfamiliar viewing geometry can make ordinary aircraft appear stationary, unusually slow or oddly shaped. Professional experience may improve some observations, but it does not substitute for an independent record tied to the same time and location. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN As the public file stands, the pilots cannot be counted as verified additional witnesses. Later publication has repeated the same brief sentence rather than supplying the underlying testimony. The claim raises the case’s potential importance, but its undocumented status also represents one of the file’s largest evidential gaps. What ordinary explanations can and cannot cover Several conventional possibilities fit individual parts of the report, although none can be demonstrated from the published material. Aircraft or helicopter lights could account for several flashing points and apparent changes of direction. In darkness, especially under cloud, an aircraft approaching or turning can seem to hover or reverse. The lack of audible sound is not decisive over a brief 30 second interval because distance, wind and local background noise can suppress it. Against this idea, the witness described a fire coloured ball rather than a recognisable aircraft, and the public record supplies no identified flight matching the observation. A nearby firework or pyrotechnic device could appear orange, flicker and last only seconds. Repeated lateral movements are harder to reconcile unless perception, drifting smoke or multiple effects were"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases/","description":"Most Landes reports were explained by familiar objects or left open because witnesses could not provide enough reliable detail.","breadcrumb":"What Landes UFO Records Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases Why Most Landes UFO Reports Were Not Mysteries Explained Cases Explained Cases Why Most Landes UFO Reports Were Not Mysteries Most Landes reports were explained by familiar objects or left open because witnesses could not provide enough reliable detail. Most Landes reports were explained by familiar objects or left open because witnesses could not provide enough reliable detail. dataset evidence Aircraft, planets, lanterns and balloons When category C means too little evidence What Bourriot Bergonce and Dax reveal landes explained cases Introduction Most UFO reports in Landes did not become lasting mysteries. They were either linked to familiar objects and lighting effects—balloons, lanterns, satellites and entertainment spotlights—or left unresolved because the original account contained too little dependable information. That distinction matters. A report can sound highly unusual without providing enough evidence to show that anything genuinely anomalous occurred. The official GEIPAN archive, maintained by the French space agency CNES, therefore tells a more instructive story than a simple catalogue of “explained” and “unexplained” sightings. Cases at Biscarrosse and between Bénesse Maremne and Angresse show how apparently erratic or silent objects can be reconstructed from weather, movement and local conditions. Reports from Bourriot Bergonce and Dax show the opposite problem: investigators could imagine several ordinary possibilities, but missing dates, delayed testimony or unanswered questions prevented a reliable conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURRIOT BERGONCE (40) 10.11.1994 GEIPAN... Familiar objects could look surprisingly strange The Landes cases demonstrate that witnesses do not need to be careless or dishonest to misidentify an ordinary object. Distance removes useful detail, darkness conceals surroundings, and a light without a visible source can appear to move in ways that seem impossible. GEIPAN consequently evaluates both the residual strangeness of a report and its “consistency”: the quantity, reliability and objectivity of the information available after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr La sous classification des cas D en D1/D2, en fonction de critères attribuant plus ou moins d’étrangeté résiduelle et de consistance au... A useful example occurred at Biscarrosse on 7 August 2021. A witness looking through a roof window watched a pale light make rapid, irregular movements for about an hour and a half. The description suggested a compact object darting around within a limited patch of sky. Yet the duration, the repeated changes of direction, the low cloud and the summer tourist setting pointed towards a sky facing searchlight whose beam was sweeping across the cloud base. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBISCARROSSE (40) 07.08.2021 GEIPAN... GEIPAN also explained why the witness’s video did not settle the matter. A nearby illuminated white structure affected the camera’s exposure and focus, making the faint distant light difficult or impossible to record even though it remained visible to the eye. Weather observations showed cloud layers low enough to reflect a powerful beam, while several night time venues operated in the wider area. Investigators could not identify the exact projector, so the case received category B—probably explained—rather than the more certain category A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBISCARROSSE (40) 07.08.2021 GEIPAN... This case is important because the strange movement was not evidence of extraordinary performance. It was part of the explanation. A beam crossing an uneven cloud layer can appear to stop, jump, reverse or accelerate, particularly when neither the beam nor its ground source is visible. A metallic sphere over Lake Cazaux A more recent Biscarrosse report reached a firmer conclusion. On 20 August 2024, a holidaymaker at the lakeside beach of Maguide saw a metallic, apparently spherical object moving steadily above the opposite shore towards Sanguinet. It remained visible for roughly 20 seconds. A check with the French air defence planning and operations centre found no aircraft movement matching the reported path. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBISCARROSSE (40) 20.08.2024 GEIPAN... The non aircraft result did not make the object anomalous. Its round form, reflective surface, horizontal movement and direction of travel were all consistent with a Mylar party balloon being carried by the prevailing wind. The summer beach setting also supplied a plausible source. GEIPAN classified the report A, meaning that investigators considered the object identified without meaningful ambiguity. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBISCARROSSE (40) 20.08.2024 GEIPAN... This is a useful warning against treating the elimination of one hypothesis as proof of an exotic alternative. Showing that an object was probably not an aircraft only narrowed the field. Shape, wind and setting provided a better explanation. Orange lights, lanterns and slow drift An orange “ball” seen by a driver travelling from Bénesse Maremne towards Angresse on 10 October 2011 produced another probable identification. The light appeared to move upwards and downwards, made no noise and was photographed by the witness. Investigators found that weak, variable winds were compatible with the movement of a flying lantern. After reviewing examples of such lanterns, the witness also accepted that explanation as plausible. GEIPAN classified the case B because the identification was persuasive but not directly proven. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D465] DE BENESSE (40) VERS ANGRESSE (40)10.10.2011 GEIPAN... The observation took place early in the morning, an unusual time for a celebratory lantern. That reduced confidence but did not outweigh the object’s colour, silence and wind compatible motion. The case illustrates how investigations weigh several imperfect clues rather than demanding that every detail fit perfectly. Other Landes entries follow the same pattern. A Soustons report from December 2019 was attributed probably to a group of satellites, while another Soustons case and the 2021 Biscarrosse sighting were associated with sky facing light displays. Such explanations are especially relevant when witnesses describe multiple lights moving together, repeated sweeps across one area, or steady points whose apparent spacing remains fixed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANPENNES MIRABEAU (LES) (13) 02.08.2016 02/08/2016 C Bouches du Rhône Manque d'informations fiables... My thoughts on the first round of UFO files Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.4M · Uploaded: May 2026 Open on YouTube Category C does not mean a deeper mystery The most easily misunderstood GEIPAN label is category C. It does not mean that investigators found persuasive evidence for an extraordinary object. It means that the report could not be analysed reliably because essential information was missing or insufficiently dependable. GEIPAN distinguishes this from category D, in which a case remains unexplained despite having evidence substantial enough to test. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Nationally, category C is a major part of the archive rather than a rare exception. GEIPAN’s published statistics listed more than 1,000 cases classified as unidentified because of missing data, and its methodological material has historically placed roughly one third of reports in the unusable or insufficient information group. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several weaknesses commonly push a Landes report towards category C: the report was made years after the observation; the exact date or time could not be established; only one witness supplied information; photographs showed the surroundings rather than the phenomenon; the object’s direction, elevation or duration was poorly defined; witnesses did not answer follow up questions; no independent astronomical, meteorological or aviation record could be matched confidently. These limitations do not show that the witness invented the event. They show that sincerity alone cannot reconstruct what was in the sky. What Bourriot Bergonce reveals about delayed testimony The Bourriot Bergonce case concerned an observation said to have occurred on 10 November 1994 but reported in 2010, approximately 16 years later. The witness recalled being a child in the back of a car on a country road. Through the tops of trees, the witness saw an intense green light and a nearby luminous point that seemed to accelerate, change direction abruptly and disappear northwards. No other witness account was obtained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBOURRIOT BERGONCE (40) 10.11.1994 GEIPAN... The colour and speed initially suggested a meteor, bright fireball or atmospheric re entry. The apparent change of direction was harder to reconcile with a single object, although two separate meteors occurring close together could have created that impression. GEIPAN did not promote"},{"title":"When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations","url":"/loir-et-cher/","description":"Loir-et-Cher has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single persuasive mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations Loir et Cher has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single persuasive mystery. Loir et Cher has a documented UFO history, but not one dominated by a single persuasive mystery. Its most useful cases instead show how convincing aerial experiences can arise from ordinary objects seen under poor conditions. loir et cher Introduction That pattern matters. Loir et Cher is not devoid of unusual testimony, but the surviving evidence does not support claims of a persistent local UFO hotspot, repeated military encounters or confirmed exotic craft. The department is better understood as a compact case study in witness perception, official investigation and the value of revisiting old reports with improved maps, imagery and technical knowledge. Ternay: the department’s strongest case was later downgraded The Ternay incident stands apart because it had several witnesses, prompt reporting and substantial documentation. At about 11.40 pm on 22 May 1981, four members of one family were travelling by car near Ternay. They saw a brightly illuminated phenomenon that appeared low in the sky near a small wood. The driver stopped, and the group watched it for roughly ten to fifteen minutes. Accounts described a dark, elongated or lens shaped mass carrying strong white, blue green and red orange lights. Some witnesses estimated it at about eight metres long and several hundred metres away. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The driver initially wondered whether it was an aircraft approaching the military aviation area around Tours, but rejected that interpretation because the light seemed stationary and unlike a conventional aeroplane. After it appeared to disappear behind trees, the witnesses drove towards the area and encountered what they described as thick mist or smoke. No sound, landing trace or measurable physical effect was established. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The reporting process was unusually strong for a rural sighting. The principal witness telephoned the gendarmerie within minutes. Officers visited his home, returned with him the next morning for a three hour reconstruction, searched for possible ground traces and interviewed all four witnesses separately over the following days. The case file included drawings, photographs and a location plan. Those features gave the report considerably more substance than a late recollection or unsupported newspaper anecdote. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Why the original interpretation seemed plausible Several elements made the experience feel airborne: the lights appeared above or beside a nearer clump of trees; the witnesses could not clearly see the horizon in darkness and overcast weather; the light seemed to change position as the car moved; branches may have intermittently obscured the source, producing apparent flashes; distance and size were estimated without a reliable reference object. These are important weaknesses rather than accusations against the witnesses. A sincere observer can accurately report what an object looked like while being wrong about where it was. When the horizon is invisible, powerful lights on rising ground may appear suspended in the sky. Movement by the observer also creates parallax: a distant source seems to shift in relation to nearer trees or buildings. GEIPAN’s re analysis regarded these effects as central to the Ternay sighting. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The later forestry explanation The case was once classified as unexplained. In 2018, however, GEIPAN re examined it using geographical reconstruction, aerial photographs and its accumulated experience of similar reports. Investigators considered illuminated agricultural machinery and forestry equipment operating about two kilometres south of the witnesses. They favoured forestry work because aerial photographs showed that woodland in the relevant line of sight had been cut between May 1978 and July 1981. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The proposed reconstruction was that powerful work lights on higher ground appeared to be a low flying object. The witnesses’ moving car made the lights shift against a nearer wood about 400 metres away, while trees around the machinery created the reported blinking and colour changes. When the group drove towards the nearer wood, they searched in the wrong place because the true source was much farther away and in a different direction. GEIPAN classified the case as B: probably explained, rather than proven beyond every doubt. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... That distinction is fair. No machinery log or named operator was recovered to demonstrate that a particular vehicle was working at that exact time. The blue and green lights reported by three witnesses were also not considered typical of forestry equipment. Nevertheless, the revised explanation accounts for the apparent altitude, movement, flashing, disappearance and lack of physical traces more economically than an unknown aircraft. The later investigation therefore weakened the extraordinary interpretation substantially. Blois in 1965: a triangular object with a conventional match A second historically interesting case occurred near Blois on 23 July 1965. Multiple people reportedly watched an immobile, metallic looking triangular object reflecting sunlight at about 6 pm. Because the report was submitted long after the event, its evidential value is limited: memories can change, and contemporary meteorological or flight records may no longer be available. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr BLOIS (41) 23.07.1965 GEIPAN... GEIPAN classified the case as B and considered a tetrahedral stratospheric research balloon the probable explanation. Such balloons could present a bright, geometric appearance when sunlight caught their reflective surfaces, while their great distance made them seem stationary. The case is a useful reminder that “triangular” does not automatically imply a structured aircraft. Shape is often inferred from illuminated surfaces rather than observed as a complete solid outline. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr BLOIS (41) 23.07.1965 GEIPAN... The Blois report remains more interesting than a simple point of light because several witnesses apparently perceived the same unusual geometry. Even so, there is no recorded radar return, photograph, close range detail, manoeuvre or interaction with aircraft. The official balloon interpretation fits the object’s brightness, immobility and daytime visibility without requiring an exotic cause. Pierre Bescond: Why France Studied UFOs at the Highest Level Channel: Something is Up Open on YouTube The 1990 wave: impressive testimony, but a national re entry event Loir et Cher also lay beneath one of France’s most famous mass sighting episodes. At about 7 pm on 5 November 1990, witnesses across a very large part of the country reported formations of lights, silent triangular shapes and enormous dark objects moving broadly from west to east. Gendarmerie files include reports from several separated observers who agreed on the timing and general direction but did not see a supporting structure joining the lights. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Print 90307323 3697 90M.tif (23 pages)... Some local and specialist UFO accounts have argued that the Soviet rocket re entry identified that evening cannot explain every individual observation. They point to reports of low altitude, turns, huge apparent dimensions or objects lingering after the main event. Those objections deserve to be recorded, but they do not overturn the central astronomical identification. GEIPAN’s national file classifies the main 5 November phenomenon as an atmospheric re entry and retained that conclusion in an update published in January 2025. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN A fragmenting rocket stage can generate several bright bodies travelling in formation. At night, witnesses may connect those lights into the outline of one dark craft, particularly when no stars are visible between them. Distance is also easily underestimated: an object hundreds of kilometres away may seem to pass above a nearby road or town. Apparent silence is therefore unsurprising, while perspective can make parallel tracks look like turns or changes of direction. This does not prove that every report filed in Loir et Cher that evening described the same object. A mass event can attract unrelated aircraft, stars and local lights into the reporting stream. The strongest conclusion is narrower: the timing, direction and nationwide distribution establish a major conventional stimulus, so individual claims require independent"},{"title":"1990 Wave","url":"/1990-wave-5e855a/","description":"Loir-et-Cher's reports from November 1990 formed part of a nationwide spectacle now chiefly attributed to a fragmenting rocket stage.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations","level":2,"text":"1990 Wave Why France Saw Giant UFOs in 1990 Loir et Cher UFOs 1990 Wave 1990 Wave Why France Saw Giant UFOs in 1990 Loir et Cher's reports from November 1990 formed part of a nationwide spectacle now chiefly attributed to a fragmenting rocket stage. Loir et Cher's reports from November 1990 formed part of a nationwide spectacle now chiefly attributed to a fragmenting rocket stage. event time window What witnesses across Loir et Cher described How a rocket re entry created a formation of lights Why some observers still dispute the full explanation loir et cher 1990 wave Introduction On the evening of 5 November 1990, people across Loir et Cher joined one of the largest mass sightings in French UFO history. At about 7 pm, witnesses saw several brilliant lights travelling broadly from west to east. Many interpreted the formation not as separate points but as the outline of a vast, silent triangular or elongated craft moving at low altitude. The strongest evidence now indicates that the main spectacle was the atmospheric re entry of the third stage of a Soviet Proton rocket. Its breaking, glowing fragments crossed France at great height, but their brightness, slow apparent motion and stable grouping created a remarkably persuasive illusion of something nearby and solid. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation service, classifies the national event as explained. Loir et Cher reports from Binas, Villavard and the road towards Sasnières form part of that same documented case family. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The remaining debate is therefore not chiefly whether a rocket re entered that evening. That is strongly established. The real question is whether every report attributed to the wave fits that single event equally well. A UFO Night in France : November 5, 1990… They Watched the Skies and Never Forgot Channel: Chacha Paranormal Open on YouTube What witnesses across Loir et Cher described The surviving GEIPAN archive records several observations within Loir et Cher. Five individual testimony entries are attached to Binas, while three are associated with Villavard and another with the nearby C13 road towards Sasnières. Their presence in the national file is important: they show that the department was not merely mentioned retrospectively in press summaries, but was represented in the official collection of witness material. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Across France, descriptions varied considerably. Some people reported a luminous trail or a procession of glowing balls. Others described a triangle, a broad rectangular platform or an immense dark body carrying rows of lights. Drawings collected from witnesses show how points of light were often joined mentally into the edges of a single craft, sometimes complete with windows, engines or a dark underside. [SatObs] satobs.org Sat Obs Sat Obs Several recurring features explain why the experience was so memorable: Great apparent size. The formation occupied a large angle of sky, encouraging estimates of an object hundreds of metres or even kilometres long. Low apparent altitude. Witnesses frequently believed that it was just above houses, trees or roads. Little or no sound. The absence of an aircraft like engine reinforced the impression of extraordinary technology. Orderly movement. The lights maintained a broadly coherent direction, making them look attached to one structure rather than scattered debris. A long viewing time. Compared with a typical meteor, the phenomenon seemed slow and could remain visible long enough for observers to stop cars, call relatives or examine it carefully. These impressions were not confined to inexperienced sky watchers. CNES astronaut Jean Pierre Haigneré observed the event, and technicians at the Aire sur l’Adour balloon centre delayed a launch so that they could watch it. Civil and military aviation personnel also reported striking interpretations. Expert status did not prevent confusion because the spectacle was genuinely unusual and offered few reliable clues to distance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... For Loir et Cher, this is the central historical value of the episode. It was not a distinctive local craft operating only over the department. Local residents were viewing one element of a huge, geographically continuous event that was also reported from western France through the centre of the country and towards Alsace. How a rocket re entry created a formation of lights The official reconstruction began almost immediately. By around 8 pm, CNES security services were receiving calls from gendarmerie units reporting an unexplained luminous phenomenon. SEPRA, the CNES service then responsible for unusual aerospace events and atmospheric re entries, checked with civil aviation, the military, meteorological services and NASA. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... NASA identified the re entering object as catalogue number 20925, international designation 1990 094C, associated with a Soviet Gorizont communications satellite launch. CNES orbital specialists then confirmed a path crossing France from the Bay of Biscay towards Alsace. According to the official archive, an experienced satellite observer had independently alerted SEPRA on 6 November and placed the object at approximately 103 kilometres altitude over the Bay of Biscay, descending to about 83 kilometres as it left eastern France. The identification was released to news agencies on 9 November. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Contemporary reporting likewise described burning rocket material travelling on a south west to north east route across France at about 7 pm. [Le Monde.fr] lemonde.fr Le Monde.frLes débris d'un mystère Trois jours auront suffi pour...14 Nov 1990 — Après cinq semaines de voyage orbital, les débris de la... The physical mechanism accounts for the most common observations. A rocket stage returning from orbit does not necessarily appear as one compact object. As aerodynamic forces and heating increase, panels, tanks and other components can separate. Each fragment may become incandescent or acquire a luminous wake. From many kilometres below, the observer sees a cluster of bright objects following similar trajectories. GEIPAN’s later simulation describes the debris cloud as passing at roughly 90 to 50 kilometres altitude, with speed declining from about 7 to 2 kilometres per second. Those figures sound incompatible with reports of a slow, low flying craft, but distance transforms apparent movement. An object travelling extremely fast at high altitude can cross the viewer’s field of vision gradually, especially when its track runs partly towards or away from the observer rather than directly overhead. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Three visual effects then turned a debris swarm into a “giant UFO”. Brightness suggested proximity. People normally associate brilliant lights with nearby aircraft, vehicles or buildings. Without a visible horizon or known scale, exceptionally bright fragments seemed far lower than they really were. GEIPAN identifies this illusion of closeness as a major feature of the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The mind supplied a connecting surface. When several lights move together, observers naturally group them as parts of one object. The dark sky between the lights may then be perceived as a dark body, even though no surface has been independently detected. The wide variety of witness drawings—from triangles to rectangles and rounded platforms—supports the interpretation that observers were imposing different shapes upon a common arrangement of luminous points. [SatObs] satobs.org Sat Obs Sat Obs Fragment trails resembled engines or beams. Ablating debris can produce streaks, flares and changing colours. Witnesses may interpret these as exhausts, searchlights, illuminated windows or propulsion systems, particularly when the leading fragments and trailing material maintain an organised appearance. The Loir et Cher sightings fit this mechanism because they occurred within the same narrow time window and along the broad national progression. Their evidential strength lies in confirming the geographical spread, not in separating the department from the re entry track. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why the official explanation is unusually strong Many UFO explanations are tentative because they depend on a possible aircraft, star or balloon that was never specifically"},{"title":"Ternay","url":"/ternay/","description":"The 1981 Ternay case shows how a well-documented family sighting was later weakened by a detailed ground-based explanation.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Met Stronger Explanations","level":2,"text":"Ternay Did Forestry Lights Explain the Ternay UFO? Loir et Cher UFOs Ternay Ternay Did Forestry Lights Explain the Ternay UFO? The 1981 Ternay case shows how a well documented family sighting was later weakened by a detailed ground based explanation. The 1981 Ternay case shows how a well documented family sighting was later weakened by a detailed ground based explanation. entity What the four witnesses reported How gendarmes investigated the scene Why GEIPAN later favoured forestry machinery loir et cher ternay Introduction The Ternay sighting of 22 May 1981 became one of Loir et Cher ’s strongest official UFO cases because four family members watched the same luminous phenomenon for up to fifteen minutes, reported it almost immediately and helped gendarmes reconstruct the event the next morning. The witnesses described a dark, elongated form with intense white, blue green and red orange lights, apparently hovering near a small wood. No physical trace was found. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... For decades, the case remained officially unexplained. A detailed GEIPAN re examination published in 2018 changed that assessment. Using terrain profiles, aerial photographs and reconstructed sightlines, investigators concluded that the witnesses had probably seen the lights of forestry machinery working about two kilometres away, not an object 300–400 metres away in the air. The explanation is persuasive but not conclusive: no particular machine or operator was identified, and the exact date of the documented tree felling remains uncertain. The case is therefore best described as probably explained , not definitively solved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... What the four witnesses reported At about 11.40–11.45 pm, four relatives were travelling from Ternay towards Saint Martin des Bois in a Peugeot 404 after a music rehearsal. One passenger first noticed a light and suggested that it might be an aircraft. The driver also initially considered an aeroplane approaching the Tours aviation area, but rejected that impression because the phenomenon appeared stationary and unlike an ordinary aircraft. He stopped the car so that the group could observe it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The broad outline was consistent across the four statements. They reported a single, strongly illuminated phenomenon close to a small wood, apparently low above the ground. The observation lasted roughly ten to fifteen minutes. All four regarded it as stationary during much of that period, although some described an apparent approach or changes of position before the car stopped. No one reported hearing an engine, rotor or other unusual sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The descriptions were similar but not identical: The driver described a dark, elongated, lens like mass, perhaps eight metres long, with white, blue green and red orange lights. A second witness could not determine a clear shape and chiefly remembered bright white and red lights. The third and fourth witnesses described an oval or elongated lens, numerous coloured lights and blue reflections or beams beneath it. Several witnesses thought the red orange lights marked the two ends of a larger dark body. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... This agreement gave the report considerable weight, but it must be interpreted carefully. The witnesses were members of one family, travelling together and discussing the phenomenon as they watched it. Their accounts therefore confirm a shared visual event, but they are not equivalent to four observers independently encountering the same object from separate locations. The conditions were also poor for estimating shape, height and distance. It was a very dark rural night under a heavily clouded sky, with no clear horizon. Two witnesses openly acknowledged that they could not describe the object precisely. Against such a background, separated points of light can be mentally joined into the outline of a single solid object, while the apparent distance of an unfamiliar light source may be wrong by a large margin. GEIPAN’s later analysis treated this loss of depth and horizon information as the central weakness in the original interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The disappearance and the mist The most dramatic part of the account came after the main observation. The lights appeared to disappear behind the small wood. The driver and his daughter later approached from the other side by the D116, looking for the object. They reported that thick mist, smoke or cloud briefly obscured the wood. After turning back, the daughter noticed small red orange, star like lights roughly a metre above the ground. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... These details encouraged the idea that a craft had descended or landed. Yet none of the witnesses actually saw an object touch the ground. “Landing” was an inference based on the lights becoming hidden by the trees and appearing on either side of them. The mist was not sampled or otherwise established as an effect produced by the phenomenon; damp, unsettled conditions were already present, and the witnesses themselves were unsure whether they had seen smoke or fog. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The small lights seen near the ground are potentially compatible with a terrestrial vehicle. They are also one of the weaker parts of the testimony because only one of the two returning witnesses included them in the quoted account, and the pair did not stop again despite having returned specifically to search for the phenomenon. GEIPAN regarded this as evidence that the final lights did not appear as extraordinary as the main display. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... How gendarmes investigated the scene The speed and thoroughness of the initial response distinguish Ternay from many historical UFO stories. The principal witness contacted the gendarmerie within minutes of returning home. Officers went to his home and arranged a daylight examination. At about dawn, they accompanied him back to the area and spent several hours reconstructing the observation and searching the presumed disappearance site. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The principal witness gave a formal statement on 23 May. The other three were interviewed separately over the following days. The resulting case file contained four witness accounts, two sketches, four site photographs and a location plan. That documentation allowed later investigators to compare what each person remembered and to reconstruct the roads, viewing directions and intervening terrain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... No indentation, scorching, damaged vegetation, residue or other physical trace was found. Nor did the investigation establish radar confirmation, an independent witness outside the family or an identifiable aircraft operating at the reported position. The lack of traces does not disprove an airborne light, but it substantially weakens the suggestion that an eight metre vehicle landed beside the wood. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The original enquiry nevertheless had a significant limitation: it concentrated on the small wood that the witnesses believed was 300–400 metres away. That distance depended on their assumption that the phenomenon was beside or immediately behind the wood. The investigation did not establish that the lights and the wood were at the same range. This later proved decisive. Every Type Of Forestry Machine Explained Channel: Everyone Of Them Open on YouTube Why the apparent distance was probably wrong The 2018 reconstruction separated two features that the witnesses had visually combined: a nearby small wood used as a reference point, and a more distant area of rising farmland and woodland lying in roughly the same direction. GEIPAN’s terrain profiles showed that the ground south of the observers gradually rose towards fields near the locality of L’Airerie. A brightly illuminated vehicle there, approximately two kilometres away, could remain visible above or beside the nearer trees. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Because the true horizon was indistinct, lights on the higher ground could appear suspended against the dark sky. The witnesses then used the nearer wood to estimate the source as only 300–400 metres distant and about ten metres above the ground. Once that assumed distance was accepted, the spacing between the lights naturally suggested an object several metres long. At two kilometres, however, the same angular separation could represent the lamps fitted across a large ground vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr"},{"title":"Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist...","url":"/which-loire-atlantique-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Loire-Atlantique has a long but uneven record of unusual-sky reports, ranging from “flying saucer” stories during the nationwide 1954 wave to modern...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Loire Atlantique has a long but uneven record of unusual sky reports, ranging from “flying saucer” stories during the nationwide 1954 wave to modern... Loire Atlantique has a long but uneven record of unusual sky reports, ranging from “flying saucer” stories during the nationwide 1954 wave to modern observations near Nantes, Saint Nazaire and the department’s coast. loire atlantique Introduction One Nantes incident from December 1979 is still classified as unexplained by France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN. Yet that label does not mean an extraordinary craft was established: it means the available material did not support a satisfactory identification. Loire Atlantique’s UFO history is therefore best understood as a mixture of genuine witness puzzlement, recurring perceptual traps, incomplete reporting and a small residue of cases that cannot confidently be reconstructed. [Geipan] geipan.fr NANTES (44) 15.12.1979 15/12/1979 D Loire Atlantique Phénomène étrange à très étrange/ de consistance moyenne à forte 04/09/200... The 1954 wave reached Saint Nazaire The autumn of 1954 produced France’s most famous nationwide “flying saucer” wave. Reports multiplied rapidly in newspapers, often describing discs, cigars, luminous balls or objects said to hover and accelerate. Loire Atlantique participated in this wider atmosphere, although surviving local accounts are not as well documented as the best known cases elsewhere in France. One frequently repeated Saint Nazaire report concerns an observation from the grounds of the Aristide Briand school in October 1954. A retrospective compilation says that a witness and a large crowd watched an aerial phenomenon for roughly two minutes in clear weather. The precise date is uncertain, and the account survives principally through later UFO literature rather than a readily accessible police file or contemporary technical investigation. It should therefore be treated as an historical claim, not as a verified mass sighting with securely preserved testimony. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org voir ici.]... Date: octobre 1954.... Lieu: Saint Nazaire (L. A.) Durée: environ 2 minutes. Météo: beau temps, ciel sans... The main value of this material is cultural and archival. It shows that the national wave was being discussed and remembered in the Saint Nazaire area, but it does not provide enough reliable positional, astronomical or aviation information to determine what people actually saw. Contemporary reporting may still yield further detail: the Loire Atlantique departmental archives provide full text searching across digitised local newspapers, including material extending into 1954. That archive offers a firmer route for testing later summaries against what was published at the time. [loire atlantique.fr] archives numerisees.loire atlantique.fr Presse consultable en mode texte Archives Loire AtlantiquePériodiques et titres de presse conservés aux Archives départementales de Loire Atlantique et couvrant la périod... This distinction matters because 1954 accounts are especially vulnerable to repetition. A short newspaper item can become more definite with each retelling, while missing facts—direction, elevation, exact duration, weather and witness independence—remain missing. The Saint Nazaire stories belong in the department’s UFO history, but their evidential value is limited unless original articles or official records can be tied to each claim. Nantes, December 1979: the department’s main unresolved case The most prominent officially unresolved Loire Atlantique case occurred in Nantes during the early hours of 15 December 1979. GEIPAN’s catalogue retains it as a category D report, its classification for an observation left unexplained despite the information available. The surviving official summary describes a witness observing an unusual phenomenon from a third floor location at around 2 a.m. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This case matters because category D entries are comparatively uncommon in the modern GEIPAN catalogue. They represent the residue left after obvious or probable explanations have not been established. They are not, however, confirmations of an unknown vehicle. GEIPAN explicitly defines category D in procedural terms: the reported phenomenon remains unexplained on the evidence held by the investigators. [Geipan] geipan.fr Il sera enfin systématiquement averti lors de la publication du dossier d’enquête relatif à son observation sur notre site cnes geipan.fr... The Nantes case also illustrates the limits of older files. Public catalogue entries may preserve a classification without offering the quantity of material now expected from a strong investigation: several independent witnesses, calibrated angular estimates, photographs with metadata, weather records, radar data and a clear timeline. An unusual description can therefore remain unexplained partly because the original observation was genuinely difficult to identify and partly because later investigators cannot recover enough information to test competing hypotheses. That makes “unresolved” the correct but narrow conclusion. The file deserves attention within Loire Atlantique because it is the department’s clearest official category D case, not because it demonstrates an extraordinary origin. Later publication has preserved the claim, but it has not apparently added decisive corroboration. When dramatic reports become ordinary objects Several Loire Atlantique files show how an initially striking experience can weaken under reconstruction. These cases are among the most informative in the department because they reveal the mechanisms that make ordinary lights appear structured, fast or responsive. The “following object” at Le Pellerin On 11 June 1994, a motorist near Le Pellerin reported a low object apparently moving alongside her car. She described a lemon like form, multicoloured flashing lights and what seemed to be a cable or projection. The object appeared to manoeuvre, vanish and reappear before disappearing rapidly. The case was initially classified as unexplained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A later GEIPAN review changed the assessment to category B: probably a model aircraft or helicopter operating near a model flying site. Investigators noted that the reported direction matched the club’s flying area, the low altitude suited model operations, and the flashing colours resembled aviation lights. The witness was driving at night with the windows closed, conditions that made sound, distance and shape difficult to judge. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The impression that the object kept pace with the car was not treated as proof of intelligent pursuit. A distant or laterally moving light can seem to “follow” a vehicle because its bearing changes little against a dark background. Vegetation can also make a light vanish abruptly, creating the impression of sudden acceleration. No photograph or second independent testimony was available, so the final explanation remained probable rather than certain. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The case is important because the later inquiry weakened the extraordinary interpretation without accusing the witness of fabrication. Her experience may have been accurately remembered as strange, while her estimates of size, distance and movement were unreliable. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube A triangle over Nantes was a star cluster Two people walking in Nantes shortly after midnight on 20 October 2013 noticed several evenly spaced lights forming a triangle. Cloud movement appeared to give the formation motion, and one light looked orange. GEIPAN reconstructed the sky and found that the positions matched the Hyades star cluster, with the nearby orange star Aldebaran accounting for the differently coloured point. The case was classified A, meaning identified with high confidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This is a useful local example of an apparently geometric “craft” produced by unrelated stars. Human perception naturally joins bright points into a single shape, especially when clouds remove surrounding reference stars. Fatigue and walking beneath moving cloud can further encourage the impression that the whole formation is travelling. Lanterns on the road to Nantes At about 2.20 a.m. on 16 April 2011, an automobilist and passenger travelling from Treillières towards Nantes saw roughly ten orange luminous balls moving silently across the sky and fading one after another. GEIPAN classified the event as a"},{"title":"Nantes 1979","url":"/nantes-1979/","description":"The 1979 Nantes sighting remains officially unexplained, but its category D label reflects missing answers rather than proof of an exotic craft.","breadcrumb":"Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"Nantes 1979 Why Is the 1979 Nantes Sighting Still Unresolved? Loire Atlantique Nantes 1979 Nantes 1979 Why Is the 1979 Nantes Sighting Still Unresolved? The 1979 Nantes sighting remains officially unexplained, but its category D label reflects missing answers rather than proof of an exotic craft. The 1979 Nantes sighting remains officially unexplained, but its category D label reflects missing answers rather than proof of an exotic craft. event time window What the witness reported on 15 December 1979 What GEIPAN category D actually means Which missing records prevent a firmer conclusion loire atlantique nantes 1979 Introduction The Nantes sighting of 15 December 1979 remains one of the clearest examples of an officially unresolved UFO report in Loire Atlantique , but also one of the easiest to misunderstand. At about 2 a.m., a 70 year old woman watching from a third floor flat reported a silent, orange, phosphorescent, cigar shaped phenomenon moving slowly from north to south before disappearing behind the urban landscape. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, still publishes the case as category D: unidentified after examination. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 15.12.1979 GEIPAN... That classification does not establish that the witness saw an exotic aircraft. It records a narrower conclusion: no conventional explanation was judged sufficiently convincing from the material available. The case has no known photograph, instrument reading, physical trace or second independent witness. Its continuing mystery therefore rests on an apparently unusual report combined with serious gaps in the surviving public record. What was reported at 2 a.m.? GEIPAN dates the observation to Saturday 15 December 1979, at approximately 2 a.m., in Nantes. Its case database describes a single orange, phosphorescent object, shaped like a cigar and held in a vertical position. The witness said it moved slowly and silently from north to south, eventually vanishing into or behind the cityscape rather than fading away in open sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 15.12.1979 GEIPAN... The more detailed witness entry adds several useful points: The witness was a 70 year old woman. She observed from an urban setting and apparently from inside or immediately beside her third floor home. Only one phenomenon was seen. It was described as cylindrical, orange or fire coloured, and completely silent. The apparent speed was recorded as slow. No environmental effect was specified. The apparent size and distance were not determined. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 15.12.1979 GEIPAN... These details create a coherent basic narrative, but not a precise reconstruction. “Cigar shaped”, for example, may describe a genuinely elongated body, a bright vertical streak, a light distorted by cloud or glass, or simply the witness’s best verbal comparison. Without an angular measurement, even the object’s apparent scale is unknown. It might have occupied a large part of the view or only a very small area of sky. The disappearance behind the urban landscape is likewise important. It suggests that buildings, roofs or other foreground obstructions ended the sighting, rather than the phenomenon performing an objectively measurable departure. That leaves its distance indeterminate. A nearby light, a distant aircraft or atmospheric effect, and a much more remote object could all appear to cross the same line of sight. GEIPAN’s public data also record rain and thunderstorms as the weather conditions associated with the observation. That information deserves attention because cloud, precipitation, reflections and variable visibility can alter the apparent shape and colour of lights. However, the public case page does not provide an original meteorological report, cloud base measurement or a detailed account of how those conditions affected the view. The weather entry therefore identifies an investigative consideration, not an established explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 15.12.1979 GEIPAN... Why did GEIPAN leave it unexplained? GEIPAN, part of the French space agency CNES, collects witness reports, examines possible astronomical, meteorological, aviation and human explanations, and publishes its conclusions. Its partners can include the gendarmerie, police, the French Air and Space Force, the national weather service and scientific specialists. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Under GEIPAN’s classification system, category A means securely identified, B means probably identified, C means that the case cannot be analysed adequately because information is lacking, and D means that it remains unidentified after investigation. Category D is now subdivided into D1 and D2: D1 generally covers strange reports with moderate evidential consistency, such as a single witness without imagery, while D2 requires stronger supporting material, such as independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr e malgré les éléments en possession du GEIPAN.Read more... The Nantes file retains the older, unsplit D label rather than a modern D1 or D2 designation. GEIPAN’s database describes it as strange to very strange, with medium to strong consistency, and lists its latest displayed update as 23 June 2021. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 15.12.1979 GEIPAN... The most cautious reading is that investigators did not find a satisfactory match for the reported combination of features: an orange, apparently self luminous appearance; a vertically elongated shape; slow north to south movement; an absence of audible engine noise; and a sighting that ended behind the urban skyline. None of those characteristics is individually extraordinary. Aircraft can be inaudible at sufficient distance, lights can look elongated through cloud or optical distortion, and orange illumination is common among aircraft, fires, lanterns and atmospheric phenomena. The difficulty lies in showing that any one explanation fits the entire reported sequence rather than merely one or two elements. GEIPAN’s methodology assesses both “strangeness” and “consistency”. Strangeness concerns how poorly the strongest conventional hypothesis fits the observation after investigation. Consistency concerns the quantity and reliability of the information available. A report can therefore be relatively strange without being evidentially strong in the everyday sense. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan D1/D2), D1 et D2 étant des subdivisions de D. La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN. La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D1/D2) s'ap... That distinction is crucial in Nantes. The report appears consistent enough for GEIPAN not to dismiss it as unusable, yet it lacks the independent evidence that would make an unusual interpretation compelling. Témoignage René OVNI 1979 Channel: Olivier Cellier Open on YouTube What category D does—and does not—mean The category D label is sometimes presented in UFO discussions as official confirmation of an unknown craft. That overstates the evidence. GEIPAN defines D as an unidentified phenomenon after investigation, not as an extraterrestrial vehicle or even necessarily a solid object. Its French terminology refers to an unidentified aerospace phenomenon because the witness’s experience may involve a light, atmospheric effect, perception error or other event that was interpreted as an object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr e malgré les éléments en possession du GEIPAN.Read more... GEIPAN’s published statistics also show that category D cases form a small minority of its catalogue. As of the current statistics page, about 3.1 per cent of published classified cases are unidentified after investigation, while roughly 30.1 per cent remain unidentified primarily because of insufficient data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatisticsBy Classification. By Year of Observation. By Region. By Department. Par... UAP's that are classed as type C or D (D1/D2... The Nantes case sits awkwardly near the boundary between those ideas. It is formally D rather than C, suggesting that GEIPAN considered the testimony sufficiently usable to assess. Yet many of the measurements needed for a confident modern reassessment are absent from the public entry. Its unresolved status therefore means: No explanation has been demonstrated. GEIPAN did not identify the phenomenon as an aircraft, astronomical object, balloon, meteorological effect or other known source. The observation retains some internal coherence. The database preserves a definite time, direction of movement, colour, shape, speed description and witness position. The physical nature of the phenomenon was not established. “Object” is part of the witness description, not a verified technical finding. The classification is not proof of advanced technology. An unresolved identification may result from a genuinely unusual event, an imperfectly documented ordinary event, or a"},{"title":"Likely Explanations","url":"/likely-explanations/","description":"Later reviews show how model aircraft, stars, lanterns and distant lights can produce vivid reports of structured or pursuing objects.","breadcrumb":"Which Loire Atlantique UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"Likely Explanations How Strange Loire Atlantique Sightings Found Ordinary Explanations Loire Atlantique UFOs Likely Explanations Likely Explanations How Strange Loire Atlantique Sightings Found Ordinary Explanations Later reviews show how model aircraft, stars, lanterns and distant lights can produce vivid reports of structured or pursuing objects. Later reviews show how model aircraft, stars, lanterns and distant lights can produce vivid reports of structured or pursuing objects. mechanism The Le Pellerin object that seemed to follow a car How darkness and motion distort distance and speed Why later reviews can overturn unexplained classifications loire atlantique likely explanations Introduction Some of Loire Atlantique ’s most striking UFO reports did not fade because witnesses withdrew their stories. They became less mysterious because investigators reconstructed where people were standing, what direction they were looking, how clouds and darkness altered the scene, and which ordinary lights were actually present. A supposed object following a car near Le Pellerin was eventually judged likely to have been an illuminated model aircraft. A triangular formation over Nantes matched the Hyades star cluster. A huge red orange form over the coast proved consistent with the Moon rising through cloud. Other reports were traced to Thai lanterns or powerful lights on a working ship. These cases matter because they show how a witness can accurately describe a vivid experience while misjudging its distance, scale or movement. They also demonstrate why an “unexplained” classification is not permanent: later mapping tools, astronomical software, weather data and traffic records can turn an extraordinary looking report into an ordinary object. The Le Pellerin object that seemed to follow a car The department’s clearest example of apparent pursuit occurred near Le Pellerin on 11 June 1994. At about 11.45 p.m., a woman driving along a small country road noticed something moving beside her at what appeared to be very low altitude. She described a lemon shaped object covered in flashing, multicoloured lights, with what looked like a cable or projection on top. It disappeared from view, reappeared and seemed to keep pace with her car before vanishing rapidly. The original case was classified as unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPELLERIN (LE) (44) 11.06.1994 GEIPAN... That description sounds more elaborate than a distant point of light. Yet the later GEIPAN review found that the observation was directed mainly towards an authorised flying area belonging to a nearby model aircraft club. The object’s low apparent elevation suited model aircraft operations, while its coloured flashing lights broadly resembled aviation navigation and anti collision lighting. The witness was also viewing it at night from inside a moving car with the windows closed, circumstances that made both its outline and any engine noise difficult to recognise. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPELLERIN (LE) (44) 11.06.1994 GEIPAN... The investigation did not establish the identity of a particular model or pilot. GEIPAN therefore stopped short of calling the identification certain. Instead, it reclassified the case as category B: probably explained by a model aircraft or model helicopter. That distinction is important. The conclusion rests on a close fit between the location, lighting, apparent altitude and flight behaviour, rather than on a recovered object or a flight log. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPELLERIN (LE) (44) 11.06.1994 GEIPAN... Several apparently exotic details could also be reproduced without invoking an unusual craft. The “cable” may have been part of the model’s tail or structure briefly illuminated by its lights. Its apparent splitting into two forms could have resulted from a change in orientation, with a dimmer section of the fuselage becoming visible, or from partial obstruction by vegetation. A sudden disappearance behind trees could easily look like instant acceleration into the distance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPELLERIN (LE) (44) 11.06.1994 GEIPAN... Most significant was the impression that the object deliberately followed the car. GEIPAN linked this to a familiar “following light” effect. A distant light whose direction changes little relative to the observer can seem to travel alongside a moving vehicle. Because its true distance is unknown, the witness may interpret changes in brightness, orientation or visibility as forward movement, retreat or evasive manoeuvres. In the Le Pellerin case, the nearby road, hedges and trees supplied an apparent foreground trajectory even though there was no reliable way to place the light among those features. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPELLERIN (LE) (44) 11.06.1994 GEIPAN... How darkness creates speed, size and pursuit Night observations are difficult not because witnesses are necessarily inattentive, but because many of the cues used to judge distance disappear. A small bright object nearby and a powerful light many kilometres away can occupy the same angle in the observer’s vision. Unless the object passes in front of a feature at a known distance, its physical size and speed cannot be calculated reliably. GEIPAN explicitly separates what was perceived from the interpretation added to it. Investigators consider short term visual effects, uncertain reconstruction of paths, assumptions about distance and speed, emotional response and later memory. The service notes that an ordinary stimulus can acquire additional strangeness through apparent motion, cloud movement or the absence of a recognisable shape. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Several mechanisms recur in the Loire Atlantique files: Relative motion: a distant light can appear to accompany a moving car because its bearing changes slowly. Loss of brightness: a lantern, light or partly obscured astronomical object may seem to shoot away when it dims or disappears. Moving clouds: stars or the Moon can appear to move when clouds drift across them. Poor angular estimates: witnesses commonly report metres, kilometres or aircraft like dimensions when only an apparent size in the sky was actually available. Foreground association: a light beyond trees, buildings, turbines or a coastline can be mistakenly placed among them rather than far behind them. These are not claims that observers imagined the light itself. They explain how a real light can acquire a false trajectory, structure or sense of intention. Cette nuit là, ils ont TOUS vu des OVNIS Channel: 100% Docs Crimes & Faits Divers Open on YouTube Stars that formed a triangular object over Nantes Shortly after midnight on 20 October 2013, two people walking in Nantes noticed a group of evenly spaced lights forming what looked like a triangle. The arrangement suggested a structured object rather than random stars, and cloud movement may have strengthened the impression that the formation itself was moving. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 20.10.2013 GEIPAN... GEIPAN reconstructed the viewing direction and found that the pattern matched the Hyades, the prominent V shaped star cluster near Aldebaran. The lights occupied the expected positions, while the single orange toned point corresponded to the appearance of Aldebaran beside the cluster. The case was classified A, meaning that GEIPAN considered the astronomical identification certain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 20.10.2013 GEIPAN... This case illustrates how several independent lights can be perceived as one object. Human vision naturally groups points into shapes, particularly when they have roughly regular spacing. Once the lights are understood as the corners or illuminated parts of a dark body, the empty sky between them becomes the imagined surface of the object. Clouds then appear to pass behind or across that supposed body, although the stars themselves remain fixed. GEIPAN did not describe the witnesses as having seen the stars incorrectly. Its conclusion was that they interpreted a genuine celestial pattern under conditions of surprise, tiredness and uncertain cloud movement. That distinction preserves the value of the testimony while explaining why its initial meaning was misleading. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNANTES (44) 20.10.2013 GEIPAN... When the Moon looked enormous and moved along the coast An older coastal case shows how strongly atmosphere and geography can transform a familiar object. On 16 July 1979, two witnesses at Le Pouliguen reported a very large red orange and yellow luminous form above Pornichet. It appeared stationary at first, then seemed to move towards Saint Nazaire while its surrounding halo gradually diminished. The report was originally treated as unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPOULIGUEN (LE) (44) 16.07.1979 GEIPAN... A later review placed the Moon low above Pornichet in precisely the relevant direction. At an elevation of only"},{"title":"Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation?","url":"/which-loire-ufo-stories-survived/","description":"Loire has a documented UFO history, but it is not built around a single compelling encounter, radar incident or military interception.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation? Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation? Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation? Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation? Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation? Loire has a documented UFO history, but it is not built around a single compelling encounter, radar incident or military interception. Loire has a documented UFO history, but it is not built around a single compelling encounter, radar incident or military interception. Its most useful cases instead show how unusual lights acquire extraordinary interpretations and how later investigation can reduce their mystery. loire Introduction The central finding is therefore restrained. Loire has produced sincere and sometimes striking reports, yet the accessible official files do not currently reveal a strong unexplained case supported by several independent witnesses, reliable imagery, radar data or physical traces. Some reports remain unidentified, but mainly because vital details were never recorded. Others became less mysterious once investigators checked aircraft routes, wind direction, astronomy or familiar airborne objects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The official record is small and mostly conventional France’s official body for investigating unusual aerial reports is GEIPAN, a technical unit of the national space agency CNES. It receives first hand testimony, compares observations with known aerospace, astronomical, meteorological and perceptual phenomena, anonymises its files and publishes conclusions. Its role is not to prove or disprove extraterrestrial visitation, but to determine what the available evidence can reasonably support. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN’s classifications are essential for reading the Loire cases correctly: A means that the phenomenon has been identified. B means that an explanation is considered probable. C means that identification is impossible because the information is insufficient or unreliable. D means that a sufficiently documented phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. A C case is therefore not stronger than an A or B case. It is usually weaker because investigators lack the measurements, corroboration or records needed to test possible explanations. GEIPAN assesses both residual strangeness and “consistency”, meaning the amount and reliability of the available information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The department’s published cases span Roanne, Le Coteau, Villemontais, Ambierle, Saint Chamond, Firminy, Saint Haon le Châtel, Saint Just Saint Rambert, Noirétable, Rive de Gier, Chalmazel, Montbrison, La Talaudière and Pralong. The pattern is notable: the visible record is dominated by A, B and C classifications rather than a well supported D case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr PRALONG (42) 29.07.2023 29/07/2023 A Loire Lanterne thai 22/10/2024 2023 07 29... ROANNE (42) 29.03.1976 29/03/1976 C ... Published: July 29, 2023 The 1954 stories are more folklore than firm evidence Loire was not untouched by France’s famous flying saucer wave of autumn 1954. Later catalogues associate the department with supposed sightings around Feurs, Craintilleux, Saint Étienne and Saint Chamond. These stories give Loire a place in the cultural history of the national wave, but their evidential quality varies from poor to openly doubtful. The most colourful account concerns Craintilleux, near Saint Étienne. Newspaper retellings described a giant double faced figure, compared with Hitler, apparently entering a saucer without opening a door. The witnesses were said to be performers or writers, and even contemporary coverage treated the tale as absurd or possibly useful material for a sketch. Later UFO catalogues nevertheless detached the story from its mocking context and presented versions of it as a close encounter. One specialist reconstruction concludes that the Feurs or Craintilleux narrative was a press invention or joke that became more serious as it passed through later compilations. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. A second version placed a landed object near a broken down car in the Forez plain on the night of 15–16 September 1954. Several people from Saint Étienne supposedly saw a small being flee when approached. Yet the surviving chain appears to depend on later summaries of newspaper material rather than a complete police file, signed contemporary testimony or trace evidence. Even researchers who repeated the account noted uncertainty about its details, while later critics connected it to the same journalistic fabrication surrounding the Craintilleux story. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This does not make every Loire report from 1954 false. It means that the most dramatic local narrative cannot safely be separated from its original humorous and sceptical framing. The case is historically valuable because it shows how a joke, ambiguous report or sensational article can become a seemingly factual “landing case” when copied into catalogues without its source context. Saint Haon le Châtel lost its unexplained status The most important Loire case for understanding later reassessment occurred near Saint Haon le Châtel during the early hours of 21 May 2004. Two witnesses reported several coloured lights surrounding what appeared to be a dark triangular mass. They described varied movement, a low passage and a dull engine like noise before the phenomenon departed towards the mountains. The case was once classified as unexplained, making it one of the department’s more prominent claims. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 GEIPAN later reopened the file and changed the classification to B: a probable light aircraft. Several details supported that conclusion. The observation lay near the axis of Roanne Airport, which permits night visual flying; the object travelled towards other airfields beyond the mountains; the witnesses heard a sound resembling an aircraft or helicopter; and the reported red, green and yellow lights were compatible with aviation lighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 The investigators also identified weaknesses that prevented a firmer reconstruction. No precise angular sizes or elevations were recorded, and the exact arrangement of the lights was unclear. Some reported movements could have resulted from the apparent wandering of a small light stared at against a dark background. GEIPAN further noted that an extreme interpretation formed early in the sighting may have affected later perception or memory. The witnesses had spoken to a UFO association and the press before being interviewed by the gendarmerie, creating additional opportunities for the story to become fixed before formal questioning. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 The case remains interesting because the witnesses apparently saw something real and unfamiliar, not because the final evidence points to an exotic craft. Its history shows that “unexplained” is not a permanent status. Better reconstruction, aviation context and closer attention to how testimony was obtained weakened the original claim. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Ordinary lights repeatedly looked extraordinary Several Loire cases illustrate how easily familiar objects can appear unconventional when distance and scale are unknown. At Saint Chamond on 13 August 1998, members of a family watched yellow, red and green points scintillating in the night sky. GEIPAN identified the source as Antares, a bright star whose light can flicker and change colour when viewed through turbulent air, especially at a low elevation. Multiple witnesses did not prevent the error because they were all looking from nearly the same place through the same atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT CHAMOND (42) 199822 Mar 2007 — SAINT CHAMOND (42) 1998; Date d'observation. 13/08/1998; Région. Rhône Alpes; Département... At Noirétable on 5 July 2010, a witness saw a light travelling steadily from west to east. GEIPAN judged the International Space Station to be the probable explanation. Satellites can be especially striking in dark rural skies because they move silently, follow a straight path and may fade abruptly when entering the Earth’s shadow. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open"},{"title":"1954 Stories","url":"/1954-stories/","description":"Loire's most dramatic 1954 encounters may owe more to jokes, press invention and later retelling than to reliable witness evidence.","breadcrumb":"Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation?","level":2,"text":"1954 Stories Did Loire's 1954 UFO Landing Really Happen? Loire UFOs 1954 Stories 1954 Stories Did Loire's 1954 UFO Landing Really Happen? Loire's most dramatic 1954 encounters may owe more to jokes, press invention and later retelling than to reliable witness evidence. Loire's most dramatic 1954 encounters may owe more to jokes, press invention and later retelling than to reliable witness evidence. event time window The Feurs and Craintilleux accounts How humorous reporting became UFO lore What evidence survives and what is missing loire 1954 stories Introduction The best known Loire “landing” story from France’s 1954 flying saucer wave almost certainly did not happen as later UFO catalogues described it. The surviving account concerns four theatre performers returning from Craintilleux on the night of 15–16 September. Their car lights failed, a glowing egg shaped craft supposedly landed near the Loire, and two men approached a towering, helmeted occupant with a Hitler like moustache and a second face on the back of its head. Those details are not the marks of a well documented encounter. They are the comic centrepiece of a newspaper story whose exaggerated language, theatrical witnesses and absurd ending strongly indicate parody or deliberate invention. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Frédéric Veldemant et Poletto, en débouchant dans la carrière, avaient presque failli se heurter à une forme haute, d'aspect plutôt menac... The case matters because later summaries stripped away much of that humour. Once reduced to a car stoppage, a landed object, several witnesses and a fleeing humanoid, the tale looked like a conventional close encounter. It then circulated under both Feurs and Craintilleux, acquiring a seriousness that its earliest surviving presentation did not deserve. The story is therefore less useful as evidence of an unexplained landing than as an example of how press entertainment can harden into UFO history. The Feurs and Craintilleux accounts The fullest surviving version was published by Le Charollais Dimanche on 19 September 1954. It named four people from Saint Étienne: an alleged actor, Frédéric Veldemant; his daughter Lisianne; his son in law Jo Poletto; and a companion named Arnaud. They were said to be driving back after their theatrical troupe had performed in Craintilleux. At about 12.15 am, near quarries between the village and a place rendered as Rivat, the vehicle’s lighting failed because of a bad electrical contact. Veldemant got out to make a repair. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Frédéric Veldemant et Poletto, en débouchant dans la carrière, avaient presque failli se heurter à une forme haute, d'aspect plutôt menac... He supposedly heard a low humming noise and saw a reddish object pass overhead before coming down roughly 200 metres away. The article alternated between describing it as an egg and as a cigar with cut off ends. It was estimated at about ten metres long and three metres wide, although the night was dark and the witnesses were initially hundreds of metres away. Its sound was likened to the purring of a young cat. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Frédéric Veldemant et Poletto, en débouchant dans la carrière, avaient presque failli se heurter à une forme haute, d'aspect plutôt menac... Veldemant and Poletto then walked towards the object with a torch. They allegedly encountered a figure more than two metres tall, wearing something like a diving suit and a faceted helmet. When illuminated, its face resembled Adolf Hitler, complete with a small moustache and an angry expression. When it turned away, the men discovered another, smiling face on the back of its head. The two faced being then moved towards the closed craft, apparently entering it without any visible door. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... Nothing in the surviving text resembles a careful witness deposition. The narration is florid, repeatedly pauses to heighten suspense and openly plays with familiar science fiction imagery. Even the witnesses’ profession is part of the joke: the people reporting the most theatrical Loire encounter of 1954 were themselves presented as actors. A later newspaper discussion, published by L’Est Républicain on 5 October, treated the Craintilleux episode as too childish to accept, remarking that its actor authors had at least obtained material for a sketch or novel. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... The geographical labels later became confused. The original narrative placed the journey around Craintilleux and the nearby Loire plain, while later UFO lists commonly filed the event as “Feurs”. Feurs lies in the Forez area, but it is not simply another name for Craintilleux. This shifting location is a warning that catalogue entries were being copied and compressed rather than reconstructed from a stable body of testimony. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... How humorous reporting became UFO lore The transformation began when later compilers extracted the basic incident structure and dropped the elements that made its doubtful character obvious. A typical condensed version said that a group from Saint Étienne was stranded by a vehicle failure near Feurs, that an unidentified craft landed close by, and that a being fled when two witnesses tried to approach it. Another detail, concerning a nearby farmer’s wife who heard a strange hum and noticed an agitated dog, gave the summary an appearance of independent corroboration. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... In this shortened form, the story contained nearly every feature valued by older close encounter catalogues: several named or counted witnesses; a vehicle fault; a low or landed object; an occupant; an apparent reaction from an animal; and a precise date during the 1954 national wave. Yet most of those points came from the same newspaper narrative. They were not separate layers of evidence. The farmer, the dog, the performers and the craft did not survive as independent statements, police interviews or documents from different sources. They survived as ingredients in one story. The effect of compression is especially clear in the treatment of the car. In the original version, the lights went out because of a “bad contact”, and Veldemant stopped to repair them. Later retellings turned this ordinary electrical fault into a vehicle interference motif, sometimes leaving open the possibility that the craft caused the breakdown. The chronology had not become stronger; the wording had merely become more suggestive. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Frédéric Veldemant et Poletto, en débouchant dans la carrière, avaient presque failli se heurter à une forme haute, d'aspect plutôt menac... The creature underwent the same transformation. Remove the Hitler moustache, the two faces and the impossible passage through a seamless hull, and what remains is simply “a small being” or “an entity” seen near a landed object. One later catalogue summary even described it as a little figure running away from the witnesses, substantially changing the original image of a figure said to be more than two metres tall. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... French researcher Michel Figuet eventually marked the case with a press credibility code meaning that it had been invented by the press. Other writers identified it as a hoax associated with performers and pointed to the openly comic presentation in Le Charollais Dimanche . Nevertheless, database and website entries continued to repeat the stripped down encounter, often citing later UFO books rather than the newspaper from which the story originated. [ufologie.patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 15 septembre, Feurs, Loire... This copying process explains why the episode could appear more impressive decades later than it did in October 1954. Contemporary readers were shown an angry Hitler faced giant with a cheerful second face. Later readers were offered a compact “humanoid landing case” with a stalled car and multiple witnesses. 5:29 Ancient Aliens: 300+ \"Flying Saucer\" Incidents in France (Season 19) History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 203.0K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube What survives and what is missing The case has a surviving period newspaper text, which is more than can be said for some alleged incidents from the 1954 wave. But a surviving article is evidence that a story was published, not proof that the described event occurred. In this instance, the"},{"title":"Official Findings","url":"/official-findings/","description":"GEIPAN files show how aircraft, stars and familiar airborne objects repeatedly acquired extraordinary interpretations across Loire.","breadcrumb":"Which Loire UFO Stories Survived Investigation?","level":2,"text":"Official Findings Why Loire's Strangest Lights Became Less Mysterious Official Findings Official Findings Why Loire's Strangest Lights Became Less Mysterious GEIPAN files show how aircraft, stars and familiar airborne objects repeatedly acquired extraordinary interpretations across Loire. GEIPAN files show how aircraft, stars and familiar airborne objects repeatedly acquired extraordinary interpretations across Loire. dataset evidence The Saint Haon triangle reassessment Stars, aircraft and other recurring explanations Why unidentified does not always mean unexplained loire official findings Introduction Official investigation made Loire ’s unusual lights less mysterious by testing witness descriptions against ordinary sources: aircraft lighting and routes, bright stars, the International Space Station, balloons, windborne lanterns and gaps in the original testimony. The clearest example is the 2004 Saint Haon le Châtel “black triangle”, once classed as unexplained but later reassessed as the probable sighting of a small aircraft. Its coloured lights, engine noise and flight path all fitted an aeronautical explanation better than an unknown craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 That does not mean every Loire report has been conclusively solved. Some explanations remain probable rather than proved, while other cases are unclassifiable because the photographs, timings or angular measurements are too poor. The important lesson from the department’s GEIPAN files is that “unidentified” can describe missing evidence, not an extraordinary object. Investigation repeatedly weakened the more dramatic interpretations by reconstructing what witnesses could actually have seen. The Saint Haon triangle reassessment Shortly after 1.30 a.m. on 21 May 2004, two witnesses near Saint Haon le Châtel reported a flashing light that appeared to change direction, approach them and resolve into several coloured lights surrounding a dark triangular mass. They heard a low engine sound and believed the phenomenon passed overhead at low altitude before travelling towards the mountains. The account reached a UFO association and the press before the witnesses were interviewed by the gendarmerie, helping the case acquire the qualities of a classic close range triangular object sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 The case was originally placed in GEIPAN’s unexplained category. A later review changed it to classification B: a phenomenon probably identified, in this instance as a light aircraft operating under visual flight rules at night. GEIPAN did not claim to have located an indisputable flight record for a particular aeroplane. Instead, it argued that several independent details converged on the same conventional explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 The witnesses were looking close to the axis of Roanne airport, which was authorised for night visual flying. The reported object travelled in the direction of other airfields beyond the mountains. Its dull engine sound resembled an aircraft or helicopter, while the red, green and yellow lights were compatible with aviation lighting. Seen against a dark sky, separated lights could also have encouraged the eye to construct a single solid shape between them, making an ordinary aeroplane appear to be a large black triangle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 Investigators also examined details that seemed harder to reconcile with an aircraft. The witnesses mentioned a possible blue light, changes in colour and irregular rising or descending movements. GEIPAN judged that none of these observations was strong enough to overturn the aircraft hypothesis. A plane could carry additional lights, while small apparent movements of an isolated night time light may be produced by involuntary eye motion. This effect can make a fixed or smoothly moving point appear to wander when there is no clear visual reference against which to judge it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 Just as important was what the file lacked. Investigators had no reliable angular measurements showing the object’s height above the horizon or apparent size. The precise positions of the coloured lights were not recorded, and parts of the testimony appeared inconsistent. GEIPAN therefore rated the case’s evidential consistency as low and acknowledged that it lay close to category C, meaning too poorly documented to identify. The B verdict was not “this aeroplane was proved beyond doubt”, but “an aeroplane is the most credible explanation supported by the surviving evidence”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.200421 May 2004 — SAINT HAON LE CHATEL (42) 21.05.2004; Date d'observation. 21/05/2004; Région. R... Published: May 2004 The reassessment matters within Loire’s UFO history because it reverses the usual storytelling direction. The case did not become more impressive as additional evidence emerged. Its extraordinary features became less secure when investigators compared them with the local aviation setting and examined how darkness, emotion and incomplete measurements could alter the witnesses’ interpretation. Stars, aircraft and other recurring explanations The Saint Haon investigation was not an isolated debunking exercise. Across Loire, GEIPAN’s published files show the same basic method applied to very different looking reports: establish the direction and duration, compare the object’s movement with wind or orbital data, examine photographs and ask whether the colour, flickering or apparent manoeuvres match a familiar source. A striking example occurred at Saint Chamond on 13 August 1998. Several members of one family saw scintillating points displaying yellow, red and green colours around midnight. GEIPAN identified the source as Antares and classified the case A, its category for an identified phenomenon. A bright star low enough in the sky can appear to flash or change colour because its light is being disturbed by layers of moving atmosphere. Without a visible horizon or nearby reference, the same point can also seem less stable than it really is. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT CHAMOND (42) 1998SAINT CHAMOND (42) 1998. Date d'observation. 13/08/1998. Région... 22/03/2007. Type de phénomène. Antares... The colours therefore did not require a multi light craft. They were part of the clue that led back to an astronomical source. This is a recurring difficulty in night sightings: witnesses understandably describe what appears to be happening, but apparent colour changes, motion and structure do not necessarily belong to a nearby physical object. At Noirétable on 5 July 2010, GEIPAN identified the International Space Station as the source of a report and classified it B. The station can look surprisingly bright as it crosses the sky in silence, then fade rapidly when it enters the Earth’s shadow. Its steady passage can seem unlike an ordinary aircraft because it has no flashing navigation lights and produces no audible engine noise. The Loire case index records the ISS conclusion as probable rather than certain, illustrating how orbital calculations can provide a strong match even when the original report does not contain enough detail for absolute identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Windborne objects account for another group of apparently purposeful movements. On 25 July 2009, witnesses at Saint Just Saint Rambert watched a silent white sphere move steadily through the valley before appearing to climb, accelerate and vanish. GEIPAN concluded that it was probably a balloon, possibly a weather balloon. Its slow, regular movement and lack of noise fitted a drifting object, while the apparent acceleration could be explained by its entering a faster air current or rising into the sky until it became difficult to see. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT JUST SAINT RAMBERT (42) 25.07.2009Type de phénomène. Ballon. Etrangeté. 0.38. Documents... Témoignages. Témoignages. SAINT J... A later Montbrison case provided better visual evidence. On 10 April 2022, a witness photographed a metallic looking object that moved slowly in a straight line, rotated irregularly and intermittently reflected sunlight. GEIPAN"},{"title":"Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-loiret-ufo-cases-still-resist/","description":"Loiret has a richer official record of unusual aerial reports than its quiet, largely rural image might suggest, but the evidence does not support a single...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Loiret has a richer official record of unusual aerial reports than its quiet, largely rural image might suggest, but the evidence does not support a single... Loiret has a richer official record of unusual aerial reports than its quiet, largely rural image might suggest, but the evidence does not support a single dramatic “UFO story”. loiret Introduction The most important incident occurred in January 1982, when two motorway gendarmes reported a pale airborne mass and patches of greenish light moving across the landscape beside the A10 north of Orléans. A later review by France’s official investigation service could not make the leading helicopter explanation fit all the evidence and retained the case as unexplained. Other prominent Loiret reports have fared less well: a red object seen near Saint Florent in 1979 was eventually identified as the setting Moon, while several recent reports have been attributed to aircraft, stars, distress flares or atmospheric re entry. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... The 1982 A10 case Shortly before 4.35 am on 23 January 1982, two gendarmes were returning along the A10 after reaching the limit of their motorway patrol area near Allaines. They first noticed an indistinct pale form in the sky. As their car continued southwards, they saw areas of the countryside illuminated to the east of the motorway, including fields, trees, farm buildings and part of an electricity pylon. One witness described the ground light as pale green; the other remembered it as grey white. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The observation continued for roughly 20 minutes and extended across nearly 30 kilometres of motorway. The witnesses stopped, switched off the engine and got out, but heard no unusual sound. Neither saw a conventional beam connecting the luminous mass to the illuminated ground. The second witness thought the aerial form remained stationary while the car’s movement made different parts of the landscape pass through the light; the first regarded the lighting as moving from place to place. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... This was not simply a story recorded years later. The gendarmerie opened a formal file, interviewed the men, reconstructed their route, photographed relevant locations and collected weather, astronomical and electricity line information. The case papers were forwarded to the Loiret prefect, military authorities and the Ministry of Defence. A contemporary article from the regional newspaper La République du Centre was also placed in the dossier. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A woman from Ingré had separately written to the local press about something unusual seen several days earlier. However, investigators did not treat this as a confirmed local “flap”. She was not formally interviewed, her account remained imprecise and the later GEIPAN review found no secure basis for connecting the two events. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Why the leading explanation fell short The most obvious conventional theory was a helicopter using a powerful searchlight. It could account for a vague airborne glow partly obscured by cloud, the absence of clearly visible navigation lights and the shifting illumination of fields, buildings and pylons. Sound can also be difficult to hear at a distance under some wind and atmospheric conditions, while a beam may be invisible from the side in clean air. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The difficulties were substantial. Meteorological records placed cloud layers at approximately 1,200 and 1,500 metres. A helicopter above or within that cloud would have required an unusually powerful light to illuminate the ground so clearly. GEIPAN’s review also noted that the narrow section of pylon apparently lit did not resemble the broad spread expected from a conventional airborne searchlight. Equipment capable of producing the required effect was not known to have been fitted to French civil or military helicopters in 1982, and no relevant flight from the nearby base could be established. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Astronomical confusion was also considered. Bright planets can appear to follow a moving car, particularly when seen intermittently through trees or cloud. Yet that cannot readily explain distinct areas of nearby terrain appearing illuminated. GEIPAN estimated the astronomical theory as very weak and the helicopter theory as possible but insufficiently probable. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The eventual judgement was cautious rather than sensational. Investigators considered the testimony reasonably consistent because there were two trained witnesses, a prompt gendarmerie inquiry, a route reconstruction and detailed supporting records. Against that, there were no photographs or video, no angular measurements, some differences between the witnesses and no later field interview conducted to modern standards. The review described the file as lying close to the boundary between unexplained and unusable, but retained its category D classification: unexplained in the available evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... “Unexplained” does not mean extraterrestrial. It means that the investigators could not demonstrate a sufficiently convincing conventional cause from the evidence preserved. The absence of radar confirmation, physical traces, independent roadside witnesses or imagery sharply limits what can now be concluded. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The triangular object near Orléans in 1980 Another striking Loiret record concerns an observation on 27 March 1980 near the Orléans district of La Source. At about 10 pm, a driver and passenger reported a dark triangular mass with flashing red and white lights at its corners. They estimated its span at roughly 50 metres and said it initially appeared stationary before banking, climbing and departing rapidly. The encounter was said to last four or five minutes, with no sound heard through the closed windows. [Geipan] geipan.fr LA SOURCE (45) 1980 GEIPAN... GEIPAN lists the case as category D because no satisfactory explanation was established. However, its evidential quality is weaker than that of the 1982 motorway incident. The public summary states that no further information was collected. There is no comparable reconstruction, technical analysis, radar record or body of independent documentation. The witnesses’ estimate of size and distance therefore cannot be checked, and the reported navigation like lights leave open an aviation explanation that the surviving file cannot test. [Geipan] geipan.fr LA SOURCE (45) 1980 GEIPAN... For Loiret’s UFO history, the case is memorable because its triangular description predates the widely publicised Belgian wave of 1989–91. That resemblance should not be overstated: similar shapes can arise when observers mentally join several lights against a dark sky, and a matching description does not establish a common type of object. The Saint Florent sighting that became the Moon The Saint Florent case of 30 June 1979 is the clearest local example of how a dramatic report can be resolved decades later. Four people travelling on the D54 towards Villemurlin saw a red phenomenon apparently descending towards the road. Their descriptions varied between a banana shape and a teardrop. Frightened, they turned back and reported the sighting to the gendarmerie within minutes. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... Gendarmes visited the location that night and returned the next day. They found a recently burned patch of brushwood, prompting an early suggestion that glowing embers or a small whirlwind of sparks had caused the sighting. That theory matched the red colour and approximate direction but did not fit the reported appearance and apparent movement particularly well. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... A later GEIPAN reassessment used astronomical software to reconstruct the sky. The Moon was setting almost exactly along the road’s axis, reddish near the horizon and presenting a crescent compatible with the witnesses’ descriptions. Its apparent descent and sideways movement were consistent with observation from a moving car. GEIPAN therefore reclassified the event as category A, an identified setting Moon. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979"},{"title":"A10 Case","url":"/a10-case/","description":"Two motorway gendarmes watched unusual light effects for nearly 30 kilometres, yet the helicopter theory never fully matched the record.","breadcrumb":"Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"A10 Case Why the 1982 A10 Sighting Remains Unresolved Loiret A10 Case A10 Case Why the 1982 A10 Sighting Remains Unresolved Two motorway gendarmes watched unusual light effects for nearly 30 kilometres, yet the helicopter theory never fully matched the record. Two motorway gendarmes watched unusual light effects for nearly 30 kilometres, yet the helicopter theory never fully matched the record. event time window What the gendarmes reported along the motorway How investigators tested helicopters and astronomy Why category D does not mean extraterrestrial loiret a10 case Introduction The A10 sighting of 23 January 1982 remains the strongest officially documented unexplained aerial case associated with Loiret . Two motorway gendarmes travelling south towards Orléans watched an indistinct pale form in the sky and successive areas of countryside apparently illuminated beside the road. Their observation lasted about 20 minutes and unfolded over nearly 30 kilometres, continuing from the Eure et Loir side of the departmental boundary into Loiret. The case matters not because the witnesses proved that they saw an extraordinary craft, but because an unusually detailed contemporary file survived. Investigators reconstructed the route, photographed locations, checked weather and astronomical conditions, contacted the nearby Orléans Bricy air base and examined the possibility of a helicopter carrying a searchlight. That explanation could reproduce parts of the account, but it required equipment, altitude and operating circumstances that did not fit comfortably together. GEIPAN therefore classified the report as unexplained while explicitly acknowledging that the missing measurements and the passage of time prevent a firm conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... What the gendarmes reported along the motorway The encounter began at about 4.35 am after the patrol had turned around near the limit of its motorway sector and was heading south on the A10. The driver first noticed a poorly defined pale mass ahead, high in the windscreen and approximately aligned with the motorway. Unsure of what he was seeing, he asked his passenger, who confirmed that he could also see an unusual form. The passenger described it as roughly cone like, with its narrower end pointing upwards, although neither man could estimate its altitude or give it a sharp outline. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Near the Allaines toll area, the driver then saw a bright patch illuminating trees close to the road. His first thought was practical rather than exotic: a vehicle might have overturned in a ditch. When the patrol reached the location, there was no accident and the illumination had gone. Farther south, another area of ground appeared lit several hundred metres east of the motorway. The driver described this illumination as pale green, while the passenger remembered a grey white or light grey glow. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The most unusual detail concerned electricity pylons and farm buildings. The driver said that part of a high voltage pylon became sharply visible, including its metal crosspieces and cables, while adjacent parts remained dark. He believed the illuminated section was oblique, suggesting light arriving from above. Later, hangars, trees and further stretches of ground appeared in the lit area. Yet neither witness saw a conventional beam linking the pale aerial form to the landscape. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Their descriptions were broadly compatible but not identical. The driver thought the pale mass and ground illumination moved with the patrol. When he stopped near kilometre point 69, he said the phenomenon also appeared to stop; after the car restarted, it seemed to move again. The passenger offered a more cautious interpretation. He suspected that the luminous area might have remained stationary while the changing viewpoint from the moving car caused different fields, pylons and buildings to pass through it. This disagreement is important because it leaves open whether the apparent pursuit was a real movement or a perspective effect. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... At one stage the men pulled over, stopped the engine and got out. The driver directed a powerful torch towards the pale mass but saw nothing further. Neither man heard an unusual noise. The observation was intermittent rather than continuous, however: they were travelling in a vehicle, their attention was divided, and the aerial form was lost from view during parts of the route. The later reconstruction divided the incident into ten phases extending from around kilometre point 61 to the Saran Ormes area, a journey of roughly 29 to 32 kilometres depending on which end point is used. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Why the record is stronger than an ordinary sighting Many historical UFO reports depend on a single retrospective story. The A10 case is different because the witnesses gave formal statements within days, while the landscape and their memories were still comparatively fresh. Gendarmes returned to the route, matched observations to kilometre points and produced photographs, maps and a sketch of the illuminated pylon. The file also incorporated weather information, astronomical data, motorway records, electricity line details and a contemporary local newspaper article. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The witnesses also had some practical advantages. They were accustomed to motorway patrol work, road accidents and night time visual conditions. The driver did not begin with an extraterrestrial interpretation: he initially treated the first ground illumination as a possible traffic emergency. The passenger stated that he had little particular interest in UFOs beyond ordinary media coverage. These points do not guarantee accurate perception, but they reduce the likelihood that the account was consciously constructed to match a preferred UFO narrative. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... Their evidence nevertheless has clear limits: There were no photographs, films, instrument readings or independent radar data. Neither witness measured angles, altitude, apparent size or the width of the illuminated areas. The two men disagreed over the colour of the light and whether the phenomenon itself moved. Almost all of the account was formed while they were travelling at night in a moving car. No toll worker, motorway employee or resident along the route supplied a confirmed matching observation. These omissions matter because a detailed verbal account can still preserve a mistaken interpretation. A reconstruction can show where witnesses believed events occurred, but without angular measurements it cannot securely determine the position, height or true movement of the source. GEIPAN judged the file’s consistency to be good, especially because it involved two witnesses and a prompt official inquiry, while also criticising the lack of angular data and original imagery. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... A woman from Ingré had separately written to the local press about an unusual observation made six days earlier. The original investigators included her letter in the dossier, but she was not formally interviewed and her account remained imprecise. The later review therefore declined to connect it to the motorway event. The surviving evidence supports one well recorded encounter, not a demonstrated regional wave. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The Helicopter: Everything You Need to Know Channel: Megaprojects Open on YouTube How astronomy was tested and rejected An astronomical source was worth considering because distant bright objects can appear to accompany a moving vehicle. This familiar perspective effect, sometimes called the “following light” illusion, occurs because a planet or star is so far away that its direction changes very little as the observer travels. The passenger’s impression that the aerial form and illuminated zone may have been stationary made this possibility relevant. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word CR AC JPA MPD.docx... The sky check did not produce a convincing match. The Moon was not above the horizon, although Jupiter, Saturn and Mars were present. GEIPAN considered it unlikely that a planet, normally point like to the unaided eye, would be perceived as an extended pale mass through substantial cloud. More decisively, an astronomical object could not account for the reported illumination of nearby trees, hangars and selected sections of pylons. The review assigned the planetary explanation a very low indicative probability of 10 per cent. [Geipan] geipan.fr"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-a74976/","description":"The setting Moon, aircraft, stars, flares and re-entry show how sincere witnesses can misread striking lights in difficult conditions.","breadcrumb":"Which Loiret UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases How Loiret's Strangest Sightings Were Explained Loiret UFOs Explained Cases Explained Cases How Loiret's Strangest Sightings Were Explained The setting Moon, aircraft, stars, flares and re entry show how sincere witnesses can misread striking lights in difficult conditions. The setting Moon, aircraft, stars, flares and re entry show how sincere witnesses can misread striking lights in difficult conditions. mechanism The Saint Florent object that proved to be the Moon Why movement, darkness and fear distort interpretation What explained cases reveal about unresolved reports loiret explained cases Introduction Some of Loiret ’s most dramatic aerial reports did not collapse because witnesses were dishonest or careless. They gained ordinary explanations when investigators reconstructed where the observer was looking, how the observer and light were moving, what the weather allowed them to see and which astronomical or aviation objects were present at the time. The clearest example is the red object reported near Saint Florent in June 1979. Four frightened witnesses believed that a strangely shaped light was descending towards the road. A later review found that its colour, position, shape and apparent descent matched the setting Moon. Other Loiret cases have been attributed to military aircraft, distress flares, balloons and probable atmospheric re entry. Together, they show why a sincere description can be accurate in its basic details yet mistaken in its interpretation. They also provide a useful standard for judging the department’s genuinely unresolved reports: an explanation must fit the complete observation, not merely resemble one part of it. The Saint Florent object that proved to be the Moon At about 12.30 am on 30 June 1979, four people were travelling along the D54 between Saint Florent and Villemurlin when they noticed a red phenomenon ahead of them. Different witnesses described it as a banana shape with upturned ends or as a drop. It appeared to descend towards the road and then move slowly. No engine noise or flashing navigation lights were reported. Alarmed, the group turned back towards Coullons and contacted the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... The initial investigation appeared to uncover a suggestive physical clue. At the apparent location of the phenomenon, gendarmes found the remains of a brushwood fire, still warm, which farmers had lit two days earlier. They considered whether glowing embers lifted by the air might have produced the red, pear shaped light. Yet that theory did not fit the observation particularly well. A nearby fire could explain colour and perhaps shape, but not convincingly reproduce the object’s reported position and slow apparent movement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... The stronger explanation emerged when the old case was reconstructed with improved astronomical software. The Moon was setting almost exactly along the direction of the road, at an azimuth of about 281 degrees. Its low position explained why the witnesses saw the light ahead rather than high in the sky. Its reddened appearance was normal for a Moon close to the horizon, where its light passes through more atmosphere. The visible crescent also matched the witnesses’ banana like outline. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... The reported “descent” was especially revealing. A celestial object nearing the horizon genuinely moves downwards over time, but the witnesses interpreted that slow astronomical motion as a nearby object approaching the landscape. Trees beside the road then hid the Moon, creating the impression that the phenomenon had disappeared behind local vegetation. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that examines unusual aerospace reports, ultimately classified the case as a definite misidentification of the setting Moon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... This finding does not mean that the witnesses invented the shape, colour or movement. On the contrary, several of those details were real. The error lay in distance and identity. A familiar object hundreds of thousands of kilometres away was mentally placed near the road because darkness provided few reliable depth cues. Why movement, darkness and fear distort interpretation Many explained Loiret cases occurred while the witness was driving. That matters because the observer’s own motion alters the apparent motion of lights outside the vehicle. A distant object may seem to follow the road, stop when the car stops or accelerate when its viewing angle changes. Rain, windscreens, reflections and roadside trees further interrupt the view. The Saint Florent witnesses were travelling at night, became frightened and made decisions while trying to understand an unfamiliar looking light. GEIPAN’s reassessment explicitly distinguished between seeing and interpretation: the witnesses’ visual report was not necessarily defective, but fatigue, driving and fear influenced what they thought the light represented. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT FLORENT (45) 30.06.1979 GEIPAN... A later A19 sighting illustrates the same mechanism with an aircraft. At about 9.30 pm on 15 February 2018, a driver between Beaune la Rolande and Chilleurs aux Bois reported a triangular form with red, white and apparently pale blue lights. The object seemed to make two unusual turns, cross the motorway and then travel alongside it before disappearing into cloud. The witness opened a window but heard no engine noise. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[A19] de BEAUNE LA ROLANDE (45) vers CHILLEURS AUX BOIS (45) 15.02.2018 GEIPAN... Those details sounded stranger when described as the behaviour of an unknown object than when reconstructed as the view of a conventional aircraft from a moving car. The witness was driving at roughly 130 kilometres per hour, in darkness and active rain, with the windscreen wipers operating. He was also overtaking another vehicle during part of the sighting. Such conditions made it difficult to judge shape, distance and sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[A19] de BEAUNE LA ROLANDE (45) vers CHILLEURS AUX BOIS (45) 15.02.2018 GEIPAN... GEIPAN found that the light colours corresponded broadly to aviation lighting and that the object’s final direction pointed towards Orléans Bricy air base, about 30 to 40 kilometres away. The changing apparent speed could be explained by the aircraft turning while the observer’s car also moved. A slow moving military transport aircraft, possibly a C 130 Hercules, was therefore considered the probable source. The investigation could not confirm a particular flight because too much time had passed, so the case was placed in category B—probably explained—rather than treated as certain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[A19] de BEAUNE LA ROLANDE (45) vers CHILLEURS AUX BOIS (45) 15.02.2018 GEIPAN... That distinction is important. An ordinary explanation can be strong without being complete. The investigators acknowledged a small mismatch concerning the reported number of red lights. They nevertheless judged the aircraft theory to fit the overall combination of direction, lighting, movement, weather and nearby military activity much better than an unknown craft. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube When a triangle was really an A400M A more conclusive aircraft identification followed a sighting on 21 October 2021. A motorist travelling from Chevilly towards Sougy noticed a small green light moving slowly at low apparent altitude. It then seemed to accelerate suddenly. The witness stopped, left the vehicle and obtained a photograph, but described the object as silent and apparently triangular. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D6] DE CHEVILLY (45) VERS SOUGY (45) 21.10.2021 GEIPAN... Investigators traced the report to a military Airbus A400M associated with Orléans Bricy air base. Its navigation lights matched the reported colours, and its position was consistent with an aircraft approaching the base after low altitude training. The sudden acceleration was apparent rather than real: as the aircraft turned, its motion became more sideways across the witness’s field of view, making its angular speed seem greater. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D6] DE CHEVILLY (45) VERS SOUGY (45) 21.10.2021 GEIPAN... The estimate of distance had been far more dramatic than the reconstruction. The witness initially thought the object might have been only about 30 metres away. GEIPAN calculated that it was probably around 3.6 kilometres distant. At that range, an aircraft can appear compact and its structural details can disappear into the dark, leaving only a simple shape inferred from the arrangement of lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D6] DE CHEVILLY (45) VERS SOUGY (45)"},{"title":"Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-lot-et-garonne-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Lot-et-Garonne has a genuine but modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Lot et Garonne has a genuine but modest UFO history. Lot et Garonne has a genuine but modest UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by radar interceptions, military alerts or mass sightings; instead, it consists mainly of isolated witness reports, a handful of cases from the 1970s, and modern observations that were often traced to stars, planets, balloons, lanterns or spacecraft. lot et garonne Introduction The department is nevertheless useful as a small scale study of how UFO stories develop. Newspaper accounts can make an encounter sound decisive, while official case files often reveal missing measurements, uncertain distances and plausible conventional explanations. Lot et Garonne therefore matters less for one conclusive mystery than for the contrast between memorable testimony and the limited evidence available to test it. The official record is mostly explained or incomplete France has maintained an unusual degree of official openness about unidentified aerial reports. The National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES, created its first dedicated investigation group in 1977. Its present service, GEIPAN, collects testimony, consults bodies including the police, gendarmerie, aviation authorities, military and meteorological specialists, and publishes case summaries and supporting documents. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... GEIPAN’s categories are important when reading the Lot et Garonne files: A means that an explanation is established with high confidence. B means that a conventional explanation is considered probable. C means that the information is too weak or incomplete for a sound conclusion. D , now divided into more precise subcategories, denotes a sufficiently well documented observation that remains unexplained. An unexplained classification does not mean that an alien vehicle has been identified. It means that investigators could not match the reported details confidently to a known phenomenon. GEIPAN explicitly weighs both “strangeness” and “consistency”: an unusual description counts for little if the observation is poorly recorded or rests on unreliable data. Cases with insufficient information are placed in category C rather than treated as mysteries. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The published Lot et Garonne catalogue illustrates this distinction. It includes reports attributed to Sirius, Venus, a Thai lantern, a balloon, a laser and the International Space Station, alongside several category C cases and the unresolved Casteljaloux and Montayral observations. The departmental list therefore points towards a mixed record in which ordinary astronomical and human made objects account for much of the reported activity. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANMONTAYRAL (47) 23.10.2015, 23/10/2015, D1, Lot et Garonne, Phénomène étrange de consistance moyenne ou for... The 1954 wave reached the department, but the sources are fragile France experienced an exceptional burst of “flying saucer” reporting in the autumn of 1954. Lot et Garonne appears in contemporary newspaper derived catalogues several times. Reports include a bluish, phosphorescent disc at Sainte Bazeille on 25 September, a high altitude “cigar” apparently following the Garonne near Marmande on 28 September, and luminous globes around Bournel and Montpezat d’Agenais in early October. A very brief catalogue entry also records an observation at Puymirol on 11 November. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 25...The national newspaper Paris Presse, reported observations on page 6 for Septe... These reports establish that the national wave had a local expression, but they are generally too thin for modern investigation. Many survive through short newspaper notices, later catalogues or references to publications that are difficult to verify in full. Exact viewing directions, durations, weather conditions and angular sizes are often missing. That makes it impossible to test aircraft, astronomical objects, meteors or perceptual effects with much confidence. The most colourful 1954 story concerns a fisherman near Villeneuve sur Lot who supposedly saw a saucer descend silently onto the River Lot and a small occupant emerge to collect water in a bottle. The available retelling says it originated in a regional newspaper, but the chain of transmission is uncertain and the account lacks a surviving official investigation, physical evidence and independent witnesses. It belongs to the folklore of the 1954 wave, but it is considerably weaker than a case supported by original statements or contemporary police records. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org 1954 10 france villeneuvesurlot 1954 10 france villeneuvesurlot Researchers can consult digitised newspaper holdings through the Bibliothèque nationale de France’s local press portal, which lists historical titles for the districts of Agen, Marmande, Villeneuve sur Lot and Nérac. That archival route is more reliable than treating later UFO catalogues as equivalent to original reporting, although not every relevant issue or article is necessarily available online. [presselocaleancienne.bnf.fr] presselocaleancienne.bnf.fr Villes, villages de Lot et Garonne Villes, villages de Lot et Garonne 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lachapelle in 1971: a powerful story with an uncertain paper trail The best known popular account from Lot et Garonne concerns farmer Jean Cellot at Lachapelle on 19 November 1971. According to a retrospective article published by La Dépêche in 1999, Cellot was ploughing after dark when an intense light illuminated his tractor and the surrounding field “like daylight”. He described a silent object resembling two saucers placed together, with several circular openings underneath. It allegedly hovered briefly, moved away slowly and then disappeared rapidly. Two other farmers were said to have seen it approach the field. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr 241886 ovni en lot et garonne 241886 ovni en lot et garonne The account is memorable because it combines close proximity, structured form, powerful illumination and multiple claimed observers. The article also says that the gendarmerie and CNES investigated. Yet the publicly searchable GEIPAN material reviewed for the department does not present a corresponding Lachapelle case file, and the newspaper story was written nearly three decades after the event. Its descriptions may derive from earlier testimony, but the article does not supply the original statements, precise witness locations, meteorological analysis or an accessible official conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN That does not demonstrate that the witnesses invented the event. It means that the strongest version of the story cannot presently be checked against the primary investigative material that would matter most. Claims that it was one of the department’s most serious officially accepted manifestations should therefore be treated cautiously. The case is locally important as oral and press history, but evidentially it remains less secure than its vivid narrative suggests. The unresolved Casteljaloux case On 23 October 1978, at about 8 p.m., a witness near Casteljaloux reported watching a round, luminous object for several minutes. The object was described as orange, sparkling and silent, with apparent changes in speed and direction. The witness also reported seeing a ball of fire emerge from it before it suddenly disappeared. A dog accompanying the witness was said to be frightened. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978CASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978; Observation date. 23/10/1978; region. Aquitaine; Department. Lot et Ga... GEIPAN retains the case as category D, indicating that it was considered both sufficiently strange and sufficiently substantial to remain unexplained under the classification then used. This gives Casteljaloux a stronger official status than the department’s category C reports. Its limitations are still considerable. The gendarmerie found no second witness to corroborate the movements, emitted object or animal reaction. No photograph, instrument reading, radar trace or physical effect is listed in the public summary. Distance and size could not be measured independently, making reported acceleration difficult to distinguish from the apparent motion of a light whose range was unknown. The result is a legitimate unresolved report, but one resting on a single perceptual account"},{"title":"Historic Sightings","url":"/historic-sightings/","description":"The 1954 reports and the vivid Lachapelle encounter reveal how memorable local stories can outgrow the surviving evidence.","breadcrumb":"Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Historic Sightings How Strong Are Lot et Garonne's Famous Historic Sightings? Lot et Garonne UFOs Historic Sightings Historic Sightings How Strong Are Lot et Garonne's Famous Historic Sightings? The 1954 reports and the vivid Lachapelle encounter reveal how memorable local stories can outgrow the surviving evidence. The 1954 reports and the vivid Lachapelle encounter reveal how memorable local stories can outgrow the surviving evidence. event time window How the 1954 national wave appeared locally The Lachapelle tractor encounter and its missing paper trail What newspapers, archives and later retellings can prove lot et garonne historic sightings Introduction Lot et Garonne ’s best known historic UFO stories are memorable, but not equally well supported. The local reports from the autumn 1954 French “flying saucer wave” are mostly brief newspaper items with too little observational detail for confident reconstruction. The celebrated Lachapelle encounter of 19 November 1971 is richer and more dramatic: farmer Jean Cellot reportedly saw a brilliantly illuminated, disc like object hover over his tractor. Yet its surviving public paper trail is surprisingly weak, and later accounts contain an important discrepancy about the time of the event. The fairest assessment is therefore cautious. The 1954 reports show that the national wave reached Lot et Garonne, but most are historical leads rather than robust cases. Lachapelle rests on a named witness and a detailed narrative, but the readily accessible evidence does not include the original gendarmerie statement, contemporary scientific analysis, photographs, measurements or a published CNES case file. Later reporting preserved the story more effectively than it verified it. How the 1954 wave appeared locally France experienced an exceptional concentration of flying saucer reports during September and October 1954. The phenomenon was both observational and cultural: newspapers were already accustomed to presenting mysterious lights, discs, cigars and alleged landings as a recognisable category of news. The National Library of France’s RetroNews history of the subject shows how rapidly the “flying saucer” idea had entered the press after 1947, giving witnesses and journalists a ready made vocabulary for ambiguous things seen in the sky. RetroNews Le site de presse de la BnF [retronews.fr] retronews.fr Retro News Le site de presse de la BnFwww.retronews.fr... Lot et Garonne was not one of the great centres of the wave, but scattered reports appeared across the department’s two river valleys. The surviving references suggest a short lived cluster rather than one sustained, independently corroborated event. One early account concerned Sainte Bazeille, near Marmande. A newspaper report reproduced in later case catalogues said that two people saw a bluish, phosphorescent disc cross the sky in late September. The available excerpt provides no duration, direction, angular size, weather information or description of how sharply defined the “disc” appeared. Those omissions leave open numerous ordinary possibilities, including a meteor, aircraft lighting, an astronomical object seen through haze or a brief atmospheric effect. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Même chose en Savoie, non loin du lac du Bourget, où l'un des témoins, un médecin de Chambéry, ancien observateur d'artillerie, a noté se... At Marmande on 28 September, a retired butcher named Mr Gay was said to have watched a high altitude “flying cigar” apparently following the Garonne. The known newspaper text came from France Soir several days later. A subsequent catalogue treated the report as probably conventional, but without the original witness statement there is no reliable way to calculate the object’s path, speed or actual relationship to the river. A distant aircraft moving along the observer’s line of sight could easily have appeared to follow a geographical feature that was invisible to the object itself. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 28 septembre, Marmande, Lot et Garonne... Further fragmentary reports followed in October: At Aiguillon, later catalogues list a “cigar” accompanied by discs, sometimes with a time of 8.30 pm. The accessible source chain leads mainly through UFO books and databases rather than a detailed contemporary statement. One later reviewer judged the information “totally insufficient” and noted that large NATO air exercises were taking place in France during the period, although that is only a possible context, not a demonstrated explanation for this particular report. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Aiguillon, Lot et Garonne... A national newspaper round up said that luminous globes had been seen at Bournel and Montpezat d’Agenais. The locations were mentioned in a single sentence among reports from several parts of France, without named witnesses, times, trajectories or durations. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 7 octobre, Marcillac, Gironde... Other catalogues contain brief departmental entries whose underlying newspaper material has not been reproduced in full. Their repetition across databases can give an impression of independent confirmation, although several databases may ultimately be copying the same short article or book entry. This distinction matters. Ten catalogue listings do not necessarily represent ten separate bodies of evidence. A single newspaper sentence can pass into a book, then into several databases, and finally return online as multiple apparently distinct references. Unless the transmission chain is traced backwards, repetition measures the popularity of a story rather than its reliability. The Villeneuve sur Lot tale shows how a case can grow The most spectacular alleged Lot et Garonne incident from 1954 is also one of the weakest. A painter reportedly fishing beside the Lot at Villeneuve sur Lot saw a saucer settle silently on the river. A small being supposedly emerged, filled a bottle with water and departed in the craft. The story has circulated widely because its imagery is unusually vivid. Later catalogues supplied a witness surname, sometimes rendered as Molenaer or Moleaner, and added details such as a distance of about 100 metres and a rapid departure. Most versions point ultimately to a report attributed to La Dépêche du Midi of 18 October 1954 and to UFO books published decades afterwards. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, octobre, Villeneuve sur Lot, Lot et Garonne... The difficulty is that later repetition did not strengthen the evidence. It mainly reproduced the same narrative. No photograph, physical trace, second witness or original interview transcript accompanies the accessible accounts. Even the date and time remain imprecise. Several retellings simply say “October”, while some databases assign noon without showing how that time was established. More damagingly, UFO researcher Michel Figuet later marked the Villeneuve sur Lot account as a hoax in a list intended to remove unreliable cases from his catalogue. The surviving reference gives no detailed explanation of how the hoax was established, so it would be excessive to call the matter conclusively debunked. Nevertheless, a case rejected as a hoax by one of the principal French catalogue compilers cannot responsibly be presented as a strong close encounter. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, octobre, Villeneuve sur Lot, Lot et Garonne... Villeneuve sur Lot therefore illustrates a recurring problem in the 1954 material. The more colourful a report is, the more likely it is to be quoted; but quotability does not repair missing documentation. For departmental history, it belongs as an example of how the national wave entered local folklore, not as evidence that a landed craft and occupant were reliably observed. Lachapelle’s tractor encounter The Lachapelle story is more substantial because it names the principal witness and describes a coherent sequence. According to a long retrospective article published by La Dépêche du Midi in 1999, Jean Cellot was ploughing a four hectare field on the night of 19 November 1971. A light initially appeared beyond bushes near a stream. He reportedly assumed that a neighbour was also working late, then watched the light rise, move towards him and stop above his tractor. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr OVN I en Lot et Garonne OVNI en Lot et Garonne ladepeche.fr... Cellot described an intense golden illumination that made the field seem like daylight. The object was portrayed as two saucers placed together, with approximately ten round openings or lights beneath it. Frightened, he jumped from the tractor and ran towards his brother’s home. The object allegedly remained briefly over the vehicle, moved away slowly and silently, then vanished rapidly once Cellot returned to"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-6f2cde/","description":"Casteljaloux and Montayral remain unexplained, but both depend on single witnesses and lack photographs or independent confirmation.","breadcrumb":"Which Lot et Garonne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Why Do Casteljaloux and Montayral Remain Unexplained? Lot et Garonne Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Why Do Casteljaloux and Montayral Remain Unexplained? Casteljaloux and Montayral remain unexplained, but both depend on single witnesses and lack photographs or independent confirmation. Casteljaloux and Montayral remain unexplained, but both depend on single witnesses and lack photographs or independent confirmation. dataset evidence What witnesses reported at Casteljaloux and Montayral Why official investigators left the cases unresolved What missing evidence prevents stronger conclusions lot et garonne unresolved cases Introduction Casteljaloux in 1978 and Montayral in 2015 form the strongest unresolved strand in Lot et Garonne ’s official UFO record. Both remain classified as unexplained by GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation service, because the reported features did not fit any conventional explanation well enough. Yet neither case supplies strong evidence for an extraordinary aircraft. Each rests on one witness, with no photograph, video, radar confirmation or independently verified second observation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978 GEIPAN... The cases are valuable precisely because they show what “unexplained” means in official practice. Casteljaloux is a dramatic but sparsely documented night time account involving an orange luminous form, unusual movements and a frightened dog. Montayral is less spectacular but much better investigated: a stationary black form was reconstructed on site, compared with aircraft, balloons, astronomical objects and possible visual effects, then left unresolved because the leading balloon explanation remained too uncertain. Neither file confirms what was present; both record the limits of what investigators could establish. What was reported at Casteljaloux and Montayral? The two observations occurred exactly 37 years apart, on 23 October, but otherwise bear little resemblance. There is no evidence that the shared date has any significance, and the files do not suggest a recurring phenomenon. Their connection is evidential rather than physical: they are isolated Lot et Garonne reports in which an unusual description survived official scrutiny without receiving a satisfactory identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978 GEIPAN... Casteljaloux: an orange luminous object in 1978 At about 20:40 on 23 October 1978, a 60 year old man in agricultural surroundings near Casteljaloux reported seeing a single luminous phenomenon for several minutes. GEIPAN’s summary describes it as round or disc like, scintillating and emitting orange light. According to the witness, it changed speed and direction, made no sound and disappeared suddenly. He also said that a ball of fire emerged from it. His dog was reportedly frightened during the encounter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978 GEIPAN... The catalogue data give an estimated distance of 200 metres and an apparent or inferred diameter of about one metre. Those figures should not be treated as measurements. With an unfamiliar light against the night sky, a lone observer normally has no reliable scale by which to determine an object’s true distance or size. GEIPAN itself stresses that witnesses can sincerely assign distance, speed and physical form to poorly referenced phenomena even when those properties cannot be established visually. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978 GEIPAN... What gives the Casteljaloux report its apparent strength is the combination of several unusual features: variable movement, silence, an orange glow, the reported ejection of a “fireball” and an animal reaction. What weakens it is that virtually all those features come from the same uncorroborated account. The gendarmerie obtained no other testimony supporting the sighting, despite its reported duration and conspicuous appearance. The public file contains no photograph, instrument reading, physical trace or detailed modern reconstruction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCASTELJALOUX (47) 23.10.1978 GEIPAN... The dog’s behaviour adds human interest but little diagnostic value. An animal may react to its owner, an unseen sound, another animal or an unrelated disturbance. Without a veterinary effect, recorded noise or independent witness, fear cannot demonstrate that the aerial phenomenon caused the reaction. Similarly, the phrase “ball of fire” is striking but too imprecise to distinguish between a separate light, a visual after image, fragmentation, cloud effects or a mistaken reconstruction of a moving luminous source. Montayral: a black form beside an aerodrome The Montayral observation occurred in daylight near Fumel aerodrome. At around 17:31 on 23 October 2015, a man who had stopped to walk his dog noticed what he described as a black, opaque spot surrounded by a black haze in an otherwise clear blue sky. He thought it was high and distant. The central form appeared roughly round, with a curved protrusion likened to the handle of an inverted teapot. It remained in the same apparent position until approximately 17:58. When the witness briefly looked towards his car, he could no longer find it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete492 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The description was unusually perceptual rather than mechanical. The witness compared the sight to missing pixels on a screen, a black mark added to a photograph, or a dark hole with distorted edges. He did not report lights, windows, exhaust, rotation, occupants or any direct interaction with the surroundings. No noise came from the phenomenon, although an unrelated engine sound was audible elsewhere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete492 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A later on site reconstruction placed the line of sight at approximately 44 degrees north east and seven degrees above the horizon. The apparent width was estimated at half the Moon’s diameter, about 0.25 degrees. Those angular estimates allowed investigators to model possibilities but not to calculate a unique distance: an object half a metre wide might have been roughly 100 metres away, while a 20 metre object could have been several kilometres distant. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete492 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... That uncertainty is central to the case. The witness interpreted the blurred appearance as evidence that the form was near the far hills, but blur is not a dependable range finder. Atmospheric contrast, eyesight, focus, glare, motionless staring and the object’s own outline can all affect sharpness. The report therefore establishes an apparent dark form in a particular direction, not a confirmed large object suspended kilometres away. 9:08 UFO Crash Retrieval The Kalahari UAP Incident // 3D CGI Animation / Unreal Engine 5 Channel: Mac Mave Studios · Views: 12.8M · Uploaded: June 2023 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why did investigators leave the cases unresolved? GEIPAN does not use “unexplained” as a synonym for extraterrestrial. Its classification weighs residual strangeness against consistency: investigators ask how poorly the observation fits known phenomena and whether the available information is reliable enough to support that judgement. A case may remain unexplained when no proposed cause exceeds the service’s confidence threshold, even though one ordinary explanation appears more plausible than the alternatives. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr missions methodes et resultats GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN. It is based on 2 mai... The difference between the two Lot et Garonne files is substantial. Casteljaloux retains a broad category D classification, but the surviving public material offers little insight into detailed hypothesis testing. Montayral received a field investigation, cognitive interview, geographical reconstruction, weather and astronomical checks, examination of air traffic data and review by GEIPAN’s expert panel. It was classified D1, meaning unexplained with moderate rather than extreme strangeness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMONTAYRAL (47) 23.10.2015 GEIPAN... For Montayral, investigators considered several possibilities: An astronomical object did not fit the black appearance or the way the phenomenon ceased to be visible. A conventional aeroplane could not readily remain in one apparent position for about 20 minutes. A hovering helicopter could remain stationary, but should probably have produced audible noise at the distance implied by the witness’s estimates. The available air traffic information did not support one in the relevant position. An eye condition or visual floater did not fit well because such effects normally move with the eye or persist in other"},{"title":"Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny?","url":"/which-lot-ufo-stories-survive-close/","description":"The UFO history of Lot is less a story of one decisive mystery than of repeated misidentification, incomplete evidence and occasional episodes that became...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny? Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny? Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny? Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny? Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny? The UFO history of Lot is less a story of one decisive mystery than of repeated misidentification, incomplete evidence and occasional episodes that became... The UFO history of Lot is less a story of one decisive mystery than of repeated misidentification, incomplete evidence and occasional episodes that became more dramatic in local memory than in official records. lot Introduction Three episodes stand out. A strange report near Figeac in September 1954 belongs to France’s great post war “flying saucer” wave but survives with too little dependable information for a conclusion. A close range claim near Sauzet on 5 November 1990 became linked to a nationwide UFO controversy, although most observations that evening were caused by a re entering rocket body. More recently, several residents reported a silent oval object above Cahors in April 2020, but the available photographs and testimony did not establish what it was. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954 T1FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Observation date. 27/09/1954; region. Midi Pyrénées; Department. Lot; Classifi... The official record is mostly ordinary GEIPAN—the Study and Information Group on Unidentified Aerospace Phenomena—is the specialist unit of France’s national space agency, CNES. It gathers witness reports, obtains police or gendarmerie records where available, compares observations with astronomical, meteorological and aviation data, and publishes anonymised case files. An “unidentified” report in this system does not mean an alien craft: it means that an observer could not identify something at the time, or that investigators lacked enough information to settle the matter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr aintaining the anonymity of witnesses... Its classifications are essential to understanding Lot’s record: A means that the phenomenon has been identified. B means that an explanation is considered probable, although absolute proof is unavailable. C means that the evidence is too weak or incomplete for a sound judgement. D1 or D2 is reserved for reports retaining significant unexplained features after investigation, with D2 requiring the stronger combination of strangeness and evidential consistency. GEIPAN stresses that category C is not evidence of an extraordinary phenomenon. It usually indicates missing, contradictory or unusable information. Classifications can also be revised when new evidence appears. [Geipan] geipan.fr Il est important de noter que les cas classés C ne sont absolument pas du même ordre que les cas classés A, B, D1 ou D2.Ils résultent en... The department’s published entries illustrate this distinction. A 2000 Cahors report was classified A and attributed to an aircraft. A 2010 observation at Camboulit was considered a Thai lantern. A bright object seen repeatedly by amateur astronomers at Durbans in August 2019 was identified as Telkom 3, a failed Indonesian communications satellite tumbling in an abnormal orbit and therefore varying noticeably in brightness. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Other Lot observations were linked to a star, a general astronomical cause, a meteor or atmospheric re entry. These mundane solutions are not dismissals of the witnesses. Satellites, tumbling rocket bodies and meteors can behave in ways that are genuinely unfamiliar: they may brighten suddenly, appear to change speed, fragment into several lights or seem much lower than they really are. GEIPAN’s national experience is that most sufficiently documented reports can ultimately be associated with conventional objects or phenomena. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Figeac in 1954: a striking story with missing foundations The oldest prominent official file from Lot concerns an observation near Figeac on 27 September 1954. Three testimonies are associated with the case, including that of a ten year old girl. The surviving record describes a single unusual object and refers to a technological sounding bang or detonation, but basic investigative details—including dependable weather information and a reconstructable trajectory—are absent. GEIPAN consequently classifies the case C for lack of reliable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954 T1FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Observation date. 27/09/1954; region. Midi Pyrénées; Department. Lot; Classifi... Its date matters because September and October 1954 produced an exceptional wave of French “flying saucer” reporting. Newspapers across the country printed accounts of luminous discs, cigar shaped objects and apparent landings. Lot participated in that atmosphere. On 4 October, residents of Castelfranc reportedly watched a luminous object manoeuvring for some time in the direction of Anglars Juillac; about an hour later, a seemingly similar light was reported from Cazals. The account survives through a reproduction of a contemporary article from La Dépêche du Midi , rather than through a complete modern investigation. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The closeness of these dates makes a local “flap” tempting to imagine, but the evidence does not support treating the reports as one connected event. There is no reliable trajectory linking Figeac, Castelfranc and Cazals, no surviving instrument data and no documented physical trace. Descriptions such as “luminous object” are too broad to distinguish an unusual aircraft from a bright celestial object, meteor, balloon or perception affected by distance and atmospheric conditions. The 1954 material remains historically valuable because it shows how Lot experienced France’s first great popular UFO era. It is weak as proof of an extraordinary aerial event. Contemporary newspaper reports establish that stories were circulating; they do not establish that the objects were technologically anomalous. The Sauzet claim and the night of 5 November 1990 Lot’s most intriguing close range account is associated with the evening of 5 November 1990, when thousands of people across France reported a procession of lights. A later local article described a Cahors computer specialist driving near Sauzet shortly before 7 pm. According to an investigator quoted by the newspaper, the witness believed that a large object passed extremely close to him. The story became noteworthy precisely because it appeared more intimate and localised than the distant lights reported by most observers that night. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Open source on ladepeche.fr. The wider event, however, has a well supported conventional explanation. CNES identified the principal phenomenon as the atmospheric re entry of the Soviet rocket body designated 1990 094C. Its path crossed France from the west south west towards the east north east at an altitude of roughly 100 kilometres, breaking into numerous glowing components. Such a formation can create the illusion of one enormous dark structure carrying several lights, particularly when observers cannot see the object’s true distance. [Geipan] geipan.fr A noter qu'un témoin, membre d'un réseau d'observation particulièrement efficace, informait le SEPRA dès le 06 novembre qu'il s'agissait... The episode became controversial because witness descriptions frequently sounded more structured than a shower of debris. Some people reported triangles, rectangular formations or a vast silent craft flying at apparently low altitude. Later simulations nevertheless showed that the direction, timing and broad appearance of the main reports were consistent with the re entry. GEIPAN has also acknowledged that public explanation was slow and inadequate in 1990, allowing rumours of a concealed UFO wave to take hold. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 That does not automatically resolve every statement made that evening. Reports well outside the calculated time, path or appearance of the re entry would need to be assessed separately. The difficulty with the Sauzet story is that its strongest details appear mainly in retrospective reporting rather than in a readily accessible, fully documented official case file containing precise timing, sightlines, independent witnesses and contemporary measurements. The fairest assessment is therefore twofold. The nationwide spectacle was overwhelmingly a rocket body re entry. The Sauzet account may represent an unusually close perception of that spectacle, an unrelated conventional object, or a report whose remaining uncertainty comes from insufficient documentation. Its unresolved aspects should not be treated as evidence that the national explanation failed. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-a34388/","description":"Reports from Figeac, Castelfranc and Cazals capture the excitement of France's 1954 saucer wave but leave too little evidence for firm conclusions.","breadcrumb":"Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny?","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave Did Lot Experience a UFO Wave in 1954? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave Did Lot Experience a UFO Wave in 1954? Reports from Figeac, Castelfranc and Cazals capture the excitement of France's 1954 saucer wave but leave too little evidence for firm conclusions. Reports from Figeac, Castelfranc and Cazals capture the excitement of France's 1954 saucer wave but leave too little evidence for firm conclusions. event time window The Figeac report and what survives Castelfranc, Cazals and the local press Why the sightings cannot be linked securely lot 1954 wave Introduction Lot did experience a small cluster of flying saucer reports during France’s extraordinary autumn of 1954, but the surviving evidence does not establish a coherent local “UFO wave”. The department’s best known claim involved three children near Figeac who described a small stranger and a box like object on 27 September. October brought reports of bright objects around Castelfranc, Cazals and Anglars Juillac. Yet these incidents were not supported by photographs, radar records, physical debris or a dependable chain of independent testimony. The Figeac case was recorded by the gendarmerie, making it more substantial than a mere newspaper rumour. Even so, the children’s accounts conflicted over timing, investigators found no trace at the site, and no independent witness confirmed the alleged departure of the object. GEIPAN therefore classifies it as category C: unidentified because reliable information is lacking, not because an extraordinary craft survived investigation. The later Lot reports are weaker still, and at least one nearby sighting was eventually identified as a scientific balloon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Date d'observation. 27/09/1954; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Lot; Classifi... The Figeac report and what survives On the morning of 27 September 1954, three children aged about ten, eight and five were walking to school near Ceint d’Eau, east of Figeac. According to the surviving official summary, they encountered a short figure beside the road. The figure reportedly fired a shot or produced a sharp detonation, frightening the children. The two older witnesses also spoke of a box like object in a neighbouring meadow and said that it later rose rapidly into the air. Their teacher informed the gendarmerie, which questioned the children the following morning. [Geipan] geipan.fr FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954 T1FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Observation date. 27/09/1954; region. Midi Pyrénées; Department. Lot; Classifi... Contemporary newspaper versions added more colourful details. One report described a small man wearing a brick red outfit who spoke in an incomprehensible language before entering a craft roughly “as big as three school desks”. It was said to rise vertically, silently and quickly towards the south. National coverage treated the incident with an openly playful tone, presenting the “flying box” as the latest variation after flying saucers, cigars and discs. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 25, Sainte Bazeille, Lot et Garonne... That contrast matters. The official file preserves a frightening encounter involving a small figure, a loud report and an alleged object in a field. The newspaper account turns it into a compact “Martian” story with an exotic costume, mysterious speech and a dramatic vertical departure. It is impossible to tell how much of this detail came directly from the children, how much was simplified through repeated reporting, and how much reflected the language of the national saucer craze. The most serious difficulty is the inconsistency within the testimony itself. GEIPAN’s summary states that one older child placed the object’s departure at about 8.40 in the morning, while the eldest apparently said it occurred around midday. That is not a minor difference in estimating seconds or direction; it changes whether the departure was part of the original encounter or something supposedly observed several hours later. The available witness records also give different apparent sizes for what was seen. [Geipan] geipan.fr FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954 T1FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Observation date. 27/09/1954; region. Midi Pyrénées; Department. Lot; Classifi... The gendarmerie found no marks or other physical traces in the field, and investigators located no additional witness who had seen the figure, heard the alleged shot or watched the object leave. There is no surviving photograph, material sample, medical evidence or detailed contemporary reconstruction of sight lines and distances. GEIPAN consequently describes the case as lacking both information and corroboration, with contradictory elements in the accounts. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954FIGEAC (46) 27.09.1954; Date d'observation. 27/09/1954; Région. Midi Pyrénées; Département. Lot; Classifi... This does not prove that the children invented the incident. They may have encountered an unfamiliar adult, heard a nearby gunshot or mechanical bang, and connected the experience with an object already present in the field. Fear, age and questioning by adults could then have affected the order and interpretation of events. A prank, misunderstanding or embellished childhood report remains possible, but the surviving documentation is too limited to select confidently between them. Castelfranc, Cazals and the local press A week after the Figeac story, La Dépêche du Midi reported another unusual observation in Lot. At about 7 p.m. on 4 October, people in Castelfranc reportedly watched a luminous object making prolonged movements in the direction of Anglars Juillac. It then appeared to travel towards the south west and disappear. About an hour later, a similar phenomenon was reported from Cazals, roughly 15 kilometres north of Castelfranc. Witnesses compared the objects to large, bright stars. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 4, Castelfranc, Lot... The wording sounds intriguing but contains almost none of the measurements needed for a useful investigation. No witnesses are named. Their exact positions are unknown. The report gives no angular elevation, duration beyond “prolonged”, colour, apparent size, changes in brightness or reliable account of how the supposed movement was judged. It is also unclear whether Castelfranc and Cazals observed one continuing phenomenon, two unrelated lights, or the same astronomical object interpreted from different places. A later reconstruction found that Venus was exceptionally bright and low in the west south west from Castelfranc that evening, close to the reported direction of disappearance. It was due to set at about 7.37 p.m. The reported resemblance to a large bright star, the low western position and the disappearance towards the horizon make Venus a credible explanation for at least the first sighting. Mars and the Moon were also visible, although their positions fit less closely. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 4, Castelfranc, Lot... That proposal cannot be treated as a proven identification because the original newspaper note is too imprecise. Descriptions of “manoeuvres” may indicate genuine apparent motion, but stationary astronomical objects often seem to shift when viewed through moving cloud, haze, trees or an unsteady reference point. Without named witnesses or a fuller record, the case cannot be tested beyond observing that an obvious bright candidate occupied the relevant part of the sky. On 15 October, another report came from the same part of the Lot valley. A spherical object was said to have remained in the sky throughout the afternoon and to have been visible from Anglars Juillac, Prayssac and Castelfranc. Unlike the 4 October lights, this observation has a strong later explanation: it was identified as a large high altitude scientific balloon used for cosmic ray research and launched from Italy. A photograph of the balloon was obtained by the Haute Provence Observatory, and French UFO writers subsequently acknowledged the identification. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 15, Anglars Juillac, Lot... The balloon case is particularly useful because it shows why several reports in the same district should not automatically be joined together. A bright evening “star” on 4 October and a daylight sphere on 15 October may appear to form a repeating pattern when listed in a catalogue. In evidential terms, however, they differed in date, appearance and likely cause. One may have been Venus; the other was a scientific balloon. Geographical repetition alone does not demonstrate a common source. Why the sightings cannot be linked securely Calling these incidents the “1954 Lot wave” is reasonable as historical shorthand, provided that “wave” means a brief burst of reports rather"},{"title":"Sauzet 1990","url":"/sauzet-1990/","description":"The Sauzet story sits inside a nationwide night of sightings largely explained by a rocket-body re-entry, yet its strongest local details remain poorly","breadcrumb":"Which Lot UFO Stories Survive Close Scrutiny?","level":2,"text":"Sauzet 1990 Was the Sauzet UFO More Than Falling Debris? Lot UFOs Sauzet 1990 Sauzet 1990 Was the Sauzet UFO More Than Falling Debris? The Sauzet story sits inside a nationwide night of sightings largely explained by a rocket body re entry, yet its strongest local details remain poorly The Sauzet story sits inside a nationwide night of sightings largely explained by a rocket body re entry, yet its strongest local details remain poorly event time window The close range account near Sauzet How the rocket body re entry crossed France What remains uncertain and why lot sauzet 1990 Introduction The Sauzet case is remembered as one of Lot’s most dramatic UFO stories, but the central difficulty is simple: its extraordinary details come from a single account recorded years after the event, while the date, general direction and broad appearance closely match a well documented rocket body re entry seen across France. On 5 November 1990, shortly after 19:00, more than a thousand witnesses watched a long formation of lights cross a clear night sky. CNES, the French space agency, later reconstructed it as the break up of a Soviet Proton rocket stage travelling through the upper atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... The Sauzet witness, however, reportedly described something much nearer: an enormous dark mass passing overhead, stopping beside the road and displaying two stationary orange lights. That is why the claim still matters within Lot’s UFO history. It appears to offer a close range exception to the nationwide explanation. Yet no photograph, physical trace, independent witness or contemporaneous public case file has emerged to establish that the close range episode was a separate object. Francia: un encuentro con los investigadores de ovnis Channel: Perfil Open on YouTube The close range account near Sauzet The version most readily available today was published by the regional newspaper La Dépêche in December 2015. It was presented through Monique Gabrielli, a local UFO investigator who had collected the testimony of an unnamed computer specialist working in Cahors. According to this retelling, the man was driving home shortly before 19:00 on 5 November 1990. Near Trespoux Rassiels, he noticed white lights towards the south west, moving very slowly and apparently low in the sky. As he continued along the winding road through the Villesèque area, the lights repeatedly shifted between the front and side of his field of view. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Ovnis: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet Ovnis: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet The account became more dramatic on reaching Sauzet. The witness reportedly saw an enormous mass pass above him and then appear to remain stationary to the right of the road, between the village church and a junction. He stopped, got out of the car and crossed the road towards Luzech. One round orange light appeared, followed by another similar light farther to the left. Both were said to remain fixed while the dark mass stayed between the roads towards Luzech and Albas. Gabrielli also reported that the witness’s initial fear gave way to an unusually deep calm once the orange lights appeared. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Ovnis: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet Ovnis: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet Taken literally, this is not merely a report of distant fragments. It describes a large structured object at apparently low altitude, with a period of hovering and a strong psychological effect on the observer. Those features explain why the Sauzet story has been promoted as one of the evening’s closest encounters rather than another view of the re entry. They are also precisely the features that require the strongest supporting evidence. The published account identifies no second witness, photograph, video, radar return, ground mark or effect on the car. It provides no direct transcript in the witness’s own words and no measurements made at the scene. The newspaper’s accompanying illustration is a reconstruction, not an image captured during the observation. The witness is not named, limiting any independent examination of his route, sightlines or original recollection. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Les ovnis dans le Lot: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet Les ovnis dans le Lot: la vérité est peut être apparue à Sauzet A further weakness is the delay in documentation. A detailed sceptical review of the 5 November cases states that the Sauzet testimony was collected about five years afterwards. That review argues that the narrative contains two phases: an extended view of distant lights during the drive, followed—after a break in visibility—by the apparent close passage near Sauzet. Its author suggests that the second phase may represent the re entry as subsequently enlarged and reorganised in memory, rather than a distinct nearby craft. This is an interpretation rather than a demonstrated solution, but the reported delay does materially reduce confidence in fine details such as exact height, size, alignment and duration. [univers ovni.com] univers ovni.com 5 novembre 1990: Les ovnis fabriqués par des ufologuesCe dossier, retranscrit sur plusieurs sites Internet, est devenu la référence des... How the rocket body re entry crossed France The conventional explanation is unusually strong because it rests on more than a general resemblance to a meteor. The object associated with the event was the third stage of a Soviet Proton launch made on 3 November 1990. The launch carried the Gorizont 21 communications satellite, designated 1990 094A; the decaying rocket stage was catalogued separately as 1990 094C. Satellite observation records identify that stage as re entering on 5 November and being visible from several European countries. [skyrocket.de] space.skyrocket.de Gunter's Space PageGorizont 1 33 (11F662) / Tongastar 1 / Rimsat 1, 2 / PASI 1...12 Nov 2025 — Gorizont satellites are part of the Rus... CNES’s later simulation placed the beginning of major fragmentation at about 94 kilometres altitude over the Bay of Biscay. The resulting debris cloud crossed France while descending from roughly 90 to 50 kilometres and slowing from about seven to two kilometres per second. The simulation modelled several bodies of different densities, while recognising that the real stage probably separated into dozens or hundreds of pieces spread over tens of kilometres. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... That combination explains several apparently “craft like” features reported that evening: Many lights moving together: separate burning fragments could look like lamps attached to one vast body. A dark structure between lights: the eye may perceptually connect bright points into a solid outline, especially against a black sky. Slow, majestic motion: artificial re entries can remain visible for tens of seconds or longer, unlike the brief streak commonly associated with a natural meteor. [The Aerospace Corporation] aerospace.org The Aerospace CorporationWhat Does a Reentry Look LikeThe general rule of thumb is that natural meteor reentries happen quickly and typic... Apparent low altitude: without familiar scale or distance cues, a bright formation tens of kilometres high can seem to be just above trees, buildings or a road. Changes in shape: fragments with different mass and drag separate unevenly, brighten at different moments and leave individual trails. GEIPAN notes that nearly all witnesses perceived the phenomenon as low, even though the debris was high in the atmosphere. Its explanation is not that observers invented what they saw, but that unusual brightness, angular size and duration created a powerful illusion of proximity. The agency also acknowledges that early uncertainty, delayed orbital information and intense media coverage helped preserve the impression that the official account had been improvised after the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Sauzet’s geography does not by itself disprove the re entry. A driver moving along a twisting rural road continually changes orientation. A distant formation following a broadly steady path can therefore appear ahead, then to one side, then ahead again. Nearby trees, bends, slopes and buildings can interrupt the view, making a continuous event feel divided into separate episodes. Apparent hovering is also possible when an object moves partly towards or away from the observer rather than rapidly across the line of sight. The timing is broadly compatible as well, although the surviving narrative is not precise. The local account places the first sighting a little before 19:00, while GEIPAN describes the"},{"title":"Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed","url":"/why-lozeres-strangest-ufo-reports/","description":"Lozère has no single celebrated UFO case comparable with France’s best-known incidents at Cussac, Valensole or Trans-en-Provence.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed Lozère has no single celebrated UFO case comparable with France’s best known incidents at Cussac, Valensole or Trans en Provence. Lozère has no single celebrated UFO case comparable with France’s best known incidents at Cussac, Valensole or Trans en Provence. lozere Introduction The most important cases are a 1985 “falling red object” near Grandrieu that proved to be an expired marine distress flare, and a 1998 airline encounter over Mende that was later divided into two separate events. The first was identified as aircraft lights; the second remained unclassified only because the available information was too poor to test a plausible explanation involving Saturn and visual illusion. Other Lozère entries in France’s official GEIPAN archive are similarly marked by missing data rather than strong evidence of something genuinely inexplicable. [Geipan] geipan.fr GRANDRIEU (48) 1985GRANDRIEU (48) 1985. Date d'observation. 08/04/1985. Région. Languedoc Roussillon. Département. Lozère. Classifi... What the official record actually contains France’s official body for such reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It receives witness statements, consults bodies including the gendarmerie, civil aviation, the armed forces and the national weather service, and publishes anonymised case material. Its categories are important: an A case is identified, B is probably identified, C lacks enough reliable information, and D remains unidentified after investigation. A C classification therefore does not mean that investigators found an extraordinary object; it means they could not reach a dependable conclusion. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... That distinction is central to Lozère. The publicly indexed departmental cases found in the archive include: Saint Hilaire de Lavit, July 1983: category C because reliable information was lacking. Grandrieu and La Panouse, 8 April 1985: category A after identification of the object. Arzenc de Randon, 23 August 1990: category C because the available evidence was insufficient. Mende, 12 September 1998: one category A component and one category C component, separated during a later review. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... This is not the profile of a department dominated by high consistency, unresolved events. The archive instead contains a small mixture of explained observations and reports too incomplete to support firm interpretation. Grandrieu in 1985: a frightening object with a mundane source On 8 April 1985, several witnesses near La Panouse and Grandrieu saw a large vermilion red object with an irregular, pointed shape apparently dropping steeply behind a mountain. The sight was alarming enough to suggest an aircraft in distress. The gendarmerie was contacted immediately and officers were themselves able to observe the phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr GRANDRIEU (48) 1985GRANDRIEU (48) 1985. Date d'observation. 08/04/1985. Région. Languedoc Roussillon. Département. Lozère. Classifi... The subsequent inquiry traced it to an expired marine distress flare fired by a resident of a nearby hamlet. GEIPAN classified the case A: identified after investigation. The incident is valuable precisely because the original witnesses were not simply inventing a story. They saw a real, bright object behaving in an unusual way, and their concern about a possible crash was reasonable. What changed the assessment was not disbelief in the witnesses but the discovery of a source capable of reproducing the reported colour, descent and apparent position. [Geipan] geipan.fr GRANDRIEU (48) 1985GRANDRIEU (48) 1985. Date d'observation. 08/04/1985. Région. Languedoc Roussillon. Département. Lozère. Classifi... Lozère’s terrain probably intensified the effect. Against a dark mountain skyline, an unfamiliar flare can appear larger, nearer and more solid than it is. Its downward motion may also seem to end behind a ridge, creating the impression of a crash or landing. The case is therefore a useful warning against treating vivid descriptions such as “ogive shaped”, “voluminous” or “falling vertically” as reliable measurements of an object’s physical form. It also shows the value of immediate reporting. Because the gendarmerie became involved while the event was still current, investigators could connect the observation with a specific local action. Had the account survived only as a later recollection, it might have entered regional UFO folklore as an unexplained object descending into the mountains. The Mende airline case: one encounter, two different conclusions The most technically interesting Lozère file began at about 21:46 on 12 September 1998. The crew of an airliner near Mende reported two flashing lights passing well above their aircraft. They initially suspected two military aircraft flying one over the other. During radio contact with air traffic control, the crew later described a light apparently turning back towards them, zigzagging, rising and falling. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1Département. Lozère. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 22/08/2019. Type de phénomène. Avio... The case was originally treated as a single unexplained aviation event. When GEIPAN revisited it, however, investigators separated it into two phases. In the first phase, later reconstruction showed that the crew had seen the anti collision lights of two long haul aircraft. Air traffic control had not identified them in real time, but their tracks could be recovered afterwards. GEIPAN therefore reclassified this part as category A. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1Département. Lozère. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 22/08/2019. Type de phénomène. Avio... The second phase was more difficult. GEIPAN considered Saturn a plausible source for the later steady light. Once the aircraft lights had disappeared, the crew may have shifted attention to the bright planet and interpreted it as the same phenomenon returning. Apparent darting or zigzag motion could have been produced by the autokinetic effect, in which a stationary point of light seems to move when stared at against a dark, featureless background. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1Département. Lozère. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 22/08/2019. Type de phénomène. Avio... That explanation was not considered secure. The record consisted mainly of a transcript of the live radio exchange rather than separate, detailed interviews with both pilots. Investigators could not establish exactly when the aircraft lights were lost, whether the observation was continuous, how large the reported movements were or whether both crew members perceived the same thing. The flight crew were also dealing with an apparent traffic anomaly, operational procedures and the approach to landing. GEIPAN therefore classified the second phase C: not demonstrated to be extraordinary, but impossible to settle because too much reliable information was missing. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1Département. Lozère. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 22/08/2019. Type de phénomène. Avio... This revised assessment weakened the original impression of a single object manoeuvring around an airliner. It did not prove that every detail was caused by Saturn, but it showed that most of the event belonged to an identifiable sequence involving real aircraft, changing visual attention and an incomplete record. The older and thinner reports Two other official entries illustrate the limits of archival evidence. Saint Hilaire de Lavit, dated only to July 1983, and Arzenc de Randon on 23 August 1990 are both category C cases. GEIPAN’s published index describes them as lacking reliable information. Without sufficiently precise timing, direction, duration, witness interviews or supporting records, unusual testimony cannot be compared properly with aircraft movements, astronomical objects, meteors, balloons or atmospheric effects. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... An earlier press story from the national UFO wave of 1954 is associated with Ancelpont, in the commune of Saint Symphorien. A regional newspaper reportedly described a serviceman seeing a fast moving “ball of fire” at about 23:00, with green, red and orange flames and a noticeable humming sound. The observation lasted only a few seconds. A later specialist catalogue judged the description consistent with a meteor, although that retrospective interpretation is based on a press account rather than a modern official investigation. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The Ancelpont report matters mainly as evidence that"},{"title":"Grandrieu Flare","url":"/grandrieu-flare/","description":"A frightening red object over Lozere became a model case of how prompt investigation can turn a vivid UFO report into a firm identification.","breadcrumb":"Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed","level":2,"text":"Grandrieu Flare How a Distress Flare Became a UFO Lozere UFO Files Grandrieu Flare Grandrieu Flare How a Distress Flare Became a UFO A frightening red object over Lozere became a model case of how prompt investigation can turn a vivid UFO report into a firm identification. A frightening red object over Lozere became a model case of how prompt investigation can turn a vivid UFO report into a firm identification. event time window What witnesses saw near Grandrieu How investigators traced the flare Why mountain terrain distorted the event lozere grandrieu flare Introduction On the evening of 8 April 1985, families near La Panouse and Grandrieu in Lozère saw an intensely bright red object sink towards a mountain ridge. Its apparent size, irregular outline and steep descent made it look less like a distant point of light than a substantial craft about to crash. The gendarmerie was called at once, and officers arriving in the area saw the phenomenon themselves. The alarm was genuine, but the object was not extraordinary. Investigators traced it to an expired marine distress flare launched from a nearby hamlet. The Grandrieu case therefore became one of Lozère’s clearest identified sightings: a vivid report supported by several witnesses, yet solved because the inquiry began while memories, locations and the source of the light could still be checked. GEIPAN, the French official investigation body, classifies it as category A, meaning that the phenomenon was identified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGRANDRIEU (48) 1985 GEIPAN... 6:53 UFO's Over Palmdale August 2022 Channel: FADE TO BLACK Radio · Views: 5.2K · Uploaded: November 2022 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube What witnesses saw near Grandrieu The first report reached the gendarmerie at about 8.35 pm. An agricultural worker said that he had noticed an intense red light roughly south south east of his home, apparently settling behind a mountain several kilometres away. Fearing an aircraft was in difficulty, he requested assistance. The officers left immediately rather than treating the call as a routine or obviously mistaken UFO report. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At approximately 8.50 pm, while approaching the area, the officers saw a vermilion red object disappearing behind a ridge. Their report described an irregular, pointed or shell like outline, exceptional brightness and an apparent diameter about half that of the full Moon. It descended slowly, seemed briefly stationary and then vanished behind the hills. The visible phase lasted only four or five seconds, and there was no detectable noise or smell. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The civilian witnesses gave broadly compatible accounts, although their estimates and shape descriptions varied. One man described a red light descending behind the summit and thought it was perhaps 20 metres above the ridge when first noticed. Another, using 10×50 binoculars, saw a brilliant object with red internal lines or “ribs”, descending diagonally for roughly 20 seconds before disappearing. To the unaided eye, it appeared about the size of the full Moon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The surviving drawings show why the report initially sounded unusual. One witness drew nested red octagonal forms; another produced an elongated, rounded outline crossed by a web of red lines. These were not drawings of a conventional aircraft seen clearly at close range. They were attempts to give structure to a dazzling, unfamiliar light observed briefly against a dark sky, in one case through binoculars. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several details nevertheless pointed towards a pyrotechnic source: the dominant colour was bright red; the object descended slowly rather than travelling like an aircraft; some witnesses thought it was hanging from, or descending beneath, a parachute; it disappeared at the mountain skyline; no engine noise accompanied it; each appearance was brief. None of these details alone proves that the object was a flare. Together, however, they describe the expected appearance of a red parachute distress signal much better than a crashing aeroplane or a structured airborne craft. How investigators traced the flare The speed of the local response was decisive. Officers recorded observation directions, questioned the witnesses separately, photographed the relevant viewpoints and searched the area for traces. They also contacted military and civil aviation control centres, which reported no corresponding aerial incident. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The breakthrough came after local press coverage prompted a resident of a nearby hamlet to contact the gendarmerie. He owned a farm south of the witnesses’ position and admitted launching a red marine distress flare from his yard during the relevant period. He described it as a rocket that climbed to about 200 metres, produced a powerful red light and then descended beneath a parachute. The flare had been found in a cardboard tube and was marked as having expired in 1976, nine years before the incident. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Its reported flight path crossed the line of sight in which the witnesses had seen the object. The launch time matched the observation window, while the flare’s colour, descent, brightness and parachute assembly accounted for the most striking features. The launcher's previous experience with similar signals during military service also meant that he could recognise the device and its behaviour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Marine distress flares are designed to attract attention at long range. Red rocket parachute flares are internationally recognised distress signals, and the parachute slows the burning light’s descent so that it remains conspicuous in the sky. At a distance, the parachute, suspension cords and smoke may be invisible while the bright source dominates the observer’s view. [Royal Yachting Association] rya.org.uk Royal Yachting Association Distress Flares Safety Equipment Royal Yachting Association Distress Flares Safety Equipment The identification therefore did not rest merely on saying that the object “looked like a flare”. Investigators found a person in the correct area who possessed the correct kind of flare, admitted launching it at the relevant time and described a trajectory consistent with the witnesses’ direction of observation. GEIPAN’s category A conclusion reflects that convergence of independent details. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGRANDRIEU (48) 1985 GEIPAN... 2:00 Twenty Nine Palms UFO Flares Identified Channel: Mick West · Views: 26.2K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 2 minutes Open on YouTube Why the mountains created a crash illusion The most important perceptual feature was the ridge. Witnesses saw the light descending towards a dark mountain profile and then disappearing at the skyline. With no visible ground beneath the light and no reliable indication of its true distance, the brain naturally treated the ridge as the object’s likely destination. Disappearance behind the mountain then resembled impact or landing. That interpretation was understandable but not geometrically secure. A light can vanish at a ridge because it is descending far beyond it, because it burns out near the same line of sight, or because the terrain simply blocks the final stage of its flight. From one observation point, these different situations can look almost identical. The apparent size was equally misleading. Witnesses compared the light with half a full Moon or a full Moon, yet such comparisons describe its angular size in the field of view, not its physical dimensions. Without a known distance or familiar outline, a small, brilliant nearby source can be perceived as a much larger object farther away. Research on visual distance perception confirms that darkness and reduced environmental context make distance judgements less dependable because many of the normal depth cues are absent. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Visual distance perception indoors, outdoors, and in the darkby JM Dukes · 2022 · Cited by 13 — The purpose of Experiment 1 was to... Brightness also encouraged the impression of solidity. An intense light can bloom in the eye, obscure fine detail and create changing edges. Through binoculars, glare, the burning composition, smoke and parts of the parachute system may have produced the “ribs” and irregular contours shown in the witness drawings. The drawings remain valuable records of what the observers perceived, but they are not reliable engineering diagrams of the source. The Grandrieu event is therefore better described as a crash illusion than as a fabricated report. The witnesses correctly detected a real object, accurately reported its strong red colour and downward movement, and reasonably alerted the authorities. Their mistake lay"},{"title":"Mende Airliner","url":"/mende-airliner/","description":"The Mende pilot report changed from one unexplained encounter into an identified aircraft sighting and a weaker unresolved visual episode.","breadcrumb":"Why Lozere's Strangest UFO Reports Changed","level":2,"text":"Mende Airliner Why the Mende UFO Case Split in Two Lozere UFO Files Mende Airliner Mende Airliner Why the Mende UFO Case Split in Two The Mende pilot report changed from one unexplained encounter into an identified aircraft sighting and a weaker unresolved visual episode. The Mende pilot report changed from one unexplained encounter into an identified aircraft sighting and a weaker unresolved visual episode. event time window The pilots' original report The aircraft lights identified later Saturn, autokinesis and missing testimony lozere mende airliner Introduction The Mende airline encounter of 12 September 1998 did not end with a single verdict. A later reconstruction by France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, found that the pilots’ dramatic account was best divided into two episodes. The first involved two flashing lights that approached, passed well above the aircraft and receded; flight path records strongly identified these as two ordinary airliners. The later episode involved one or two apparently manoeuvring lights. Saturn offers a plausible explanation, with the reported movement possibly produced by the autokinetic illusion, but the surviving evidence is too incomplete to prove it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1 GEIPAN... That split matters because the case was originally treated as one unexplained event. The review showed that a continuous sounding cockpit narrative can conceal a change of target: the witnesses may first have watched real aircraft and then, after losing them, transferred the same interpretation to a bright celestial object. GEIPAN therefore classified the aircraft phase as identified, category A, while placing the later visual phase in category C — unusable because reliable information was missing, not confirmed as extraordinary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1 GEIPAN... 0:22 Pilot Witnesses UFO Flying Near Plane 1162504 Channel: RM Videos · Views: 60.3K · Uploaded: December 2020 · Length: 22 seconds Open on YouTube The pilots’ original report Proteus Airlines flight PRB8081 was travelling from Toulouse towards Dijon and was near Mende when its crew contacted Marseille air traffic control at 21:46 UTC, equivalent to 23:46 local summer time. The aircraft was flying at flight level 180, roughly 5.5 kilometres above sea level. The available record does not consist of later, separately signed statements from both pilots. It is principally a transcript of their live radio exchange, accompanied by a SEPRA registration sheet and flight path material prepared by the south east air navigation centre. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The first report was straightforward. The crew asked about traffic at their nine o’clock position and described “two flashing points”. The controller could see no nearby matching traffic on the display being used at that moment, although more distant aircraft appeared in the broader area. The pilots then said the lights seemed to be approaching. At 21:51 they judged that they were probably two military aircraft, one above the other, which had crossed well above them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The absence of an immediate radar identification made the situation more unsettling than a routine crossing. Marseille control checked for military activity and was told that none was known. The crew continued to see the lights after they had passed, now towards the aircraft’s three o’clock position. At 21:54, however, the description changed: the pilots said the lights appeared to turn back towards them. Over the following minutes, they alternated between speaking of one point and two, saying that the phenomenon rose, fell, moved rapidly and zigzagged. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. By 22:04 UTC, the crew said that this unusual movement had been visible for about ten minutes. Their attention then shifted to the procedure for filing a report and, shortly afterwards, to descent and transfer to another control frequency. The transcript contains no clear final statement that the lights vanished, moved away or were positively identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Read as a continuous story, the exchange sounds like two mysterious objects approaching, crossing the aircraft and then returning to perform erratic manoeuvres. GEIPAN’s later reconstruction challenged precisely that assumption of continuity. The aircraft lights identified later When the case was first processed, it was classified as unexplained. GEIPAN subsequently reopened it as part of a wider review of older files, using flight path reconstruction and astronomical software unavailable or less readily applied during the original assessment. Its 2019 investigation concluded that the opening phase matched two scheduled airliners: Finnair flight FIN2156 at flight level 310 and LTU International Airways flight LTU1411 at flight level 350. PRB8081 was much lower, at flight level 180. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. At 21:46, the two higher aircraft lay west of PRB8081, at approximately 19 and 26 nautical miles respectively. That position corresponded with the crew’s initial report of lights at nine o’clock. Their description as two flashing points was also consistent with anti collision lights, which can remain visible at considerable distances at night even when the aircraft’s body and other details cannot be resolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The timing strengthened the identification. At 21:49 the pilots said the lights were approaching; the reconstructed tracks showed the three flights converging. At 21:51 the crew reported that the two objects had passed well above them; the recorded routes and altitude differences showed the same crossing geometry. At 21:53 the pilots said the lights were now far away towards the east, again broadly matching the continuing tracks of FIN2156 and LTU1411. GEIPAN judged that this correspondence in number, direction, timing, altitude relationship and apparent behaviour removed any significant strangeness from the first phase. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The controller’s failure to identify the flights in real time does not overturn that reconstruction. The investigation noted that full route information was recovered later, possibly by consulting data that had not been visible on the controller’s immediate display or by reconstructing primary radar returns. The precise reason for the real time omission was not firmly established, and GEIPAN itself remarked that it was surprising. Even so, the later flight tracks provide substantially stronger evidence than the controller’s momentary inability to attach identities to the lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This phase was separated out as the Mende T1 case and classified A: an identified observation of aircraft. GEIPAN assigned it very low strangeness and low evidential consistency because the underlying phenomenon was ordinary and the surviving witness material was limited. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1 GEIPAN... 5:59 Pilot Spots UFO Zoom Past Mid Flight (S5) The Proof Is Out There Channel: HISTORY · Views: 68.8K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Why the second phase became a different case The identification becomes weaker after about 21:54. By then, the two airliners were moving farther away, while the crew began reporting a return, rapid vertical motion and zigzagging. At 21:56 the relevant aircraft lights were around 28 nautical miles, or 52 kilometres, away — near the estimated limit at which such anti collision lights could be seen under ideal conditions. By 22:04 the separation had grown to about 66 nautical miles, or 122 kilometres. GEIPAN concluded that the crew could no longer have been watching the same aircraft at the end of the report. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The directions also ceased to agree closely. At the point when the pilots said the phenomenon seemed to turn back, the airliners were at an azimuth of about 70 degrees. A true three o’clock bearing from PRB8081’s heading was about 90 degrees, leaving an angular discrepancy of roughly 20 degrees. This was not an enormous difference in an informal cockpit report, but it opened the possibility that another bright object had entered the crew’s attention. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO PRB] MENDE (48) 12.09.1998 T1 GEIPAN... Saturn was then visible in the eastern sky at an azimuth of approximately 100 degrees and an elevation a little above 20 degrees. It was bright, white looking and close to the direction indicated by the pilots. GEIPAN proposed that the crew may have lost the receding aircraft lights and then mistaken Saturn for their reappearance, still expecting to see the same unexplained traffic. The fact"},{"title":"Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore","url":"/maine-et-loires-ufo-record-without-the/","description":"Maine-et-Loire has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is more modest than local folklore sometimes suggests.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore Maine et Loire has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is more modest than local folklore sometimes suggests. Maine et Loire has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but its official history is more modest than local folklore sometimes suggests. The department’s strongest documented pattern is not a single spectacular “classic” encounter supported by radar, pilots and physical evidence. maine et loire Introduction The most useful way to understand Maine et Loire’s UFO history is therefore to separate three categories: reports with plausible ordinary explanations, cases that cannot be assessed because essential details are missing, and genuinely well documented anomalies. In the official material available for the department, the first two categories dominate. That makes Maine et Loire less a UFO “hotspot” than a revealing example of how unusual sky sightings are reported, preserved and sometimes weakened by later investigation. The 1977–79 cluster is the department’s clearest historical episode The late 1970s produced the most noticeable concentration of older official cases in Maine et Loire. Reports were recorded at Angers in April 1977, Pouancé in December 1977, Saumur in 1978 and Challain la Potherie in June 1979. Their proximity in time gives the appearance of a local flap, meaning a period when reports become unusually frequent. Yet the surviving files do not establish that these witnesses saw a common phenomenon. The cases were reported at different places, on different dates and with differing descriptions, and the official classifications chiefly reflect inadequate evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T129 Apr 1977 — ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977. Observation date. 29/04/1977. region. Pays de la Loire. Department... The Angers incident of 29 April 1977 is the most vivid of the group. According to the GEIPAN summary, two people made observations at about 9.15 pm and 10.15 pm. They described the slow passage of a cigar shaped object displaying lights of different colours and moving parallel to the River Maine. One observation reportedly lasted about ten minutes. That duration and the repeated sighting make the story more interesting than a momentary flash, but GEIPAN ultimately classified it as category C because the testimony was too sparse to support a reliable reconstruction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGERS (49) 29.04.1977Le 29 avril 1977 à 21h15 et à 22h15 deux personnes observent le lent déplacement d'un engin en forme de cigar... That classification is crucial. Category C does not mean “unexplained after a strong investigation”. It means that the available information is insufficient or unreliable. Without dependable angular positions, direction of travel, elevation, weather, comparison points and independent accounts, investigators cannot establish whether a light was nearby and slow, or distant and apparently slow. Nor can they properly compare it with aircraft, astronomical objects or other traffic. The Pouancé reports followed in December 1977. Accounts describe repeated observations on 19 and 20 December of a bright object showing green, orange and red lights, sometimes appearing to stop before moving again. Such behaviour can sound highly unusual when described verbally, but apparent stopping and colour changes are common difficulties in night observation: a distant light moving towards or away from a witness may seem stationary, while atmospheric distortion and viewing angle can alter its colour. GEIPAN again classified the case C for lack of reliable information. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr pouance 1977 1200468 Celles ci ont eu lieu les 19 décembre vers 22h et 20 décembre à 4h du matin et vers 22h. Les... Saumur’s 1978 report and the Challain la Potherie case of 10 June 1979 received the same broad assessment. Their presence shows that reports were not confined to Angers, but their evidential weakness prevents the late 1970s sequence from being treated as a demonstrated regional wave of one unknown object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr CHALLAIN LA POTHERIE (49) 10.06.1979 10/06/1979 C Maine et Loire Manque d'informations fiables 20/09/2016 1979 06 10... Later Angers area cases remained thin rather than extraordinary Further reports entered the official record during the 1990s and 2010s, including Saint Sylvain d’Anjou in April 1995, Angers in October 1996 and Angers again in June 2019. All three were placed in category C because investigators lacked sufficiently dependable information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Saint Sylvain d’Anjou case is particularly instructive because it was later re examined. GEIPAN notes that older files may be revisited using improved software and accumulated investigative experience. In this instance, however, the review did not uncover enough new material to support either a conventional identification or a strong unresolved classification. The result remained category C. Later scrutiny therefore weakened any attempt to present the case as a robust mystery: the problem was not that every ordinary explanation had failed, but that the original information did not permit meaningful testing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Grâce à de nouveaux moyens techniques (logiciels) et à l'expérience d'enquête acquise depuis toutes ces dernières années, ce réexamen abo... The 2019 Angers report reached the same evidential dead end. It survives in the public database and associated documentation, but its official classification is again “lack of reliable information”. This demonstrates the value and limitation of the GEIPAN archive. Publication confirms that somebody reported something unusual; it does not certify the reported object as physically anomalous. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. There is also an important difference between a report being unresolved and being resistant to explanation. A case can remain open because the witness supplied no exact direction, the observation was reported too late, the date is uncertain or no photograph, radar trace or independent testimony survives. That is quite different from a case in which high quality measurements remain anomalous after aircraft, satellites, astronomical bodies, balloons and atmospheric effects have been carefully excluded. Many recent “UFOs” were identifiable objects The official database contains a useful counterweight to the unresolved stories: several Maine et Loire sightings were explained in ordinary terms. In Thorigné d’Anjou on 15 August 2012, the reported phenomenon was assessed as an aircraft. An Angers sighting on 12 June 2014 and a Cholet report from 24 November 2016 were attributed to condensation trails. A Cholet observation on 13 June 2014 was classified as a satellite, while a Loire Authion report from July 2017 was identified as a bird. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN These explanations are not trivial dismissals. They show why distant objects are difficult to judge without reference points: Aircraft can appear stationary when flying approximately towards an observer, then seem to accelerate or turn sharply as their angle changes. Condensation trails can look solid or luminous , especially near sunrise or sunset, when the aircraft itself may be too distant to see. Satellites can brighten suddenly as reflective surfaces catch sunlight, then fade while continuing silently across the sky. Birds can produce misleading images , particularly in single photographs where distance, scale and motion cannot be recovered. The overall official French figures reinforce this pattern. CNES currently reports that 24.6 per cent of GEIPAN cases are perfectly identified and 39.7 per cent are probably identified. A further 32.4 per cent remain unidentified because information is lacking, while only 3.3 per cent remain unidentified after investigation. These national percentages should not be mechanically applied to Maine et Loire, but they clarify why a category C case carries far less evidential weight than a genuinely investigated category D case. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES 7:52 UFO Emits Weird White Material Onto Small French Town Close Encounters Channel: Quest TV · Views: 1.4M · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube No strong aviation or military case defines the department Aviation and military involvement often gives a UFO report greater apparent authority, particularly when pilots, air traffic controllers or radar operators provide independent data. Maine et Loire has civil aviation activity around Angers and a wider regional aviation"},{"title":"1977 79 Cluster","url":"/1977-79-cluster/","description":"Angers, Pouance, Saumur and Challain-la-Potherie produced a striking run of reports, but the files do not prove a single regional phenomenon.","breadcrumb":"Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore","level":2,"text":"1977 79 Cluster Was Maine et Loire's 1970 s UFO Wave Real? Maine et Loire UFOs 1977 79 Cluster 1977 79 Cluster Was Maine et Loire's 1970 s UFO Wave Real? Angers, Pouance, Saumur and Challain la Potherie produced a striking run of reports, but the files do not prove a single regional phenomenon. Angers, Pouance, Saumur and Challain la Potherie produced a striking run of reports, but the files do not prove a single regional phenomenon. event time window What witnesses reported across the department Why the Angers and Pouance cases stood out Why the surviving files remain category C maine et loire 1977 79 cluster Introduction Between April 1977 and June 1979, witnesses in Angers, Pouancé, Saumur and Challain la Potherie reported slow moving lights or elongated objects in the sky. Placed together on a timeline, the cases resemble a Maine et Loire UFO wave. The surviving evidence, however, does not show that one phenomenon travelled across the department or that the sightings were physically connected. All four cases are classified C by GEIPAN, the French space agency’s official unit for investigating unidentified aerial phenomena. That designation is often misunderstood. It does not mean that investigators eliminated conventional explanations and were left with an extraordinary mystery. It means that the reports could not be assessed reliably because essential information was missing or weak. The late 1970s sequence matters chiefly as an example of how several memorable but incomplete reports can acquire the appearance of a coherent regional “flap” when viewed retrospectively. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN What witnesses reported across the department The sequence began in Angers on 29 April 1977. GEIPAN’s records contain three witness entries, including an observation beginning at about 9.15 pm. The fuller case summary describes two observation periods, around 9.15 pm and 10.15 pm, involving a slowly moving, cigar like form carrying differently coloured lights. It was said to travel roughly parallel to the River Maine; one observation reportedly lasted about ten minutes. The duration and number of witnesses make Angers the most substantial looking case in the group, but weather conditions and other important observational details were not established in the surviving record. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T1ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T1 · ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T2 · ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T3. Témoin. Date de l'obser... Pouancé followed on 20 December 1977. Two witnesses were recorded, and the object was entered in GEIPAN’s database as a single point like phenomenon, larger than a star and showing green, red and orange colours. One witness’s observation direction was recorded as south, but the weather, apparent speed, sound and environmental effects remained unknown or unspecified. Those omissions are important because a coloured point of light near the horizon can represent anything from an aircraft to an astronomical source distorted by the atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPOUANCE (49) 20.12.1977 T2 GEIPAN... At Saumur on 1 March 1978, two people travelling in a vehicle reported a long, bulky, mainly red luminous object moving slowly from south to north at about 6.30 pm. They watched it for seven or eight minutes and heard no unusual sound. No additional witnesses were found, and GEIPAN’s summary explicitly states that there was insufficient information to determine what had been seen. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAUMUR (49) 1978 GEIPAN... The final named case occurred at Challain la Potherie on 10 June 1979. The official entry confirms the date, location, associated gendarmerie report and category C classification, but the publicly indexed summary preserves little usable detail beyond an observation of a slowly moving phenomenon above the horizon. Its very sparseness illustrates the central difficulty with the supposed cluster: Challain adds another dot to the map, but not enough evidence to establish a shared object, flight path or physical cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHALLAIN LA POTHERIE (49) 10.06.1979CHALLAIN LA POTHERIE (49) 10.06.1979; Observation date. 10/06/1979; region. Pays de la Loire... On the surface, the reports have genuine similarities. Most involved nocturnal or twilight lights, apparently slow movement and durations long enough for witnesses to pay attention. Angers and Saumur both included elongated or cigar like descriptions, while Angers and Pouancé involved coloured lights. Yet these common features are broad. They are also typical of many difficult night sky reports in which witnesses cannot judge an object’s distance, scale or orientation. Why Angers and Pouancé stood out Angers is the strongest narrative in the sequence because it combines multiple witnesses, two reported observation periods and a distinctive route along an urban geographical feature. A ten minute passage sounds less easily dismissed than a momentary light. The description of a cigar shaped body with several colours also creates a memorable mental image rather than an anonymous point in the sky. But the wording may imply more physical certainty than the underlying observation warrants. At night, several separated lights on a distant aircraft can be mentally joined into the outline of a single body. A nearly head on aircraft may appear almost stationary before seeming to move sideways as its heading changes. Without dependable angular measurements, investigators cannot calculate whether an object was low and close, high and distant, or simply following a normal aviation route. No photograph, radar return, physical trace or independent instrument record is attached to the official case. GEIPAN therefore retained it as an unworkable category C report rather than an unexplained category D case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T1ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T1 · ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T2 · ANGERS (49) 29.04.1977 T3. Témoin. Date de l'obser... Pouancé attracted attention for a different reason: the reported colours and apparent changes in movement. Red, green and orange lights can suggest a structured craft, especially when witnesses say the source stopped and restarted. They can also be compatible with aviation lighting, changing viewing angles or atmospheric scintillation—the rapid variation in brightness and colour produced when light passes through unstable air. The official witness data do not settle the issue. The phenomenon is recorded as point like rather than as a clearly resolved body; its apparent speed is unknown, and no reliable weather information survives. The case therefore contains an interesting perception but too few constraints to distinguish between competing explanations. The fact that two people noticed it improves confidence that some visible stimulus was present, but it does not establish the stimulus’s distance, nature or extraordinary character. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPOUANCE (49) 20.12.1977 T2 GEIPAN... The Angers and Pouancé reports consequently stand out as stories, not as measurements. Their vivid elements—coloured lights, apparent hovering and long duration—are precisely the features most dependent on uncertain depth perception and interpretation in darkness. A cluster on paper, not a demonstrated wave The four reports cover more than two years and widely separated parts of Maine et Loire. Angers lies near the department’s centre, Saumur well to the east, and Pouancé and Challain la Potherie towards its north western edge. No surviving official analysis links their directions of travel, viewing angles or times to a single route. There is also no evidence that the same witnesses, aircraft, military activity or local investigator connected the files when they were created. The official catalogue further complicates any neat four case narrative. Other Maine et Loire entries fall within or close to the same period, including a category B case at Beaufort en Vallée in May 1978, a category C report at Noyant la Gravoyère in January 1979 and another category B entry at Cholet in July 1979. This broader record suggests a mixed run of reports rather than four recognised encounters forming one officially defined wave. Some cases remained unusable; others were considered probably identifiable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANCase list; SAUMUR (49) 1978, 01/03/1978, C · Maine et Loire, Manque d'informations fiables; NOYANT LA GR... That distinction matters because a cluster can be created by selection. Choose four category C reports with visually interesting descriptions and they resemble a pattern. Include the surrounding category B cases and the picture becomes less dramatic: residents were reporting various unfamiliar sights, some of which investigators believed had ordinary causes and some of which lacked enough information for any conclusion. Nor is the spacing especially persuasive"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-110702/","description":"GEIPAN records show that many local sightings were aircraft, trails, satellites or birds, while others stayed open only because key details were missing.","breadcrumb":"Maine et Loire's UFO Record Without the Folklore","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases Why Most Maine et Loire UFO Reports Stayed Weak Maine et Loire UFOs Explained Cases Explained Cases Why Most Maine et Loire UFO Reports Stayed Weak GEIPAN records show that many local sightings were aircraft, trails, satellites or birds, while others stayed open only because key details were missing. GEIPAN records show that many local sightings were aircraft, trails, satellites or birds, while others stayed open only because key details were missing. dataset evidence What GEIPAN categories actually mean Aircraft, trails, satellites and birds Unresolved versus resistant to explanation maine et loire explained cases Introduction Official investigation did not turn Maine et Loire ’s UFO archive into a simple list of “real” and “false” sightings. It produced a more useful distinction: some reports could be matched confidently or probably to ordinary phenomena; some remained untestable because dates, directions or other essential facts were unreliable; and only a well supported residue could properly count as unexplained. In the department’s published GEIPAN record, explained and information poor cases dominate. That matters because an unresolved report is easily mistaken for evidence that investigators encountered something extraordinary. GEIPAN’s categories do not support that reading. A case placed in category C is not a mystery that defeated every explanation. It is a report that cannot be analysed reliably. By contrast, a genuinely unexplained category D case must combine residual strangeness with enough consistent evidence to justify that judgement. Maine et Loire illustrates how demanding that separation can be. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr baisse cas d GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... What GEIPAN categories actually mean GEIPAN, the French space agency CNES unit responsible for investigating reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, assesses both strangeness and consistency . Strangeness describes how poorly the reported event fits known explanations after investigation. Consistency reflects the amount and reliability of the evidence: the precision of the account, the number and independence of witnesses, photographs or recordings, and whether basic details agree with verifiable conditions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ou D2) doivent avoir fait l'objet d'une enquête... The resulting categories are not a scale running from “least alien” to “most alien”: Category A means the phenomenon has been identified with little or no ambiguity. Category B means an ordinary explanation is considered highly probable, although not demonstrated as completely as in category A. Category C means the report is not sufficiently reliable or detailed to analyse. Category D means the phenomenon remains unexplained despite evidence judged adequate for investigation. GEIPAN has also used D1 and D2 to distinguish cases of moderate and stronger consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr baisse cas d GEIPANForte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... The crucial boundary is therefore not simply between “explained” and “unexplained”. It is between a report that resists explanation after meaningful testing and one that merely lacks enough information to be tested. GEIPAN explicitly states that stronger apparent strangeness requires stronger consistency. An extraordinary description supported only by vague or contradictory testimony may end as C rather than D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ou D2) doivent avoir fait l'objet d'une enquête... This helps explain the large share of weak older cases in Maine et Loire. Reports such as the 1977 Angers cigar shaped object or the repeated lights near Pouancé sound intriguing in summary form, but their category C status signals an evidential failure: too little dependable positional, temporal or observational information survived to reconstruct the events. Their continued lack of explanation should not be confused with a positive finding that no ordinary cause would fit. Aircraft and their trails could look stationary or structured Aircraft related misidentifications were not always straightforward reports of a moving plane with visible navigation lights. Perspective, sunset illumination and persistent condensation trails could make aviation activity appear like a fixed luminous object, a triangular form or an object changing colour. A November 2016 case at Cholet demonstrates the problem. A witness and his son first saw a bright, elongated phenomenon low in the south western sky. Later, through binoculars, the witness reported two dark, apparently stationary triangular forms. The duration and lack of obvious motion seemed to count against aircraft, and the witness himself considered but rejected condensation trails. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN nevertheless judged aircraft trails to be the probable explanation. Short trails behind separate engines can spread apart and appear as divergent arms rather than parallel lines. Near sunset, high altitude trails may remain brightly illuminated after the lower sky has darkened; minutes later, similar trails can appear grey or black depending on their position relative to the Earth’s shadow. Distance also makes the aircraft’s movement difficult to perceive, especially when foreground clouds repeatedly mask the view. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The investigators did acknowledge an inconsistency: the witness’s stated observation time and the dark appearance of the later trails were not an ideal match. That prevented an unequivocal category A conclusion. The case was classified B because the trail hypothesis accounted for most of the reported features, but the surviving timings did not allow complete proof. This is a useful example of official caution working in both directions: a plausible explanation was accepted without pretending that every detail had been resolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Another Angers report from June 2014 was likewise attributed to aircraft condensation trails illuminated by the setting sun. Together, these cases show why a distant, slowly changing luminous form cannot be assessed from shape alone. Investigators need the viewing direction, solar position, cloud conditions and likely air traffic geometry before “stationary object” becomes a meaningful physical description. 21:29 Classified Soviet UFO Investigation Revealed Russia’s Project Blue Book Channel: The Infographics Show · Views: 217.0K · Uploaded: October 2023 · Length: 21 minutes Open on YouTube Satellites explained movement that seemed deliberate Satellites repeatedly provided explanations for silent lights whose movement appeared too smooth, unusual or coordinated to witnesses. A 1978 report near Beaufort en Vallée involved several people watching an orange spherical light that seemed stationary for several minutes before departing silently towards the north west. GEIPAN classified it B as a probable satellite passage. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The more instructive case occurred at Cholet shortly after midnight on 13 June 2014. A witness using a new telescope noticed two bright points travelling together while maintaining the same separation. He also reported apparent pauses and supplied drawings and a photograph. The pairing made the observation seem more structured than a routine satellite sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Investigators compared the time, direction, brightness, angular separation and trajectory with orbital information. Those details matched a known pair of American NOSS military satellites. The apparent stops were attributed to interrupted visibility: the objects temporarily became difficult to follow against moonlight and a crowded star field, then were reacquired. The submitted photograph apparently showed the region where the lights disappeared rather than the moving objects themselves; two nearby stars may subsequently have been mistaken for them in the image. GEIPAN classified the case A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case shows what moved a report from unusual testimony to firm identification. “Two lights travelling together” was not dismissed as a generic satellite explanation. The hypothesis became persuasive because several independent measurements—time, trajectory, spacing and brightness—converged on a particular orbital pair. Birds produced one of the department’s strangest looking formations The Loire corridor also created misidentifications that were neither technological nor astronomical. At Loire Authion on 23 July 2017, two campers reported a silent stream of lights moving from west to east. The first witness estimated that he had seen roughly a hundred yellow points over two or three"},{"title":"What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows","url":"/what-manches-ufo-record-actually-shows/","description":"Manche has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history. The official record is not dominated by landings, radar interceptions or military confrontations.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows Manche has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history. The official record is not dominated by landings, radar interceptions or military confrontations. Manche has a documented but comparatively modest UFO history. The official record is not dominated by landings, radar interceptions or military confrontations. Instead, it consists mainly of isolated reports of lights, fireballs and apparently structured objects, most of which were later explained or judged too poorly documented to assess. manche Introduction That pattern matters. Manche is a coastal department with busy civilian and military airspace, ports, maritime traffic and long, unobstructed horizons. Such conditions make unusual lights easier to notice, but they also create many opportunities for misidentifying aircraft, condensation trails, balloons, satellites, astronomical objects and atmospheric entries. GEIPAN’s published files therefore tell a less sensational but more useful story: unexplained does not mean extraterrestrial, while an ordinary explanation may become persuasive only after direction, timing, weather and witness evidence have been checked. [Geipan] geipan.fr Depuis 40 ans, le GEIPAN a analysé plus de 8000 témoignages représentant environ 3000 cas d’observations... What the official record contains GEIPAN, the unit of the French space agency CNES responsible for unidentified aerospace phenomena, collects direct witness reports, investigates suitable cases and publishes anonymised files. Its categories distinguish identified or probably identified events from cases lacking reliable information and those that remain unexplained after investigation. GEIPAN stresses that testimony is the starting point of an enquiry, but that estimates of an unknown light’s distance, size and speed are inherently unreliable without familiar reference points. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The published Manche cases form a small and uneven sequence rather than a sustained departmental “flap”. The official list includes reports from Chef du Pont and Saint Germain le Gaillard in 1979; Cerisy la Salle in 1983; Brix in 1989; several locations in 1993 and 1994; Saint Samson de Bonfossé in 2009; Isigny le Buat in 2010; Saint Lô and Tourlaville in 2012; Saint Germain sur Ay in 2013; Saint Côme du Mont in 2014; and Carentan les Marais in 2024. The classifications range from straightforward identifications to cases left unusable through lack of information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This distribution does not show a clear hotspot centred on Cherbourg, the Cotentin coast or any particular installation. Nor does the public record establish a recurring link with radar, military aircraft or the nuclear facilities of northern Cotentin. The geographical spread is better understood as a mixture of sporadic reporting, ordinary aerial activity and occasional observations that could not be reconstructed confidently. Saint Lô in 2012: the strongest unresolved case The sighting at Saint Lô on 4 April 2012 is the most significant officially unresolved Manche case. A witness reported two luminous points in the night sky. According to the GEIPAN summary, one followed an approximately straight course while the second appeared to move around it. The case was classified D1, the label then used for an unexplained phenomenon supported by a moderate or substantial amount of usable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The unusual feature was not merely that two lights were visible. Aircraft, satellites and celestial objects routinely appear as lights at night. What made the account harder to resolve was the reported relative movement: one point seemed to circle or manoeuvre around the other while both progressed across the sky. Investigators could not match that complete description convincingly to a known satellite passage, conventional aircraft configuration or simple astronomical object. Even so, the classification has important limits. It records that GEIPAN did not find a satisfactory explanation from the available information; it does not certify that the lights were solid craft, that their apparent motions were measured independently, or that they displayed technology beyond conventional aviation. The case appears to have relied chiefly on one witness rather than simultaneous radar records, calibrated imagery or several observers at separated locations. A point of light against a dark sky can also appear to drift, stop or change direction because of involuntary eye movements and the absence of stable visual references. GEIPAN specifically warns about these perceptual effects. [Geipan] geipan.fr etape 1 etape 1 Saint Lô therefore deserves to be called unresolved, but not conclusive. It is stronger than a vague recollection and weaker than a multi sensor aviation incident. Later reporting has not produced independent radar data, additional contemporary witnesses or higher quality recordings that materially strengthen the original claim. Its evidential status remains much as it was when classified: genuinely unidentified within the file, but open to ordinary explanations that could not be tested retrospectively. The 1979 reports: striking descriptions, weak reconstruction Two of the earliest published Manche files date from spring 1979. At Chef du Pont on 22 March, the reported phenomenon was a luminous ball making back and forth movements. GEIPAN placed the case in category C because reliable information was insufficient for a firm conclusion. The public case page identifies a gendarmerie record but does not present the event as a solved or strongly evidenced encounter. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A little over a month later, on 27 April, a witness at Saint Germain le Gaillard described the passage of an object of large apparent dimensions. This case was also classified C. That classification is crucial: “large” describes the witness’s impression, not a verified physical measurement. Without a known distance, even a small nearby object or an arrangement of lights can be perceived as something enormous farther away. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The two reports are sometimes tempting to join into a local wave because they occurred within weeks of each other. The available record does not justify that conclusion. The descriptions differ, the supporting material is limited and there is no demonstrated common trajectory, repeated witness group or shared aviation event. They are best treated as two unresolved reports with insufficient data, not as evidence of coordinated activity over the department. The 1983 Cerisy la Salle “ball of fire” belongs to the same weak evidence category. A fiery object may represent a meteor or atmospheric re entry, but the historic file did not contain enough dependable information to establish an identification. GEIPAN explains that bright meteors can remain visible for several seconds, change colour, fragment and appear far closer to the observer than they really are. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Brix in 1989: a dramatic trace with an earthly cause The Brix case of October 1989 is one of Manche’s most instructive incidents because it began with apparently physical evidence. A farmer found a large burnt area in a meadow. Cases involving traces on the ground can seem stronger than sightings of distant lights, since something measurable has clearly occurred. The enquiry, however, did not support an extraordinary aerial event. GEIPAN’s published summary concluded that the damage was probably caused by the accidental ignition of hydrocarbons in part of the field. The file contains an enquiry report, soil analysis, photographs, press material and a police record, yet the final classification was B: probably identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Brix illustrates why the amount of documentation should not be confused with evidence for a UFO. A heavily documented case may become less mysterious as testing progresses. In this instance, the physical trace allowed investigators to consider combustion and contamination rather than relying solely on memory. The later evidence weakened an exotic interpretation instead of strengthening it. It also demonstrates the value of preserving mundane explanations in departmental UFO history. A focus only on unexplained cases would miss how investigations actually work: hypotheses are narrowed by examining soil, burn patterns, local activity and possible fuels. The interesting outcome is not that something inexplicable scorched the field, but that an apparently sensational trace proved compatible with an accidental fire. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K ·"},{"title":"Cases Explained","url":"/cases-explained/","description":"Manche's wider record shows how dramatic reports can remain uncertain, gain ordinary explanations or collapse through lack of evidence.","breadcrumb":"What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows","level":2,"text":"Cases Explained From Fireballs to Burnt Fields in Manche Manche UFO Files Cases Explained Cases Explained From Fireballs to Burnt Fields in Manche Manche's wider record shows how dramatic reports can remain uncertain, gain ordinary explanations or collapse through lack of evidence. Manche's wider record shows how dramatic reports can remain uncertain, gain ordinary explanations or collapse through lack of evidence. dataset evidence The 1979 and 1983 reports with too little evidence How the Brix burnt field received an earthly explanation What recurring explanations reveal about Manche sightings manche cases explained Introduction Manche ’s UFO record is valuable less for spectacular mysteries than for the way cases separate into three very different outcomes: reports that cannot be tested, unusual events with plausible but unproved natural explanations, and incidents largely resolved by physical evidence. The contrast is clearest in the department’s 1979 and 1983 files and in the burnt field discovered near Brix in October 1989. The early sightings sound dramatic: an orange ball making rapid movements, another unexplained report a month later, and a blue fireball that whistled, exploded and frightened cattle. Yet the official files contain too little dependable information to decide what the witnesses actually saw. Brix began with apparently stronger evidence—a large area of scorched ground and a report of intense blue light—but laboratory analysis pointed towards burning petroleum rather than a landed craft. Together, these cases show why vivid testimony and visible traces are not automatically strong evidence, and why an ordinary explanation can emerge only after a proper investigation. The 1979 and 1983 reports had too little evidence France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, classifies cases according to both their residual strangeness and the consistency of the available information. A category C case is not a confirmed anomaly. It means that the material is too weak or incomplete for a reliable conclusion. Missing direction measurements, uncertain duration, no independent witnesses or a report made too late can prevent investigators from testing even a sincere and striking account. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPANA classification methodology consolidated over decades... Since 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C... The repeating orange ball at Chef du Pont At about 11 p.m. on 22 March 1979, a witness at Chef du Pont reported an orange ball high in the sky, apparently about the size of a football at arm’s length. It was said to move horizontally in rapid back and forth motions, then vertically while showing green reflections. According to the official summary, a similar display occurred in the same place and at roughly the same time the following night. [Geipan] geipan.fr CHEF DU PONT (50) 22.03.1979CHEF DU PONT (50) 22.03.1979. Date d'observation. 22/03/1979. Région. Basse Normandie. Département. Man... That repetition sounds important, because a phenomenon returning on schedule might suggest a regular astronomical object, a distant light seen through unstable air, or recurring activity along the same route. But the file lacks the measurements needed to test those possibilities. There was no second witness, no reliable angular position, no usable estimate of duration and no evidence linking the reported light to a physical object. The description of a football sized ball also illustrates a common problem in night sky reports. Without a known distance, an apparent size cannot be converted into an actual size. A small nearby light and an enormous distant object can occupy the same angle in the observer’s field of view. Apparent darting or oscillation may also be exaggerated when a bright point is watched against a dark, featureless sky, although the surviving evidence is insufficient to show that this happened at Chef du Pont. GEIPAN therefore did not identify the object, but neither did it judge the report strong enough to preserve as an unexplained case. It remains category C: an account that cannot now support much beyond the fact that the witness saw something unusual. Saint Germain le Gaillard added another incomplete report A second Manche observation was recorded at Saint Germain le Gaillard on 27 April 1979. The public GEIPAN index likewise classifies it as category C because of insufficient reliable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANSAINT GERMAIN LE GAILLARD (50) 27.04.1979 27/04/1979 C Manche Manque d'informations fiables 22/0... Its significance lies mainly in what it does not establish. Two reports within five weeks can look like the beginning of a local wave, but proximity in date is not enough to demonstrate a shared cause. The available record does not show matching trajectories, colours, durations, witness groups or environmental conditions. There is no documented radar confirmation, aircraft encounter or physical trace connecting the two incidents. For Manche’s wider history, the pair should therefore be treated as scattered reports rather than a proven 1979 cluster. They may have involved different stimuli entirely. Once the missing observational detail is recognised, the apparent pattern becomes much weaker. The Cerisy la Salle fireball The event dated 29 January 1983, associated in GEIPAN’s file with Cerisy la Salle and witness statements from the Savigny area, was more dramatic. Two farmers reported a peculiar bluish luminous phenomenon. One described a fireball approximately the apparent size of a ball, moving horizontally, producing a whistling sound and then exploding. The livestock were reportedly alarmed. Investigators searched for ground traces but obtained no further physical information. [Geipan] geipan.fr CERISY LA SALLE (50) 1983CERISY LA SALLE (50) 1983; Date d'observation. 29/01/1983; Région. Basse Normandie; Département. Manche... GEIPAN noted that the description suggested a manifestation of lightning. A rare luminous electrical phenomenon, sometimes loosely called ball lightning, could account for the glowing appearance, short duration and association with an explosive sound. However, the file does not contain enough meteorological or physical evidence to demonstrate that explanation. It remains a possibility, not a solved identification. The case is nevertheless weaker than its vivid narrative suggests. No residue, impact point or damaged object was found. The apparent horizontal movement and size were estimates made during a brief, startling event. The whistle and explosion could have been part of the same phenomenon, a nearby electrical discharge, or sounds whose exact sequence was difficult to reconstruct afterwards. Cerisy la Salle was therefore also placed in category C. This does not imply that nothing happened or that the farmers invented the event. It means the surviving evidence cannot distinguish securely among an unusual electrical event, another short lived atmospheric phenomenon or a misunderstood combination of light and sound. [Geipan] geipan.fr CERISY LA SALLE (50) 1983CERISY LA SALLE (50) 1983; Date d'observation. 29/01/1983; Région. Basse Normandie; Département. Manche... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The Brix burnt field gained an earthly explanation The Brix case initially appeared stronger because investigators were not dealing only with a distant light. On the morning of 27 October 1989, a farmer discovered a large, irregular burnt area in a pasture. It measured roughly 17 metres in length and up to four metres across. Vegetation had been destroyed, the soil was blackened and parts of the surface appeared sunken by about 10 to 15 centimetres. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A second witness told gendarmes that during the night of 25–26 October a dog had behaved unusually and that an intense bluish beam had been visible above the relevant field. A 20 kilovolt power line ran approximately 50 metres from the mark. Those details gave the incident several features commonly associated with a supposed landing case: disturbed animals, anomalous light and a substantial physical trace. Unlike the 1979 reports, however, the Brix evidence could be sampled and tested. SEPRA, the predecessor of today’s GEIPAN, attended the site after the local gendarmerie was unable to determine the cause. Investigators took soil cores, documented the mark and arranged laboratory analysis. The results changed the character of the case: The soil had been burnt to a depth of roughly two to three centimetres. Grey discolouration in the clay indicated exposure to considerable heat. Samples from the affected area gave off a strong petroleum smell. Analysis confirmed the presence of kerosene. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The official hypothesis was that fuel had been released by an"},{"title":"Saint Lo 2012","url":"/saint-lo-2012/","description":"Two lights moving in an unusual relationship made Saint-Lo the strongest officially unexplained UFO case in Manche.","breadcrumb":"What Manche's UFO Record Actually Shows","level":2,"text":"Saint Lo 2012 Why the Saint Lo Lights Remain Unexplained Manche UFO Files Saint Lo 2012 Saint Lo 2012 Why the Saint Lo Lights Remain Unexplained Two lights moving in an unusual relationship made Saint Lo the strongest officially unexplained UFO case in Manche. Two lights moving in an unusual relationship made Saint Lo the strongest officially unexplained UFO case in Manche. event time window What the witness reported Why investigators could not identify the lights What the evidence does and does not prove manche saint lo 2012 Introduction On 4 April 2012, at about 8.15 pm, a man standing in his garden on the southern outskirts of Saint Lô saw two bright points cross the twilight sky. One moved steadily; the other appeared to loop around it, make abrupt angular turns, approach it and then fall rapidly behind. The display lasted only about 15 seconds before both lights vanished into or behind cloud. France’s official investigators could not match the reported movements to aircraft, satellites, balloons, astronomical objects or other familiar causes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT LO (50) 04.04.2012 GEIPAN... That conclusion makes Saint Lô the strongest officially unexplained case in Manche ’s published record. It does not establish that an extraordinary craft was present. The case rests on one brief visual observation, with no photograph, video or matching radar track. Its importance lies instead in the unusual relationship between the lights and in the unusually detailed attempt to test ordinary explanations. GEIPAN’s present file retains the case in classification D — unidentified after investigation — while the investigation report uses the older, more specific label D1. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT LO (50) 04.04.2012 GEIPAN... What the witness reported The witness was outside smoking a cigarette when two small yellowish white lights attracted his attention. The sky was reportedly clear around him, although a large cloud lay towards the north. The lights travelled across roughly 30 degrees of sky, from an easterly direction towards the north, and remained continuously visible until they reached the cloud. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The first light was not especially remarkable. It followed what the witness described as a straight, even course. The second was the reason the event became difficult to classify. It appeared to move repeatedly around the first, tracing ellipses, making sharp changes of direction and briefly coming into contact with it before dropping back. Both were described as very bright, point like and silent, with no visible trail or halo. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The sighting ended quickly. After approximately 15 seconds, the lights disappeared through or behind the northern cloud. The witness moved within the garden to look beyond it, but they did not reappear. He could not estimate their distance, altitude or physical size, which is unsurprising for isolated lights viewed against the sky without a known reference object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Several features strengthen the account as testimony. The witness completed GEIPAN’s questionnaire on 6 April, only two days after the event, and had made notes and photographic reconstructions soon afterwards. He did not claim to know what the lights were, and the file records that he tried to consider the experience rationally rather than immediately assigning it an exotic origin. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... There were also major limitations from the outset. He was the only witness. No image was taken during the sighting, no instrument was used and no physical effect was reported. The entire case therefore depends on how accurately one person perceived and remembered a very brief pattern of lights at an unknown distance. GEIPAN nevertheless judged the report sufficiently detailed to investigate rather than placing it among cases that cannot be assessed for lack of information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Why ordinary explanations did not fit GEIPAN first reviewed the case remotely, then commissioned an investigator to visit the site. The investigation included measurements of the viewing directions, weather and astronomical checks, a request for military radar information, and a later cognitive interview intended to recover the witness’s recollection without leading him. The formal investigation report was completed in June 2014. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The investigation did not show that every conventional explanation was physically impossible. Rather, none available to GEIPAN reproduced the complete report: two bright points travelling together, with one apparently making rapid non linear movements around the other. Astronomical objects were a poor match. The sighting occurred before sunset — calculated in the report as 8.34 pm at Caen — and the principal visible astronomical object in the relevant part of the sky was a nearly full Moon to the east south east. A star, planet or the Moon could not account for two lights crossing a substantial portion of the sky in 15 seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A bright satellite pass also failed to match. Investigators considered an Iridium satellite flare, which could briefly appear exceptionally bright, but their satellite check found no negative magnitude pass at the relevant time. More importantly, a single satellite flare would not explain one point repeatedly circling or sharply changing position around another. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Space debris was judged unlikely. A re entry can produce several lights moving together and can sometimes give the impression of a formation. GEIPAN argued that a conspicuous re entry would probably have generated reports across a much wider area. The direction described in the file was also considered inconsistent with the expected motion of a re entering object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Windborne lights did not fit the reported speed or direction. Balloons and sky lanterns move with the wind and generally change their relative positions gradually. Regional data indicated northerly to north easterly winds, whereas the reported lights crossed the sky rapidly and one seemed to execute abrupt manoeuvres. The same incompatibility weakened other drifting atmosphere proposals. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Aircraft were the most obvious comparison, but no satisfactory match emerged. A pair of aircraft seen at different distances can appear to cross or change relative positions, especially when only their lights are visible. Aerobatic or model aircraft could also produce curved paths. However, the witness heard no engines, and the reported sharp turns seemed unlike the apparent motion expected from ordinary aircraft over a residential area. The investigators also considered ultralights, drones and model aircraft but rated these explanations as very weak. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The weakness of the aircraft explanation depends heavily on accepting the witness’s description literally. At an unknown range, a modest change in the viewing geometry of two independent lights can appear much more dramatic than their actual movement. GEIPAN’s report recognised that the available direction and trajectory data were too limited for an objective reconstruction. This is an important qualification: investigators could reject specific matches, but they could not calculate the lights’ true speeds, separation or flight paths. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete354 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... 13:48 Navy pilots describe encounters with UFOs Channel: 60 Minutes · Views: 13.2M · Uploaded: May 2021 · Length: 13 minutes Open on YouTube What the radar check established The radar enquiry is the most substantial element distinguishing Saint Lô from a routine unexplained light report. GEIPAN asked military air traffic control for recorded tracks around Saint Lô between 8.00 and 8.30 pm. The returned plot contained four identified general aviation tracks and two detections without complete identifying"},{"title":"Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven","url":"/why-marnes-strangest-ufo-cases-remain/","description":"Marne has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but it does not have a single, well-supported “landmark UFO case” comparable with the...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven Marne has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but it does not have a single, well supported “landmark UFO case” comparable with the... Marne has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but it does not have a single, well supported “landmark UFO case” comparable with the best known French incidents. marne Introduction Marne has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but it does not have a single, well supported “landmark UFO case” comparable with the best known French incidents. Its history is instead a revealing mixture of brief lights, objects seen from moving cars, suspected vehicle effects, ground marks and reports made near places associated with aviation or the military. After investigation, many have been attributed to the Moon, Venus, meteors, balloons, helicopters, satellites, lanterns or artificial lighting. Many others remain unclassified only because the surviving information is too weak to test properly. That distinction is crucial. In the current records of GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, a case marked “C” does not mean that an extraordinary craft was established. It normally means that reliable information is missing. Marne’s most intriguing reports, including the Broyes–Sommesous vehicle incidents and a triangular light observation over Reims, fall mainly into this evidentially frustrating category rather than the strongest unresolved class. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... What the official record actually contains GEIPAN’s published catalogue includes several dozen entries associated with Marne, concentrated mainly from the late 1970s onwards. Reims appears repeatedly, alongside places such as Bétheny, Châlons en Champagne, Broyes, Sommesous, Sézanne, Fismes, Suippes, Avize and Mareuil le Port. The list should not be read as a census of alien looking objects: it is a record of reports submitted or inherited by the official investigation service, including cases later identified and reports that proved too incomplete to assess. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPAN classifies reports by considering two separate qualities: the residual strangeness after possible explanations have been examined, and the “consistency” or evidential strength of the file. Class A means an identified phenomenon; B indicates a probable explanation; C denotes insufficient or unreliable information; D is reserved for a genuinely unexplained report supported by evidence of adequate quality. Older files may use D1 and D2 subdivisions. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... This framework helps explain Marne’s overall pattern: Identified cases include astronomical objects, meteors, the International Space Station and satellite trains. Probably identified cases include weather balloons, lanterns, helicopters and projected lights. Category C cases often contain striking descriptions but lack independent witnesses, precise directions, photographs, instrument records or complete contemporary statements. Strong category D cases are conspicuously absent from the main modern Marne group visible in GEIPAN’s catalogue. The department’s mystery lies chiefly in incomplete documentation, not in a body of well corroborated extraordinary events. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Broyes and Sommesous vehicle incidents The most memorable Marne file concerns two motorists who separately reported brilliant light and apparent vehicle disruption around the turn of 2000. The incidents were initially treated as especially unusual, but later review weakened rather than strengthened their official status. On 31 December 1999, a motorist travelling near Sommesous reported a powerful light and disturbances affecting the vehicle. Ten days later, at about 9 pm on 10 January 2000, another driver on the D39 near the Broyes water tower said an intense light entered the car through its open roof. According to the account, the engine and radio stopped, although the ignition remained on. After roughly a minute the light faded, the radio resumed operation and the driver was able to restart the car. The two locations are in southern Marne, and GEIPAN treated the reports together because of their broadly similar descriptions and close dates. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000BROYES (51) 10.01.2000. Résumé. Observations à quelques jours d'écart par deux automobilistes d'une lumière v... The Broyes account has the features that make a UFO story compelling: a close seeming light, a frightened witness and an apparent physical effect. Yet the later investigation found that the case file lacked the material needed to establish what happened. Although the gendarmerie and SEPRA, GEIPAN’s predecessor, had investigated soon after the event, the retained documentation contained only a brief summary. There were no complete recordings or transcripts of the interviews and reconstructions, no instrument data from the car and no independently recorded measurement of the light. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete189 Compte rendu enquete189 Investigators could not offer a convincing ordinary physical explanation for the Broyes description. However, they also could not demonstrate that the engine and radio failures were caused by an external aerial phenomenon rather than a mechanical fault, a misunderstood sequence of events or details intensified in recollection. The result was a reassessment from the stronger unexplained category to C: highly strange as narrated, but poorly supported. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000BROYES (51) 10.01.2000. Résumé. Observations à quelques jours d'écart par deux automobilistes d'une lumière v... This is perhaps the clearest lesson in Marne’s UFO record. Strangeness and evidential strength are not the same thing. A dramatic account can remain unexplained because investigators lack information, while still being too weak to support any exceptional conclusion. Reims: repeated reports without a decisive case As Marne’s largest city, Reims naturally generates more reports than small rural communes. It also has a strong aviation association through the former Air Base 112 at Bétheny and the wider air traffic environment. That setting can make military or radar interpretations seem attractive, but the published Marne files do not establish a sustained sequence of confirmed military encounters. One of the more distinctive Reims reports occurred on 15 November 1979, when a witness described a luminous triangular object in motion. GEIPAN classifies the case C because the information available is insufficient. The shape is noteworthy because triangular UFO reports later became prominent during the Belgian wave of 1989–91, but resemblance in shape alone does not connect the Reims report to those events. Without strong positional data, multiple independent statements, photographs or radar confirmation, “triangle” remains a witness description rather than evidence of a particular type of aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv Other Reims reports demonstrate how ordinary sources can acquire unusual appearances: On 5 February 1977, a stationary, silent light towards the west was identified as Venus. On 23 January 1981, a round glow in the eastern sky was identified as the Moon. A 2009 group of photographed lights was attributed to photographic artefacts. Lights reported in April 2011 were considered likely to have come from projected laser displays. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv export cas pub 20251127093552.csv These solutions are not dismissals of the witnesses. Venus can appear extraordinarily bright close to the horizon, especially when thin cloud, haze and atmospheric turbulence make it seem to pulse or move. The Moon can look unfamiliar when partly obscured. Camera reflections and internal lens effects can produce shapes that were not visible in the sky itself. Projected lights may appear to travel across cloud without an obvious source on the ground. The military context therefore matters mainly as an investigative question: were aircraft operating, were radar or air traffic records checked, and was the sighting in a plausible approach corridor? It should not be treated"},{"title":"Vehicle Cases","url":"/vehicle-cases/","description":"Two drivers reported intense lights and vehicle failures, but missing records later weakened Marne's most dramatic UFO case.","breadcrumb":"Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven","level":2,"text":"Vehicle Cases Did a Bright Light Disable Two Cars? Marne UFOs Vehicle Cases Vehicle Cases Did a Bright Light Disable Two Cars? Two drivers reported intense lights and vehicle failures, but missing records later weakened Marne's most dramatic UFO case. Two drivers reported intense lights and vehicle failures, but missing records later weakened Marne's most dramatic UFO case. event time window What the two drivers reported How investigators tested the claims Why the cases were downgraded marne vehicle cases Introduction The Broyes and Sommesous reports are Marne ’s best known claims that an unexplained light interfered with moving cars. Two lone drivers, ten days apart around New Year 2000, described a sudden white light accompanied by engine failure or dashboard disruption. The apparent repetition made the incidents look stronger than an isolated sighting and initially helped them receive an unexplained classification. Later review produced a less dramatic result. Investigators found no physical object, damage, instrument reading or independently documented electrical disturbance. The Broyes inquiry had been extensive, but most of its interviews and reconstruction records were missing from the surviving file. Sommesous received almost no follow up at all, and its witness came forward only after hearing about Broyes. GEIPAN therefore reclassified both reports as category C: cases with significant strangeness but insufficient reliable information, rather than established unexplained vehicle effects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000 GEIPAN... What the two drivers reported The first incident occurred near Sommesous on 31 December 1999, between about 7.30 and 8 pm. A woman driving alone at roughly 90 kilometres per hour on the N4 reported seeing a brief whitish flash ahead. She said it was not dazzling and initially paid little attention to it. Almost immediately, every warning light on the dashboard illuminated and the engine stopped, leaving the car coasting. According to her statement, normal operation returned within a fraction of a second and the engine restarted without her turning the key. She heard no sound and saw no defined object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... The driver later had the battery terminals checked at her father’s suggestion, but the mechanic reportedly found nothing abnormal. That check is useful, but limited: it does not establish that a light affected the car, nor does it exclude a brief ignition, fuel system or electrical fault that had disappeared before inspection. No detailed mechanical report, component testing or examination of the vehicle under comparable conditions survives in the case file. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSOMMESOUS (51) 31.12.1999 GEIPAN... The more striking event occurred near Broyes on 10 January 2000, at about 9 pm. A young man driving alone on the D39 near the village water tower said a very intense light suddenly entered through his open sunroof and left him unable to see. He reported that the engine and radio stopped while the ignition remained on. After the light disappeared, the dashboard and radio returned to normal and he restarted the car. He went home visibly frightened, and his parents contacted the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000 GEIPAN... The initial police statement gave the duration as roughly one minute. During a later reconstruction, however, three drives through the location reduced the estimated interval to between 15 and 30 seconds. That correction does not disprove the experience, especially after a frightening event, but it shows why contemporaneous reconstructions matter: subjective time can expand sharply under stress. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... Neither witness described a structured craft, outline, movement across the sky or identifiable source. The shared core was narrower: an unlocated white light and a vehicle malfunction occurring at about the same time. This distinction matters because later summaries sometimes turn the reports into encounters with an object that neither driver actually claimed to have seen. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... How investigators tested the claims The immediate response to Broyes was comparatively serious. Gendarmes visited the driver’s home, took his statement and accompanied him back to the location. They photographed the road and vehicle, questioned neighbours and contacted the electricity service, Reims Air Base 112 and SEPRA, the CNES unit then responsible for unusual aerospace reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... At the site, officers noted a clear, strongly starry sky, visible aviation traffic and no public lighting. They found no reported disturbance on the nearby electrical line. Air Base 112 said that no military flight had been scheduled that evening, although the later GEIPAN review observed that the surviving SEPRA report did not preserve the full results of every civil and military aviation enquiry. The absence of a scheduled military flight therefore removes one obvious possibility but does not amount to comprehensive exclusion of all aircraft, vehicles or powerful lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000 GEIPAN... SEPRA investigators arrived on 13 January, only three days after the Broyes event. Their work included interviews with the witness and his family, a visit to his doctor and employer, two reconstructions at the scene and a joint debriefing with the gendarmerie. The contemporary three page report described the witness’s versions as mutually consistent apart from the revised duration. Family members, his doctor, employer and the investigating officer regarded him as normally calm and serious and believed that something had genuinely upset him. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That is meaningful evidence about sincerity and emotional response. It is not, however, proof of the cause. A witness may be honest, frightened and mistaken at the same time. The report established that the young man appeared deeply affected; it did not establish that an external aerial phenomenon had disabled the car. The investigation also failed to identify a local source capable of illuminating the vehicle as described. The road had no public lighting, and a daylight inspection did not reveal an obvious installation that could project a bright beam down through the sunroof. SEPRA’s provisional conclusion in January 2000 was therefore that no answer could then be given for the reported event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Sommesous was handled very differently. The second witness was discovered when gendarmes visiting the electricity office discussed Broyes with her manager. She then mentioned her own experience and was interviewed on 11 January. No investigators returned with her to the N4, the exact location was not established, and neither SEPRA nor the gendarmerie conducted the type of reconstruction, environmental checks or vehicle examination performed for Broyes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... The pairing of the cases therefore created an impression of corroboration stronger than the underlying records support. They were not independent simultaneous observations of one light. They occurred on different roads, roughly 30 kilometres apart, ten days apart, and the Sommesous statement was made after its witness had heard about the Broyes story. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word CR AC JPA MP.docx... Where the strongest evidence stops The best supported elements are modest but important. Both witnesses made formal statements. The Broyes report prompted rapid police and SEPRA involvement, and the driver’s distress was observed by several people. A reconstruction refined his timing, while basic checks found no public lighting, known power line disturbance or scheduled military flight that readily accounted for his description. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000 GEIPAN... What is missing is equally important: No photograph, video, radar return or physical trace records either light. No passenger or roadside witness independently saw the Broyes event. No defined object, direction, angular size or trajectory was reported. No detailed diagnostic examination linked either vehicle failure to an external electromagnetic cause. Sommesous lacked a precise location, scene reconstruction and prompt technical investigation. Much of the original Broyes investigation survives only as a short summary rather than recordings, interview notes and complete reconstruction data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBROYES (51) 10.01.2000 GEIPAN... The missing Broyes records are especially damaging because the inquiry apparently generated richer material than is now available. The 2018 review says that photographs and maps remained"},{"title":"Official Record","url":"/official-record-00305c/","description":"Marne's official files show how planets, meteors, lights and weak documentation can turn unusual reports into identified or inconclusive cases.","breadcrumb":"Why Marne's Strangest UFO Cases Remain Unproven","level":2,"text":"Official Record What Marne's Official UFO Files Actually Show Marne UFOs Official Record Official Record What Marne's Official UFO Files Actually Show Marne's official files show how planets, meteors, lights and weak documentation can turn unusual reports into identified or inconclusive cases. Marne's official files show how planets, meteors, lights and weak documentation can turn unusual reports into identified or inconclusive cases. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classes A to D work Recurring explanations across Marne Why category C does not mean extraordinary marne official record Introduction Marne ’s official UFO record is less a catalogue of enduring mysteries than a record of how unusual experiences are tested, explained and graded. The files published by GEIPAN, the unit of France’s national space agency that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, include reports attributed to Venus, the Moon, meteors, the International Space Station, satellite trains, balloons, lanterns, lasers, helicopters, drones and even photographic artefacts. A large second group is labelled category C—not because officials found evidence of extraordinary craft, but because the information was too incomplete or unreliable for a defensible conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr le présent niveau d'étrangeté et de...Read more... That distinction changes how Marne’s UFO history should be read. A vivid account may finish as category A when astronomical or technical checks provide a close match. A comparatively ordinary looking light may remain category C when the direction, time or witness statement is too vague to investigate. Classification therefore measures the state of the evidence after enquiry, not how dramatic the original story sounds. How GEIPAN’s A to D system works GEIPAN assesses two qualities separately. The first is residual strangeness : how unusual the event remains after realistic explanations have been considered. The second is consistency : how strong and usable the evidence is. Consistency can be increased by independent witnesses, precise timings and viewing directions, photographs, video, radar information, physical traces or a detailed contemporary statement. It is weakened by delayed recollection, uncertain dates, vague locations, missing documents and the absence of information needed to reconstruct the sky or local environment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... The principal classifications can be understood in plain terms: Category A means the reported phenomenon has been identified with little or no meaningful ambiguity. Category B means GEIPAN considers one conventional explanation highly probable, although the match is not strong enough for category A. Category C means the report cannot be analysed adequately because important information is absent or unreliable. Category D is reserved for a report that remains unexplained despite evidence sufficient for a meaningful investigation. Earlier versions of the system divided unresolved cases into D1 and D2. D1 indicated an unexplained case of moderate consistency, while D2 required stronger evidence, such as several independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. GEIPAN has also stressed that classifications can change when old cases are re examined or new information appears. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr baisse cas d baisse cas d This explains an apparently surprising feature of Marne’s files: some reports once regarded as unexplained have later become straightforward category A cases. Better astronomical software, satellite records, mapping tools and systematic reconstruction can test possibilities that were difficult to verify when the original gendarmerie statement was taken. Astronomical objects produced some of the strongest illusions Planets and the Moon account for several memorable Marne reports because they can appear exceptionally bright, coloured, enlarged or apparently mobile when seen near the horizon. Long observation does not necessarily protect a witness from misidentification. In fact, a distant astronomical object can remain visible for much longer than an aircraft or meteor, giving the observer time to develop an impression that it is hovering or following them. A Reims report from 5 February 1977 illustrates the process. Two witnesses watched a silent luminous ball towards the west for an extended period. The file had originally been placed in category C, but a later review compared the reported direction, duration, appearance and brightness with the sky at the time. Venus occupied the relevant part of the sky and was unusually conspicuous. GEIPAN therefore reclassified the observation as category A. The review did not accuse the witnesses of invention or faulty eyesight; it concluded that they had accurately seen a bright object but misinterpreted what it was. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Suippes case of 16 May 1982 is even more instructive because it once carried an unexplained classification. Two women travelling by car described an orange, half moon shaped light that appeared to remain over the town and then to follow them in a series of sideways movements. Re examination established that the Moon was rising in the observed direction. Its apparent pursuit of the vehicle was consistent with the familiar parallax illusion in which a very distant object seems to retain the same position relative to a moving observer. The case was changed from category D to category A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Other Marne entries reinforce the pattern. GEIPAN identifies a 1979 report at Merfy as the Moon, a 1981 Reims light as the Moon and a 2007 Moussy observation as Venus. These cases show why investigators need an accurate time, direction and viewing position: without them, an astronomical explanation may be strongly suspected but impossible to demonstrate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr le présent niveau d'étrangeté et de...Read more... Brief moving lights were matched to meteors, spacecraft and satellites Reports involving fast or steadily moving points of light can often be checked against time specific records. This is where modern cases have an advantage over many older Marne files: satellite passes, launches and major meteors leave searchable external traces. At Dormans on 19 November 2009, the reported moving light was identified as the International Space Station. Such passes can look unusual because the station is silent, very bright and apparently unaccompanied by the navigation lights expected on an aircraft. Its visibility and path, however, are predictable, allowing a category A identification when the witness timing and trajectory match. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN A far more visually striking example occurred on the RN4 from Brugny Vaudancourt towards Nancy on 27 March 2021. A motorist reported between roughly 50 and 100 silent white lights arranged one above another. GEIPAN identified the display as a Starlink satellite train. The incident demonstrates how new technologies repeatedly create new UFO forms: a line or procession of recently deployed satellites can look coordinated and artificial because it is coordinated and artificial, but it is not an unknown aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN At Les Petites Loges on 8 November 2022, a very short observation of a rapidly moving luminous point was classified as a meteor. Duration is crucial in such cases. A light crossing part of the sky in seconds is more compatible with a meteor than with a planet, while the availability of reports from other locations can show that several witnesses observed the same atmospheric event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN These identifications are not based solely on resemblance. A convincing match normally combines the object’s direction, apparent speed, duration and time with external astronomical or spaceflight information. When one of those elements is missing, the same type of report may remain category C rather than being promoted to a speculative explanation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons, lanterns and aircraft explained slower objects Slow, silent or apparently stationary objects form another recurring Marne group. These are often harder to identify with complete certainty because their paths depend on local wind, viewing geometry and human activity. They therefore frequently receive category B rather than category A. On 12 November 1977, two people at Châlons sur Marne observed an elongated luminous object that appeared initially stationary and"},{"title":"Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test","url":"/mayennes-ufo-stories-under-the-evidence/","description":"Mayenne’s UFO history is real in the limited sense that residents have reported unusual lights and apparent objects, newspapers have preserved several...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test Mayenne’s UFO history is real in the limited sense that residents have reported unusual lights and apparent objects, newspapers have preserved several... Mayenne’s UFO history is real in the limited sense that residents have reported unusual lights and apparent objects, newspapers have preserved several striking claims, and France’s official investigation system records cases from the department. mayenne Introduction The department’s best known story is the alleged landing near Méral during France’s great flying saucer wave of October 1954. It became memorable because the witness reportedly described a luminous domed craft, a dark figure inside and a sticky white deposit on his clothes. Yet the surviving record is largely a chain of press reports and later UFO catalogues, with conflicting dates and details and no preserved scientific examination of the supposed material. Later official cases are less dramatic: a 1985 report at Bais was classed as probably identified, while a 2009 light at Villiers Charlemagne remained unclassified because the information was too weak. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne... The 1954 wave reached Mayenne The national setting matters because Mayenne’s most famous reports did not appear in isolation. France experienced an exceptional burst of “flying saucer” stories in the autumn of 1954, with newspapers publishing accounts of discs, cigars, orange globes, landed objects and supposed occupants. UFO researchers later treated that period as one of the defining European sighting waves, while sociological interpretations have stressed the role of publicity, expectation and the repeated circulation of familiar imagery. [agence martienne.fr] editions.agence martienne.fr À peine figurent ils sur les étals des libraires qu’une exceptionnelle vague de soucoupes déferle sur la France; on dénombre jusqu’à 60... One early Mayenne linked report concerned a luminous disc said to have been visible around Angers, Le Lion d’Angers and Château Gontier at roughly the same time. The surviving summaries describe a brief regional observation rather than a close encounter, and they are not sufficiently detailed to reconstruct the object’s path, angular size, weather conditions or possible astronomical and aviation explanations. Its value is therefore mainly historical: it shows that Mayenne was already being drawn into the wider western French reporting wave before the Méral story became prominent. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Un objet en forme de disque a été observé (aucune date n'est mentionnée) simultanément par des personnes différentes, vers 20h20, volant... The weakness is typical of many 1954 cases. A report repeated in several catalogues can look well corroborated, but multiple later references may all descend from one short news item. Repetition is not the same as independent confirmation. Méral: Mayenne’s landmark claim The Méral incident is the department’s central UFO story because it combines nearly every feature that made the 1954 wave famous: an apparent landing, a structured object, intense light, a possible occupant and a claimed physical residue. In the fullest commonly repeated version, a farmer near the road between Méral and Saint Poix saw an orange luminous form descend and settle in a field. On approaching, he reportedly perceived a flattened dome or disc with a level underside, bright enough to illuminate the surrounding countryside. A dark human like silhouette was said to be visible through the object. After several minutes, the light changed colour, the object departed rapidly towards the north, and a glowing cloud or filaments descended over the site. The witness later claimed that his clothes were coated in a white, slightly sticky substance resembling paraffin. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne... Those details explain the case’s lasting appeal. It was not merely a distant point of light: as retold, it offered a close range craft, an occupant and material evidence. The story was cited by influential UFO writers, including Aimé Michel and Jacques Vallée, and entered catalogues of landings, entities and physical trace cases. A local Château Gontier newspaper was among the contemporary publications later listed as carrying the account. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne... Why the record is less solid than the story The case’s evidential problems are substantial. First, the date is unstable. Different sources place the event on 10, 11 or 14 October 1954. Some versions describe an early morning observation, while others place it at nightfall or around 8 pm. The reported object also varies in diameter, duration and appearance. Such changes do not prove that nothing happened, but they show that the surviving narrative was not transmitted consistently. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne... Second, the witness’s alleged residue was never preserved in a documented chain of custody or analysed in a way that can now be checked. The substance was said to disappear, leaving no recoverable sample. Claims of physical evidence therefore rest entirely on testimony rather than laboratory results. Third, the widely cited “independent” sources are often later compilations repeating earlier press material. Catalogues can help trace how a story developed, but they cannot replace an original signed statement, photographs, gendarmerie records, weather data or a site investigation. Finally, the case predates the creation of France’s official UFO investigation body by more than two decades. No surviving GEIPAN case file provides a modern reconstruction of the incident. The honest assessment is therefore that Méral is historically important but evidentially fragile: unresolved as a story, not established as an extraordinary event. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the official files contain France created its national investigation programme within the French space agency, CNES, in 1977. Today it operates as GEIPAN, which collects witness reports, works with police, gendarmerie, aviation and scientific specialists, and publishes anonymised files and conclusions. Its task is to identify reported phenomena where possible, not to prove extraterrestrial visitation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPAN’s classifications are important when reading Mayenne cases. A case can be identified or probably identified, left open because the information is insufficient, or remain unexplained after investigation. An “unidentified” label may therefore mean that the file is too incomplete to decide, rather than that investigators found strong evidence of an exotic craft. Nationally, CNES reports that the large majority of cases are identified, probably identified or lack adequate data; only a small fraction remain unexplained after investigation. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES Bais, 21 February 1985 GEIPAN’s database records a Mayenne case at Bais on 21 February 1985 with classification B, meaning that the phenomenon was considered probably identified. The public search listing does not currently provide a useful description of what the witness saw or state the proposed explanation beyond describing it as an identified phenomenon that is not specified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANBAIS (53) 1985 21/02/1985 B Mayenne Phénomène identifié non indiqué 22/03/2007 1985 02 21TOURC... That limited entry should not be inflated. It confirms an official report and an assessment leaning towards a conventional cause, but it does not support a detailed narrative about the object, witnesses or investigation. Villiers Charlemagne, August 2009 The most accessible modern Mayenne file concerns a witness at Villiers Charlemagne who reported a round white light moving unusually in the sky without flashing or audible sound. The account was submitted long after the event, and even the precise date was uncertain. No supporting witness was found. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLIERS CHARLEMAGNE (53) .08.2009 GEIPAN... Investigators considered an aircraft or helicopter and activity associated with a nearby quarry, but the delay and lack of precise timing prevented meaningful checks. GEIPAN classified the report C because reliable information was missing. This is a good example of the difference between unexplained and unexplainable : the agency did not"},{"title":"Méral Case","url":"/méral-case/","description":"Mayenne's most famous UFO story combines a luminous craft, an alleged occupant and physical residue, but its surviving evidence is fragile.","breadcrumb":"Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test","level":2,"text":"Méral Case Did a UFO Really Land Near Méral? Mayenne Méral Case Méral Case Did a UFO Really Land Near Méral? Mayenne's most famous UFO story combines a luminous craft, an alleged occupant and physical residue, but its surviving evidence is fragile. Mayenne's most famous UFO story combines a luminous craft, an alleged occupant and physical residue, but its surviving evidence is fragile. event time window The witness story and reported landing Conflicting dates, details and source chains Why the physical residue claim remains unproven mayenne Introduction The alleged landing near Méral on 11 October 1954 is Mayenne ’s best known UFO story, but it is not a strong physical evidence case. A local labourer, Baptiste Potin, reportedly saw an orange light descend near the road towards Saint Poix, take on the appearance of a luminous domed disc and reveal a dark silhouette within it. After the object departed, he was said to have found a sticky white deposit on his clothes. What survives, however, is principally a newspaper story copied into other newspapers and later UFO catalogues. No sample of the white material is known to have been preserved, no laboratory report has surfaced, and no contemporary technical investigation can be reconstructed. Even basic details—date, time, witness name, direction of departure and the behaviour of the residue—changed as the story passed from one source to another. The case therefore matters less as proof of a landing than as a clear example of how a vivid local report became more definite in repeated retelling. The reported encounter on the Saint Poix road The fullest early account identifies the witness as Baptiste Potin, aged 40 and living in Méral. At about 6 a.m. on Monday 11 October 1954, he was cycling from the village towards his workplace at the farm of Les Barres. While climbing the road in the direction of Saint Poix, he reportedly noticed a bright orange ball high ahead of him. It seemed to descend as he approached the top of the hill. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... The light was then said to settle near a transformer roughly 600 metres away, apparently blocking his route. Once on or close to the ground, it looked less like a sphere and more like a flattened disc topped by a dome. The earliest detailed versions give a diameter of about four to six metres and say its glare illuminated hedges, trees and surrounding countryside for approximately 200 metres. The object appeared translucent, “like nylon” in one account, with an indistinct dark form visible in its central or domed section. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... Potin reportedly stopped at a distance because he was frightened. After eight to ten minutes, the object became redder, rose without making a sound and accelerated towards the north west, broadly in the direction of Le Pertre. He then continued along the road and passed the place where it had apparently been. A luminous mist or cloud was said to remain there, releasing something compared with glittering snow. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... At Les Barres, Potin reportedly noticed white fluff or a wax like coating on his clothing. It was described as slightly sticky between the fingers and difficult to brush away, leaving white streaks on the fabric. A local newspaper added that the witness was so shaken that he took several days’ rest. Contemporary reporting presented him as a respected local man whose neighbours regarded him as trustworthy, although this character endorsement was not accompanied by a recorded interview, medical examination or formal assessment of his observational reliability. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... This account contains the elements that made Méral unusually memorable within Mayenne: an apparent descent, a structured craft rather than a remote light, a possible occupant, prolonged observation and an alleged material deposit. Yet nearly every one of those elements depends upon the accuracy of a single witness story as transmitted through journalists. A strong story built on a narrow source chain The number of publications carrying the Méral report can create the impression of broad corroboration. Archived versions show substantially the same short account appearing in regional and national newspapers, often using nearly identical phrasing: an orange ball, a domed disc five or six metres across, dazzling illumination over 200 metres, a black form inside, a ten minute stay and paraffin like material on the witness’s clothes. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... That repetition is important historically, but weak evidentially. The matching wording indicates that many reports were probably reproductions of a shared news agency dispatch or of one earlier local article, not separate interviews with Potin. Ten newspapers repeating one report still provide only one underlying testimony unless they conducted independent enquiries, located additional witnesses or documented physical evidence. A report in Les Nouvelles de Sablé , published on 16 October, offered useful local detail and explicitly named Potin. Another account attributed to Les Nouvelles de Bretagne et du Mans also placed the event on Monday morning at 6 a.m. and described the route, transformer, estimated distance and destination farm. These are the closest surviving sources to the alleged event, but the available versions are reproduced in a specialist UFO archive rather than supported by accessible original interview notes or official files . [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... There is also no clearly independent witness to the object itself. A young boy at Le Pertre, about 12 kilometres away, was reportedly frightened by something resembling a saucer approaching the ground later that same day. The timing, direction and description are too imprecise to establish that he saw the same phenomenon. The Le Pertre story may show that unusual object reports were circulating locally, but it cannot securely corroborate Potin’s encounter. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Une grande émotion régnait dans la région de ovni Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Le Pertre...Hebdomadaire \"LES NOUVELLES DE SABLE\" (SABLE,72) du sam 16/10... Dates, names and details drifted in later accounts The strongest warning sign in the Méral record is not one dramatic contradiction but the gradual instability of ordinary facts. The best supported date is Monday 11 October 1954, because early reports describe an event on “Monday morning” and newspapers published it later that week. Yet later catalogues assigned it to 14 October, and at least one retelling mistakenly placed it on 11 November. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... The witness’s identity also changed. The local accounts name Baptiste Potin, but some later books and catalogues call the witness “M. Duvivier” and describe him simply as a farmer. Potin was reportedly travelling to work at a farm; converting him into the farmer who owned the field is a small but meaningful alteration. It illustrates how later summaries can silently reshape a case while appearing to add authoritative detail. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 11 octobre, Méral, Mayenne Patrick GrossBaptiste Potin, 40 ans, résident de Méral, partait à sa journée aux Barres... Other variations include: a four to five metre craft becoming five to six metres across; departure towards the north, north west, Le Pertre or another nearby direction; a light that turned red becoming one that changed from white to red; material that remained as stubborn streaks becoming material that soon vanished without a trace; a dark, motionless form becoming an occupant moving inside the craft; an object seen about 600 metres away being described more loosely as a close approach. None of these differences proves that the original witness invented the experience. Memory, editing, translation and newspaper condensation can all create minor discrepancies. Collectively, however, they make precise reconstruction impossible. Claims about the object’s size, transparency, occupant"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-e6f82a/","description":"The Bais and Villiers-Charlemagne files show how official investigators separate probable explanations from reports too weak to assess.","breadcrumb":"Mayenne's UFO Stories Under the Evidence Test","level":2,"text":"Official Files What Do Mayenne's Official UFO Files Show? Mayenne UFOs Official Files Official Files What Do Mayenne's Official UFO Files Show? The Bais and Villiers Charlemagne files show how official investigators separate probable explanations from reports too weak to assess. The Bais and Villiers Charlemagne files show how official investigators separate probable explanations from reports too weak to assess. dataset evidence How GEIPAN investigates and classifies reports The probably identified Bais case Why Villiers Charlemagne remained unresolved mayenne official files Introduction Mayenne ’s official UFO record is modest and, above all, cautious. The published files do not reveal a strong cluster of unexplained incidents, radar contacts or military encounters. Instead, they show how France’s official investigators distinguish between reports that have a likely ordinary explanation and reports that cannot be assessed because too little reliable information survives. Two files make that distinction especially clear. At Bais in February 1985, investigators judged that the witnesses had probably mistaken a star for an unusual aerial object, producing a category B classification. At Villiers Charlemagne, a white light reported years after the event remained in category C—not because it was exceptionally mysterious, but because the date, supporting testimony and observational detail were too weak for a meaningful investigation. Together, the cases show that “unresolved” does not necessarily mean “inexplicable”. It may simply mean that the evidence is inadequate. [Geipan] geipan.fr BAIS (53) 1985 21/02/1985 B Mayenne Phénomène identifié non indiqué 22/03/2007 1985 02 21... How GEIPAN investigates and classifies reports France’s official system is run by GEIPAN, a technical service of the French space agency CNES. It collects witness reports, analyses possible explanations, archives the material and publishes anonymised case files. Reports may arrive directly from members of the public or through organisations including the gendarmerie, police and civil aviation authorities. Investigators can also consult specialists in astronomy, meteorology, aerospace tracking, psychology and image analysis. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... The familiar A, B, C and D labels are not a simple scale running from “ordinary” to “extraordinary”. They describe the outcome of the investigation: A: the phenomenon has been identified with a high degree of confidence. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, though not conclusively proved. C: the report cannot be properly assessed because the available information is insufficient or unreliable. D: enough information exists for investigation, but no satisfactory explanation has been found. Older published files may also use D1 and D2, subdivisions intended to distinguish between unexplained cases of different evidential strength. GEIPAN now treats both as forms of category D. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... The underlying method weighs two separate questions. The first is residual strangeness : after testing known explanations, how unusual does the observation remain? The second is consistency , meaning the amount and reliability of the evidence. Consistency is strengthened by precise times, several independent witnesses, coherent descriptions, photographs, video, radar data or physical traces. It is weakened by long delays, uncertain dates, incomplete questionnaires, dependent witnesses or vague memories. [Geipan] geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN... This distinction is crucial when reading Mayenne’s files. A category C report is not necessarily more mysterious than a category B report. In fact, it may be less impressive. Category B says that investigators found a likely answer. Category C says that they lacked enough dependable material to decide either way. GEIPAN’s national figures reinforce that point. As of March 2026, roughly two thirds of its published cases were classified A or B, about 30 per cent were category C, and just over 3 per cent remained category D after investigation. The large category C share reflects an evidence problem, not a large reserve of strong unexplained cases. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... Bais: probably a star, not an unknown craft The Bais observation occurred at about 10 p.m. on 21 February 1985 and involved two witnesses. GEIPAN’s archive classifies it as category B, meaning that the proposed explanation was considered probable rather than demonstrated beyond doubt. The case summary identifies the likely cause as a star. [Google Docs] docs.google.com Docs GEIPAN Google DocsGEIPAN Etudes de cas Google DriveBAIS (53) 1985, 48,25, 366,667, 21/02/1985, 53, Mayenne, Pays de la Loire, observation p... That conclusion may sound too simple, but stars and bright planets regularly generate unusual light reports. A bright point seen without useful foreground references can appear to move, change colour or remain unnaturally stationary. Atmospheric turbulence can make it shimmer, pulse or seem to shift position. Small involuntary eye movements may also produce the autokinetic effect, in which a fixed light against a dark background appears to wander. GEIPAN itself lists stars, planets and low light perception effects among the common sources of apparently extraordinary observations. [Phys.org] phys.org Open source on phys.org. The Bais classification does not mean that the witnesses fabricated their experience or failed to see anything. It means that investigators believed the reported appearance and behaviour could most plausibly be reproduced by an astronomical source viewed under misleading conditions. The category B label also preserves an appropriate degree of uncertainty. Without an independently recorded direction, elevation, exact duration and reconstruction of the witnesses’ positions, investigators may be unable to prove which star was involved. A probable identification can nevertheless be stronger than leaving the case unexplained, provided the proposed object was visible in the reported area and its appearance fits the description. For Mayenne’s wider UFO history, Bais matters because it demonstrates how an initially puzzling light can lose much of its mystery once investigators compare the account with ordinary sky objects and known visual effects. It offers no evidence of a structured craft, radar return, aviation hazard or military activity. Its official value lies in the reasoning behind the classification, not in the dramatic quality of the sighting. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Villiers Charlemagne: unresolved because the evidence was too weak The Villiers Charlemagne file concerns a round white light reportedly seen moving in unusual ways in August 2009. The witness recalled no flashing lights and no sound. GEIPAN considered two local possibilities: a helicopter using a searchlight and machinery operating around the nearby Carrière de la Fosse quarry, north west of the observation point. Neither hypothesis could be tested properly. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLIERS CHARLEMAGNE (53) .08.2009 GEIPAN... The central difficulty was delay. GEIPAN recorded the testimony in 2011, roughly two years after the alleged event, and even the precise date was uncertain. The observation may have occurred on 25 or 26 August 2009, but the published file does not establish a firm day. No second witness came forward, and there was no photograph, video, radar record or contemporary official report against which the memory could be checked. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLIERS CHARLEMAGNE (53) .08.2009 GEIPAN... Those gaps prevented investigators from carrying out the checks that would normally give an inquiry traction. With a secure date and time, they might have examined helicopter movements, quarry operations, weather, aircraft routes, satellite positions or astronomical objects. Without them, almost any retrospective comparison would risk fitting an explanation to an uncertain memory. GEIPAN also described the report as having low strangeness. The witness had seen a light, rather than a clearly defined vehicle, and the reported movements did not produce corroborating physical or technical evidence. The agency therefore decided that a full investigation was not justified and classified the case C for lack of information, pending any additional material. [Geipan] geipan.fr VILLIERS CHARLEMAGNE (53) .08.2009 GEIPAN... This is an important corrective to a common misunderstanding. Villiers Charlemagne was not placed in category D. GEIPAN did not conclude that a well documented phenomenon had resisted all conventional explanations. It concluded that the observation was not sufficiently documented to"},{"title":"Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence","url":"/meurthe-et-moselle/","description":"Meurthe-et-Moselle has one genuinely important place in French UFO history: the 1982 “Amaranth” case near Nancy, in which a biologist reported a silent, oval...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence Meurthe et Moselle has one genuinely important place in French UFO history: the 1982 “Amaranth” case near Nancy, in which a biologist reported a silent, oval... Meurthe et Moselle has one genuinely important place in French UFO history: the 1982 “Amaranth” case near Nancy, in which a biologist reported a silent, oval object hovering at close range above his garden. meurthe et moselle Introduction Beyond that landmark case, the department’s record is less exotic than popular retellings suggest. The 1954 national “flying saucer wave” produced colourful reports around Toul and Longwy, while modern GEIPAN files show that many apparently strange lights or objects have been identified as meteors, planets, satellites, balloons, birds or sky lanterns. Meurthe et Moselle therefore offers a useful miniature of French UFO history: striking testimony, serious official inquiry, persistent uncertainty and a large background of ordinary phenomena seen under misleading conditions. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The Amaranth case: Nancy’s central UFO mystery At about 12.35 pm on 21 October 1982, a 30 year old biologist was standing in the garden of his home in the Nancy suburbs when, according to his statement, he noticed a shining object approaching from the south east. He initially took it for an aircraft. He then described it descending into the narrow garden and remaining suspended above the ground for roughly 20 minutes before departing silently and rapidly. He reported the event to the local gendarmerie that evening, only a few hours after it had supposedly occurred. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witness’s occupation strongly influenced the reception of the story. The attending officer considered his professional standing, desire for anonymity and restrained manner to support his credibility. GEPAN was notified two days later and conducted a reconstruction at the property, taking photographs and measurements, interviewing the witness and contacting nearby military airfields. The official technical report treated the observation as unusually favourable: it had allegedly happened in daylight, at short range and over several minutes, with the object viewed from different angles. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Those features make the case memorable, but they do not independently verify it. There was no second eyewitness, no photograph of the object, no radar record publicly tied to it and no recovered material. The investigation could assess the internal detail of one person’s account, but it could not compare separate descriptions or establish the object’s distance and size through triangulation. GEPAN itself acknowledged that the absence of another witness prevented this kind of corroboration. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The wilted plants The case became known as “Amaranth” because the witness noticed that some flowering amaranths near the reported hovering position had wilted. Gendarmes collected plant samples the following morning, and GEPAN arranged biochemical examinations of affected and apparently unaffected material. The wilted sample contained about half as much water as the comparison sample, confirming that it was genuinely dried out. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That finding is sometimes presented as a physical trace left by the object, but the technical report was much more cautious. Sampling and storage were imperfect; some plants had already been uprooted; and the analysis began too late to preserve short lived biochemical changes reliably. Several measured differences could be connected with seed maturity, soil composition, fertiliser, injury, parasites or poor preservation rather than an external aerial phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Weather offered another ordinary possibility. The nearby station had recorded temperatures below freezing and light mist or fog on the previous day. GEPAN’s own report suggested that exposed parts of the flower bed might therefore have suffered weather related drying. Its final position was that plant biochemistry could not explain the difference between the wilted and control samples under the conditions in which the evidence had been collected. In other words, the damage was real, but its cause was not established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Why the official investigation did not settle the case GEPAN found no reasonable basis for simply rejecting the witness’s account. It did not identify obvious signs of deception or a recognised tendency towards distorted perception. At the same time, the report did not demonstrate that a physical craft had occupied the garden. Its closing discussion emphasised how difficult it was to interpret isolated traces and noted that supposedly unusual cases differed greatly in shape, colour, sound and other reported characteristics. Nothing showed that they represented one recurring phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This distinction matters. An unresolved report is not positive evidence of an extraterrestrial vehicle. It means that the available information did not permit a dependable identification. In the Amaranth case , the strongest evidence remains a prompt, detailed statement by a seemingly sincere witness. The weaknesses are equally clear: one observer, no instrument record and environmental samples that could not be causally connected to what he reported seeing. The main sceptical challenge Later critics argued that investigators gave too much weight to the witness’s profession and to the plants. A review published by the French Association for Scientific Information maintained that autumn wilting was unremarkable and that inconsistencies in the account should have prompted closer consideration of a complex visual illusion or a hallucinatory experience. A separate review in Skeptical Inquirer made essentially the same point, arguing that the purported physical effect was weakly linked to the reported object and that frost provided a straightforward explanation for the flowers. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical Inquirer These criticisms do not prove that the witness hallucinated, nor do they establish a specific object that he misidentified. Their force lies instead in exposing a gap in the original inquiry. GEPAN investigated aviation, weather, site geometry and plant chemistry, but its published report could not rigorously test a private perceptual event. Because the observation occurred without independent confirmation, any interpretation based on illusion, memory, an unusual psychological episode or an unseen conventional object remains difficult to prove or disprove. The most defensible assessment is therefore narrower than either extreme. The Amaranth case is not a demonstrated hoax and should not be dismissed merely because it sounds extraordinary. It is also not a scientifically verified landing or close approach by an unknown craft. It is a strong piece of witness testimony accompanied by weak physical evidence. 1:12:25 Aliens in the Archives: A Conversation about the History of the UFO Phenomenon, Lunch at the Library Channel: American Philosophical Society · Views: 846 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 1 hour 12 minutes Open on YouTube The 1954 wave around Toul and Longwy Meurthe et Moselle also participated in France’s famous autumn 1954 wave , when reports of “flying saucers” multiplied across the country. Local newspaper archives preserve several dramatic stories from October, including an account by a prison officer travelling to work near Écrouves in dense fog. He said that his motorcycle headlamp illuminated a metallic, circular form about two metres wide, with a dome and openings around its edge. Two colleagues reportedly described a disc with a glowing trail later that day, while another observation was reported around Longwy. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr toul invasion de soucoupes volantes L'Est RépublicainLes incroyables histoires de Meurthe et Moselle. Toul: l'«invasion» de soucoupes volantes de 1954... These reports are historically interesting because they show how quickly a common narrative developed. Witnesses did not merely describe lights; some interpreted what they saw as secret foreign weapons or devices associated with contemporary fears about military technology. Newspapers linked separate incidents across Lorraine, encouraging readers to treat them as parts of a regional invasion rather than unrelated observations. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr toul invasion de soucoupes"},{"title":"Amaranth Case","url":"/amaranth-case/","description":"Nancy's best-known UFO case rests on detailed testimony, careful official inquiry and plant damage that was real but never causally explained.","breadcrumb":"Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence","level":2,"text":"Amaranth Case Did Anything Physical Hover Over the Nancy Garden? Meurthe et Moselle UFOs Amaranth Case Amaranth Case Did Anything Physical Hover Over the Nancy Garden? Nancy's best known UFO case rests on detailed testimony, careful official inquiry and plant damage that was real but never causally explained. Nancy's best known UFO case rests on detailed testimony, careful official inquiry and plant damage that was real but never causally explained. entity The witness account and garden reconstruction What the wilted plants did and did not prove Official conclusions and later sceptical challenges meurthe et moselle amaranth case Introduction The Amaranth case is the best known UFO incident in Meurthe et Moselle , but it is not a case in which physical evidence proved that an extraordinary craft hovered over a garden. On 21 October 1982, a biologist living near Nancy reported watching a silent, oval object descend into his small enclosed garden, remain close to the ground for about 20 minutes and then rise rapidly out of sight. Wilted amaranth plants were found nearby, prompting a detailed investigation by GEPAN, the French space agency unit then responsible for unidentified aerospace phenomena. The inquiry was unusually thorough for a single witness report. Gendarmes collected plants, investigators reconstructed the viewing geometry, military radar records were checked and laboratory scientists examined the vegetation. Yet the central difficulty never disappeared: the object was seen by only one person, was not photographed and left no independently verified trace. The plants were genuinely dried out, but their condition could not be securely linked to the reported object. The case therefore remains unresolved in the limited sense that no explanation has been established—not verified evidence of an exotic vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witness account and garden reconstruction At about 12.35 pm, the witness said he was outside his home when he noticed something shining as it approached from the south east. He initially thought it was an aeroplane. According to his statement to the gendarmerie later that day, the object descended towards him and stopped roughly one metre above the lawn. He described an oval form about 1.5 metres across and 80 centimetres thick, with a polished metallic lower half and a blue green upper section. It produced no sound, heat, cold, smoke or other sensation that he could detect. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The most striking claim was proximity. The witness said he first watched from around 1.5 metres away and then approached to approximately half a metre. During the observation he went upstairs to fetch a loaded camera, returned and attempted to take a picture, but the camera failed—a defect he said it had displayed before. After about 20 minutes, the object supposedly rose vertically and rapidly until it vanished. He reported no indentation, scorching or flattened grass, although he said the grass blades briefly stood upright as the object departed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The report reached the authorities quickly. The witness and his wife went to the local gendarmerie at about 6 pm, less than six hours after the event. A police telex was sent the following morning, and GEPAN was informed on 23 October. Investigators travelled from Toulouse and examined the property on 29 October, taking measurements, photographs and further plant samples. This relatively prompt response reduced some of the problems that affect cases investigated months or years later. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The site itself mattered. The garden was only about 10.5 square metres, surrounded by walls, fencing, buildings and vegetation. Investigators reconstructed the witness’s movements and the alleged position and path of the object within this confined space. They found that his successive accounts were broadly consistent and that the reported geometry could be fitted into the garden. GEPAN’s psychological and narrative assessment found no clear contradiction or obvious sign that he was inventing the story. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That finding is often misunderstood. Consistency supports the possibility that the witness was sincere, but sincerity is not the same as accuracy. A lone observer can honestly misjudge an object’s distance, dimensions, movement or method of support. Because there was no second witness, investigators could not compare independent descriptions or triangulate the object’s position. Even a detailed reconstruction therefore remained dependent on the witness’s own estimates. The witness’s occupation also influenced how the case was received. The attending gendarme regarded his scientific employment, social position, request for discretion and restrained presentation as reasons to take him seriously. Those were reasonable grounds for conducting an inquiry, but later critics argued that GEPAN placed too much confidence in the idea of a “qualified” witness. Scientific training in biology does not make a person immune to visual misinterpretation, especially when viewing an unexpected object without a reliable distance reference. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. What the wilted plants did and did not prove The plants turned an unusual sighting into a potentially testable case. When the witness returned to the garden later on 21 October, he noticed that the upper parts of several amaranths near the reported hovering position appeared dried and folded. Gendarmes visited the next morning and observed a visible contrast between damaged and apparently normal plants. The affected leaves were curled and dehydrated, while flower clusters had changed colour and bent over their stems. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Samples were collected in several stages. The gendarmerie took material on 22 and 27 October; GEPAN gathered another series on 29 October. Some specimens were sealed quickly, while others remained in open plastic bags before being repackaged days later. The specialist laboratory ultimately concentrated on two samples gathered on 22 October: one wilted plant and one comparison plant regarded as normal. The researchers did not control the original sampling and warned that the delay and storage conditions limited interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The simplest measurable difference was water content. The comparison sample contained about 80 per cent water, while the wilted specimen contained about 40 per cent. That confirmed an obvious fact: the damaged plant really had lost substantial moisture. The laboratory also detected variations in sugars and amino acids, particularly in the flowers. Some differences were real, but several could be related to plant maturity, normal nitrogen transport, bacterial activity during storage or other forms of stress. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The researchers did not find a distinctive biochemical signature that identified a source of injury. They noted that slightly elevated compounds in the wilted material could reflect damage from parasites, physical injury or excessive fertiliser. Differences in the flowers might also have arisen because their seeds were more mature than those of the comparison plant. Most importantly, refrigeration followed by later freezing did not stop continuing enzymatic changes, so the samples no longer preserved a reliable snapshot of the plants at the time of the reported event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Weather offered a plausible ordinary cause. The laboratory discussion noted that temperatures had fallen below freezing before the sighting and that parts of the flower bed, positioned beside a fence, might have been affected unevenly by cold and damp conditions. The scientists explicitly stated that the drying could therefore have been climatic. Their final judgement was that plant biochemistry, given the condition of the samples, could not explain why some plants looked different from others. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This distinction is crucial. The investigation established that some plants were wilted. It did not establish: exactly when the damage began; that the affected and comparison plants had been in identical condition beforehand; that frost, disease, fertiliser, maturity or local exposure could be excluded; that the alleged object was positioned close enough to cause the damage; or that a single physical mechanism connected the sighting, the grass movement and the wilted flowers. The plant evidence was therefore an observation requiring explanation, not a forensic trace identifying an extraordinary craft. GEPAN also explored the witness’s statement that grass blades rose vertically during the departure. A laboratory"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-d93b7f/","description":"Reports around Toul and Longwy show how fog, wartime anxieties and newspaper storytelling helped turn separate sightings into a regional saucer wave.","breadcrumb":"Strange Reports, Strong Testimony and Uncertain Evidence","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave How Lorraine's Saucer Wave Took Shape Meurthe et Moselle UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave How Lorraine's Saucer Wave Took Shape Reports around Toul and Longwy show how fog, wartime anxieties and newspaper storytelling helped turn separate sightings into a regional saucer wave. Reports around Toul and Longwy show how fog, wartime anxieties and newspaper storytelling helped turn separate sightings into a regional saucer wave. event time window The encounters reported near Toul and Longwy How newspapers connected separate sightings Fog, military fears and later interpretation meurthe et moselle 1954 wave Introduction The 1954 reports around Toul and Longwy did not form a single, closely documented incident. They were a small cluster of separate observations that newspapers connected during France’s autumn “flying saucer” wave. The best known account came from Roger Thiriet, a prison officer who said that on 10 October he encountered a small metallic craft beside a fogbound road near Toul. Reports from the Longwy area described luminous discs, strange sounds and, in one case, a scorched patch of ground. These stories matter because they show how a regional UFO wave took shape. Poor visibility, delayed testimony, Cold War fears and repeated press comparisons made unrelated events appear mutually supporting. Yet the surviving evidence is largely newspaper based. There are no photographs of the objects, no published laboratory result from the alleged Longwy area ground trace and no surviving body of independent statements strong enough to establish that an extraordinary craft was present. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'Est Républicain, France, le 20 octobre 1954... The encounters reported near Toul and Longwy The central Toul story began before dawn on Sunday, 10 October 1954. Roger Thiriet, aged 40, was riding a motorised bicycle from Blénod lès Toul to his job at the Écrouves prison. Near the railway bridge over the old RN60, at the junction for Charmes la Côte, his headlamp reportedly illuminated an aluminium coloured form about ten metres ahead. The morning was dark and heavily foggy, a detail recorded in the original newspaper account rather than added by later sceptics. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'Est Républicain, France, le 20 octobre 1954... Thiriet described a circular object approximately 2 to 2.4 metres across and 1.4 metres high, topped by a dome and apparently fitted with two windows. He thought it was resting directly on or close to the road. After stopping his engine and moving towards it, he said, the object rose silently, without smoke or flame. He could follow its ascent for only a few metres before darkness and fog concealed it. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'Est Républicain, France, le 20 octobre 1954... Several features made the story attractive to newspapers. The witness had a respectable occupation, gave unusually precise dimensions and claimed a close encounter rather than a distant light. He also portrayed himself as cautious: he waited more than a week before speaking because he feared ridicule. According to the contemporary article, the continuing stream of saucer stories eventually persuaded him to report his own experience. That delay, however understandable, meant that the account had already been exposed to days of national and regional UFO coverage before it was recorded publicly. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'Est Républicain, France, le 20 octobre 1954... No physical trace was found. Thiriet examined the road when he returned from work but reported no mark, damage or residue. There was also no second witness at the roadside, no photograph and no independent measurement of the alleged object. The dimensions therefore depended on his estimate of distance in dense fog, illuminated mainly by a vehicle headlamp. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Toul L'Est RépublicainToul Les incroyables histoires de Meurthe et Moselle. Roger et le mystère de la soucoupe... The Longwy material is less straightforward than later summaries sometimes suggest. Retrospective articles mention an observation on the same date, either at about 1.30 in the morning or later in the day, involving a disc or luminous object with a reddish trail. Some versions connect the afternoon observation with two of Thiriet’s colleagues, while others describe an unnamed observer near Longwy. The limited identification of the witnesses and the differences between retellings make it unsafe to treat these references as a firmly corroborated sighting of the same object. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Toul L'Est RépublicainToul Les incroyables histoires de Meurthe et Moselle. Roger et le mystère de la soucoupe... A more detailed northern Meurthe et Moselle report followed during the night of 10–11 October near Doncourt, north west of Longwy. Newspaper accounts said that people at an isolated farm were awakened at about 1.30 am by crackling and whistling sounds. Looking outside, they saw a flat, intensely luminous object apparently resting on a hillside by a wood. It was described through comparison rather than measurement—roughly three times the apparent size of the setting Sun—before rising vertically and disappearing. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Doncourt Village, Moselle... Searchers later reported finding a circular burnt area about 1.5 metres across on a muddy track near the wood. Stones were said to have turned brick red, while soil and vegetation appeared scorched. Samples were supposedly taken for laboratory examination. This sounds like the strongest physical element in the cluster, but no published analytical result has been located. The trace was not demonstrated to have been created during the observation, and accounts do not establish that witnesses saw the luminous object make direct contact with that exact patch. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Doncourt Village, Moselle... These distinctions are important. The Toul case was a solitary close range road encounter in fog. The loosely reported Longwy observations involved aerial lights. The Doncourt account involved a bright phenomenon on or beyond a hillside and a subsequently discovered burnt area. Their proximity in date and department encouraged comparison, but their reported appearances and evidential quality were not the same. How newspapers connected separate sightings The original Toul article did more than reproduce a witness statement. Its opening placed the event within a “round” of saucers and assorted craft, noting that the Toul sky had previously seemed neglected except for an earlier disc reported near Gondreville. The language effectively recruited Toul into a story already under way: the remarkable point was not merely what Thiriet claimed to have seen, but that the national phenomenon had now reached the district. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'Est Républicain, France, le 20 octobre 1954... The same process operated around Longwy. Reports from Doncourt appeared beside dispatches about luminous discs, landed machines, stalled engines and alleged occupants elsewhere in France. A local reader therefore encountered each new story within a ready made framework. A light on a hillside was not presented first as an unknown light; it became another “saucer”. A burnt patch was not initially treated as an isolated fire site; it became a possible landing trace. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Doncourt Village, Moselle... News agencies amplified that effect. The Doncourt report was shortened for national circulation, while place names were sometimes altered or confused. Later catalogues variously used Doncourt, Doncourt Village, Doncourt lès Longuyon, Moncourt and even incorrect departmental labels. What appears in a modern list as several matching cases may therefore include duplicate versions of one newspaper story rather than several independent events. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 11 octobre, Doncourt Village, Moselle... The Toul account also acquired apparent support through the way reports were placed together. Thiriet’s silence ended after he heard about other sightings, and the newspaper immediately mentioned a reportedly similar object at Longwy. Yet similarity was asserted rather than demonstrated. A small aluminium coloured structure apparently found on a road in headlamp beams is not obviously identical to a distant disc leaving a red trail. The connection came chiefly from the shared label “flying saucer” and the date of 10 October. This does not mean that witnesses necessarily fabricated their experiences."},{"title":"What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals","url":"/what-meuses-ufo-record-actually-reveals/","description":"Meuse has a real but comparatively modest UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals Meuse has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Meuse has a real but comparatively modest UFO history. Its official record is dominated not by a single landmark encounter, radar chase or military confrontation, but by scattered reports of lights, glowing objects and apparent aerial formations between the 1970s and the early twenty first century. meuse Introduction The department nevertheless offers a useful case study in how UFO stories develop. Meuse combines rural observing conditions, military aviation around Étain Rouvres, the nationwide French “flying saucer” wave of 1954, a small cluster of reports in 1976 and modern investigations by the French space agency’s GEIPAN unit. The strongest overall conclusion is cautious: unusual things have certainly been reported, but the published evidence does not identify a robust, well documented Meuse case that remains unexplained after a full official investigation. What the official record contains France’s principal public source is GEIPAN, the unit within the National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES, that collects, investigates, archives and publishes reports of unidentified aerial or aerospace phenomena. GEIPAN does not investigate extraterrestrial visitation. It compares witness accounts with known astronomical, meteorological, aviation and human made phenomena, using assistance from the police, gendarmerie, military radar services, scientific specialists and trained volunteer investigators. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPAN’s categories are crucial to understanding the Meuse files: A means the phenomenon was identified. B means it was probably identified. C means it could not be identified because the available information was inadequate. D means it remained unidentified despite sufficiently substantial investigation. A category C report is therefore an evidential dead end, not a confirmed anomaly. GEIPAN explicitly distinguishes lack of information from a genuinely resistant case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN A published departmental listing includes reports from Void Vacon, Gondrecourt le Château, Montmédy, Commercy, Revigny sur Ornain, Verdun, Charny sur Meuse, Lacroix sur Meuse, Chardogne, Bar le Duc, Sauvigny and Richecourt. The dates range from July 1976 to November 2020. Most are classified A, B or C; the available Meuse listing does not display a category D case. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... That pattern matters more than the raw number of reports. Meuse has generated genuine witness experiences, but the official archive is weighted towards recognised phenomena and cases too weakly documented to test. It does not presently support the idea of a persistent, technically documented UFO hotspot. The 1950s: memorable stories, fragile foundations Meuse entered modern UFO folklore during the French flying saucer wave of 1954. Newspapers and later catalogues recorded hundreds of reports across France that autumn, often as short notices about discs, cigars or brilliant lights. The north east was especially active in the surviving press record, although many accounts were repetitive, second hand or too vague to reconstruct accurately decades later. One Meuse report was placed near Gondrecourt le Château in early October 1954. A later catalogue entry described an oval disc, said to appear several times larger than a star, moving from east to west and reportedly viewed through binoculars by a married couple. The location appears in different forms in secondary compilations, including “Vondrecourt”, while some catalogue versions provide neither an original report nor enough contextual detail to establish the precise date, duration, direction or astronomical conditions. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UN DISQUE OVALE, cinq fois plus gros qu'une étoile, a été vu à Vondrecourt (Meuse) par M. et Mme Thomas, électriciens, qui l'ont observé... This is historically interesting because it places Meuse within the national 1954 wave, but it is weak as physical evidence. There is no known photograph, radar confirmation, trace evidence or complete contemporary investigation attached to the commonly repeated version. The changing place names and reliance on later catalogues also illustrate how a small newspaper item can become more definite in retelling than it was at the time. An even more dramatic story is sometimes associated with Dugny sur Meuse in 1951. In its later form, workers with a disabled vehicle supposedly saw an orange globe and a tall, human looking woman who entered it before the object departed or changed appearance. The story appears chiefly in later “humanoid encounter” catalogues rather than in a readily verifiable contemporary police file or newspaper archive. [Reddit] reddit.com Group of workers see a tall, beautiful woman enter a UFO Group of workers see a tall, beautiful woman enter a UFO The evidential problems are severe. The witnesses are not securely documented in accessible primary material, the date is imprecise, and the account contains elaborate details that are difficult to trace back to a contemporaneous source. It is best treated as department level UFO folklore—not as a reliable close encounter case. The 1976 cluster The clearest concentration in the official Meuse chronology occurred in the second half of 1976. GEIPAN’s catalogue lists reports at Void Vacon on 13 July, Gondrecourt le Château on 21 October, Commercy on 20 November and Montmédy on 21 November. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... At first glance, four reports in five months may suggest a local “flap”. Yet the classifications weaken that interpretation. Void Vacon is category B, meaning investigators considered a conventional explanation probable, although the summary does not specify the identified phenomenon. The other three are category C because dependable information is missing. A cluster of dates therefore exists, but the record does not establish that the witnesses observed the same phenomenon—or even similar phenomena. The October and November reports also occurred during a period when French public awareness of UFOs was high. News coverage, specialist groups and growing official interest made witnesses more likely to label and report unfamiliar lights. France created its first dedicated CNES study group the following year, in 1977, partly because sightings and public enquiries required a systematic response. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN For readers, the key distinction is between a reporting cluster and an anomalous event cluster . The former means several people contacted authorities or investigators within a limited period. The latter would require evidence that the incidents shared measurable characteristics, trajectories, timings or detection records. The surviving Meuse files establish the first but not the second. Une Histoire d'archives: des OVNIS en Bourgogne Channel: France 3 Bourgogne Franche Comté Open on YouTube Lacroix sur Meuse and the lantern problem The Lacroix sur Meuse observation of 4 May 2008 is one of the department’s most informative modern cases because the witness supplied enough detail for comparison with a known phenomenon. At about 10.45 pm, roughly ten orange, fire coloured spherical lights were reported under heavily clouded skies. They appeared silent and apparently stationary, with no reported environmental effects. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN classified the case B and identified Chinese or Thai style sky lanterns as the probable explanation. These small paper balloons contain a flame that heats the air inside. At night, their bodies may be invisible while the flames resemble independent orange objects. When several are released together, they can appear to hover, change spacing or move in formation, particularly when viewed from an uncertain distance. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The case demonstrates why silence and formation flight are not, by themselves, strong evidence of an exotic craft. A group of lanterns can reproduce the reported colour, multiplicity, apparent stillness and lack of sound. Cloud cover can further remove stars and landscape references that would otherwise help a witness judge motion and distance. A similar explanation appears in the Bar le Duc report of 1 September 2011, classified B as a probable lantern sighting, while the Sauvigny report of 20 October 2015 was classified A as an identified lantern event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... These cases show a recurring feature of modern UFO"},{"title":"1976 Cluster","url":"/1976-cluster/","description":"Four reports in five months created the appearance of a local flap, but the surviving files do not show a single shared phenomenon.","breadcrumb":"What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"1976 Cluster Was Meuse's 1976 UFO Flap Really Connected? Meuse UFOs 1976 Cluster 1976 Cluster Was Meuse's 1976 UFO Flap Really Connected? Four reports in five months created the appearance of a local flap, but the surviving files do not show a single shared phenomenon. Four reports in five months created the appearance of a local flap, but the surviving files do not show a single shared phenomenon. event time window The four reports and their timelines Why a reporting cluster is not an event cluster What the official classifications leave unresolved meuse 1976 cluster Introduction Meuses apparent UFO flap of 1976 was a cluster of reports, not a single unfolding event. Between 13 July and 21 November, witnesses at Void Vacon, Gondrecourt le Chteau, Commercy and near Montmdy reported several different kinds of lights: a briefly expanding white sphere at dawn, two orange red objects travelling one after another, pairs of slow moving lights, and a red streak followed by a descending white point. The compressed timetable made the department look unusually active, especially when the last two files occurred on consecutive nights. Yet the surviving records contain no shared trajectory, matching appearance, radar confirmation or physical evidence linking the observations. The official outcome is correspondingly cautious. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that now publishes the files, classifies Void Vacon as probably explained and the other three cases as lacking enough reliable information. The cluster remains interesting as a piece of Meuse UFO history, but it does not amount to evidence for one repeated or coordinated phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The four reports and their timelines The sequence extended across four calendar months, although it touched five named months when counted inclusively from July to November. More importantly, the sightings were separated by substantial gaps until the two November reports. What looks like a compact wave in a yearly case list was therefore a mixture of one summer observation, one October incident and two closely timed November files. 13 July Void Vacon: At about 5.45 am, two people finishing night work at a quarry saw a stationary white sphere appear and grow progressively larger. The observation lasted only four or five seconds and was silent. The gendarmerie inquiry noted clear weather, an extensive view from elevated ground and the phenomenons apparent position towards the Vaucouleurs ridge, roughly eight kilometres away. The archived conclusion was that the witnesses had probably seen an optical effect involving reflected sunlight shortly after sunrise. GEIPAN consequently lists the case as category B: probably identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This is the least anomalous member of the cluster in official terms. Its timing at dawn, extremely short duration and changing apparent size are compatible with a transient illumination or reflection, although the published summary does not specify the exact reflecting surface or atmospheric mechanism. The classification is therefore probabilistic rather than a demonstrated reconstruction. Even so, it differs fundamentally from the three later category C files: investigators thought an ordinary explanation was more likely than not. 21 October Gondrecourt le Chteau: Three witnesses positioned at two separate locations reported a bright red orange ball moving slowly from south east to north west between approximately 6.30 and 6.45 pm. The first passage lasted about 45 seconds before the light disappeared abruptly. A few minutes later, two of the witnesses saw what appeared to be a second, similar object following the same general route. The night was described as clear, the lights were silent and the witnesses characterised them as point like despite informally comparing their apparent size with a circular ball. [GEIPAN] geipan.fr GONDRECOURT LE CHTEAU (55) 21.10.1976GONDRECOURT LE CHTEAU (55) 21.10.1976. Date d'observation. 21/10/1976. Rgion. Lorraine. Dp... Gondrecourt is the most substantial of the four reports because it involved three witnesses and observations from two places. That gives the account more weight than an isolated sighting, but not enough information to calculate altitude, distance or speed. The surviving gendarmerie material records where the witnesses stood and the direction they believed the lights travelled, yet it does not provide a triangulated track, photographs, radar data or a firmly established angular elevation. GEIPAN therefore leaves it in category C for insufficient information rather than treating it as a resistant unexplained case. [Geipan] geipan.fr GONDRECOURT LE CHTEAU (55) 21.10.1976GONDRECOURT LE CHTEAU (55) 21.10.1976. Date d'observation. 21/10/1976. Rgion. Lorraine. Dp... 2021 November Commercy: At about 5.30 pm on both dates, several people reportedly watched two rounded points of light moving slowly across the sky. No sound was heard. The official summary is unusually brief: it does not state whether precisely the same witnesses observed the lights on both evenings, whether the directions matched, how long either observation lasted, or how far apart the two lights appeared. The file is classified C because those missing details prevent a dependable identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The two night wording makes Commercy especially tempting to interpret as a repeated phenomenon. However, repetition cannot be established merely because two observations occurred at a similar hour. Around 5.30 pm in late November, bright astronomical objects, aircraft lights and other distant sources can all appear conspicuous, particularly near twilight. Without bearings, elevation changes and accurate durations, it is impossible to test whether the two evenings involved the same source or only superficially similar lights. 21 November Montmdy area: At 8.55 pm, a witness near Thonne la Long first noticed a red streak which vanished. The same person and a second witness then watched a white point move along a curved, descending path. The gendarmerie report indicates that the first witness called relatives or nearby acquaintances to look, but no independent reports beyond the small group were ultimately collected. GEIPAN summarises the case as a moving point of light and classifies it C for lack of reliable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The red streak could suggest a meteor, while the later white light could suggest an aircraft, another astronomical object or a continuation misperceived as a separate phase. These are possibilities, not findings. Bright meteors commonly appear as short luminous tracks and can be visible over large areas, but the Montmdy file contains neither enough independent reports nor instrumental records to test that explanation confidently. [Center for NEO Studies] cneos.jpl.nasa.gov Open source on nasa.gov. Why a reporting cluster is not an event cluster A genuine event cluster would normally show connections beyond calendar proximity. Investigators would look for matching times, consistent directions, comparable speeds and appearances, overlapping witness locations, radar or aviation records, or an identifiable phenomenon capable of recurring across the area. The 1976 Meuse files do not supply that pattern. The reports instead differ in several important ways: Void Vacon occurred at dawn in July and lasted only seconds. Gondrecourt involved orange red lights moving in sequence on an October evening. Commercy concerned two slow points seen around twilight on two November dates. Montmdy involved a red streak and a curved, descending white light later at night. Even the descriptions use different observational categories: a growing stationary sphere, travelling luminous balls, paired points and a streak followed by a point. Silence is the main recurring feature, but silence is not unusual when distant aircraft, meteors, satellites or astronomical lights are observed without a reliable estimate of range. The geography also argues against treating the reports as one localised episode. Void Vacon and Commercy lie in the south central part of Meuse; Gondrecourt le Chteau is farther south west; Montmdy and Thonne la Long are in the departments far north. The locations cover a long northsouth spread rather than forming a compact hotspot around one airfield, industrial site or landscape feature. No official file identifies a common military exercise, flight corridor or radar contact connecting them. The consecutive dates of the Commercy and Montmdy reports are the strongest apparent link, but their reported times and behaviour do not match. Commercys observations were around 5.30 pm and involved two slowly moving rounded lights. The Montmdy area report occurred at 8.55 pm and began with a red streak before a white light followed a curved descent. Nothing in the published"},{"title":"Lantern Cases","url":"/lantern-cases/","description":"Several striking formations of silent orange lights were probably sky lanterns rather than structured aircraft or unknown craft.","breadcrumb":"What Meuse's UFO Record Actually Reveals","level":2,"text":"Lantern Cases Why Orange UFO Formations Fooled Meuse Witnesses Meuse UFOs Lantern Cases Lantern Cases Why Orange UFO Formations Fooled Meuse Witnesses Several striking formations of silent orange lights were probably sky lanterns rather than structured aircraft or unknown craft. Several striking formations of silent orange lights were probably sky lanterns rather than structured aircraft or unknown craft. mechanism The Lacroix sur Meuse sighting How sky lanterns mimic formation flight Later cases that strengthened the explanation meuse lantern cases Introduction Several of Meuse ’s most striking modern “orange UFO” reports are best explained not as structured aircraft, but as groups of small sky lanterns. The key cases occurred at Lacroix sur Meuse in 2008, Bar le Duc in 2011 and Sauvigny in 2015. Witnesses described silent orange or yellowish lights, sometimes arranged in lines or apparent formations and sometimes seeming to move unusually fast. Those details sound dramatic, yet they match the recognised behaviour of paper lanterns carried by the wind. The importance of these cases lies in the pattern. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unusual aerospace reports, initially treated the Lacroix sur Meuse sighting as only probably explained. Later Meuse reports supplied better corroboration: movement matching the wind, photographs showing the characteristic orange glow and, at Sauvigny, confirmation that lanterns had been released after a funeral. Together, the cases show how separate points of light can create a convincing impression of a large, coordinated craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr LACROIX SUR MEUSE (55) 04.05.2008LACROIX SUR MEUSE (55) 04.05.2008. Date d'observation. 04/05/2008. Région. Lorraine. Département... 2:55 UFO Sighting Christmas Eve 2013 Belgium Multiple Orange Glowing Lights Invasion Channel: Dolph C. Volker · Views: 34.0K · Uploaded: December 2013 · Length: 2 minutes 55 seconds Open on YouTube The Lacroix sur Meuse sighting At about 10.45 pm on 4 May 2008, a witness at Lacroix sur Meuse reported roughly ten orange, fire coloured lights. The observation was made under very cloudy or overcast conditions. The objects were described as spherical, completely silent and apparently stationary, with no reported effect on the surroundings. GEIPAN eventually classified the case as B , meaning that the phenomenon was probably identified, and listed the likely cause as sky lanterns. [Geipan] geipan.fr LACROIX SUR MEUSE (55) 04.05.2008LACROIX SUR MEUSE (55) 04.05.2008. Date d'observation. 04/05/2008. Région. Lorraine. Département... This was not a trivial looking sight. A group of ten lights hanging in the night sky can easily suggest a formation rather than ten independent objects. With no visible wings, navigation lights, engine noise or connecting structure, the mind naturally tries to organise the points into a recognisable shape. Depending on their spacing, witnesses may perceive a triangle, line, arc or much larger dark object carrying lights around its outline. The apparent stillness does not rule out lanterns. A lantern drifting towards or away from an observer may show little sideways movement. Very weak winds can also produce slow motion that is hard to detect without a fixed reference point. Clouds further reduce depth cues, making it difficult to judge whether a light is nearby and small or distant and large. GEIPAN’s guidance notes that lanterns are routinely perceived as much farther away than they really are, leading witnesses to overestimate both their size and their speed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word les lanternes volantes une meprise couranteV3.3... There is no published evidence that the Lacroix lights were recovered or that investigators identified the people who launched them. That is why the case was classified B rather than A. The explanation rests on a close match between the observation and a known phenomenon, not on direct proof of a specific release. It is therefore fairer to call the sighting probably explained than conclusively solved. How lanterns mimic formation flight A flying lantern is essentially a miniature hot air balloon. A flame or paraffin burner heats the air inside a lightweight paper envelope, lifting it to an altitude that may reach roughly 100 to 200 metres under suitable conditions. It then travels with the local airflow, producing no engine sound and usually showing a warm orange, red orange or yellow orange glow. Once the fuel is exhausted, the light fades or goes out and the dark paper envelope may become invisible against the night sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word les lanternes volantes une meprise couranteV3.3... Several characteristics make lanterns unusually effective UFO lookalikes: They are silent. Silence feels inconsistent with an aircraft, especially when a light seems low or fast. For an object simply floating with the air, however, silence is exactly what should be expected. They share a common course. Lanterns released together enter broadly the same wind flow. This produces apparently coordinated movement without any physical connection or piloting. They form temporary geometrical patterns. Three lights can look like the corners of a triangle; larger groups can suggest a polygon, a line of craft or lights fixed to the edge of one dark vehicle. Their spacing changes slowly. Small differences in ascent, turbulence and release time make a formation expand, contract or rearrange itself, which can resemble controlled manoeuvring. Their disappearance looks abrupt. When a flame extinguishes, the light may appear to shoot away, enter cloud or vanish instantaneously because the unlit envelope cannot be seen. GEIPAN identifies this tendency to join separate lights into a single outline as a familiar feature of visual perception. The observer sees real luminous points but may infer a solid body between them even though no such body is visible. That distinction is central to the Meuse reports: witnesses were not necessarily mistaken about seeing orange lights; the likely error concerned what those lights represented. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word les lanternes volantes une meprise couranteV3.3... Distance is another major source of confusion. At night, an isolated light provides almost no dependable information about scale. GEIPAN gives the illustrative example of a lantern at about 100 metres altitude moving at 10 kilometres per hour being perceived as ten times farther away and therefore travelling at around 100 kilometres per hour. The exact figures will vary, but the principle is robust: an incorrect distance estimate automatically creates an incorrect estimate of size and speed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMicrosoft Word les lanternes volantes une meprise couranteV3.3... Wind can seem contradictory too. A witness may feel calm air at ground level while a light drifts steadily above. Wind speed and direction can differ only tens of metres higher, while buildings, woodland and valleys create local currents. In Meuse, where settlements are surrounded by open countryside and shallow valleys, a lantern may follow airflow that is not obvious to an observer standing in a sheltered garden or street. 58:27 Darcy Weir on why AARO might be trying to debunk Puerto Rico's Aguadilla object Reality Check Channel: NewsNation · Views: 224.9K · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 58 minutes Open on YouTube Bar le Duc matched the wind The Bar le Duc report of 1 September 2011 supplied a clearer behavioural match. At about 10.35 pm, a witness on a terrace saw three yellowish lights travelling rapidly and silently from east to west. They eventually disappeared behind cloud. GEIPAN compared their course with the recorded wind direction and concluded that the movement was fully consistent with lanterns released somewhere east of the town, possibly near the stadium. The case was classified B: probable sky lanterns. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBAR LE DUC (55) 01.09.2011 GEIPAN... The three light arrangement is particularly instructive. Three separate points are enough to suggest a triangular craft, especially when they maintain broadly similar spacing. Yet the reported features offer no independent evidence of a solid triangle: there was no visible surface, no outline blocking stars or clouds, no engine noise and no radar or aviation record cited in the published case. What looked like formation flight can therefore be explained by three buoyant objects responding to the same air current. The disappearance behind cloud also supports an ordinary atmospheric interpretation. Lanterns fly low enough to encounter local cloud or mist, and their modest brightness can be lost quickly when obscured. Nothing in the report requires extraordinary acceleration or a sudden technical departure. Even here, GEIPAN did not claim to have located a launcher or recovered debris. The classification remained B rather than A. Nevertheless, the agreement"},{"title":"How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time","url":"/how-morbihans-ufo-mysteries-changed-over/","description":"Morbihan’s UFO history is less a catalogue of enduring mysteries than a record of how striking lights, incomplete memories and dramatic newspaper language...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time Morbihan’s UFO history is less a catalogue of enduring mysteries than a record of how striking lights, incomplete memories and dramatic newspaper language... Morbihan’s UFO history is less a catalogue of enduring mysteries than a record of how striking lights, incomplete memories and dramatic newspaper language can turn ordinary aerial events into extraordinary stories. morbihan Introduction The official record therefore matters more for what it teaches about investigation than for any single unexplained encounter. France’s space agency unit, GEIPAN, has revisited older Morbihan files, compared testimony with astronomy, weather and air traffic information, and sometimes overturned classifications that once appeared mysterious. Published Morbihan cases include securely explained reports, probable explanations and files that remain unresolved only because too little reliable information survives. The available evidence does not establish a landmark encounter with an unknown craft, but it does preserve several revealing examples of how UFO reports arise, spread and change over time. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casGroupe d'études et d'informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés.... Classification · Department S... The 1954 wave around Pontivy Autumn 1954 was the defining period in Morbihan’s popular UFO history. France was experiencing an intense national wave of “flying saucer” reporting, and local newspapers treated unusual lights as part of that larger story. The Morbihan accounts are vivid, but the surviving evidence is mainly press reporting rather than police files, photographs, physical traces or contemporary technical investigation. On the morning of 5 October, La Liberté du Morbihan reported that a motor industry representative driving home towards Pontivy saw a brilliantly illuminated phenomenon apparently following the railway. According to the short article, the light was strong enough for him to switch off his car headlights while the road remained illuminated. The observation reportedly lasted about 30 seconds before the phenomenon disappeared rapidly. The witness was unnamed, and the report supplied no precise location, direction, angular size, weather information or independent corroboration. A meteor is possible, but the surviving description is too sparse to support a confident identification. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 5 octobre 1954, Pontivy, Morbihan... A much larger cluster followed before dawn on 4 November. Residents travelling to work in Pontivy reportedly saw a dazzling white ball or flash that illuminated the surroundings for several seconds. Similar reports came from the countryside around Le Sourn, Melrand and several small localities. Some later retellings described an oval object at low altitude, lights resembling headlamps and, in one account, something apparently rising from near the ground. No traces were found at the alleged location. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, November 4, Pontivy, Morbihan... The geographical pattern weakens the landing interpretation. Comparable luminous observations were reported at roughly the same time over a broad area extending beyond Morbihan to Redon, Nantes, Saint Nazaire, La Baule, Brest and the Vendée. Reports of a fast glowing ball, sparks, momentary daylight like illumination and sightings separated by many kilometres are much more consistent with a bright meteor than with a small craft moving between villages at low altitude. Even contemporary coverage raised a meteor as a possible explanation, although another newspaper floated an unsupported story about a secret anti fog rocket. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, November 4, Pontivy, Morbihan... The episode also shows how later catalogues can strengthen a story without adding new evidence. A brief newspaper description of an atmospheric light was subsequently presented in specialist literature as a possible landing case. Details from separate witnesses and nearby villages were grouped into a single narrative, making the event appear more structured and craft like than the earliest Pontivy report alone would suggest. The most cautious reading is that a genuine, unusually bright aerial phenomenon was seen across the region, while the claims of a landed or departing object rest on fragmentary, second hand material. Groix: a mystery reclassified as the Moon The Groix case of 13–14 April 1982 is Morbihan’s clearest example of a dramatic report being substantially weakened by later analysis. Three people travelling around the island by car watched a powerful light for an extended period and felt that it followed them, stopped when they stopped and resumed moving with them. A fourth person was awakened and also saw a bright, stationary object. For some witnesses, the observation continued from about 2.45 am until 5 am; a similar light was reported again the following night. [Geipan] geipan.fr GROIX (56) 14.04.1982 GEIPAN... The gendarmerie investigated several days later. Officers found no ground marks, electrical disruption or additional independent witnesses. They also checked with the Lann Bihoué naval air base, which reported no relevant observation. The investigators did not challenge the witnesses’ honesty, but noted inconsistencies concerning the date and the reported direction or speed of the light. [Geipan] geipan.fr GROIX (56) 14.04.1982 GEIPAN... GEPAN, GEIPAN’s predecessor, originally placed the case in the unexplained category. A modern reassessment instead classified it as an identified observation of the Moon. The conclusion rested on several mutually supporting details: The phenomenon remained visible for hours and moved slowly from west towards east. Witnesses repeatedly described a partial circular form compatible with the Moon’s phase. Its reported position lay in the sector where the Moon was present. A witness saw an object of the same appearance in the same part of the sky the following night. The apparent pursuit of the car is a familiar distance illusion: a remote astronomical object changes little in bearing as an observer moves through a nearby landscape. GEIPAN also noted that the witnesses said the sky was sufficiently clear for prolonged viewing yet did not report seeing the Moon separately. That absence matters. Had the mysterious object been something else in the same general direction, the Moon should also have been conspicuous. Thin or moving cloud could have altered its appearance and brightness, helping prevent immediate recognition. [Geipan] geipan.fr GROIX (56) 14.04.1982 GEIPAN... Groix is important because the original witnesses need not have fabricated anything. They appear to have experienced a genuinely puzzling observation. The error lay in interpreting a distant, familiar object under unusual viewing conditions. It also demonstrates why “unexplained” is not a permanent verdict: better astronomical software, reconstructed sight lines and accumulated experience can change an old classification decades later. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the official Morbihan files contain GEIPAN is part of the French space agency CNES and has collected, analysed and archived reports since the creation of its predecessor in 1977. It receives testimony directly and through organisations including the gendarmerie, police, civil and military aviation, Météo France and scientific partners. Cases are assessed using two central ideas: the reliability and quantity of the available information, called consistency, and the amount of strangeness remaining after known explanations have been tested. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Its categories are easily misunderstood. Class A means that the phenomenon has been identified; B means that it is probably identified; C means that there is insufficient information to identify it; and D means that it remains unidentified after investigation. A class C report is therefore not strong evidence for an extraordinary object. It usually indicates that the record is too weak to decide anything. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN The Morbihan catalogue reflects this distinction. Recent and revisited entries include: Plouhinec, 2020: a daylight “spiralling” plume photographed over the sea was identified as an aircraft"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-5d5dbc/","description":"GEIPAN records show how astronomy, weather and witness consistency can turn a famous mystery into an ordinary but misunderstood event.","breadcrumb":"How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time","level":2,"text":"Official Files Why Morbihan's Unexplained Cases Keep Changing Morbihan UFOs Official Files Official Files Why Morbihan's Unexplained Cases Keep Changing GEIPAN records show how astronomy, weather and witness consistency can turn a famous mystery into an ordinary but misunderstood event. GEIPAN records show how astronomy, weather and witness consistency can turn a famous mystery into an ordinary but misunderstood event. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies witness reports The Groix sighting reidentified as the Moon What weak and unresolved files really mean morbihan official files Introduction Official investigation has repeatedly changed the status of Morbihan ’s best known unusual sighting reports. Cases once filed as unexplained have later been identified as the Moon, thunderstorms or auroral activity; others have been downgraded not because an ordinary cause was proved, but because the surviving evidence was too weak to support any firm conclusion. France’s official archive therefore shows that “unexplained” is not a permanent verdict and does not mean that investigators have confirmed an extraordinary craft. The clearest example is the long observation made on the island of Groix in April 1982. Witnesses regarded the bright object as something other than the Moon, and the case was initially treated as unexplained. A later GEIPAN review reconstructed the sky and concluded that the reported light’s position, appearance and slow movement matched the Moon closely enough for the file to be reclassified as fully identified. Other Morbihan records demonstrate the remaining possibilities: probable explanations where the fit is strong but incomplete, and unresolved files whose main feature is missing or unreliable information rather than compelling evidence of an unknown vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete17 GEIPANNOTES D'ENQUETE GROIX (56) 13.04.1982June 27, 2017 — 13 Apr 1982 — Une vérification via Stellarium pour Groix (coordonnées: 47°38... Published: June 27, 2017 How GEIPAN’s classifications change a case GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, collects witness reports, examines possible explanations and publishes anonymised case material. Its partners include the gendarmerie, police, civil and military aviation bodies, the national weather service and scientific organisations. That network can supply information that witnesses usually lack, such as astronomical positions, weather conditions, aircraft movements and reports from other locations. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Its four main classifications describe the result of the investigation, not how dramatic the original testimony sounded: A: the phenomenon has been identified. B: it has probably been identified, although some uncertainty remains. C: it cannot be identified because the available information is inadequate. D: it remains unidentified despite investigation and sufficiently usable evidence. The distinction between C and D is especially important. A C file is not a strong mystery. It means that the report cannot be tested properly because details are missing, contradictory or too imprecise. A D case has resisted explanation after investigators judged the evidence adequate enough for meaningful analysis. GEIPAN also states that C and D files may be revisited when new information or improved analytical methods become available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ified due to lack of data or information.Read more... Reclassification is therefore built into the system. In 2017 GEIPAN announced that it had re examined roughly twenty older D cases from 1978–98 and moved them into A, B or C. The review covered forty witness statements and included the Groix case. GEIPAN attributed such changes partly to better software and partly to the investigative experience accumulated since the original inquiries. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr geipan revisite cas GEIPANLe GEIPAN publie 3 nouveaux cas D1 et revisite...28 Jul 2017 — Le GEIPAN publie 3 nouveaux cas D1 et revisite une vingtaine d'anci... This matters because a classification can change in two very different directions. An investigation may discover a persuasive conventional source, moving a report to A or B. Alternatively, it may decide that the original D label overstated the quality of the evidence, moving the case to C. The second outcome does not explain what the witness saw; it corrects how much confidence should be placed in the mystery. Groix: a three hour mystery becomes the Moon On the night of 14 April 1982, several people on Groix observed a conspicuous light over the sea. According to GEIPAN’s summary, the sighting continued from different parts of the island, with some witnesses watching until about 5 a.m. One person returned home and woke another witness at around 3 a.m.; that person also saw a bright, apparently fixed object and reported a similar observation the following night. The long duration and multiple witnesses helped the event acquire the appearance of a substantial case rather than a fleeting mistake. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGROIX (56) 14.04.1982GROIX (56) 14.04.1982. Date d'observation. 14/04... Sur L'île de Groix... PAN alors que les témoins affirmen... The witnesses did not believe they were looking at the Moon. Some testimony explicitly distinguished the object from it, while another witness reportedly stated that there was no Moon visible. Such statements can sound decisive in a retelling: if several observers knew the Moon and rejected it, why should investigators identify it as the cause? GEIPAN’s later review treated that disagreement as something to test rather than accept at face value. Investigators used astronomical reconstruction software to calculate the Moon’s position from Groix during the observation. They found that it was large and bright, with about 70 per cent of its disc illuminated, and that it occupied the same restricted eastern to south eastern area in which the phenomenon had been reported. Near the end of the sighting it would have been roughly 20 degrees above the ocean in the stated viewing direction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete17 GEIPANNOTES D'ENQUETE GROIX (56) 13.04.1982June 27, 2017 — 13 Apr 1982 — Une vérification via Stellarium pour Groix (coordonnées: 47°38... Published: June 27, 2017 The apparent behaviour also fitted. A celestial object watched for several hours changes position gradually, and its movement can be difficult to judge when the observer has few fixed reference points. Thin cloud, haze or uneven cloud cover can hide the Moon’s recognisable outline, enlarge its glow or make its brightness fluctuate. GEIPAN notes more generally that bright planets, stars and other fixed lights can also seem to move because of autokinesis, a visual effect that occurs when a small light is viewed against a dark, poorly referenced background. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete17 GEIPANNOTES D'ENQUETE GROIX (56) 13.04.1982June 27, 2017 — 13 Apr 1982 — Une vérification via Stellarium pour Groix (coordonnées: 47°38... Published: June 27, 2017 The investigators therefore gave more weight to the reconstructed geometry than to the witnesses’ confidence that the Moon was absent. The final record lists Groix as classification A, with “Moon” as the phenomenon, very low strangeness and only moderate evidential consistency. The case was no longer treated as an unexplained object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This conclusion does not require the witnesses to have been dishonest or careless. It shows how recognition can fail when a familiar object appears in unfamiliar conditions. A bright Moon seen through broken cloud over a dark sea may look larger, more isolated or more structured than expected. Several people can then reinforce one another’s interpretation while still observing the same ordinary source. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why several witnesses did not settle the issue Multiple testimony usually improves a file because it can confirm that something visible was present. It does not automatically establish the object’s identity, distance or physical size. In the Groix case, the additional witnesses strengthened the conclusion that a bright light occupied the sky for a long period. They did not provide independent measurements proving that it was nearby, mechanical or separate from the Moon. Witness agreement can also be less independent than it first appears. People observing together may use the same estimated direction, repeat the same comparisons or adopt another person’s interpretation. Waking someone and drawing attention to a particular part of the sky confirms visibility, but it may also guide that person towards the"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-096610/","description":"The Pontivy sightings grew from brief reports of brilliant lights into a wider story of possible landings and mysterious craft.","breadcrumb":"How Morbihan's UFO Mysteries Changed Over Time","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave Did a Meteor Spark Pontivy's UFO Wave? Morbihan UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave Did a Meteor Spark Pontivy's UFO Wave? The Pontivy sightings grew from brief reports of brilliant lights into a wider story of possible landings and mysterious craft. The Pontivy sightings grew from brief reports of brilliant lights into a wider story of possible landings and mysterious craft. event time window The October and November sightings How the reports spread across western France Why later retellings made the event stranger morbihan 1954 wave Introduction Pontivy’s best known episode in France’s 1954 flying saucer wave was probably not a single encounter with a mysterious craft. It was a short sequence of luminous sightings whose meaning changed as the story was repeated. A poorly documented report on 5 October described a brilliant light seen by a motorist near Pontivy. A much larger event on 4 November was witnessed across Morbihan and far beyond, with people describing a white flash, a fast moving glowing ball and sparks. That broad, near simultaneous pattern strongly favours a bright meteor for the November event. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... The debate matters because later accounts made the Pontivy episode considerably stranger than the earliest surviving newspaper report. A brief atmospheric phenomenon became an oval machine, two headlamps and even a possible take off from the ground near Le Sourn. Yet no physical trace was found, no photograph is known, and no contemporary technical investigation survives. The case is therefore most useful as an example of how an impressive natural event can develop into a “landing” story through retelling, cataloguing and the expectations of a national UFO wave. Flying Carrots & Little Divers The French UFO Wave of 1954 The Dark Histories Podcast Channel: Vintage Radio Waves Open on YouTube The October and November sightings The first Pontivy report appeared during the most intense period of France’s autumn 1954 UFO wave. On the morning of 5 October, an unnamed representative of the Renault motor company was said to be driving home from Saint Brieuc when he saw what the newspaper called a flying saucer apparently following the railway towards Pontivy. Its light was reportedly so strong that he switched off his car headlights while the road remained illuminated. The phenomenon was visible for about 30 seconds before disappearing quickly. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 5 octobre 1954, Pontivy, Morbihan... That is almost everything the surviving account tells us. It gives no precise observation point, clock time, direction of travel, elevation, apparent size, colour, weather conditions or named witness. The wording “following the railway” may describe the observer’s visual impression rather than a measured course. A meteor, aircraft light or another bright aerial phenomenon cannot be tested properly against such a thin description. The specialist catalogue preserving the article accordingly treats a meteor as possible but leaves the case unidentified because no matching regional reports have been found for that morning. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 5 octobre 1954, Pontivy, Morbihan... The 4 November event is much better supported, although not necessarily more mysterious. Shortly before 7 a.m., people travelling to work in Pontivy reportedly saw a dazzling light illuminate the surroundings for several seconds. Witnesses interviewed by Les Nouvelles du Morbihan described a luminous ball producing a white, lightning like glare, and the newspaper said the phenomenon was also seen in the surrounding countryside. Unlike the solitary October account, this was evidently a multiple witness event. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... Later versions associated the same morning with reports from Locmaria, Kervern, Guériellec, Kermer, Kerlay, Melrand and Le Sourn. Two farmers were said to have seen a low, white oval object moving south while appearing to roll. Near Le Sourn, another farmer reportedly encountered two brilliant lights resembling headlamps and believed that an object five to seven metres long rose from near the ground. He returned after daylight but found no marks or other physical evidence. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... These added details create the impression of a close encounter, but their documentary status is weaker than the basic Pontivy report. The detailed “landing” narrative is known through a 1994 article in a UFO magazine, published four decades after the event and based on material attributed to the regional press. The surviving citation trail does not provide contemporary interview notes, police statements or a technical site report with which to test how accurately the witnesses were quoted. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... How the reports spread across western France The strongest argument for a meteor comes from geography. At roughly 7.15 a.m. on 4 November, more than 50 workers near Redon reportedly saw a luminous ball moving rapidly and throwing off sparks. A bus driver travelling towards Muzillac noticed an intense light flooding the road before seeing a phosphorescent object for a few seconds. Postal workers and other witnesses around Redon also reported the phenomenon, although their estimates of its direction differed. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... The same newspaper account listed observations at Les Sables d’Olonne, Nantes, Saint Nazaire, La Baule, La Roche Bernard, Pontivy and Brest. This distribution stretches across a substantial part of western France. It is difficult to reconcile with a small vehicle hovering at low altitude near a Pontivy farm, but it is the pattern expected when a very bright object burns high in the atmosphere and can be seen from places separated by many tens or hundreds of kilometres. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... A fireball is an unusually bright meteor. Such events can appear brighter than Venus, produce a terminal flash or visible fragments, and illuminate the landscape for several seconds. Because they occur high above the ground, observers across a wide region may see the same event while reporting different apparent directions, shapes and distances. Modern fireballs have likewise generated reports over large areas of France and neighbouring regions. [esa.int] blogs.esa.int Unidentified Fiery Object! Rocket Science ESA's blogs23 Jan 2020 — Fireballs are caused as small asteroids strike Earth's atmosphere, entirely or almost entirel... That helps explain several features of the 4 November accounts: The brief duration: most descriptions concern only a few seconds. The sudden white glare: witnesses compared it with lightning or daylight. The glowing ball and sparks: these are characteristic descriptions of a bright, fragmenting meteor. The wide viewing area: reports extended from Brittany to the Loire Atlantique coast and the Vendée. The lack of traces near Le Sourn: a distant atmospheric event would leave nothing at the place where one observer believed it had risen. The reported times are not perfectly consistent. Some later Morbihan accounts give about 6.30 a.m., while Pontivy and Redon reports cluster nearer 7.00–7.15. That difference may reflect rounded times, imperfect clocks, later transcription or the merging of separate observations. It prevents a completely precise reconstruction, but it does not rescue the landing theory: the descriptions of a brilliant, short lived light across a broad region remain far more coherent as a fireball event than as several low level craft sightings. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... Contemporary newspapers themselves considered a meteor. La Gazette de Redon presented the possibilities as a saucer, meteor or “anti fog rocket”. The rocket suggestion came from another newspaper, which claimed that a secret experimental device was being tested between Saint Nazaire and Donges. No supporting technical evidence accompanied that claim, and it appears to have been speculation rather than the result of an official inquiry. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 4 novembre, Pontivy, Morbihan... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why later retellings made the event stranger The earliest known Pontivy article of 4 November used cautious language. It called the"},{"title":"Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-moselle-ufo-cases-still-resist/","description":"Moselle’s UFO history is not built around one universally recognised “classic” case.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Moselle’s UFO history is not built around one universally recognised “classic” case. Moselle’s UFO history is not built around one universally recognised “classic” case. Instead, it is a layered record of dramatic 1954 newspaper stories, a handful of officially unresolved observations, military aircraft misidentifications, astronomical and satellite sightings, balloons, lanterns and at least one proven photographic hoax. moselle Introduction The strongest modern material comes from GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its Moselle files include cases resolved as aircraft, helicopters, the International Space Station, satellites and balloons, alongside a smaller number that remain unidentified after investigation. The department’s most useful lesson is therefore not that it has produced proof of extraordinary craft, but that careful re examination can separate genuinely puzzling observations from cases that only looked inexplicable at first. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN(DPT) MOSELLE (57) 15.10.2014 GEIPAN... Moselle’s record at a glance Moselle has generated reports across several distinct periods. The best known historical concentration came during the nationwide French “flying saucer” wave of autumn 1954. Later official records include unresolved cases at Guébling in 1978, Metz in 1979 and Ars sur Moselle in 1990, as well as numerous weaker or explained reports from the 1980s onwards. Recent cases around Metz, Kanfen, Yutz and Walscheid have often been attributed to aircraft, satellites, the International Space Station, lanterns or balloons. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN These categories should not be treated as equivalent. GEIPAN evaluates both a report’s “strangeness” and its “consistency”: how difficult it is to match with a known phenomenon, and how much reliable information supports it. Class A means an explanation is regarded as nearly certain, B means probable, C means the available information is inadequate, and D1 or D2 denotes an unexplained case with sufficient supporting material. An unexplained classification means that investigators could not identify the phenomenon from the evidence available; it does not establish an extraterrestrial origin. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN That distinction is especially important in Moselle, where the surviving record ranges from detailed witness interviews and official enquiries to short newspaper items repeated through later UFO literature. The 1954 wave: vivid stories, uneven evidence Autumn 1954 produced an exceptional surge of flying saucer reporting across France. Moselle’s share included alleged landings, humanoid encounters, strange lights, supposed vehicle effects and ordinary meteors reported in the language of the saucer craze. A specialist catalogue assembled from contemporary press and later UFO sources lists reports from places including Pournoy la Chétive, Turquestein, Saint Avold, Jouy aux Arches, Faulquemont and Longeville lès Saint Avold. The same catalogue also labels several Moselle entries as probable meteors, misinterpretations, inventions or insufficiently documented claims. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap sightings catalogue for the Moselle department... The central problem is source quality. Most of these incidents pre date France’s official investigation programme by more than two decades. Their details often come from newspaper dispatches, later books and catalogues rather than preserved technical files. Repetition across several publications may show that a story circulated widely, but it does not necessarily provide independent confirmation. Pournoy la Chétive and the “three legged saucer” One of Moselle’s most memorable stories arose near Pournoy la Chétive in October 1954. Contemporary press accounts said that three children reported seeing a landed saucer and a strange passenger near the village. The children reportedly repeated the story to parents, the mayor and their teacher, while residents divided between belief and ridicule. Later versions describe a hairy faced being or a figure dressed rather like a cassock, supposedly speaking in an incomprehensible language. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, 8 octobre, Pournoy La Chétive, MoselleA POURNOY LA CHETIVE, près de Verny (Moselle), trois enfants soutiennent qu'il... The case has remained part of local UFO folklore because it combines a claimed landing, several young witnesses and an alleged occupant. Yet its evidential weaknesses are substantial. The witnesses were children, the account reached the public through sensational press coverage, and there is no surviving physical evidence or modern official investigation capable of testing the claims. Differences between retellings—including the number of children emphasised and the being’s appearance—also warn against treating every later detail as secure. Pournoy la Chétive is therefore historically important as an example of how the 1954 wave was experienced and reported in Moselle. It is not strong evidence that a non human visitor landed there. Turquestein: the most dramatic roadside claim The Turquestein incident of 20 October 1954 is more detailed and more troubling, although still dependent largely on contemporary journalism. A 25 year old lorry driver, Jean Schonbrenner, reportedly encountered an intense yellow orange light across the road while travelling near Turquestein. He said his engine stopped, his hands felt fixed to the wheel and he experienced heat as an illuminated, inverted cone like object rose and moved away. Reports stated that the gendarmerie investigated but found no physical traces on the road. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This case matters because it includes several elements valued by UFO researchers: close range, an apparently structured object, a claimed vehicle effect, a physical sensation and police involvement. But each element rests overwhelmingly on one witness’s account. No trace, instrument record or independent observation is known to have confirmed the event. Later sceptical commentary also attacked the witness’s credibility, although the allegation appears to have been based on a conversation with his former employer rather than a documented contemporary medical or police finding. That criticism should therefore be treated cautiously rather than used as a decisive debunking. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The fairest assessment is that Turquestein remains a striking but weakly verifiable historical report. Its details are richer than those of many 1954 stories, yet the lack of surviving objective evidence prevents a reliable reconstruction. 5:29 Ancient Aliens: 300+ \"Flying Saucer\" Incidents in France (Season 19) History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 203.0K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube The Sarreguemines photographs that collapsed Moselle’s 1954 history also contains a clear warning about photographic evidence. Three spectacular pictures said to show a luminous saucer near Sarreguemines were offered to the press. The object was later revealed to be a small model made from domestic materials, photographed under bright bulbs. According to the contemporary account, the pictures had originally been produced as a student prank; a photographic employee then tried to present them as genuine and confessed after the student alerted the authorities. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This was not simply a vague misidentification. It was a documented false presentation of staged photographs. Its importance lies in showing how rapidly apparent “proof” could be manufactured and distributed during the 1954 media wave. It also provides a useful counterweight to claims that sceptical explanations are always speculative. Some Moselle reports cannot now be resolved, but the Sarreguemines episode was exposed through identifiable materials, witnesses and an admission. Unresolved cases should not be assumed fraudulent; equally, dramatic photographs should not be accepted merely because they look persuasive. Ars sur Moselle: a surviving official unknown The Ars sur Moselle case of 27 February 1990 is one of the department’s clearest officially unresolved reports. GEIPAN records that a young couple travelling by car saw a silent round or oval object fitted with three powerful white lights. They estimated it at roughly 2.5 to 3 metres across, moving slowly at very low altitude. After following it for about ten minutes, they said it"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-9929c3/","description":"The 1954 wave produced Moselle's most vivid landing, occupant and roadside stories, but surviving evidence is uneven and often press-led.","breadcrumb":"Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave Why Did Moselle Erupt With UFO Stories in 1954? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave Why Did Moselle Erupt With UFO Stories in 1954? The 1954 wave produced Moselle's most vivid landing, occupant and roadside stories, but surviving evidence is uneven and often press led. The 1954 wave produced Moselle's most vivid landing, occupant and roadside stories, but surviving evidence is uneven and often press led. event time window How the 1954 wave spread across Moselle Pournoy la Chetive, Turquestein and other landmark reports Hoaxes, meteors and the limits of press evidence moselle 1954 wave Introduction Moselle ’s 1954 UFO wave was dramatic in story but fragile in evidence. During a few crowded weeks in autumn, newspapers reported luminous discs, supposed landings, roadside paralysis, humanoid occupants and even close range photographs. The best known claims came from Pournoy la Chétive, where children described a small hairy figure beside a three legged craft, and Turquestein, where a driver said a glowing object stopped his vehicle and temporarily paralysed him. These stories matter because they became some of the department’s most frequently repeated UFO cases. Yet most survive through newspaper articles and later UFO catalogues rather than contemporary police files, physical evidence or technical investigation. Several reports were quickly traced to meteors, ordinary people, misunderstandings or deliberate deception. Others remain unverified rather than genuinely unexplained. Moselle’s wave is therefore most revealing as a case study in how real observations, frightened interpretation, journalistic amplification and outright hoaxing could merge during France’s extraordinary flying saucer season of 1954. How the wave spread across Moselle France experienced an exceptional concentration of flying saucer reports during September and October 1954. Contemporary newspapers carried new accounts almost daily, often placing several unrelated sightings in the same article. Reports of lights, discs and “cigars” appeared alongside alleged landings and encounters with small beings. Municipal archives in Nantes, reviewing the same national wave, show how newspapers freely mixed apparently sincere observations with exposed pranks and mundane incidents, including a supposed hairy “Martian” elsewhere in France that was reportedly an escaped performing goat. [Archives Nantes] archives.nantes.fr Archives Nantes Ils arrivent! Quand la presse évoque de mystérieuses rencontres... Nantes Métropole ArchivesL’automne 1954 est une période où les observations d’ov... Moselle fitted this national pattern closely. One specialist catalogue, compiled from contemporary press and later UFO literature, lists 36 reported incidents in the department during 1954, with nearly all concentrated between 19 September and 1 November. The geographical spread included Metz, Pournoy la Chétive, Turquestein, Saint Avold, Jouy aux Arches, Faulquemont, Guébling, Freistroff, Hettange Grande and several smaller villages. The catalogue itself does not treat them as equally credible: it marks some as unidentified, but describes others as probable meteors, possible aircraft or balloons, press inventions, witness inventions, misinterpretations and a photographic hoax. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap sightings catalogue for the Moselle department... The apparent density of the wave can be misleading. Several entries may represent different descriptions of the same astronomical event. Reports from Metz and Plappeville on 16 October, for example, were associated in the catalogue with a meteor seen at roughly the same time. Faulquemont’s report on 19 October was also assessed as a probable meteor, while other cases were considered too poorly documented to judge. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap sightings catalogue for the Moselle department... The press environment encouraged reports to acquire a recognisable flying saucer shape. A distant light was no longer merely a light: it might become a disc with windows. A meteor appearing to descend could become a landed craft. A person carrying a torch could be interpreted as an occupant. Once newspapers had established “Martians”, three legged saucers and stalled engines as familiar motifs, later witnesses and reporters had a ready made vocabulary with which to describe ambiguous experiences. This does not mean that every witness lied or imagined the entire event. Many people probably saw genuine lights, meteors, aircraft or unfamiliar objects. The central historical question is how accurately those initial observations survived the process of retelling. Moselle’s most celebrated cases show that the story printed a few days later could be considerably more extraordinary than the evidence underneath it. Pournoy la Chétive: the three legged saucer The most famous Moselle occupant story was reported at Pournoy la Chétive, south of Metz, on the evening of 8 October 1954. Three children had been roller skating near the cemetery as darkness fell. According to the first local newspaper account, they said that a round luminous craft, about 2.5 metres across and marked with dark, yellow and white stripes, descended nearby and rested on three legs. A small figure then appeared carrying a light. The being was described as roughly 1.2 metres tall, with large eyes, a hairy face and a black garment resembling a priest’s cassock. It supposedly spoke in an incomprehensible language before the frightened children fled. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Pournoy La Chétive, Moselle... The account had several features that made it ideal newspaper material: young witnesses, a landed machine, a clearly described occupant and a village divided between believers and mockers. The children reportedly repeated the story to their parents, teacher and mayor. A teenager, Robert Maguin, was also said to have seen a luminous object at a distance, although he did not claim to have observed the small figure. Versions of the story soon appeared in regional and national newspapers, sometimes altering the children’s ages, the time of the encounter and other details. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Pournoy La Chétive, Moselle... What is often omitted from later retellings is that a corrective article appeared almost immediately. A local enquiry reportedly identified the “Martian” as a resident of the village. The remaining aerial component was subsequently interpreted by sceptical investigators as a bright meteor or shooting star whose apparent descent gave the impression that it had landed. In that reconstruction, the children combined two separate things: a luminous object in the sky and a local person carrying a lamp. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Pournoy La Chétive, Moselle... Later investigations complicated rather than settled the case. In accounts of interviews conducted during the late 1980s, some witnesses continued to recall an unexplained light. Jean Pierre Hirsch was said to remember a strong white blue glow that appeared to descend, while Robert Maguin reportedly confirmed seeing a coloured luminous phenomenon from farther away. Gilbert Calba, the oldest child, acknowledged a light but denied or avoided confirming the occupant story. Claims that grass failed to regrow at a landing site for ten years emerged only decades after the event and do not appear to have been supported by contemporary photographs, measurements or soil samples. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 8, Pournoy La Chétive, Moselle... The late testimony does not restore the original three legged saucer narrative. It indicates that at least some of the children probably did see a striking light, but the extraordinary structural details became less secure, not more secure, under later questioning. The only specific “occupant” identified by the near contemporary enquiry was an ordinary inhabitant. Pournoy la Chétive is therefore best classified as a real childhood fright transformed into a much stronger close encounter story. A meteor and misidentified person explanation cannot be proved with absolute certainty, but it accounts for more of the evidence than a landed craft does. Most importantly, it appeared within days rather than being invented many decades later. Turquestein: paralysis on the road The Turquestein report of 20 October 1954 is Moselle’s most famous vehicle interference claim. Jean Schoubrenner, a young driver from the Sarrebourg area, reportedly encountered a luminous object on or beside Route Nationale 393 at about 6.30 or 6.45 in the evening. Newspaper versions described the object as a cone or glowing form with a phosphorescent base and a yellow orange upper section."},{"title":"GEIPAN Files","url":"/geipan-files-3a6b0c/","description":"Official Moselle files show how aircraft, satellites and balloons are separated from a smaller group of sightings that remain unexplained.","breadcrumb":"Which Moselle UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Files What Do Moselle's Official UFO Files Actually Prove? GEIPAN Files GEIPAN Files What Do Moselle's Official UFO Files Actually Prove? Official Moselle files show how aircraft, satellites and balloons are separated from a smaller group of sightings that remain unexplained. Official Moselle files show how aircraft, satellites and balloons are separated from a smaller group of sightings that remain unexplained. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies strangeness and evidence Ars sur Moselle and other unresolved observations Aircraft, satellites, balloons and solved cases moselle geipan files Introduction Moselle ’s official UFO record does not prove that extraordinary craft have crossed the department. It proves something more limited, but still useful: when reports are checked against aircraft movements, orbital objects, astronomy, balloons and witness circumstances, many apparently mysterious sightings become ordinary. A smaller residue remains officially unidentified because the available evidence has not yielded a satisfactory explanation. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for unidentified aerospace phenomena, lists several Moselle cases in its unexplained category, notably Guébling in 1978, Metz in 1979, Ars sur Moselle in 1990 and Rémilly in 1993. Yet the same archive contains many more reports classified as identified, probably identified or too poorly documented to assess. That balance is essential. An unexplained file records the limit of an investigation, not confirmation of an alien vehicle or even proof that a solid object was present. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUEBLING (57) 07.01.1978 GEIPAN... How GEIPAN separates mystery from missing information GEIPAN does not classify a sighting simply according to how unusual it sounded to the witness. Its method considers two linked questions: how strange the observation remains after comparison with known phenomena, and how consistent the supporting evidence is. Consistency depends on the quantity, precision and reliability of the information collected, including the number and independence of witnesses, the coherence of their accounts and the presence of photographs, radar records or other objective material. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The archive’s main categories are: A: the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B: a known explanation is considered probable. C: no identification can be made because the information is inadequate. D: the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. Since 2008, GEIPAN has further distinguished D1 and D2 cases according to the strength and strangeness of the evidence, although many older Moselle pages still display the broader legacy label “D”. A case can also be reconsidered if new information emerges or older records are re examined. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN This makes the difference between C and D especially important. A C case is not a compelling mystery merely because it lacks an explanation. It is usually a report that cannot be tested properly: the time may be uncertain, the direction missing, the witness unavailable or the description too vague. Moselle’s archive contains numerous such entries, including reports from Bouzonville, Phalsbourg, Metz, Guénange, Hayange, Florange, Thionville and Fameck. Their status means “insufficient evidence”, not “unexplainable phenomenon”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPAN also stresses that testimony is vulnerable to ordinary perceptual effects. Distance and speed are difficult to estimate for an unidentified light, moving observers can make an aircraft appear stationary, and memory may incorporate later interpretations. This does not imply dishonesty. It explains why a sincere, detailed account can still describe an aircraft, planet or satellite in unfamiliar terms. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Moselle observations still listed as unexplained Guébling, 7 January 1978 At about 4.50 am, a witness travelling by moped near Guébling reported three very bright lights close to ground level. According to GEIPAN’s summary, one light separated from the others, approached and appeared to follow the witness as far as the entrance to a neighbouring village, where it disappeared. No sound was heard. Investigators checked whether a helicopter had been operating in the area and found no corresponding flight. The phenomenon remained unidentified and the case was classified D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUEBLING (57) 07.01.1978 GEIPAN... The case is memorable because it involved apparent interaction: the light did not merely remain in the distance but seemed to pursue the rider. That impression, however, is also one of its evidential weaknesses. With a single moving witness and no confirmed distance to the light, a fixed or distant source can appear to maintain its position relative to an observer. The official page records the helicopter check but does not present radar data, photographs, physical traces or an independent second witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGUEBLING (57) 07.01.1978 GEIPAN... Guébling therefore deserves its unresolved status without being treated as strong proof of an unknown machine. The report contains genuine residual strangeness, particularly the low apparent altitude and silent pursuit, but the public file offers limited objective information with which to test alternative explanations decades later. Metz, 3 December 1979 The Metz case has a broader witness base. At approximately 4.50 pm, two people reported a triangular object producing two trails of red smoke. They said it stopped for several seconds while passing above a telecommunications tower and then resumed its movement. They followed it visually until it disappeared, with the red trails reportedly remaining visible. A third person saw a bright point at roughly the same time, although GEIPAN could not establish that this was the same phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMETZ (57) 1979 GEIPAN... Investigators found no matching air traffic, leaving the observation classified D. Its daylight or twilight timing, reported shape, smoke like trails and multiple witnesses make it more substantial than a brief report of an isolated nocturnal light. The claimed halt above a prominent tower is the detail that most resists a simple aircraft explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMETZ (57) 1979 GEIPAN... Even so, the evidence is not as convergent as a quick summary may suggest. The third witness described only a bright point, and GEIPAN explicitly states that the two observations could not be correlated. Nor does the public summary cite radar confirmation, photographs or independently measured movement. The absence of identified traffic weakens the ordinary aircraft hypothesis, but it does not establish that no aircraft, atmospheric effect or misjudged trajectory was involved. 7:52 UFO Emits Weird White Material Onto Small French Town Close Encounters Channel: Quest TV · Views: 1.4M · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Ars sur Moselle, 27 February 1990 A young couple driving near Ars sur Moselle reported a silent, round or oval object resembling a rugby ball, equipped with three powerful white lights. They estimated its diameter at roughly 2.5 to 3 metres and said it travelled slowly at very low altitude. After they followed it for about ten minutes, it allegedly departed at very high speed. GEIPAN’s investigation did not match the report to a known aircraft or another recognised phenomenon, and the case remains classified D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANARS SUR MOSELLE (57) 1990 GEIPAN... This is arguably Moselle’s most vivid official unresolved account. It combines two witnesses, prolonged observation, apparent structure, low altitude, silence and an abrupt change in speed. Those features make it much harder to dismiss as a momentary glimpse of a star or satellite. They do not, however, provide independently verified measurements. The estimated size assumes a distance that the witnesses could not objectively know. A bright object against a dark sky may appear closer, lower and more sharply defined than it is. Following the light by car also complicates judgements of motion: changes in road direction and relative position can create striking apparent accelerations or periods of hovering. GEIPAN itself regards such moving observer effects as an important source of apparently extraordinary aircraft behaviour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The case remains interesting because no specific conventional source was demonstrated, not because the reported dimensions or acceleration were technically confirmed. Its evidential foundation is still principally the couple’s testimony."},{"title":"What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal","url":"/what-nievres-ufo-files-actually-reveal/","description":"Nièvre has a modest but revealing UFO history. The French space agency’s public case data records only a small group of official reports from the department,...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal Nièvre has a modest but revealing UFO history. The French space agency’s public case data records only a small group of official reports from the department,... Nièvre has a modest but revealing UFO history. The French space agency’s public case data records only a small group of official reports from the department, ranging from puzzling coloured lights in the 1970s to recent observations identified as satellites, planets, balloons or sky lanterns. nievre Introduction Of the 13 Nièvre cases in the GEIPAN public dataset available for this review, none is classified as a genuinely unexplained category D case. Three are securely identified, six are probably identified, and four remain unidentifiable because the evidence is insufficient. That distinction matters: “insufficient information” does not mean that an extraordinary craft was detected. It means that the surviving testimony cannot support either a firm conventional explanation or a stronger anomalous claim. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ation précédemment classé D et nommé...Read more... What the official record contains France’s principal official body for these reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the National Centre for Space Studies, or CNES. It collects witness accounts submitted directly or passed through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation services, checks possible astronomical, atmospheric and human causes, and publishes case material where possible. The organisation began as GEPAN in 1977, later became SEPRA and has operated as GEIPAN since 2005. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. GEIPAN uses classifications that describe the evidential outcome rather than the emotional force of a sighting: A: identified with a high level of confidence. B: probably identified, although some uncertainty remains. C: impossible to identify because the available information is inadequate. D: unexplained after investigation, with sufficiently strong and unusual evidence. The Nièvre files are concentrated between 1976 and 2023. They include reports from Châteauneuf Val de Bargis, Lormes, Decize, Chaulgnes, Cosne Cours sur Loire, Guérigny, Saint Léger des Vignes, Narcy, Gien sur Cure and La Charité sur Loire. This is a dispersed departmental record rather than a clear hotspot centred on Nevers, an airfield or a strategic installation. The cases also do not form an obvious short, intense “flap” in which many closely related sightings occurred within days or weeks. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr ation précédemment classé D et nommé...Read more... The overall pattern is: three category A cases, attributed to the International Space Station or a planetary conjunction; six category B cases, including probable sky lanterns, a wind borne balloon, a satellite flare and an older case whose published identification is not specified; four category C cases, left undecided because crucial observational details were absent or unreliable. That balance makes Nièvre valuable as a study in how UFO reports are resolved, rather than as evidence for an exceptional concentration of unknown aircraft. The older cases remain the weakest Châteauneuf Val de Bargis, 19 July 1976 The earliest published Nièvre case concerns a stationary green luminous phenomenon that reportedly disappeared towards the north east. It is classified C. The official record does not preserve enough dependable information to reconstruct its angular position, duration, movement, environmental circumstances or relationship to known objects in the sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A green light can have many origins, including an aircraft navigation light, a bright astronomical object distorted by atmospheric conditions, a meteor, fireworks or an inaccurately remembered colour impression. None can be selected confidently here. The sensible conclusion is narrower: something was reported, but the record is too slight to determine what the witness saw. This case illustrates a recurring problem with older UFO archives. A brief statement may preserve the most dramatic feature—colour, sudden departure or apparent immobility—while omitting the measurements needed for later testing. Without a precise time, viewing direction, elevation and duration, astronomical and aviation checks become much less reliable. Lormes, October 1978 The Lormes report described red glows at night, apparently preceded by a whistling sound. GEIPAN ultimately placed it in category B, meaning that investigators considered a conventional identification probable, although the current searchable record describes the phenomenon only as identified without specifying the cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The combination of light and sound may initially seem stronger than a light only report. In practice, it introduces another uncertainty. Witnesses can associate a noise with a distant light even when the two have separate sources, particularly if the light’s distance is unknown. Aircraft, fireworks and other human activity remain plausible broad possibilities, but the accessible summary does not justify choosing among them. The case has therefore weakened with time rather than strengthened. No published radar confirmation, independent technical record or later corroborating investigation has emerged to turn it into a major Nièvre incident. 21:09 A ‘Falling Star’ That Wasn’t… Close Encounters 112 Channel: SpaceRip · Views: 39.3K · Uploaded: December 2025 · Length: 21 minutes Open on YouTube Decize, 1979 At Decize, a witness reported a moving ball with orange reflections. GEIPAN classifies the file C because reliable information is lacking. The database gives the observation date as 5 December 1979, while retaining the broader case title “Decize 1979”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANDECIZE (58) 1979 05/12/1979 C Nièvre Manque d'informations fiables 02/09/2008 1979 12 05... Orange luminous balls are among the least diagnostic forms in UFO reporting. A distant aircraft seen nearly head on, a celestial body close to the horizon, a meteor, an illuminated balloon or a fire related object may all appear as a compact orange light when distance and structure cannot be judged. The word “ball” often describes an apparent patch of light rather than a clearly resolved spherical object. As with Châteauneuf Val de Bargis, the official decision does not certify an unknown vehicle. It records the failure of the evidence to discriminate between ordinary possibilities. Cosne and Saint Père: vivid testimony, limited proof The most visually elaborate Nièvre account in the official files occurred on 9 November 2011 during a journey from Cosne sur Loire towards Saint Père. Witnesses described a blue arc like display followed by a large orange wheel that appeared stationary and then vanished abruptly. The report was made to the gendarmerie, and GEIPAN assembled witness material, weather information and an investigation report. Nevertheless, it remains category C because the information was not sufficiently reliable for identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This is important because the strangeness of a description and the strength of a case are not the same thing. “Large wheel”, “blue arc” and sudden disappearance sound highly unusual, but an observer who does not know an object’s distance cannot reliably calculate its physical size. A small nearby effect and a very large distant phenomenon may occupy the same apparent angle in the sky. Several possibilities could produce parts of the account: reflections or internal glare through a vehicle window, atmospheric optical effects, illuminated cloud, distant artificial lighting, or more than one unrelated stimulus interpreted as a continuous event. Yet the documentation does not establish any of these decisively. Nor does it provide independent imagery, radar data or a precisely traceable object. The two witness element makes the observation more credible as a real experience, but it does not automatically settle its cause. People travelling together share the same viewing conditions and can influence one another’s description, direction of attention and interpretation. GEIPAN’s category C judgement is therefore appropriately cautious. Satellites and planets solved three cases The clearest lesson from the modern Nièvre reports is that familiar objects can look unfamiliar when brightness changes distort the observer’s sense of motion and distance. At Chaulgnes on 25 June 2010, a luminous spherical object was reported moving through the sky. GEIPAN matched the observation to a passage of the International Space Station and classified it A. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. At Gien sur Cure on 24 May 2017, two witnesses who had been observing Jupiter saw a very"},{"title":"Solved Sightings","url":"/solved-sightings-490592/","description":"Satellites, planets, balloons and sky lanterns account for most modern Nievre reports that investigators could resolve.","breadcrumb":"What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Solved Sightings How Ordinary Objects Became Nievre UFOs Solved Sightings Solved Sightings How Ordinary Objects Became Nievre UFOs Satellites, planets, balloons and sky lanterns account for most modern Nievre reports that investigators could resolve. Satellites, planets, balloons and sky lanterns account for most modern Nievre reports that investigators could resolve. mechanism The International Space Station over Chaulgnes Planets, balloons and satellite flares Why familiar lights can appear extraordinary nievre solved sightings Introduction Most modern UFO reports in Nièvre that investigators could resolve were not caused by rare aircraft or unknown technology. They arose from familiar things seen under unfamiliar conditions: the International Space Station, bright planets, satellite reflections, drifting balloons and sky lanterns. These objects can look extraordinary because a dark sky removes the visual clues people normally use to judge distance, speed and size. The official record is especially useful because several reports came with enough detail—time, direction, duration, photographs or weather conditions—to test ordinary explanations. The resulting identifications do not suggest that witnesses invented what they saw. In most cases, they accurately noticed a striking light but understandably misread its scale or movement. Nièvre’s solved sightings therefore show how investigation can turn a compelling personal experience into a testable astronomical or atmospheric event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The International Space Station over Chaulgnes On 25 June 2010, a witness at Chaulgnes reported a luminous spherical object crossing the night sky at about 23:40. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, identified it as a passage of the International Space Station and classified the case A, its category for a securely explained report. [Geipan] geipan.fr CHAULGNES (58) 25.06.2010Observation du déplacement d'un objet sphérique lumineux: passage de l' ISS. Description. Le 25 juin 2010... The ISS is unusually effective at creating UFO reports because it does not resemble an aircraft when seen with the naked eye. It normally appears as a bright, steady point, without the red, green or flashing navigation lights expected from a plane. It moves smoothly across a large part of the sky and may seem to appear or disappear abruptly when it enters or leaves the Earth’s shadow. GEIPAN notes that visible satellites commonly travel in a straight line for several minutes and can rival a bright star or, in the ISS’s case, Venus. [Geipan] geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. Those characteristics explain why the Chaulgnes sighting could feel more unusual than its eventual identification suggests. Calling the light “spherical” did not establish that a visible globe or structured craft was present. At great distance, any unresolved point source is perceived as a small disc or point of light. Without a known size, the observer also had no dependable way to judge its altitude or speed. A second Nièvre case reinforces the identification pattern. GEIPAN lists a 24 May 2017 observation at Gien sur Cure as another category A ISS case. Two securely identified station sightings in the same department show that the Chaulgnes report was not an isolated or improvised explanation: the ISS repeatedly produced the type of bright, silent and regular movement that witnesses associated with an unknown object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The decisive evidence in such cases is not simply that a satellite might have been visible. Investigators can compare the reported time and viewing direction with calculated orbital passages. Satellite paths are predictable, so agreement between the observed route and the computed pass can support a much stronger conclusion than resemblance alone. GEIPAN recommends recording the moment when a light passes a known star because precise timing and position allow this kind of check. [Geipan] geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. Two “objects” that were Venus and Jupiter The Saint Léger des Vignes case of 30 June 2015 shows that apparent movement is not always real movement. Between 23:30 and midnight, a witness watched two yellow orange lights positioned one above the other. They seemed to move very slowly, remained visible for a long period and were photographed. GEIPAN reconstructed the sky and found Venus and Jupiter close together above the west north western horizon. The two lights sank towards the horizon exactly as the planets would, leading to a category A identification as a planetary conjunction. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LEGER DES VIGNES (58) 30.06.2015 GEIPAN... The photographs did not strengthen an extraordinary interpretation. Instead, they gave investigators additional material to compare with sky charts. GEIPAN’s reconstructions for 23:33 and 23:55 showed the planets in the reported arrangement and approaching the horizon. This is an important difference between documentation and proof: an image may confirm that a light was present without showing that it was nearby, solid or behaving anomalously. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LEGER DES VIGNES (58) 30.06.2015 GEIPAN... Planets become convincing UFOs for several connected reasons. Venus can be much brighter than surrounding stars, while Jupiter is also conspicuous. Near the horizon, atmospheric disturbance can make their light flicker, change colour or appear less stable. With few surrounding reference points, a stationary light may also seem to drift. GEIPAN describes this illusion as autokinesis: small involuntary eye movements can make a fixed point appear to move against a dark, featureless background. [Geipan] geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. The Saint Léger des Vignes report was therefore not solved merely because “there were planets in the sky”. The explanation matched the number of lights, their relative positions, their slow apparent movement, their colour, the observation period and their disappearance near the horizon. That cumulative agreement is why the case received a secure rather than merely probable identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT LEGER DES VIGNES (58) 30.06.2015 GEIPAN... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons that seemed to change speed and direction In daylight, ordinary airborne objects create a different problem. At Narcy on 23 August 2016, a witness and a roofer saw a very bright object moving through a clear morning sky. It appeared to approach them, changed direction, made no sound and eventually disappeared towards the horizon. The change of course was the feature that made the report seem most unusual. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNARCY (58) 23.08.2016 GEIPAN... Yet the witness also described movement resembling a balloon carried irregularly by the wind. Weather records showed changing winds around the time of the observation, making a wind borne object a plausible explanation. GEIPAN concluded that it was probably a round object, particularly a child’s balloon, and classified the report B: likely explained, but with less certainty than a category A case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNARCY (58) 23.08.2016 GEIPAN... The case demonstrates how distance uncertainty changes the apparent behaviour of an object. The observer did not know whether the bright object was a small balloon hundreds of metres away or something much larger and farther off. Without that information, perceived speed could not be calculated reliably. A lightweight object moving across changing air currents could also turn, rise or slow without following the steady course expected from an aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNARCY (58) 23.08.2016 GEIPAN... A reflective balloon may look particularly strange because sunlight reaches it unevenly as it rotates. It can brighten suddenly, fade, appear silvery or white and briefly lose any recognisable outline. The lack of engine noise then seems significant, even though silence is exactly what a drifting balloon would produce. Narcy remained only a probable identification because the object was not recovered or photographed clearly, but the observed motion, weather and witness description all favoured an ordinary airborne object. Sky lanterns over Guérigny and Cosne Cours sur Loire GEIPAN attributes two other modern Nièvre reports to sky lanterns: Guérigny on 20 July 2013 and Cosne Cours sur Loire on 5 July 2014. Both are category B cases, meaning that the explanation was considered probable rather than demonstrated beyond reasonable doubt. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Sky lanterns are small paper balloons lifted by heated air from a flame suspended beneath them. At night, the"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases-37c032/","description":"Nievre's strangest reports remain unresolved mainly because key details, measurements and independent records are missing.","breadcrumb":"What Nievre's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases Why Nievre's Strangest Sightings Remain Unresolved Nievre UFOs Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases Why Nievre's Strangest Sightings Remain Unresolved Nievre's strangest reports remain unresolved mainly because key details, measurements and independent records are missing. Nievre's strangest reports remain unresolved mainly because key details, measurements and independent records are missing. dataset evidence The 1976, 1979 and 2011 case records What category C really means How missing details weaken extraordinary claims nievre unresolved cases Introduction Nièvre’s strangest official sighting reports remain unresolved for a less dramatic reason than the word “unidentified” suggests: the surviving evidence is not strong enough to decide what happened. The key files from 1976, 1979 and 2011 contain vivid descriptions of coloured lights, large apparent forms and abrupt disappearances, but they lack dependable measurements, timely radar checks, photographs or fully consistent testimony. GEIPAN therefore classifies them as category C—unidentifiable because reliable information is missing—not category D, meaning unexplained after a sufficiently substantial investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr missions methodes et resultat GEIPANMISSIONS, METHODES ET RESULTATS25 Feb 2021 — Sinon l'observation est déclarée non exploitable (Cas C) par manque de données fiables... This distinction is central to Nièvre’s UFO history. A category C report can sound extraordinary while remaining evidentially weak. It may conceal an unusual event, but it may equally preserve a distorted view of an aircraft, meteor, astronomical object, balloon or projected light. The official record does not establish which interpretation is correct. It establishes that the available material cannot support a confident conclusion. The 1976, 1979 and 2011 case records Châteauneuf Val de Bargis, July 1976 At about 3 am on 19 July 1976, a 50 year old witness at Châteauneuf Val de Bargis reported a single green phenomenon in a clear night sky. The GEIPAN database describes it as roughly ten times the apparent size of a star, initially stationary and then moving rapidly, without any reported sound. A gendarmerie statement survives, but the case is classified C for lack of reliable information. [Geipan] geipan.fr CHATEAUNEUF VAL DE BARGIS (58) 19.07.1976 GEIPAN... The description is memorable, but most of the details needed for reconstruction are absent or too uncertain. “Ten times a star” is not an angular measurement: stars have no visible disc to the unaided eye, so such comparisons usually express brightness, visual impact or an indistinct halo rather than physical size. Nor does the public summary provide a firmly measured elevation, an accurately plotted direction throughout the event or a duration detailed enough to test possible astronomical and aviation explanations. The reported colour also carries less diagnostic value than it first appears. Green can be produced or perceived in several circumstances, including navigation lighting, meteors, fireworks, optical distortion and bright point sources viewed through haze or imperfect eyesight. The sudden rapid departure could represent genuine movement, but it could also reflect the observer losing sight of a light, a changing line of sight or a brief object reaching the end of its visible path. With one witness, no photograph and no independent instrument record, those possibilities cannot now be separated. The most defensible conclusion is therefore deliberately narrow. A witness reported a striking green light, but the archive does not preserve enough geometry or corroboration to establish its source. The case remains open in an administrative sense, not strong in an evidential one. Decize, December 1979 The Decize file is more detailed and involved several witnesses. At around 5.40 pm on 5 December 1979, witnesses described a ball with orange reflections, approximately comparable in apparent size to an orange or football. It reportedly moved vertically, sometimes stopped, then departed towards the north east at high speed. The event was said to last about fifteen minutes. A similar phenomenon was reportedly seen again the following evening at approximately the same place and time, although the descriptions of its apparent size and behaviour varied. [Geipan] geipan.fr DECIZE (58) 05.12.1979 T2 GEIPAN... Multiple witnesses and a repeated observation make Decize more interesting than a fleeting single person light report. They do not, however, automatically make it reliable enough for a category D classification. The gendarmerie record contains ordinary language estimates rather than a calibrated reconstruction. Descriptions such as “the size of a football” are especially problematic without knowing whether the comparison means held at arm’s length, seen at a presumed distance or simply experienced as visually prominent. The record also leaves major uncertainties about weather, exact bearings and the relationship between the witnesses’ accounts. It is unclear whether each observer independently described the phenomenon before discussing it with the others. That matters because witnesses watching together can influence one another’s estimates of colour, motion and shape without intending to do so. The apparent stops and changes in brightness are intriguing but not uniquely extraordinary. A distant light moving towards or away from an observer may appear nearly stationary. Thin cloud, haze or intervening trees can change its brightness. A low astronomical object may seem to move against foreground features, while an aircraft viewed nearly head on can remain in roughly the same part of the sky before turning and apparently accelerating away. Conversely, the reported duration would be long for a normal meteor. No one conventional explanation fits the surviving summary cleanly—but the data are too incomplete to test the alternatives properly. Decize is consequently unresolved because too many plausible branches remain open. It is not a case in which conventional explanations were comprehensively excluded. The D33 road sighting, November 2011 The 9 November 2011 report between Cosne Cours sur Loire and Saint Père is Nièvre’s most extensively investigated category C case. Three people travelling by car reported unusual coloured light effects at about 9.05 or 9.10 pm. One witness first saw blue flashes or a blue lit section of sky. Another described two superimposed, differently coloured discs that seemed to rotate. A later observation was described as a huge, stationary orange wheel that disappeared in a fraction of a second. The principal observations lasted only about five to ten seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete95 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Unlike the older cases, this report prompted an on site investigation. A GEIPAN field investigator met the witnesses, revisited the observation points, recorded interviews, took photographs and compared drawings. The inquiry identified nearby high voltage lines, the A77 motorway, the Belleville nuclear power station and two nightclubs in broadly relevant directions. It also checked the Moon’s position and obtained weather information from the Avord station. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete95 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The investigators regarded a powerful nightclub projection system, or “sky tracker”, as the most plausible conventional lead. One nearby venue possessed such equipment, a local resident said projected lights were often visible in the reported direction, and one witness recalled fog or low cloud that could have made a beam unusually conspicuous. However, the club manager said the system was normally used on Friday and Saturday nights, later in the evening, whereas the sighting occurred on a Wednesday at about 9.10 pm. No operating log existed that could confirm or rule out a brief test or accidental activation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete95 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The testimony also contained important contradictions. The main witness said the sky was clear and insisted that a solid mass was visible between the orange lights. Another witness recalled grey, cloudy or foggy conditions in the direction of the phenomenon. The nearest cited weather station, some 45 kilometres away, reported heavy cloud, but that could not prove the exact conditions along the road. The witnesses also did not all see the same form at the same moment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete95 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... A radar check might have helped test an aircraft hypothesis, but GEIPAN recorded that relevant radar traces were not requested in time. Nor could the witnesses’ drawings establish real size or distance. A light that seems enormous may be small and nearby, or large"},{"title":"Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification","url":"/nords-ufo-history-between-mystery-and/","description":"Nord’s UFO history is dominated by one extraordinary night: 10 September 1954, when Marius Dewilde said that he encountered two small figures and a...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification Nord’s UFO history is dominated by one extraordinary night: 10 September 1954, when Marius Dewilde said that he encountered two small figures and a... Nord’s UFO history is dominated by one extraordinary night: 10 September 1954, when Marius Dewilde said that he encountered two small figures and a mysterious craft beside a railway line at Quarouble, near Valenciennes. nord Introduction Beyond Quarouble, Nord’s record is less sensational but more instructive. Local reports range from apparently unusual lights and triangular formations to straightforward misidentifications of balloons, reflections and other ordinary objects. Modern investigations by GEIPAN, the French state body that examines unidentified aerospace phenomena, show that “unidentified” usually describes the limits of the available evidence rather than proof of an exotic craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le 7 septembre 2024 en début de soirée, depuis leur domicile de Sainghin en Weppes (Nord, 59), deux témoins (T1 et T2, père et fils) obse... Quarouble and the making of a French UFO classic Marius Dewilde lived in a modest railway property near a level crossing at Quarouble. According to the earliest core of his account, his dog became agitated at about 10.30 pm on 10 September 1954. Dewilde went outside, noticed a dark mass near the tracks and then saw two small figures. As he approached, he said, a powerful light dazzled or immobilised him. The figures entered the object, which departed. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, Nord A new flying saucer allegedly descended from the sky and landed, during the... The setting gave the story unusual force. It did not concern a distant point of light but an alleged object on or beside a railway, occupants at close range and a direct physical effect on the witness. These features made Quarouble easy to dramatise in newspapers and later books: a working man, an isolated house, a barking dog, strange visitors and an apparent machine leaving the scene. The case also appeared just as France was entering the intense reporting period now called the 1954 UFO wave. Stories of discs, cigars, luminous objects and supposed occupants circulated rapidly through regional and national newspapers. Quarouble therefore became more than one man’s report. It helped provide a narrative model through which later observations were described and understood. Historians have treated the case as part of the wider cultural impact of post war technology, science fiction, Cold War anxiety and changing ideas about outer space. [Springer] link.springer.com UFO Encounters and the Problem of Empire in Postwar Franceby J Miller · 2012 · Cited by 4 — On 10 September 1954, a young metalwo... Published: September 1954 That wider setting matters because UFO waves are not simply collections of independent observations. Each published story can influence what later witnesses notice, how they interpret an ambiguous sight and which details journalists consider newsworthy. This does not establish that witnesses were dishonest. It does mean that the order of reporting, press coverage and later retelling is essential when assessing the strength of any particular case. How strong is the Quarouble evidence? The strongest point in Quarouble’s favour is the apparent immediacy of Dewilde’s report. He consistently maintained that something alarming had happened outside his home, and investigators and journalists visited the site. His account contained a recognisable sequence rather than a vague memory of a light in the sky. The railway location also appeared to offer the possibility of physical corroboration. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, Nord A new flying saucer allegedly descended from the sky and landed, during the... However, the case has serious limitations. There was no independent close range witness. Dewilde’s wife and child were inside the house, and the decisive elements—the beings, the beam and the departure—rested on his testimony. A single sincere witness can accurately report a frightening experience while still misjudging distance, shape, duration or cause. The physical evidence was inconclusive. Accounts refer to marks or impressions near the railway, but the location was already an industrial transport environment where damage, indentations and disturbed ground were not inherently remarkable. No recovered object, instrument record or securely documented material trace demonstrated that an unknown craft had been present. Later summaries sometimes treat the marks more confidently than the surviving evidence permits. [UFO ARCHIVES] ufo archives.com quarouble 1954 ufo archivesOn September 10, 1954, Marius Dewilde reported two small beings and a craft beside the railway at Quarouble. The case became... Published: September 10, 1954 The narrative developed over time. The earliest story should be separated from later claims, embellishments and contact experiences associated with Dewilde. UFO cases often acquire detail through repeated interviews, memory reconstruction and popular retelling. A dramatic element appearing years later cannot automatically be used to strengthen the original 1954 report. The careful modern approach is therefore to work from the earliest available press accounts and distinguish them from later versions. [UFO ARCHIVES] ufo archives.com quarouble 1954 ufo archivesOn September 10, 1954, Marius Dewilde reported two small beings and a craft beside the railway at Quarouble. The case became... Published: September 10, 1954 No explanation fits every reported feature. Suggestions have included trespassers, smugglers, railway activity, an unusual aircraft, misperception and invention. None has been established beyond reasonable doubt. That leaves Quarouble unresolved in the ordinary historical sense, but “unresolved” is not the same as “confirmed extraterrestrial encounter”. The case lacks the independent witnesses, reliable measurements and well preserved physical evidence that would be needed to support such a conclusion. Its lasting importance is therefore cultural and historical as much as evidential. Quarouble shows how a short, vivid witness report can become a national reference point even when the underlying record remains uncertain. Quarouble 1954 : L'histoire de Marius Dewilde Bande Annonce (2025) Channel: Quarouble 1954 Open on YouTube The 1954 wave across Nord Quarouble was not Nord’s only contribution to the autumn 1954 wave . Reports appeared around Roubaix and other towns during the weeks when newspapers across France were publishing large numbers of saucer stories. Some witnesses described reddish discs, luminous shapes or objects apparently hovering near the horizon. Later investigation has weakened several of these reports. Nord based sceptical researcher Dominique Caudron examined a large collection of regional newspaper editions and argued that many sightings reported on 3 October 1954 were observations of a reddish Moon seen through broken cloud. Under such conditions, the Moon may appear flattened, enlarged, divided into sections or alternately visible and hidden, creating the impression of an object moving or changing shape. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Dominique Caudron Dominique Caudron This explanation is significant because it addresses a cluster rather than one isolated report. When numerous witnesses in neighbouring towns describe a reddish or orange object in approximately the same part of the sky, an astronomical object partly obscured by cloud may explain the geographical pattern better than multiple independent craft. It also shows why contemporary newspaper descriptions cannot always be counted as separate corroborating events: several articles may describe the same visible stimulus. Not every 1954 report in Nord can be reconstructed with equal confidence. Precise times, viewing directions and weather conditions are frequently missing. Many reports survive through clippings or later catalogues rather than complete police files. GEIPAN has acknowledged that numerous historical cases from 1954 are difficult to classify because the surviving information is too weak or imprecise. Its historical publication programme distinguishes genuinely strange, well supported cases from those that remain unclassifiable simply because dependable information is missing. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The most defensible reading is therefore that Nord experienced both a real reporting wave and a mixture of causes. Some people probably saw the Moon, stars, aircraft or atmospheric effects under unfamiliar conditions. Others may have interpreted"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-381e4a/","description":"Nord's autumn 1954 sightings reveal how the Moon, cloud, newspapers and incomplete records could turn shared stimuli into a regional saucer wave.","breadcrumb":"Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave What Drove Nord's Great 1954 UFO Wave? Nord UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave What Drove Nord's Great 1954 UFO Wave? Nord's autumn 1954 sightings reveal how the Moon, cloud, newspapers and incomplete records could turn shared stimuli into a regional saucer wave. Nord's autumn 1954 sightings reveal how the Moon, cloud, newspapers and incomplete records could turn shared stimuli into a regional saucer wave. event time window Where and when the regional reports appeared The reddish Moon and other ordinary explanations Why press coverage and missing data still matter nord 1954 wave Introduction Nord’s great UFO wave of autumn 1954 was probably not one single mysterious phenomenon. It was a mixture of very different reports: the famous Quarouble encounter , brief observations of reddish discs or crescents, distant lights, and clusters of apparently similar objects reported from towns across the department. The most revealing episode came on 3 October, when witnesses around Lille, Roubaix, Dunkirk and the Sambre valley described a red, hovering or slowly descending shape. Later reconstruction indicates that many of them were looking at the same ordinary object: a low waxing crescent Moon, reddened and repeatedly distorted or hidden by cloud. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 3, Lille, NordSunday, at nightfall, the moon shone on a clear day in the... That explanation does not settle every Nord report from 1954. It does, however, show how shared atmospheric conditions, heightened public attention and fast moving newspaper coverage could transform several ambiguous observations into the appearance of a regional saucer invasion. The wave matters less as evidence of a coordinated aerial visitation than as a case study in how genuine experiences can be misinterpreted, copied into the language of the time and preserved in incomplete records. Where and when the reports appeared The local wave began in a dramatic form on 10 September 1954 at Quarouble, near Valenciennes. Marius Dewilde said that he encountered two small figures and an unidentified craft beside the railway close to his home. Newspapers quickly carried the story, giving Nord readers a vivid local example of the “flying saucer” reports already circulating internationally. Whatever happened at Quarouble, its publicity helped establish an interpretive frame: unusual lights or shapes seen afterwards were no longer merely puzzling sights but possible spacecraft. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, Nordufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, Nord... During the following weeks, reports appeared from across the industrial and coastal geography of Nord. Accounts associated with 3 October included Lille, Roubaix, Dunkirk, Annoeullin, Aulnoye Aymeries and nearby communities in the wider Nord–Pas de Calais region. Descriptions varied, but several recurring features stand out: a reddish or orange object low in the sky; a crescent, disc, globe or flattened shape; apparent hovering or very slow movement; partial disappearance followed by reappearance; a dark line or division across the luminous object; an apparent descent towards the horizon. These similarities initially made the reports seem mutually reinforcing. A single witness might mistake a distant light, but dozens or hundreds of people in different towns appeared to be describing much the same thing. Dominique Caudron, a Nord based sceptical investigator, later assembled a much broader chronology than the better known UFO catalogues. His reconstruction listed 46 reports from northern France on 3 October, many concentrated within roughly an hour and a half. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Yet the cluster was not as uniform as the word “wave” suggests. Some reports occurred at different times, faced different directions or contained details absent from the others. Newspaper summaries sometimes merged observations from Nord and Pas de Calais, while later catalogues reduced complicated articles to a place, a time and a few sensational words. A “reddish crescent partly crossed by cloud” could become a “disc with a central band”; an object sinking naturally towards the horizon could be described as landing; and a report of one light could acquire a formation of accompanying saucers through repetition. The most defensible historical picture is therefore not of identical craft passing methodically over Nord. It is of a reporting surge containing a large, tightly grouped family of lunar misidentifications alongside a smaller number of observations that require separate assessment. The reddish Moon behind the largest cluster The Moon explanation is unusually strong for the evening of 3 October because it matches the date, direction, appearance and behaviour reported in several Nord towns. Astronomical records show that the Moon was a waxing crescent, about one third illuminated, on 3 October 1954. It was approaching first quarter and would have appeared low in the south western or western evening sky before setting. A crescent at low altitude can look much more orange or red than it does higher in the sky because its light travels through a longer path in the atmosphere. [theskylive.com] theskylive.com moon calendar moon calendar Cloud supplied the stranger details. Contemporary discussion preserved from Lille described the Moon shining clearly at first, then entering a hazy zone above the horizon. It reappeared reddish and deformed, was crossed by a stratus cloud, and finally faded into thicker cloud. That sequence closely resembles the reported behaviour of a luminous object that changed shape, carried a dark bar, vanished and reappeared before descending. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org ufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, October 3, Lille, NordSunday, at nightfall, the moon shone on a clear day in the... The same pattern appears in individual case reconstructions: Roubaix: the reported object had the Moon’s appearance and lay in the same direction. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Around 2017, \"skeptical\" ufologist Dominique Caudron, based in the Nord, cataloged sightings in the Nord region for October 3, 1954, negl... Published: October 3, 1954 Dunkirk: the phenomenon was reportedly first taken to be the Moon and behaved like it; later analysis again identified a low crescent altered by cloud. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Moon?\", the regional newspaper Le Nouveau Nord Maritime for October 7, 1954, recalled that there had been many observations in the Nord d... Published: October 7, 1954 Aulnoye Aymeries: a luminous crescent with a white glow was reported in the direction occupied by the Moon and displayed no movement that required an independent aircraft. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Annoeullin: later researchers specifically grouped the observation with the Moon misidentifications of that evening, although it continued to appear without explanation in some catalogues. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This is more persuasive than simply noting that “the Moon was visible”. A useful identification must reproduce several independent characteristics at once. Here, the proposed source was present at the relevant time, in the reported sector of sky, with the right reddish crescent shape. Its apparent downward motion followed normal setting, while moving cloud could create changes of outline, brightness and visibility. The witnesses did not need to be foolish or dishonest. A familiar object can become genuinely difficult to recognise when it is seen near the horizon through broken cloud, especially without visible landscape detail to provide scale. The Moon may appear to hover close to the observer, to follow a moving vehicle or to travel when cloud layers pass in front of it. Once a witness starts interpreting it as a nearby object, its angular size can be mentally converted into a large but distant machine. OVNI / UFO : \"Le dossier des soucoupes volantes\" LOB & GIGI Channel: BULOT BLEU Productions Open on YouTube Not every sighting had the same cause The Moon reconstruction should not be stretched into a universal answer for Nord’s entire 1954 wave. Reports made before moonrise, after moonset, in another direction or involving rapid movement need different explanations. Caudron’s own chronology distinguished observations that did not fit the setting Moon from the large group that did. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Around 2017, \"skeptical\" ufologist Dominique Caudron, based in the Nord, cataloged sightings in the Nord region for October 3, 1954, negl... Published: October 3, 1954 Other ordinary sources available in an industrial, densely populated border department included aircraft, meteors, balloons, searchlights, bright stars and planets, reflections"},{"title":"Quarouble","url":"/quarouble/","description":"The famous Quarouble encounter remains compelling as a story but weak as proof once its single witness, uncertain traces and later additions are separated.","breadcrumb":"Nord's UFO History Between Mystery and Misidentification","level":2,"text":"Quarouble How Strong Is the Quarouble Encounter Evidence? Nord UFOs Quarouble Quarouble How Strong Is the Quarouble Encounter Evidence? The famous Quarouble encounter remains compelling as a story but weak as proof once its single witness, uncertain traces and later additions are separated. The famous Quarouble encounter remains compelling as a story but weak as proof once its single witness, uncertain traces and later additions are separated. event time window What Marius Dewilde originally reported Witness testimony, physical traces and missing corroboration How later retellings changed the case nord quarouble Introduction The Quarouble encounter of 10 September 1954 is one of the best known stories in Nord’s UFO history, but its reputation rests more on narrative power than on strong, recoverable evidence. Marius Dewilde said that he found a dark object beside the railway near his home, saw two small figures and was immobilised by a brilliant beam before the object departed. Police and journalists visited the site, marks were reported on a wooden sleeper, and several people nearby described a moving light in the sky. Those points make Quarouble more substantial than an unsupported story remembered decades later. They do not, however, independently establish the presence of a landed craft or its occupants. Dewilde alone reported the close encounter; the alleged traces were never tied conclusively to the event; and nearby sky observations may have involved a meteor. Most importantly, the story became much more elaborate in later versions. The earliest account remains intriguing, but later additions generally weaken rather than strengthen its value as evidence. What Marius Dewilde originally reported Dewilde was a 34 year old metalworker living in a former railway crossing house near Quarouble, east of Valenciennes. At about 10.30 pm on Friday 10 September, according to the account published by newspapers within days, his dog began barking or howling. He went outside with a torch, initially mistook a dark mass near the line for a cart and then heard movement close to his garden fence. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, NordQuarouble at P. N. 79 on the railway track operated by the National Coal Mines... When Dewilde directed his torch towards the noise, he said that he saw two beings no more than about a metre tall. They appeared broad shouldered and wore dark, close fitting clothing resembling diving suits. Light from his torch reportedly reflected from their head coverings as though from glass. Contemporary reports differ over whether the figures passed close to him or whether he moved to intercept them, but they agree that he had only a brief view and could not describe basic features clearly. One early newspaper account noted that he was not even certain whether they had visible arms. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 10, Quarouble, NordQuarouble at P. N. 79 on the railway track operated by the National Coal Mines... He then reported that a bright light, often described in the press as green, came from the dark object and left him unable to move. Once the light went out, the figures had disappeared and the object rose rapidly. Early descriptions gave it an oblong, bell like or flattened shape, roughly five or six metres across and about three metres high. Its departure direction also varied between reports, with west, south west and the direction of Valenciennes all appearing in the developing record. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, l'affaire Marius Dewilde, Quarouble, France, 1954... This early core contains the case’s most important claims: an unexplained dark object close to the railway; two small, suited figures seen briefly in poor light; a dazzling light and an apparent inability to move; a rapidly departing luminous object. It is a memorable sequence, but nearly all of it depends on Dewilde’s perception and recollection. His wife and young son were inside and did not witness the decisive events. The nearby residents later associated with the case saw only lights in the sky, not the object on the tracks, the figures or the beam. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Marius Dewilde Marius Dewilde There is no strong reason to assume automatically that Dewilde invented the entire episode. He reported it promptly, subjected himself to police and press questioning and suffered considerable unwanted attention. Contemporary commentary even observed that a deliberate joke appeared to have brought him inconvenience rather than obvious benefit. Sincerity, however, is not the same as observational accuracy. Darkness, surprise, a barking dog, an unfamiliar object and a very short viewing time could produce an honest but mistaken reconstruction of events. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 10 septembre 54, Quarouble, Nord... Do the railway marks count as physical evidence? The marks on the railway sleeper are frequently presented as the strongest evidence that a heavy machine landed at Quarouble. Contemporary reporting confirms that investigators noticed something, but it also shows how quickly a modest observation became a dramatic claim. A report published on 16 September said that three air police inspectors examined the site. They found no footprints or other traces of the two alleged figures. They did, however, identify five damaged areas, each covering roughly four square centimetres, on one wooden crossbeam or sleeper. The marks were said to be arranged symmetrically, with measured spaces between them. One inspector reportedly remarked that a machine resting on supports might leave marks of this kind. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Dewilde affair, 1954... That description deserves attention because it was printed only six days after the encounter and includes measurements rather than merely stating that “landing traces” existed. Even so, several problems prevent it from serving as proof of Dewilde’s craft. First, no reliable public record demonstrates when the marks were created. A railway sleeper in an industrial transport setting could have been damaged by maintenance equipment, handling, fastening systems, discarded machinery or earlier activity. Symmetry can make a mark appear designed, but it does not determine its cause. Second, the marks were not documented before the site became associated with a flying saucer. There was therefore no uncontaminated “before and after” comparison. The sleeper was examined because Dewilde had already indicated the area, creating a risk that ordinary damage would be interpreted through the expectations generated by his story. Third, widely repeated claims about a vehicle weighing approximately 30 tonnes appear in later press and UFO literature, attributed to unnamed railway engineers. The accessible accounts do not provide the engineers’ calculations, the size and type of the supposed landing supports, the condition of the wood or a technical report that could be checked independently. A weight cannot be inferred confidently from shallow surface damage without those variables. [ovni007.tripod.com] ovni007.tripod.com Car si le public demeure sceptique vis à vis de tout ce qui se rapporte aux \"Soucoupes volantes\", il n'en va pas de même de la police de... Fourth, the evidential trail is incomplete. Reports say that stones or samples were collected and that the police, air authorities and other services took an interest, but no conclusive laboratory result has entered the publicly accessible case record. Claims that ballast was scorched, magnetised or otherwise transformed are much less securely established than the simple observation that one sleeper bore marks. Without preserved samples, documented handling and published analyses, later descriptions cannot be verified. The most defensible conclusion is therefore narrow: investigators saw unusual looking damage on a railway sleeper near the position indicated by Dewilde. It was not demonstrated that the damage was fresh, that it had been caused that night or that it resulted from a landed aerial vehicle. The marks prevent the case from being entirely testimony only, but they do not provide independent confirmation of its extraordinary interpretation. Did other witnesses corroborate the encounter? Several residents around Onnaing, Vicq and Valenciennes reportedly saw a red or changing light at approximately the time of Dewilde’s experience. A 16 September account mentioned Edmond Auverlot and another man at Onnaing, along with three young people at Vicq. Other versions changed names, witness groupings and timing, but retained the general claim that a red light crossed the local sky. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Dewilde affair, 1954... These observations may support the limited proposition that something"},{"title":"Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports","url":"/why-oise-keeps-producing-convincing-ufo/","description":"Oise has a genuine but uneven UFO history. The department appears repeatedly in France’s official archive of unidentified aerospace phenomena, with reports...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports Oise has a genuine but uneven UFO history. The department appears repeatedly in France’s official archive of unidentified aerospace phenomena, with reports... Oise has a genuine but uneven UFO history. The department appears repeatedly in France’s official archive of unidentified aerospace phenomena, with reports ranging from the nationwide sighting wave of 1954 to modern encounters involving apparent triangular craft, unusual lights and objects seen near roads or aviation facilities. oise Introduction The strongest unexplained case is the Compiègne incident of 1 January 1980, when several military personnel independently watched an orange luminous object appear three times above their base. The most instructive explained case occurred near Silly le Long in 2015: witnesses described a large, stationary V shaped object, but a detailed reconstruction linked the experience to a light aircraft and a powerful illusion of relative motion. Together, the two cases show why Oise matters. Its skies combine military sites, busy civil air routes, rural viewing conditions and enough official documentation to distinguish enduring puzzles from persuasive misidentifications. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980Picardie Département Oise Classification D Date de mise a jour 22/03/2007. Le 1er janvier 1980. Une lueur intense... What the official record actually contains France’s official body for reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects witness accounts, examines environmental and aviation information, consults bodies such as the gendarmerie, civil aviation authorities, the Air and Space Force and the national weather service, and publishes investigated cases where sufficient material exists. Its purpose is to investigate unusual observations, not to prove extraterrestrial visitation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr or advanced (or futuristic) technologies... GEIPAN classifies reports according to both the reliability of the available information and the degree of unexplained strangeness left after investigation. In broad terms: A means the phenomenon has been identified. B indicates that an explanation is considered highly probable. C means there is too little reliable information to reach a conclusion. D denotes a phenomenon that remains unidentified after investigation, with the modern system dividing this category more precisely into D1 and D2. An unexplained classification does not establish that an extraordinary craft was present. It means that investigators could not satisfactorily identify the reported phenomenon from the evidence available. Conversely, a category C report may sound spectacular while possessing less evidential value than a carefully investigated category A case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... A local news review published in April 2026 reported 51 Oise observations in the GEIPAN database. That figure is useful as a measure of recorded activity, but it should not be treated as 51 unsolved incidents. The department’s files include confirmed or probable aircraft, astronomical bodies, lanterns and artificial lights, as well as incomplete reports and a small number of unresolved cases. [Evasion FM] evasionfm.com Dans l'Oise, cette antenne du centre national d'études spatiales (CNES) a recensé 51 observations. La...Read more... The Compiègne military base case of 1980 The most prominent unresolved Oise case took place shortly before midnight on 1 January 1980 at Margny lès Compiègne. Several military personnel on duty reported seeing a small orange luminous sphere appear on three separate occasions, at approximately 23:16, 23:33 and 23:46. Each time, it emerged in roughly the same area, moved slowly along a horizontal path and was accompanied by an intense glow. The witnesses reported no engine noise, smoke or other obvious sign of an aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980Picardie Département Oise Classification D Date de mise a jour 22/03/2007. Le 1er janvier 1980. Une lueur intense... The account contains details that give it more weight than a fleeting single witness light in the sky. There were several observers in a controlled military setting, the phenomenon reportedly repeated its appearance, and the witnesses were familiar enough with aviation to reject an ordinary aircraft or helicopter. The GEIPAN file also records that dogs at the base howled during each appearance, although this behavioural detail is not independent physical evidence and cannot establish what caused the event. No civilian witnesses were identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980Picardie Département Oise Classification D Date de mise a jour 22/03/2007. Le 1er janvier 1980. Une lueur intense... GEIPAN classified the incident as D under the system then in use, describing it as a strange to very strange phenomenon supported by evidence of medium to high consistency. The case therefore remains officially unexplained. It is important, however, not to overstate what the file proves. The published summary identifies no radar confirmation, photograph, recovered material or instrument reading. The description also lacks enough precise positional and meteorological information to reconstruct the object’s distance, size or actual speed. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Several conventional possibilities remain conceivable, including a distant pyrotechnic object, a luminous balloon, an astronomical source distorted by atmospheric conditions or an aircraft viewed under unusual geometry. None has been demonstrated from the surviving record. The correct assessment is therefore narrower than either “alien craft” or “debunked”: multiple military witnesses reported a recurring luminous phenomenon, and the available investigation did not establish its cause. The 1954 wave reaches Oise Oise also participated in the celebrated French “flying saucer” wave of 1954, when newspapers across the country carried a rapid succession of reports about discs, cigar shaped objects and mysterious lights. The period is significant because it generated a large body of local testimony, but it also demonstrates how press attention can encourage unrelated observations to be grouped into a single dramatic narrative. On 30 August 1954, the regional newspaper L’Oise Matin reportedly described a conspicuous light seen at about 20:00 over Creil. According to the surviving press summary, civilians and several officers at the nearby air base noticed the phenomenon. The newspaper offered little detail and openly wondered whether it was a guided rocket or a “saucer”. There is no surviving official investigation capable of determining what was seen. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Another Oise report was recorded at Lacroix Saint Ouen on 7 October 1954. GEIPAN now lists it as category C because reliable information is insufficient. The surviving file is essentially archival rather than the product of a modern investigation conducted while evidence and witnesses were readily available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases belong within the broader national autumn wave, which peaked in press coverage between September and November. Contemporary reporting often placed credible observations, misunderstandings and rumours alongside one another. L’Oise Matin itself published a wide ranging “firework display of saucers” article in mid October, illustrating how the subject had become a popular newspaper spectacle rather than merely a series of isolated witness reports. [L'Observateur.fr] lobservateur.fr nord en 1954 etranges phenomenes observes nord en 1954 etranges phenomenes observes The historical importance of 1954 in Oise therefore lies less in one proven landmark encounter than in the interaction between witnesses, military associations and local journalism. Reports near Creil acquired additional authority because officers were said to have seen the light, but the absence of detailed records prevents a reliable modern judgement. The wave remains culturally important and evidentially fragile. Why aviation produces convincing Oise sightings Oise is especially prone to aviation related misidentifications. The department contains the military complex at Creil, Paris–Beauvais Airport and smaller aerodromes, while its southern and eastern skies are influenced by traffic associated with the wider Paris region. Creil’s military history includes operational aircraft and air surveillance functions, making aviation a natural part of both the physical landscape and local UFO"},{"title":"Aircraft Illusions","url":"/aircraft-illusions/","description":"The Silly-le-Long case shows how aircraft lights, road travel and relative motion can create a convincing giant V-shaped object.","breadcrumb":"Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports","level":2,"text":"Aircraft Illusions How Aircraft Lights Become Giant Triangular UFOs Oise UFOs Aircraft Illusions Aircraft Illusions How Aircraft Lights Become Giant Triangular UFOs The Silly le Long case shows how aircraft lights, road travel and relative motion can create a convincing giant V shaped object. The Silly le Long case shows how aircraft lights, road travel and relative motion can create a convincing giant V shaped object. mechanism The Silly le Long encounter How relative motion creates false stillness Why Oise is especially prone to aviation misidentifications oise aircraft illusions Introduction Oise’s most revealing triangular UFO report is not one that survived investigation, but one that showed how convincingly an ordinary aircraft can become a vast, hovering V shaped object. Near Silly le Long shortly after midnight on 31 May 2015, two people travelling along the N2 saw white and red lights apparently fixed above the road. As they approached, they perceived a broad dark mass, seemingly motionless and suspended at low altitude. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unusual aerial reports, later reconstructed the event using the witnesses’ route, an aircraft’s recorded position and a three dimensional simulation. It concluded that the object was a light aircraft performing night circuits near Le Plessis Belleville airfield. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSILLY LE LONG (60) 31.05.2015 GEIPAN... The case matters because the witnesses were not merely confusing one light with another. Several interacting effects—relative motion, poor depth cues, aircraft lighting, road geometry and uncertain size estimates—combined to create the impression of a giant triangular craft. It offers a practical model for understanding other apparently slow, silent or hovering shapes reported over Oise. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The Silly le Long encounter The witnesses were travelling north east in a van between Le Plessis Belleville and Nanteuil le Haudouin. At first they noticed two bright white lights and a red light, low ahead and slightly to the left. They were accustomed to seeing aircraft associated with the Paris region, but these lights appeared unusually low and, more importantly, did not seem to advance. The driver slowed as the van approached a bridge over the N2. Both witnesses then perceived a wide, dark form associated with the lights; the driver later described it as a very flattened V. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSILLY LE LONG (60) 31.05.2015 GEIPAN... After the van passed beneath the bridge, the passenger lost sight of the phenomenon because of the vehicle’s roof and lack of rear visibility. The driver continued looking sideways, backwards and through a mirror while still driving. He reported a dark, apparently solid structure with two large rectangular white areas at its rear. The experience lasted somewhere between roughly one minute and four minutes, depending on which witness estimate is used. The vehicle’s noise also made the absence of an audible engine difficult to interpret. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several details strengthened the witnesses’ initial confidence. They knew the road, returned to the location later and recorded their accounts quickly. One drawing was made about an hour after the sighting, and the GEIPAN questionnaire was completed within roughly 36 hours. The two witnesses also tried not to compare their descriptions beforehand. These are useful precautions, but they do not remove perceptual error: prompt and sincere testimony can still preserve a mistaken interpretation formed during the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The investigation found a Cirrus SR20 light aircraft conducting repeated touch and go manoeuvres at Le Plessis Belleville. A touch and go is a training exercise in which an aircraft lands briefly and immediately takes off again, producing repeated low altitude circuits around an airfield. The aircraft’s recorded route placed it in approximately the same viewing directions as the reported phenomenon during the critical period. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN did not stop at saying that an aeroplane happened to be nearby. Investigators revisited the road with the witnesses, modelled the van’s movement and compared it with the aircraft’s GPS derived track. They also examined weather, astronomy, road works, nearby industrial activity and the possibility of an exceptional road convoy. The fixed structure and convoy alternatives did not fit the location, lighting or later absence of any object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. How relative motion creates false stillness The central mechanism was what GEIPAN calls the pivot effect . An observer and an aircraft can both be moving while the line of sight between them changes very little for a short period. From the observer’s viewpoint, the aircraft then remains near the same point against the landscape or sky, even though it is travelling normally. A familiar daytime comparison is a distant vehicle on a curving road. It may seem almost stationary when its sideways movement across the viewer’s field of vision is minimal. At night, the effect becomes much stronger because the observer sees bright points without a clearly visible fuselage, distance marker or continuous background. The brain therefore has little direct information with which to distinguish a nearby hovering object from a more distant aircraft moving partly towards or away from the observer. At Silly le Long, the geometry became especially persuasive near the bridge. The aircraft was travelling in the opposite general direction to the van, but its changing flight path and the van’s forward movement caused their connecting sightline to pivot around roughly the same area. The bridge provided a prominent reference point, making the lights seem fixed above or beside it. GEIPAN’s reconstruction found that this moment corresponded closely with the witnesses’ strongest impression that the object was stationary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSILLY LE LONG (60) 31.05.2015 GEIPAN... This is not simply the autokinetic effect, in which an isolated stationary light appears to wander against a featureless background. The Silly le Long aircraft really was moving, and so were the witnesses. Nevertheless, both mechanisms illustrate the same wider problem: night vision is poor at recovering motion when reliable reference points are absent or misleading. Aviation safety guidance warns that isolated lights in darkness can appear to move, remain fixed or occupy false relationships with the horizon. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Spatial D Federal Aviation Administration Spatial D The experience after the bridge was even less suitable for precise judgement. Only the driver continued observing, alternately through a side window and mirror while controlling the vehicle. The apparent object was then behind or beside him rather than directly ahead. Attention was divided among the road, the bridge, the mirror and a phenomenon already interpreted as extraordinary. Under these conditions, small changes in viewing angle can be missed, and brief flashes may be remembered as larger illuminated surfaces. 23:56 The longstanding mystery of BLACK TRIANGLE UFOs Channel: Sandboxx · Views: 749.7K · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 23 minutes Open on YouTube Why lights become a triangular body Aircraft lighting does not normally present observers with a neat diagram of an aeroplane. Its appearance depends on viewing angle, banking, glare, atmospheric clarity and which lights are directed towards the observer. Landing lights can dominate everything else when an aircraft faces roughly towards the viewer. Red and green navigation lights may appear, disappear or merge with brighter sources during turns. Anti collision lights produce flashes that can be assigned to imagined corners or edges. At Silly le Long, the witnesses initially interpreted the lights as aircraft like. The strangeness emerged because their apparent stillness seemed to rule out an aeroplane. Once that conclusion was made, the darkness between the lights was perceived as part of a single massive body. This is a natural act of visual organisation: when lights maintain a stable arrangement, the brain tends to treat them as belonging to one object and fills in the space between them. The triangular or V shaped outline was therefore not necessarily a clear silhouette observed independently of the lights. One witness acknowledged that the front point had not been well seen and that drawings made the contours sharper than they appeared during the encounter. The passenger was less certain of a specific shape, remembering mainly a broad, flat mass and an impressive span."},{"title":"Compiègne Case","url":"/compiègne-case/","description":"Multiple military witnesses saw a recurring orange light above their base, but the surviving record cannot establish its cause.","breadcrumb":"Why Oise Keeps Producing Convincing UFO Reports","level":2,"text":"Compiègne Case Why the Compiègne Sighting Still Resists Explanation Oise UFOs Compiègne Case Compiègne Case Why the Compiègne Sighting Still Resists Explanation Multiple military witnesses saw a recurring orange light above their base, but the surviving record cannot establish its cause. Multiple military witnesses saw a recurring orange light above their base, but the surviving record cannot establish its cause. event time window What the military witnesses reported Why GEIPAN left the case unexplained What the surviving evidence cannot prove oise Introduction The Margny lès Compiègne sighting of 1 January 1980 remains the strongest officially unresolved UFO case in Oise. Several military personnel reported an orange luminous object appearing three times above or near their base between about 23:16 and 23:46. It moved slowly along a broadly horizontal path, produced no reported engine noise or smoke, and repeatedly emerged in the same part of the sky. France’s official investigation archive still classifies the incident as category D: unidentified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... That classification makes the case important, but it does not prove that an extraordinary aircraft was present. Its real significance lies in the tension between relatively strong witness testimony and weak surviving objective evidence. The observers were numerous and aviation connected, yet the public file contains no photograph, radar trace, recovered material or precise reconstruction capable of establishing distance, size or cause. What the military witnesses reported The incident occurred at Margny lès Compiègne, then associated with the 6th Combat Helicopter Regiment. The official summary says that military personnel on duty saw a small orange luminous sphere at approximately 23:16, 23:33 and 23:46. On each appearance it reportedly emerged in the same area, travelled slowly on a horizontal course and was accompanied by an intense glow. No smoke was visible, and the witnesses heard nothing resembling an aircraft or helicopter. Contemporary press reporting later described guards at the regiment watching the movements of an orange ball over the camp. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... The repetition is the case’s most unusual feature. A brief point of light can easily be a meteor, aircraft lamp, firework or astronomical object. Three appearances separated by roughly 17 and 13 minutes are harder to fit to a single fleeting event. They suggest either a recurring source, one phenomenon repeatedly moving into view, or several similar objects following a regular route. The surviving witness entries broadly agree on the central description. Three witnesses characterised the phenomenon as spherical or ball like, while another used an oval description. Orange or fire like colouring appears throughout. One entry records an apparent size comparable to an apple held at an unspecified distance, and two mention a trail or tail. The reported movement ranges from slow to variable, with one witness estimating that the source was between five and ten kilometres away. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARGNY LES COMPIEGNE (60) 01.01.1980 T1 GEIPAN... These details should not be combined too literally into a precise model. Apparent size descriptions are highly sensitive to viewing distance, which was unknown. A light judged to be “apple sized” may represent a small nearby object, a distant intense source or glare around a point light. Likewise, a fiery colour and apparent trail can describe a physical emission, atmospheric distortion, retinal glare or the visual persistence of a moving light. The file also records an unusual reaction from dogs kept at the base: they reportedly howled during each appearance. GEIPAN’s witness metadata preserves possible biological or environmental effects for two observers. This is memorable corroborative testimony, but it is not an instrument reading. The dogs’ behaviour was interpreted by people who were already watching an unfamiliar light, and no record establishes whether they reacted directly to the phenomenon, to an inaudible sound, to activity among the guards or merely by coincidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... Why the testimony carries unusual weight The case is stronger than the typical solitary report of a distant light because several people observed the event in an organised military setting. Four witness records survive in GEIPAN’s database, covering men aged 20 to 30. Their descriptions overlap on the main features: an orange or fiery luminous form, generally ball shaped, seen at night over an artificial or urbanised environment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMARGNY LES COMPIEGNE (60) 01.01.1980 T1 GEIPAN... Their professional setting also matters. Personnel at a helicopter regiment could reasonably be expected to have greater familiarity with aircraft movements, navigation lights and rotor noise than an average observer. The official account specifically states that the witnesses were confident the light was not produced by an aeroplane or helicopter. That judgement deserves attention, particularly because the reported source was silent and appeared repeatedly near an aviation facility. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... Professional experience is not immunity from misidentification, however. Aviation personnel can still misjudge the range and movement of an isolated light, especially at night when there is no visible horizon or foreground reference. A distant aircraft approaching nearly head on may seem stationary before appearing to move sideways; glare can conceal its navigation lights and structure; and sound may be absent or delayed because of distance, wind direction and ambient base noise. GEIPAN itself treats witness credibility as only one part of a case’s “consistency”, alongside the amount of information, independence of witnesses and presence of photographs, measurements or other records. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... Nor are four names automatically equivalent to four fully independent observations. The men were at the same location, could communicate with one another and may have directed each other’s attention towards the object. Shared viewing is valuable because it rules out a purely individual visual disturbance, but discussion among observers can also align later descriptions. The public database does not provide enough detail to reconstruct exactly who saw each appearance first, where every observer stood or how much information they exchanged before giving their accounts. 3:39 The Proof Is Out There: UFO SPOTTED ON U.S. MILITARY BASE (Season 2) History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 3.2M · Uploaded: September 2021 · Length: 3 minutes 39 seconds Open on YouTube Why GEIPAN left the case unexplained GEIPAN, the investigation unit of France’s national space agency CNES, distinguishes cases that lack enough information from cases that remain unidentified despite investigation. Category C means that the available material is insufficient for a conclusion. Category D means that investigators possessed enough consistent information to take the report seriously but found no known explanation that satisfactorily covered its remaining unusual features. The Compiègne file retains the older category D label and is described as having medium to strong consistency and an unusual to very unusual character. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... The features most resistant to a simple explanation are their combination rather than any single detail: the light allegedly returned three times within half an hour; each appearance began in approximately the same area; several military witnesses observed it; its path was described as slow and horizontal; no aircraft noise or smoke was reported; the observers rejected an ordinary aeroplane or helicopter; the base’s dogs reportedly reacted during all three appearances. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCOMPIEGNE (60)1980 GEIPAN... A meteor does not readily explain three slow, similar passages. A fixed planet or bright star does not match repeated appearances followed by horizontal travel, unless clouds, observer movement or mistaken timing substantially altered the perception. A conventional aircraft is plausible in an aviation environment, but the witnesses’ descriptions of silence, fiery orange colouring and recurring appearance would still need to be reconciled. Fireworks or signal flares could provide orange light, but ordinary flares generally descend rather than follow a sustained horizontal route, and the file reports neither smoke nor a launch source. None of those difficulties proves that the source was exotic. It means only that the public record does not contain"},{"title":"Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-orne-ufo-cases-still-resist/","description":"Orne’s UFO history is not defined by a single celebrated encounter or a sustained wave of sightings.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Orne’s UFO history is not defined by a single celebrated encounter or a sustained wave of sightings. Orne’s UFO history is not defined by a single celebrated encounter or a sustained wave of sightings. orne Introduction The strongest Orne case presently available is the June 2020 sighting at Aunay les Bois. Three witnesses described two bluish lights moving silently across the countryside, and an extensive investigation found no satisfactory conventional explanation. The 2013 Ménil Hubert sur Orne case also remains unresolved, although it rests on one witness and no imagery. By contrast, the vivid 1992 Argentan encounter was left unclassified in any meaningful evidential sense because investigators lacked reliable supporting information. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The strongest Orne case: Aunay les Bois in 2020 Between midnight and 1 am on 3 June 2020, three members of a family were in the garden of a rural house at Aunay les Bois when they saw a phenomenon dominated by two bluish lights. They estimated that it remained visible for five to ten seconds, moving horizontally at a constant speed from the south south east towards the east. It made no reported sound and briefly passed behind a row of poplar trees before leaving their field of view. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The case matters because it was not classified as unexplained merely because witnesses could not name what they had seen. GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena study group operated by France’s national space agency CNES, collected separate technical questionnaires, drawings and cognitive interviews. Investigators also visited the location and analysed the geometry of the sighting. GEIPAN considered the information relatively strong, although it noted that some questionnaire answers appeared to have been discussed among the witnesses, reducing their independence to a degree. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... Several ordinary explanations were tested. An atmospheric entry or fragmenting meteor: the blue colour, straight path and elongated appearance offered a superficial match. However, investigators found no corresponding report from astronomical observers or automated meteor camera networks. They considered that an event of the apparent scale described should probably have been recorded elsewhere. Illuminated balloons: available wind data did not fit the reported direction and speed. At altitudes where balloons would follow the prevailing wind, the calculated wind speed was substantially below the apparent movement reported by the witnesses. One or more drones: the estimated speed could fit nearby drones, and military or civilian machines can carry coloured lights. The geometry created a difficulty, however. Because the lights appeared to pass behind trees about 200 metres away, a single object matching the witnesses’ angular estimates would have needed to be more than nine metres wide. A formation of several drones remained possible but was judged unlikely. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... GEIPAN therefore classified Aunay les Bois as category D: unidentified after investigation. That conclusion does not identify the lights as an exotic vehicle. It means only that the available evidence was reasonably substantial and that none of the examined explanations fitted well enough. The absence of photographs, radar data or independent witnesses elsewhere still prevents a confident reconstruction of distance, size and speed. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... This distinction is central to understanding the case. Its value lies not in proving an extraordinary origin but in showing what a comparatively serious unresolved report looks like: multiple witnesses, documented viewing geometry, site inspection, competing hypotheses and a stated account of the remaining limitations. Ménil Hubert sur Orne: unexplained, but dependent on one witness At about 11.45 pm on 29 May 2013, a motorist near Ménil Hubert sur Orne reported a dark, pebble shaped phenomenon several tens of metres above the ground. It carried two red lights, described as being towards the front and rear, and produced a high pitched humming sound. The object moved slowly towards the north west and disappeared behind a hedge after roughly a minute. [Geipan] geipan.fr MENIL HUBERT SUR ORNE (61) 29.05.2013 GEIPAN... GEIPAN regarded the description as moderately strange because the shape and accompanying sound did not closely match a familiar misidentification. Investigators found no specific reason to doubt the witness’s sincerity, and a gendarmerie record formed part of the case documentation. The sighting was eventually retained as an unexplained category D case, having originally appeared in the more specific D1 subcategory used for unexplained reports of moderate strangeness and consistency. [Geipan] geipan.fr MENIL HUBERT SUR ORNE (61) 29.05.2013 GEIPAN... Its weaknesses are equally clear. There was only one known witness, no photograph or video, and the description was comparatively brief. Even the stated date required correction during the investigation: the event occurred on 29 May, not 30 May as written in the questionnaire. GEIPAN explicitly noted that further witnesses could either have strengthened the case or supplied information leading to an ordinary explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr MENIL HUBERT SUR ORNE (61) 29.05.2013 GEIPAN... Ménil Hubert sur Orne is therefore a genuine unresolved report, but not a high confidence demonstration of unusual technology. Its status depends mainly on the absence of a satisfactory match, not on instrument readings or independently corroborated physical evidence. 5:29 Ancient Aliens: 300+ \"Flying Saucer\" Incidents in France (Season 19) History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 203.0K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Argentan in 1992: the most dramatic story, but not the best evidence Orne’s most spectacular published claim occurred near Argentan shortly after 1 am on 3 August 1992. According to the account preserved by GEIPAN, a motorist saw a white, lens shaped patch of mist above a roadside embankment. As the mist faded, he reportedly perceived a dark grey oval object about ten metres from the vehicle and three metres above the ground. Its upper section appeared transparent, revealing what looked like a human shaped pilot wearing a silver suit and helmet. [Geipan] geipan.fr ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992 GEIPAN... The driver woke his wife, who reportedly saw the scene as well. Violet rays then appeared near or above the vehicle, prompting the couple to drive away. The wife was said to have watched a cylindrical object depart at very high speed. No sound was reported. [Geipan] geipan.fr ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992 GEIPAN... Despite its vividness, GEIPAN classifies the report as category C because reliable information is lacking. The public file contains a summary and questionnaire but no investigation establishing the precise road, viewing conditions, astronomical environment, possible vehicles, medical or perceptual factors, physical traces, radar records or independent witnesses. The official conclusion is not that a humanoid piloted craft remained unexplained after eliminating alternatives; it is that there was insufficient dependable material to determine what happened. [Geipan] geipan.fr ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992 GEIPAN... This is an important credibility lesson. Narrative detail is not the same as evidential strength. Argentan is more memorable than Aunay les Bois, but Aunay les Bois is the better investigated case. The Argentan account may reflect a sincere experience, a distorted perception, a dream like episode, an embellished memory or an unknown event. The surviving record cannot distinguish among them. Alençon in 2014: when the photographs weakened the claim A report from Alençon initially appeared more promising because it included photographs. On 27 October 2014, a witness said that a dark triangular object with fixed lights at its corners crossed the sky rapidly and silently at 7.54 pm. He reported a second passage in the opposite direction at 11.43 pm and supplied two photographs that he associated with the sightings. [Geipan] geipan.fr ALENCON (61) 27.10.2014 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s re examination found serious inconsistencies. Contrast enhancement indicated that the supposed moving feature remained fixed relative to a nearby building. The images’ recorded dates and times did not correspond to the two claimed passages, while the photographs were separated by only three minutes rather than almost four hours. The witness did not answer a later request to clarify when the"},{"title":"Argentan 1992","url":"/argentan-1992/","description":"The 1992 Argentan story is Orne's most dramatic encounter, but missing records and weak corroboration sharply limit what can be concluded.","breadcrumb":"Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Argentan 1992 Did Argentan's Spectacular Encounter Have Solid Evidence? Orne UFOs Argentan 1992 Argentan 1992 Did Argentan's Spectacular Encounter Have Solid Evidence? The 1992 Argentan story is Orne's most dramatic encounter, but missing records and weak corroboration sharply limit what can be concluded. The 1992 Argentan story is Orne's most dramatic encounter, but missing records and weak corroboration sharply limit what can be concluded. entity The roadside mist, oval craft and reported pilot The second witness and the claimed high speed departure Why dramatic detail could not overcome missing evidence orne argentan 1992 Introduction The Argentan encounter of 3 August 1992 is Orne’s most spectacular recorded UFO story: a holidaying driver said that a misty oval object appeared beside his moving car, with a human like figure visible inside, before violet rays crossed above the vehicle and a larger green, cigar shaped object departed at extreme speed. His wife reportedly confirmed parts of the sighting. Yet the official conclusion was not “unexplained”. GEIPAN, the French space agency’s unit for unidentified aerospace phenomena, placed the case in category C because it lacked reliable information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992Geipan. Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les... ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992. Observation date. 03/08... That distinction matters. Argentan is memorable because of its detail, not because its evidence is strong. The surviving file is essentially one principal witness’s completed questionnaire, with no separate statement from his wife, no identified independent road witness, no photograph, no radar record, no physical trace and no documented technical investigation of the vehicle. The result is a vivid claim that cannot now be reconstructed or tested with confidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992Geipan. Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les... ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992. Observation date. 03/08... The roadside mist, oval craft and reported pilot The principal witness said that he was driving on a departmental road at about 1 am, during the night of 2–3 August 1992. He described a long, straight road through open fields and estimated his speed at roughly 100 kilometres per hour. The sky was said to be clear and cloudless, with no wind, and the area was away from houses. His wife and three children were in the car, although the children were asleep. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. According to his account, he first noticed a lenticular halo of mist above the roadside vegetation on his left, about ten metres from the car and around three metres above the ground. As the mist faded, he said that an oval, dark grey object became visible. He estimated it at approximately three metres across and two metres high. Its upper part appeared transparent. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The most striking element was what he believed he saw inside. He described a human form in a piloting position, its body leaning slightly backwards, with one arm bent at a right angle. The figure appeared to wear a silver suit and a helmet which reminded him of those represented in images of ancient Egyptian royalty. The front of the oval reportedly ended in a rounded projection. No sound was heard. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This level of detail gives the encounter its narrative force, but it does not automatically make the observation precise. The witness was viewing through a car window at night while travelling quickly. Even if the object was genuinely only ten metres away, the car would cover nearly 28 metres each second at 100 kilometres per hour. Unless the phenomenon moved alongside the vehicle, the closest view may therefore have lasted only moments. The file provides no measured duration, surveyed position or exact road alignment from which the claimed distance and size can be independently checked. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The apparent mist is also difficult to interpret. It could have been part of the reported phenomenon, but it could equally have affected the witness’s perception of outline, transparency and depth. The questionnaire records a clear night and no general fog, which makes a small isolated patch seem unusual, but no meteorological data or site examination survives in the public file to establish what was present near the road. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The second witness and the claimed high speed departure The driver wrote that he immediately regarded the sight as abnormal and woke his wife, asking whether she could see the same thing. She reportedly said that she could. He then flashed the car’s headlights. In response, he claimed, violet coloured beams appeared in short bursts above the car, at a height of roughly ten metres, arranged in a triangular pattern. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. He said that fear led him to ask his wife whether he should slow down or accelerate. She told him to accelerate. The oval object on the left then disappeared from his view. He asked her to turn around and observe through the rear window. She reportedly saw, several hundred metres above them, an elongated green object resembling a cylinder or cigar, which then vanished at very high speed. Neither witness reported hearing any sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A second observer would normally strengthen a case, but the surviving record limits that advantage. The wife did not provide a separate published questionnaire or independently timed narrative. Everything attributed to her appears within her husband’s account. She was also awakened after he had already interpreted the sight as an unidentified craft and was asked directly whether she saw the same thing. That does not invalidate her reported observation, but it means the file cannot test how much the two descriptions agreed before discussion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. There is also a notable difference between what each spouse allegedly saw at the end. The husband’s close object was a small grey oval containing a figure; his wife’s departing object was a green cigar or cylinder hundreds of metres overhead. They may have been two views of one phenomenon, two separate phenomena, or impressions produced under very different viewing conditions. The dossier contains no geometry, drawings from both witnesses or sequence analysis capable of choosing between those possibilities. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The account mentions the red rear lights of another vehicle more than a kilometre ahead. That vehicle could have provided valuable independent corroboration, particularly if violet beams or a fast moving luminous object had crossed the road. No statement from its occupants appears in the available record, and there is no indication that they were identified. The children in the witnesses’ car were asleep and therefore added no further testimony. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 64' Débat : Un ancien pilote de chasse raconte le jour où il a croisé un OVNI Channel: TV5MONDE Info Open on YouTube Why dramatic detail could not overcome missing evidence GEIPAN’s category C is sometimes misunderstood as a lesser form of “unexplained”. It is better read as “not reliably assessable”. In its published Argentan entry, GEIPAN identifies the phenomenon as a single silent oval object, reportedly about ten metres away and three metres in diameter, but labels the case as lacking reliable information. Its classification therefore says more about the weakness of the record than about the possible nature of the object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992Geipan. Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les... ARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992. Observation date. 03/08... The principal evidential gaps are substantial: No independent second testimony: the wife’s observations are relayed through the main witness rather than preserved in her own account. No contemporary physical record: there are no photographs, video recordings, radar data or authenticated audio communications. No precise location in the public file: identifying details were redacted, preventing an independent reconstruction of the road, sight lines, vegetation and possible nearby light sources. No known trace investigation: the object was said to remain above roadside plants, but the file records no damage, residue, marks, radiation measurement or site inspection. No corroboration from other road users: the vehicle visible ahead was not linked to a witness statement. No firm timing: estimates of distance, height, size and speed are given, but the public record does not establish the duration of each phase accurately enough for meaningful calculations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANARGENTAN (61) 03.08.1992Geipan."},{"title":"Modern Cases","url":"/modern-cases-9f9525/","description":"The Aunay-les-Bois and Menil-Hubert cases show why some well-investigated sightings remain unexplained without proving exotic craft.","breadcrumb":"Which Orne UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Modern Cases Why Orne's Modern UFO Cases Remain Unresolved Orne UFOs Modern Cases Modern Cases Why Orne's Modern UFO Cases Remain Unresolved The Aunay les Bois and Menil Hubert cases show why some well investigated sightings remain unexplained without proving exotic craft. The Aunay les Bois and Menil Hubert cases show why some well investigated sightings remain unexplained without proving exotic craft. event time window The Aunay les Bois sighting and its three witnesses How investigators tested meteors, balloons and drones What Menil Hubert adds and where both cases remain weak orne modern cases Introduction Orne’s two strongest modern UFO cases remain unresolved for a straightforward reason: investigators found enough detail to reject several familiar explanations, but not enough objective evidence to identify what the witnesses saw. At Aunay les Bois in June 2020, three adults reported two bluish lights crossing the rural night sky in silence. GEIPAN, the French national space agency’s specialist investigation group, conducted interviews, visited the site and tested meteor, balloon and drone hypotheses before retaining an unexplained classification. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The May 2013 Ménil Hubert sur Orne sighting is weaker but still significant. A lone motorist described a dark, pebble shaped phenomenon with red lights and a high pitched hum. Investigators found no obvious conventional match, yet the absence of photographs, additional witnesses or measurable traces sharply limits what can be concluded. Together, the cases illustrate the most important distinction in Orne’s modern record: “unexplained” means that no tested explanation fitted the available testimony, not that an extraordinary craft was established. [Geipan] geipan.fr MENIL HUBERT SUR ORNE (61) 29.05.2013 GEIPAN... The Aunay les Bois sighting and its three witnesses Between midnight and 1 am on 3 June 2020, three adults were spending the evening around a fire in the garden of a family house in the countryside at Aunay les Bois. They saw a phenomenon dominated by two similarly bright bluish lights, apparently accompanied by a halo or smaller points of light. It moved horizontally at a steady speed, roughly from the south south east towards the east, and remained visible for an estimated five to ten seconds. No sound was reported. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The viewing conditions gave investigators something more useful than a simple description of lights against an empty sky. The witnesses said the phenomenon briefly disappeared behind a row of poplar trees before continuing out of sight. Because the trees provided a known foreground reference, GEIPAN could attempt to constrain the object’s apparent path and maximum possible distance. The reported elevation was low, around 15 degrees above the horizon, while hedges restricted parts of the witnesses’ wider field of view. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The presence of three witnesses strengthened the case, but not as much as three wholly independent accounts would have done. Each completed a technical questionnaire and produced drawings, and investigators later conducted cognitive interviews intended to recover details without relying only on a brief written statement. GEIPAN nevertheless noted signs that some questionnaire responses may have been agreed or discussed beforehand. That does not show dishonesty, but it makes similarities between the accounts less powerful as independent confirmation. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... GEIPAN also carried out a site visit and examined the witnesses’ angular estimates. Its current case page gives the report a consistency score of 0.72 and a strangeness score of 0.53. In GEIPAN’s terminology, consistency concerns the quantity and reliability of the information collected, while strangeness concerns how poorly the reported features fit recognised phenomena. These figures are investigative ratings, not probabilities that the sighting was extraordinary. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... Aunay les Bois therefore stands above most casual light reports in Orne. It involved several witnesses, a defined route across the sky, foreground landmarks, detailed questionnaires, drawings, interviews and an on site reconstruction. Yet it still depended almost entirely on human estimates. Nobody obtained a photograph or video, and no independent sensor fixed the lights’ distance, altitude or true speed. That last weakness matters because apparent size and motion depend heavily on distance. Two nearby lights moving moderately fast can look similar to a much larger and faster phenomenon farther away. The witnesses’ impression was real evidence about what appeared in their field of view, but it could not by itself establish the object’s physical dimensions. How investigators tested meteors, balloons and drones The Aunay les Bois report was not left unexplained simply because the lights looked unusual. GEIPAN tested several ordinary possibilities against the colour, direction, duration, apparent speed and viewing geometry. A fragmenting meteor or atmospheric entry initially offered a reasonable comparison. Bright meteors can appear blue, develop an elongated or divided form and follow an apparently straight, shallow path. The short duration also fitted better than it would for many aircraft encounters. Investigators found no corresponding observation in the astronomical and automated camera records they consulted, however. They argued that an atmospheric event large and bright enough to match the witnesses’ apparent estimates should probably have been recorded elsewhere. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... This is a meaningful negative finding because France had a national meteor monitoring infrastructure by 2020. The FRIPON network began installing approximately 100 all sky cameras in 2016 and was designed to detect bright atmospheric entries and reconstruct their trajectories through multiple observations. A missing detection cannot prove that no meteor occurred: cameras have weather, coverage and sensitivity limitations. It nevertheless weakens the meteor hypothesis when combined with the absence of corroborating public reports. [www.fripon.org] fripon.org www.fripon.org History – www.fripon.org Starting from 2016, scientists from neighboring countries were interested in joining the scientific project through the use of the FRIPON... Illuminated balloons could reproduce multiple lights, silence and a slow passage. The problem was their expected movement. Balloons at sufficient altitude would broadly follow the prevailing wind, but GEIPAN found the wind direction poorly aligned, or incompatible, with the witnesses’ reported track. The measured wind speeds at the relevant levels were also substantially below the estimated apparent speed of the phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... That conclusion depends on the accuracy of the reconstructed path and distance. A witness can estimate direction reasonably well when trees and buildings provide references, but estimating speed remains difficult without knowing range. The balloon explanation was therefore judged unsatisfactory rather than physically impossible under every conceivable set of assumptions. One or more drones formed the most flexible remaining hypothesis. A drone a few hundred metres away could move at a compatible speed, remain comparatively quiet and carry coloured lighting. GEIPAN consulted a French Navy drone instructor, who said that blue lights may be used on military drones but would normally flash. The reported lights were not described that way. Civilian night flying was also restricted, although an unlawful flight could not be excluded merely because it would have breached regulations. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The foreground trees produced the main difficulty for a single drone explanation. Using the witnesses’ angular estimates and the claim that the phenomenon passed behind trees about 200 metres away, GEIPAN calculated that one object joining the two principal lights would have needed to be more than nine metres across. That is far larger than a normal recreational drone. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... A formation of separate drones avoids that size problem. Two or more machines could fly with spacing that created the appearance of one broad phenomenon. GEIPAN did not call this impossible, but judged it unlikely because it required several coordinated drones, unusual lighting and a night operation for which no supporting evidence emerged. [Geipan] geipan.fr AUNAY LES BOIS (61) 03.06.2020 GEIPAN... The important point is that each explanation failed differently. The meteor hypothesis lacked external astronomical confirmation. Balloons did not fit the"},{"title":"Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained","url":"/why-paris-ufo-cases-rarely-stay/","description":"Paris has a rich cultural association with flying saucers, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than its reputation suggests.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained Paris has a rich cultural association with flying saucers, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than its reputation suggests. Paris has a rich cultural association with flying saucers, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than its reputation suggests. The strongest official records from the department of Paris—number 75—mostly involve distant lights, balloons, aircraft, photographic artefacts or reports that cannot be assessed because crucial measurements are missing. paris Introduction Three episodes best explain the pattern. A photographed sighting in 1997 was initially treated as unexplained but later downgraded after investigators discovered processing artefacts and possible memory contamination. A detailed half hour observation in 2019 was classified as a probable cluster of reflective balloons. In 2025, photographs, video and weather data allowed another unusual looking object to be identified as wind borne balloons. The national mass sighting of 5 November 1990, visible across much of France and relevant to Parisian observers, demonstrates how an atmospheric re entry can generate descriptions of enormous structured craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr PARIS (75) 24.08.1997 GEIPAN... Is Paris a major UFO hotspot? Paris produces sightings because it combines a large population with unusually complicated skies. Residents may see aircraft approaching or leaving the wider Paris airport system, helicopters, drones, balloons, satellites, astronomical objects and reflected lights, often through haze, between buildings or from balconies with restricted views. A statistical study of French reports found that population density was significantly associated with the geographical intensity of reported unidentified aerial phenomena. That does not mean dense areas attract mysterious craft; it means more potential observers generally produce more reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The official record does not show Paris standing out as a concentration of strong, unexplained cases. Searches of the GEIPAN database reveal Paris reports classified as aircraft, children’s balloons, reflective balloons, meteoroids, photographic artefacts and cases lacking reliable information. Examples include a 1980 meteoroid, a 2008 photograph containing a bird artefact, a 2010 children’s balloon, a 2015 balloon and a 2017 aircraft sighting. More recent entries include a reflective balloon in February 2024 and balloons in June 2025. [Geipan] geipan.fr Case name Observation date Classification Department Phenomenon Date of update Observation date Link... PARIS (75) 30.07.2... This is important because a database entry is not the same thing as an unexplained event. GEIPAN, the unit established within the French space agency CNES in 1977, records the witness’s initial uncertainty and then investigates possible causes. It deliberately uses “unidentified aerial phenomenon” rather than “UFO”, partly because a witness may have seen a light, image artefact or perceptual effect rather than a physical object. Its work draws on police and gendarmerie reports, trained investigators, scientific specialists, meteorological information and, where necessary, civil or military aviation data. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The 1997 photographs that made the case less reliable On 24 August 1997, a witness reported seeing and photographing two phenomena over Paris. The case received more official attention than a typical distant light report. Aviation gendarmes interviewed the witness, viewed nine slides and sent eight of them to specialist police and forensic laboratories. SEPRA, the predecessor of GEIPAN, also studied the material and originally placed the case in category D, meaning that it remained unidentified after investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr PARIS (75) 24.08.1997 GEIPAN... The later review exposed a serious problem: the apparent objects in the photographs were not reliable images of what the witness had described seeing. A boomerang like mark proved to be chemical residue from photographic development. A separate pentagonal form appeared only on a paper print made after a slide had been cleaned and had no equivalent on the slide itself. Investigators therefore concluded that this second form was also an artefact rather than an object recorded in the sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr PARIS (75) 24.08.1997 GEIPAN... The case also illustrates how later information can reshape memory. The witness’s initial description compared the phenomenon to a piece of greasy paper carried by the wind, with ragged edges. After being shown the pentagonal printing artefact, later accounts increasingly described a remarkable five sided object. GEIPAN judged that the witness had probably been influenced, consciously or unconsciously, by the image presented during the investigation. [Geipan] geipan.fr PARIS (75) 24.08.1997 GEIPAN... That finding did not prove that the witness saw nothing. It meant that the photographs could no longer support the visual account, while the original observation lacked dependable angular size, elevation and distance measurements. Weather information was compatible with a light object such as paper, debris or a balloon moving with the wind. In 2018 GEIPAN reclassified the event from D to C: insufficient reliable information. This distinction matters. Category C does not describe a genuinely anomalous object; it identifies a report that cannot be resolved because the evidence is inadequate or internally compromised. [Geipan] geipan.fr PARIS (75) 24.08.1997 GEIPAN... OVNIS : Militaires & scientifiques brisent le silence Documentaire AT Channel: World View – Science Open on YouTube The 2019 “metallic craft” seen for half an hour The most substantial recent Paris case occurred on the morning of 25 August 2019. A couple on an eleventh floor terrace in the 15th arrondissement watched a brilliant white object for about 30 minutes. They used binoculars and a small telescope and attempted to photograph and film it with smartphones. They described a reflective, chrome like form that appeared to rotate, with two body like sections and rods or loops hanging beneath it. Its apparent path seemed slow and curved, and it briefly disappeared before reappearing and rapidly fading from sight. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word PARIS (75) 25.08.2019 CR final.docx... Several features made the report worth investigating. There were two witnesses, their descriptions were detailed, and the duration was far longer than a meteor or brief optical flash. Astronomical checks excluded the Sun, Moon and Venus as direct matches. Aircraft information did not reveal an obvious aeroplane corresponding to the reported appearance, while the shape described through the telescope did not fit an ordinary helicopter or known drone particularly well. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word PARIS (75) 25.08.2019 CR final.docx... Yet the apparently strong evidence had important weaknesses. The photographs and videos were too poorly focused for useful object identification. Smartphone zoom enlarged blur rather than revealing dependable structure. No independent witness came forward, despite the observation supposedly covering a broad area of densely populated Paris in clear daylight. The couple also estimated that the object was above high altitude aircraft, but an observer cannot reliably determine the distance or altitude of an unfamiliar featureless object without a known scale. Something small and nearby can appear similar to something large and distant. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word PARIS (75) 25.08.2019 CR final.docx... GEIPAN’s preferred explanation was an assembly of helium filled reflective balloons joined by rods or strings. Such a cluster could account for the intense sunlight reflections, the changing shape as it rotated and the apparently dangling structures. Fine scale weather modelling showed weak winds that changed direction with altitude, offering a plausible explanation for the seemingly circular or erratic path. Investigators suggested that expansion and bursting at altitude might explain the sudden disappearance, although they acknowledged that this part could not be demonstrated. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word PARIS (75) 25.08.2019 CR final.docx... No specific balloon release was identified in news or social media records. GEIPAN therefore stopped short of calling the explanation certain and classified the case B: a probable assembly of reflective balloons. The result is more nuanced than either “mystery solved” or “unexplained craft”. The balloon hypothesis fits most observed features, but it remains a reconstruction rather"},{"title":"Key Cases","url":"/key-cases/","description":"Paris's best-documented cases show how photographs, witness memories and weather data can weaken or redirect an extraordinary claim.","breadcrumb":"Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained","level":2,"text":"Key Cases How Paris's Strongest UFO Cases Unravelled Paris UFO Files Key Cases Key Cases How Paris's Strongest UFO Cases Unravelled Paris's best documented cases show how photographs, witness memories and weather data can weaken or redirect an extraordinary claim. Paris's best documented cases show how photographs, witness memories and weather data can weaken or redirect an extraordinary claim. event time window The 1997 photographs and the problem of image artefacts The 2019 metallic object and the reflective balloon hypothesis The 2025 balloon case and the value of better data paris key cases Introduction Paris ’s most instructive UFO cases are not those that stayed mysterious, but those whose meaning changed as investigators separated what witnesses remembered from what photographs actually recorded, and compared apparent motion with wind and weather data. Three cases show the progression clearly. In 1997, supposed photographic evidence dissolved into processing marks and a later memory shift. In 2019, a well observed metallic looking object became a probable cluster of reflective balloons. In 2025, clearer timing, images, video and wind reconstruction supported a firm balloon classification. Together, they show that more evidence does not always strengthen an extraordinary claim; often it reveals which parts of the story are reliable and which are not. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... These cases also clarify what an official classification means. GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit within the French space agency CNES, collects and investigates witness reports with help from bodies including the gendarmerie, police, the Air and Space Force and Météo France. A report may begin as unidentified simply because the observer did not recognise it. The investigation then asks whether the available information supports an identification, a probable explanation, insufficient data or a genuinely unexplained result. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. The 1997 photographs did not show the reported objects On 24 August 1997, a man looking from a Paris apartment window reported two dark objects moving away towards the north east. He described the nearer one at first as resembling a piece of greasy paper blown by the wind, with irregular or torn looking angles. He left the window briefly to collect a camera and took a series of photographs. Because the sighting produced physical images, it received unusually extensive attention: aviation gendarmes interviewed him, examined nine slides and sent eight of them to the central photographic laboratory of the gendarmerie and then to its criminal research institute. SEPRA, GEIPAN’s predecessor, later reviewed the case and originally classified it as unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... The apparent photographic confirmation did not survive detailed examination. A boomerang shaped mark that had attracted the gendarmes’ attention appeared in the same place on several images and was identified as residue from photographic development. After the slides were cleaned, a paper enlargement contained a separate hollow, five sided form. Investigators found no matching shape on the underlying slide, meaning that this striking “object” had been introduced during printing rather than captured in the Paris sky. None of the surviving slides showed an object corresponding to the witness’s original description. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... The investigation then uncovered a second, less tangible problem: the account itself changed after the witness saw the five sided printing artefact. When first shown the enlargement, he did not identify the pentagonal form as the object he had observed. In accounts produced months later, however, his original paper like shape became an extraordinary five sided solid. GEIPAN considered this consistent with memory contamination—the possibility that later information had been absorbed into the remembered sighting, consciously or unconsciously. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... That finding did not prove that the witness had seen nothing. It did establish that the photographs could not be used as evidence for what he saw. The remaining visual account lacked dependable angular size, elevation and distance measurements. Estimates such as an altitude of 5,000 metres were not useful because the distance of an unfamiliar airborne object cannot normally be judged from appearance alone. Weather records showed a light wind whose direction was compatible with the reported movement, leaving paper, debris or a balloon as ordinary possibilities. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... GEIPAN therefore moved the case from category D, then used for unexplained cases, to category C: insufficient reliable information. This was not a debunking in the narrow sense of identifying one specific object. It was a reassessment of evidential value. The photographs had appeared to make the case stronger; examination showed that they contained unrelated artefacts, while exposure to one of those artefacts may have altered the later testimony. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPARIS (75) 24.08.1997Observation de deux objets dans le ciel parisien: manque d'information. Description. Le GEIPAN continue à pub... The 2019 metallic object became a balloon hypothesis The Paris sighting of 25 August 2019 began with much better testimony. A couple watched a brilliant white object from an eleventh floor terrace in the 15th arrondissement for about half an hour in clear morning daylight. They used the naked eye, binoculars and a telescope, and also took smartphone photographs and videos. Through the telescope, the object appeared metallic or chrome like, highly reflective and composed of joined forms whose appearance changed as it rotated. Its apparent path curved slowly across the sky before it diminished or disappeared. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete633 COMPTE RENDU D'ENQUÊTE9 Apr 2024 — Le 25/08/2019 à Paris deux personnes (T1 l'épouse et T2 le mari) observent sur leur terrasse dur... This was a high consistency report in GEIPAN’s terminology: two witnesses supplied detailed descriptions, sketches, trajectory information and visual recordings. Yet consistency measures how much apparently dependable information exists; it does not measure how exotic the cause is. The case demonstrates why those two questions must be kept separate. A witness can give a careful and sincere description of an object whose identity remains ordinary. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete633 COMPTE RENDU D'ENQUÊTE9 Apr 2024 — Le 25/08/2019 à Paris deux personnes (T1 l'épouse et T2 le mari) observent sur leur terrasse dur... The images did not resolve the object’s structure as clearly as the witnesses had hoped. Small, distant targets occupy very few pixels in a smartphone image. Digital zoom enlarges those pixels but does not recover missing detail, while fluctuating autofocus can produce rapidly changing shapes and apparent extensions. GEIPAN judged the photographs and videos useful for establishing that a bright phenomenon was present, but too blurred and unstable for a secure technical identification from morphology alone. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete633 COMPTE RENDU D'ENQUÊTE9 Apr 2024 — Le 25/08/2019 à Paris deux personnes (T1 l'épouse et T2 le mari) observent sur leur terrasse dur... Investigators instead tested whether the reported behaviour matched a drifting object. Météo France’s AROME atmospheric model showed weak winds whose direction changed with altitude between roughly 250 and 2,750 metres. That vertical variation could produce the slow, curving trajectory described by the couple without requiring powered flight. The intense daytime brilliance was compatible with Mylar balloons, whose metallic surfaces can reflect sunlight strongly, while the telescopic appearance resembled several balloons joined together and rotating in the air. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete633 COMPTE RENDU D'ENQUÊTE9 Apr 2024 — Le 25/08/2019 à Paris deux personnes (T1 l'épouse et T2 le mari) observent sur leur terrasse dur... The sudden disappearance was less certain. GEIPAN suggested that helium expansion at altitude might have burst the balloons, or that changes in orientation and distance could have reduced their visibility. No contemporary report, social media"},{"title":"Paris Skies","url":"/paris-skies/","description":"Dense population, busy airspace and restricted urban views help explain why unusual lights and objects are often reported over Paris.","breadcrumb":"Why Paris UFO Cases Rarely Stay Unexplained","level":2,"text":"Paris Skies Why Paris Produces So Many UFO Reports Paris UFO Files Paris Skies Paris Skies Why Paris Produces So Many UFO Reports Dense population, busy airspace and restricted urban views help explain why unusual lights and objects are often reported over Paris. Dense population, busy airspace and restricted urban views help explain why unusual lights and objects are often reported over Paris. population context Population density and the number of potential witnesses Aircraft, balloons, drones and lights in complex urban skies How GEIPAN separates unexplained cases from weak or identified reports paris paris skies Introduction Paris generates many UFO reports for a fairly simple reason: it places an exceptionally large number of potential witnesses beneath a crowded, visually confusing sky. Aircraft, helicopters, balloons, drones, satellites and illuminated objects are often seen through haze or between buildings, with few reliable clues to their distance, size or speed. A familiar object can therefore look stationary, enormous or oddly shaped. This creates reports , not necessarily a concentration of genuinely unexplained events. A statistical study of French UFO records found that population density was significantly associated with the geographical intensity of reporting. Paris also sits within one of Europe’s busiest aviation systems. Yet its official GEIPAN files repeatedly resolve unusual looking objects as aircraft, lanterns, reflective balloons or photographic artefacts, while many other reports remain unassessable because witnesses could not supply enough positional or timing information. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr 2015 09 01 Spatial Point Pattern Analysis of the Unidentified We model the unidentified aerial phenomena observed in France during the last 60 years as a spatial point pattern.Read more... More people means more opportunities to report something The first mechanism is statistical rather than mysterious. Paris concentrates residents, commuters, tourists, police officers, transport workers and people looking from offices, balconies and streets into a relatively small area. Even when only a tiny proportion notices or reports an unfamiliar light, the absolute number of reports can be substantial. Research using French sighting data supports this observer based explanation. A spatial analysis covering roughly six decades found population density to be one of the variables significantly associated with the intensity of reports. The authors did not claim that dense populations attracted unusual craft. The more cautious interpretation is that heavily populated places supply more observers and therefore more chances for an ordinary aerial event to be noticed, misunderstood and recorded. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr 2015 09 01 Spatial Point Pattern Analysis of the Unidentified We model the unidentified aerial phenomena observed in France during the last 60 years as a spatial point pattern.Read more... Paris also creates opportunities for several people to report the same object independently. A reflective balloon passing over central districts may be visible from different streets and windows, producing what initially sounds like a witness cluster. Multiple witnesses can strengthen confidence that something was genuinely present, but they do not by themselves establish that it was extraordinary. Everyone may have been looking at the same balloon, aircraft or astronomical object from slightly different angles. This distinction matters when reading local UFO history. A high number of witnesses can improve an investigation by providing several sightlines, photographs or timestamps. It can also make an ordinary event seem more impressive before those accounts are compared with wind direction, flight movements and the object’s apparent path. Paris provides too few visual clues Urban observation is often surprisingly poor. Buildings obscure the horizon and restrict sightings to narrow sections of sky. A witness may see an object only after it emerges above a roof and lose it seconds later behind another block. Without watching its complete path, it becomes difficult to judge whether it was approaching, receding, drifting or following a normal flight route. The night sky makes this problem worse. A distant light usually carries no visible scale. If the observer does not know its distance, they cannot reliably calculate its size or speed. A small nearby object and a much larger distant object may occupy the same apparent angle in the sky. An aircraft seen almost head on can appear to hover because its forward movement is directed mainly towards the observer rather than across their field of view. GEIPAN’s investigative approach reflects these limitations. It assesses both the consistency of a report—how much reliable information is available—and its residual strangeness after ordinary explanations have been tested. A sighting may sound remarkable while still having low evidential value if the direction, elevation, duration, weather conditions or original image files are missing. Category C cases are not confirmed anomalies; they are reports for which investigators lack enough dependable information to reach a conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPANFebruary 26, 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évalua... Published: February 26, 2021 Parisian streets add another difficulty: witnesses frequently describe direction using landmarks rather than compass bearings. “Above the Eiffel Tower” may mean only that the object appeared along the same line of sight. It does not prove that the object was physically close to the tower. From another district, the same light could be kilometres beyond it. Aircraft fill the wider Paris sky Paris is surrounded by a major network of airports, airfields and controlled routes. Charles de Gaulle and Orly together handled almost 107 million passengers in 2025. In the first nine months of that year alone, the two airports recorded more than 520,000 aircraft movements. These figures do not mean that every aircraft crosses central Paris, where routes and overflight are regulated, but they demonstrate the scale of aviation activity in the surrounding region. [Paris Aéroport] parisaeroport.fr adp december 2025 traffic figures adp december 2025 traffic figures Published: december 2025 Aircraft can become difficult to recognise when only their lights are visible. Landing lights may dominate the outline of an approaching aeroplane, while navigation and anti collision lights can seem to blink in unfamiliar sequences as the aircraft turns. Clouds or haze may hide the fuselage entirely. Perspective can make separate lights appear to form a triangle or other structured shape. The GEIPAN database contains Paris related reports eventually attributed to aircraft. One report made while travelling towards Paris in November 2021 was classified as a probable aeroplane sighting. Other aviation linked files remain inconclusive because investigators lacked reliable information, illustrating the difference between an unidentified report and strong evidence for an unusual object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casListe des cas; [AERO AFR] PARIS (75) 13.08.2009, 13/08/2009, C; [AERO AF422] DE PARIS (75) VERS BOGOTA (COG.) 09... Helicopters, police or emergency activity and occasional authorised aerial operations add further variety. Paris airspace is heavily controlled, but regulation does not make the sky empty. It means that investigators may need exact times, directions and flight data before determining whether an unfamiliar light matched known traffic. Balloons repeatedly create convincing objects Balloons are among the most important explanations in the Paris record because they can behave in ways that look unlike powered aircraft. They drift silently, change apparent direction with local air currents and may rise or fall slowly. Metallic party balloons can flash brilliantly when sunlight reaches them and then seem to disappear when their reflective surface turns away. Their shapes are also deceptive. Several balloons tied together may separate and regroup, producing shifting triangles, lines or clusters. At a distance, the strings and individual outlines vanish, leaving only bright points or a single irregular form. A balloon rotating in the wind may alternate between a dark silhouette and an intense reflection. A detailed GEIPAN investigation into an observation over Paris on 25 August 2019 illustrates the pattern. The witness watched several objects for about half an hour. Investigators considered the date, appearance and behaviour, noting that reflective Mylar balloons commonly generate comparable reports. The event coincided with public celebrations marking the anniversary of the Liberation of Paris, providing a plausible context for balloon releases. The case was assessed as probably involving a"},{"title":"Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","url":"/which-pas-de-calais-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Pas-de-Calais has a substantial but uneven UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Pas de Calais has a substantial but uneven UFO history. Pas de Calais has a substantial but uneven UFO history. pas de calais Introduction The most important conclusion is therefore a qualified one. Pas de Calais does possess at least one case that France’s official investigation service still classifies as genuinely unidentified: the repeated Longuenesse observations of September and October 1990. Yet even this case lacks the instrumental evidence needed to establish what was present. Several more spectacular stories, including Berck’s enormous triangle, are officially unclassified because the surviving information is too weak. Conversely, some initially puzzling incidents have become useful examples of successful identification. Taken together, the department’s files favour careful case by case analysis over claims of either a hidden invasion or universal witness error. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990LONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990. Date d'observation. 08/09/1990. Région. Nord Pas de Calais. Département. Pa... Why Pas de Calais produces so many unusual sky reports Pas de Calais occupies an unusually busy and visually exposed piece of northern France. Its western edge faces the English Channel, while its inland towns lie beneath or close to routes connecting Britain, Belgium, northern France and the Paris region. Beaches and coastal cliffs around Calais, Wimereux, Ambleteuse, Boulogne sur Mer and Berck offer wide, unobstructed horizons. Inland, the former mining belt and the open countryside around Arras and Saint Omer also provide long sight lines. These conditions do not cause UFOs, but they increase opportunities for ambiguous observations. Aircraft seen head on may appear stationary before turning suddenly. High altitude contrails can glow red or orange when the aircraft itself is almost invisible. Lights over the Channel can be difficult to place because the observer lacks familiar reference points. A nearby bird or insect photographed against distant scenery can appear to be a large object far away. GEIPAN’s Pas de Calais case list contains examples of all these problems, including aircraft, illuminated trails, balloons, astronomical bodies, lasers and birds. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The department also sits near the Belgian border, an area that statistical research has identified as having a relatively high density of French reports not fully accounted for by population alone. A study of 1,969 GEIPAN cases recorded between 1951 and 2013 found that reporting intensity varied geographically and that parts of northern France and the Belgian frontier retained elevated residual concentrations after several environmental and demographic factors were considered. The authors did not claim that this proved extraordinary craft; they treated the pattern as a question requiring better explanation. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. That distinction is essential. A cluster of reports may reflect unusual phenomena, but it may also reflect busy airspace, favourable viewing conditions, high population density, local media interest, cultural familiarity with UFO stories or differences in people’s willingness to report. Pas de Calais illustrates why geographical concentration alone cannot settle the cause of individual sightings. The 1954 wave and the making of a local legend The autumn of 1954 remains the foundational period in French UFO history. Newspapers across the country printed accounts of discs, cigars, luminous balls, supposed landings and encounters with small beings. Stories appeared in rapid succession, creating a feedback loop in which each new report made subsequent unusual lights easier to interpret as “flying saucers”. Northern France was particularly prominent because of the publicity surrounding the Quarouble case, although Quarouble is in the Nord department rather than Pas de Calais. [Complete France] completefrance.com Complete France Flying saucers in France: A history of UFO sightings Complete FranceFlying saucers in France: A history of UFO sightingsDecember 21, 2022 — 21 Dec 2022 — In 1954, railway worker Marius Dewil... Published: December 21, 2022 This administrative distinction is often blurred in popular retellings. “Nord Pas de Calais” was long used as a regional label, so cases from Nord and Pas de Calais are frequently grouped together. Marius Dewilde’s claim that he saw small beings and a landed craft near a railway line at Quarouble belongs to the wider regional story but should not be counted as a Pas de Calais incident. Its relevance lies in the publicity it generated shortly before coastal reports appeared farther west. [Complete France] completefrance.com Complete France Flying saucers in France: A history of UFO sightings Complete FranceFlying saucers in France: A history of UFO sightingsDecember 21, 2022 — 21 Dec 2022 — In 1954, railway worker Marius Dewil... Published: December 21, 2022 Pas de Calais’s own landmark from that wave is the photograph associated with Émile Turpin, a railway inspector from the Boulogne area. Contemporary press material presented Turpin as having photographed a mysterious object near Ambleteuse or from the dunes at Pointe aux Oies, between Wimereux and Ambleteuse. The date most consistently preserved in specialist archival reproductions is 2 October 1954, although later summaries have sometimes supplied different dates. The discrepancy itself is a warning about relying on repeated secondary retellings rather than the earliest available clipping. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. The published image shows a dark or bright edged shape against the sky. Turpin reportedly described a fast moving object crossing the coastal scene and considered it unlike familiar aircraft. Newspapers treated the photograph as sensational evidence and reproduced it prominently. This gave the case durability: unlike dozens of one paragraph witness accounts, it offered a visible object, a named photographer and a recognisable location. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Yet its technical value is limited. A single photograph provides no secure distance, scale or speed unless those quantities can be established independently. The apparent object could be large and distant, small and nearby, or produced by a flaw, airborne debris or an effect within the photographic process. Without the original negative, complete camera details, a sequence of frames, verified exposure information and independent observers, the image cannot demonstrate that a structured vehicle crossed the coast. The witness’s professional background adds biographical context but cannot replace physical measurement. Experience around railways or ordinary aircraft may help a person recognise familiar machines, yet it does not eliminate all possible misperceptions, especially when an object is observed briefly against an empty sky. The strongest defensible conclusion is that the Wimereux Ambleteuse photograph is an important historical artefact of the 1954 wave, not a conclusive photograph of an extraordinary craft. Its lasting value is cultural as much as evidential. It shows how local newspapers turned a fleeting observation into a durable regional mystery. Pas de Calais’s departmental archives hold an extensive collection of local press, including more than a thousand titles, making newspaper preservation central to reconstructing how such stories circulated. These collections can reveal what was claimed and when, but repeated newspaper reproduction should not be mistaken for multiple independent confirmations. [Archives Pas de Calais le Département] archivespasdecalais.fr Open source on archivespasdecalais.fr. What GEIPAN’s classifications really mean Modern assessment of French cases centres on GEIPAN, the unit within the French space agency CNES responsible for collecting, studying and publishing reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Witnesses may submit structured questionnaires, and reports made to the gendarmerie can be forwarded for specialist analysis. Investigators compare testimony with astronomical data, weather, aircraft activity, satellites, balloons, photography and other possible causes. Field investigations are used in a minority of cases when the available evidence justifies them. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d baisse cas d GEIPAN’s four broad classifications are commonly misunderstood. Category A denotes a phenomenon identified with confidence. Category B means a probable identification. Category C is used when too little dependable information survives to decide what happened."},{"title":"1954 Photo","url":"/1954-photo/","description":"The famous Wimereux-Ambleteuse image became a regional legend, but missing negatives and uncertain scale sharply limit what it can prove.","breadcrumb":"Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"1954 Photo Did the 1954 Coastal Photograph Capture a Saucer? Pas de Calais 1954 Photo 1954 Photo Did the 1954 Coastal Photograph Capture a Saucer? The famous Wimereux Ambleteuse image became a regional legend, but missing negatives and uncertain scale sharply limit what it can prove. The famous Wimereux Ambleteuse image became a regional legend, but missing negatives and uncertain scale sharply limit what it can prove. event time window What Emile Turpin Reportedly Saw How Newspapers Built the Legend Why the Photograph Cannot Settle the Case pas de calais 1954 photo Introduction The celebrated coastal photograph associated with Wimereux and Ambleteuse does not prove that a flying saucer crossed Pas de Calais in 1954. It records a small, disc like form in the sky, accompanied by Émile Turpin’s account of a fast, silent, metallic object. Yet the original negatives are no longer available for examination, the object’s distance and size cannot be established, and even the reported date became confused in later retellings. France’s official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, therefore classifies the case as category C: not explained, but too poorly documented for a reliable conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr WIMEREUX (62) 02.10.1954 GEIPANOctober 2, 1954 — WIMEREUX (62) 02.10.1954... La description trop succincte et l’absence de ph... Published: October 2, 1954 The case remains important because it became Pas de Calais’s defining image from the great French UFO wave of 1954. It shows how a striking photograph can acquire lasting authority through newspaper reproduction even when the evidence needed to test it has gradually disappeared. What Émile Turpin Reportedly Saw The location was Pointe aux Oies, a stretch of dunes on the Channel coast between Wimereux and Ambleteuse, north of Boulogne sur Mer. Turpin, a 34 year old inspector with the French national railway company and an experienced amateur photographer, said he had gone there intending to photograph a prehistoric burial site. His camera was already prepared when he noticed an unusual object and took two exposures in quick succession. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... In the fuller account published after a reinvestigation in 1973, Turpin described a light metallic disc with a central bulge and a bronze like reflection. He said it descended across his field of view, executed a steep turning or reversing movement and climbed away. Its apparent diameter, he recalled, was similar to that of the Moon. He heard no sound, although he acknowledged that noise from the sea might have masked it, and compared its speed with that of a jet fighter. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... Turpin said he took the first photograph without changing the camera settings, which had been selected for a subject only five to seven metres away. He then advanced the film and took a second image. When he tried to alter the settings for a third exposure, the object had disappeared. Later timing tests suggested that two or three seconds separated the photographs. The camera was reported as a Foca 35 mm model with a 35 mm Oplar lens, using fast Kodak Tri X film at about 1/100 second. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... Those details make the story more substantial than a photograph with no witness account. Two exposures were reportedly taken, the witness was already carrying a loaded camera for an unrelated purpose, and the published narrative included equipment and exposure information. None of this, however, establishes the object’s nature. It merely shows that a reasonably specific observation story became attached to the surviving pictures. The date problem The earliest press coverage gives the event as 2 October 1954 at about 4.30 pm. La Voix du Nord reported the photograph in mid October, and France Soir published it on 16 October under a caption identifying the location as the dunes between Ambleteuse and Wimereux. A photograph could not have appeared in a newspaper on 16 October if it had been taken on 24 October, the date later repeated in several UFO catalogues. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, la vague française de 1954 dans la presse... The official GEIPAN record also adopts 2 October. It states that the gendarmerie learned of the event through the press and interviewed Turpin on 15 October. Later UFO literature nevertheless shifted the incident to 24 October, apparently through copying, cataloguing mistakes or confusion introduced during the 1973 reinvestigation. A photographic catalogue of 1954 cases specifically warns that the episode has been misplaced or misdated in several sources. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... The most defensible chronology is therefore: 2 October 1954: the reported observation and photographs; 14–16 October: regional and national newspaper publication; 15 October: the gendarmerie interview, according to GEIPAN; 18 October: the dated gendarmerie report listed in the surviving case bibliography; 1973: a later reinvestigation published in the UFO magazine Lumières dans la Nuit . [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... This confusion is not a minor clerical point. When a famous case cannot retain a stable date, later claims about weather, aircraft movements or contemporary corroboration become harder to check. 1:10:21 The Venezuelan UFO Wave of 1954: Invasion of the Hairy Humanoids Channel: Preston Dennett · Views: 22.8K · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 1 hour 10 minutes Open on YouTube How Newspapers Built the Legend The image appeared at the ideal moment to become a sensation. France was in the middle of its extraordinary autumn 1954 wave, when newspapers were publishing almost daily stories about discs, luminous objects, supposed landings and strange occupants. Against that background, a clear coastal landscape containing an apparently structured object was more memorable than another verbal account of a distant light. The newspaper framing encouraged readers to see a machine. One regional account described a moderately bright object travelling rapidly from west to east and asserted that a trained eye could distinguish it from an aeroplane, helicopter, airship or flying wing. The village of Ambleteuse was visible below, giving the picture a recognisable Pas de Calais setting and an apparent sense of scale. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, la vague française de 1954 dans la presse... That apparent scale is deceptive. The village and coastline are distant background features, but the unidentified form has no demonstrated relationship to them. It could be a large object far away, a small object much nearer to the camera, or an artefact arising during exposure, processing or reproduction. A photograph flattens all those possible distances onto the same sheet of paper. National distribution strengthened the photograph’s reputation. France Soir presented it as the latest photograph of a “saucer” from Pas de Calais, while an Associated Press caption circulated the question of whether a flying saucer had hovered over the area. Repetition converted an uncertain image into a recognised event: the “Ambleteuse saucer”, even though the observation point was near Wimereux and the official file eventually used Wimereux as its location. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, France Soir, Paris, France, page 9, October 16, 1954... Published: October 16, 1954 The use of quotation marks and questions allowed newspapers to avoid making a formal claim while still supplying the desired interpretation. Readers did not encounter an unidentified blur in a neutral photographic test. They encountered a “saucer” during a national saucer wave. What the Investigation Actually Established The gendarmerie did make enquiries, but surviving accounts differ over their scope and certainty. The 1973 reinvestigation said officers checked possible weather balloons, helicopters, new aircraft, astronomical bodies and movements extending across northern France, Belgium and Britain, without finding a match. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 2 octobre, Wimereux, Pas de Calais... That sounds impressive, but it survives mainly through later UFO publications rather than through a complete modern evidential file. GEIPAN’s summary is more restrained. It says the gendarmerie learned of the photograph through press coverage, met Turpin on 15 October and produced a brief report. The surviving GEIPAN copies do not contain the photographic prints that were apparently attached to the first version of that"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-65ee08/","description":"GEIPAN's records show why one repeated police sighting remains unidentified while many stranger-sounding reports stay weak or become explained.","breadcrumb":"Which Pas de Calais UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Official Files Why Longuenesse Remains Officially Unidentified Pas de Calais UFOs Official Files Official Files Why Longuenesse Remains Officially Unidentified GEIPAN's records show why one repeated police sighting remains unidentified while many stranger sounding reports stay weak or become explained. GEIPAN's records show why one repeated police sighting remains unidentified while many stranger sounding reports stay weak or become explained. dataset evidence The Repeated Longuenesse Observations What GEIPAN Categories Actually Mean Unresolved, Unclassifiable and Successfully Explained Cases pas de calais official files Introduction The Longuenesse reports remain the strongest officially unresolved UFO case in Pas de Calais , but “unresolved” needs careful handling. During several nights in September and October 1990, a local witness and police patrols reported stationary or slowly moving lights near Longuenesse and Saint Omer. The principal witness described an oval form edged with flashing red and green lights; officers independently recorded bright coloured points and, on one occasion, four lights travelling in a V formation. France’s official UFO service still lists the case as category D: unidentified after investigation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990Observation durant plusieurs jours et durant plusieurs heures à partir de 21h30 de formes ovoides avec d... That classification makes Longuenesse unusual, not proven extraordinary. The surviving file contains repeated observations, police testimony and references to photographs, giving it more substance than a vague one off story. Yet it offers no published radar return, calibrated image, physical trace or reliable measurement of distance and altitude. Longuenesse therefore matters chiefly because it shows how an official investigation can reach a genuine “we do not know” without endorsing spacecraft, secret technology or any other specific explanation. 14:41 GEIPAN : dans les coulisses de l’organisme qui étudie les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés Channel: france tv · Views: 4.5K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 14 minutes Open on YouTube The Repeated Longuenesse Observations The case began on the evening of 8 September 1990. A 52 year old notary’s clerk reported seeing what appeared to be an oval object surrounded by eight or nine flashing red and green lights. It could remain stationary, he said, but could also move at roughly the apparent speed of an aircraft. No sound was heard. Similar phenomena were reported on 9 and 11 September and again around the middle of October, usually appearing from about 9.30 pm and sometimes remaining visible for hours. On one night, the witness said it was still present when he awoke at approximately 4 am. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan longuenesse 1990 pv n755 1990307161 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1990 09 01214, LONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... Those repetitions are the first reason the file carries more weight than many UFO reports. A fleeting light can easily be a meteor, aircraft reflection or momentary perceptual error. A phenomenon apparently returning on separate nights provides investigators with opportunities to compare directions, timings and behaviour. It can also create complications, however: repeated appearances may indicate a recurring astronomical object, scheduled aircraft traffic, ground based lighting or several unrelated stimuli being interpreted as one continuing mystery. The police involvement is the case’s most important evidential feature. After being called to the area, officers reported seeing through binoculars a bright, fixed point north of a local car dealership, above the treetops, emitting blue green and red flashes. They also saw other white lights moving across the sky. A separate patrol near a canal bridge reported four luminous points arranged in a V and moving from east to west. Police forensic personnel were reportedly called and took photographs. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan longuenesse 1990 pv n755 1990307161 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1990 09 01214, LONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... This does not mean that every witness saw the same physical object. The records contain several descriptions: one oval form with perimeter lights, a bright stationary point, moving white lights and a four light V formation. These could have been different aspects of one phenomenon, but they could equally have been several ordinary objects seen in the same period. The official file preserves the observations; it does not demonstrate that they all belonged to a single craft. The principal witness added two striking details during a formal interview on 22 October. Through binoculars, he believed he could see intermittent moving lines inside the oval form. He also reported disruption to two television channels, accompanied by an audible hum. That apparent electrical effect sounds impressive, but its value is limited because neighbours did not report the same interference and no technical examination established a connection with the lights. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan longuenesse 1990 pv n755 1990307161 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1990 09 01214, LONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... Why the File Was Not Easily Explained The 1990 police paperwork recorded at least two ordinary suggestions: reflections from lasers used by a local nightclub and the Soviet space station Mir. Neither was established as an adequate explanation, and the case remained open. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan longuenesse 1990 pv n755 1990307161 UFO TransparencyGEIPAN Case 1990 09 01214, LONGUENESSE (62) 08.09.1990, GEIPAN / CNES · 2007 · UFO Transparency... A laser hypothesis might account for coloured light projected onto cloud or haze, especially if the apparent source remained fixed or moved slowly. Pas de Calais contains other official cases in which nightclub or display lasers were successfully identified. For example, GEIPAN classifies a 1988 Hulluch report as probably caused by a laser and a 1993 Le Portel case as definitively laser related. That local precedent makes the suggestion reasonable, but Longuenesse included reports of apparently structured lights, long duration and movements across different directions. The available file does not show that investigators identified a specific venue, beam direction or weather layer capable of reproducing the full sequence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Mir was also an understandable candidate because an orbiting spacecraft can appear as a bright moving point. It is far less suitable for an object said to remain in roughly the same area for hours, return throughout a night or display persistent red and green lights. A low Earth orbiting spacecraft crosses a local sky in minutes rather than hovering until early morning. The Mir suggestion therefore appears in the police record as speculation, not a demonstrated match. Aircraft remain a plausible partial explanation. Red and green navigation lights are standard features of aeroplanes, while a head on aircraft can appear almost stationary before turning. Distance and speed are exceptionally difficult to judge at night, particularly when the observer has no known object size or reliable background reference. GEIPAN itself uses aircraft cases to demonstrate how perspective can create the impression of a hovering object, a silent triangle or an oddly shaped luminous body. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Aircraft do not neatly resolve everything in the Longuenesse account, however. The alleged hours long duration, repeated stationary appearance and reports of multiple formations would require either recurring traffic, misassociation of several aircraft, or an additional fixed light such as a star or distant ground source. No published reconstruction identifies particular flights or radar tracks. The aircraft hypothesis is therefore credible but incomplete on the evidence presently available. Astronomical scintillation is another possibility for the fixed coloured point. Bright stars low in the sky can appear to flash red, green and blue because atmospheric turbulence refracts their light. Binoculars may enlarge the blurred pattern without revealing a solid object. Yet an astronomical explanation would need the recorded compass direction, elevation and exact observation times to be checked against the sky. The public summary does not present such a calculation. 1:49 Un OVNI survole la France Archive INA Channel: INA Société · Views: 97.9K · Uploaded: June 2017 · Length: 1 minute 49 seconds Open on YouTube What GEIPAN Categories Actually Mean GEIPAN is a technical unit of France’s national space agency, CNES. It collects witness"},{"title":"When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations","url":"/puy-de-dome/","description":"Puy-de-Dôme has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but it does not have one dominant, well-documented “classic” case comparable with...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations Puy de Dôme has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but it does not have one dominant, well documented “classic” case comparable with... Puy de Dôme has a genuine record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena, but it does not have one dominant, well documented “classic” case comparable with France’s best known UFO incidents. Its departmental history is instead a collection of brief lights, apparent stars, luminous spheres, photographic anomalies and occasional aviation reports. puy de dome Introduction The most instructive Puy de Dôme cases were eventually linked to Venus, aircraft, meteors, distant lightning, reflections, lanterns or probable balloons. Other reports remain unclassified in practical terms because too little reliable information survived. That distinction matters: an unresolved file is not necessarily evidence of an extraordinary object. In many local cases, it means that investigators lacked photographs, precise directions, corroborating witnesses or timely meteorological and aviation data. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... What the official record actually shows France’s official body for these reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects testimony, consults the police, gendarmerie, aviation authorities, military air operations services, meteorologists and technical specialists, and publishes anonymised case files. GEIPAN deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerial phenomenon” because witnesses may have seen a light, atmospheric effect or optical artefact rather than a physical flying object. Its remit is to explain reported strangeness, not to investigate extraterrestrial visitation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The classification system is essential when reading Puy de Dôme’s files: A: explained with strong supporting evidence. B: probably explained, although definitive proof is unavailable. C: insufficiently documented or too unreliable for a firm conclusion. D: still unexplained after investigation. Nationally, CNES currently reports that only a small minority of published cases remain unexplained after investigation; a much larger share cannot be resolved simply because the data are inadequate. This national pattern closely matches the character of the accessible Puy de Dôme archive: more ordinary explanations and evidential gaps than robust anomalies. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Clermont Ferrand, 1991: several witnesses, but still Venus The early morning sighting of 19 September 1991 is probably the department’s most useful case for understanding how witness credibility differs from identification. At about 5.20 am, a Clermont Ferrand resident noticed a brilliant stationary point in the eastern or south eastern sky. He watched it again after reaching home and contacted the gendarmerie at Cournon d’Auvergne. A duty gendarme saw the light for several minutes, another caller independently reported it, and the Clermont Ferrand police station also became involved. The principal witness continued watching for nearly an hour. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This was therefore not a casual, unsupported tale. GEIPAN considered the case consistent and explicitly stated that the sincerity of the witnesses, including the police and gendarmerie personnel, was not in doubt. Yet their descriptions were also highly diagnostic: a fixed, star like white light, very small in apparent size, visible for a long period in a clear sky and showing no detail through binoculars. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. When GEIPAN re examined the old file in 2013, astronomical reconstruction placed Venus in the reported direction. At the time it was exceptionally bright, with a magnitude of about −4.4, and its position, colour and apparent behaviour closely matched the testimony. GEIPAN reclassified the case from an inadequately resolved category to category A: a definite misidentification of Venus. The conclusion did not accuse anyone of inventing the event. The witnesses accurately saw a bright phenomenon but interpreted a familiar planet as something unusual, probably under the combined effects of darkness, fatigue and surprise. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case matters because multiple witnesses do not automatically make an extraordinary explanation more likely. Several people sharing the same viewpoint can independently see the same real light and still make the same understandable mistake. The aviation connection was real, but not mysterious One of the most striking regional reports occurred on 20 September 1956, when two military pilots flying near Brioude, south of Puy de Dôme, noticed a large aluminium coloured spherical object. They climbed in an attempt to approach it but were unable to close the distance and abandoned the effort after roughly twenty minutes. Taken alone, the description could sound like a notable military encounter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO MIL] BRIOUDE (43) 20.09.1956 GEIPAN... The crucial evidence came from Clermont Ferrand. The director of the Puy de Dôme observatory confirmed that a large translucent balloon had been visible over the area throughout the day, drifting slowly at an altitude of approximately 20 kilometres. Its origin was not established, and neither the observatory nor the regional weather services claimed responsibility for releasing it. Nevertheless, GEIPAN judged the phenomenon to be a stratospheric balloon and classified the case B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO MIL] BRIOUDE (43) 20.09.1956 GEIPAN... The pilots’ inability to catch it was not considered evidence of extraordinary performance. The balloon remained many kilometres away, so changes in the aircraft’s position did little to alter its apparent bearing. A distant object against an almost featureless sky can also seem to accompany an aircraft because the observer lacks reliable visual cues for distance and relative motion. The mystery that remained was the balloon’s operator, not the physical nature of the object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO MIL] BRIOUDE (43) 20.09.1956 GEIPAN... A more recent aviation linked case shows how much modern records can add. At Espinasse on 12 October 2023, a witness saw intense white lights moving rapidly across the sky and captured a limited video. GEIPAN used information from France’s national air operations centre and identified the lights as four military aircraft. The file was classified A. This is an important contrast with older reports: access to flight and radar information can turn an impressive looking light formation into an ordinary, verifiable movement of aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANESPINASSE (63) 12.10.2023 GEIPAN... GEIPAN : Tout savoir sur les OVNIS et Phénomènes Aérospatiaux (PAN) Channel: Science & Vie Open on YouTube Billom and Sauxillanges: unresolved because the data are weak Not every Puy de Dôme report has received a firm explanation. Near Billom in February 1976, a motorist described a brilliant red sphere moving rapidly from north to south at apparently low altitude. The observation lasted only a few seconds. The original police record contained uncertainty even about the exact date, while other people questioned in the area reported seeing nothing unusual. GEIPAN classified the case C because there was not enough dependable information to test possible explanations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBILLOM (63) 17.02.1976 GEIPAN... The label does not mean that investigators recognised an unexplainable craft. A brief red light could have several ordinary causes, including an aircraft, meteor, flare or distant light seen under misleading conditions. With no precise angular measurements, confirmed timing, photograph or independent corroboration, choosing between them would be speculation. The same evidential problem affected a report made by two motorists travelling from Sauxillanges towards Collanges on 23 September 2012. They described several round luminous objects appearing and disappearing among clouds. Investigators considered metallic balloons reflecting sunlight: the cloudy weather, wind direction and apparently slow relative movement were compatible with that idea. But no balloon release or independent observation could be confirmed, so GEIPAN retained category C rather than treating the hypothesis as proven. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D996] DE SAUXILLANGES (63) VERS COLLANGES (63) 23.09.2012 GEIPAN... These cases illustrate the most easily misunderstood part of UFO classification. “Unidentified” may describe the limits of the file rather than the properties of the phenomenon. The less"},{"title":"Explained Cases","url":"/explained-cases-d41807/","description":"Puy-de-Dome's best-documented sightings show how sincere witnesses can mistake Venus, balloons or aircraft for something extraordinary.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations","level":2,"text":"Explained Cases Why Credible Witnesses Still Misidentify UFOs Puy de Dome Explained Cases Explained Cases Why Credible Witnesses Still Misidentify UFOs Puy de Dome's best documented sightings show how sincere witnesses can mistake Venus, balloons or aircraft for something extraordinary. Puy de Dome's best documented sightings show how sincere witnesses can mistake Venus, balloons or aircraft for something extraordinary. mechanism The Clermont Ferrand Venus sighting Pilots, balloons and deceptive distance How modern flight data resolves strange lights puy de dome explained cases Introduction Puy de Dôme’s explained sightings make a simple but important point: a credible witness can describe a real light or object accurately and still identify it wrongly. Police officers, motorists, experienced sky watchers and people familiar with military aircraft have all reported apparently extraordinary phenomena in the department. Several reports were later matched to Venus, balloons or known aircraft movements. This is not evidence that the witnesses lied or were careless. The usual problem is that the night sky provides few reliable clues to distance, scale and speed. A bright planet can appear to hover above a sensitive site; a distant balloon may seem to follow an aircraft; high flying jets may look slower, quieter or less recognisable than expected. Puy de Dôme’s official files therefore show why witness credibility and object identification must be assessed separately. Reliable testimony establishes that something was seen. It does not, by itself, establish what that something was. The Clermont Ferrand light was real — and it was Venus The most instructive local example occurred before dawn on 19 September 1991. At about 5.20 am, a Clermont Ferrand resident noticed an intense white light in the eastern or south eastern sky. He saw it again after reaching home and contacted the gendarmerie at Cournon d’Auvergne. The duty gendarme observed the same light, another caller independently reported it, and Clermont Ferrand police also became involved. The principal witness watched it for nearly an hour. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This was a comparatively strong piece of testimony. There were several observers, including police and gendarmerie personnel, and the descriptions were mutually compatible. GEIPAN’s later review explicitly said that the witnesses’ sincerity and credibility were not in doubt. The phenomenon itself was unquestionably present. Yet the details that made the report reliable also made the explanation possible. The light was: stationary or apparently stationary; very small in angular size; white and exceptionally bright; compared with a large star; visible in a clear sky for a long period; still featureless when examined with binoculars. When GEIPAN reconstructed the sky for Clermont Ferrand at 5.25 am, Venus occupied the reported part of the eastern sky. Its magnitude was approximately −4.4, making it by far the brightest star like object visible at that time. Its position, colour and apparent lack of movement matched the testimony closely. The old case, originally left in a poorly resolved category, was consequently reclassified as category A: a definite identification of Venus. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Notes d'enquete57 Notes d'enquete57 The investigation did not conclude that the observers had imagined the light. Rather, they saw Venus correctly but interpreted it under unfamiliar circumstances. Darkness, surprise and possible fatigue made an exceptionally bright planet seem anomalous. Because none of the witnesses recognised the object as Venus, its very brilliance became evidence of strangeness rather than a clue to its identity. A similar mechanism appeared at Bertignat on 3 July 2014. Several witnesses watched and filmed a bright object apparently hovering above the military site at Pierre sur Haute. It seemed crescent shaped and showed changing colours. Mapping placed the reported line of sight towards the base, but astronomical reconstruction showed Venus low in the same part of the sky. Atmospheric turbulence near the horizon accounted for the changing shape and colour. The apparent position over a military installation was a chance alignment that made an otherwise ordinary light feel significant. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases show why context affects interpretation. A light above rooftops may be dismissed as a planet. The same light apparently above a military facility can be perceived as surveillance, a secret aircraft or an unknown machine, even though the visual information has not changed. Pilots and balloons: expertise cannot supply missing distance Professional experience improves observation, but it cannot remove the basic limitations of vision. A striking regional example began on 20 September 1956, when two military pilots near Brioude attempted to approach a large aluminium coloured sphere. The pilots climbed towards it but seemed unable to reduce the distance, eventually abandoning the pursuit after about twenty minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case lies in neighbouring Haute Loire, but the decisive evidence came from Puy de Dôme. The Clermont Ferrand observatory reported that a translucent, roughly spherical balloon had been visible over the region for much of the day. It was drifting slowly at approximately 20 kilometres altitude and disappeared only when it ceased to be illuminated by the Sun. GEIPAN calculated that the balloon was initially about 17 kilometres from the pilots. Even after they climbed to roughly 10 kilometres altitude, it remained about 10 kilometres away. At such distances, the pilots had little visual information from which to judge the balloon’s true size, range or relative motion. Their impression that it was maintaining its distance or following them was therefore a normal effect of perspective, not evidence of controlled manoeuvring. The case was classified B because the balloon’s exact origin could not be established, although the object type was considered highly probable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This is especially revealing because pilots are often treated as virtually infallible UFO witnesses. Pilots are highly trained to recognise aircraft, assess hazards and interpret instruments. But an unidentified object without a known size, clear background or radar range remains difficult to place in three dimensional space. Expertise cannot calculate distance from a featureless point or sphere when the visual scene supplies no scale. Puy de Dôme’s balloon reports also show the difference between a plausible explanation and a demonstrated one. On 23 September 2012, a motorist and passenger travelling from Sauxillanges towards Collanges saw several rounded lights appearing and disappearing among clouds. GEIPAN found that metallic balloons reflecting sunlight could account for the observations. The local wind would have carried balloons in a way that made them appear almost stationary relative to the moving car. A release following a celebration or sporting event was also plausible. However, no actual balloon release was traced, so the file remained category C rather than being declared solved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That restraint matters. “Probably balloons” is not the same conclusion as “confirmed balloons”. A good ordinary explanation must match the reported appearance and movement, but investigators should not upgrade it to certainty without independent evidence. 1:57 Jupiter and Venus 'could be mistaken for UFOs' Channel: The Telegraph · Views: 22.9K · Uploaded: March 2012 · Length: 1 minute 57 seconds Open on YouTube Aircraft rarely look exactly as witnesses expect Several Puy de Dôme cases arose because real aircraft did not resemble the witness’s mental picture of an aircraft. Sound may arrive late or not be noticed. Navigation lights change with viewing angle. Wings can disappear against a bright or dark sky, while an aircraft seen head on may look like a single stationary light. At Lapeyrouse on 3 April 2015, a motorist and two passengers reported an unfamiliar aircraft moving slowly and silently at apparently very low altitude. GEIPAN consulted military air traffic control, which confirmed that a military aircraft had crossed the area at about 4 pm, flying at approximately 2,100 feet, or 700 metres, on a north to north westerly course. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witnesses’ reported time was around fifteen minutes later, and their altitude estimate differed from the recorded value. Those discrepancies did not overturn the identification. Human estimates made during a brief, unexpected observation are seldom precise enough to compete with an independently recorded flight. The confirmed presence, route and unusual low level operation of the military aircraft accounted for the essential"},{"title":"Unresolved Files","url":"/unresolved-files/","description":"Some Puy-de-Dome reports remain unidentified not because they defy explanation, but because the surviving evidence is too limited to test one.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Met Ordinary Explanations","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Files What Unresolved UFO Files Actually Mean Puy de Dome Unresolved Files Unresolved Files What Unresolved UFO Files Actually Mean Some Puy de Dome reports remain unidentified not because they defy explanation, but because the surviving evidence is too limited to test one. Some Puy de Dome reports remain unidentified not because they defy explanation, but because the surviving evidence is too limited to test one. dataset evidence The brief red sphere near Billom The luminous objects reported at Sauxillanges Why missing data prevents firm conclusions puy de dome unresolved files Introduction In Puy de Dôme, some UFO reports remain unresolved for a mundane but important reason: there is too little surviving information to test an explanation properly. France’s official investigator, GEIPAN, places such reports in category C, meaning that the observation is not analysable because reliable data are missing. That is different from category D, where a case remains unexplained despite having enough evidence for meaningful investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The brief red sphere reported near Billom in 1976 and the luminous objects seen between Sauxillanges and Collanges in 2012 illustrate this distinction. Both accounts may describe genuine visual experiences, but neither provides strong evidence for an extraordinary craft. Billom offers only a few seconds of testimony, an uncertain date and no corroborating witness. Sauxillanges is better documented, yet the most plausible explanation — sunlit metallic balloons moving with the wind — could not be independently confirmed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBILLOM (63) 17.02.1976 GEIPAN... The brief red sphere near Billom The Billom file concerns a motorist who, at about 8.50 pm in February 1976, reportedly saw a very bright red sphere moving rapidly from north to south at apparently low altitude. The observation lasted only a few seconds. People questioned in the surrounding area said that they had noticed nothing unusual, and GEIPAN ultimately classified the report as category C because the available information was insufficient. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBILLOM (63) 17.02.1976 GEIPAN... Even the date is uncertain. The published case title gives 17 February 1976, while GEIPAN’s description refers to 27 February and explicitly notes an imprecision in the original police record. This is more than a minor archival blemish. The exact date and time determine which aircraft, astronomical objects, meteors, balloons and weather conditions can be checked. Without a secure date, even a promising explanation may be impossible to verify or reject. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBILLOM (63) 17.02.1976 GEIPAN... The description itself contains too few measurable details. “Low altitude” is an impression rather than a calculated height, because the witness had no known distance or size against which to judge the object. Likewise, “very rapid” describes apparent movement across the field of view, not a physical speed. A small nearby light and a much larger distant phenomenon can produce similar angular motion. GEIPAN’s methodology warns that witnesses commonly infer distance and speed for unfamiliar objects even when the visual information does not allow those quantities to be determined. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Several conventional possibilities therefore remain open. A meteor or fragmenting fireball can appear as a brilliant coloured sphere and cross part of the sky in seconds. An aircraft light seen through haze may look red and isolated, especially when the body of the aircraft is invisible. A flare, distant firework or other transient light might also fit portions of the account. None can responsibly be adopted as the answer, however, because the record lacks a precise trajectory, angular elevation, duration, weather description, sound information and independent confirmation. The absence of corroboration does not prove that nothing was visible. A short event may easily be missed by people indoors or looking elsewhere. It does, however, prevent triangulation and removes an important check on the witness’s estimates. Billom is therefore unresolved only in the limited sense that no testable identification survives. It is not a well supported anomaly that resisted a detailed technical investigation. The luminous objects reported at Sauxillanges The Sauxillanges report is more informative. At 5.05 pm on 23 September 2012, a motorist and his companion were travelling towards Collanges when they saw several round luminous objects appearing and disappearing among clouds. The official file contains witness material, a route map and weather information, but no independent report from another location. GEIPAN classified the case as category C. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Here investigators could construct a plausible ordinary explanation. The witnesses were travelling south south west. Weather information from Clermont Ferrand indicated a fairly cloudy sky and a strong southerly wind. GEIPAN suggested that metallic balloons reflecting sunlight could account for round bright objects that repeatedly vanished behind cloud. Because the wind was blowing broadly towards the approaching witnesses, the balloons could also have appeared nearly stationary or slow moving from the car. The Sunday afternoon timing was considered compatible with balloons released after a celebration or sporting event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This explanation fits several otherwise puzzling features at once: Round, bright appearance: reflective foil balloons can produce intense flashes when sunlight strikes them at a favourable angle. Repeated disappearance: patchy cloud can hide and reveal drifting objects without the objects themselves switching off. Apparent hovering: an object moving roughly along the observer’s line of sight shows little sideways motion. Multiple objects: party balloons are commonly released in groups rather than singly. The case nevertheless remained category C because plausibility is not confirmation. Investigators did not locate a documented balloon release, another witness who had seen the objects from a different position, a usable photograph or a record that fixed their height and path. GEIPAN therefore lacked the additional cross check needed to move the case into category B, its classification for a probable explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Sauxillanges is consequently stronger than Billom as an investigative file but weaker as evidence of something extraordinary. It contains enough context to suggest a coherent explanation, yet not enough to prove it. Later examination narrowed the range of possibilities rather than strengthening the UFO claim. Why missing data prevents firm conclusions GEIPAN evaluates cases using two broad ideas: strangeness , meaning how far the observation appears to lie from known phenomena, and consistency , meaning the quantity and reliability of the information supporting it. Consistency depends on such matters as the number and independence of witnesses, the precision of their answers, internal coherence, photographs, video and other records. When the information is too weak to validate either an explanation or an unexplained status, the case is placed in category C. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN For brief light sightings in Puy de Dôme, a small number of missing details can make the difference between a solved and permanently indeterminate file. Exact time and date allow investigators to reconstruct the sky, check aircraft movements, compare meteor reports and obtain relevant weather records. Billom’s date discrepancy severely limits that work. Direction and angular elevation distinguish a planet near the horizon from an aircraft, balloon or meteor. A statement such as “ahead”, “above the road” or “low in the sky” may be too broad for reconstruction. Duration and uninterrupted observation matter because different phenomena operate on different timescales. A meteor usually lasts seconds; a balloon or aircraft can remain visible much longer. Estimates made after a startling event may also be imprecise. Observer movement can create misleading apparent motion. From a moving vehicle, changes in viewing angle may make a distant light appear to follow the car, remain stationary or change direction. GEIPAN specifically treats such perception effects as part of its investigations rather than assuming that reported motion directly represents an object’s true flight path. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Independent corroboration is especially valuable. Two people in the same car confirm that an experience was shared, but they have almost the same viewing geometry"},{"title":"Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained","url":"/strange-lights-over-pyrenees-atlantiques/","description":"The UFO history of Pyrénées-Atlantiques is less a story of one celebrated “close encounter” than of repeated misidentifications tested against an unusually...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained The UFO history of Pyrénées Atlantiques is less a story of one celebrated “close encounter” than of repeated misidentifications tested against an unusually... The UFO history of Pyrénées Atlantiques is less a story of one celebrated “close encounter” than of repeated misidentifications tested against an unusually varied setting: Atlantic coast, mountain valleys, busy air routes, Pau airport and extensive civil and military aviation activity. pyrenees atlantiques Introduction The department’s most valuable case is a December 2022 series involving a Civil Security helicopter crew and an air traffic controller near Pau. Experienced aviation witnesses, night vision equipment and official statements made it a comparatively strong report. Yet a detailed investigation still concluded that the lights were probably meteors, including members of the Geminid shower. That outcome captures the department’s main lesson: credible witnesses can accurately report something genuinely striking while being mistaken about its distance, height or nature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr 14/12/2022; Région. Aquitaine; Département. Pyrénées Atlantiques; Classification. B; Date de... What the official record actually contains France’s principal public source is GEIPAN, the unit of the national space agency CNES that collects and analyses reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its modern system distinguishes cases that are identified, probably identified, too poorly documented to assess, and genuinely unexplained after investigation. The categories are not a ladder from “ordinary” to “alien”: a category C report, for example, means insufficient evidence, not a mysterious object supported by strong evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Not very far from the alien hypothesis stands the conspiracy theory which is also continuously present in the GEIPAN work: “the GEIPAN ha... The published Pyrénées Atlantiques files include reports from Pau, Bayonne, Biarritz, Orthez, Urepel, Arudy, Guéthary, Arbus and the Béarn valleys. The visible pattern is dominated by three outcomes: Identified or probably identified phenomena , including aircraft, helicopters, birds, flying lanterns, stars and meteors. Old or incomplete reports , which cannot be reconstructed confidently. A small number of initially impressive aviation related sightings , whose value lies in the quality of the subsequent investigation rather than in proof of an exotic craft. Examples are revealing. An observation at Urepel in February 1985 was classified as identified: the principal light matched Arcturus, while another reported phenomenon was considered probably to have been an aircraft whose noise was weakened by the wind. A Pau report from May 2012 was classified as probably explained by flying lanterns. At Bayonne, twelve reddish orange lights seen in August 2014 were also attributed to lanterns; a September report was linked to a helicopter; and a brief 2019 formation was assessed as birds. An Orthez case from December 2008 was identified as an aircraft. [Geipan] geipan.fr UREPEL (64) 02.02.1985Le GEIPAN classe ce cas en A: observation de Arcturus. Le PAN du 4 février correspond probablement à une obs... These conclusions do not mean that witnesses invented their experiences. They show how ordinary objects can become difficult to recognise when seen at night without a dependable scale, horizon or distance. A light thought to be hovering nearby may be a star; a formation apparently moving silently may be distant birds; and a sequence of orange lights may be small lanterns moving with the wind. The December 2022 Pau aviation cases The department’s most substantial recent episode unfolded on 13 and 14 December 2022 around Pau airport at Uzein and the route northwards from the mountain town of Bedous. It matters because the witnesses were not casual sky watchers. They included a helicopter captain, an onboard mechanic and an air traffic controller, and the principal crew were conducting mountain night training with night vision binoculars. Statements were collected through aviation and gendarmerie channels, giving investigators than is available in most civilian reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr 14/12/2022; Région. Aquitaine; Département. Pyrénées Atlantiques; Classification. B; Date de... Dark forms near Uzein On 13 December, air traffic personnel at Uzein reported successive dark shapes accompanied by lights. GEIPAN eventually classified the case as identified, favouring flying lanterns. The published file gives it low residual strangeness and relatively good informational consistency. This distinction is important: the report was not dismissed as foolish or fabricated. Rather, the recorded motion and appearance fitted a known explanation once the circumstances were reconstructed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 1:57 Jupiter and Venus 'could be mistaken for UFOs' Channel: The Telegraph · Views: 22.9K · Uploaded: March 2012 · Length: 1 minute 57 seconds Open on YouTube Luminous trails seen from a helicopter The following evening, at about 18:35, a Civil Security helicopter was returning towards Pau after flying near Oloron Sainte Marie. The captain saw a bright, apparently horizontal trail with a large oblong form at its front. It disappeared after several seconds. Further luminous trails followed, and an air traffic controller separately saw a light crossing part of the sky. The crew contacted the control tower to ask whether fast aircraft or military activity could account for what they had seen; none was reported on radar or otherwise known to the controller. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête28 Oct 2024 — [AERO] de BEDOUS (64) vers PAU (64) 14.12.2022. Direction Technique et Numérique. Direction Adj... That absence of known traffic initially strengthens the report against an aircraft explanation, but it does not establish an unknown vehicle. GEIPAN compared the stated directions, durations and apparent elevations with astronomical conditions. The later trails appeared from the direction of the Geminid meteor shower’s radiant, which was extremely low in the north eastern sky. From the witnesses’ viewing geometry, meteors coming from that low radiant could appear to travel almost horizontally rather than falling steeply downwards. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête28 Oct 2024 — [AERO] de BEDOUS (64) vers PAU (64) 14.12.2022. Direction Technique et Numérique. Direction Adj... The dates were also highly significant. The observation came only hours after the 2022 Geminids reached their peak. The reported lights lasted roughly two to five seconds, followed straight paths and produced an impression of burning material and sparks. Investigators calculated that the apparent speed of the first, larger phenomenon was compatible with the lower range of fireball velocities. Their preferred reconstruction was a grazing fireball followed by three smaller Geminid meteors. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête28 Oct 2024 — [AERO] de BEDOUS (64) vers PAU (64) 14.12.2022. Direction Technique et Numérique. Direction Adj... GEIPAN classified the case B—probably identified—rather than A because the explanation could not be proved beyond doubt. Automated meteor camera systems and the International Meteor Organization’s witness database did not yield a matching record. The first object’s apparently dark, oblong “head”, seen through night vision equipment, also left some uncertainty. Nevertheless, the convergence of timing, direction, duration and meteor like appearance made a meteor sequence substantially more likely than an aircraft or unconventional craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case is especially useful because it resists two simplistic interpretations. Experienced pilots are not immune to perceptual error, particularly when an object’s distance is unknown; but neither were their observations casually brushed aside. Their experience produced a detailed report that could be tested against radar information, geometry, weather, astronomy and known meteor activity. Why older sightings remain weaker Several Pyrénées Atlantiques entries sound intriguing until the quality of the surviving evidence is examined. GEIPAN lists a Biarritz report dated only to August 1951, a Bayonne observation from December 1978 and another Biarritz account assigned broadly to 2007. All are category C because the available information is insufficient for a reliable conclusion. A 2022 report from Arbus and a 2014 Guéthary case received the same treatment. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The 1951 Biarritz entry"},{"title":"GEIPAN Cases","url":"/geipan-cases/","description":"Official files from Pau, Bayonne, Biarritz and nearby valleys reveal repeated confusion involving aircraft, lanterns, stars, birds and weak records.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Cases What Do the Department's UFO Files Actually Show? Pyrenees Atlantiques UFOs GEIPAN Cases GEIPAN Cases What Do the Department's UFO Files Actually Show? Official files from Pau, Bayonne, Biarritz and nearby valleys reveal repeated confusion involving aircraft, lanterns, stars, birds and weak records. Official files from Pau, Bayonne, Biarritz and nearby valleys reveal repeated confusion involving aircraft, lanterns, stars, birds and weak records. dataset evidence The main GEIPAN cases by place and date Why stars, aircraft, lanterns and birds look strange How weak records differ from genuinely unexplained cases pyrenees atlantiques geipan cases Introduction The published GEIPAN files for Pyrénées Atlantiques show a department rich in unusual looking observations but poor in firm evidence for extraordinary objects. Across Pau, Bayonne, Biarritz, Orthez and the mountain valleys, investigators repeatedly encounter the same causes: aircraft seen from misleading angles, bright stars distorted near the horizon, lanterns drifting with the wind, birds crossing dark skies and reports too old or incomplete to test properly. A November 2025 export of GEIPAN’s public database contained 40 records assigned to the department. Fifteen were classified A, meaning an explanation was established with high confidence; thirteen were B, meaning probably explained; eleven were C, meaning there was not enough reliable information to decide; and only one retained the older D classification for an unexplained case. The departmental record is therefore dominated not by unresolved encounters but by identification and evidential failure. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr export cas pub 20251127093552.csv GEIPANFichiers CSV Cas... Pyrénées Atlantiques Aquitaine BIARRITZ (64) .08.1951. OBSERVATION DU DÉPLACEMENT SILENCIEUX AVEC CHANGEMENT... That distinction matters. A witness may have seen something genuinely striking, and may describe it honestly, without having observed an unknown craft. GEIPAN’s files are most useful when read as investigations into distance, perspective, memory and incomplete information rather than as a simple catalogue of “UFO sightings”. The main cases by place and date The files cover more than seventy years, but their value varies enormously. Some contain several statements, aviation checks or reconstructed sight lines. Others are little more than recollections submitted decades after the event. Several representative cases show how the classifications work in practice: Place and dateInitial impressionGEIPAN assessmentEvidential lessonBiarritz, August 1951A silent, disc like mass with green lit areas changed direction near the coastC: insufficient informationThe account was submitted in 1975, with no exact date and no independent documentationBayonne, 28 December 1978A light appeared to make irregular and angular movements for 20–30 minutesC: insufficient informationAircraft, satellites and perceptual movement were possible, but the old report could no longer be checkedUrepel, 2 February 1985A brilliant, colour changing object apparently beyond the mountain horizonA: ArcturusAstronomy software and reconstructed direction transformed an old “unexplained” caseBiarritz airport, 16 January 2001Controllers at Biarritz and San Sebastián saw a fixed, scintillating light over the seaA: CapellaProfessional witnesses and safety checks did not prevent a stellar misidentificationOrthez, 2 December 2008A large dark triangular form with green, red and white lights appeared low and nearly stationaryA: military transport aircraftGendarmerie enquiries and radar records identified a low flying TransallPau, 6 May 2012Three orange lights moved silently; one appeared to fallB: probable sky lanternsColour, duration, wind and apparent extinction fitted lantern behaviourBayonne, 24 August 2014Twelve sparkling orange red lights moved and fadedB: probable sky lanternsA summer weekend, tourist setting and gradual extinction favoured a festive releaseBayonne, 16 March 2019Seven pale discs crossed the sky silently in formationB: probable birdsSpeed, altitude, season, local wetlands and visible white plumage supported a flockArbus, 16 May 2022Four warm white lights appeared aligned and stationaryC: insufficient informationThe available geometry and supporting evidence were inadequate for a reliable conclusionBedous–Pau flight, 14 December 2022A helicopter crew saw several intense lights during a night training flightB: probable meteorsStrong witnesses and equipment improved the file, but did not remove ambiguity about distance and trajectory These cases are not equally significant. The 1951 Biarritz account is vivid but evidentially weak because its date was uncertain and the statement was recorded roughly 24 years later. By contrast, the Orthez case benefited from prompt gendarmerie work, several coherent witnesses and records of a planned low altitude military flight. One sounds more dramatic; the other is far more useful historically. The database also reveals how official conclusions may change. GEIPAN has revisited older files using modern astronomical software, mapping tools and a more explicit classification method. Urepel, once treated as unexplained, was reclassified after the reported direction closely matched Arcturus. GEIPAN also separated a second observation two nights later, judging that one probably involved an aircraft whose noise had been reduced by the wind. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUREPEL (64) 02.02.1985 GEIPAN UREPEL (64) 02.02.1985... Le GEIPAN classe ce cas en A: observation de Arcturus... Why ordinary lights become extraordinary objects The recurring explanations are not random. Each exploits a familiar weakness in night observation: the sky usually supplies no dependable scale, distance or fixed frame of reference. Stars that shimmer, move and change colour The Urepel and Biarritz airport files are especially instructive because the witnesses were not simply mistaking a normal looking star for an aircraft. They saw lights whose appearance had been altered by atmospheric conditions. At Urepel, Arcturus was low enough for its light to pass through a long and unstable path in the atmosphere. Turbulence can make a bright star scintillate, break into colours and appear to jump slightly. GEIPAN also considered an unusual refraction effect that could have lifted its apparent position above the local horizon. The close match in direction, plus a similar report by an independent group near Somport, supported the identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUREPEL (64) 02.02.1985 GEIPAN UREPEL (64) 02.02.1985... Le GEIPAN classe ce cas en A: observation de Arcturus... The January 2001 Biarritz case is even more revealing. Air traffic controllers at Biarritz and San Sebastián saw the same bright light and conducted appropriate checks for civil traffic, military activity and balloons. The absence of a confirmed radar target initially increased the mystery. A site reconstruction later showed that both viewing directions were effectively parallel, placing the source extremely far away. GEIPAN’s re examination identified the bright star Capella, affected by unusually strong scintillation. The lesson is not that controllers are unreliable. It is that aviation expertise is designed primarily to interpret aircraft, traffic patterns and instrument data. A star distorted by the atmosphere can fall outside those familiar patterns and therefore look more, not less, anomalous. 23:56 The longstanding mystery of BLACK TRIANGLE UFOs Channel: Sandboxx · Views: 749.7K · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 23 minutes Open on YouTube Aircraft that appear stationary or silent Aircraft are among the most persistent sources of reports because their apparent behaviour depends heavily on viewing geometry. A plane travelling nearly towards an observer can seem motionless for several minutes. Its landing lights may dominate the view while its body and navigation lights remain difficult to see. When it turns, the apparently fixed light can suddenly move sideways or disappear. The Orthez report shows how this can create an imposing shape. Witnesses described a large, dark triangular mass at low altitude with several coloured lights. Yet enquiries found that a C 160 Transall military transport was conducting a planned low level mission in the area, supported by radar information. The reported green, reddish and white lights were compatible with aircraft lighting, while the impression of hovering arose from its approach and relative movement. The case was classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Silence is less decisive than witnesses often assume. Wind direction, distance, terrain and background noise can suppress or delay engine sound. In the mountain Urepel file, GEIPAN considered that a north westerly wind could have carried aircraft noise away from the observers. Similar effects matter throughout the Béarn valleys, where ridges obstruct both"},{"title":"Pau Sightings","url":"/pau-sightings/","description":"The December 2022 Pau sightings show how skilled aviation witnesses can encounter something striking yet misjudge its distance and nature.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights Over Pyrenees Atlantiques Explained","level":2,"text":"Pau Sightings Were the Pau Helicopter Lights Really Meteors? Pyrenees Atlantiques UFOs Pau Sightings Pau Sightings Were the Pau Helicopter Lights Really Meteors? The December 2022 Pau sightings show how skilled aviation witnesses can encounter something striking yet misjudge its distance and nature. The December 2022 Pau sightings show how skilled aviation witnesses can encounter something striking yet misjudge its distance and nature. event time window What the helicopter crew and controller reported How radar, geometry and the Geminids were checked Why GEIPAN chose a probable meteor explanation pyrenees atlantiques pau sightings Introduction On the evening of 14 December 2022, a French Civil Security helicopter crew returning towards Pau reported four fast, luminous trails apparently travelling almost horizontally near the aircraft. An air traffic controller at Pau Pyrénées Airport then saw a similar brief light. The witnesses were experienced aviation professionals, the crew were using night vision binoculars, and radar and military traffic checks found no matching aircraft. Even so, France’s official UFO investigation unit, GEIPAN, concluded that the most likely explanation was natural: an initial grazing fireball followed by three meteors associated with the Geminid shower. The finding was not presented as absolute identification. It was classified B, meaning “probably identified”, because no photograph, camera detection or independently reconstructed meteor trajectory confirmed the explanation. The case matters within Pyrénées Atlantiques because it shows how a well observed event can remain visually baffling while its timing, direction, duration and appearance point towards an ordinary astronomical cause. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO] DE BEDOUS (64) VERS PAU (64) 14.12.2022 GEIPAN... What the helicopter crew and controller reported The helicopter left the Bedous area in the Aspe valley after a mountain training exercise and headed back towards Pau. At about 18:35, shortly after passing Oloron Sainte Marie, it was flying at roughly 2,000 feet. Visibility was reported as excellent, with the ground weather station at Pau recording no significant cloud and horizontal visibility of about 36 to 44 kilometres. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The pilot first noticed a luminous trail moving from east to west. Through night vision binoculars, he perceived a white trail attached to a large, blurred, dark oblong form. He judged the apparent object to be slightly above the helicopter and larger than an airliner, although no dependable distance was available from which to calculate its true size. It crossed the view in about four seconds and seemed to fade or “evaporate” west of Pau airport. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The pilot’s first thought was a military jet, partly because a military exercise had recently been taking place. He rejected that impression almost immediately because the light appeared to travel much faster than a fighter. Looking towards the north west, he then saw three more trails at intervals of roughly 30 seconds to one minute. These appeared to move from the north east towards the south west, each lasting only a few seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The onboard mechanic missed the first three sightings while occupied with other duties, but saw the fourth after lowering his own night vision equipment. He described a rounded luminous head extending into a trail, with an appearance resembling combustion. He considered it faster than a fighter aircraft but slower than the shooting stars he was accustomed to seeing during night flights. He did not report the large dark body described by the pilot during the first event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The pilot radioed Pau air traffic control to ask whether any fast aircraft were operating nearby. The controller knew of no military activity and saw no corresponding radar return. Shortly afterwards, the controller observed a separate light with the naked eye for two or three seconds towards the west or south west. The helicopter crew did not see that particular event, so it cannot be proved that all three witnesses watched the same meteor. Nevertheless, their reports established that several short lived luminous phenomena occurred in the same general period. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[AERO] DE BEDOUS (64) VERS PAU (64) 14.12.2022 GEIPAN... Why experienced witnesses still found the lights extraordinary The witnesses’ professional backgrounds make the report valuable, but they do not eliminate the central problem of night observation: a light against the sky usually provides no reliable scale or distance. Without a known object beside it, apparent altitude, size and proximity are largely inferred rather than directly perceived. That limitation explains the most dramatic element of the pilot’s account. A form judged “larger than an airliner” need not have been a large nearby object. A meteor hundreds of kilometres away can subtend a conspicuous angle in the sky, while a bright head, wake, flare or contrast effect may be interpreted as a solid body. The pilot later estimated the first phenomenon’s apparent length as five to ten centimetres when measured at arm’s length, but that angular measure could not by itself reveal its distance or physical size. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... Night vision binoculars added sensitivity but also removed useful visual cues. The pilot did not see the first four events with the naked eye. His equipment rendered the scene in monochrome shades ranging from pale green to near black, meaning that a bright meteor head, an uneven trail or local image saturation could produce contrasts unlike ordinary unaided vision. GEIPAN treated the reported dark oblong head as the main feature that did not fit the meteor explanation neatly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... Experience itself can also create a misleading comparison. Both crew members were familiar with brief, steeply sloping shooting stars. They therefore regarded the slower, near horizontal tracks as abnormal. GEIPAN’s response was that meteor appearance varies with speed and viewing geometry: a meteor travelling nearly across the observer’s line of sight, with its radiant close to the horizon, may follow a long, apparently level path rather than dropping visibly downwards. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube How radar, geometry and the Geminids were checked The investigation considered an aviation explanation before settling on meteors. The Gendarmerie des Transports Aériens obtained information from the National Air Operations Centre and examined available radar material. According to the official report, recorded aircraft tracks did not correlate with the sightings. Radio records also show that another control station suggested at the time that the light looked like a meteor or shooting star. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete697 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The absence of a radar return did not prove that the lights were meteors. Many objects or atmospheric effects may not appear on conventional air traffic radar. It did, however, weaken the crew’s initial hypothesis of a fast aircraft operating near Pau, especially when combined with the duration of only two to five seconds and the lack of navigation lights, engine noise or a continuing flight path. The astronomical timing was unusually favourable to the meteor hypothesis. The observation occurred on 14 December, the date of the 2022 Geminid maximum. The International Meteor Organization’s calendar placed the shower’s predicted peak at about 13:00 UTC that day, only several hours before the Pau sightings. It listed a theoretical zenithal hourly rate of up to 150 under ideal conditions. This figure is not the number a particular observer should expect to see; it is a standardised rate for a perfectly dark sky with the meteor radiant overhead. [imo.net] imo.net International Meteor Organization2022 Meteor Shower CalendarJuly 19, 2021 — Geminid (004 GEM) shower peak night December 13/14 has a wani... Published: July 19, 2021 For Pau at about 18:35 local time, GEIPAN calculated that the Geminid radiant—the point from which shower"},{"title":"Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist...","url":"/which-pyrenees-orientales-ufo-cases/","description":"Pyrénées-Orientales has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar encounter or military mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist... Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Pyrénées Orientales has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar encounter or military mystery. Pyrénées Orientales has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar encounter or military mystery. Its record is better understood as a series of mostly brief light sightings, concentrated around Perpignan and the department’s main roads, towns and mountain horizons. pyrenees orientales Introduction The department’s most useful cases show how UFO stories change when old testimony is checked against flight paths, astronomy, weather and witness conditions. The 1976 Thuir sighting remains the clearest officially unresolved example. By contrast, a supposed 1981 cluster was weakened when GEIPAN re examined it and found that three different events had been wrongly treated as one. Recent filmed lights over Perpignan have proved more identifiable, not less: a January 2025 report was classified as sky lanterns. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPANMarch 15, 2012 — THUIR (66) 25.03.1976... Le 25 mars 1976 vers 14h30 un automobiliste et ses pass... Published: March 15, 2012 The department’s UFO record in brief France’s official body for these reports is GEIPAN, part of the national space agency CNES. It collects witness statements submitted directly or through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities, then compares them with meteorological, astronomical, aeronautical and technical information. A phenomenon remains “unidentified” only when the available evidence does not support a sufficiently reliable explanation. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — Tout d'abord, le GEIPAN collecte les témoignages relatifs aux phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés (PAN), généra... GEIPAN’s classifications are important because they distinguish mystery from lack of data. Category A denotes an identified phenomenon; B means a likely explanation; C indicates that the information is insufficient; and D is reserved for cases that remain unidentified after investigation. Older D cases may now appear as D1 or D2, broadly reflecting differences in evidential strength and strangeness. Within Pyrénées Orientales, the published record contains examples across this range: Identified: an advertising balloon over Perpignan in June 2008; the International Space Station and space shuttle near Millas in March 2009; a star related photographic artefact at Saint André in August 2013; and sky lanterns over Perpignan in January 2025. [Geipan] geipan.fr PERPIGNAN (66) 23.06.2008Pyrénées Orientales. Classification. A. Date de mise a jour. 09/07/2019. Type de phénomène. Ballon publici... Probably explained: a fast moving light reported from Perpignan in February 2011 was considered a probable sighting of the International Space Station. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Weak or witness dependent: a dramatic 2015 Perpignan account was attributed by GEIPAN to seriously disturbed visual perception. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Still unresolved: the March 1976 observation near Thuir retains a D1 classification, meaning that it remains unexplained but does not constitute proof of an exotic vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPANMarch 15, 2012 — THUIR (66) 25.03.1976... Le 25 mars 1976 vers 14h30 un automobiliste et ses pass... Published: March 15, 2012 This pattern matters more than the raw number of reports. Pyrénées Orientales does not stand out in the official material as a securely demonstrated national hotspot. Its cases instead illustrate the ordinary mixture found throughout France: sincere witnesses, ambiguous night time lights, occasional poor documentation and a minority of reports that resist firm identification. Thuir, 1976: the strongest unresolved case On 25 March 1976, at about 2.30 pm, an automobile passenger and others travelling on the departmental road between Thuir and Camélas reported a luminous object moving from east to west. The gendarmerie record states that the witnesses were travelling on the CD48 after joining it from the CD615. GEIPAN still lists the incident as D1, describing it as a strange phenomenon supported by evidence of medium or stronger consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPANMarch 15, 2012 — THUIR (66) 25.03.1976... Le 25 mars 1976 vers 14h30 un automobiliste et ses pass... Published: March 15, 2012 The daytime setting gives the case more interest than a distant nocturnal point of light. Several occupants were reportedly present, and the observation was documented promptly enough to enter the official archive. It is therefore more substantial than an anonymous internet report or a recollection recorded many years later. Nevertheless, the evidential ceiling is low. No radar confirmation, photograph, physical trace or independently measured trajectory is attached to the publicly accessible summary. The description of a luminous “craft” reflects witness interpretation rather than a demonstrated structure. With no reliable distance, dimensions or speed, even apparently unusual motion cannot be reconstructed confidently. The D1 label should therefore be read narrowly: investigators did not establish a satisfactory conventional identification from the surviving file. It does not mean that GEIPAN confirmed an intelligently controlled vehicle, still less an extraterrestrial one. The case’s importance lies in being one of the department’s few durable official unknowns, while also showing why “unresolved” and “strong evidence” are not interchangeable. A separate 1976 report mentioned in the departmental archive involved a person said to have seen an object near a sports ground on the evening of 5 April. The gendarmerie learned of it through press coverage several days later, an indirect reporting route that makes the episode harder to assess. It should not be merged with the Thuir observation merely because the dates are close. [Google Docs] docs.google.com Docs GEIPAN Docs GEIPAN The 1981 cluster that became three ordinary cases For years, observations from Bourg Madame and the eastern Pyrenees in autumn 1981 appeared to form a particularly intriguing departmental cluster. GEIPAN’s later review substantially weakened that impression. The original file combined reports from Sainte Léocadie on 19 September, Corneilla de Conflent on 27 September and Vernet les Bains on 3 October. Because the three observations had been treated as manifestations of one phenomenon, the apparent geographical spread and repeated activity helped create a high level of strangeness. Reanalysis found that they were separate events of different types and should never have been grouped together. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE LEOCADIE (66) 19.09.1981 GEIPAN... At Sainte Léocadie, a witness stopped beside the road and later continued watching from home. Alternating red and white lights seemed stationary above the mountains before moving away. Investigators found errors in both the witness account and the original gendarmerie reconstruction, particularly concerning the observation point and the object’s relationship to the landscape. Once corrected, the appearance and movement were compatible with a helicopter operating over Spanish territory; available navigation information supported that interpretation. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE LEOCADIE (66) 19.09.1981 GEIPAN... This revision is one of the most instructive episodes in the department’s UFO history. Mountain horizons make angular position and distance difficult to judge, especially when an aircraft is seen across an international border. A light that appears to hover over a ridge may actually be travelling towards or away from the observer many kilometres beyond it. More importantly, the case demonstrates how a “flap” can be created by record keeping. Three reports occurring within a fortnight may look mutually reinforcing, yet that pattern disappears when their dates, locations, visual characteristics and plausible causes are examined separately. Later investigation did not strengthen the 1981 mystery; it dismantled the cluster on which much of its original force depended. 7:52 UFO Emits Weird White Material Onto Small French Town Close Encounters Channel: Quest TV · Views: 1.4M · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Perpignan’s dramatic 2015 account The most elaborate modern narrative in the official departmental archive occurred in Perpignan on 2 February 2015. A witness first described an orange red ball, then a very large silent black object with a rounded triangular front and rectangular windows. It allegedly approached slowly, hovered,"},{"title":"Cases Rechecked","url":"/cases-rechecked/","description":"Rechecking locations, flight paths and witness conditions turned several striking local reports into separate, ordinary events.","breadcrumb":"Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"Cases Rechecked How Pyrenees Orientales UFO Mysteries Were Reclassified Pyrenees Orientales UFOs Cases Rechecked Cases Rechecked How Pyrenees Orientales UFO Mysteries Were Reclassified Rechecking locations, flight paths and witness conditions turned several striking local reports into separate, ordinary events. Rechecking locations, flight paths and witness conditions turned several striking local reports into separate, ordinary events. dataset evidence The 1981 cluster that was really three events Aircraft, satellites, lanterns and mistaken visual cues What later reviews reveal about local UFO flaps pyrenees orientales cases rechecked Introduction Official review did not solve every UFO report in Pyrénées Orientales, but it dismantled the department’s most striking apparent cluster. Three sightings from September and October 1981 had originally been grouped under the name “Bourg Madame” and classified as a single unexplained case. When GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation unit, re examined the file decades later, it found three separate events involving different places, witnesses, descriptions and likely causes. One was probably a helicopter over Spain, another was compatible with a light moving at ground level, and the third was too poorly documented to distinguish confidently from ordinary aircraft. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINTE LEOCADIE (66) 19.09.1981 GEIPAN... That change matters because the original mystery depended heavily on aggregation. Once errors in maps, sightlines, distances and aviation checks were corrected, there was no longer a coherent regional wave requiring one extraordinary explanation. Other local cases involving satellites, spacecraft and sky lanterns reinforce the same lesson: several unusual looking lights seen within one department do not necessarily form a connected phenomenon. The 1981 cluster was really three events The original gendarmerie file brought together observations made on 19 September at Sainte Léocadie, 27 September at Corneilla de Conflent and 3 October at Vernet les Bains. They occurred within a fortnight in the mountainous south west of Pyrénées Orientales, and the witnesses’ statements were collected during the following weeks. Investigators found no additional witnesses, but the geographical and temporal proximity encouraged the cases to be treated as parts of one episode. The file was sent to GEPAN, GEIPAN’s predecessor, and initially received the unexplained Category D classification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... The later review began with a simple but decisive comparison. The Sainte Léocadie witness described several adjacent red and white lights that appeared stationary and then moved away. At Corneilla de Conflent, the report concerned a single dim red point with a halo, comparable in apparent size to a bright star. At Vernet les Bains, the witness described two rounded, reddish yellow objects following the same route about a minute apart. These were not matching accounts of one recognisable object. They were three differently described phenomena on three different nights. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... This distinction removed much of the apparent strangeness. A repeated regional phenomenon should normally display some continuity in appearance, motion, timing or circumstances. Here, the main continuity had been supplied by the paperwork: neighbouring communes, dates close together and a shared administrative file. GEIPAN explicitly concluded that treating the reports as one phenomenon had artificially increased their perceived significance. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... Maps and flight paths changed the Sainte Léocadie case The first report, made on 19 September 1981, concerned lights seen south of Sainte Léocadie while the witness was driving towards Bourg Madame. After stopping near a water tower, the witness watched several white lights with an alternating red light, apparently stationary above the mountains for roughly 15 minutes. The lights were later seen again from the witness’s home, moving slowly towards Spain. No solid body was observed, even through binoculars. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... The original geographical reconstruction contained important errors. The gendarmes had placed the witness near the Bourg Madame water tower rather than the one at Sainte Léocadie. The reported reference point, boundary marker 504, was also associated with the wrong mountain: it lies near Cime de Coma Morera, not on Puigmal. Modern terrain reconstruction further showed that the marker itself was hidden from the stated viewing position by intervening slopes. The old map could therefore not securely establish where the lights were in relation to the landscape. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... Once the viewpoint was corrected, the observation direction led across the Spanish border. The 1981 aviation enquiries had covered French traffic, including information associated with Perpignan and French air traffic control, but no equivalent request had been made to Spanish controllers. An aircraft operating over Spain could therefore be absent from the records originally used to reject a conventional explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... GEIPAN considered a helicopter the best fit. Alternating red and white lights were compatible with aircraft lighting, while hovering and slow movement suited a helicopter better than a fixed wing aeroplane. The witness’s failure to see extra structural detail through binoculars also suggested that the apparent “row” of lights was angularly very small and potentially distant. The review rated the helicopter hypothesis at 75 per cent and reclassified the case as Category B: probably identified. It did not claim to have found a particular flight or registration. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... The result is therefore a probable explanation, not a demonstrated identification. Its strength lies less in proving exactly which helicopter was present than in showing that the original exclusion of aviation was incomplete. The crucial airspace lay across a national border that had not been checked. A red “UFO” may have been moving on the ground The Corneilla de Conflent observation looked superficially aerial because the witness saw a dim red light moving beneath the higher mountain skyline. It travelled steadily for about five minutes, apparently from the direction of Villefranche de Conflent towards Sahorre. A faint red halo surrounded it, and no definite shape was visible. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... The reanalysis reconstructed the witness’s position and line of sight through village streets. The visible track covered only about 14 degrees and remained extremely low against the ridges. Weather information indicated that mist was beginning to form, offering a plausible reason for the reported halo even though the witness had not noticed fog locally. More importantly, nothing in the geometry required the light to be in the sky. It could have been on the nearer Saint Clément ridge or on a road farther away. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... GEIPAN calculated illustrative speeds for both possibilities. A light covering the reconstructed track in five minutes would move at roughly 5.2 kilometres per hour if it were on the nearer ridge—about walking speed—or about 16.8 kilometres per hour if it were on the more distant road. Either was compatible with a person carrying a light or a slow terrestrial vehicle. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... The agency did not classify the report as a positively identified car or pedestrian, because road traffic from 1981 could no longer be reconstructed. Instead it assigned Category C, meaning that the case was not usable for a firm aerospace determination. This is an important distinction: the report was removed from the unexplained aerial category because its own sightline allowed an ordinary ground based source. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Bourg Madame (66) 1981 version 2 AC JPA MPD.docx... Mountain country makes this kind of mistake particularly easy. A light seen against several overlapping ridges may appear suspended between them, while darkness removes the depth cues needed to decide whether it is hundreds of metres away or several kilometres distant. The Corneilla review did not prove that the witness"},{"title":"Thuir 1976","url":"/thuir-1976/","description":"The Thuir sighting remains officially unexplained, but missing measurements and physical evidence sharply limit what can be claimed.","breadcrumb":"Which Pyrenees Orientales UFO Cases Still Resist...","level":2,"text":"Thuir 1976 Why the Thuir UFO Case Remains Unresolved Pyrenees Orientales UFOs Thuir 1976 Thuir 1976 Why the Thuir UFO Case Remains Unresolved The Thuir sighting remains officially unexplained, but missing measurements and physical evidence sharply limit what can be claimed. The Thuir sighting remains officially unexplained, but missing measurements and physical evidence sharply limit what can be claimed. event time window What the witnesses reported on the road near Thuir What the official record supports Why unexplained does not mean extraterrestrial pyrenees orientales thuir 1976 Introduction The Thuir sighting of 25 March 1976 is the clearest surviving officially unresolved UFO case in Pyrénées Orientales, but it is not the department’s strongest proof of an extraordinary aircraft. Three people travelling by car reported a bright, oval or disc like form crossing a blue afternoon sky near Thuir. Two adults gave statements to the gendarmerie, and all three witnesses supplied broadly similar drawings. No photograph, radar return, physical trace or independent report confirmed the observation. A later review by France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, found the testimony sufficiently coherent and unusual to retain an unexplained classification. Yet the same review stressed that the location, angles, distance and apparent dimensions had not been recorded accurately enough to test ordinary possibilities such as an aircraft, helicopter or balloon. Thuir therefore matters less as evidence of extraterrestrial visitation than as a clear example of the boundary between a genuinely unresolved report and a scientifically strong case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPAN... What the witnesses reported on the road near Thuir The observation occurred at about 2.30 pm on 25 March 1976 while three people were travelling on a departmental road in the Thuir–Camélas area. According to GEIPAN’s summary, they saw a single luminous phenomenon moving from east to west. It appeared large, oval and topped by a dome, remained visible for roughly one minute and then disappeared abruptly. The sighting was therefore unusual in several respects: it happened in daylight, apparently showed a definite outline rather than merely a point of light, and lasted long enough for more than a momentary glimpse. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPAN... Two adult witnesses were formally interviewed by the gendarmerie. A third witness, a child, did not give a separate police deposition but produced a drawing. GEIPAN’s database records one adult witness as aged 70 and another as aged 40. One account describes the phenomenon as ovoïd, extremely fast and completely silent; the other records a rapid object of less easily categorised form. Both place the sighting at approximately the same time. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 T1 GEIPAN... The surviving sketches are among the case’s most memorable features. They show broadly similar saucer like outlines: a flattened or oval lower body with a rounded dome above it. The drawings are not identical, but GEIPAN judged them mutually coherent. They provide evidence that the witnesses shared a reasonably stable visual impression rather than reporting three entirely different things. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... That agreement must nevertheless be treated carefully. The witnesses occupied the same vehicle, belonged to the same family group and could have discussed the event before drawing it. Although the original record said the sketches were made separately, GEIPAN later noted that their true independence was difficult to verify. Similar drawings therefore strengthen the case more than a lone recollection would, but much less than matching reports from unrelated observers at different locations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... What the official record actually supports The strongest evidence is the existence of a prompt, formal gendarmerie file rather than a story recorded many years later. The adults committed their accounts to an official record, and the sketches were preserved. There is no obvious sign that the basic narrative was created through decades of retelling. The case was also circulated through official channels, including departmental, judicial, defence and air region authorities, showing that it was treated as a report requiring documentation rather than dismissed as local gossip. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com intl fr geipan thuir 1976 pv n1081 1976309236 1976, GEIPAN D1, multiple witnesses, Air... UFO or flying saucer was.p.4; The file was distributed on April 10 1976 to the Préfet des Py... GEIPAN’s 2011 reassessment identified several positive features: the observation took place in daylight against a blue sky; the witnesses described visible contours and a defined shape; two adults supplied formal statements; three sketches showed a broadly consistent configuration; the report contained enough apparent strangeness to resist an immediate, confident identification. These points distinguish Thuir from many weak night time reports involving a distant light with no visible structure. GEIPAN described the testimony as precise in some respects, although still lacking crucial detail. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The negative evidence is equally important. No unrelated witness reported the object, despite its claimed large appearance and daytime passage. There was no photograph, film, radar confirmation, aircrew report, ground trace or measurable effect on the vehicle or surroundings. GEIPAN’s public case summary explicitly states that no additional testimony was received. The entire case therefore rests on the recollections of people sharing one observation point. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTHUIR (66) 25.03.1976 GEIPAN... The records also preserve a puzzling psychological detail. One witness described a form of “inhibition” that apparently delayed discussion of the event for about 24 hours. GEIPAN called this surprising but did not treat it as proof of an external influence. Without contemporary medical evidence or a clearer description, it cannot reliably distinguish shock, uncertainty, reluctance to speak, later interpretation or any supposed physical effect. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The missing measurements that prevent a firm conclusion The central weakness is not that investigators found evidence against the witnesses. It is that the original inquiry did not secure enough geometry to reconstruct what they saw. The precise position of the car and phenomenon was not established adequately. Nor were dependable elevation angles, angular size, distance estimates, landmarks, timed bearings or a detailed trajectory preserved. GEIPAN concluded that the available statements could neither confirm nor sufficiently restrict ordinary hypotheses including an aeroplane, light aircraft, balloon or helicopter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This matters because descriptions such as “large”, “fast” and “low” are not independent measurements. An observer cannot determine an unfamiliar object’s real size without knowing its distance. A small nearby object and a large distant one can occupy the same apparent width in the sky. Speed presents the same difficulty: rapid angular movement may indicate a fast distant aircraft, a slower nearby object or the changing viewpoint of witnesses in a moving car. The road setting introduces further uncertainty. GEIPAN has documented other cases in which the combined motion of a witness’s car and an aircraft creates a misleading impression of hovering, sudden acceleration or an abnormal path. That does not prove an aircraft explanation at Thuir, but it shows why the vehicle’s speed, direction and changing sightlines should have been recorded precisely. Without them, investigators cannot reproduce the observation or determine how much of the reported movement belonged to the phenomenon and how much to the observers. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Daylight improves the opportunity to perceive shape, but it does not remove ambiguity. Glare can obscure wings, tails or surface detail. Reflected sunlight may make an aircraft fuselage or canopy appear luminous while less reflective structures become difficult to see. A partly obscured aircraft, balloon or other conventional object can consequently produce a compact outline unlike its familiar appearance. These remain possibilities rather than demonstrated explanations because the Thuir file contains too little positional and"},{"title":"Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious","url":"/why-rhones-ufo-mysteries-rarely-stay/","description":"Rhône has no single UFO incident that stands above all others as a well-documented, genuinely unexplained landmark.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious Rhône has no single UFO incident that stands above all others as a well documented, genuinely unexplained landmark. Rhône has no single UFO incident that stands above all others as a well documented, genuinely unexplained landmark. Its significance lies instead in a long sequence of reports from Lyon, its suburbs, the Monts du Lyonnais and the Beaujolais area, ranging from brief lights and silent objects to alleged triangles and unusual shapes. rhone Introduction That distinction is central to understanding Rhône’s UFO history. The department offers a useful picture of how ordinary observations become mysteries, how newspapers and local investigators keep them in public memory, and how later technical checks often reduce their apparent strangeness. It is a history of sincere witnesses and occasionally puzzling reports, but also of distance errors, ambiguous lights, delayed reporting and incomplete records. For historical continuity, “Rhône” here includes Lyon and neighbouring communes recorded under department number 69 in older and current UFO archives. Since 1 January 2015, the Lyon metropolitan authority and the Rhône departmental council have been separate territorial bodies, although official and popular uses of “Rhône” still commonly encompass the wider Lyon area. [Rhone] rhone.gouv.fr Elections metropolitaines 2020 Elections métropolitaines 2020 Elections locales Elections politiques Elections et citoyenneté Actions de l’État Les servi... From the 1954 wave to the first official records Rhône appears in accounts of France’s famous autumn 1954 “flying saucer” wave, when newspapers across the country published an extraordinary number of reports. One catalogue places a sighting in Lyon at about 3.55 am on 18 September: a lone witness allegedly saw an object rise into the sky at very high speed. The surviving account is extremely brief, lacks independent corroboration and appears to derive from later catalogues rather than a complete contemporary investigation. It is therefore historically interesting but evidentially weak. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 18, Lyon, RhôneAimé Michel indicates that on September 18, 1954 there was an observation of a fly... Published: September 18, 1954 The importance of 1954 is less that it established an exceptional Rhône case than that it created a national reporting climate. Unusual lights were now interpreted through the new language of “saucers”, while newspapers helped sightings in one region influence perceptions elsewhere. Historical research into French UFO culture shows that the period encouraged networks of amateur investigators, specialist publications and later organised study groups. Lyon also became part of that intellectual history through researchers and astronomers connected with the city and its universities, rather than through one decisive local encounter. [Interfas] interfas.univ tlse2.fr Interfas Histoire de l'ufologie en France Des premières recherches...1 Dec 2017 — Des premières recherches individuelles sur les soucoupes volantes à la constitution de réseaux d... More useful departmental documentation begins in the late 1970s, when police and gendarmerie reports entered the national archives later published by the French space agency’s UFO unit. At La Tour de Salvagny on 4 September 1977, two people reported a very bright white ball travelling rapidly and silently for several seconds. The gendarmerie found no physical evidence or confirming witnesses, and the case was classified as lacking sufficient reliable information. It remains unidentified only in the limited sense that the available record cannot support a firm conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOUR DE SALVAGNY (LA) (69) 04.09.1977TOUR DE SALVAGNY (LA) (69) 04.09.1977. Date d'observation. 04/09/1977. Région. Rhône Alpes. Dé... A year later, on 28 October 1978, witnesses around Mornant and Soucieu en Jarrest described a fast luminous object or ball with a trailing feature. The surviving official material records several witnesses and a gendarmerie statement, giving the episode more documentary substance than many newspaper only stories. Nevertheless, it was placed in category B, meaning that investigators considered an ordinary explanation probable even though the precise phenomenon was not firmly identified in the published summary. [Geipan] geipan.fr SOUCIEU EN JARREST (69) 28.10.1978 T3MORNANT (69) 28.10.1978; Date d'observation. 28/10/1978; Région. Rhône Alpes; Département... The shell shaped object near Saint Symphorien d’Ozon One of Rhône’s more memorable official cases occurred on 17 October 1980 near Saint Symphorien d’Ozon and Sérézin du Rhône, south of Lyon. Four witnesses reported an object whose shape was compared to a scallop shell. The sighting entered the record through a gendarmerie statement, which gives it greater value than an anonymous anecdote or a story reconstructed decades later. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Even so, the official classification is B rather than unexplained. This matters because the archive’s dramatic descriptive phrase can easily be detached from the investigators’ judgement. The published material does not establish an unknown craft, military response, radar contact or physical effect. It preserves what the witnesses said and indicates that the report was considered probably explicable, although the public summary does not specify the final identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A Lyon report from 5 May 1981 illustrates an even more serious credibility problem. A witness claimed to have seen a black sphere, perhaps three metres across, moving slowly several kilometres away before suddenly disappearing. The subsequent inquiry judged the witness insufficiently credible and expressed strong doubt that the reported event had occurred as described. Its inclusion in an official archive therefore does not authenticate the claim; it documents both the allegation and the investigators’ reservations. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases show why official records must be read beyond their titles. A file headed “observation of a UFO” may end as an explained case, a probable identification, an unusable report or a claim undermined by the inquiry itself. The COMETA Report The Government of France on UFOs Channel: Too Deep UFOs/ETs ? Open on YouTube Lyon’s aviation connection cuts both ways Lyon’s urban lights, busy airspace and nearby airports make aviation an unavoidable part of the department’s UFO story. Aircraft can generate convincing reports when their navigation lights are viewed head on, when engine noise is masked by distance or traffic, or when a helicopter’s searchlight is seen without a clear view of the airframe. An incident reported over Lyon on 27 August 1997 involved a low, apparently silent object directing a bright beam towards witnesses. That description sounds unusual in isolation. GEIPAN’s investigation, however, concluded that the witnesses had observed a helicopter. The case demonstrates how a searchlight can dominate perception while the aircraft carrying it remains difficult to see against the night sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A more striking example came on 15 May 2014, when a gendarmerie helicopter crew filmed a brilliant, scintillating object above Lyon for more than ten minutes. The light was visible to the naked eye for roughly two and a half hours and seemed noteworthy enough to be recorded by an airborne police camera. Investigators reconstructed its direction, duration and lack of movement and identified it with certainty as the star Antares. The involvement of trained personnel and sophisticated equipment did not prevent a celestial misidentification; it simply provided better data with which to resolve it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case is particularly important because professional status is sometimes treated as proof that a witness could not be mistaken. GEIPAN explicitly warns that education or occupational experience does not by itself validate an interpretation. Its method separates the sincerity of the observer from the accuracy of distance, movement and object identification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Triangles, orange lights and cases left open by missing data The Meyzieu sighting of 3 February 2009 is one of the better known unresolved Rhône reports. A pedestrian described several silent orange lights moving in a triangular arrangement. Sky lanterns were an immediate possibility because the sighting occurred soon after Chinese New Year, but available weather data suggested that the"},{"title":"Sky Misidentifications","url":"/sky-misidentifications/","description":"Aircraft lights, bright stars, lanterns and missing positional data explain why sincere witnesses can still report extraordinary objects.","breadcrumb":"Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Sky Misidentifications Why Ordinary Lights Look Extraordinary Over Lyon Rhone UFOs Sky Misidentifications Sky Misidentifications Why Ordinary Lights Look Extraordinary Over Lyon Aircraft lights, bright stars, lanterns and missing positional data explain why sincere witnesses can still report extraordinary objects. Aircraft lights, bright stars, lanterns and missing positional data explain why sincere witnesses can still report extraordinary objects. mechanism Helicopters, searchlights and busy urban airspace How Antares fooled trained airborne observers Lanterns, wind data and unresolved orange lights rhone sky misidentifications Introduction Lyon produces convincing UFO reports not because its skies are uniquely mysterious, but because they are unusually rich in ambiguous lights. Commercial aircraft approach Lyon Saint Exupéry, business jets and helicopters use Lyon Bron, stars and planets hang above the urban horizon, and lanterns drift across sightlines broken by roofs, hills and trees. At night, witnesses often see brightness and direction but cannot reliably judge distance, size or speed. That combination can make an ordinary light appear stationary, silent, triangular, rapidly accelerating or impossibly large. Rhône’s official case records repeatedly show sincere observers describing something extraordinary before positional checks, astronomy, weather data or flight information supply a more conventional explanation. The lesson is not that witnesses are foolish. It is that a point of light without a clear background gives the human visual system very little information from which to reconstruct a three dimensional event. How a UFO works Channel: WVW Open on YouTube Busy airspace hides the aircraft Lyon is served by two substantial aviation centres. Lyon Saint Exupéry handled 10.5 million passengers in 2024 and offered more than 130 direct destinations, while Lyon Bron is described by its operator as France’s third largest business aviation airport. Helicopters, private aircraft, airliners and airport related lighting therefore form a normal part of the wider city’s night environment. [Lyon Aéroport] corporate.lyonaeroports.com Lyon Aéroport Aéroports de Lyon Lyon Aéroport Lyon Aéroport Aéroports de Lyon Lyon Aéroport An approaching aircraft does not always look like an aircraft. Viewed nearly head on, its landing lights may dominate while its body, wingtip lights and apparent sideways movement remain invisible. It can consequently resemble a bright object hovering in one place. As the aircraft turns, descends or passes behind buildings, the light may suddenly dim, split into several points or seem to shoot away. Sound is also an unreliable guide: wind direction, urban noise, distance and the aircraft’s orientation can all delay or mask it. This becomes especially persuasive when the observer lacks a fixed horizon. A light seen between tower blocks or above a dark ridge cannot easily be assigned a distance. A small nearby helicopter and a much larger distant airliner may occupy a similar angle in the sky. Once the distance estimate is wrong, every other estimate follows it: apparent size, altitude and speed can all become dramatically inflated. Lyon Airport’s own public flight tracking service illustrates the information usually missing from an eyewitness account. Its CONSTAS tool can identify an aircraft’s type, altitude and trajectory after the event. Those are precisely the measurements that a witness normally cannot recover from sight alone. A report saying that a light “crossed the whole sky in seconds” is therefore much weaker than a timed observation with compass bearings, elevation angles and an exact location. [riverains.lyonaeroports.com] riverains.lyonaeroports.com Suivi des vols et des niveaux de bruit Lyon Aéroport Suivi des vols et des niveaux de bruit Lyon Aéroport Searchlights and reflected illumination add another layer. Beams directed at low cloud can produce bright patches that move, merge or vanish as the light source changes direction. Thin cloud may conceal the beam itself while leaving only the illuminated spot visible. From the ground, that detached patch can appear to be a luminous object moving above the cloud rather than light projected onto it. GEIPAN’s Rhône catalogue includes cases ultimately attributed to clouds, aircraft, contrails and other familiar phenomena, showing how often the reported “object” is an interpretation rather than a clearly observed solid craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casObservation aéronautique Observation maritime. Rhône Antares 31/08/2017 2014 05 15. Rhône Avion de ligne 06/11/2014... Published: May 15, 2014 How Antares fooled airborne observers The clearest Rhône example occurred above Lyon on 15 May 2014. At about 11 pm, gendarmes patrolling in a helicopter observed and recorded an intensely bright, strongly scintillating phenomenon. It was filmed for more than ten minutes with an onboard camera and remained visible to the naked eye for roughly two and a half hours. This was not a fleeting impression from an inexperienced passer by: it involved trained airborne personnel, sustained attention and video evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLYON (69) 15.05.2014 GEIPAN... Yet GEIPAN classified the case as a certain misidentification of Antares, the bright reddish star in the constellation Scorpius. Several features pointed in the same direction. The light remained fixed in the sky, its position matched the star’s expected location relative to the Moon, and the recording showed the intense twinkling typical of a bright star observed at low elevation through a thick and turbulent layer of atmosphere. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLYON (69) 15.05.2014 GEIPAN... The camera made the sighting look stranger rather than clearer. GEIPAN noted an unusual truncated, crown like image around the point of light. Such shapes can be produced when a bright point source is enlarged, defocused, digitally zoomed or distorted by the optical and electronic processing of a camera. The recording therefore established that a light was present, but did not prove that the apparent shape belonged to a structured object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLYON (69) 15.05.2014 GEIPAN... Antares also demonstrates why professional status does not eliminate perceptual error. Pilots and aircrew are trained observers within aviation tasks, but night vision still depends on limited visual cues. Aviation guidance describes the autokinetic effect, in which a stationary point of light against a dark or featureless background appears to wander. Without surrounding reference points, the eye cannot securely determine whether the source is moving or whether small eye movements are creating the impression of motion. [Skybrary] skybrary.aero In darkness or in a featureless environment there is no reference point, so the movement of the single point is undefined.... To prevent... Scintillation can supply the remaining illusion of activity. Near the horizon, starlight passes through more atmosphere than it does overhead. Changing air density bends and distorts the light, causing rapid variations in brightness and colour. To an observer expecting an aircraft or unknown machine, these variations can resemble rotating lights, pulsation or controlled signalling. A camera’s autofocus and exposure system may exaggerate them further. The case matters because it overturns two common assumptions: that a long observation must be stronger than a brief one, and that a video must be more reliable than memory. Duration helped investigators only because the light’s fixed position could be checked. The video was useful only after its artefacts were separated from the underlying point source. Before that analysis, both features made the report appear more impressive. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lanterns create silent formations Orange lights are among the most persuasive recurring reports around Lyon. They may move slowly without noise, maintain loose formations and disappear one after another. Those details can suggest a large silent craft or a coordinated group of objects. They are also characteristic of sky lanterns: lightweight paper envelopes lifted by a flame and carried by the wind. On 28 June 2013, a Lyon witness saw three orange points forming a triangle, followed by another larger triangle of three lights and then a single point. The lights travelled above buildings for several minutes without audible sound. The shape was compelling because the observer naturally connected the individual lights into larger objects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN’s reconstruction changed the interpretation. Although the witness believed the lights had moved from west to east, comparison of the video with the local scene indicated a track closer to north–south or north north"},{"title":"Landmark Cases","url":"/landmark-cases-d7cb04/","description":"The department's best-known sightings become more revealing when witness claims are read alongside gendarmerie files and GEIPAN classifications.","breadcrumb":"Why Rhone's UFO Mysteries Rarely Stay Mysterious","level":2,"text":"Landmark Cases Which Rhone UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? Rhone UFOs Landmark Cases Landmark Cases Which Rhone UFO Cases Still Resist Explanation? The department's best known sightings become more revealing when witness claims are read alongside gendarmerie files and GEIPAN classifications. The department's best known sightings become more revealing when witness claims are read alongside gendarmerie files and GEIPAN classifications. event time window From the 1954 wave to the first official files The shell shaped object and other memorable reports Why unidentified does not always mean unexplained rhone landmark cases Introduction Rhône’s best known UFO reports do not form a single, undisputed “classic case”. Instead, they show how very different observations can acquire the same mysterious label before investigation separates them into three broad groups: probably explained events, reports too poorly documented to assess, and historical stories whose surviving evidence is too thin for a modern verdict. The most memorable local descriptions include a brilliant silent ball, a fireball with a luminous trail, a dark shell shaped craft and formations of triangular lights. The official outcomes are less dramatic than the witness accounts. The French space agency’s investigation unit, GEIPAN, has interpreted several Rhône cases as probable atmospheric re entries, aircraft, lanterns, clouds, reflections or satellites. Other reports remain unidentified chiefly because essential information was missing or supplied too late. That distinction matters: in GEIPAN’s system, a case classified “C” for insufficient data is not equivalent to a genuinely unexplained “D” case supported by substantial evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN26 Feb 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évaluation de deux no... From the 1954 wave to the first official files Rhône entered modern flying saucer history during France’s great reporting wave of 1954. A frequently repeated catalogue entry places an observation in Lyon at about 3.55 am on 18 September, when a witness allegedly saw an object rise at very high speed. Another catalogue records a Lyon report on 30 July. These entries help establish that the city participated in the national wave, but the surviving online accounts are extremely brief and do not provide the detailed statements, independent witnesses, photographs or investigative paperwork needed for a strong evidential case. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, September 18, Lyon, Rhôneufo UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, September 18, Lyon, Rhône... The wider importance of 1954 was cultural as much as observational. Newspaper coverage across France supplied witnesses with a new vocabulary of saucers, cigars and mysterious aerial machines, while amateur investigators began creating catalogues and organised research networks. Historical scholarship on French UFO study traces the subsequent growth of associations, publications and investigators, including intellectual activity connected with Lyon. Rhône’s early reports therefore matter mainly as part of the reporting climate from which French ufology emerged, rather than as verified encounters that later investigation failed to explain. [Interfas] interfas.univ tlse2.fr Interfas Histoire de l'ufologie en France Des premières recherches...1 Dec 2017 — Des premières recherches individuelles sur les soucoupes volantes à la constitution de réseaux d... A more useful evidential period begins in the late 1970s, when gendarmerie statements and other official documents were incorporated into the records now published by GEIPAN, the National Centre for Space Studies unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena. These files do not automatically validate what witnesses believed they saw. Their value lies in preserving dates, directions, durations and testimony that can be compared with astronomical, meteorological and aviation information. La Tour de Salvagny, 4 September 1977 At about 11 pm, two people at La Tour de Salvagny saw a very bright white ball moving rapidly through the sky. It made no sound and disappeared into the distance after several seconds. The gendarmerie could find no physical evidence or corroborating witnesses, and the short duration left little opportunity to determine size, distance or true speed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOUR DE SALVAGNY (LA) (69) 04.09.1977TOUR DE SALVAGNY (LA) (69) 04.09.1977. Date d'observation. 04/09/1977. Région. Rhône Alpes. Dé... GEIPAN classifies the report as C , meaning that it cannot be analysed reliably because too much information is missing. The white ball has not been positively identified, but that does not make it a strong unexplained case. A distant light can appear extraordinarily fast when its distance is unknown, and silence offers little help when the source may have been high or far away. The official verdict is therefore evidential suspension, not confirmation of an anomalous craft. Mornant and Soucieu en Jarrest, 28 October 1978 The following year produced a better documented and more memorable event. At about 7.10 pm, several witnesses around Mornant and Soucieu en Jarrest reported a luminous ball travelling rapidly from east to west. It appeared to have a bright trail, followed an apparently horizontal path, made no audible sound and extinguished suddenly. A gendarmerie record and several witness accounts gave investigators more material than was available in the 1977 case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMORNANT (69) 28.10.1978Probable observation d'une rentrée atmosphérique.... Le 28 octobre 1978 vers 19h10 plusieurs témoins observ... GEIPAN’s verdict is B : a probable atmospheric re entry. A meteoroid, or possibly re entering material, can produce a bright fireball and trailing luminosity while appearing to cross a large part of the sky. Such objects are frequently perceived as travelling horizontally because observers see only a shallow section of their long trajectory. The lack of sound is also unsurprising; distant sonic effects may arrive much later or not be heard at all. This case illustrates why multiple witnesses do not necessarily establish an exotic event. Their accounts strengthen confidence that a real luminous phenomenon occurred, but the shared features also make comparison with known natural events easier. Here, corroboration strengthened the observation while weakening the extraordinary interpretation. The shell shaped object and other memorable reports Saint Symphorien d’Ozon, 17 October 1980 Rhône’s most visually distinctive official case occurred shortly after 11.15 pm near Saint Symphorien d’Ozon and Sérézin du Rhône. Several witnesses first noticed two powerful lights apparently approaching at an estimated altitude of about 200 metres. As the lights turned, they perceived a dark body shaped like a scallop shell, with two steady red lights at its sides. Unlike many silent light reports, this object was associated with a low, dull sound. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The unusual shape makes the incident memorable, but it also shows how perspective can transform a conventional aircraft. At night, bright forward facing lights may conceal most of an approaching vehicle. When it turns, navigation lights and a dark outline become visible, while the observer’s impression of the body is reconstructed from a few illuminated reference points. Estimates of altitude and dimensions made without a known distance are especially unreliable. GEIPAN classifies the case as B and considers it very likely that the witnesses saw an unidentified aircraft, possibly an airship. “Unidentified aircraft” here means that investigators did not establish a registration or exact model; it does not mean that the object behaved beyond known aviation. The lights, turning movement and audible engine like noise collectively favour an ordinary airborne vehicle over a silent or radically unconventional craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The file nevertheless retains some uncertainty. A suggested airship is not the same as a conclusively traced airship flight, and the surviving public summary does not name an operator. The responsible reading is therefore “probably conventional aircraft”, not “fully solved” and not “evidence of an unknown technology”. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Meyzieu, 3 February 2009 At Meyzieu, a witness reported several oval orange lights travelling silently in a triangular arrangement shortly after 7 pm. The immediate possibility was a release of sky lanterns associated with celebrations around Chinese New Year. Investigators noted a complication, however: the reported direction appeared to be"},{"title":"What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal","url":"/what-saone-et-loires-ufo-files-actually/","description":"Saône-et-Loire has a documented UFO history, but not an especially dramatic one.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal Saône et Loire has a documented UFO history, but not an especially dramatic one. Saône et Loire has a documented UFO history, but not an especially dramatic one. Its official record is dominated by sightings later attributed to the Moon, aircraft, satellites, lanterns, lasers and, more recently, Starlink formations. A smaller group could not be assessed because the evidence was inadequate. saone et loire Introduction This matters because the department offers a useful corrective to popular UFO storytelling. Witnesses often appear sincere and may describe something genuinely startling, yet sincerity does not establish that an extraordinary object was present. In Saône et Loire, later reconstruction has repeatedly shown how ordinary phenomena can seem close, structured or even responsive when seen at night without reliable distance cues. The department’s cases therefore tell a history not only of strange lights, but of how such reports are recorded, tested and sometimes radically reinterpreted. What the official record actually contains France has an unusually accessible official UFO archive. GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES, collects reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, examines possible explanations and publishes anonymised case material. Statements made to the gendarmerie may be forwarded to GEIPAN, giving some cases a documentary trail that includes witness interviews, police reports, diagrams and later technical analysis. [cnes.fr] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... Its classifications require careful reading: A means the phenomenon has been identified. B means an ordinary explanation is considered highly probable. C means the available information is too weak for a credible analysis. D , now sometimes divided into D1 and D2, means the phenomenon remains unexplained after investigation, with the stronger category reserved for cases combining high strangeness with substantial evidence. GEIPAN assesses both the residual strangeness of a report and its “consistency”: the quantity and reliability of the usable information. An unexplained label therefore does not mean alien, supernatural or physically anomalous. It means that the investigation did not produce a satisfactory identification from the evidence available. [Geipan] geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPANFebruary 26, 2021 — La classification du GEIPAN (A/B/C/D) s'appuie sur l'évalua... Published: February 26, 2021 The published Saône et Loire entries span towns and rural roads across the department, including Charolles, Louhans, Saint Usuge, Tournus, Toulon sur Arroux, Mâcon, Chagny, Vincelles, Paray le Monial, Autun, La Petite Verrière and Morey. The outcomes are varied but revealing: some reports have been solved confidently, some only probably, and several remain unassessable because descriptions, timings or corroborating material were insufficient. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Toulon sur Arroux: the department’s main unresolved case The strongest candidate for a landmark Saône et Loire incident occurred at about 6.45 am on 29 July 1987. Two people travelling by car reported seeing a large, bright white mass moving rapidly above a communal forest. They compared its colour to polished metal and estimated its length at roughly 20 metres. The observation lasted only a few seconds; no sound or smell was noticed, and the object disappeared below the visible horizon. GEIPAN’s published summary states that no explanation could be advanced and retains a D classification. [Geipan] geipan.fr TOULON SUR ARROUX (71) 1987 GEIPAN... Several features make the report more interesting than a routine light in the sky. There were two witnesses, the object was perceived against trees rather than in an empty sky, and its appearance was described as a substantial mass rather than a simple point of light. The early morning timing may also have provided some ambient light, potentially giving the observers more visual context than in a fully dark nocturnal sighting. Those strengths do not remove the central weaknesses. The encounter was extremely brief, apparently produced no photograph or instrument record, and contains no independently established distance. The quoted 20 metre length was therefore an impression, not a measurement. An aircraft, balloon, cloud fragment or other object can look very different when distance and scale are unknown, particularly when viewed from a moving vehicle near a wooded horizon. The case is consequently unresolved rather than confirmed as extraordinary. Its official entry supplies no indication of radar detection, military involvement, physical traces, effects on the vehicle or additional witnesses along the route. It survives because the reported appearance and movement were not matched satisfactorily to a known source, not because the record demonstrates exotic technology. [Geipan] geipan.fr TOULON SUR ARROUX (71) 1987 GEIPAN... Saint Usuge: a frightening encounter reinterpreted The Saint Usuge incident of 7 April 1980 is arguably the department’s most instructive case because it shows how a sincere, detailed and frightening encounter can later receive a conventional explanation. At about 2 am, two women driving near the River Seille reported a red orange light at apparently very low altitude. It seemed to follow the river, move close to the ground in a series of leaps, fall beside the road and then rise again as another car approached. Alarmed, the witnesses turned around and subsequently went to the gendarmerie. The experience reportedly lasted around ten minutes. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The report was originally treated as insufficiently explained. When GEIPAN re examined it with improved astronomical software and accumulated investigative experience, however, it found that the Moon was rising in the direction indicated by the witnesses. Its shape, colour, apparent size and duration were compatible with a low Moon seen through atmospheric haze. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The apparently intelligent movement was central to the mystery, yet it also supported the lunar explanation. A distant light viewed from a moving vehicle can seem to travel alongside the observer because its direction changes very little relative to the car. Trees, bends, slopes and roadside obstacles then make it appear to stop, jump or descend. When witnesses assume the light is nearby, these shifts can be interpreted as movements by an object within the landscape. GEIPAN explicitly did not question the witnesses’ honesty or basic credibility. Instead, it distinguished perception from interpretation: the women saw a real luminous object but misjudged its distance and behaviour under conditions of night driving, fatigue and rising fear. The case was reclassified as A, an identified observation of the rising Moon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This later finding substantially weakened the original UFO interpretation. It also demonstrates why an early official file should not be regarded as a final verdict. Old cases can change category when investigators gain better astronomical data, mapping tools or reconstruction methods. UFO e Difesa: a cosa dobbiamo prepararci? Analisi del Rapporto Cometa a cura di Wiki Brigades Channel: LA CASE Books. Open on YouTube Louhans and the late 1970s cluster Several reports from 1978 to 1980 give Saône et Loire the appearance of a modest local flap. Yet the official records do not reveal a single coherent phenomenon. At Charolles and Digoin in September 1978, several people observed a light travelling at very high altitude on successive evenings. Its steady speed, repeated route and regular north east to south west movement led investigators to identify it as an artificial satellite. The multiplicity of witnesses made the event better documented, but also made its orderly, repeatable behaviour easier to explain. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A wider Louhans area report on 9 October 1979 involved six listed witnesses in Louhans and nearby Bruailles. The surviving GEIPAN entry records a single object and includes a gendarmerie report, but its present classification is B: probably identified, although the public case page does not specify the retained conventional explanation. One witness described a technological or motor like sound, showing why the episode may have seemed more object like than a distant celestial light. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The Louhans file is a reminder that official labels can"},{"title":"Solved Cases","url":"/solved-cases-c46678/","description":"Cases blamed on the Moon, satellites and other ordinary sources show how distance, motion and fear can transform familiar sights.","breadcrumb":"What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Solved Cases How Ordinary Lights Became Saone et Loire UFOs Solved Cases Solved Cases How Ordinary Lights Became Saone et Loire UFOs Cases blamed on the Moon, satellites and other ordinary sources show how distance, motion and fear can transform familiar sights. Cases blamed on the Moon, satellites and other ordinary sources show how distance, motion and fear can transform familiar sights. entity The Saint Usuge Moon misidentification Satellites and the late 1970 s sighting cluster Why official classifications can change saone et loire solved cases Introduction Several of Saône et Loire’s most memorable UFO reports were ultimately explained not by secret aircraft or unknown technology, but by the Moon, artificial satellites, aircraft and other ordinary sources. The important point is not that the witnesses were dishonest or careless. In several cases they described what they saw clearly and reported genuine fear. The error arose when an unfamiliar viewing angle, darkness and uncertain distance turned a familiar light into an apparently nearby, moving object. The clearest example is the Saint Usuge incident of April 1980. Originally left without a satisfactory identification, it was later re examined and classified as a definite Moon misidentification. Other departmental cases show a similar progression: repeated lights became a satellite passage, a noisy low level object became a probable helicopter, and a recent luminous “bar” was matched to a newly launched Starlink formation. Together, these files show why an official UFO classification is a working conclusion rather than a permanent verdict. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT USUGE (71) 07.04.1980 GEIPAN... The Saint Usuge light that seemed to follow a car At about 2 am on 7 April 1980, two women travelling near Saint Usuge saw a red orange light apparently hovering at very low altitude near the River Seille. As they continued along departmental roads, it appeared to follow the watercourse and then move close to the ground in a series of jumps. The experience became frightening enough for the witnesses to retreat during part of the encounter. Their sincerity was not disputed, and the presence of two witnesses gave the report reasonable documentary strength. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT USUGE (71) 07.04.1980 GEIPAN... The case nevertheless illustrates a basic problem in night time observation: without a known object beside a light, the eye has little reliable information about its distance. A large, distant body can therefore be interpreted as a smaller object nearby. Movement creates an additional trap. When observers travel in a vehicle, a distant light may remain in roughly the same direction for a long time, creating the impression that it is keeping pace with them. GEIPAN’s later reconstruction found that the report closely matched the Moon rising near the horizon. Its colour, apparent size, shape, direction and prolonged visibility were all compatible with a low Moon. The supposed jumps and changes of course were interpreted as consequences of the car’s movement, interruptions in visibility and the witnesses’ changing perspective. The final impression that the light veered away was also consistent with the road changing direction while the Moon remained distant. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT USUGE (71) 07.04.1980 GEIPAN... This distinction matters. The investigation did not say that the witnesses had failed to see a light. It concluded that they had accurately perceived a bright celestial source but misinterpreted its location and behaviour. GEIPAN explicitly linked the escalating strangeness to night driving, tiredness, stress and panic rather than to dishonesty or defective eyesight. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT USUGE (71) 07.04.1980 GEIPAN... The administrative history is equally revealing. Saint Usuge had initially been placed in category C, meaning that the available information was considered insufficient for identification. On reassessment, investigators judged that classification unjustified and moved the case to category A: a phenomenon regarded as securely identified. It therefore changed not from “alien” to “ordinary”, but from an unresolved evidential file to a solved astronomical misidentification. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT USUGE (71) 07.04.1980 GEIPAN... Satellites in the 1978 reports A group of observations associated with Charolles and Digoin in September 1978 shows how repetition can strengthen an ordinary explanation rather than an extraordinary one. Witnesses reported a luminous object moving at high altitude at about 9 pm on successive evenings. It travelled steadily from north east to south west, maintained a regular speed and remained visible for a broadly consistent period. [Geipan] geipan.fr CHAROLLES (71) 07.09.1978 GEIPAN... Those features are unlike the erratic manoeuvres commonly emphasised in dramatic UFO accounts. They are precisely the details investigators need when checking an orbital source: the light followed a smooth, unbroken path; its speed appeared constant; it was seen high in the sky; it returned on more than one evening at a similar time; there was no reported engine noise or interaction with the ground. GEIPAN concluded that the witnesses had seen an artificial satellite and classified the case as A. The repeated observation was particularly useful because an orbiting object may return on a recognisable track, with its exact visibility governed by the observer’s position and whether sunlight still reaches the satellite after local sunset. [Geipan] geipan.fr CHAROLLES (71) 07.09.1978 GEIPAN... The case also demonstrates why the word “object” in a witness account can mislead. A point of light crossing the sky may be described as a craft because the observer naturally assumes that visible motion requires local propulsion. A satellite is moving extremely quickly in physical terms, but its great distance makes its angular progress across the sky appear slow and controlled. Without a visible body, wings or exhaust, the mind supplies an object around the light. Not every late 1970s report in the department had the same cause. Near Louhans and Bruailles on 9 October 1979, several people reported a bright object apparently flying at low altitude with a powerful noise that frightened witnesses and animals. One witness eventually recognised what appeared to be the rear lights of a helicopter. GEIPAN therefore treated the incident as a probable aircraft observation and classified it B rather than A, reflecting a persuasive but less conclusive identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr LOUHANS (71) 1979 GEIPAN... The comparison is useful. The 1978 satellite case was identified through regularity, altitude and repeated passage. The 1979 case was interpreted through noise, apparent low altitude and recognisable aviation lighting. Similar labels such as “bright object” concealed very different conventional causes. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Modern satellite trains recreate the same mystery Satellite misidentification did not end with the early space age. On 4 August 2024, two witnesses at Morey saw what they described as an enormous pale bar made of white bulbs. It travelled silently along a curved route from north west to north east, crossed the Great Bear constellation and disappeared after roughly one to one and a half minutes. Its apparent length, measured at arm’s length, made it seem more like a large structured object than separate distant points. [Geipan] geipan.fr MOREY (71) 04.08.2024 GEIPAN... GEIPAN matched the sighting to 23 Starlink satellites launched from Vandenberg earlier that day. Soon after launch, such satellites remain close together and can appear as a compact line or luminous bar before spreading into their operational orbits. They remain visible when they are illuminated by the Sun even though the ground beneath the observer is already dark. [Geipan] geipan.fr MOREY (71) 04.08.2024 GEIPAN... The identification rested on more than a general resemblance. Investigators reconstructed the train’s passage and found that: its timing matched the Morey observation; it entered from the reported north western direction; it crossed the same part of the sky near the Great Bear; its curved apparent path matched the witnesses’ account; it disappeared towards the north east as it entered the Earth’s shadow; observations from elsewhere in France and Switzerland recorded the same passage. The detailed report rated the satellite hypothesis very highly and noted that the duration shown in an independent Swiss video was consistent with the witnesses’ estimate. [Geipan] geipan.fr Microsoft Word Microsoft Word MOREY (71) 04.08.2024 CR FINAL.docx... One apparent objection was that neighbouring observers saw Starlink on the following night and thought it looked different. The investigation found that this did not weaken the identification."},{"title":"Toulon Case","url":"/toulon-case/","description":"The 1987 Toulon-sur-Arroux sighting remains unexplained, but its brief duration and uncertain scale sharply limit what can be claimed.","breadcrumb":"What Saone et Loire's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Toulon Case Why the Toulon sur Arroux Sighting Still Resists Explanation Saone et Loire Toulon Case Toulon Case Why the Toulon sur Arroux Sighting Still Resists Explanation The 1987 Toulon sur Arroux sighting remains unexplained, but its brief duration and uncertain scale sharply limit what can be claimed. The 1987 Toulon sur Arroux sighting remains unexplained, but its brief duration and uncertain scale sharply limit what can be claimed. event time window What the two witnesses reported Why the case remains officially unexplained What the missing evidence prevents US from knowing saone et loire toulon case Introduction The Toulon sur Arroux sighting of 29 July 1987 is the strongest unresolved case in Saône et Loire’s official UFO record, but “strongest” needs qualification. At about 6.45 am, two men travelling in the same car reported a large, brilliant white mass moving rapidly above woodland. They saw it for only a few seconds, heard no sound and watched it disappear below the visible horizon. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, still lists the case as category D: unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULON SUR ARROUX (71) 1987 GEIPAN... That classification makes Toulon sur Arroux important within the department, yet it does not establish that an extraordinary craft was present. The sighting rests entirely on a short visual encounter by two people sharing one viewpoint. No photograph, radar return, physical trace or independently positioned witness appears in the published file. The case resists explanation mainly because the reported object was unusual and the surviving information does not permit a reliable reconstruction. What the two witnesses reported GEIPAN dates the observation to 29 July 1987 at approximately 6.45 am. Its summary says that two witnesses in a car noticed a “voluminous and brilliant” white mass moving rapidly over a communal forest near Toulon sur Arroux. They compared its colour or surface appearance to polished metal and estimated its length at about 20 metres. The phenomenon remained visible for several seconds before disappearing beneath the horizon. Neither witness reported noise or an unusual smell. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULON SUR ARROUX (71) 1987 GEIPAN... The individual witness entries add only limited detail. Both witnesses were men, recorded as aged 20 and 40. Both described a single object in a rural setting, moving quickly and producing no sound. The older witness’s record specifies a white colour and broadly clear or daylight conditions; the younger witness’s weather field is left unknown. Neither public entry supplies a conventional geometric shape, instead placing the reported form in an uncategorised field. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSANVIGNES LES MINES (71) 29.07.1987 T1 GEIPAN... These points give the account a coherent core: two people reported seeing the phenomenon at the same time; both agreed that it was a single, fast moving object or mass; brightness, whiteness and silence were central features; the observation ended because the phenomenon passed out of sight, rather than fading while stationary. There is nevertheless an important distinction between two witnesses and two independent observations . The men were together in the same moving vehicle and viewed the event from essentially the same location. Their agreement supports the claim that both experienced a striking sight, but it does not provide the triangulation that witnesses separated by a known distance could have supplied. Because they shared the same viewing angle and surroundings, they were also exposed to the same possible visual ambiguity. The estimate of roughly 20 metres is particularly uncertain. An observer can determine an object’s physical length only when its distance is known or can be calculated. The sky ordinarily provides few dependable distance cues, especially when an unfamiliar object is seen against woodland or open horizon. GEIPAN’s modern questionnaires therefore ask witnesses to record apparent size —for example, a comparison measured at arm’s length—as well as estimated distance. Apparent angular size and actual physical size are not interchangeable. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire technique R282 Questionnaire technique 2025.0001275December 10, 2025 — 9 Oct 2025 — Votre témoignage est capital et unique. Taille apparente exp... Published: December 10, 2025 The reported 20 metre length should consequently be treated as an impression, not a measurement. A relatively small nearby object and a much larger distant object can occupy the same angle in a witness’s field of view. Without a recorded angular size, a securely identified foreground reference or a second observation point, the published evidence cannot establish which scale was involved. Why the case remains officially unexplained GEIPAN still classifies Toulon sur Arroux as D and describes it as a strange to very strange phenomenon with medium to strong consistency. The case page was updated on 23 June 2021 without an ordinary explanation replacing that classification. Its conclusion remains simply that no explanation could be advanced. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANTOULON SUR ARROUX (71) 1987 GEIPAN... Under GEIPAN’s system, classification depends on two broad measures: the residual strangeness of the phenomenon after investigation and the consistency, or usable quality, of the information. A D classification means that the available inquiry did not produce a sufficiently convincing identification. It is not a finding that the witnesses saw an extraterrestrial vehicle, an unknown technology or an object violating known physics. GEIPAN also permits old C and D cases to be revisited when new information becomes available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Since 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN.It is based on 2 main criteria: the weirdness and th... Several features explain why the original report appeared difficult to identify. The witnesses described something apparently large, fast and metallic looking at comparatively low apparent altitude. The absence of perceived engine noise seemed inconsistent with an ordinary aircraft close enough to appear 20 metres long. The object was also described as a bright mass rather than as a distant point of light, while its rapid passage gave the observers little time to inspect it. At the same time, none of those features is decisive. Silence does not prove that an object lacked an engine: perceived sound depends on true distance, wind, surrounding vehicle noise, flight direction and how long the observer waits after seeing it. A brilliant sunlit aircraft, balloon, cloud fragment, airborne material or atmospheric event can lose recognisable detail when viewed briefly against a bright morning sky. “Polished metal” may describe reflected light rather than a directly observed surface. A meteor or fireball is another general possibility for a bright, rapid and silent phenomenon, although the official summary does not endorse it. The reported appearance above trees and movement towards the horizon would not by itself distinguish an atmospheric object from something much farther away. However, the 20 metre size impression and description as a voluminous mass cannot be tested against that idea because the file provides no angular dimensions, trajectory bearings or detailed duration. Aircraft and helicopters likewise remain broad possibilities rather than demonstrated solutions. Critical reviews of French official UFO investigations have shown that conventional aircraft, including helicopters observed under unusual lighting or geometry, can be reported as surprisingly shaped, apparently silent objects. Those reviews also caution that a category D label may sometimes survive because mundane alternatives were not fully tested, rather than because every conventional explanation was conclusively eliminated. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical Inquirer There is no publicly documented match between the Toulon sur Arroux report and a particular flight, meteorological event or astronomical phenomenon. It would therefore be misleading to declare the case solved as an aircraft, meteor or optical effect. The defensible conclusion is narrower: several ordinary categories remain conceivable, but the surviving data are not detailed enough to select one confidently. Measuring the Sky using Angular Diameter and Separation Astronomy Masters Program Channel: Space, But Messier! Open on YouTube What the missing evidence prevents us from knowing The case’s greatest weakness is not an obvious contradiction in the testimony. It is the absence of measurements and corroborating records needed to turn an arresting recollection into a testable event. The public case material does not provide a precise observation point or road direction, although the linked testimony titles refer to Sanvignes les Mines"},{"title":"From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files","url":"/sarthe/","description":"Sarthe’s UFO history is not dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar chase or mass encounter.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files Sarthe’s UFO history is not dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar chase or mass encounter. Sarthe’s UFO history is not dominated by a single celebrated landing, radar chase or mass encounter. Instead, it offers a revealing mixture of sensational newspaper era stories, ordinary lights misidentified from moving vehicles, incomplete reports, and one comparatively strong unresolved case over Le Mans in 2010. sarthe Introduction The clearest conclusion is cautious. Several Sarthe sightings have been convincingly explained as the Moon, aircraft, balloons, stars, condensation trails or camera reflections. Others remain unassessable because too little reliable information survives. The 21 November 2010 triangular light report remains officially unexplained, but it rests on one witness and no photograph, radar return or independent confirmation. In Sarthe, “unexplained” generally means that the available evidence does not support a satisfactory identification—not that an extraordinary craft has been established. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... The 1954 wave reached Sarthe—but the record is fragile France experienced an extraordinary burst of flying saucer reporting in the autumn of 1954, with newspapers carrying numerous accounts of discs, cigars, luminous eggs and landed machines. Sarthe appeared within that national wave, particularly around Le Mans, Saint Jean d’Assé, Ballon, Lavenay and Arnage. Contemporary press summaries described blue or red lights, an “egg shaped” object and, in one striking story, a luminous cigar allegedly associated with the temporary failure of a milk lorry’s engine and headlights near Saint Jean d’Assé. [wikipedia.org] Wikipedia Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 The Saint Jean d’Assé report, dated 7 or 8 October depending on the retelling, is the most memorable. A milk collector named in surviving newspaper transcriptions as Alexandre Tremblais or Trembaix reportedly found that his lorry’s engine and lights had stopped. After leaving the cab, he saw a red and blue luminous “cigar” pass overhead; the vehicle supposedly functioned again after it disappeared. The story was published as part of a wider stream of reports in which stalled engines, paralysis and coloured beams had become familiar motifs. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Alexandre Tremblais, was driving his truck yesterday morning on a small road near Saint Jean d'Assé (Sarthe) when he suddenly... It would be a mistake, however, to treat this as a well documented mechanical effects case. The accessible record is mainly a chain of newspaper extracts and later catalogues. No surviving engineering inspection, original witness interview, police file, weather reconstruction or independently timed corroboration has emerged in the principal public sources. Even a modern specialist catalogue that preserves extensive 1954 material judges the Saint Jean d’Assé information insufficient and raises the possibility of an ordinary explanation. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Other Sarthe stories from the same wave are weaker still. Reports of “stars as large as the Moon” at Ballon, a “flying egg” at Lavenay, workers affected by a green beam east of Le Mans, and two dark red glows seen by an Arnage weather technician are historically interesting because they show how quickly varied observations acquired saucer like language. They do not form a coherent body of technical evidence. The descriptions differ sharply, timings are uncertain, and the accounts survive mostly through sensational press summaries rather than complete contemporary case files. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. Sarthe’s 1954 material is therefore best understood as part of a national media and reporting wave, not as proof of a local concentration of one identifiable phenomenon. The stories reveal what residents and journalists reported, but they rarely preserve enough information to determine what was physically present. The 1977 “following object” was the Moon A much better documented Sarthe incident occurred on 2 October 1977. Between about 9.50 pm and 10 pm, motorists in two different parts of the department reported an orange, rounded light that seemed to follow them. Witnesses from the areas around Challes, Volnay and Mansigné made statements to the gendarmerie, giving the case a stronger documentary foundation than most of the 1954 newspaper reports. [Geipan] geipan.fr SARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN... At first, the former French official investigation service SEPRA classified the incident as category C because there was not enough information to identify it. Decades later, an outside researcher proposed an astronomical explanation, which the French space agency’s GEIPAN unit re examined and accepted. The object was the Moon. The case is now classified A, meaning identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr SARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN... The apparent pursuit is a familiar perceptual effect. When a very distant light is viewed from a moving car, its direction changes little relative to the observer. Nearby trees, houses and bends in the road move rapidly across the visual field, while the distant object appears to maintain its position and can therefore seem to be keeping pace with the vehicle. GEIPAN describes the Sarthe report as a typical “following ball” case and notes that the witnesses’ orange, flattened disc closely resembled the Moon under the conditions reported. [Geipan] geipan.fr SARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN... This case is important because the witnesses were not necessarily careless or dishonest. Two independent groups reported broadly compatible impressions, and they took the matter seriously enough to contact the authorities. Yet multiple sincere witnesses can share the same mistaken interpretation when they observe the same astronomical object from moving vehicles. The later identification strengthened the evidence that something real was seen while weakening the claim that it was an unusual aircraft. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The unresolved triangle over Le Mans Sarthe’s strongest officially unresolved case occurred in Le Mans on 21 November 2010. At approximately 5.55 pm, a resident opened a ninth floor kitchen window and noticed three lights—one red and two blue—approaching through cloudy conditions from the east. The lights appeared to define a darker triangular shape. As it passed over the building, the witness reported hearing a rustling or displacement of air. The observation lasted about ten to fifteen seconds. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... GEIPAN considered the report relatively consistent because the witness completed a detailed questionnaire and investigators conducted a site inquiry. The official assessment gave the case comparatively high residual strangeness: the described formation did not closely match the common astronomical, balloon or lantern misidentifications encountered in routine investigations. The phenomenon’s movement nevertheless remained within the general capabilities of present day flying objects; the case did not involve impossible acceleration, disappearance on radar or independently measured performance. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... The central weakness is that there was only one witness. No photograph or video was taken, no second observer was found, and the published summary mentions no radar, air traffic control or physical evidence. Estimates of size, distance and speed are especially uncertain when an unfamiliar object is seen briefly against cloud without a known scale reference. A small nearby object and a larger distant aircraft can produce similar angular impressions. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... Possible conventional explanations include an aircraft seen from an unusual angle, several lights whose apparent alignment created an illusory outline, or a less common civil or recreational flying object. Le Mans Arnage airport lies south of the city and supports business, private and training aviation, so aircraft activity belongs among the normal possibilities in the wider area. However, the published investigation did not establish a specific flight, aircraft type or other source matching the witness’s account. [Le Mans Airport] lemans.aeroport.fr Open source on aeroport.fr. The official page, updated in June 2026, displays the broad classification D and describes the case in its text as D1—an unexplained phenomenon of moderate or strong evidential consistency. That"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-1f2f16/","description":"Sarthe's dramatic 1954 reports reveal how press coverage, memory and missing records shaped the department's early UFO history.","breadcrumb":"From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave How Sarthe Joined France's 1954 UFO Wave Sarthe UFOs 1954 Wave 1954 Wave How Sarthe Joined France's 1954 UFO Wave Sarthe's dramatic 1954 reports reveal how press coverage, memory and missing records shaped the department's early UFO history. Sarthe's dramatic 1954 reports reveal how press coverage, memory and missing records shaped the department's early UFO history. event time window The Saint Jean d'Asse lorry story Other reports around Le Mans and rural Sarthe What survives in press archives and what does not sarthe 1954 wave Introduction Sarthe joined France’s great flying saucer wave in the autumn of 1954 through a cluster of vivid but poorly preserved newspaper reports. The best known concerned a milk lorry driver near Saint Jean d’Assé whose engine and headlights allegedly failed before a brilliantly coloured, cigar shaped object crossed the sky. Other stories mentioned an egg shaped light at Lavenay, enormous star like objects at Ballon, red glows over the Arnage weather station and a supposed green ray near Le Mans. These accounts matter because they mark the beginning of Sarthe’s recorded UFO history, but they do not amount to a securely documented local invasion or even a clearly defined sequence of related events. Most survive as brief press items copied between newspapers and later catalogues. Dates, names and details changed as the stories were retold, while police statements, technical examinations and full witness interviews are generally absent. Sarthe’s 1954 wave is therefore most useful as a study of how observation, newspaper language and later memory combined to create a durable UFO narrative. The Saint Jean d’Assé lorry story The central Sarthe report was published by France Soir on 10 October 1954. According to the paper, Alexandre Tremblais was driving a lorry on a minor road near Saint Jean d’Assé when its engine stopped and its headlights went out. After he left the cab, he reportedly saw a luminous object described as cigar shaped, with intense blue and red colouring, pass overhead. The vehicle was then said to operate normally again. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Alexandre Tremblais, was driving his truck yesterday morning on a small road near Saint Jean d'Assé (Sarthe) when he suddenly noticed tha... A separate press version identified him as a milk collector and placed the occurrence at about 6.20 in the morning on Friday, 8 October. It said the lorry had stopped “for an unknown reason” and that its lights had failed, but the surviving newspaper summaries do not establish whether the mechanical trouble was investigated or merely assumed to be connected with the light. The account was rapidly reproduced in other newspapers, including La Croix and Le Courrier de Saône et Loire . [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Alexandre Tremblais, was driving his truck on a small road near Saint Jean d'Assé (Sarthe) when he suddenly noticed that his vehicle's en... The report has several features that made it memorable. It combined a lone traveller, a deserted rural road, vehicle interference and an unusually coloured aerial object. Such elements gave the story more dramatic weight than an ordinary distant light. They also fitted motifs already appearing in the national wave, where newspapers were publishing accounts of stalled engines, beams, landed discs, cigar shaped machines and witnesses temporarily unable to move. Yet almost every important evidential question remains unanswered. The accessible sources provide no repair record for the lorry, no statement from a mechanic, no precise observation duration, no measured direction or elevation, and no independent witness who saw both the object and the vehicle failure. There is no publicly available gendarmerie file confirming that officers inspected the scene or interviewed Tremblais promptly. Even the witness’s surname became unstable. Most versions call him Alexandre Tremblais, while some later transcriptions use Trembaix. That may be a simple printing or copying error, but it illustrates the fragility of a story known chiefly through repeated press extracts rather than an original signed deposition. [ufology.patrickgross.org] ufology.patrickgross.org Alexandre Trembaix, milk collector, said that the truck he was driving had suddenly stopped, for an unknown reason yesterday morning on a... The date is similarly troublesome. Contemporary wording points to 8 October because the report of 10 October described the event as occurring on the previous Friday. Later catalogues often place it on 7 October, apparently grouping it with the heavy concentration of reports published around that date. The distinction matters: once a case is shifted by a day, it can be made to appear part of a tighter geographical or chronological pattern than the original reporting supports. A modern specialist catalogue assesses the Saint Jean d’Assé sighting as probably attributable to a meteor, although that conclusion is necessarily tentative because the source material is so limited. A bright meteor could account for a fast luminous object, changing colours and a brief spectacle before dawn. It would not, by itself, explain a genuine simultaneous electrical and engine failure. The difficulty is that the mechanical effect was never documented well enough to establish that there was anything unusual to explain. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org 06:20 a.m.... 7 Saint Jean d'Assé, Sarthe. 06:20 a.m.... 7 Lavenay, Sarthe. 06:05 a.m. 1(x) DD ?... (4+) ?... The most defensible verdict is therefore not that the incident was solved, nor that a craft disabled the lorry. It is that a dramatic newspaper claim survives without the records needed to test its most important feature. Other reports around Le Mans and rural Sarthe The lorry story did not appear alone. During the same burst of coverage, France Soir printed a compact list of reports from Sarthe and elsewhere. Its phrasing was strikingly compressed: large star like objects at Ballon, a flying egg at Lavenay, two dark red glows observed by a weather technician at Arnage, and a green “petrifying ray” that supposedly affected Renault workers near the Le Mans–Paris road. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. These items are often presented together in later UFO catalogues, but their evidential quality varies and there is no clear proof that they represented one physical phenomenon. Arnage offers the strongest observational detail. A technician at the Le Mans–Arnage weather station reportedly observed two powerful dark red lights near the zenith at 6.08 in the morning. They were described as very high, silent and moving without a visible smoke trail. The observer intended to prepare a report and reportedly avoided claiming that the lights were necessarily flying saucers, saying that specialists would have to determine their nature. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. That measured language is important. It shows that at least one Sarthe witness distinguished between seeing something unfamiliar and identifying it as an extraordinary craft. However, the promised meteorological report has not become readily accessible in the main public case collections. Nor is there a surviving radar confirmation. Without duration, trajectory, weather data or astronomical reconstruction, aircraft, meteors and other distant lights remain possible. Lavenay is much more weakly documented. The newspaper reported only that Maurice Burge and several witnesses had seen a “flying egg”. It supplied no date, time, direction, duration or description of movement. Ufologist Aimé Michel later assigned the sighting to shortly after six in the morning on 7 October, although the specialist reconstruction notes that his stated source was the same newspaper item that had omitted those details. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. This is a clear example of information becoming more precise in retrospect without an obvious new primary source. Later readers may encounter a confident date and time and assume that they came from a witness interview. The surviving chain instead suggests that those details entered the literature during later cataloguing. Ballon survives largely as a phrase. Two people were said to have seen “stars as large as the Moon”. The words convey brightness or apparent size but do not tell us whether the lights moved, how long they remained visible or where they appeared in relation to the horizon. The same modern catalogue that treats some neighbouring reports as possible meteors labels the Ballon information wholly insufficient. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org 06:20 a.m.... 7 Saint Jean d'Assé, Sarthe. 06:20 a.m.... 7 Lavenay, Sarthe. 06:05 a.m. 1(x) DD ?... (4+) ?... The green ray story near Le Mans is the most sensational and least secure. France Soir claimed that Renault workers had been pinned to the ground by a green ray beside a"},{"title":"Official Cases","url":"/official-cases/","description":"GEIPAN's Sarthe files show how sightings can move from mystery to identification, weak evidence or continued uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"From Saucer Headlines to Official UFO Files","level":2,"text":"Official Cases Why Some Sarthe UFO Cases Stay Unexplained Sarthe UFOs Official Cases Official Cases Why Some Sarthe UFO Cases Stay Unexplained GEIPAN's Sarthe files show how sightings can move from mystery to identification, weak evidence or continued uncertainty. GEIPAN's Sarthe files show how sightings can move from mystery to identification, weak evidence or continued uncertainty. dataset evidence The unresolved 2010 triangle over Le Mans How the 1977 following light became the Moon What identified and category C cases reveal sarthe official cases Introduction France’s official GEIPAN archive shows that a Sarthe sighting can end in three very different ways: it may be identified, judged probably explained, or left unresolved because either the phenomenon remains genuinely puzzling or the evidence is too weak to test properly. The distinction matters. An official “unexplained” classification does not mean that an extraordinary craft has been proved, while a case placed in category C is not evidence of a deep mystery—it usually means that reliable information is missing. The most important Sarthe comparison is between the triangular light report over Le Mans on 21 November 2010 and the orange light reported by motorists on 2 October 1977. The first remains officially unexplained after a detailed witness questionnaire and field investigation, although it rests on a single brief observation. The second once lacked an answer but was later identified as the Moon. More recent files involving balloons, lasers, stars, photographic reflections and inadequate data show how GEIPAN separates unusual experiences from strong evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... The unresolved 2010 triangle over Le Mans At about 5.55 pm on 21 November 2010, a resident on the ninth floor of a building in Le Mans opened a kitchen window and noticed lights approaching from the east through a cloudy sky. The witness described three lights—one red and two blue—arranged around what appeared to be a dark triangular form. As it passed over the building, the witness also reported hearing a sound resembling air being disturbed. The entire event lasted only ten to fifteen seconds. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... GEIPAN regarded the report as unusually well described for a single witness case. The questionnaire was completed in detail, and an investigation at the location supported the basic geometry and viewing circumstances given by the witness. The reported combination of coloured lights, a dark triangular area and a close overhead passage did not closely match the common confusions routinely found in the archive. GEIPAN assigned an “strangeness” score of 0.62 and retained the case as category D, more specifically D1: an unexplained, relatively strange event supported by evidence of moderate rather than exceptional strength. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... That classification is important, but it needs careful interpretation. GEIPAN defines a D1 case as strange yet supported only by moderate consistency—for example, a single testimony without a photograph, video, physical trace or independent witness. D2 is reserved for stronger cases involving features such as several independent observers, instrumental records or physical effects. The Le Mans report therefore survives official scrutiny because no satisfactory identification was found, not because its description was independently proved. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... Several weaknesses prevent a stronger conclusion: Only one person reported the event. No second observer confirmed the lights, shape, route or sound. There was no photograph or video. The triangular outline depends on visual perception under cloudy, low light conditions. No published radar or air traffic confirmation supports the account. Nothing in the accessible case record establishes an independently tracked object. The observation was very brief. Ten to fifteen seconds allowed little opportunity to judge distance, altitude, scale or the relationship between separate lights. Its motion was not aerodynamically impossible. GEIPAN noted that, despite the unfamiliar appearance, the reported movement remained compatible with the general dynamics of present day flying objects. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... A conventional aircraft cannot simply be declared the answer. The witness’s description did not match an ordinary aircraft closely enough for GEIPAN to adopt that explanation, and the available record does not identify a particular flight. Yet the case also lacks the independent evidence needed to demonstrate that three lights really marked the corners of one solid object. At night, separate lights can be mentally grouped into a shape, especially when the space between them appears darker than the surrounding cloud. That remains a possible perceptual mechanism rather than a demonstrated solution. The 2010 triangle is therefore Sarthe’s clearest example of a legitimately unresolved official case with limited evidential reach. It is stronger than a vague light reported long afterwards, but much weaker than a multi witness event supported by imagery, radar or a trace. Later review had not supplied a conventional identification by GEIPAN’s June 2026 update, so the case remains D. [Geipan] geipan.fr MANS (LE) (72) 21.11.2010 GEIPAN... How the 1977 following light became the Moon The Sarthe case of 2 October 1977 shows that an unexplained file can change when someone reconstructs the sky rather than concentrating only on the witnesses’ impression of movement. Between roughly 9.50 pm and 10 pm, motorists in two separate parts of the department reported a rounded orange light, slightly flattened at the top, which seemed to accompany or follow their vehicles. Statements were made to the gendarmerie, giving the file more documentary substance than a single informal recollection. [Geipan] geipan.fr GeipanSARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN The case was initially classified C by SEPRA, an earlier CNES unit, because the available analysis had not produced an answer. In 2013, however, a member of the public submitted an astronomical explanation to GEIPAN. After examining it, the agency accepted that the object was the Moon and reclassified the case as A—identified without ambiguity. [Geipan] geipan.fr GeipanSARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN The key was the familiar “following Moon” illusion. A nearby tree, house or roadside sign changes position rapidly as a car moves, but a distant celestial body remains in almost the same direction. With few visual reference points at night, the observer may interpret that stable bearing as the light keeping pace with the vehicle. Bends in the road, intermittent obstruction and changes in the foreground can make the apparent pursuit more convincing. The Sarthe report was particularly suitable for this explanation because the observed characteristics were not highly exotic. The light was round, orange and astronomically positioned; its apparent behaviour was typical of a very distant object viewed from moving vehicles. GEIPAN considered the testimony consistent—including reports from two independent groups—but judged the phenomenon itself only weakly strange because it closely resembled the Moon. [Geipan] geipan.fr GeipanSARTHE (72) 02.10.1977 GEIPAN This case corrects two common misunderstandings. First, multiple witnesses do not automatically rule out misidentification: independent observers can encounter the same Moon, planet, aircraft or atmospheric effect. Second, an initial failure to explain a report is not permanent evidence against ordinary causes. Old cases may be reclassified when better astronomical software, mapping tools, flight data or a more precise reconstruction becomes available. GEIPAN explicitly states that classifications other than a firmly identified A case can change when new information or further investigation supplies a validated explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... The 1977 file is therefore more informative than a simple “debunking”. It shows that good faith witnesses can accurately report colour, direction and apparent motion while still drawing the wrong conclusion about distance. The experience of being followed was real; the movement attributed to the light was not. 23:56 The longstanding mystery of BLACK TRIANGLE UFOs Channel: Sandboxx · Views: 749.7K · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 23 minutes Open on YouTube What identified and category C cases reveal GEIPAN’s Sarthe listings contain a broad mixture of category A, B, C and D outcomes. Category A means that the phenomenon has been clearly identified. Category B means that the proposed"},{"title":"Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade","url":"/why-savoies-ufo-mysteries-usually-fade/","description":"Savoie has a genuine UFO record, but not a single landmark incident comparable with France’s best-known close-encounter cases.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade Savoie has a genuine UFO record, but not a single landmark incident comparable with France’s best known close encounter cases. Savoie has a genuine UFO record, but not a single landmark incident comparable with France’s best known close encounter cases. Its history is instead a collection of mountain lights, distant objects, photographs and short lived local scares. savoie Introduction This makes Savoie useful as a case study in how UFO stories develop. Alpine terrain can hide an object’s true horizon, scale and direction; tourist activity supplies lanterns and paragliders; and bright objects seen against dark ridges may appear nearer, larger or faster than they really are. Official records show active investigation, but they do not currently establish a persistent military, radar or unexplained aerial mystery centred on the department. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997CHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997. Date d'observation. 10/04/1997. Région. Rhône Alpes. Département. Savoie. Classif... What Savoie’s official record actually contains France has an unusual advantage for studying local UFO history: the National Centre for Space Studies, CNES, maintains GEIPAN, a public unit that collects, investigates and archives reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Reports may arrive directly from witnesses or through the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities. GEIPAN stresses that it is not an organisation devoted to proving extraterrestrial visits; its task is to identify ordinary causes where possible and to describe the remaining uncertainty when it cannot. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr or advanced (or futuristic) technologies.Read more... Its classification system matters. Category A indicates an identified phenomenon; B means a likely identification without absolute proof; C means that the information is too weak for a dependable conclusion; and D is reserved for a sufficiently documented event that remains strange after investigation. Since 2008, GEIPAN has refined the last category into D1 and D2, while judging reports through two main measures: how strange the event remains after analysis and how consistent the supporting information is. A category C report is therefore not a stronger mystery than a category B case. It is often simply a poorer file. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The searchable Savoie material is dominated by A, B and C cases rather than a clear run of robust D cases. Examples include an old report from Le Bourget du Lac in February 1964, Pralognan la Vanoise in February 1985 and Le Châtelard in October 2017, all retained as category C because reliable information was insufficient. These entries have historical value, but their unresolved status should not be mistaken for evidence of unusual technology. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANCHATELARD (LE) (73) 05.10.2017 Classification: C Department: Savoie Date of update: 11/10/2018... Chambéry: several mysteries, several ordinary answers Chambéry provides the clearest miniature history of the Savoie phenomenon. Reports have attracted press attention, but later information has repeatedly weakened their more exotic interpretations. On 10 April 1997, an observation over Chambéry entered the official archive. GEIPAN now classifies it A and identifies the phenomenon as a distress flare. The file has high evidential consistency and almost no residual strangeness, showing that an initially puzzling light can be resolved when documentation and contextual checks are strong enough. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997CHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997. Date d'observation. 10/04/1997. Région. Rhône Alpes. Département. Savoie. Classif... A more public episode occurred on the night of 28–29 July 2012. A Chambéry resident described strange stationary points and red orange lights arranged in a V before apparently disappearing behind the mountains. Local coverage encouraged further reports of discs, circles and luminous rings. Within days, however, readers and witnesses supplied the missing context: the lights were linked to airborne lanterns. The sequence is instructive because later reporting did not merely add sceptical speculation; it produced a specific local explanation that reduced the original claim’s strength. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? Another local report in November 2013 concerned a brilliant white light above the Épine range, with apparent red and green colouring. Contemporary reporting listed atmospheric effects, a planet, a lantern and an unidentified object as possibilities, but did not document independent witnesses, radar confirmation, photographs suitable for analysis or a later official identification. It remains a good example of a weakly sourced newspaper sighting: interesting as testimony, but too thin to support a major claim. The reported red and green components are also compatible with an aircraft seen nearly head on or through unstable air, although the surviving account does not permit a firm conclusion. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréSAVOIE. Chambéry: une grosse lumière blanche dans le Le Dauphiné LibéréSAVOIE. Chambéry: une grosse lumière blanche dans le GEIPAN’s more recent Chambéry files reinforce the same pattern. A July 2015 report was classed B and attributed to a Thai lantern. In June 2018, two witnesses saw an object that GEIPAN investigated through witness statements, viewing geometry and contextual checks; the case was eventually classed B as an illuminated balloon. The official conclusion was probable rather than absolute, but it substantially weakened the idea of an anomalous craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 03.06.2018CHAMBERY (73) 03.06.2018. Date d'observation. 03/06/2018. Région. Rhône Alpes. Département. Savoie. Classif... Entremont le Vieux shows why photographs are not decisive Entremont le Vieux, in the Chartreuse area, has produced two useful modern case studies. The first occurred on 12 August 2018. A witness reported moving lights and supplied photographs, allowing GEIPAN to undertake a comparatively detailed investigation. The case was ultimately classed B and attributed to sky lanterns. The presence of images strengthened the file’s consistency, but did not make the objects extraordinary; instead, the images helped investigators test an ordinary explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu d'enquête Compte rendu d'enquête A second report began with a bright white streak photographed on 12 December 2024. The witness later supplied another image taken on 13 January 2025. GEIPAN assessed the case as category A and astronomical in origin. This is particularly relevant to present day UFO discussion because cameras often record streaks, reflections or movement blur that the eye did not perceive in the same form. A photograph proves that light reached the sensor; it does not, by itself, establish the distance, size or physical nature of the source. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The two Entremont cases also caution against treating repeated reports from one locality as evidence of a “hotspot”. Similar places can generate different observations for recurring practical reasons: clear mountain skies, strong contrast with dark ridges, tourism, outdoor celebrations and unobstructed views of astronomical objects. A geographical cluster becomes significant only if the events share well documented characteristics that survive investigation. These two did not. Paragliders, lanterns and the problem of Alpine distance A 19 June 2023 observation made while travelling from Bourg Saint Maurice towards Peisey Nancroix was eventually classified B and interpreted as a paraglider. This is a strongly Savoie specific source of confusion. A canopy seen at a distance can appear as a curved, oval or triangular body; turns can create abrupt changes in apparent speed; and a pilot may be invisible against a bright sky. Mountains further complicate the judgement because observers often lack a continuous horizon against which to estimate height and distance. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr R] DE BOURG SAINT MAURICE (73) VERS PEISEY NANCROIX (73) 19.06.2023 19/06/2023 B Savoie parapente 25/01/2026 2023 06 19... Published: June 19, 2023 Lantern cases create a different illusion. Their orange or red glow, slow drift and gradual disappearance can suggest controlled flight. Several lanterns launched together may form lines, triangles or a V, while changing wind speeds can make the pattern appear to expand or manoeuvre. When a lantern burns out near a ridge, it may seem to accelerate behind the mountain even though"},{"title":"Chambery Cases","url":"/chambery-cases/","description":"Chambery's best-known sightings show how distress flares, lanterns and illuminated balloons can become convincing UFO stories.","breadcrumb":"Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade","level":2,"text":"Chambery Cases How Chambery's UFO Mysteries Were Explained Savoie UFOs Chambery Cases Chambery Cases How Chambery's UFO Mysteries Were Explained Chambery's best known sightings show how distress flares, lanterns and illuminated balloons can become convincing UFO stories. Chambery's best known sightings show how distress flares, lanterns and illuminated balloons can become convincing UFO stories. entity The 1997 distress flare identification Lantern reports and the 2012 sighting wave Weak evidence, balloons and unresolved testimony savoie chambery cases Introduction Chambéry’s best known UFO scares are notable less for what remained mysterious than for how ordinary objects acquired an extraordinary appearance. A dramatic 1997 incident, initially suspected to involve a meteorite, was traced through laboratory analysis to a distress flare that damaged a parked car. A wave of red and orange lights in July 2012 was linked within days to illuminated paper balloons released at a local festival. Later official reports from 2015 and 2018 again pointed towards sky lanterns or LED lit balloons, although the 2018 case retained some awkward details and was judged only probably, rather than conclusively, identified. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997 GEIPAN... Together, these cases show why Chambéry matters within Savoie ’s UFO history. Several sightings sounded persuasive when described by frightened witnesses, yet became much less unusual once investigators checked debris, event schedules, wind direction and the behaviour of small illuminated objects. The 1997 scare was a flare, not a fallen mystery object The incident of 10–11 April 1997 was unusually physical for a UFO report. A witness was awakened by an intense white light and a sound resembling fireworks. Looking outside, he found the roof of his car burning and contacted the police. Firefighters and police collected fragments from the scene, giving investigators something more useful than distant lights or memory alone: recoverable material that could be tested. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997 GEIPAN... The first interpretation briefly made the case more dramatic. A University of Savoie laboratory identified basalt in the samples and suggested that a meteorite might have fallen. That possibility was challenged almost immediately. A specialist who visited the site expressed strong doubts, and a mineralogist at the National Museum of Natural History in Paris also rejected the meteorite explanation after examining debris. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997 GEIPAN... Further analysis changed the case decisively. Samples obtained by SEPRA, the predecessor of today’s GEIPAN investigation unit, were studied by additional laboratories. Testing by the central CNRS laboratory at Solaize detected substances including sodium, magnesium, potassium, sulphur and carbon, a combination consistent with the composition of marine or terrestrial distress flares. GEIPAN ultimately classified the case as category A, meaning identified, and concluded that a ground launched flare had caused the vehicle fire. The file records very low residual strangeness and high evidential consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997 GEIPAN... This was therefore not simply a vague light retrospectively labelled a flare. The explanation emerged from a sequence of competing hypotheses and material tests. The mistaken meteorite diagnosis also illustrates an important caution: an early scientific sounding opinion is not necessarily the final answer, particularly when samples may be contaminated, incomplete or interpreted without the wider scene in mind. The case nevertheless had investigative weaknesses. GEIPAN states that it did not receive the police log and later had neither direct testimony nor access to the witness. Its published conclusion rests heavily on the substantial laboratory work already completed. The physical evidence was strong enough to identify the cause, but the missing first hand access prevented investigators from reconstructing every stage as efficiently as they might have done. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 10.04.1997 GEIPAN... Lantern reports and the 2012 sighting wave Chambéry’s most widely publicised cluster occurred on the evening of 28 July 2012. Witnesses described stationary points, red and orange lights descending in a V formation, a disc apparently surrounded by luminous circles and an orange ring shining behind clouds. Reports came from Chambéry and nearby La Ravoire and Barberaz, while an online forum gathered roughly fifteen accounts. Some witnesses said the experience caused anxiety and sleeplessness. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? The descriptions sounded more structured than a simple group of drifting lights. One observer said he had witnessed five separate appearances and doubted that balloons could perform the apparent “choreography”. The objects were variously described as discs, rings and rapidly disappearing formations. No report had yet been formally made to the gendarmerie, although internet users assembled photographs, sketches and testimony for submission to GEIPAN. At the time of the first newspaper article, Thai style sky lanterns were already being discussed but had not yet persuaded the main witnesses. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? Le Dauphiné LibéréCHAMBERY. Des Ovnis dans le ciel de Savoie? The mystery weakened quickly because the date, place and appearance could be matched to a known activity. Organisers of the Festivital event at Buisson Rond park confirmed that illuminated paper balloons had been released repeatedly between about 8.30 pm and midnight. The organisers recognised their balloons in a published photograph, and participants in the release also came forward. The timing corresponded closely with the reported succession of lights. [Ballons Pirates] ballonspirates.wordpress.com Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates These were small hot air balloons containing candles, described by their makers as biodegradable paper balloons. Depending on atmospheric conditions, they could rise to considerable heights and travel many kilometres. From the ground, the flame supplied the reported red orange colour, while the paper envelope and haze could produce halos, rings or apparently solid luminous forms. Sequential launches also explain why witnesses reported several waves rather than one brief group. [Ballons Pirates] ballonspirates.wordpress.com Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates The objection that the lights seemed to move against the wind was weaker than it first appeared. Local air movement felt at street level need not match winds higher above the ground, particularly around Chambéry’s basin and surrounding slopes. Lights carried in different air layers may change separation, accelerate relative to an observer or appear to reverse direction when seen without a reliable distance reference. Contemporary responses noted that glider and paraglider pilots were familiar with such differences between surface and higher level airflow. [Ballons Pirates] ballonspirates.wordpress.com Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates Ballons Pirates Des Ovni à Chambéry! Ballons Pirates The 2012 episode is particularly revealing because the explanation did not depend on a generic claim that the lights “looked like lanterns”. There was a confirmed launch at the correct location and time, testimony from the people involved and visual recognition of the objects in press photographs. The later evidence therefore substantially weakened an extraordinary interpretation, even though some witnesses had initially found the balloons’ apparent formation too deliberate to be accidental. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Similar lights returned in 2015 On 6 July 2015, two people in Chambéry observed between fifteen and twenty pink orange lights travelling silently in a line towards Aix les Bains. The sighting lasted only about ten to fifteen seconds. GEIPAN classified it as category B: probably identified as Thai style sky lanterns, though not proven with the direct event evidence available in the 2012 case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHAMBERY (73) 06.07.2015 GEIPAN... Several features supported that interpretation. The lights appeared on a summer night, were similarly coloured, produced no sound and moved together in a regular stream. Weather records showed a light southerly wind at Chambéry–Aix les Bains, compatible with the"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-6adf64/","description":"GEIPAN's Savoie archive separates explained cases from weak files, showing why unresolved does not automatically mean extraordinary.","breadcrumb":"Why Savoie's UFO Mysteries Usually Fade","level":2,"text":"Official Files What Savoie's Official UFO Records Actually Show Savoie UFOs Official Files Official Files What Savoie's Official UFO Records Actually Show GEIPAN's Savoie archive separates explained cases from weak files, showing why unresolved does not automatically mean extraordinary. GEIPAN's Savoie archive separates explained cases from weak files, showing why unresolved does not automatically mean extraordinary. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies reported phenomena Why category C means insufficient information Recurring explanations across the department savoie official files Introduction Savoie ’s official UFO files prove something more modest, but more useful, than a hidden aerial mystery. They show that people across the department have reported genuinely puzzling lights and objects, and that careful investigation often turns those experiences into recognisable phenomena: planets, distress flares, satellites, aircraft contrails, balloons, lanterns, insects, paragliders and atmospheric re entry events. Other reports remain unclassified because the available evidence is too poor to test properly. That distinction is crucial. In the public archive maintained by GEIPAN, the French government unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, an unexplained report is not automatically evidence of an extraordinary object. A weak file and a strong unexplained case occupy different categories. The searchable Savoie record is dominated by explained, probably explained and insufficiently documented cases, rather than a body of well supported incidents that resisted investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN How GEIPAN classifies a Savoie sighting GEIPAN, part of France’s National Centre for Space Studies, does not classify a case according to how dramatic the witness’s story sounds. Its assessment uses two linked measures: the consistency of the evidence and the residual strangeness left after ordinary explanations have been considered. Consistency reflects the quantity and reliability of the information collected. A report supported by accurate timings, several independent witnesses, original photographs, known viewing directions and relevant aviation or astronomical data will be stronger than a recollection submitted years later with no precise location or duration. Residual strangeness asks how unusual the event still appears after investigators compare it with known phenomena. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN The resulting categories are easy to misunderstand: A: the phenomenon has been identified. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, although not proved beyond doubt. C: no identification is possible because essential information is missing or unreliable. D: the event remains unexplained despite an investigation based on sufficiently useful evidence. GEIPAN has also used D1 and D2 subdivisions to distinguish between unexplained cases of moderate evidential strength and highly strange cases supported by stronger material, such as independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. It stresses that classifications may change when new evidence appears. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN This means the categories are not a scale running from “ordinary” to “alien”. Category C does not sit above B as a more mysterious result. It usually means that investigators cannot do enough with the file. Why category C does not prove an extraordinary object Several Savoie entries are labelled C with the stated reason that reliable information is lacking. They include reports from Le Bourget du Lac in February 1964, La Motte Servolex in March 1979, Salins les Thermes in August 1980, Tignes in February 2008 and Aix les Bains in August 2010. The archive preserves these reports, but their classification records an evidential failure rather than a successful demonstration of something anomalous. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The Saint Étienne de Cuines report of 24 August 1980 illustrates the problem. A witness described a very bright object seen at about 3.30 am, with what appeared to be a pale pink parachute shape briefly visible beneath it. The bright object reportedly remained visible for more than ten minutes. The description is interesting, but GEIPAN classifies the case C because the information is insufficient for a dependable identification or reconstruction. There is no measurement establishing the object’s distance, altitude, actual size or physical connection to the apparent “parachute”. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT ETIENNE DE CUINES (73) 24.08.198024 Aug 1980 — Geipan Groupe. SAINT ETIENNE DE CUINES (73) 24.08.1980 Date d'observation 24/0... Such files may remain unresolved indefinitely because the missing information cannot be recovered. A precise direction might have allowed an astronomical comparison. A reliable sequence of times could have been checked against aircraft or satellite movements. Independent witnesses might have shown whether the perceived shape was stable or subjective. Without those elements, competing explanations cannot be properly separated. The honest conclusion is therefore not that an unknown craft was present. It is that the historical testimony is too incomplete to decide what was present. Category C protects that distinction by preventing speculation from being mistaken for an investigative result. Explained cases reveal the archive’s real value The strongest evidence in Savoie’s official record often comes from cases that no longer look mysterious. These files show what investigators can establish when observations contain enough detail to compare with external information. At Chambéry on 10 April 1997, a puzzling aerial light was ultimately classified A and identified as a distress flare. Elsewhere in the department’s archive, a 7 October 2001 event was attributed with high probability to atmospheric re entry, a January 2010 sighting at Saint François Longchamp to a weather balloon, and a July 2022 report at Avrieux to the International Space Station. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN Aiton’s 20 November 2021 case is particularly instructive because photographs alone did not settle the matter. The witness saw diffuse, dark formations against the evening sky and supplied eight images. GEIPAN’s published file includes an investigation report and a radar reconstruction. The result was category A: aircraft contrails, with very low residual strangeness and reasonably good consistency. The lesson is not that photographs are worthless, but that they become useful only when their time, direction, geometry and environmental setting can be checked. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAITON (73) 20.11.2021 GEIPAN... The Entremont le Vieux investigation shows how phone photography can create additional apparent detail rather than clarify it. Beginning in December 2024, a witness repeatedly observed white luminous forms and later photographed what seemed, under digital enlargement, to resemble an object. GEIPAN corrected an initially mistaken photograph date, compared the observations with the sky and identified the principal lights as Mars and Jupiter. The file was classified A, with low strangeness and comparatively good supporting information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANENTREMONT LE VIEUX (73) 12.12.2024 GEIPAN... This is one of the archive’s most important findings. A sincere witness can accurately report seeing a bright light while misjudging what the light was, how far away it lay or whether magnified camera artefacts represented real structure. Investigation may validate the experience without validating the witness’s interpretation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Probable explanations are evidence, not dismissals Category B cases are sometimes treated as though investigators merely guessed at an explanation. In reality, a B classification usually means that several details fit a known cause, but the decisive object cannot be independently recovered or confirmed. On 6 July 2015, two people near Chambéry reported between 15 and 20 pink orange lights moving silently in a line towards Aix les Bains. GEIPAN judged Thai style sky lanterns to be the probable explanation. The colour, orderly release pattern, summer timing and movement matched lantern behaviour, while the direction was compatible with the recorded southerly wind. Because no individual lantern was recovered or traced to a specific event, the conclusion remained probable rather than certain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes"},{"title":"Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","url":"/which-seine-et-marne-ufo-cases-still/","description":"Seine-et-Marne has no single, coherent “UFO wave” unique to the department.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation? Seine et Marne has no single, coherent “UFO wave” unique to the department. Seine et Marne has no single, coherent “UFO wave” unique to the department. seine et marne Introduction The wider record is instructive precisely because its outcomes differ. A brilliant “craft” seen from Meaux proved to be Venus; orange lights over Courtry were probably festive lanterns; green discs filmed at Mitry Mory were judged likely to be projected on clouds. Seine et Marne also lay beneath the celebrated 5 November 1990 French “UFO wave”, now firmly identified as the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. Together, these cases show how witness sincerity, aviation experience and even police involvement can strengthen a report without automatically identifying what was seen. [Geipan] geipan.fr MEAUX (77) 19.12.1978 GEIPAN... What the departmental record actually contains France’s official body for investigating unusual aerial reports is GEIPAN, a technical service of the national space agency CNES. It uses the broader term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” because many reports involve lights, atmospheric effects or apparent shapes rather than demonstrably solid flying objects. GEIPAN classifies cases as A when securely explained, B when an explanation is highly probable, C when the information is inadequate, and D when the phenomenon remains unexplained after investigation. A D classification means “unexplained with the available evidence”, not “alien spacecraft”. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... The published Seine et Marne files include all four kinds of outcome. They range from an explained astronomical observation at Meaux and probable lanterns at Courtry to information poor reports from places such as Sivry Courtry, Jablines, Gouvernes, Vaux le Pénil and Savigny le Temple. The database also contains the D classified Ussy sur Marne and AF3532 cases. This uneven mixture argues against treating every departmental entry as evidence of the same underlying phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr MEAUX (77) 1989 /06/1989 B Seine et Marne Phénomène identifié non indiqué 22/03/2007 1989 06 01SIVRY COURTRY (77) 04.05.199... Nor does the public record demonstrate that Seine et Marne is a special national hotspot. The department is large, populous and crossed by busy air routes around the Paris region. It contains airfields and aviation facilities, but proximity to aviation can generate both valuable professional observations and more opportunities to mistake aircraft, balloons, lights or distant atmospheric effects. A statistical study of French reports found that population density and several environmental or industrial variables influenced their geographical distribution; it did not identify Seine et Marne as one of the principal unexplained residual clusters. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. The 5 November 1990 “wave”: dramatic, widespread and explained On the evening of 5 November 1990, witnesses across France reported an immense formation of lights, often interpreted as a dark triangular or elongated craft moving silently at low altitude. The event became one of the country’s best known UFO episodes. Seine et Marne was part of that national pattern: GEIPAN’s archive includes a report from Lagny sur Marne among the many local files collected from across France. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The scale of the event initially appeared impressive. CNES security services received calls from numerous gendarmerie units, and reports continued for days. Some witnesses described structured craft rather than separate lights, while the brightness made the phenomenon seem much nearer than it really was. Even experienced observers found it strange. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The subsequent investigation, however, produced unusually strong identification evidence. NASA supplied orbital information for the re entry of an object associated with a Soviet Proton launch, and CNES orbit calculations confirmed a path crossing France from the Bay of Biscay towards the east. GEIPAN concluded that observers had seen the disintegrating third stage of the rocket, travelling at high altitude as a long cloud of glowing debris. It classifies the event A: explained without ambiguity. Contemporary reporting likewise described how orbital information resolved the mystery within days. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... This episode matters to Seine et Marne’s UFO history because it demonstrates how a real and spectacular aerial event can generate apparently incompatible descriptions. Separate fragments can look like lights attached to a single dark body. With no familiar distance reference at night, witnesses may place a high altitude event just above houses or fields and infer impossible size or speed. GEIPAN’s later simulation work stressed the variability of such estimates and the possibility that intense light could remain visible through thin cloud. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The 1990 wave therefore should not be used as evidence that a giant craft crossed Seine et Marne. Its value is almost the reverse: it is a well documented example of how sincere mass testimony can preserve the appearance of an extraordinary machine even when the physical source is independently known. AF3532 near Coulommiers: the strongest aviation case The most important Seine et Marne aviation report occurred on 28 January 1994. An Air France Airbus A320 flying from Nice to London was above the Coulommiers area when a senior cabin crew member in the cockpit noticed something to the aircraft’s left. He initially thought it might be a weather balloon. The captain and co pilot described a dark reddish brown or brown disc like phenomenon that appeared to change form before vanishing suddenly. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The captain contacted air traffic control at Reims and was told that no known traffic was present in the relevant sector. More unusually, a military radar at Cinq Mars la Pile recorded an unidentified track for about 50 seconds. According to GEIPAN’s summary, the track crossed the aircraft’s route, did not correspond to a filed flight plan and disappeared at approximately the same time as the crew’s visual phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. These details make AF3532 more substantial than a typical single witness light report: the principal observers were professional aircrew; more than one person saw the phenomenon; the observation occurred in daylight; air traffic control found no matching declared aircraft; a separate radar system recorded an unidentified return nearby. Yet the evidential limits are equally important. GEIPAN has remained cautious about assuming that the radar track and the visible phenomenon were the same thing. The apparent object was not photographed, its distance was unknown, and estimates of size derived from an unknown distance are inherently unstable. The official investigation states that the radar signature did not precisely reproduce the visual description. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A weather balloon was an obvious possibility because the first observer himself suggested one. A later check with Météo France found that a balloon had crossed the aircraft’s path, but about an hour earlier; by the reported observation time it should already have burst and been descending far to the south east. That weakened the simplest balloon explanation without ruling out every form of balloon, airborne material or unusual optical effect. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN continues to classify AF3532 as D, describing it as an unexplained case of medium to strong consistency and marked strangeness. The file was updated in June 2026, but the classification remained unchanged. This persistence strengthens the statement that no satisfactory identification has yet been established. It does not strengthen claims about the phenomenon’s origin beyond that. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The most defensible assessment is therefore that AF3532 is Seine et Marne’s best documented unresolved aviation case. Its multiple professional witnesses and associated radar information deserve serious attention. Its weaknesses—uncertain range, no image, delayed formal statements and an unproven visual radar correlation—prevent a firmer conclusion. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Ussy sur Marne: three gendarmes and a"},{"title":"Solved Sightings","url":"/solved-sightings-986e22/","description":"Rocket debris, Venus, lanterns and projected lights show how convincing sightings can emerge from familiar physical causes.","breadcrumb":"Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","level":2,"text":"Solved Sightings How Seine et Marne's Most Convincing UFOs Were Explained Seine et Marne UFOs Solved Sightings Solved Sightings How Seine et Marne's Most Convincing UFOs Were Explained Rocket debris, Venus, lanterns and projected lights show how convincing sightings can emerge from familiar physical causes. Rocket debris, Venus, lanterns and projected lights show how convincing sightings can emerge from familiar physical causes. mechanism The 5 November 1990 rocket re entry Venus, lanterns and projected lights in local reports Why distance and darkness distort witness impressions seine et marne solved sightings Introduction Some of Seine et Marne ’s most convincing UFO reports were not invented, exaggerated or made by obviously unreliable witnesses. People genuinely saw brilliant lights, apparent formations and objects that seemed to hover, accelerate or vanish. What changed was the identification: astronomical checks, orbital data, weather records, aviation context, photographs and video showed that several dramatic observations had familiar causes. The clearest examples are strikingly varied. The immense “craft” crossing France on 5 November 1990 was debris from a Soviet rocket stage re entering the atmosphere. A luminous object watched from Meaux by a resident and gendarmes was Venus. Orange lights over Courtry closely matched flying lanterns. Green discs filmed above Mitry Mory were probably spots cast on cloud by a ground based projector. These cases matter because they show that a sincere and detailed account can accurately preserve the appearance of an event while still misidentifying its distance, scale or nature. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The 1990 “giant craft” was falling rocket debris The evening of 5 November 1990 produced one of the most spectacular UFO episodes ever recorded in France. At about 7pm, thousands of people saw a vast procession of lights travelling across a clear sky. Reports often described an enormous dark triangle or elongated vehicle, apparently moving silently at low altitude. Seine et Marne lay beneath the observed path, and GEIPAN’s national case file includes testimony from Lagny sur Marne among the reports gathered from across the country. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The scale and consistency of the reports initially made the incident seem unusually strong. Gendarmerie brigades contacted the Centre national d’études spatiales, or CNES, in large numbers. The former official investigation service SEPRA received calls for more than a week, while aviation, military, meteorological and orbital authorities were consulted. Even technically experienced observers found the display remarkable. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The decisive evidence came from orbital tracking rather than from trying to judge the witnesses’ credibility. NASA identified an object associated with the Soviet Gorizont 21 launch as having re entered on 5 November. CNES orbit calculations then confirmed a trajectory across France from the Bay of Biscay towards Alsace. The explanation was released to the press on 9 November, and contemporary reporting described the event as the disintegration of pieces of a Soviet rocket. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... What witnesses had seen was a cloud of incandescent debris from the third stage of a Proton rocket. GEIPAN estimates that the material crossed the upper atmosphere at roughly 50 to 100 kilometres altitude, slowing from about seven to two kilometres per second. Separate fragments produced multiple bright points and trails spread across tens of kilometres. Seen from the ground, those points appeared to travel together as parts of one structured object. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... This explains why the sighting could look like a solid triangular machine rather than a loose collection of debris. Human vision naturally groups lights that move in a coordinated way and may supply an apparent outline between them. With no visible surface, known dimensions or reliable distance cue, observers interpreted the dark space between the lights as the body of a craft. GEIPAN notes that many accounts consequently described a low flying dark object fitted with several lights, even though the luminous fragments were extremely high and widely separated. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The explanation fits several independent features at once: the time, direction and duration of the passage; the number of lights; the reconstructed orbital path; and the apparent size remaining comparatively stable as the phenomenon crossed the sky. That last detail is important. A nearby vehicle passing overhead should show a pronounced change in apparent size, whereas a very distant debris field may not. GEIPAN therefore classifies the event as category A, meaning securely identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The fact that some witnesses rejected the conclusion does not create contrary physical evidence. The experience felt close, structured and potentially threatening, while the explanation placed it tens of kilometres above them. That gap between lived impression and measured geometry is precisely what makes the event so instructive: the testimony conveyed how the display looked, but not how far away it was. Venus became a moving light over Meaux On the morning of 19 December 1978, a witness in Meaux watched an unfamiliar bright point from a balcony for more than an hour. It appeared to oscillate in the clear south eastern sky before becoming lost in the Sun’s glare. Gendarmes attended the apartment and also saw a luminous point in the reported direction, at an estimated elevation of about 35 degrees. [Geipan] geipan.fr MEAUX (77) 19.12.1978 GEIPAN... Police in Paris had investigated a similar bright object the previous day and concluded that it was astronomical. A celestial position check for the Meaux observation placed Venus in the same south eastern sector at roughly 25 to 30 degrees elevation. GEIPAN consequently classified the report as category A: a misidentification of Venus. [Geipan] geipan.fr MEAUX (77) 19.12.1978 GEIPAN... The involvement of gendarmes strengthens the evidence that a light was present; it does not establish that the light was an unusual aircraft. This distinction is central to solved UFO cases. Multiple credible people can corroborate an observation while sharing the same mistaken interpretation because they are all viewing the same unfamiliar appearance under the same conditions. Venus is especially effective at producing such reports because it is exceptionally bright and can remain visible as the surrounding sky grows lighter. Without nearby reference points, a small luminous source can also seem to drift, oscillate or change position. GEIPAN describes several perception effects that complicate such observations, including apparent motion in isolated lights and attempts to infer distance and speed from objects whose size is unknown. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... A related illusion occurs when an observer is travelling. A distant star or planet changes little in direction while nearby trees, buildings and road features move rapidly across the field of view. The light may therefore seem to follow the vehicle, stop when the vehicle stops or manoeuvre around landmarks. In another officially explained Venus case, GEIPAN called this the “following ball” illusion and showed how an initial underestimate of distance made the apparent pursuit seem physically real. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan(DPT) BAS RHIN (67) 11.04.1980 GEIPAN... The Meaux file is valuable because the explanation does not depend on dismissing the witnesses’ account. The long duration, fixed general direction and astronomical position are the very details that identify the source. What appeared at first to support the presence of a hovering object instead became evidence for a planet. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lanterns can appear to stop and shoot away At about 11pm on 29 August 2009, a group on a terrace in Courtry saw a red orange light followed by roughly ten more. The lights travelled together, appeared to stop for around 30 seconds and then disappeared individually. They made no audible sound. Fifteen people reportedly watched, although only one submitted a formal account and photographs to GEIPAN several years later. [Geipan] geipan.fr COURTRY (77) 29.08.2009 GEIPAN... The description closely matched flying lanterns: warm orange colour, several lights released in succession, wind driven movement and individual extinction as each flame went out. The photographs were also compatible with that"},{"title":"1994 Cases","url":"/1994-cases/","description":"Two 1994 reports stand out because professional witnesses, gendarmes and radar records gave them unusual evidential weight.","breadcrumb":"Which Seine et Marne UFO Cases Still Defy Explanation?","level":2,"text":"1994 Cases Why the 1994 Seine et Marne Sightings Still Matter 1994 Cases 1994 Cases Why the 1994 Seine et Marne Sightings Still Matter Two 1994 reports stand out because professional witnesses, gendarmes and radar records gave them unusual evidential weight. Two 1994 reports stand out because professional witnesses, gendarmes and radar records gave them unusual evidential weight. event time window The Air France encounter near Coulommiers The gendarmes' triangular light report near Ussy sur Marne What the evidence proves and what remains uncertain seine et marne 1994 cases Introduction Two Seine et Marne sightings from 1994 remain more persuasive than most reports in the department’s official record. On 28 January, an Air France cockpit crew near Coulommiers saw an unfamiliar dark, changing form while an unidentified military radar return was recorded in the wider area. On 24 June, three gendarmes near Ussy sur Marne reported a silent triangular object that apparently approached their patrol car before departing rapidly. Both cases are still classified as unexplained by GEIPAN, the investigation service of France’s space agency, CNES. Their importance lies in the quality of the witnesses, the existence of formal records and, in the Air France case, instrumental data. Yet neither case provides proof of an extraordinary aircraft, much less an extraterrestrial one. The radar contact cannot be securely matched to the object seen by the pilots, while the gendarmes’ account has no independent sensor record, photograph or outside witness. They are strong unresolved reports rather than solved mysteries. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... The Air France encounter near Coulommiers At 13:14 on 28 January 1994, Air France flight AF3532, an Airbus operating between Nice and London, was passing over the Coulommiers area. A chief steward who was in the cockpit noticed something to the left of the aircraft and initially suggested that it might be a weather balloon. The captain and co pilot then observed it themselves, describing a brown or dark red form that appeared to alter its shape before disappearing abruptly. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... An air defence chronology produced from information received that afternoon described a dark, bell shaped object perhaps 10 to 20 nautical miles away and about 2,000 feet below the aircraft. It was reportedly visible for roughly a minute. Later estimates of its dimensions were extremely large, but those figures depended on an uncertain distance: without a known range, apparent angular size cannot be converted reliably into physical size. [Geipan] geipan.fr Chronologie CODA Chronologie CODA The crew contacted air traffic control at Reims and were told that no known traffic was present in the relevant sector. The episode therefore cannot easily be dismissed as a straightforward encounter with another scheduled aircraft. This is one reason it has remained prominent among French aviation reports. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... The radar return: valuable but not a confirmation The case’s strongest evidence is an unidentified return recorded by the military radar at Cinq Mars la Pile. According to GEIPAN’s current summary, the track lasted about 50 seconds, crossed the path of AF3532, corresponded to no filed flight plan and vanished at approximately the same time as the visual phenomenon. Radar information of this kind is more valuable than a witness account alone because it may indicate that some physical reflector was present in the airspace. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... The crucial qualification is that investigators did not establish a secure visual radar correlation. The radar track’s position and movement were not fully consistent with the object described from the cockpit. GEIPAN consequently warns that the two observations may have involved separate things. The radar evidence strengthens the case by showing unexplained activity near the aircraft’s route, but it does not prove that the radar was tracking the form seen by the crew. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... This distinction matters because the case is sometimes retold as a perfectly matched radar and visual encounter. The official assessment is more cautious. There was an unusual visual report and an unidentified radar track with a suggestive temporal and geographical relationship, but the available data do not allow them to be fused into one certain event. Why a weather balloon was investigated The steward’s initial suggestion made a balloon an obvious possibility. GEIPAN later asked Météo France to reconstruct the course of the radiosonde launched from Trappes that day. The balloon was released at 12:18 local time and, at 13:14, had reached about 17,400 metres near Joigny, well to the south east of the Air France aircraft. It did not burst until approximately 13:48. [Geipan] geipan.fr Objet... Météo France also noted that high altitude winds could deform a balloon, potentially accounting for an apparently changing outline. However, the calculated location did not match the sighting, and even a greatly expanded meteorological balloon would have been only a few metres across rather than the hundreds of metres suggested by the witnesses’ most dramatic estimates. GEIPAN therefore rejected that particular radiosonde as a satisfactory explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Objet... A loose or damaged balloon envelope remains theoretically possible, as does an unusual atmospheric optical effect, but GEIPAN has found no evidence firm enough to support either proposal. These are residual possibilities, not established solutions. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... The delayed testimony weakens the file Despite the professional setting, AF3532 is not a flawless case. The captain apparently received an instruction to file a gendarmerie report, yet the principal formal witness statements were not made until about three years later, after the incident had attracted press attention. Delay creates opportunities for memory to become simplified, enlarged or unconsciously influenced by discussion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... The steward who first noticed the phenomenon did not provide a published testimony, removing an important independent perspective. GEIPAN nevertheless found the captain’s and co pilot’s accounts broadly coherent. Its current classification is D1: unexplained, moderately well supported and markedly strange, but weakened by late reporting, the missing steward’s statement and the uncertain relationship between the radar track and the visual sighting. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« AF3532 » [AERO AFR] COULOMMIERS (77) 28.01.1994 GEIPAN... Later investigation therefore produced a mixed result. It weakened the popular claim of unambiguous radar confirmation, but it also eliminated the specific radiosonde that seemed the most obvious conventional candidate. The case became more precisely unresolved rather than either confirmed or debunked. The gendarmes’ triangular light report near Ussy sur Marne At about 02:40 on 24 June 1994, three gendarmes on night patrol were travelling along the D3E towards Changis sur Marne. Near Ussy sur Marne, lights above the A4 motorway drew their attention. As the patrol approached, the lights appeared to resolve into three yellow white points arranged as a triangle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUSSY SUR MARNE (77) 24.06.1994 GEIPAN... The officers described a dark triangular form with a luminous centre and its point directed downwards. They could not determine its altitude or dimensions—an important limitation—but believed that it was initially stationary. After they stopped, it reportedly moved slowly towards them while maintaining roughly the same height, then stopped above or very close to their vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. When the patrol resumed its journey, the object was said to have moved westwards, passed above them and accelerated away so rapidly that only a faint point remained on the horizon. The officers reported no engine noise, smoke or smell, despite having the car windows open on a warm night. Their account placed the total observation at about ten minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUSSY SUR MARNE (77) 24.06.1994 GEIPAN... Why the police record carries weight The strongest element is the documentary chain. The witnesses were serving gendarmes engaged in routine patrol duties, and the surviving file is a National Gendarmerie report rather than a story reconstructed solely through later UFO publications. It includes their narrative, a diagram of three lights joined by a triangular outline, a map of the"},{"title":"Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove...","url":"/why-seine-maritimes-strangest-sightings/","description":"Seine-Maritime has a substantial but mostly undramatic UFO record.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove... Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove Extraordinary Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove... Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove... Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove Extraordinary Seine Maritime has a substantial but mostly undramatic UFO record. Seine Maritime has a substantial but mostly undramatic UFO record. Public databases contain dozens of reports from Rouen, Le Havre, Dieppe, the Pays de Caux and smaller inland communities, yet most were either identified, probably identified or left undecided because the evidence was too weak. seine maritime Introduction The department’s UFO history therefore matters less as proof of extraordinary craft than as a revealing study of how unusual lights become stories. Bright planets, meteors, atmospheric re entry, aircraft, illuminated balloons and event lighting repeatedly produced sincere reports. A smaller number resisted explanation, but usually without radar confirmation, physical evidence or enough independent testimony to establish what actually happened. From the 1954 wave to official investigation Seine Maritime participated in the great French “flying saucer” wave of autumn 1954, when newspapers across the country carried an exceptional number of reports. Local retrospective accounts describe several sightings around Fécamp and the Pays de Caux, including reports on 28 September of luminous, oval objects and later groups of lights west of Fécamp harbour. One account says that a municipal official and about 15 other residents saw the earlier display. [Site de havre secret!] havre secret.fr Site de havre secret!Les Dossiers Secrets de la \"Vague de 54'\" en Pays Cauchois 2 engins lumineux de forme ovoïde et laissant une traînée lumineuse envahissent le ciel au nord ...Read more... Those reports are historically interesting, but their evidential value is limited. They survive mainly through press retellings and later catalogues rather than instrument records or complete contemporary case files. The descriptions—luminous shapes, trails and groups of lights—could fit unusual meteors, aircraft seen under poor conditions or other ordinary phenomena. The national 1954 wave also created a powerful expectation effect: once “flying saucers” became front page news, ambiguous lights were more likely to be noticed, discussed and reported in the same language. A supposed photograph of a disc over Rouen, generally dated to March 1954 but sometimes given a later date, became part of UFO literature after appearing in a British aviation magazine. Its origin is uncertain, the pilot was not properly identified and no recognisable Rouen landscape appears in the frame. Later comparison suggested that it might be a cropped or distorted copy of the famous McMinnville photographs from the United States. The image is therefore better treated as an unresolved publication mystery than as reliable photographic evidence of an object over Rouen. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 12 mars, Rouen...L'auteur dit que la dernière photographie authentique de soucoupe volante qu'il con... Systematic French investigation came later. CNES, the French space agency, created GEPAN in 1977; after institutional changes, the work is now carried out by GEIPAN. The unit collects witness accounts, gendarmerie statements and reports from aviation or other authorities, then checks possible astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human explanations before publishing case material. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... The cases that best show the department’s pattern No single Seine Maritime incident has achieved the status of France’s best known UFO cases. The department is more useful as a collection of contrasting examples: some visually striking, some weakly documented and several convincingly reduced to familiar causes. Dieppe, 18 June 1976. Two people reported a long, luminous, cigar shaped object travelling at apparently low altitude from north east to south west at about 9.30 pm. It moved steadily and made no audible sound. GEIPAN classified the case C, meaning that it lacked sufficiently reliable information for a conclusion. Only one formal testimony was collected, with no photograph, radar record or independent measurement of distance, size or altitude. [Geipan] geipan.fr DIEPPE (76) 18.06.1976DIEPPE (76) 18.06.1976; Date d'observation. 18/06/1976; Région. Haute Normandie; Département. Seine Mariti... This is an important distinction. A case classified C is not an officially confirmed anomaly. It means that the investigation could not establish a cause because the record was too incomplete. A distant aircraft, cloud illuminated by the setting Sun or other conventional object may appear large, silent and “cigar shaped” when its range is unknown. Rouen, 25 January 1980. A witness and gendarmes saw two points brighter than ordinary stars at about 10 pm. The lights appeared to rise slowly during the evening and could be seen again on later clear nights. GEIPAN concluded that they were probably astronomical objects and placed the case in category B, used where a conventional explanation is strongly favoured but not absolutely demonstrated. [Geipan] geipan.fr ROUEN (76) 25.01.198022 Mar 2007 — Date d'observation. 25/01/1980; Région. Haute Normandie; Département. Seine Maritime; Classif... The Rouen file shows why the presence of police witnesses does not automatically make a sighting extraordinary. Gendarmes can confirm that lights were visible, but identification still depends on direction, time, apparent movement, weather and astronomical reconstruction. Repeated visibility on successive clear nights strongly weakens the idea of a transient craft. Le Havre, 25 November 1989. A man initially reported three lights forming a triangle. When formally interviewed several weeks later, he instead described five orange discs and relied partly on a newspaper article while giving his statement. GEIPAN classified the report C because the contradictions made meaningful analysis impossible. [Geipan] geipan.fr HAVRE (LE) (76) 25.11.1989Geipan. Groupe d'études et d'informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés. Le Geipan... S... This case illustrates how later reporting can weaken rather than strengthen a claim. Memory is reconstructive, especially when a witness is exposed to press descriptions before completing a detailed account. Changes in the number, shape or arrangement of objects are not proof of dishonesty, but they greatly reduce confidence in precise reconstruction. Criquetot l’Esneval, 2–3 July 1992. Two teenagers described a red, rugby ball shaped object surrounded by flames. It appeared briefly stationary before descending rapidly. The following day they found flattened wheat and marks in a field. Investigators established that storms had damaged several fields in the area, so the ground effects were not linked securely to the aerial observation. The light itself remained unidentifiable because no further evidence was available, resulting in a C classification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The lesson is significant: physical traces matter only when a clear chain connects them to the reported phenomenon. Discovering damaged crops after a sighting is not enough when weather provides an independent and widespread cause. Ouville l’Abbaye: strange, but reported too late One of the department’s more intriguing narratives concerns Ouville l’Abbaye in 1981. A witness recalled seeing five or six red orange lights around the edge of a dark form near a farm. The display allegedly moved slowly, reversed direction and then accelerated upwards. A second person remembered a curved row of red lights but supplied only limited information. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr ouville l abbaye 1981 1008550 ouville l abbaye 1981 1008550 The difficulty is that the detailed report was made more than 30 years later. Even the date was uncertain, with October or November both possible. That delay prevented meaningful checks of weather, local agricultural activity, aircraft movements or contemporary press reports. GEIPAN considered machinery or work lights as a possibility, although the reported height and departure did not fit neatly. It ultimately classified the case C: sufficiently strange to resist a confident explanation, but too unreliable and incomplete to qualify as a well supported unknown. [CarteOvni.fr] carteovni.fr ouville l abbaye 1981 1008550 ouville l abbaye 1981 1008550 Ouville l’Abbaye is a useful warning against treating “unexplained” and “well evidenced” as synonyms. A story can contain unusual behaviour yet remain weak because the date, geometry and witness record are uncertain. In evidential terms, a modest event documented immediately is often more valuable than a spectacular memory recorded decades afterwards. Why orange lights became"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-5aaaae/","description":"GEIPAN files show how weak testimony, astronomy, weather and lanterns turned dramatic reports into cautious conclusions.","breadcrumb":"Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove...","level":2,"text":"Official Files How GEIPAN Judged Seine Maritime's UFO Reports Seine Maritime UFOs Official Files Official Files How GEIPAN Judged Seine Maritime's UFO Reports GEIPAN files show how weak testimony, astronomy, weather and lanterns turned dramatic reports into cautious conclusions. GEIPAN files show how weak testimony, astronomy, weather and lanterns turned dramatic reports into cautious conclusions. dataset evidence What GEIPAN categories actually mean Contradictions, delayed testimony and missing measurements Planets, storms and lanterns as recurring explanations seine maritime official files Introduction Official investigations did not find a single explanation for Seine Maritime ’s UFO reports. Instead, GEIPAN—the specialist unit of the French space agency CNES—reconstructed each sighting from its direction, duration, weather, astronomical position, aircraft activity and the quality of the testimony. Bright planets, stars distorted by the atmosphere, illuminated lanterns, aircraft and event lighting accounted for several apparently dramatic reports. Other files remained undecided because witnesses reported too late, supplied contradictory estimates or lacked photographs, precise bearings and independent confirmation. That distinction is central to understanding the department’s official record. A report classed as unexplained is not necessarily strong evidence of an extraordinary object, while a probable explanation does not mean investigators proved every detail. The published files show a deliberately cautious system in which the strength of the evidence matters as much as the strangeness of the story. What GEIPAN categories actually mean GEIPAN receives witness questionnaires and supporting material, creates a case file, conducts an initial assessment and then checks possible astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human causes. It works with organisations including the gendarmerie, police, the French Air and Space Force, Météo France and scientific specialists. Human testimony remains the starting point, but photographs, videos, sketches and instrument records can increase a case’s evidential value. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Its classifications are based on two broad considerations: residual strangeness , meaning how unusual the phenomenon still appears after ordinary explanations have been tested, and consistency , meaning the quantity and reliability of the available information. The categories are therefore conclusions about an investigation, not labels for different kinds of craft: A: the phenomenon has been identified. B: a conventional explanation is considered probable, although it cannot be demonstrated beyond doubt. C: there is insufficient reliable information to determine what happened. D: the phenomenon remains unidentified after investigation. GEIPAN can reopen C or D files if useful new information appears. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANIts historyClassification A: Phenomenon perfectly identified after investigation. · Classification B: Phenomenon probably identifie... This framework prevents a common misunderstanding in discussions of Seine Maritime cases. Category C does not mean that investigators encountered something more mysterious than a category B case. It often means the opposite: the observation was too poorly documented to support either an ordinary identification or a robust unexplained finding. The Dieppe report of 18 June 1976 illustrates the problem. Two people reportedly watched a long, luminous, cigar shaped phenomenon moving steadily from north east to south west without audible sound. Yet only one formal testimony was collected, and the published record contains no photograph, measured bearing, reliable altitude, radar evidence or second independent account. GEIPAN therefore placed it in category C for lack of dependable information rather than declaring the reported object genuinely anomalous. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDIEPPE (76) 18.06.1976DIEPPE (76) 18.06.1976; Date d'observation. 18/06/1976; Région. Haute Normandie; Département. Seine Mariti... When the testimony could not support the story The weakest Seine Maritime files repeatedly contain the same limitations: delayed reporting, very short viewing times, uncertain dates, estimates made without reference points and witnesses who did not submit separate accounts. These weaknesses matter because apparent speed, size and distance in a dark sky cannot usually be measured by eye alone. A striking example comes from Dieppe in 1989. The witness did not report the experience until December 2009, roughly twenty years after the event, and could not give an exact observation date. The alleged triangular form was visible for less than five seconds, no other testimony was obtained and no contemporary investigation was possible. GEIPAN classified the case C, explicitly noting that the age of the report and shortage of information made meaningful inquiry extremely difficult. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDIEPPE (76) .11.1989Témoignage récent (2 décembre 2009) d'une observation ancienne (courant novembre décembre 1989). Un témoin r... At Montivilliers on 25 April 1992, a witness described three lights forming a triangle and apparently rotating for about twenty minutes. Such a description sounds substantial, but the published file contains no further information capable of fixing the lights’ position, separation, altitude or movement. The result was again category C—not because investigators established the presence of a triangular vehicle, but because they had too little material to test the impression. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The Trouville file of 24 August 2011 demonstrates an even more complicated outcome. Investigators found that some recurring lights could be explained by aircraft, satellites and the bright star Arcturus undergoing strong atmospheric scintillation. Those parts of the report might have justified an A classification. However, the witnesses also described a dark mass and beams of white or purple light for which the file contained too few concrete details. GEIPAN therefore retained category C for the combined case. The classification did not imply that every reported feature had resisted explanation; it reflected an inseparable mixture of identified observations and poorly documented claims. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These cases show why witness sincerity and evidential strength must be treated separately. A person may accurately describe how an experience appeared while being unable to supply the measurements needed to identify its cause. Conversely, a detailed account written many years later may feel vivid but offer investigators little verifiable information. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Planets and stars that appeared to move Astronomical objects were among the most straightforward recurring explanations. The difficulty is not simply that witnesses failed to recognise a planet. Atmospheric conditions, low viewing angles and intervening buildings or clouds can make a fixed light appear larger, coloured, flickering or intermittently mobile. On 1 April 2017, three people in Rouen noticed a bright, low light that seemed to flash in several colours. The reporting witness compared it with a star but thought it looked unusually large and brilliant. GEIPAN checked the viewing direction and found Jupiter low in the south east, above the hospital building specified in the account. At that altitude, atmospheric turbulence and diffusion could produce pronounced colour changes, while mist or cloud could explain the light’s disappearance. The case was classed A as Jupiter. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The investigation also exposed an important weakness in apparently multiple witness reports. Although two other people were present, they did not provide their own questionnaires after GEIPAN requested them. The file therefore rested on one formal testimony rather than three independently comparable accounts. Investigators suggested that the other observers may not have experienced the same degree of strangeness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A 1980 Rouen case had a similar astronomical pattern. A witness and gendarmes observed two points brighter than ordinary stars. The lights seemed to rise slowly as the evening progressed and reappeared on subsequent clear nights. That repetition was highly significant: a genuinely transient object would not normally occupy a predictable position night after night, whereas celestial bodies follow regular apparent paths as the Earth rotates. GEIPAN judged the report a probable observation of astronomical objects and classified it B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These files show that apparent"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-f702d7/","description":"Press reports, shared expectations and a disputed Rouen photograph shaped the department's most famous early wave.","breadcrumb":"Why Seine Maritime's Strangest Sightings Rarely Prove...","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave What Fueled Seine Maritime's 1954 UFO Wave? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave What Fueled Seine Maritime's 1954 UFO Wave? Press reports, shared expectations and a disputed Rouen photograph shaped the department's most famous early wave. Press reports, shared expectations and a disputed Rouen photograph shaped the department's most famous early wave. event time window Sightings around Fecamp and the Pays de Caux The disputed photograph said to show Rouen How newspapers and expectation shaped the wave seine maritime 1954 wave Introduction The 1954 UFO wave in the Pays de Caux is best understood not as one prolonged encounter, but as a cluster of loosely documented reports that gathered force through repetition. Around Fécamp, residents described red discs, oval lights and luminous trails over two successive evenings in late September. Newspapers linked these observations together, praised some witnesses as particularly reliable and presented the absence of an immediate explanation as evidence that something genuinely mysterious was moving through the local sky. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 27 septembre, Fécamp, Seine Maritime... Reassessment weakens the more dramatic interpretation. The surviving accounts rarely preserve exact timings, viewing angles, weather conditions or independent records from coastal observers. No photograph, radar trace or physical evidence connects the Fécamp sightings. Meanwhile, the celebrated “Rouen photograph” commonly attached to the department’s 1954 history appears to have been misdated and may simply be a transformed copy of an older American UFO image. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 12 mars, Rouen, Seine Maritime... The wave still matters within Seine Maritime ’s UFO history, but mainly because it shows how sincere observations, uncertain celestial lights and press storytelling can combine into a memorable regional event. Sightings around Fécamp and the Pays de Caux The best documented local sequence began on the evening of Monday 27 September 1954. According to an article published a week later in Le Courrier Cauchois , a resident living near the sea reported seeing a red disc moving above Yport, roughly five kilometres south west of Fécamp. The semaphore lookout was alerted and prepared binoculars, but did not manage to observe the reported object. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 27 septembre, Fécamp, Seine Maritime... That detail is important. The involvement of a coastal watch station gave the story an air of official confirmation, yet the lookout did not independently verify the object. What survives is therefore one person’s statement, relayed through a newspaper, rather than a corroborated maritime observation. The following evening produced the report that became the centrepiece of the Pays de Caux wave. Alexandre Dubar, identified as the director of Fécamp’s municipal technical services, was said to be near the beach when he noticed two luminous, oval points towards the north west. They reportedly moved vertically, and other residents were later said to have made the same observation. A separate retrospective version added luminous trails and claimed that the display occurred twice. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 27 septembre, Fécamp, Seine Maritime... Dubar’s professional position strengthened the story rhetorically. The newspaper described him as unquestionably trustworthy and argued that a collective visual error could therefore be ruled out. That conclusion does not follow, however. Several sincere people can misinterpret the same distant light, especially when they are standing together, sharing directions and influencing one another’s attention. The newspaper did not provide the witnesses’ individual statements, establish whether they observed simultaneously or record what each person saw before discussing it with the others. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 27 septembre, Fécamp, Seine Maritime... The evidential gaps are substantial: No precise duration is consistently preserved. The supposed vertical movement was not measured against a fixed landmark. The brightness, apparent size and angular separation of the two lights were not recorded. There is no surviving photograph or instrument reading. The semaphore observation system did not confirm the first evening’s report. Later versions contain details not clearly present in the earliest surviving summary. These omissions do not show that witnesses invented the incident. They mean that ordinary explanations cannot now be tested properly. One reconstruction notes that Mars was low in the southern to south western sky during the first evening. Its direction does not neatly match every version of the Yport report, so it cannot be claimed as a firm solution. It nevertheless illustrates the difficulty of relying on approximate directions supplied after the event. A bright planet near the horizon can appear reddish, fluctuate in poor atmospheric conditions and seem to move when seen against haze or drifting cloud. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 27 septembre, Fécamp, Seine Maritime... The second evening’s north western lights invite other possibilities, including aircraft approaching or turning over the Channel, distant navigation lights, meteors or astronomical objects distorted near the horizon. None can be demonstrated from the available record. The responsible classification is therefore unresolved through lack of information , not unexplained after the elimination of ordinary causes. A wave built from separate stories Reports elsewhere in Seine Maritime helped turn isolated observations into a departmental “wave”. In Petit Couronne, near Rouen, ferry operator Jean Andrieux and Michel Leroux reportedly watched a large white light with a smaller green light beneath it for about twenty minutes. The object was said to descend slightly and depart silently towards the south west. Paris Normandie stressed that both men had sound reputations and were not prone to hallucination. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, saucers in the 1954 French Press... The structure of this report resembles the Fécamp coverage. A striking but poorly quantified light was followed by an assurance of witness respectability. The article offered no angular measurements, astronomical checks or comparison with aircraft traffic. Character references were effectively used as a substitute for technical evidence. Other Seine Maritime entries from October 1954 are even thinner. Catalogues contain brief reports associated with Yvetot, Dieppe, Aumale, Beuzeville la Guérard and smaller Pays de Caux communities, but many derive from short press items or later compilations. Some preserve little more than a place, approximate date and description such as a violent light, glowing object or unusual movement. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the 1954 French flap, octobre, Yvetot, Seine MaritimeOctober 21, 2011 — October 1954, Yvetot, Seine Maritime:... 1... Published: October 21, 2011 At least one departmental report demonstrates why contemporary clustering should not automatically be treated as evidence of a common cause. An object reported near Aumale on 16 October coincided with a large meteor seen across France at about the same time. A bright meteor can produce dramatically different descriptions depending on distance, viewing direction and whether witnesses see its main body, trail or fragmentation. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. An earlier episode on 7 January 1954 provides an even clearer comparison. A brilliant fireball was observed from several northern French locations, including parts of Seine Maritime, and was discussed by some members of the public in flying saucer terms. Contemporary astronomical opinion favoured a meteoric body, while coastal semaphore stations communicated along the Channel as the phenomenon was traced. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Open source on patrickgross.org. These examples do not explain every autumn report. They show instead that the label “wave” combines events of unequal quality and probably unequal origin. Some may have been astronomical; others may have involved aircraft, balloons or lights whose distance was misjudged. A few remain genuinely indeterminate because the reports are too incomplete. The disputed photograph said to show Rouen The strongest looking piece of evidence associated with Seine Maritime in 1954 is also the least securely connected to that year. The familiar image shows a dark, domed disc against an empty background. It has repeatedly been described as a UFO photographed over Rouen by an unnamed French military pilot in March 1954. Later books embellished"},{"title":"How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified...","url":"/how-seine-saint-denis-turned-ufos-into/","description":"Seine-Saint-Denis has produced a steady but not especially dramatic record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified... How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified Phenomena How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified... How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified... How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified Phenomena Seine Saint Denis has produced a steady but not especially dramatic record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena. Seine Saint Denis has produced a steady but not especially dramatic record of reported unidentified aerial phenomena. Its best documented cases do not point to a single local “flap”, secret military episode or persistent unexplained hotspot. seine saint denis Introduction The department’s most instructive incident occurred over Saint Denis in April 2009, when two witnesses reported a large, silent, oval object. The sighting initially appeared unusually strong because it involved two observers, a photograph, airport infrastructure and a police investigation. It was eventually traced to a 20 metre helium balloon intended for an event at the Stade de France. Other local reports have remained unresolved mainly because too little reliable information survived, rather than because investigators found strong evidence of extraordinary craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... What the official record actually contains France’s official body for examining such reports is GEIPAN, a unit of the national space agency CNES. It collects witness accounts, publishes case files and works with police, the gendarmerie, aviation authorities, defence organisations and specialist investigators. GEIPAN deliberately uses the broader term “unidentified aerial phenomenon” because a witness may have seen a light, reflection or atmospheric effect rather than a physical flying object. It does not investigate extraterrestrial life as such. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPAN places cases into practical evidence categories: A: the explanation is established with high confidence. B: a conventional explanation is probable but not fully proved. C: the evidence is too incomplete or unreliable for a sound conclusion. D1 or D2: the report remains unexplained despite having enough information to analyse, with D2 reserved for the stranger and better supported cases. This distinction matters in Seine Saint Denis. Several older entries are classified C, including reports from Les Lilas in 1977, Le Blanc Mesnil in 1981, Villemomble in 1986, Noisy le Sec in 1988, Gagny in 1996 and Rosny sous Bois in 2010. A C classification does not mean that investigators confirmed something inexplicable. It means that dates, directions, testimony, documentation or other essential details were insufficient. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The published department record also includes clearly identified events. Pantin’s May 1993 report was attributed to Venus. A Montfermeil sighting in August 2013 was classified as an Iridium satellite flare. More recent reports from Noisy le Grand and Neuilly sur Marne have involved artificial light displays rather than unknown vehicles. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANMONTFERMEIL (93) 19.08.2013 19/08/2013 A Seine Saint Denis Flash iridium 15/06/2015 2013 08 19... Published: August 19, 2013 The Saint Denis “saucer” that became a balloon At about 11am on 4 April 2009, two pedestrians in Saint Denis noticed what they described as a large grey oval object. It appeared to oscillate, produced no audible engine noise and left no visible trail. They watched it for around five minutes before it gradually disappeared towards the east. Taken by itself, that account had several familiar features of a classic UFO report: an apparently solid form, silent movement, prolonged observation and more than one witness. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... The case became more interesting when a journalist contacted GEIPAN with an anonymous photograph said to have been taken around midday near Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport. Examination of the image metadata indicated that it had actually been taken at 9.22am. Details visible in an enlargement, including a company logo on a hangar, placed the photographer near taxiways at Le Bourget Airport rather than at Charles de Gaulle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... Airport and police enquiries then provided the decisive evidence. Le Bourget’s control tower had recorded an inflatable flying saucer shaped balloon north of the airfield at approximately the same time. Police recovered the object and identified it as a helium balloon about 20 metres across, prepared for entertainment connected with a sporting event at the Stade de France. GEIPAN classified the report A: an identified balloon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... This remains the department’s landmark case because it demonstrates both the value and the limitations of witness testimony. The pedestrians were not necessarily careless or dishonest. A large balloon can move silently, rotate in the wind and appear to travel under its own control when its distance is uncertain. Their description was broadly compatible with what was physically present. What transformed the report from an intriguing mystery into an identified event was not speculation but the combination of image analysis, geographical reconstruction, airfield records and police recovery. The case also shows why proximity to airports does not automatically strengthen an extraordinary claim. Seine Saint Denis includes Le Bourget Airport and lies beneath some of the busiest airspace in Europe. Aviation infrastructure provides useful records and professional observers, but it also fills the sky with aircraft, balloons, lights and unusual viewing angles. Le Raincy and the missing searchlight source A more ambiguous case began at about 3.30am on 31 October 2009 in Le Raincy. A resident preparing to close his curtains noticed a flattened circular patch of light apparently tracing an elliptical route across the sky. A second light later entered the same circuit, seeming to pursue and pass through the first. The witness woke his partner, went outside and continued watching. He estimated that the display lasted about 90 minutes and was still visible when he went to bed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 Compte rendu enquete271 The unusual motion, silence and apparent transparency of the lights initially made them difficult for the witnesses to interpret. GEIPAN’s investigator, however, noticed characteristics typical of powerful rotating projectors: repeated paths, moving patches on cloud, no audible source and dark gaps where light could not reflect from thinner or broken cloud. A lighting specialist confirmed that two automated searchlights could produce irregular, apparently independent shapes several kilometres from their source. Buildings and trees could conceal the lower beams, leaving only the bright patches on the cloud base visible. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 Compte rendu enquete271 Investigators found that two such projectors had been rented for the opening of a large electronics shop at the Domus shopping centre in nearby Rosny sous Bois, less than three kilometres from the witnesses. Publicity confirmed that searchlights had been placed on the shopping centre’s roof. The awkward detail was timing: the organiser said they normally operated between 8pm and midnight, while the witnesses reported seeing the lights several hours later. Investigators could not confirm that those particular machines had remained active all night, and enquiries with local nightclubs and authorities produced no alternative documented source. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPAN therefore classified the case B rather than A. Its conclusion was that rotating searchlights probably caused the sighting, although the exact operating site and timetable could not be proved. This is a useful example of the difference between a plausible explanation and a demonstrated one. The observed behaviour closely matched projected light, but the final chain of evidence retained a gap. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 Compte rendu enquete271 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why distant displays look like objects in the clouds The same mechanism appeared again in a better documented report from Neuilly sur Marne on 26 August 2025. Beginning at 9.17pm, a resident watched coloured lights towards the east for nearly two hours. His wife and a neighbour also saw parts of the display. The phenomena included circular halos, a faint blue sphere, a short lived cone shaped beam and areas of blue and red"},{"title":"Light Displays","url":"/light-displays/","description":"Reports from Le Raincy and Neuilly-sur-Marne show how distant projectors can create silent, moving shapes that appear to exist inside clouds.","breadcrumb":"How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified...","level":2,"text":"Light Displays Why Moving Lights Look Like Objects in Clouds Seine Saint Denis Light Displays Light Displays Why Moving Lights Look Like Objects in Clouds Reports from Le Raincy and Neuilly sur Marne show how distant projectors can create silent, moving shapes that appear to exist inside clouds. Reports from Le Raincy and Neuilly sur Marne show how distant projectors can create silent, moving shapes that appear to exist inside clouds. mechanism The Le Raincy searchlight mystery The Disneyland display seen from Neuilly sur Marne How clouds hide the true light source seine saint denis light displays Introduction Several striking UFO reports from Seine Saint Denis were probably not sightings of objects at all. They were sightings of light striking cloud. When a powerful ground based projector sweeps across a low or broken cloud layer, the illuminated patch can appear to move independently, circle another light, stop abruptly or pass through a second “object”. Buildings and trees may hide the projector itself, while clean air between the source and the cloud leaves no visible beam connecting the two. The Le Raincy case of October 2009 and the Neuilly sur Marne case of August 2025 show this mechanism particularly well. The earlier incident remained only probably explained because investigators could not prove which installation was operating at the reported hour. The later case was supported by videos, direction measurements and a known Disneyland Paris performance, allowing GEIPAN, the French official investigation service, to classify it as conclusively identified. Together, they demonstrate why silence, complex movement and an apparently cloud level location are not reliable evidence of a solid craft. The Le Raincy searchlight mystery At about 3.30am on 31 October 2009, a resident of Le Raincy noticed a flattened luminous “ball” repeatedly tracing an elliptical path in the sky. A second light later entered the same circuit, appeared to chase the first and repeatedly crossed through it. The witness woke his partner, went outside and continued watching. He estimated that the display remained visible for at least an hour and a half and was still continuing when he went to bed at about 5am. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The report sounded more elaborate than a simple wandering spotlight. The two forms appeared milky white, partly opaque and partly perforated by dark gaps. They moved silently and followed recurring paths, yet seemed capable of overlapping without collision. The witness looked for an obvious beam or nearby source but found none. He therefore placed the phenomenon tens of metres above the house, effectively treating the illuminated area as a nearby object rather than as a distant patch on cloud. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... GEIPAN’s investigator saw several clues pointing in another direction. The repetitive elliptical motion, the silence and the apparent interpenetration of the lights were all compatible with two rotating sky facing projectors. The dark “holes” within the luminous forms could be openings or thin areas in broken cloud, where there was too little material to scatter light back towards the observers. What seemed to be a structured body was therefore potentially a changing intersection between beams and an uneven cloud base. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The suburban viewing geometry was crucial. The witnesses were surrounded by houses and other buildings, and their available view of the horizon was restricted. A specialist consulted during the inquiry explained that a multi beam projector could create irregular moving effects several kilometres from its installation. Once roofs, trees or walls blocked the source, a distant observer might see only the illuminated cloud patches, not the originating beam. The lights would then seem to exist by themselves in the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Investigators found a persuasive local lead. Two automatically rotating sky projectors had been hired for an electronics store opening at the Domus shopping centre in Rosny sous Bois, less than three kilometres from the witnesses’ home. An online advertisement showed that a sky projector had been installed on a shopping centre roof. The equipment had been present from 24 October until 1 November, covering the date of the sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The problem was timing. The event organiser said the projectors normally operated from 8pm until midnight, whereas the Le Raincy observation began around 3.30am. Investigators could not confirm that the Domus installation had continued through the night. Thirteen nearby nightclubs were contacted without producing an alternative source, and checks for relevant official authorisations also failed to identify one. Halloween celebrations or overnight equipment tests remained possible, but neither was established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... GEIPAN therefore classified Le Raincy as category B: a conventional explanation was probable but not fully demonstrated. That is an important distinction. The inquiry did not establish an unknown airborne vehicle, but neither did it prove the precise projector responsible. The strongest conclusion was narrower: the observed behaviour was characteristic of rotating light projected onto clouds, while the source identification remained incomplete. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete271 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The Disneyland display seen from Neuilly sur Marne A much better documented example occurred in Neuilly sur Marne on 26 August 2025. Beginning at 9.17pm, a resident watched coloured lights in the eastern sky for almost two hours. His wife and a neighbour also saw parts of the display. The principal witness recorded videos and telephoned the police, giving investigators considerably more material than was available in the Le Raincy file. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNEUILLY SUR MARNE (93) 26.08.2025 GEIPAN... The sequence was varied enough to suggest more than ordinary searchlights. Two luminous halos drew circles across the underside of the cloud. A dim bluish sphere appeared intermittently, followed by a briefly illuminated inverted cone shape. Later, separate parts of the sky glowed blue and then red. The effects appeared silent, confined to a particular sector and detached from any visible activity on the ground. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNEUILLY SUR MARNE (93) 26.08.2025 GEIPAN... The witness considered and rejected simple projectors, partly because no beam could be seen rising from the ground. He also doubted ordinary drones because the lights seemed to move within, behind or above the cloud layer. This response is understandable but illustrates the central perceptual trap: an observer can identify the cloud as the surface carrying the light yet still assume that the source must be physically near that surface. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNEUILLY SUR MARNE (93) 26.08.2025 GEIPAN... GEIPAN reconstructed the viewing direction as roughly 75 to 95 degrees, centred near 85 degrees east. That line pointed towards Disneyland Paris. The resort was presenting Disney Tales of Magic , a roughly 20 minute night time production based around Sleeping Beauty Castle and Main Street. Its 2025 staging employed 15 very bright lasers, 60 powerful lighting fixtures, projections, strobes, fountains, pyrotechnics and drones. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNEUILLY SUR MARNE (93) 26.08.2025 GEIPAN... The different reported forms matched different parts of such a production. Moving circular patches were consistent with scanning lights spreading across the underside of cloud. The inverted cone could result from several concentrated beams converging around the castle area. The later blue and red glows were compatible with broader lighting and pyrotechnic sequences. The witness was not seeing the show as its audience saw it; he was seeing selected fragments displaced onto cloud and stripped of their ground level context. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANNEUILLY SUR MARNE (93) 26.08.2025 GEIPAN... Duration does not undermine this explanation. Although the main performance lasted about 20 minutes, the witness’s two hour account contained changing episodes rather than one continuously identical object. Preparation, repeated lighting activity, the principal show and later effects can create a longer period of intermittent illumination when viewed from a distance. More importantly, the videos,"},{"title":"Saint Denis Case","url":"/saint-denis-case/","description":"A dramatic two-witness sighting became a model of how photographs, airport records and police recovery can resolve a UFO report.","breadcrumb":"How Seine Saint Denis Turned UFOs Into Identified...","level":2,"text":"Saint Denis Case How the Saint Denis Saucer Became a Balloon Seine Saint Denis Saint Denis Case Saint Denis Case How the Saint Denis Saucer Became a Balloon A dramatic two witness sighting became a model of how photographs, airport records and police recovery can resolve a UFO report. A dramatic two witness sighting became a model of how photographs, airport records and police recovery can resolve a UFO report. event time window What the witnesses reported How investigators traced the object What the case reveals about testimony seine saint denis saint denis case Introduction The Saint Denis sighting of 4 April 2009 is one of Seine Saint Denis ’s most useful UFO cases precisely because it did not remain unidentified. Two witnesses saw a large, silent, grey oval object apparently oscillating in the sky. A photograph later surfaced, airport radar records were checked, the camera position was reconstructed and the police recovered the object. It proved to be a 20 metre helium balloon shaped like a flying saucer and intended for an event at the Stade de France. GEIPAN, the French official body that examines unidentified aerial phenomena, classifies the case as conclusively explained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... The case matters because the original report was not absurd or obviously fabricated. Its strongest features — two observers, several minutes of viewing, a daylight photograph and proximity to major airports — initially made it appear unusually substantial. The investigation shows how seemingly persuasive evidence can still point towards the wrong interpretation until time, place, scale and independent records are brought together. What the witnesses reported At about 11am, two pedestrians in Saint Denis noticed a single grey or metallic object in the sky. They described it as oval or roughly spherical, with an apparent size comparable to an airliner. It seemed to oscillate around its axis and then move away without engine noise, exhaust or a visible trail. GEIPAN’s summary gives a viewing duration of approximately five minutes, ending as the object gradually disappeared towards the east. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... The witnesses were both recorded as being around 50 years old. Their reactions were characterised as active curiosity rather than panic, and both independently described one silent, solid looking object. One witness reported looking initially towards the south west and described a generally straight eastward movement. The other also compared its apparent size with that of an aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 T1 GEIPAN... Those details explain why the report sounded striking. In ordinary language, a large object that looks metallic, changes orientation and moves silently resembles the popular image of a flying saucer. Yet none of those features, taken alone, establishes that the object was powered, distant or technologically unusual. A drifting inflatable can reproduce the same impression: Its broad surface may appear metallic when lit by the sun. Rotation and deformation in the wind can look like controlled oscillation. A balloon has no engine and therefore produces no expected aircraft noise. Without a reliable distance reference, its true size, altitude and speed are difficult to judge. A purpose built saucer shape naturally encourages observers to describe what they see as a saucer. The witnesses’ comparison with an airliner is therefore best understood as an estimate of apparent size, not a measurement. An observer who assumes that an object is far away will tend to infer that it is very large. If it is nearer than assumed, the same visual angle can be produced by a much smaller object. How investigators traced the object The identification did not come from a single lucky guess. It emerged from several separate lines of inquiry that gradually corrected the original assumptions about the sighting. The photograph changed the investigation On 10 April, six days after the sighting, a journalist contacted GEIPAN about an anonymous photograph said to have been taken at around noon near Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport. The image showed an oval object in daylight, apparently illuminated consistently with the rest of the scene. An initial technical examination found no visible sign that the object had been digitally inserted or that the image had been assembled from separate elements. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... The camera file contained a more precise time: 9.22am on 4 April. The photograph was therefore genuine evidence of an object present that morning, but not evidence that every detail supplied with the image was correct. The reported time was inaccurate, and the claimed location soon proved inaccurate as well. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. An early image analysis treated the supposed Roissy location as provisional. Using brightness comparisons and atmospheric scattering assumptions, the analyst tentatively estimated that the photographed object might have been 150–200 metres from the camera, 15–25 metres above the ground and only 50–80 centimetres across. Crucially, the document labelled these calculations provisional and recommended a local inquiry, better geometry and wind data before drawing a conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Those initial dimensions did not survive the later identification. This is not evidence that the inquiry failed. It illustrates how calculations can produce misleading precision when their starting assumptions — particularly the photographer’s position and the scene’s geometry — are uncertain. Balloon Boy Hoax EXPLAINED The Truth Behind the Viral Panic Channel: Factual America Podcast Open on YouTube A hangar logo relocated the photograph Civil aviation personnel could not match the image comfortably to Charles de Gaulle Airport. A small background detail eventually broke the impasse: an enlarged view revealed the logo of an aviation company on a hangar. The company had premises at Le Bourget but not at Roissy. Investigators used the hangar’s appearance and orientation to place the photographer near taxiways W2, F3 or V2 at Le Bourget, probably facing south. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This correction was decisive. “A strange object photographed at Roissy” and “a strange object photographed at Le Bourget” were not interchangeable claims. Once the actual location was recognised, investigators could compare the image with records from the correct airport and with activity in the surrounding area. The episode also gives the photograph its real evidential value. Its most important information was not the blurry oval in the sky but the ordinary structures near the edge of the frame. A logo, hangar and taxiway layout provided firmer geographical evidence than the anonymous description accompanying the picture. Radar neither proved nor disproved the balloon A review of radar records initially found no unidentified primary return above Roissy or within 15 nautical miles of the airport; the recorded detections were correlated with known traffic. That result weakened the idea of a substantial unknown aircraft operating near Charles de Gaulle, but it did not by itself identify the object. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Nor should the absence of an unidentified radar return be treated as evidence that nothing physical was present. Radar visibility depends on an object’s materials, size, altitude, orientation and position within coverage. A low level inflatable need not appear as a conspicuous, unexplained aircraft track. The radar check was useful mainly because it tested the original airport claim and found no corresponding unknown traffic. Once the photograph was relocated to Le Bourget, aviation personnel revisited the relevant direction. The developing inquiry was no longer trying to reconcile the picture with unexplained activity at Roissy; it was examining a low altitude object in the vicinity of another airfield. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Airport and police records supplied the answer The strongest evidence came from records independent of the two witnesses and the anonymous photographer. The gendarmerie inquiry established that at 11am — the time of the Saint Denis observation — the Le Bourget control tower had reported a flying saucer shaped inflatable balloon north of the airfield, outside the airport zone. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANSAINT DENIS (93) 04.04.2009 GEIPAN... The object was subsequently brought down or otherwise neutralised and taken into the custody of the territorial police. It was identified as a helium filled balloon about 20 metres in diameter, prepared as entertainment for a sporting event at the Stade de France. GEIPAN accordingly records the phenomenon as a balloon and places the"},{"title":"Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers","url":"/why-sommes-ufo-record-still-resists-easy/","description":"Somme has a modest but unusually instructive UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landing” or a large modern wave.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers Somme has a modest but unusually instructive UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landing” or a large modern wave. Somme has a modest but unusually instructive UFO history. Its official record is not dominated by one celebrated “landing” or a large modern wave. Instead, it contains a mixture of strong historical testimony, military observations, poorly documented reports and later cases that investigators could explain as planets, aircraft, lanterns or lasers. somme Introduction This mixed record matters because it shows how the meaning of “unidentified” changes from case to case. Some Somme reports remain open because several witnesses described genuinely unusual events. Others are unclassified in any useful sense because investigators lacked reliable information. A third group became less mysterious once direction, weather, astronomy and flight data were checked. The department’s history therefore supports neither blanket belief nor blanket dismissal: it rewards careful attention to witness quality, contemporary documents and the difference between an unexplained case and an uninvestigable one. The 1954 wave reached Somme early France experienced an exceptional concentration of “flying saucer” reports in the autumn of 1954. Somme’s official files record cases at Harponville and Contay, Le Crotoy, Hem HardINVAL, Doullens and Long, while contemporary newspaper material also carried reports from the Abbeville area. The clustering is historically significant, but it does not prove that all the witnesses saw the same phenomenon. The reports vary greatly in detail, distance and evidential quality. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; BERNAVILLE (80) 16.01.1980, 16/01/1980, B; FLIXECOURT (80) 1980, 08/05/1980, B; ABBEVILL... Local press coverage helped create the sense of a regional “flap”. An Abbeville report published in October described an elderly woman’s alleged sighting above the Saint Paul marshes, but the newspaper treated the story with conspicuous humour. Another article announced that mysterious objects were again crossing the Picardy sky after a supposed lull. These pieces are useful evidence of what people were saying, but they are weaker as evidence of what was physically present: some accounts were second hand, brief or shaped by the playful tone of saucer era journalism. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org France 1954, mi septembre, Abbeville, SommeUn jeune garçon boucher était venu dire au bureau de ce journal qu'une femme d'Abbevil... GEIPAN, the official French unit within the national space agency CNES, classifies reports according to both their remaining strangeness and the consistency of the available evidence. Category A cases are securely explained; B cases have a highly probable explanation; C cases lack enough dependable information; and D cases remain unexplained after analysis. This distinction is essential when reading the Somme files. A category C entry is not necessarily more mysterious than a category B one—it is often simply less useful. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... Harponville–Contay: Somme’s strongest ground level case On the morning of 7 September 1954, two men were cycling to work along the road between Harponville and Contay. According to the gendarmerie account retained by GEIPAN, the weather was clear and the witnesses saw a dark, circular or cylindrical form in a field roughly 200 metres from the road. Its upper surface appeared slightly domed, and the object seemed to oscillate. The report was made early in the French wave, before the largest burst of national publicity later that month. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The case is important because it was not a fleeting point of light seen at night. It involved two witnesses, daylight conditions, a claimed object near the ground and a formal police record. GEIPAN still places it in category D, describing it as strange to very strange and supported by evidence of medium to strong consistency. Within Somme’s official archive, it is one of the clearest examples of a report that is unresolved rather than merely incomplete. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That classification does not establish that a manufactured craft was present. The surviving description contains no photograph, instrument reading or physical trace that can now be independently tested. Distance and dimensions were estimated by observers in an unexpected situation, and later summaries cannot recover details that were never measured. Nor does the file demonstrate occupants, take off performance or any connection with aviation or military activity. The case’s strength is therefore comparative. It is stronger than a solitary light report because there were two observers, daylight and a specific apparent location. Its weakness is that nearly all the evidence remains testimonial. The most balanced judgement is that something unusual was reported with reasonable consistency, but the surviving record is not sufficient to identify what it was. Doullens: military witnesses without radar confirmation The Doullens incident of 19 October 1954 is the department’s most important military connected case. Between about 1.30 and 1.40 in the morning, several servicemen at a radar station—including a private, a corporal major and a sergeant—reported an unusual aerial phenomenon beneath a continuous layer of cloud. The account developed in three phases, with different witnesses seeing portions of the event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The witnesses’ military setting gives the report historical interest, but it is often misunderstood. The station’s radar was not operating that night, so the incident was not a visual observation confirmed by radar. The personnel were associated with a radar installation; they did not obtain a radar track. That missing distinction substantially limits claims that Somme produced a verified military sensor case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The cloud layer is also important. A luminous source below or against low cloud can appear enlarged, diffused or structured, while uncertain distance makes speed and size difficult to judge. Conversely, the number and occupational background of the observers make casual invention less likely and provide more than a single unsupported recollection. GEIPAN continues to classify Doullens as a D case with relatively substantial consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; AMIENS (80) 15.02.1982, 15/02/1982, C · Somme, Manque d'informations fiables; MOREUIL (80... The fairest conclusion is that Doullens remains an unresolved visual military report, not a radar confirmed encounter. Its lasting value lies in the contemporary testimony and multiple witnesses. Its central evidential gap is the absence of working instrumentation, photographs or physical effects capable of deciding between an unusual atmospheric or aviation event and something genuinely novel. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Roye shows how a dramatic account can become ordinary At 12.05 a.m. on 13 May 1980, two gendarmes patrolling the A1 motorway near the Roye toll area noticed a luminous object. Another officer at their control centre confirmed that a light was visible. One witness attempted to photograph it, but the developed film contained no usable image. As the patrol continued north, the light seemed to accompany the vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Taken alone, the account sounds impressive: police witnesses, apparent motion and an unsuccessful photographic attempt. GEIPAN’s later assessment, however, identifies the phenomenon as Venus and classifies the case A. The impression that the object followed the car is consistent with the well known effect produced by a very distant light whose direction changes little as an observer moves. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; AMIENS (80) 15.02.1982, 15/02/1982, C · Somme, Manque d'informations fiables; MOREUIL (80... Roye is one of the most useful Somme cases because it demonstrates why witness credibility and object identification are separate questions. The gendarmes need not have been careless or dishonest. A bright planet viewed under unfamiliar circumstances can appear strikingly close, stationary or apparently mobile, especially when the observer lacks reliable distance"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-5cfde0/","description":"Somme's 1954 sightings ranged from humorous press stories to two well-documented encounters that remain officially unexplained.","breadcrumb":"Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave What Made Somme's 1954 UFO Wave Different? Somme UFO Files 1954 Wave 1954 Wave What Made Somme's 1954 UFO Wave Different? Somme's 1954 sightings ranged from humorous press stories to two well documented encounters that remain officially unexplained. Somme's 1954 sightings ranged from humorous press stories to two well documented encounters that remain officially unexplained. event time window How the national wave reached Somme Harponville Contay and the daylight roadside encounter Doullens and the limits of military testimony somme 1954 wave Introduction Autumn 1954 gave Somme its most important concentration of unidentified aerial reports, but the wave was not a single coherent mystery. It mixed rumours, newspaper jokes, mistaken balloons and poorly documented lights with two substantially stronger incidents: the daylight Harponville–Contay encounter of 7 September and the military observation at Doullens on 19 October. Both are still classified as unexplained by GEIPAN, the French government’s specialist unit for unidentified aerospace phenomena. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... What made these two cases different was not simply that witnesses found them strange. Harponville–Contay involved two men watching a structured object at relatively close range for several minutes in clear daylight, followed by a prompt gendarmerie investigation. Doullens involved several servicemen at a radar station who independently observed a coloured phenomenon beneath cloud cover. Neither case proves that an extraordinary craft visited Somme. Both nevertheless contain enough detail and witness consistency to resist the easy explanations that account for weaker reports from the same period. How the national wave reached Somme France experienced an exceptional surge of flying saucer stories during September and October 1954. Reports of discs, luminous balls, cigar shaped objects and alleged landings appeared almost daily in the press. Somme became part of that national atmosphere very early: the Harponville–Contay incident occurred on 7 September, before many of the country’s most widely publicised cases and before the wave reached its greatest intensity. Contemporary newspapers were reporting the Somme encounter by 9 September. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... The timing matters. Harponville–Contay cannot easily be dismissed as a late imitation produced after weeks of overwhelming local publicity. Flying saucer ideas were already familiar in France, but this particular report preceded the most intense phase of the national wave. That strengthens the case against a simple explanation based on witnesses copying the detailed form of a recently celebrated incident. It does not remove the possibility of expectation, exaggeration or misidentification. Newspaper coverage was already shaping how unusual objects were described, and once the Harponville story appeared it helped stimulate further rumours within Somme. On the following evening, claims circulated that another saucer had landed near Estrées Deniécourt and Foucaucourt en Santerre. Reports spoke of numerous observers and even a man who had touched the object, but an official inquiry encountered reluctant or disappearing witnesses. Later accounts treated the episode as a prank for which the alleged participants admitted responsibility. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 8 septembre, Estrées Deniecourt, Somme... That rapid progression—from a serious report to an apparent copycat story—captures the difficulty of interpreting a “wave”. A cluster may contain sincere observations, ordinary objects recast as saucers, rumours that become more detailed as they spread, and deliberate jokes. The reports are historically connected through publicity and public attention, but they need not share one physical cause. Somme’s newspaper record also contains examples of stories that lost their mystery quickly. In October, debris from an object said to have exploded near Boismont, close to Saint Valery sur Somme, was identified as material from a Royal Air Force weather balloon. It was initially absorbed into the saucer narrative but became an example of how unfamiliar aviation or meteorological equipment could generate convincing local excitement. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Another false saucer!\" The mysterious craft which broke out in the Picardy sky, and whose debris was scattered in a pasture at Boismont... Other entries survive with so little information that no responsible conclusion is possible. A later catalogue lists reports from Long, Abbeville and Arry at the end of October, but describes some of them as wholly insufficient for assessment and notes a possible meteor in the Arry case. Such fragments establish that people were watching the sky; they do not establish that anything exceptional was present. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org 31 Long, Somme. Oct. 31 Abbeville, Somme. Evening. Totally insufficient information. Oct. 16 Ham, Somme. Evening. Oct. 16 Saint Sulpice... The value of the 1954 Somme wave therefore lies less in its raw number of reports than in the contrast between them. Harponville–Contay and Doullens stand out precisely because most neighbouring stories were weaker, second hand, humorous, admitted hoaxes or eventually explained. Harponville–Contay: the daylight roadside encounter At about 7.30 am on 7 September 1954, two men were cycling to work on the minor road between Harponville and Contay. GEIPAN’s summary describes clear weather without fog. In a field approximately 200 metres from the road, the witnesses noticed a dark circular form that appeared to be at or immediately above ground level and was moving with a slight oscillation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... The men did not merely glimpse it while passing. They approached until they were roughly 100 metres away. Their descriptions referred to a broad cylindrical or disc like body with a slightly domed upper surface and an opening around its side. Contemporary retellings compared its shape to an unfinished haystack with an upturned plate placed on top—a rural comparison that conveyed form rather than claiming technical knowledge. Later sources commonly estimated the object at about ten metres across and three metres high, although measurements made by witnesses at an uncertain distance should be treated cautiously. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... The reported departure is the most unusual part of the account. The object supposedly rose silently at an angle for about 15 metres, then climbed vertically. The witnesses said that grey blue smoke escaped from a pipe like feature underneath as it ascended. The entire observation lasted about four or five minutes, after which the frightened men went directly to the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... Several features make this Somme’s strongest civilian case: Two witnesses were present. Their agreement does not guarantee accuracy, but it reduces the likelihood of a wholly private illusion or invented experience. The observation occurred in daylight and clear weather. This removes many common night sky ambiguities involving bright planets, stars and distant lights. The duration was substantial. Several minutes offered more opportunity for inspection than a meteor, flash or rapidly passing aircraft would allow. The object appeared structured. The witnesses described shape, movement and a possible opening rather than only a light. The report was made promptly. Immediate contact with the gendarmerie is more valuable than an account reconstructed decades later. Investigators did not question the men’s general credibility. GEIPAN records no reason to regard them as dishonest. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... The case also has important weaknesses. The gendarmes found no marks in the field, despite the initial impression that the object had been on or close to the ground. No independent witness was located, no photograph was taken and no material evidence was recovered. The object’s dimensions and distance were estimates, while descriptions reproduced in later UFO books acquired small discrepancies in the witnesses’ names, the time and whether the object was actually resting on the field. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[CD47] DE HARPONVILLE (80) VERS CONTAY (80) 07.09.1954 GEIPAN... A helicopter is the most frequently discussed conventional comparison. Oscillation, hovering, a door like feature and a climbing departure could all suggest a rotary wing aircraft seen from an unfamiliar angle. One witness reportedly said that he had seen helicopters"},{"title":"Case Verdicts","url":"/case-verdicts/","description":"Official files show why some Somme reports remain unexplained while others became planets, aircraft, lanterns, lasers or unusable evidence.","breadcrumb":"Why Somme's UFO Record Still Resists Easy Answers","level":2,"text":"Case Verdicts Why Some Somme UFO Cases Stay Unexplained Somme UFO Files Case Verdicts Case Verdicts Why Some Somme UFO Cases Stay Unexplained Official files show why some Somme reports remain unexplained while others became planets, aircraft, lanterns, lasers or unusable evidence. Official files show why some Somme reports remain unexplained while others became planets, aircraft, lanterns, lasers or unusable evidence. dataset evidence What GEIPAN categories actually mean The difference between unexplained and uninvestigable How later checks resolved dramatic sightings somme case verdicts Introduction Somme ’s official UFO record is less a catalogue of mysteries than a demonstration of how evidence changes a verdict. Some reports that sounded dramatic at first were later matched to Venus, airliners, advertising lasers, the International Space Station or floating lanterns. Other cases were placed in category C because photographs, timings or witness detail were too poor to support any conclusion. Only a small historical core, notably Harponville–Contay and Doullens in 1954, remains officially unexplained after review. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le temps est... That distinction matters. In GEIPAN’s system, “unexplained” does not mean “extraterrestrial”, while “lacking an explanation” does not automatically mean a case is genuinely strange. A report can remain unidentified because it is strong enough to resist ordinary explanations, or because it is too incomplete to investigate. Somme contains clear examples of both outcomes, as well as cases in which later calculations substantially weakened the original interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan methodologie classification geipan What the GEIPAN categories actually mean GEIPAN, the French national space agency’s specialist unit for unidentified aerospace phenomena, classifies cases by combining two questions. First, how well does a known explanation account for the observation after investigation? Second, how consistent is the evidence, meaning how much reliable information is actually available? Consistency can be strengthened by prompt reporting, several independent witnesses, precise directions, photographs, radar records or detailed contemporary documents. It is weakened by long delays, vague times, uncertain locations, dependent witnesses and poor quality images. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan methodologie classification geipan The familiar letter grades therefore describe different evidential situations: Category A means that the phenomenon has been identified with little or no remaining ambiguity. Category B means that one explanation is considered highly probable, but a detail needed for certainty is missing. Category C means that the evidence is not usable enough to reach a sound conclusion. Category D means that the case remains unexplained despite having enough information to merit analysis. Some D cases are further divided into D1 and D2 according to their strangeness and evidential strength. D1 generally denotes an unusual but moderately supported report; D2 requires both high strangeness and much stronger consistency, such as independent testimony, recordings or physical traces. GEIPAN also stresses that classifications other than a firmly established A can change when new information or better analytical methods become available. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan methodologie classification geipan This prevents two common misunderstandings. Category B is not simply a dismissive label: investigators may have reconstructed a highly convincing explanation but lack the data needed to identify the exact aircraft or lantern launch. Conversely, category C is not a reservoir of especially puzzling UFOs. It often contains the weakest reports in the archive. Unexplained is not the same as uninvestigable The contrast between Somme’s category C and category D files is the clearest way to understand the department’s record. Both groups lack a final identification, but for almost opposite reasons. A D case contains enough coherent material for investigators to recognise that something unusual was reported, yet no ordinary hypothesis adequately accounts for the whole description. A C case fails earlier in the process: investigators cannot establish the geometry, chronology or reliability required to compare the event properly with aircraft, astronomical objects or atmospheric effects. GEIPAN describes category C as an inability to conclude, not as evidence that the reported phenomenon defied explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr methodologie classification geipan methodologie classification geipan The 1996 Allery case is a straightforward Somme example. An amateur astronomer noticed unexplained dark traces on photographs taken while observing a comet. The images were too difficult to interpret, and there was no separate visual observation or dependable information establishing when and how the marks were produced. GEIPAN therefore classified the case C for lack of reliable evidence. The traces remained unidentified, but there was no strong basis for treating them as objects in the sky rather than photographic, processing or equipment related artefacts. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several reports from the 1954 wave have the same evidential problem. Cases at Le Crotoy, Hem HardINVAL and Long were preserved because witnesses described unusual discs or coloured lights, but the surviving information was insufficient for a meaningful modern reconstruction. Missing angular measurements, incomplete timings and limited corroboration make it impossible to test many possible explanations. Their category C status says more about the condition of the historical files than about the physical nature of what was seen. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr baisse cas d baisse cas d This is especially important with old newspaper reports. A vivid phrase such as “flying disc” can outlive the practical details needed to assess it: exact viewing direction, duration, cloud cover, whether the witness was moving and whether another observer saw the phenomenon independently. Once those details are lost, neither believers nor sceptics can reconstruct the event securely. Why Harponville–Contay stayed open The Harponville–Contay encounter of 7 September 1954 remained unexplained because its surviving record is substantially richer than most category C reports. Two men cycling to work independently described a dark circular or cylindrical form in a field about 200 metres from the road. They approached to roughly 100 metres and said that it rose silently, first at an angle and then vertically, with grey blue smoke apparently emerging from a tube. Their observation reportedly lasted four or five minutes, and they went promptly to the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le temps est... Those features give investigators more to work with than a single distant light. There were two direct witnesses, a daylight setting, an approximate distance, a prolonged observation and a contemporary police record. Yet the file contains no photograph, radar record or physical object. Investigators found no trace in the field, while estimates of size and distance still depended on human perception. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le temps est... Ordinary possibilities can be proposed, but none has been demonstrated from the surviving evidence. Agricultural machinery, a balloon, an unusual aircraft or a misunderstanding of a more distant object might account for selected features, but each leaves difficulties, particularly the reported rise from the field, silence and apparent exhaust. The official D verdict therefore means that GEIPAN did not find an explanation with sufficient probability. It does not certify that a structured craft landed there, still less establish its origin. The lack of ground traces is relevant but not decisive. It weakens any claim that a heavy object physically rested in the field, yet the witnesses’ account does not provide enough technical information to estimate its weight, contact area or exact position. The case remains one of Somme’s stronger historical reports because the testimony is comparatively detailed, not because it contains conclusive material evidence. Why Doullens remained difficult The Doullens case of 19 October 1954 is open for different reasons. Several military personnel at a radar station saw a coloured luminous phenomenon beneath an estimated stratocumulus layer. The account describes rapid movement, an abrupt stop, several minutes of apparent hovering and repeated colour changes. More than one observer saw the stationary phase after the sentry alerted his superiors. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOULLENS (80) 19.10.1954Le mardi 19 octobre 1954 entre 1h30 et 1h40 plusieurs militaires (dont un 2e classe, un caporal chef et un... The witnesses’ military roles and the structured reporting add"},{"title":"Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case","url":"/tarn-et-garonne/","description":"Tarn-et-Garonne has produced memorable UFO reports, but its official record is more instructive than sensational.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case Tarn et Garonne has produced memorable UFO reports, but its official record is more instructive than sensational. Tarn et Garonne has produced memorable UFO reports, but its official record is more instructive than sensational. The department’s best known incident occurred beside the Golfech nuclear power station in 2010, where two workers reported a large triangular arrangement of lights. tarn et garonne Introduction That distinction runs through the department’s UFO history. Reports from Montauban, Mas Grenier, Pompignan, Valence d’Agen and other communes have later been linked to meteors, aircraft, the Moon, birds and illuminated balloons. Several older accounts remain unresolved only because too little dependable information survives. The resulting picture is not of a sustained local “flap” supported by radar, photographs or physical evidence, but of scattered observations whose evidential value varies sharply. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Aerospace Phenomena) sightings... Golfech: the department’s strongest modern case On the evening of 6 October 2010, two contractors working at the Golfech nuclear power station reported seeing a dark or indistinct triangular phenomenon marked by three pale lights and a red central light. Its apparent proximity to a sensitive nuclear site made the story unusually compelling, and online discussion later helped turn it into Tarn et Garonne’s most prominent UFO case. Local newspaper coverage in 2011 described the rapidly growing internet interest around the incident. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Tarn et Garonne... D’un autre côté, l’hypothèse de l’avion, proposée dans un premier temps par le GEIPAN, souffre de plusieurs incohéren... GEIPAN’s investigation was more cautious. An aircraft was initially considered, but investigators found problems with that explanation. Accepting it would require substantial errors in the witnesses’ reconstruction and an aircraft flying low enough to be conspicuous while escaping both radar detection and the power station’s surveillance arrangements. Access to the site enabled a further investigation in October 2013, several years after the observation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Tarn et Garonne... D’un autre côté, l’hypothèse de l’avion, proposée dans un premier temps par le GEIPAN, souffre de plusieurs incohéren... This sounds, at first, like a strong unresolved case. Yet GEIPAN ultimately placed Golfech in category C, meaning that the available information was not sufficiently reliable or complete to support a firm analysis. Category C does not mean “confirmed unexplained craft”. GEIPAN treats such files separately from cases that remain anomalous after a sufficiently detailed investigation: they are cases on which investigators cannot responsibly decide. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 06/10/2010 C Tarn et Garonne Manque d'informations fiables 17/05/2016 ... Several weaknesses explain that restraint: The observation was brief and occurred at night. The apparent triangular outline may have been inferred from the positions of lights rather than clearly seen as a solid body. No publicly established photograph, video or physical trace confirms the reported shape. The investigation took place long after the event, limiting checks of transient circumstances. The failure to identify a matching aircraft does not, by itself, establish that the lights belonged to an extraordinary vehicle. Golfech therefore remains important because investigators could not make the early aircraft hypothesis fit comfortably, not because evidence demonstrated an exotic origin. Later investigation preserved the uncertainty rather than strengthening the claim into a category D case. Most reported objects became ordinary phenomena The wider Tarn et Garonne record shows why individual descriptions cannot safely be taken at face value. GEIPAN compares testimony with astronomical conditions, aircraft movements, meteor activity, photographs, geography and other available data. Its categories distinguish identified cases, probable identifications, inadequate files and cases retaining significant unexplained features after investigation. The classification depends both on “strangeness” and on consistency—the quantity and reliability of the information gathered. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN... Montauban’s silent light At about 10.10 pm on 9 September 2011, several people in one Montauban neighbourhood reported a fast, silent luminous phenomenon moving from south to north. GEIPAN classified the event B: probably a bright meteor, or bolide. A meteor can appear much larger and nearer than it is, while its high altitude means that no corresponding sound is necessarily heard during the visual event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The case is a useful reminder that multiple witnesses do not automatically rule out an ordinary explanation. Several people watching the same unusual meteor may give a report greater consistency, but they are still observing one natural event under the same difficult viewing conditions. A separate regional event on 2 August 2011 produced an even broader cluster of reports across southern France. Witnesses described a powerful flash visible from Tarn et Garonne, Haute Garonne, Tarn, Ariège and Aude; one lorry driver saw the area illuminated while travelling between Montauban and Castelsarrasin. GEIPAN identified the phenomenon as a bolide rather than a local craft. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Ovni: de nouveaux témoignages Ovni: de nouveaux témoignages Mas Grenier’s “jellyfish” On 8 August 2020, witnesses near Mas Grenier reported a silent, low looking object with blue halos and an appearance compared to a jellyfish. It seemed to pass above a cherry tree, move in a straight line with an undulating motion and fade towards the southern horizon after roughly 30 seconds. The vivid description could easily support an exotic retelling. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. GEIPAN instead classified the case A and identified the object as an illuminated balloon. The apparent waviness, silence and self contained glow were compatible with a light, drifting object viewed at night. This is one of the clearest local examples of an initially strange form becoming less mysterious once its motion and lighting are considered together. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The Moon, aircraft and birds Other department files show the range of familiar objects capable of generating UFO reports: An observation at Albias on 17 January 2009 was classified A and attributed to the Moon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A report made while travelling on the A62 from Valence d’Agen towards Toulouse on 11 August 2013 was classified A and identified as an airliner. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A Pompignan observation on 17 November 2016 was also classified A, although the search listing does not expose the identified phenomenon in its summary. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. At Valence d’Agen on 9 April 2023, a couple saw a phenomenon while in the garden of a residential care home. GEIPAN’s later investigation classified it B and judged a bird the probable explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The Valence d’Agen case is especially useful in an age of phone imagery. A recorded object may look structured or fast because of distance, focus, digital enlargement and the lack of fixed visual references. Video does not remove ambiguity automatically; it becomes valuable only when investigators can establish scale, direction, duration and the camera’s behaviour. Weak files are not hidden mysteries Several Tarn et Garonne cases carry category C classifications. These include Montauban on 6 October 1980, Cazals on 28 July 1986, Moissac in September 1998, Réalville on 13 January 2009 and Golfech in 2010. Their shared classification means that investigators lacked enough dependable information to reach a credible conclusion. It does not mean that each case resisted all conventional explanations. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. This matters because UFO catalogues often flatten very different outcomes into a single label such as “unexplained”. A poorly documented light seen decades ago is not evidentially equivalent to a well recorded event checked against radar, weather, astronomy and aviation data. GEIPAN explicitly warns that category C cases sit outside the normal progression from identified to genuinely"},{"title":"Golfech Case","url":"/golfech-case/","description":"The Golfech sighting remains compelling because an aircraft explanation fitted poorly, yet the surviving evidence was too weak for a firm conclusion.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case","level":2,"text":"Golfech Case Why the Golfech UFO Case Remains Unresolved Tarn et Garonne Golfech Case Golfech Case Why the Golfech UFO Case Remains Unresolved The Golfech sighting remains compelling because an aircraft explanation fitted poorly, yet the surviving evidence was too weak for a firm conclusion. The Golfech sighting remains compelling because an aircraft explanation fitted poorly, yet the surviving evidence was too weak for a firm conclusion. event time window What the two workers reported Why the aircraft theory caused problems Why GEIPAN still classified the file as inadequate tarn et garonne golfech case Introduction The Golfech case remains unresolved, but not in the strongest sense of that word. At 20:17 on 6 October 2010, two contractors outside a building at the Golfech nuclear power station in Tarn et Garonne reported a dark triangular phenomenon carrying three flashing white lights and a flashing red light near its centre. One witness recorded about 23 seconds on a mobile phone. An aircraft seemed the obvious explanation, yet GEIPAN—the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena—later found that the available radar, geometry and witness estimates did not make an ordinary aircraft fit comfortably. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 GEIPAN... That did not make the event a confirmed unexplained craft. The recording failed to capture the claimed object clearly, the witnesses disagreed over its interpretation, important distance and altitude estimates were unreliable, and investigators lacked access to potentially decisive surveillance material. GEIPAN therefore classified Golfech as category C: unidentified because the information was insufficiently reliable, rather than category D for a phenomenon left unexplained after an adequately supported investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 GEIPAN... What the two workers reported The witnesses, identified publicly only as T1 and T2, were taking a break near an administrative building at the entrance to the power station. They described lights moving across the night sky and associated them with a black triangular form. According to GEIPAN’s final summary, the reported configuration consisted of three flashing white lights arranged around a flashing red light. Both witnesses said the phenomenon was silent, although investigators later noted that pumps, ventilation and other machinery created substantial background noise at the site. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 GEIPAN... T1 gave the more extraordinary account. He described a large, sharply defined, matt black triangular object, apparently very low and at times almost overhead. His estimates placed it only tens or hundreds of metres away, with an apparent width of roughly 10 to 15 degrees—many times the apparent diameter of the Moon. He reported acceleration and changes in direction but no engine noise, airflow, smell or environmental effect. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 T1 GEIPAN... T2 broadly agreed on the visible arrangement—a triangular appearance, white lights and a central red light—but was far less certain about what it represented. On the recording itself he repeatedly suggested that it might be an aircraft or a meteorological object. In his subsequent gendarmerie statement, he again identified it as an aircraft. During a later outside interview, however, he described a triangular object and said that the experience had disturbed him. GEIPAN regarded these shifts as a serious obstacle to judging his testimony. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This distinction matters. The two men did not simply provide two independent, matching accounts of an extraordinary vehicle. They were together, reacted to each other in real time and strongly disagreed over the meaning of what they saw. T1 was already interested in UFOs; T2 later told the gendarmes that his colleague’s enthusiasm may have influenced the incident. Conversely, T2’s changing explanations may partly reflect a desire to distance himself from the publicity. The evidence does not establish which influence was stronger. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr Une histoire d'ovni à la centrale de Golfech fait le buzz sur internet Une histoire d'ovni à la centrale de Golfech fait le buzz sur internet The video recorded the wrong lights The mobile phone film initially appeared to give Golfech an advantage over a testimony only case. In practice, it became one of the main reasons for caution. The file lasted just over 23 seconds and measured only 176 by 144 pixels, at fewer than eight frames per second with heavy compression. Frame by frame analysis found no detectable black triangular surface and no red light within the supposed triangle. Several visible light points remained fixed relative to one another when the camera moved, contradicting the idea that the recorded group itself was travelling continuously across the sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... GEIPAN compared the filmed points with the power station’s lighting layout during its later site visit. The positions corresponded closely to fixed lamp posts. Investigators concluded that the prominent “triangle” visible in the video consisted of ground lights, not the aerial phenomenon described by the witnesses. The recording may contain a much fainter residual light higher in the frame, but the image quality was too poor to determine whether this was part of the reported event, a reflection within the camera or another artefact. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... This does not necessarily prove that nothing crossed the sky. GEIPAN reconstructed the cameraman’s position and found that he had aimed above the lamp posts, broadly where a moving phenomenon described by T1 might have passed. A slight panning motion also suggested that T2 was attempting to follow something. The agency proposed a plausible recording failure: unable to see the faint aerial lights on his phone screen, T2 may have centred the only visible lights—the lamp posts—while the actual phenomenon continued beyond them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... The result is an unusual evidential split. The audio supports the fact that both men were reacting to something at the time, but the pictures do not verify its shape, altitude, speed or even its presence in the frame. T1 later treated the film as evidence for the triangle, although the technical analysis showed that the recorded triangle was unrelated to the claimed moving lights. GEIPAN warned that repeatedly viewing the footage may subsequently have influenced how the original sighting was remembered. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANGOLFECH (82) 06.10.2010 GEIPAN... Témoin d'un OVNI, le GeiPAN enquête. Channel: Le Foutoir d'AstronoGeek Open on YouTube Why the aircraft theory caused problems An aircraft remained the closest conventional match. Three white lights and a flashing red beacon can resemble normal aviation lighting, especially when perspective makes separated lights appear to define a triangular outline. The continuous industrial noise could also have masked an engine, and the witnesses may have perceived a dark surface between lights through a familiar visual effect in which the mind supplies a contour around isolated points. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... T2’s immediate reaction strengthens this explanation. Before online debate, lengthy interviews or later reconstruction, he can be heard saying that it was an aircraft. His gendarmerie evidence maintained that interpretation. A conventional explanation therefore did not arise merely as an official attempt to dismiss an awkward report; it was proposed by one of the two observers during the event itself. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... Yet the detailed investigation exposed difficulties. Military air surveillance records reconstructed traffic around Agen airport, about 16 kilometres from Golfech. They showed local traffic around the relevant time and a high altitude aircraft crossing the power station area at approximately 20:06, but no radar detected aircraft that clearly matched the phenomenon at 20:17. A site visit also established that aircraft approaching Agen could not be seen from the witnesses’ precise position in the manner assumed during the initial inquiry. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete86 GEIPANModéle de document par défaut CNES version 1.5 mars 1999... To reproduce T1’s reported angular size and proximity, an aircraft would have needed to be"},{"title":"Likely Causes","url":"/likely-causes/","description":"Meteors, balloons, aircraft, the Moon and birds explain many local reports that initially appeared structured, silent or unusually close.","breadcrumb":"Strange Lights, Weak Files and One Landmark Case","level":2,"text":"Likely Causes What Usually Caused Tarn et Garonne's UFO Reports? Likely Causes Likely Causes What Usually Caused Tarn et Garonne's UFO Reports? Meteors, balloons, aircraft, the Moon and birds explain many local reports that initially appeared structured, silent or unusually close. Meteors, balloons, aircraft, the Moon and birds explain many local reports that initially appeared structured, silent or unusually close. mechanism Meteors and bright fireballs Balloons, aircraft and astronomical objects Why video and multiple witnesses can still mislead tarn et garonne likely causes Introduction Most investigated UFO reports from Tarn et Garonne have turned out to involve ordinary objects or natural events: bright meteors, illuminated balloons, distant aircraft, the Moon, projected lights and even birds. These explanations matter because the original descriptions could sound far stranger than their eventual causes. Witnesses reported silent shapes, hovering lights, apparent triangles and objects that seemed only tens of metres away. The department’s official files therefore offer a useful warning against judging a sighting by vivid language alone. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, compares testimony with astronomy, weather, aircraft movements, geography, video and local events. Its conclusions range from category A, where an identification is considered secure, to category B, where an ordinary explanation is probable rather than certain. Across France, CNES currently lists about 24.6 per cent of investigated cases as fully identified and 39.7 per cent as probably identified. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN CNES GEIPAN CNES... Meteors can look close, shaped and silent A meteor or atmospheric re entry does not always resemble the familiar thin streak of a “shooting star”. A bright fireball may appear as a glowing oval, teardrop or compact object, sometimes changing size or brightness as it crosses the sky. Because observers usually have no reliable distance reference, something many kilometres above them can seem low, nearby and structured. The Montauban sighting of 9 September 2011 illustrates this problem particularly well. At about 10.10 pm, several people in the same neighbourhood saw a luminous phenomenon travelling rapidly from south to north. Two independent accounts described a white teardrop or bean like form, visible for between several seconds and half a minute. It appeared silent, did not illuminate its surroundings and seemed to shrink as it moved away. [Geipan] geipan.fr MONTAUBAN (82) 09.09.2011 GEIPAN... Investigators initially considered illuminated lanterns connected with Montauban’s local festivities, but the witnesses rejected that interpretation and the reported speed did not fit a slowly drifting lantern. GEIPAN’s later analysis instead favoured an atmospheric entry by a meteoroid or piece of space debris. The case was classified B: probably a bright fireball, rather than conclusively identified from direct instrumental evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr MONTAUBAN (82) 09.09.2011 GEIPAN... The incident shows why multiple witnesses do not necessarily prove that an unusual vehicle was present. People watching the same brief light from roughly the same area share the same lack of scale and distance cues. Their agreement that the event was fast, bright and silent strengthens the evidence that something real crossed the sky, but it does not by itself establish what that object was. A similar mechanism probably explains the Caussade report of 31 July 2011. A witness described a very fast, white, oval phenomenon that made no audible sound and quickly disappeared towards the horizon. GEIPAN found its speed, brightness and direction compatible with atmospheric entry, possibly associated with the Perseid meteor period, and forwarded the information to specialists at the Institute of Celestial Mechanics and Ephemeris Calculation and the National Museum of Natural History. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCAUSSADE (82) 31.07.2011 GEIPAN... These cases also explain why the absence of noise is weak evidence against a meteor. Light reaches an observer almost immediately, whereas any sound generated far away arrives later and may never be heard at all. A short observation can therefore end before the witness has any reason to associate a delayed sound with what was seen. Balloons can resemble coherent craft The Mas Grenier case of 8 August 2020 is one of Tarn et Garonne’s clearest examples of an apparently structured object being produced by several ordinary objects moving together. At about 10 pm, witnesses saw what they described as a luminous, jellyfish shaped object passing silently above a cherry tree. It carried bluish halos, followed a broadly straight course while undulating and gradually became a point of light towards the southern horizon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAS GRENIER (82) 08.08.2020 GEIPAN... That account could easily be retold as a close encounter with a single manoeuvring craft. The investigation produced a much more specific chain of evidence. Weather records showed a weak north to south wind matching the reported direction. Aircraft tracking did not show a relevant overflight. A village function room lay a few streets north of the observation point, and the local council confirmed that a wedding had taken place that evening. More decisively, the wedding party had released bunches of white balloons, each fitted with an LED light, near the reported time. A photograph of the preparations was also supplied. GEIPAN classified the case A: a certain misidentification of a cluster of illuminated balloons. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAS GRENIER (82) 08.08.2020 GEIPAN... The “jellyfish” impression was not irrational. A bouquet of balloons can maintain a loose collective outline while its individual parts bob, rotate and change spacing. Lights attached to the balloons create the appearance of illuminated features on a larger body, while darkness hides the strings and unlit surfaces linking the visible points. Slow wind driven movement also explains the silence and apparent wavering. Illuminated lanterns caused other local reports. At Montauban on 27 September 2014, GEIPAN judged a reported light to be probably a flying lantern and assigned it category B. [Geipan] geipan.fr MONTAUBAN (82) 27.09.2014 GEIPANSeptember 27, 2014 — MONTAUBAN (82) 27.09.2014... Lanterne thai... Le GEIPAN classe ce cas \"... Published: September 27, 2014 The Albias file from January 2009 likewise records a second, independent observation whose characteristics probably matched a lantern, although the primary event that night had a different explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANALBIAS (82) 17.01.2009 GEIPAN... These distinctions are important. Mas Grenier was not resolved merely because balloons seemed possible; investigators found the actual local release. Where no release was independently confirmed, GEIPAN used the more cautious category B. 3:15 Why Are Many UFO Sightings Just Misidentification? All About Myths and Conspiracies Channel: All About Myths and Conspiracies · Views: 9 · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 15 seconds Open on YouTube Aircraft can appear stationary or triangular Aircraft are easiest to recognise when they cross the observer’s field of view sideways. When an aircraft approaches almost directly, however, its angular position may change very little. A bright landing light can then appear stationary for many minutes, while wings, fuselage and navigation lights remain difficult to see. That geometry was central to the A62 case of 11 August 2013. While travelling between the Valence d’Agen area and Toulouse at around 7 am, a driver and passenger watched a white point that appeared almost motionless. Two short white trails extended behind it, producing what looked like a triangle without a base. The observation lasted about twenty minutes, ending when the car’s changing position carried the witnesses past it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[A62] de VALENCE D'AGEN (82) vers TOULOUSE (31) 11.08.2013 GEIPAN... GEIPAN examined the high altitude weather conditions and compared the appearance with known aircraft displaying short condensation trails. It concluded that the object was certainly an airliner and classified the report A. The apparent triangular form did not require a triangular vehicle: the luminous point and two diverging contrails supplied the visible corners and sides, while the witnesses’ viewpoint concealed the ordinary aircraft generating them. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[A62] de VALENCE D'AGEN (82) vers TOULOUSE (31) 11.08.2013 GEIPAN... This case highlights a broader problem with reports made from moving cars. The witness is not observing from a fixed platform. Road bends, vehicle speed, foreground objects and changes in viewing angle can make a distant object seem to stop, follow the car or move unexpectedly. The aircraft’s true speed may be high even while its"},{"title":"Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation","url":"/tarns-ufo-stories-between-mystery-and/","description":"Tarn has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landmark” encounter comparable with France’s...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation Tarn has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landmark” encounter comparable with France’s... Tarn has a genuine place in French UFO history, but not because it contains a single, securely documented “landmark” encounter comparable with France’s best known national cases. Its importance lies instead in the contrast between memorable local stories and what later investigation has actually established. tarn Introduction The official record therefore offers no persuasive evidence of an extraordinary craft operating over Tarn. One case remains formally unexplained, but its surviving evidence is limited. Tarn matters because it demonstrates how press excitement, sincere perception and delayed investigation can turn an unidentified sight into a durable local mystery without proving an exotic cause. Gaillac in 1952: Tarn’s defining UFO story On 27 October 1952, residents of Gaillac reportedly watched a remarkable daytime display involving numerous bright, disc like objects, a larger cigar shaped form and quantities of fine white filaments descending over the town. Contemporary and later accounts describe a large crowd gathering to watch the phenomenon. A local newspaper report appeared within days, and the incident has since been paired with a similar event at Oloron, in south western France, ten days earlier. [ladepeche.fr] ladepeche.fr une \"pluie de cheveux d'anges\" Le 27 octobre 1952, vers seize heures, documents sur les ovnis... The Gaillac story is memorable for three reasons. It involved many reported witnesses rather than one isolated observer; it occurred in daylight; and it included material said to resemble cobwebs or cotton threads. These features made it far harder to dismiss in popular memory than a brief light seen at night. A later history of UFO investigations in Tarn treated Gaillac as the starting point for more than half a century of local reports, claiming that over one hundred unusual appearances had been collected across the department by 2005. That figure reflects a private archival project rather than a count of verified unexplained events, but it shows how strongly the episode shaped local UFO culture. [Occitanie Livre & Lecture] occitanielivre.fr ovni 50 ans denquetes dans le tarn apparitions insolites en occitanie Occitanie Livre & LectureOvni, 50 ans d'enquêtes dans le Tarn: apparitions insolites...15 Jul 2006 — De l'armada de soucoupes volantes... The story has recently gained renewed attention because reports about French sightings were circulated within American intelligence channels during the intense international interest of 1952. The Central Intelligence Agency was then assessing whether the wider flying saucer wave might have military, technological or propaganda implications. The existence of an intelligence document or press summary, however, is not confirmation that the reported objects were physically extraordinary. Declassified CIA material shows that the agency collected large amounts of open reporting while generally accepting that most cases arose from natural phenomena, aircraft or misinterpretation. [cia.gov] cia.gov PROPOSE D STUDY ON THE \"FLYING SAUCERS\" PHENOMENA CIA FOIA (foia.cia.gov PROPOSE D STUDY ON THE \"FLYING SAUCERS\" PHENOMENA CIA FOIA (foia.cia.gov The spider silk explanation The falling filaments are the strongest clue to a conventional explanation. Young spiders disperse by releasing silk into the air and allowing wind and rising currents to carry them, sometimes in very large numbers. When sunlight catches airborne strands and nearby objects provide little sense of scale, clusters of silk and the spiders attached to them can appear as bright points, discs or moving formations. Deposited silk may also seem to vanish quickly because fine strands collapse, dry or become difficult to see once handled. [uapcodex.org] uapcodex.org UAP Codex Oloron / Gaillac · Angel Hair · UAP Codex The coincidence between the two episodes, separated by days and distance, cemented th That the key material always evaporates before it can be analyzed is not a mystery but, in itself, a fact consistent with spider silk, no... This hypothesis fits the reported “cobweb” material better than the idea that machines released an unknown substance. It does not reconstruct every visual detail, particularly the alleged large cigar shaped object, but those details survive chiefly through witness recollections and newspaper retellings rather than photographs, calibrated observations or preserved samples. The sensible conclusion is not that every person imagined the event. It is that many people may have watched a real but unfamiliar natural spectacle and interpreted its separate elements through the newly popular language of flying saucers. Later reporting has strengthened Gaillac’s status as a famous story, but not its evidential strength. No verified physical sample, instrument record or official investigation conducted at the time has emerged to establish an exotic explanation. The case remains historically significant and visually intriguing, while the spider ballooning interpretation remains the most coherent account of its most distinctive feature. Murat sur Vèbre: a mystery resolved after 39 years A case from 30 July 1978 shows why old sightings sometimes become less mysterious when their geometry is reconstructed. At about 3.45 am, a moped rider near Murat sur Vèbre saw an intense orange glow low in the sky. A second witness also reported a yellow orange light. The first described a disc like form with two domes, apparently moving or swaying, before seeming to depart. Gendarmes interviewed both witnesses and examined the area, but found neither additional observers nor ground traces. The case was originally recorded as unexplained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete7 Notes d'enquete7 When the French government’s UFO investigation unit, GEIPAN, re examined the file, investigators compared the witnesses’ stated direction with an astronomical reconstruction. The reported bearing was within roughly one or two degrees of the rising Moon, which was only about 7.5 degrees above the horizon and would have appeared orange. Patchy cloud could have concealed the familiar lunar outline while allowing changing shapes and brightness to show through gaps. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete7 Notes d'enquete7 The supposed motion was also less decisive than it first appeared. A fixed light viewed through trees and moving clouds can seem to sway, while a reduction in brightness may be interpreted as an object rapidly receding. When contacted in 2017, the principal witness clarified that the final movement had mainly seemed to be “forwards” or away from him, which was consistent with the light fading behind cloud rather than physically accelerating into the distance. GEIPAN reclassified the case as category A, meaning an identified phenomenon: the Moon. [Geipan] geipan.fr Notes d'enquete7 Notes d'enquete7 The reassessment did not accuse either witness of dishonesty. GEIPAN explicitly accepted their sincerity and regarded the two accounts as reasonably substantial. The error lay in interpreting an unfamiliar view of a familiar object. This is one of Tarn’s most useful cases because it separates witness credibility from observational accuracy: an honest, detailed account can still support the wrong physical conclusion. [Today in History] 8 January 1981 – UFO Sighting in Trans en Provence, France Channel: Qrio Open on YouTube Bertre in 1989: the department’s strongest unresolved file The most notable officially unresolved Tarn case occurred at a rural house near Bertre during the night of 3–4 September 1989. A retired man said he awoke shortly after 4 am to powerful beams illuminating his room and the surrounding field. On going outside, he reportedly saw a bright spherical or oval light rising from near the roof. The property was subsequently found to have damaged and discoloured tiles near the chimney. The witness reported the event to the gendarmerie at Puylaurens, and investigators from the French space agency’s specialist unit visited the site and took samples. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The local press treated the damaged roof as possible physical evidence. Photographs and reports described a hole and a section of tiles that appeared darker or scorched. Yet the same reporting also recorded an immediate complication: the roof had already been affected by a severe storm, and temporary measures had reportedly been taken to deal with weather damage. That created an obvious alternative origin for at least some of the physical marks. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr."},{"title":"Gaillac 1952","url":"/gaillac-1952/","description":"Gaillac's mass daytime sighting became Tarn's defining UFO story, but drifting spider silk may explain its strangest feature.","breadcrumb":"Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation","level":2,"text":"Gaillac 1952 Did Spider Silk Create Gaillac's UFO Spectacle? Tarn Gaillac 1952 Gaillac 1952 Did Spider Silk Create Gaillac's UFO Spectacle? Gaillac's mass daytime sighting became Tarn's defining UFO story, but drifting spider silk may explain its strangest feature. Gaillac's mass daytime sighting became Tarn's defining UFO story, but drifting spider silk may explain its strangest feature. event time window What witnesses reported over Gaillac Why the falling filaments mattered How later evidence changed the case tarn gaillac 1952 Introduction On 27 October 1952, residents of Gaillac in Tarn reportedly watched groups of bright, disc like forms moving around a larger pale cylinder in a clear afternoon sky. Fine white threads then drifted down and caught on trees and overhead wires. The display became Tarn’s defining flying saucer story because it combined daylight, numerous witnesses, an apparently structured formation and supposed physical material. The most persuasive later interpretation is less exotic but unusually well matched to the evidence: mass “ballooning” by small spiders, which travel through the air on extremely fine silk. Sunlit bundles of silk can look like brilliant objects, while accumulating strands produce the cobweb like fall later called “angel hair”. This does not prove that every remembered detail was spider silk. It does, however, explain the case’s strangest and most tangible feature without requiring an unknown aircraft or substance. The absence of photographs, preserved samples and a contemporary scientific investigation leaves Gaillac historically important but evidentially weak. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... What witnesses reported over Gaillac The earliest readily accessible press account appeared in Le Méridional on 29 October 1952, only two days after the event. It placed the sighting at about 5pm on Monday, 27 October, beneath a clear blue sky. According to the report, 64 year old Madame Doré, who lived on the Toulouse road, looked upwards after her hens became agitated. Her son and members of the Corbières family then joined her. The article said the objects travelled from south towards east, turned slowly in pairs and glittered in the sunlight. Four were initially visible, followed by roughly a dozen more. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... A long, whitish “cylinder” was said to appear among the smaller forms, apparently giving off a white plume. The entire episode reportedly lasted around twenty minutes. Afterwards, white fibres resembling glass wool descended and remained attached to branches and electrical wires for at least two hours. The newspaper claimed that the filaments became gelatinous and “melted” when handled. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... Later retellings sharpened and expanded this basic account. Some describe exactly sixteen circular objects, blue light around their edges, a cigar shaped “command” object and a crowd of about one hundred people. A 2026 reconstruction by La Dépêche du Midi also gives an earlier start time of about 4pm, a shorter display of roughly ten minutes and a north westerly departure. These differences do not necessarily mean that the sighting was invented. They show, however, that the familiar modern narrative is not a single fixed body of testimony: it is a composite built from press reports, later interviews and decades of UFO literature. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... That distinction matters. A large group may sincerely agree that something unusual is in the sky while remaining poor at estimating its size, distance and physical form. With no stable background reference, small nearby objects can be mistaken for large distant ones. Once one observer calls a bright point a disc or identifies a loose cluster as a formation, others may adopt the same interpretation without being dishonest. Gaillac therefore supports the claim that a striking aerial display occurred; it does not independently establish that engineered craft were present. The incident also followed a remarkably similar report at Oloron, around 170 kilometres to the south west, on 17 October. Witnesses there likewise described a large elongated form, numerous smaller bodies and falling white fibres. The ten day separation made the pairing central to later UFO accounts. Yet the repetition can be read in two opposite ways: as evidence of the same mysterious technology appearing twice, or as evidence of the same seasonal natural process occurring under similar weather conditions across south western France. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... Why the falling filaments mattered The threads transformed Gaillac from an unusual visual report into what seemed to be a physical evidence case. Lights and shapes can be dismissed as mistakes, but material apparently falling from them suggests that observers encountered something with substance. “Angel hair” subsequently became the standard UFO term for delicate white or silvery filaments reported after aerial sightings. In Gaillac, however, no verifiable specimen survives. There is no documented chain of custody showing who collected a sample, how it was stored or whether a qualified laboratory examined it. The contemporary newspaper merely reported that the material became gelatinous and disappeared. Later accounts use stronger terms such as “evaporated” or “sublimated”, but those descriptions appear to rest on witnesses’ impressions rather than controlled testing. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org UFOS at close sight: the newspapers, Gaillac, France, 1952... Fine spider silk does not literally evaporate in a person’s hand. It is a protein fibre with considerable strength relative to its diameter. Nevertheless, individual strands can be nearly invisible, collapse into tiny clumps, adhere to skin or break when disturbed. Moisture and handling can also change the appearance of a loose mass. An observer expecting a substantial cotton like substance might therefore conclude that it had dissolved when the almost weightless threads simply became impossible to distinguish. Modern research describes spider silk as a protein based material rather than a volatile chemical, so the dramatic language of instant evaporation should be treated cautiously. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The elaborate structure of spider silk Silk fibers have tensile strengths comparable to steel.Read more... The material’s behaviour before handling is more important than the uncertain claim about what happened afterwards. The fibres reportedly: descended slowly through otherwise clear air; stretched between or around luminous points; accumulated on branches and wires; resembled cobwebs or glass wool; appeared in large quantities during an afternoon in October. That combination is highly compatible with gossamer: very fine silk released by spiders during aerial dispersal. It is much harder to reconcile with aircraft exhaust, ordinary smoke or weather balloons. The filaments are therefore not simply an unexplained residue attached to the UFO story. They are the principal reason a natural explanation became possible. Experts Still Can’t Explain What Fell From the Sky That Day Close Encounters 209 Channel: HauntTV Open on YouTube How spider silk can create an aerial spectacle Spider “ballooning” occurs when a spider releases one or more silk lines and is lifted or dragged into the air. Small spiders and spiderlings commonly use the technique to disperse, although experiments have shown that surprisingly large spiders can also become airborne using many threads. Their movement depends on local airflows, rising warm air and, according to laboratory work, atmospheric electrical forces acting on charged silk. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)An observational study of ballooning in large spiders PubMed Central (PMC)An observational study of ballooning in large spiders PMCby M Cho · 2018 · Cited by 57 — This work represents the m... The process can produce far more silk than the familiar single thread trailing from one garden spider. When many animals disperse under favourable conditions, strands collect into sheets, streamers and tangled masses. They may cover fields and vegetation or remain suspended in the air at different heights. Aerodynamic research confirms that spiders can release multiple threads forming broad, fan like structures rather than one compact line. [APS Link] link.aps.org Link Ballooning in spiders using multiple silk threads Phys Rev. Eby C Habchi · 2022 · Cited by 8 — In this paper, three dimensional numerical simulations of ballooning in spiders using multiple si... Bright"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-f42632/","description":"Tarn's official files show how sincere testimony can lead to a lunar explanation, an unresolved classification or no firm conclusion.","breadcrumb":"Tarn's UFO Stories Between Mystery and Explanation","level":2,"text":"Official Files Why Some Tarn UFO Cases Were Reclassified Tarn UFOs Official Files Official Files Why Some Tarn UFO Cases Were Reclassified Tarn's official files show how sincere testimony can lead to a lunar explanation, an unresolved classification or no firm conclusion. Tarn's official files show how sincere testimony can lead to a lunar explanation, an unresolved classification or no firm conclusion. dataset evidence The Moon explanation at Murat sur Vebre The unresolved roof damage case at Bertre What official classifications really mean tarn official files Introduction Official investigation changed the meaning of several Tarn UFO reports without necessarily challenging the honesty of the people involved. At Murat sur Vèbre, two witnesses described a large orange object with an unusual shape and apparent movement. A later review found that its direction matched the Moon to within roughly one or two degrees, leading GEIPAN, the French government’s unidentified aerospace phenomena unit, to classify the case as an identified lunar misperception. At Bertre, by contrast, investigators examined a dramatic light, alleged roof damage and collected samples, yet the surviving official record does not establish what caused the event. The case remains unexplained in the database. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMURAT SUR VEBRE (81) 30.07.1978La Lune rousse au levant ou au couchant est à l'origine de nombreuses méprises pour les témoins (voi... Together, the files show why “unidentified” is an investigative status, not evidence of an extraordinary vehicle. They also show that sincere testimony, physical looking traces and an unresolved classification carry different evidential weight. The Moon explanation at Murat sur Vèbre In the early hours of 30 July 1978, a moped rider returning from a celebration near Castanet le Haut saw an immense orange glow while travelling through the Murat sur Vèbre area. A second person reportedly observed it from the same location. The sighting lasted about five minutes, and the witnesses described a complex object rather than an obvious astronomical body. Gendarmes questioned them on 1 August, but their local enquiries found neither additional witnesses nor traces on the ground. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMURAT SUR VEBRE (81) 30.07.1978La Lune rousse au levant ou au couchant est à l'origine de nombreuses méprises pour les témoins (voi... For years, a report such as this could retain much of its mystery because the original description sounded specific: an orange, structured form that appeared to sway and then disappear. The later GEIPAN review shifted attention away from the verbal image of an object and towards measurable circumstances. Investigators reconstructed the viewing direction and found that it corresponded to the Moon’s position to within about one or two degrees. The case is now classified A , meaning that GEIPAN regards the phenomenon as identified without significant ambiguity; the database names the cause as a reddish Moon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMURAT SUR VEBRE (81) 30.07.1978La Lune rousse au levant ou au couchant est à l'origine de nombreuses méprises pour les témoins (voi... That conclusion does not require the witnesses to have invented anything. A low Moon can look orange or red because its light passes through more of the atmosphere. Broken cloud may obscure its familiar circular outline while allowing irregular bright areas to remain visible. Those openings can seem to form domes, discs or other structured shapes. Moving cloud can also create an impression that the light itself is rocking, changing form or moving independently. GEIPAN specifically proposed this mechanism for the reported complex shape and apparent swaying at Murat sur Vèbre. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMURAT SUR VEBRE (81) 30.07.1978La Lune rousse au levant ou au couchant est à l'origine de nombreuses méprises pour les témoins (voi... The review also retained an important limitation. Weather records available nearly forty years later could not confirm whether the necessary cloud arrangement was present. The explanation therefore rests chiefly on the exceptionally close match between the reported direction and the Moon, combined with a well known pattern of lunar misidentification. The missing cloud data weaken the reconstruction, but they do not outweigh the positional match in GEIPAN’s assessment. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMURAT SUR VEBRE (81) 30.07.1978La Lune rousse au levant ou au couchant est à l'origine de nombreuses méprises pour les témoins (voi... Murat sur Vèbre is valuable within Tarn’s UFO history precisely because the original observation was not vague or obviously trivial. Two people saw something memorable, supplied a seemingly elaborate description and reported apparent motion. Yet once investigators tested the sightline rather than taking the perceived shape literally, an ordinary astronomical source became the strongest explanation. The case illustrates the difference between accurately reporting an experience and accurately identifying its physical cause. The unresolved roof damage case at Bertre The Bertre case, near Puylaurens, followed a very different investigative path. At about 4.30 am on 4 September 1989, a retired man was awakened by intense light passing through partly closed shutters. He went outside, saw a brightly illuminated area in a field below the house and then looked back towards the roof. There, he described a silent, stationary luminous form resembling a gas cylinder or bottle. It reportedly went dark after a short interval. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete R2 Compte rendu enquete R2 The report attracted unusual attention because it appeared to include physical effects. Gendarmes noted what they called signs of heated roof tiles. The witness also complained that the roof had developed serious leaks, requiring containers in the loft. Contemporary press accounts described displaced or discoloured tiles, localised loss of moss and a slight sag in part of the roof. Those reports made the incident seem more substantial than a light only observation, although newspaper descriptions were not themselves laboratory findings. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete R2 Compte rendu enquete R2 SEPRA, then the CNES service responsible for such enquiries, conducted a site investigation on 12 September. It used a helicopter to inspect or photograph the roof and collected tiles and biological material, including moss. The investigator described the witness’s repeated account as broadly coherent and noted that he did not appear to be seeking a particular outcome. The report also recorded that national and regional press activity disrupted the enquiry. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete R2 Compte rendu enquete R2 The document’s conclusion was explicitly provisional. At that stage, SEPRA considered a helicopter, the Moon and a balloon unlikely explanations. It said that the remaining possibilities were deliberate fabrication or an unexplained phenomenon, and that analysis of the physical and biological samples would be needed to resolve the doubt. That wording is much narrower than later retellings suggesting that officials confirmed an unknown craft. Investigators had ruled out several initial ideas, not established a positive cause. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete R2 Compte rendu enquete R2 The present GEIPAN entry classifies Bertre as D , describing an unexplained phenomenon of medium to strong consistency. The record was updated in June 2026; earlier listings had displayed the older D1 label. The current classification indicates that the available material has not produced a validated conventional explanation, but it does not identify the source of the light or prove a connection between the sighting and the roof’s condition. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan« LES TUILES » BERTRE (81) 04.09.1989 T1 GEIPAN... The central evidential problem is that the publicly available file contains the provisional 1989 report, photographs, press material and witness records, but no clearly documented final laboratory result demonstrating that the tiles were altered by an external aerial phenomenon. The roof damage may be relevant, coincidental, pre existing or inadequately characterised; the surviving published material does not settle the issue. An unexplained classification is therefore appropriate in the literal sense: investigators could not confidently reconstruct what happened. Bertre should consequently be treated as unresolved rather than strong proof of something exotic. Its strengths are the prompt gendarmerie involvement, an on site CNES investigation, collected samples and a witness account judged coherent. Its weaknesses are the single principal observer, uncertain weather information in the current database, no measured distance to the phenomenon, heavy media intrusion and the absence of a publicly demonstrated causal link between the light and the roof. [Geipan]"},{"title":"Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread","url":"/territoire-de-belfort/","description":"The UFO history of the Territoire de Belfort is less dramatic than its reputation might suggest.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread The UFO history of the Territoire de Belfort is less dramatic than its reputation might suggest. The UFO history of the Territoire de Belfort is less dramatic than its reputation might suggest. Public records do not reveal a celebrated landing case, a sustained local “flap”, a radar confrontation or a well documented military encounter. territoire de belfort Introduction That modest record is still useful. It shows how easily unfamiliar shapes, lighting and movement can turn everyday objects into convincing anomalies, particularly when witnesses lack a clear sense of distance. It also illustrates an important distinction in French official investigations: a case may remain unidentified simply because too little reliable information exists, not because investigators have found evidence of an extraordinary craft. In the Territoire de Belfort, the strongest recurring pattern is therefore not unexplained technology but incomplete observation followed, where enough evidence becomes available, by conventional identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... What the official record contains France has an unusually accessible official archive of unusual aerial observations. The Groupe d’études et d’informations sur les phénomènes aérospatiaux non identifiés, or GEIPAN, is part of the French space agency CNES. Created in 1977, it gathers reports, analyses possible explanations and publishes case material for the public. French officials generally use the term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” rather than “UFO”, because witnesses may have seen a light, atmospheric effect or distant object rather than a clearly defined craft. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... GEIPAN’s public search results identify four cases explicitly assigned to the Territoire de Belfort: Beaucourt, 2 April 1979 — category A , meaning the phenomenon was identified. Belfort, 4 May 1981 — category A , also identified. Grandvillars to Delle, 14 April 2013 — category C , meaning it could not be identified because the available evidence was insufficient. Belfort, 21 January 2020 — category A , identified as a kite. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... This is not necessarily a complete census of everything ever reported locally. Many observations are never formally submitted, historical newspaper material can remain undigitised, and older official files do not always preserve a detailed public explanation. Nevertheless, the published record offers no category D case for the department: in GEIPAN terminology, category D is reserved for a phenomenon that remains unidentified after an investigation supported by adequate information. Category C, by contrast, indicates an evidential shortfall. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr · Classification B: Phenomenon probably identified after investigation. · Classification C:...Read more... The department therefore does not currently stand out as a French UFO hotspot. A national spatial study using decades of GEIPAN data found that report distribution was influenced by such factors as population density and environmental conditions, while its principal unexplained concentrations occurred in other areas, including parts of northern, western and south eastern France. The Territoire de Belfort was not highlighted as an anomalous cluster. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. The black sphere between Grandvillars and Delle The most instructive officially unresolved report occurred during a night time drive on 14 April 2013. At about 1.30 am, a motorist travelling from Grandvillars towards Delle saw what she described as a black sphere moving in a straight line. It remained visible for only a few seconds before disappearing behind trees. No independent witness was found. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... At first glance, a dark spherical object travelling across the night landscape sounds more unusual than a simple point of light. Yet the observation offered almost none of the information needed to establish its true nature. There was no photograph, measurable distance, known size, recorded sound, physical trace, radar return or second viewing position. The witness had also completed a very long day, a circumstance that GEIPAN considered relevant when evaluating visual reliability. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... Investigators did not claim to have solved the sighting. They noted that ordinary confusions such as a nocturnal bird, a brief illusion or a fatigue related visual error could not be excluded. The object’s straight path and short visibility made the episode only weakly strange, despite its unusual reported shape. GEIPAN therefore judged that a more extensive investigation was not justified and classified the case C because of missing information and corroboration. The expected gendarmerie report had not reached the organisation when the case assessment was published. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... This classification is often misunderstood. Category C does not mean that investigators examined strong evidence and failed to explain it. It means that they lacked enough dependable material to decide between competing possibilities. The Grandvillars–Delle report is unresolved in the narrow sense that no final identification can be proved, but it is not a strong unexplained case. Its evidential value is low precisely because nearly every important measurement is absent. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... The Belfort “craft” that became a kite The department’s best known recent UFO story began on the evening of Saturday 28 July 2018. Several people in Belfort noticed an unfamiliar object apparently hovering over the city for more than half an hour. Early descriptions referred to a black rectangular form surrounded by an orange glow, with changes in colour before it disappeared. Suggestions ranged from an advanced drone to an experimental solar aircraft. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Belfort L'Est Républicain Belfort The case initially seemed persuasive because it involved multiple observers, a prolonged duration and at least one photograph. Those features are normally more useful than a brief single witness sighting. The object also looked unlike the familiar silhouette of an aeroplane or helicopter when viewed from below. Local reporting therefore treated “UFO” in its literal sense: something in the sky had not yet been identified. [btlv.fr] btlv.fr BELFOR T: un OVNI observé en Franche Comté? BELFOR T: un OVNI observé en Franche Comté? The mystery did not last. Follow up reporting identified the object as a kite. A retrospective local news gallery likewise captioned the photographed object as a kite rather than an unknown aircraft. The unusual rectangular appearance, apparent stationary flight and illumination were therefore not signs of exotic performance; they were characteristics of a tethered object seen under unfamiliar lighting conditions. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr Open source on estrepublicain.fr. The episode is important because it demonstrates how later evidence can reverse the impression created by an initial report. Multiple witnesses did not guarantee an extraordinary event because the witnesses were all looking at the same ambiguous object from broadly similar positions. Nor did a long duration make the object more mysterious: hovering for half an hour is difficult for many aircraft but entirely compatible with a kite held aloft by steady wind. It also shows why photographs do not automatically settle UFO disputes. An image may confirm that something was present without establishing its size, distance or construction. Against a dark sky, an illuminated kite can appear self propelled and much larger or farther away than it really is. Once the correct object class is recognised, the apparently anomalous behaviour becomes ordinary. Real UFO sighting caught on tape in a remote French town Channel: Rumble Viral Open on YouTube The 2020 observation and the value of reconstruction A separate report from Belfort on 21 January 2020 also ended with a kite identification. GEIPAN initially received a questionnaire describing an unexplained phenomenon over the city, but the completed investigation placed the case in category A: perfectly identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete635 Compte rendu enquete635 The recurrence of a kite explanation is"},{"title":"Kite Cases","url":"/kite-cases/","description":"The 2018 and 2020 Belfort sightings show how illuminated kites can resemble hovering aircraft until later evidence changes the story.","breadcrumb":"Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread","level":2,"text":"Kite Cases How Belfort's UFO Craft Became a Kite Belfort UFOs Kite Cases Kite Cases How Belfort's UFO Craft Became a Kite The 2018 and 2020 Belfort sightings show how illuminated kites can resemble hovering aircraft until later evidence changes the story. The 2018 and 2020 Belfort sightings show how illuminated kites can resemble hovering aircraft until later evidence changes the story. event time window The 2018 sighting and its first reports Why multiple witnesses and photographs still misled The 2020 reconstruction and confirmed identification territoire de belfort kite cases Introduction Two Belfort sightings, in 2018 and 2020, show how an ordinary kite can be interpreted as a hovering aircraft when distance, scale and attachment to the ground are not visible. The first report attracted several witnesses and photographs of a dark rectangular object apparently remaining above the city for about half an hour. Suggestions included a drone, a balloon and even an experimental solar aircraft. Within a day, however, local information pointed to a large kite flown from the Pépinière district. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Belfort Un Ovni dans le ciel belfortain?July 31, 2018 — 31 Jul 2018 — Ce n'est visiblement ni une montgolfière, ni un cerf volant, ni un ballon... Published: July 31, 2018 The 2020 observation was more formally documented. A witness supplied a detailed account, a photograph, estimated directions and a drawing to GEIPAN, the French space agency’s investigation group. The report was regarded as evidentially sound, yet the object was conclusively identified as the same kind of camera carrying kite. Together, these cases are among the clearest examples in the Territoire de Belfort of reliable witnesses accurately reporting something unusual while initially misunderstanding what it was. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le cas est de bonne consistance: présence d’une photo et très bonne qualité du témoignage (voir le compte rendu d'enquête).En cherchant... Vol d'essai d'un \"drone\" cerf volant Channel: Photo KAP34 Open on YouTube The 2018 object that looked unlike any aircraft On the evening of Saturday 28 July 2018, several Belfort residents noticed a dark object above the city. Early reporting described it as rectangular, surrounded by an orange looking glow and apparently stationary for more than 30 minutes. Some witnesses believed the colour changed shortly before the object disappeared from view. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Belfort Un Ovni dans le ciel belfortain?July 31, 2018 — 31 Jul 2018 — Ce n'est visiblement ni une montgolfière, ni un cerf volant, ni un ballon... Published: July 31, 2018 Those details encouraged comparisons with aircraft even though the reported behaviour did not fit an ordinary aeroplane. The object seemed to hover, made no obvious rapid crossing of the sky and had no recognisable wings or fuselage. A witness suggested that it might be a solar aircraft prototype, while the newspaper also raised the increasingly familiar possibility of a sophisticated drone. The original article explicitly treated a kite as an unlikely option, illustrating how an explanation can be dismissed when observers imagine only a small, familiar toy rather than a large specialist model. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Belfort Un Ovni dans le ciel belfortain?July 31, 2018 — 31 Jul 2018 — Ce n'est visiblement ni une montgolfière, ni un cerf volant, ni un ballon... Published: July 31, 2018 The apparent mystery weakened almost immediately. Readers familiar with the object reported that it was a large kite operated from the Pépinière area of Belfort. It had reportedly been seen repeatedly since the beginning of July and, according to local accounts, similar flights had taken place there for several years. The conspicuous brightness was attributed to reflected light from a GoPro camera carried by the kite rather than to lamps or an aircraft propulsion system. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Insolite L'Ovni qui survolait Belfort? C'était un cerf volant1 Aug 2018 — Qu'était donc cet engin qui a survolé le ciel belfortain dans la soirée... That first explanation was cautiously worded in the press as the “most plausible” identification rather than the conclusion of a technical investigation. Later material considerably strengthened it. GEIPAN’s subsequent review located films showing the object, identified a cable holding it in position and found that the filmed views were consistent with the Pépinière sector. Its report also noted further observations in August 2018, including one lasting approximately two hours, and statements from residents who confirmed that the object was a kite. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete635 GEIPANModèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The long duration was therefore not evidence against a kite. It was one of the most useful clues in its favour. A tethered object can occupy roughly the same part of the sky for a prolonged period while shifting, twisting or rising slightly in the wind. A powered aircraft could hover, but doing so over an urban area for half an hour would normally introduce other clues: engine or rotor noise, purposeful manoeuvres, navigation lights or a clear departure. None was securely established in the 2018 accounts. Why witnesses and photographs still gave the wrong impression The Belfort case is not best understood as careless observation. The witnesses noticed real features: a dark shape, apparent immobility, changing orientation and unusual brightness. The mistake occurred when those features were combined into an assumed aircraft rather than recognised as parts of a kite system. The central problem was unknown distance . When an object has no familiar size and is seen against open sky, an observer cannot reliably determine how large or distant it is. A relatively nearby kite may therefore be experienced as a much larger craft farther away. Estimates of altitude, separation between components and physical dimensions can appear precise while depending on an untested assumption about distance. The object’s outline added to the confusion. Large kites need not have the diamond shape associated with children’s toys. Specialist designs can be rectangular, inflated, sculptural or almost animal like. In November 2020, for example, a Belfort enthusiast flew a 32 square metre kite shaped like a trilobite from the Pépinière area using 200 metres of line. That was a different documented flight, but it demonstrates the size and visual unfamiliarity that local kite activity could produce. [L'Est Républicain] estrepublicain.fr L'Est Républicain Belfort Un trilobite dans le ciel de la PépinièreNovember 8, 2020 — 8 Nov 2020 — Denis, Belfortain passionné de cerfs volants depuis trente ans... Published: November 8, 2020 Photography did not automatically resolve the uncertainty. The 2018 images established that something was present, but a small object occupying few pixels supplied little dependable information about its construction or distance. A reflective camera housing could appear as a halo, while atmospheric haze, exposure and digital enlargement could soften the kite’s edges. Photographs are valuable evidence, but they are not self interpreting; they become decisive only when matched with location, shape, movement and an independently known object. Multiple witnesses also increased confidence without necessarily improving identification. People viewing the same unfamiliar object from the same general area share many of the same limitations. Several reports can strongly confirm that an observation occurred, yet still reproduce a common error about scale or nature. GEIPAN itself distinguishes the quality of a case’s documentation from its level of strangeness: a well described incident may be convincingly explained, while a poorly documented one may remain unresolved merely because essential information is missing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMissions, Methods and Achievements July 25, 2019 — One or several witnesses are the starting point of a GEIPAN case. Reports of the sighting include weirdness … along with... Published: July 25, 2019 10:35 Congress Fell for a BALLOON? MICK WEST Breaks Down The Hellfire Missile UFO Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 302 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube The 2020 sighting recreated the same illusion At about 4.45–5.00 pm on 21 January 2020, a Belfort resident looking from a sixth floor flat saw a dark object apparently suspended in the sky. Below it were four elongated, ribbon like forms in different colours—remembered as red, green, blue and possibly yellow. The upper object seemed to rotate slowly around a vertical axis but remained in the same general direction until fading daylight prevented further observation, roughly 30 minutes later. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le cas est de bonne consistance: présence d’une"},{"title":"Black Sphere","url":"/black-sphere/","description":"The 2013 black-sphere report remained unresolved because nearly every useful measurement, record and independent check was missing.","breadcrumb":"Why Belfort's UFO Record Is Mostly Misread","level":2,"text":"Black Sphere Why the Grandvillars Black Sphere Remains Unsolved Belfort UFOs Black Sphere Black Sphere Why the Grandvillars Black Sphere Remains Unsolved The 2013 black sphere report remained unresolved because nearly every useful measurement, record and independent check was missing. The 2013 black sphere report remained unresolved because nearly every useful measurement, record and independent check was missing. dataset evidence What the driver reported on the road to Delle Missing measurements, witnesses and records Why category C does not mean strong mystery territoire de belfort black sphere Introduction The Grandvillars–Delle black sphere report remains the Territoire de Belfort ’s principal officially unresolved sighting, but it is unresolved for a limited and important reason: the evidence was too weak to support either identification or serious anomaly. At about 1.30 am on 14 April 2013, a motorist on the N1019 reported seeing a dark, apparently spherical object cross her view for no more than about five seconds before trees hid it. No passenger confirmed the sighting, no independent witness came forward, and no photograph, reliable angular measurement, speed estimate or subsequent physical record established what was present. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that examines unusual aerial reports, therefore placed the case in category C: not identified because reliable information and corroboration were missing. This is not the same as category D, which is used when a sufficiently documented case remains unexplained after investigation. The distinction makes this brief roadside observation more valuable as a lesson in evidential limits than as evidence of an extraordinary object. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. What the driver reported on the road to Delle The witness was a 30 year old woman returning home with her husband and son after a meal with friends near Illzach. She said that she was driving towards Delle on the N1019, roughly one or two kilometres from the Swiss border. On a gentle downhill section, she was travelling at about 90 kilometres per hour before slowing to around 70 during the observation. The night appeared clear, and lights from the nearby customs area illuminated part of the sky and landscape ahead. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 According to her written account, a black sphere appeared from the right, seemingly from behind trees about 200 metres ahead, and moved horizontally towards the left. She blinked several times because she initially wondered whether her vision was at fault, then braked for a better look. The object continued moving until it disappeared behind trees on the left. She estimated that the whole episode lasted five seconds or less. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 The structured questionnaire described one non luminous, black, spherical object with an apparent size comparable to a bowl. Elsewhere in the same form, however, the witness estimated its distance at roughly one kilometre rather than 200 metres. Its speed was not measured, its altitude was not established, and no sound or environmental effect was documented. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 That difference between the narrative distance and the later estimate is not evidence that the witness was dishonest. It illustrates the difficulty of reconstructing a brief night time observation from a moving car. A dark silhouette contains few visual cues from which to calculate range. Without a known distance, an apparent size such as “the size of a bowl” cannot be converted into an actual diameter: a small nearby bird, a larger distant object and a shadowed feature against the sky might produce comparable angular impressions. The witness’s immediate reaction was cautious rather than sensational. She told her husband that she thought she might have hallucinated and later said that she wanted an explanation rather than asserting that she had seen an extraterrestrial vehicle. She also stated that she had not consumed alcohol, did not wear glasses, was not taking medication or drugs and had extensive driving experience. Those details make the report a sincere account worth recording, but they do not supply the missing measurements needed to identify the stimulus. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 Why the other occupants did not provide corroboration Although the driver was not alone in the vehicle, the case still rests on a single observation. Her son was asleep in the rear, while her husband was struggling to stay awake and did not report seeing the sphere. The event was so brief that the driver had little opportunity to direct another person’s attention towards it before trees blocked the view. GEIPAN’s summary accordingly states that no other testimony was obtained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 This matters because independent witnesses can test several parts of a sighting at once. Agreement about direction, shape, duration and movement would show that the perception was not confined to one observer. Differences between witnesses can also reveal whether reflections, fatigue or viewing position affected what each person saw. Here, the passengers establish that the journey took place, but not that an external sphere crossed the road or sky. The absence of corroboration does not disprove the report. It does mean that the case cannot distinguish confidently between several possibilities: a real but ordinary object, such as a nocturnal bird seen in silhouette; a nearby object whose distance and size were misjudged; a fleeting visual effect involving trees, lighting or the windscreen; a momentary perceptual error associated with fatigue and night driving; or a physical object that cannot now be identified. GEIPAN explicitly mentioned an illusion, a nocturnal bird or a fatigue related visual problem as possibilities. It did not claim to have demonstrated any one of them. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... Missing measurements, witnesses and records The public file is more substantial than a one line anecdote because it contains a completed questionnaire, a hand marked map and references to environmental photographs and a sketch. Yet almost every item capable of testing the sighting remains approximate or absent. The witness supplied a general location near the Delle interchange and customs area, but the material does not establish a measured line of sight, angular elevation, angular width, exact trajectory or reference object of known dimensions. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 The most consequential gaps are straightforward: No usable size measurement. “The size of a bowl” describes an apparent impression, but the questionnaire does not record whether that comparison was made at arm’s length or by some other method. Without angular size and reliable range, the object’s physical dimensions cannot be calculated. No reliable distance. The account refers both to something about 200 metres ahead and to an estimated object distance of one kilometre. Neither figure was triangulated or tied to a fixed point on the landscape. No speed calculation. A leftward passage lasting only a few seconds sounds rapid, but apparent motion depends on distance, observer movement and viewing angle. The witness herself was driving at motorway speed and then braking, creating additional relative movement. No independent record. There was no photograph or video of the object, no radar report, no trace, and no second witness who observed it. The two environment photographs listed in the questionnaire could assist with orientation, but they do not record the event itself. [Geipan] geipan.fr Questionnaire terre R570 Questionnaire terre R570 No completed official cross check. GEIPAN stated that it had not received the expected gendarmerie report when it assessed the case. That missing document might have fixed the date of the initial declaration, preserved additional questioning or confirmed whether local checks were attempted. The currently published file still presents only the questionnaire as its documentary attachment and retains the category C classification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[N1019] de GRANDVILLARS (90) vers DELLE (90) 14.04.2013 GEIPAN... The file’s later website update does not appear to have strengthened the underlying evidence. GEIPAN’s current case page gives an update date of 23 June 2021, but the description, classification and documentary list remain essentially unchanged: the event is still labelled as lacking reliable information, with no published independent witness statement or later identification. The safest conclusion is that the administrative record was refreshed"},{"title":"From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights","url":"/val-d-oise/","description":"Val-d’Oise has one of France’s best-known alleged alien-abduction stories, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than that reputation suggests.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights Val d’Oise has one of France’s best known alleged alien abduction stories, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than that reputation suggests. Val d’Oise has one of France’s best known alleged alien abduction stories, but its documented UFO history is less dramatic than that reputation suggests. The 1979 disappearance of Franck Fontaine at Cergy Pontoise became a national media sensation before a participant later described it as a hoax. val d oise Introduction The wider pattern in Val d’Oise is dominated by misidentified aircraft, lanterns, balloons, meteors, drifting debris and reports too incomplete to investigate properly. This is unsurprising in a densely populated department beside Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport and beneath busy approach and departure routes. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, has repeatedly used flight paths, radar information, weather data and photographs to resolve apparently strange local lights. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMONT (95) 18.03.201330 Jan 2015 — DOMONT (95) 18.03.2013; Date d'observation. 18/03/2013; Région. Autre; Département. Val d'Oi... Cergy Pontoise: the case that became a national spectacle In the early hours of 26 November 1979, three young men were reportedly preparing to travel to a market when Franck Fontaine disappeared near Cergy Pontoise. His companions, Jean Pierre Prévost and Salomon N’Diaye, said they had seen a luminous object or mist around the car and could no longer find him. Fontaine reappeared on 2 December, about a week later, claiming to have little or no memory of the missing period. Contemporary national reporting treated the disappearance cautiously but gave the extraordinary allegation enormous exposure. [lemonde.fr] lemonde.fr les extra terrestres ont rendu leur otage 2775190 1819218 Le Monde.frLES \" EXTRA TERRESTRES \" ONT RENDU LEUR OTAGE4 Dec 1979 — Le 26 novembre, à 4 heures du matin, un Jeune homme de Cergy Pontois... The case acquired several features that made it unusually attractive to the press: there was a genuinely missing person rather than a momentary light in the sky; two companions supplied an apparently coherent extraordinary account; gendarmes conducted searches and questioned those involved; Fontaine returned alive, allowing further interviews and speculation; the story emerged only two years after Close Encounters of the Third Kind , when public interest in alien contact was especially strong. The authorities’ involvement is sometimes presented as evidence that officials took an extraterrestrial explanation seriously. In reality, the gendarmes first had to treat Fontaine as a missing person. Accounts indicate that his companions were questioned, checks were made for alcohol or drugs, and the surrounding area was searched. Those actions established that a disappearance had been reported; they did not verify the claimed luminous object or an alien abduction. [VICE] vice.com L'affaire de l'ovni de Cergy Pontoise Qu'attendre de plus de la part de gendarmes mal réveillés et peu enclins à écouter des histoires d'ovnis...Read more... The story continued after Fontaine’s return. UFO writer Jimmy Guieu promoted the affair as a genuine contact case, while Prévost became associated with predictions or messages concerning a possible future manifestation. In August 1980, roughly 2,000 people reportedly gathered at Cergy in anticipation of a promised event. Nothing unusual occurred. [Encyclopedia.com] encyclopedia.com Cergy Pontoise Hoax As the story continued to unfold, flying saucer enthusiast Jimmy Guieu published a book length account of the story e Cergy Pontoise Hoax As the story continued to unfold, flying saucer enthusiast Jimmy Guieu published a book length account of the story e Why the abduction claim collapsed The decisive problem is not merely that investigators failed to find an alien craft. It is that Prévost later stated that the affair had been staged. Subsequent press retrospectives have therefore treated Cergy Pontoise as a celebrated media hoax rather than a surviving unexplained case. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Liste de canulars d'ovnis Liste de canulars d'ovnis Some retellings preserve an air of ambiguity because Fontaine did disappear for several days and because later accounts by the participants were not always consistent. Yet inconsistency does not strengthen the extraterrestrial interpretation. There is no independently verified photograph, radar return, physical trace or outside witness establishing that an unusual craft was present. The central narrative depended on the same small group that produced it. Cergy Pontoise remains important to Val d’Oise UFO history for a different reason: it demonstrates how a modest local report can expand through police activity, headline coverage, specialist advocacy and public expectation. Its lasting value is sociological and historical, not evidential. The case shows that an investigation can be real even when the extraordinary story prompting it is false. Maffliers: Val d’Oise’s clearest officially unresolved case At about 6.06 am on 29 June 2012, a witness near Maffliers reported seeing a stationary white luminous phenomenon over or beyond a wheat field. According to the GEIPAN file, the object appeared low on the horizon and remained visible long enough for the witness to observe it carefully. GEIPAN ultimately classified the report D1, its category for a phenomenon that remains unidentified despite having a meaningful, though not necessarily strong, evidential record. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This classification makes Maffliers unusual within the department. It does not mean that GEIPAN identified an exotic vehicle. A D1 label means that the reported phenomenon could not be satisfactorily matched to an ordinary explanation using the available information. GEIPAN’s classifications take account of both “strangeness” and “consistency”: how unusual the report appears and how much reliable, usable information supports it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMissions, Methods and Achievements GEIPANMissions, Methods and Achievements The case’s strengths are limited but genuine. The witness supplied a detailed statement; the observation occurred in daylight or early morning light rather than complete darkness; and investigators considered several conventional possibilities without closing the file as an aircraft, astronomical object or simple optical effect. Its weaknesses are equally important. It was a single witness observation, apparently without a clear photograph, instrument reading or physical trace. Estimating the distance of an isolated light is extremely difficult. A light that appears to hover over a field may actually be much farther away, and without known scale or movement its size, altitude and speed cannot be securely calculated. Maffliers is therefore unresolved in the proper sense: the records do not support a confident ordinary identification, but they also do not establish extraordinary technology. It is stronger than a poorly documented rumour and considerably weaker than a multiple witness, instrument confirmed aviation case would be. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why aircraft repeatedly look strange over Val d’Oise The eastern part of Val d’Oise lies close to Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport, while much of the department is crossed by air traffic serving the Paris region. Aircraft seen almost head on can appear nearly stationary because their movement towards the observer produces little sideways motion. Their landing lights may resemble brilliant orange or white spheres, disappearing when the aircraft turns or when buildings and cloud obscure the beam. A March 2013 case at Domont illustrates this effect particularly well. A witness watched several orange and white lights for more than an hour, used binoculars, took photographs and called gendarmes, who also saw the lights. That combination might initially sound stronger than an ordinary single witness report. GEIPAN nevertheless reconstructed the viewing direction and matched the lights to aircraft approaching and departing Charles de Gaulle. The alignment of the photographs, buildings, runway headings and flight paths allowed the case to be classified A, meaning positively identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMONT (95) 18.03.201330 Jan 2015 — DOMONT (95) 18.03.2013; Date d'observation. 18/03/2013; Région. Autre; Département. Val d'Oi... A 2023 report from a motorist travelling on the A16 was resolved with even more direct aviation evidence. The witness described a slow, white or pearly object with an"},{"title":"Cergy Hoax","url":"/cergy-hoax/","description":"The 1979 disappearance became a national alien-abduction sensation before a participant said the story had been staged.","breadcrumb":"From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights","level":2,"text":"Cergy Hoax How Cergy Pontoise Became France's Famous UFO Hoax Val d'Oise UFOs Cergy Hoax Cergy Hoax How Cergy Pontoise Became France's Famous UFO Hoax The 1979 disappearance became a national alien abduction sensation before a participant said the story had been staged. The 1979 disappearance became a national alien abduction sensation before a participant said the story had been staged. event time window The disappearance and Fontaine's return Police searches, press coverage and public expectation The confession, contradictions and lasting legacy val d oise cergy hoax Introduction The Cergy Pontoise affair began as a real missing person investigation and ended as France’s most famous alleged alien abduction hoax. On 26 November 1979, Franck Fontaine disappeared after two companions claimed that a luminous object and thick mist had surrounded his car in Cergy’s Justice Mauve district. He returned about a week later, apparently unaware of the elapsed time. Police searches, rapidly changing testimony, national television coverage and predictions of future extraterrestrial contact turned the case into a public spectacle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... The story’s evidential value, however, is extremely weak. France’s official UFO investigators found contradictions, evasiveness and no convincing physical support for an unusual craft. In 1983, Jean Pierre Prévost, one of the principal witnesses, said that he had organised the affair and that Fontaine had spent the missing days hidden in an apartment. Later refusals to accept that confession preserved controversy among a few believers, but they did not produce independent evidence capable of restoring the original abduction claim. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... The disappearance and Fontaine’s return In the early hours of 26 November 1979, Fontaine, Prévost and Salomon N’Diaye were preparing to travel to the market at Gisors to sell clothes. The account later presented to investigators was that the men noticed a light near the local power installations. Fontaine drove towards it while his companions went to obtain a camera. They claimed that mist and several small flashing spheres then gathered around the vehicle and that, once the luminous phenomenon departed, Fontaine was no longer inside. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... This was more dramatic than an ordinary report of a distant light. A named person was missing, a car remained at the scene, and two companions claimed to have witnessed the apparent disappearance. The gendarmes therefore had to consider mundane but serious possibilities, including an accident, voluntary disappearance, violence or a staged event. Their search was evidence that Fontaine could not immediately be found, not confirmation that the extraordinary explanation was credible. Accounts of the operation indicate that fields were searched, the banks of the Oise were examined and lock keepers were alerted. Le Parisien’s retrospective reported that about two hectares were combed and that the river brigade at Conflans Sainte Honorine became involved. Investigators reportedly found neither Fontaine nor physical traces of an airborne object. [leparisien.fr] leparisien.fr VIDE O. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres VIDEO. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres Le Parisien The first difficulties appeared during the initial interviews. GEIPAN’s present case summary, based on the earlier GEPAN investigation, records discrepancies concerning the men’s activities before the disappearance, the position of the car and the supposed light’s trajectory. Re enactments did not produce a stable, mutually consistent chronology. The witnesses also displayed markedly different behaviour, ranging from apparent indifference to intense excitement. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... Fontaine returned at the beginning of December. Contemporary retellings commonly date his reappearance to 2 December, while the official summary says his mother notified the gendarmerie on 3 December. The small dating difference reflects when the return became known rather than providing evidence of anything paranormal. Fontaine was found with relatives and a journalist at one of his companions’ homes, after news of his return had already reached a radio station. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... His initial explanation was limited. He said that he had seen lights and mist around the car, fallen asleep and later regained awareness in a cabbage field, without realising that approximately a week had passed. He reportedly wore the same clothes and still carried the money he had possessed before disappearing. Those details helped create the appearance of a sudden interruption in ordinary life, but they could not establish where he had actually been. [VICE] vice.com L’affaire de l’ovni de Cergy Pontoise L’affaire de l’ovni de Cergy Pontoise... More importantly, Fontaine’s account expanded after his return. What began as little or no memory gradually became talk of an agreeable stay with extraterrestrials, a laboratory like environment, moving luminous spheres and communications with non human beings. The progression occurred amid intense press attention and contact with committed UFO advocates. That does not prove precisely how each detail originated, but it substantially weakens the claim that the later narrative was an immediate, unaltered recollection. [leparisien.fr] leparisien.fr VIDE O. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres VIDEO. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres Le Parisien Police searches became mistaken proof The presence of police and official UFO investigators gave the affair an authority that it did not deserve. In popular retellings, a search by gendarmes can easily become “the authorities investigated an alien abduction”. In reality, police involvement followed automatically from a report that a young man had disappeared. Officers did not need to believe in a spacecraft before searching fields, checking the river or questioning the last people known to have seen him. GEPAN—the unit within France’s national space agency then responsible for studying unidentified aerospace phenomena—was notified on the day of the report. Yet the press was also contacted almost immediately. By 27 and 28 November, journalists and private UFO investigators were gathering in Cergy Pontoise and broadcasting increasingly elaborate interpretations before objective evidence had been secured. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... This overlap between investigation and publicity mattered. According to the official account, the principal witnesses repeatedly avoided efforts to obtain concrete, testable information while speaking more freely to journalists and sympathetic private investigators. Their statements changed during press conferences and interviews. Rather than converging towards a clearer factual record, the case acquired new episodes, messages and personalities. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... The case also developed apparent physical evidence that did not survive examination. A row of withered cabbages in the field associated with Fontaine’s return was presented as possible damage from the luminous object. GEPAN checked the plants and found that they were normal cabbages deliberately left by their owner to decompose as fertiliser. Laboratory analysis of samples supported that ordinary explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... A blood analysis was also discussed in connection with claims that Fontaine had experienced weightlessness. GEIPAN’s summary states that the result made such a stay unlikely. Even without relying heavily on that test, the central evidential problem remains straightforward: there was no independently verified photograph, radar detection, landing trace, instrument record or uninvolved witness demonstrating the presence of an extraordinary craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCERGY PONTOISE (95) 1979 GEIPAN... Local people later recalled seeing unexplained lights in the wider area, but retrospective reports of lights cannot establish that they were connected to Fontaine’s disappearance. They lack the necessary timing, positional detail and independent documentation. At most, they show that the publicity became attached to a wider body of local memories—a familiar process in which a prominent story encourages people to reinterpret ambiguous experiences through the same lens. [leparisien.fr] leparisien.fr VIDE O. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres VIDEO. Cergy : il y a 36 ans, Franck passait sept jours avec des extraterrestres Le Parisien 12:21 Drones,"},{"title":"GEIPAN Cases","url":"/geipan-cases-a8799d/","description":"Official files show why one Maffliers light remains unresolved while stronger-looking reports were matched to ordinary aircraft.","breadcrumb":"From Alien Abduction Hoax to Unresolved Lights","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Cases Why Some Val d'Oise UFO Reports Stay Unresolved Val d'Oise UFOs GEIPAN Cases GEIPAN Cases Why Some Val d'Oise UFO Reports Stay Unresolved Official files show why one Maffliers light remains unresolved while stronger looking reports were matched to ordinary aircraft. Official files show why one Maffliers light remains unresolved while stronger looking reports were matched to ordinary aircraft. dataset evidence What the Maffliers D1 classification means How flight paths and radar resolved other sightings The limits of witness estimates, photographs and single reports val d oise geipan cases Introduction GEIPAN’s Val d’Oise files show that an unexplained report is not necessarily the most visually impressive one. Several witnesses have described formations, fiery orange lights or oddly shaped objects, yet investigators matched them to ordinary aircraft by reconstructing sight lines, consulting aeronautical charts and checking radar tracks. The quieter Maffliers case remains unresolved because its most plausible explanation — the rising Sun distorted by unusual cloud layers — does not fit the reported direction. That distinction matters. GEIPAN, the French space agency CNES’s investigation unit for unidentified aerospace phenomena, does not classify cases according to how dramatic a witness’s story sounds. It asks whether a proposed explanation fits the time, direction, movement and environmental conditions, and whether the available evidence is sufficiently reliable. In Val d’Oise, those tests have firmly resolved some striking reports while leaving one brief, single witness observation open. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Le GEIPAN (Groupe d’Études et d’Informations sur les Phénomènes Aérospatiaux Non identifiés) est un service technique du CNES (Centre N... GEIPAN : Tout savoir sur les OVNIS et Phénomènes Aérospatiaux (PAN) Channel: Science & Vie Open on YouTube What the Maffliers classification means At about 6.06am on 29 June 2012, a walker near Maffliers reported seeing a huge white luminous object stationary above a field beside woodland. The witness said the object appeared flat underneath and concave above. After less than 20 seconds, it supposedly rose slightly, tilted and departed rapidly into the cloud cover without detectable noise or smoke. No second witness, photograph, video or instrumental record confirmed the observation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAFFLIERS (95) 29.06.2012 GEIPAN... GEIPAN investigators considered an ordinary but visually powerful explanation: the rising Sun seen between cloud layers. The timing, brightness and unusual weather made that hypothesis attractive. The previous day had reportedly brought heat and heavy rain, followed by substantial cloud cover on the morning of the sighting. A low Sun shining through gaps or interacting with layered cloud can produce an object like patch of intense light whose apparent size and boundaries are difficult to judge. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAFFLIERS (95) 29.06.2012 GEIPAN... Three reported details prevented investigators from adopting that explanation with confidence: The direction was wrong. GEIPAN calculated a difference of about 54 degrees between the witness’s stated viewing direction and the Sun. Investigators visited the site and concluded that ordinary uncertainty in the witness’s orientation could not account for such a large discrepancy. The light appeared to tilt before disappearing. GEIPAN acknowledged that glare or perception might create an impression of movement or inclination, but could not establish that this had happened. Its apparent size was extreme. The witness’s estimate was almost ten times the apparent diameter of the Sun. People commonly overestimate the size of the Sun or Moon near the horizon, but the reported exaggeration was unusually large. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAFFLIERS (95) 29.06.2012 GEIPAN... A second site visit explored whether a complex reflection might have displaced sunlight into the reported direction, but investigators found no satisfactory optical mechanism. Because the exact cloud and atmospheric conditions could not be reproduced, the investigation reached an evidential dead end: the observation resembled a known natural phenomenon, but an important part of the geometry did not fit. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAFFLIERS (95) 29.06.2012 GEIPAN... The file has an apparent classification wrinkle. Its explanatory text describes it as a D1 case: an unexplained phenomenon of medium evidential consistency. GEIPAN historically used D1 for strange cases supported by moderate evidence, often involving one witness and no recording, while D2 denoted unexplained cases with greater strangeness or consistency. The live Maffliers page, updated on 16 June 2026, now displays the broader classification D , although its retained conclusion still says D1. GEIPAN’s current classification guidance notes that the D1/D2 subdivision was used after 2008 but that its public scheme has since been presented more generally as A, B, C and D. The substantive verdict has not become stronger: the case remains unexplained on the available information, not confirmed as an extraordinary craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMAFFLIERS (95) 29.06.2012 GEIPAN... How flight paths resolved brighter reports The contrast with a March 2013 report from Domont is especially useful. A witness watched five orange and white lights appear and disappear over more than an hour, viewed them through binoculars, photographed them and called the gendarmes. The lights were described in vivid terms, including an impression resembling an orange object on fire. On testimony alone, that report could sound more substantial than the short Maffliers encounter: it lasted longer, produced photographs and was observed in the presence of police officers. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMONT (95) 18.03.201330 Jan 2015 — DOMONT (95) 18.03.2013; Date d'observation. 18/03/2013; Région. Autre; Département. Val d'Oi... The photographs nevertheless supplied the information needed to solve it. GEIPAN compared buildings in the images with the local landscape to establish the viewing direction. One prominent light lay on an azimuth of approximately 266 degrees, matching the published approach heading for runway 27R at Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport. Lines projected from the runway and the witness’s position passed between the same buildings and through the photographed lights. Other images corresponded to aircraft departing from additional Charles de Gaulle runways. GEIPAN therefore classified the report A , meaning the explanation was established: several airliners approaching or departing the airport. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMONT (95) 18.03.201330 Jan 2015 — DOMONT (95) 18.03.2013; Date d'observation. 18/03/2013; Région. Autre; Département. Val d'Oi... The apparent appearances and disappearances were not evidence of objects materialising. An aircraft approaching nearly head on can look like a stationary bright point because its sideways motion is minimal. Its landing lights may dominate while the fuselage remains invisible, particularly at night or through haze. A slight turn, cloud passage or change in viewing angle can then cause the light to weaken abruptly. Multiple aircraft using parallel runways can produce a slowly changing formation that appears organised without representing a single structured object. The Domont reconstruction was persuasive because several independent elements — photographic direction, runway alignment and known airport procedures — converged on the same explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMONT (95) 18.03.201330 Jan 2015 — DOMONT (95) 18.03.2013; Date d'observation. 18/03/2013; Région. Autre; Département. Val d'Oi... A 2023 observation along the A16 between Montmorency and Beauvais provides an even clearer example of instrumental checking. A motorist reported a white, pearly object shaped like an inverted V moving slowly on a slightly descending path. No image of the object was obtained, but the witness supplied a precise location, photographs of the observation site and a drawing. A radar map from France’s air defence planning and operations centre showed a Cessna 152 in the correct area at the stated time, performing a manoeuvre over the motorway in the reported direction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN found that the aircraft’s colour, speed, altitude and trajectory agreed with the account. Broken cumulus cloud could have hidden parts of the aeroplane and altered its apparent outline, while traffic and cabin noise inside the car could explain why the engine was not heard. The case was classified A as an identified Cessna 152. Here, a radar track outweighed the witness’s unusual shape description because it supplied a time stamped object following the required path. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel:"},{"title":"What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal","url":"/what-val-de-marnes-ufo-files-actually/","description":"Val-de-Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal Val de Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Val de Marne has a genuine but modest place in French UFO history. Its public record is not dominated by a single celebrated encounter, a military confrontation or a sustained regional “flap”. val de marne Introduction The department’s dense urban landscape and proximity to Paris Orly Airport give residents an unusually busy sky. Aircraft, approach lights, atmospheric effects, balloons, lanterns and, more recently, satellite formations all provide potential sources of confusion. Yet proximity to an airport does not automatically make a report an aviation or radar case. In the published record, most Val de Marne observations remain civilian visual sightings rather than instrument confirmed incidents. [Geipan] geipan.fr MAISONS ALFORT (94) 27.06.2011 GEIPAN... The department’s strongest unresolved case The most important published Val de Marne case is the observation at Choisy le Roi on 21 May 2012. GEIPAN, the specialist unit of the French space agency CNES, currently lists it as a category D case: a phenomenon left unidentified after investigation, with medium or strong evidential consistency. The published summary describes a silent flying object that appeared stationary before moving away. The classification was reaffirmed in a database update dated 16 June 2026. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr choisy le roi 94 Observations OVNI à Choisy Le Roi — CarteOvni.frD1 21 mai 2012 CHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012. Observation d'un objet volant silencieux st... That classification makes Choisy le Roi unusual within the department. A category D result is not awarded simply because a witness could not name what they saw. GEIPAN assesses both the residual strangeness of an observation and its “consistency”, meaning the quantity, precision and reliability of the information gathered. Category A means identified; B means probably identified; C means that identification is impossible because the information is inadequate; D means that a sufficiently substantial case remains unexplained after known possibilities have been considered. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Even so, “unexplained” should not be translated as “extraterrestrial”. GEIPAN explicitly states that it investigates unusual aerospace observations using recognised scientific knowledge and does not operate as an extraterrestrial life research group. A D classification records the limits of the investigation, not the origin of the phenomenon. It may reflect an unusual object, an uncommon combination of ordinary circumstances, or missing contextual information that cannot now be reconstructed. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The Choisy le Roi report is therefore best understood as Val de Marne’s principal unresolved file, but not as a confirmed technological mystery. Its importance lies in its relative evidential strength compared with the department’s many explained or poorly documented observations. There is no publicly demonstrated radar trace, independently recorded flight data or physical residue that would elevate it to the level of a multi sensor aviation incident. Maisons Alfort and the problem of misleading motion A much more widely reported sighting occurred at Maisons Alfort on 27 June 2011. Witnesses described a red orange luminous object moving silently through the night sky. Contemporary coverage presented the experience vividly: two residents initially believed that a fast, low object was approaching and passing overhead, while other people in the Paris area also reported seeing the light. [leparisien.fr] leparisien.fr Des ovnis dans le ciel parisien? Des témoins racontent Des ovnis dans le ciel parisien? Des témoins racontent GEIPAN ultimately classified the phenomenon as B, probably identified, with a Thai style sky lantern as the proposed explanation. The official file records a clear night, an urban setting and a relatively low strangeness score. It also contains the witness questionnaire, weather information and an image supplied during the inquiry. [Geipan] geipan.fr MAISONS ALFORT (94) 27.06.2011 GEIPAN... The case illustrates how difficult it is to judge speed, distance and size when a light is seen against a dark sky without a secure reference point. A small lantern at moderate distance can seem large, fast or low, particularly when wind changes its apparent direction. Its flame produces the orange or reddish colour frequently reported in modern UFO sightings, while the absence of engine noise can make the object seem technologically unusual. This does not mean that the witnesses invented or carelessly described the event. They reported a real visual experience that was initially difficult for them to interpret. The distinction is important: a sincere witness can be wrong about an object’s distance or speed without being dishonest. The Maisons Alfort file became useful precisely because investigation weakened the exotic interpretation while preserving the witnesses’ account as evidence of how the phenomenon appeared from the ground. [Geipan] geipan.fr Methodology GEIPANClassification in A, B, C, D1 or D2 The objectivity of this data is weighted according to a specific table, part of the GEIPAN methodology.... A revisit process, or a new a... Créteil produced a closely related example on 18 November 2009. GEIPAN also classified that observation as B and attributed it to a sky lantern. Taken together, Créteil and Maisons Alfort show that lantern releases were a recurring source of strange orange lights over the department during this period, rather than evidence of a local wave of structured craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN Older reports are often unresolved for weaker reasons Several Val de Marne files remain open only because their documentation is too thin. That difference is essential when comparing cases. At Nogent sur Marne on 31 October 1980, a train passenger reported two luminous points that seemed to follow the train at about 6.45 in the morning. GEIPAN classifies the case as C because no additional reliable information was collected. The apparent pursuit could have resulted from a distant stationary light remaining in roughly the same direction as the observer moved, an effect familiar to passengers watching the Moon, stars or lights near the horizon. However, the surviving information is not sufficient to test a particular explanation. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Other category C entries include Bonneuil sur Marne in August 1979, Saint Maur des Fossés in August 1999 and, more recently, Cachan on 12 September 2025. In each instance, the category means that the available evidence is inadequate, not that investigators established the presence of an extraordinary object. The Cachan file was published with that classification in March 2026. [carteovni.fr] carteovni.fr bonneuil sur marne 1979 0800655 bonneuil sur marne 1979 0800655 This is one reason raw UFO totals can be misleading. Combining categories C and D produces a list of “unidentified” reports, but the two groups answer different questions. A D case survives investigation despite reasonably substantial evidence. A C case cannot support a firm conclusion because the evidence never became strong enough. For readers assessing Val de Marne’s record, Choisy le Roi and Nogent sur Marne should therefore not be treated as equally mysterious. [Geipan] geipan.fr How does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPANThe classification process is done according a quantitative and qualitative assessment of two parameters: the consistency (C) of... Orly brings aviation into the story, but not always mystery Paris Orly Airport makes aviation a natural part of the department’s UFO history. Aircraft approaching or departing the airport can appear unusually bright when their landing lights face an observer. They may seem stationary during a head on approach, then apparently change direction as the viewing angle shifts. Busy airspace also provides access to flight information that can help investigators test an aircraft explanation. One published aviation report dated 8 October 2000 involved a flight from Orly towards Grenoble. GEIPAN eventually classified the observed phenomenon as B and identified a bird as the likely explanation. The case is significant not because it remained mysterious, but because even observations made from aircraft can be affected by limited viewing time, relative motion and uncertainty about distance. Professional aviation experience improves a report’s potential value, but it does not make misidentification impossible. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... More recent flight reports connected"},{"title":"Choisy Case","url":"/choisy-case/","description":"The 2012 Choisy-le-Roi sighting remains Val-de-Marne's strongest unresolved file, but its category D status does not prove an exotic craft.","breadcrumb":"What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Choisy Case Why Choisy le Roi Remains Val de Marne's Key Mystery Val de Marne UFOs Choisy Case Choisy Case Why Choisy le Roi Remains Val de Marne's Key Mystery The 2012 Choisy le Roi sighting remains Val de Marne's strongest unresolved file, but its category D status does not prove an exotic craft. The 2012 Choisy le Roi sighting remains Val de Marne's strongest unresolved file, but its category D status does not prove an exotic craft. event time window What the witness reported in 2012 Why GEIPAN retained category D What evidence is still missing val de marne choisy case Introduction The sighting reported at Choisy le Roi on 21 May 2012 is the strongest published unresolved UFO case in Val de Marne . At about 10.12 pm, a lone motorist described a silent, illuminated object that appeared to stop above the road, changed shape and colour, then climbed into cloud. France’s official investigation service, GEIPAN, considered a festive balloon the only plausible conventional explanation but concluded that it did not adequately reproduce the reported behaviour. The case was therefore retained in category D, historically described more specifically as D1: unexplained after investigation, but supported by only moderate evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... That status makes Choisy le Roi important within Val de Marne, where other prominent reports have generally been explained, judged probably explained or left unusable through lack of information. It does not, however, establish that an exotic craft was present. The file rests on one brief visual observation, without photographs, independent witnesses, radar data or physical evidence. Francia: un encuentro con los investigadores de ovnis Channel: Perfil Open on YouTube What the witness reported in 2012 The observation began while the witness, a 40 year old man, was driving through a built up part of Choisy le Roi. GEIPAN records the weather as very cloudy or overcast. At 10.12 pm, he noticed a luminous phenomenon moving approximately from north to south. When he stopped at a red light, he obtained a clearer view and described a roughly cylindrical form, tapering downwards in a way he compared to a pointed icon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... The witness then got out of his vehicle. He said that the phenomenon had become stationary in front of him and that an intense light appeared to emerge from several small window like openings. An orange luminous sphere or glow surrounded the object. After remaining still for several seconds, it reportedly rose, appeared to change into an inverted V shape, turned white and disappeared into the low cloud layer. GEIPAN gives the total duration as approximately 15 seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... The published testimony record describes the object as silent and gives an estimated distance of 45 metres. Its apparent dimensions were recorded as roughly five to seven metres high, with a base about five metres across. These figures should be read as the witness’s estimates rather than measured dimensions. With an unfamiliar object against a cloudy night sky, there was no verified reference distance from which its true size could be calculated. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... Several features made the report more unusual than a routine account of a distant light: the witness perceived a structured, three dimensional object rather than a point; he described illuminated openings and an orange surrounding glow; the phenomenon appeared to halt above the road; it was reportedly silent at what seemed to be close range; its shape and colour appeared to change as it departed. Those details are the core of the mystery. They are also dependent entirely on one person’s perception during a short, surprising event. Why GEIPAN retained category D GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of the French space agency CNES, does not classify a case solely according to whether the witness recognised what was seen. Investigators assess two separate qualities: residual strangeness , meaning how poorly the report fits known phenomena after plausible explanations have been tested, and consistency , meaning the quantity, precision and reliability of the available information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLe glossaire du GeipanLa classification d'un cas nécessite l'évaluation, d'une part, de son degré de consistance (C) et, d'autre pa... The current Choisy le Roi record gives a strangeness score of 0.60 and a consistency score of 0.70. In GEIPAN’s framework, an unexplained classification can be retained when the information is considered sufficiently consistent to support the remaining level of strangeness. The case page now displays the broader category D, while its written conclusion and older database entries retain the D1 wording used for a strange but moderately supported case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... The distinction matters. A category C case is one in which investigators cannot reach a useful conclusion because the information is inadequate. A D or D1 case is different: GEIPAN considers the observation sufficiently documented to analyse, yet finds that the plausible explanations tested do not fit well enough. D1 commonly includes precisely the limitation found here—a single witness with a detailed account but no photograph or recording. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLe glossaire du GeipanLa classification d'un cas nécessite l'évaluation, d'une part, de son degré de consistance (C) et, d'autre pa... For Choisy le Roi, the official summary identifies a festive balloon as the only plausible conventional hypothesis considered. Such a balloon could account for silence, slow movement, bright internal lighting, drifting and apparent changes caused by rotation or partial obscuration. GEIPAN nevertheless judged the hypothesis difficult to reconcile with the full account, especially the apparent stop in front of the witness, the strongly structured form with illuminated “windows”, and the subsequent change of shape during ascent. It therefore concluded that the reported phenomenon could not be explained and classified it as an object of unknown nature. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... The case’s standing is reinforced by comparison with the rest of Val de Marne’s published record. The much publicised Maisons Alfort lights of 2011 were classified B and attributed probably to a Thai lantern. A Saint Maur des Fossés report from July 2012 was also classified B, with a balloon proposed. Other department cases, including reports from Maisons Alfort and Saint Maur, have been placed in category C because too little reliable information was available. GEIPAN search results continue to single out Choisy le Roi as the department’s D classified case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why category D is not proof of an exotic craft The strongest reason for caution is that the file contains no evidence independent of the witness’s recollection. There is one testimony, no photograph or video, no second observer whose account can be compared, and no publicly documented radar or aviation record confirming that an object occupied the reported position. GEIPAN itself notes the surprising absence of other witnesses, given that the event supposedly occurred over a road in a densely populated town. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCHOISY LE ROI (94) 21.05.2012 GEIPAN... The apparent distance and dimensions are particularly uncertain. Humans can estimate the size of an unfamiliar object only when its distance is known, and estimate distance reliably only when its size or surrounding position is known. At night, against cloud, that circular problem becomes acute. A small illuminated balloon tens or hundreds of metres away can appear much larger and closer, while its rotation may create the impression that its outline is changing. GEIPAN has itself warned in discussions of lantern and balloon cases that witness distance estimates are often unreliable when the observed object cannot first be identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 The reported stationary phase also has alternatives that cannot be fully excluded. A drifting object may appear motionless when viewed head on, when its movement briefly aligns with the observer’s line of sight, or when a moving witness stops and the visual geometry changes. GEIPAN recognises an “apparent stationary” or pivot effect in which relative movement can make an airborne object seem fixed against the sky. Whether that mechanism applies here cannot now be demonstrated, but the"},{"title":"Common Explanations","url":"/common-explanations-15535f/","description":"Lanterns, aircraft, birds and misleading motion explain many local reports, while poor documentation leaves others impossible to assess.","breadcrumb":"What Val de Marne's UFO Files Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Common Explanations Why Strange Lights Over Val de Marne Mislead Witnesses Common Explanations Common Explanations Why Strange Lights Over Val de Marne Mislead Witnesses Lanterns, aircraft, birds and misleading motion explain many local reports, while poor documentation leaves others impossible to assess. Lanterns, aircraft, birds and misleading motion explain many local reports, while poor documentation leaves others impossible to assess. mechanism Lanterns and orange lights over Paris suburbs Aircraft, birds and apparent motion near Orly Why weak evidence produces category C cases val de marne common explanations Introduction Many strange light reports from Val de Marne are best understood as sincere mistakes rather than fabricated stories or evidence of extraordinary craft. The department combines a dense, brightly lit urban skyline with one of France’s busiest aviation environments. From a balcony, road or garden, witnesses often see a distant light without reliable clues to its size, height or distance. A lantern can appear to accelerate, an approaching aircraft can seem stationary, and a bird lit from below can resemble a solid luminous object. The official record reflects this pattern. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, has attributed Val de Marne reports to lanterns, balloons, birds, fireworks, clouds, the Moon, the Sun, a meteor and an aircraft condensation trail. Numerous other files remain unclassified in practical terms because the information supplied was too weak to test any explanation. Only a small minority survive investigation as genuinely unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; MAISONS ALFORT (94) 05.07.2020, 05/07/2020, A, Val de Marne, Trainée de condensat... Lanterns and orange lights over the suburbs Orange or reddish lights are among the most misleading sights in the local record. Small paper sky lanterns contain a flame that heats the air inside and carries them upwards. At night, the paper body may be invisible, leaving only a warm, flickering light. Because the lantern drifts with air currents, its movement can look smooth, silent and purposeful. Changes in wind or perspective may create the impression that it has stopped, turned or suddenly moved away. The best known Val de Marne example occurred at Maisons Alfort on 27 June 2011. Witnesses reported a red orange luminous phenomenon moving silently through the sky. The sighting attracted press interest because it appeared dramatic and because reports of lights were also circulating elsewhere around Paris. GEIPAN nevertheless classified the case B—probably identified—and judged a Thai style sky lantern to be the most plausible explanation. The classification does not imply that the witnesses were careless. It means that the reported colour, silence, motion and appearance were sufficiently consistent with a familiar object whose true distance was difficult to judge. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; MAISONS ALFORT (94) 05.07.2020, 05/07/2020, A, Val de Marne, Trainée de condensat... Maisons Alfort was not an isolated result. GEIPAN’s Val de Marne listings identify lanterns in cases from Créteil in November 2009, Saint Maurice in September 2014, Le Perreux sur Marne in June 2015 and Limeil Brévannes in December 2015. The recurrence matters more than any individual report. It shows that similar descriptions—orange light, silence, slow travel and uncertain altitude—have repeatedly produced the same ordinary explanation in different parts of the department. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; LIMEIL BREVANNES (94) 07.12.2015, 07/12/2015, B · Val de Marne, Lanterne thai; V... Lanterns also encourage witnesses to infer a larger object connecting several separate lights. A group released together may spread out, converge visually or seem to maintain a formation. The eye naturally seeks structure, particularly when there is no visible horizon or body around the lights. GEIPAN investigations elsewhere have noted that several lantern like lights can be perceived as lamps attached to a single dark mass, even when the witnesses have actually seen independent drifting objects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete533 GEIPANCOMPTE RENDU D'ENQUÊTEOctober 28, 2019 — les hypothèses privilégiées par l'enquêteur sont: des méprises avec des lanternes thaïlan... Published: October 28, 2019 This is why descriptions such as “silent”, “orange” and “moving together” are useful clues but not proof of anything exotic. They can be precisely the features expected from lanterns. Aircraft, birds and apparent motion near Orly Val de Marne’s aviation setting creates a second major source of confusion. Paris Orly Airport lies partly within the department, while arrival, departure and radar directed routes extend across the surrounding urban area. Groupe ADP reports more than 200,000 annual aircraft movements at Orly, and official aviation studies describe variations and dispersion in flight paths over densely populated communities. The result is a sky containing aircraft at different heights, headings and stages of approach throughout much of the day and evening. [Paris Aéroport] parisaeroport.fr Paris Aéroport Paris Orly Airport Paris AéroportParis Orly Airport Groupe ADP networkOrly airport handled 216,101 movements, on the rise at 6.1% compared to 2024, and re... Yet an aircraft does not always look like an aircraft. Seen nearly head on, its landing lights may remain in almost the same part of the sky for several minutes. With little visible sideways movement, the light can appear to hover. When the aircraft turns, its forward facing lights may dim abruptly, producing an apparent high speed departure or disappearance. Haze and low cloud may hide the fuselage and navigation lights while enlarging or diffusing the brightest lamp. Conversely, an aircraft travelling across the observer’s line of sight may seem much faster than it is if the witness assumes it is nearby. Human observers cannot determine the size, distance and speed of an isolated point of light independently. An object interpreted as small and close will appear to be moving at one speed; the same angular movement interpreted as large and distant produces a completely different estimate. Without radar, flight records, photographs containing reference points or observations from separate locations, claims of exceptional speed are therefore difficult to substantiate. The urban environment compounds the problem. Buildings block the beginning and end of a flight path, leaving witnesses with only a short segment of movement. Street lighting reduces night vision, while reflections from windows can introduce lights that move when the observer changes position. Patchy cloud may repeatedly conceal and reveal an aircraft, creating apparent jumps or pulses. Birds provide an even less intuitive explanation. GEIPAN classified reports at Le Plessis Trévise in June 2017 and Charenton le Pont in October 2020 as bird related. At dusk or at night, a pale bird may catch illumination from roads, buildings or the setting Sun while its wings and body remain indistinct. Wingbeats can create rhythmic flashes; banking can make the light vanish; and a flock may briefly resemble a structured formation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPANListe des nouveaux cas; MAISONS ALFORT (94) 05.07.2020, 05/07/2020, A, Val de Marne, Trainée de condensat... Such identifications are easy to dismiss after the event because the witness may remember a “light” rather than an animal. But perception begins with incomplete visual information. The brain supplies the most plausible object it can construct at the time. In an area where residents expect aircraft but see them from unusual angles, both ordinary aeroplanes and less expected objects such as birds can acquire apparently mechanical behaviour. En quête d'ovnis : qui sont les chasseurs d'aliens ? Channel: Investigation Open on YouTube Ordinary objects can produce extraordinary movement Misidentification is often caused less by the object itself than by the way motion is perceived. Against a featureless night sky, there may be no stable background from which to judge whether a light is moving. Small involuntary eye movements can make a stationary light appear to drift, a well known perceptual effect sometimes called autokinesis. Even without that effect, movement by the witness—walking, driving or leaning around a window—can be wrongly attributed to the observed light. Clouds create another moving reference frame. A bright planet, the Moon or an aircraft light seen through travelling cloud may appear to race in the opposite direction. In July 2023, a Champigny sur Marne report was identified as the Moon; a Nogent sur Marne case from October that year was attributed to cloud. A Vincennes report from September"},{"title":"Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases","url":"/why-vars-ufo-reputation-rests-on-two/","description":"Var occupies a distinctive place in French UFO history because it contains one of the country’s best-known alleged landing cases: the 8 January 1981 incident...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases Var occupies a distinctive place in French UFO history because it contains one of the country’s best known alleged landing cases: the 8 January 1981 incident... Var occupies a distinctive place in French UFO history because it contains one of the country’s best known alleged landing cases: the 8 January 1981 incident at Trans en Provence. That report was not merely a distant light in the sky. var Introduction Yet Var’s wider record is much less dramatic than the fame of Trans en Provence suggests. The official archive contains many reports later attributed to aircraft, balloons, meteors, lanterns, lasers, photographic effects and other ordinary causes. Several older entries remain unresolved chiefly because the information is too weak to test. Only a small number combine genuine strangeness with reasonably substantial evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... The department’s history is therefore best understood not as a continuous “hotspot”, but as one landmark physical trace case, one notable modern close range report at Saint Zacharie, and a much larger background of ambiguous or eventually explained observations. Trans en Provence: the case that defined Var At about 5 p.m. on 8 January 1981, a man was working on a masonry structure protecting a water pump at his property in Trans en Provence. According to his statement, a whistling sound drew his attention to a circular grey object descending a few dozen metres away. He described it as roughly 2.5 metres across and 1.7 metres high, with a thicker band around its middle and protruding shapes beneath it. The episode lasted only 30 to 40 seconds. The object allegedly rose vertically and disappeared rapidly without visible smoke or flame. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... The witness then found a roughly two metre wide mark with darkened areas and apparent scraping around part of its circumference. The local gendarmerie attended the following day, interviewed him, photographed the site and took soil and wild lucerne samples at several distances from the trace. That speed matters: it reduced, though did not eliminate, the risk that weather, later traffic or repeated handling had completely altered the scene before examination. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... GEPAN, the predecessor of today’s GEIPAN unit within the French space agency CNES, conducted its own site visit on 17 February. It commissioned several laboratories to examine the soil, deposits and vegetation. The current official case page continues to classify the incident as category D, meaning that the investigation did not establish an explanation despite the available evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... What the laboratory work actually found The official synthesis reported significant compression of the ground, indications of heating below 600°C, dark material possibly associated with combustion, and small deposits including iron, iron oxide, phosphates and zinc. Investigators suggested that some deposits might have resulted from painted material rubbing against the ground. They also reported biochemical changes in lucerne samples that appeared to vary with distance from the marked area. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... Those findings are the main reason Trans en Provence became internationally famous. Unlike an unsupported story about a light, the file contained photographs, police documentation, physical samples and analyses from several laboratories. CNES still presents the underlying technical note, gendarmerie record, photographs and specialist reports through the case archive. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... The results did not, however, identify an exotic craft. GEIPAN’s own summary says the analyses did not prove the witness’s account. They indicated that an unusual physical event had affected the site, but they did not establish what caused it. The suggested role of an intense electrical field in the plant changes was a hypothesis, not a detected field or a demonstrated mechanism. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... That distinction is essential. “Unexplained” means that the available investigation did not settle the cause. It does not mean that a spacecraft was confirmed. Why the physical trace remains disputed The strongest sceptical challenge is that the marks may have been produced by an ordinary vehicle operating on the property. Critics have argued that the pattern resembled tyres slipping or turning on the ground and that vehicles connected with the witness’s building work provided a straightforward source. A published critical review of France’s official UFO studies states that the attending officers considered the marks consistent with tyres and argues that GEPAN did not adequately pursue that possibility. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical Inquirer This interpretation addresses an important weakness in the case: no independent witness saw the alleged object, and no instrument recorded its arrival or departure. The physical trace therefore has to carry much of the evidential weight. If that trace arose from a vehicle, machinery or previous activity, the laboratory findings would no longer independently support the aerial narrative. The official and sceptical readings nevertheless do not fit together perfectly. GEIPAN’s published summary describes significant compression, heating and material deposits, while the tyre explanation treats these features as either ordinary consequences of ground activity or as results overinterpreted during the investigation. The dispute is not simply over whether a mark existed; it is over whether the mark required an unusual cause and whether the sampling design could reliably distinguish that cause from normal environmental and mechanical effects. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... There are further limitations: The principal observation came from one person. There was no independent visual confirmation. The site was not a controlled laboratory scene. Vehicles, construction activity, soil variation and vegetation stress all provide possible confounding factors. Samples were collected at different times. The gendarmerie sampled promptly, but GEPAN’s later visit occurred more than a month after the event. No object specific material was recovered. The reported elements were not shown to require an unknown technology. The biological effects lacked a unique signature. Plant stress can have numerous causes, and a pattern of change does not by itself identify the agent responsible. The result is a case stronger than a bare anecdote but weaker than its popular reputation as scientific proof. It remains unresolved in the official archive, while a coherent mundane alternative has been advanced without being demonstrated beyond dispute. 1981 🇫🇷 UFOB [REPORT] Renato Nicolai's sighting and GEPAN research (1984) Channel: UFOB Your UAP Library Open on YouTube Saint Zacharie: Var’s other substantial unresolved report A much later Var case occurred near Saint Zacharie on 12 January 2015. The witness reported seeing, at comparatively close range, a silent oval mass of dark grey appearance carrying lights. GEIPAN’s summary describes the phenomenon as moving without noise and records relatively high ratings for both strangeness and evidential consistency. The case page was updated in June 2026 and remains classified as unexplained. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT ZACHARIE (83) 12.01.2015 GEIPAN... The published summary also calls it a D1 case. In GEIPAN terminology, D1 generally denotes a strange but moderately supported unexplained observation, often based on a single witness and lacking decisive photographic, video or physical evidence. D2 is reserved for cases with stronger consistency, such as several independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. [Geipan] geipan.fr Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPAN... Saint Zacharie is therefore interesting for different reasons from Trans en Provence. It offers a close range description with apparently good witness information, but it has no comparable physical sample programme. Its unresolved status reflects the difficulty of matching the reported form, movement and silence to a known object, not proof that an extraordinary vehicle was present. The case also illustrates why official classifications should be read as provisional assessments. GEIPAN explicitly notes that D cases may later move to another category if new information, comparison data or a better explanation emerges."},{"title":"Modern Cases","url":"/modern-cases-43e634/","description":"The 2015 Saint-Zacharie report shows how a close, silent and strange sighting can remain unexplained even without photographs or physical traces.","breadcrumb":"Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases","level":2,"text":"Modern Cases Why Saint Zacharie Still Defies a Clear Explanation Var UFOs Modern Cases Modern Cases Why Saint Zacharie Still Defies a Clear Explanation The 2015 Saint Zacharie report shows how a close, silent and strange sighting can remain unexplained even without photographs or physical traces. The 2015 Saint Zacharie report shows how a close, silent and strange sighting can remain unexplained even without photographs or physical traces. event time window The close range sighting reported in 2015 What an official D1 classification does and does not mean How Saint Zacharie fits Var's wider pattern of explained and weak cases var modern cases Introduction Saint Zacharie is the strongest modern unresolved sighting in Var, but it is not a modern equivalent of the celebrated Trans en Provence case. On 12 January 2015, one man reported that a large, oval, metallic looking form passed within a few metres of his home before climbing silently over nearby trees. GEIPAN investigated the location, reconstructed the reported movement, examined possible balloon, airship and psychological explanations, and ultimately found none convincing enough to close the case. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT ZACHARIE (83) 12.01.2015 GEIPAN... What keeps the report interesting is its combination of apparent proximity, sustained viewing and environmental reference points. What keeps it weak is equally important: there was only one witness, no photograph, no radar confirmation and no physical trace. Saint Zacharie therefore illustrates a central lesson of Var’s recent UFO history. A report can remain genuinely unexplained after investigation without becoming proof of an extraordinary craft—or even proof that the witness’s estimated distance, size and movement were objectively correct. The close range sighting reported in 2015 The incident began at about 7 a.m. on a cold Monday morning. The witness, a 60 year old man living in a mobile home outside Saint Zacharie, had got up, lit the heater and started making coffee. He then noticed a diffuse light through a glazed door facing a small plot where he did not expect any vehicle or normal source of illumination. According to his account, he stepped onto the terrace and saw an object approaching almost directly towards him. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... He described the phenomenon as an oval or elongated mass with a light grey, metallic appearance. A white but non dazzling light was visible at the front, while a rear light appeared white with a greenish tint. When the object passed side on, he also reported a structured yellow and red marking that reminded him of a commercial logo. His later reconstruction compared its length with that of a small car, although the investigation noted that his size estimates varied by roughly a factor of two between accounts. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The most striking part of the story was the reported passage between two mobile homes separated by only about six metres. The witness estimated that the object came within roughly three metres of him and moved at little more than walking pace, perhaps 2–3 kilometres per hour. He said it produced no engine noise, hum, airflow or disturbance of nearby branches. After he walked around his home to continue watching, it moved north north east, tilted, gained some speed and height, and disappeared above the trees. GEIPAN’s reconstruction suggested at least about a minute of observation, although the witness’s own estimate ranged from one to five minutes because he said he had lost his sense of time. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... Those nearby structures make Saint Zacharie more substantial than a typical report of a point of light. The mobile homes, path, shed and trees supplied apparent scale and distance references, while the witness changed position and claimed to see the phenomenon from the front, side and rear. Yet these details are still derived from one person’s perception and later reconstruction. There was no independent measurement showing that the object really was car sized or only three metres away. What the investigation could and could not test GEIPAN received the witness’s telephone report on 20 January, eight days after the event. An investigator was appointed on 27 January and met him the following day for an interview, on site reconstruction and what the report describes as a cognitive interview intended to recover the account without unnecessarily leading the witness. The completed case was later considered by GEIPAN’s expert panel in March 2017. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The field inquiry confirmed the physical layout but found no supporting witness. Other people had been present in neighbouring mobile homes, yet none had seen the event. Checks with the municipal police, gendarmerie and local area produced no matching report. The file also records that the witness initially approached the gendarmerie but that no formal statement was taken there. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... Weather information from the nearest available station, about 24 kilometres away at Aix en Provence, indicated clear conditions and very light northerly wind. That wind direction was opposite to the reported north north easterly movement, but GEIPAN correctly stopped short of treating this as decisive: wind in a valley can differ from measurements at a distant station. It was still fully dark at 7 a.m., and because the reported phenomenon was so close, an astronomical body was not considered a useful explanation. No relevant radar trace was recovered. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The absence of radar evidence does little either way. A small, low flying object may not be recorded or retained by the systems available for examination, while an entirely perceptual event would naturally leave no radar trace. Similarly, the lack of physical traces is not surprising for something said to have remained airborne. It does, however, mean that every important characteristic—shape, size, distance, silence and trajectory—rests on testimony alone. Mystères : OVNI à Trans en Provence ! Avec Rudy & @mikealdebaran Channel: La Séance de Minuit Open on YouTube Why the ordinary explanations fell short GEIPAN’s main physical candidate was an escaped advertising balloon. Such balloons can be several metres long, can have an oval body, may carry branding and can drift silently. Darkness might also conceal fins, tethering equipment or other features that would make the object immediately recognisable in daylight. In basic appearance, therefore, a balloon is not a strained comparison. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... The difficulties were contextual rather than based on any impossible performance. The reported path took the form very close to roofs, branches and other obstacles without an obvious collision. A loose balloon could follow such a line by chance, and GEIPAN noted that the witness did not actually describe active avoidance turns. Nevertheless, investigators considered it difficult to explain why a large branded balloon would be loose before dawn in an isolated rural setting, then apparently rise away without being identified or reported elsewhere. The inquiry assigned the balloon hypothesis a likelihood below 30 per cent, an investigator’s judgement rather than a statistically calculated probability. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... A small airship or controlled lighter than air craft could better account for apparent steering, but it created further problems. The witness reported no gondola, pilot, propeller, control surface, motor noise or downward airflow despite claiming several viewing angles at very short range. GEIPAN regarded an airship explanation as still less plausible, particularly given the location, hour and difficulty of piloting so close to obstacles in darkness. [Geipan] geipan.fr Compte rendu enquete457 Modèle de document par défaut CNES version 2.0 Janvier 2010... It is worth separating “not validated” from “physically impossible”. Nothing in the reported speed or climb required extraordinary acceleration. GEIPAN explicitly observed that the stated size, shape and speed were not beyond those of familiar objects. The unresolved element was the whole combination: an apparently large, silent, illuminated object in an unlikely place, following a fortunate low level path, without leaving independent evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr SAINT ZACHARIE (83)"},{"title":"Trans Case","url":"/trans-case/","description":"France's most famous physical-trace case remains compelling because samples were tested, yet disputed because an ordinary vehicle may explain the marks.","breadcrumb":"Why Var's UFO Reputation Rests on Two Cases","level":2,"text":"Trans Case Did Anything Unusual Land at Trans en Provence? Var UFOs Trans Case Trans Case Did Anything Unusual Land at Trans en Provence? France's most famous physical trace case remains compelling because samples were tested, yet disputed because an ordinary vehicle may explain the marks. France's most famous physical trace case remains compelling because samples were tested, yet disputed because an ordinary vehicle may explain the marks. event time window The witness account and the first site inspection What the soil and plant tests actually found The tyre explanation and unresolved weaknesses var trans case Introduction The 8 January 1981 incident at Trans en Provence remains the defining UFO case in the Var because it joined an unusual eyewitness account to something that could be photographed, sampled and tested. Gendarmes examined the site the next morning, collected soil and vegetation, and passed the material into an investigation by GEPAN, the official unit then operating within France’s space agency, CNES. Laboratory work found genuine physical changes in part of the ground and measurable differences in nearby plants. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... That does not establish that an extraordinary craft landed there. The central difficulty is attribution: were the marks produced by the object described by the sole witness, or were investigators analysing an unrelated trace made by an ordinary vehicle? The photographs and reports support the conclusion that the surface had been compressed, scraped and contaminated with small deposits. They do not securely identify what caused those effects. The later tyre mark argument therefore weakens the case considerably, without resolving every feature of it. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R The witness account and the first site inspection At about 5 p.m., the witness was completing masonry work around a water pump on his hillside property. He said a slight whistling sound drew his attention to a grey, circular object descending towards a lower terrace. In his first statement, he described something roughly 2.5 metres across, with a thicker band around its middle and several circular shapes underneath. He saw no flame or smoke. After remaining near or on the ground for only a few seconds, it reportedly rose, passed above the trees and departed rapidly. The whole sighting was estimated at 30 to 40 seconds, with the witness never closer than about 30 metres. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... He approached the spot afterwards and noticed a roughly two metre circular marking with darker, scraped looking sections. His wife and neighbours saw the trace the following morning, after which the gendarmerie was contacted. Officers arrived at about 11.30 a.m. on 9 January, interviewed the witness, photographed and measured the marks, and collected soil and wild lucerne samples. This prompt response is one of the case’s strongest features: the original samples were not collected months or years after the report. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R The officers described two apparent concentric circles, about 2.20 and 2.40 metres in diameter, forming a band approximately ten centimetres wide. Two roughly opposite portions of that band were particularly dark and bore striations resembling sideways scraping. A private investigator who visited several days later gave a somewhat different description, seeing a horseshoe shaped area rather than two neatly opposed sections. GEPAN’s own report recorded this difference instead of treating every account of the trace as identical. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R There were nevertheless important limitations from the beginning. No second person saw the object. The physical marks were on a compacted earth terrace close to a vehicle entrance, rather than in an isolated area that machinery could not reach. GEPAN learned of the report on 12 January but did not conduct its full site visit until 17 February, after heavy weekend rain, visits by private UFO investigators and local publicity. The first gendarmerie samples retained value, but the scene itself was no longer controlled by the time the official scientific team arrived. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R What the soil tests actually found Four laboratories examined different aspects of the soil. The results indicated that part of the marked area was harder and more compact than control soil. Microscopic examination showed curved grooves, surface erosion and a piece of flint that appeared cut or polished. GEPAN summarised this as evidence that strong mechanical pressure, probably involving impact and friction, had acted on the surface. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R The often repeated claim that the ground had been heated to between 300°C and 600°C is stronger than the underlying report warrants. GEPAN concluded that frictional heating had probably occurred and that the temperature had not exceeded about 600°C, because the calcium carbonate grains lacked changes expected above that level. The report did not establish a precise lower temperature, nor did it show that the entire trace had been subjected to extreme heat. In practical terms, the analysis found signs compatible with pressure and rubbing, not a clearly defined high temperature landing event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R Small deposits included iron or iron oxide, phosphate, zinc and carbon rich material. One laboratory thought the black particles resembled combustion residues; another noted that the zinc, phosphate and carbon matrix could have come from a black primer paint rubbed against the ground. These findings were unusual enough to justify investigation, but none was exotic. Iron oxides, petroleum related residues, paint ingredients and friction marks are all compatible with ordinary mechanical equipment as well as with the witness’s unspecified “machine”. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R The soil evidence therefore answers one question while leaving another open. Something had physically pressed and scraped the terrace. The tests did not determine whether it was the object reported on 8 January, a vehicle that had previously manoeuvred there, or another heavy item. Even the official GEIPAN summary now states that the analyses provided neither a quantitative reconstruction of the event nor proof of the witness’s narrative. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... 1981: Trans en Provence case UFO Landed Channel: The Hidden Truth Open on YouTube Why the plant results attracted attention Wild lucerne was collected from the marked zone and at increasing distances from it. Michel Bounias, a biochemist at the French National Institute for Agricultural Research, examined photosynthetic pigments, amino acids, sugars and several other biochemical indicators. He reported multiple changes that appeared strongest close to the trace and diminished with distance. These included lower concentrations or altered forms of chlorophyll and other pigments, which he interpreted as premature ageing or physiological stress. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... The distance pattern made the plant work more interesting than a simple claim that vegetation “looked burnt”. It suggested a localised influence centred near the marked area, and Bounias argued that straightforward causes such as ordinary heat did not readily account for the full collection of measurements. GEPAN considered an intense electric field among the possibilities but did not identify a mechanism. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... However, the plant analysis cannot be treated as an independent detector of a landed craft. The samples were not taken under a pre planned experimental protocol, and those collected at different distances could also have differed in age, moisture, sunlight, soil conditions or physical damage. Some control samples were collected later than the first specimens, creating another possible source of variation. Statistical correlations can show that sampled plants differed; they cannot by themselves prove what stressed them or when the stress occurred. [Geipan] geipan.fr Note techn16 R Note techn16 R This distinction is frequently lost in popular retellings. The plants supplied evidence of local biochemical disturbance, not evidence of extraterrestrial technology. GEPAN’s own conclusion was cautious: the cause remained undetermined, and the laboratory findings did not demonstrate that the witness’s account was correct. [Geipan] geipan.fr TRANS EN PROVENCE (83) 08.01.1981 GEIPAN... 1981, Investigators Couldn't Explain What They Found in France Trans en Provence Channel: Unexplained Encounters Open on YouTube The tyre explanation The strongest sceptical challenge is that the apparent landing ring resembles marks made when a vehicle"},{"title":"Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate","url":"/why-vaucluses-strangest-sightings-still/","description":"Vaucluse has a genuine UFO history, but it is less a story of one decisive “landing” than of repeated waves of ambiguous lights, dramatic newspaper reports...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate Vaucluse has a genuine UFO history, but it is less a story of one decisive “landing” than of repeated waves of ambiguous lights, dramatic newspaper reports... Vaucluse has a genuine UFO history, but it is less a story of one decisive “landing” than of repeated waves of ambiguous lights, dramatic newspaper reports and a smaller number of official files. The department’s strongest pattern is not extraordinary physical evidence. vaucluse Introduction Three periods stand out. The national flying saucer wave of 1954 produced reports around Fontaine de Vaucluse and the famous tongue in cheek anti saucer by law at Châteauneuf du Pape. A much richer local cluster followed in 1977–79, documented by journalists and gendarmes. Later cases around Avignon and Bollène entered the public archive of GEIPAN, the unidentified aerospace phenomena unit of France’s space agency, CNES. Some remain unanalysable rather than truly unexplained; others have persuasive conventional explanations. [ledauphine.com] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The 1954 wave reached Fontaine de Vaucluse October 1954 was the defining “flying saucer” season in France, and Vaucluse shared in it. A local historical account describes observers at Fontaine de Vaucluse using binoculars to watch what appeared variously as a bright cigar or a disc with a dome and changing lights. Fighters were reportedly sent from the Orange Caritat air base, adding a military dimension that made the episode sound more substantial than a simple light in the sky. [Avignon Cité Millénaire] avignoncitemillenaire.com Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire The most important later detail, however, points towards a conventional explanation. An Italian physics institute was searching for a stratospheric research balloon that had crossed into France. The balloon was said to be about 28 metres wide and to carry scientific equipment. A separate “fireball” reported during the same period was identified by an observatory near Digne as a meteor. These explanations do not prove that every witness saw the same object, but they substantially weaken the idea that the Fontaine de Vaucluse episode required an exotic craft. [Avignon Cité Millénaire] avignoncitemillenaire.com Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire This is a recurring difficulty in historical UFO research. Newspaper descriptions make an object appear to change shape, colour and behaviour, yet these variations may reflect different viewing angles, different phenomena or witnesses interpreting a distant light without knowing its size. The 1954 Vaucluse material is memorable, but it survives mainly through retrospective local accounts rather than a complete, modern investigation file. The period also produced the department’s best known piece of UFO folklore. In 1954, the mayor of Châteauneuf du Pape issued a municipal order banning flying saucers and “flying cigars” from landing, taking off or flying over the commune, with offending craft supposedly liable to impoundment. It was humour and publicity rather than evidence of an incident, but it shows how deeply the flying saucer wave had entered local culture. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The 1977–79 flap created Vaucluse’s richest archive The most important period in the department’s UFO history began in the late 1970s. Journalist Jean Leclaire ran a regular “unusual files” column between 1976 and 1980, gathering letters and witness reports from across Vaucluse and nearby areas. He later estimated that he had recorded around 30 observations in 1977 alone, involving witnesses from varied professional backgrounds. His archive is valuable because it preserves the social history of the flap: what people reported, how the press treated the stories and how explanations emerged after publication. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. Yet Leclaire also stressed that many reports eventually had ordinary explanations. Lights repeatedly seen near the Dentelles de Montmirail, for example, were attributed to the powerful landing lights of Fouga Magister training aircraft operating at night. That episode illustrates why repeated sightings do not automatically establish a persistent unknown phenomenon. A regular flight route or training exercise can generate an apparent “hotspot”, especially when mountains obscure the aircraft body and leave only a bright light visible. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. One surviving gendarmerie file from the end of 1977 shows how quickly a striking report could unravel. On 31 December, a driver saw an intense orange light descend near a road, briefly illuminating the surroundings. Gendarmes checked the site, contacted meteorological services and interviewed additional witnesses. The enquiry then discovered that a nearby resident had fired small fireworks and two illuminating flares during the same time window. The file includes the markings and operating instructions from a recovered military style “ground white star parachute” signal flare. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. That case matters because the initial testimony contained features often treated as signs of something exceptional: a luminous sphere, apparent controlled descent, unusual brightness, silence and a witness who felt surrounded by light. The investigation nevertheless found a credible source close to the reported location and time. It is a useful warning against judging a case solely by how dramatic the witness experience sounds. Bédarrides: a landing claim without supporting traces The most prominent unresolved local story from this period concerns Bédarrides. On 25 April 1979, two sisters reportedly saw a luminous object descend and appear to land roughly 200 metres away. Leclaire arranged for soil from the alleged site to be analysed, but no unusual result was found. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. The absence of a detectable trace does not prove that the witnesses invented the observation. It does, however, leave the case dependent on testimony alone. There is no publicly accessible record of radar confirmation, photography, instrument data or a lasting physical effect. For that reason, Bédarrides is better described as an unresolved but weakly supported landing claim than as evidence that an unknown craft touched down in Vaucluse. What the official files actually establish France’s official contribution to the subject comes from GEIPAN, a unit within CNES created in 1977 to collect, investigate and archive reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Its categories distinguish clearly explained observations, probable explanations, cases that cannot be analysed because information is insufficient, and genuinely unexplained cases that remain puzzling despite usable evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr quelle l'hypothèse retenue par le GEIPAN est... This distinction is essential when reading the Vaucluse record. “Unidentified” may mean that investigators lacked enough information, not that they found evidence of a technologically extraordinary object. La France a sa Zone 51… et elle est dans le Vaucluse Channel: Le Progrès Open on YouTube Avignon, June 2005: striking but unanalysable At about 4.15 am on 28 June 2005, a witness in Avignon reported a dark, boomerang like form passing slowly overhead. It appeared silent, carried no visible lights and remained in view for approximately eight to ten seconds. Mist and a strong breeze reportedly developed several minutes later, but no second witness or instrument record was found. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAVIGNON (84) 28.06.2005Dans la nuit du 27 au 28 juin 2005 vers 4 heures 15, un témoin observe le passage assez lent d'une forme som... GEIPAN classifies the case as category C: insufficient reliable information. That is a weaker finding than “unexplained after investigation”. A single short observation of a dark shape offers few dependable clues about size, range, altitude or speed. Birds, an aircraft seen under unusual lighting, cloud structure or a perceptual misjudgement cannot be tested properly after the event. The file therefore preserves an intriguing experience without establishing an anomalous craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANAVIGNON (84) 28.06.2005AVIGNON (84) 28.06.2005. Observation date. 28/06/2005. region. Provence Alpes Cote d'azur. Department. Vaucl... Avignon, May 2008: the lantern explanation Another official"},{"title":"1954 Wave","url":"/1954-wave-1a5527/","description":"Balloon and meteor explanations complicate the famous 1954 sightings, while Chateauneuf-du-Pape's by-law shows their lasting cultural impact.","breadcrumb":"Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate","level":2,"text":"1954 Wave What Was Seen Over Vaucluse in 1954? 1954 Wave 1954 Wave What Was Seen Over Vaucluse in 1954? Balloon and meteor explanations complicate the famous 1954 sightings, while Chateauneuf du Pape's by law shows their lasting cultural impact. Balloon and meteor explanations complicate the famous 1954 sightings, while Chateauneuf du Pape's by law shows their lasting cultural impact. event time window Fontaine de Vaucluse witness reports The balloon and meteor explanations How the saucer by law became folklore vaucluse 1954 wave Introduction France’s nationwide flying saucer wave reached Vaucluse most visibly on 14 October 1954, when residents of Fontaine de Vaucluse reported a large, pale object apparently hovering above the village. Contemporary newspapers described a disc with a rounded top, changing coloured lights and no obvious sound or smoke. Two military jets from Orange Caritat were sent to investigate, giving the story the appearance of an attempted aerial interception. Yet the pilots subsequently reported seeing no unknown craft, while a large scientific balloon travelling across southern France offered a strong conventional explanation. A separate fiery object reported during the same period was identified as a meteor. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... The episode matters because it shows how the 1954 wave worked at local level. A real observation, excited witnesses, hurried press reporting and military activity became woven into a much stronger story than the available evidence could securely support. Eleven days later, the mayor of Châteauneuf du Pape turned the saucer craze into lasting folklore by issuing a humorous ban on flying discs and cigars. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com l arrete du 25 octobre 1954 Le Dauphiné LibéréChateauneuf du Pape. L’arrêté du 25 octobre 1954... What did Fontaine de Vaucluse witnesses report? The alarm began around lunchtime on 14 October. According to accounts published the following day, children first drew attention to a white object moving or hovering slowly above Fontaine de Vaucluse. A crowd gathered, and some observers examined it through large binoculars. They described a broad white disc surmounted by a dome, likening the profile to a silver bowler hat. Its apparent outline reportedly shifted between a disc, a diamond and a domed shape as it changed orientation. Bright points around its lower edge were said to vary from white or violet to red. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... The reported duration was unusually long for a fleeting light: versions of the story place the object over the area for roughly an hour or more. Witnesses said there was no smoke and no audible engine. These details helped distinguish the sighting, in the public imagination, from an ordinary aircraft passing overhead. They also encouraged newspapers to treat it as one of the more impressive incidents of the French wave. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... Some of the description nevertheless depends on viewing conditions that were never adequately documented. The surviving reports do not provide dependable measurements of elevation, angular size, distance or wind direction. Without those, an observer cannot determine whether a pale object is a modest balloon relatively nearby or an enormous craft much farther away. Descriptions such as “disc”, “dome” and “diamond” may record a changing viewing angle, optical effects in binoculars or attempts to interpret an unfamiliar object whose actual scale was unknown. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... The use of binoculars did not necessarily solve that problem. High contrast objects viewed against a bright sky can acquire coloured fringes, distorted edges or an apparent structure that changes as focus and alignment shift. The Fontaine de Vaucluse reports are therefore valuable as witness testimony, but they are not the equivalent of calibrated astronomical or military observations. Did military jets chase the object? The involvement of Orange Caritat air base became the most dramatic element of the story. Fontaine de Vaucluse officials contacted the base, and two jets already available for flying duties were directed over the area. From the ground, their turns and changes of altitude appeared to some witnesses to be a pursuit. Early newspaper accounts even claimed that the pilots had sighted the object by radio, dived towards it and been left behind when it accelerated away. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... That account was contradicted almost immediately. The French air authorities issued a statement saying that the two experienced officers had searched at different altitudes but had observed no unknown craft. Other contemporary reporting indicated that the pilots arrived only after the reported object had disappeared, rather than engaging it in a high speed chase. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... This contradiction is central to any assessment of the case. The crowd saw jets manoeuvring in the same part of the sky where the object had been reported. It was easy to interpret that sequence as interception and pursuit, especially once journalists and bystanders exchanged information by telephone. The military account, however, does not support the claim that the pilots acquired, followed or were outrun by an extraordinary vehicle. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... The scramble still has evidential value, but not in the way sometimes claimed. It confirms that local officials made a detailed enough report for the air base to check it. It does not confirm that military observers saw a saucer. The difference between responding to a report and independently verifying it is one of the most important distinctions in the whole Vaucluse episode. Pronounce Châteauneuf du Pape (CORRECTLY) Channel: Julien Miquel Open on YouTube Why a balloon is the leading explanation A scientific balloon was already being discussed at Orange on the day of the Fontaine de Vaucluse sighting. Contemporary reporting said that pilots knew of a balloon seen earlier travelling towards the south east. A later local historical account connects the broader run of southern French observations with a notice from a physics institute in Milan seeking the recovery of a stratospheric balloon that had crossed into France. The balloon reportedly measured about 28 metres across, weighed around 110 kilograms and carried scientific equipment. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 14 octobre, Fontaine de Vaucluse, Vaucluse... Such an object could account for several otherwise striking features: Slow or apparently stationary movement: a high altitude balloon can seem motionless when viewed from a distance, especially when its movement is mostly towards or away from the observer. Changing shape: a large balloon, its suspended payload and rigging may present different profiles as they rotate or are seen through haze. Silence: a drifting balloon would produce no aircraft engine noise audible from the ground. Brightness and colour changes: sunlight reflected from a pale envelope or metallic equipment can change sharply with orientation, cloud cover and viewing instruments. Disappearance into cloud: a balloon entering haze or a cloud layer can appear to vanish suddenly without making any extraordinary manoeuvre. The balloon hypothesis is not proven by a surviving recovery record tying a particular serial numbered instrument directly to Fontaine de Vaucluse. The historical evidence available online does not include a precise trajectory calculation matching the balloon’s location to the village at the reported time. It is therefore more accurate to call it a strong explanation than a conclusively documented identification. [Avignon Cité Millénaire] avignoncitemillenaire.com Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire Avignon Cité Millénaire Anecdotes 7 Soucoupes volantes Avignon Cité Millénaire Even so, the hypothesis fits the basic observation better than the later “faster than jets” narrative. The object was initially described as lingering silently in daylight for a prolonged period, behaviour compatible with a balloon but difficult to reconcile with the claim that it was demonstrating extreme speed. The spectacular acceleration appears chiefly in the crowd’s interpretation of the jets’ arrival, while the pilots themselves denied seeing an unknown"},{"title":"Case Files","url":"/case-files-3c1b0a/","description":"Gendarmerie and GEIPAN files show how flares, aircraft lights and lanterns produced dramatic reports without requiring exotic craft.","breadcrumb":"Why Vaucluse's Strangest Sightings Still Fascinate","level":2,"text":"Case Files How Vaucluse UFO Reports Were Tested Case Files Case Files How Vaucluse UFO Reports Were Tested Gendarmerie and GEIPAN files show how flares, aircraft lights and lanterns produced dramatic reports without requiring exotic craft. Gendarmerie and GEIPAN files show how flares, aircraft lights and lanterns produced dramatic reports without requiring exotic craft. dataset evidence The 1977 79 sighting flap Gendarmerie enquiries and recovered flares What GEIPAN classifications really mean vaucluse case files Introduction Vaucluse ’s most useful UFO files are not necessarily its most mysterious ones. Several show, in unusually concrete detail, how dramatic night time reports were reduced to ordinary causes: fireworks and illuminating flares, aircraft landing lights, formation flying military jets and, in later decades, sky lanterns. These explanations did not depend on dismissing witnesses. They emerged by comparing testimony with times, directions, weather, local activity and independent enquiries. The lesson is important for the department’s wider UFO history. A witness may accurately report a bright orange object, silent movement or several lights holding formation while still misidentifying what produced them. The strongest Vaucluse investigations therefore distinguish between the sincerity of an observation and the reliability of conclusions about distance, size or machinery. Official classifications also matter: an unexplained file is not automatically evidence of an extraordinary craft, while an explained case may remain valuable because it shows exactly how the error arose. [Gendarmerie National] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés dans le ciel? Gendinfo, Gendarmerie nationale... The 1977–79 flap produced evidence as well as stories The late 1970s sighting wave created Vaucluse’s richest surviving mixture of newspaper coverage, witness statements and gendarmerie paperwork. Journalist Jean Leclaire, who ran a regular column on unusual reports between 1976 and 1980, later said that he recorded about 30 observations during 1977 alone. Reports came from people in varied professions and described fireballs, flying lights and apparently structured objects. Yet Leclaire also emphasised that many incidents acquired conventional explanations once somebody checked local aviation or other activity. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. One recurring mystery involved lights around the Dentelles de Montmirail. According to Leclaire’s retrospective account, residents had watched luminous objects appear repeatedly in the same part of the sky over several weeks. Repetition could easily have made the reports seem stronger: the phenomenon was not a fleeting one off, and different people were apparently noticing it. The explanation, however, was the powerful forward facing lights of Fouga Magister jet trainers operating at night. Viewed almost head on, aircraft can appear stationary or move very slowly because much of their motion is towards the observer rather than across the field of view. When they turn, dim their lights or pass behind terrain, they may seem to vanish abruptly. [Le Dauphiné Libéré] ledauphine.com Open source on ledauphine.com. This example matters because it exposes a common weakness in night sky testimony. Without visible wings, a horizon or a known distance, a landing light is not experienced as “an aircraft with a bright lamp”. It is experienced simply as an intense light. The observer may then infer a large nearby object when the true source is a smaller aircraft several miles away. Silence does not settle the question either: wind, terrain, engine orientation and distance can make a jet difficult to hear even while its lights remain conspicuous. The flap also generated cases that remained poorly resolved. A 1977 gendarmerie file from the Malaucène area records a woman and her daughter seeing an orange red rectangular form descend towards a hill and apparently disappear behind it. Gendarmes revisited the viewpoint, checked the supposed direction of travel, questioned nearby residents and patrolled the presumed area of disappearance. They found no landing site, damage or physical trace, while press requests produced no additional witness able to clarify the event. The file illustrates competent local checking, but not an identified cause: the absence of corroboration left the report evidentially weak rather than positively extraordinary. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. That distinction is central to reading the period. Some 1977–79 stories were explained; some were inadequately documented; others retained an unexplained residue. They should not be combined into one undifferentiated “wave”. A cluster may reveal increased public attention and repeated misidentifications as readily as it reveals a single unknown aerial source. The New Year lights were traced to flares and fireworks The clearest Vaucluse case from the 1977 wave occurred on 31 December around Beaumettes, Goult, Bonnieux and Caumont sur Durance. Several people reported an orange luminous phenomenon after 7pm. The timing, multiple observers and geographical spread could have made the incident sound like a substantial aerial event. The decisive evidence came afterwards, when a local person who had heard neighbours discussing the reports went to the gendarmerie. He stated that he had launched a New Year firework display between about 7pm and 7.30pm, including two illuminating flares and four Chinese rockets. GEIPAN’s published summary identifies the reported lights as the display. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBEAUMETTES (84) 31.12.1977 GEIPAN... This was stronger than merely saying that the lights “looked like fireworks”. Investigators had a matching source, place, date and time, supplied by the person responsible. The explanation also accounted for the colour and behaviour. Illuminating flares can remain bright much longer than ordinary bursting fireworks, descend slowly under a small parachute and appear nearly motionless when viewed from a distance. Different observers may see the same flare from different angles and place it over different landmarks, producing apparently conflicting trajectories. The case nevertheless contains a useful complication. GEIPAN notes a separate report from roughly 5pm in which a motorist briefly saw an apparently stationary object before losing sight of it. That earlier observation was too sparse and lacked supporting witnesses. It therefore could not simply be folded into the later, well explained fireworks episode. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBEAUMETTES (84) 31.12.1977 GEIPAN... This is good investigative discipline. An identified event at 7.15pm does not automatically explain every light reported in the same district that evening. Equally, the existence of one unanalysable report does not overturn the direct evidence explaining the main cluster. Historical UFO accounts often become more mysterious when separate observations are merged; the Beaumettes file becomes clearer when its components are kept apart. GEIPAN : Tout savoir sur les OVNIS et Phénomènes Aérospatiaux (PAN) Channel: Science & Vie Open on YouTube Aircraft formations repeatedly looked like structured objects A later Vaucluse case shows how a group of ordinary aircraft can create the impression of one organised or unusually shaped object. On 5 July 2002, witnesses in Vaucluse and neighbouring Bouches du Rhône reported six or eight white lights moving together. Some described a line, others a circle or two triangles. Observations came from locations including Cucuron, Villelaure and La Motte d’Aigues, and durations varied from several minutes to much longer estimates. The one consistent feature was that the lights moved as a coordinated group. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN(DPT) VAUCLUSE (84) 05.07.2002 GEIPAN... GEIPAN later re examined the file after comparable formation sightings in 2008 were identified as Patrouille de France flights. It concluded that the 2002 lights were also the French Air and Space Force display team. That conclusion is especially revealing because witnesses did not agree on the formation’s geometry. A rigid airborne formation can appear triangular, circular or linear depending on the observer’s position, the aircrafts’ relative depth and which lights are most visible. Perspective turns a three dimensional arrangement into a changing two dimensional pattern. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN(DPT) VAUCLUSE (84) 05.07.2002 GEIPAN... The same mechanism appeared again in July 2021, when several people in Vaucluse reported six lights moving in formation. Information supplied to GEIPAN linked them to a night flight by the Patrouille de France. The recurrence is more informative than either report alone: two episodes separated by nearly two decades produced similar descriptions because the same kind of aviation"},{"title":"When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky","url":"/when-strange-lights-crossed-the-vendee/","description":"Vendée has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single spectacular, well-documented mystery.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky Vendée has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single spectacular, well documented mystery. Vendée has a genuine UFO history, but not one dominated by a single spectacular, well documented mystery. Its public record is better understood as a mixture of striking mass sightings, ordinary objects seen under confusing conditions, incomplete witness reports and a smaller body of older claims preserved mainly by local enthusiasts. vendee Introduction The department’s most memorable episode came on 5 November 1990, when residents saw an apparently enormous, silent formation of lights crossing the night sky. The event initially looked compelling because it produced numerous reports and detailed descriptions. It was later matched closely to the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. More recent Vendée cases have likewise been attributed to the International Space Station, Venus, lanterns and a SpaceX launch, while several others remain unclassified only because the available information is too poor for a reliable conclusion. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Or, la majorité des témoignages de la rentrée atmosphérique du 05 novembre 1990 relatent l'observation d'un engin sombre, muni de multipl... The night Vendée saw a vast silent craft At about 7pm on 5 November 1990, lights crossed France from the Atlantic side towards the north east. Witness statements collected by gendarmes included descriptions of clusters of white or coloured lights, triangular or trapezoidal outlines, apparent low altitude and complete silence. Some observers believed they could make out the body of a vast structured object behind the lights. The phenomenon was reported across much of Western Europe, but Vendée’s position near its approach from the Bay of Biscay made the display especially prominent locally. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The reports are valuable because they show how sincere witnesses can agree on the broad features of an event while misjudging its distance, size and physical structure. What appeared to be one low flying machine was actually a long train of glowing debris tens of kilometres above the ground. Because the fragments moved together across a clear sky, the eye could connect separate lights into the outline of a single dark vehicle. The lack of audible engine noise then reinforced the impression that something technologically unusual was passing overhead. [Geipan] geipan.fr Or, la majorité des témoignages de la rentrée atmosphérique du 05 novembre 1990 relatent l'observation d'un engin sombre, muni de multipl... The Centre national d’études spatiales, or CNES, initially checked possibilities including meteors, balloons and returning spacecraft. Information supplied through NASA identified the re entering object as the platform associated with Gorizont 21. GEIPAN, the CNES unit responsible for investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena, now classifies the event as an unambiguous category A case: the atmospheric re entry of the third stage of a Soviet Proton rocket. Contemporary reporting noted that its time and trajectory matched the observed passage over France. [geipan.fr] geipan.fr Or, la majorité des témoignages de la rentrée atmosphérique du 05 novembre 1990 relatent l'observation d'un engin sombre, muni de multipl... This explanation is considerably stronger than a loose resemblance. A later CNES reconstruction placed the debris at roughly 90 to 50 kilometres altitude, slowing from about seven to two kilometres per second. It also reproduced the west south west to east north east course described by witnesses. More than a thousand reports from across France provided a geographical sequence that a local aircraft, hoax or isolated astronomical object could not explain. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Yet some witnesses rejected the conclusion because it conflicted with what they felt they had seen at close range. That reaction is important. A strong emotional impression does not make a witness dishonest, but neither does confidence guarantee an accurate estimate of altitude or dimensions. The 1990 event remains Vendée’s clearest demonstration that a highly impressive, multi witness “craft” can result from a real but misperceived aerospace event. [Geipan] geipan.fr Or, la majorité des témoignages de la rentrée atmosphérique du 05 novembre 1990 relatent l'observation d'un engin sombre, muni de multipl... What the official Vendée files actually contain GEIPAN’s records do not support the idea that Vendée is an exceptional national hotspot of unexplained activity. Instead, the published cases reveal the same recurring identification problems found elsewhere in France: bright planets, satellites, festive lanterns, rockets and reports that arrive without enough positional or timing information to test properly. A statistical study of French reports found that reporting patterns are influenced by factors such as population, humidity and proximity to certain types of infrastructure; it did not identify Vendée among the country’s strongest unexplained geographical concentrations. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Several Vendée entries are straightforward identifications: Department wide report, February 1976: classified A and attributed to the Moon. Île d’Yeu, 17 March 2009: identified as the International Space Station and the space shuttle. Montaigu, 22 May 2009: identified as an International Space Station pass. Treize Septiers, 3 July 2016: judged probably to involve sky lanterns, supported by analysis of photographs and video. Foussais Payré, 2 December 2024: classified A as an observation of Venus. Vendée report of 4 May 2021: linked to the exhaust cloud effects of a Falcon 9 launch rather than an unknown vehicle. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. These cases matter because they cover several common sources of apparent UFO behaviour. The International Space Station can resemble a bright, steadily moving light with no audible engine. Venus may appear unusually large or mobile when seen through thin cloud or without fixed visual reference points. Lanterns drift silently in groups and change formation with the wind. Rocket exhaust released at high altitude can form a glowing cloud that expands, changes shape and seems unlike any familiar aircraft. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête GEIPANCompte rendu d'enquête Other Vendée files have category C status, meaning that they are not securely explained but are also not sufficiently documented to qualify as robust mysteries. Published examples include reports from Pouzauges in 1977, La Roche sur Yon in July and September 2009, Saint Jean de Monts in August 2010 and Nieul sur l’Autise in February 2011. In each case, the central problem is a lack of reliable information rather than evidence of extraordinary performance. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casListe des nouveaux cas; SAINT JEAN DE MONTS (85) 05.08.2010, 05/08/2010, C · Vendée, Manque d'informations fiables... That distinction is essential. A category C case is not equivalent to a category D case. It generally means that investigators cannot reconstruct the sightline, duration, direction, weather, astronomical conditions or possible flight path with enough confidence to select an explanation. Calling such a file “unexplained” without mentioning its evidential weakness gives it more weight than the official assessment supports. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d baisse cas d 5:33 Military pilot close encounter with UFO: 'This is going to sound weird' Channel: NewsNation · Views: 1.7M · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Local archives, enthusiasts and older claims Vendée’s broader UFO folklore extends beyond the searchable GEIPAN catalogue. Local researcher Jérôme Choloux was reported to have assembled about fifty regional accounts dating back to 1937. Such collections can preserve newspaper cuttings and personal testimony that might otherwise disappear, especially for incidents recorded before France established a formal national investigation service. [larochesuryon.maville.com] larochesuryon.maville.com Open source on maville.com. Their historical value should not be confused with evidential strength. Older cases frequently lack original statements, precise dates, photographs, weather records or independent witnesses. Some survive only through retrospective summaries. A report that appears vivid in a later compilation may therefore be impossible to test against aircraft movements, astronomical positions or contemporary documents. The reported 1975 sighting near Fontenay le Comte illustrates the problem. A secondary UFO database describes several people at a meal and a red luminous ball descending"},{"title":"1990 Sighting","url":"/1990-sighting/","description":"Vendee's most dramatic mass sighting became a landmark example of how real space debris can resemble a vast silent craft.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky","level":2,"text":"1990 Sighting Did Vendee See a Giant Craft in 1990? Vendee UFO Files 1990 Sighting 1990 Sighting Did Vendee See a Giant Craft in 1990? Vendee's most dramatic mass sighting became a landmark example of how real space debris can resemble a vast silent craft. Vendee's most dramatic mass sighting became a landmark example of how real space debris can resemble a vast silent craft. event time window What witnesses reported across Vendee How investigators identified the rocket stage Why the craft impression remained persuasive vendee 1990 sighting Introduction On the evening of 5 November 1990, people across Vendée watched a broad formation of lights move silently through the dark sky. Some described a triangle, delta, diamond or platform; others thought they saw a huge solid body carrying coloured lamps, searchlights and glowing exhaust trails. The scale of the reports made the episode one of the most memorable supposed UFO events in the department’s history. The evidence now strongly indicates that Vendée did not witness a giant craft. The lights formed part of an exceptional atmospheric re entry: the third stage of a Soviet Proton rocket breaking apart high above France. Its timing, direction, orbital identification and reconstructed path all match the main body of reports. What makes the case important is therefore not an enduring mystery, but the unusually clear demonstration that real space debris can produce a convincing impression of a vast, low flying and silent machine. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Incursions extraterrestres Juste Ciel 5 Channel: CNES Open on YouTube What witnesses reported across Vendée The display appeared shortly after 7pm, when many people were still outdoors, travelling home or looking across open rural horizons. GEIPAN’s national archive lists Vendée reports from places including Barbâtre, Saint Gervais, La Barre de Monts and La Chapelle Achard. These locations spread the event from the coastal and marsh areas of north west Vendée into the department’s interior, fitting a phenomenon seen across a wide section of sky rather than a local object hovering over one commune. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Statements preserved in the gendarmerie files show both a strong common core and striking variation. Witnesses repeatedly mentioned: numerous white, orange, red or blue lights moving together; a general south west to north east passage; glowing trails or luminous “powder” behind the formation; an apparent duration of several tens of seconds; an impression of low altitude and great size; little or no audible noise. One farmer reported at about 6.58pm that an “immense delta” with rounded wings crossed from south west to north east. Points of light appeared to define its outline, while two broad luminous trails marked its passage. He thought it was flying slowly at low altitude, although he observed it for only 15 to 20 seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Another witness described many white lights arranged symmetrically around what seemed to be a bulky trapezoidal object. A powerful white beam appeared to shine backwards, and a pale trail remained visible after the lights had disappeared. The witness estimated the object at only 30 to 50 metres above the ground and roughly the volume of a house, but also emphasised that it was the complete silence that made the sight especially disturbing. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Other statements in the same collection produced still larger estimates. One observer imagined an object between 100 and 300 metres across; a father and son compared a rectangular or diamond shaped platform to a rugby pitch. Their descriptions differed over the exact outline, colours and apparent movement, yet converged on the impression of an enormous illuminated structure passing without engine noise. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. These accounts should not be dismissed as fabricated simply because their size and altitude estimates were wrong. They were recorded promptly, often by gendarmes, and many witnesses clearly struggled to describe something genuinely unfamiliar. Their testimony is strong evidence that an extraordinary luminous display occurred. It is much weaker evidence for the witnesses’ interpretation of that display as a single solid vehicle. How investigators identified the rocket stage The event was never merely assigned a convenient explanation after the fact. French investigators established a specific orbiting object whose re entry matched the night, time and route of the sightings. Within an hour, CNES security services were receiving calls from gendarmerie brigades reporting a luminous phenomenon crossing a large part of France. SEPRA, the CNES service then responsible for unusual aerospace reports and atmospheric re entries, checked with civilian and military aviation authorities, meteorological services and NASA. On 8 November, NASA supplied the identification of object 20925, also catalogued as 1990 094C and associated with the Gorizont 21 mission. CNES orbital specialists then confirmed that its path crossed France from the Bay of Biscay towards Alsace. The identification was released to press agencies on 9 November, and contemporary national reporting described the object as fragments of a Soviet rocket disintegrating during re entry. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... The object was the third stage of a Proton launcher used for a Soviet communications satellite mission two days earlier. A knowledgeable observer had already advised SEPRA on 6 November that re entry object 1990 094C was the likely cause. The calculated course placed it at about 103 kilometres above the Bay of Biscay and around 83 kilometres high as it left eastern France. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... GEIPAN’s later reconstruction describes the visible phenomenon as a cloud of fragments descending through roughly 100 to 50 kilometres altitude. Its speed fell from about seven to two kilometres per second as atmospheric drag broke up and heated the stage. Different pieces glowed with different intensities and produced separate trails, while the complete train stretched over tens of kilometres. From the ground, this created not a compact point like an ordinary meteor, but a slow looking procession of lights occupying a large angular area of sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... Several features reinforce the identification: Timing. The principal reports cluster at about 7pm, matching the calculated re entry window. Direction. Vendée witnesses generally saw the phenomenon moving from the south west or west towards the north east or east, consistent with a track entering France from the Bay of Biscay. Geographical sequence. Reports continued across France and beyond Vendée, which is difficult to reconcile with a local aircraft or craft flying at the low altitudes claimed by witnesses. Appearance. Multiple glowing fragments, long trails and changes in brightness are expected when a large rocket stage breaks into pieces during atmospheric entry. Silence. At tens of kilometres altitude, no nearby engine noise would accompany the lights. Any delayed sonic effects would not necessarily be connected by witnesses with the already distant display. GEIPAN classifies the episode as category A, meaning an observation explained without ambiguity. Its current case page identifies the phenomenon specifically as the atmospheric re entry of a Proton third stage, rather than leaving it as a probable or unresolved interpretation. [Geipan] geipan.fr RENTREE ATMOSPHERIQUE 5 NOVEMBRE 1990 GEIPAN... A UFO Night in France : November 5, 1990… They Watched the Skies and Never Forgot Channel: Chacha Paranormal Open on YouTube Why the formation looked like a solid craft The hardest part of the case is not explaining the luminous fragments. It is explaining why so many people believed that those fragments were fixed to a dark, structured machine. At night, a light in an otherwise empty sky provides almost no dependable distance information. Unless observers recognise the object and already know its approximate size, they cannot calculate how far away it is from vision alone. A brilliant fragment 80 kilometres away can therefore be interpreted as a smaller lamp a few hundred metres away. Once the assumed distance becomes too small, the apparent dimensions of the entire formation become enormous: house sized, pitch sized or even larger. GEIPAN notes that witnesses to bright fireballs commonly place them below 1,000 metres even when they are actually tens of kilometres high. [Geipan] geipan.fr Nouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... A second effect concerns shape. Human vision naturally groups lights that move in the same direction and at the same"},{"title":"Official Cases","url":"/official-cases-6f19cf/","description":"The official record is dominated by planets, satellites, lanterns, rockets and reports too incomplete for firm conclusions.","breadcrumb":"When Strange Lights Crossed the Vendee Sky","level":2,"text":"Official Cases What Do Vendee's Official UFO Files Show? Vendee UFO Files Official Cases Official Cases What Do Vendee's Official UFO Files Show? The official record is dominated by planets, satellites, lanterns, rockets and reports too incomplete for firm conclusions. The official record is dominated by planets, satellites, lanterns, rockets and reports too incomplete for firm conclusions. dataset evidence The cases identified with confidence Why some reports remain category C What the records reveal about local patterns vendee official cases Introduction Vendée’s official UFO record is less mysterious than its reputation might suggest. In the GEIPAN files published by France’s national space agency, most sufficiently documented reports are attributed to familiar objects or events: the Moon and bright planets, satellites and spacecraft, aircraft, sky lanterns, meteors, rocket re entries and even effects created by public displays. A sizeable second group remains in category C, not because investigators found strong evidence of an extraordinary craft, but because the reports arrived too late or lacked accurate times, directions, photographs or independent witnesses. A November 2025 export of GEIPAN’s public database contains 46 entries assigned to Vendée. Thirteen are classified A, meaning confidently explained; 15 are B, meaning the proposed explanation is highly probable; 17 are C, meaning the evidence is insufficient for analysis; and only one is listed as D, unexplained despite usable information. The central finding is therefore not a hidden concentration of exceptional incidents, but a practical lesson in how identification depends on evidence quality. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr phénomène étrange non identifié. Le 15...Read more... Le GEIPAN enquête l'étrange Vincent Costes (directeur du GEIPAN) Channel: InfiniSciences Open on YouTube The cases identified with confidence GEIPAN, the official French body that collects and investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, separates a witness’s experience from the eventual identification. A sighting may appear startling and still receive an A classification when its timing, position and appearance match a known source without significant ambiguity. Category B is used where the fit is strong but some uncertainty remains. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr PAN de catégorie D: observation inexpliquée malgré les éléments en possession du GEIPAN.... Le GEIPAN, de part son expérience, évalue... Vendée’s explained files repeatedly fall into a small number of recognisable families. The Moon, planets and stars. Some of the oldest reports involved bright astronomical bodies seen through haze, cloud or an unfamiliar gap in the landscape. A February 1976 case, formerly associated with Luçon and once treated as unexplained, was later reclassified as an A case involving the Moon. GEIPAN judged its strangeness to be low when reviewed under modern criteria. A March 1993 report from Saint Gilles Croix de Vie, describing an imposing luminous object in a cloudy sky, was also identified as the Moon seen through thin cloud. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The most recent clear example came from Foussais Payré on 2 December 2024. Two witnesses recorded a bright object that appeared bluish, oval and capable of erratic movement. GEIPAN’s analysis found that the underlying source was Venus. The apparent motion came from a hand held smartphone, while night mode and digital zoom altered the planet’s colour and shape. A supposed high speed departure was contradicted by the footage, which showed the camera moving across fixed foreground features. The case was classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This case matters because it shows that photographs do not automatically settle a sighting. Images can strengthen an investigation, but only when investigators account for exposure settings, stabilisation, zoom, camera movement and optical artefacts. A bright point with no surrounding reference can also appear to move through the autokinetic effect, a known illusion in which small eye movements make a stationary light seem mobile against a dark sky. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Satellites and spacecraft. Vendée’s clear coastal skies and wide horizons make artificial satellites conspicuous, particularly shortly after sunset or before dawn, when an observer is in darkness but an orbiting object remains sunlit. On 17 March 2009, a report from Île d’Yeu was identified as the combined passage of the International Space Station and the space shuttle. Two months later, a luminous object moving west to east over Montaigu, apparently making a brief zigzag before disappearing, was classified A as an ISS passage. The apparent deviation did not outweigh the match in time and trajectory. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A Challans report from 8 August 2012 illustrates why similar events sometimes receive a B rather than an A. The witness saw two bright objects apparently travelling in parallel at high altitude. Satellite records showed several relevant passages, including an exceptionally bright ISS accompanied by docked cargo vehicles. GEIPAN considered satellites the probable explanation but could not determine exactly which objects produced every part of the observation, so the case remained B. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Sky lanterns. Orange or flame coloured lights moving silently in groups occur frequently in the later Vendée files. Reports from Montaigu and Barbâtre in May 2008, La Rabatelière on New Year’s Day 2014, Saint Georges de Pointindoux in August 2014, Bessay in August 2015 and Talmont Saint Hilaire during the 2015–16 New Year celebrations were all classified as definite or probable lantern sightings in the published dataset. Their common features include warm colour, slow drift, lack of sound, changing formations and gradual extinction. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr phénomène étrange non identifié. Le 15...Read more... The Saint Georges de Pointindoux case is particularly instructive. Witnesses reported about a dozen red orange points forming a geometric arrangement before disappearing. Rather than treating the formation itself as evidence of a structured object, GEIPAN compared the lights’ colour, movement and fading with wind borne lanterns and assigned a B classification. Separate, less adequately documented observations associated with the episode were left in category C. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Aircraft, meteors and other human activity. A motorist travelling from La Ferrière towards La Roche sur Yon on 13 January 2017 reported successive intense lights. Investigation linked them to aircraft and classified the case A. A filmed object near Beaulieu sous la Roche in July 2017, trailing orange light at dusk, was likewise identified as an aircraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. A rapid flash followed by a blue “ball” seen between Sainte Gemme la Plaine and Moreilles on 26 August 2021 was assessed as a probable meteor, or bolide. Its extremely short duration was central to that conclusion. In Les Epesses in October 2019, a filmed circular form was traced not to an object in flight but to a smoke ring created by a pyrotechnic performance. These cases demonstrate the breadth of sources entering the official system: GEIPAN investigates what a witness could not identify, not merely reports that resemble a conventional flying craft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Atmospheric re entry also appears in the departmental record. An April 2003 observation at Angles was judged probably to have involved re entering space debris. Such events can seem unusually large or slow because multiple glowing fragments remain visible together, inviting witnesses to perceive one structured vehicle rather than a dispersed stream of material. CNES identifies rocket stages, spacecraft debris and satellites among the recurring sources of apparently extraordinary aerial displays. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr phénomène étrange non identifié. Le 15...Read more... Why some reports remain category C Category C is often misunderstood as a lesser version of “unexplained”. GEIPAN defines it differently: these are reports that cannot be analysed because reliable information is missing. They are not equivalent to category D cases, in which investigators possess enough evidence to test ordinary explanations but still cannot identify the phenomenon. GEIPAN notes that category C sits outside the normal progression from explained to unexplained because no secure conclusion can be reached at all. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr PAN de catégorie D: observation inexpliquée malgré les éléments en possession du GEIPAN.... Le GEIPAN, de part son expérience, évalue... Vendée’s files contain several clear examples of how a potentially interesting story becomes untestable. At"},{"title":"From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files","url":"/vienne/","description":"Vienne’s UFO history is not dominated by one nationally famous incident.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files Vienne’s UFO history is not dominated by one nationally famous incident. Vienne’s UFO history is not dominated by one nationally famous incident. vienne Introduction The department does contain cases that remain uncertain, notably a 2009 observation at Chasseneuil du Poitou. Yet uncertainty in the GEIPAN archive usually means that information is insufficient, not that investigators have established the presence of an extraordinary craft. Vienne’s real significance lies in showing how UFO stories change when they move from newspaper narrative to documented investigation. The 1954 wave reached rural Vienne France experienced an exceptional concentration of “flying saucer” reports during September and October 1954. Newspapers published accounts of discs, luminous objects and alleged occupants almost daily, and reports frequently borrowed imagery already circulating in the national press. Vienne contributed several stories to this wider wave, most notably at Béruges and Lavoux. [wikipedia.org] Wikipedia Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 At Béruges, west of Poitiers, farmer Édouard Thébault reportedly saw a circular luminous object on or close to the ground on 7 October 1954. Later retellings describe an object roughly three metres across, with a beam of light sweeping the surrounding landscape. The surviving case history is largely built from newspaper reports and later UFO catalogues rather than a complete contemporary investigation file. That makes the episode memorable but difficult to assess: there is no securely documented physical trace, photograph, radar record or independently preserved technical evidence. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org the 1954 French flap, October 7, Béruges, Vienne Patrick GrossThe author indicates that on October 7, 1954, in Béruges close to... Published: October 7, 1954 The Lavoux story, dated 9 October, is stranger. A farmer cycling in the evening allegedly encountered a figure dressed in something resembling a diving suit, which directed two beams of light towards him. Some versions add temporary paralysis or an inability to move. The account became part of later “close encounter” catalogues, but those catalogues do not agree on its interpretation; at least one later compilation marked the case as explained without providing enough surviving evidence to make that conclusion especially persuasive. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: France 1954, 9 octobre 1954, Lavoux, Vienne.... (sceau archives ovni.org). Scan. CAS N° 221. Cat J VALLEE. Enquêt... These cases matter historically because they illustrate the weaknesses of the 1954 material. They appeared during a national media wave in which witnesses, journalists and later investigators were already familiar with recurring motifs: landed discs, helmeted beings, light beams and paralysis. That does not prove fabrication, but it raises the possibility of expectation, memory reshaping and newspaper embellishment. Without original statements taken promptly under controlled conditions, the most dramatic details cannot be separated confidently from later retelling. There is also a geographical trap for researchers: the celebrated 30 September 1954 encounter at Marcilly sur Vienne did not occur in the department of Vienne. Marcilly sur Vienne is in neighbouring Indre et Loire. It should therefore be treated as a regional comparison, not counted as a Vienne case. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 Vague d'observations de l'automne 1954 The Forêt de Moulière reports of 1976 The department’s clearest local “flap” developed around the Forêt de Moulière, north east of Poitiers, during the summer and early autumn of 1976. The wooded landscape gave the reports a memorable setting, but the surviving evidence comes mainly through articles in La Nouvelle République and reproductions preserved by specialist UFO archives. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976L'article ci dessous est paru dans le quotidien La Nouvelle République, Franc... One report concerned an object allegedly seen near the forest by a witness named Mazelier. Another witness, Jean Marie Préchart, an employee of the French statistics agency, came forward to say that he had observed something similar on the morning of 8 September. A further account, attributed to a witness identified as Al Hashimi, concerned an observation dating back to about 19 June but disclosed only after the later stories had appeared. [pourquoipaspoitiers.over blog.fr] pourquoipaspoitiers.over blog.fr article 26829584 article 26829584 The sequence is important because it demonstrates how a local cluster can grow. An initial article encourages people to reinterpret earlier experiences, witnesses compare descriptions, and the press then presents the resulting reports as a developing wave. This can uncover genuinely independent observations, but it can also introduce selection effects: ordinary lights are more likely to be reported once a place has acquired a UFO reputation. The available archive does not establish that the Moulière witnesses all saw the same phenomenon. Their reports were separated by time, and no shared photograph, radar detection or recoverable object links them. The forest and its approaches also offer difficult viewing conditions, including broken horizons, moving vehicles, aircraft seen through gaps in trees and uncertain estimates of distance. The cases are therefore valuable as local social and press history, but weak as evidence for one persistent or technologically unusual object. A GEIPAN entry from Magné on 10 September 1976 was eventually placed in category B, meaning that investigators considered an ordinary identification probable, although the published search listing does not specify the proposed phenomenon. That official classification further weakens the idea that every Vienne report from the period formed part of one unexplained event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Témoignage René OVNI 1979 Channel: Olivier Cellier Open on YouTube What official investigations found France’s official UFO system began in 1977 and is now run by GEIPAN, the Group for Studies and Information on Unidentified Aerospace Phenomena. It receives reports directly and through bodies such as the gendarmerie and civil aviation authorities, checks astronomical, meteorological and aerospace data, and publishes case files where privacy rules permit. Gendarmes may take formal witness statements and forward them for specialist examination. [gouv.fr] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les etrangetes dans le ciel GEIPAN’s classifications are often misunderstood. Category A denotes an identified phenomenon; B indicates a probable explanation; C means that reliable information is inadequate; and D is reserved for cases retaining significant strangeness after investigation, with the modern system distinguishing D1 and D2. Classification depends on both the residual strangeness and the “consistency” of the evidence. A category C case is therefore not a stronger mystery than an explained case—it is frequently just a case that cannot be tested properly. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Several Vienne files demonstrate the value of this process: Saint Martin l’Ars, 17 March 2009: a luminous observation was identified as the International Space Station and the Space Shuttle, producing a category A conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Poitiers, 5 and 6 September 2009: reports were classified B and attributed probably to Thai lanterns. Similar lantern explanations also appear in the Nieuil l’Espoir and Jaunay Clan records. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Civray, 19 August 2010: GEIPAN concluded that several astronomical and space related misidentifications had been combined in the witness’s interpretation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Vouzailles, 14 January 2014: the phenomenon was classified B as a probable fireball or bright meteor. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Berthegon, 13 February 2019: the report was identified as an aircraft and classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Antran, 29 August 2024: a later official reassessment classified the light as a laser. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. This range is more representative of Vienne’s documented UFO history than its most dramatic legends. Lights that seem stationary may be astronomical objects; apparent formations may be"},{"title":"Mouliere Flap","url":"/mouliere-flap/","description":"The Mouliere reports show how separate sightings, difficult terrain and newspaper attention can combine into a local UFO wave.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files","level":2,"text":"Mouliere Flap Did Mouliere Really Have a UFO Wave? Vienne Mouliere Flap Mouliere Flap Did Mouliere Really Have a UFO Wave? The Mouliere reports show how separate sightings, difficult terrain and newspaper attention can combine into a local UFO wave. The Mouliere reports show how separate sightings, difficult terrain and newspaper attention can combine into a local UFO wave. event time window The sightings reported around the forest How press coverage expanded the cluster Why the reports do not prove one object vienne mouliere flap Introduction The Forêt de Moulière reports of 1976 are often described as Vienne ’s clearest local UFO “flap”: a short period in which several people reported unusual objects around the same wooded district, while newspaper coverage encouraged further witnesses to come forward. Yet the surviving record does not establish repeated appearances by one extraordinary craft. It preserves one dramatic two night account, a set of photographs whose evidential history is unclear, and several later reports that differed in date, setting and appearance. Reconsidered today, Moulière is most valuable as a case study in how a local wave forms. Difficult viewing conditions, uncertain estimates of distance, delayed testimony and press driven comparison turned separate observations into a single story. Some incidents may have involved genuinely puzzling lights, but the available evidence is too fragmented to reconstruct them securely, let alone prove that they shared one cause. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... The sightings reported around the forest The central witness was Jean François Mazelier, a 20 year old medical student living at Jaunay Clan. According to an article published by La Nouvelle République on 9 September 1976, he reported two observations at Le Peu, near Saint Georges lès Baillargeaux, on a road crossing or skirting the Moulière area. The forest itself is a large wooded massif north east of Poitiers, spread across several communes and broken by roads, clearings and neighbouring heathland. This landscape matters because trees can obstruct the horizon, remove familiar distance cues and make a remote light appear to move between nearby trunks. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... Mazelier dated his first experience to 31 July at about 11.30 pm. While driving from Jaunay towards Chauvigny, he said that his car engine stopped and that its headlights and radio also failed. He then saw what he described as a huge cigar shaped object, perhaps 100 metres long and 30 metres high. It reportedly changed colour from red to yellow, bright white and green before disappearing after roughly four minutes. He added that the weather was stormy and that a high voltage power line ran close to the location. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... This is the most striking report in the cluster because it combines an aerial sighting with an alleged effect on a vehicle. However, almost every technically important detail remains unverified. The surviving article does not identify the car, describe any repair or inspection, establish whether the engine and electrical system failed simultaneously, or record whether the vehicle restarted immediately. There is no known contemporary test of the nearby power line, no independently documented electromagnetic reading and no second witness mentioned in the account. The incident is therefore dramatic in narrative terms but weak in recoverable technical evidence. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... Mazelier said that he returned to the same place on 21 August, again at about 11.30 pm, and saw a similar but smaller object. This time he estimated its length at 20 to 30 metres and said that it was visible between two trees for approximately seven minutes. He reportedly took around 30 photographs, of which 22 were considered successful. One image was reproduced by the newspaper, while he said that he was retaining the others for examination by scientific publications or competent authorities. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... The photographs sound impressive because of their number, but quantity is not the same as independent confirmation. A sequence of frames could potentially reveal movement, exposure effects or changing geometry, but only if the original negatives, camera settings, lens, exposure times, shooting position and full sequence are available. The surviving public account supplies none of those details, and no accessible technical report has been located showing that all 22 images were examined under controlled conditions. Modern GEIPAN practice likewise treats photographs as useful only when they can be tied to a documented direct observation and investigated alongside contextual information. [patrickgross.org] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... A third report appeared after the first newspaper article. Jean Marie Préchart, described as a model maker employed by the French national statistics institute and living at Montamisé, reportedly saw a similar object in the Moulière sector early on 8 September. The surviving follow up article provides almost no description of its duration, apparent direction, angular size, colour or behaviour. It is therefore impossible to determine whether “similar” reflected the witness’s original description or a comparison made after Mazelier’s story had become public. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS dans la région de Poitiers, France, 1976... The same follow up article added a still earlier sighting by a Poitiers shopkeeper, identified as Mr Al Hashimi. He said that at about 5 pm around 19 June, he had watched a yellow or golden, egg shaped object moving slowly from north to south over Poitiers. He estimated that it was perhaps a kilometre away and visible for nearly a minute. He had not reported it at the time, but came forward after reading about the other observations. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS dans la région de Poitiers, France, 1976... This account broadens the supposed wave geographically and chronologically, but it also weakens the argument for one recurring object. It occurred in daylight, over urban Poitiers rather than necessarily over the forest, and involved an egg shaped golden object rather than Mazelier’s large, colour changing night time cigar. No photograph, companion witness or measured bearing was reported. The article’s decision to add it to the regional file reflected its general strangeness, not a demonstrated physical link to the Moulière incidents. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS dans la région de Poitiers, France, 1976... How press coverage expanded the cluster The newspaper sequence shows the flap developing almost in real time. Mazelier’s July and August claims were published on 9 September under a headline asking whether UFOs were appearing in the forest and highlighting the alleged 22 photographs. Nine days later, the paper introduced further testimony with the observation that one witness often calls forth another. That wording is unusually revealing: the newspaper itself recognised that publicity was helping to create the visible cluster. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS en forêt de Moulières, France, 1976... Press attention does not mean that later witnesses were dishonest. People commonly remain silent after an ambiguous experience because they expect others to report it, fear ridicule or doubt their own interpretation. Publication can give them a reason to speak. Mr Al Hashimi explicitly said that he had initially assumed that other residents would report the object he had seen, and only approached the newspaper after the Moulière stories appeared. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org Les OVNIS vus de près: la presse, OVNIS dans la région de Poitiers, France, 1976... The difficulty is that publicity also changes the conditions under which testimony is collected. Once a highly specific story is public — a luminous cigar, a forest location, repeated appearances and numerous photographs — later witnesses may compare uncertain memories with that template. Journalists may also group observations through loose similarities while overlooking differences in shape, time, direction and setting. By the second article, “Moulière” had become the organising label for reports that may not all have concerned the forest or the same type of phenomenon. [Ufologie] ufologie.patrickgross.org"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files-9c80c7/","description":"GEIPAN records distinguish unexplained cases from weak reports and show how meteors, aircraft and spacecraft can become apparent UFOs.","breadcrumb":"From Flying Saucers to Official Case Files","level":2,"text":"Official Files What Vienne's Official UFO Files Actually Show Vienne UFOs Official Files Official Files What Vienne's Official UFO Files Actually Show GEIPAN records distinguish unexplained cases from weak reports and show how meteors, aircraft and spacecraft can become apparent UFOs. GEIPAN records distinguish unexplained cases from weak reports and show how meteors, aircraft and spacecraft can become apparent UFOs. dataset evidence How reports enter the French investigation system What the GEIPAN categories really mean Explained, uncertain and unresolved Vienne cases vienne official files Introduction Official investigation changed Vienne ’s UFO story by replacing a loose collection of unusual testimonies with a graded body of evidence. The department’s files no longer support a simple choice between “explained” and “mysterious”. They show several very different outcomes: bright planets mistaken for hovering lights, lanterns interpreted as silent formations, satellites and spacecraft seen without recognition, meteors reconstructed from camera data, and reports that remain unclassified only because the evidence is too weak. That distinction matters most in the cases labelled C. In GEIPAN’s system, a C case is not a phenomenon that resisted a complete investigation; it is one that could not be identified because reliable information was missing. Vienne’s best known example, the 2009 Chasseneuil du Poitou report, therefore occupies a much weaker evidential position than its unusual description might suggest. Across the department, the official files have generally weakened extraordinary interpretations while preserving a smaller residue of uncertainty. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... How reports enter the French investigation system France has maintained an official system for examining unusual aerial reports since 1977. The present body, GEIPAN, operates within the national space agency CNES and continues the work of its predecessors GEPAN and SEPRA. Its role is to collect testimony, analyse it, investigate where possible, archive the material and publish anonymised conclusions. It does not set out to prove alien visitation, and it deliberately uses the wider term “unidentified aerospace phenomenon” because witnesses do not always observe a clearly defined physical object. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... A modern report normally begins with a detailed technical questionnaire completed by a direct witness. Investigators seek the exact time, location, direction of view, angular movement, duration, weather, visibility and circumstances of the observation. Drawings, photographs, video and other records may be added. A case can then be assigned to a trained investigator, who may contact witnesses, visit the location or reconstruct lines of sight. GEIPAN also works with the gendarmerie, police, civil aviation, the armed forces, Météo France and specialists in fields such as astronomy, meteorology, imaging, plasma physics and psychology. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANUnderstanding a Phenomenon GEIPAN GEIPANUnderstanding a Phenomenon GEIPAN This procedure changes the value of a sighting in two ways. First, it turns an impression such as “a light moved strangely” into claims that can be checked against aircraft routes, astronomical positions, satellite passes, wind direction and other external data. Secondly, it records how much of that checking is actually possible. An intriguing account with no secure time, direction or independent witness may remain unidentified, but it does not carry the same weight as an unexplained event supported by radar, photographs or multiple independent observations. That is why the official archive looks very different from newspaper and UFO catalogue accounts. Press stories naturally foreground the most striking description. GEIPAN foregrounds whether the description can be tested. What the GEIPAN categories really mean GEIPAN classifies reports according to both their remaining strangeness and their consistency. “Strangeness” concerns how poorly the observation matches known phenomena after investigation. “Consistency” concerns the quantity, reliability and objectivity of the available information. A remarkable story can therefore receive a low evidential rating if it comes from one uncertain account, while a spectacular fireball may be classified with confidence because cameras and independent reports establish what happened. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN The four main categories are easily misunderstood: A: the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, although the evidence is not complete enough for category A. C: no identification can be made because information is missing or unreliable. D: the phenomenon remains unidentified after an investigation supported by sufficiently substantial evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr cation D: Phenomenon not identified after investigation... For readers of Vienne’s files, the crucial distinction is between C and D. Both may be casually described as “unexplained”, but they mean different things. A D classification says investigators had enough material to test the main conventional hypotheses and still failed to identify the phenomenon. A C classification says they could not perform that level of testing. The official Vienne list is notable for what it does not contain: the published cases visible in the department’s current archive are predominantly A, B and C, rather than a strong group of well supported D cases. The database includes identifications involving Venus, Jupiter, aircraft, satellites, the International Space Station and Space Shuttle, lanterns, a meteor and, most recently, projected light reflected from clouds. Its C entries are generally marked by missing reliable information rather than by verified extraordinary behaviour. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Categories are also not intended to be permanent monuments. GEIPAN states that C and D cases can be revisited when new information becomes available. Nationally, the organisation has re examined older cases using improved digital tools and stricter criteria; it reported that 50 older D files were reinvestigated and explained in 2017. This history is a warning against treating any archive label as a final scientific verdict. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Explained Vienne cases changed the pattern The most important contribution of the official files is cumulative. Any single explanation may seem unremarkable, but together they reveal the recurring sources of Vienne’s apparent UFOs. Lanterns over Poitiers Shortly after midnight on 5 September 2009, two witnesses in Poitiers watched four intense orange lights move north to south for about five minutes. The lights made no sound, appeared to rise and then disappeared towards the west. Those features can sound impressive when presented without context: several lights, a formation, silence and an unusual trajectory. GEIPAN classified the case B, concluding that it probably involved Thai style sky lanterns. Their colour, slow wind driven movement, apparent ascent and silence matched the account. Two closely dated reports elsewhere in Vienne — another Poitiers observation on 6 September and a Nieuil l’Espoir case on 3 October — received the same probable explanation. Rather than three independent mysteries, the archive points to a recognisable type of misidentification recurring across the department. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPOITIERS (86) 05.09.2009 GEIPAN... The importance of these cases lies less in proving the precise source of each individual light than in showing how easily lanterns generate classic UFO characteristics. At night, distance is hard to judge, wind is not visible and separate lights may appear to maintain an organised formation. A B classification preserves some uncertainty while making clear that an exotic explanation is unnecessary. Spacecraft mistaken for a strange conjunction At Saint Martin l’Ars on 17 March 2009, a report was eventually classified A as the International Space Station and the Space Shuttle. This was a period when the two vehicles could appear as conspicuous moving lights following related paths. Without an orbital check, such a pairing could be remembered as structured or coordinated objects. With accurate timing and satellite data, the sighting became a straightforward aerospace identification. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... Other Vienne reports show the same value"},{"title":"Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple","url":"/why-vosges-ufo-reports-rarely-stay/","description":"The UFO history of Vosges is less a story of one famous “landing” than a revealing collection of roadside lights, aircraft misidentifications, atmospheric...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple The UFO history of Vosges is less a story of one famous “landing” than a revealing collection of roadside lights, aircraft misidentifications, atmospheric... The UFO history of Vosges is less a story of one famous “landing” than a revealing collection of roadside lights, aircraft misidentifications, atmospheric events and a small number of reports that remain unresolved because the evidence is incomplete. vosges Introduction That contrast matters. Vosges offers a useful department level case study in how sincere testimony can describe something genuinely alarming without establishing an exotic craft. It also shows why “unidentified” may mean either that a phenomenon resisted investigation or simply that too little reliable information survived to identify it. The strongest unresolved case On 12 November 1992, at about 5 pm, a motorist travelling on the D23 from Sapois towards Vagney reported a large red, flashing mass approaching the vehicle rapidly and without audible noise. The encounter lasted only a few seconds, but the witness estimated that the phenomenon passed within about 50 metres. GEIPAN describes the report as an observation of a red mass coming very close to a vehicle and retains it in category D: unidentified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D23] de SAPOIS (88) vers VAGNEY (88) 12.11.1992 GEIPAN... This is important within Vosges because it combines several features usually regarded as making a report more interesting: apparent proximity, movement towards the witness, silence and a strong emotional reaction. It is not merely a distant point of light. On the other hand, the official public summary contains no photograph, radar record, physical trace or independent witness account. The estimated distance and size therefore depend on a brief observation made by a frightened driver, circumstances in which perception can be especially uncertain. A category D ruling should not be read as confirmation of an extraordinary vehicle. GEIPAN defines category D as a phenomenon that remains unidentified after investigation, while category C means that identification is prevented by missing or inadequate information. Its assessment considers both the residual strangeness of a report and the consistency, quantity and reliability of the available evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Sapois–Vagney report is therefore the clearest unresolved entry in the department’s official record, but it is not a landmark case supported by instrumentation or multiple independent observers. Its status is best expressed narrowly: something unusual was reported, and the surviving evidence has not produced a satisfactory identification. The Épinal roadside encounter of 1976 An earlier report from Épinal has some of the same close range, road encounter character. At around 4.30 am on 10 July 1976, a motorist said that an unfamiliar craft was flying at low altitude above the road ahead. The witness described a bright red object about four or five metres long and roughly one metre in diameter, with a black band along its length, a raised cabin like feature and a flashing light. After the driver stopped, the object appeared to reverse towards the car, prompting the witness to leave. [Geipan] geipan.fr EPINAL (88) 10.07.1976 GEIPAN... The description is vivid, but the official file illustrates the weakness of a detailed story unsupported by follow up evidence. GEIPAN records that the gendarmerie obtained no additional information, and the case is classified C because reliable data are lacking. There is no publicly documented second witness, photograph, identified flight, measured trajectory or physical effect. [Geipan] geipan.fr EPINAL (88) 10.07.1976 GEIPAN... It is tempting to treat the witness’s estimate of dimensions as evidence that the object was nearby. In reality, an observer cannot reliably calculate the physical size or distance of an unfamiliar airborne light without a known scale. A small nearby object, a larger distant aircraft and a light seen through haze can produce similar angular impressions. GEIPAN itself stresses that witnesses frequently infer speed, distance and size from objects they have not identified, and that emotion, memory and cultural expectations can alter the resulting account. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Épinal case remains locally memorable because of its apparent interaction with the motorist, but its official classification is not “mysterious after a strong investigation”. It is “not identifiable because the investigation had too little material to work with”. The Dompaire triangle that became an aircraft The Dompaire incident of 30–31 March 2007 initially sounded much stronger. Four young witnesses reported white and red lights forming what appeared to be a metallic triangle in a dark, stormy sky. They watched the phenomenon repeatedly over several hours and believed that it moved at changing speeds, travelled back and forth and at one stage passed only 15 to 20 metres above their vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007 GEIPAN... The investigation found a much more ordinary but unusually presented cause. Air traffic enquiries showed that a Swiss Hello AG MD 90, a development of the DC 9, had been conducting repeated “touch and go” training circuits at Épinal–Mirecourt Airport. GEIPAN records 30 practice approaches during the afternoon, followed by further circuits between 7.47 pm and 9.16 pm and a final departure at 9.45 pm. The witnesses’ movements repeatedly placed them beneath the aircraft’s route. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007 GEIPAN... GEIPAN concluded that the group had seen the training aircraft under unfamiliar conditions. Repeated circuits made the lights return in a way that could seem purposeful, while darkness, cloud and the absence of a clear view of the fuselage made it difficult to recognise an aeroplane. A later light seen near 2 am was probably a lamp or vehicle light, an interpretation consistent with the witnesses’ own account of several confusions during the night. The case is now category A, meaning identified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007 GEIPAN... Dompaire is arguably the most instructive Vosges case because it had multiple witnesses, a long duration and a dramatic triangular interpretation. Those features did not prevent a conventional explanation. Instead, they gave investigators enough detail to compare the observations with actual aviation activity. The incident also supplies the department’s clearest aviation connection. It involves a civilian aircraft using Épinal–Mirecourt rather than a secret military programme or unexplained radar target. No equally well documented Vosges case in the official material establishes an unknown object tracked simultaneously by aircrew, military personnel and radar. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube How dramatic lights acquired ordinary explanations Several Vosges files show recurring mechanisms that turn familiar lights into strange looking phenomena. In July 2007, a couple driving from Remiremont towards Dinozé watched a pale blue white, misty oval or ring moving silently across low cloud. It appeared to follow their vehicle, remained visible intermittently for about 35 minutes and was observed over more than 20 kilometres. Investigators linked the effect to a multi beam sky projector used by a nightclub in Épinal. Light projected onto the cloud base created luminous rings whose apparent movement changed with the witnesses’ own motion. GEIPAN classified the case A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDE REMIREMONT (88) VERS DINOZE (88) 06.07.2007 GEIPAN... At Plombières les Bains in September 2013, two witnesses saw five bright red lights arriving successively and moving silently in the same direction. A video was posted online, but a field investigation examined the timing, wind direction, measurements and witness information and located the site of a private release of sky lanterns. GEIPAN classified the observation as fully explained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANPLOMBIERES LES BAINS (88) 08.09.2013 GEIPAN... In Thaon les Vosges in March 2021, a stationary, multicoloured point of light observed at about 11 pm was identified as Sirius. Bright stars low in the sky can flash red, blue and white because atmospheric turbulence refracts their light, particularly when they are viewed through a long path of unstable air close to the horizon. GEIPAN classified the case A and recorded relatively strong"},{"title":"Close Encounters","url":"/close-encounters/","description":"The Sapois-Vagney and Epinal road encounters remain striking, but their official status depends heavily on limited witness evidence.","breadcrumb":"Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple","level":2,"text":"Close Encounters Which Vosges Close Encounters Remain Unexplained? Vosges UFOs Close Encounters Close Encounters Which Vosges Close Encounters Remain Unexplained? The Sapois Vagney and Epinal road encounters remain striking, but their official status depends heavily on limited witness evidence. The Sapois Vagney and Epinal road encounters remain striking, but their official status depends heavily on limited witness evidence. event time window The Sapois Vagney encounter and its category D status The Epinal roadside report and the limits of category C What witness estimates can and cannot establish vosges close encounters Introduction The Vosges close encounters that remain unexplained are not the cases with the most evidence. They are the cases in which a striking personal experience survived, but the information needed to test it did not. Two roadside reports dominate this part of the department’s UFO history: a flashing red mass said to have approached a car between Sapois and Vagney in November 1992, and a craft like object reported above a road near Épinal in July 1976. France’s official investigation body, GEIPAN, places the first in category D, meaning unidentified after investigation. The Épinal report is category C: unidentified because the evidence is inadequate. That distinction is crucial. Neither file contains photographs, radar confirmation, physical traces or independent witnesses. They remain interesting as testimony, but they do not establish that an unknown aircraft — still less an extraterrestrial vehicle — was present. Ovnis inexpliqués près de Rennes : reconstitution filmée du cas d'Étrelles 🛸 Channel: Le Télégramme Open on YouTube The Sapois–Vagney encounter and its category D status On 12 November 1992, a woman driving on the D23 from Sapois towards Vagney reported being frightened by a large flashing red mass that approached rapidly and silently. According to GEIPAN’s public summary, the event lasted only a few seconds and the phenomenon passed at an estimated distance of about 50 metres. The agency states simply that it could not be identified and retains the case in category D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D23] de SAPOIS (88) vers VAGNEY (88) 12.11.1992 GEIPAN... The description has several features that make it stand out from ordinary reports of distant lights. The phenomenon apparently came towards the car rather than merely crossing the sky. It appeared large, close and three dimensional, and no engine or aerodynamic noise was heard. GEIPAN’s witness record describes a single red, roughly ovoid phenomenon, variable apparent motion, total silence and no specified environmental effects. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D23] de SAPOIS (88) vers VAGNEY (88) 12.11.1992 GEIPAN... Those qualities explain why the case occupies an important place in Vosges UFO history. A nearby object apparently interacting with a witness’s route is harder to dismiss casually than an isolated point of light. The emotional reaction also appears immediate rather than a product of later publicity: the driver felt sufficiently threatened to interpret the event as an approach. Yet nearly every apparently precise feature comes from the same brief observation by one person. There is no second viewpoint from which to calculate the object’s position. The published file offers no photograph, radar return, aircraft record, physical damage or trace beside the road. Even the reported distance of 50 metres is an estimate, not a measurement. There is also an unresolved inconsistency in the public record. The case summary says the observation occurred at about 5 pm, while the associated witness data page lists a local time of 19:55 and describes very cloudy or overcast conditions. The surviving online material does not explain the difference. It may be a database entry issue, a later transcription error or information drawn from different parts of the original submission, but it weakens attempts to reconstruct lighting conditions or check possible traffic and astronomical explanations precisely. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D23] de SAPOIS (88) vers VAGNEY (88) 12.11.1992 GEIPAN... What category D actually means GEIPAN’s category D does not certify the presence of an exotic object. It means that, after the available information was considered, no conventional explanation was judged sufficiently convincing. GEIPAN assesses both residual strangeness — how poorly known phenomena fit the report — and consistency , meaning the quantity and reliability of the evidence. A case can therefore remain unexplained while still resting chiefly on testimony. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Sapois–Vagney record is an older, sparsely documented D case rather than one of GEIPAN’s modern investigations supported by a published site reconstruction, detailed hypothesis testing and multiple technical appendices. The database was updated in June 2021, but the public entry does not show that new evidence was obtained or that a fresh explanation was tested. Its continued category D status therefore preserves the original lack of identification; it does not demonstrate that later investigation strengthened the claim. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPAN[D23] de SAPOIS (88) vers VAGNEY (88) 12.11.1992 GEIPAN... A fair assessment is that the report has high narrative strangeness but limited evidential reach . Something alarming was experienced, and the description is not readily reducible to a named phenomenon from the public file. But the material is too thin to distinguish confidently among a genuinely nearby object, a more distant light misjudged as close, an unusual view of an aircraft or vehicle, or a short lived perceptual misunderstanding. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The Épinal roadside report and the limits of category C The earlier Épinal case is more visually elaborate. At about 4.30 am on 10 July 1976, a motorist reported an unfamiliar object flying at low height above the road ahead. The witness described it as approximately four or five metres long and about one metre in diameter, bright red, with a black band running along its length. Its upper section seemed to carry a cabin like structure and a flashing light. When the driver stopped to observe, the object appeared to reverse towards the car, prompting an immediate departure. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEPINAL (88) 10.07.1976 GEIPAN... The surviving gendarmerie statement adds the immediacy of a contemporary record. It indicates that the witness reported the event to the authorities soon afterwards and described following the phenomenon while driving before stopping. This is preferable to an account first published decades later, because it reduces the opportunity for the core story to evolve through repeated retelling. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Nevertheless, the official conclusion is not “unexplained after a strong investigation”. GEIPAN assigns category C and labels the file as lacking reliable information. Its summary says that no further information was collected by the gendarmerie. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANEPINAL (88) 10.07.1976 GEIPAN... That result reflects several decisive gaps: Only one witness is documented. There is no independent observer elsewhere on the road. No instrument recorded the event. The file contains no photograph, radar data or air traffic documentation. The route geometry was not securely reconstructed. The published material does not establish exact bearings, angular dimensions or the object’s position against fixed landmarks. The estimates depend on an assumed distance. A claimed length of four or five metres is meaningful only if the object’s range was known. The apparent reversal is ambiguous. Movement by the witness, changes in viewing angle or the disappearance of familiar visual references can create an impression of approach or retreat. The detail of the description may feel persuasive, but detail and accuracy are not the same thing. A witness can sincerely report colour divisions, lights and a cabin like outline while remaining mistaken about the nature, distance or structure of what was seen. GEIPAN itself stresses that reports often combine the visual stimulus with the observer’s rapid interpretation of an unfamiliar scene. Emotion can increase this effect, particularly during a short encounter that appears threatening. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The Épinal case is therefore unresolved in a weaker sense than Sapois–Vagney. Category C means that the file cannot support either a convincing identification or a robust unexplained classification. The phenomenon has no established explanation, but the absence of an explanation arises mainly from missing data. What witness estimates can and cannot"},{"title":"Explained Sightings","url":"/explained-sightings/","description":"Aircraft circuits, nightclub beams and sky lanterns show how unfamiliar viewing conditions turned ordinary lights into convincing UFO reports.","breadcrumb":"Why Vosges UFO Reports Rarely Stay Simple","level":2,"text":"Explained Sightings How Ordinary Lights Became Vosges UFOs Explained Sightings Explained Sightings How Ordinary Lights Became Vosges UFOs Aircraft circuits, nightclub beams and sky lanterns show how unfamiliar viewing conditions turned ordinary lights into convincing UFO reports. Aircraft circuits, nightclub beams and sky lanterns show how unfamiliar viewing conditions turned ordinary lights into convincing UFO reports. mechanism The Dompaire triangle and the training aircraft Cloud projections, moving vehicles and false pursuit Sky lanterns, repeated lights and field investigation vosges explained sightings Introduction Several of the most dramatic UFO reports from Vosges were eventually traced to ordinary lights seen under unfamiliar conditions. A triangular “craft” near Dompaire matched an airliner repeatedly circling Épinal–Mirecourt airport. A glowing oval that seemed to chase a car between Remiremont and Dinozé was a nightclub projector sweeping the cloud base. Groups of silent red or orange lights at Plombières les Bains and Bussang were identified as sky lanterns released during local festivities. These cases matter because the witnesses were not necessarily careless or dishonest. Each ordinary source produced features commonly treated as signs of something extraordinary: silence, sudden changes of direction, hovering, formation flight and apparent pursuit. GEIPAN, the investigation unit of France’s national space agency CNES, solved the stronger cases by reconstructing routes, checking aviation or event records, comparing wind direction and timing, and returning to the observation sites. The results show how a sincere and vivid sighting can become convincing before the observer has enough information to judge distance, scale or movement. The Dompaire triangle was an aircraft circuit On the night of 30–31 March 2007, four young people travelling around Dompaire, Ubexy and Bouxurulles repeatedly saw red and white lights in a dark, stormy sky. The lights appeared to form a metallic triangle that moved backwards and forwards at changing speeds. At one point, the witnesses believed it passed only 15 to 20 metres above their vehicle. Their observations continued intermittently from about 8 pm until 2 am and left them deeply impressed. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007Elles forment un triangle de couleur métallique qui se déplace en va et vient et à allure variable avant de... Taken on its own, the testimony sounds like a close encounter with a large triangular machine. The investigation became much less mysterious once aircraft movements were checked. A Swiss Hello AG MD 90, a later version of the DC 9 airliner, had been using Épinal–Mirecourt airport for crew training. It made repeated “touch and go” circuits, in which an aircraft lands and immediately takes off again rather than stopping. GEIPAN recorded 30 such approaches during the afternoon, followed by further circuits between 7.47 pm and 9.16 pm and a final departure towards Zurich at 9.45 pm. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007Elles forment un triangle de couleur métallique qui se déplace en va et vient et à allure variable avant de... That repetitive flight pattern explains why the lights appeared, disappeared and returned from different directions. An aircraft flying a circuit alternately approaches the observer, turns across the field of view and heads away. Its landing lights can become intensely bright when directed towards the witness, then fade rapidly during a turn. Red, green and white navigation or anti collision lights can also be perceived as the corners of a single dark object when the aircraft body is invisible against cloud or night sky. The witnesses’ impression that the triangle passed extremely low is less reliable than it first appears. At night, observers usually lack the surface detail, shadows and familiar background objects needed to estimate distance. A large aircraft farther away can therefore look like a smaller object nearby. Its noise may arrive late, be masked inside a vehicle or be reduced by wind, terrain and the direction in which the aircraft is travelling. The sightings reported after the training aircraft had left were also examined. GEIPAN noted that the group had sometimes mistaken street lamps or vehicle lights for the phenomenon while moving between locations. A freight aircraft landed at Épinal at 1.58 am, offering another conventional source for the final airborne light. The agency classified the Dompaire case as category A, meaning fully identified, with the main observations attributed to the MD 90’s training circuits. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDOMPAIRE (88) 30.03.2007Elles forment un triangle de couleur métallique qui se déplace en va et vient et à allure variable avant de... The case is especially instructive because the witnesses’ description was not simply “a plane”. They perceived an object making unusual movements and approaching dangerously closely. The solution came not from dismissing that experience but from matching it with an aircraft whose real flight path naturally produced repeated, changing views. Cloud projections can seem to follow a vehicle A different mechanism produced an equally unsettling encounter on 6 July 2007. At about 1 am, a couple driving from Remiremont towards Dinozé saw a pale blue white, vapour like oval in the cloudy sky. They described an inclined ring with smaller luminous circles around its outer edge. The light moved silently and appeared to follow their car for more than 20 kilometres. They continued to see it after reaching home, and the intermittent observation lasted roughly 35 minutes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDE REMIREMONT (88) VERS DINOZE (88) 06.07.2007Le 6 juillet 2007 aux environs d'une heure du matin, un automobiliste et sa femme von... GEIPAN concluded that the display came from a multi beam sky projector operated by a nightclub in Épinal. Such equipment sends narrow beams upwards and moves them through programmed patterns. When the beams strike low cloud, the observer does not see the complete column of light. Instead, the illuminated patch on the cloud base can resemble a solid oval, ring or group of discs floating in the sky. The identification rested on several converging details. The shape resembled known sky projector patterns; the nightclub possessed the appropriate equipment at the time; and the projector operated on Thursday nights as well as Fridays and Saturdays. The sighting occurred in the early hours of a Friday. Investigators also found that the phenomenon’s visibility and behaviour were consistent with the projected light reaching the cloud layer. A later site reconstruction reproduced the relevant viewing conditions closely enough for GEIPAN to classify the case as category A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANDE REMIREMONT (88) VERS DINOZE (88) 06.07.2007Le 6 juillet 2007 aux environs d'une heure du matin, un automobiliste et sa femme von... The apparent pursuit is a familiar visual effect. A distant light has very little parallax: as the observer moves, it does not shift noticeably against its background. It can therefore remain in roughly the same direction from a moving car, much as the Moon seems to follow a traveller. In the Remiremont case, the projected pattern could also sweep or reappear as the nightclub equipment moved its beams and as gaps or variations in the cloud changed what was visible. Road bends further complicated the impression. When a vehicle turns and a distant light remains visible through successive stretches of road, the observer may interpret the repeated reappearance as deliberate tracking. Fear then changes what receives attention: every return of the light becomes evidence of pursuit, while moments when buildings, trees or hills block it can be remembered simply as temporary disappearances. The quality of the witnesses’ recollections actually helped solve the case. Notes made near the time, a drawing and a careful reconstruction gave investigators enough precise information to test the projector hypothesis. This is an important contrast with poorly documented sightings: detailed testimony does not guarantee an extraordinary answer, but it makes ordinary explanations easier to check rather than merely speculate about. A related but less conclusively documented report occurred at Fresse sur Moselle on 1 September 2012. A yellow circle repeatedly appeared and disappeared in cloud on a Saturday evening. GEIPAN considered a lighting display the probable explanation, although gendarmes did not locate a nearby establishment operating one. Because powerful beams can illuminate clouds from more than ten kilometres away, the agency classified the low strangeness case as category B, meaning probably rather than perfectly identified. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFRESSE SUR MOSELLE (88) 01.09.2012La description du phénomène (cercle jaune apparaissant ou disparaissant dans les nuages) fait pen..."},{"title":"Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record","url":"/why-yonnes-ufo-history-looks-stranger/","description":"Yonne has a substantial local tradition of UFO reporting, but its official record is less dramatic than the folklore might suggest.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record Yonne has a substantial local tradition of UFO reporting, but its official record is less dramatic than the folklore might suggest. Yonne has a substantial local tradition of UFO reporting, but its official record is less dramatic than the folklore might suggest. The department has produced repeated reports of strange lights, formations and apparently structured objects, especially during wider regional or national waves. yonne Introduction Yonne has a substantial local tradition of UFO reporting, but its official record is less dramatic than the folklore might suggest. The department has produced repeated reports of strange lights, formations and apparently structured objects, especially during wider regional or national waves. Yet the most accessible cases in France’s official GEIPAN archive are chiefly identified phenomena or reports lacking enough reliable information for a firm conclusion. Examples have been attributed to meteors, atmospheric re entry, satellites, stars, balloons and celebratory lanterns. [Geipan] geipan.fr MONETEAU (89) 26.08.2016MONETEAU (89) 26.08.2016; Date d'observation. 26/08/2016; Région. Bourgogne; Département. Yonne; Classi... Two periods matter most when placing Yonne in French UFO history. The first is the multi witness Burgundy cluster of September 1988, when similar observations were reported across several communes. The second is 5 November 1990, when witnesses throughout France—including Yonne—saw a spectacular procession of lights later linked by the official investigation to the atmospheric re entry of a Soviet rocket stage. These episodes show why numerous sincere witnesses can describe something extraordinary without establishing that an extraordinary craft was present. [Geipan] geipan.fr COULANGES SUR YONNE (89) 05.09.1988 T1Classification. B. Date of update. 22/03/2007. Type of phenomenon. Phénomène... COULANGES SU... What the official record actually contains France has an unusually open system for handling unusual aerial reports. GEIPAN, part of the French space agency CNES, collects testimony, examines possible explanations and publishes case material. It does not set out to prove extraterrestrial visitation. Reports made to the gendarmerie may be recorded in formal statements and passed to GEIPAN, giving researchers access to contemporary descriptions rather than memories reconstructed decades later. [gouv.fr] gendarmerie.interieur.gouv.fr Gendarmerie National Comment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés Gendarmerie NationalComment la gendarmerie prend elle en compte les étrangetés...July 2, 2022 — 2 Jul 2022 — Une fois les témoignages ét... Published: July 2, 2022 GEIPAN’s classifications are essential to understanding the Yonne material: Category A means the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. Category B means an explanation is considered probable, although the identification may not be completely conclusive. Category C means the available information is too weak or incomplete for useful analysis. Category D is reserved for cases that remain unexplained after investigation and possess sufficient evidential consistency. The distinction between C and D is especially important. A report classified C is not an officially certified mystery; it is usually a case in which investigators cannot reconstruct the event reliably. Yonne’s publicly indexed examples include numerous A, B and C cases, but the department does not emerge from the accessible GEIPAN record as a concentration of strong, well documented D cases. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casJOIGNY (89) 30.03.2009, 30/03/2009, B, Yonne, Bolide, 20/12/2012, 2009 03 30, voir. YONNE (89) 30.09.1977, 26/09/19... Published: March 30, 2009 The geographical spread is broad rather than concentrated around a single military installation or recognised hotspot. Entries appear around Auxerre, Avallon, Sens, Joigny, Monéteau, Pont sur Yonne, Saint Julien du Sault and smaller rural communes. This distribution is compatible with ordinary reporting patterns: sightings tend to occur where people are outdoors, travelling or looking across relatively open horizons. A statistical study of French reports found that population, environmental conditions and human infrastructure can influence where sightings are recorded, cautioning against treating raw case counts as a map of genuinely anomalous activity. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casJOIGNY (89) 30.03.2009, 30/03/2009, B, Yonne, Bolide, 20/12/2012, 2009 03 30, voir. YONNE (89) 30.09.1977, 26/09/19... Published: March 30, 2009 September 1988: a genuine regional cluster One of Yonne’s most notable episodes occurred during the night of 4–5 September 1988. GEIPAN groups reports from Yonne with observations elsewhere in Burgundy and neighbouring areas. Witness locations listed in the official file include Coulanges sur Yonne, Crain, Lucy sur Cure, Courson les Carrières, Saint Brancher, Domecy sur Cure, Quarré les Tombes, Asquins and Blannay, alongside reports from Nièvre and more distant communes. [Geipan] geipan.fr COULANGES SUR YONNE (89) 05.09.1988 T1Classification. B. Date of update. 22/03/2007. Type of phenomenon. Phénomène... COULANGES SU... The number and geographical spread of witnesses make the event historically important. This was not simply one person reporting a fleeting point of light. People in different places described an unusual luminous phenomenon during a limited time window, creating the appearance of a regional “flap”—a period in which reports suddenly multiply. However, multiple witnesses do not automatically establish a solid object. A distant luminous event can be visible across a large territory, while variations in viewing angle, cloud, haze, terrain and expectation produce markedly different descriptions. A train of atmospheric debris, a bright astronomical event or an aircraft seen under unusual conditions may be reported as separate lights, a formation or the illuminated outline of a much larger object. GEIPAN ultimately classifies the Burgundy September 1988 group as B, meaning a conventional explanation was considered probable rather than the case remaining a strong unexplained anomaly. The public entry does not provide a neatly stated identification, which limits how confidently an outside reader can reconstruct the official reasoning. The episode therefore occupies an intermediate position: it was a real cluster of sincere reports, but the official record does not support presenting it as evidence of an unknown craft. [Geipan] geipan.fr COULANGES SUR YONNE (89) 05.09.1988 T1Classification. B. Date of update. 22/03/2007. Type of phenomenon. Phénomène... COULANGES SU... November 1990: Yonne inside a national UFO wave The most famous wave connected with Yonne was not confined to the department. At about 7 p.m. on 5 November 1990, thousands of people across France reported a large luminous display moving generally from west to east. Descriptions included glowing balls, coloured lights, triangles, beams and enormous silent structures. Contemporary gendarmerie statements preserve the immediacy of the experience: some witnesses described a reddish luminous trail accompanied by a larger light and a smaller point, visible for tens of seconds without sound. [Geipan] geipan.fr PV n°600 (1990307266).pdfle 05 novembre 1990 vers 19 heures, de rentrée atmosphérique (SEPRA). la France s'est endormie... Yonne researcher Rémy Fauchereau later devoted a publication specifically to the November 1990 reports in the department. That local documentation matters because national explanations can obscure the diversity of individual testimony. Some observers reported a straightforward procession of lights; others perceived a coherent vehicle or additional objects apparently behaving differently. [yannickpetit.fr] yannickpetit.fr Open source on yannickpetit.fr. The dominant official explanation is atmospheric re entry. CNES and GEIPAN associate the main spectacle with debris from a Soviet Proton rocket stage crossing the atmosphere. A re entry can fragment into several luminous pieces, generating a wide, slowly changing formation. Because the fragments share a common trajectory, the dark spaces between them may be interpreted as the body of an immense craft. Perspective also makes a high altitude event appear lower, slower and closer than it is. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5...10 Mar 2015 — La forte luminosité du phénomène du 5 Novembre 1990 a pu le l... Later modelling strengthened the re entry interpretation. GEIPAN published a new simulation in 2015 and emphasised how brightness, thin cloud and"},{"title":"Official Record","url":"/official-record-36ee65/","description":"Official files show repeated reports across Yonne, yet most are identified, probably explained or too incomplete for a firm conclusion.","breadcrumb":"Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record","level":2,"text":"Official Record Does Yonne's Official UFO Record Support the Folklore? Yonne UFOs Official Record Official Record Does Yonne's Official UFO Record Support the Folklore? Official files show repeated reports across Yonne, yet most are identified, probably explained or too incomplete for a firm conclusion. Official files show repeated reports across Yonne, yet most are identified, probably explained or too incomplete for a firm conclusion. dataset evidence How GEIPAN classifies unusual aerial reports The spread of cases across Yonne Lanterns, meteors, satellites and other explanations yonne official record Introduction France’s official GEIPAN archive supports the view that people in Yonne have repeatedly seen genuinely puzzling things in the sky. It does not, however, provide strong evidence that the department is a centre of extraordinary or technologically inexplicable activity. The clearest pattern is more ordinary: bright stars mistaken for hovering objects, satellites, meteors, balloons, atmospheric re entry and floating lanterns, alongside reports too incomplete for investigators to decide what happened. That distinction matters. GEIPAN, the unit within France’s CNES space agency that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena, records what witnesses reported rather than treating every report as proof of an unknown craft. Its files therefore offer something local folklore cannot: a way to separate a memorable experience from the strength of the evidence behind it. In Yonne, the official record contains sincere and sometimes vivid testimony, but comparatively little that remains both well documented and unexplained after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN How GEIPAN decides whether a case is mysterious GEIPAN’s familiar A, B, C and D labels are not a simple scale running from “uninteresting” to “alien”. Investigators assess two separate qualities: the remaining strangeness of the observation after possible explanations have been tested, and the consistency of the evidence. Consistency includes the number and independence of witnesses, the detail and coherence of their accounts, photographs, accurate directions and timings, and other information that can be checked. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN The resulting categories are best read as follows: A: the phenomenon has been identified with high confidence. B: an ordinary explanation is considered probable, although not completely demonstrated. C: the information is too incomplete or unreliable to support a conclusion. D: the case remains unexplained after investigation and has enough consistency to justify that status. Category C is the one most easily misunderstood. It does not mean that investigators have confirmed a baffling phenomenon. It means that they cannot make a sound judgement. A distant light reported without a precise time, direction, duration or angular movement may remain unidentified simply because the necessary information was never obtained. GEIPAN explicitly describes C cases as non exploitable: neither an explanation nor genuine residual strangeness can be established. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN GEIPANLa méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN This method explains why the Yonne files can contain striking witness language without producing an equally striking official conclusion. The archive asks a different question from local legend. It does not ask whether the experience felt extraordinary; it asks whether the recorded details exclude ordinary causes. The Yonne record is widespread, but not concentrated Reports indexed under Yonne are associated with places across the department, including Auxerre, Avallon, Brion, Joigny, Monéteau, Pont sur Yonne, Saint Julien du Sault, Sens and smaller rural communes. The distribution does not reveal an obvious official hotspot centred on a single airfield, military site or narrow geographical corridor. Instead, it resembles a broad collection of observations made from homes, roads, villages and open countryside. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de casListe des cas; [N77] DE NEUVY SAUTOUR (89) VERS SAINT FLORENTIN (89) 11.05.2023, 11/05/2023, C · Yonne, Manque d'i... Nor does the archive show one distinctive “Yonne phenomenon”. Witnesses have reported stationary points, triangular arrangements of lights, fast luminous bodies, orange objects, silent formations and distant shapes. Yet similar descriptions can arise from very different causes. A triangle may be three separate lights rather than one solid object. An apparently stationary object may be a star. A brilliant green body with a trail may be a meteor. A silent orange light may be a paper lantern moving with the wind. The public index also spans several decades. Entries include cases from the 1970s and early 1980s, later observations around Joigny and Pont sur Yonne, lantern reports in the 2010s, identified objects in 2019 and newer files from the 2020s. This long time range demonstrates continued reporting, not necessarily continued anomalous activity. Changes in available evidence, reporting routes, public awareness, photography and investigative practice make raw comparisons between decades unreliable. [Geipan] geipan.fr YONNE (89) 30.09.1977 26/09/1977 B Yonne Phénomène identifié non indiqué 27/09/2010 1977 09 26... MALEMORT SUR CORREZE (19)... Stars and cameras can create convincing “craft” The Auxerre observation of 17 September 2019 is one of the most useful Yonne files because the witness supplied more information than is available in many brief reports. At about 5.45 am, a bright white phenomenon appeared stationary in the early morning sky. It seemed to change intensity and was described as having fainter lines that produced a V or triangular form. The object remained visible for more than an hour, and photographs appeared to show three lights arranged in a V. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN reconstructed the viewing direction and compared it with the sky at the stated time. Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, occupied the corresponding area and followed a movement consistent with the observation. Its atmospheric scintillation explained the apparent changes in brightness. The supposed V shape in the photographs was attributed chiefly to camera movement: enlarging an unsteady image had turned a point source into an apparently structured pattern. The case was classified A. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The importance of the Auxerre file goes beyond identifying one star. It illustrates why photographs do not automatically make a case stronger. A blurred or hand held exposure can introduce streaks, duplicated points and geometric forms that the eye did not clearly see. In this instance, the supporting material allowed investigators to explain both the original light and the more dramatic feature that appeared in the images. The case also shows that witness credibility and mistaken identification are not opposites. GEIPAN considered the testimony reasonably consistent and detailed. The conclusion was not that the witness invented the event, but that a real astronomical object, atmospheric effects and photographic artefacts combined to produce an unfamiliar impression. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Lanterns explain several silent orange lights Floating lanterns recur in Yonne’s official files because they reproduce several features often associated with UFO reports: silence, warm orange or reddish light, slow or uncertain movement and apparently coordinated formations. At Brion on 3 July 2010, a witness described a flame red object that initially appeared stationary before moving silently southwards. GEIPAN noted the summer weekend timing, the proximity of a venue used for celebrations and weddings, the flame like colour and the object’s behaviour. Even the reported helical appearance could be reproduced by seeing the structure of a lantern from below. The evidence was limited to one witness and a relatively sparse gendarmerie statement, so the lantern explanation was judged probable rather than certain and the case was classified B. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. A similar interpretation was reached at Monéteau on 26 August 2016. A couple saw three orange lights passing silently in a triangular arrangement before trees blocked the view. GEIPAN compared their direction of travel with local wind information and found it compatible with low altitude lanterns. The observation lasted only seconds, leaving room for errors in"},{"title":"Major Waves","url":"/major-waves/","description":"Two mass-sighting episodes shaped Yonne's UFO reputation, but later investigation made both less mysterious than they first appeared.","breadcrumb":"Why Yonne's UFO History Looks Stranger Than Its Record","level":2,"text":"Major Waves How Two Night Sky Waves Shaped Yonne's UFO Story Yonne UFOs Major Waves Major Waves How Two Night Sky Waves Shaped Yonne's UFO Story Two mass sighting episodes shaped Yonne's UFO reputation, but later investigation made both less mysterious than they first appeared. Two mass sighting episodes shaped Yonne's UFO reputation, but later investigation made both less mysterious than they first appeared. event time window The September 1988 Burgundy witness cluster The 5 November 1990 national spectacle What later explanations changed yonne major waves Introduction Yonne ’s modern UFO reputation was shaped less by a succession of isolated mysteries than by two unusually memorable night sky episodes. In the early hours of 5 September 1988, residents across Yonne and neighbouring parts of Burgundy saw a brilliant oval light with a long reddish trail; some then heard several explosions. On 5 November 1990, a much larger national audience watched a silent procession of lights cross France, often interpreting it as an enormous triangle or formation of aircraft. Both episodes involved many sincere witnesses, wide geographical coverage and phenomena dramatic enough to defeat ordinary estimates of distance and size. Yet later investigation substantially reduced their mystery. GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that studies unusual aerospace reports, classifies the 1988 Burgundy cluster as probably explained by a major atmospheric entry. The 1990 spectacle was traced much more firmly to the re entry and fragmentation of a Soviet Proton rocket stage. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGOGNE SEPTEMBRE 1988 GEIPAN... The importance of the two waves therefore lies not in evidence for unknown craft, but in what they reveal about mass sightings: a real and exceptional object can be observed accurately in broad outline while still generating sharply mistaken impressions of altitude, dimensions and structure. The September 1988 Burgundy witness cluster Shortly after 1 a.m. on 5 September 1988, a powerful light crossed the sky over central France. GEIPAN’s summary describes a yellowish or white oval glow, accompanied by a large reddish trail, moving rapidly for only a few seconds. The event was seen across several departments, principally in Burgundy, but reporting was especially concentrated in Yonne. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGOGNE SEPTEMBRE 1988 GEIPAN... The surviving official file demonstrates that this was a genuine multi location event rather than a story built around one dramatic witness. GEIPAN links testimony from numerous Yonne communes, including Lucy sur Cure, Courson les Carrières, Coulanges sur Yonne, Crain, Saint Brancher, Domecy sur Cure, Quarré les Tombes, Asquins and Blannay. Further observations came from Nièvre, Essonne and the Lyon area. The geographical spread matters because people separated by many kilometres were evidently reacting to the same brief phenomenon. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGOGNE SEPTEMBRE 1988 GEIPAN... Individual entries preserve the ordinary, fragmentary character of the observations. A witness at Crain recorded a single white phenomenon at about 1.15 a.m. and reported an explosive bang. At Coulanges sur Yonne, another witness described a single yellow or amber object during a clear night, again accompanied by a detonation or bang. These are not elaborate accounts of windows, occupants or manoeuvring machinery. They are reports of intense light, rapid passage and sound—the basic signature expected from a large object entering or breaking up in the atmosphere. [Geipan] geipan.fr CRAIN (89) 05.09.1988 T6 GEIPAN... Why it initially appeared extraordinary The combination of light and delayed sound made the episode unusually impressive. GEIPAN records four or five detonations around the time the phenomenon disappeared, heard particularly clearly in Yonne and Nièvre. A bright object crossing the sky, apparently followed by explosions, naturally invites interpretations involving an aircraft, missile or solid machine. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGOGNE SEPTEMBRE 1988 GEIPAN... But a few seconds of night time observation provides almost no dependable scale. Without a known distance, the same angular image can be interpreted as a small nearby object or a very large, distant one. A glowing trail can also be read as exhaust, while fragmentation can resemble separate lights travelling in formation. The witnesses were therefore capable of accurately reporting colour, direction and sound without being able to identify the physical source. GEIPAN ultimately placed the collective Burgundy file in Category B, meaning that its preferred explanation is considered highly probable rather than absolutely demonstrated. Its published conclusion is that the witnesses probably saw a large atmospheric entry. The archive does not specify a securely identified parent body, so calling it a proven meteorite or named piece of space hardware would go beyond the official finding. [Geipan] geipan.fr BOURGOGNE SEPTEMBRE 1988 GEIPAN... That distinction is important. The 1988 wave is probably explained , not merely dismissed. Its breadth supports the reality of the observed event, while its light, trail, duration and detonations support a natural or space debris entry rather than a fleet of independently manoeuvring objects. The weaker material attached to the wave Later UFO literature has sometimes treated the September episode as a broader series of encounters, including more elaborate claims said to have occurred later in the night. Such accounts may be interesting as local testimony, but they should not automatically be merged with the well corroborated 1.15 a.m. fireball like passage. The official cluster is strongest where independently reported features converge: a brilliant single phenomenon, a fast crossing, a reddish trail and explosive sounds. Claims involving stationary craft, landings or prolonged activity require their own chronology and supporting records. Similarity of date alone does not establish that they had the same cause. This separation strengthens rather than weakens the historical account. It prevents a well attested atmospheric event from lending borrowed credibility to stories with fewer witnesses or less secure documentation. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The 5 November 1990 national spectacle The second wave was on a different scale. At around 7 p.m. on 5 November 1990, more than a thousand witnesses across France reported a large luminous phenomenon moving through a clear evening sky. Yonne was not the centre of an independent local incident; it lay within the broad part of France from which the spectacle could be seen. Its place in Yonne’s history comes from local participation in a national event whose apparent form closely matched the era’s idea of a giant triangular UFO. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr simulation rentree atmospherique 1990 GEIPANNouvelle simulation de la rentrée atmosphérique du 5 novembre 1990 GEIPAN... Descriptions varied dramatically. Some observers saw a collection of separate lights or glowing fragments. Others perceived one immense solid triangle outlined by orange lights, with an intense white light and a trail behind it. Many thought it was very low, perhaps only hundreds of metres away. Pilots reportedly interpreted it as a formation of aircraft using afterburners. Despite these differences, the principal reports shared a rectilinear passage, a broadly south west to north east direction and an observation time clustered around 7 p.m. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The official response was unusually substantial. SEPRA, GEIPAN’s predecessor within the French space agency CNES, received calls continuously for more than a week. Its summary counted 1,108 documents: 233 gendarmerie statements, 860 reports from private individuals and five civil or military aviation forms. Technicians, pilots, engineers and astronomers were among those who reported the phenomenon. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. The quantity and quality of the witnesses made the episode harder to explain away casually. CNES astronaut Jean Pierre Haigneré had also observed it and regarded it as strange. That did not prove an unconventional craft was present, but it showed that expertise alone did not make the phenomenon immediately recognisable. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. How the 1990 investigation identified the source SEPRA asked NASA, Météo France, observatories and civil and military air navigation services whether an astronomical event or satellite re entry could account for the reports. On 8 November, NASA supplied orbital information for object 1990 094C, associated with the Soviet Gorizont 21 mission. The data placed the descending rocket stage on a course from the Bay of Biscay across France towards Alsace at the relevant time. CNES"},{"title":"What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal","url":"/what-yvelines-ufo-reports-actually/","description":"Yvelines has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but its UFO history is not dominated by one universally recognised landmark incident.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal Yvelines has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but its UFO history is not dominated by one universally recognised landmark incident. Yvelines has a substantial official record of unusual aerial reports, but its UFO history is not dominated by one universally recognised landmark incident. yvelines Introduction The strongest local cases are the 1993 Ecquevilly sighting and the 2003 Buchelay triangle. Both are still classified as unexplained by GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerial and aerospace phenomena. Yet neither produced photographs, radar confirmation, physical traces or corroboration from independent witnesses. They matter chiefly because they show the difference between a genuinely unresolved report and proof of an extraordinary craft. Across Yvelines as a whole, the official archive points less towards a concentrated “hotspot” than towards the recurring difficulty of interpreting distant lights in a busy, densely populated airspace. [Geipan] geipan.fr BUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... The two strongest unresolved cases Ecquevilly, 15 November 1993 At about 4.30 am, an Ecquevilly resident was awakened by barking dogs. According to his account, both the dogs and his horses appeared to be looking in the same direction. From a window, he saw stationary lights apparently illuminating the ground roughly 300 metres west of his home. The lights then approached and passed over the house as a silent circular arrangement of white, yellow and bluish lights. The observation lasted approximately 20 to 25 seconds. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... The witness reported the incident to the gendarmerie nine days later and subsequently supplied sketches. Officers found no physical traces and located no additional witnesses. GEIPAN revisited the file in 2018, using the original statement, drawings and photographs taken by the gendarmes to reconstruct the apparent path and size of the phenomenon. It concluded that the display was probably smaller in angular size than the witness’s drawings suggested, but still too large and too quiet to fit comfortably with its main conventional hypothesis: a nearby helicopter. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... This is arguably the most evidentially interesting Yvelines case because the original investigation took place soon after the event and the surviving documentation allowed a later review. The witness gave a reasonably detailed account, and the claimed silence is difficult to reconcile with a helicopter passing close enough to appear as large as described. GEIPAN therefore retained a D classification, meaning unexplained under the classification system applied to the file. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... The limitations remain considerable. There was only one human witness, the reported reactions of the animals cannot independently establish what caused them, and no image, sound recording, radar return or physical effect was documented. GEIPAN itself described the case as lying near the boundary between unexplained and insufficiently documented, noting that the full field reconstruction expected under modern procedures could not be conducted decades later. The case is unresolved, but not strongly corroborated. [Geipan] geipan.fr ECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... Buchelay, 5 February 2003 The Buchelay report is more dramatic but less extensively documented in the public case summary. At about 6.50 pm, a witness described a large triangular object carrying nine orange rings. It reportedly remained stationary for several seconds, produced a slight aircraft like noise, then turned by roughly 45 degrees and climbed out of sight beneath a cloudy sky. GEIPAN classifies it as an unexplained D case. [Geipan] geipan.fr BUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... Its triangular form invites comparison with reports from the Belgian UFO wave of 1989–91 and with later popular images of silent or slow moving triangular craft. That resemblance is suggestive rather than evidential. A familiar shape in UFO culture does not demonstrate that separate witnesses saw the same kind of object, and Buchelay lacks the independent observations, radar data or aviation records needed to test whether the report concerned an aircraft, several lights seen as one structure, or something genuinely unusual. The case’s official status should therefore be read precisely. GEIPAN judged the described phenomenon sufficiently strange and the available testimony sufficiently usable to leave it unexplained. That does not mean the agency verified the existence, dimensions or performance of a solid triangular craft. The file establishes an unresolved observation by a witness, not a confirmed machine of unknown origin. [Geipan] geipan.fr BUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... Aviation is central, but not evidence of a hidden military pattern Yvelines lies within the complex aviation environment west of Paris. This makes aircraft related reports particularly important when interpreting its UFO record. The departmental archive includes sightings near Rambouillet, Vélizy Villacoublay, Châteaufort and other places where witnesses may see airliners, light aircraft, helicopters or distant landing lights under changing angles and atmospheric conditions. A useful example occurred near Rambouillet in August 2011. A witness filmed a slowly moving point of light that disappeared behind trees and reported having seen a similar phenomenon two months earlier. GEIPAN found the sighting closely resembled an aircraft approaching to land. Because investigators could not identify the particular flight with enough confidence, they used classification B—probably identified—rather than A, fully identified. [Geipan] geipan.fr RAMBOUILLET (78) 10.08.2011 GEIPAN... The distinction is important. An unresolved detail, such as the absence of a confirmed flight number, need not make an observation deeply mysterious. GEIPAN assessed the Rambouillet light as low in strangeness and consistent with an aeroplane, while acknowledging that the available evidence did not permit absolute identification. This is a better description than either “explained beyond doubt” or “unknown craft”. [Geipan] geipan.fr RAMBOUILLET (78) 10.08.2011 GEIPAN... A more unusual aviation report came from an Airbus A320 pilot near Rambouillet on 7 January 2000. The pilot saw two balloons, one blue and one white, beneath the aircraft, apparently carrying something resembling a radar reflector. Météo France stated that they were not its weather balloons, and military enquiries did not establish their origin. GEIPAN nevertheless classified the case B because the nature of the objects—balloons—was not seriously in doubt; only who launched them remained unknown. [Geipan] geipan.fr Geipan[AERO AFR] RAMBOUILLET (78) 07.01.2000 GEIPAN... These cases show why references to airfields, pilots or military enquiries must be handled cautiously. Aviation involvement can strengthen the documentation of a report, but it does not automatically increase its exotic significance. In the Rambouillet balloon case, a professional pilot and official enquiries clarified what the objects were even though their source was never found. No public Yvelines file presently demonstrates a confirmed radar track or military interception of an unexplained craft. UFO hoax fools several French media • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English Open on YouTube Most reports become ordinary once the setting is reconstructed The Yvelines archive contains numerous examples in which a striking first impression gave way to an ordinary explanation. GEIPAN listings identify reports attributed to Venus at Beynes, a kite at Ablis, thunder at Bailly, a photographic insect at Buc, space debris at Chevreuse, meteoroids near Le Chesnay, aircraft at Saint Germain en Laye and Émancé, and lanterns in Châteaufort, Chatou and Conflans Sainte Honorine. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN GEIPANRecherche de cas GEIPAN The 2018 Houilles case illustrates the process. Two witnesses saw a dark, silent, oval looking object against an urban sky. Such a description can sound technically impressive when separated from distance and scale, but those quantities could not be estimated by the witnesses. GEIPAN’s investigation concluded that the object was a balloon and classified the case A. [Geipan] geipan.fr HOUILLES (78) 23.08.2018 T2 GEIPAN... Another instructive episode occurred on 13 March 1989, when witnesses in Yvelines and Brittany saw broad areas of coloured light in clouds. The phenomenon was initially unusual enough to prompt gendarmerie enquiries. A later GEIPAN review compared the duration, colours and scale with"},{"title":"Ordinary Explanations","url":"/ordinary-explanations/","description":"Many Yvelines sightings became less mysterious once investigators reconstructed flight paths, viewing angles and ordinary aerial objects.","breadcrumb":"What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Ordinary Explanations Why So Many Yvelines UFOs Look Like Aircraft Ordinary Explanations Ordinary Explanations Why So Many Yvelines UFOs Look Like Aircraft Many Yvelines sightings became less mysterious once investigators reconstructed flight paths, viewing angles and ordinary aerial objects. Many Yvelines sightings became less mysterious once investigators reconstructed flight paths, viewing angles and ordinary aerial objects. population context How local air traffic shapes unusual sightings Aircraft and balloon cases from Rambouillet Why probably identified does not mean fully proven yvelines ordinary explanations Introduction Many UFO reports in Yvelines became less mysterious once investigators compared witness descriptions with flight paths, wind direction, viewing angles and familiar aerial objects. This is not surprising. The department lies beneath busy western Paris airspace and contains several civil airfields, as well as the military base at Vélizy Villacoublay. Aircraft approaching head on, training flights, helicopters, drifting balloons and sky lanterns can all appear unusually slow, silent or strangely shaped when seen at night or without a reliable sense of distance. [gouv.fr] ecologie.gouv.fr Ministères Écologie et Territoire Rapport Environnement Ministères Écologie et TerritoireRapport Environnement Ministère de la Transition écologiqueJune 2, 2009 — 23 Jul 2008 — Sur les aérodr... Published: June 2, 2009 The official GEIPAN archive therefore tells an important part of Yvelines UFO history through cases that were identified, or probably identified, rather than left unexplained. Reports from Rambouillet, Les Mureaux, Plaisir, Saint Germain en Laye and other communes were linked to aircraft. Others were attributed to balloons or lanterns. These conclusions do not imply that witnesses invented what they saw. They show how ordinary objects can produce genuinely puzzling experiences when only their lights, silhouettes or apparent movements are visible. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANYvelines, Escadrille d'avions, 15/10/2012, 2009 02 27, voir. RAMBOUILLET (78) 10.08.2011, 10/08/2011, B, Y... Published: February 27, 2009 How local air traffic creates unusual sightings Yvelines contains an unusually varied aviation environment. Toussus le Noble, now officially known as Paris Saclay Versailles Airport, supports general aviation; Saint Cyr l’École and Chavenay Villepreux host flying clubs and training activity; Les Mureaux is another local airfield; and Vélizy Villacoublay remains a military base. Direction Générale de l’Aviation Civile material also records circuit and noise management arrangements at Toussus, Saint Cyr, Chavenay and Mantes Chérence, confirming that repeated light aircraft movements form part of the department’s normal soundscape and skyline. [gouv.fr] ecologie.gouv.fr Ministères Écologie et Territoire Rapport Environnement Ministères Écologie et TerritoireRapport Environnement Ministère de la Transition écologiqueJune 2, 2009 — 23 Jul 2008 — Sur les aérodr... Published: June 2, 2009 At night, however, an aircraft is rarely perceived as a complete aircraft. A witness may see a bright landing light before seeing the red, green or flashing navigation lights. When an aeroplane is flying nearly towards the observer, its position against the background can change very little for several seconds. It may consequently appear to hover, brighten or grow larger before suddenly turning and moving sideways. The engine may be difficult to hear because of distance, wind, traffic noise, closed windows or the direction in which the sound is projected. Perspective can also create apparent formations and solid shapes. Three separated lights may be interpreted as the corners of a triangular craft, even when they belong to one conventional aircraft or to several aeroplanes following a similar route. A banking turn can alter which lights are visible and make them seem to rotate, merge or disappear. GEIPAN has used exactly this kind of comparison elsewhere, matching three lights, one of them flashing, and a reported path with an aircraft approaching an aerodrome. [Geipan] geipan.fr NOISIEL (77) 13.03.2009 GEIPANMarch 13, 2009 — La description du phénomène (3 points lumineux se déplaçant ensemble dont 1 \"clign... Published: March 13, 2009 These effects matter especially in Yvelines because observers may be looking across open countryside, wooded horizons or suburban rooftops towards active flying areas. A light can seem close because it is bright, yet brightness alone provides no dependable distance. Once distance is misjudged, estimates of size, speed and silence can all become misleading. Aircraft cases around Rambouillet and western Yvelines The GEIPAN catalogue contains a series of Yvelines reports ultimately associated with aircraft rather than unknown craft. A Rambouillet observation from 10 August 2011 was classified B and attributed to an aeroplane. Under GEIPAN’s system, category B means that the proposed explanation is considered very probable after investigation, although absolute proof has not been obtained. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN... That distinction is important. The classification does not necessarily mean that investigators identified a registration number, pilot and exact flight. It means that the reported appearance and movement were sufficiently consistent with an aircraft for that to be the strongest explanation. In a department with several airfields and extensive general aviation activity, an aeroplane hypothesis is not merely a generic possibility; it can be tested against the direction of travel, duration, lighting pattern, expected local traffic and the witness’s position. Other Yvelines files reinforce the pattern: A Les Mureaux report dated 27 February 2009 was classified B and attributed to a formation of aircraft. A Plaisir sighting on 12 April 2015 was classified B as a light aeroplane. A Magny les Hameaux observation from 6 February 2017 was classified B as an aircraft. A Saint Germain en Laye case from 30 May 2020 was classified A, meaning identified, and attributed to an aeroplane. An Émancé report from 14 September 2024 was later classified A as an airliner. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANYvelines, Escadrille d'avions, 15/10/2012, 2009 02 27, voir. RAMBOUILLET (78) 10.08.2011, 10/08/2011, B, Y... Published: February 27, 2009 The Les Mureaux case is particularly useful as a reminder that multiple lights do not necessarily require one large object. Aircraft flying together, or appearing aligned from a particular viewpoint, can generate a structured display. If witnesses connect the lights mentally, they may perceive a single formation or outline where no solid outline was visible. Rambouillet also appears in an earlier aviation report from 7 January 2000. GEIPAN lists it as an airborne observation, classified B and attributed to a balloon. This case broadens the point: even trained aircrew can encounter an object that is initially difficult to recognise when scale, range and relative motion are uncertain. Professional experience improves the quality of a report, but it does not remove the basic visual ambiguities involved in observing a small object against an empty sky. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPAN Recherche de cas GEIPAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Balloons that look controlled or metallic Balloons are among the most deceptive objects in the Yvelines archive because they can appear to behave in ways that witnesses associate with machines. They may pause in a local current, climb, descend, change direction as winds vary with altitude or flash brightly when sunlight reflects from a metallic surface. A foil balloon can look grey or black from one angle and brilliantly illuminated from another. Several local cases were assigned this explanation. A Carrières sous Poissy report from 1 October 2011 was classified B as a hot air balloon or thermal balloon. A Houilles sighting on 23 August 2018 was classified A as a balloon. The earlier airborne Rambouillet case was also attributed to a balloon. Together they show that balloons were not a marginal explanation applied to one isolated report, but a recurring source of misidentification in different parts of the department. [Geipan] geipan.fr Recherche de cas GEIPANYvelines, Escadrille d'avions, 15/10/2012, 2009 02 27, voir. RAMBOUILLET (78) 10.08.2011, 10/08/2011, B, Y... Published: February 27, 2009 Investigators normally look for several converging clues rather than relying on shape alone. A slow movement compatible with the wind, an absence of engine noise, changes in brightness and an irregular or gently curving path all favour a balloon hypothesis. GEIPAN investigations outside Yvelines illustrate the same method: comparing reported"},{"title":"Unexplained Cases","url":"/unexplained-cases/","description":"The Ecquevilly and Buchelay sightings remain unexplained, but both rely on limited witness evidence rather than physical proof.","breadcrumb":"What Yvelines UFO Reports Actually Reveal","level":2,"text":"Unexplained Cases Why Ecquevilly and Buchelay Remain Unexplained Yvelines UFOs Unexplained Cases Unexplained Cases Why Ecquevilly and Buchelay Remain Unexplained The Ecquevilly and Buchelay sightings remain unexplained, but both rely on limited witness evidence rather than physical proof. The Ecquevilly and Buchelay sightings remain unexplained, but both rely on limited witness evidence rather than physical proof. event time window What witnesses reported at Ecquevilly and Buchelay Why GEIPAN retained an unexplained classification What evidence is missing from both cases yvelines unexplained cases Introduction Yvelines ’ strongest officially unexplained UFO reports are two single witness cases: a silent circle of lights seen at Ecquevilly in November 1993 and a low, triangular object reported at Buchelay in February 2003. Both remain classified D—unexplained—in the public archive of GEIPAN, the French space agency unit that investigates unidentified aerospace phenomena. Yet the two files are not equally strong. Ecquevilly has a prompt gendarmerie inquiry, sketches, site photographs and a detailed later review. Buchelay survives mainly as a brief witness account with no published supporting investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... They therefore deserve attention for a precise reason: investigators could not confidently match either report to a known aircraft, astronomical object or other ordinary cause. That does not make them evidence of an extraterrestrial craft. It means only that the available information left a significant residue of uncertainty—a distinction that is essential when judging Yvelines’ UFO history. What witnesses reported at Ecquevilly and Buchelay The two sightings occurred almost a decade apart and described very different looking phenomena. Their common feature was an apparent object seen nearby for a short period by one person, without independent confirmation. Ecquevilly: silent lights above an isolated home At about 4.30 am on 15 November 1993, an Ecquevilly resident was awakened by his dogs barking unusually. He said that the dogs and his horses were all looking in the same direction. From another room, he then saw lights west of the house, apparently low above the ground and illuminating the surface beneath them. His first thought was that a police helicopter might be searching for a stolen vehicle. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... The absence of noise made him doubt that explanation. He opened the window but still heard nothing. The lights remained stationary for roughly ten seconds, then moved slowly towards the house. As they passed overhead, he described a circular arrangement of strong and weaker white, yellow and slightly bluish lights, together with unusual dark or bluish flashes. The whole episode lasted approximately 20 to 25 seconds. By the time he went outside, the phenomenon had disappeared. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... The witness did not report the event immediately. He gave a statement to the gendarmerie on 24 November, nine days later, and subsequently supplied explanatory drawings. Gendarmes visited the location, photographed the surroundings and conducted enquiries among neighbours, but found no traces and obtained no additional sighting. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... Buchelay: a triangle with nine orange rings The Buchelay report dates from 5 February 2003. GEIPAN records a 30 year old man observing a single triangular phenomenon in an urbanised area during cloudy or partly cloudy conditions. The archive places it roughly 60 to 80 metres from the witness and gives an estimated span of 13 to 15 metres, although such figures appear to derive from the witness’s perception rather than measured geometry. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... According to GEIPAN’s summary, the object carried nine orange ring like lights and emitted a faint sound resembling an aircraft. It remained stationary for several seconds, tilted or turned by approximately 45 degrees, then departed on a rising trajectory and disappeared. The database describes the movement as fast and involving changes of direction and altitude. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... There is a minor time discrepancy within the published material. The case summary says the observation occurred at about 18.50, while the detailed witness entry lists 19.50. The public file does not explain the difference. It is not enough to invalidate the sighting, but it illustrates why the Buchelay record is harder to assess than the better documented Ecquevilly case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why GEIPAN retained an unexplained classification GEIPAN does not use “unexplained” as a synonym for “extraordinary craft”. Its classification weighs both residual strangeness—how difficult the reported behaviour is to explain—and consistency, meaning the quantity and quality of information available for analysis. CNES states that only a small minority of reports remain unidentified after investigation; a far larger share cannot be identified simply because the data are inadequate. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr missions methodes et resultats GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPANSince 2008, a more detailed classification (A/B/C/D1/D2) has been used by the GEIPAN. It is based on 2 mai... Historically, D cases have sometimes been divided into D1 and D2. D1 generally denotes an unusual report of moderate consistency, such as a single witness case without images, while D2 indicates stronger documentation, potentially including independent witnesses, recordings or physical traces. That distinction helps place the Yvelines cases in perspective: neither possesses the kind of multiple source evidence associated with the strongest possible unresolved files. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr nouveaux documents externes GEIPANMise à disposition de nouveaux documents externes2 Oct 2015 — Les PAN D1 qui correspondent. Les PAN D2 qui correspondent … méthodol... Ecquevilly resisted its best conventional hypothesis During a formal re examination completed in 2018, GEIPAN used the original statement, the witness’s sketches and photographs taken by the gendarmes to reconstruct the phenomenon’s apparent route. Investigators concluded that its angular size was probably about five degrees at closest approach—smaller than the drawings initially implied, but still substantial. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... A helicopter was the principal conventional possibility. It fitted parts of the account: low altitude, several lights, slow movement and the witness’s own initial interpretation. GEIPAN nevertheless found it difficult to reconcile with the claimed silence. Its reconstruction suggested that an EC145 sized helicopter would need to have been roughly 55 metres away to appear as large as reported. In quiet night time conditions, with the window open, such an aircraft should have been conspicuously audible. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... Other reported details also weakened the helicopter hypothesis: the unusually large number of lights, the absence of recognisable navigation or anti collision lights, and the witness’s description of illumination affecting the ground. GEIPAN therefore kept the case in category D, assigning relatively high published scores for both strangeness and consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... That conclusion was qualified rather than emphatic. The investigators explicitly described Ecquevilly as lying near the boundary between an unexplained case and one that was insufficiently documented. Because the re examination took place 25 years later, GEIPAN could not conduct the kind of prompt field reconstruction and cognitive interview that its modern methodology would normally require. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANECQUEVILLY (78) 15.11.1993 GEIPAN... Buchelay remains unexplained largely because the file is sparse Buchelay’s D classification rests on a report that is visually distinctive and not readily matched to an obvious explanation: a large triangle apparently hovering at close range before banking and climbing rapidly. A conventional aircraft could account for the faint aircraft like sound and perhaps the orange lights, but ordinary fixed wing aircraft do not remain stationary, while a helicopter does not naturally match the reported triangular body and nine ring lighting pattern. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANBUCHELAY (78) 05.02.2003 GEIPAN... However, the public archive gives no comparable technical discussion, site reconstruction or elimination of alternatives. It does not"}]};
